tangular piece of paper as described in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Making greeting card\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/070_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many corners can you count on the rectangular paper?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"By looking at the rectangular paper in the figure, we can count 4 corners.\",\"topic\":\"Making greeting card\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/070_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text suggest making for different occasions?\",\"options\":[\"Paper fans\",\"Greeting cards\",\"Wooden toys\",\"Clay models\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly tells the student to make different types of greeting cards as per the occasions.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many sticks of equal length are needed to try making a square?\",\"options\":[\"3 sticks\",\"4 sticks\",\"5 sticks\",\"6 sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks, \'Can you make a square using four sticks of equal length?\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To make a square, the four sticks used must be of ______.\",\"options\":[\"different lengths\",\"short lengths\",\"equal length\",\"long lengths\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the sticks used for the square must be of \'equal length\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many sticks of equal length are used to try making a rectangle?\",\"options\":[\"4 sticks\",\"5 sticks\",\"6 sticks\",\"8 sticks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks, \'Can you make a rectangle using 6 sticks of equal length?\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have 6 sticks of equal length, which shape besides a rectangle can you try to make?\",\"options\":[\"A square\",\"A circle\",\"A triangle\",\"A star\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks, \'Can you make a triangle using 6 sticks of equal length?\' right after mentioning the rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many sticks of equal length are mentioned to try making two triangles?\",\"options\":[\"4 sticks\",\"5 sticks\",\"6 sticks\",\"3 sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks, \'Can you make two triangles using 5 sticks of equal length?\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these shapes requires the fewest sticks to make a single shape according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Square\",\"Rectangle\",\"Triangle using 6 sticks\",\"Greeting card\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The square uses 4 sticks, while the rectangle and the triangle mentioned use 6 sticks.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which two shapes can be attempted using exactly 6 sticks of equal length?\",\"options\":[\"Square and Triangle\",\"Rectangle and Triangle\",\"Square and Rectangle\",\"Two triangles and a Square\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using 6 sticks for both a rectangle and a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first activity mentioned in the topic text?\",\"options\":[\"Making a square\",\"Making a triangle\",\"Making greeting cards\",\"Making a rectangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first sentence of the text is \'Make different types of greeting cards as per the occasions.\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The text asks if you can make a triangle using 6 sticks of equal length. This means the sticks must all be ______.\",\"options\":[\"the same size\",\"different sizes\",\"broken into pieces\",\"curved like a circle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Equal length means all sticks are the same size.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21340,7,2,'Maths','en','Addition (with carry-over)','[\"Addition means finding the total when two or more numbers are put together.\",\"When adding numbers, start from the ones place and move to the tens place.\",\"If the sum in the ones place is 10 or more, carry over the extra tens to the tens place.\",\"Carry-over means moving the tens digit of a sum to the next higher place value.\",\"Example: In 24 + 16, 4 + 6 = 10, write 0 in ones and carry 1 to tens place.\",\"Then add tens: 2 + 1 + 1 (carry) = 4, so the answer is 40.\",\"Number line helps visualize addition by making forward jumps equal to the second number.\",\"A jump of ten means moving forward by 10 steps on the number line or number strip.\",\"Using a number chart (1 to 100), find the first number and move down or across to add.\",\"Moving down one row on the chart adds 10; moving right adds 1.\",\"Beads or sticks can be grouped in tens to make counting easier during addition.\",\"Always check if the ones place sum needs a carry-over before adding the tens.\",\"Practice with real-life examples like adding money or counting objects helps understanding.\",\"Addition is commutative: changing the order of numbers does not change the sum.\",\"Zero added to any number gives the same number.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what do ten ones together make?\",\"options\":[\"One jump of five\",\"One jump of tens\",\"Two jumps of tens\",\"Six unit jumps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Ten ones together make one jump of tens.\'\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many steps of one must you count forward on a number line to add 16 to 24?\",\"options\":[\"10 steps\",\"6 steps\",\"16 steps\",\"24 steps\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to take 16 jumps by counting forward by steps of one starting from 24.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of 24 + 16 as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"30\",\"34\",\"40\",\"46\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states 24 + 16 = 40 across all methods.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the jump of tens method, how is the number 16 split to add it to 24?\",\"options\":[\"8 + 8\",\"10 + 6\",\"12 + 4\",\"15 + 1\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the breakdown as 24 + 16 = 24 + 10 + 6.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the jump of tens method used for addition?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the numbers smaller\",\"It is done slowly\",\"It was done quickly\",\"It uses more beads\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'By using jumps of tens, addition was done quickly.\'\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When using a string of beads to add 24 + 16, what is the first step?\",\"options\":[\"Count 24 beads ahead of 16\",\"Count 10 beads ahead of 24\",\"Count 16 beads ahead of 24\",\"Count 6 beads ahead of 10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'For performing this addition using string of beads, count 16 beads ahead of 24.\'\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To add 16 using the jump of tens method on a number strip, how many unit jumps are needed after the jump of ten?\",\"options\":[\"1 unit jump\",\"10 unit jumps\",\"6 unit jumps\",\"16 unit jumps\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'take one jump of ten and 6 unit jumps\'.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are using the \'jump of tens\' method for 24 + 16, which calculation represents this correctly?\",\"options\":[\"24 + 1 + 6\",\"24 + 10 + 6\",\"20 + 4 + 1\",\"24 + 10 + 10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the jump of tens addition as 24 + 10 + 6.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which method mentioned in the text involves taking \'steps of one\'?\",\"options\":[\"Addition on the number line\",\"Addition by jump of tens\",\"Addition using string of beads\",\"Addition by counting tens\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifies counting forward by steps of one when performing addition on the number line.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the methods: Which one is specifically described as \'fun\' and \'quick\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Counting beads\",\"Steps of one on a number line\",\"Jump of tens\",\"Counting backwards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text titles the section \'Fun with Jump of Tens\' and notes it was done quickly.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Jump of Tens\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is mentioned in the text to solve the addition 26 + 7?\",\"options\":[\"A ruler\",\"1 to 100 number chart\",\"A set of beads\",\"A calculator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the example 26 + 7 is solved using the 1 to 100 number chart.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific action is mentioned for solving 26 + 7 on the number chart?\",\"options\":[\"Jump of ones\",\"Jump of tens\",\"Jump of fives\",\"Skip counting by two\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'Jump of ones\' specifically for the example 26 + 7.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which addition problem does the text ask to solve using another method?\",\"options\":[\"26 + 7\",\"26 + 17\",\"24 + 16\",\"1 to 100\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text begins by asking \'Which other method can be used for the addition of 24 + 16 = ?\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, the 1 to 100 number chart is used for which operation?\",\"options\":[\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\",\"Addition\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text describes using the chart for the addition of examples like 26 + 7 and 26 + 17.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides 26 + 7, which other example is solved using the 1 to 100 number chart?\",\"options\":[\"26 + 17\",\"24 + 16\",\"1 + 100\",\"7 + 17\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists 26 + 17 as an example solved using the 1 to 100 number chart.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you are using the 1 to 100 number chart for addition, what are you expected to do after observing?\",\"options\":[\"Memorize the chart\",\"Understand the method\",\"Draw the chart\",\"Color the numbers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Observe carefully and understand\' regarding the examples on the chart.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To solve 26 + 7 using the \'Jump of ones\' method on a chart, how many jumps would you take?\",\"options\":[\"26 jumps\",\"1 jump\",\"7 jumps\",\"100 jumps\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In addition on a number chart, the second number (7) represents the number of \'ones\' jumps to be taken.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the common method used for both 26 + 7 and 26 + 17?\",\"options\":[\"Using a 1 to 100 number chart\",\"Using a vertical arrangement\",\"Using fingers\",\"Using mental math only\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states both examples are solved using the 1 to 100 number chart.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to apply the chart method to 24 + 16, what tool would you need?\",\"options\":[\"A number line to 20\",\"A 1 to 100 number chart\",\"A multiplication table\",\"A geometry box\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests the 1 to 100 number chart as the primary method for these addition examples.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text ask to \'Observe carefully and understand\' the chart examples?\",\"options\":[\"To learn how to draw numbers\",\"To learn how to count to 100\",\"To learn the method of addition using the chart\",\"To find errors in the chart\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The instruction is given so students can learn how the addition examples are solved using the chart.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21341,7,2,'Maths','en','Subtraction (by untying ten and counting backward)','[\"Subtraction means taking away one number from another.\",\"When subtracting, we can untie a ten to make more units if needed.\",\"Untying one ten gives 10 units to help subtract larger unit numbers.\",\"After untying, subtract the units first, then the tens.\",\"Example: 15 - 9: untie one ten from 15 to get 5 tens? No, 15 is 1 ten and 5 units. Untie the ten to get 0 tens and 15 units, then subtract 9 units to get 6.\",\"Vertical arrangement helps organize tens and units columns.\",\"Always start subtracting from the units column.\",\"If units in the top number are smaller, untie a ten from the tens column.\",\"After untying, add 10 to the units before subtracting.\",\"The tens column reduces by one when a ten is untied.\",\"Subtraction by counting backward means counting back the second number from the first.\",\"On a number strip, jump backward by the number being subtracted.\",\"Each jump back is one unit; count the jumps to find the difference.\",\"You can also jump back by tens first, then by units for faster subtraction.\",\"Check your answer by adding the difference to the subtracted number to get the original number.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which tool is mentioned in the text to help do the subtraction of 20 - 16?\",\"options\":[\"Number strip\",\"Ruler\",\"Clock\",\"Blocks\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifically asks to do the subtraction of 20 - 16 with the help of the number strip.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the number strip, what other object is used to practice subtraction by counting backward?\",\"options\":[\"Crayons\",\"Strings of beads\",\"Marbles\",\"Sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions practicing subtraction by counting backward using strings of beads and number strip both.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the subtraction 32 - 18, what is the alternative to \'Jump of Units\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Jump of Fives\",\"Jump of Twos\",\"Jump of Tens\",\"Jump of Hundreds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Subtract by Jump of Units 32 – 18\' and \'Subtract by Jump of Tens 32 – 18\'.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When practicing subtraction by counting backward, what should the second number in the pair contain?\",\"options\":[\"Only units\",\"Zero tens\",\"More than one tens\",\"No numbers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to take \'more than one tens in the second number\' for the pair of numbers taken for subtraction.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are using the \'Jump of Units\' method for 36 - 19, what are you counting?\",\"options\":[\"Tens\",\"Ones\",\"Fives\",\"Twos\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Jump of Units refers to counting by ones\\/units.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific subtraction problem is used in the text to demonstrate both \'Jump of Units\' and \'Jump of Tens\'?\",\"options\":[\"20 - 16\",\"36 - 19\",\"10 - 5\",\"15 - 7\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Subtract by Jump of Units 36 – 19\' and \'Subtract by Jump of Tens 36 – 19\'.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What method is being practiced when we use a number strip to move from a larger number to a smaller number?\",\"options\":[\"Counting forward\",\"Counting backward\",\"Addition\",\"Multiplication\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The topic title and text focus on \'Practice counting backward\' for subtraction.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the instructions, if the first number in a subtraction pair has one ten, the second number should have:\",\"options\":[\"Zero tens\",\"Exactly one ten\",\"More than one tens\",\"No units\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'take one ten and more than one tens in the second number\'.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a valid method for subtraction mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Jump of Units\",\"Jump of Threes\",\"Jump of Fours\",\"Jump of Sixes\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions \'Subtract by Jump of Units\'.\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which two tools are paired together for practicing subtraction?\",\"options\":[\"Number strip and Ruler\",\"Strings of beads and Number strip\",\"Beads and Fingers\",\"Number strip and Abacus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Practice subtraction by counting backward using strings of beads and number strip both.\'\",\"topic\":\"Practice counting backward\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is suggested in the text to solve the subtraction examples?\",\"options\":[\"A ruler\",\"A number chart of 1 to 100\",\"A calculator\",\"An abacus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the examples are to be solved using a number chart of 1 to 100.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example 46 - 7, what specific method is mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Ten jump\",\"Unit jump\",\"Hundred jump\",\"Double jump\"],\"ans