ost with ₹ 70.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Suraj bought two toys for ₹ 25 and ₹ 15. What is the total price of these two toys?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 30\",\"₹ 35\",\"₹ 40\",\"₹ 45\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"₹ 25 + ₹ 15 equals ₹ 40, which is Suraj\'s total amount.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Nihal spends all his money on two toys, what will the total amount be?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 40\",\"₹ 55\",\"₹ 65\",\"₹ 70\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Nihal has ₹ 70, so the total for his toys would be ₹ 70.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Renuka buys two toys with all her money, how much money will the shopkeeper get?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 40\",\"₹ 55\",\"₹ 65\",\"₹ 70\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Renuka has ₹ 55, so the shopkeeper receives ₹ 55.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To get the highest amount of money from selling two toys, the shopkeeper should look at the friend with:\",\"options\":[\"The least money\",\"The most money\",\"Exactly ₹ 40\",\"Exactly ₹ 55\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The highest amount comes from the person with the largest budget, which is Nihal.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the four friends, whose toy purchase would give the shopkeeper the highest amount?\",\"options\":[\"Suraj\'s toys\",\"Renuka\'s toys\",\"Shabana\'s toys\",\"Nihal\'s toys\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Nihal has the highest amount (₹ 70) to spend on toys.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the highest total amount mentioned in the text that can be spent on toys?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 40\",\"₹ 55\",\"₹ 65\",\"₹ 70\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"₹ 70 is the maximum value provided in the list of friends\' money.\",\"topic\":\"Highest Amount Calculation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21346,7,2,'Maths','en','Use of Multiplication','[\"Multiplication means repeated addition of the same number.\",\"The symbol for multiplication is \'×\'.\",\"The result of multiplication is called the product.\",\"Multiplying any number by 1 gives the same number.\",\"Multiplying any number by 0 gives 0.\",\"The order of numbers in multiplication does not change the product (commutative property).\",\"2 × 3 means 2 added 3 times: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\",\"Multiplication helps to find total when items are in equal groups.\",\"We can use multiplication to find total wheels, total cost, total objects, etc.\",\"Multiplication tables help us remember products of numbers from 1 to 10.\",\"To multiply, we can count in rows or columns — both give the same answer.\",\"Example: 3 rows of 4 = 3 × 4 = 12, same as 4 columns of 3 = 4 × 3 = 12.\",\"Use real-life examples like coins, books, wheels, kites to understand multiplication.\",\"Always check: number of groups × number in each group = total.\",\"Practice multiplication facts to solve problems quickly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who counted the children using rows?\",\"options\":[\"Pinky\",\"Chintu\",\"The teacher\",\"The drill master\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Chintu counted the children using rows.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where were the children standing for the drill?\",\"options\":[\"In the classroom\",\"In the garden\",\"In the school ground\",\"In the library\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that children were standing in the school ground for a drill.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What method did Pinky use to count the children?\",\"options\":[\"Using rows\",\"Using columns\",\"Counting one by one\",\"Using fingers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Pinky counted using columns.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text say about the answers Chintu and Pinky got?\",\"options\":[\"Chintu\'s answer was bigger\",\"Pinky\'s answer was bigger\",\"Both answers are the same\",\"Both answers are different\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Both answers are the same\'.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To find the total number of children using rows, what do we multiply the number of children in one row by?\",\"options\":[\"Number of columns\",\"Number of rows\",\"Total number of children\",\"Number of drills\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Number of children in one row × Number of rows = Total number of children.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical sign is used in the text to calculate the total number of children?\",\"options\":[\"+\",\"-\",\"×\",\"÷\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the multiplication sign (×) to calculate the total number of children.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many children are there in one column?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"2\",\"6\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows \'Number of children in one column 2\'.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/109_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the total number of children shown?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"6\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the calculation \'2 × 3 = 6\', where 6 is the total number of children.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/109_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If there are 2 children in one column and there are 3 columns, how many children are there in total?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"4\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula from the text, 2 × 3 = 6.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If there are 3 children in one row and there are 2 rows, how many children are there in total?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"3\",\"2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the row method (3 children × 2 rows), the answer is 6, which is the same as the column method.\",\"topic\":\"Use of Multiplication\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the answer to 4 × 2?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"8\",\"2\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, 4 × 2 = 8.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many wheels does one tricycle have?\",\"options\":[\"2 wheels\",\"4 wheels\",\"3 wheels\",\"5 wheels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that one tricycle has 3 wheels.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we have 4 books in 2 groups and 2 books in 4 groups, are the total answers the same?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, they are the same\",\"No, they are different\",\"One is bigger\",\"One is smaller\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 4 × 2 and 2 × 4 both equal 8, so the answers are the same.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of 4 × 5?\",\"options\":[\"9\",\"15\",\"20\",\"25\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on multiplication facts, 4 times 5 is 20.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The price of one eraser is ₹5. What is the total cost of 5 erasers?\",\"options\":[\"₹10\",\"₹20\",\"₹25\",\"₹15\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cost of one eraser (5) × Number of erasers (5) = 25.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Shankar has 10 coins of ₹2 each. What is the total amount of money he has?\",\"options\":[\"₹12\",\"₹20\",\"₹8\",\"₹10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Value of one coin (2) × Number of coins (10) = 20.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If 3 × 4 = 12, then what is the value of 4 × 3?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"12\",\"1\",\"10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that changing the order in multiplication (like 4x2 and 2x4) gives the same answer.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To find the total wheels of 5 tricycles, which multiplication should we do?\",\"options\":[\"3 × 5\",\"3 + 5\",\"5 - 3\",\"3 × 3\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states: Number of wheels in one tricycle × Number of tricycles = Total wheels.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, there are 5 tricycles. If each tricycle has 3 wheels, how many wheels are there in total?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"12\",\"15\",\"18\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"There are 5 tricycles, and 5 groups of 3 wheels is 5 × 3 = 15.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/109_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, there are 4 trees in one column. How many total trees are there in 5 such columns?\",\"options\":[\"9\",\"16\",\"20\",\"25\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Trees in one column (4) × Number of columns (5) = 20 total trees.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/110_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, there are 8 kites. If the price of one kite is ₹4, what is the total cost of all these kites?\",\"options\":[\"₹12\",\"₹28\",\"₹32\",\"₹36\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Price of one kite (4) × Number of kites (8) = 32.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/110_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the price of one kite?\",\"options\":[\"₹5\",\"₹8\",\"₹4\",\"₹2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'The price of one kite is ₹4\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solve word problems\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the table used to find multiplication answers in this topic?\",\"options\":[\"Addition Table\",\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"Number Line\",\"Subtraction Grid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the table as a \'Multiplication Table-Grid\'.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the example shown in the grid, what is 5 × 3?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"10\",\"15\",\"20\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 5 × 3 = 15 is shown as an example.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of 1 × 8 as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"7\",\"8\",\"9\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists 1 × 8 = 8 as an example of multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are students asked to observe after coloring a multiplication table?\",\"options\":[\"The colors\",\"The pattern\",\"The size\",\"The paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs students to \'Observe the pattern so formed in the table.\'\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 4 × 2 = 8, which other multiplication gives the same answer 8 according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"2 × 2\",\"2 × 4\",\"4 × 4\",\"1 × 4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists 4 × 2 = 8 and 2 × 4 = 8 as examples giving the same answer.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the multiplication grid, what number is written in the box where row 5 and column 5 meet?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"20\",\"25\",\"30\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The grid shows that 5 × 5 = 25.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which number is circled in the first column to start the example calculation?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"3\",\"5\",\"15\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the figure, the number 5 is circled in the leftmost column.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/111_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the grid, what is the answer for 10 × 1?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"10\",\"11\",\"0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The grid shows that 10 × 1 = 10.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which multiplication is shown at the very top of the pink box?\",\"options\":[\"1 × 8 = 8\",\"4 × 2 = 8\",\"2 × 4 = 8\",\"8 × 1 = 8\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first example in the pink box in the figure is 1 × 8 = 8.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/111_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If we know 2 × 3 = 6 from the grid, what is 3 × 2?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"9\",\"3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The grid shows that 2 × 3 and 3 × 2 both result in 6.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the grid, the row for 3 shows: 3, 6, 9. What number should be in the next empty box?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"11\",\"12\",\"15\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern of 3s (3 × 4), the next number is 12.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which multiplication from the grid results in the answer 16?\",\"options\":[\"3 × 4\",\"4 × 4\",\"5 × 3\",\"2 × 5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The grid shows the number 16 at the intersection of row 4 and column 4.\",\"topic\":\"Multiplication Table-Grid\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many books did the man have with him at the beginning?\",\"options\":[\"8 books\",\"6 books\",\"4 books\",\"2 books\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text starts with the man saying, \'I have 8 books with me.\'\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Between which two children were the books being shared?\",\"options\":[\"Priya and Hemant\",\"Priya and the teacher\",\"Hemant and his friend\",\"Two students from class\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text addresses the children as \'Priya and Hemant\'.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what objects can be used to learn equal sharing?\",\"options\":[\"Jodo-blocks\",\"Crayons\",\"Pencils\",\"Paper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using \'objects\\/pictures\\/jodo-blocks\\/symbols\' for practice.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many books did Hemant get at the very end of the distribution?\",\"options\":[\"2 books\",\"8 books\",\"4 books\",\"6 books\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"At the end, Hemant says, \'I also got 4 books\'.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What mathematical concept is equal sharing related to, as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Repeated addition\",\"Repeated subtraction\",\"Measurement\",\"Counting backwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states students should understand \'equal sharing and repeated subtraction\'.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When 6 books were left and the man gave one more book to each child, how many books were left then?\",\"options\":[\"5 books\",\"2 books\",\"4 books\",\"0 books\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Alright, I will give one more book to each one of you... Now, 4 books are left.\'\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the man gives one book to Priya and one book to Hemant in one turn, how many books are removed from his hand in total during that turn?\",\"options\":[\"1 book\",\"2 books\",\"3 books\",\"4 books\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Giving one to each of the two children means 1 + 1 = 2 books are distributed.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many books is the man holding in his hands to start the sharing?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"6\",\"8\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The man is holding a stack of 8 books, which matches the text \'I have 8 books with me.\'\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/112_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what request does Priya make to the man?\",\"options\":[\"To give her all the books\",\"To give her more books than Hemant\",\"To give Hemant more books\",\"To not share the books at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Priya\'s speech bubble in the figure says, \'Give me more books than Hemant!\'\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/112_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If there are 8 books and they are shared equally between 2 people, how many does each person get?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"8\",\"1\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that sharing 8 books between Priya and Hemant results in 4 books each.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The man said, \'Now 2 books are left. I will give one book each to both of you.\' After this step, how many books are left to distribute?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"1\",\"0\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"When the last 2 books are given (one to each), the text says \'Now all the notebooks are distributed,\' meaning 0 are left.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the man decide to give the books one by one to each child instead of giving Priya more?\",\"options\":[\"Because he wanted to keep the books\",\"To make sure he gave equal notebooks to both\",\"Because Hemant asked for more\",\"Because he forgot how to count\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The man says, \'No, I will give equal notebooks to both of you,\' to ensure fairness.\",\"topic\":\"Equal Sharing\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who are the students sharing the pencils in the first activity?\",\"options\":[\"Mohan, Mayuri and Sonali\",\"Sarina, Kunal and Ikara\",\"Mohan, Kunal and Sonali\",\"Mayuri, Sarina and Ikara\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions Mohan, Mayuri and Sonali as the students sharing pencils.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many children are sharing the marbles in the second activity?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The marbles are distributed among Sarina, Kunal, and Ikara, which makes 3 children.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does it mean to \'distribute equally\' among the students?\",\"options\":[\"Give all pencils to one student\",\"Give different numbers to each student\",\"Give each student the same number of items\",\"Give pencils only to the first student\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Equal distribution means every participant receives the same quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these children is part of the marble sharing activity?\",\"options\":[\"Mohan\",\"Mayuri\",\"Ikara\",\"Sonali\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Sarina, Kunal, and Ikara as the children sharing marbles.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the very first thing you must do before distributing the pencils?\",\"options\":[\"Write the names\",\"Count and write the total number\",\"Give them to Mohan\",\"Sharpen the pencils\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text starts with the instruction to \'Count & write the total number of pencils\'.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If there are 6 pencils in total, how many pencils will Mohan, Mayuri, and Sonali each get if shared equally?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"6 pencils shared equally among 3 students (6 ÷ 3) equals 2 pencils each.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If there are 9 marbles to be shared among Sarina, Kunal, and Ikara, how many marbles does each child receive?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"9\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"9 marbles shared equally among 3 children (9 ÷ 3) equals 3 marbles each.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Mayuri receives 4 pencils in an equal distribution, how many pencils must Sonali receive?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In an equal distribution, every student gets the same number, so Sonali must get 4 if Mayuri got 4.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If Sarina, Kunal, and Ikara each receive 2 marbles, what was the total number of marbles?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"3 children getting 2 marbles each means 2 + 2 + 2 = 6 total marbles.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where should you write the number of pencils each student gets?\",\"options\":[\"At the top of the page\",\"Next to their names\",\"Under the total number\",\"On the back of the book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Write the number of pencils each one gets next to their names.\'\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have 3 students but only 2 pencils, can you distribute them equally so that everyone gets at least one pencil?\",\"options\":[\"Yes\",\"No\",\"Maybe\",\"Only if you count again\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"You cannot give 3 people one pencil each if you only have 2 pencils total.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which group of names belongs to the marble activity?\",\"options\":[\"Mohan, Mayuri, Sonali\",\"Sarina, Kunal, Ikara\",\"Mohan, Sarina, Kunal\",\"Mayuri, Sonali, Ikara\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text groups Sarina, Kunal, and Ikara together for the marble distribution.\",\"topic\":\"Count and distribute\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the equal distribution of objects in a group called?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Division\",\"Multiplication\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that equal distribution of objects in a group is called Division.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What method is used to make equal groups according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Repeated Addition\",\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"Repeated Multiplication\",\"Counting forward\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using \'Repeated Subtraction to make equal groups\'.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many total guavas were brought by the mother?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"2\",\"8\",\"0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure explicitly states \'Total guavas brought by mother = 8\'.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/114_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If Meera has 8 guavas and takes out one group of four, what is the subtraction sentence?\",\"options\":[\"8 - 2 = 6\",\"8 - 4 = 4\",\"4 - 4 = 0\",\"8 - 8 = 0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows 8 - 4 = 4 for the first group of four guavas.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which subtraction sentence shows that no guavas are left after making the second group?\",\"options\":[\"8 - 4 = 4\",\"4 - 2 = 2\",\"4 - 4 = 0\",\"2 - 2 = 0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the second group with the calculation 4 - 4 = 0.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many times was the number 4 subtracted from the number 8 in the example?\",\"options\":[\"1 time\",\"2 times\",\"4 times\",\"8 times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that the number 4 was subtracted from the number 8 at the most 2 times.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many guavas are in each group made by Meera?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"2\",\"4\",\"0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says Meera made groups of four each.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When Meera makes groups of four from 8 guavas, how many total groups does she get?\",\"options\":[\"4 groups\",\"2 groups\",\"8 groups\",\"1 group\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Meera states, \'Mother, I made two groups each of four guavas.\'\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the total number of guavas when a group of four is taken out?\",\"options\":[\"It increases by 4\",\"It stays the same\",\"It is subtracted by 4\",\"It is multiplied by 4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The process uses repeated subtraction, so 4 is subtracted from the total each time.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"From 8 guavas, what is the most number of times 4 guavas can be taken out?\",\"options\":[\"4 times\",\"8 times\",\"1 time\",\"2 times\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that from 8 guavas, 4 guavas can be taken out at the most 2 times.\",\"topic\":\"Repeated Subtraction\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21347,7,2,'Maths','en','Division','[\"- Division is sharing a set of objects equally among a number of people or groups.\",\"- The result of division is called the *quotient* – the number each person gets.\",\"- Division can be done by repeated subtraction: keep taking away the same number until nothing is left.\",\"- When the same number can be taken away many times, the number of times it is taken is the quotient.\",\"- If after taking away the same number there is nothing left, the division is exact.\",\"- In maths, division is written with the symbol ‘+’ (e.g., 10 + 2 = 5 means 10 divided by 2 equals 5).\",\"- To find the number of groups, divide the total number of objects by the size of each group.\",\"- If you have 8 books and 2 friends, each gets 4 books (8 ÷ 2 = 4).\",\"- If you have 10 stones and 2 people, each gets 5 stones (10 ÷ 2 = 5).\",\"- Division helps us understand equal sharing and repeated subtraction.\",\"- Practising with real objects (books, stones, marbles) makes division clear.\",\"- The total number of groups is the quotient; the number of objects each group has is the divisor.\",\"- When dividing, always check that the total number of objects equals the product of the quotient and divisor.\",\"- Division is the opposite of multiplication.\",\"- In the book, division is shown as “10 + 2 = 5” and read as “10 divided by 2 equals 5.”\",\"- Use the same number of objects in each group to keep the sharing fair.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is dividing a given set of objects into equal groups called?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Division\",\"Multiplication\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that dividing a set of objects into equal groups is called division.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Repeatedly subtracting the same number of objects is another name for what?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Division\",\"Multiplication\",\"Counting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines division as repeatedly subtracting the same number of objects.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Division means dividing objects into which type of groups?\",\"options\":[\"Unequal groups\",\"Different groups\",\"Equal groups\",\"Large groups\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions \'Equal Groups\' in the definition of division.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you have 12 total objects and subtract 3 each time, how many times can you subtract them?\",\"options\":[\"2 times\",\"3 times\",\"4 times\",\"5 times\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"12 divided by 3 (or subtracting 3 four times: 12-3=9, 9-3=6, 6-3=3, 3-3=0) equals 4.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the process of division, we subtract the same number again and again as many times as _____.\",\"options\":[\"we want\",\"possible\",\"two times\",\"ten times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to subtract the same number again and again as many times as possible.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'take away\' mean in the context of the division definition?\",\"options\":[\"To add\",\"To multiply\",\"To subtract\",\"To group\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'take away\' as a synonym for \'Subtract\'.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have 16 objects and subtract 4 each time, how many times can you subtract until you reach zero?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Subtracting 4 from 16 repeatedly (16-4=12, 12-4=8, 8-4=4, 4-4=0) happens 4 times.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table, if 10 is the total and 2 is subtracted each time, the result is 5. What does 5 represent?\",\"options\":[\"Total objects\",\"Objects subtracted each time\",\"Maximum number of times subtracted\",\"The number of objects left\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table column header identifies this number as the \'Maximum number of times subtracted\'.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To perform division correctly using subtraction, what must be true about the number being subtracted?\",\"options\":[\"It must be a different number each time\",\"It must be the same number each time\",\"It must get smaller each time\",\"It must get larger each time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we must subtract the \'same number\' again and again.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If we have a set of 10 objects and we want to divide them by 5, how many times can we subtract 5?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"1\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"10 - 5 = 5 (once), 5 - 5 = 0 (twice). So, 5 can be subtracted 2 times.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Division\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What operation is performed when we share stones equally among persons?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that sharing stones equally is an operation of division.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When we share 10 stones among 2 persons, what type of sharing is it called?\",\"options\":[\"Fast sharing\",\"Equal sharing\",\"Unequal sharing\",\"Random sharing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the term \'Equal sharing among\' for the first example.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you share 10 stones equally among 2 persons, how many stones does each person get?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sharing 10 stones equally between 2 people results in 5 stones for each person.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many persons are the stones shared among in the first row?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"5\",\"10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first row of the table explicitly says \'among 2 persons\'.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/115_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the total number of stones mentioned in the second example?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"5\",\"8\",\"10\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The second example starts with \'from 10 stones\'.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/115_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"From 10 stones, if we make groups of 2 stones each, how many groups will there be?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Making groups of 2 from 10 stones results in 5 groups.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Making groups of a certain number of items is an example of which operation?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Division\",\"Multiplication\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that both sharing and grouping are operations of division.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When we make groups of 2 stones from a total of 10, what does the number 2 represent?\",\"options\":[\"Total number of stones\",\"Number of groups\",\"Stones in each group\",\"Number of persons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the grouping example, 2 represents the \'Stones in each group\'.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, are the answers for the sharing example and the grouping example the same or different?\",\"options\":[\"Same\",\"Different\",\"Larger\",\"Smaller\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks to discuss if they are same or different; in both cases, the result is 5.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the examples provided, both sharing equally and making equal groups are ways to perform ____.\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Division\",\"Counting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that in both examples, the operation of division was performed.\",\"topic\":\"Sharing and Grouping\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used for division in mathematics?\",\"options\":[\"+\",\"-\",\"×\",\"÷\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the symbol \'÷\' is used for division.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the operation \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\' read in words?\",\"options\":[\"10 plus 2 equals 5\",\"10 minus 2 equals 5\",\"10 divided by 2 equals 5\",\"10 times 2 equals 5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\' is read as \'10 divided by 2 equals 5.\'\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\', what does the symbol \'÷\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Division\",\"Multiplication\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the symbol \'÷\' is used for the operation of division.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following shows a division operation written correctly in mathematics?\",\"options\":[\"10 + 2 = 12\",\"10 ÷ 2 = 5\",\"10 - 2 = 8\",\"10 × 2 = 20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\' as the example of how a division operation is written.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to write \'6 divided by 3 equals 2\' using symbols, which symbol should you use between 6 and 3?\",\"options\":[\"+\",\"÷\",\"-\",\"×\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The symbol \'÷\' is used to represent \'divided by\' in a mathematical operation.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'10 divided by 2 equals 5\', which word represents the \'=\' symbol?\",\"options\":[\"Divided\",\"By\",\"Equals\",\"Ten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that the \'=\' symbol is read as \'equals\'.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical operation is being performed in the example \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\'?\",\"options\":[\"Multiplication\",\"Division\",\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that this operation is written for division.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How would you write \'8 divided by 4 equals 2\' using mathematical notation?\",\"options\":[\"8 + 4 = 2\",\"8 - 4 = 2\",\"8 ÷ 4 = 2\",\"8 × 4 = 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the notation \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\' as a guide, \'8 divided by 4 equals 2\' becomes \'8 ÷ 4 = 2\'.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Look at the expression \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\'. Which number is the result of the division?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"2\",\"5\",\"÷\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the example \'10 ÷ 2 = 5\', the number 5 is what the operation equals.\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text say about the symbol \'÷\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is used for adding numbers.\",\"It is used for division.\",\"It is used for subtracting numbers.\",\"It is used for naming numbers.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The very first sentence of the text states: \'The symbol “ ÷” is used for division.\'\",\"topic\":\"Symbol and Notation\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21348,7,2,'Maths','en','Mathematical Puzzles','[\"- A mirror shows a reversed image of numbers and shapes.\",\"- Counting shapes means writing how many of each type (circles, triangles, rectangles).\",\"- A number puzzle asks you to add numbers to reach a target sum (e.g., 48 or 74).\",\"- Hidden number names are written in words; you must write them as digits (e.g., TEN = 10).\",\"- In a Snakes and Ladders board, landing on a snake’s mouth sends you down to its tail; landing on a ladder’s foot takes you up to its top.\",\"- Some board squares have special rules (e.g., you may need to roll a specific number to move).\",\"- Price puzzles use given numbers to find the cost of items like a doll or an apple.\",\"- In number‑sum puzzles, you place numbers in a grid so that each row and column adds to a specified total.\",\"- Use each number only once unless the puzzle says otherwise.\",\"- Practice helps you solve more complex puzzles quickly.\",\"- Always read the question carefully before starting.\",\"- Write answers clearly in the space given.\",\"- Check your work by adding again to avoid mistakes.\",\"- A good strategy is to start with the largest numbers first.\",\"- Remember that puzzles are meant to be fun and to sharpen thinking skills.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the name of the game that Rajat and Pranita are playing?\",\"options\":[\"Hide and Seek\",\"Treasure Hunt\",\"Ludo\",\"Snake and Ladder\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Rajat and Pranita are playing a game called \'Treasure Hunt.\'\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many coins are there in the golden bag?\",\"options\":[\"Three\",\"Two\",\"Five\",\"Ten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in the golden bag, there are five coins.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many coins are there in the silver bag?\",\"options\":[\"Five\",\"Three\",\"Eight\",\"Zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in the silver bag, there are three coins.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which tree does Pranita\'s path pass near?\",\"options\":[\"Mango tree\",\"Neem tree\",\"Banyan tree\",\"Coconut tree\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Pranita has decided to take the path that passes near the banyan tree.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is listed as a \'correct answer\' in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"7 + 3\",\"5 + 2\",\"2 x 5\",\"10 + 5\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In the provided text, 10 + 5 is listed under the section for correct answers.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is listed as an \'incorrect answer\' in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"11 - 5\",\"7 + 3\",\"19 - 4\",\"9 + 4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the provided text, 7 + 3 is listed under the section for incorrect answers.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Rajat finds one golden bag and one silver bag, how many coins will he have in total?\",\"options\":[\"5 coins\",\"3 coins\",\"8 coins\",\"2 coins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A golden bag has 5 coins and a silver bag has 3 coins; 5 + 3 = 8.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Pranita finds two silver bags, how many coins will she get?\",\"options\":[\"3 coins\",\"6 coins\",\"5 coins\",\"10 coins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Each silver bag has 3 coins, so two silver bags have 3 + 3 = 6 coins.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which tree is located at the top right corner of the garden maze?\",\"options\":[\"Mango tree\",\"Banyan tree\",\"Apple tree\",\"Peepal tree\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows the banyan tree (with hanging roots) in the top right corner.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"7\\/117_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many golden bags (bags with blue ties) are shown sitting together in one spot on the right side of the maze?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows two golden bags placed together in a single section on the right side.\",\"topic\":\"Treasure Hunt\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",