ndreds\",\"Untie hundreds to get tens, then untie a ten to get units\",\"Subtract hundreds first, then units\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes untieing 1 hundred to get 10 tens, then untieing 1 ten from those to get units.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction by untieing\",\"part\":16,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the example in the text, how many total pages are there in the book?\",\"options\":[\"126\",\"150\",\"24\",\"100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'Total pages in a book = 150\'.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the subtraction calculation provided in the text, what does the result \'24\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Total pages\",\"Pages read\",\"Pages not read\",\"Books sold\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the text labels the result 24 as \'Pages not read\'.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a book exhibition has 775 books and 283 books are sold, how many books are left in the exhibition?\",\"options\":[\"492\",\"592\",\"1058\",\"512\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Subtracting 283 from 775 (775 - 283) gives 492.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a correctly framed addition word problem for the information: \'Mango trees = 217, Chikoo trees = 476\'?\",\"options\":[\"How many more mango trees are there than chikoo trees?\",\"How many chikoo trees were cut down?\",\"What is the total number of mango and chikoo trees?\",\"How many mango trees are left?\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To frame an addition problem, we ask for the total number of items combined.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the book exhibition example, how many books were sold?\",\"options\":[\"775\",\"150\",\"283\",\"126\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'out of which 283 books were sold\' for the book exhibition.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When framing a word problem to find how many pages are \'left\' to read, which mathematical operation should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses subtraction to find how many pages are left for Sayali to read.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which digit is shown in the \'Tens\' (T) column of the final answer row?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"2\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the bottom row of the figure, the digit under the \'T\' column is 2.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"13\\/045_105.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the value in the \'Units\' (U) column after borrowing from the Tens place to perform the subtraction?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"6\",\"10\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the 0 in the units place crossed out and replaced by 10 in the top row.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"13\\/045_105.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Using the information \'Mango trees = 217, Chikoo trees = 476\', what is the total number of trees?\",\"options\":[\"683\",\"693\",\"259\",\"793\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Adding 217 and 476 (217 + 476) equals 693.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the person mentioned in the example framed from the book information?\",\"options\":[\"Sagar\",\"Sayali\",\"Sita\",\"Salma\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The example framed in the text starts with \'Sayali read 126 pages...\'.\",\"topic\":\"Framing word problems\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, up to what number should we create number bonds?\",\"options\":[\"Up to 10\",\"Up to 20\",\"Up to 50\",\"Up to 100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to create number bonds up to 20.\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 5 + 4 = 9, what do we get when we subtract 4 from the sum 9?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"4\",\"9\",\"1\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The rule states that if one number is subtracted from the sum, we get the other number.\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the rule for the co-relation between addition and subtraction?\",\"options\":[\"Adding two numbers gives zero.\",\"Subtracting the sum from a number gives the other number.\",\"From the sum of two numbers, if one number is subtracted, we get the other number.\",\"Subtraction and addition are not related.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This is the exact definition provided in the textbook text.\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 7 + 3 = 10, which subtraction sentence follows the rule mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"10 - 7 = 3\",\"7 - 3 = 4\",\"10 + 3 = 13\",\"3 - 7 = 0\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Subtracting one of the numbers (7) from the sum (10) gives the other number (3).\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the example 5 + 4 = 9, what is the \'sum\'?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"4\",\"9\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the addition 5 + 4 = 9, 9 is the result or the sum.\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we know that 8 + 2 = 10, how can we find the number 2 using subtraction?\",\"options\":[\"8 - 2\",\"10 - 8\",\"10 - 2\",\"8 + 10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Subtracting one number (8) from the sum (10) gives the other number (2).\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Complete the number bond logic: If 12 + 5 = 17, then 17 - 5 = ?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"17\",\"12\",\"22\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Applying the rule: Sum (17) minus one number (5) equals the other number (12).\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these pairs shows the correct co-relation between addition and subtraction?\",\"options\":[\"6 + 4 = 10 and 10 - 6 = 4\",\"6 + 4 = 10 and 6 - 4 = 2\",\"6 + 4 = 10 and 10 + 4 = 14\",\"6 + 4 = 10 and 10 - 10 = 0\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first option correctly demonstrates subtracting one number from the sum to get the other.\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the sum of two numbers is 15 and one number is 7, what is the other number based on the rule?\",\"options\":[\"15\",\"7\",\"8\",\"22\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"15 (sum) - 7 (one number) = 8 (the other number).\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the teacher use number bonds up to 20?\",\"options\":[\"To teach multiplication only.\",\"To be used while teaching addition and subtraction.\",\"To teach how to write numbers.\",\"To teach division.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states number bonds should be used while teaching addition and subtraction.\",\"topic\":\"Rule\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21452,13,3,'Maths','en','Fractions','[\"A fraction represents a part of a whole.\",\"The numerator is the number of parts taken (top number).\",\"The denominator is the total number of equal parts (bottom number).\",\"Half is written as 1\\/2, meaning one part out of two equal parts.\",\"Quarter is written as 1\\/4, meaning one part out of four equal parts.\",\"Three-fourths is written as 3\\/4, meaning three parts out of four equal parts.\",\"When numerator and denominator are equal, the fraction equals one whole (e.g., 2\\/2 = 1).\",\"In proper fractions, numerator is less than denominator (e.g., 1\\/2, 3\\/4).\",\"In improper fractions, numerator is greater than or equal to denominator (e.g., 5\\/4, 4\\/3).\",\"Unit fractions have numerator 1 (e.g., 1\\/2, 1\\/4, 1\\/5).\",\"Fractions can be read as \'numerator upon denominator\' (e.g., 3\\/4 is \'three upon four\').\",\"To write a fraction, count shaded parts for numerator and total equal parts for denominator.\",\"Fractions like half, quarter, three-quarters are used in daily life (e.g., half an apple).\",\"Comparing fractions: same denominator, larger numerator means larger fraction.\",\"Fractions help us share things equally among people.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who gave the laddu to Pihu?\",\"options\":[\"Dada\",\"Mother\",\"Teacher\",\"Friend\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Mother has given one laddu.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many laddus did Mother give at the start?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"One\",\"Three\",\"Half\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'Mother has given one laddu.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Let\'s Think\' section, what other food item is mentioned for dividing into two equal parts?\",\"options\":[\"A biscuit\",\"A chapati\",\"A bhakari\",\"An apple\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to compare dividing a bhakari and a laddu into two equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Dada offer to do when Pihu brought the laddu?\",\"options\":[\"Eat the whole laddu\",\"Make two parts\",\"Give it back to Mother\",\"Make four parts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dada says, \'Pihu, bring it here, I\'ll make two parts.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What specific kind of parts did Pihu insist on making?\",\"options\":[\"Small parts\",\"Big parts\",\"Two equal parts\",\"Unequal parts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Pihu says, \'I\'ll make two equal parts.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Pihu correct Dada by saying \'Not just two parts\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because she wanted more than two parts\",\"Because she wanted the parts to be exactly equal\",\"Because she did not want to share\",\"Because she wanted to give the laddu to Mother\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pihu emphasizes making \'equal\' parts to ensure a fair division.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have one laddu to share with one friend so that both get the same amount, how should you divide it?\",\"options\":[\"Into two equal parts\",\"Into two unequal parts\",\"Into three parts\",\"Into many small pieces\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"To share fairly between two people, the item must be divided into two equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Pihu follows the same method for a bhakari as she did for the laddu, how many equal parts will the bhakari have?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text discusses dividing both a laddu and a bhakari into two equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is Pihu holding in her hands in the final scene on the right?\",\"options\":[\"A whole laddu\",\"Two equal parts of a laddu\",\"A bhakari\",\"Nothing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Pihu holding two halves of the laddu after dividing it.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"13\\/049_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many children are shown participating in the conversation about the laddu?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows two children, Pihu and Dada, talking and sharing.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"13\\/049_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When a shape is divided into two equal parts, what is each part called?\",\"options\":[\"A whole\",\"A quarter\",\"A half\",\"A group\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if a shape is divided into two equal parts, each part is called half.\",\"topic\":\"Do The Activity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step mentioned in the activity to divide a circular paper into two equal parts?\",\"options\":[\"Color the paper\",\"Fold the paper\",\"Tear the paper\",\"Draw on the paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity starts by instructing to take a circular paper and fold it.\",\"topic\":\"Do The Activity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a group of marbles and divide them into two equal parts, what is each part called?\",\"options\":[\"Half of the group\",\"The whole group\",\"One marble\",\"A big group\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says if any group is divided into two equal parts, each part is called half of that group.\",\"topic\":\"Do The Activity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, which of these English letters can be divided into two equal parts?\",\"options\":[\"F\",\"G\",\"M\",\"L\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Letter M can be folded vertically to form two equal matching parts.\",\"topic\":\"Do The Activity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many equal parts are formed when you fold a paper once and open the fold?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when you open the fold, two equal parts are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Do The Activity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What must be true about two parts of an object for them to be called \'halves\'?\",\"options\":[\"They must be different sizes\",\"They must be equal\",\"They must be different shapes\",\"They must be small\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition of half requires the parts to be equal.\",\"topic\":\"Do The Activity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you cut a circular paper exactly on the fold, what do you get?\",\"options\":[\"Two whole circles\",\"One small circle\",\"Each part is half of the whole circle\",\"A squ