esults in a pentagon.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What type of paper is used for the folding activity in \'Fun with Maths\'?\",\"options\":[\"Circular piece of paper\",\"Triangular piece of paper\",\"Square piece of paper\",\"Rectangular piece of paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Take another square piece of paper\' for the folding activity.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If folding one corner of a square adds one side to make a pentagon (5 sides), how many sides will the shape have if two opposite corners are folded?\",\"options\":[\"Four sides\",\"Five sides\",\"Six sides\",\"Seven sides\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Folding one corner creates a 5-sided shape; folding a second opposite corner adds another side, creating a 6-sided shape (hexagon).\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these shapes is NOT listed in the section where you must name objects from your surroundings?\",\"options\":[\"Triangle\",\"Square\",\"Rectangle\",\"Pentagon\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text asks for surrounding objects for Circle, Triangle, Square, and Rectangle, but not for Pentagon.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What action is performed on the corners of the paper to observe new shapes?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting\",\"Folding\",\"Coloring\",\"Tearing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text repeatedly mentions \'fold\' and \'folded\' to describe how the shapes are changed.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, how should the corners of the square be folded to observe different shapes?\",\"options\":[\"All at once\",\"One by one\",\"Only the opposite ones\",\"Only the top ones\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'fold each corner one by one\' to observe which shapes are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Ma