 three vertices, and three angles.\",\"A 2D shape has only one face and lies flat on a surface.\",\"A prism has identical top and bottom faces, and its vertical faces are rectangles.\",\"A pyramid has a base of any shape, triangular vertical faces, and meets at a point at the top.\",\"The number of vertices, edges, and faces helps identify 3D shapes.\",\"Joining dots can form triangles, squares, and rectangles.\",\"Shapes can be classified by their sides, angles, and vertices.\",\"Using a squared corner helps identify angle types.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is formed wherever two lines meet?\",\"options\":[\"A circle\",\"An angle\",\"A square\",\"A straight line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Wherever two lines meet; an angle is formed.\'\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is an angle formed on a chair according to the lesson?\",\"options\":[\"Between the legs and the floor\",\"Between the seat and the backrest\",\"Between the seat and brackets\",\"On the four corners of the seat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions an angle is formed between the seat and brackets of a chair.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of a tree is mentioned as a place where an angle is formed?\",\"options\":[\"Between the leaves\",\"Between the branches\",\"Inside the fruit\",\"Under the roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Angle between the branches of a tree\' as an example.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is formed at the place where two roads meet?\",\"options\":[\"A dead end\",\"A circle\",\"An angle\",\"A bridge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Where two roads meet, an angle is formed.\'\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which activity can we see an angle being formed by the body?\",\"options\":[\"Sleeping\",\"A yoga pose\",\"Running\",\"Singing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'Angle formed in a yoga pose\' as an example.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are looking at a pair of scissors, where should you look to find the angle?\",\"options\":[\"At the finger holes\",\"Between the two blades\",\"At the sharp edge of one blade\",\"On the screw only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states an angle is formed between the two blades of a pair of scissors.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When looking at a clock to find an angle, which parts should you observe?\",\"options\":[\"The numbers 1 to 12\",\"The round shape of the clock\",\"The two hands of the clock\",\"The glass covering the clock\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies an angle is formed between the two hands of a clock.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned in the text as a place where an angle is formed?\",\"options\":[\"Between two fingers\",\"Between two roads\",\"Between two clouds\",\"Between two blades of scissors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Clouds are not mentioned in the text as an example of where angles are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definition \'Wherever two lines meet; an angle is formed\', which of these body parts forms an angle?\",\"options\":[\"Between two fingers\",\"The tip of the nose\",\"The surface of the skin\",\"The hair on the head\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the \'Angle between the two fingers\'.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main idea of the text provided?\",\"options\":[\"Angles are only found in math books\",\"Angles are formed only by straight roads\",\"Angles can be found in many different places in our surroundings\",\"Angles are only made by human hands\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text encourages looking around for angles formed at different places in the surroundings.\",\"topic\":\"Angles\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a squared corner called?\",\"options\":[\"An acute angle\",\"A right angle\",\"An obtuse angle\",\"A straight angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a squared corner is a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is an angle smaller than a squared corner called?\",\"options\":[\"Right angle\",\"Obtuse angle\",\"Acute angle\",\"Big angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an angle smaller than the squared corner as an acute angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is an angle greater than a squared corner called?\",\"options\":[\"Obtuse angle\",\"Acute angle\",\"Right angle\",\"Small angle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines an angle greater than the squared corner as an obtuse angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 4, how is a squared corner made?\",\"options\":[\"By using a ruler\",\"By drawing a line\",\"By folding a paper\",\"By looking at a clock\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activity 4 involves folding a paper to make a squared corner.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of making a squared corner from paper?\",\"options\":[\"To draw pictures of letters\",\"To verify the types of angles\",\"To clean the classroom\",\"To measure the length of a paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says you can verify the types of angles using the squared corner you made.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 1, what are students asked to mark in the pictures of letters?\",\"options\":[\"The colors of the letters\",\"The size of the letters\",\"The places where an angle is formed\",\"The names of the letters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activity 1 asks to mark the places where an angle is formed in the pictures of letters.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you use your paper squared corner to check an angle and the angle is smaller than the corner, what have you found?\",\"options\":[\"A right angle\",\"An obtuse angle\",\"An acute angle\",\"A squared corner\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"An angle smaller than the squared corner is an acute angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"You are looking at an angle in your classroom. It is much larger than your squared corner. What type of angle is it?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angle\",\"Right angle\",\"Obtuse angle\",\"Small angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"An angle greater than the squared corner is an obtuse angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool does the text suggest using to check if a given angle is a right angle?\",\"options\":[\"A pencil\",\"A squared corner\",\"A picture of a letter\",\"The classroom wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that using the squared corner, you can check whether a given angle is a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the squared corner used as a reference for all three types of angles mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is the only angle in a classroom\",\"Because it helps determine if other angles are smaller or greater\",\"Because it is made of paper\",\"Because it is used to mark letters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The squared corner acts as a standard to compare if other angles are smaller (acute) or greater (obtuse).\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If an angle is exactly the same shape as your paper fold corner, it is not acute or obtuse. What is it?\",\"options\":[\"A right angle\",\"A small angle\",\"A large angle\",\"A folded angle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A squared corner is specifically defined as a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activities, where can angles be found according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only in paper folds\",\"Only in letters\",\"In letters, the classroom, and pictures\",\"Only in squared corners\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activities 1, 2, and 3 show angles in letters, the classroom, and pictures.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Angles\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table for three-dimensional shapes, which of these is an element to be counted?\",\"options\":[\"Number of colors\",\"Number of faces\",\"Number of straws\",\"Number of lines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook table for 3-D shapes lists \'Number of faces\' as one of the elements to observe.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the table for 2-D shapes, which three elements are listed to be counted?\",\"options\":[\"Vertices, sides, and angles\",\"Faces, edges, and vertices\",\"Sides, faces, and angles\",\"Vertices, edges, and faces\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The 2-D shapes table specifically asks for the number of vertices, sides, and angles.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What name is given to the straws used to make the 2-D shapes in the diagram?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic Straws\",\"Magic Straws\",\"Jodo Straws\",\"Maths Straws\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the 2-D shapes are made using \'Jodo Straws\'.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When observing 2-D shapes like a triangle or a square, what is the correlation between the number of vertices and the number of sides?\",\"options\":[\"The number of vertices is always more than the sides\",\"The number of vertices is always less than the sides\",\"The number of vertices is equal to the number of sides\",\"There is no relation between them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"By counting the elements in the provided shapes, students identify that the number of vertices equals the number of sides.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a 3-D shape has a \'Related 2-D shape\' listed as a square, what does this tell us about the 3-D shape?\",\"options\":[\"It has no vertices\",\"It is made of circles\",\"Its faces are square in shape\",\"It has only two sides\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Related 2-D shape\' column helps identify the shape of the faces of the 3-D object.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many vertices (black dots) does the triangle have?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Counting the black dots (vertices) on the triangle in the Jodo Straws diagram shows there are 3.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/033_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the 2-D shapes table, how many sides does a pentagon have?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"5\",\"6\",\"3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A pentagon is a 2-D shape with 5 sides as shown in the table.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which shape has the highest number of vertices and sides?\",\"options\":[\"Hexagon\",\"Pentagon\",\"Octagon\",\"Trapezium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The octagon in the Jodo Straws diagram has 8 vertices and 8 sides, which is more than the others shown.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/033_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the 2-D shapes table, which shape is unique because it has no straight sides or vertices to count?\",\"options\":[\"Square\",\"Triangle\",\"Circle\",\"Rectangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The circle in the table has a curved boundary and no corners (vertices) or straight sides.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to complete the table for a hexagon, how many angles would you record?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"5\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since a hexagon has 6 sides and 6 vertices, it also has 6 angles.\",\"topic\":\"3D and 2D shapes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many vertices does a rectangle have?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a rectangle has four vertices.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is true about the sides of a square?\",\"options\":[\"Only opposite sides are equal\",\"All four sides are equal in length\",\"It has only three sides\",\"No sides are equal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, all four sides of a square are equal in length.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many sides does a triangle have?\",\"options\":[\"2 sides\",\"3 sides\",\"4 sides\",\"5 sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that a triangle has 3 sides.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of angles are found in a rectangle?\",\"options\":[\"Small angles\",\"Right angles\",\"Large angles\",\"No angles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all four angles of a rectangle are right angles.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A two-dimensional shape can be placed completely on a _________.\",\"options\":[\"Curved surface\",\"Flat surface\",\"Round surface\",\"Pointed surface\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a two-dimensional shape can be placed completely on a flat surface.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a shape has 4 vertices and its opposite sides are equal in length, what is it?\",\"options\":[\"A triangle\",\"A square\",\"A rectangle\",\"A circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a rectangle as having four vertices and opposite sides equal in length.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many faces does a two-dimensional shape have?\",\"options\":[\"Only one face\",\"Two faces\",\"Three faces\",\"Four faces\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a two-dimensional shape has only one face.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is a common feature shared by both a square and a rectangle?\",\"options\":[\"Both have only three angles\",\"Both have all four sides equal\",\"Both have four right angles\",\"Both have three vertices\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that both the rectangle and the square have four right angles.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you draw a shape with 3 vertices and 3 angles, which shape have you drawn?\",\"options\":[\"Square\",\"Rectangle\",\"Triangle\",\"Flat surface\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A triangle is defined in the text as having three vertices and 3 angles.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly distinguishes a square from a rectangle based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"A square has four vertices but a rectangle does not\",\"All four sides of a square are equal, while only opposite sides of a rectangle are equal\",\"A square has right angles but a rectangle does not\",\"A square has four angles but a rectangle has three\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies all sides are equal for a square, but only opposite sides are equal for a rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Features of 2D shapes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many sticks are used in the first activity to try making a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"2 sticks\",\"3 sticks\",\"4 sticks\",\"12 sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions trying to make a triangle with 3 sticks of different lengths.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, do 3 sticks always form a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No, not always\",\"Only if they are equal\",\"Only if they are long\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 3 sticks don\'t always form a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"At the most, how many squares can be made with 12 sticks of equal length?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since a square has 4 equal sides, 12 sticks divided by 4 equals 3 squares.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Watching an ant move on an object helps to clarify the concept of which two things?\",\"options\":[\"Length and Breadth\",\"Squares and Triangles\",\"2D and 3D\",\"Sticks and Shapes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that watching an ant move clarifies the concept of 2D and 3D.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To make squares in the activity, what should be the length of the 12 sticks?\",\"options\":[\"Different lengths\",\"Equal length\",\"Increasing lengths\",\"Short lengths\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies using 12 sticks of equal length to make squares.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The text suggests trying to make different triangles using how many sticks of different lengths?\",\"options\":[\"3 sticks\",\"4 sticks\",\"5 sticks\",\"12 sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Try to make different triangles with 4 sticks of different lengths.\'\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given 3 sticks of different lengths, what shape are you asked to try making first?\",\"options\":[\"A square\",\"A circle\",\"A triangle\",\"A rectangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first instruction is to try making a triangle with 3 sticks.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What condition helps clarify the concept of 2D and 3D when an ant is moving?\",\"options\":[\"Watching from a point\",\"Measuring the sticks\",\"Counting the squares\",\"Using 12 sticks\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'watching from a point\' will clarify the concept.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"On what types of objects is the ant left to move in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"Only 2D objects\",\"Only 3D objects\",\"2D or 3D objects\",\"Only flat objects\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions an ant left on a 2D or 3D object.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of the activity involving 4 sticks of different lengths?\",\"options\":[\"To make one big square\",\"To make different triangles\",\"To feed the ant\",\"To measure the sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to try to make different triangles with 4 sticks of different lengths.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the shape of the vertical faces of all prisms?\",\"options\":[\"Triangles\",\"Rectangles\",\"Circles\",\"Squares\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the vertical faces of all the prisms are rectangles.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the vertical faces of a pyramid meet?\",\"options\":[\"At the base\",\"At two different points\",\"At a single point at the top\",\"They do not meet at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the vertical faces of a pyramid meet at a single point at the top.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the top and bottom faces of a prism?\",\"options\":[\"They are different shapes\",\"They are identical\",\"The top is always smaller\",\"The bottom is always a rectangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the top and bottom faces of the prism are identical.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the type of a pyramid decided?\",\"options\":[\"From the shape at the base\",\"From the number of points at the top\",\"From the size of the vertical faces\",\"From the height of the shape\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the type of a pyramid is decided from the shape at the base.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for a Rectangular prism?\",\"options\":[\"Cube\",\"Pyramid\",\"Cuboid\",\"Pentagon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Rectangular prism (cuboid)\' as one of the types.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a prism has five sides on its top and bottom faces, what is it called?\",\"options\":[\"Square prism\",\"Hexagonal prism\",\"Triangular prism\",\"Pentagonal prism\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"A prism with a five-sided (pentagonal) base is called a Pentagonal prism.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the shape of the vertical faces of a pyramid?\",\"options\":[\"Rectangular\",\"Triangular\",\"Square\",\"Pentagonal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines pyramids as 3D shapes whose vertical faces are triangular.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A 3D shape has a square base and its vertical faces meet at a single point. What is this shape?\",\"options\":[\"Square prism\",\"Square pyramid\",\"Rectangular prism\",\"Triangular pyramid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A shape with a square base whose faces meet at a single point is a Square pyramid.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a characteristic of a prism but NOT a pyramid?\",\"options\":[\"It is a 3D shape\",\"It has a base\",\"Its vertical faces are rectangles\",\"It has a name based on its shape\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Prisms have rectangular vertical faces, while pyramids have triangular vertical faces.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a shape is a Hexagonal prism, what must be true about its top and bottom faces?\",\"options\":[\"They are triangles\",\"They are rectangles\",\"They are identical hexagons\",\"They meet at a single point\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a prism, the top and bottom faces are identical and the name (Hexagonal) tells us the shape.\",\"topic\":\"Prisms and Pyramids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 1, what is fixed in the centre of the cardboard to help draw a circle?\",\"options\":[\"A pencil\",\"A nail\",\"A bangle\",\"An eraser\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states to tie one end of the thread to the nail and fix it in the centre of the cardboard.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which object is used to trace a circle in Activity 3?\",\"options\":[\"A wire\",\"A piece of cardboard\",\"A bangle\",\"A string\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activity 3 says to \'Use a bangle to trace a circle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the first activity, what is tied to the other end of the string to draw the shape?\",\"options\":[\"A nail\",\"A wire\",\"A bangle\",\"A chalk\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions to tie the other end of the string to a chalk.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are circles called when they are drawn using the same centre but have different measures?\",\"options\":[\"Square circles\",\"Concentric circles\",\"Wire circles\",\"Bangle circles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines drawing several circles of different measures using the same centre as concentric circles.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is a circular shape created using a piece of wire in Activity 2?\",\"options\":[\"By cutting it\",\"By bending it\",\"By tying it to a nail\",\"By tracing it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity 2 says to take a piece of wire and \'Bend it into a circle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What movement must the chalk make to draw a circle in Activity 1?\",\"options\":[\"Move in a straight line\",\"Rotate around the nail\",\"Move up and down\",\"Stay in the centre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'rotate the chalk around the nail to draw a circle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to draw circles of many different sizes accurately, which tool from Activity 3 should you use?\",\"options\":[\"A bangle\",\"A piece of wire\",\"A compass\",\"A square cardboard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests using a compass to set the appropriate measure and draw circles of different sizes.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to Activity 4, what is the final step to make a figure after cutting out circles from a notebook page?\",\"options\":[\"Trace them with a bangle\",\"Join them together\",\"Bend them like wire\",\"Tie them to a nail\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity 4 says to cut the circles out and \'join them to make the given figure\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 1, if you keep the nail in the same spot but change the length of the string, what will be the result?\",\"options\":[\"The circles will have different centres\",\"The circles will be concentric\",\"The circles will become squares\",\"The circles will be the same size\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the same centre with different measures (string lengths) creates concentric circles.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity involves changing a straight object into a curved shape?\",\"options\":[\"Activity 1 (String and Chalk)\",\"Activity 2 (Wire)\",\"Activity 3 (Bangle)\",\"Activity 4 (Notebook page)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity 2 involves bending a piece of wire, which is typically straight, into a circular shape.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the nail fixed in the centre of the cardboard in Activity 1?\",\"options\":[\"To hold the chalk\",\"To act as the fixed point for rotation\",\"To cut the cardboard\",\"To trace the bangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The nail acts as the centre around which the chalk rotates to maintain a circular shape.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step required to create the figure described in Activity 4?\",\"options\":[\"Join the circles together\",\"Bend a wire\",\"Draw circles of different sizes on a page\",\"Fix a nail in cardboard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity starts by instructing to \'Draw circles of different sizes on a page of the notebook\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"create\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21573,21,4,'Maths','en','Addition and Subtraction','[\"Addition means combining two or more numbers to find the total.\",\"Subtraction means taking away one number from another to find the difference.\",\"The symbol for addition is \'+\', and for subtraction is \'-\'.\",\"When adding, start from the rightmost digit (ones place) and move left.\",\"When subtracting, if the top digit is smaller, borrow from the next left digit.\",\"Addition and subtraction are inverse operations.\",\"You can check subtraction by adding the difference to the subtracted number.\",\"Number bonds show how numbers can be split and combined (e.g., 60 + 40 = 100).\",\"Adding zero to a number gives the same number.\",\"Subtracting zero from a number gives the same number.\",\"The order of numbers in addition does not change the sum (commutative property).\",\"In subtraction, changing the order changes the result (not commutative).\",\"To add large numbers, align digits by place value (ones, tens, hundreds, thousands).\",\"To subtract large numbers, align digits by place value and borrow if needed.\",\"Estimation helps to check if an answer is reasonable.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the total population of Songaon?\",\"options\":[\"3765\",\"7436\",\"3671\",\"7475\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the population of Songaon is 7436.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How much money did Shamrao receive as milk payment for 10 days?\",\"options\":[\"₹5920\",\"₹8972\",\"₹9750\",\"₹7964\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Shamrao received ₹9750 as the milk payment.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which nursery has 7475 saplings?\",\"options\":[\"Vanraj Nursery\",\"Songaon Nursery\",\"Vanashree Nursery\",\"Kumar Nursery\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there are 7475 saplings in Vanashree Nursery.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To find the number of women in Songaon when the total population and number of men are known, which operation is used?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\",\"Subtraction\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The problem is solved by subtracting the number of men from the total population.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the flood relief fund, what does finding the \'difference\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Adding both fund amounts together\",\"Subtracting the smaller fund amount from the larger one\",\"Dividing the funds equally\",\"Multiplying the funds by two\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Difference in mathematics refers to the result of subtracting one number from another.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the number 3671 represent in the Songaon example?\",\"options\":[\"The number of men\",\"The total population\",\"The number of women\",\"The number of children\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that Songaon has 3671 women.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Songaon has a population of 7436 and 3765 are men, how many women are there?\",\"options\":[\"3671\",\"3771\",\"4671\",\"3661\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"7436 - 3765 = 3671.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many more saplings does Vanashree Nursery (7475) have than Vanraj Nursery (5967)?\",\"options\":[\"1408\",\"1508\",\"2508\",\"1518\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"7475 - 5967 = 1508.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Shamrao received ₹9750 and spent ₹5920 on cattle food. What is the amount left with him?\",\"options\":[\"₹3830\",\"₹4830\",\"₹3730\",\"₹3230\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"9750 - 5920 = 3830.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Kanya Vidyalaya collected ₹8972 and Kumar Vidyamandir collected ₹7964. What is the difference in their fund amounts?\",\"options\":[\"₹1012\",\"₹1008\",\"₹1108\",\"₹908\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"8972 - 7964 = 1008.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction Word Problems\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the altitude of Kanchanjunga peak mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"8586 metre\",\"1646 metre\",\"6940 metre\",\"7586 metre\"],\"answer\":0,\"explai