tres (cm), metres (m), and kilometres (km).\",\"1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m.\",\"To convert metres to centimetres, multiply by 100.\",\"To convert centimetres to millimetres, multiply by 10.\",\"To convert kilometres to metres, multiply by 1000.\",\"Half metre = 50 cm, quarter metre = 25 cm.\",\"Two half metres make one metre; four quarter metres make one metre.\",\"Instruments like measuring tape, scale, and rope are used to measure length.\",\"Estimation means guessing the length before measuring it.\",\"Actual length is the real measurement taken with a tool.\",\"Weight is measured in grams (g) and kilograms (kg).\",\"1 kg = 1000 g.\",\"Half kg = 500 g, quarter kg = 250 g.\",\"Weights can be combined in different ways to make a total weight.\",\"The length of an object does not change if it is held vertically, horizontally, or slantingly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what must be used to measure the length of objects on the school ground correctly?\",\"options\":[\"Any stick\",\"Correct units\",\"Hand spans\",\"Footsteps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that to measure the length of objects on the school ground, correct units have to be used.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects is specifically mentioned in the table to be measured?\",\"options\":[\"Length of a notebook\",\"Height of a building\",\"Length of the spear\",\"Width of a classroom\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'length of the spear\' is listed as item number 5 in the measurement table.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is suggested for students to use when measuring length, breadth, and height?\",\"options\":[\"Science Kit\",\"Maths Kit\",\"Sports Kit\",\"Drawing Kit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'For Teachers\' section mentions using material from the Maths Kit for measurements.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step students should take before measuring the objects listed in the table?\",\"options\":[\"Start measuring immediately\",\"Decide which units to use\",\"Ask the principal\",\"Draw the objects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs students to \'Decide which units you will use to measure their length\' before writing them down.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where should the discussion about the difficulties of measuring kho-kho pillars be held?\",\"options\":[\"On the playground\",\"In the principal\'s office\",\"In the class\",\"At home\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'A discussion should be held in the class about the difficulties...\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides length and width, what other dimension does the text suggest students can measure?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Temperature\",\"Depth\",\"Time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'For Teachers\' section mentions measuring length, breadth, height, depth, width, and distance.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which object is shown at row number 4 for measurement?\",\"options\":[\"Height of the stumps\",\"Length of the spear\",\"Length of the stick with ghungaroo\",\"Width of the bat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the table, row 4 shows the \'length of the stick with ghungaroo\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/055_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which activity shown on the ground corresponds to item number 6 in the table?\",\"options\":[\"Children playing volleyball\",\"Children playing near the kho-kho pillars\",\"Children playing with a bat\",\"Children running towards the school\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Item 6 in the table is the \'distance between two kho-kho pillars\', which are visible on the far left of the school ground.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/055_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you are measuring the \'width of the bat\' and the \'distance between two kho-kho pillars\', which measurement would likely require a larger unit like a metre?\",\"options\":[\"Width of the bat\",\"Distance between two kho-kho pillars\",\"Height of the stumps\",\"Length of the ghungaroo\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Distance between pillars is much longer than the width of a bat, making a larger unit more appropriate.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why might it be difficult to measure the distance between kho-kho pillars using only a small measuring scale?\",\"options\":[\"The scale is too heavy\",\"The distance is too long for a small scale to be convenient\",\"The pillars are too high\",\"The scale might break\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text prompts a discussion on difficulties, implying that a small scale is impractical for long distances like that between pillars.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the \'length of the bat\' and the \'width of the bat\', which statement is true based on the objects described?\",\"options\":[\"The width is usually greater than the length\",\"The length is usually greater than the width\",\"They are always exactly the same\",\"Neither can be measured in units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Physically, a cricket bat\'s length is significantly greater than its width.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'For Teachers\' section, how should students learn about the use of units?\",\"options\":[\"By memorizing a list of units\",\"Through discussion and practical opportunities to measure\",\"By watching a video\",\"By only reading the textbook\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says use of units should be discussed and opportunities should be available to measure using material.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Discuss\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What tool should be used to measure the actual distance in the ball-throwing activity?\",\"options\":[\"A ruler\",\"A measuring tape\",\"A compass\",\"A scale\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to use a measuring tape to measure the actual distance.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should be used to mark the spot where the ball lands first?\",\"options\":[\"A stone\",\"A piece of wood\",\"A chalk\",\"A pencil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions say to mark the spot using a chalk.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"About how many metres away from the starting line should the children throw the ball?\",\"options\":[\"Two metres\",\"Four metres\",\"Six metres\",\"Eight metres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states children should throw the ball about six metres away from the starting line.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the teacher\'s note, what should students do before measuring the actual distance?\",\"options\":[\"Mark the spot with chalk\",\"Write the estimated length\",\"Clean the starting line\",\"Calculate the average distance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The teacher\'s note says to ask students to first write estimated length and then measure.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main skill intended to be developed through this activity?\",\"options\":[\"Skill of throwing a ball far\",\"Skill of drawing straight lines\",\"Skill of correct approximation\",\"Skill of jumping high\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that from this activity, the skill of correct approximation should be developed.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the ball throw, which other game is suggested for teachers to play for this activity?\",\"options\":[\"High jump\",\"Long jump\",\"Running race\",\"Shot put\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The teacher\'s note specifically suggests playing the long jump game.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student\'s ball lands at a distance of 7 metres, which column in the \'Approximate distance\' table should they tick?\",\"options\":[\"less than 6 m\",\"6 m\",\"more than 6 m\",\"0 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 7 metres is greater than 6 metres, the \'more than 6 m\' column is appropriate.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a ball lands exactly at the 6-metre mark, where should the tick mark be placed in the table?\",\"options\":[\"In the \'less than 6 m\' column\",\"In the \'6 m\' column\",\"In the \'more than 6 m\' column\",\"In the \'Actual distance\' column\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table provides a specific column for \'6 m\' for approximate distance recording.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table provided in the text, how many different categories are available to record the \'Approximate distance\'?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table shows three columns under approximate distance: more than 6 m, less than 6 m, and 6 m.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the teacher compare the estimated length with the actual distance measured?\",\"options\":[\"To check how correct the approximation is\",\"To see who is the strongest student\",\"To practice using a chalk\",\"To make the game last longer\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'How correct is the approximation as the actual?\' to develop estimation skills.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In short, how is \'Metre\' written?\",\"options\":[\"mt\",\"m\",\"mtr\",\"me\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in short, Metre is written as \'m\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the short form for \'centimetre\'?\",\"options\":[\"c\",\"ct\",\"cm\",\"cen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that centimetre is written as \'cm\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the metre rope markings, how many centimetres make 1 metre (1m)?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"50 cm\",\"25 cm\",\"100 cm\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions 1m (100 cm).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many centimetres are there in a half metre?\",\"options\":[\"25 cm\",\"50 cm\",\"75 cm\",\"100 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states half metre is 50 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the length of a quarter metre in centimetres?\",\"options\":[\"25 cm\",\"50 cm\",\"75 cm\",\"100 cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states quarter metre is 25 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you put a half metre rope together twice, what length do you get?\",\"options\":[\"Half metre\",\"Quarter metre\",\"One metre\",\"Three quarters of a metre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Half metre put together twice equals one meter.\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many times must a quarter metre be put together to equal one metre?\",\"options\":[\"Two times\",\"Three times\",\"Four times\",\"Five times\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'One quarter metre put together four times equals one metre.\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What do you get if you take one quarter metre three times?\",\"options\":[\"One metre\",\"Half metre\",\"Three quarters of a metre\",\"Quarter metre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'One quarter metre taken three times equals three quarters of a metre.\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total length when a half metre and one quarter metre are joined together?\",\"options\":[\"One metre\",\"Three quarters of a metre\",\"Half metre\",\"Quarter metre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'Half metre and one quarter metre together makes three quarters of a metre.\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these combinations equals exactly one metre in length?\",\"options\":[\"Half metre and one quarter metre\",\"One quarter metre taken three times\",\"One quarter metre and three quarters of a metre\",\"Three quarters of a metre and half metre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'One quarter metre and three quarters of a metre joined together equals one metre in length.\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many centimetres are there in 1 metre?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"50 cm\",\"100 cm\",\"1000 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1m = 100 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the result of 3 × 100 cm?\",\"options\":[\"30 cm\",\"300 cm\",\"3000 cm\",\"3 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the calculation 3 m = 3 × 100 cm = 300 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If two ropes of length 100 cm each are joined together, what is their total length in centimetres?\",\"options\":[\"100 cm\",\"200 cm\",\"300 cm\",\"400 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if two ropes of 100 cm each are joined, their total length is 200 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which calculation is used to convert 4 metres into centimetres?\",\"options\":[\"4 + 100\",\"4 - 100\",\"4 × 100\",\"100 ÷ 4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern in the text (3m = 3 × 100 cm), 4m is calculated as 4 × 100 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 metre + 1 metre = 2 metres, what is the equivalent in centimetres?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm + 10 cm = 20 cm\",\"100 cm + 100 cm = 200 cm\",\"50 cm + 50 cm = 100 cm\",\"200 cm + 200 cm = 400 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 1m = 100cm, adding 1m + 1m is the same as adding 100cm + 100cm to get 200cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects is NOT mentioned in the text for students to estimate and measure?\",\"options\":[\"Tables\",\"Verandas\",\"Doors\",\"Blackboards\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists tables, verandas, and doors, but does not mention blackboards.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a rope that is 5 metres long, how many centimetres long is it?\",\"options\":[\"50 cm\",\"500 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"5000 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Applying the conversion rule 1m = 100cm, 5m equals 5 × 100 = 500 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A 1m rope is cut into two equal parts, and then each of those is cut into two equal parts. When these four parts are joined end to end, what is the total length?\",\"options\":[\"1 m\",\"2 m\",\"4 m\",\"100 m\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that when these four parts are joined end to end, the length is 1m.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To convert any number of metres into centimetres, what mathematical operation do we perform with the number 100?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows conversion by multiplying the number of metres by 100 (e.g., 3 × 100).\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a student cuts a 1m rope into two equal parts, and then cuts each of those into two equal parts, how many total small pieces will they have?\",\"options\":[\"2 pieces\",\"3 pieces\",\"4 pieces\",\"8 pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cutting the rope into two parts and then cutting each of those two into two more results in four parts.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the conversion of 2m, 3m, and 4m shown in the text, what is the correct way to write the conversion for 2 metres?\",\"options\":[\"2 m = 2 × 10 cm = 20 cm\",\"2 m = 2 × 100 cm = 200 cm\",\"2 m = 2 + 100 cm = 102 cm\",\"2 m = 200 × 100 cm = 20000 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the conversion: 2 m = 2 × 100 cm = 200 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Besides objects like tables and doors, what else are students asked to measure in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Their weight\",\"Each other\'s height\",\"The length of the classroom\",\"The thickness of a book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Let them measure each others height.\'\",\"topic\":\"Metre-Centimetre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many equal parts is one centimetre unit divided into?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"10\",\"100\",\"12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that every centimetre unit is divided into 10 small, equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the length of the die?\",\"options\":[\"one centimetre\",\"six centimetres\",\"two centimetres\",\"ten millimetres\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the length of the above die is one centimetre.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"1 centimetre is equal to how many millimetres?\",\"options\":[\"1 mm\",\"5 mm\",\"10 mm\",\"100 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula: 1 centimetre = 10 millimetres.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is each small part of a centimetre unit called?\",\"options\":[\"Metre\",\"Millimetre\",\"Gram\",\"Kilometre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that each of these small parts measures one millimetre in length.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When the length of an object is small, which unit is used to measure it?\",\"options\":[\"Metre\",\"Centimetre\",\"Millimetre\",\"Kilometre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when the length of objects is small, it is measured in millimetres.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An eraser is 2 cm 7 mm long. How else can this length be written?\",\"options\":[\"27 mm\",\"207 mm\",\"72 mm\",\"9 mm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that 2 cm 7 mm can also be said as 27 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 cm = 10 mm, then what is the length of 3 cm in millimetres?\",\"options\":[\"13 mm\",\"3 mm\",\"30 mm\",\"300 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the conversion 3 cm = 3 × 10 mm = 30 mm as shown in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The length of a pencil is 6 cm. What is its length in millimetres?\",\"options\":[\"6 mm\",\"60 mm\",\"16 mm\",\"600 mm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 1 cm = 10 mm, 6 cm = 6 × 10 = 60 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To convert 4 cm into millimetres, which calculation is correct?\",\"options\":[\"4 + 10\",\"4 - 10\",\"4 × 10\",\"4 ÷ 10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the conversion as 4 cm = 4 × 10 mm = 40 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects is suggested in the text to be measured in millimetres?\",\"options\":[\"Length of a road\",\"Thickness of a book\",\"Height of a building\",\"Length of a playground\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Let\'s Think\' section gives the thickness of a book as an example for millimetres.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"An eraser\'s length is between 2 cm and 3 cm. If it measures 27 mm, how many small millimetre parts are there after the 2 cm mark?\",\"options\":[\"2 parts\",\"10 parts\",\"7 parts\",\"3 parts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"27 mm is 2 cm and 7 mm, meaning there are 7 small parts after the 2 cm mark.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these units is smaller than a centimetre according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Metre\",\"Millimetre\",\"Kilometre\",\"Decimetre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a centimetre is divided into 10 small parts called millimetres.\",\"topic\":\"Centimetre-Millimetre\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the short form \'km\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Kilogram\",\"Kilometre\",\"Kilo-metre\",\"Kilomillimetre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'km\' stands for kilometre.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit is used to measure the distance between two villages or cities?\",\"options\":[\"Centimetre\",\"Metre\",\"Kilometre\",\"Millimetre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the unit kilometre is used to measure the distance between two villages or cities.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the topic text, what is the distance from Jamb (Budruk) to Mukhed?\",\"options\":[\"30km\",\"23km\",\"24km\",\"4km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the distance from Jamb (Budruk) to Mukhed is 23km.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what unit does the teacher say is used to measure the distance between two places?\",\"options\":[\"Metre\",\"Centimetre\",\"Kilometre (km)\",\"Millimetre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the figure, the teacher explains that Kilometre (km) is the unit used to measure the distance between two places.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/060_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many metres are there in 1 kilometre?\",\"options\":[\"100 m\",\"500 m\",\"1000 m\",\"250 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Let\'s Remember\' section states 1 km = 1000 m.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many metres are equal to 1\\/4 km?\",\"options\":[\"500 m\",\"750 m\",\"250 m\",\"1000 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1\\/4 km = 250 m.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 km is 1000 m, how many metres make 7 km?\",\"options\":[\"700 m\",\"7000 m\",\"70 m\",\"7500 m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following the conversion rule (7 x 1000), 7 km equals 7000 m.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these distances is mentioned by the girl as being on the signboard?\",\"options\":[\"30km\",\"15km\",\"10km\",\"50km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The girl in the figure says the signboard reads 30km, 23km, 24km, and 4km.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/060_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"A distance of 6000 m is equal to how many kilometres?\",\"options\":[\"60 km\",\"6 km\",\"600 km\",\"0.6 km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the conversion pattern in the text, 6 km = 6 x 1000 m = 6000 m.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct way to write \'Half km\'?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/4 km\",\"3\\/4 km\",\"1\\/2 km\",\"1\\/3 km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines Half km as 1\\/2 km.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If three quarters km is 750 m, what is the sum of 1\\/4 km and half km in metres?\",\"options\":[\"1000 m\",\"500 m\",\"750 m\",\"250 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"1\\/4 km (250 m) + 1\\/2 km (500 m) = 750 m, which is three quarters km.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many metres are there in 5 km according to the conversion method shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"500 m\",\"5000 m\",\"50 m\",\"5500 m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula 5 x 1000 m, the result is 5000 m.\",\"topic\":\"Kilometre-Metre\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects is NOT mentioned in the \'Fun with Maths\' text for measuring length?\",\"options\":[\"Pen\",\"Pencil\",\"Stick\",\"Eraser\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions pen, pencil, stick, and book as objects to measure.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what happens to the length of an object when its position is changed from horizontal to slanting?\",\"options\":[\"The length increases\",\"The length decreases\",\"The length remains the same\",\"The length cannot be measured\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the length of any object remains the same regardless of whether it is measured vertically, horizontally, or at a slant.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which position shows the object standing straight up between points A and B?\",\"options\":[\"Horizontal\",\"Vertical\",\"Slanting\",\"Circular\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the straight upright position as \'Vertical\'.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/061_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the position where the object is tilted at an angle?\",\"options\":[\"Vertical\",\"Horizontal\",\"Slanting\",\"Straight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the tilted position as \'Slanting\'.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/061_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Shubhangi has three ropes for her measurement activity. What are their specific lengths?\",\"options\":[\"10m, 5m, and 1m\",\"10m, 6m, and 1m\",\"15m, 5m, and 1m\",\"10m, 6m, and 5m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Shubhangi has ropes measuring 10m, 6m, and 1m.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you measure a book vertically and then measure it horizontally using standard units, what will you observe about the measurements?\",\"options\":[\"The vertical measurement will be longer\",\"The horizontal measurement will be longer\",\"Both measurements will be equal\",\"The slanting measurement will be the shortest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that length remains the same regardless of the orientation when using standard units.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Shubhangi needs to measure a distance of 15m. How can she use her 10m, 6m, and 1m ropes to do this?\",\"options\":[\"By adding 10m and 6m ropes only\",\"By adding 10m, 6m and then subtracting 1m\",\"By adding 10m and 1m ropes only\",\"By adding 6m and 1m ropes only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"10m + 6m - 1m = 15m, which is a way to combine the given rope lengths.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A stick is cut into 10 equal parts, and each part is 30 cm long. What is the total length of the stick in metres?\",\"options\":[\"300 m\",\"30 m\",\"3 m\",\"0.3 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"10 parts of 30 cm each equals 300 cm. Since 100 cm = 1 m, 300 cm equals 3 m.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To find how many 25 cm bricks are needed for a 5 km road, what is the most important first step?\",\"options\":[\"Multiply 25 by 5 directly\",\"Convert the road length of 5 km into centimetres\",\"Divide 25 by 5\",\"Subtract 25 from 5000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To calculate the number of bricks, both the brick length and road length must be in the same unit (centimetres).\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student claims that a pencil becomes shorter when held at a slant. Based on the \'I Understood\' section, how would you evaluate this claim?\",\"options\":[\"The student is correct because slanting lines look shorter\",\"The student is incorrect because length remains the same in any position\",\"The student is correct if they use a standard ruler\",\"The student is incorrect because only vertical objects stay the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that length remains the same regardless of whether it is measured vertically, horizontally, or at a slant.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit of measurement is mentioned in the rope problem involving Shubhangi?\",\"options\":[\"Centimetre (cm)\",\"Metre (m)\",\"Kilometre (km)\",\"Millimetre (mm)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rope lengths for Shubhangi are given as 10m, 6m, and 1m.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How can Shubhangi measure a distance of exactly 5m using only her 6m and 1m ropes?\",\"options\":[\"By placing the 6m and 1m ropes end to end\",\"By folding the 6m rope in half\",\"By measuring the difference between the 6m rope and the 1m rope\",\"By cutting 1m off the 10m rope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The difference between 6m and 1m (6 - 1) is 5m.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the book \'Lilavati\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Bhaskaracharya\",\"Aryabhata\",\"Brahmagupta\",\"Varahamihira\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that Verse 5 of ‘Lilavati’ was written by the Indian mathematician Bhaskaracharya.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the ancient units of measurement, how many \'jav\' make one \'Angul\'?\",\"options\":[\"4 jav\",\"8 jav\",\"24 jav\",\"2000 jav\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that 8 jav = 1 Angul.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Bhaskaracharya\'s explanation, 4 Krosha is equal to which unit?\",\"options\":[\"1 Dand\",\"1 Hasta\",\"1 Yojan\",\"1 Angul\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 4 Krosha = 1 Yojan.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which city was the athlete Neeraj Chopra born?\",\"options\":[\"Panipat\",\"Tokyo\",\"Mumbai\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states he was born on 24 December 1997 in Panipat, Haryana.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 Hasta is equal to 24 Angul, how many Angul will be there in 2 Hastas?\",\"options\":[\"24 Angul\",\"12 Angul\",\"48 Angul\",\"8 Angul\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 1 Hasta = 24 Angul, then 2 Hastas = 24 x 2 = 48 Angul.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If 2000 Dand make 1 Krosha, how many Krosha will 8000 Dand make?\",\"options\":[\"2 Krosha\",\"4 Krosha\",\"6 Krosha\",\"8 Krosha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 2000 Dand = 1 Krosha, then 8000 Dand \\/ 2000 = 4 Krosha.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these units is the smallest in the sequence of measurement?\",\"options\":[\"Hasta\",\"Dand\",\"Angul\",\"Krosha\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows a sequence where Angul is smaller than Hasta, which is smaller than Dand, which is smaller than Krosha.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Neeraj Chopra won a gold medal in 2021. In which year was he honoured with the Padma Shri award?\",\"options\":[\"1997\",\"2020\",\"2021\",\"2022\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states he was honoured with the Padma Shri award by the Government of India in 2022.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, which equipment is the athlete holding in his hand to throw?\",\"options\":[\"A bat\",\"A javelin\",\"A racket\",\"A discus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Neeraj Chopra, a javelin thrower, holding a long spear-like javelin.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/062_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific Olympic medal is shown next to the athlete?\",\"options\":[\"Silver medal\",\"Bronze medal\",\"Gold medal\",\"Platinum medal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a gold-colored medal from Tokyo 2020, which matches the text stating he won a gold medal.\",\"topic\":\"Indian Knowledge System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/062_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many grams are equal to 1 kilogram?\",\"options\":[\"100 g\",\"500 g\",\"1000 g\",\"2000 g\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1 kg = 1000 g.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which city was the weightlifter Mira Chanu born?\",\"options\":[\"Tokyo\",\"East Imphal\",\"Manipur\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions she was born in East Imphal, Manipur.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To balance 3 kg of puffed rice, how many 1 kilogram weights should be used together?\",\"options\":[\"1 weight\",\"2 weights\",\"3 weights\",\"4 weights\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'I will use 3 weights of 1 kilogram for it. 1 kilogram + 1 kilogram + 1 kilogram = 3 kilograms.\'\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 1000 g + 1000 g + 1000 g + 1000 g + 1000 g are added together, how many kilograms does it make?\",\"options\":[\"3 kg\",\"4 kg\",\"5 kg\",\"6 kg\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 1000 g is 1 kg, adding it five times equals 5 kg.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the multiplication method shown in the text, what is the value of 4 kg in grams?\",\"options\":[\"400 g\",\"4000 g\",\"40 g\",\"4400 g\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows 4 kg = 4 × 1000 g = 4000 g.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you have one 2 kg weight, how many 500 gram weights are needed to balance a 3 kg load?\",\"options\":[\"One 500 g weight\",\"Two 500 g weights\",\"Three 500 g weights\",\"Four 500 g weights\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that 2 kilograms + 500 grams + 500 grams = 3 kilograms.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these combinations will NOT balance a 3 kg weight?\",\"options\":[\"2 kg + 1 kg\",\"1 kg + 1 kg + 1 kg\",\"2 kg + 500 g + 500 g\",\"1 kg + 500 g + 500 g\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"1 kg + 500 g + 500 g equals 2 kg, which does not balance 3 kg.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did the Government of India honour Mira Chanu with the Padma Shri award?\",\"options\":[\"1994\",\"2018\",\"2020\",\"2021\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states she was honoured with the Padma Shri award in 2018.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct way to calculate the grams in 3 kg using multiplication?\",\"options\":[\"3 × 100 g\",\"3 × 500 g\",\"3 × 1000 g\",\"3 + 1000 g\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern in the text, 3 kg = 3 × 1000 g.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what makes Mira Chanu\'s silver medal win at the Tokyo Olympics unique?\",\"options\":[\"She is the youngest weightlifter.\",\"She is the first Indian woman to win a silver medal in weightlifting at the Olympics.\",\"She lifted the heaviest weight ever recorded.\",\"She won the medal in her home state of Manipur.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies her as the first Indian woman to achieve this specific feat.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what sport is the athlete Mira Chanu performing to win her medal?\",\"options\":[\"Wrestling\",\"Weightlifting\",\"Gymnastics\",\"Boxing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show Mira Chanu lifting a barbell, which is the sport of weightlifting.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what equipment is the athlete lifting above her head to demonstrate weight?\",\"options\":[\"A weighing pan\",\"A bag of puffed rice\",\"A barbell with circular weights\",\"A silver medal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The image shows her lifting a barbell with large circular weights on both ends.\",\"topic\":\"Weight\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the traditional small unit of weight used in older days?\",\"options\":[\"Gram\",\"Kilogram\",\"Chatak\",\"Poha\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'chatak\' as a traditional small unit of weight used in older days.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many grams (g) make 1 kilogram (kg) as per the teacher\'s note?\",\"options\":[\"100 g\",\"500 g\",\"1000 g\",\"10 g\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1000g means 1 kg.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which grocery item is listed at serial number 6?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar\",\"Salt\",\"Ground nut\",\"Toor dal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows \'ground nut\' at serial number 6.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/067_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Adhpav\' mean according to the lesson?\",\"options\":[\"Double of pav\",\"Half of pav\",\"One kilogram\",\"Quarter of pav\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines \'Adhpav\' as \'half of pav\'.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which fruit or vegetable is listed at serial number 5?\",\"options\":[\"7 cucumbers\",\"10 onions\",\"7 lemons\",\"8 tomatoes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists \'7 lemons\' at serial number 5.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/067_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the girl wondering about?\",\"options\":[\"How to cook the grocery\",\"How complex weights like \'one and half kilo\' are measured\",\"Where to buy the grocery from\",\"How much the grocery costs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The thought bubble shows her wondering how kilo and a quarter, one and half kilo, etc., are measured.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/066_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student weighs 10 packets of 100 g each, what will be the total weight in kilograms?\",\"options\":[\"10 kg\",\"1 kg\",\"0.5 kg\",\"100 kg\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says 10 packets of 100 g weighed together is 1000g, which means 1 kg.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Ankita needs to bring \'one and half kg\' of an item. Which item on her list matches this weight?\",\"options\":[\"Poha\",\"Moong dal\",\"Rawa\",\"Sugar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The grocery list states: \'4) moong dal-one and half kg\'.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Ankita\'s list includes \'rock sugar - quarter kg\' and \'dried coconut - half kg\'. Which item is heavier?\",\"options\":[\"Rock sugar\",\"Dried coconut\",\"Both are equal\",\"Cannot be determined\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A half kg is heavier than a quarter kg.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Ankita buys \'rawa\', what weight measure is she looking for according to her list?\",\"options\":[\"One and quarter kg\",\"One and half kg\",\"One and three quarter kg\",\"Two and quarter kg\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The list specifies \'5) rawa-one and three quarter kg\'.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Ankita\'s grocery list, which of these items has the highest weight?\",\"options\":[\"Jowar\",\"Sugar\",\"Rice\",\"Rawa\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rice is 2 3\\/4 kg, which is the largest quantity among the options (Jowar is 2 1\\/2, Sugar is 2 1\\/4, Rawa is 1 3\\/4).\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the estimation activity for fruits and vegetables, what is the correct sequence of steps to follow?\",\"options\":[\"Measure actual weight, then estimate\",\"Estimate the weight, then measure actual weight\",\"Only estimate and do not measure\",\"Ask the storekeeper for the weight directly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Estimate the weights... Later write down the actual weights after measuring it\'.\",\"topic\":\"Grocery List Activity\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21576,21,4,'Maths','en','Data Handling','[\"Data handling means collecting, organizing, and showing information clearly.\",\"A pictograph uses pictures or symbols to represent data.\",\"Each symbol in a pictograph stands for a certain number of items (scale).\",\"Scale helps to draw fewer symbols when numbers are large.\",\"Title, labels, and key are important parts of a pictograph.\",\"To read a pictograph, count the symbols and multiply by the scale value.\",\"Pictographs help compare quantities easily.\",\"Symbols should be clear, neat, and of the same size.\",\"One column is for the item name, another for pictures, and another for numbers (if shown).\",\"Always choose a scale that divides the data values evenly.\",\"If scale is 1 symbol = 5 items, then 3 symbols mean 15 items.\",\"Pictographs are used in real life to show data like trees, vehicles, or sales.\",\"We can find the most, least, or total from a pictograph.\",\"Drawing a pictograph requires collecting data first.\",\"Symbols should be chosen carefully to represent the data correctly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In previous standards, which method was used to organize data?\",\"options\":[\"Bar graphs\",\"Tally marks\",\"Pie charts\",\"Pictograms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in previous standards, students organized data using tally marks.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What will be used to represent data in this standard according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Numbers only\",\"Tally marks\",\"Pictures\",\"Lines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that pictures will be used to represent data in this standard.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the board displaying the scholarship information located in Adarsh School?\",\"options\":[\"In the Principal\'s office\",\"Near the main gate\",\"Inside the classroom\",\"In the library\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the board is near the main gate of Adarsh School.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many scholarship holding students were there in the academic year 2021-22?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"9\",\"13\",\"15\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the table in the figure, the number of students for 2021-22 is 13.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/069_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, in which academic year is the number of scholarship holding students the most?\",\"options\":[\"2020-21\",\"2021-22\",\"2022-23\",\"2023-24\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table shows that 2023-24 has the highest number (15) of scholarship holding students.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/069_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"By how much is the number of students holding scholarship more in 2022-23 than in the year 2020-21?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In 2022-23 there were 9 students and in 2020-21 there were 7; 9 minus 7 equals 2.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What specific information does the board at Adarsh School display?\",\"options\":[\"Number of teachers\",\"Number of scholarship holding students\",\"School holiday list\",\"Daily attendance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the board displays the number of scholarship holding students.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which year shows a decrease in the number of scholarship students compared to the year immediately before it?\",\"options\":[\"2021-22\",\"2022-23\",\"2023-24\",\"2020-21\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The number dropped from 13 (in 2021-22) to 9 (in 2022-23).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the data provided, which year had the lowest number of scholarship holders?\",\"options\":[\"2023-24\",\"2022-23\",\"2021-22\",\"2020-21\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Comparing all values (7, 13, 9, 15), 7 is the lowest, which corresponds to the year 2020-21.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the school mentioned in the data handling example?\",\"options\":[\"Zilla Parishad School\",\"Adarsh School\",\"Primary School\",\"Secondary School\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly names the school as \'Adarsh School\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step to make a pictograph according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Drawing pictures\",\"Collecting information\",\"Writing numbers\",\"Choosing a title\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that to make a pictograph, first the information has to be collected.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should be selected to represent the data in a chart?\",\"options\":[\"Colors\",\"Numbers only\",\"Symbols\",\"Letters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that symbols should be selected to represent the data in the chart.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many neem trees were found around the school in the given example?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"2\",\"14\",\"12\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text specifies there are 12 neem trees around the school.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a pictograph, what does the number of symbols in a column indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The height of the tree\",\"The quantity of the item\",\"The age of the tree\",\"The name of the item\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to use as many symbols as the quantity indicates.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why should we collect pictures and information in one place?\",\"options\":[\"For handling the data\",\"To make the book heavy\",\"To hide the information\",\"To draw on the walls\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states we collect pictures and information in one place for handling the data.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When making the tree pictograph in the text, how many columns were used for the chart?\",\"options\":[\"One column\",\"Two columns\",\"Three columns\",\"Five columns\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'First let us make a three column chart.\'\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to represent 5 mango trees in a pictograph, how many symbols of mango trees should they draw?\",\"options\":[\"2 symbols\",\"5 symbols\",\"10 symbols\",\"1 symbol\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The quantity indicates the number of symbols to be used.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many pictures are drawn in the row for \'ashoka\' trees?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"8\",\"14\",\"2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"By counting the pictures in the ashoka row of the figure, there are 14 symbols.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/070_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which tree has the smallest number of pictures represented?\",\"options\":[\"neem\",\"coconut\",\"banayan\",\"ashoka\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows only 2 pictures for the banyan tree, which is the least among all.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/070_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the information provided in the text, what is the total number of trees collected around the school?\",\"options\":[\"30\",\"36\",\"20\",\"26\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Total trees = 12 (neem) + 8 (coconut) + 2 (banyan) + 14 (ashoka) = 36.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of using different pictures for each kind of tree in the pictograph?\",\"options\":[\"To make the chart look colorful\",\"To show the information about trees clearly\",\"To fill up the empty space\",\"To practice drawing different shapes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Different pictures are used to represent and distinguish the information about different kinds of trees.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student draws 10 symbols for 8 coconut trees, which rule of pictographs from the text are they breaking?\",\"options\":[\"Information must be collected first\",\"Symbols should represent the quantity indicated\",\"Use three columns only\",\"Write the name of the tree in the first column\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the column should use as many symbols as the quantity indicates; 10 symbols for 8 items is incorrect.\",\"topic\":\"Representing Data using Pictures\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How many guava saplings did the farmer plant according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"40\",\"60\",\"80\",\"100\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the farmer planted 100 guava saplings.\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason for using a scale in a pictograph?\",\"options\":[\"To make the symbols look bigger\",\"To avoid drawing a lot of pictures and save time\",\"To change the number of saplings planted\",\"To use different symbols for each tree\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The student and teacher discuss that drawing many pictures is time-consuming, so a scale is used to reduce the number of symbols.\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the scale chosen by the teacher, how many saplings does one symbol indicate?\",\"options\":[\"1 sapling\",\"5 saplings\",\"10 saplings\",\"100 saplings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher suggests indicating 10 saplings using one symbol.\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the teacher choose 10 as the scale for these specific numbers (100, 80, 60, 40)?\",\"options\":[\"Because 10 is the smallest number\",\"Because all the numbers are divisible by 10\",\"Because there are 10 types of trees\",\"Because the farmer asked for it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that 100, 80, 60, and 40 are numbers divisible by 10.\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If we use the scale of 1 symbol = 10 saplings, how many symbols are needed for 40 custard apple saplings?\",\"options\":[\"4 symbols\",\"10 symbols\",\"40 symbols\",\"1 symbol\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since 40 divided by 10 is 4, we need 4 symbols.\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the maximum number of symbols needed for any single type of tree in this pictograph?\",\"options\":[\"100 symbols\",\"80 symbols\",\"10 symbols\",\"4 symbols\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that we will just need to draw at the most 10 symbols (for the 100 guava saplings).\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total number of saplings planted by the farmer in all?\",\"options\":[\"200\",\"240\",\"280\",\"300\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Total = 100 (guava) + 80 (orange) + 60 (pomegranate) + 40 (custard apple) = 280.\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the farmer had planted 50 mango saplings, how many symbols would be drawn for mangoes using the scale of 10?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"10\",\"50\",\"2\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using a scale of 10, 50 saplings would be represented by 5 symbols (50 ÷ 10 = 5).\",\"topic\":\"Using scale for making a Pictograph\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many pot symbols are 