pic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which fruit boxes were sold the least according to the pictograph?\",\"options\":[\"Mango\",\"Chickoo\",\"Grapes\",\"Custard apple\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Chickoo has the lowest number of symbols (2), meaning they were sold the least.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the representation of data help us to do?\",\"options\":[\"Count the fruits one by one\",\"Interpret the information\",\"Draw new pictures\",\"Buy more fruits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that representation of data helps to interpret the information.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What can we compare using the information in the pictograph?\",\"options\":[\"The price of fruits\",\"The weight of fruits\",\"The sale of fruits\",\"The taste of fruits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions we can compare the sale of fruits using the pictograph.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What information does this specific pictograph show?\",\"options\":[\"Sale of the fruit seller for one week\",\"Sale of the fruit seller for one day\",\"Number of trees in an orchard\",\"Price list of different fruits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes it as a pictograph showing sale of the fruit seller for one day.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 symbol represents 5 boxes, how many boxes of mangoes were sold?\",\"options\":[\"3 boxes\",\"10 boxes\",\"15 boxes\",\"20 boxes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"There are 3 symbols for mango. Since 1 symbol = 5 boxes, 3 symbols = 3 x 5 = 15 boxes.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total number of fruit boxes sold in one day?\",\"options\":[\"14 boxes\",\"50 boxes\",\"70 boxes\",\"60 boxes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Total symbols = 3 (mango) + 2 (chickoo) + 5 (grapes) + 4 (custard apple) = 14 symbols. Total boxes = 14 x 5 = 70.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many symbols are shown for the fruit \'custard apple\'?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Looking at the row for custard apple in the figure, there are 4 box symbols.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many more boxes of grapes were sold than chickoo?\",\"options\":[\"3 boxes\",\"5 boxes\",\"10 boxes\",\"15 boxes\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Grapes have 5 symbols and Chickoo has 2 symbols. Difference = 3 symbols. 3 symbols x 5 boxes = 15 boxes.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"By looking at the pictograph, which statement is true about the sale of mangoes and custard apples?\",\"options\":[\"Mangoes sold more than custard apples\",\"Custard apples sold more than mangoes\",\"Both sold the same amount\",\"Mangoes sold twice as much as custard apples\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Custard apple has 4 symbols (20 boxes) while mango has 3 symbols (15 boxes), so custard apple sold more.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the scale \'1 symbol = 5 boxes\' used instead of drawing every single box?\",\"options\":[\"To make the chart look colorful\",\"To make the representation of large data easier to read\",\"Because the seller only has 5 boxes\",\"To hide the actual number of boxes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Scales are used in pictographs to represent larger quantities simply so the data can be interpreted easily.\",\"topic\":\"Interpreting Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21577,21,4,'Maths','en','Notion of Maps (Najri Nakasha)','[\"A map is a drawing that shows the location of places or objects.\",\"Maps help us understand where things are in relation to each other.\",\"There are different views of an object: front view, side view, and top view.\",\"Front view shows how an object looks when seen from the front.\",\"Side view shows how an object looks when seen from the side.\",\"Top view shows how an object looks when seen from above.\",\"We can use blocks or boxes to build models and draw their views.\",\"A grid helps us locate positions using rows and columns.\",\"In a grid, we can give instructions like \'draw in row 2, column 3\'.\",\"A sketch map is a simple drawing showing important places in an area.\",\"Sketch maps use symbols or labels to show departments, roads, or buildings.\",\"We can draw a map of our school showing classrooms, office, playground, etc.\",\"Maps do not need to be detailed; they show main features clearly.\",\"To read a map, we look at labels, symbols, and directions.\",\"We can create our own maps by observing and recording what we see.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which three types of views of objects are mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Left, Right, and Back views\",\"Front, Side, and Top views\",\"Inside, Outside, and Bottom views\",\"Near, Far, and Middle views\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions front view, side view, and top view as the types of views learned.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which view of the aeroplane is described as \'very beautiful\'?\",\"options\":[\"Front view\",\"Top view\",\"Side view\",\"Bottom view\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Side view of the aeroplane is very beautiful.\'\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When you look at a cupboard from directly above, which view are you observing?\",\"options\":[\"Front view\",\"Side view\",\"Top view\",\"Inside view\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Looking at an object from above is defined as the top view.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which box is designated for drawing the cupboard as seen from the side?\",\"options\":[\"The first box\",\"The second box\",\"The third box\",\"The last box\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The middle column in the table is labeled \'Side View\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many different views of the cupboard are you instructed to draw in the table?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table has three columns for three different views: Front, Side, and Top.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you are standing right in front of the cupboard doors to open them, which view are you seeing?\",\"options\":[\"Side View\",\"Top View\",\"Front View\",\"Bottom View\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Standing directly before the doors corresponds to the front view of the cupboard.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions that students have already learned about views of objects in which standard?\",\"options\":[\"The current standard\",\"The previous standard\",\"The next standard\",\"Kindergarten\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'In the previous standard, we learnt the side view, front view and top view of the objects.\'\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of the activity described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To fly an aeroplane\",\"To draw a map of the school\",\"To draw different views of a cupboard\",\"To count the number of cupboards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity specifically instructs to draw the front, side, and top views of a cupboard.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which view would be best to show the height and width of the cupboard doors clearly?\",\"options\":[\"Top View\",\"Front View\",\"Side View\",\"Bottom View\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The front view shows the full face of the cupboard, including the doors.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If a student draws only the flat rectangular roof of the cupboard, which box in the activity should they put it in?\",\"options\":[\"Front View\",\"Side View\",\"Top View\",\"None of these\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The roof of the cupboard is visible in the top view.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Cupboard Views\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who are the two children mentioned in the text who made buildings of different shapes?\",\"options\":[\"Ajay and Vijay\",\"Jay and John\",\"Ram and Rahim\",\"Sam and Tom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that Jay and John made buildings of different shapes.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Building Blocks\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What material did the children use to make the buildings in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"Clay\",\"Paper\",\"Blocks\",\"Sticks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Jay and John used blocks to make buildings.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Building Blocks\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which view shows the building as seen from the side?\",\"options\":[\"Front view\",\"Top view\",\"Side view\",\"Bottom view\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions front view, top view, and side view as the different ways to look at the buildings.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity: Building Blocks\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\"