rio, if you fill the 5-litre vessel and pour it into the 3-litre vessel until it is full, how much water is left in the 5-litre vessel?\",\"options\":[\"1 litre\",\"2 litres\",\"3 litres\",\"4 litres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Subtracting the 3 litres poured out from the 5 litres leaves 2 litres in the larger vessel.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To fill a 5-litre can using the least number of steps, which combination of jars should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Five 1-litre jars\",\"Two 2-litre jars and one 1-litre jar\",\"One 2-litre jar and three 1-litre jars\",\"Three 2-litre jars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the 2-litre jar twice (4L) and the 1-litre jar once (1L) takes only 3 steps, which is the minimum.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the third scenario, how many people must the milk seller distribute the milk to?\",\"options\":[\"2 people\",\"3 people\",\"4 people\",\"12 people\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to help the milk seller distribute milk equally among 3 people.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 12 litres of milk are distributed equally among 3 people, how many litres does each person get?\",\"options\":[\"3 litres\",\"4 litres\",\"5 litres\",\"6 litres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"12 litres divided by 3 people equals 4 litres per person.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which measuring jars are kept alongside the barrel of cooking oil?\",\"options\":[\"3 litre and 5 litre jars\",\"5 litre and 2 litre jars\",\"2 litre and 1 litre jars\",\"7 litre and 3 litre jars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states measuring jars of 2 litres and 1 litre capacity are kept alongside.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To fill all three empty cans with 5 litres each, what is the total number of times the jars are used if each can takes 3 jar measurements?\",\"options\":[\"3 times\",\"6 times\",\"9 times\",\"15 times\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If one can requires 3 uses of the jars, then 3 cans require 3 multiplied by 3, which is 9.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these vessel capacities is NOT available to the milk seller in the third scenario?\",\"options\":[\"3 litre\",\"5 litre\",\"7 litre\",\"4 litre\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The milk seller has vessels of 3, 5, and 7 litres; he does not have a 4-litre vessel.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the third scenario, how can the milk seller get exactly 4 litres using the 7-litre and 3-litre vessels?\",\"options\":[\"Fill the 7-litre vessel and pour into the 3-litre vessel once\",\"Fill the 3-litre vessel twice and pour into the 7-litre vessel\",\"Fill the 5-litre vessel and pour into the 3-litre vessel\",\"Fill the 7-litre vessel and pour into the 5-litre vessel\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Filling the 7L vessel and removing 3L by filling the 3L vessel leaves exactly 4L in the 7L vessel.\",\"topic\":\"Fun with Maths\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, in which unit should you estimate the amount of water wasted?\",\"options\":[\"Millilitres\",\"Litres\",\"Kilograms\",\"Metres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically asks to estimate \'Approximately how many litres of water is wasted\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where are you instructed to make the table for this activity?\",\"options\":[\"On a blackboard\",\"In your textbook\",\"In your notebook\",\"On a chart paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Make the following table in your notebook.\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first column heading required in the table?\",\"options\":[\"Place\",\"Solution\",\"Sr. No.\",\"Litres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table structure provided in the text starts with \'Sr. No.\' as the first column.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides listing the place and the amount of water wasted, what else must you suggest?\",\"options\":[\"The cost of water\",\"Solutions\",\"The time of wastage\",\"The name of the person wasting water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to \'suggest solutions\' for the water being wasted.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal of listing places from your surroundings in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"To find where water gets wasted\",\"To find where water is stored\",\"To measure the height of water tanks\",\"To draw pictures of water\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks to \'List the places from your surroundings where water gets wasted.\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you find a leaking tap in the school garden, which column of the table should \'School Garden\' be written in?\",\"options\":[\"Sr. No.\",\"Solution\",\"Approximately how many litres of water is wasted\",\"Place\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The \'Place\' column is meant for the location where water wastage is observed.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you decide that \'fixing the tap\' is the best way to stop wastage, which column does this information belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Place\",\"Solution\",\"Sr. No.\",\"Litres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fixing the tap is a \'Solution\', which is the final column in the table.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a column heading mentioned in the textbook table?\",\"options\":[\"Place\",\"Solution\",\"Type of water\",\"Sr. No.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Type of water\' is not listed as one of the four columns in the provided table structure.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table structure, what information links a \'Place\' to a \'Solution\'?\",\"options\":[\"The amount of water wasted in litres\",\"The color of the water\",\"The price of the bucket\",\"The distance of the place\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table includes \'Approximately how many litres of water is wasted\' to quantify the problem before suggesting a solution.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to \'estimate\' the litres of water wasted rather than just listing the place?\",\"options\":[\"To practice drawing tables\",\"To understand the scale of wastage to suggest better solutions\",\"To fill up the notebook pages\",\"To learn how to spell \'Approximately\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Estimating the volume (litres) helps in understanding the severity of the wastage mentioned in the exercise.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21583,21,4,'Maths','en','Measurement of Time','[\"Time is measured in hours, minutes, and seconds.\",\"There are 60 minutes in 1 hour and 60 seconds in 1 minute.\",\"A digital clock shows time using numbers (digits) only, e.g., 10:15.\",\"An analog clock shows time using hour and minute hands on a dial.\",\"In an analog clock, the shorter hand shows hours and the longer hand shows minutes.\",\"When the minute hand completes one full round, 60 minutes pass.\",\"When the hour hand moves from one number to the next, 1 hour passes.\",\"10:15 means 15 minutes past 10 in the morning.\",\"To find time after some minutes, add the minutes to the given time.\",\"To find time before some minutes, subtract the minutes from the given time.\",\"2 hours 30 minutes = 150 minutes, so they are equal.\",\"A sundial tells time using the shadow of the sun.\",\"Bruhat Samrat Yantra in Jaipur is a large sundial built by Sawai Jai Singh.\",\"In 12 hours, the minute hand crosses the hour hand 11 times.\",\"Activities like yoga, reading, or studying can be timed using a clock.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a clock called that displays time using only digits?\",\"options\":[\"Analog clock\",\"Digital clock\",\"Sundial\",\"Bruhat Samrat Yantra\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a digital clock as one that displays time using only digits.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does an analog clock use to show time in hours and minutes?\",\"options\":[\"Digits\",\"Shadows\",\"Hands\",\"Sunlight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, an analog clock uses hands to show time.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which century was the \'Bruhat Samrat Yantra\' built?\",\"options\":[\"15th century\",\"18th century\",\"19th century\",\"20th century\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the Rajput King Sawai Jai Singh built it in the 18th century.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who built the \'Bruhat Samrat Yantra\' in Jaipur?\",\"options\":[\"Pawan\",\"Sawai Jai Singh\",\"A Rajput Queen\",\"A clock maker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the Rajput King Sawai Jai Singh of Jaipur as the builder.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of device is the \'Bruhat Samrat Yantra\'?\",\"options\":[\"A digital clock\",\"An analog clock\",\"A huge sundial\",\"A small wristwatch\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the Bruhat Samrat Yantra as a huge sundial.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a sundial like the Bruhat Samrat Yantra indicate accurate time?\",\"options\":[\"By using digits\",\"By using clock hands\",\"By using the shadow cast by the sun\",\"By using a battery\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that it uses the shadow cast by the sun to indicate time.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the time \'15 minutes past 10\' written in digits?\",\"options\":[\"15:10\",\"10:00\",\"10:15\",\"10:30\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 15 minutes past 10 is written as 10:15.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Pawan left for school at 10:15. If it took him 15 minutes to reach, at what time did he arrive?\",\"options\":[\"10:20\",\"10:30\",\"10:45\",\"11:00\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Adding 15 minutes to 10:15 results in 10:30 as per the calculation in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Pawan reached school at 10:30 and his journey took 15 minutes, at what time did he leave his house?\",\"options\":[\"10:00\",\"10:15\",\"10:45\",\"11:00\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that leaving at 10:15 and taking 15 minutes results in reaching at 10:30.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these devices is an ancient method of measuring time mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Digital clock\",\"Analog clock\",\"Bruhat Samrat Yantra\",\"Mobile phone clock\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Bruhat Samrat Yantra is mentioned as an ancient device built in the 18th century.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary difference between a digital clock and an analog clock based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Digital uses shadows, Analog uses digits\",\"Digital uses digits, Analog uses hands\",\"Digital uses hands, Analog uses shadows\",\"Digital is ancient, Analog is modern\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes them by the use of digits versus hands.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the Bruhat Samrat Yantra located?\",\"options\":[\"Maharashtra\",\"Rajasthan\",\"Gujarat\",\"Madhya Pradesh\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions it was built in Jaipur, in Rajasthan.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Observe\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what time does Naman start doing Yoga every day?\",\"options\":[\"6:00\",\"6:10\",\"6:40\",\"7:00\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Naman starts doing Yoga at 6:10 in the morning.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What activity does Naman perform in the morning between 6:10 and 6:40?\",\"options\":[\"Reading a book\",\"Going to school\",\"Doing Yoga\",\"School Assembly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Naman starts doing Yoga at 6:10 and stops at 6:40.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what time does Raju leave his home to go to school?\",\"options\":[\"9:00\",\"9:15\",\"9:45\",\"10:00\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Raju leaves home at 9:45 in the morning to go to school.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many minutes does Shrawani spend reading her story book?\",\"options\":[\"15 minutes\",\"30 minutes\",\"45 minutes\",\"60 minutes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says Shrawani finished reading the story in 45 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"For how many minutes does Naman do Yoga every morning?\",\"options\":[\"20 minutes\",\"30 minutes\",\"40 minutes\",\"50 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Naman starts at 6:10 and stops at 6:40; the difference is 30 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Shrawani started reading at 6:15 in the evening. At what time of the day was she reading?\",\"options\":[\"Morning\",\"Afternoon\",\"Evening\",\"Night\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions she started reading at 6:15 in the evening.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Raju leaves at 9:45 and reaches school at 10:00, how long is his journey?\",\"options\":[\"10 minutes\",\"15 minutes\",\"20 minutes\",\"25 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The time difference between 9:45 and 10:00 is 15 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Shrawani started reading at 6:15 in the evening and finished in 45 minutes. At what time did she finish reading?\",\"options\":[\"6:45\",\"7:00\",\"7:15\",\"7:30\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Adding 45 minutes to 6:15 results in 7:00.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Naman decided to do Yoga for 50 minutes starting at 6:10, at what time would he stop?\",\"options\":[\"6:50\",\"7:00\",\"7:10\",\"6:40\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"6:10 plus 50 minutes equals 7:00.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how much time passes between Raju reaching school and the start of the School Assembly?\",\"options\":[\"15 minutes\",\"20 minutes\",\"40 minutes\",\"35 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The arrow between \'He reached school\' and \'The School Assembly\' indicates \'After 20 minutes\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/120_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, if the School Assembly starts 20 minutes after Raju reaches school at 10:00, and the First period starts 40 minutes after the Assembly, at what time does the First period start?\",\"options\":[\"10:40\",\"10:20\",\"11:00\",\"11:20\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Assembly is at 10:20 (10:00 + 20 mins). First period is 10:20 + 40 mins = 11:00.\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"21\\/120_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the First period starts at 11:00, at what time does the Second period start according to the timeline?\",\"options\":[\"11:30\",\"11:35\",\"11:45\",\"12:00\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The timeline shows the Second period starts 35 minutes after the First period starts (11:00 + 35 mins = 11:35).\",\"topic\":\"Do the Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"On which date was the great mathematician Shrinivas Ramanujan born?\",\"options\":[\"22 December 1887\",\"27 April 1920\",\"22 December 1914\",\"15 August 1887\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states Shrinivas Ramanujan was born on 22 December 1887.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which state of India was Ramanujan born?\",\"options\":[\"Maharashtra\",\"Tamil Nadu\",\"Kerala\",\"Karnataka\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he was born at Erode in Tamil Nadu State.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many years did Ramanujan take to complete his primary education?\",\"options\":[\"Five years\",\"Seven years\",\"Two years\",\"Four years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he completed his primary education in just two years.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical value could Ramanujan state verbally to any decimal place?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the equator\",\"The square root of 2 and π\",\"The sum of 1729\",\"The area of a cloth shop\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He could verbally state the values of the square root of 2 and π (the irrational number) to any decimal place.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was Ramanujan\'s first job to help reduce his family\'s hardships?\",\"options\":[\"Professor at Cambridge\",\"Cloth shop owner\",\"Treasury clerk at Madras Port Trust\",\"High School teacher\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He started working as a treasury clerk at the Madras Port Trust to support his family.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Ramanujan\'s college scholarship discontinued?\",\"options\":[\"He moved to England\",\"He failed because he neglected subjects other than mathematics\",\"He was too young for the scholarship\",\"He started working at the Port Trust\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He neglected subjects like English and failed, resulting in the scholarship being discontinued.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In what year did Ramanujan reach Cambridge University for further studies?\",\"options\":[\"1887\",\"1920\",\"1914\",\"1919\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he reached Cambridge University in 1914.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Ramanujan was the first Indian to receive which honor?\",\"options\":[\"A scholarship to Madras Port Trust\",\"A fellowship from the Royal Society of London\",\"A degree from Town High School\",\"The title of \'Precious Cut Diamond\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Royal Society of London awarded him a fellowship, and he was the first Indian to receive this honor.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If Ramanujan was born in 1887 and reached Cambridge in 1914, how old was he when he reached the University?\",\"options\":[\"31 years old\",\"27 years old\",\"16 years old\",\"22 years old\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"1914 minus 1887 equals 27 years.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why did Ramanujan fall ill while in England?\",\"options\":[\"Due to overworking at the Port Trust\",\"Due to the unsuitable climate of England\",\"Because he failed his exams\",\"Because he missed his motherland\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he fell ill due to the unsuitable climate of England.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is special about the number 1729 according to Ramanujan?\",\"options\":[\"It is the year he returned to India\",\"It can be written as the sum of two cubes in two different ways\",\"It is the length of the Earth\'s equator\",\"It is the number of hours he worked in a year\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ramanujan explained it can be written as 10^3 + 9^3 and 12^3 + 1^3.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which calculation can be used to get the number 1729?\",\"options\":[\"10³ + 9³\",\"10² + 9²\",\"12³ + 9³\",\"12² + 1²\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ramanujan stated 1729 is the sum of 10^3 + 9^3.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how much time did Ramanujan dedicate to mathematics daily while working?\",\"options\":[\"10-12 hours\",\"All his free time after 10-12 hours of work\",\"Only 2 hours a day\",\"He only studied math on weekends\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He worked 10-12 hours a day and spent the rest of his free time studying mathematics.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When did Ramanujan pass away?\",\"options\":[\"February 1919\",\"December 22, 1887\",\"April 27, 1920\",\"March 31, 1914\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he finally passed away on April 27, 1920.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Prof. Hardy mean when he said, \'I could learn a lot from him\'?\",\"options\":[\"Hardy did not know any mathematics\",\"Ramanujan\'s mathematical knowledge was vast and original\",\"Ramanujan was a poor student\",\"Hardy wanted Ramanujan to teach at Cambridge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It reflects Ramanujan\'s genius and the fact that he had proven many theories that even experts could learn from.\",\"topic\":\"Great Indian Mathematician – Ramanujan\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(21584,21,4,'Maths','en','Patterns','[\"Patterns are designs that repeat in a regular way.\",\"Growing patterns increase or decrease by a fixed rule.\",\"Patterns can be made using shapes, numbers, or objects.\",\"Tiling means covering a surface with shapes without gaps or overlaps.\",\"Even numbers end with 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.\",\"Odd numbers end with 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.\",\"Multiplication tables show patterns in the units place.\",\"Triangular numbers form a triangle pattern: 1, 3, 6, 10, ...\",\"Patterns help us predict what comes next.\",\"Arrangements involve ordering items in different ways.\",\"Combinations are selections where order does not matter.\",\"Permutations are arrangements where order matters.\",\"Symmetry means both sides of a line are mirror images.\",\"Repeating patterns have a core that occurs again and again.\",\"Number patterns follow a rule like adding 2 or multiplying by 3.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In previous standards, which shapes were used to form growing patterns?\",\"options\":[\"One-dimensional shapes\",\"Two-dimensional shapes\",\"Three-dimensional shapes\",\"Four-dimensional shapes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in previous standards, we studied growing patterns formed by using two-dimensional shapes.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Remember\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of shapes are we now understanding for growing patterns?\",\"options\":[\"Flat shapes\",\"Two-dimensional shapes\",\"Three-dimensional shapes\",\"Circular shapes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Now let us understand the growing patterns formed by using three-dimensional shapes.\'\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s Remember\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which fruit is mentioned in the text as having a specific style of repeating pattern?\",\"options\":[\"Apple\",\"Mango\",\"Pineapple\",\"Banana\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":