or\",\"A scientist\",\"A pilot\",\"A lawyer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Carver studied hard and became a scientist.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these items did Carver and his students collect for their project?\",\"options\":[\"New machines\",\"Gold and silver\",\"Cans, bottles, and boxes\",\"Books and computers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists cans, bottles, boxes, pots, and pans as items they collected.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Carver have to go from place to place to find a college?\",\"options\":[\"He liked traveling\",\"Many colleges did not take Black students\",\"He was looking for his friends\",\"He wanted to find the biggest college\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'did not lose heart\' mean in the context of Carver\'s life?\",\"options\":[\"He became very ill\",\"He forgot his way\",\"He did not give up hope\",\"He stopped studying\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It means he remained determined despite the difficulties he faced in finding a college.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the purpose of collecting thrown-away items at the Institute?\",\"options\":[\"To clean the streets\",\"To sell them for money\",\"To make simple instruments for a laboratory\",\"To build a new house\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Carver told his students they would make simple instruments from these items to set up their laboratory.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How long did George Washington Carver work at the Tuskegee Institute?\",\"options\":[\"For a few months\",\"For ten years\",\"All his life\",\"Until he finished his project\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Carver worked there all his life.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to follow Carver\'s example for a science fair, what should they do?\",\"options\":[\"Buy expensive equipment from a shop\",\"Use discarded materials to create something useful\",\"Ask the teacher to do the work\",\"Only read about experiments\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carver showed that one can use \'useless\' things to do great things without spending much money.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Carver\'s method of setting up a laboratory shows that he was...\",\"options\":[\"Lazy and did not want to work\",\"Resourceful and creative\",\"Wealthy and liked to spend money\",\"Afraid of new things\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using thrown-away items to build a functional laboratory is a sign of being resourceful.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What important lesson did Carver teach his students through his laboratory project?\",\"options\":[\"Science is only for rich people\",\"One must always buy new things\",\"One does not have to spend a lot of money to do great things\",\"Black students cannot become scientists\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says he showed his students that one does not have to spend a lot of money to do great things.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text put the word \'useless\' in single quotes when describing the things Carver found?\",\"options\":[\"Because the items were actually very expensive\",\"Because the items were not really useless; Carver found new uses for them\",\"Because the items were broken and could not be fixed\",\"Because the students did not like the items\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The quotes indicate that while others thought the items were useless, Carver proved they were valuable.\",\"topic\":\"The Tuskegee Institute\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In which place did the poor farmers with small farms live?\",\"options\":[\"Alabama\",\"New York\",\"California\",\"Texas\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that Carver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the farmers grow cotton even though it made the soil poor?\",\"options\":[\"Because they liked the flowers\",\"Because there was profit in it\",\"Because Carver told them to\",\"Because the soil was already rich\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions farmers grew cotton because there was profit in it.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what was the negative effect of growing cotton?\",\"options\":[\"It made the farmers happy\",\"It made the soil rich\",\"It made the soil poor\",\"It produced too much sugar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says \'But cotton made the soil poor.\'\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which crops did Carver suggest to make the soil rich again?\",\"options\":[\"Cotton and tobacco\",\"Peanuts and beans\",\"Sugar and ink\",\"Paper and plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carver told farmers to grow peanuts and beans to make the soil rich again.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What problem did the farmers face after following Carver\'s advice to grow peanuts and beans?\",\"options\":[\"The crops did not grow at all\",\"The soil became even poorer\",\"They had so many crops they did not know what to do with them\",\"They could not find any profit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, what is George Washington Carver doing to help the farmers?\",\"options\":[\"He is selling peanuts in a market\",\"He is working in a laboratory to find new uses for plants\",\"He is planting cotton in a large field\",\"He is building a museum for the farmers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Carver working with scientific equipment, which relates to the text describing him looking for new uses for plants.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"27\\/087_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many different things did Carver make from peanuts?\",\"options\":[\"118\",\"1943\",\"300\",\"50\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he made as many as 300 things from peanuts.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these items did Carver NOT make from peanuts according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Boot-polish\",\"Plastic\",\"Cotton\",\"Paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cotton is a crop farmers grew; the text lists sugar, ink, boot-polish, colors, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, and milk as peanut products.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Look at the plant in the given figure. According to Carver\'s advice in the text, what is the main benefit of growing this specific plant?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the soil poor\",\"It is used to make cotton\",\"It makes the soil rich again\",\"It is only used for profit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a peanut plant, and the text says crops like peanuts make the soil rich again.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"27\\/087_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many uses did Carver find for the sweet potato?\",\"options\":[\"300\",\"118\",\"1943\",\"10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things.\'\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to the demand for these crops after Carver found new uses for them?\",\"options\":[\"The demand decreased\",\"The crops were in great demand\",\"The farmers stopped growing them\",\"The scientist stopped working\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\'\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the place where Carver worked used for today?\",\"options\":[\"It is a cotton farm\",\"It is a peanut factory\",\"It is a museum\",\"It is a hospital\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes by saying the place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\",\"topic\":\"Helping Farmers and New Uses\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21670,27,5,'English','en','Question Bank / Dice for your Game','[\"A cube net is a 2D shape that can be folded to make a 3D cube.\",\"A standard cube has 6 faces, 12 edges, and 8 vertices.\",\"When making a dice from a net, each face gets one word or symbol.\",\"Flaps are extra parts of the net used to glue and hold the cube together.\",\"You must cut the net carefully along the outer lines before folding.\",\"Fold the flaps inside so they are not visible on the outside of the cube.\",\"Let the glue dry completely before using the dice to avoid smudging.\",\"Each dice in the game has words on its faces — one word per face.\",\"In the game, players roll two dice and act out the word-color combination shown.\",\"The star symbol on the dice means you get a mark without performing any action.\",\"The game is played in groups of 5 to 8 students.\",\"The player with the highest number of marks after all rounds wins the game.\",\"The dice game helps improve reading, listening, and quick thinking skills.\",\"Always follow the instructions on the back cover board for the correct actions.\",\"Playing games like this makes learning fun and interactive.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where can the player find the \'board\' for this game?\",\"options\":[\"On the front cover\",\"On the back cover\",\"In the middle of the book\",\"On the first page\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states to use the ‘board’ given on the back cover.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many marks does a player get for successfully performing an action?\",\"options\":[\"2 marks\",\"5 marks\",\"1 mark\",\"10 marks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, if the player performs the action in 1 minute, he\\/she gets 1 mark.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What two examples of words are mentioned as appearing on the top sides of the dice?\",\"options\":[\"\'red\' and \'circle\'\",\"\'blue\' and \'triangle\'\",\"\'green\' and \'square\'\",\"\'yellow\' and \'star\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'green\' and \'square\' as examples of words on the top sides of the dice.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should a player do immediately after throwing the dice?\",\"options\":[\"Count the dots on the dice\",\"See the words on the top sides of the dice\",\"Wait for the next player\'s turn\",\"Move their piece on the board\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The instructions say to throw the dice and then see the words on the top sides.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the special meaning of the \'star\' symbol in the game?\",\"options\":[\"The player loses a turn\",\"The player must throw the dice again\",\"The player gets 1 mark without doing anything\",\"The player wins the game instantly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the star means you get 1 mark without doing anything.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the winner of the game determined?\",\"options\":[\"The player who reaches the end first\",\"The player with the highest marks\",\"The player who performs the most actions\",\"The player who gets the most stars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the player with the highest marks is the winner.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the dice show \'green\' and \'square\', what must the player do next?\",\"options\":[\"Read the action in the green square and perform it\",\"Find a green square on the back cover and color it\",\"Wait for 1 minute before throwing again\",\"Give 1 mark to the next player\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says the player has to read the action in the green square and perform it.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A player completes their assigned action in 40 seconds. How many marks do they receive?\",\"options\":[\"0 marks\",\"1 mark\",\"2 marks\",\"No marks because it was too fast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rule is to perform the action in 1 minute to get 1 mark; 40 seconds is within that limit.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific time limit given to perform an action in this game?\",\"options\":[\"30 seconds\",\"1 minute\",\"2 minutes\",\"5 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the player must perform the action in 1 minute.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the instructions, which of these is a requirement to earn a mark for an action?\",\"options\":[\"Doing the action in more than 1 minute\",\"Reading the action aloud to other players\",\"Performing the action within the 1-minute time limit\",\"Rolling a star on the dice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To get 1 mark, the player must perform the action in 1 minute.\",\"topic\":\"How to play\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which subject have you previously studied \'nets\'?\",\"options\":[\"Science\",\"Mathematics\",\"History\",\"Geography\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'You have studied ‘nets’ in mathematics.\'\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What material should you use to copy the cube net?\",\"options\":[\"Plain paper\",\"Cardsheet\",\"Newspaper\",\"Cloth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The instructions say to \'Copy this ‘cube net’ on a cardsheet.\'\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do with the flaps to make a cube?\",\"options\":[\"Cut them off\",\"Fold them inside\",\"Fold them outside\",\"Glue them to the table\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Fold the flaps inside and make a cube.\'\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many total nets are you asked to cut out according to the instructions?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Six\",\"Twelve\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to cut one net, then \'Cut out one more net in the same way.\'\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these words should be written on Net 1?\",\"options\":[\"Square\",\"Circle\",\"Pink\",\"Diamond\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Net 1 labels include green, pink, blue, yellow, violet, and orange.\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are labeling Net 2, which word would you use?\",\"options\":[\"Violet\",\"Orange\",\"Green\",\"Kite\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Net 2 labels include square, circle, diamond, oval, kite, and rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the number of words listed for each net, how many squares does one cube net have?\",\"options\":[\"Four\",\"Five\",\"Six\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"There are six words listed for each net (e.g., green, pink, blue, yellow, violet, orange), and the text says to write \'one word in each square\'.\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific name of the net mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Circle net\",\"Cube net\",\"Rectangle net\",\"Triangle net\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically refers to it as a \'cube net\'.\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sequence of actions after cutting out the net?\",\"options\":[\"Fold flaps, make cube, unfold\",\"Unfold, fold flaps, make cube\",\"Make cube, unfold, fold flaps\",\"Cut flaps, fold cube, unfold\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says: \'Fold the flaps inside and make a cube. Unfold.\'\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which category of words is used to label Net 2?\",\"options\":[\"Colors\",\"Shapes\",\"Sizes\",\"Numbers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Net 2 uses words like square, circle, diamond, oval, kite, and rectangle, which are all shapes.\",\"topic\":\"Labelling the Nets\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where should you put the glue according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"On the sides of the cube\",\"On the flaps of the net\",\"On the back cover of the book\",\"On the game board\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'put some glue on the flaps of one net\'.\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What shape is formed after sticking the flaps to the sides?\",\"options\":[\"A square\",\"A triangle\",\"A cube\",\"A rectangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to stick them on to the sides \'to make a cube\'.\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What must you do immediately after sticking the flaps to the sides?\",\"options\":[\"Start playing the game\",\"Let the glue dry\",\"Make groups of 5-8\",\"Throw the dice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs, \'stick them on to the sides to make a cube. Let the glue dry.\'\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many cubes do you need to make in total to have your dice ready?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to make one cube and then \'Repeat the process to make the other cube\', totaling two.\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the instructions, what is the \'net\' used for?\",\"options\":[\"To catch objects\",\"To make a cube\",\"To clean the glue\",\"To cover the book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The net is the starting material that is glued and folded to make a cube.\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have finished making one cube, what is the next step to complete the set?\",\"options\":[\"Read the back cover\",\"Repeat the process for the other cube\",\"Join a group of 10 people\",\"Put glue on the sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Repeat the process to make the other cube.\'\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the game located that you will play with these dice?\",\"options\":[\"On the front cover of the book\",\"On the back cover of the book\",\"Inside the dice\",\"On the flaps of the net\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions to \'play the game given on the back cover of this book.\'\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the suggested group size for playing the game?\",\"options\":[\"2 to 4 people\",\"5 to 8 people\",\"8 to 10 people\",\"Exactly 5 people\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'make groups of 5-8\'.\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to \'let the glue dry\' before playing?\",\"options\":[\"To make the dice look colorful\",\"To ensure the cube stays together\",\"To make the dice heavier\",\"To change the shape of the cube\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Letting the glue dry is a necessary step in the construction process to ensure the flaps stay stuck to the sides.\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the flaps stuck onto to form the cube?\",\"options\":[\"The other cube\",\"The sides of the net\",\"The back cover\",\"The glue bottle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'stick them on to the sides to make a cube.\'\",\"topic\":\"Making the Dice\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21671,27,5,'English','en','All about Money','[\"Money is used to buy things and pay for services.\",\"Long ago, people used barter system to exchange goods.\",\"Barter means trading one thing for another without using money.\",\"People used stones, salt bricks, shells, and cowries as money in the past.\",\"Grains, cattle, and farm produce were also used in exchange for goods.\",\"Later, precious metals like gold, silver, and copper were used as money.\",\"Paper money was first used in China about 1000 years ago.\",\"Paper notes have no value of their own but are a promise to pay.\",\"Only the government is allowed to print paper money.\",\"We should not write on, tear, crumple, or damage money notes.\",\"We must be careful when spending money.\",\"People work hard to earn money.\",\"Saving money wisely helps in future needs.\",\"Spending money carelessly can lead to problems.\",\"Money makes exchange easier than barter system.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the text define \'Currency\' as?\",\"options\":[\"Gold and Silver\",\"Cash Money\",\"Bank accounts\",\"Work and Labor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states \'Currency : Cash Money\'.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which currency is used in England according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Dollars\",\"Rupees\",\"Pounds\",\"Paise\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'In England, they use pounds.\'\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why do we all need money?\",\"options\":[\"To plant trees\",\"To travel to different countries\",\"To buy things and pay people who work for us\",\"To collect different types of paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions we need money to buy things and pay workers.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In what two forms do we see money today?\",\"options\":[\"Leaves and seeds\",\"Coins and notes\",\"Stones and shells\",\"Gold and diamonds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Today, we see money in the form of coins and notes.\'\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is buying a book in the United States, what currency will they most likely use?\",\"options\":[\"Pounds\",\"Rupees\",\"Dollars\",\"Paise\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in the United States, they use dollars.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is true about money used in different countries?\",\"options\":[\"Every country uses the same notes\",\"Money is known by different names in different countries\",\"Money is grown on trees in the United States\",\"Only India uses coins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that different countries use different money known by different names.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these is listed under the \'coins\' category?\",\"options\":[\"a five-rupee note\",\"a fifty-paise coin\",\"twenty rupees\",\"five hundred-rupees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the fifty-paise coin in the \'coins\' column.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"27\\/090_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific value is shown as both a coin and a note?\",\"options\":[\"Fifty-paise\",\"One-rupee\",\"Five-rupee\",\"Fifty-rupees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure lists \'a five-rupee coin\' and \'a five-rupee note\'.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"27\\/090_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a \'two thousand-rupee\' item as shown in the diagram, how would you classify it?\",\"options\":[\"As a coin\",\"As a note\",\"As a dollar\",\"As a pound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram places the two thousand-rupee item under the \'notes\' column.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how does the money in India differ from the money in the United States?\",\"options\":[\"India uses money but the United States does not\",\"India uses coins but the United States only uses trees\",\"They use different coins, notes, and names for their money\",\"Only the United States uses cash money\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that different countries use different coins and notes with different names.\",\"topic\":\"Modern Currency\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the system of exchanging one thing for another called in the past?\",\"options\":[\"Buying\",\"Barter\",\"Selling\",\"Banking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that exchanging one thing for another was known as \'barter\'.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these shells were used as money long ago?\",\"options\":[\"Conch shells\",\"Snail shells\",\"Cowries\",\"Oyster shells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that people used shells and cowries as money in the past.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why was using special stones as money a problem?\",\"options\":[\"They were too small to see\",\"They were difficult to carry\",\"They were very easy to break\",\"They were not pretty\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks the reader to imagine how difficult it must have been to carry stones used as money.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which seasoning was shaped into bricks and used as money?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar\",\"Pepper\",\"Chilli\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that people used bricks of salt as money in the past.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the past, what did people give in return for clothes and tools?\",\"options\":[\"Gold coins\",\"Paper notes\",\"Grain and farm produce\",\"Plastic cards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states people gave grain, farm produce, cattle, etc., in return for clothes and tools.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a farmer gives a goat to a blacksmith to get a new tool, what system is he using?\",\"options\":[\"The credit system\",\"The barter system\",\"The digital system\",\"The coin system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Exchanging one thing (a goat) for another (a tool) is the definition of barter.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A worker receives chickens after working all day on a farm. In the text, these chickens are called:\",\"options\":[\"Gifts\",\"Prizes\",\"Wages\",\"Pets\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that things like chickens were given as wages.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was NOT used as money according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Special stones\",\"Shells\",\"Credit cards\",\"Cowries\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions stones, shells, and cowries, but does not mention credit cards as early money.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text describe the use of stones as money as \'unbelievable\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because stones have no value today\",\"Because stones are very heavy and hard to move as currency\",\"Because stones are found everywhere for free\",\"Because stones cannot be divided into smaller parts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The author highlights the difficulty of carrying stones to show why it seems unbelievable to use them as money.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the main difference between ancient money and modern money?\",\"options\":[\"Ancient money was always made of paper\",\"Ancient money was often useful things like grain or cattle\",\"Modern money is harder to carry than stones\",\"Ancient money was only used by rich people\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that early money consisted of physical goods like grain, cattle, and salt rather than coins and notes.\",\"topic\":\"Early Forms of Money\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these metals was NOT mentioned as being used for money in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Silver\",\"Iron\",\"Copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions gold, silver, and copper as precious metals used for money, but not iron.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where was paper money first used?\",\"options\":[\"India\",\"China\",\"America\",\"England\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that paper money was first used in China.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did people start using bits of precious metals as money?\",\"options\":[\"Because they were very heavy\",\"Because they were easier to carry around\",\"Because they were very cheap\",\"Because they were made of paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says it was easier to carry bits of precious metals around compared to previous forms of money.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"About how many years ago was paper money first used?\",\"options\":[\"100 years ago\",\"500 years ago\",\"1000 years ago\",\"5000 years ago\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions paper money was first used about 1000 years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the actual value of the paper used to make a money note?\",\"options\":[\"It is worth exactly what is printed on it\",\"It is worth more than gold\",\"It has no value of its own\",\"It is very expensive to buy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that paper money or notes have no value of their own.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, a paper note is a \'promise\' to do what?\",\"options\":[\"To give that much money\",\"To buy more gold\",\"To travel to China\",\"To help the government\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines a note as a \'promise\' to give that much money.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a private company wants to print its own currency notes, are they allowed to do so?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, if they have enough paper\",\"No, only the government is allowed to print notes\",\"Yes, if they use gold ink\",\"No, only people in China can print notes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text clearly states that notes are printed by the government and no one else is allowed to print them.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between precious metal money and paper money mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Metal money is only used in China\",\"Paper money is harder to carry\",\"Paper money has no value of its own, unlike metal\",\"Metal money is a promise, but paper is not\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies metals are precious (have value), while paper notes have no value of their own.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, who ensures that a paper note is valid and accepted?\",\"options\":[\"The shopkeeper\",\"The person who first used it in China\",\"The government\",\"The person who carries it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the government prints the notes and no one else is allowed to, the government is the authority behind the \'promise\'.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a 10-rupee note, what does it represent based on the definition in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A bit of precious copper\",\"A promise to give 10 rupees worth of value\",\"A piece of paper that is 1000 years old\",\"A gift from the government\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says a note is a \'promise\' to give that much money.\",\"topic\":\"Paper Money\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what should you not do to currency notes?\",\"options\":[\"Keep them in a wallet\",\"Write on them\",\"Use them to buy books\",\"Earn them by working\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that you should not write on the notes.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How do people get the money they have, according to the passage?\",\"options\":[\"They find it on the road\",\"They ask for it\",\"They work hard to earn it\",\"They win it in games\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'People have to work hard to earn money!\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these actions is mentioned as a way of damaging notes?\",\"options\":[\"Spending\",\"Earning\",\"Crumpling\",\"Saving\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists crumpling as a way notes are damaged.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main advice given in the passage regarding money?\",\"options\":[\"Money should be spent quickly\",\"Money must be used carefully\",\"Money should be kept in a box\",\"Money is easy to get\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The opening sentence states, \'You must use your money carefully.\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why should we be careful when we spend money?\",\"options\":[\"Because notes are very heavy\",\"Because it takes hard work to earn it\",\"Because shops do not take notes\",\"Because we should never buy anything\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links careful spending to the fact that people work hard to earn money.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'crumple\' mean in the context of the text?\",\"options\":[\"To make something smooth\",\"To crush or wrinkle paper\",\"To clean something with water\",\"To color something with a pen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Crumpling refers to crushing the paper notes, which damages them.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Rahul wants to follow the advice in the text, what should he do with his pocket money?\",\"options\":[\"Write his name on every note\",\"Fold and crush the notes into a ball\",\"Handle the notes gently and spend wisely\",\"Tear the notes into small pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Handling notes gently and spending wisely aligns with using money carefully and not damaging it.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a correct application of the rule \'do not damage notes\'?\",\"options\":[\"Using a note to scribble a phone number\",\"Keeping notes flat and clean in a purse\",\"Tearing a note to share it with two people\",\"Washing a note to see if the color runs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Keeping notes flat and clean prevents tearing, crumpling, or writing on them.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following actions is categorized by the author as \'not using money carefully\'?\",\"options\":[\"Working hard at a job\",\"Buying necessary items\",\"Tearing a currency note\",\"Saving money for the future\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tearing a note is specifically mentioned as something you should not do.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement best evaluates the value of money?\",\"options\":[\"Money is just paper and can be treated poorly\",\"Money is valuable because of the effort required to earn it\",\"Money should only be used for writing notes\",\"Money is easy to replace if it gets torn\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text emphasizes that because people work hard to earn it, it must be handled with care.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21672,27,5,'English','en','Only One Mother','[\"The poem \'Only One Mother\' is written by George Cooper.\",\"It describes a scene with hundreds of butterflies on purple clover in sunny weather.\",\"Despite many beautiful things in nature, the poem emphasizes that there is only one mother in the wide world.\",\"The poem highlights the uniqueness and irreplaceable role of a mother.\",\"Words like \'purple clover\', \'sunny weather\', \'butterflies\', and \'dewdrops\' help create a vivid natural image.\",\"The central idea is that while many things exist in abundance, a mother is one of a kind.\",\"The tone of the poem is appreciative and reflective.\",\"Students should recite the poem with proper intonation and emotion.\",\"The poem encourages thinking about what is unique and precious in life.\",\"It contrasts quantity (hundreds of butterflies) with singularity (one mother).\",\"The poem is part of the English curriculum to develop reading, comprehension, and emotional awareness.\",\"Key vocabulary includes: clover, lawn, dewdrops, wide world, only one.\",\"The poem teaches values of love, gratitude, and respect for mothers.\",\"Imagery in the poem helps visualize a peaceful, natural setting.\",\"The message is universal: every child has one special mother.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where are the hundreds of stars located according to the poem?\",\"options\":[\"On the shore\",\"In the purple clover\",\"In the pretty sky\",\"On the lawn\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first line of the poem states, \'Hundreds of stars in the pretty sky\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What can be found \'on the shore together\'?\",\"options\":[\"Birds\",\"Shells\",\"Bees\",\"Lambs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions \'Hundreds of shells on the shore together\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what do the birds do as they go by?\",\"options\":[\"They fly fast\",\"They go singing\",\"They build nests\",\"They greet the dawn\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'Hundreds of birds that go singing by\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In what kind of weather are the hundreds of lambs mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Rainy weather\",\"Sunny weather\",\"Cloudy weather\",\"Cold weather\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem specifies \'Hundreds of lambs in the sunny weather\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What greets the \'dawn\' in the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Butterflies\",\"Bees\",\"Dew drops\",\"Stars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'Hundreds of dew drops to greet the dawn\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What color is the clover where the bees are found?\",\"options\":[\"Green\",\"Purple\",\"Yellow\",\"White\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions \'Hundreds of bees in the purple clover\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where are the hundreds of butterflies located?\",\"options\":[\"In the sky\",\"On the shore\",\"On the lawn\",\"In the clover\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'Hundreds of butterflies on the lawn\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between the stars, shells, and birds compared to a mother in the poem?\",\"options\":[\"There are hundreds of them, but only one mother\",\"They are all in the sky, but mother is on earth\",\"They are all animals, but mother is a person\",\"They are all small, but mother is big\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem contrasts \'hundreds\' of various things with \'only one mother\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase does the poet use to describe the extent of the world?\",\"options\":[\"The pretty world\",\"The sunny world\",\"The wide world over\",\"The purple world\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The final line of the poem is \'But only one mother the wide world over\'.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the poem \'Only One Mother\'?\",\"options\":[\"George Washington\",\"George Cooper\",\"Robert Frost\",\"Balbharati\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The name George Cooper is printed at the end of the poem as the author.\",\"topic\":\"Poem - Stanza 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21673,27,5,'English','en','At the Market','[\"The chapter \'At the Market\' shows different conversations between buyers and sellers.\",\"People buy vegetables, fruits, grains, and other daily items at the market.\",\"Sellers show varieties and tell prices to help customers choose.\",\"Customers ask about price, quality, and availability before buying.\",\"Polite words like \'please\', \'thank you\', and \'excuse me\' are used in market talks.\",\"Some items sold include potatoes, tomatoes, rice, corn-cobs, cakes, and juice.\",\"Buyers may ask for specific things like no ice in juice or a carry-bag.\",\"Sellers may refuse plastic bags and offer alternatives like cloth or paper bags.\",\"Conversations can be enacted or role-played to practice speaking English.\",\"Listening carefully and reading aloud helps improve pronunciation and fluency.\",\"The market is a place where people exchange goods and money for daily needs.\",\"Different stalls sell different things, so people go to various sellers.\",\"Freshness and quality are important when buying food items.\",\"Bargaining or discussing price is common in markets.\",\"Helping each other, like carrying bags, shows kindness in market interactions.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does Aaji want to buy for the whole year?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Wheat\",\"Potatoes\",\"Tomatoes\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the first conversation, Aaji says, \'I want to buy rice for the whole year.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many bubble-makers does Grandpa decide to buy?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Grandpa says, \'Let’s buy three. Two for you children and one for me!\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who sent the list of grocery items to the Uncle?\",\"options\":[\"Aaji\",\"Grandpa\",\"Mother\",\"The children\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The child says, \'Mother has sent this list of grocery items for the month.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the seller tell Aaji not to worry about the price of the rice?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is free\",\"Because it is not very costly\",\"Because it is the best quality\",\"Because Aaji is his friend\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The seller says, \'Don’t worry about the price. It is not very costly.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the second conversation, what will the customer give the seller for the tomatoes?\",\"options\":[\"More money\",\"A list of items\",\"Another bag\",\"Potatoes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The customer says, \'Wait. I’ll give you another bag for the tomatoes.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When does the Uncle agree to deliver the grocery items?\",\"options\":[\"Next week\",\"Tomorrow\",\"Today\",\"In a few days\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Uncle says, \'Sure. We’ll deliver them today.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the seller in the second conversation, which vegetable is \'nice and fresh\'?\",\"options\":[\"Potatoes\",\"Rice\",\"Tomatoes\",\"Onions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The seller says, \'Would you like to buy tomatoes? These are nice and fresh.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the instruction in the text, what should we avoid using at the market?\",\"options\":[\"Paper bags\",\"Plastic bags\",\"Cloth bags\",\"Money\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'Avoid plastic bags.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Grandpa buy three bubble-makers instead of just two?\",\"options\":[\"One was for the seller\",\"He wanted one for himself\",\"He had three children with him\",\"One was for Aaji\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Grandpa says he is buying \'Two for you children and one for me!\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What two things does Aaji ask the seller to show or tell her about the rice?\",\"options\":[\"Weight and color\",\"Varieties and prices\",\"Taste and smell\",\"Bag and delivery\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Aaji says, \'Show me the varieties you have and tell me the prices.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does the seller describe the specific rice variety he recommends to Aaji?\",\"options\":[\"It is very expensive\",\"It is not good for the year\",\"It is good and not very costly\",\"It is the only variety available\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The seller says, \'Aaji, this variety is good... It is not very costly.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which conversation involves a request for home delivery of items?\",\"options\":[\"The conversation with Aaji\",\"The conversation about tomatoes\",\"The conversation about bubble-makers\",\"The conversation with the Uncle about the grocery list\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In the fourth conversation, the child asks, \'Can you deliver them today?\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 1\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the conversation, what does Person B like to have with roasted corn-cobs?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar and honey\",\"Salt and lemon-juice\",\"Butter and pepper\",\"Chilli powder and oil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the first dialogue, B says, \'Oh, I love them with salt and lemon-juice.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does Person A want to find out about the corn-cobs?\",\"options\":[\"If they are fresh\",\"Where they are grown\",\"At what price they are being sold\",\"If they are sweet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A says, \'Let’s see at what price he is selling them.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is Person B\'s opinion about cakes in the second dialogue?\",\"options\":[\"B loves all kinds of cakes\",\"B is not very fond of cakes\",\"B thinks they are too expensive\",\"B wants to buy a chocolate cake\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"B explicitly states, \'But I’m not very fond of cakes.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does Person B notice about the cake shop?\",\"options\":[\"It is very old\",\"It is a new shop\",\"It is very crowded\",\"It is closed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"B asks, \'This is a new cake shop, isn’t it?\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the third conversation, what does the customer ask for when they realize they don\'t have a bag?\",\"options\":[\"A paper bag\",\"A cloth bag\",\"A plastic carry-bag\",\"A cardboard box\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The customer says, \'I don’t have one. Give me a plastic carry-bag.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the tomato seller say \'Sorry\' to the customer?\",\"options\":[\"He has no tomatoes left\",\"The tomatoes are very expensive\",\"He does not keep plastic carry-bags\",\"He forgot to weigh the tomatoes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The seller says, \'Sorry. I don’t keep plastic carry-bags.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the customer\'s initial request regarding ice in the juice?\",\"options\":[\"Put lots of ice\",\"Put ice in only one glass\",\"Don\'t put ice in the juice\",\"Put ice on the side\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A says, \'And don’t put ice in the juice.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many glasses of juice does the customer order?\",\"options\":[\"One small glass\",\"Two full glasses\",\"Three half glasses\",\"Four large glasses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Please give us two full glasses.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What final decision does Mamma make about the ice in the juice?\",\"options\":[\"No ice at all\",\"A lot of ice in one glass\",\"Just a little ice in each glass\",\"Ice only for herself\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mamma says, \'OK, put just a little ice in each glass.\'\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the dialogues, which item is the seller refusing to provide for environmental or shop policy reasons?\",\"options\":[\"Roasted corn\",\"Delicious cakes\",\"Plastic carry-bags\",\"Ice cubes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The tomato seller specifically states he does not keep plastic carry-bags.\",\"topic\":\"Market Conversations - 2\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21674,27,5,'English','en','He Knows the Workman','[\"Jaikishan is a skilled stone carver who works in a dark temple corner.\",\"He carves a devadoota (angel) figure with great care and love.\",\"Even though no one sees his work, Jaikishan believes God sees it.\",\"The story teaches that work should be done sincerely, not for praise.\",\"Jaikishan carved the entire devadoota, including garments, very carefully.\",\"The garments looked thin and soft, though made of stone.\",\"The man in the corner questioned why Jaikishan worked so hard unseen.\",\"Jaikishan replied that God sees his work and knows the workman.\",\"The story highlights honesty, dedication, and inner satisfaction in work.\",\"It is adapted from a story by John Martis.\",\"The theme is \'Work is worship\'.\",\"Doing work with devotion brings inner peace and moral strength.\",\"One should do their best, even if no one appreciates it.\",\"True workmanship comes from love and duty, not external reward.\",\"The story encourages students to value effort and integrity in tasks.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who was the king when the new temple was being built?\",\"options\":[\"King Akbar\",\"King Ashoka\",\"King Shivaji\",\"King Vikram\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the temple was being built at the time of King Ashoka.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where was the new temple being built?\",\"options\":[\"In a small village\",\"In the capital city\",\"In a thick forest\",\"On a high mountain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the temple was being built in the capital city.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the people try their best to achieve while building the temple?\",\"options\":[\"To finish it in one day\",\"To make it small and simple\",\"To make it strong and beautiful\",\"To make it the tallest in the world\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says they tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did the rich people contribute to the construction of the temple?\",\"options\":[\"They cut the timber\",\"They prepared the stones\",\"They gave their money\",\"They did the carving work\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions that rich people gave their money.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person was skilled at working with wood, which task from the text would they perform?\",\"options\":[\"Giving money\",\"Cutting timber\",\"Preparing stones\",\"Building the foundation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Timber refers to wood used for building, so a woodworker would cut timber.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, who was involved in building the great temple?\",\"options\":[\"Only the rich people\",\"Only the workmen\",\"Everyone in the capital\",\"Only the King\'s soldiers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Everyone helped to build the great temple.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which animals are carved in a row on the temple pillar?\",\"options\":[\"Lions and Tigers\",\"Elephants and Horses\",\"Dogs and Cats\",\"Birds and Fish\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows rows of elephants and horses carved into the stone pillar.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"27\\/096_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what tools is the workman using to carve the stone?\",\"options\":[\"A saw and a drill\",\"A hammer and a chisel\",\"A brush and paint\",\"A needle and thread\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The picture shows the workman holding a hammer and a chisel to shape the stone.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"27\\/096_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which two specific materials are mentioned in the text as being prepared for the temple?\",\"options\":[\"Bricks and cement\",\"Stones and timber\",\"Gold and silver\",\"Glass and sand\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions others helped to \'prepare the stones, and cut the timber.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text suggest about the community\'s attitude toward the temple?\",\"options\":[\"They were forced to work\",\"They did not care about the quality\",\"It was a collective effort by all\",\"Only the workmen were interested\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text emphasizes that all people helped and everyone put in their best work.\",\"topic\":\"The Building of the Temple\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What was Jaikishan\'s profession?\",\"options\":[\"A carpenter\",\"A stone carver\",\"A temple priest\",\"A painter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Jaikishan was the stone carver.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What kind of things did Jaikishan carve in stone?\",\"options\":[\"Only names of people\",\"Beautiful figures and designs\",\"Wooden furniture\",\"Simple straight lines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he carved beautiful figures and designs in stone.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What physical challenge did Jaikishan face because he was old?\",\"options\":[\"He could not see at all\",\"He could not walk\",\"His hands shook a little\",\"He could not speak\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that because he was old, his hands shook a little.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Despite his shaking hands, how is Jaikishan described as a worker?\",\"options\":[\"A lazy workman\",\"A bad workman\",\"A good workman\",\"A fast workman\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Yet, he was a good workman.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the proverb mentioned in the text, what does a \'bad workman\' do?\",\"options\":[\"He works in the dark\",\"He blames his tools\",\"He asks for more help\",\"He carves beautiful designs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text includes the proverb: \'A bad workman blames his tools.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where was Jaikishan told to carve the figure of the devadoota?\",\"options\":[\"In the center of the temple\",\"On the ceiling of the temple\",\"In a corner of the temple\",\"Outside the temple gates\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he was told to carve the figure in a corner of the temple.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why would people not be able to see the devadoota clearly?\",\"options\":[\"Because it was too small\",\"Because the corner was nearly always dark\",\"Because it was covered with a curtain\",\"Because it was carved behind a pillar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the corner was nearly always dark, so no one would see it clearly.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the devadoota would people be able to see clearly?\",\"options\":[\"The hands\",\"The feet\",\"The face\",\"The wings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the fact that Jaikishan \'took great care with his work\' tell us about him?\",\"options\":[\"He was a dedicated and responsible workman\",\"He was trying to finish the work quickly\",\"He only cared about the parts people could see\",\"He was worried about his tools\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Taking great care despite his age and the location of the work shows his dedication.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If Jaikishan was a \'bad workman\', how might he have reacted to his shaking hands?\",\"options\":[\"He would have practiced more\",\"He would have blamed his tools for any mistakes\",\"He would have asked for a brighter corner\",\"He would have carved a smaller figure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies a bad workman shifts responsibility to his tools.\",\"topic\":\"The Dark Corner\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What specific figure was Jaikishan carving in the stone?\",\"options\":[\"A king\",\"A devadoota\",\"A soldier\",\"A flower\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Jaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face.\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What material were the garments of the devadoota made from?\",\"options\":[\"Soft cloth\",\"Wood\",\"Stone\",\"Clay\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the garments were carved in stone.\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the visitor do before he started talking to Jaikishan?\",\"options\":[\"He laughed loudly\",\"He smiled\",\"He helped carve the stone\",\"He complained about the darkness\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the man saw Jaikishan\'s work and then \'The man smiled.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Jaikishan continue carving after finishing the beautiful face?\",\"options\":[\"He was told to do so by the visitor\",\"He felt that just the face was not enough\",\"He wanted to make the stone look hard\",\"He was trying to fix a mistake\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'“But this is not enough,” he said. He went on carving.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did the stone garments appear after Jaikishan finished carving them?\",\"options\":[\"Rough and heavy\",\"Thin and soft\",\"Shiny and bright\",\"Broken and cracked\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the garments were carved in stone but \'looked thin and soft.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the visitor\'s main question to Jaikishan?\",\"options\":[\"How much will you sell this for?\",\"Why do you take such trouble with the garments?\",\"When will you finish this work?\",\"Who taught you how to carve stone?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The visitor asked, \'Why do you take such trouble with the garments?\'\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to work \'lovingly\' like Jaikishan, how should they complete their drawing?\",\"options\":[\"Finish it as fast as possible\",\"Draw only the parts the teacher will see\",\"Work with great care and affection on every part\",\"Ask someone else to finish the difficult parts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Working lovingly implies putting care and heart into the entire work, just as Jaikishan did.\",\"topic\":\"The Visitor\'s Question\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the visitor\'s words, what would happen if Jaikishan stopped working on the hidden parts?\",\"options\":[\"The statue would break\",\"No one would notice because it is in a dark corner\",\"The visitor would be angry\",\"Jaikishan would lose his job\"],\"an