er\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that the number of pairs is equal to the product of the number of objects in the two groups.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Ajay has 3 shirts and 2 pairs of trousers, how many different ways does he have of pairing them off?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"1\",\"3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"3 shirts multiplied by 2 trousers equals 6 different pairs.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To make different pairs from two collections, how many objects should be taken from each collection every time?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"All objects\",\"None\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says: Make as many different pairs as possible, taking one object from each collection every time.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If one collection has 2 objects and the other collection has 4 objects, how many ways are there to form pairs?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The product of 2 and 4 is 8.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Suresh has 3 balls (A, B, C) and 3 bats (P, Q, R). How many ways can he pair off a ball and a bat?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"6\",\"9\",\"12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"3 balls multiplied by 3 bats equals 9 ways.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many total photographs were taken of the three friends wearing every type of hat?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"8\",\"15\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"There are 3 boys and 5 types of hats, so 3 x 5 = 15 photographs.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/027_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the first collection has 3 objects and the second has 4, what is the correct number of ways to form pairs?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"1\",\"12\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the table and the rule of products, 3 x 4 = 12.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/027_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a group of boys took a total of 12 photos and there were 4 types of hats, how many boys were in the group?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"8\",\"16\",\"48\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since the product is 12 and one group is 4, the other group must be 12 divided by 4, which is 3.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student has 5 notebooks and 2 pens. How many different ways can they pair one notebook with one pen?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"3\",\"10\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the product rule from the text, 5 x 2 = 10.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rule provided in the text, if one group has 0 objects, how many pairs can be formed?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"The number of objects in the other group\",\"0\",\"Cannot be determined\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The number of pairs is the product; any number multiplied by 0 is 0.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the pair (Green S, White T) from Ajay\'s example?\",\"options\":[\"Green Shirt and White Trousers\",\"Green Socks and White T-shirt\",\"Green Shirt and White Tie\",\"Grey Shirt and White Trousers\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"S stands for Shirt and T stands for Trousers as per the text.\",\"topic\":\"Pairing off objects from two groups\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the teacher, what does division mean?\",\"options\":[\"Adding numbers together\",\"Making equal parts of a given number\",\"Multiplying two numbers\",\"Finding the difference between numbers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that division means making equal parts of a given number.\",\"topic\":\"Division\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Division is also described as subtracting a number ____ from a given number.\",\"options\":[\"once\",\"twice\",\"repeatedly\",\"randomly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines division as subtracting a number repeatedly from a given number.\",\"topic\":\"Division\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many divisions can be obtained from one multiplication fact like 9 × 4 = 36?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shubha mentions that we know we get two divisions from one multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"Division\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From the multiplication 9 × 4 = 36, which of these is a correct division?\",\"options\":[\"36 ÷ 6 = 6\",\"9 ÷ 4 = 36\",\"36 ÷ 9 = 4\",\"4 ÷ 9 = 36\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that from 9 × 4 = 36, we get 36 ÷ 4 = 9 and 36 ÷ 9 = 4.\",\"topic\":\"Division\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To solve 354 ÷ 6 mentally, how did Sarag split the number 354?\",\"options\":[\"350 + 4\",\"300 + 54\",\"200 + 154\",\"340 + 14\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sarag explains his mental calculation by splitting 354 into 300 + 54.\",\"topic\":\"Division\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the division 4925 ÷ 7, why did Shubha take 49 hundreds to divide?\",\"options\":[\"Because 4 thousands cannot be divided by 7 into whole thousands\",\"Because 49 is a smaller number than 7\",\"Because the remainder must be 49\",\"Because 7 is a two-digit number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shubha says we cannot divide 4 thousands by 7 into whole thousands, so they take 49 hundreds.\",\"topic\":\"Division\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the division 4925 ÷ 7, what digit is written in the tens place of the quotient?\",\"options\