that 45 × 9 = 405 and since 405 > 392, 9 cannot be the next digit.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 35 kilograms of wheat cost 910 rupees, how do you find the rate of wheat per kilogram?\",\"options\":[\"Multiply 910 by 35\",\"Add 910 and 35\",\"Divide 910 by 35\",\"Subtract 35 from 910\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when we divide 910 by 35, we will get the per kg rate of wheat.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to Example (4), what is the rate per kilogram of wheat if 35 kg costs 910 rupees?\",\"options\":[\"35 rupees\",\"26 rupees\",\"45 rupees\",\"91 rupees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that the rate per kilogram of wheat is 26 rupees.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When dividing 84 by 45, why is 1 written in the tens place of the quotient?\",\"options\":[\"Because 45 × 2 = 90, and 90 is greater than 84\",\"Because 45 × 1 = 45, and 45 is greater than 84\",\"Because 84 is less than 45\",\"Because 45 is a two-digit number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that as 90 > 84, we have to write 1 in the tens place.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the division 9842 ÷ 45, why is 8 chosen for the units place instead of 9?\",\"options\":[\"Because 45 × 8 = 360, which is less than 392\",\"Because 45 × 8 = 405, which is greater than 392\",\"Because 8 is the last digit of the dividend\",\"Because 45 × 8 = 392\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 45 × 8 = 360, and since 360 < 392, 8 is written in the units place.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the dividend being divided by 25?\",\"options\":[\"170\",\"4254\",\"175\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the number 4254 inside the division bracket, which is the dividend.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/028_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the final remainder of the division?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"25\",\"170\",\"4\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the final subtraction resulting in 0004 at the bottom, which is the remainder.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/028_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What are the quotient and remainder for Example (2) mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Quotient 170, Remainder 4\",\"Quotient 218, Remainder 32\",\"Quotient 305, Remainder 23\",\"Quotient 26, Remainder 0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists Example (2) as: Quotient 305, Remainder 23.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the teacher suggest guessing the quotient for 98 divided by 45?\",\"options\":[\"Divide 9 by 4\",\"Multiply 9 by 4\",\"Subtract 4 from 9\",\"Add 9 and 4\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Dividing 9 by 4, we will get 2 in the quotient\' to guess the digit.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the formula in Example (4), which of the following is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Rate = Cost + Weight\",\"Cost = Weight × Rate\",\"Weight = Cost × Rate\",\"Rate = Weight ÷ Cost\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula: Weight of wheat in kg × rate of wheat per kg = cost of wheat.\",\"topic\":\"Dividing by a two-digit number\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21740,30,5,'Maths','en','Fractions','[\"- A fraction shows part of a whole using a numerator (top number) and a denominator (bottom number).\",\"- The fraction ½ means one part out of two equal parts.\",\"- If two fractions have the same value, they are called equivalent fractions (e.g., ½ = 2⁄4 = 3⁄6).\",\"- To make a fraction equivalent, multiply or divide both numerator and denominator by the same non‑zero number.\",\"- A fraction with the same numerator and denominator (e.g., 3⁄3) equals 1.\",\"- If the numerator is larger than the denominator, the fraction is improper; it can be written as a mixed number (e.g., 7⁄3 = 2 ½).\",\"- When fractions have the same denominator, the one with the larger numerator is larger.\",\"- When fractions have the same numerator, the one with the smaller denominator is larger.\",\"- To compare unlike fractions, convert them to equivalent fractions with a common denominator.\",\"- Adding like fractions: add numerators, keep the same denominator. Example: 2⁄5 + 3⁄5 = 5⁄5 = 1.\",\"- Subtracting like fractions: subtract numerators, keep the same denominator. Example: 4⁄5 – 1⁄5 = 3⁄5.\",\"- Adding or subtracting unlike fractions: first make a common denominator, then add or subtract numerators.\",\"- A mixed fraction has an integer part and a proper fraction part (e.g., 1 ⅔).\",\"- Fractions can represent parts of collections, distances, money, etc.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are fractions that have the same value called?\",\"options\":[\"Different fractions\",\"Equivalent fractions\",\"Equal numerators\",\"Common denominators\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that fractions of equal value are called equivalent fractions.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the fraction 2\\/4, what is the number 4 called?\",\"options\":[\"Numerator\",\"Equal part\",\"Denominator\",\"Equivalent\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the bottom number in a fraction as the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If one bhakari is divided into four equal parts and two parts are given away, what fraction of the bhakari is given?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/4\",\"2\\/4\",\"4\\/2\",\"3\\/4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if two of the four equal parts are given away, it is shown as 2\\/4.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following fractions is equivalent to 1\\/2 according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"2\\/3\",\"3\\/6\",\"2\\/5\",\"6\\/15\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states 1\\/2 = 2\\/4 = 3\\/6.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the value of a fraction when the numerator and denominator are multiplied by the same non-zero number?\",\"options\":[\"The value increases\",\"The value decreases\",\"The value remains the same\",\"The fraction becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Multiplying both by the same non-zero number results in an equivalent fraction, which has the same value.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To get an equivalent fraction by division, what must the numerator and denominator have?\",\"options\":[\"A common divisor\",\"The same value\",\"A zero\",\"Different values\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says if the numerator and denominator have a common divisor, the fraction obtained by dividing them is equivalent.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Find the equivalent fraction of 5\\/6 that has a denominator of 30.\",\"options\":[\"10\\/30\",\"15\\/30\",\"25\\/30\",\"20\\/30\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As 6 x 5 = 30, we multiply the numerator 5 by 5 to get 25\\/30.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If 15\\/40 is converted to an equivalent fraction with denominator 8, what is the numerator?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"15\",\"1\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"40 divided by 5 is 8, so 15 divided by 5 is 3.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which fraction did Minu obtain by dividing the numerator and denominator of 6\\/12 by 3?\",\"options\":[\"3\\/6\",\"1\\/2\",\"2\\/4\",\"6\\/4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says Minu obtained 2\\/4 by dividing 6 and 12 by 3.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 2\\/5 = 6\\/15, how many times the numerator 2 is the numerator 6?\",\"options\":[\"Two times\",\"Three times\",\"Five times\",\"Six times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sonu observes that three times 2 is 6.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the example of the two equal circles, why is 2\\/3 equal to 4\\/6?\",\"options\":[\"Because the circles are different sizes\",\"Because the coloured parts are equal\",\"Because the denominators are the same\",\"Because the numerators are the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that we see the coloured parts of the two circles are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the equivalent fraction of 6\\/12 when both numerator and denominator are divided by their common divisor 6?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2\",\"2\\/4\",\"3\\/6\",\"6\\/6\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"6 divided by 6 is 1, and 12 divided by 6 is 2, resulting in 1\\/2.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a melon is divided into 6 equal parts and shared equally by two people, what fraction does each person get?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/6\",\"2\\/6\",\"3\\/6\",\"6\\/6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if 6 parts are shared by 2 people, each gets 3\\/6.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these sets of fractions are all equivalent to 1\\/2 according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"6\\/12, 3\\/6, 2\\/4\",\"2\\/3, 4\\/6, 6\\/15\",\"5\\/6, 25\\/30, 3\\/8\",\"1\\/2, 2\\/5, 6\\/15\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states 6\\/12 = 3\\/6 = 2\\/4 = 1\\/2.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rule for equivalent fractions, if you multiply the numerator by 4, what must you do to the denominator to keep the fraction equivalent?\",\"options\":[\"Divide it by 4\",\"Multiply it by 4\",\"Add 4 to it\",\"Keep it the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the numerator and denominator must be multiplied by the same non-zero number.\",\"topic\":\"Equivalent fractions\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are fractions such as 1\\/7, 4\\/7, and 6\\/7, which have equal denominators, called?\",\"options\":[\"Unlike fractions\",\"Like fractions\",\"Equal fractions\",\"Multiple fractions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines fractions with equal denominators as \'like fractions\'.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Fractions such as 3\\/8, 1\\/4, and 9\\/11, which have different denominators, are known as:\",\"options\":[\"Like fractions\",\"Equivalent fractions\",\"Unlike fractions\",\"Common fractions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Fractions with different denominators are called \'unlike fractions\' in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the text\'s example of converting 5\\/6 and 7\\/9, which number is identified as a multiple of both 6 and 9?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"15\",\"18\",\"27\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 18 is a multiple of both 6 and 9.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these sets of numbers could also be chosen as common denominators for 5\\/6 and 7\\/9?\",\"options\":[\"12 and 24\",\"36 and 54\",\"9 and 6\",\"15 and 14\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions we could also choose numbers like 36 and 54 as common denominators.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When converting 3\\/8 and 5\\/16 into like fractions, why is 16 chosen as the common denominator?\",\"options\":[\"Because 16 is twice of 8\",\"Because 8 is larger than 16\",\"Because 3 and 5 are odd numbers\",\"Because 16 is a prime number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states it is easy to make 16 the common denominator because 16 is twice of 8.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the first step taken to subtract the two fractions?\",\"options\":[\"Subtracting the numerators immediately\",\"Making 8 the common denominator\",\"Multiplying 5\\/8 by 2\",\"Dividing 3\\/4 by 2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the step \'Let us make 8 the common denominator of the given fractions.\'\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/039_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, what is the equivalent fraction of 3\\/4 when the denominator is changed to 8?\",\"options\":[\"5\\/8\",\"1\\/8\",\"6\\/8\",\"3\\/8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows 3\\/4 = (3 x 2) \\/ (4 x 2) = 6\\/8.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/039_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"To convert 3\\/7 and 4\\/4 into like fractions with a denominator of 28, what calculation does the text show for the second fraction?\",\"options\":[\"3 × 7 \\/ 4 × 7 = 21\\/28\",\"4 × 4 \\/ 7 × 4 = 16\\/28\",\"3 × 4 \\/ 7 × 4 = 12\\/28\",\"7 × 3 \\/ 4 × 3 = 21\\/12\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly shows 3 × 7 \\/ 4 × 7 = 21\\/28 as one of the steps for Example (2).\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are 6\\/16 and 5\\/16 considered \'like fractions\'?\",\"options\":[\"They have the same numerator\",\"They are both equivalent to 3\\/8\",\"They have the same denominator\",\"They are both multiples of 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"By definition, like fractions are those whose denominators are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are converting 5\\/6 and 7\\/9, and you choose 18 as the common denominator, what does 5\\/6 become?\",\"options\":[\"10\\/18\",\"15\\/18\",\"14\\/18\",\"12\\/18\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows 5\\/6 = (5 × 3) \\/ (6 × 3) = 15\\/18.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following lists the multiples of 6 as provided in the topic text?\",\"options\":[\"6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36\",\"6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21\",\"6, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28\",\"1, 2, 3, 6\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists multiples of 6 as: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, .....\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the method for converting unlike fractions, what is the primary goal of finding a common multiple for the denominators?\",\"options\":[\"To make the numerators equal\",\"To make the denominators equal so they become like fractions\",\"To reduce the fractions to their simplest form\",\"To find the largest possible denominator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that finding a common multiple allows us to make the denominators the same, creating like fractions.\",\"topic\":\"Like and unlike fractions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In like fractions, which fraction is the greater fraction?\",\"options\":[\"The one with the smaller numerator\",\"The one with the greater numerator\",\"The one with the greater denominator\",\"The one with the smaller denominator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in like fractions, the fraction with the greater numerator is the greater fraction.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the value of fractions with the same numerator as the denominator increases?\",\"options\":[\"The value increases\",\"The value stays the same\",\"The value decreases\",\"The value becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the value of fractions with the same numerator decreases as the denominator increases.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To compare unlike fractions, what must we convert them into?\",\"options\":[\"Like fractions with different denominators\",\"Equivalent fractions with the same denominators\",\"Fractions with numerator 1\",\"Fractions with the same numerators\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that to compare unlike fractions, we convert them into equivalent fractions so that their denominators are the same.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is \'4\\/5 is greater than 3\\/5\' written in symbols?\",\"options\":[\"4\\/5 < 3\\/5\",\"4\\/5 = 3\\/5\",\"4\\/5 > 3\\/5\",\"3\\/5 > 4\\/5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly shows that \'4\\/5 is greater than 3\\/5\' is written as 4\\/5 > 3\\/5.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the boy-on-blocks analogy, when is it easy to tell who is taller?\",\"options\":[\"When the blocks are of different heights\",\"When the boys are sitting down\",\"When both blocks are of the same height\",\"When the boys stand on the floor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sonu says that if both blocks are of the same height, it is easy to tell who is taller.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a strip is divided into 5 equal parts, what does each individual part represent?\",\"options\":[\"5\\/1\",\"1\\/5\",\"5\\/5\",\"2\\/5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if a strip is divided into 5 equal parts, it means each part is 1\\/5.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can 35 be used as a common denominator for the fractions 3\\/5 and 4\\/7?\",\"options\":[\"Because 35 is the sum of 5 and 7\",\"Because 35 is a prime number\",\"Because 35 can be divided by both 5 and 7\",\"Because 35 is greater than both 5 and 7\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sonu explains that 5 x 7 can be divided by both 5 and 7, so 35 can be the common denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When fractions have the same denominators, what decides which fraction is bigger?\",\"options\":[\"The denominators\",\"The numerators\",\"The equivalent fraction\",\"The total number of parts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The teacher states that if fractions have the same denominators, their numerators decide which fraction is bigger.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Compare 2\\/3 and 2\\/5 based on the rule for equal numerators. Which is true?\",\"options\":[\"2\\/3 is smaller than 2\\/5\",\"2\\/3 is equal to 2\\/5\",\"2\\/3 is greater than 2\\/5\",\"2\\/5 is greater than 2\\/3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the numerators are the same, the fraction with the smaller denominator (3) is the greater fraction.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the equivalent fraction of 3\\/5 when the denominator is converted to 35?\",\"options\":[\"15\\/35\",\"21\\/35\",\"20\\/35\",\"30\\/35\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As shown in the text: 3\\/5 = (3 x 7) \\/ (5 x 7) = 21\\/35.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the equivalent fraction of 4\\/7 when the denominator is converted to 35?\",\"options\":[\"20\\/35\",\"21\\/35\",\"28\\/35\",\"12\\/35\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"As shown in the text: 4\\/7 = (4 x 5) \\/ (7 x 5) = 20\\/35.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Using the rule for like fractions, which of the following is correct?\",\"options\":[\"3\\/8 > 4\\/8\",\"3\\/8 < 4\\/8\",\"3\\/8 = 4\\/8\",\"4\\/8 < 3\\/8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 2 in the text explicitly states that 3\\/8 < 4\\/8.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are comparing 3\\/5 and 4\\/7, which conclusion is correct based on their equivalent fractions?\",\"options\":[\"3\\/5 < 4\\/7 because 21 < 20\",\"3\\/5 = 4\\/7 because they are equivalent\",\"3\\/5 > 4\\/7 because 21\\/35 > 20\\/35\",\"4\\/7 > 3\\/5 because 7 is greater than 5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows 21\\/35 > 20\\/35, therefore 3\\/5 > 4\\/7.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rule for equal numerators, which of these fractions is the smallest?\",\"options\":[\"2\\/3\",\"2\\/4\",\"2\\/5\",\"All are equal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The value decreases as the denominator increases when numerators are the same; 5 is the largest denominator here.\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A strip is divided into 8 equal parts. 3 parts are red and 4 parts are blue. Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[\"The red part is bigger than the blue part\",\"The blue part is bigger than the red part\",\"The red and blue parts are equal\",\"The white part is the biggest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"3\\/8 < 4\\/8, so the 4 parts (blue) represent a greater fraction than the 3 parts (red).\",\"topic\":\"Comparing fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When adding like fractions, what do we do with the numerators of the fractions?\",\"options\":[\"We add the numerators\",\"We subtract the numerators\",\"We multiply the numerators\",\"We keep the first numerator only\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that when adding like fractions, we add the numerators of the two fractions.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the denominator written when adding like fractions?\",\"options\":[\"The denominators are added together\",\"The denominator is written as it is\",\"The denominator is multiplied by two\",\"The denominator is changed to one\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the rule in the text, when adding like fractions, we write the denominator as it is.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the numerator and denominator of a fraction are equal, what is the value of the fraction?\",\"options\":[\"Zero\",\"The value of the numerator\",\"One\",\"The value of the denominator\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'If the numerator and denominator of a fraction are equal, the fraction is equal to one.\'\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the fraction 6\\/6 represent according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"6 parts are left over\",\"Only 1 part out of 6 is taken\",\"All 6 of the 6 equal parts are taken\",\"The figure is divided into 12 parts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that 6\\/6 means that all 6 of the 6 equal parts are taken, which is 1 whole figure.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a correct way to write the number 1 as a fraction?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/0\",\"1\\/2\",\"5\\/5\",\"5\\/1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that 7\\/7 = 1, 10\\/10 = 1, and 5\\/5 = 1, showing that any fraction with equal numerator and denominator equals 1.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How can a whole figure that is not divided into parts be written as a fraction?\",\"options\":[\"0\\/1\",\"1\\/1\",\"1\\/0\",\"0\\/0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if we do not divide a figure into parts but keep it whole, it can be written as 1, and 1 = 1\\/1.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of adding 3\\/8 + 1\\/8 + 2\\/8?\",\"options\":[\"6\\/24\",\"5\\/8\",\"6\\/8\",\"6\\/1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the example in the text, 3\\/8 + 1\\/8 + 2\\/8 = (3+1+2)\\/8 = 6\\/8.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the sum of 2\\/5 and 3\\/5?\",\"options\":[\"5\\/10\",\"1\",\"6\\/5\",\"2\\/5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows 2+3\\/5 = 5\\/5 = 1.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, into how many equal parts has the strip been divided?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"7\",\"2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and the figure show a strip divided into 7 equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/036_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the total fraction represented by the parts colored with both patterns?\",\"options\":[\"3\\/7\",\"2\\/7\",\"5\\/7\",\"7\\/7\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows 3 parts of one pattern and 2 parts of another, making the total colored part 5\\/7.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/036_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a fraction is obtained by dividing both the numerator and denominator by their common divisor, what is the relationship between the new fraction and the original one?\",\"options\":[\"The new fraction is greater\",\"The new fraction is smaller\",\"The new fraction is equivalent\",\"The new fraction is unrelated\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the fraction obtained by dividing by a common divisor is equivalent to the given fraction.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical statement correctly follows the logic provided in the text for the number 1?\",\"options\":[\"1 = 2\\/2 = 3\\/3 = 4\\/4\",\"1 = 1\\/2 = 2\\/3 = 3\\/4\",\"1 = 1\\/1 + 1\\/1\",\"1 = 0\\/1\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1 = 1\\/1 = 2\\/2 = 3\\/3 and so on.\",\"topic\":\"Addition of like fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"When subtracting a fraction from another like fraction, where do we write the difference between the numerators?\",\"options\":[\"In the denominator\",\"In the numerator\",\"As a whole number\",\"It is not written\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we write the difference between the numerators in the numerator.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What do we do with the common denominator when subtracting like fractions?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract the denominators\",\"Add the denominators\",\"Write the common denominator in the denominator\",\"Multiply the denominators\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the rule, we write the common denominator in the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example 7\\/13 - 5\\/13, what is the \'common denominator\'?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"5\",\"2\",\"13\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies 13 as the common denominator for these two fractions.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a figure is divided into 5 equal parts and 4 of them are coloured, what part of the figure is coloured?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/5\",\"5\\/4\",\"4\\/5\",\"3\\/5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if 4 out of 5 equal parts are coloured, 4\\/5 part is coloured.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When we remove the colour from one of the four coloured parts in a figure of 5 equal parts, what subtraction are we performing?\",\"options\":[\"4\\/5 - 1\\/5\",\"5\\/5 - 4\\/5\",\"4\\/5 - 3\\/5\",\"1\\/5 - 4\\/5\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Removing colour from one part means subtracting 1\\/5 from the original 4\\/5 coloured parts.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we subtract 3\\/12 from 5\\/12 in the example of Raju and Sanju?\",\"options\":[\"To find the total sugarcane they have\",\"To find the difference between their parts\",\"To find the common denominator\",\"To divide the sugarcane equally\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'To find out the difference, we must subtract.\'\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Subtract: 7\\/13 - 5\\/13.\",\"options\":[\"12\\/13\",\"2\\/0\",\"2\\/26\",\"2\\/13\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"7\\/13 - 5\\/13 = (7-5)\\/13 = 2\\/13.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of 4\\/5 - 1\\/5?\",\"options\":[\"3\\/5\",\"5\\/5\",\"3\\/0\",\"4\\/0\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"4\\/5 - 1\\/5 = (4-1)\\/5 = 3\\/5.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Raju got 5\\/12 part of a sugarcane and Sanju got 3\\/12 part, how much extra part did Raju get?\",\"options\":[\"8\\/12\",\"2\\/12\",\"2\\/24\",\"5\\/12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Raju got 5\\/12 - 3\\/12 = 2\\/12 extra.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A figure has 4\\/5 part coloured. If the remaining coloured part after a subtraction is 3\\/5, how much colour was removed?\",\"options\":[\"4\\/5\",\"3\\/5\",\"1\\/5\",\"2\\/5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 4\\/5 - 1\\/5 = 3\\/5, it means 1\\/5 part was removed.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the process of subtracting like fractions correctly?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract the first numerator from the second and keep the denominator\",\"Subtract the second numerator from the first and write the denominator as it is\",\"Subtract both numerators and both denominators\",\"Add the numerators and subtract the denominators\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says we subtract the second numerator from the first and write the denominator as it is.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the numerator of the answer when we subtract 5\\/13 from 7\\/13?\",\"options\":[\"13\",\"12\",\"2\",\"7\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The numerator is the difference: 7 - 5 = 2.\",\"topic\":\"Subtraction of like fractions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21741,30,5,'Maths','en','Angles','[\"Right angle is 90°\",\"Acute angle is less than 90°\",\"Obtuse angle is more than 90° and less than 180°\",\"Angle is formed by two intersecting lines\",\"Vertex is the common point of the two lines\",\"The arms of an angle are the two lines\",\"An angle is named with three letters, middle is vertex (e.g., ∠ABC)\",\"The symbol ∠ represents an angle\",\"Protractor is a semicircle with 0° at the centre and 180° at the edge\",\"Each degree on a protractor is 1°\",\"To measure an angle, centre of protractor on vertex, baseline on one arm, read degree on other arm\",\"Extending arms does not change the measure\",\"Parallel lines never intersect\",\"Perpendicular lines form a right angle (90°)\",\"A right angle can be made by folding a paper in half and then folding again\",\"To verify a right angle, use a protractor to check it is 90°\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the common point where two lines join to form an angle called?\",\"options\":[\"Arm\",\"Vertex\",\"Segment\",\"Ray\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the common point where two lines join is called the \'vertex\'.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In an angle formed by lines BA and BC, what are these lines called?\",\"options\":[\"Vertices\",\"Corners\",\"Arms\",\"Points\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the teacher, the lines that join to form an angle are called \'arms\'.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used to represent the word \'angle\'?\",\"options\":[\"∆\",\"∠\",\"∟\",\"∡\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the symbol \'∠\' represents the word \'angle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When naming an angle with three letters, which letter represents the vertex?\",\"options\":[\"The first letter\",\"The last letter\",\"The middle letter\",\"Any letter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher explains that the letter in the middle represents the vertex of the angle.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an angle is formed by lines BA and BC, which point is the common point?\",\"options\":[\"Point A\",\"Point B\",\"Point C\",\"Point ABC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the lines BA and BC, the letter B is common to both.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many letters are used to name an angle according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher states that an angle is named by three letters.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the arms of an angle are YX and YZ, what is one correct way to write the name of the angle using the symbol?\",\"options\":[\"∠XYZ\",\"∠YXZ\",\"∠YZX\",\"∠Y\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since Y is the common point (vertex), it must be in the middle: ∠XYZ.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an angle is named ∠LMN, which letter represents the vertex?\",\"options\":[\"L\",\"M\",\"N\",\"LMN\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the name ∠LMN, the middle letter M represents the vertex.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If ∠PQR is one correct name for an angle, which other name is also correct for the same angle?\",\"options\":[\"∠PRQ\",\"∠QRP\",\"∠RQP\",\"∠RPQ\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says an angle can be named in two ways (e.g., ABC or CBA), keeping the vertex in the middle.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the components of an angle based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Two vertices and one arm\",\"One vertex and two arms\",\"Three vertices and two arms\",\"One vertex and one arm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes one common point (vertex) and two lines (arms) joining together.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'angle BAC\' an incorrect name for an angle formed by lines BA and BC?\",\"options\":[\"Because B is the vertex and must be in the middle\",\"Because A is the vertex and must be in the middle\",\"Because C is the vertex and must be in the middle\",\"Because the letters must be in alphabetical order\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The vertex (common point B) must be the middle letter when naming the angle.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these angle types is NOT mentioned in the introductory text?\",\"options\":[\"Right angle\",\"Acute angle\",\"Obtuse angle\",\"Straight angle\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text only mentions right, acute, and obtuse angles.\",\"topic\":\"Components of angles, naming an angle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the geometric shape of a protractor?\",\"options\":[\"Circular\",\"Rectangular\",\"Semi-circular\",\"Triangular\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a protractor is semi-circular in shape.\",\"topic\":\"The protractor\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Into how many equal parts is the semi-circular edge of a protractor divided?\",\"options\":[\"90\",\"180\",\"360\",\"100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the semi-circular edge is divided into 180 equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"The protractor\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the unit \'one degree\' written in mathematical symbols?\",\"options\":[\"1\'\",\"1°\",\"1\\\"\",\"1^\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, \'One degree\' is written as \'1°\'.\",\"topic\":\"The protractor\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the diameter of the circle, of which the protractor is a half part, called?\",\"options\":[\"The center line\",\"The radius\",\"The baseline or line of reference\",\"The degree line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the diameter of that circle as the baseline or line of reference.\",\"topic\":\"The protractor\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which two directions are the divisions on a protractor marked?\",\"options\":[\"Upwards and Downwards\",\"Clockwise and Anticlockwise\",\"Only from left to right\",\"Only from right to left\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states divisions are marked anticlockwise (right to left) and clockwise (left to right).\",\"topic\":\"The protractor\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the \'centre of the protractor\'?\",\"options\":[\"The midpoint of the semi-circular edge\",\"The point marked 90 degrees\",\"The centre of the circle of which the protractor is a half part\",\"The end point of the baseline\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the centre of the protractor as the centre of the circle of which it is a half part.\",\"topic\":\"The protractor\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are reading the markings on a protractor from right to left, which direction are you using?\",\"options\":[\"Clockwise\",\"Anticlockwise\",\"Vertical\",\"Circular\"],\"a