\"It will be 50°\",\"It will be 40°\",\"It will be 0°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text demonstrates that positioning the protractor differently on either arm results in the same measure of 40°.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring angles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which angle has a measure of 55°?\",\"options\":[\"∠PQR\",\"∠LMN\",\"∠XYZ\",\"∠RST\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'Measure of ∠RST is: 55°\'.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring angles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example of drawing ∠ABC, which point is identified as the vertex?\",\"options\":[\"Point A\",\"Point B\",\"Point C\",\"Point D\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'B is the vertex of ∠ABC\'.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is mentioned to be used first to draw the arm BC?\",\"options\":[\"Protractor\",\"Compass\",\"Ruler\",\"Divider\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'First draw arm BC with a ruler.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the names of the two arms of ∠ABC as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"AB and BC\",\"BA and BC\",\"AC and BC\",\"AB and AC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions \'BA and BC are its arms\'.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where exactly should the centre of the protractor be placed to draw an angle at vertex B?\",\"options\":[\"On point A\",\"On point C\",\"On point B\",\"At the 70° mark\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructions state \'Put the centre of the protractor on B.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When placing the protractor, where should the baseline be positioned?\",\"options\":[\"On the vertex B\",\"On the arm BC\",\"On the point marking 70°\",\"Above the line BC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Place the protractor so that the baseline lies on arm BC.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, on which side of the vertex Q does the arm QR lie?\",\"options\":[\"Left side\",\"Right side\",\"Top side\",\"Bottom side\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows arm QR extending to the right of vertex Q, and the text confirms \'The arm of the angle is on the right of Q\'.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/048_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the teacher, why was Rahul\'s angle incorrect?\",\"options\":[\"He used the wrong vertex\",\"He did not use a ruler\",\"He counted from the left where there was no arm\",\"He marked 70 degrees instead of 80\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher explains that Rahul measured from the left where there was nothing under the baseline.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are drawing an angle and the arm lies to the right of the vertex, from which division should you start counting?\",\"options\":[\"The 0 near the right-side point\",\"The 180 near the right-side point\",\"The 0 on the left side\",\"The 90 degree mark\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states \'Count the divisions starting from the 0 near point C\' (where the arm lies).\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"After marking the point for the required measure and lifting the protractor, what is the next step to complete the angle?\",\"options\":[\"Draw a baseline\",\"Mark the vertex\",\"Draw a line from the vertex through the marked point\",\"Count the divisions again\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Draw a line from vertex B through the point marking the 70° angle.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which scale is being used to mark the angle from the left side, resulting in an incorrect measurement?\",\"options\":[\"The scale starting with 0 on the right\",\"The scale starting with 0 on the left\",\"The scale starting with 90 in the middle\",\"The scale starting with 180 on the right\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the line passing through 80 on the outer scale which starts from 0 on the left.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/048_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the arm of an angle is placed on the left side of the vertex, what would be the correct way to measure it?\",\"options\":[\"Count from the 0 on the right\",\"Count from the 0 on the left\",\"Always count from the right\",\"Count from the 90 degree mark\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the teacher\'s logic, we must count from the side on which the arm lies.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can we conclude about the importance of the \'0\' mark on the protractor when drawing an angle?\",\"options\":[\"It doesn\'t matter which 0 you use\",\"You must always use the 0 on the left\",\"You must use the 0 that lies on the arm of the angle\",\"You must always use the 0 on the right\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher points out that Rahul failed because he counted from a side where there was no arm.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing an angle of the given measure\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the measure of a right angle according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"90°\",\"120°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that angles of measure 90° are called right angles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"An angle which measures less than a right angle is called an ______.\",\"options\":[\"Obtuse angle\",\"Right angle\",\"Acute angle\",\"Large angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an acute angle as an angle which measures less than a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If ∠LMN is an obtuse angle, what is true about its measure?\",\"options\":[\"It measures exactly 90°\",\"It measures less than 90°\",\"It measures more than 90°\",\"It measures 0°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that an obtuse angle measures more than 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these describes an acute angle?\",\"options\":[\"More than a right angle\",\"Less than a right angle\",\"Equal to a right angle\",\"Exactly 90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an acute angle as being less than a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student draws an angle that measures 60°, what type of angle is it?\",\"options\":[\"Right angle\",\"Obtuse angle\",\"Acute angle\",\"90° angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 60° is less than 90°, it is an acute angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"An angle measures 110°. Based on the definitions, what category does it fall into?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angle\",\"Right angle\",\"Obtuse angle\",\"Less than a right angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 110° is more than 90°, it is an obtuse angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare a right angle and an obtuse angle. Which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"A right angle is more than an obtuse angle\",\"An obtuse angle is more than a right angle\",\"They both measure 90°\",\"An obtuse angle is less than 90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an obtuse angle as measuring more than 90° (a right angle).\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If we have ∠ABC, ∠RST, and ∠LMN as described in the text, which one is a right angle?\",\"options\":[\"∠RST\",\"∠LMN\",\"∠ABC\",\"None of these\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that ∠ABC is a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the classification of an angle if its measure increases from 90° to 100°?\",\"options\":[\"It stays a right angle\",\"It becomes an acute angle\",\"It becomes an obtuse angle\",\"It becomes less than a right angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Increasing beyond 90° makes it \'more than a right angle\', which is an obtuse angle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which relationship is correct according to the textbook definitions?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angle < Right angle < Obtuse angle\",\"Obtuse angle < Right angle < Acute angle\",\"Right angle < Acute angle < Obtuse angle\",\"Acute angle = Right angle = Obtuse angle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Acute is less than 90°, Right is 90°, and Obtuse is more than 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Types of angles\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the measure of the angle formed by folding a sheet of paper roughly in half and then making another fold at any point?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the angle between those two lines will be a right angle, which measures 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is mentioned in the text to verify the measure of a right angle?\",\"options\":[\"Ruler\",\"Compass\",\"Protractor\",\"Divider\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to verify the measure of the angle with the help of a protractor.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which parts of a ladder are parallel to each other?\",\"options\":[\"The horizontal steps\",\"The vertical legs\",\"The top edge\",\"The bottom edge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the vertical legs of the ladder are parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to parallel lines when they are extended on either side?\",\"options\":[\"They eventually meet\",\"They cut each other\",\"They do not intersect\",\"They become curved\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that parallel lines do not intersect, no matter how far they are extended on either side.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the result of tracing the lines made by folding the rectangular paper edge-to-edge?\",\"options\":[\"The lines are perpendicular to each other\",\"The lines are parallel to each other\",\"The lines will intersect at the center\",\"The lines form a right angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text explain that lines traced from these folds are parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/049_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is true about the two lines shown at the bottom left?\",\"options\":[\"They are not parallel because they are different lengths\",\"They are parallel even though they are not of equal length\",\"They will intersect if extended to the right\",\"They are not straight lines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the figure states that the lines shown are not of equal length, yet they are parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/049_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you draw lines along both sides of a ruler and then remove the ruler, what is the relationship between the two lines?\",\"options\":[\"They are perpendicular\",\"They are parallel\",\"They are intersecting\",\"They are of different shapes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the two lines drawn along both sides of a ruler are parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects can be used to draw parallel lines by tracing along its opposite sides?\",\"options\":[\"A circular plate\",\"A triangular set-square\",\"A rectangular object\",\"A sharpened pencil tip\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions we can use several rectangular objects to draw parallel lines.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'do not intersect\' mean in the context of parallel lines?\",\"options\":[\"They are not the same length\",\"They do not cut each other\",\"They are not straight\",\"They are not vertical\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'do not intersect\' as \'they do not cut each other\'.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a requirement for two lines to be parallel?\",\"options\":[\"They must be of equal length\",\"They must be vertical\",\"They must not cut each other when extended\",\"They must be drawn with a protractor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text emphasizes that parallel lines do not intersect (cut each other) regardless of length or extension.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property is NOT necessary for lines to be parallel according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Being straight\",\"Being of equal length\",\"Not intersecting\",\"Maintaining the same direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that lines do not have to be of equal length to be parallel.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"When folding paper to create a right angle as described in the first paragraph, what is the relationship between the two resulting lines?\",\"options\":[\"They are parallel\",\"They are intersecting\",\"They never meet\",\"They are the same line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the angle between the two lines is a right angle (90°), which means they meet or intersect at that angle.\",\"topic\":\"Parallel lines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the measure of the angle formed by a pole standing straight and its shadow on the ground?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the angle formed by a pole and its shadow on the ground is 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When two lines form an angle of 90° with each other, they are said to be ______ to each other.\",\"options\":[\"parallel\",\"perpendicular\",\"opposite\",\"diagonal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the definition in the text, lines forming a 90° angle are perpendicular.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of angle is formed by the adjacent sides of wooden planks?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angle\",\"Obtuse angle\",\"Right angle\",\"Straight angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that adjacent sides of wooden planks form angles of 90°, which is a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is drawn between two lines to show that they are perpendicular?\",\"options\":[\"A circle\",\"A symbol\",\"A dot\",\"A cross\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that to show two lines are perpendicular, a symbol is drawn between them.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you measure the angle between two adjacent sides of your notebook, what will the measurement be?\",\"options\":[\"A right angle\",\"An acute angle\",\"An obtuse angle\",\"A zero angle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says that since the two sides of a notebook are perpendicular, the angle between them is a right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the relationship between the horizontal lines on a sheet of paper?\",\"options\":[\"They are perpendicular to each other\",\"They are parallel to each other\",\"They form a 90° angle\",\"They are adjacent to each other\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the horizontal lines on the paper are parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the vertical margin line on a paper relate to the horizontal lines?\",\"options\":[\"It is parallel to them\",\"It is perpendicular to them\",\"It never touches them\",\"It forms an acute angle with them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the vertical margin line forms a right angle with horizontal lines, making it perpendicular.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of perpendicular lines mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Two horizontal lines on a paper\",\"The pole and the sky\",\"Adjacent sides of a book\",\"The shadow and the sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies adjacent sides of books as forming 90° angles, making them perpendicular.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student sees a vertical pole and its shadow. If the pole is standing straight, what can the student conclude about the lines formed?\",\"options\":[\"The lines are parallel\",\"The lines are perpendicular\",\"The lines form an acute angle\",\"The lines do not form an angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the pole and shadow form a 90° angle, they are perpendicular to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are told that two lines are perpendicular, what is the exact degree of the angle between them?\",\"options\":[\"0°\",\"45°\",\"90°\",\"100°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definition of perpendicular lines in the text is that they form an angle of 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly distinguishes between the lines on a notebook page?\",\"options\":[\"All lines on the page are perpendicular\",\"All lines on the page are parallel\",\"Horizontal lines are parallel, but the margin is perpendicular to them\",\"Horizontal lines are perpendicular, but the margin is parallel to them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states horizontal lines are parallel, while the vertical margin is perpendicular to those horizontal lines.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the adjacent sides of a wooden plank considered perpendicular?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are both straight\",\"Because they form a right angle of 90°\",\"Because they are parallel to the ground\",\"Because they are horizontal lines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Perpendicularity is defined by the formation of a 90° (right) angle between two lines.\",\"topic\":\"Perpendicular lines\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21742,30,5,'Maths','en','Circles','[\"A circle is a set of all points equidistant from a centre.\",\"The distance from the centre to any point on the circle is called a radius.\",\"All radii of a circle are equal in length.\",\"A straight line joining two points on the circle is called a chord.\",\"If a chord passes through the centre, it is called a diameter.\",\"The diameter is the longest chord and is twice the length of a radius.\",\"The centre of a circle is the point from which all radii are drawn.\",\"The interior of a circle is the area inside the boundary; the exterior is outside.\",\"Points on the boundary are called points on the circle.\",\"An arc is a part of the circle’s circumference between two points.\",\"An arc is named by its two end points and a third point on the arc, e.g. arc PSQ.\",\"The circumference is the total length around the circle.\",\"To draw a circle, use a compass: fix the centre, set the radius, rotate the pencil.\",\"When a circle is cut into two parts, each part is an arc.\",\"All diameters of a circle are equal in length.\",\"If the radius is 5 cm, the diameter is 10 cm.\",\"If the diameter is 6 cm, the radius is 3 cm.\",\"The interior and exterior of a circle can be identified by marking points inside, outside, or on the circle.\",\"A chord, radius, and diameter can be identified by their endpoints and the centre.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the line joining the centre of the circle to any point on the circle called?\",\"options\":[\"Chord\",\"Diameter\",\"Radius\",\"Centre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the line joining the centre to any point on the circle is called a radius.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A line joining any two points on a circle is called a:\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Chord\",\"Centre\",\"Point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a chord as a line joining any two points on a circle.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a chord that passes through the centre of the circle called?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Small chord\",\"Centre line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a chord passing through the centre of the circle is called a diameter.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true about the radii of a circle?\",\"options\":[\"They are all of different lengths\",\"A circle has only one radius\",\"They are all of the same length\",\"Radii do not touch the centre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that all radii of a circle are of the same length.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If P is the centre of a circle and M is a point on the circle, what is the line PM?\",\"options\":[\"A chord\",\"A diameter\",\"A radius\",\"A centre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As per the example in the text, the line joining centre P to point M on the circle is a radius.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many chords and diameters can a circle have?\",\"options\":[\"Only one of each\",\"Exactly two\",\"Many\",\"None\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a circle has many chords and diameters.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If chord AB passes through the centre P, which of these is also a correct name for AB?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Centre\",\"Point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says if chord AB passes through the centre, it is also a diameter.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If M and N are two points on a circle, what is the line MN called?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Centre\",\"Chord\",\"Diameter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the example of points M and N to define a chord.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definitions provided, which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Every chord is a diameter\",\"Every diameter is also a chord\",\"A radius is a type of chord\",\"A diameter does not join two points\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a diameter as a chord passing through the centre, meaning all diameters are chords.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the diagram mentioned, if O is the centre, what would a line from O to any point on the circle be?\",\"options\":[\"A diameter\",\"A chord\",\"A radius\",\"A centre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A line from the centre (O) to any point on the circle fits the definition of a radius.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between a simple chord and a diameter?\",\"options\":[\"A chord joins two points, but a diameter does not\",\"A diameter must pass through the centre, but a chord may not\",\"A chord is always longer than a diameter\",\"A diameter is not a line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that a diameter is a special chord that passes through the centre.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is line PM called a radius in the text\'s diagram?\",\"options\":[\"Because it joins two points on the circle\",\"Because it passes through the centre\",\"Because it joins the centre P to a point M on the circle\",\"Because it is the longest line in the circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines radius as the line joining the centre to any point on the circle, like PM.\",\"topic\":\"Radius, chord and diameter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is used to draw a circle according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Ruler\",\"Compass\",\"Protractor\",\"Divider\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'We use a compass to draw a circle.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is found on one arm of the compass?\",\"options\":[\"A metal point\",\"An eraser\",\"A ruler scale\",\"A permanent ink pen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'One arm of the compass has a metal point.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the point at which the metal tip of the compass is held called?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Centre\",\"Circumference\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the centre as the point at which the metal tip of the compass is held.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What shape is created by turning the pencil point around the fixed metal tip?\",\"options\":[\"A square\",\"A triangle\",\"A circle\",\"A rectangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the shape created by the pencil point will be a circle.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Before drawing, what should be done with the pencil point and the metal tip?\",\"options\":[\"They should be kept far apart\",\"They should be aligned\",\"The pencil should be shorter than the metal tip\",\"The metal tip should be removed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Align the metal tip of the compass with the pencil point.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the distance between the pencil point and the metal tip represent?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the compass\",\"The thickness of the paper\",\"The radius of the circle\",\"The centre of the circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that to draw a circle of a given radius, a distance equal to the radius is kept between the tips.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the distance between the pencil point and the metal tip is 3 cm, what is the radius of the circle?\",\"options\":[\"6 cm\",\"1.5 cm\",\"3 cm\",\"9 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides an example where a distance of 3 cm results in a radius of 3 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To draw a circle with a radius of 5 cm, what distance must be kept between the pencil point and the metal tip?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"Any distance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the distance between the tips must be equal to the radius.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the diagram mentioned in the text, if \'C\' is the centre, what was placed exactly at point C?\",\"options\":[\"The pencil point\",\"The ruler\",\"The metal tip\",\"The paper edge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the point at which the metal tip is held is the centre.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the pencil point while drawing the circle?\",\"options\":[\"To stay steady on one point\",\"To turn around the metal tip\",\"To measure the distance from the edge of the paper\",\"To hold the metal tip in place\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'turn the pencil point around\' the metal tip to create the shape.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to hold the metal tip \'steady\' on the chosen point?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the centre from moving\",\"To make the pencil darker\",\"To sharpen the metal tip\",\"To measure the 3 cm distance\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"If the metal tip (the centre) is not held steady, the shape will not be a perfect circle around a single centre.\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a necessary condition for drawing a circle of a specific \'given radius\'?\",\"options\":[\"Using a very long pencil\",\"Keeping a specific distance between the metal tip and pencil point\",\"Moving the metal tip while turning the pencil\",\"Using a piece of paper that is exactly 3 cm wide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'To draw a circle of a given radius, a distance equal to the radius is kept between the pencil point and the metal tip.\'\",\"topic\":\"Drawing a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"The diameter of a circle is ___ the length of its radius.\",\"options\":[\"half\",\"twice\",\"three times\",\"the same as\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the diameter of a circle is twice the length of its radius.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is true about all the diameters of a circle?\",\"options\":[\"They are of different lengths\",\"They are half the length of the radius\",\"They are all of the same length\",\"Only two diameters can have the same length\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that all the diameters of a circle are of the same length.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the length of one radius of a circle is 3 cm, what is the length of its diameter?\",\"options\":[\"1.5 cm\",\"3 cm\",\"6 cm\",\"9 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the diameter is twice the radius, 3 cm × 2 = 6 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Every radius of a circle is ___ the length of its diameter.\",\"options\":[\"twice\",\"half\",\"double\",\"equal to\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook concludes from the folding activity that every radius is half the length of its diameter.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a circle has a diameter of 10 cm, what is the length of its radius?\",\"options\":[\"5 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"2 cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The radius is half the length of the diameter, so half of 10 cm is 5 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a circle, diameter AB is 8 cm long. If another diameter CD is drawn on the same circle, how long will it be?\",\"options\":[\"4 cm\",\"16 cm\",\"8 cm\",\"12 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"All diameters of the same circle are of the same length.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, when the paper is folded at P so that point B falls on point A, what happens to radius PB?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes longer than PA\",\"It falls exactly on radius PA\",\"It stays away from radius PA\",\"It becomes a diameter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that when folded, radius PB falls exactly on radius PA, meaning they coincide.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/053_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the radius of the first circle is measured as 1 cm, what is the recorded length of its diameter?\",\"options\":[\"1 cm\",\"2 cm\",\"3 cm\",\"4 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure explicitly shows that for a radius of 1 cm, the diameter is 2 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"30\\/053_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In a circle with center P and diameter AB, which of these are the radii?\",\"options\":[\"Only AB\",\"Only point P\",\"Segments PA and PB\",\"Segments AP and AB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that PA and PB are radii of the circle when AB is the diameter and P is the center.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is mentioned in the text to measure and verify the relationship between diameters and radii?\",\"options\":[\"Compass\",\"Divider\",\"Ruler\",\"Protractor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Test 1 in the text instructs to measure the diameters and radii with a ruler.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you draw a circle and find that one radius is 4 cm, what can you conclude about any other radius you draw in that same circle?\",\"options\":[\"It must be 8 cm\",\"It must be 4 cm\",\"It will be 2 cm\",\"It can be any length\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since all radii are half of the same diameter, all radii in a circle are equal in length.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student draws a circle with a radius of 5 cm. What is the total length of the diameter?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"15 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Diameter is twice the radius, so 5 cm × 2 = 10 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Relationship between radius and diameter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the game \'Land and Sea\', where are the children who are in the \'sea\' located?\",\"options\":[\"Outside the circle\",\"Inside the circle\",\"On the boundary of the circle\",\"Far away from the playground\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that children inside the circle are in the \'sea\'.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these points is located in the exterior of the circle?\",\"options\":[\"Point P\",\"Point T\",\"Point C\",\"Point R\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists A, B, C and D as points in the exterior of the circle.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the length of a string that has been wound once around a circular bowl and then straightened out called?\",\"options\":[\"The radius\",\"The diameter\",\"The interior\",\"The circumference\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines the length of the straightened part as the circumference of the circle or bowl.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If children are playing on \'land\', where are they standing in relation to the circle?\",\"options\":[\"Inside the circle\",\"Outside the circle\",\"At the centre T\",\"In the sea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that children outside the circle are on \'land\'.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is used to measure the straightened string to find the circumference?\",\"options\":[\"A compass\",\"A divider\",\"A ruler\",\"A set-square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Measure the straightened part with a ruler\'.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example given in the text, what was the measured circumference of the bowl?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm 5 mm\",\"12 cm 3 mm\",\"15 cm\",\"5 cm 2 mm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies the circumference of the circle is 12 cm 3 mm.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which group of points are all located in the interior of the circle?\",\"options\":[\"A, B, C, D\",\"P, Q, R, T\",\"P, A, Q, B\",\"T, A, B, C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states P, Q, R and T are points in the interior of the circle.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To measure the circumference of a circular object using a string, how many times should you wind the string around it?\",\"options\":[\"Once\",\"Twice\",\"Three times\",\"Halfway\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Wind a string once around the bowl\'.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what must you do to the string after winding it around the bowl to measure the circumference?\",\"options\":[\"Cut it into pieces\",\"Tie it in a knot\",\"Unwind and straighten it out\",\"Keep it wound on the bowl\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Unwind this circle and straighten it out\' before measuring.\",\"topic\":\"The circumference of a circle\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is each part of a broken plastic bangle called?\",\"options\":[\"A full circle\",\"A straight line\",\"An arc of a circle\",\"A center point\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that each part of a broken bangle is an arc of a circle.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When two points P and Q are placed on a circle, into how many parts do they divide the circle?\",\"options\":[\"One part\",\"Two parts\",\"Three parts\",\"Four parts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that two points P and Q divide the circle into two parts.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are points P and Q called in relation to the arcs they create?\",\"options\":[\"Middle points\",\"Center points\",\"End points\",\"Break points\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that P and Q are the end points of both arcs.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a three-letter name like \'arc PSQ\' used instead of just \'arc PQ\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because circles must have three points\",\"To show the length of the arc\",\"To know which of the two arcs we are speaking of\",\"Because the bangle broke into three pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that from the name \'arc PQ\', we cannot say which of the two arcs we are speaking of, so a third point is added.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a circle is divided by points A and B, and point C lies on one of the parts, what is the correct three-letter name for that arc?\",\"options\":[\"arc ABC\",\"arc ACB\",\"arc CAB\",\"arc CBA\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following the example of \'arc PSQ\' where P and Q are end points and S is in the middle, the name would be arc ACB.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the name \'arc PRQ\', which point is the \'additional point\' taken on the arc?\",\"options\":[\"Point P\",\"Point R\",\"Point Q\",\"Point A\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a three-letter arc name, the middle letter represents the additional point taken on the arc.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a bangle breaks at points X and Y, how many arcs are formed?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says if a bangle breaks at two points, it splits into two parts, each being an arc.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the purpose of points S and R in the names \'arc PSQ\' and \'arc PRQ\'?\",\"options\":[\"They are the points where the bangle broke\",\"They are the centers of the circle\",\"They help distinguish between the two different arcs created by P and Q\",\"They are the end points of the arcs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Additional points are used to give each arc a unique three-letter name to avoid confusion.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a three-letter name of an arc mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"arc AB\",\"arc PQ\",\"arc PSQ\",\"arc PQS\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically names \'arc PSQ\' and \'arc PRQ\' as examples.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we only use the end points to name an arc (e.g., arc PQ), why is this information incomplete?\",\"options\":[\"It does not specify the circle\'s size\",\"It could refer to either of the two arcs formed by those points\",\"It does not include the radius\",\"It only describes a straight line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that from a two-letter name, we cannot say which of the two arcs we are speaking of.\",\"topic\":\"An arc of a circle\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21743,30,5,'Maths','en','Multiples and Factors','[\"- A *factor* (or divisor) of a number is a number that divides it exactly with no remainder.\",\"- A *multiple* of a number is the product of that number and another whole number.\",\"- If a number can be divided by another without a remainder, the dividend is a multiple of the divisor.\",\"- 12 has the factors 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12; 16 has 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16.\",\"- Numbers that have only two factors (1 and the number itself) are called *prime* numbers.\",\"- Numbers that have more than two factors are called *composite* numbers.\",\"- The number 1 is neither prime nor composite.\",\"- A pair of numbers that share no common factor other than 1 are called *co‑prime* (or *relatively prime*).\",\"- To test divisibility by 2: last digit 0,2,4,6,8 → yes.\",\"- To test divisibility by 5: last digit 0 or 5 → yes.\",\"- To test divisibility by 10: last digit 0 → yes.\",\"- A *twin prime* pair differs by 2 (e.g., 3 & 5, 5 & 7).\",\"- The Sieve of Eratosthenes is a method to find all primes up to a given limit by striking out multiples of each prime.\",\"- Multiples of 4 are always multiples of 2, but not all multiples of 2 are multiples of 4.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to Sanju, what does it mean when \'none are left over\' after putting items into groups?\",\"options\":[\"The remainder will be 1\",\"The remainder will be 0\",\"The division is impossible\",\"The group size is 12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sanju states that \'None left over means that the remainder will be 0\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for numbers that divide a given number exactly?\",\"options\":[\"Remainders\",\"Groups\",\"Factors\",\"Multiples\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dada explains that numbers which divide a number exactly are called divisors or factors.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Anju say we can make groups of two with 12 pedhas?\",\"options\":[\"Because 12 is divisible by 2\",\"Because 2 is a large number\",\"Because 12 minus 2 is 10\",\"Because there are two people\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Anju says, \'12 is divisible by 2, so we can make groups of two.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we NOT make groups of five with 12 pedhas?\",\"options\":[\"Because 5 is a prime number\",\"Because 12 is not divisible by 5\",\"Because 5 is more than 2\",\"Because Dada does not like the number 5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Manju explains that groups of five cannot be made because 12 is not divisible by 5.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is a factor of 16?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 as factors of 16.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Dada gives 12 people 1 pedha each, how many pedhas will be left over?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"12\",\"0\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sanju mentions giving 12 people 1 pedha each as a way to have none left over (remainder 0).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the complete list of factors of 12 mentioned by Dada?\",\"options\":[\"1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\",\"2, 3, 4, 6, 12\",\"1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\",\"1, 2, 4, 8, 16\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dada states that 12 is exactly divisible by 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which number is mentioned as a factor of both 12 and 16 in the text?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists 4 as a factor for both 12 and 16.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the remainder when a number is divided by one of its factors?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"The number itself\",\"0\",\"It depends on the group\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines factors as numbers that divide a number exactly, meaning the remainder is 0.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these numbers is NOT a factor of 12?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"6\",\"9\",\"12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Manju states we cannot make groups of 7, 8, 9, 10 or 11 because 12 is not divisible by them.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Sagar says we can make groups of four. What does this imply about the number 4?\",\"options\":[\"4 is a factor of 12\",\"4 is the remainder\",\"4 is not divisible by 12\",\"4 is the total number of pedhas\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since 12 pedhas can be put into groups of four without remainders, 4 is a factor of 12.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Dada wants to put all 12 pedhas into a single equal group, how many pedhas will be in that group?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"6\",\"12\",\"0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sanju suggests making \'one group of twelve\' as a way to leave no pedhas over.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Factors\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"When the division of a dividend leaves no remainder, what is the dividend called in relation to the divisor?\",\"options\":[\"A factor\",\"A multiple\",\"A quotient\",\"A remainder\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when division leaves no remainder, the dividend is said to be a multiple of the divisor.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example 20 ÷ 5 = 4 with remainder 0, which number is the multiple of 5?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"0\",\"20\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says \'20 is a multiple of 5\' because it is divided by 5 with no remainder.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a dividend is a multiple of a divisor, it is the product of the divisor and the ______.\",\"options\":[\"Remainder\",\"Dividend\",\"Sum\",\"Quotient\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Dada explains that in such a case, the dividend is the product of the divisor and the quotient.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Suraj divided 84 chalksticks into groups of 6. How many exact groups did he make?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"14\",\"84\",\"20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that 84 is exactly divided by 6 and the quotient is 14, meaning 14 groups of 6 can be made.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If 84 is a multiple of 6, then what is 6 in relation to 84?\",\"options\":[\"A multiple\",\"A dividend\",\"A factor\",\"A product\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Suraj mentions that if 84 is the multiple of 6, then 6 is a factor of 84.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these numbers is NOT mentioned in the text as a multiple of 6?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"18\",\"21\",\"36\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists 6, 12, 18, 36, and 84 as multiples of 6, but notes that 21 is not a multiple of 5 (and by extension not listed for 6).\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How can you check if a number of chalksticks is a multiple of 6?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract 6 from the number\",\"Multiply the number by 6\",\"Divide the number by 6 and check if the remainder is 0\",\"Add 6 to the number\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dada says to see if a number is a multiple of 6, divide it by 6; if the remainder is 0, it is a multiple.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, every number in the 7 times table is a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Factor of 7\",\"Multiple of 7\",\"Remainder of 7\",\"Divisor of 7\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'the numbers in the 7 times table are multiples of 7\'.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a number is exactly divisible by 3, it is also called a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Multiple of 3\",\"Factor of 3\",\"Quotient of 3\",\"Remainder of 3\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says each number in the 3 times table is exactly divisible by 3 or is a multiple of 3.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is 21 not considered a multiple of 5 in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because 21 is smaller than 5\",\"Because 21 is in the 5 times table\",\"Because dividing 21 by 5 leaves a remainder\",\"Because 21 is a factor of 5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies 21 is not a multiple because a multiple requires the division to leave no remainder.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Anju correctly guesses that the word \'multiple\' is related to which operation?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Anju says, \'I think it must be related to multiplication,\' and Dada confirms she is right.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we have 36 chalksticks and we can make exact groups of 6 with none left over, what does this tell us about 36?\",\"options\":[\"36 is a factor of 6\",\"36 is a multiple of 6\",\"36 is a divisor of 6\",\"36 is not divisible by 6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if we can make exact groups with none left over, those numbers are multiples of the group size.\",\"topic\":\"Multiples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many millilitres (ml) make 1 litre?\",\"options\":[\"100 ml\",\"500 ml\",\"1000 ml\",\"1500 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1 litre is 1000 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many millilitres are there in one and a half litres?\",\"options\":[\"1000 ml\",\"1200 ml\",\"1500 ml\",\"2000 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that one and a half litres is 1500 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 1000 = 200 × 5, what is the relationship between 1000 and 200?\",\"options\":[\"1000 is a factor of 200\",\"1000 is a multiple of 200\",\"200 is a multiple of 1000\",\"1000 is not divisible by 200\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that 1000 = 200 × 5 means 1000 is a multiple of 200.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a 200 ml measure be used to measure out 1 litre of milk?\",\"options\":[\"Because 1000 is a multiple of 200\",\"Because 200 is larger than 1000\",\"Because 1 litre is exactly 200 ml\",\"Because 200 is not divisible by 1000\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Suraj explains that since 1000 is a multiple of 200, we can measure 1 litre with a 200 ml measure.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many measures of 200 ml are required to make exactly 1 litre?\",\"options\":[\"2 measures\",\"4 measures\",\"5 measures\",\"10 measures\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 5 measures of 200 ml make 1 litre.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can the 200 ml measure NOT be used to measure out 1500 ml of milk?\",\"options\":[\"Because 1500 is a multiple of 200\",\"Because 1500 is not divisible by 200\",\"Because 200 is a multiple of 1500\",\"Because 1500 is less than 200\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Anju states that 1500 is not divisible by 200, so it is not a multiple of 200.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If Dada has 400 grams of chana, can he make pouches of 60 grams each without any leftovers?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, because 400 is a multiple of 60\",\"Yes, because 60 is a multiple of 400\",\"No, because 400 is not a multiple of 60\",\"No, because 60 is not divisible by 400\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Anju explains that it is not possible because 400 is not a multiple of 60.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To find how much more chana is needed for 60g pouches, which multiple of 60 must be found?\",\"options\":[\"The multiple that comes directly before 400\",\"The multiple that comes directly after 400\",\"The multiple that is exactly 400\",\"The first multiple of 60\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Anju says we have to find the multiple of 60 that comes directly after 400.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the value of 60 × 7 according to the calculation in the text?\",\"options\":[\"360\",\"400\",\"420\",\"460\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the calculation 60 × 7 = 420.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many grams of extra chana are needed to reach the next multiple of 60 after 400?\",\"options\":[\"10 grams\",\"20 grams\",\"40 grams\",\"60 grams\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the next multiple is 420 and we have 400, we need 20 grams more.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a quantity is not a multiple of a measure, what does it imply about divisibility?\",\"options\":[\"The quantity is divisible by the measure\",\"The quantity is not divisible by the measure\",\"The measure is a multiple of the quantity\",\"There will be no leftovers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links being a multiple to being divisible; if it is not a multiple, it is not divisible.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the multiple of 60 that is less than 400?\",\"options\":[\"300\",\"360\",\"420\",\"460\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies 60 × 6 = 360 as the multiple before 400.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of Multiples\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the test for divisibility by 2, which of these digits should be in the units place?\",\"options\":[\"1, 3, 5, 7, or 9\",\"0, 2, 4, 6, or 8\",\"Only 0\",\"Only 2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states a number is divisible by 2 if it has 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8 in the units place.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A number is a multiple of 10 if it has which digit in the units place?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"2\",\"0\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The test for divisibility by 10 states that any number with 0 in the units place is a multiple of 10.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What digits must be in the units place for a number to be a multiple of 5?\",\"options\":[\"0 or 2\",\"2 or 5\",\"5 or 0\",\"5 or 10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that any number with 5 or 0 in the units place is a multiple of 5.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a number is \'exactly divisible\' by 2, it is also called a ______ of 2.\",\"options\":[\"Factor\",\"Multiple\",\"Sum\",\"Product\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'multiple of 2\' and \'exactly divisible by 2\' interchangeably.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which digit in the units place is common to the divisibility tests for 2, 5, and 10?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"2\",\"0\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The digit 0 appears in the rules for all three numbers (2, 5, and 10).\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To determine if a large number is a multiple of 5 or 10, which place value digit do we need to check?\",\"options\":[\"Tens place\",\"Hundreds place\",\"Units place\",\"Thousands place\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"All three rules mentioned in the text depend on the digit in the units place.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a number has 4 in the units place, which of the following is true based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"It is divisible by 5\",\"It is a multiple of 10\",\"It is exactly divisible by 2\",\"It is divisible by both 2 and 5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The rule for 2 includes the digit 4 in the units place.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these numbers from the text is exactly divisible by 2?\",\"options\":[\"53\",\"74\",\"85\",\"99\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"74 has a 4 in the units place, making it divisible by 2 according to the rule.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following numbers is a multiple of 5?\",\"options\":[\"62\",\"70\",\"84\",\"91\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"70 has a 0 in the units place, which satisfies the test for divisibility by 5.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rules, which number is a multiple of 10?\",\"options\":[\"105\",\"500\",\"222\",\"45\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"500 has a 0 in the units place, meeting the criteria for divisibility by 10.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a number has 0 in its units place, which of these statements is correct according to the rules?\",\"options\":[\"It is divisible by 2 only\",\"It is divisible by 5 only\",\"It is divisible by 10 only\",\"It is divisible by 2, 5, and 10\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The digit 0 is included in the divisibility rules for 2, 5, and 10.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the numbers 52, 74, 80, 96, and 98 all considered multiples of 2?\",\"options\":[\"Because they all end in 0\",\"Because they all have 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8 in the units place\",\"Because they are all greater than 50\",\"Because they are in the 5 times table\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"These numbers follow the specific units-place rule for divisibility by 2.\",\"topic\":\"Tests for divisibility\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is a number called if it has only two factors, 1 and the number itself?\",\"options\":[\"Composite number\",\"Prime number\",\"Even number\",\"Odd number\"