n canoes with a central hole) and sledges pulled by dogs.\",\"- The land is covered with snow and ice, and in spring the snow melts to reveal flowers.\",\"- Simple future tense is used for plans and promises about tomorrow, next week, or future events.\",\"- In simple future, we use ‘will’ or ‘shall’ + base verb (e.g., I will go, We shall sing).\",\"- The word ‘Eskimo’ means ‘eaters of raw meat’ but is not used by them; they prefer Inuit.\",\"- Igloos are still used today as temporary shelters for camping or experiments.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where is the land of the Eskimos located according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"South Pole\",\"Greenland\",\"Iceland\",\"America\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Eskimos live in Greenland, very close to the North Pole.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the American Indian word \'Eskimo\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"People of the snow\",\"Hunters of the north\",\"Eaters of raw meat\",\"Builders of ice houses\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines Eskimo as an American Indian word meaning \'eaters of raw meat.\'\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which term do the people of this land prefer to use when speaking of themselves?\",\"options\":[\"Eskimo\",\"Indian\",\"Inuit\",\"Greenlander\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that they do not use the word Eskimo for themselves but use the term \'Inuit.\'\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the land of the Eskimos described as a \'frozen desert\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is very hot and sandy\",\"Because it is covered only with snow and ice\",\"Because no one lives there\",\"Because there is no water available\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes it as a frozen desert because there is only snow and ice wherever you see.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these things is NOT found in the land of the Eskimos?\",\"options\":[\"Snow\",\"Ice\",\"Trees\",\"Raw meat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'no trees\' as one of the things missing in that land.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"For how many months of the year is the region dark and extremely cold?\",\"options\":[\"Two months\",\"Four months\",\"Six months\",\"Eight months\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states it is extremely cold and dark all the time for four months of the year.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The land of the Eskimos is located very close to which pole?\",\"options\":[\"South Pole\",\"East Pole\",\"West Pole\",\"North Pole\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states the Eskimos live very close to the North Pole.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a visitor went to Greenland during the four months of extreme cold mentioned in the text, what would they experience?\",\"options\":[\"Bright sunshine all day\",\"Continuous darkness\",\"Heavy rainfall\",\"Hot summer heat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says that during those four months, the region is dark all the time.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions there are \'no houses made of bricks and cement.\' What does this suggest about the buildings there?\",\"options\":[\"They are made of wood\",\"They are made of alternative materials like snow\",\"There are no buildings at all\",\"They use metal to build houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since there are no bricks, cement, or trees, the inhabitants must use the available snow and ice for shelter.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the main reason why there is \'no summer heat\' in this region?\",\"options\":[\"It is located near the North Pole\",\"There are too many cars\",\"The houses are made of bricks\",\"It is an American Indian land\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The proximity to the North Pole and the description of it as a frozen desert explain the lack of summer heat.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what material is used to build the dome-shaped house?\",\"options\":[\"Bricks and cement\",\"Wooden planks\",\"Blocks of snow or ice\",\"Mud and straw\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a house (igloo) constructed from blocks of snow, consistent with the text saying there are no bricks.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"32\\/054_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how has the person adapted to the environment described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"By wearing light cotton clothes\",\"By wearing heavy, hooded fur-like clothing\",\"By driving a car on the snow\",\"By planting trees around the house\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the person wearing thick, hooded clothing to survive the extreme cold mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"In Eskimo Land\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"32\\/054_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What material is used to make Eskimo summer houses?\",\"options\":[\"Large blocks of ice\",\"Sealskin\",\"Wooden planks\",\"Thick mud\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that summer houses are tents made of sealskin.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which season do Eskimos live in dome-shaped houses made of ice?\",\"options\":[\"Summer\",\"Spring\",\"Winter\",\"Autumn\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in winter they live in dome-shaped houses made out of large blocks of ice.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The shape of an igloo is compared to which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"A tall cylinder\",\"A square box\",\"A round bowl placed upside down\",\"A flat triangular roof\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the house as looking like a round bowl, placed upside down.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do Eskimos enter their winter houses?\",\"options\":[\"By walking through a large door\",\"By climbing a ladder\",\"By crawling through a tunnel\",\"By jumping through the roof\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a long narrow tunnel leads into the house and they crawl into their houses.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nature of the life that Eskimos usually lead?\",\"options\":[\"A settled life in one village\",\"A wandering life\",\"A life spent only on boats\",\"A life in modern cities\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The very first sentence of the text states that Eskimos usually live a wandering life.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it very warm inside an igloo according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they use fire inside\",\"Because the ice blocks are naturally hot\",\"Because the inside walls are covered with thick animal skin\",\"Because the tunnel keeps the sun out\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains it is warm inside because the inside walls are covered with thick animal skin.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an Eskimo family is moving during the summer, what type of shelter are they most likely to carry?\",\"options\":[\"Large blocks of ice\",\"A dome-shaped bowl\",\"A sealskin tent\",\"A long narrow tunnel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since they lead a wandering life and use sealskin tents in summer, this is the most logical shelter to use while moving.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which feature of the winter house helps in keeping the cold wind out and the heat in?\",\"options\":[\"The round shape of the bowl\",\"The long narrow tunnel\",\"The wandering lifestyle\",\"The use of sealskin in winter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the long narrow tunnel as the entrance, which physically restricts the direct flow of air compared to a large door.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description, what is the main difference between summer and winter Eskimo housing?\",\"options\":[\"Summer houses are for wandering, winter houses are for staying forever\",\"Summer houses use animal products, while winter houses use frozen water blocks\",\"Summer houses are dome-shaped, while winter houses are tents\",\"Summer houses are warm, while winter houses are cold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text contrasts summer tents (sealskin) with winter houses (ice blocks).\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can we conclude about the interior of an igloo from the text?\",\"options\":[\"It is made entirely of ice with no covering\",\"It is uncomfortable and cold\",\"It is modified with animal skin to provide warmth\",\"It is large enough to walk upright through the entrance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states it is very warm inside because the walls are covered with thick animal skin.\",\"topic\":\"Eskimo Houses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the spear that Eskimos use to kill seals?\",\"options\":[\"Javelin\",\"Harpoon\",\"Trident\",\"Arrow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Eskimo kills the seal with a spear called a harpoon.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the animal is used by Eskimos to make their clothes?\",\"options\":[\"Flesh\",\"Fat\",\"Skin\",\"Bones\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, their skins are used to make clothes.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what animal has been successfully hunted and is lying on the ice?\",\"options\":[\"A walrus\",\"A fish\",\"A seal\",\"A whale\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a seal lying on the ice next to the hunters, which matches the hunting description in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"32\\/054_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does an Eskimo make a hole in the ice while hunting?\",\"options\":[\"To catch fish with a net\",\"To wait for a seal to come up for air\",\"To get drinking water\",\"To build an igloo\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains he makes a hole and waits till a seal comes up for some air.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how is the flesh of seals and walrus often consumed in winter?\",\"options\":[\"It is cooked over a fire\",\"It is dried in the sun\",\"It is eaten raw\",\"It is salted and stored\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that their flesh is often eaten raw.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If an Eskimo needs to light a lamp, which part of the seal or walrus would they use?\",\"options\":[\"The fur\",\"The fat\",\"The skin\",\"The flesh\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the fat from these animals is used for making oils to light the lamps.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what tool are the hunters on the ice holding to hunt the animals?\",\"options\":[\"Fishing rods\",\"Harpoons\",\"Bow and arrows\",\"Wooden clubs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The hunters are shown holding long spears, which the text identifies as harpoons.\",\"topic\":\"Hunting and Food\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"32\\/054_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes how Eskimos use the animals they hunt?\",\"options\":[\"They hunt only for sports and fun\",\"They use differen