broken by the gusts” shows a **metaphor** (a figure of speech).\",\"- Rhyming pairs in the poem include “sorrow” and “mend” (near rhyme).\",\"- The bird’s **force of the storm** is described as **great**.\",\"- The bird can’t **mend** its eggs because they are broken.\",\"- The problem for the birds is their **living** situation after the storm.\",\"- The poem encourages caring for birds and building bird feeders.\",\"- A **bird feeder** should have holes for the stick and be filled with seeds.\",\"- The bird feeder is hung on a tree so the bird can feed safely.\",\"- The poem’s main theme is the bird’s search for a new home after a storm.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the poem \'Now Where will this Bird Rest?\'\",\"options\":[\"Mahadevi Verma\",\"Sarojini Naidu\",\"Rabindranath Tagore\",\"Ruskin Bond\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem is written by Mahadevi Verma and translated from Hindi.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What sound does the bird make in the poem when it is called?\",\"options\":[\"Tweet-tweet\",\"Chirp-chirp\",\"Cheen-cheen\",\"Coo-coo\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the bird cries out \'Cheen-cheen\'.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what has happened to the tree visible through the window?\",\"options\":[\"It is covered in flowers\",\"Its branches are broken and bent by the storm\",\"It is being cut down by people\",\"It is growing inside the house\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a bent tree with broken branches on the ground, illustrating the storm\'s force.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"32\\/068_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to the bird\'s nest during the storm?\",\"options\":[\"It was moved to a cupboard\",\"It was hidden under the branches\",\"It was blown away\",\"It was mended by the children\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'The poor bird\'s nest was blown away\'.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the bird refuse to stay inside the house?\",\"options\":[\"It is looking for food\",\"It is not a domestic pet and belongs in nature\",\"The cupboard is too small\",\"It is afraid of the children\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem implies the bird\'s distress and natural instinct to be outside despite the storm.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the third stanza, what is one major problem the speaker faces in helping the bird?\",\"options\":[\"They cannot find the bird\",\"They cannot bring a tree into the house\",\"The bird is too loud\",\"The storm is still going on\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The speaker asks, \'Where can we bring a tree into the house?\' as a reason they cannot easily help.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to help a bird after a storm based on the poem\'s context, which difficulty must they address regarding the eggs?\",\"options\":[\"Finding a way to hatch them\",\"Keeping them in a cupboard\",\"Mending broken eggs\",\"Feeding the eggs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem asks \'How can it mend its broken eggs?\', showing that broken eggs are a tragic consequence.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description \'Branches are broken by the gusts\', what does the word \'gusts\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"Heavy rainfall\",\"Strong sudden rushes of wind\",\"Thunder and lightning\",\"Falling leaves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gusts refer to the force of the wind in the storm that broke the branches.\",\"topic\":\"Now Where will this Bird Rest?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the emotional state of t