mplete angle: exactly 360° (full circle).\",\"A straight line contains two right angles.\",\"If a straight angle is divided into three equal parts, each part is 60° (acute).\",\"The starting point of a ray is the point from which it begins.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is used to denote points in geometry according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Small letters in English\",\"Numbers from 1 to 10\",\"Capital letters in English\",\"Geometric symbols\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that capital letters in English are used to denote points, such as point P, point Q, and point R.\",\"topic\":\"Revision and Point\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A point is described as having no length, no width, and no ______.\",\"options\":[\"Color\",\"Thickness\",\"Name\",\"Shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook explicitly mentions that a point has no length, width, or thickness.\",\"topic\":\"Revision and Point\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is a point physically created on paper?\",\"options\":[\"By drawing a long line\",\"By placing a sharp pencil and making a mark\",\"By shading a large area\",\"By using a ruler to measure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a point as a mark made by placing a sharp pencil on the paper.\",\"topic\":\"Revision and Point\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are basic geometric concepts like point, line, and plane mentioned in the context of a playground?\",\"options\":[\"To count the number of players\",\"To explain the rules of Kabaddi\",\"They are used in designing the playground\",\"To determine the speed of the players\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says geometric shapes and basic concepts are used in designing any playground, like the kabaddi field shown.\",\"topic\":\"Revision and Point\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a correct way to name a point based on the textbook\'s examples?\",\"options\":[\"point 