C\'s width is 20 mm, by what factor did the width decrease?\",\"options\":[\"Factor of 4\",\"Factor of 3\",\"Factor of 2\",\"Factor of 5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"40 divided by 20 equals 2, meaning the factor is 2.\",\"topic\":\"Proportional Relationship\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing Pictures A and C, what can be concluded about the change in their dimensions?\",\"options\":[\"Only the length changed by a factor of 2\",\"Only the width changed by a factor of 2\",\"Both length and width changed by the same factor of 2\",\"Neither length nor width changed by a factor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both 60\\/30 and 40\\/20 result in the same factor, confirming Sarika\'s observation.\",\"topic\":\"Proportional Relationship\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the requirement for two pictures to have a \'proportional relationship\' in their dimensions?\",\"options\":[\"They must have the same length\",\"They must have the same width\",\"Their length and width must change by the same factor\",\"Their length must be double their width\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies proportionality occurs when dimensions change by the same factor.\",\"topic\":\"Proportional Relationship\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a new Picture E had dimensions that did not change by the same factor as Picture A, what would Soham likely say?\",\"options\":[\"Picture E looks exactly like Picture A\",\"Picture E does not look the same as Picture A\",\"Picture E is in a proportional relationship with Picture A\",\"Picture E is similar to Picture C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Soham states that pictures do not look the same if their dimensions haven\'t changed by the same factor.\",\"topic\":\"Proportional Relationship\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table, what is the length of Picture D?\",\"options\":[\"60 mm\",\"40 mm\",\"30 mm\",\"20 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table explicitly states that the length of Picture D is 30 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the width of Picture D as shown in the dimensions table?\",\"options\":[\"20 mm\",\"30 mm\",\"40 mm\",\"60 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows the width of Picture D is 40 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which other picture has the same length as Picture D?\",\"options\":[\"Picture A\",\"Picture B\",\"Picture C\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both Picture C and Picture D have a length of 30 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which picture has a width that is equal to the width of Picture D?\",\"options\":[\"Picture A\",\"Picture B\",\"Picture C\",\"Picture D has a unique width\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Picture A and Picture D both have a width of 40 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unit of measurement used for the dimensions of Picture D in the table?\",\"options\":[\"Centimetres (cm)\",\"Metres (m)\",\"Millimetres (mm)\",\"Kilometres (km)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table headers specify that length and width are measured in mm (millimetres).\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ratio of the length of Picture D to the length of Picture A?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2\",\"2\\/1\",\"3\\/4\",\"1\\/3\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Length of D is 30 and A is 60; 30\\/60 simplifies to 1\\/2.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If we compare the width of Picture D to the width of Picture B, what is the resulting ratio?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2\",\"2\\/1\",\"1\\/1\",\"3\\/2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Width of D is 40 and B is 20; 40\\/20 simplifies to 2\\/1.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the length of Picture D compare to the length of Picture B?\",\"options\":[\"It is the same\",\"It is double\",\"It is half\",\"It is 10 mm more\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Picture D\'s length is 30 mm, which is half of Picture B\'s length of 60 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true regarding the dimensions of Picture D and Picture C?\",\"options\":[\"They have the same width but different lengths\",\"They have the same length but different widths\",\"They have identical dimensions\",\"Picture D is smaller than Picture C in both dimensions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Both have a length of 30 mm, but D has a width of 40 mm while C has a width of 20 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a new picture E was made by doubling both dimensions of Picture D, what would its width be?\",\"options\":[\"60 mm\",\"80 mm\",\"120 mm\",\"40 mm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Doubling the width of Picture D (40 mm) results in 80 mm.\",\"topic\":\"Dimensions of Picture D\",\"part\":21,\"bloom\":\"create\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21824,34,6,'Maths','en','Ratio and Proportion','[\"- A ratio shows how many times one quantity is compared to another, written as a:b.\",\"- The simplest form of a ratio is obtained by dividing both terms by their greatest common divisor.\",\"- If two ratios are equal, the quantities are in proportion.\",\"- To compare lengths and widths of similar figures, reduce the ratio of length to width to its simplest form.\",\"- Ratios have no units; before comparing, make the units equal (e.g., meters to meters).\",\"- Proportion of a to b is written a:b; the ratio of a to b is also a:b.\",\"- Common examples: 3 parts sand : 1 part cement → 3:1; 5 cups peanut butter : 2 cups jaggery → 5:2.\",\"- In a ratio a:b, \'a\' is the first term and \'b\' is the second term.\",\"- When converting units, use the same unit for both terms before forming a ratio.\",\"- The unitary method: find the price of one item by dividing the total price by the quantity, then multiply by the desired quantity.\",\"- For age ratios, reduce the ratio of ages to simplest form to compare ages.\",\"- Ratios can be used to compare lengths, weights, distances, prices, and numbers of people.\",\"- To check if two figures are similar, compare their corresponding side ratios; if equal, they are similar.\",\"- Ratios are useful in everyday problems like sharing food, buying items, and measuring distances.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is the proportion 1:3 read?\",\"options\":[\"one and three\",\"one is to three\",\"one by three\",\"one plus three\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1:3 is read as \'one is to three\'.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of a ratio as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A number that shows the total sum of two quantities\",\"A number that shows the difference between two quantities\",\"A number that shows how many times one quantity is compared to another\",\"A number that shows the product of two quantities\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a ratio as a number that shows how many times one quantity is compared to another.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the concrete mixture example, how many parts of sand are mixed with one part of cement?\",\"options\":[\"1 part\",\"2 parts\",\"3 parts\",\"5 parts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that to make concrete, 3 parts of sand are mixed with one part of cement.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the ratio 1:3 for cement to sand, what does the number \'1\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The parts of sand\",\"The parts of cement\",\"The total parts of concrete\",\"The number of cups used\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the proportion of cement to sand is 1:3, where 1 corresponds to cement.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the proportion of peanut butter to jaggery (5:2) read in the laddu example?\",\"options\":[\"five as to two\",\"five and two\",\"five to two\",\"five divided by two\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the proportion 5:2 is read as \'five as to two\'.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the proportion of cement to sand is 1:3, how is this written as a ratio in fractional form?\",\"options\":[\"$\\\\frac{3}{1}$\",\"$\\\\frac{1}{2}$\",\"$\\\\frac{1}{3}$\",\"$\\\\frac{3}{3}$\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the ratio of cement to sand is written as $\\\\frac{1}{3}$.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Sanjeev used 5 cups of peanut butter and 2 cups of jaggery. What is the ratio of peanut butter to jaggery?\",\"options\":[\"$\\\\frac{2}{5}$\",\"$\\\\frac{5}{2}$\",\"$\\\\frac{5}{7}$\",\"$\\\\frac{2}{7}$\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the proportion of peanut butter to jaggery is 5:2, which is written as the ratio $\\\\frac{5}{2}$.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a construction worker needs to write the proportion of cement to sand using a colon, which is correct based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"3:1\",\"1:1\",\"1:3\",\"3:3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the proportion of cement to sand in concrete is 1:3.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the proportion of peanut butter to jaggery is 5:2, what would be the proportion of jaggery to peanut butter?\",\"options\":[\"5:2\",\"2:5\",\"2:2\",\"5:5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By analyzing the relationship, if peanut butter to jaggery is 5:2, the reverse (jaggery to peanut butter) is 2:5.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the concrete example, if the ratio of cement to sand is 1:3, which statement is true regarding the quantities?\",\"options\":[\"There is more cement than sand.\",\"The amount of sand is three times the amount of cement.\",\"The amount of cement is three times the amount of sand.\",\"Sand and cement are mixed in equal parts.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A ratio of 1:3 (cement to sand) means there are 3 parts of sand for every 1 part of cement, making sand three times the quantity of cement.\",\"topic\":\"Ratio and Proportion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In what form are ratios always written according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Multiplied form\",\"Simplest form\",\"Expanded form\",\"Original form\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Ratios are always written in simplest form.\'\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is Kundan\'s current age as mentioned in the illustrative example?\",\"options\":[\"48 years\",\"20 years\",\"12 years\",\"5 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Kundan\'s current age is 12 years\'.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many orange flowers does the florist use to make a garland in the example?\",\"options\":[\"20\",\"12\",\"5\",\"3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the florist uses \'12 orange flowers\'.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the age of Kundan\'s father in the given example?\",\"options\":[\"12 years\",\"20 years\",\"36 years\",\"48 years\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'his father\'s age is 48 years\'.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the simplest form of the ratio of Kundan\'s age to his father\'s age?\",\"options\":[\"12\\/48\",\"1\\/4\",\"4\\/1\",\"1\\/2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The calculation shows 12\\/48 is simplified to 1\\/4 by dividing both numbers by 12.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the ratio of yellow flowers to orange flowers (5\\/3), what does the number 5 represent?\",\"options\":[\"The total number of flowers\",\"The number of orange flowers\",\"The number of yellow flowers\",\"the florist\'s age\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the ratio $\\\\frac{\\\\text{(yellow flowers)}}{\\\\text{(orange flowers)}} = \\\\frac{5}{3}$, 5 corresponds to the yellow flowers.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the ratio of Kundan\'s age to his father\'s age written as 1\\/4 instead of 12\\/48?\",\"options\":[\"Because 12 is smaller than 48\",\"Because ratios must be written in simplest form\",\"Because age cannot be written as a large fraction\",\"Because 48 is a multiple of 12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that ratios are always written in simplest form, which is why 12\\/48 is reduced to 1\\/4.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To find the ratio of orange flowers to yellow flowers, which value is placed in the numerator?\",\"options\":[\"Number of yellow flowers\",\"Number of orange flowers\",\"Total number of flowers\",\"The number 4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The ratio is written as $\\\\frac{\\\\text{(orange flowers)}}{\\\\text{(yellow flowers)}}$, so orange flowers go in the numerator.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a florist uses 20 yellow flowers and 12 orange flowers, what is the ratio of yellow flowers to orange flowers in simplest form?\",\"options\":[\"3\\/5\",\"20\\/12\",\"5\\/3\",\"4\\/3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The calculation $\\\\frac{20}{12} = \\\\frac{20 \\\\div 4}{12 \\\\div 4} = \\\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the simplest form.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the florist is making a garland. If he follows the example in the text using 20 yellow and 12 orange flowers, what is the ratio of orange flowers to yellow flowers?\",\"options\":[\"5\\/3\",\"12\\/20\",\"3\\/5\",\"1\\/4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text for the florist example, the ratio of orange to yellow flowers is 3\\/5.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/066_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the florist uses two colors of flowers. Based on the text, what divisor was used to reduce the ratio of 20 yellow flowers and 12 orange flowers to its simplest form of 5\\/3?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"5\",\"12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows $(20 \\\\div 4)$ and $(12 \\\\div 4)$ were used to get the simplest form.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/066_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the two illustrative examples. Which of the following is common to the final answers for both Kundan\'s age ratio and the flower ratios?\",\"options\":[\"Both final ratios have 3 as the denominator\",\"Both final ratios are written in their simplest form\",\"Both final ratios use the number 12\",\"Both final ratios compare age\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text emphasizes for both examples that ratios are always written in simplest form.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If \'a\' and \'b\' are two numbers, how is the proportion of \'a\' to \'b\' written as a ratio?\",\"options\":[\"a + b\",\"a - b\",\"a × b\",\"a\\/b\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the proportion of a to b is written as a ratio a\\/b.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the ratio a\\/b, what is the technical name for the term \'a\'?\",\"options\":[\"Second term\",\"First term\",\"Middle term\",\"Final term\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the ratio a\\/b, \'a\' is called the first term.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the ratio a\\/b, what is the technical name for the term \'b\'?\",\"options\":[\"First term\",\"Second term\",\"Third term\",\"Denominator term\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the ratio a\\/b, \'b\' is called the second term.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used to represent the proportion of \'a\' to \'b\' before writing it as a fraction?\",\"options\":[\"a = b\",\"a > b\",\"a:b\",\"a + b\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the proportion of a to b is written as a:b.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When finding the ratio of shaded cells to total number of cells, which value acts as the second term?\",\"options\":[\"The number of shaded cells\",\"The number of unshaded cells\",\"The total number of cells\",\"The difference between shaded and unshaded cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a ratio, the second quantity mentioned (total number of cells) becomes the second term (denominator).\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If we want to find the ratio of unshaded cells to shaded cells, what would be the first term?\",\"options\":[\"The number of unshaded cells\",\"The number of shaded cells\",\"The total number of cells\",\"The sum of all cells\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first quantity mentioned in the ratio description becomes the first term.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the total number of cells counted?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"14\",\"16\",\"20\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The grid is 4 by 4, which equals 16 total cells.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/066_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the ratio of shaded cells to unshaded cells?\",\"options\":[\"6:10\",\"10:6\",\"6:16\",\"10:16\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"There are 6 shaded cells and 10 unshaded cells, so the ratio is 6:10.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/066_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the figure, if the ratio of unshaded cells to the total number of cells is calculated, what is the value of the first term?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"10\",\"16\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"There are 10 unshaded cells, which serves as the first term in this specific ratio.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/066_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the ratio of shaded cells to total cells is written as 6\\/16, which statement is true regarding the terms?\",\"options\":[\"6 is the second term and 16 is the first term\",\"16 is the first term and 6 is the second term\",\"6 is the first term and 16 is the second term\",\"Both 6 and 16 are first terms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the ratio a\\/b, the numerator is the first term and the denominator is the second term.\",\"topic\":\"Do it\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the length of the wall built by Anil?\",\"options\":[\"25 feet\",\"10 feet\",\"50 feet\",\"5 feet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Anil built a 50 feet long wall.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many bags of cement did Sandeep use for his wall?\",\"options\":[\"10 bags\",\"5 bags\",\"25 bags\",\"2 bags\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Sandeep used 5 bags of cement.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the simplified ratio of the length of Anil\'s wall to Sandeep\'s wall?\",\"options\":[\"1:2\",\"50:10\",\"2:1\",\"5:10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The ratio 50:25 simplifies to 2:1.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Anil think his wall would be stronger than Sandeep\'s?\",\"options\":[\"His wall was longer\",\"He used more cement bags than Sandeep\",\"His wall was 50 feet high\",\"He used better quality cement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Anil thought his wall was stronger because he used more cement (10 bags) than Sandeep (5 bags).\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does it mean when the ratio of the length of the walls and the ratio of the cement bags are the same?\",\"options\":[\"The walls are of equal length\",\"The walls are not strong\",\"The length and the amount of cement are in proportion\",\"Anil used too much cement\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that since both ratios are the same, the length and cement are in proportion.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ratio of the number of cement bags used by Anil to the number used by Sandeep?\",\"options\":[\"10:5\",\"5:10\",\"50:25\",\"25:5\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Anil used 10 bags and Sandeep used 5 bags, so the ratio is 10:5.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the ratio of lengths is 2:1 and the ratio of cement bags is 2:1, what can be concluded?\",\"options\":[\"Anil\'s wall is twice as strong\",\"The values are in proportion\",\"Sandeep\'s wall is stronger\",\"The ratios are different\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When ratios are the same, the quantities are in proportion.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Sandeep wanted to build a wall as long as Anil\'s (50 feet) using the same proportion, how many bags would he need?\",\"options\":[\"5 bags\",\"15 bags\",\"10 bags\",\"20 bags\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To maintain the 2:1 ratio for a 50 feet wall, 10 bags are needed (50\\/5 = 10).\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ratio of the length of Sandeep\'s wall to the length of Anil\'s wall?\",\"options\":[\"2:1\",\"1:2\",\"5:10\",\"25:10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sandeep\'s wall is 25 feet and Anil\'s is 50 feet, so 25:50 = 1:2.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze Anil\'s claim: \'My wall is stronger because I used 10 bags and Sandeep used only 5.\' Is he mathematically correct?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, more cement always means more strength\",\"No, because the ratio of cement to wall length is the same for both\",\"Yes, because his wall is longer\",\"No, because Sandeep\'s wall is shorter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Strength depends on the proportion; since the ratios are equal (2:1), the relative amount of cement is the same.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which calculation is used in the text to determine if the walls are built in proportion?\",\"options\":[\"Subtracting the number of bags from the length\",\"Adding the lengths of both walls\",\"Comparing the ratio of lengths to the ratio of cement bags\",\"Multiplying the bags by the length\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text calculates and compares the ratio of lengths and the ratio of cement bags.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the relationship between the length of the wall and the cement used?\",\"options\":[\"They are in proportion\",\"They are unrelated\",\"Anil used more cement per foot than Sandeep\",\"Sandeep used more cement per foot than Anil\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that the length of the wall and the amount of cement used are in proportion.\",\"topic\":\"Understand\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the unit of a ratio?\",\"options\":[\"Meters\",\"Kilometers\",\"A ratio does not have any unit\",\"Grams\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'A ratio does not have any unit.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What must be done to the units of two quantities before finding their ratio?\",\"options\":[\"They must be multiplied\",\"Their units must be made equal\",\"They must be kept in different units\",\"The larger unit must be ignored\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'To find the ratio of two quantities, their units must be made equal.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which student correctly followed the rule of making units equal before finding the ratio?\",\"options\":[\"Student \'A\'\",\"Student \'B\'\",\"Both Student \'A\' and Student \'B\'\",\"Neither Student \'A\' nor Student \'B\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Student \'B\' converted 2 km to 2000 m to make the units equal, whereas Student \'A\' did not.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/067_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why is the method used by Student \'A\' considered incorrect in a mathematical discussion?\",\"options\":[\"The division is wrong\",\"The names are swapped\",\"The units (m. and km.) are not made equal\",\"The distance of 1700 m is written incorrectly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Student \'A\' attempted to find a ratio using different units (m. and km.), which violates the rule that units must be equal.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/067_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many meters does Ramesh walk every day according to the discussion text?\",\"options\":[\"1700 meters\",\"2 meters\",\"2000 meters\",\"17 meters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ramesh walks 2 km, which Student \'B\' correctly converts to 2000 meters.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the distance covered by Suresh every morning?\",\"options\":[\"2 km.\",\"1700 meters\",\"2000 meters\",\"17 km.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Suresh walks 1700 meters every morning\'.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the simplified ratio of the distance covered by Suresh to the distance covered by Ramesh?\",\"options\":[\"1700 \\/ 2\",\"17 \\/ 20\",\"20 \\/ 17\",\"170 \\/ 2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Student \'B\' calculates the ratio as 1700\\/2000, which simplifies to 17\\/20.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If we need to find the ratio of two distances, and one is in meters while the other is in kilometers, what is the first step?\",\"options\":[\"Divide the numbers directly\",\"Convert both to the same unit\",\"Add the two distances\",\"Ignore the units and just use the numbers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rule states that to find the ratio, units must be made equal first.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which value did Student \'B\' use for the distance covered by Ramesh to make the units equal to Suresh\'s distance?\",\"options\":[\"2 km.\",\"200 m.\",\"2000 m.\",\"1700 m.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Student \'B\' used 2000 m. as the equivalent of 2 km.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the final answer 17\\/20 not have \'meters\' written next to it?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a small number\",\"Because the student forgot to write it\",\"Because a ratio does not have any unit\",\"Because the units were different\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a ratio does not have any unit.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a requirement for finding the ratio of two quantities?\",\"options\":[\"The quantities must be in different units\",\"The quantities must be in the same unit\",\"The answer must always be a decimal\",\"One quantity must be zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that units must be made equal, meaning they must be in the same unit.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ratio of Suresh\'s distance to Ramesh\'s distance as shown in the final step of the correct calculation?\",\"options\":[\"17\\/20 m.\",\"17\\/20 km.\",\"17\\/20\",\"1700\\/2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The final ratio is 17\\/20, and as per the rule, it has no units.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the Unitary Method when finding the price of many items from the price of many items?\",\"options\":[\"Find the price of one item using division\",\"Find the price of one item using multiplication\",\"Add the prices of all items\",\"Subtract the price of one item from the total\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the Unitary Method, we first find the price of one item from the price of many items using division.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the Unitary Method, which operation is used to find the price of many items once the price of one item is known?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, finding the price of many items from the price of one item is done using multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many items are contained in \'a dozen\'?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"12\",\"20\",\"100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the example of Suyash, the cost of a dozen saplings is calculated using the number 12 (144 ÷ 12).\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does Sanchita need to calculate the cost of 75 pencils?\",\"options\":[\"To sell them at her school\",\"To give one pencil each to 75 friends on her birthday\",\"To honour 75 teachers on Guru Purnima\",\"To fill 75 boxes of pencils\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Sanchita wants to distribute 1 pencil each to 75 friends on her birthday.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what specific type of plant is the boy buying to honour his teachers?\",\"options\":[\"Rose saplings\",\"Mango saplings\",\"Tulsi saplings\",\"Neem saplings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text associated with the illustration of Suyash mentions he wants to give Tulsi saplings.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/068_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ratio of 5 rupees to 80 paise after converting them to the same unit?\",\"options\":[\"5 : 80\",\"80 : 500\",\"25 : 4\",\"4 : 25\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the calculation: 500\\/80 = 25\\/4.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a box of 10 pencils costs Rs. 70, what is the cost of a single pencil?\",\"options\":[\"Rs. 7\",\"Rs. 10\",\"Rs. 70\",\"Rs. 700\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text calculates the price of a pencil as 70 ÷ 10 = 7 rupees.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the price of one Tulsi sapling is Rs. 12, how much will Suyash pay for 10 saplings?\",\"options\":[\"Rs. 144\",\"Rs. 120\",\"Rs. 100\",\"Rs. 22\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the calculation: 10 × 12 = Rs. 120.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To find the ratio of 5 rupees to 80 paise, why is it necessary to convert 5 rupees into 500 paise?\",\"options\":[\"To make the numbers larger for division\",\"Because ratios can only be found between quantities of the same unit\",\"Because 80 is a multiple of 5\",\"To convert the final answer into rupees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text demonstrates converting rupees to paise so that both quantities in the ratio are in paise.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text and the given figure, if the boy needs to honour 10 teachers, how many saplings is he shown handling or purchasing in total for his requirement?\",\"options\":[\"12 saplings\",\"10 saplings\",\"1 sapling\",\"75 saplings\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Suyash wants to honour 10 teachers, so he needs 10 saplings.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/068_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical expression correctly represents the process of finding the cost of 75 pencils if 10 pencils cost Rs. 70?\",\"options\":[\"(70 + 10) × 75\",\"(70 ÷ 10) × 75\",\"(75 ÷ 10) × 70\",\"(70 × 10) ÷ 75\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Unitary Method involves dividing the total cost by the number of items (70 ÷ 10) to find the unit price, then multiplying by the new quantity (75).\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If the nursery owner had quoted the price for 1 sapling directly as Rs. 12, which step of the Unitary Method would Suyash have skipped?\",\"options\":[\"The multiplication step\",\"The division step to find the price of one\",\"The step of counting the teachers\",\"The step of paying the money\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The division step is used specifically to find the price of one item from many; if the price of one is already known, this step is unnecessary.\",\"topic\":\"Unitary Method\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If \'a\' and \'b\' are two numbers, how is the proportion of \'a\' to \'b\' written?\",\"options\":[\"a + b\",\"a - b\",\"a : b\",\"a x b\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the proportion of a to b is written as a:b.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the ratio expression a\\/b, what is the name given to the term \'b\'?\",\"options\":[\"First term\",\"Second term\",\"Final term\",\"Equal term\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the ratio a\\/b, b is called the second term.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ratio of two numbers \'a\' and \'b\' written as in fractional form?\",\"options\":[\"a - b\",\"a + b\",\"b \\/ a\",\"a \\/ b\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines the ratio of a to b as a\\/b.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a necessary condition for calculating the ratio of two quantities?\",\"options\":[\"The first term must be larger than the second term.\",\"Their units must be made equal.\",\"The units must be different.\",\"Both numbers must be prime numbers.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that while calculating the ratio of two quantities, their units must be made equal.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What can be said about the unit of a ratio?\",\"options\":[\"It is always measured in kilograms.\",\"It is the same as the unit of the first term.\",\"A ratio does not have any unit.\",\"It is the same as the unit of the second term.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a ratio does not have any unit.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the ratio a\\/b, what is \'a\' called?\",\"options\":[\"First term\",\"Second term\",\"Unit term\",\"Proportion\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a is called the first term.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To find the price of one item from the price of many items, which mathematical operation is used in the unitary method?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines finding the price of one item from many by division.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you know the price of one item, how do you find the price of many items using the unitary method?\",\"options\":[\"By division\",\"By multiplication\",\"By subtraction\",\"By adding the units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that finding the price of many items from the price of one item is done by multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the method that involves finding the price of one item first and then the price of many items?\",\"options\":[\"Ratio method\",\"Proportion method\",\"Unitary method\",\"Equality method\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines this two-step process as the unitary method.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it incorrect to calculate the ratio of 5 meters to 200 centimeters without changing the values?\",\"options\":[\"Because the first term is smaller than the second.\",\"Because the units are not equal.\",\"Because ratio cannot be calculated for length.\",\"Because the numbers are not divisible.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that units must be made equal before calculating the ratio.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21825,34,6,'Maths','en','Triangles and Squares','[\"A triangle is a closed shape made by joining three non‑collinear points with straight lines.\",\"A triangle has 3 vertices, 3 sides (arms) and 3 interior angles.\",\"The symbol A is used to denote a triangle (e.g., △ABC).\",\"The three sides of a triangle are usually named by the two vertices they join (e.g., side AB).\",\"The three angles of a triangle are named by the vertex at the centre (e.g., ∠ABC).\",\"If all three sides are equal, the triangle is equilateral.\",\"If two sides are equal, the triangle is isosceles.\",\"If all sides are different, the triangle is scalene.\",\"If one angle is 90°, the triangle is right‑angled.\",\"If one angle is >90°, the triangle is obtuse‑angled.\",\"If all three angles are <90°, the triangle is acute‑angled.\",\"The interior of a triangle is the region inside its three sides; the exterior is everything outside it.\",\"A quadrilateral has 4 vertices, 4 sides, 4 angles and 2 diagonals.\",\"Opposite sides of a quadrilateral do not share a vertex; adjacent sides share one vertex.\",\"Opposite angles of a quadrilateral do not share a side; adjacent angles share a side.\",\"The line joining two opposite vertices of a quadrilateral is called a diagonal.\",\"A quadrilateral can be named in many ways by starting at any vertex and going clockwise or counter‑clockwise.\",\"The maximum number of distinct names for a quadrilateral is 8 (4 starting points × 2 directions).\",\"In a triangle, the sum of the three interior angles is always 180°.\",\"In a quadrilateral, the sum of the four interior angles is always 360°.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the minimum number of matchsticks required to make a closed shape?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks for the minimum number of matchsticks to make a closed shape, and the answer is 3.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used to represent a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"∠\",\"Δ\",\"□\",\"⊥\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the symbol Δ is used to represent a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A triangle is a closed shape formed by joining three ______ points with line segments.\",\"options\":[\"collinear\",\"straight\",\"non-collinear\",\"parallel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the definition in the text, a triangle is formed by joining three non-collinear points.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many vertices does a triangle have?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a triangle has three vertices.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In ΔPQR, which of the following is a side?\",\"options\":[\"Point P\",\"∠PQR\",\"Side PQ\",\"ΔPQR\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Side PQ, Side QR, and Side PR as the sides of ΔPQR.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Can a closed shape be formed using only 2 matchsticks?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No\",\"Only if they are long\",\"Yes, if they are joined at one end\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The revision section implies that a closed shape cannot be formed using only 2 matchsticks.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for the \'sides\' of a triangle mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Legs\",\"Arms\",\"Hands\",\"Sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'three sides (arms)\' in the definition of a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If ∠QRP is an angle in ΔPQR, what is another way to write its name?\",\"options\":[\"∠PQR\",\"∠RQP\",\"∠PRQ\",\"∠QPR\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that ∠QRP can also be written as ∠PRQ or simply ∠R.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these are the vertices of ΔPQR?\",\"options\":[\"Side PQ, Side QR, Side PR\",\"∠P, ∠Q, ∠R\",\"Point P, Point Q, Point R\",\"ΔP, ΔQ, ΔR\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Point P, Point Q, and Point R as the vertices of ΔPQR.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many angles does a triangle have in total?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definition states that a triangle has three angles.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the name ΔPQR, what does the letter \'Q\' represent if it is referred to as a \'Point Q\'?\",\"options\":[\"A side\",\"An arm\",\"A vertex\",\"A line segment\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Point P, Point Q, and Point R as vertices.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the result of joining three non-collinear points with line segments?\",\"options\":[\"A closed shape called a triangle\",\"An open shape with two sides\",\"A square with three angles\",\"A straight line segment\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines a triangle as a closed shape formed by joining three non-collinear points with line segments.\",\"topic\":\"Triangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the area outside the triangle called?\",\"options\":[\"Interior of the triangle\",\"Exterior of the triangle\",\"Triangular region\",\"Boundary of the triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the area outside the triangle is called the exterior of the triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following points are located \'on\' the triangle ΔABC?\",\"options\":[\"R, K, U\",\"P, S, N\",\"A, B, C, L, J, D\",\"Only A, B, and C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the points on the triangle are A, B, C, L, J, D.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is formed by combining a triangle and its interior?\",\"options\":[\"An exterior region\",\"A shaded area\",\"A triangular region\",\"A point set\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a triangular region as a triangle and its interior.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which points are mentioned as being in the interior of the triangle?\",\"options\":[\"Points P, S, N\",\"Points A, B, C\",\"Points R, K, U\",\"Points L, J, D\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that points R, K, U are the interior points of the triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the description of ΔABC, how is the interior of the triangle visually represented?\",\"options\":[\"As a dotted area\",\"As a shaded area\",\"As an open area\",\"As a white area\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the shaded area in the figure is the interior of the triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a point \'M\' is placed in the shaded area of ΔABC, what type of point is it?\",\"options\":[\"Exterior point\",\"Point on the triangle\",\"Interior point\",\"Vertex point\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the shaded area is the interior, any point within it is an interior point.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a point \'Q\' is located in the area outside the triangle, it is classified as which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"An interior point\",\"An exterior point\",\"A point on the triangle\",\"A triangular region point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The area outside the triangle is the exterior, so points there are exterior points.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which group of points belongs to the exterior of the triangle?\",\"options\":[\"R, K, U\",\"A, B, C\",\"P, S, N\",\"L, J, D\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies points P, S, N as the exterior points of the triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these points is NOT an interior point and is NOT an exterior point?\",\"options\":[\"Point R\",\"Point P\",\"Point L\",\"Point U\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point L is listed as a point \'on the triangle\', whereas R and U are interior and P is exterior.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are asked to identify the \'Triangular Region\' of ΔABC, what must you include?\",\"options\":[\"Only the points P, S, and N\",\"Only the shaded area\",\"The triangle itself and its shaded interior\",\"Only the points A, B, and C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the triangular region as the triangle and its interior together.\",\"topic\":\"Interior and Exterior of Triangle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many total types of triangles are there based on sides and angles together?\",\"options\":[\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\",\"Six\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states there are six types of triangles: three based on sides and three based on angles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A triangle in which all three sides are of equal length is called a\\/an ______ triangle.\",\"options\":[\"Scalene\",\"Isosceles\",\"Equilateral\",\"Acute angled\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an equilateral triangle as one where all three sides are of equal length.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the game described in the text, what should students do when they are \'in the pond\'?\",\"options\":[\"Jump outside the triangle\",\"Stand on the vertices\",\"Jump inside the triangle\",\"Stand on the sides\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The game instructions state: \'Jump inside the triangle when you are in the pond\'.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many types of triangles are classified specifically based on their sides?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Six\",\"One\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there are three types based on sides (equilateral, isosceles, and scalene).\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A triangle that has one obtuse angle and two acute angles is known as a\\/an ______ triangle.\",\"options\":[\"Acute angled\",\"Right angled\",\"Obtuse angled\",\"Isosceles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an obtuse angled triangle as having one obtuse angle and two acute angles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the defining characteristic of a scalene triangle according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"All three sides are of equal length\",\"The length of two sides are equal\",\"The length of all three sides are different\",\"All three angles are acute\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the length of all three sides are different, it is a scalene triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In a right angled triangle, how many angles are acute angles?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"None\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes a right angled triangle as having one right angle and two acute angles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a classification of triangles based on their angles?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angled triangle\",\"Isosceles triangle\",\"Right angled triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Isosceles is a classification based on sides, not angles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the lengths l(PS), l(SJ), and l(PJ) are all measured to be equal, what type of triangle is it?\",\"options\":[\"Scalene triangle\",\"Isosceles triangle\",\"Equilateral triangle\",\"Right angled triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if all three sides are of equal length, it is an equilateral triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/073_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if l(RK) is equal to l(RU), but l(KU) is different, which category does triangle RKU belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Equilateral triangle\",\"Isosceles triangle\",\"Scalene triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the length of two sides are equal, it is an isosceles triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/073_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student measures a triangle and finds the sides are 5 cm, 7 cm, and 8 cm, what type of triangle is it based on the side classification?\",\"options\":[\"Equilateral\",\"Isosceles\",\"Scalene\",\"Acute angled\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since all three side lengths are different, it is a scalene triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, triangle GHM has a specific symbol at vertex H. Based on the text, what does this indicate about the triangle?\",\"options\":[\"It is an acute angled triangle because all angles are equal.\",\"It is a right angled triangle because one angle is a right angle.\",\"It is an obtuse angled triangle because one angle is large.\",\"It is a scalene triangle because the sides look different.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The symbol at H denotes a right angle, and the text classifies a triangle with one right angle as a right angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/074_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the tables in the text, which two properties are used to categorize triangles into six different types?\",\"options\":[\"Vertices and Names\",\"Ponds and Gardens\",\"Lengths of sides and Measures of angles\",\"Inside and Outside positions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides two tables: one for types based on sides (lengths) and one for types based on angles (measures).\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a triangle is described as \'acute angled\', what must be true about every one of its angles?\",\"options\":[\"Only one angle must be acute.\",\"Exactly two angles must be acute.\",\"All three angles must be acute.\",\"At least one angle must be a right angle.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states for an acute angled triangle: \'All three angles are acute angles.\'\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"A triangle has angles measuring 30°, 60°, and 90°. According to the classification rules in the text, which group does it belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angled triangle\",\"Right angled triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\",\"Equilateral triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if one angle is a right angle (90°), it is a right angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Triangles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How many students are required to form a group for the Mathematical Game described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"10 students\",\"11 students\",\"12 students\",\"13 students\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to first form a group of 12 students for the game.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total length of the rope used in the Mathematical Game?\",\"options\":[\"11 meters\",\"12 meters\",\"10 meters\",\"1 meter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The game instructions specify taking a 12 meter long rope.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many knots are tied on the rope for the Mathematical Game?\",\"options\":[\"12 knots\",\"11 knots\",\"10 knots\",\"1 knot\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to tie 11 knots, one knot for every meter.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the game, how many students are standing at the position where the two ends of the rope meet?\",\"options\":[\"1 student\",\"2 students\",\"11 students\",\"12 students\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states one student will stand holding both ends of the rope.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Think about\' section, can an obtuse angled triangle also be an acute angled triangle?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No\",\"Only if it is isosceles\",\"Only if it is scalene\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By definition and the logic of the \'Think about\' question, a triangle cannot be both obtuse and acute.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which type of triangle can also be a right angled triangle?\",\"options\":[\"Equilateral triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\",\"Isosceles triangle\",\"Acute angled triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'Can an isosceles triangle be a right angled triangle?\', implying this specific combination is possible.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the 12-meter rope is used to make an equilateral triangle (all sides equal), what will be the length of each side?\",\"options\":[\"3 meters\",\"4 meters\",\"6 meters\",\"2 meters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A 12-meter rope divided into three equal sides results in 4 meters per side.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the Mathematical Game, what is the distance between any two consecutive knots?\",\"options\":[\"12 meters\",\"11 meters\",\"1 meter\",\"2 meters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to tie one knot for every meter on a 12-meter rope.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A triangle with all three sides of equal length is always which type of triangle based on angles?\",\"options\":[\"Right angled triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\",\"Acute angled triangle\",\"Scalene triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Think about\' section links triangles with three equal sides to acute angled triangles.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many different types of triangles based on \'sides\' are the students asked to make in the game?\",\"options\":[\"Two types\",\"Three types\",\"Four types\",\"Six types\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks students to make \'all three types of triangles based on sides\'.\",\"topic\":\"Think about and Mathematical Game\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many non-collinear points are required to be joined to form a quadrilateral as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states to take four non-collinear points A, B, C, D to form a quadrilateral.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A quadrilateral is defined as what type of figure in the text?\",\"options\":[\"An open figure\",\"A closed figure\",\"A three-sided figure\",\"A circular figure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the quadrilateral as a \'closed figure\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, in which directions can a quadrilateral be read or written?\",\"options\":[\"Only clockwise\",\"Only counter-clockwise\",\"Clockwise or counter-clockwise\",\"In any random order of vertices\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states they are read or written in a clockwise or counter-clockwise (anti-clockwise) direction.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From which vertex can the reading and writing of a quadrilateral begin?\",\"options\":[\"Only the top vertex\",\"Only the vertex labeled \'A\' or \'P\'\",\"Any vertex of the quadrilateral\",\"Only the bottom-left vertex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says reading and writing can be done starting from any vertex of the quadrilateral.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if we start at vertex P and read in a clockwise direction, what is the correct name?\",\"options\":[\"PQRS\",\"PSRQ\",\"PRSQ\",\"PSQR\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Starting at P and moving clockwise follows the order P -> Q -> R -> S.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of the following is a correct counter-clockwise name for the quadrilateral?\",\"options\":[\"PQRS\",\"PSRQ\",\"QRSP\",\"QPSR\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Starting at P and moving counter-clockwise follows the order P -> S -> R -> Q.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When joining two points of a quadrilateral, where must the remaining two points be located relative to the line joining them?\",\"options\":[\"On opposite sides of the line\",\"On the same side of the line\",\"On the line itself\",\"One on the line and one off the line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if any two points are joined, the remaining two points will be on the same side of the line joining them.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a quadrilateral is written as \'SRQP\', in which direction is it being read?\",\"options\":[\"Clockwise\",\"Counter-clockwise\",\"Vertical\",\"Horizontal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the table in the text, SRQP is listed under the \'Counter clockwise\' column.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the patterns shown in the text, what is the maximum number of ways a single quadrilateral can be named?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"6\",\"8\",\"12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"There are 4 vertices, and from each vertex, you can go in 2 directions (clockwise and counter-clockwise), totaling 8 ways.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a valid clockwise name for a quadrilateral if one of its clockwise names is PQRS?\",\"options\":[\"PSRQ\",\"QRSP\",\"QPSR\",\"SRPQ\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"QRSP follows the same clockwise sequence as PQRS, just starting from a different vertex.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'PRQS\' not a valid name for the quadrilateral shown in the figure?\",\"options\":[\"It does not start with P\",\"It uses lowercase letters\",\"It does not follow a clockwise or counter-clockwise order\",\"It only uses four letters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Naming must follow the vertices in order around the perimeter (clockwise or counter-clockwise); PRQS skips across the figure.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"To ensure a figure is a quadrilateral, the four points chosen on a paper must be:\",\"options\":[\"Collinear\",\"Non-collinear\",\"In a straight line\",\"At the same point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text begins by instructing to \'Take four non-collinear points\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reading and writing of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A quadrilateral and its interior together form the ____.\",\"options\":[\"Exterior of the quadrilateral\",\"Region of the quadrilateral\",\"Boundary of the quadrilateral\",\"Vertex of the quadrilateral\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a quadrilateral and its interior together form the region of the quadrilateral.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many pairs of opposite sides does a quadrilateral have?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, there are two pairs of opposite sides in a quadrilateral.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Sides of a quadrilateral that do not have a common vertex are called ____.\",\"options\":[\"Adjacent sides\",\"Parallel sides\",\"Opposite sides\",\"Diagonals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines opposite sides as sides that do not have a common vertex.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If two sides of a quadrilateral have a common vertex, they are called ____.\",\"options\":[\"Opposite sides\",\"Adjacent sides\",\"Diagonals\",\"Interior sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines adjacent sides as two sides of a quadrilateral that have a common vertex.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In quadrilateral ABCD, which side is opposite to side AD?\",\"options\":[\"Side AB\",\"Side BC\",\"Side CD\",\"Side AC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the opposite side of side AD is side BC.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In quadrilateral ABCD, side AB and side BC share the common vertex B. What kind of sides are they?\",\"options\":[\"Opposite sides\",\"Adjacent sides\",\"Diagonals\",\"Exterior sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since they share a common vertex B, they are adjacent sides as per the definition.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is common between two adjacent angles of a quadrilateral?\",\"options\":[\"A common vertex\",\"A common side\",\"No common side\",\"A common diagonal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if two angles have one common side, they are adjacent angles.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many pairs of adjacent angles are there in a quadrilateral like ABCD?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there are four pairs of adjacent angles in ABCD.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If two angles of a quadrilateral have no common side, they are called ____.\",\"options\":[\"Adjacent angles\",\"Opposite angles\",\"Interior angles\",\"Exterior angles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines opposite angles as two angles that have no common side.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In quadrilateral ABCD, angle ABC and angle ADC do not have a common side. Therefore, they are ____.\",\"options\":[\"Adjacent angles\",\"Opposite angles\",\"Exterior angles\",\"Supplementary angles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Angles with no common side are opposite angles according to the text.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The line segment joining the vertices of the opposite angles of a quadrilateral is called a ____.\",\"options\":[\"Side\",\"Vertex\",\"Diagonal\",\"Region\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a diagonal as the line segment joining the vertices of opposite angles.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these is a diagonal of quadrilateral PQRS?\",\"options\":[\"Side PQ\",\"Side QR\",\"Segment PR\",\"Side RS\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the figure, segment PR joins the opposite vertices P and R, making it a diagonal.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/077_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which pair of vertices are joined to form the diagonal that passes horizontally through the center?\",\"options\":[\"P and R\",\"P and Q\",\"S and R\",\"S and Q\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows diagonal SQ joining the opposite vertices S and Q.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/077_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, where are points L, E, M, N, and T located in relation to the quadrilateral?\",\"options\":[\"Inside the quadrilateral\",\"Outside the quadrilateral\",\"On the quadrilateral\",\"In the exterior region\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists Point L, E, M, N, T as \'Points on the quadrilateral\'.\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many diagonals can be drawn in a quadrilateral?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states there are two diagonals in a quadrilateral (e.g., PR and SQ in PQRS).\",\"topic\":\"Elements of Quadrilaterals\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many vertices does a triangle have according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a triangle has three sides, three vertices, and three angles.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for the sides of a triangle mentioned in the \'At a glance\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Edges\",\"Lines\",\"Arms\",\"Borders\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions \'three sides (arms)\' for a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many diagonals does a quadrilateral have?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a quadrilateral has four vertices, four sides, four angles, and two diagonals.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a type of triangle classified based on its sides?\",\"options\":[\"Right angled triangle\",\"Acute angled triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\",\"Isosceles triangle\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists equilateral, isosceles, and scalene triangles as types based on sides.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many pairs of adjacent sides does a quadrilateral have?\",\"options\":[\"Two pairs\",\"Three pairs\",\"Four pairs\",\"Six pairs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a quadrilateral has four pairs of adjacent sides.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A quadrilateral has how many pairs of opposite angles?\",\"options\":[\"One pair\",\"Two pairs\",\"Three pairs\",\"Four pairs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that a quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite angles.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is asked to identify a shape with 4 vertices and 2 diagonals, which shape should they choose?\",\"options\":[\"Triangle\",\"Quadrilateral\",\"Scalene triangle\",\"Equilateral triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a quadrilateral as having four vertices and two diagonals.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A shape is described as having three arms and three angles. Based on the text, what is this shape?\",\"options\":[\"Quadrilateral\",\"Square\",\"Triangle\",\"Diagonal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a triangle as having three sides (arms) and three angles.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a classification of a triangle based on its angles?\",\"options\":[\"Acute angled triangle\",\"Right angled triangle\",\"Scalene triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Scalene is a classification based on sides, whereas the others are based on angles.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly distinguishes the pairs found in a quadrilateral?\",\"options\":[\"It has 4 pairs of opposite sides and 2 pairs of adjacent sides.\",\"It has 2 pairs of opposite sides and 4 pairs of adjacent sides.\",\"It has 4 pairs of opposite angles and 2 pairs of adjacent angles.\",\"It has 2 pairs of opposite sides and 2 pairs of adjacent sides.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states a quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides and four pairs of adjacent sides.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21826,34,6,'Maths','en','Equations','[\"- An **expression** is a combination of numbers and operations such as +, −, ×, ÷, and parentheses.\",\"- The **order of operations** (PEMDAS) tells us which part of an expression to solve first:\",\"1. Parentheses (brackets)\",\"2. Multiplication and Division (left to right)\",\"3. Addition and Subtraction (left to right)\",\"- A **single‑variable equation** has one unknown symbol (a variable) and the same expression on both sides of the equal sign.\",\"- The **solution of an equation** is the value of the variable that makes the two sides equal.\",\"- Adding, subtracting, multiplying or dividing the same number on both sides keeps the equation balanced.\",\"- A **variable** can be any letter a, b, c … z. It represents an unknown number.\",\"- When a number is multiplied by a variable, the number is written before the variable and the × sign is omitted: 5a means 5 × a.\",\"- The **properties of equality** (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) let us change both sides of an equation in the same way.\",\"- To solve an equation, isolate the variable on one side using the inverse operation.\",\"- Example: 3x = 12 → x = 4 (divide both sides by 3).\",\"- An **equality** is a statement that two expressions have the same value, e.g. 6×5 = 30 and 30+15 = 45.\",\"- When two expressions are equal, we can write them with the = sign: 2×7 = 20−6.\",\"- In a word problem, first translate the situation into an equation, then solve for the unknown.\",\"- Always check the answer by substituting it back into the original equation.\",\"- Practice: write expressions for “a number plus 9”, “a number divided by 8”, “four times a number”, etc.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is an arrangement of mathematical operations involving two or more numbers called?\",\"options\":[\"Equation\",\"Expression\",\"Operation\",\"Brackets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an arrangement of mathematical operations involving two or more numbers as an \'expression\'.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the rules for evaluating expressions, which operation should be performed first?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Multiplication\",\"Operations in brackets\",\"Subtraction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rule 1 states that the operation given in brackets (parentheses) should be performed first.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the order of symbols for evaluating expressions, what does the letter \'A\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Arrangement\",\"Actions\",\"Arithmetic\",\"Addition\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table shows that \'A\' stands for Addition in the order of symbols.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct value of the expression 8 + 4 × 2 – 6?\",\"options\":[\"18\",\"10\",\"12\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following the rules: 8 + (4 × 2) - 6 = 8 + 8 - 6 = 16 - 6 = 10.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an expression contains both multiplication and division, in what order should they be performed?\",\"options\":[\"Multiplication always comes before division\",\"Division always comes before multiplication\",\"In the order they occur from left to right\",\"In the order they occur from right to left\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rule 2 states multiplication and division should be performed in the order in which they occur from left to right.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of brackets are referred to as \'{}\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Round brackets\",\"Square brackets\",\"Curly braces\",\"Simple brackets\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'{}\' as curly braces.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression [18 + 5(7-3)] + 64, which part should be solved first?\",\"options\":[\"18 + 5\",\"5 + 64\",\"(7-3)\",\"18 + 64\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rule 5 states that if more than one type of bracket is used, the actions in the innermost brackets should be performed first.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of the expression 81 ÷ (5 + 4) - 2?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"11\",\"9\",\"5\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"81 ÷ (9) - 2 = 9 - 2 = 7.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Rohan and Gracie get different answers for the same expression?\",\"options\":[\"They used different numbers\",\"One of them did not follow the rules for performing operations\",\"The expression had two correct answers\",\"They used different types of pencils\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that different answers occur because rules for performing mathematical operations must be followed.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what operation is represented by the symbol \'÷\' under the letter \'D\'?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Multiplication\",\"Division\",\"Subtraction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows \'D\' stands for Division with the symbol \'÷\'.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/080_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, which operation is performed last in the sequence?\",\"options\":[\"Brackets\",\"Multiplication\",\"Addition\",\"Subtraction\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table shows \'S\' for Subtraction as the final step in the order of symbols.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/080_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which expression correctly represents: \'24 flowers in a basket, plus 2 people adding 5 flowers each\'?\",\"options\":[\"24 + 5 + 2\",\"24 + 2 × 5\",\"(24 + 2) × 5\",\"24 × 2 + 5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustrative example 3 shows the expression for this scenario is 24 + 2 × 5.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If there is more than one operation inside a bracket, which rules should be followed?\",\"options\":[\"Only addition and subtraction rules\",\"The same order of operation rules used for general expressions\",\"Always do multiplication first regardless of position\",\"Perform operations from right to left\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rule 4 states if there is more than one operation in brackets, follow the standard rules and do them first.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final step in solving the expression [18 + 20] + 64 as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"18 + 20\",\"38 + 64\",\"5 × 4\",\"7 - 3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The example shows the final step is adding the result of the square bracket (38) to 64.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Between Rohan (who got 18) and Gracie (who got 10) for the expression 8 + 4 × 2 – 6, whose answer was correct?\",\"options\":[\"Rohan\",\"Gracie\",\"Both were correct\",\"Neither was correct\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that Gracie\'s answer (10) is correct because she followed the order of operations.\",\"topic\":\"Expressions\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what mathematical concept is represented by the balanced scale?\",\"options\":[\"Inequality\",\"Equality\",\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a balanced scale with the word \'Equality\' written above it.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the horizontal position of the two pans indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The left side is heavier\",\"The right side is heavier\",\"Both sides have the same value\",\"The values are not related\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A balanced scale represents equality, meaning both sides have the same value.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which numbers did Sharvari use to make expressions that have a value of 21?\",\"options\":[\"Numbers from 1 to 10\",\"Numbers from 5 to 9\",\"Numbers from 1 to 4\",\"Any prime numbers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Sharvari made expressions by using numbers from 1 to 4.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression 80 × (15 + 8 - 3) + 5, which operation should be performed first according to the steps shown?\",\"options\":[\"80 × 15\",\"15 + 8\",\"8 - 3\",\"3 + 5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following the order of operations, the innermost parentheses (15 + 8) are solved first.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the value of the expression 28 + 12 - 25?\",\"options\":[\"15\",\"40\",\"25\",\"65\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"28 + 12 = 40; 40 - 25 = 15.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the simplification of 65 ÷ 5 × 2 - 20, what is the result of the first step (65 ÷ 5)?\",\"options\":[\"15\",\"13\",\"12\",\"11\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"65 divided by 5 equals 13.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a correct verbal example for the expression (5 + 4) - 8?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract 8 from the sum of 5 and 4\",\"Add 8 to the sum of 5 and 4\",\"Multiply 5 and 4 then subtract 8\",\"Divide 5 by 4 and subtract 8\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"(5 + 4) is the sum, and - 8 means subtracting 8 from that sum.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final value of the expression 8 × 4 - 3 × 6?\",\"options\":[\"14\",\"50\",\"18\",\"32\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"8 × 4 = 32 and 3 × 6 = 18; 32 - 18 = 14.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression 210 ÷ [125 - {5 + 3 (14 - 9)}], which calculation is performed inside the curly brackets first?\",\"options\":[\"5 + 3\",\"125 - 5\",\"3 × (14 - 9)\",\"210 ÷ 125\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The multiplication 3 × (14-9) must be done before adding 5 inside the curly bracket.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following expressions was created by Sharvari using numbers 1 to 4 to equal 21?\",\"options\":[\"4 (3 + 2) + 1\",\"4 × 3 + 2 + 1\",\"1 + 2 + 3 + 4\",\"4 × 4 + 4 + 1\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists 4 (3 + 2) + 1 as one of Sharvari\'s expressions.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which verbal example best describes the expression (6 × 12) ÷ 4?\",\"options\":[\"The product of 6 and 12 divided by 4\",\"Add 6 and 12 then divide by 4\",\"Multiply 6 by the quotient of 12 and 4\",\"Subtract 4 from the product of 6 and 12\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"6 × 12 is a product, which is then divided by 4.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Solve the expression: 112 - 18 × 2 ÷ 9 + 2.\",\"options\":[\"110\",\"100\",\"112\",\"22\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"18 × 2 = 36; 36 ÷ 9 = 4; 112 - 4 + 2 = 108 + 2 = 110.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.1\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What symbol is used to indicate that there is no equality between two expressions?\",\"options\":[\"=\",\"+\",\"≠\",\"÷\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the ≠ symbol is used to indicate that there is no equality.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In an equality, what is true about the values on the left and right sides of the \'=\' sign?\",\"options\":[\"The left side is always greater\",\"The values are equal\",\"The right side is always greater\",\"The values are always zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the values in the left and right sides of the \'=\' sign are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following arrangements is an example of equality mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"30 ÷ 15 = 18 - 8\",\"6 × 5 = 14 + 16\",\"3 + 4 = 14 ÷ 7\",\"2 × 7 = 20 - 4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly shows that 6 × 5 = 30 and 14 + 16 = 30, so they are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the values of two expressions like 3+4 and 14 ÷ 2 are equal, the arrangement is called:\",\"options\":[\"Subtraction\",\"Multiplication\",\"Equality\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the arrangement of two equal expressions as equality.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to an equality if you add the same number to both sides?\",\"options\":[\"The left side becomes greater\",\"The right side becomes greater\",\"It results in equality\",\"The equality is broken\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The property of addition states that adding the same number to both sides results in equality.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If (6+4) = (5×2), what is the result of (6+4) + 5 according to the Addition property table?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"15\",\"20\",\"25\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table shows (6+4)+5 = 10 + 5 = 15.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the Subtraction property, if we subtract 2 from both sides of the equality (6+4) = (5×2), what is the final value?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"6\",\"8\",\"12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows (6+4)-2 = 10 - 2 = 8.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Multiplication property, if (6+4) × 3 = 30, then (5×2) × 3 must be:\",\"options\":[\"15\",\"20\",\"30\",\"60\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Multiplying both sides by the same number results in equality, so both sides will equal 30.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the arrangement 30 ÷ 15 ≠ 18 - 8 used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To show that the expressions are equal\",\"To show that the first number is 30\",\"To show that these expressions are not equal\",\"To show the property of division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses this example to show that 2 is not equal to 10, hence they are not equal.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the Division property example, if (6+4) ÷ 2 = 5, what must be the value of (5×2) ÷ 2 to maintain equality?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dividing both sides by the same number results in equality; since the left side is 5, the right side must be 5.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which property is being demonstrated when the text says \'Subtracting the same number from both sides results in equality\'?\",\"options\":[\"Property of Addition\",\"Property of Subtraction\",\"Property of Multiplication\",\"Property of Division\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This is the definition of the property of subtraction given in the table.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the examples 2 × 7 = 14 and 20 - 6 = 14, what conclusion does the text draw?\",\"options\":[\"2 × 7 ≠ 20 - 6\",\"2 × 7 = 20 - 6\",\"14 is a prime number\",\"20 - 6 is greater than 2 × 7\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since both expressions have the value 14, the text concludes 2 × 7 = 20 - 6.\",\"topic\":\"Equality and its Properties\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What symbol is used to show that the values on the left and right sides of an expression are the same?\",\"options\":[\"+\",\"-\",\"=\",\"≠\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the \'=\' symbol to indicate that the values on both sides are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example \'18÷3 [ ] 9×3\', what is the value of the expression on the left side?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"6\",\"9\",\"27\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"18 divided by 3 equals 6.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol correctly fills the box in the expression \'5+9 [ ] 2×7\'?\",\"options\":[\"=\",\"≠\",\"+\",\"×\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since 5+9 = 14 and 2×7 = 14, the values are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the expressions \'6÷2+4\' and \'13-6\'. Which statement is correct based on their values?\",\"options\":[\"The left side is greater than the right side.\",\"The right side is greater than the left side.\",\"The values on both sides are equal.\",\"The values on both sides are not equal.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"6÷2+4 = 3+4 = 7, and 13-6 = 7. Both sides equal 7.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the symbol \'≠\' as used in the textbook exercises?\",\"options\":[\"The values are equal\",\"The values are not equal\",\"The values must be added\",\"The values must be multiplied\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The symbol \'≠\' is used when the values on the left and right sides are different.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To make the values on the left and right sides equal in \'16+4 = [ ] + 8\', what number must be written in the blank space?\",\"options\":[\"20\",\"12\",\"8\",\"28\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"16+4 = 20. To make the right side 20, we need 12+8.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation \'26 ÷ [ ] = 5 + 8\', what is the calculated value of the right side?\",\"options\":[\"13\",\"26\",\"2\",\"40\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The right side is 5 + 8, which equals 13.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Find the correct number for the blank space in the equation \'38 - [ ] = 21 ÷ 3\'.\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"31\",\"45\",\"38\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"21 ÷ 3 = 7. Therefore, 38 - 31 = 7.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, \'13+7 = 4 × 5\' is provided as an example of what?\",\"options\":[\"An inequality\",\"An example of equality\",\"A division operation\",\"A subtraction expression\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically lists this under the heading \'Examples of Equality\'.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal when filling the blank space in the equation \'26 ÷ [ ] = 5 + 8\'?\",\"options\":[\"To make the values on the left and right sides equal\",\"To make the left side larger than the right side\",\"To make the right side equal to zero\",\"To change the symbols for operations\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The instructions state to write the number so that the values on the left and right sides are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.2\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the letter used to represent an unknown number called?\",\"options\":[\"Constant\",\"Variable\",\"Symbol\",\"Formula\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a letter used for an unknown number is called a \'variable\'.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is \'5 times a number\' written mathematically when using a variable \'a\'?\",\"options\":[\"a + 5\",\"a - 5\",\"5a\",\"a\\/5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, 5 times a number is written as a × 5 = 5a.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the primary benefit of using symbols in mathematics?\",\"options\":[\"It makes writing manageable and easy to understand\",\"It makes the numbers look larger\",\"It helps in drawing circles\",\"It replaces the need for addition\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the use of symbols makes writing manageable and easy to understand.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is the correct way to write \'a number is four less than a number\' using the variable \'a\'?\",\"options\":[\"4 - a\",\"a + 4\",\"a - 4\",\"4a\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly gives the example: \'The number that is four less than a number = a - 4\'.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If \'a\' is a variable, how is the expression \'a number subtracted from 15\' written?\",\"options\":[\"a - 15\",\"15 - a\",\"15 + a\",\"15a\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'If a number is subtracted from 15, the difference = 15 - a\'.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for the diameter of a circle \'d = 2 x r\', what does the letter \'r\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Diameter\",\"Centre\",\"Radius\",\"Line segment\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the radius and diameter are denoted by the letters r and d respectively.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When multiplying a letter and a number, where is the number usually written?\",\"options\":[\"After the letter\",\"Before the letter\",\"Above the letter\",\"Below the letter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the number is written before the letter and the multiplication sign is not written.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which rule states that the sum of two numbers remains the same even if their order is reversed?\",\"options\":[\"a + 0 = a\",\"a x 1 = a\",\"a + b = b + a\",\"a x 0 = 0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'a + b = b + a\' as the rule where the sum is the same regardless of order.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the variable \'x\', how would you write \'the sum of a number and 3\'?\",\"options\":[\"x - 3\",\"3x\",\"x + 3\",\"x \\/ 3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern in the text (a + 3), the sum of a number and 3 is x + 3.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of multiplying any number \'a\' by 0?\",\"options\":[\"a\",\"1\",\"0\",\"2a\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the rule \'a x 0 = 0\', meaning the multiplication of any number and 0 is zero.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the result of dividing both sides of the equality by the same number?\",\"options\":[\"The equality changes\",\"It results in equality\",\"The left side becomes larger\",\"The right side becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The property in the figure states: \'Dividing both sides by the same number results in equality.\'\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the missing value for the example \'5 + 0\'?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"1\",\"5\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the rule a + 0 = a, so 5 + 0 must be 5.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/084_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a circle has a radius \'r\', how is the diameter \'d\' calculated according to the formula in the text?\",\"options\":[\"d = r + 2\",\"d = r - 2\",\"d = 2 x r\",\"d = r \\/ 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula: Diameter = 2 Radius, which is d = 2 x r.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which letters can be used as a variable for an unknown number?\",\"options\":[\"Only a, b, and c\",\"Only x, y, and z\",\"Any of the letters a, b, c, ... z\",\"Only capital letters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'For an unknown number, any of the letters a, b, c,...z can be used.\'\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rules for multiplication, what is the value of \'a x 1\'?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"0\",\"a\",\"a + 1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the rule \'a x 1 = a\'.\",\"topic\":\"Arithmetic Expression\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How is \'The sum of a number and 9\' written using the letter \'x\'?\",\"options\":[\"x + 9\",\"x - 9\",\"9x\",\"x \\/ 9\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The word \'sum\' indicates the addition of a number and 9.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If \'y\' is a number, how do we write \'A number divided by 8\'?\",\"options\":[\"8 \\/ y\",\"y - 8\",\"y \\/ 8\",\"8y\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The expression represents a number (y) being divided by the divisor 8.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these represents \'Multiplication of a number and 0\'?\",\"options\":[\"n + 0\",\"n - 0\",\"n \\/ 0\",\"n × 0\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Multiplication of a variable and 0 is written as the variable times 0.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the statement \'12 is subtracted from a number\' written as an expression?\",\"options\":[\"12 - x\",\"x - 12\",\"x + 12\",\"12x\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Subtracted from\' means the number 12 is taken away from the variable.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the mathematical expression for \'4 times a number\'?\",\"options\":[\"4 + m\",\"m \\/ 4\",\"4m\",\"m - 4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Times\' indicates multiplication between the coefficient 4 and the variable.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the letters P, S, and C, what is the correct formula for Profit?\",\"options\":[\"P = C - S\",\"P = S + C\",\"P = S - C\",\"P = S \\/ C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As per the text, Profit equals Selling price (S) minus Cost price (C).\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Using the letters L, C, and S, how is the rule for Loss written?\",\"options\":[\"L = S - C\",\"L = C - S\",\"L = C + S\",\"L = S \\/ C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As per the text, Loss equals Cost Price (C) minus Selling Price (S).\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a number is represented by \'a\', how do you write \'Multiply a number by 1\'?\",\"options\":[\"a + 1\",\"a - 1\",\"a × 1\",\"a \\/ 1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The expression shows the multiplication of the variable \'a\' and the number 1.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula L = C - S, what does the letter \'C\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Calculation\",\"Cost price\",\"Common price\",\"Center\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the context of Profit and Loss formulas, C represents Cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which expression correctly represents \'12 is subtracted from a number\' if the number is \'k\'?\",\"options\":[\"k - 12\",\"12 - k\",\"12 \\/ k\",\"k + 12\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"To subtract 12 from k, we write k - 12.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 9.3\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is a mathematical arrangement like \'x + 5 = 12\' called?\",\"options\":[\"Expression\",\"Equation\",\"Variable\",\"Operation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a mathematical arrangement like x+5=12 is an \'Equation\'.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation x + 5 = 12, what does the letter \'x\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"A constant\",\"A sum\",\"A variable\",\"A stick\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that \'x\' is a variable.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what defines a \'single variable equation\'?\",\"options\":[\"An equation with only one number\",\"An equation with two different variables\",\"An arrangement where only one variable is used in the expressions\",\"An arrangement with no variables\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A single variable equation is defined as having only one variable used in the expressions on either or both sides.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a single variable equation provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"x + y = 12\",\"9 = 3p\",\"2 + 3 = 5\",\"m + n = 7\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"9=3p is one of the specific examples of a single variable equation listed in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Pradeep has \'x\' apples and Sachin gives him 5 more to make a total of 12, how many apples did Pradeep have initially?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"17\",\"7\",\"12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If x + 5 = 12, then x must be 7 (12 - 5 = 7).\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table, what is the rule to find the number of sticks?\",\"options\":[\"(3 × number of triangles) + 1\",\"(2 × number of triangles) + 1\",\"(2 × number of triangles) - 1\",\"number of triangles + 2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table header defines the rule as (2 x number of triangles) + 1.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Using the rule (2 × number of triangles) + 1, how many sticks are needed for 3 triangles?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"7\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"(2 × 3) + 1 = 6 + 1 = 7.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If \'n\' represents the number of triangles, how would you write the expression for the number of sticks?\",\"options\":[\"n + 1\",\"2n\",\"2n + 1\",\"n + 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern (2 × number of triangles) + 1, for \'n\' triangles it becomes 2n + 1.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation \'2x + 5 = x + 7\', how many different variables are used?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"None\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Although \'x\' appears on both sides, it is the same single variable.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many sticks are required to make 5 triangles according to the pattern in the table?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"11\",\"12\",\"13\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Applying the rule: (2 × 5) + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you used 9 sticks to make triangles following the table\'s rule, how many triangles did you make?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"5\",\"2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If (2 × n) + 1 = 9, then 2n = 8, so n = 4.\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these mathematical arrangements is NOT a single variable equation based on the definition provided?\",\"options\":[\"a - 6 = 8\",\"m \\/ 5 = 7\",\"x + y = 10\",\"2x + 5 = x + 7\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definition states a single variable equation uses only one variable; \'x + y = 10\' uses two (x and y).\",\"topic\":\"Equations in One Variable\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is a value of the variable that balances or satisfies a given equation called?\",\"options\":[\"A factor of the equation\",\"A solution of the equation\",\"A balance of the equation\",\"A variable of the equation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the value of the variable that balances or satisfies the equation as the \'solution of the equation\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Under what condition is an equation considered balanced?\",\"options\":[\"Only if the left side is greater than the right side\",\"Only if the right side is greater than the left side\",\"Only if the values on both sides of the equation are the same\",\"Only if the variable is equal to zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states an equation is balanced only if the values on both sides of the equation are the same.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation y + 6 = 7, what is the value of the right hand side?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"y\",\"0\",\"7\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the right hand side of the equation y + 6 = 7 as 7.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we take y = 0 in the equation y + 6 = 7, what is the value of the left side?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"6\",\"7\",\"1\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, if y = 0, the left side = y + 6 = 0 + 6 = 6.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is y = 0 not a solution for the equation y + 6 = 7?\",\"options\":[\"Because the left side is equal to the right side\",\"Because the left side is not equal to the right side\",\"Because zero cannot be a solution\",\"Because the right side is 6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states y = 0 is not a solution because the left side (6) does not equal the right side (7).\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To solve the equation a + 7 = 12, what operation is performed on both sides?\",\"options\":[\"Add 7 to both sides\",\"Subtract 12 from both sides\",\"Subtract 7 from both sides\",\"Multiply both sides by 7\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The illustrative example shows subtracting 7 from both sides to solve a + 7 = 12.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation a + 7 = 12, what is the final value of \'a\' after solving?\",\"options\":[\"19\",\"7\",\"12\",\"5\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text shows the calculation a = 12 - 7, which results in a = 5.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Ishwari thought of a number \'x\' and tripled it, how is this expressed mathematically in the text?\",\"options\":[\"x + 3\",\"3x\",\"x \\/ 3\",\"x - 3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the number is x, then three times that number is 3x.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To solve the equation 3x = 75, what step is taken to isolate x?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract 3 from both sides\",\"Divide both sides by 3\",\"Multiply both sides by 3\",\"Divide both sides by 75\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the step \'Divide both sides by 3\' to solve 3x = 75.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the solution for the number Ishwari thought of in the equation 3x = 75?\",\"options\":[\"25\",\"75\",\"3\",\"15\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that x = 25, so the number Ishwari had in mind is 25.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A scale remains balanced only if the weight on the left and right sides is equal. What does the text compare this to?\",\"options\":[\"A variable\",\"A number in the mind\",\"An equation\",\"A subtraction problem\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the balanced scale as an analogy for an equation being balanced.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the trial method for y + 6 = 7, which value of y makes the left side equal to the right side?\",\"options\":[\"y = 0\",\"y = 2\",\"y = 1\",\"y = 3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text demonstrates that for y = 1, the left side (1+6) equals the right side (7).\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Equation\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21827,34,6,'Maths','en','Magical Mathematics - 1','[\"- A smart card trick uses cards numbered 1 to 31 to guess a chosen number.\",\"- A five‑pointed star contains a regular pentagon inside it.\",\"- Each interior angle of a regular pentagon is 108° (since 180°×(5−2)\\/5).\",\"- The star’s outer angles can be found by subtracting the pentagon’s interior angles from 180°.\",\"- In a regular polygon with n sides, each interior angle is 180°×(n−2)\\/n.\",\"- The sum of all interior angles of a triangle is always 180°.\",\"- A pentomino is a shape made by joining five equal squares edge‑to‑edge.\",\"- There are 12 distinct pentomino shapes, each named after a letter that resembles its shape.\",\"- A pentomino can tile a rectangle without gaps or overlaps if the rectangle’s area is a multiple of 5.\",\"- The area of a 5×12 rectangle is 60, which can be filled by twelve 5‑square pentominoes.\",\"- A 4×15 rectangle (area 60) and a 3×20 rectangle (area 60) can also be tiled by pentominoes.\",\"- The same area (60) allows many different tilings, showing a correlation between rectangle dimensions and pentomino placement.\",\"- Addition‑subtraction grids are tables where the sum of the numbers in each row or column equals a given total.\",\"- Such grids can be made for any pair of numbers that add to the same total, not just fractions.\"]','[{\"q\":\"How many numbers are written on each individual card in this game?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"16\",\"31\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Each card has 16 numbers written on it\'.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the range of numbers a friend can think of for this game?\",\"options\":[\"1 to 16\",\"1 to 100\",\"1 to 31\",\"1 to 15\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the numbers are \'from 1 to 31\'.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where can you find the trick to identifying the number your friend thought of?\",\"options\":[\"On the back of the card\",\"In the teacher\'s handbook\",\"Elsewhere in the book\",\"In the next chapter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'The trick to identifying the number... is given elsewhere in the book\'.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step the friend needs to take in this game?\",\"options\":[\"Write down a number\",\"Think of a birth date\",\"Add the numbers on the card\",\"Find the trick\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Ask your friend to think of his birth date\'.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What must the friend observe carefully after thinking of a number?\",\"options\":[\"The colors of the cards\",\"The total number of cards\",\"Which cards contain the number he thought of\",\"The order of the numbers on the first card\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Ask your friend to observe carefully which cards contain the number he thought of\'.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these numbers is present on the card?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"8\",\"15\",\"20\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Looking at the card in the figure, 15 is listed in the second row.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/089_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a friend\'s birth date is 5, which card sets from the text contain this number?\",\"options\":[\"Only the first set\",\"The first and third sets\",\"Only the second set\",\"The fifth set\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Checking the text, 5 appears in the first set (1, 3, 5...) and the third set (4, 5, 6...).\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, what is the common property of all the numbers shown on this specific card?\",\"options\":[\"They are all even numbers\",\"They are all odd numbers\",\"They are all multiples of 5\",\"They are all greater than 10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"All numbers on the card in the figure (1, 3, 5, 7, etc.) are odd numbers.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/089_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"By looking at all the sets of numbers in the text, which number appears in every single set?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"15\",\"16\",\"31\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The number 31 is the last number listed in every one of the five sets provided in the text.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a friend says their number is on the card shown in the figure and also on the card starting with 16, which number could it be?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"15\",\"17\",\"2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"17 is an odd number (found in the figure) and is also in the range 16-31 (found in the last set).\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the topic that describes this game?\",\"options\":[\"Number Magic\",\"Smart Cards\",\"Birth Date Trick\",\"Card Game\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The topic heading is \'1) Smart Cards\'.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, if a friend thinks of a number and it is not on any of the cards, what can we conclude?\",\"options\":[\"The friend is cheating\",\"The number is not between 1 and 31\",\"The cards are missing numbers\",\"The trick will not work\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The game only covers numbers \'from 1 to 31\', so a number not on any card must be outside this range.\",\"topic\":\"1) Smart Cards\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Instruction 1, what is the sum of the measures of two angles that form a straight line?\",\"options\":[\"90°\",\"180°\",\"270°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Instruction 1 explicitly states that the sum of two angles forming a straight line is 180°.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the sum of the measures of all the angles of a triangle as per Instruction 3?\",\"options\":[\"90°\",\"120°\",\"180°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Instruction 3 states that the sum of the measures of all the angles of a triangle is 180°.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a characteristic of a regular pentagon according to Instruction 2?\",\"options\":[\"Only two sides are equal\",\"All sides and angles are equal in measure\",\"All angles are different\",\"It has six sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Instruction 2 states that all sides and angles of a regular pentagon are equal in measure.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What geometric figure is formed inside a five-pointed star according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A regular hexagon\",\"A triangle\",\"A regular pentagon\",\"A square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a regular pentagon is formed inside a five-pointed star.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If one angle of a regular pentagon is known, what can be said about the other four angles?\",\"options\":[\"They are all different\",\"They are all equal to the known angle\",\"They add up to 180°\",\"They are half of the known angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Instruction 2 states all angles of a regular pentagon are equal, so they must all match the known angle.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main objective when given the measure of one angle in the star?\",\"options\":[\"To draw a new star\",\"To find and state the measures of all the angles in the star\",\"To calculate the length of the sides\",\"To change the star into a hexagon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the goal is to \'find and state the measures of all the angles in the star\' from one given angle.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a star has more than six points, what shape is formed inside it?\",\"options\":[\"A triangle\",\"A straight line\",\"A polygon\",\"A circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the figure formed inside a star with more than six points as a \'polygon\'.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If one angle on a straight line is 108°, what is the measure of the other angle forming the line?\",\"options\":[\"72°\",\"82°\",\"108°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using Instruction 1 (180° - 108° = 72°).\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a triangle, if two angles are 30° and 70°, what is the measure of the third angle based on Instruction 3?\",\"options\":[\"100°\",\"180°\",\"80°\",\"50°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The sum must be 180°. 180 - (30 + 70) = 80°.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A pentagon can be divided into 3 triangles. Based on Instruction 3, what is the sum of the measures of all angles of a pentagon?\",\"options\":[\"180°\",\"360°\",\"540°\",\"720°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If one triangle is 180°, three triangles making a pentagon equal 3 × 180° = 540°.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When moving from a five-pointed star to a six-pointed star, what is the most likely change regarding the internal figure?\",\"options\":[\"The internal figure will disappear\",\"The number of sides of the internal polygon will increase\",\"The sum of the angles will remain 180°\",\"The internal figure will become a triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies a relationship between the number of points and the internal polygon; more points result in a polygon with more sides.\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which set of instructions is necessary to find the measure of the star\'s points if the internal pentagon\'s angles are known?\",\"options\":[\"Only Instruction 2\",\"Only Instruction 3\",\"Instructions 1 and 3\",\"Instruction 1 only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To find the star point angles, one needs to use the linear pair (Instruction 1) and the triangle sum property (Instruction 3).\",\"topic\":\"2) Star\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"A pentomino is a shape formed by connecting how many squares of the same size?\",\"options\":[\"3 squares\",\"4 squares\",\"5 squares\",\"6 squares\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a pentomino as a shape formed by connecting 5 squares of the same size.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how must the squares be connected to form a pentomino?\",\"options\":[\"Corner-to-corner\",\"Side-by-side\",\"Overlapping each other\",\"Separated by gaps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition states the squares must be connected side-by-side.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is an English letter assigned to each pentomino shape?\",\"options\":[\"To show the order they were discovered\",\"To help you remember it according to its shape\",\"To indicate the color of the squares\",\"To show the size of the squares\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that English letters are given according to the shape to help you remember the pentomino.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which English letter represents the pentomino that is shaped like a plus sign?\",\"options\":[\"P\",\"X\",\"T\",\"U\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the first diagram, the shape labeled \'X\' is formed like a plus sign.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/090_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Tessellation\' mean when completing a rectangle with pentominoes?\",\"options\":[\"Squares are of different sizes\",\"Shapes are placed with gaps\",\"Shapes are overlapping\",\"No overlap and no gaps\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines Tessellation as \'No Overlap, No Gaps\'.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many small squares in total make up the completed 6x10 rectangle?\",\"options\":[\"16 squares\",\"50 squares\",\"60 squares\",\"70 squares\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A 6x10 rectangle has an area of 60 squares (6 multiplied by 10).\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/090_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the dimensions of the empty grid provided for completion?\",\"options\":[\"6 x 10\",\"4 x 15\",\"3 x 20\",\"5 x 12\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The label next to the empty grid in the third figure clearly states \'5x12\'.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/091_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you are asked to complete a 4 x 15 grid using pentominoes, how many pentominoes will you need to fit perfectly?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"12\",\"15\",\"60\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A 4x15 grid has 60 squares. Since each pentomino has 5 squares, 60 divided by 5 equals 12 pentominoes.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the similarity between the dimensions 6x10, 5x12, 4x15, and 3x20 mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They all have the same perimeter\",\"They all have the same total area of 60 squares\",\"They all use a different number of pentominoes\",\"They are all square shapes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"All these pairs of dimensions multiply to 60, which is the total area covered by the 12 pentominoes.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of paper is suggested to be used for cutting out pentominoes?\",\"options\":[\"Plain white paper\",\"Circular paper\",\"Square or rectangular grid paper\",\"Triangular grid paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Take a square or rectangular grid paper\' to cut out pentominoes.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the shapes shown, how many possible ways to form a pentomino are provided in the lesson?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"10\",\"12\",\"60\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first figure shows 12 distinct pentomino shapes labeled with letters.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When placing pentominoes in a grid, what should you understand the correlation between?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the paper and the shape\",\"The area of the rectangle and the pentominoes placed in it\",\"The English alphabet and the grid size\",\"The thickness of the paper and the squares\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to \'Note the area of each rectangle and the pentominoes placed in it\' and \'Understand the correlation between these two\'.\",\"topic\":\"3. Pentominoes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21828,34,6,'Maths','en','Decimal Fractions','[\"Decimal fraction is a fraction whose denominator is 10, 100, 1000, …\",\"The decimal point separates the integer part from the fractional part\",\"Digits to the right of the decimal point represent tenths, hundredths, thousandths…\",\"One tenth = 1\\/10, one hundredth = 1\\/100, one thousandth = 1\\/1000\",\"To write a decimal fraction, put a decimal symbol (.) after the units place\",\"If a common fraction has denominator 10, 100, 1000… it can be written directly as a decimal\",\"If the denominator is not a power of 10, multiply numerator and denominator to make it so\",\"To convert a decimal to a common fraction, count the digits after the point, write the number without the point over 10^n, and reduce\",\"When adding or subtracting decimals, align the decimal points vertically\",\"Compare decimals first by integer part, then by tenths, hundredths, etc.\",\"The place value of a digit is the digit times its positional value (e.g., 6 in 54.632 is 6×0.1 = 0.6)\",\"Half = 0.5, quarter = 0.25, three‑quarters = 0.75, one‑and‑a‑quarter = 1.25\",\"Decimal fractions are used in everyday life: price tags, petrol pumps, scientific measurements\",\"Reading a decimal: “five point three” means 5 + 3\\/10\",\"Writing a decimal: 5.3 = 5 + 3\\/10\",\"The integer part of a decimal is the part before the point\",\"The digit in the ten’s place is the first digit to the left of the point\",\"The place value of a digit is the digit multiplied by its positional value\",\"When converting 0.75 to a fraction, write 75\\/100 and reduce to 3\\/4\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are fractions whose denominators are 10, 100, 1000, etc., called?\",\"options\":[\"Proper fractions\",\"Improper fractions\",\"Decimal fractions\",\"Mixed fractions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that fractions with denominators of 10, 100, 1000, etc., are called decimal fractions.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the decimal symbol (·) placed when writing numbers?\",\"options\":[\"Before the tens place\",\"After the units place in the integer part\",\"After the tenths place\",\"Before the hundreds place\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the decimal symbol is placed after the units place in the integer part.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'one tenth\' mean in terms of parts of a whole?\",\"options\":[\"10 parts out of 100 equal parts\",\"1 part out of 100 equal parts\",\"1 part out of 10 equal parts\",\"10 parts out of 10 equal parts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines one tenth as taking exactly 1 part out of 10 equal parts of a whole.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which place is located immediately to the right of the unit\'s place?\",\"options\":[\"Ten\'s place\",\"One hundredth place\",\"One tenth place\",\"One thousandth place\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that on the right side of the unit\'s place, the one tenth place is the first one.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of the decimal symbol (·)?\",\"options\":[\"To show the start of the number\",\"To indicate the end of the integer part\",\"To separate tens and hundreds\",\"To multiply the number by ten\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the symbol is used to indicate the end of the integer part.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the mixed fraction 5 3\\/10 written in decimal form?\",\"options\":[\"0.53\",\"5.03\",\"5.3\",\"53.0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the example that the mixed fraction 5 3\\/10 is written as 5.3.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How is the decimal number 0.05 read?\",\"options\":[\"Five point zero\",\"Zero integer point zero five\",\"Zero point fifty\",\"Five hundredths integer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that 0.05 is read as \'zero integer point zero five\'.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a \'0\' written in the decimal place when writing 5\\/100 as 0.05?\",\"options\":[\"Because 100 has two zeros\",\"To show that there is nothing in that place\",\"Because it is a rule for all decimals\",\"To make the number larger\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that we write 0 in the decimal place keeping in mind that there is nothing in that place.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a daily life example where decimal notation is used?\",\"options\":[\"Counting pages in a book\",\"Meter readings at petrol pumps\",\"Naming days of the week\",\"Counting students in a class\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists price tags and meter readings at petrol pumps as daily life examples.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you follow the pattern 10, 100, 1000, what would be the denominator of the next decimal fraction?\",\"options\":[\"2000\",\"1100\",\"10,000\",\"100,000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the sequence of denominators as 10, 100, 1000, 10,000.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these fractions is NOT a decimal fraction according to the textbook definition?\",\"options\":[\"7\\/10\",\"9\\/100\",\"3\\/50\",\"12\\/1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Decimal fractions must have denominators like 10, 100, 1000, etc. 50 is not in that specific list.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the decimal system, what is the correct order of places from left to right starting from the units place?\",\"options\":[\"Units, Tenths, Hundredths\",\"Units, Tens, Hundreds\",\"Tenths, Units, Hundredths\",\"Hundredths, Tenths, Units\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that on the right side of the unit\'s place, one tenth and then one hundredth places are there.\",\"topic\":\"Decimal Fraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the place value of the digit 5 in the number 54.632?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"50\",\"0.5\",\"500\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In 54.632, the digit 5 is in the Ten\'s place, so its value is 5 x 10 = 50.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how should the number 54.632 be read?\",\"options\":[\"Fifty-four point six hundred thirty-two\",\"Fifty-four decimal point six three two\",\"Five four decimal six three two\",\"Fifty-four point sixty-three two\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the reading as \'Fifty-four decimal point six three two\'.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which position value is represented by 1\\/10 in the decimal system?\",\"options\":[\"Ten\'s place\",\"Unit\'s place\",\"One-Ten\'s place\",\"One-hundredth\'s place\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table identifies 1\\/10 as the value for the \'One-Ten\'s place\'.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which digit is located in the \'One-hundredth\'s place\' for the number 54.632?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"4\",\"3\",\"2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows the digit 3 corresponds to the \'One-hundredth\'s place\'.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/093_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the expanded form of the number 54.632?\",\"options\":[\"50 + 4 + 6 + 3 + 2\",\"5 + 4 + 6\\/10 + 3\\/100 + 2\\/1000\",\"50 + 4 + 6\\/10 + 3\\/100 + 2\\/1000\",\"50 + 4 + 6\\/100 + 3\\/10 + 2\\/1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The expanded form is the sum of the place values: 50 + 4 + 6\\/10 + 3\\/100 + 2\\/1000.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the decimal value shown for the digit in the \'One-thousandth\'s place\'?\",\"options\":[\"0.2\",\"0.02\",\"0.002\",\"0.0002\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows the place value for the digit 2 in the One-thousandth\'s place is 0.002.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/093_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Using the illustrative examples, what is the expanded form of 3.342?\",\"options\":[\"3 + 3\\/10 + 4\\/100 + 2\\/1000\",\"30 + 3 + 4\\/10 + 2\\/100\",\"3 + 3\\/100 + 4\\/10 + 2\\/1000\",\"3 + 3 + 4 + 2\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Example 1 shows 3.342 = 3 + 3\\/10 + 4\\/100 + 2\\/1000.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a number is written in expanded form as 40 + 5 + 3\\/10 + 5\\/1000, what is the number in decimal form?\",\"options\":[\"45.35\",\"45.305\",\"45.035\",\"4.5305\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on Example 2, 40 + 5 + 3\\/10 + 5\\/1000 equals 45.305.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the expanded form of 45.305, why is the term for the \'One-hundredth\'s place\' missing in the final addition 40 + 5 + 3\\/10 + 5\\/1000?\",\"options\":[\"Because the digit in that place is 1\",\"Because the digit in that place is 0\",\"Because the place does not exist\",\"Because it is a whole number\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 2 shows 0 x 1\\/100 = 0, so it is omitted in the simplified expanded form.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the patterns shown in the text, which digit has the smallest place value in the number 54.632?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"4\",\"6\",\"2\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The digit 2 is in the One-thousandth\'s place (1\\/1000), which is the smallest value among the positions listed.\",\"topic\":\"Expanded forms of decimal fractions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step when comparing two decimal fractions according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Compare the digits in the first decimal place\",\"Compare the integers\",\"Compare the digits in the second decimal place\",\"Count the total number of digits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that first, we must compare the integers.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rule of comparing integers, which of the following is true?\",\"options\":[\"24.27 > 32.48\",\"24.27 < 32.48\",\"24.27 = 32.48\",\"32.48 < 24.27\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the example 24.27 < 32.48 because 24 is less than 32.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the integers of two decimal fractions are equal, what should be compared next?\",\"options\":[\"The last digit of the number\",\"The digits in the first decimal place\",\"The digits in the second decimal place\",\"The number of zeros\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states if integers are equal, the digits in the first decimal place after the decimal point should be compared.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the comparison of 9.42 and 9.72, why is 9.42 smaller than 9.72?\",\"options\":[\"Because the integer 9 is smaller than 9\",\"Because the digit 4 in the first decimal place is smaller than 7\",\"Because 2 is smaller than 7\",\"Because there are more digits in 9.72\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the integers are equal (9), we compare the first decimal place (4 and 7).\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When are the digits in the second decimal place compared?\",\"options\":[\"When the integers are different\",\"When the first decimal place digits are different\",\"When both the integers and the first decimal place digits are the same\",\"Always as the first step\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states if the first decimal place digits are also the same, then the second decimal place should be compared.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Apply the rules to compare these numbers: 12.56 and 12.59. Which is correct?\",\"options\":[\"12.56 > 12.59\",\"12.56 < 12.59\",\"12.56 = 12.59\",\"12.59 < 12.56\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The integers (12) and first decimal place (5) are the same, so we compare the second decimal place (6 < 9).\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which comparison correctly follows the example provided in the text for equal integers?\",\"options\":[\"8.78 > 8.71\",\"9.42 < 9.72\",\"24.27 < 32.48\",\"32.48 > 24.27\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically uses 9.42 < 9.72 as the example for when integers are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Compare 45.12 and 45.18. Which digit determines the comparison?\",\"options\":[\"The digit 4 in the tens place\",\"The digit 5 in the units place\",\"The digit 1 in the first decimal place\",\"The digits 2 and 8 in the second decimal place\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Since the integers and first decimal place are identical, the second decimal place digits (2 and 8) determine the comparison.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the comparison 8.78 > 8.71. Which rule from the text was applied last to reach this conclusion?\",\"options\":[\"Comparing the integers\",\"Comparing the first decimal place\",\"Comparing the second decimal place\",\"Comparing the third decimal place\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In 8.78 and 8.71, the integers and first decimal place are the same, so the second decimal place (8 and 1) is the final comparison point.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If we have two numbers where the integers are different, do we need to compare the decimal places?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No, we only compare the integers first\",\"Only if the first decimal place is zero\",\"Only if the second decimal place is zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to Rule 1, we compare integers first; if they are different (like 24 and 32), that determines the comparison.\",\"topic\":\"Comparison of Decimal Fractions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the number 34.26, what is the place value of the digit 2?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"0.2\",\"0.02\",\"20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The digit 2 is in the tenths place, so its value is 0.2.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the number 8.88, what is the place value of the last digit 8?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"0.8\",\"0.08\",\"0.008\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The last digit 8 is in the hundredths place, so its value is 0.08.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following numbers is larger: 0.76 or 0.8?\",\"options\":[\"0.76\",\"0.8\",\"They are equal\",\"0.08\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"0.8 is equivalent to 0.80, which is larger than 0.76.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing 70.08 and 70.4, which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"70.08 is larger than 70.4\",\"70.4 is larger than 70.08\",\"70.08 and 70.4 are equal\",\"70.08 is smaller than 7.04\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the tenths place, 4 is greater than 0, making 70.4 larger than 70.08.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the place value of the digit 1 in the number 0.81?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"0.1\",\"0.01\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The digit 1 is in the hundredths place, so its value is 0.01.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these numbers is smaller: 0.2 or 0.4?\",\"options\":[\"0.2\",\"0.4\",\"Both are equal\",\"0.02\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Comparing the tenths place, 2 is less than 4, so 0.2 is smaller.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the numbers 7.8, 7.66, 8.06, and 8.68 are arranged in ascending order, which number comes first?\",\"options\":[\"7.8\",\"7.66\",\"8.06\",\"8.68\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ascending order starts with the smallest number, which is 7.66.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which number is larger: 7.4 or 7.35?\",\"options\":[\"7.35\",\"7.4\",\"7.04\",\"They are equal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"7.4 is equivalent to 7.40, which is larger than 7.35.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the number 6.64, what is the place value of the digit 4?\",\"options\":[\"0.4\",\"0.04\",\"4\",\"40\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The digit 4 is in the hundredths place, so its value is 0.04.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following represents the descending order for the set: 8.6, 8.59, 8.09, 8.8?\",\"options\":[\"8.09, 8.59, 8.6, 8.8\",\"8.8, 8.6, 8.59, 8.09\",\"8.8, 8.59, 8.6, 8.09\",\"8.6, 8.8, 8.59, 8.09\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Descending order goes from largest to smallest: 8.8 > 8.6 > 8.59 > 8.09.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Questions\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, a common fraction can be written as a decimal fraction if its denominator is 10 or a:\",\"options\":[\"Multiple of 10\",\"Prime number\",\"Single digit\",\"Negative number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the denominator is 10 or a multiple of 10, it can be written as a decimal fraction.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What must the denominator be to convert a common fraction into a decimal fraction?\",\"options\":[\"Any odd number\",\"10, 100, 1000, etc.\",\"Always 1\",\"A number less than the numerator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that while converting, the denominator has to be 10, 100, 1000, etc.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the denominator is not 10, 100, or 1000, what should be done to the numerator and denominator?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract a suitable number\",\"Multiply by a suitable number\",\"Add 10 to both\",\"Divide only the numerator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says we multiply numerator and denominator by a suitable number to make the denominator 10, 100, 1000, etc.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a fraction\'s denominator cannot be converted to 10, 100, or 1000 by multiplication, what is the alternative method mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Dividing numerator and denominator by the same number\",\"Multiplying the numerator by zero\",\"Adding the denominator to the numerator\",\"Ignoring the denominator\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that if multiplication doesn\'t work, the fraction can be converted by dividing the numerator and denominator by the same number.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To convert the fraction 1\\/2 into a decimal fraction, what \'suitable number\' should we multiply both the numerator and denominator by to get a denominator of 10?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"10\",\"5\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Multiplying the denominator 2 by 5 gives 10, which is the required form for a decimal fraction.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these denominators is already in the correct form for a decimal fraction as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"25\",\"100\",\"50\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists 10, 100, 1000 as the required forms for denominators.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we have a fraction with a denominator of 200, what operation could we perform to make the denominator 100?\",\"options\":[\"Multiply by 2\",\"Divide by 2\",\"Subtract 100\",\"Add 100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions dividing the numerator and denominator by the same number to reach a denominator like 100.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we multiply or divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same number?\",\"options\":[\"To make the denominator 10, 100, 1000, etc.\",\"To make the fraction larger\",\"To make the numerator equal to the denominator\",\"To eliminate the fraction entirely\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The goal stated in the text is to make the denominator 10, 100, 1000, etc., for conversion.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which condition is necessary for a common fraction to be written as a decimal fraction?\",\"options\":[\"The numerator must be a multiple of 10\",\"The denominator must be 10 or convertible to a power of 10\",\"The fraction must be an improper fraction\",\"The denominator must be smaller than the numerator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text emphasizes that the denominator must be 10, 100, 1000, etc., or be converted to that form.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of applying the rule for converting a fraction where the denominator is not already 10, 100, or 1000?\",\"options\":[\"The fraction becomes a whole number\",\"The denominator is changed to a power of 10 using multiplication or division\",\"The numerator is always reduced to 1\",\"The fraction value is changed to 10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains the process of using multiplication or division to transform the denominator into 10, 100, 1000, etc.\",\"topic\":\"Conversion of common fractions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is the number 32.46 written in the numerator when converting it to a common fraction?\",\"options\":[\"32.46\",\"3246\",\"32\",\"46\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the number is written in the numerator without considering the decimal point.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When converting a decimal fraction to a common fraction, what determines the number of zeros written after 1 in the denominator?\",\"options\":[\"The number of digits before the decimal point\",\"The total number of digits in the number\",\"The number of digits after the decimal point\",\"The value of the first digit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that zeros in the denominator correspond to the number of digits after the decimal point.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Convert the decimal fraction 0.7 into a common fraction as shown in the examples.\",\"options\":[\"7\\/1\",\"7\\/10\",\"7\\/100\",\"7\\/1000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example (i) in the text shows 0.7 = 7\\/10.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the fraction 32.46, there are two digits after the decimal point. How many zeros are written after 1 in the denominator?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"None\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that for two digits after the decimal point, two zeros are written after 1 in the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common fraction form of 0.17 according to the illustrative examples?\",\"options\":[\"17\\/10\",\"1.7\\/100\",\"17\\/100\",\"17\\/1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example (ii) in the text shows 0.17 = 17\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the common fraction for 0.045 have 1000 in the denominator?\",\"options\":[\"Because there are three digits after the decimal point\",\"Because the number starts with a zero\",\"Because the number ends with a five\",\"Because there are four digits in total\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The rule states the number of zeros in the denominator matches the number of digits after the decimal point (0, 4, and 5).\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the common fraction for 25.382?\",\"options\":[\"25382\\/100\",\"25382\\/10\",\"25382\\/1000\",\"25.382\\/1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example (iv) in the text shows 25.382 = 25382\\/1000.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a decimal fraction has only one digit after the decimal point, what will be the denominator of its common fraction?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"10\",\"100\",\"1000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One digit after the decimal point means one zero is written after 1 in the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is the correct way to write 32.46 as a common fraction as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"3246\\/10\",\"3246\\/100\",\"3246\\/1000\",\"32.46\\/100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly converts 32.46 to 3246\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a common fraction is written as 45\\/1000, how many digits must have been after the decimal point in its original decimal form?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since there are three zeros in the denominator, there must be three digits after the decimal point.\",\"topic\":\"Converting decimal fractions\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the decimal fraction representation of \'Half\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"0.25\",\"0.5\",\"0.75\",\"1.5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Half\' is written as 0.5.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is \'Three Quarter\' written as a decimal fraction?\",\"options\":[\"0.25\",\"0.5\",\"0.75\",\"1.75\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'Three Quarter\' as 0.75.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When adding or subtracting decimal fractions, how should they be placed vertically?\",\"options\":[\"So that the last digits are aligned\",\"So that the first digits are aligned\",\"So that the decimal point is below the decimal point\",\"So that the units place is below the tenths place\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states decimal fractions should be placed so the decimal point is below the decimal point.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of subtracting 0.7 from 1.8?\",\"options\":[\"1.1\",\"2.5\",\"0.9\",\"1.5\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Following the example in the text, 1.8 - 0.7 = 1.1.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Find the sum of 0.3 and 0.4 as given in the textbook examples.\",\"options\":[\"0.1\",\"0.7\",\"0.12\",\"7.0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the example (1) Add 0.3 and 0.4, which equals 0.7.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what digit is carried over to the \'Units\' column during the addition of 3.64 and 5.4?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"1\",\"3\",\"5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a \'1\' carried over from the Tenths column (6+4=10) to the Units column.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/096_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the digit in the \'Tenths\' place of the final answer after subtracting 11.35 from 20?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"8\",\"6\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The result row in the figure shows the digit 6 in the Tenths column.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/096_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the decimal fraction for \'One and a Quarter\'?\",\"options\":[\"1.5\",\"0.25\",\"1.75\",\"1.25\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states \'One and a Quarter 1.25\'.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Solve the subtraction problem: 0.86 - 0.34.\",\"options\":[\"0.50\",\"0.52\",\"1.20\",\"0.42\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Subtracting 0.34 from 0.86 gives 0.52.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct common fraction for the decimal 0.07?\",\"options\":[\"7\\/10\",\"70\\/100\",\"7\\/100\",\"7\\/1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"0.07 has two decimal places, which represents seven hundredths or 7\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are adding 2.3 and 18.84, where must the decimal point of 18.84 be placed?\",\"options\":[\"Directly below the 3 in 2.3\",\"Directly below the decimal point of 2.3\",\"To the right of the 3 in 2.3\",\"Directly below the 2 in 2.3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rule is to place the decimal point below the decimal point for correct alignment.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the sum of 8.85 and 0.51?\",\"options\":[\"8.36\",\"9.36\",\"9.46\",\"8.46\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Adding 8.85 and 0.51 results in 9.36.\",\"topic\":\"Addition and subtraction\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is a common fraction called if its denominator is 10 or a multiple of 10?\",\"options\":[\"A proper fraction\",\"A decimal fraction\",\"An improper fraction\",\"A mixed fraction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a decimal fraction as a fraction where the denominator is 10 or a multiple of 10.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where are decimal fractions used for very precise measurements?\",\"options\":[\"In history and geography\",\"In scientific experiments or engineering\",\"Only in grocery stores\",\"In sports and games\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that decimal fractions are used in scientific experiments or engineering for precise measurements.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary benefit of converting a common fraction into a decimal fraction?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the number larger\",\"It makes comparisons easier\",\"It removes the need for a numerator\",\"It makes the fraction common\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, converting a common fraction into a decimal fraction makes comparisons easier.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How should decimal fractions be arranged when adding or subtracting them?\",\"options\":[\"Align the last digits of the numbers\",\"Align the first digits of the numbers\",\"Arrange so the decimal point is under the decimal point\",\"Ignore the decimal points until the end\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to arrange them so that the decimal point is under the decimal point.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following denominators would make a common fraction a \'decimal fraction\'?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"100\",\"25\",\"12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A decimal fraction must have a denominator of 10 or a multiple of 10; 100 is a multiple of 10.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are using a calculator to do store calculations, which system works easily?\",\"options\":[\"The Roman system\",\"The decimal system\",\"The binary system\",\"The ancient system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the decimal system works easily when doing calculations in a store or using a calculator.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why is the decimal system preferred for store calculations?\",\"options\":[\"Because it works easily for calculations\",\"Because it uses only whole numbers\",\"Because it is the oldest system\",\"Because it makes prices higher\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states the decimal system works easily when doing calculations in a store.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To add 2.5 and 10.75 correctly, which rule from the text must be followed?\",\"options\":[\"Add the whole numbers first, then the decimals\",\"Arrange them so the decimal point is under the decimal point\",\"Convert them to common fractions first\",\"Multiply both by 10 before adding\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that while adding, the decimal point must be arranged under the decimal point.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which scenario would a scientist most likely use decimal fractions according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"When writing a biography\",\"When doing very precise measurements\",\"When naming a new planet\",\"When counting the number of test tubes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links decimal fractions to scientific experiments for very precise measurements.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the utility of decimal fractions in engineering?\",\"options\":[\"They are used because they are easier to write than words\",\"They are used because they allow for very precise measurements\",\"They are used only when calculators are not available\",\"They are used to make engineering drawings look better\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text highlights their use in engineering for very precise measurements.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21829,34,6,'Maths','en','Profit and Loss','[\"Profit is the extra money earned when selling price is higher than cost price.\",\"Loss is the money lost when selling price is lower than cost price.\",\"Cost Price (CP) = amount spent to buy or make a product, including all expenses.\",\"Selling Price (SP) = amount received from selling the product.\",\"Profit = SP – CP.\",\"Loss = CP – SP.\",\"Total Cost Price = Original Cost Price + Other Expenses (transport, wages, etc.).\",\"If SP > CP → profit; if SP < CP → loss.\",\"To find profit or loss, subtract the smaller amount from the larger one and note the sign.\",\"When calculating profit or loss, always use the same units (e.g., rupees).\",\"A shopkeeper may increase sales by good display, timely sale, quality, advertising, and choosing a suitable place.\",\"If a customer cannot pay the full amount, the seller may give the item on credit, but it may lead to loss if not paid.\",\"Profit and loss concepts help students understand real‑world buying and selling.\",\"Profit and loss problems often involve simple arithmetic: multiplication, addition, subtraction.\",\"Remember: Profit = Selling Price – Cost Price; Loss = Cost Price – Selling Price.\",\"When a product is sold for the same price as it was bought, there is no profit and no loss (break‑even).\",\"In a fair or market, extra expenses like transport and wages increase the total cost price.\",\"If the selling price is less than the cost price, the seller suffers a loss.\",\"When the selling price is more than the cost price, the seller earns a profit.\",\"Profit and loss calculations are useful for planning business ventures and charitable activities.\",\"The amount given for social work is usually taken from the profit after all expenses are covered.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the amount spent on the purchase of goods called?\",\"options\":[\"Selling price\",\"Profit\",\"Cost price\",\"Loss\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher explains to Shraddha that the amount spent on purchasing seedlings is the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In business, what is another term used for \'benefit\'?\",\"options\":[\"Loss\",\"Profit\",\"Cost Price\",\"Selling Price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The teacher states that in business, we call benefit as profit.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When is a profit made in a transaction?\",\"options\":[\"When selling price is equal to cost price\",\"When selling price is greater than cost price\",\"When selling price is less than cost price\",\"When cost price is greater than selling price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the selling price is greater than the cost price, there is a profit.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if a person receives less money from a sale than the amount they spent to buy the item?\",\"options\":[\"They make a profit\",\"They make a benefit\",\"There is a loss\",\"The cost price increases\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher explains that if the selling price is less than the cost price, there is a loss.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Shraddha purchased seedlings for ₹1250 and sold them for ₹1560, how much profit did she make?\",\"options\":[\"₹310\",\"₹2810\",\"₹1560\",\"₹1250\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Profit = Selling Price - Cost Price = 1560 - 1250 = 310.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula to calculate Profit?\",\"options\":[\"Profit = Cost Price + Selling Price\",\"Profit = Cost Price - Selling Price\",\"Profit = Selling Price - Cost Price\",\"Profit = Selling Price + Benefit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines Profit as Selling Price minus Cost Price.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula to calculate Loss?\",\"options\":[\"Loss = Selling Price - Cost Price\",\"Loss = Cost Price - Selling Price\",\"Loss = Cost Price + Selling Price\",\"Loss = Selling Price + Profit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines Loss as Cost Price minus Selling Price.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student collects ₹1560 by selling plants, what does this amount represent in mathematical terms?\",\"options\":[\"Cost Price\",\"Loss\",\"Profit\",\"Selling Price\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The teacher identifies the money collected by selling plants as the selling price.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific condition mentioned for a loss to occur?\",\"options\":[\"Selling price > cost price\",\"Selling price < cost price\",\"Selling price = cost price\",\"Cost price < selling price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure explicitly states \'When selling price < cost price, there is a loss.\'\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/099_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how is Profit defined in terms of a formula?\",\"options\":[\"Profit = cost price - selling price\",\"Profit = selling price + cost price\",\"Profit = selling price - cost price\",\"Profit = cost price - benefit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows \'Profit = selling price - cost price\'.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/099_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a shopkeeper buys a book for ₹500 and sells it for ₹450, what is the result of this transaction?\",\"options\":[\"A profit of ₹50\",\"A loss of ₹50\",\"A profit of ₹450\",\"A loss of ₹500\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since SP (450) < CP (500), it is a loss. Loss = 500 - 450 = 50.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Riya says, \'we earned more than we spent.\' What does this imply about the relationship between Selling Price (SP) and Cost Price (CP)?\",\"options\":[\"SP is less than CP\",\"SP is equal to CP\",\"SP is greater than CP\",\"CP is greater than SP\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Earning more than spending means the selling price is higher than the cost price, resulting in profit.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the teacher congratulate the students regarding the \'Green Fair\' transaction?\",\"options\":[\"Because they spent more than they earned\",\"Because they made a loss of ₹310\",\"Because their selling price was greater than the cost price, resulting in profit\",\"Because they sold all the plants at the cost price\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher congratulates them because they made a benefit (profit) as the selling price was higher than the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a group of students spent ₹2000 on materials and collected ₹1800 from sales, calculate the loss based on the formulas provided.\",\"options\":[\"₹200\",\"₹3800\",\"₹1800\",\"₹2000\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Loss = Cost Price - Selling Price = 2000 - 1800 = 200.\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definitions in the text, which situation describes a \'Loss\'?\",\"options\":[\"Spending ₹100 and receiving ₹150\",\"Spending ₹100 and receiving ₹100\",\"Spending ₹150 and receiving ₹100\",\"Spending ₹50 and receiving ₹75\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A loss occurs when the cost price (spending) is higher than the selling price (receiving).\",\"topic\":\"Cost Price and Selling Price\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the formula used in the banana seller example, what is Profit equal to?\",\"options\":[\"Cost price - Selling price\",\"Selling price - Cost price\",\"Purchase price + Selling price\",\"Selling price + Cost price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Profit = Selling price - Cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When is a loss occurred in a transaction?\",\"options\":[\"When selling price > cost price\",\"When selling price = cost price\",\"When selling price < cost price\",\"When purchase price is zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The vegetable seller example shows loss occurs when selling price is less than cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the tomato seller\'s example, how many kilograms of tomatoes were spoiled?\",\"options\":[\"342 kg\",\"318 kg\",\"25 kg\",\"24 kg\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that 24 kg of tomatoes were spoiled.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the purchase price of 16 dozen bananas in the first example?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 720\",\"₹ 480\",\"₹ 45\",\"₹ 240\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the purchase price of 16 dozen bananas = ₹ 480.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the banana seller\'s transaction, why is it concluded that a profit was made?\",\"options\":[\"Because the cost price was higher than the selling price\",\"Because the selling price was higher than the cost price\",\"Because all 16 dozen bananas were sold\",\"Because the seller bought them for ₹ 480\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Profit is made when selling price > cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the vegetable seller suffer a loss despite selling tomatoes at ₹ 25 per kg?\",\"options\":[\"Because he sold all 342 kg\",\"Because the total selling price of ₹ 7,950 was less than the cost price of ₹ 8,208\",\"Because he bought the tomatoes for ₹ 25 per kg\",\"Because he made a profit of ₹ 258\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A loss occurred because the total selling price was less than the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first factor mentioned in the text to consider when determining the selling price?\",\"options\":[\"The number of customers\",\"The tricks to increase sales\",\"Factors to make a profit\",\"The weight of the goods\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first introductory question asks about factors considered to make a profit.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a fruit seller buys 16 dozen bananas at ₹ 480 and sells them at ₹ 45 per dozen, what is the total selling price?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 480\",\"₹ 240\",\"₹ 720\",\"₹ 600\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Selling price = 16 dozen × ₹ 45 = ₹ 720.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Calculate the loss for the vegetable seller who bought tomatoes for ₹ 8,208 and sold them for ₹ 7,950.\",\"options\":[\"₹ 258\",\"₹ 318\",\"₹ 240\",\"₹ 25\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Loss = Cost price - Selling price = 8,208 - 7,950 = 258.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the vegetable seller\'s case, how was the total selling price of ₹ 7,950 calculated?\",\"options\":[\"By multiplying 342 kg by ₹ 25\",\"By multiplying 318 kg by ₹ 25\",\"By subtracting 24 kg from ₹ 8,208\",\"By adding ₹ 258 to the purchase price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Selling price of 318 kg tomatoes = 318 × 25 = ₹ 7,950.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the impact of the 24 kg of spoiled tomatoes on the transaction?\",\"options\":[\"It increased the selling price\",\"It reduced the quantity available for sale, contributing to the loss\",\"It made the cost price higher\",\"It resulted in a profit of ₹ 258\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Only 318 kg out of 342 kg were sold because 24 kg were spoiled, leading to a lower total selling price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the examples, which statement is true regarding the calculation of profit and loss?\",\"options\":[\"Profit and loss are calculated by comparing the total purchase price and total selling price\",\"Profit is always ₹ 240 in every transaction\",\"Loss is only calculated if goods are spoiled\",\"Selling price is always higher than the purchase price\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Both examples show that profit or loss is determined by comparing the total purchase price and total selling price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the formula for \'Total cost price\'?\",\"options\":[\"Original cost price - Other expenses\",\"Original cost price + Other expenses\",\"Selling price + Other expenses\",\"Selling price - Original cost price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: Total cost price = Original cost price + Other expenses.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the organic crop mentioned on the signboard?\",\"options\":[\"Organic Spinach\",\"Organic Wheat\",\"Organic Methi\",\"Organic Rice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The signboard in the figure clearly says \'Organic Methi\'.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/101_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When is a profit made in a purchase and sale transaction?\",\"options\":[\"When the cost price is greater than the selling price\",\"When the selling price is equal to the cost price\",\"When the selling price is greater than the cost price\",\"When there are no other expenses\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions profit was made because the selling price > the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these expenses did Akash incur for transportation?\",\"options\":[\"₹850\",\"₹1,250\",\"₹1,650\",\"₹800\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states: He spent ₹800 on transportation to transport 450 bundles.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what must be included in the cost price before selling an item?\",\"options\":[\"Only the cost of seeds\",\"Only the wages paid to workers\",\"The cost of manufacturing and all other expenses incurred\",\"Only the transportation cost\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the cost of manufacturing and all other expenses incurred have to be included in the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Akash spent ₹850 on seeds, ₹1,250 on manure, ₹1,650 on wages, and ₹800 on transport, what is his total cost price?\",\"options\":[\"₹4,000\",\"₹4,550\",\"₹11,250\",\"₹6,700\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Total cost price = 850 + 1,250 + 1,650 + 800 = ₹4,550.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what activity are the people performing at the stall?\",\"options\":[\"Buying and selling bundles of fenugreek\",\"Spraying chemical fertilizers\",\"Calculating the total cost price on a board\",\"Manufacturing seeds for the farm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a transaction where bundles of Methi (fenugreek) are being sold to customers.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/101_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Akash sold 450 bundles of Methi at ₹25 per bunch. What was the total selling price?\",\"options\":[\"₹4,550\",\"₹10,000\",\"₹11,250\",\"₹9,500\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Selling price = Total bundles × Price per bundle = 450 × 25 = ₹11,250.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct formula to calculate profit as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Profit = Cost price - Selling price\",\"Profit = Selling price - Cost price\",\"Profit = Selling price + Cost price\",\"Profit = Total expenses + Wages\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly gives the formula: Profit = Selling price - Cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the cost of manure and wages added to the cost of seeds to find the total cost price?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are part of the selling price\",\"Because they are expenses incurred to produce the item\",\"Because they are considered profit\",\"Because they reduce the total investment\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Total cost price includes the original cost plus all other expenses incurred for the item.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Akash\'s total cost price was ₹4,550 and his selling price was ₹11,250, how much profit did he make?\",\"options\":[\"₹5,700\",\"₹6,700\",\"₹7,700\",\"₹6,500\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Profit = 11,250 - 4,550 = ₹6,700.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If Akash had to pay an additional ₹200 for a market stall fee, how would this affect the total cost price?\",\"options\":[\"The total cost price would decrease by ₹200\",\"The total cost price would remain ₹4,550\",\"The total cost price would increase to ₹4,750\",\"It would only affect the selling price\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Any additional expense incurred for the item must be added to the total cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze Akash\'s transaction: If he had sold each bunch at ₹10 instead of ₹25, what would be the result?\",\"options\":[\"He would make a higher profit\",\"He would make a loss because the selling price (₹4,500) would be less than the cost price (₹4,550)\",\"The total cost price would decrease\",\"The profit would remain ₹6,700\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"At ₹10 per bunch, SP = 450 x 10 = 4,500. Since 4,500 < 4,550, it would be a loss.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Progressive farmer\' imply about Akash\'s method of growing Methi in the text?\",\"options\":[\"He used chemical fertilizers\",\"He grew it organically using manure and organic pesticides\",\"He did not pay wages to workers\",\"He did not transport his goods to the market\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he decided to grow fenugreek organically using manure and organic pesticides.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as an expense in Akash\'s total cost price calculation?\",\"options\":[\"Seeds\",\"Wages\",\"Transportation\",\"Signboard advertising cost\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"While he put up a signboard, the text only lists seeds, manure\\/pesticides, wages, and transportation in the numerical calculation of cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Total purchase price, profit and loss\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the formula to calculate the Total cost price?\",\"options\":[\"Total cost price = Original cost price - Other expenses\",\"Total cost price = Original cost price + Other expenses\",\"Total cost price = Selling price + Profit\",\"Total cost price = Selling price ÷ Number of items\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: Total cost price = Original cost price + Other expenses.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the likely result of treating customers angrily?\",\"options\":[\"They will buy more goods\",\"They will bring more customers\",\"They will not come back\",\"They will offer a higher price\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that angry treatment prevents customers from coming back.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"On what basis do charitable organizations sell goods as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Maximum profit basis\",\"High loss basis\",\"No profit, no loss basis\",\"Credit basis\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions charitable organizations selling goods on a \'no profit, no loss\' basis.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the illustrative example, how much did Sangeeta spend on transportation?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 10,800\",\"₹ 5,250\",\"₹ 750\",\"₹ 1,400\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Sangeeta spent ₹ 750 on transportation.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If Sangeeta\'s original cost price was ₹ 10,800 and transportation was ₹ 750, what was her Total cost price?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 11,550\",\"₹ 10,050\",\"₹ 11,000\",\"₹ 12,550\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Total cost price = 10,800 + 750 = ₹ 11,550.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What can be learned from experiencing a loss in business according to the introductory questions?\",\"options\":[\"How to treat customers angrily\",\"A lesson for the future\",\"How to increase transportation costs\",\"Nothing can be learned\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'What will you learn from a loss?\', implying there is a lesson to be learned.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To find the Selling price when Profit and Total cost price are known, which formula is used?\",\"options\":[\"Selling price = Profit - Total cost price\",\"Selling price = Profit ÷ Total cost price\",\"Selling price = Profit + Total cost price\",\"Selling price = Total cost price - Profit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows: Selling price = Profit + Total cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the total selling price for 12 sarees is ₹ 16,800, what is the selling price of one saree?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 1,200\",\"₹ 1,400\",\"₹ 1,500\",\"₹ 1,100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Selling price of a saree = 16,800 ÷ 12 = ₹ 1,400.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the saree example, why is the transportation cost added to the original cost price?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a type of profit\",\"To calculate the total amount spent to acquire the goods\",\"To reduce the selling price\",\"Because the GST was not paid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Expenses like transportation are added to the original cost to find the actual total cost incurred.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the total profit made by Sangeeta in the example provided?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 1,400\",\"₹ 11,550\",\"₹ 5,250\",\"₹ 16,800\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states she made a profit of ₹ 5,250.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the Sangeeta example, if the number of sarees was 10 instead of 12, but the total selling price remained ₹ 16,800, how would the price of one saree change?\",\"options\":[\"It would decrease\",\"It would increase\",\"It would stay the same\",\"It would become zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dividing the same total (16,800) by a smaller number (10 instead of 12) results in a higher value per item.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a charitable organization operates on a \'no profit, no loss\' basis, what must be true about their Selling Price?\",\"options\":[\"Selling Price is greater than Total Cost Price\",\"Selling Price is less than Total Cost Price\",\"Selling Price is equal to Total Cost Price\",\"Selling Price is double the Total Cost Price\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'No profit, no loss\' means the selling price exactly covers the total cost price without any extra gain.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the activity suggest you go to talk to shopkeepers?\",\"options\":[\"To the nearest park\",\"To the nearest market\",\"To a factory\",\"To a school\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly tells the student to \'Go to the nearest market and talk to the shopkeepers.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of obtaining information from the shopkeepers in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"To learn how to decorate a shop\",\"To count the number of items in the shop\",\"To express an opinion on the impact of various points on profit and loss\",\"To ask for a discount on goods\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'From the information obtained, express your opinion on the impact of each point on the profit and loss of the shopkeepers.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which point in the survey refers to how goods are displayed to customers?\",\"options\":[\"Quality of the item\",\"Timely sale of the goods\",\"Arrangement for the presentation of goods\",\"Selection of a suitable place for sale\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 1 in the text is \'Arrangement for the presentation of goods.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many specific points are listed in the activity to observe and obtain information?\",\"options\":[\"Four\",\"Five\",\"Six\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists exactly six numbered points for the market survey.\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a shopkeeper ensures that an item people want is always in stock, which survey point are they addressing?\",\"options\":[\"Advertising the product\",\"Availability of the item in demand\",\"Selection of a suitable place\",\"Timely sale of goods\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Point 3 specifically mentions \'Availability of the item in demand.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A shopkeeper puts up a poster to explain the features of a new soap. Which point from the activity does this represent?\",\"options\":[\"Quality of the item\",\"Arrangement for presentation\",\"Advertising and providing information about the product\",\"Timely sale of the goods\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 5 covers \'Advertising and providing information about the product.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why would \'Selection of a suitable place for sale\' be important for a shopkeeper\'s profit?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the shop look colorful\",\"It helps in getting more customers, which impacts profit and loss\",\"It is required by the school activity\",\"It helps the shopkeeper sleep better\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity asks students to analyze how the selection of a place (Point 6) impacts profit and loss.\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a shopkeeper sells perishable fruits quickly before they spoil, which point are they successfully managing?\",\"options\":[\"Timely sale of the goods\",\"Advertising the product\",\"Arrangement for presentation\",\"Selection of a suitable place\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Selling goods before they spoil or lose value relates to Point 2: \'Timely sale of the goods.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, what is the likely result if a shopkeeper sells items of very poor quality?\",\"options\":[\"The arrangement of goods will improve\",\"It will have an impact on the profit and loss of the shopkeeper\",\"The shopkeeper will move to a new place\",\"Advertising will become unnecessary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity requires students to link points like \'Quality of the item\' (Point 4) to their impact on profit and loss.\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a key factor to observe regarding the product itself to understand profit and loss?\",\"options\":[\"The name of the shopkeeper\",\"The color of the shop walls\",\"Quality of the item\",\"The distance from the student\'s house\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 4 explicitly lists \'Quality of the item\' as a factor to observe for the survey.\",\"topic\":\"Activity - Market survey\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When does a profit occur according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"When the selling price is more than the cost price\",\"When the selling price is less than the cost price\",\"When the selling price is equal to the cost price\",\"When the cost price is more than the selling price\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a profit occurs when the selling price is more than the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula to calculate Profit?\",\"options\":[\"Profit = Cost price - Selling price\",\"Profit = Selling price + Cost price\",\"Profit = Selling price - Cost price\",\"Profit = Original cost price + Other expenses\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines Profit = Selling price - Cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A loss occurs when the selling price is ______ the cost price.\",\"options\":[\"more than\",\"equal to\",\"less than\",\"double\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a loss occurs when the selling price is less than the cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is the correct formula for Loss?\",\"options\":[\"Loss = Selling price - Cost price\",\"Loss = Cost price - Sale price\",\"Loss = Original cost price + Other expenses\",\"Loss = Sale price - Cost price\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Loss = Cost price - Sale price.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the \'Total cost price\' calculated?\",\"options\":[\"Selling price - Cost price\",\"Original cost price - Other expenses\",\"Original cost price + Other expenses\",\"Sale price + Profit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Total cost price = Original cost price + Other expenses.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a shopkeeper sells an item for a price lower than what he paid to buy it, what is the result?\",\"options\":[\"A profit\",\"A loss\",\"Total cost price\",\"Other expenses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When the selling price is less than the cost price, it results in a loss.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the cost price of a book is ₹250 and it is sold for ₹300, what is the profit?\",\"options\":[\"₹550\",\"₹50\",\"₹250\",\"₹300\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using Profit = SP - CP: 300 - 250 = 50.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A toy bought for ₹150 is sold for ₹120. Calculate the loss.\",\"options\":[\"₹30\",\"₹270\",\"₹20\",\"₹150\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using Loss = CP - Sale price: 150 - 120 = 30.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A merchant buys a cupboard for ₹5000 and pays ₹500 for transportation. What is the Total cost price?\",\"options\":[\"₹5000\",\"₹4500\",\"₹5500\",\"₹500\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Total cost price = Original cost (5000) + Other expenses (500) = 5500.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the Total cost price of an object is ₹800 and the Selling price is ₹720, which of the following is true?\",\"options\":[\"Profit = ₹80\",\"Loss = ₹80\",\"Profit = ₹1520\",\"Loss = ₹720\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since SP (720) is less than CP (800), it is a loss: 800 - 720 = 80.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21830,34,6,'Maths','en','Percentage','[\"Percentage is a fraction with denominator 100.\",\"It is written as ‘percent’ or with the symbol %.\",\"The word ‘percent’ comes from Latin ‘per centum’ meaning ‘per hundred’.\",\"To convert a fraction to a percent, make the denominator 100 by multiplying numerator and denominator by the same number.\",\"Example: 2\\/5 = (2×20)\\/(5×20) = 40\\/100 = 40%.\",\"To convert a decimal to a percent, multiply by 100 and add % sign.\",\"Example: 0.25 × 100 = 25%.\",\"To convert a percent to a fraction, put the percent number over 100 and simplify.\",\"Example: 75% = 75\\/100 = 3\\/4.\",\"To find a percentage of a number, multiply the number by the percent (as a decimal).\",\"Example: 30% of 90 = 90 × 0.30 = 27.\",\"To find what percent one number is of another, divide the first by the second and multiply by 100.\",\"Example: 32 is 40% of 80 because 32 ÷ 80 × 100 = 40%.\",\"Percentages are used in discounts, subsidies, attendance, voting, and many real‑life situations.\",\"A 40% discount on a Rs.100 item means the price is 60% of the original, i.e., Rs.60.\",\"When comparing percentages, the higher percent means a larger share.\",\"If a class has 60 students and 48 attended, attendance % = 48 ÷ 60 × 100 = 80%.\",\"In a 100‑point exam, 90 marks is 90% and 75 marks is 75%.\",\"A 15% higher mark means the difference is 15% of the total marks.\",\"The symbol % is read as ‘percent’ and is written as %.\",\"The symbol °\\/ is used for ‘per thousand’ (‰).\"]','[{\"q\":\"A ratio in which the denominator is 100 is called a ______.\",\"options\":[\"fraction\",\"decimal\",\"percent\",\"multiple\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a ratio in which the denominator is 100 is called percent.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The word \'percent\' is derived from which Latin word?\",\"options\":[\"Per centum\",\"Percentage\",\"Per hundred\",\"Centum ratio\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the word \'percent\' is derived from the Latin word \'per centum\'.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the Latin word \'per centum\'?\",\"options\":[\"Per ten\",\"Per thousand\",\"Per unit\",\"Per hundred\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, \'per centum\' means \'per hundred\'.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the symbol \'%\' read?\",\"options\":[\"Ratio\",\"Percent\",\"Hundred\",\"Fraction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the symbol % is read as \'percent\'.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is another name for \'percent\'?\",\"options\":[\"Percentage\",\"Denominator\",\"Decimal\",\"Score\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that percent is also called percentage or per cent.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it easy to compare the scores of Sakhi and Sunny in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because their marks are the same\",\"Because the denominator of both their ratios is 100\",\"Because they both study mathematics\",\"Because they both got 45 marks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that having the denominator as 100 makes it easy to compare.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 0.07 = □ \\/ 100, what is the correct number in the box?\",\"options\":[\"0.7\",\"7\",\"70\",\"0.07\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"0.07 is 7 hundredths, which is 7\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Sakhi got 45 marks out of 50. What is her score in percentage?\",\"options\":[\"45%\",\"50%\",\"90%\",\"75%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sakhi\'s marks are 45\\/50 = 90\\/100, which is 90%.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Sunny got 45 marks out of 60. What is his score in percentage?\",\"options\":[\"45%\",\"75%\",\"60%\",\"90%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sunny\'s marks are 45\\/60 = 3\\/4 = 75\\/100, which is 75%.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Sakhi got 90% and Sunny got 75%. By how much percent did Sakhi score more than Sunny?\",\"options\":[\"10%\",\"20%\",\"15%\",\"5%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Sakhi got 15% more marks than Sunny (90 - 75 = 15).\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Even if two students get the same marks, why must we compare the total marks earned against the maximum marks?\",\"options\":[\"To see who is taller\",\"To determine who actually performed better\",\"To make the calculation harder\",\"To change the subject\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states you must compare against maximum marks to determine who performed better.\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To convert the fraction 4\\/5 into a percentage, by what number should 5 be multiplied to make the denominator 100?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"25\",\"20\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To make the denominator 100, 5 must be multiplied by 20 (5 x 20 = 100).\",\"topic\":\"Percentage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the news report mentioned in the text, what was the voter turnout in the assembly elections?\",\"options\":[\"40%\",\"60%\",\"63%\",\"100%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there was a 63% voter turnout in the state in the assembly elections.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the percentage of discount announced on ready-made garments in the advertisement?\",\"options\":[\"63%\",\"40%\",\"60%\",\"100%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the advertisement has announced a 40% discount on ready-made clothes.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the percentage value shown on the discount board?\",\"options\":[\"63%\",\"100%\",\"40%\",\"60%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The sign board in the picture clearly displays \'40% OFF\'.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/105_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure of the newspaper, what is the percentage of voter turnout reported?\",\"options\":[\"40%\",\"63%\",\"100%\",\"60%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The newspaper clipping in the image shows \'63% voter turnout\'.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/105_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a dress costs ₹ 100, what does a 40% discount mean for the customer?\",\"options\":[\"The customer pays ₹ 40 more\",\"The customer gets a discount of ₹ 40\",\"The customer gets a discount of ₹ 60\",\"The customer pays ₹ 100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that 40% discount means if a dress costs ₹ 100, there will be a discount of ₹ 40.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, if the original cost is ₹ 100 and the discount is 40%, what is the final price the customer pays?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 40\",\"₹ 100\",\"₹ 60\",\"₹ 63\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that after a discount of ₹ 40 on a ₹ 100 dress, the customer will get the dress for ₹ 60.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the voter turnout is 63%, how many people voted out of every 100 voters?\",\"options\":[\"37\",\"63\",\"100\",\"40\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A percentage of 63% means 63 out of every 100.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Using the logic from the text, if a shirt costs ₹ 100 and has a 40% discount, how much money is saved by the buyer?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 60\",\"₹ 40\",\"₹ 100\",\"₹ 20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A 40% discount on ₹ 100 equals a saving (discount) of ₹ 40.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the voter turnout was 63%, what percentage of voters did not cast their votes?\",\"options\":[\"63%\",\"40%\",\"37%\",\"100%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If 63 out of 100 voted, then 100 - 63 = 37 out of 100 (37%) did not vote.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the final price of the dress mentioned as ₹ 60 in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because ₹ 60 is 40% of ₹ 100\",\"Because it is the result of subtracting the ₹ 40 discount from the ₹ 100 cost\",\"Because the voter turnout was 63%\",\"Because the discount was ₹ 60\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The price of ₹ 60 is calculated by taking the original ₹ 100 and subtracting the ₹ 40 discount.\",\"topic\":\"Discuss\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"To express a fraction as a percent, what must the denominator of the fraction be?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"50\",\"100\",\"1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that to convert a fraction to a percent, the denominator needs to be made 100.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the percent value of the decimal 0.25?\",\"options\":[\"0.25%\",\"2.5%\",\"25%\",\"250%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 3 explicitly states that 0.25 = 25\\/100 = 25%.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example 3\\/5, by what number is the denominator 5 multiplied to make it 100?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"20\",\"25\",\"50\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to multiply the denominator, 5, by 20 to make it 100.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When converting 3\\/5 to a percent, why is the numerator also multiplied by 20?\",\"options\":[\"To make an equivalent fraction\",\"To make the numerator 100\",\"To divide the fraction\",\"To change the value of the fraction\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that to make an equivalent fraction, the numerator also needs to be multiplied by the same number as the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the decimal 1.3 first written as a fraction before converting it to a percent?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/3\",\"13\\/100\",\"13\\/10\",\"1\\/30\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 2 shows the first step of converting 1.3 is writing it as 13\\/10.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct way to read the fraction 60\\/100 in words according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Six percent\",\"Sixty per hundred\",\"Sixty percent\",\"Six hundred percent\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly writes \'60% (Sixty percent)\' for the fraction 60\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Following the method for 1.3, what is the result of multiplying both the numerator and denominator of 13\\/10 by 10?\",\"options\":[\"13\\/100\",\"130\\/10\",\"130\\/100\",\"1300\\/100\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 2 shows 13\\/10 = 13×10 \\/ 10×10 = 130\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Using the calculation method for 5\\/8, what is the value of 500 divided by 8?\",\"options\":[\"6.25\",\"62.5\",\"625\",\"0.625\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 4 shows 500\\/8 = 62.5\\/100, meaning 500 divided by 8 is 62.5.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the conversion of 5\\/8, which step is used to find the numerator when the denominator is 100?\",\"options\":[\"5 + 100\",\"5 × 8\",\"500 \\/ 8\",\"8 \\/ 500\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 4 shows the process 5\\/8 = 5×100 \\/ 8 = 500\\/8 to find the percentage.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the examples of 3\\/5 and 1.3, what is the common requirement to finalize the percentage?\",\"options\":[\"The numerator must be 100\",\"The denominator must be 100\",\"The fraction must be a decimal\",\"The numerator must be smaller than the denominator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In all examples, the final step involves ensuring the denominator is 100 before writing the percent sign.\",\"topic\":\"Converting a fraction to a percent\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the fraction form of 50% as given in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2\",\"1\\/4\",\"1\\/5\",\"1\\/10\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, 50% = 50\\/100, which simplifies to 1\\/2.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is \'49 percent\' expressed as a fraction?\",\"options\":[\"49\\/10\",\"49\\/100\",\"4.9\\/100\",\"49\\/1000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that 49 percent = 49\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the improper fraction form of 5 1\\/4 % used in the calculation?\",\"options\":[\"20\\/4 %\",\"21\\/100 %\",\"21\\/4 %\",\"5\\/4 %\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows 5 1\\/4 % = 21\\/4 % as the first step of conversion.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When converting 8.5% to a fraction, what is the intermediate step before simplification?\",\"options\":[\"85\\/100\",\"8.5\\/10\",\"85\\/1000\",\"17\\/200\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows 8.5% = 8.5\\/100 = 85\\/1000.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To find \'30 percent of 90\', which calculation is performed?\",\"options\":[\"90 × 100 \\/ 30\",\"90 × 30 \\/ 100\",\"30 × 100 \\/ 90\",\"90 + 30 \\/ 100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines 30 percent of 90 as 90 × 30\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 180 pages are read out of 300, what fraction of the book is read?\",\"options\":[\"300\\/180\",\"180\\/300\",\"180\\/100\",\"60\\/300\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that 180 out of 300 pages means 180\\/300.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final fraction form of 5 1\\/4 % after calculation?\",\"options\":[\"21\\/4\",\"21\\/100\",\"21\\/400\",\"1\\/400\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text calculates 5 1\\/4 % = 21\\/4 × 1\\/100 = 21\\/400.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Sameera got 70% marks in an 800 marks exam. How many marks did she get?\",\"options\":[\"700\",\"560\",\"800\",\"540\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As per the example, 70% of 800 = 800 × 70\\/100 = 560.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student reads 180 pages out of 300, what percentage of the book have they read?\",\"options\":[\"18%\",\"30%\",\"60%\",\"80%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text calculates 180\\/300 = 60\\/100 = 60%.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To find what percent 32 is of 80, which equation is used to find x?\",\"options\":[\"x = 32\\/80 × 100\",\"x = 80\\/32 × 100\",\"x = 32 × 80 \\/ 100\",\"x = 100 \\/ 32 × 80\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows the step x = 32\\/80 × 100 to find the percentage.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the value of \'x\' if x% of 80 = 32?\",\"options\":[\"32\",\"40\",\"80\",\"20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that x = 40, therefore 40% of 80 = 32.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct simplified fraction for 8.5%?\",\"options\":[\"85\\/100\",\"17\\/20\",\"17\\/200\",\"85\\/1000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 8.5% simplifies to 17\\/200.\",\"topic\":\"To find expected percentage\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the price of the sowing machine?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 36,750\",\"₹ 1,05,000\",\"₹ 1,00,000\",\"₹ 35,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the price of the sowing machine is ₹ 1,05,000.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the percentage of subsidy provided on the sowing machine?\",\"options\":[\"30%\",\"25%\",\"35%\",\"15%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a 35% subsidy is given on the sowing machine.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the example of the sowing machine, how is the subsidy amount calculated?\",\"options\":[\"Price of the machine + Percentage\",\"Price of the machine - Percentage\",\"Price of the machine ÷ Percentage\",\"Price of the machine × Percentage\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text shows the calculation: Subsidy = Price of the sowing machine × Percentage.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the original cost price (C.P.) of the sports shoes that Rohan and Rajat wanted to buy?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 125\",\"₹ 500\",\"₹ 350\",\"₹ 375\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Rohan and Rajat went to buy sports shoes worth ₹ 500.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the price of the sowing machine is ₹ 1,05,000 and the subsidy is 35%, what is the total subsidy amount in rupees?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 36,750\",\"₹ 35,000\",\"₹ 37,650\",\"₹ 36,000\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"As per the calculation in the text, 1,05,000 × 35\\/100 = ₹ 36,750.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How much discount in rupees did Rajat receive at Ramesh Shoe Mart?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 125\",\"₹ 30\",\"₹ 150\",\"₹ 350\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rajat got a 30% discount on ₹ 500, which is 500 × 30\\/100 = ₹ 150.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"After comparing the final prices, who purchased the shoes at a lower price?\",\"options\":[\"Rohan\",\"Rajat\",\"Both paid the same price\",\"The shopkeeper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that Rajat got the shoes at a lower price (₹ 350) than Rohan (₹ 375).\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the Selling Price (S.P.) of the shoes for Rohan at Sagar Shoe Mart?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 500\",\"₹ 125\",\"₹ 375\",\"₹ 350\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"For Rohan, S.P. = 500 - 125 = ₹ 375.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between the discount amount received by Rajat and the discount amount received by Rohan?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 25\",\"₹ 50\",\"₹ 15\",\"₹ 30\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Rajat\'s discount was ₹ 150 and Rohan\'s was ₹ 125. The difference is 150 - 125 = ₹ 25.\",\"topic\":\"Word Problems\",\"par