c\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where should the arcs from points R and D be drawn to find the bisector?\",\"options\":[\"Outside the angle\",\"On the rays PG and PS\",\"Inside the angle\",\"At point P\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 5 states to draw an arc inside the angle from point R and another intersecting arc from point D.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What must be maintained when drawing the intersecting arcs from points R and D?\",\"options\":[\"The same distance between the tips of the compass\",\"A different distance for each arc\",\"The distance between point P and point G\",\"The distance between point G and point S\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Step 5 specifies maintaining the same distance between the tips of the compass when drawing arcs from R and D.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which ray is identified as the bisector of ∠GPS in the given example?\",\"options\":[\"Ray PG\",\"Ray PS\",\"Ray PR\",\"Ray PT\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Step 8 concludes that Ray PT is the bisector of ∠GPS.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If ray PT is the bisector of ∠GPS, what can be said about the measures of ∠GPT and ∠TPS?\",\"options\":[\"∠GPT is larger than ∠TPS\",\"∠TPS is larger than ∠GPT\",\"The measures of ∠GPT and ∠TPS are the same\",\"The sum of the two angles is less than ∠GPS\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 9 asks to check if the measures are the same, which is the defining property of an angle bisector.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To locate point T, from which two points are the intersecting arcs drawn?\",\"options\":[\"Points G and S\",\"Points P and R\",\"Points R and D\",\"Points P and D\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 5 describes placing the compass at point R and then at point D to draw the intersecting arcs that form point T.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the construction of the bisector, which point serves as the origin of the bisecting ray?\",\"options\":[\"Point R\",\"Point D\",\"Point T\",\"Point P\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Step 7 says to draw the ray PT, starting from the vertex P through the intersection point T.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sequence for using the compass to find the bisector after marking points R and D on the rays?\",\"options\":[\"Draw a ray from P, then draw arcs at R and D\",\"Draw arcs from R and D inside the angle, then draw ray PT\",\"Draw arcs from G and S, then join them to P\",\"Measure the angle with a protractor, then mark point T\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following steps 5, 6, and 7, one draws the arcs from R and D first, names the intersection T, and then draws the ray.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How can a student verify that the construction of the bisector Ray PT is accurate?\",\"options\":[\"By checking if Ray PG and Ray PS are equal in length\",\"By checking if Point T is the same distance from G and S\",\"By checking if the measures of ∠GPT and ∠TPS are the same\",\"By checking if Point P is the midpoint of Ray PT\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 9 suggests checking if the measures of the two resulting angles are the same to verify the bisector.\",\"topic\":\"Bisector with the help of a compass\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is used along with a ruler to draw a line segment of a given length?\",\"options\":[\"Compass\",\"Protractor\",\"Divider\",\"Set-square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a line segment of a given length can be drawn using a ruler and a divider.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the ray that divides a given angle into two equal parts?\",\"options\":[\"Perpendicular ray\",\"Angle bisector\",\"Line segment\",\"Protractor ray\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the definition, the ray that divides an angle into two equal parts is called the angle bisector.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If two lines intersect at right angles, they are said to be ______ to each other.\",\"options\":[\"parallel\",\"bisectors\",\"perpendicular\",\"equal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines lines that intersect at right angles as being perpendicular to each other.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is specifically used to measure an angle?\",\"options\":[\"Ruler\",\"Divider\",\"Protractor\",\"Compass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that an angle can be measured using a protractor.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an angle of 90° is bisected, what will be the measure of each of the two resulting parts?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"45°\",\"90°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bisecting means dividing into two equal parts; 90° divided by 2 is 45°.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To draw an angle equal in measure to a given angle (without measuring it first), which tools does the text suggest using?\",\"options\":[\"Ruler and divider\",\"Compass and ruler\",\"Protractor and ruler\",\"Divider and compass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies using a compass and ruler to draw angles equal to the measures of given angles.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A ray divides a 110° angle into two parts, each measuring 55°. Based on the text, what is this ray called?\",\"options\":[\"A perpendicular line\",\"An angle bisector\",\"A line segment\",\"A protractor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the ray divides the 110° angle into two equal parts of 55°, it fits the definition of an angle bisector.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the instructions for the 110° and 45° angles, which tool is used to perform the bisection?\",\"options\":[\"Protractor\",\"Divider\",\"Compass\",\"Set-square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to use a compass and a ruler to bisect the given angles.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the defining characteristic of two perpendicular lines?\",\"options\":[\"They never meet each other.\",\"They are always the same length.\",\"They intersect at right angles.\",\"They divide an angle into two equal parts.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that lines intersecting at right angles are perpendicular.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you need to draw a line segment of exactly 7 cm, which combination of tools is mentioned in the \'At a glance\' section for this purpose?\",\"options\":[\"Ruler and compass\",\"Ruler and protractor\",\"Ruler and divider\",\"Compass and protractor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states a line segment of a given length can be drawn using a ruler and a divider.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which ICT tool is mentioned in the textbook for practicing geometrical constructions?\",\"options\":[\"MS Paint\",\"Geogebra\",\"Calculator\",\"Notepad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly names Geogebra as the ICT tool to be used.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific web address (URL) provided to access the Geogebra tool?\",\"options\":[\"https:\\/\\/www.maths.com\",\"https:\\/\\/www.geogebra.org\\/classic\",\"https:\\/\\/www.geometry.org\",\"https:\\/\\/www.ict-tools.in\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the link https:\\/\\/www.geogebra.org\\/classic for students to use.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what should students practice drawing in Geogebra?\",\"options\":[\"Circles of different radii\",\"Line segments of different lengths\",\"Angles of different measures\",\"Rectangles of different areas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically instructs to practice drawing angles of different measures.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Geogebra is categorized as which type of tool in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Mechanical tool\",\"ICT tool\",\"Manual tool\",\"Physical tool\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text introduces Geogebra under the heading \'ICT tool\'.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to use the \'classic\' version of Geogebra, which part of the URL indicates this?\",\"options\":[\"www\",\"geogebra\",\"org\",\"classic\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The URL ends with \'\\/classic\', indicating the specific version of the tool.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text suggests using \'various tools\' within Geogebra. What is the goal of using these tools?\",\"options\":[\"To play mathematical games\",\"To practice drawing angles\",\"To calculate area\",\"To write definitions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the use of various tools in Geogebra to the practice of drawing angles.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific mathematical concept is the Geogebra link intended to help with in this section?\",\"options\":[\"Addition\",\"Multiplication\",\"Drawing angles\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text focuses on practicing drawing angles using the ICT tool.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student opens Geogebra to draw a 45-degree angle and a 90-degree angle. This matches the textbook instruction to draw angles of:\",\"options\":[\"Same measures\",\"Different measures\",\"Fixed measures\",\"Unknown measures\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions practicing drawing angles of \'different measures\'.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how many tools does Geogebra offer for drawing?\",\"options\":[\"Only one tool\",\"Two tools\",\"Various tools\",\"No tools\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the phrase \'various tools in Geogebra\'.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of the website mentioned in the ICT section?\",\"options\":[\"To provide a digital platform for geometrical practice\",\"To sell geometry boxes\",\"To watch videos on history\",\"To read stories about mathematicians\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Geogebra is provided as an ICT tool to practice drawing geometrical figures like angles.\",\"topic\":\"ICT Tools\",\"part\":32,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21832,34,6,'Maths','en','Perimeter and Area','[\"Perimeter is the total length around a closed figure.\",\"Add up all side lengths to get the perimeter.\",\"For a rectangle: P = 2 × (length + breadth).\",\"For a square: P = 4 × side length.\",\"Perimeter units are the same as side units (cm, m, etc.).\",\"Area measures the space inside a figure.\",\"For a rectangle: A = length × breadth.\",\"For a square: A = side × side.\",\"For a triangle: A = ½ × base × height.\",\"For a right‑angled triangle: A = ½ × (leg1 × leg2).\",\"If a figure is cut into two equal right triangles, each triangle’s area is half the rectangle’s area.\",\"Perimeter and area are independent; increasing one does not always increase the other.\",\"The same perimeter can give different areas for different shapes.\",\"The area of a square grows faster than its perimeter as side length increases.\",\"To find a missing side of a square from its perimeter: side = perimeter ÷ 4.\",\"To find a missing side of a rectangle from its perimeter: 2 × (length + breadth) = perimeter.\",\"Use the formula P = 2 × (length + breadth) to solve for unknown side lengths.\",\"When a wire of fixed length is reshaped into a square, each side equals wire ÷ 4.\",\"When a wire of fixed length is reshaped into a rectangle, the perimeter equals the wire length.\",\"The area of a shape can be found by counting unit squares that fit inside it.\",\"When a square is folded in half, the two resulting rectangles have the same area as the original square.\",\"The perimeter of the two rectangles together is greater than the perimeter of the original square.\",\"When a rectangle is cut into two equal parts, each part’s perimeter can be found by adding the new side created by the cut.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the definition of the perimeter of a closed figure according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the longest side of the figure\",\"The sum of the length of all the sides of the figure\",\"The product of the length and the breadth\",\"The area covered by the closed figure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the sum of the length of all the sides of a closed figure is the perimeter of that figure.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula for the perimeter of a rectangle?\",\"options\":[\"Perimeter = length + breadth\",\"Perimeter = 2 + length + breadth\",\"Perimeter = 2 × (length + breadth)\",\"Perimeter = length × breadth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly derives and states the formula: Perimeter of a rectangle = 2 × (length + breadth).\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the derivation of the formula, the perimeter of a rectangle is first written as \'length + breadth + length + breadth\'. Why is this so?\",\"options\":[\"Because a rectangle has four sides, two of which are lengths and two are breadths\",\"Because a rectangle only has two sides in total\",\"Because the length is always twice the breadth\",\"Because we only add the sides that are different\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A rectangle has four sides where opposite sides are equal, consisting of two lengths and two breadths.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the length of rectangle LMNO is 8.5 cm and the breadth is 5.5 cm, what is the sum of its length and breadth?\",\"options\":[\"13.0 cm\",\"14.0 cm\",\"28.0 cm\",\"15.0 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As shown in the illustrative example: 8.5 + 5.5 = 14.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the illustrative example, what is the final perimeter of rectangle LMNO with length 8.5 cm and breadth 5.5 cm?\",\"options\":[\"14 cm\",\"22 cm\",\"28 cm\",\"30 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The calculation in the text shows 2 × 14 = 28 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula 2 × (length + breadth), what does the number \'2\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The two diagonals of the rectangle\",\"The fact that there are two pairs of equal sides (length and breadth)\",\"The two units used for measurement\",\"The number of corners in a rectangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The formula (2 × length) + (2 × breadth) simplifies to 2 × (length + breadth) because there are two lengths and two breadths.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student adds all four sides of a rectangle individually, how does the result compare to using the formula 2 × (length + breadth)?\",\"options\":[\"The formula gives a larger result\",\"The sum of all sides gives a larger result\",\"The results will be exactly the same\",\"The formula is only an estimate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that \'Sum of the length of all the sides\' is equal to \'2 × (length + breadth)\'.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which column is intended to show the calculation using the derived formula?\",\"options\":[\"Length\",\"Breadth\",\"Sum of all sides\",\"2 x (length + breadth)\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The last column of the table in the figure is specifically titled \'2 x (length + breadth)\'.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which of these objects is NOT listed for the verification of the perimeter formula?\",\"options\":[\"Black board\",\"Notebook\",\"Mathematics book\",\"Compass box\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists Black board, Notebook, and Mathematics book; it does not list a Compass box.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What unit of measurement is mentioned for the side lengths in the introductory paragraph of the topic?\",\"options\":[\"Metres\",\"Millimetres\",\"Centimetres\",\"Inches\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The length of the sides are in centimetres.\'\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we know the \'Sum of all sides\' for a rectangle, why do we still use the formula 2 × (length + breadth)?\",\"options\":[\"To verify that the sum of sides is calculated correctly\",\"Because the formula is only for squares\",\"Because the formula uses fewer sides\",\"To make the perimeter larger\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text suggests writing dimensions in the table to \'verify the formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical operation is performed first when using the formula 2 × (length + breadth)?\",\"options\":[\"Multiplication of 2 and length\",\"Addition of length and breadth\",\"Multiplication of 2 and breadth\",\"Subtraction of breadth from length\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the formula 2 × (length + breadth), the brackets indicate that the addition of length and breadth is performed first.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for the perimeter of a rectangle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a defining property of the sides of a square?\",\"options\":[\"All sides have different lengths\",\"Only opposite sides have the same length\",\"All sides have the same length\",\"The length of the sides cannot be measured\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'All sides of a square have the same length.\'\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for the perimeter of a square?\",\"options\":[\"2 × length of side\",\"3 × length of side\",\"4 × length of side\",\"Side + Side\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly gives the formula: \'Perimeter of a square = 4 × length of side.\'\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The perimeter of a square is the sum of the length of how many sides?\",\"options\":[\"Two sides\",\"Three sides\",\"All the sides\",\"Only the length and width\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines perimeter as the \'Sum of the length of all the sides of the square.\'\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which addition expression is equivalent to the perimeter of a square?\",\"options\":[\"Side + Side\",\"Side + Side + Side\",\"Side + Side + Side + Side\",\"Side + Side + Side + Side + Side\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the calculation as \'Side + Side + Side + Side\'.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To build a single-layer fence around a square plot, what must you calculate?\",\"options\":[\"The area of the square\",\"The perimeter of the square\",\"The diagonal of the square\",\"The sum of two sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'To build a single-layer fence, you need to find the perimeter of the square.\'\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a fence is to be built in three layers around a square, how do you find the total wire needed?\",\"options\":[\"Add 3 to the perimeter\",\"Divide the perimeter by 3\",\"Multiply the perimeter by 3\",\"Multiply the side by 3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 1 shows that for three layers, we multiply the perimeter by three.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Calculate the perimeter of a square plot with a side length of 20 meters.\",\"options\":[\"40 meters\",\"60 meters\",\"80 meters\",\"100 meters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula 4 × side: 4 × 20 = 80 meters.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a square has a side of 5 cm, what is its perimeter?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"15 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"25 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Perimeter = 4 × side = 4 × 5 = 20 cm.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the perimeter of a square is 100 cm, what is the length of one side?\",\"options\":[\"20 cm\",\"25 cm\",\"50 cm\",\"400 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If 4 × Side = 100, then Side = 100 \\/ 4 = 25 cm.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many meters of wire are required for a three-layer fence around a square plot with a side of 20 meters?\",\"options\":[\"80 meters\",\"120 meters\",\"240 meters\",\"60 meters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Perimeter is 80m; for 3 layers: 3 × 80 = 240 meters.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A rectangle has a length of 28 cm and a breadth of 20 cm. If a square has the same perimeter as this rectangle, what is the side of the square?\",\"options\":[\"96 cm\",\"48 cm\",\"24 cm\",\"20 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Perimeter of rectangle = 2(28+20) = 96 cm. Side of square = 96 \\/ 4 = 24 cm.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Example 2, why is the perimeter of the rectangle divided by 4 to find the side of the square?\",\"options\":[\"Because a rectangle has 4 sides\",\"Because the perimeter of a square is 4 times the length of its side\",\"Because the length is 4 times the breadth\",\"Because there are 4 layers of wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since Perimeter = 4 × Side, to find the side, the perimeter must be divided by 4.\",\"topic\":\"The formula for the perimeter of a square\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of the perimeter of a triangle according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The product of the lengths of all three sides\",\"The sum of the length of all three sides of the triangle\",\"The length of the longest side of the triangle\",\"The difference between the longest and shortest sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the length of all three sides.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many sides must be measured to calculate the perimeter of a triangular flag?\",\"options\":[\"One side\",\"Two sides\",\"Three sides\",\"Four sides\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A triangle has three sides, and the perimeter is the sum of all of them.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Find the perimeter of a triangle with sides of length 4 cm, 6 cm, and 8 cm.\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"14 cm\",\"18 cm\",\"24 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Perimeter = 4 + 6 + 8 = 18 cm, as shown in the illustrative example.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Complete the formula: Perimeter of a triangle = Sum of the length of ______.\",\"options\":[\"two sides of the triangle\",\"all the sides of the triangle\",\"the base and height\",\"the opposite sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines the formula as the sum of the length of all the sides of the triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When calculating the perimeter of a triangle, what mathematical operation is used on the lengths of the sides?\",\"options\":[\"Multiplication\",\"Subtraction\",\"Addition\",\"Division\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The term \'sum\' indicates that addition is the operation used to find the perimeter.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a triangle has three sides each measuring 5 cm, what is its perimeter?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"15 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"25 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Perimeter = 5 + 5 + 5 = 15 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What must you consider when some sides of a figure are given in centimetres and some in metres?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore the units and just add the numbers\",\"The unit of the perimeter of the figure\",\"Only use the largest number\",\"Multiply the centimetres by the metres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Think about\' section asks students to consider the unit of perimeter when units are mixed.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student measures the sides of a triangular flag as 10 cm, 10 cm, and 12 cm. What is the perimeter?\",\"options\":[\"20 cm\",\"22 cm\",\"32 cm\",\"40 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Perimeter = 10 + 10 + 12 = 32 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the perimeter of a triangle is 20 cm and two of its sides are 7 cm and 8 cm, what is the length of the third side?\",\"options\":[\"5 cm\",\"15 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"35 cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since the sum is 20, and 7 + 8 = 15, the third side must be 20 - 15 = 5 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the perimeter of the triangle in the example 18 cm and not 192 cm?\",\"options\":[\"Because the sides were added, not multiplied\",\"Because the sides were subtracted\",\"Because only two sides were used\",\"Because the units were changed to metres\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Perimeter is the sum (4+6+8=18), whereas multiplication (4x6x8) would result in 192.\",\"topic\":\"Perimeter of a triangle\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of \'area\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The distance around a flat figure\",\"The measurement of the space occupied by a flat surface of a figure\",\"The number of points on a horizontal line\",\"The length of the longest side of a triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines area as the measurement of the space occupied by a flat surface of a figure.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which shape did Seema use that failed to completely cover the surface of the table?\",\"options\":[\"Square\",\"Rectangle\",\"Right triangle\",\"Circle\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the circle shape didn\'t completely cover the surface and some space remained uncovered.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the figure below, what is the distance between two points on a horizontal or vertical line?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 unit\",\"1 unit\",\"2 units\",\"9 units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the distance between two points on a horizontal or vertical line is 1 unit.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the area of Table 2 is described as \'30 triangle units\', what does this indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The table has 30 corners\",\"The table is 30 units long\",\"The surface is covered by 30 triangular pieces of the same size\",\"The perimeter of the table is 30 units\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes measuring the surface by placing pieces; 30 triangle units means 30 such pieces covered the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Suraj and Seema find that triangles, squares, and rectangles are better than circles for measuring area?\",\"options\":[\"They are easier to cut from card sheets\",\"They completely cover the flat surface without leaving gaps\",\"They have more sides than circles\",\"They are larger in size than circles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that circles leave uncovered space, while the surface is completely covered by triangles, squares, and rectangles.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the length of the horizontal side (top side) of the triangle in units?\",\"options\":[\"2 units\",\"3 units\",\"4 units\",\"9 units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Counting the spaces between the 4 dots on the top horizontal line, there are 3 units.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/126_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the horizontal and vertical sides are each 3 units long, why is the perimeter of the triangle more than 9 units?\",\"options\":[\"Because the third side (diagonal) is longer than 3 units\",\"Because the dots are not placed correctly\",\"Because the triangle is a right triangle\",\"Because 3 plus 3 is already 9\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Two sides sum to 6 units; for the total to be more than 9, the third side must be longer than 3 units.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/126_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Seema and Suraj used pieces of the \'same size and shape\' to measure the table. Why is this important?\",\"options\":[\"To make the card sheet look colorful\",\"To ensure the measurement unit remains consistent\",\"To make the pieces fit in a circle\",\"To reduce the number of pieces needed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using consistent sizes and shapes allows the area to be expressed in specific \'units\' like square units or triangle units.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following units is NOT mentioned in the text for measuring the area of the tables?\",\"options\":[\"Triangle units\",\"Square units\",\"Rectangle units\",\"Circle units\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions triangle, square, and rectangle units, but notes circles do not cover the surface completely.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a surface is covered by 15 square pieces of the same size, what is the area of that surface?\",\"options\":[\"15 units\",\"15 square units\",\"30 units\",\"60 units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the text\'s examples (e.g., 30 triangle units), the area is the number of pieces followed by the shape unit.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the discussion, what is the main difference between measuring perimeter and measuring area?\",\"options\":[\"Perimeter uses circles, area uses squares\",\"Perimeter measures the boundary length, while area measures the occupied flat surface\",\"Perimeter is measured in dots, area is measured in lines\",\"There is no difference between perimeter and area\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text discusses perimeter as a length (9 units) and area as the space occupied by a flat surface.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the method used by Suraj. If he uses square pieces to cover a table, what must be true for the measurement to be accurate?\",\"options\":[\"The squares must overlap each other\",\"There must be gaps between the squares\",\"The squares must cover the surface completely without gaps or overlaps\",\"The squares must be of different sizes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that for a shape to measure area correctly, it must cover the surface completely, unlike the circle.\",\"topic\":\"Area\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is used as the standard unit to measure a plane figure?\",\"options\":[\"A circle with radius 1 unit\",\"A square with side of 1 unit\",\"A triangle with base 1 unit\",\"A rectangle with length 1 unit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a square with side of 1 unit is used as the standard unit to measure a plane figure.\",\"topic\":\"Unit of area\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a square unit chosen as the standard unit of area?\",\"options\":[\"It is the largest unit\",\"It is the only unit available\",\"It is the most convenient unit\",\"It is used for measuring length\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a square unit is the most convenient unit for measuring area.\",\"topic\":\"Unit of area\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the side of a region is measured in centimeters, how is its area written?\",\"options\":[\"centimeter\",\"sq.m.\",\"square centimeter\",\"sq.km.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if the side is in cm, the area is written in square centimeter.\",\"topic\":\"Unit of area\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the brief writing for \'square kilometer\'?\",\"options\":[\"s.km.\",\"sq.km.\",\"skm\",\"sq.k.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The 