in\":\"The text states that because the numbers do not form a triplet, the triangle with sides 11, 12, 15 is not a right-angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Baudhayana - Pythagorean triplet\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are the squares of 11 and 12 as listed in the second example?\",\"options\":[\"121 and 144\",\"22 and 24\",\"110 and 120\",\"121 and 225\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides 11² = 121 and 12² = 144.\",\"topic\":\"Baudhayana - Pythagorean triplet\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the Baudhayana-Pythagoras theorem, what must be true about the numbers in a triplet?\",\"options\":[\"They must be even numbers.\",\"They must be natural numbers.\",\"They must be prime numbers.\",\"They must be consecutive numbers.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition starts by specifying \'three natural numbers\'.\",\"topic\":\"Baudhayana - Pythagorean triplet\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Baudhayana-Pythagorean Theorem, in which type of triangle does the square of the hypotenuse equal the sum of the squares of the other two sides?\",\"options\":[\"Acute-angled triangle\",\"Obtuse-angled triangle\",\"Right-angled triangle\",\"Equilateral triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the theorem applies specifically to a right-angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct formula for the Baudhayana-Pythagorean Theorem?\",\"options\":[\"(hypotenuse)² = (side 1)² - (side 2)²\",\"(hypotenuse)² = (side 1)² + (side 2)²\",\"(hypotenuse) = (side 1) + (side 2)\",\"(hypotenuse)² = (side 1) × (side 2)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the formula: (hypotenuse)² = (side 1)² + (side 2)².\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the sides of a given triangle form a Baudhayana-Pythagorean triplet, what can we conclude about the triangle?\",\"options\":[\"It is a right-angled triangle.\",\"It is an acute-angled triangle.\",\"It is an obtuse-angled triangle.\",\"It is a triangle with all equal sides.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the sides form a triplet, then the triangle is a right-angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula (hypotenuse)² = (side 1)² + (side 2)², what does the term \'hypotenuse\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The shortest side of a right-angled triangle\",\"The side opposite the right angle in a right-angled triangle\",\"The sum of all three sides\",\"The area of the triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a right-angled triangle, the hypotenuse is the side associated with the square that equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If side 1 of a right-angled triangle is 3 units and side 2 is 4 units, what is the value of (hypotenuse)²?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"12\",\"25\",\"49\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula: (3)² + (4)² = 9 + 16 = 25.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A triangle has sides that form a Baudhayana-Pythagorean triplet. If the square of the hypotenuse is 100, what is the sum of the squares of the other two sides?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"50\",\"100\",\"200\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The theorem states (hypotenuse)² is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the square of the hypotenuse is 169 and the square of one side is 144, what is the square of the other side?\",\"options\":[\"25\",\"313\",\"13\",\"12\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Based on (hypotenuse)² = (side 1)² + (side 2)², then 169 = 144 + (side 2)², so (side 2)² = 169 - 144 = 25.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following must be true for a triangle to be called a right-angled triangle according to the triplet rule?\",\"options\":[\"The sum of the sides must be even.\",\"The sides must form a Baudhayana-Pythagorean triplet.\",\"The sides must all be of equal length.\",\"The triangle must have three right angles.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states if the sides form a Baudhayana-Pythagorean triplet, then the triangle is a right-angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a triangle\'s sides are 5, 12, and 13, and we know that 5² + 12² = 13², what does this prove?\",\"options\":[\"The triangle is not right-angled.\",\"The triangle is a right-angled triangle.\",\"The triangle is equilateral.\",\"The theorem does not apply to these numbers.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the squares of the sides satisfy the equation, they form a triplet, making it a right-angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the squares of the two shorter sides of a triangle are 36 and 64, and the square of the longest side is 100, which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"The triangle is not right-angled because 36 + 64 is not 100.\",\"The triangle is right-angled because 36 + 64 = 100.\",\"The triangle is right-angled because 100 - 64 is 36.\",\"The triangle is right-angled because the sides form a Baudhayana-Pythagorean triplet.\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The equality of the sum of squares confirms the sides form a triplet, identifying it as a right-angled triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21834,34,6,'Maths','en','Bank and Simple Interest','[\"Bank is a licensed financial institution that helps people save and borrow money.\",\"A bank keeps your money safe and pays you interest on deposits.\",\"Banks give loans to businesses and individuals; borrowers pay back with interest.\",\"Interest is extra money paid for borrowing or earned for saving.\",\"Principal is the original amount of money deposited or borrowed.\",\"Period is the time for which money is kept or borrowed, usually in years.\",\"Simple interest is calculated for one year only.\",\"Interest rate is expressed as percent per annum (p.c.p.a.).\",\"Formula for simple interest: I = (P × R × T)\\/100, where P=principal, R=rate, T=period in years.\",\"Total amount to repay or receive = Principal + Interest.\",\"Savings account earns interest; current account does not.\",\"E‑KYC is a paperless way to verify identity online.\",\"A challan is a deposit slip that records details of money deposited.\",\"A cheque is a written order to pay money and is only for account holders.\",\"Banking helps in everyday life by providing safe money storage, loans, and payment services.\",\"Banks also offer electronic services like ATMs, debit\\/credit cards, and mobile banking.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which organization regulates the banking system in India?\",\"options\":[\"State Bank of India\",\"Central Bank of India\",\"Reserve Bank of India (RBI)\",\"Government Bank of India\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in India, the banking system is regulated by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI).\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A bank is defined as an authorized and licensed _______ institution.\",\"options\":[\"Educational\",\"Financial\",\"Medical\",\"Social\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a bank is an authorized and licensed financial institution.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the money that a customer deposits in a bank?\",\"options\":[\"It is spent by the government\",\"It remains safe\",\"It is lost forever\",\"It is given to other customers for free\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the money deposited in the bank remains safe.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What must a bank receive from the government to carry out financial transactions?\",\"options\":[\"A large building\",\"Official approval and license\",\"A list of all citizens\",\"Free electricity and water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that banks are given official approval and license by the government to carry out financial transactions.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person needs to perform a money transaction through an authorized service, where should they go?\",\"options\":[\"To a school\",\"To a library\",\"To a bank\",\"To a hospital\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that money transactions are carried out in banks and they provide financial transaction services.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary purpose of the Reserve Bank of India?\",\"options\":[\"To print textbooks for students\",\"To regulate the banking system in India\",\"To provide medical services to customers\",\"To build houses for the government\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the RBI regulates the banking system in India.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What kind of services does a bank provide to its customers?\",\"options\":[\"Financial transaction services\",\"Transportation services\",\"Agricultural services\",\"Communication services\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that a bank provides financial transaction services to its customers.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a bank considered an \'authorized\' institution?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has many employees\",\"Because it has a large building\",\"Because it has government approval and a license\",\"Because it is located in a city\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says banks are given official approval and license by the government, making them authorized.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A businessman wants to ensure his earnings are kept in a place where they remain safe. According to the text, what should he do?\",\"options\":[\"Keep the money at home\",\"Deposit the money in a bank\",\"Give the money to a neighbor\",\"Hide the money in a park\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that money deposited in the bank remains safe.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the role of the government in the banking sector according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The government runs every bank directly\",\"The government provides the license and approval for banks to operate\",\"The government discourages people from using banks\",\"The government only allows one bank to exist\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that banks are given official approval and license by the government to carry out transactions.\",\"topic\":\"Mathematical Discussion & Bank\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as a type of bank in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"Co-operative banks\",\"Industrial development banks\",\"Agricultural forest banks\",\"Nationalised Banks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Central, Commercial, Co-operative, Industrial development, and Nationalised Banks.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the duration for a \'medium term loan\'?\",\"options\":[\"Up to 1 year\",\"Duration up to 5 years\",\"More than 5 years\",\"Exactly 10 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines medium term loans as having a duration up to 5 years.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the interest when a person borrows an amount from the bank?\",\"options\":[\"The bank pays interest to the borrower.\",\"The borrower receives interest from the account holder.\",\"The borrower pays interest on the amount borrowed.\",\"No interest is charged on borrowed amounts.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Borrowers pay interest on the amount borrowed.\'\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which facility does the bank provide specifically for keeping valuables and important documents safe?\",\"options\":[\"ATM facility\",\"Locker facility\",\"E-Banking facility\",\"Lending facility\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the bank provides locker facility to keep valuables and important documents safe.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a farmer takes a loan for a period of 8 months to buy seeds, how would the bank classify this loan duration?\",\"options\":[\"Short-term loan\",\"Medium term loan\",\"Long term loan\",\"Fixed Deposit\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Short-term loans have a duration up to 1 year; 8 months is less than a year.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A businessman uses a \'UPI-QR code\' to receive payments. Which category of bank functions does this fall under?\",\"options\":[\"Locker facility\",\"Lending\",\"Accepting deposits\",\"E-Banking Facilities\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"UPI-QR code is listed under E-Banking Facilities in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary difference between how a bank treats an account holder versus a borrower regarding interest?\",\"options\":[\"The bank pays interest to the holder but charges interest to the borrower.\",\"The bank charges interest to the holder but pays interest to the borrower.\",\"The bank pays interest to both the holder and the borrower.\",\"The bank charges interest to both the holder and the borrower.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says account holders receive interest from the bank, while borrowers pay interest to the bank.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, where is the \'Saved money\' of individuals and organizations kept?\",\"options\":[\"In a school model\",\"In the bank\",\"With the borrowers\",\"In a locker only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows an arrow from \'Saved money\' pointing into the \'Bank\'.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/142_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, what is the flow of money when the bank provides loans?\",\"options\":[\"From the bank to businesses and other people\",\"From businesses to the account holder\",\"From the bank to the saved money circle\",\"From individuals directly to businesses\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows an arrow from the \'Bank\' to \'Businesses and other people\' for loans.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/142_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a type of deposit mentioned in the text on which the bank pays a fixed rate of interest?\",\"options\":[\"Short-term loan\",\"Recurring Deposit\",\"Debit card\",\"Net banking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Fixed Deposit and Recurring Deposit as types of deposits.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the bank do when it \'accepts deposits\' from individuals or institutions?\",\"options\":[\"It charges them a fee for keeping money.\",\"It pays interest at a fixed rate on those deposits.\",\"It gives the money to the school as a model.\",\"It converts the money into different currencies.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the bank pays interest at a fixed rate on deposits like Fixed and Recurring Deposits.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity is suggested in the text to help students understand bank functions at school?\",\"options\":[\"Opening a real nationalised bank\",\"Creating a model bank with the help of teachers\",\"Borrowing long-term loans from the teacher\",\"Selling debit cards to other students\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity 2 suggests creating a model bank at school with the help of teachers.\",\"topic\":\"Bank Functions & Activity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the abbreviation KYC stand for in banking?\",\"options\":[\"Know Your Customer\",\"Keep Your Cash\",\"Know Your Credit\",\"Keep Your Card\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, KYC stands for \'Know Your Customer\'.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following documents is mentioned specifically as an ID proof in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Electricity bill\",\"Ration card\",\"PAN card\",\"Driving license\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Aadhaar card, PAN card, or voter ID card as documents for ID proof.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of photograph must be attached to the bank account application?\",\"options\":[\"Stamp size photograph\",\"Full length photograph\",\"Passport size photograph\",\"Black and white photograph\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The bank employee states that a passport size photograph is needed with the application.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why is the KYC process important for a bank?\",\"options\":[\"To help customers save more money\",\"To prevent things like money laundering or fraud\",\"To calculate the simple interest\",\"To print new passbooks for customers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that this process is important to prevent things like money laundering or fraud.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which feature describes the E-KYC process as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It requires physical xerox copies\",\"It is a paperless and digital process\",\"It can only be done by visiting the bank\",\"It requires a signature on a physical form\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the e-KYC process is paperless and completed digitally.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How can a customer complete the E-KYC process from home?\",\"options\":[\"By sending a letter to the bank\",\"By calling the bank employee\",\"Online through a mobile phone\",\"By visiting the electricity office\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The woman in the text says she will do it online through a mobile phone.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person needs to provide proof of residential address, which document should they choose from the following list based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"PAN card\",\"Voter ID card\",\"Aadhaar card\",\"Ration card\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists ration card as one of the documents for proof of residential address.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A customer wants to open an account without using physical paper documents. Which process should they choose?\",\"options\":[\"Traditional KYC\",\"E-KYC\",\"Self-attestation\",\"Manual verification\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that E-KYC is paperless and does not require physical documents.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the primary purpose of the woman\'s visit to the bank?\",\"options\":[\"To deposit a large sum of money\",\"To open a new bank account\",\"To complain about a bank employee\",\"To buy a new computer for the bank\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The speech bubble in the figure shows the woman saying, \'I want to open a new bank account.\'\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/143_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the employee is talking about \'Important things to open an account\'. Based on the text, which of these is he most likely referring to?\",\"options\":[\"The type of furniture in the bank\",\"The woman\'s jewelry and clothes\",\"Necessary documents like ID and address proof\",\"The books kept on the desk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the figure details the documents (ID proof, address proof, etc.) required to open an account.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/143_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between the documents required for ID proof and Address proof as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"ID proof requires a mobile number, Address proof does not\",\"ID proof uses cards like PAN or Aadhaar, while Address proof includes bills or licenses\",\"ID proof must be digital, Address proof must be physical\",\"There is no difference; all documents are the same for both\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text categorizes Aadhaar\\/PAN\\/Voter ID as ID proof and electricity bills\\/licenses as address proof.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the bank employee ask for a \'self-attested\' Xerox copy of the documents?\",\"options\":[\"To ensure the documents are verified and authentic\",\"To make the application look colorful\",\"To save paper in the bank\",\"To give the customer a new account number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Self-attestation is part of the verification process mentioned by the employee to complete the account opening.\",\"topic\":\"Opening a bank account & E-KYC\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of opening a Savings Account according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"To conduct large daily business transactions\",\"To help people develop the habit of saving\",\"To avoid paying any interest to the bank\",\"To get a free passbook every month\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the purpose of a savings account is to help people develop the habit of saving.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of account is most suitable for business organizations or traders who need to conduct large daily financial transactions?\",\"options\":[\"Savings Account\",\"Personal Account\",\"Current Account\",\"Interest Account\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that business organizations and traders can open a current account for large daily transactions.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific form called that is used while depositing money in a bank?\",\"options\":[\"Passbook\",\"Check\",\"Challan\",\"Ledger\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A form containing specific information used while depositing money is called a Challan.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Jayesh Kumar has ten ₹200 notes and two ₹100 notes. What is the total amount of these specific notes?\",\"options\":[\"₹2,000\",\"₹2,100\",\"₹2,200\",\"₹2,400\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"(10 x 200) + (2 x 100) = 2000 + 200 = 2200.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how does a Current Account differ from a Savings Account regarding interest?\",\"options\":[\"Current account pays higher interest than savings account\",\"Savings account pays no interest while current account does\",\"The bank does not pay any interest on the amount in a current account\",\"Both accounts pay the same rate of interest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that for a current account, the bank does not pay any interest on the amount.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following information is NOT listed in the text as a requirement for filling out a Challan?\",\"options\":[\"Account holder\'s name\",\"Account number\",\"Home address of the payer\",\"Amount in words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists name, account number, date, amount details, amount in words, and signature, but not the home address.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Jayesh Kumar needs to deposit twelve ₹500 notes, what is the total value of these notes?\",\"options\":[\"₹5,000\",\"₹6,000\",\"₹5,500\",\"₹6,500\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"12 notes of ₹500 equals 12 x 500 = 6000.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who is responsible for recording the deposited amount in the account holder\'s passbook?\",\"options\":[\"The account holder\",\"The payer\",\"The bank staff\",\"The trader\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the bank staff records the amount deposited in the passbook.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the bank put restrictions on withdrawing money from a Savings Account?\",\"options\":[\"Because the bank does not pay interest\",\"To encourage the habit of saving money\",\"Because it is a business account\",\"To prevent the use of challans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Restrictions on withdrawals in a savings account are intended to help people develop the habit of saving.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What advice does the text give regarding the maintenance of a passbook?\",\"options\":[\"Keep it updated regularly and keep it safe\",\"Fill it yourself every time you deposit money\",\"Only use it for current accounts\",\"Give it to the trader for safe keeping\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says: \'Keep your passbook updated regularly. Also, keep it safe.\'\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the sample check, what information is required to be filled in the grid marked with the number 1?\",\"options\":[\"Account Number\",\"Date (DDMMYYYY)\",\"Amount in words\",\"Bank Branch Name\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The grid marked 1 contains the labels D, D, M, M, Y, Y, Y, Y which represents the Date.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/145_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which number indicates the specific location where the account holder must provide their signature?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The number 5 is placed directly above the word \'Signature\' on the sample check.\",\"topic\":\"Account types & Deposit Slip\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/145_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the compensation paid by the bank to depositors for keeping their money in the bank called?\",\"options\":[\"Principal\",\"Amount\",\"Interest\",\"Period\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the compensation paid by the bank to depositors or charged to borrowers is called interest.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'p.c.p.a.\' stand for in the context of interest rates?\",\"options\":[\"Percent cost per amount\",\"Percent per annum\",\"Price charged per amount\",\"Principal collected per annum\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines p.c.p.a. as \'Percent per annum\'.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the money deposited in the bank or given by the bank to the borrower called?\",\"options\":[\"Interest\",\"Amount\",\"Principal\",\"Rate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the amount deposited or given to the borrower is called principal.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula to calculate the \'Amount\'?\",\"options\":[\"Amount = Principal - Interest\",\"Amount = Principal + Interest\",\"Amount = Principal × Interest\",\"Amount = Interest - Principal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula: Amount = Principal + Interest.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the principal is ₹8,000 and the interest is ₹640, what will be the amount?\",\"options\":[\"₹7,360\",\"₹8,000\",\"₹8,640\",\"₹9,000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Amount = Principal + Interest, ₹8,000 + ₹640 = ₹8,640.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the activity table, if the Principal is ₹5,800 and the Interest is ₹380, what is the Amount?\",\"options\":[\"₹5,420\",\"₹6,180\",\"₹6,000\",\"₹5,800\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Amount = Principal + Interest = ₹5,800 + ₹380 = ₹6,180.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a borrower returns an Amount of ₹13,550 which includes an Interest of ₹1,050, what was the Principal amount borrowed?\",\"options\":[\"₹14,600\",\"₹12,500\",\"₹13,000\",\"₹12,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Principal = Amount - Interest = ₹13,550 - ₹1,050 = ₹12,500.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"While charging interest on a deposit or loan, the rate is typically given for every how many rupees?\",\"options\":[\"10 rupees\",\"1,000 rupees\",\"100 rupees\",\"1 rupee\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the rate is given for every 100 rupees.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the \'period\' in the context of simple interest?\",\"options\":[\"The total money returned\",\"The interest rate per year\",\"The amount of time for which the principal is used\",\"The extra money paid to the bank\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines period as the amount of time for which the principal is used.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the Principal is ₹35,500 and the Amount returned at the end of the term is ₹39,760, how much Interest was charged?\",\"options\":[\"₹4,260\",\"₹5,260\",\"₹4,000\",\"₹3,260\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Interest = Amount - Principal = ₹39,760 - ₹35,500 = ₹4,260.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, based on the description of Radhika\'s loan, what is the \'Period\' for which the principal was used?\",\"options\":[\"8 years\",\"A year\",\"₹25,000\",\"₹2,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text in the figure states \'After a year, Radhika returned ₹27,000\', indicating the period is one year.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/147_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, to calculate the \'Interest Charged\' of ₹2,000, what value must be placed in the first pink box of the subtraction equation?\",\"options\":[\"₹25,000\",\"₹2,000\",\"₹27,000\",\"₹8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Interest = Amount - Principal. Since the Amount is ₹27,000 and Principal is ₹25,000, the first box must be ₹27,000.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/147_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific term used in this class for interest charged for only one year?\",\"options\":[\"Compound Interest\",\"Simple Interest\",\"Annual Interest\",\"Fixed Interest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'In this class, we will consider interest charged for only one year. This is simple interest.\'\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why does the bank charge borrowers a certain amount of money?\",\"options\":[\"As a penalty for taking a loan\",\"As compensation for lending money\",\"To pay for the vegetable business\",\"To increase the principal amount\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the bank charges borrowers money as compensation for lending money.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What does a rate of \'8 p.c.p.a.\' mean for a principal of ₹100?\",\"options\":[\"The interest is ₹8 for one year\",\"The amount is ₹8 for one year\",\"The principal becomes ₹8 after one year\",\"The interest is ₹8 for every month\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that 8 p.c.p.a. means for every 100 rupees, for one year interest is ₹8.\",\"topic\":\"Charging Interest & Simple Interest\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the principal amount deposited by Kiran?\",\"options\":[\"₹5\",\"₹100\",\"₹18,000\",\"₹900\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Kiran deposits ₹18,000 in a bank.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the rate of interest mentioned in the illustrative example?\",\"options\":[\"1 p.c.p.a.\",\"5 p.c.p.a.\",\"18 p.c.p.a.\",\"100 p.c.p.a.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies the rate of interest as 5 p.c.p.a.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"For what period is the interest calculated in this example?\",\"options\":[\"5 years\",\"100 days\",\"1 year\",\"18 months\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks how much interest Kiran will get in one year.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation provided, what does the variable \'x\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The principal amount\",\"The rate of interest\",\"The time period\",\"The simple interest\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Let us assume that the interest will be x rupees\'.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the ratio of interest to principal expressed for a rate of 5 p.c.p.a.?\",\"options\":[\"100 \\/ 5\",\"5 \\/ 18,000\",\"5 \\/ 100\",\"18,000 \\/ 5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that an interest of ₹5 is given on a principal of ₹100, making the ratio 5\\/100.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which equation is used to find the interest on ₹18,000 based on the ratio method?\",\"options\":[\"x \\/ 5 = 18,000 \\/ 100\",\"x \\/ 18,000 = 5 \\/ 100\",\"x \\/ 100 = 18,000 \\/ 5\",\"x = 18,000 + 5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the equation x \\/ 18,000 = 5 \\/ 100 by expressing the ratio in two forms.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of the calculation 180 × 5 as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"800\",\"900\",\"1000\",\"1805\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the step x = 180 × 5, which equals 900.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the principal is ₹100, how much interest is earned in one year at the given rate?\",\"options\":[\"₹1\",\"₹5\",\"₹100\",\"₹180\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an interest of ₹5 is given on the principal of ₹100.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the step \'x × 100 = 18,000 × 5\', what mathematical process is being applied to the ratios?\",\"options\":[\"Addition of ratios\",\"Subtraction of principal\",\"Cross-multiplication\",\"Division of interest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The transition from x\\/18,000 = 5\\/100 to x × 100 = 18,000 × 5 is achieved through cross-multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How is the equation simplified to reach the step \'x = 180 × 5\'?\",\"options\":[\"By multiplying 18,000 by 100\",\"By dividing 18,000 by 100\",\"By adding 5 to 180\",\"By subtracting 100 from 18,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dividing 18,000 by 100 in the expression (18,000 × 5) \\/ 100 results in 180 × 5.\",\"topic\":\"Illustrative Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the amount deposited in the bank or borrowed from the bank called?\",\"options\":[\"Interest\",\"Period\",\"Principal\",\"Interest rate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines principal as the amount deposited in or borrowed from the bank.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In banking, what does the term \'Period\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"The amount of money borrowed\",\"The amount of time for which a deposit is made or a loan is taken\",\"The excess amount paid to the bank\",\"The interest on Rs 100\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, period is the amount of time for a deposit or a loan.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The interest rate is defined as the interest on what amount for one year?\",\"options\":[\"Rs 10\",\"Rs 1000\",\"Rs 100\",\"The total principal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the interest rate is the interest on Rs 100 for one year.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the \'excess amount\' received on deposits in the bank called?\",\"options\":[\"Principal\",\"Period\",\"Interest\",\"Loan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Interest is defined as an excess amount received on deposits in the bank.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a borrower repays a loan, what is the extra money they pay back in addition to the borrowed amount?\",\"options\":[\"Interest\",\"Principal\",\"Period\",\"Deposit\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Interest is the excess amount paid by borrowers to the bank while repaying the borrowed amount.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is required to define the \'Interest rate\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The total amount borrowed\",\"The excess amount received on deposits\",\"Interest on Rs 100 for one year\",\"The total time of the loan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically defines interest rate as interest on Rs 100 for one year.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Sameer borrows Rs 5000 from a bank, what is this Rs 5000 called in banking terms?\",\"options\":[\"Interest\",\"Principal\",\"Period\",\"Rate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The amount borrowed from the bank is called the principal.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student keeps a fixed deposit in a bank for 2 years. What does \'2 years\' represent in this context?\",\"options\":[\"Principal\",\"Interest rate\",\"Interest\",\"Period\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The amount of time for which a deposit is made is called the period.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definitions, which term represents the \'cost\' or \'benefit\' of using money over time?\",\"options\":[\"Principal\",\"Interest\",\"Period\",\"Deposit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Interest is the excess amount either received (benefit) or paid (cost) during banking transactions.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly distinguishes between Principal and Interest?\",\"options\":[\"Principal is the time, while Interest is the money.\",\"Principal is the initial amount borrowed\\/deposited, while Interest is the excess amount.\",\"Principal is the rate on Rs 100, while Interest is the total time.\",\"Principal is the excess amount, while Interest is the amount borrowed.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Principal as the amount deposited\\/borrowed and Interest as the excess amount.\",\"topic\":\"At a glance\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21835,34,6,'Maths','en','Data Representation','[\"Data Representation is about showing information in a clear way.\",\"A *frequency table* lists each value (score) and how many times it occurs (frequency).\",\"Columns in a frequency table: 1) Score, 2) Tally marks (visual dots), 3) Frequency (number).\",\"The sum of all frequencies is denoted by N and equals the total number of observations.\",\"*Pictograph* uses pictures or symbols to represent data; each symbol stands for a fixed number of items.\",\"*Bar graph* (or bar chart) shows data with rectangular bars; the height (or length) of each bar is proportional to the frequency.\",\"Bar graphs are easier to compare because all bars have the same width and the scale is shown on the Y‑axis.\",\"When data are large, choose a suitable scale (e.g., 1 unit = 5 persons).\",\"In a bar graph the X‑axis lists the categories and the Y‑axis lists the quantity.\",\"*Infographic* is a visual presentation that combines text, images, and charts to make complex data easy to understand.\",\"Frequency tables can be made for any type of data: marks, blood groups, saplings, animals, etc.\",\"To make a frequency table: 1) List all distinct values in ascending order, 2) Count how many times each appears, 3) Write the count in the frequency column.\",\"Tally marks are a quick visual way to show frequency: each vertical line is 1, five vertical lines are grouped with a diagonal line.\",\"When drawing a bar graph, start with the axes, choose a scale, label the categories, and then draw bars of appropriate height.\",\"The height of a bar equals the frequency; if the scale is 1 unit = 10 books, a bar of height 3 means 30 books.\",\"Bar graphs can be drawn vertically or horizontally; the orientation does not change the data.\",\"In a pictograph, each symbol represents a fixed number; e.g., one bird symbol could represent 5 birds.\",\"When data are small, a pictograph may be clearer; when data are large, a bar graph is usually better.\",\"The purpose of data representation is to make information easy to read, compare, and remember.\",\"Remember: N = total number of observations; Frequency = how many times a value occurs; Tally marks are a visual aid.\",\"When reading a bar graph, first look at the X‑axis (categories) then the Y‑axis (scale) to interpret the heights.\",\"Bar graphs can be used for many subjects: marks, books read, occupations, animal lifespans, travel time, etc.\",\"Infographics add colour, icons, and design to make data attractive and memorable.\",\"When creating a bar graph, always check that the graph is drawn to scale and that the bars are of equal width.\",\"Use graph paper to keep the bars straight and the scale accurate.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are the marks obtained by the students in the History test called?\",\"options\":[\"Score\",\"Data\",\"Frequency\",\"Tally\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the marks obtained by the students in the test are the \'data\'.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is each individual number (the marks obtained) in the given data called?\",\"options\":[\"Frequency\",\"Tally mark\",\"Score\",\"Total\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines each of these numbers (the marks obtained) as the \'score\'.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the frequency table, what does the term \'Frequency\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The marks obtained out of 10\",\"The total number of students for a specific score\",\"The sum of all marks\",\"The number of subjects in the test\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table header shows \'Total Number of students\' corresponds to \'Frequency\'.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many tally marks are drawn for the score of 2?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows three vertical lines (|||) in the Tally Marks column for the score of 2.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/149_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how many students out of 15 obtained 6 marks?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"3\",\"4\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Out of 15, 4 students got 6 marks\'.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total number of students (N) recorded in the frequency table?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"12\",\"15\",\"20\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and the table both specify that the total number of students is N = 15.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If one more student joins the class and scores 6 marks, what will be the new frequency for the score of 6?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"5\",\"6\",\"7\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The current frequency is 4; adding one more student with the same score makes it 5.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which score was obtained by the highest number of students?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"6\",\"10\",\"15\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The frequency for score 6 is 4, which is the highest value in the Frequency column.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/149_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many students scored more than 8 marks according to the data provided?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Students who scored 9 and 10 marks are counted (1 student for 9, 1 student for 10), totaling 2 students.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is used to present the collected information in an organized manner as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A paragraph\",\"A list of names\",\"A table\",\"A history book\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'A table can be used to present this information.\'\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student\'s score is 2, how many tally marks should be marked in the table?\",\"options\":[\"I\",\"II\",\"III\",\"IIII\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 3 students got 2 marks, 3 tally marks (III) are required.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"By looking at the frequency of each score, how many students scored less than 3 marks?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Scores less than 3 are 1 and 2. Frequency of 1 is 1, and frequency of 2 is 3. Total = 1 + 3 = 4.\",\"topic\":\"Revision\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of using a frequency table?\",\"options\":[\"To make information complex\",\"To present information in a simple and easy manner\",\"To hide the given information\",\"To calculate the average of scores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that frequency tables are used to present information in a simple and easy manner to understand.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many columns are there in a frequency table?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that there are three columns in a frequency table.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which column of the frequency table are the Tally marks shown?\",\"options\":[\"First column\",\"Second column\",\"Third column\",\"None of the columns\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the second column shows the Tally marks.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In what order should the scores be written in the first column?\",\"options\":[\"Descending order\",\"Random order\",\"Ascending order\",\"Alphabetical order\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the scores are written in ascending order in the first column.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the \'Frequency\' in the third column represent?\",\"options\":[\"The total number of columns\",\"The number of tally marks for each score\",\"The sum of all scores\",\"The order of the scores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines frequency as the number of tally marks for each score, written in the third column.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct heading for the first column of a frequency table?\",\"options\":[\"Frequency\",\"Tally marks\",\"Score\",\"Total\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Score\' as the first of the three columns.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a specific score appears 4 times in a data set, how many tally marks should be drawn in the second column for that score?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The number of tally marks corresponds to the number of times a score occurs.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the frequency written in the third column for a score is 5, how many tally marks must be present in the second column for that score?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"5\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The frequency is the count of the tally marks, so a frequency of 5 means there are 5 tally marks.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given the scores 12, 8, and 15, which score should be written at the top of the first column according to the rules?\",\"options\":[\"15\",\"12\",\"8\",\"Any of the three\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Scores must be in ascending order, so the smallest number (8) must come first.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which sequence correctly represents the headers of the three columns from left to right?\",\"options\":[\"Tally marks, Score, Frequency\",\"Score, Tally marks, Frequency\",\"Frequency, Tally marks, Score\",\"Score, Frequency, Tally marks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists the columns in the order: Score, Tally marks, and Frequency.\",\"topic\":\"Frequency Table\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What letter is used to represent the sum of frequencies in a data table?\",\"options\":[\"S\",\"N\",\"F\",\"T\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the sum of frequencies in a table is represented by the letter \'N\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The sum of frequencies \'N\' is always equal to the total number of ______ in the given data.\",\"options\":[\"tally marks\",\"scores\",\"observations\",\"columns\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the sum \'N\' is equal to the total number of observations (score) in the given data.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how is the frequency of 6 represented for Score 1 in the Tally Marks column?\",\"options\":[\"A group of 5 marks (4 vertical and 1 diagonal) and 1 single mark\",\"6 separate vertical lines\",\"3 groups of 2 marks each\",\"One single diagonal line\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a group of 5 tally marks followed by one single mark to represent the frequency of 6.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/150_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the frequency table for the die throws, what is the total value of N?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"20\",\"30\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the text explicitly shows \'N = 30\' at the bottom of the frequency column.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many distinct blood groups are listed for data collection?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"6\",\"8\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table lists A+, A-, B+, B-, AB+, AB-, O+, and O-, which totals 8 blood groups.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/150_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the die throw data provided in the text, how many times does the score \'6\' appear?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"5\",\"6\",\"3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Counting the number 6 in the provided list (1, 5, 3, 6, 2, 1, 4, 3, 5, 6, 2, 1, 4, 3, 5, 6, 2, 1, 4, 3, 5, 6, 2, 1, 4, 3, 5, 6, 2, 1) gives a total of 5.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the \'Score\' column in the die throw table represent?\",\"options\":[\"The number of times the die was thrown\",\"The numbers obtained on the surface of the die\",\"The total sum of all numbers\",\"The name of the student throwing the die\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the numbers obtained on the surface each time are the scores.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are collecting blood group information for a class of 45 students, what will be the value of \'N\' in your table?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"45\",\"1\",\"90\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"N represents the total number of observations, which in this case is the total number of students (45).\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a blood group mentioned in the data collection table?\",\"options\":[\"AB-\",\"O+\",\"B-\",\"C+\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table lists A, B, AB, and O groups with +\\/- factors; C+ is not listed.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a frequency table, which column is used to record each individual observation as it is counted?\",\"options\":[\"Score\",\"Frequency\",\"Tally Marks\",\"N\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tally marks are used to keep a running count of each observation before totaling them in the frequency column.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the total number of observations N is 30, and the sum of frequencies for scores 1 to 5 is 25, what must be the frequency of score 6?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"30\",\"10\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since N is the sum of all frequencies, the frequency of score 6 is 30 - 25 = 5.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to calculate the sum of frequencies \'N\' at the end of the table?\",\"options\":[\"To make the table look longer\",\"To ensure all observations have been counted correctly\",\"To list the highest score obtained\",\"To name the person who collected the data\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Calculating N verifies that the total count matches the total number of observations intended to be recorded.\",\"topic\":\"Do it.\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In today\'s modern era, what makes information easier to understand quickly?\",\"options\":[\"Writing long paragraphs\",\"Using pictographs, bar charts, and infographics\",\"Keeping information unsystematic\",\"Memorizing all the numbers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that pictographs, bar charts, and infographics make information easier to understand quickly.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how should information be presented to make it easier to understand?\",\"options\":[\"In a systematic manner\",\"In a random order\",\"Without any scale\",\"Using only text\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that information is easier to understand if it is presented in a systematic manner.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many books does one book-stack picture represent according to the scale?\",\"options\":[\"1 book\",\"10 books\",\"5 books\",\"24 books\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The scale in the pictograph explicitly states that one picture equals 5 books.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/152_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many books has Sanskruti read if there are 6 pictures next to her name?\",\"options\":[\"6 books\",\"30 books\",\"11 books\",\"60 books\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"With a scale of 1 picture = 5 books, 6 pictures represent 6 x 5 = 30 books.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/152_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the information provided, how many more books did Aafiya read than Kumudini?\",\"options\":[\"15 books\",\"5 books\",\"10 books\",\"20 books\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Aafiya read 60 books and Kumudini read 45 books; the difference is 60 - 45 = 15.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why was it difficult to accurately show the number of books read by Sushil in the pictograph?\",\"options\":[\"Sushil did not read any books\",\"The number 24 cannot be easily represented using a scale of 5 books per picture\",\"There was no space left in the table\",\"Sushil\'s name was missing from the chart\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that we could not accurately show Sushil\'s 24 books because the scale was 5.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What tool is mentioned in the text to show data like Sushil\'s books accurately?\",\"options\":[\"A ruler\",\"A calculator\",\"Graph paper\",\"A compass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that we will use graph paper to show the information accurately.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the scale used for the bar graph?\",\"options\":[\"1 unit = 5 books\",\"1 unit = 1 book\",\"1 unit = 10 books\",\"1 unit = 24 books\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The bar graph image explicitly shows the scale as 1 unit = 10 books.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/152_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Who has read the maximum number of books according to the data?\",\"options\":[\"Sanskruti\",\"Kumudini\",\"Sarang\",\"Aafiya\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Aafiya read 60 books, which is the highest number in the list.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If we draw a bar graph instead of a pictograph, what advantage does it provide for Sushil\'s data?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the chart colorful\",\"It allows us to show the number 24 accurately\",\"It reduces the number of students\",\"It changes the number of books read\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a bar graph can accurately show the number of books read by Sushil.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the pictograph, if Sarang has 8 pictures next to his name, how many books did he read?\",\"options\":[\"8 books\",\"40 books\",\"45 books\",\"13 books\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the scale 1 picture = 5 books, Sarang\'s 8 pictures represent 8 x 5 = 40 books.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"34\\/152_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which student read exactly 45 books as shown in the data table?\",\"options\":[\"Sushil\",\"Aafiya\",\"Kumudini\",\"Sanskruti\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the text lists Kumudini as having read 45 books.\",\"topic\":\"Pictograph\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What do the thick lines on a graph paper represent?\",\"options\":[\"Smaller units\",\"Larger units\",\"Equal parts\",\"Faint lines\"],\"ans