ing method, substances are separated based on which properties?\",\"options\":[\"Weight and size\",\"Colour and shape\",\"Magnetic property\",\"Solubility in water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Handpicking relies on identifying differences in the colour and shape of substances.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of using a \'mesh\' in the separation process?\",\"options\":[\"To attract magnetic substances\",\"To evaporate the liquid\",\"To separate small particles from a mixture\",\"To blow away light substances\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sieving uses a mesh to separate small particles from a mixture.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens during the process of sedimentation?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid turns into gas\",\"Heavy, insoluble solid particles settle at the bottom\",\"Light particles are blown away by wind\",\"Magnetic substances are pulled away\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sedimentation is the process where heavy, insoluble solid particles in a liquid settle at the bottom.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Filtration is a method used to separate solid substances that are:\",\"options\":[\"Dissolved in a liquid\",\"Magnetic in nature\",\"Not dissolved in a liquid\",\"Lighter than the liquid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Filtration is used to separate solid substances that are not dissolved in a liquid.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a salt farmer wants to obtain salt from seawater, which process should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Sieving\",\"Winnowing\",\"Handpicking\",\"Evaporation\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Sea salt is obtained by the evaporation of seawater.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student has a mixture of iron filings and sand. Which tool would be most effective for separation?\",\"options\":[\"A mesh\",\"A magnet\",\"Wind\",\"A filter paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Magnetic substances in a mixture can be separated using a magnet.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are different \'tricks\' or methods used for separation?\",\"options\":[\"Because all substances have the same properties\",\"Because substances in a mixture have different properties\",\"Because all mixtures are liquid\",\"Because wind is available everywhere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Methods of separation are found by identifying differences in the properties of substances.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which method would be best to separate small stones from rice if the stones are a different color than the rice?\",\"options\":[\"Handpicking\",\"Evaporation\",\"Sedimentation\",\"Winnowing\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Handpicking is used when substances can be identified and picked out by colour and shape.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a common feature of both sedimentation and filtration?\",\"options\":[\"Both use wind to separate substances\",\"Both involve separating insoluble solids from a liquid\",\"Both require a magnet\",\"Both convert liquid into gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Both methods deal with solid substances that do not dissolve in a liquid.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21859,36,6,'Science','en','Diversity in Living Organisms','[\"- Plants are living organisms that grow from seeds or spores.\",\"- Major plant groups: trees, shrubs, herbs, climbers, creepers.\",\"- Trees have strong trunks and branches that can support people.\",\"- Shrubs have branches close to the ground and are not very thick.\",\"- Herbs have very weak stems and cannot stand upright; they often wrap around supports.\",\"- Climbers grow upward by wrapping around a support or using tendrils.\",\"- Creepers spread across the ground instead of growing upward.\",\"- Leaves vary in size, shape, edges and texture; this is called leaf diversity.\",\"- Flower diversity includes shape, colour, number of petals, fragrance and season of blooming.\",\"- Fruits develop from flowers and contain seeds; some fruits have many seeds, others none.\",\"- Seed types: dicotyledonous (two seed leaves) and monocotyledonous (one seed leaf).\",\"- Leaf venation: reticulate (net‑like) in dicots; parallel in monocots.\",\"- Root types: taproot (single main root) in dicots; fibrous roots (many small roots) in monocots.\",\"- Habitat is the environment where a plant or animal lives comfortably.\",\"- Animals are grouped as terrestrial, aquatic, amphibian, aerial, etc.\",\"- Classification of animals can be based on habitat, body structure and life cycle.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where is the 250-year-old banyan tree mentioned in the text located?\",\"options\":[\"Andhra Pradesh\",\"Botanical Garden at Kolkata\",\"A forest in Mumbai\",\"Delhi Botanical Garden\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the 250-year-old banyan tree is located in the Botanical Garden at Kolkata.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the main trunk of the Kolkata banyan tree removed?\",\"options\":[\"1825\",\"1925\",\"2025\",\"1725\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the main trunk of the tree had to be removed in 1925.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the single banyan tree in Kolkata appear like a forest?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is very tall\",\"Because it is supported by more than 3,500 thick aerial roots\",\"Because it is located in a dense forest\",\"Because it has many different types of leaves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The tree stands supported by more than 3,500 thick aerial roots, making it look like a forest.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is unique about the banyan tree located in Andhra Pradesh?\",\"options\":[\"It is exactly 250 years old\",\"It is the world\'s most massive banyan tree\",\"It has no aerial roots\",\"It was planted in 1925\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the tree in Andhra Pradesh as the world\'s most massive Banyan tree.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what happened to the Kolkata banyan tree after its main trunk was removed?\",\"options\":[\"It stopped growing\",\"It died immediately\",\"It continued to grow\",\"It was replaced by a new tree\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that even after the trunk was removed in 1925, the tree has continued to grow.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text says 10,000 people can sit in the shade of the Andhra Pradesh banyan tree. What does this indicate about the tree?\",\"options\":[\"The tree is very tall\",\"The tree has a very large expanse or spread\",\"The tree is very old\",\"The tree has very few roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The ability to shade 10,000 people indicates an \'incredible expanse\' or area covered by the tree.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the numerous vertical structures that support the wide spread of the tree?\",\"options\":[\"Main trunks of different trees\",\"Artificial wooden pillars\",\"Thick aerial roots\",\"Iron fences\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the aerial roots that support the colossal banyan tree, making it look like a forest.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/044_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, why is the statement \'This is not a photograph of a forest\' necessary?\",\"options\":[\"Because the tree is very small\",\"Because the aerial roots look like many individual tree trunks\",\"Because there are no leaves on the tree\",\"Because the tree is located in a city\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The vast number of aerial roots reaching the ground creates the illusion of many separate trees forming a forest.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/044_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How many thick aerial roots support the banyan tree in the Kolkata Botanical Garden?\",\"options\":[\"Exactly 250\",\"More than 3,500\",\"Exactly 10,000\",\"Exactly 1,925\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the tree is supported by more than 3,500 thick aerial roots.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a tourist wants to see the tree that survived the removal of its main trunk, which city should they visit?\",\"options\":[\"Hyderabad\",\"Kolkata\",\"Mumbai\",\"Chennai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the tree whose trunk was removed in 1925 is located in Kolkata.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the typical height reached by shrubs like hibiscus and oleander?\",\"options\":[\"1 to 2 feet\",\"5 to 6 feet\",\"10 to 12 feet\",\"Over 20 feet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that plants like rose, oleander, hibiscus, and heena grow to a height of 5 to 6 feet.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following plants is classified as a shrub in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Neem\",\"Tamarind\",\"Heena (mehendi)\",\"Coconut\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists rose, oleander, hibiscus, and heena as examples of shrubs.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the specific characteristics of moss-like plants that grow on wet walls?\",\"options\":[\"They have long branches and deep roots.\",\"They have flowers but no leaves.\",\"They have no branches, no roots, and no flowers.\",\"They grow taller than a human.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes moss-like plants on wet walls as having no branches, no roots, and no flowers.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where does algae typically grow according to the passage?\",\"options\":[\"On dry roads\",\"On wet floors\",\"On tall palm trees\",\"Inside the microscope\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions algae growing on wet floors and notes it is even smaller than moss.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student finds a plant that is 5.5 feet tall and looks like a rose bush, how should it be classified based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"As a tree\",\"As a shrub\",\"As a grass\",\"As a moss\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plants that grow to a height of 5 to 6 feet, like roses, are called shrubs.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What tool is necessary to see the smallest plants that cannot be seen with the naked eye?\",\"options\":[\"Telescope\",\"Magnifying glass\",\"Microscope\",\"Spectacles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that some plants can only be seen through a microscope.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how does the growth of a karvanda plant compare to a neem tree?\",\"options\":[\"Karvanda grows much taller than neem.\",\"Karvanda and neem grow to the same height.\",\"Karvanda is a shrub and does not grow as large as a neem tree.\",\"Karvanda has no roots while neem has deep roots.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies shrubs like karvanda do not grow as large as trees like neem or tamarind.\",\"topic\":\"Diversity in Plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is mentioned as an example of a \'tall palm\'?\",\"options\":[\"Rose\",\"Coconut\"