s parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you try to tear a leaf with a complex network of veins from the edge to the center, what will happen?\",\"options\":[\"It will tear in a perfectly straight line.\",\"It will not tear at all.\",\"It will tear in an uneven pattern.\",\"It will dissolve in your hand.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, leaves with a complex network (reticulate venation) do not tear along a straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Parallel venation is compared to which of the following in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"A spider\'s web\",\"Ruled lines on a notebook page\",\"A road map\",\"A cracked mirror\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes parallel veins as being like the ruled lines on a notebook page.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what type of venation would you expect to find in sugarcane, jowar, and wheat?\",\"options\":[\"Reticulate venation\",\"Parallel venation\",\"Web-like venation\",\"Circular venation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sugarcane, jowar, and wheat have long, narrow leaves similar to banana\\/canna, which have parallel venation.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the local name for the Canna plant mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Tulsi\",\"Kardali\",\"Pimpal\",\"Vad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the name \'kardali\' in brackets for the Canna plant.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do leaves of holy basil not form a web-like skeleton when kept in water, unlike peepal leaves?\",\"options\":[\"They do not have veins.\",\"They have a different type of venation or structure.\",\"They are too small to see.\",\"They do not absorb water.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes the difference and asks the student to guess, implying the internal arrangement of veins differs.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what kind of network is visible in the skeleton of the leaf?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel lines\",\"A web-like network\",\"Concentric circles\",\"A single straight line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the skeleton of a peepal leaf which is described as a web-like network.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/047_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity shown in the figure, which of these leaves will tear in a straight line from the edge to the center?\",\"options\":[\"Mango leaf\",\"Banyan leaf\",\"Peepal leaf\",\"Banana leaf\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The activity explains that leaves with parallel venation, like banana, tear in a straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/048_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of the \'web-like network\' or veins in a leaf according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"To make the leaf look beautiful\",\"To help the leaf rot faster\",\"To transport water absorbed by the roots to all parts of the leaf\",\"To help the leaf grow taller\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests a connection between the water absorbed by roots and the network reaching all parts of the leaf.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these leaves would be the hardest to tear in a perfectly straight line?\",\"options\":[\"Maize\",\"Wheat\",\"Teak\",\"Sugarcane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Teak has reticulate venation (like peepal), which makes it tear unevenly, unlike the others which have parallel venation.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a leaf has reticulate venation, what can we conclude about its vein arrangement?\",\"options\":[\"The veins are parallel to each other.\",\"The veins form a complex network.\",\"There are no veins in the lamina.\",\"The veins only exist at the outer edge.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines reticulate venation as having a complex network of veins.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the peepal leaf rots away when kept in water for a few days?\",\"options\":[\"The veins\",\"The skeleton\",\"The soft part\",\"The roots\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when a peepal leaf is kept in water, its soft part rots away.\",\"topic\":\"Leaf Venation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the roots of plants usually grow?\",\"options\":[\"Above the soil\",\"Underground in the soil\",\"On the leaves\",\"Inside the stem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that roots of plants grow underground in the soil and are usually not visible from outside.\",\"topic\":\"Roots and Classification\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a root system called when a main primary root grows from the base of the stem with secondary branches growing off it?\",\"options\":[\"Fibrous root\",\"Thread root\",\"Tap root\",\"Parallel root\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A root system with a main primary root and secondary branches is named a \'Tap root\'.\",\"topic\":\"Roots and Classification\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Roots that grow like a bunch of threads from the base of the stem are known as which type of roots?\",\"options\":[\"Tap roots\",\"Fibrous roots\",\"Primary roots\",\"Reticulate roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Roots growing like a bunch of threads from the base of the stem are known as \'Fibrous roots\'.\",\"topic\":\"Roots and Classification\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a characteristic feature of dicotyledonous (dicot) plants?\",\"options\":[\"Fibrous roots\",\"Parallel venation\",\"Taproots\",\"Thread-like roots\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text men