or the instruction to \'Stay calm\' after a disaster?\",\"options\":[\"To help identify the disaster type\",\"To effectively follow safety steps and help others\",\"To memorize new phone numbers\",\"To observe cyber security\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Staying calm is an evaluative step that allows one to go to a safe place and help others as listed in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Emergency Contacts and Precautions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which service is associated with the phone number 100?\",\"options\":[\"Fire Brigade\",\"Ambulance\",\"Police Assistance\",\"Emergency Services\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly links the number 100 to Police Assistance.\",\"topic\":\"Emergency Contacts and Precautions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the immediate help given to an injured person before they reach a hospital?\",\"options\":[\"Final Treatment\",\"First Aid\",\"Medical Surgery\",\"Vaccination\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"First aid is defined as the immediate help given to an injured or ill person before hospital treatment.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a primary benefit of providing first aid?\",\"options\":[\"It replaces the need for a doctor\",\"It can save lives and reduce pain\",\"It helps in hitting the animal that bit\",\"It allows the person to run faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, first aid can save lives, reduce pain, and prevent infection.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the condition called when a large amount of blood flows from a wound?\",\"options\":[\"Internal Bleeding\",\"Sunstroke\",\"External Bleeding\",\"Blistering\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'External Bleeding\' as when a large amount of blood flows from a wound.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is bleeding, what should be done with the injured part if possible?\",\"options\":[\"Keep it below the level of the heart\",\"Raise it above the level of the heart\",\"Keep it at the same level as the feet\",\"Tie it tightly with a metal wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests raising the injured part above the level of the heart to help control bleeding.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which organs can have their functioning disturbed due to sunstroke?\",\"options\":[\"Lungs, Stomach, and Liver\",\"Brain, Heart, and Kidneys\",\"Skin, Hair, and Nails\",\"Eyes, Ears, and Nose\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sunstroke disturbs the functioning of vital organs like the brain, heart, and kidneys.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the skin of a person suffering from sunstroke?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes oily and cold\",\"It becomes sweaty and pale\",\"It becomes dry and hot\",\"It turns blue and moist\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a state of sunstroke, sweating stops and the skin becomes dry and hot.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What should you avoid giving to a person who is semi-conscious due to sunstroke?\",\"options\":[\"A wet cloth for wiping\",\"Water or any liquids to drink\",\"A shaded place to rest\",\"Light spray of water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states not to give water or liquids to a semi-conscious sunstroke patient.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a person\'s clothes catch fire, why is it dangerous for them to start running?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the person feel colder\",\"It causes the fire to flare up even more\",\"It helps the fire go out faster\",\"It prevents the fire from spreading\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Running causes the fire on the clothes to flare up even more and increases the risk of spreading the fire.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct way to extinguish fire on a person\'s clothes quickly?\",\"options\":[\"Make the person run in circles\",\"Pull off the clothes immediately\",\"Make the person roll quickly on the ground\",\"Burst any blisters that have formed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Making the person roll quickly on the ground helps extinguish the fire on clothes faster.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Regarding burns, which of the following actions is strictly prohibited by the text?\",\"options\":[\"Wiping the body with a wet cloth\",\"Bursting blisters that have formed\",\"Moving the person to a cool place\",\"Applying pressure with a clean cloth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'If blisters have formed due to the burn, do not burst them.\'\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these is listed as a \'What not to do\' for both Snake Bites and Dog Bites?\",\"options\":[\"Sucking out blood by mouth\",\"Hitting or killing the animal\",\"Use of home remedies\",\"Taking a photo of the animal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows \'Use of home remedies\' is listed under \'What not to do\' for both accidents.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/060_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, what should be done to help the doctor identify the treatment for a snake bite?\",\"options\":[\"Suck out the blood to show the doctor\",\"Take a photo of the snake if possible\",\"Make a cut at the bite site\",\"Catch the snake and bring it to the hospital\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table suggests taking a photo of the snake to show the doctor as a \'What to do\' action.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/060_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which vaccine should be obtained from a health centre immediately after a dog bite?\",\"options\":[\"Polio vaccine\",\"Rabies vaccine\",\"Sunstroke vaccine\",\"Bleeding vaccine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies getting the Rabies vaccine from a health centre after a dog bite.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where should a first aid kit be kept in a home or office?\",\"options\":[\"In a locked cupboard where no one can see it\",\"In a place where it is easily visible and can be used quickly\",\"Outside the building in a hidden spot\",\"Only in vehicles, not in offices\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The kit should be kept in a place where it is easily visible and can be used quickly.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to minimize movement of a person who has been bitten by a snake?\",\"options\":[\"To help them fall asleep faster\",\"To prevent them from getting sunstroke\",\"To avoid excessive movement which is listed as a \'What not to do\'\",\"To make it easier to take a photo\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Running or excessive movement\' as something not to do for snake bites.\",\"topic\":\"First Aid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21861,36,6,'Science','en','Properties of Substances','[\"All matter is made of tiny particles that occupy space and have mass.\",\"Solid: particles are close and fixed; shape and form stay the same.\",\"Liquid: particles are a bit apart and move; take the shape of the container.\",\"Gas: particles are far apart and move freely; fill any space.\",\"Change of state: when heat or cold changes particle arrangement (solid→liquid→gas).\",\"Density = mass ÷ volume; units g\\/cm³ or kg\\/m³.\",\"Transparent: light passes through (e.g., glass).\",\"Translucent: some light passes (e.g., frosted paper).\",\"Opaque: no light passes (e.g., wood).\",\"Magnetic substances are attracted to a magnet (iron, nickel, cobalt).\",\"Plasma: a high‑energy state found in stars and lightning.\",\"Water’s density is 1 g\\/cm³; gold’s is 19.3 g\\/cm³.\",\"If two objects have the same mass, the lighter one has greater volume.\",\"Heat makes solids melt into liquids; liquids boil into gases.\",\"Cooling a gas can make it liquid or solid (e.g., liquid nitrogen, dry ice).\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are all substances in the universe made up of?\",\"options\":[\"Large blocks\",\"Tiny particles\",\"Visible layers\",\"Only liquids\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all substances in the universe are made up of extremely minute particles.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a chair is made of wood, which of the following is the \'substance\'?\",\"options\":[\"The chair\",\"The shape\",\"The wood\",\"The size\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the chair is the object and the wood it is made from is the substance.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, several objects like a chair and a stool are shown. What is a common substance used to make these objects?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Wood\",\"Air\",\"Smoke\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions a chair can be made of wood, and the figure shows wooden furniture like the stool and chair.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What name did our ancestors give to the five elements: Earth, Water, Fire, Air, and Space?\",\"options\":[\"Panchtatve\",\"Substances\",\"Particles\",\"Matter\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ancient Indian scripts refer to these five elements as Panchtatve.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do solids like iron or wood not change their shape easily?\",\"options\":[\"Their particles are loosely bound\",\"Their particles are far apart\",\"Their particles hold each other tightly and do not move\",\"Their particles flow towards a slope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In solids, particles are very close and hold each other tightly, so they don\'t change position easily.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the tiny sparks flying off while the knife is being sharpened?\",\"options\":[\"Drops of water\",\"Small particles of red-hot iron\",\"Burnt particles of wood\",\"Particles of talcum powder\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that sparks flying while sharpening a knife are small particles of iron that become red-hot due to heat.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/065_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of liquid particles allows us to dip a finger into water easily?\",\"options\":[\"They are tightly packed\",\"They are loosely bound to each other\",\"They are red-hot\",\"They do not move at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Particles of water are loosely bound, allowing them to separate easily when a finger is dipped.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the particles of a solid when it is given enough heat?\",\"options\":[\"They move closer together\",\"They begin to move away from each other\",\"They disappear completely\",\"They turn into Panchtatve\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Heat causes the particles of a solid to move away from each other, transforming it into a liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is \'lava\' that flows out of a volcano?\",\"options\":[\"Molten rock in a liquid state\",\"A type of gaseous smoke\",\"Solid iron particles\",\"Cold water from the earth\'s crust\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lava is liquid formed by melting rocks from the earth\'s crust due to intense heat.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When an incense stick is lit, why can the fragrance be noticed far away even if the smoke is not visible?\",\"options\":[\"The particles are tightly bound\",\"The particles stay in one place\",\"The particles scatter and travel long distances\",\"The particles turn into a solid state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a gaseous state, particles scatter from one another and can travel long distances.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT one of the three states of matter mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gas\",\"Furnace\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies solid, liquid, and gas as the states of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To make objects out of glass, what must be done to the substance first?\",\"options\":[\"It must be frozen\",\"It must be melted in a furnace\",\"It must be turned into smoke\",\"It must be kept in a dark room\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that glass or iron must be melted in a furnace to make objects out of them.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the particles of a substance in the gaseous state?\",\"options\":[\"They hold on to each other tightly\",\"They flow only towards a slope\",\"They scatter in the available space\",\"They stay fixed in one position\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the gaseous state, particles go away from each other and scatter in the available space.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which state of matter is characterized by particles that separate easily and flow towards a slope?\",\"options\":[\"Solid state\",\"Liquid state\",\"Gaseous state\",\"Space state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The liquid state is defined by particles that are loosely bound and flow towards a slope.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you compare a solid and a gas, which one allows its particles to travel the longest distances by scattering?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gas\",\"Both solid and liquid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes gaseous particles as being able to travel long distances by scattering from one another.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and States of Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the state of a substance at any given instance?\",\"options\":[\"The color and smell of the substance\",\"How tightly its particles hold each other\",\"The size of the container it is kept in\",\"The weight of the substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the state of a substance is determined by how tightly its particles hold each other.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which state of matter are particles very close to each other and hold each other tightly in fixed positions?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid state\",\"Gaseous state\",\"Solid state\",\"Vapour state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in the solid state, particles are very close and hold each other tightly in fixed positions.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used when a substance remains the same but its form changes from one state to another?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical reaction\",\"Change of state\",\"Particle scattering\",\"Freezing point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'change of state\' as the process where the substance remains the same, only its state changes.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do liquids flow towards a slope and take the shape of their container?\",\"options\":[\"Their particles are independent of each other\",\"Their particles are very close and fixed\",\"Their particles are slightly further apart and loosely bound\",\"Their particles move far away from each other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that liquid particles are slightly further apart and loosely bound, allowing them to flow and take the container\'s shape.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what characteristic of the gaseous state is being demonstrated by the particles?\",\"options\":[\"Particles are holding each other tightly\",\"Particles are moving to a fixed position\",\"Particles are independent and scattering everywhere\",\"Particles are flowing towards a slope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that in the gaseous state, each particle is independent and they scatter everywhere in the available space.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/066_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the movement shown in the given figure, which substance would behave this way at normal temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Iron\",\"Milk\",\"Oxygen\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the gaseous state; the text mentions oxygen as a gas we are familiar with at normal temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/066_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is used in laboratories to keep certain things extremely cold?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid oxygen\",\"Liquid nitrogen\",\"Dry ice\",\"Boiling water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that liquid nitrogen is used in laboratories to keep things extremely cold.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is solid carbon dioxide commonly called?\",\"options\":[\"Cold ice\",\"Liquid gas\",\"Dry ice\",\"Frozen nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that solid carbon dioxide is called dry ice because it is very cold.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is used in rockets for space missions in its liquid state?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Oxygen\",\"Petrol\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that liquid oxygen is used in rockets used for space missions.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to coconut oil on cold winter days according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into vapour\",\"It solidifies\",\"It remains a liquid\",\"It turns into a gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that coconut oil solidifies on cold winter days.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a piece of wax is heated and then the heating is stopped, what sequence of changes occurs?\",\"options\":[\"It stays solid throughout\",\"It turns to gas and then to liquid\",\"It melts into liquid and then solidifies again\",\"It turns into dry ice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Heating wax causes it to melt (liquid), and stopping the heat causes it to cool back into a solid state.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are substances like stone, gold, and iron usually found in a solid state?\",\"options\":[\"They are at their normal temperature\",\"They cannot be converted to liquids\",\"They have no particles\",\"They are always cold\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text notes that stone, gold, and iron are solid at their normal temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sequence of states when a substance is cooled sufficiently from a gaseous state?\",\"options\":[\"Gas -> Solid -> Liquid\",\"Gas -> Liquid -> Solid\",\"Solid -> Liquid -> Gas\",\"Liquid -> Gas -> Solid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when cooled enough, vapour (gas) turns into liquid and liquid turns into solid.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the cap of a petrol tank is left open, the petrol level decreases. Which change of state is occurring?\",\"options\":[\"Solid to liquid\",\"Liquid to solid\",\"Liquid to vapour\",\"Vapour to liquid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Leaving the tank open allows the liquid petrol to turn into vapour (gas) and escape.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How can gases like nitrogen and oxygen be converted into a liquid state?\",\"options\":[\"By heating them\",\"By cooling them\",\"By keeping them in an open container\",\"By increasing their temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that by cooling gases like nitrogen and oxygen, they can be converted into a liquid state.\",\"topic\":\"Change of State\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are all solids, liquids, and gases made of?\",\"options\":[\"Only energy\",\"Particles\",\"Only water\",\"Empty space\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that solids, liquids, and gases are all made of particles.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which single word conveys that something is made of particles, occupies space, and has mass?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Volume\",\"Matter\",\"Plasma\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'Matter\' as something that is made of particles, occupies space, and has mass.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the state (solid, liquid, or gas) of a substance at a given instance?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the particles\",\"How tightly its particles hold each other\",\"The shape of the container\",\"The amount of water added\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the state of a substance is determined by how tightly its particles hold each other.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why does the water level rise when a large potato is dropped into a jar of water?\",\"options\":[\"The potato creates more water\",\"The potato occupies space\",\"The potato has no mass\",\"The water becomes a gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows the water level rises because the potato (matter) occupies space.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is mentioned as a separate state of matter found in the interior of the sun?\",\"options\":[\"Solid state\",\"Liquid state\",\"Gas state\",\"Plasma state\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the Plasma state as a separate state found in the sun\'s interior.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following substances takes the shape of its container?\",\"options\":[\"A brick\",\"A potato\",\"Milk\",\"A stone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that oil, milk, and water take the shape of their container.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where can the Plasma state be observed during the rainy season?\",\"options\":[\"In the clouds\",\"In lightning\",\"In the rain water\",\"In the mud\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the Plasma state occurs in lightning during the rainy season.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you push air into a liquid using a pipe, what will form according to the \'Think\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Crystals\",\"Bubbles\",\"Solids\",\"Ice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks why bubbles form when air is pushed into a liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the Plasma state considered different from the common states of matter?\",\"options\":[\"It has no particles\",\"It does not occupy space\",\"The nature of particles there is slightly different\",\"It only exists in a vacuum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says it is a separate state because the nature of particles there is slightly different due to immense energy.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of matter found in space according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Comets\",\"Only the Sun\",\"Only the Moon\",\"Empty space\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that substances in the Moon, the Sun, stars, and comets are all matter.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A brick maintains its shape whether kept vertically or horizontally. This is a property of which state?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid\",\"Gas\",\"Solid\",\"Plasma\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the brick as an example of something that maintains its shape, which is a characteristic of solids.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is required to create the conditions for the Plasma state in the sun?\",\"options\":[\"Immense amount of energy\",\"A large amount of water\",\"A plastic jar\",\"A syringe\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the Plasma state exists where an immense amount of energy is being created, like the sun\'s interior.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance is \'made of particles\', what can it be called?\",\"options\":[\"Only a liquid\",\"Matter\",\"Only a gas\",\"Non-existent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that every substance in the universe can be called matter because matter means \'made up of particles\'.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects would NOT change its shape when moved from a bowl to a jar?\",\"options\":[\"Oil\",\"Water\",\"A brick\",\"Milk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that bricks maintain their shape, unlike oil, milk, and water.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What can we conclude about the particles in a solid compared to a gas?\",\"options\":[\"They are exactly the same\",\"They hold each other with different tightness\",\"Solids have no particles\",\"Gases have no particles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the state is determined by how tightly particles hold each other.\",\"topic\":\"Matter and the Plasma State\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are the standard units used to express the density of a substance?\",\"options\":[\"g\\/ml or kg\\/L\",\"g\\/cm³ or kg\\/m³\",\"cm³\\/g or m³\\/kg\",\"g\\/m or kg\\/cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the units of density are g\\/cm³ or kg\\/m³.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Stating the mass of a substance for 1 cubic centimetre volume is called the ____ of that substance.\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Volume\",\"Density\",\"Mass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines density as the mass of a substance for 1 cubic centimetre volume.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a potato is dropped into a vessel filled to the brim with water, how much water will be pushed aside?\",\"options\":[\"Exactly as much as the mass of the potato\",\"Exactly as much as the weight of the potato\",\"Exactly as much as the volume of the potato\",\"Half the volume of the potato\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that an object pushes aside water exactly as much as the volume of that object.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is used to measure how strongly the earth pulls an object towards itself?\",\"options\":[\"Pan balance\",\"Measuring cylinder\",\"Injection syringe\",\"Spring balance\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when an object is hung on a spring balance, it measures the earth\'s pull, which is called weight.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A sample of a substance has a mass of 40 grams and a volume of 20 cm³. What is its density?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 g\\/cm³\",\"2 g\\/cm³\",\"60 g\\/cm³\",\"800 g\\/cm³\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Density = Mass \\/ Volume, 40 \\/ 20 = 2 g\\/cm³.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the volume of a liquid is measured as 25 millilitres (ml), what is its volume in cubic centimetres (cm³)?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 cm³\",\"250 cm³\",\"25 cm³\",\"0.25 cm³\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the volume of 1 cm³ liquid is 1 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to know the density of concrete and steel before constructing a bridge?\",\"options\":[\"To calculate the total weight and decide the strength of pillars\",\"To check if the steel will rust in water\",\"To measure the exact volume of water the bridge will push aside\",\"To determine the color of the foundation\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that knowing density helps calculate total weight to decide how strong the foundation or pillars need to be.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How can one estimate if a sample of gold is pure or adulterated?\",\"options\":[\"By measuring its weight on a pan balance\",\"By measuring its density and comparing it to the fixed density of pure gold\",\"By dropping it in a vessel of water to see if it floats\",\"By measuring its volume using a spring balance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that every substance has a fixed density, so measuring it helps estimate purity.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the density of pure gold as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1 g\\/cm³\",\"10.5 g\\/cm³\",\"19.3 g\\/cm³\",\"13.6 g\\/cm³\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the density of pure gold is 19.3 g\\/cm³.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these tools is described as a tall vessel made of glass or transparent plastic with markings to determine liquid volume?\",\"options\":[\"Pan balance\",\"Spring balance\",\"Measuring cylinder\",\"Vessel filled to the brim\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes a measuring cylinder as a tall vessel with markings used to determine volume.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what property is the girl on the left measuring by hanging the object on the instrument?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Weight\",\"Density\",\"Volume\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The girl is using a spring balance, which the text says measures the weight of the object.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/068_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the students are collecting the water that spills out into a tall vessel. What does the volume of this collected water represent?\",\"options\":[\"The mass of the object\",\"The density of the object\",\"The volume of the object\",\"The purity of the object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the volume of water pushed aside is precisely the volume of the object immersed.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/068_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the density of pure water?\",\"options\":[\"1 g\\/cm³\",\"19.3 g\\/cm³\",\"0.5 g\\/cm³\",\"10 g\\/cm³\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the density of pure water is 1 g\\/cm³.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical formula is used to determine the density of a substance?\",\"options\":[\"Density = Mass × Volume\",\"Density = Volume \\/ Mass\",\"Density = Mass \\/ Volume\",\"Density = Mass + Volume\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula: Density = Mass \\/ Volume.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a 1 cm³ piece of an unknown metal is found to have a mass of 8 grams, what is the density of that metal?\",\"options\":[\"1 g\\/cm³\",\"8 g\\/cm³\",\"0.125 g\\/cm³\",\"9 g\\/cm³\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Density is the mass of a 1 cm³ volume; since 1 cm³ weighs 8g, the density is 8 g\\/cm³.\",\"topic\":\"Density and its Measurement\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What are substances called through which we can see things on the other side clearly?\",\"options\":[\"Opaque substances\",\"Transparent substances\",\"Translucent substances\",\"Magnetic substances\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that substances like glass through which we can see things on the other side are called transparent.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a magnetic substance mentioned in the text besides iron and nickel?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Aluminum\",\"Cobalt\",\"Gold\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists iron, nickel, and cobalt as magnetic substances.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Substances like wood, cardboard, and soil that do not allow light to pass through are called:\",\"options\":[\"Translucent\",\"Transparent\",\"Opaque\",\"Magnetic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, wood, cardboard, metal sheets, and soil are opaque.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a translucent substance?\",\"options\":[\"Clean water\",\"Frosted glass\",\"Metal sheets\",\"Iron cupboard\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions frosted glass as a substance that allows a small amount of light to pass through, making it translucent.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common property of iron, nickel, and cobalt?\",\"options\":[\"They are all transparent\",\"They are all translucent\",\"They are attracted to a magnet\",\"They allow light to pass through them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Substances that are attracted to a magnet are called magnetic substances, which include iron, nickel, and cobalt.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we see things on the other side of a glass pane?\",\"options\":[\"Because light falls on it and stops\",\"Because it is a magnetic substance\",\"Because light passes through it\",\"Because it is an opaque substance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Light falling on glass passes through it, allowing us to see things on the other side.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when a magnet is brought near small, light objects like pins and nails?\",\"options\":[\"They are repelled by the magnet\",\"They are attracted toward the magnet\",\"They turn into translucent objects\",\"Nothing happens to them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that small, light objects like pins and nails are attracted toward a magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a translucent substance differ from a transparent one?\",\"options\":[\"It allows no light to pass through\",\"It allows all light to pass through\",\"It allows only a small amount of light to pass through\",\"It is attracted to iron\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Translucent substances allow a small amount of light to pass, unlike transparent ones which allow light to pass fully.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a decorative disc sticks to an iron cupboard, what is likely attached to the back of the disc?\",\"options\":[\"A piece of frosted glass\",\"A small black coin-like magnet\",\"A piece of oily paper\",\"A thin white plastic strip\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions a small black coin-like disc (magnet) is stuck to the back of decorative discs to make them stick to iron doors.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these materials would you choose to make a container if you need to see how much liquid is inside without opening it?\",\"options\":[\"Cardboard\",\"Metal sheet\",\"Clean plastic or glass\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Transparent materials like glass or certain plastics allow you to see through them.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to cover a window so that some light enters the room but people outside cannot see in clearly. Which material should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Clear glass\",\"Frosted glass\",\"A metal sheet\",\"A thick wooden board\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Frosted glass is translucent, meaning it allows some light but does not allow clear vision through it.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a mixture of iron nails and brass screws, how can you quickly separate the iron nails?\",\"options\":[\"By looking through them with frosted glass\",\"By using a magnet to attract the iron nails\",\"By placing them in clean water\",\"By wrapping them in oily paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Iron is a magnetic substance and will be attracted to a magnet, allowing for separation.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text\'s description of optical properties, how would you classify muddy water?\",\"options\":[\"Transparent\",\"Magnetic\",\"Opaque or Translucent\",\"Metallic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Clean water is transparent, but muddy water contains soil (opaque), making it either translucent or opaque depending on the mud.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following groups contains only materials that light cannot pass through?\",\"options\":[\"Glass, clean water, plastic\",\"Oily paper, frosted glass, milk bags\",\"Wood, cardboard, soil\",\"Iron, nickel, cobalt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Wood, cardboard, and soil are listed as opaque, meaning light does not pass through them.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a decorative disc stops sticking to an iron cupboard after the black disc falls off, what does this prove?\",\"options\":[\"The cupboard is no longer made of iron\",\"The decorative disc itself was not magnetic\",\"The decorative disc is transparent\",\"Iron is not a magnetic substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The attraction was due to the magnet (black disc); without it, the non-magnetic decorative disc cannot stick.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Substances\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are all substances made up of according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Cells\",\"Particles\",\"Liquids\",\"Crystals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that all substances are made up of particles.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which of the following can the \'Plasma\' state of matter be found?\",\"options\":[\"Deep oceans\",\"Inside caves\",\"Lightning\",\"Glaciers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Plasma is found in places like the sun, stars, and lightning.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the conversion of a substance from one state to another called?\",\"options\":[\"Binding of particles\",\"Change of state\",\"Density calculation\",\"Mass transfer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the conversion of a substance from one state to another as a change of state.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which formula is used to calculate the density of a substance?\",\"options\":[\"Mass + Volume\",\"Mass x Volume\",\"Mass \\/ Volume\",\"Volume \\/ Mass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula for density as Mass \\/ Volume.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the specific state (solid, liquid, or gas) of a substance at any given instance?\",\"options\":[\"The total mass of the particles\",\"The amount of light passing through it\",\"How tightly its particles are bound to each other\",\"The volume the substance occupies\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the state is determined by how tightly the particles are bound to each other.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what are the two essential properties of matter?\",\"options\":[\"It is transparent and has mass\",\"It occupies space and has mass\",\"It is always a solid and has volume\",\"It contains light and occupies space\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that matter occupies space and has mass.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a translucent substance differ from a transparent one?\",\"options\":[\"It allows no light to pass through\",\"It allows all light to pass through\",\"It allows only a little light to pass through\",\"It changes its state when light passes through\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines translucent substances as those through which a little light passes.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'matter\' mean in the context of this scientific topic?\",\"options\":[\"Something that is only found in stars\",\"Something made up of particles\",\"Something that is always transparent\",\"Something that has no mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the word matter means something made up of particles.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance has a mass of 50 grams and a volume of 10 milliliters, what is its density?\",\"options\":[\"500\",\"60\",\"5\",\"40\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Density = Mass \\/ Volume, 50 \\/ 10 = 5.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student is looking through a piece of wood and cannot see any light on the other side. What property does this wood show?\",\"options\":[\"Transparency\",\"Translucency\",\"Opaqueness\",\"Plasma state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Substances through which light does not pass at all are called opaque.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the binding between particles in a solid becomes loose enough for the substance to change its form, what process is described by the text?\",\"options\":[\"A change of state\",\"An increase in mass\",\"A decrease in transparency\",\"The creation of plasma\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text links the binding of particles to the state of matter and the conversion between them to a change of state.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the relationship between Plasma and the common states of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Plasma is a mixture of solid and liquid\",\"Plasma is the same as the gaseous state\",\"Plasma is a state different from solid, liquid, and gas\",\"Plasma is the state where light cannot pass through\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Plasma is a state which is different from solid, liquid, and gas.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21862,36,6,'Science','en','A Journey of Light','[\"Light is a form of energy that travels in straight lines.\",\"The eye is needed to see, but light is also essential; without light we cannot see even with open eyes.\",\"A source of light is any object that emits light; it can be natural (sun, stars, fireflies) or man‑made (lamp, bulb, torch).\",\"Light spreads in all directions from a source – this is called linear propagation.\",\"The Sun is the largest natural source of light for Earth and gives plants energy for photosynthesis.\",\"Light does not need a medium to travel; it can move through vacuum.\",\"A pinhole camera uses only a tiny hole to form an image; the image is inverted because rays travel straight.\",\"When light hits a surface it can be reflected, refracted or absorbed; reflection is when it turns back.\",\"The point where light meets a surface is the point of incidence; the perpendicular line at that point is the normal.\",\"The angle between the incident ray and the normal is the angle of incidence; the angle between the reflected ray and the normal is the angle of reflection.\",\"Laws of reflection: 1) Incident and reflected rays are on opposite sides of the normal. 2) Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection. 3) All three rays lie in the same plane.\",\"If the reflecting surface is smooth, all reflected rays are parallel – regular reflection (e.g., mirror).  If the surface is rough, reflected rays scatter – irregular reflection (e.g., stone).\",\"We see objects because light from them or from other sources is reflected to our eyes, often via irregular reflection.\",\"A pinhole camera produces an inverted image because the light that reaches the screen has crossed over.\",\"The moon is visible because sunlight is reflected from its surface, not because it emits light.\",\"Sound needs a medium; light does not, which is why we can hear sounds underwater but not see the sun through space.\",\"The image formed on a screen in a pinhole camera is clear when the hole is small and the screen is far from the source.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the term used to describe the absence of light?\",\"options\":[\"Visibility\",\"Darkness\",\"Shadow\",\"Reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the absence of light is what we call darkness.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which organ is mentioned as being closely related to the act of seeing?\",\"options\":[\"Brain\",\"Ear\",\"Eye\",\"Skin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text starts by stating that seeing is closely related to the eye.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides eyes, what else is just as essential for visibility?\",\"options\":[\"Air\",\"Light\",\"Mirror\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that for seeing or visibility, light is just as essential as the eyes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to objects in a room when it becomes completely dark?\",\"options\":[\"They disappear from the room\",\"They move to a different place\",\"They remain where they are but become invisible\",\"They change their shape\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in a dark room, objects are exactly where they are, yet we cannot see them.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these can make objects in a dark room visible?\",\"options\":[\"Opening your eyes wider\",\"A lit candle\",\"Waiting for a long time\",\"Touching the objects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says that as soon as a candle is lit, the objects become visible.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is in a pitch-black room with their eyes wide open, what will they see?\",\"options\":[\"Blurry shapes\",\"Faint colors\",\"Nothing\",\"The outline of objects\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if there is no light, we will see nothing even with our eyes wide open.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it impossible to see in a room where no lamp is turned on and no candle is lit?\",\"options\":[\"Because the eyes stop working\",\"Because objects are removed\",\"Because of the absence of light\",\"Because the room is too small\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that visibility is impossible without light, even if eyes are present.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A person enters a dark storage unit to find a box. Even though the box is right in front of them, they cannot find it. What should they do to make the box visible?\",\"options\":[\"Close their eyes tightly\",\"Turn on a lamp\",\"Search in another room\",\"Wait for their eyes to grow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text indicates that turning on a lamp makes objects in a dark room visible.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following conditions is necessary for visibility based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only having eyes\",\"Only having light\",\"Having both eyes and light\",\"Having neither eyes nor light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that light is just as essential as the eyes for seeing.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between light and seeing as described in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Seeing depends only on the strength of the eyes\",\"Light is the only thing needed to see\",\"Seeing is impossible without the presence of light\",\"Light makes objects move so we can see them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text emphasizes that without light, we see nothing, making it a requirement for visibility.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are objects or instruments that produce their own light called?\",\"options\":[\"Reflectors of light\",\"Sources of light\",\"Absorbers of light\",\"Shadows of light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines objects or instruments that produce their own light as sources of light.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what does the word \'source\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"An end\",\"A path\",\"An origin\",\"A reflection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'A source means an origin.\'\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a natural source of light mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Electric bulb\",\"Lantern\",\"Fireflies\",\"Oil lamp\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists fireflies, the sun, stars, and lightning as natural sources of light.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a lamp is lit in a dark place, surrounding objects become visible because light...\",\"options\":[\"stays inside the lamp\",\"spreads in all directions\",\"travels only upwards\",\"disappears into the dark\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that objects become visible because light emerging from a source spreads in all directions.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are things like torches and electric bulbs called \'Man-made sources of light\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are found in nature\",\"Because they produce very dim light\",\"Because they have been created by humans to give light\",\"Because they do not produce their own light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that these items are called man-made sources because they have been created by humans to give light.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student sees lightning in the sky during a storm, what type of light source are they observing?\",\"options\":[\"Man-made source\",\"Artificial source\",\"Natural source\",\"Electric source\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text classifies lightning in the sky as a natural source of light.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which group contains only man-made sources of light?\",\"options\":[\"Sun, Stars, Torch\",\"Oil lamp, Lantern, Electric bulb\",\"Fireflies, Lightning, Sun\",\"Stars, Fireflies, Lantern\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oil lamps, lanterns, and electric bulbs are all listed as man-made sources created by humans.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what can be concluded about the intensity of light from different sources?\",\"options\":[\"All sources of light have the same brightness\",\"Natural sources are always brighter than man-made sources\",\"Light from some sources is bright while from others it is dim\",\"Man-made sources never produce intense light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that light from some sources is bright and intense, while from others, it is very dim.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the common characteristic of all the objects shown?\",\"options\":[\"They are all natural sources of light\",\"They are all man-made sources of light\",\"They all use electricity to produce light\",\"They all produce light using oil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows an electric bulb, torch, street lamp, samai, diya, and lantern, all of which are man-made sources mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these is a traditional man-made source that uses oil to produce light?\",\"options\":[\"The electric bulb\",\"The torch\",\"The oil lamp (diya)\",\"The street lamp\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows an oil lamp (diya) which the text identifies as a man-made source of light.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of Light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What color light do fireflies emit from their bodies?\",\"options\":[\"Bright red light\",\"Pale blue light\",\"Green light\",\"Yellow light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that fireflies emit a green light from their bodies.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why do fireflies blink their light?\",\"options\":[\"To search for food in the dark\",\"As a signal to other fireflies nearby\",\"To frighten away large animals\",\"To help plants grow faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the blinking light is a signal sent to other fireflies nearby.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is the largest source of light for the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Fireflies\",\"Deep-sea fish\",\"The Sun\",\"Algae\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the Sun is the largest source of light for the Earth.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which specific environment can light-producing fish and algae be found?\",\"options\":[\"In shallow garden ponds\",\"In the deep parts of the ocean\",\"In fast-flowing mountain streams\",\"In marshy places during the day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that certain species of fish and algae produce light in the deep parts of the ocean.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What do plants prepare with the help of sunlight?\",\"options\":[\"Their own food\",\"Green signals for fireflies\",\"Water for the streams\",\"Marshy places for insects\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that plants prepare their own food with the help of sunlight.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"During which season are fireflies most commonly observed blinking like tiny lamps?\",\"options\":[\"Dry summer season\",\"Cold winter season\",\"Rainy season\",\"Autumn season\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes seeing fireflies on a dark night during the rainy season.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If all sunlight were blocked from a forest, what would happen to the flowers according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They would start emitting green light\",\"They would turn into micro-organisms\",\"They would not be able to bloom\",\"They would become man-made sources\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that sunlight is necessary for flowers to bloom.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the food prepared by plants that is not used for their own growth?\",\"options\":[\"It is turned into sunlight\",\"It is made available for animals\",\"It is used to produce green light\",\"It is sent to the deep ocean\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says plants use some food for growth, while the rest is available for animals.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following groups does NOT require light for their lives?\",\"options\":[\"Humans\",\"Micro-organisms\",\"Animals\",\"The text suggests all living things mentioned need light\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states light is essential for humans, micro-organisms, and animals.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a glowing organism in a deep ocean trench. How would this source of light be classified based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Man-made source\",\"Natural source\",\"Artificial signal\",\"Sun-based source\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text contrasts these organisms with man-made sources, implying they are natural.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is sunlight considered essential for animals even though they do not prepare food like plants?\",\"options\":[\"Animals need to blink like fireflies\",\"Animals depend on the food plants make using sunlight\",\"Animals need to produce green light in the forest\",\"Animals use sunlight to signal deep-sea fish\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes plants make food using sunlight, which is then available for animals.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the light of a firefly as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"A steady, man-made beam\",\"A signal that looks like a tiny blinking lamp\",\"A reflection of the Sun\'s light\",\"A heat source for deep-sea algae\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes fireflies as blinking like tiny lamps to send signals.\",\"topic\":\"Natural Sources in Nature\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What do we actually see floating in the air along the path of light?\",\"options\":[\"Water droplets\",\"Dust particles\",\"Light rays\",\"Air molecules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that we actually see the dust particles floating in the air along the path of light.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why are objects visible to us?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are large\",\"Because of light\",\"Because they are in the dark\",\"Because of dust particles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that objects are visible because of light.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"During which time can we see a beam of light entering a house through a window?\",\"options\":[\"Only at midnight\",\"Only at noon\",\"In the morning or evening\",\"Only during big events\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions seeing a beam of light entering the house through a window in the morning or evening.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which person will be able to see the candle flame?\",\"options\":[\"Person A\",\"Person B\",\"Both Person A and B\",\"Neither Person A nor B\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Person A is using a straight pipe, which allows the light from the flame to reach the eye.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/073_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the main difference between the pipes held by person A and person B?\",\"options\":[\"A\'s pipe is longer than B\'s\",\"A\'s pipe is straight while B\'s pipe is bent\",\"A\'s pipe is wider than B\'s\",\"A\'s pipe is made of a different material\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows that person A holds a straight pipe and person B holds a pipe with a bend.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/073_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is true based on the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"Light itself is easily seen\",\"Light itself is never seen\",\"Dust particles are invisible\",\"Objects are seen without light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'light itself is never seen\'.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where can beams of light be seen moving across the sky according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Inside a house\",\"During big events\",\"Through a window\",\"Inside a pipe\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions beams of light moved across the sky during big events.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a vehicle moves in the dark, what creates a visible beam of light?\",\"options\":[\"The engine\",\"The tires\",\"The headlights\",\"The dust particles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to a beam of light from the headlights of a vehicle in the dark.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment in the figure, why can person A see the flame while person B cannot?\",\"options\":[\"Person A has better eyesight\",\"Person A\'s pipe is straight, allowing light to pass\",\"Person B is further away from the candle\",\"The candle is closer to Person A\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Light travels in a path that is blocked by the bend in person B\'s pipe, whereas person A\'s straight pipe allows it to pass.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/073_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between light and dust particles as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Light is made of dust particles\",\"Dust particles make the path of light visible\",\"Dust particles hide the light\",\"Light destroys dust particles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says we see dust particles along the path of light, which makes the beam visible.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of Light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, what should be the size of the three pieces of cardboard?\",\"options\":[\"Different sizes\",\"Increasing sizes\",\"The same size\",\"Decreasing sizes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to take three pieces of cardboard of the same size.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where should the small hole be made in each cardboard?\",\"options\":[\"At the top corner\",\"Exactly in the center\",\"At the bottom edge\",\"On the right side\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The instructions state to make a small hole exactly in the center of each cardboard.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How must the cardboards be arranged to see the candle flame through the holes?\",\"options\":[\"In a semi-circle\",\"In a zig-zag pattern\",\"Exactly in a straight line\",\"One behind the other at different heights\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to arrange the pieces of cardboards exactly in a straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct position of the candle flame in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Above the cardboards\",\"Aligned with the holes\",\"Below the first hole\",\"To the side of the last cardboard\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies to place the candle so that the flame is aligned with the holes.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if you move one of the cardboards slightly to the side or up and down?\",\"options\":[\"The flame becomes brighter\",\"The flame changes color\",\"The candle flame can no longer be seen\",\"The hole becomes larger\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment asks if you can still see the flame after moving a cardboard, implying the view is blocked.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student moves the middle cardboard to the left, what must they do to see the flame again?\",\"options\":[\"Move the candle to the left\",\"Place the cardboard back in its proper position\",\"Make a new hole in the cardboard\",\"Look through the second cardboard instead\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to place the cardboard back in its proper position and observe again to see the flame.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can the flame only be seen when the holes are in a straight line?\",\"options\":[\"Because light travels in a straight line\",\"Because the cardboard is thick\",\"Because the candle is hot\",\"Because the holes are small\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The experiment demonstrates the linear propagation of light, meaning light travels in straight lines.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"From which position are you instructed to observe the flame?\",\"options\":[\"Through the hole of the first cardboard\",\"From above the cardboards\",\"From behind the candle\",\"Through the side of the cardboard\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says to look at the flame through the hole of the first cardboard.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you add a fourth cardboard with a hole that is not aligned with the others, what will be the result?\",\"options\":[\"The flame will appear blurry\",\"You will see two flames\",\"You will not be able to see the flame\",\"The light will bend to reach your eye\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on the experiment, if any cardboard is not aligned (moved to the side), the straight path of light is blocked.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the observation \'moving the cardboard up and down\' test in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"If light can travel in a curved path\",\"If the cardboard is heavy\",\"If the candle is tall enough\",\"If the holes are the same size\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Moving the cardboard tests if light can still reach the eye when the straight path is broken, proving it does not curve.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the property of light traveling in a straight line called?\",\"options\":[\"Circular propagation\",\"Linear propagation of light\",\"Medium propagation\",\"Atmospheric propagation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the property of light traveling in a straight line as \'linear propagation of light\'.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Up to what height from the Earth does the atmosphere of air exist?\",\"options\":[\"15 crore km\",\"1000 km\",\"100 km\",\"500 km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the atmosphere of air around the Earth exists only up to a height of about 100 km.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate distance of the Sun from the Earth mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"100 km\",\"15 crore km\",\"15 lakh km\",\"100 crore km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Sun is 15 crore km away from the Earth.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of sound propagation, what are substances like solids, liquids, and gases called?\",\"options\":[\"Vacuum\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Medium\",\"Linearity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines substances through which sound can travel (solids, liquids, and gases) as a \'Medium\'.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we hear the sound of a train by placing an ear against a railway track?\",\"options\":[\"Because light travels in a straight line\",\"Because sound can travel through solids\",\"Because there is a vacuum in the tracks\",\"Because the tracks are 100 km long\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The railway track is a solid, and the text explains that sound can travel through solids.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a key difference between light and sound mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Light travels through solids but sound does not\",\"Sound travels in a straight line but light does not\",\"Light does not need a medium to travel, but sound does\",\"Sound can travel through a vacuum but light cannot\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that while sound requires a medium to travel, light does not need any such medium.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What exists in the space between the Earth\'s atmosphere and the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"A vacuum\",\"A liquid medium\",\"A solid wooden plank\",\"Dense air\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that beyond the 100 km atmosphere, there is a vacuum in between the Sun and the Earth.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an astronaut is in a vacuum in space where there is no air, which of these would still reach them?\",\"options\":[\"Sound from a distant explosion\",\"Sunlight from the Sun\",\"Tapping sounds from a wooden plank\",\"Voice of a person swimming nearby\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that light does not require a medium and can travel through a vacuum, unlike sound.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are swimming underwater and hear a splash, this proves that sound travels through which medium?\",\"options\":[\"Solids\",\"Gases\",\"Liquids\",\"Vacuum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that hearing sounds while diving underwater means sound can travel through liquids.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why is the sun visible to us even though it is 15 crore km away and separated by a vacuum?\",\"options\":[\"Because the atmosphere extends all the way to the Sun\",\"Because light does not require a medium for its propagation\",\"Because light travels through the railway tracks\",\"Because the Sun is a solid medium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that light reaching us through a vacuum proves it does not require a medium for propagation.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the travel of sound through a wooden plank and light through a vacuum. What can be concluded?\",\"options\":[\"Both require a solid medium\",\"Both require a vacuum\",\"Sound needs the plank as a medium, but light needs nothing\",\"Light needs the plank as a medium, but sound needs nothing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows sound uses the plank (solid) as a medium, while light travels from the Sun through a vacuum (no medium).\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which observation provides the strongest evidence for the \'linear propagation of light\'?\",\"options\":[\"Light traveling from the Sun to Earth through a vacuum\",\"Hearing a train through a railway track\",\"Light traveling in a straight line\",\"Sound traveling through water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the property of light traveling in a straight line is called linear propagation.\",\"topic\":\"Linear Propagation of Light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What material is pasted over the square window of the pinhole camera?\",\"options\":[\"Thick black cardboard\",\"Butter paper or white kite paper\",\"A piece of clear glass\",\"A metal sheet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states to paste butter paper or white kite paper over the square window section.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT used in the construction of a pinhole camera according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Cardboard box\",\"Needle\",\"Lens\",\"Butter paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'No lens is used in this device.\'\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How do light rays emerge from the candle flame?\",\"options\":[\"Only in a straight line towards the hole\",\"Only in an upward direction\",\"In all directions\",\"Only towards the butter paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'Light rays emerge from the flame in all directions.\'\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the rays coming from the top of the flame when they pass through the hole?\",\"options\":[\"They go to the top of the screen\",\"They go to the bottom of the screen\",\"They stay in the center of the screen\",\"They are blocked by the hole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that rays coming from the top of the flame pass through the hole and go to the bottom of the screen.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What type of image of the candle flame is formed on the screen?\",\"options\":[\"A straight and blurred image\",\"A large and upright image\",\"An inverted (upside down) image\",\"A colorful and upright image\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that an inverted (upside down) image is formed because of the way light rays cross through the hole.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is this device called a \'Pinhole Camera\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it uses a very small lens\",\"Because the image is formed using only a tiny hole\",\"Because it is made of cardboard\",\"Because it is used to take photos of pins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Since an image is formed using only a tiny hole, it is called a Pinhole Camera.\'\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the size of the hole and the image quality?\",\"options\":[\"The larger the hole, the clearer the image\",\"The size of the hole does not affect the image\",\"The smaller the hole, the clearer the image\",\"A smaller hole makes the image disappear\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says: \'The smaller the hole, the clearer the image.\'\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do people use a pinhole camera to view a solar eclipse?\",\"options\":[\"To make the Sun look bigger\",\"To see the Sun in different colors\",\"Because looking at the Sun with bare eyes can damage them\",\"Because the Sun is too far to see without a box\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that looking at the Sun with bare eyes during an eclipse can damage eyes, so people use a pinhole camera.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To view an outside scene on the screen, where should the box be placed?\",\"options\":[\"In a bright garden\",\"Near a window in a dark room\",\"Under a bright electric lamp\",\"Inside a closed drawer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests: \'In a dark room, place this box near a window and try to view outside scene on a screen.\'\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a ray from the bottom of the flame reaches the top of the screen, what does this demonstrate about light?\",\"options\":[\"Light travels in a curved path\",\"Light rays cross each other as they pass through a tiny hole\",\"Light cannot pass through cardboard\",\"Light rays only travel upwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes rays from the bottom going to the top and rays from the top going to the bottom, showing how they cross through the hole.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to the clarity of the image if the needle hole was made much larger?\",\"options\":[\"The image would become clearer\",\"The image would become less clear\",\"The image would become upright\",\"The image would turn into a square\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the rule \'The smaller the hole, the clearer the image\', a larger hole would result in a less clear image.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is specifically mentioned for making the tiny hole in the cardboard?\",\"options\":[\"A nail\",\"A needle\",\"A pen\",\"A knife\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'make a tiny hole using a needle.\'\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Pinhole Camera\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the phenomenon called when light rays turn back from the surface of an object?\",\"options\":[\"Refraction of Light\",\"Reflection of Light\",\"Absorption of Light\",\"Transmission of Light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the turning back of light rays from the surface of an object as the \'Reflection of Light\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to light rays when they encounter a transparent glass obstacle?\",\"options\":[\"They turn back completely\",\"They are absorbed by the glass\",\"They pass through it\",\"They disappear\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, if the obstacle is a transparent glass, light passes through it.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the behavior of light when it hits a semi-transparent obstacle?\",\"options\":[\"All light passes through it\",\"No light passes through it\",\"Some light passes through and some reflects back\",\"Light turns back completely from the surface\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if an obstacle is semi-transparent, some light passes through and some reflects back.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ray of light falling on any surface called?\",\"options\":[\"Reflected ray\",\"Normal ray\",\"Incident ray\",\"Straight ray\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the ray of light falling on any surface as the \'Incident ray\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which point represents the \'Point of incidence\'?\",\"options\":[\"Point A\",\"Point B\",\"Point N\",\"Point O\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The diagram and text identify Point \'O\' as the point of incidence where the incident ray falls on the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the line perpendicular to the surface at the point of incidence called?\",\"options\":[\"Incident ray\",\"Normal\",\"Reflected ray\",\"Base line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A line perpendicular to the surface at the point of incidence is called the \'Normal\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which angle represents the \'angle of incidence\'?\",\"options\":[\"∠ BON\",\"∠ AOB\",\"∠ AON\",\"∠ NOB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and diagram specify that ∠AON is the angle made by the incident ray with the normal, known as the angle of incidence.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the ray that returns from the surface after hitting it?\",\"options\":[\"Incident ray\",\"Normal\",\"Reflected ray\",\"Transparent ray\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The ray that returns from the surface is called the \'Reflected ray\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student places a thick wooden board in the path of light, what will happen to the light rays?\",\"options\":[\"They will pass through the wood\",\"They will turn back from the wood\",\"They will be partially transmitted\",\"They will change into a different color\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wood is an opaque obstacle; the text states light will not pass through it and will turn back from there.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The angle made by the reflected ray with the normal is called the:\",\"options\":[\"Angle of incidence\",\"Angle of reflection\",\"Perpendicular angle\",\"Point of incidence\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The angle made by the reflected ray with the normal (∠ BON) is called the angle of reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a ray of light AO hits a surface at point O and returns as ray OB, what is ray OB called?\",\"options\":[\"Incident ray\",\"Normal\",\"Reflected ray\",\"Opaque ray\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on the reflection definitions, the ray that returns from the surface (OB) is the reflected ray.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a requirement for light to \'turn back\' completely from an obstacle?\",\"options\":[\"The obstacle must be transparent\",\"The obstacle must be semi-transparent\",\"The obstacle must be opaque\",\"The obstacle must be made of glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if there is an opaque obstacle like wood, light will not pass through and will turn back.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of Light\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the activity described, what is the name given to the perpendicular line MN?\",\"options\":[\"The Base\",\"The Normal\",\"The Reflection\",\"The Mirror Line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the perpendicular line MN is called the normal.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument from the compass box is required to measure the angles in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Divider\",\"Scale\",\"Protractor\",\"Compass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using a protractor from a compass box to measure the angles.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of using a wooden block or a water bottle in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"To hold the sticks in place\",\"To support the mirror upright\",\"To measure the height of the image\",\"To block extra light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to stand a mirror upright on the line using the wooden block or water bottle for support.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To observe the sticks in a single straight line, how should the second stick be placed?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel to the first stick\",\"Behind the mirror\",\"So it aligns with the image of the first stick\",\"Exactly on the normal MN\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity involves placing the sticks so they and the image appear to be in a single straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the first stick makes an angle of 40 degrees with the normal MN, what will be the angle of the second stick (aligned with the image) with the normal?\",\"options\":[\"20 degrees\",\"40 degrees\",\"80 degrees\",\"90 degrees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the laws of reflection implied in the activity, the angles made with the normal are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the instructions, how many different angles should you repeat the activity for to check the measurements?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically instructs to \'Repeat this activity for three different angles\'.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the adhesive tape or thread mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To mark the angles\",\"To color the sticks\",\"To ensure the mirror doesn\'t fall\",\"To draw the normal MN\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states to use tape or thread to ensure the mirror doesn\'t fall.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you place the coloured stick exactly on the normal MN, where will its image appear?\",\"options\":[\"At a 90-degree angle to the normal\",\"It will disappear\",\"Exactly on the normal line\",\"On the edge of the paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Placing the stick on the normal means the angle is zero; thus, the image will also align with the normal.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where should the point M be marked on the white sheet of paper?\",\"options\":[\"At the very corner\",\"Near the center of the straight line\",\"On the wooden block\",\"On the mirror surface\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to mark a clear point near the center of the straight line and name it M.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the student doing to prepare the experiment setup?\",\"options\":[\"Measuring the length of the stick\",\"Drawing a perpendicular line using a protractor\",\"Sticking the mirror to the paper\",\"Coloring the pen refills\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a student using a protractor and pencil to draw a line at a 90-degree angle (the normal).\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/075_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which required material is being placed on the white sheet of paper to mark the point M?\",\"options\":[\"A wooden block\",\"A protractor\",\"Adhesive tape\",\"A water bottle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows a protractor being used on the paper to mark the perpendicular line from point M.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/075_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student finds that the first stick makes a 60-degree angle with the normal, what can they conclude about the angle of the image seen in the mirror?\",\"options\":[\"The image is at 30 degrees to the normal\",\"The image is at 90 degrees to the normal\",\"The image makes the same 60-degree angle with the normal\",\"The image does not make an angle with the normal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity leads to the observation that the angles made by the stick and its image with the normal are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Try this - Laws of Reflection\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Laws of Reflection, what is the relationship between the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"The angle of incidence is greater than the angle of reflection\",\"The angle of incidence is smaller than the angle of reflection\",\"The angle of incidence and the angle of reflection are of equal measure\",\"The angle of incidence is always double the angle of reflection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection are of equal measure as one of the Laws of Reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal lie during reflection?\",\"options\":[\"In different planes\",\"In the same plane\",\"On the same side of the normal\",\"Parallel to each other\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One of the Laws of Reflection states that the incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal all lie in the same plane.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the reflection called when parallel light rays falling on a smooth surface remain parallel to each other after reflection?\",\"options\":[\"Irregular reflection\",\"Regular reflection\",\"Diffused reflection\",\"Scattered reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'Regular reflection\' as parallel rays remaining parallel after reflecting from a smooth surface like a plane mirror.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we see a stone on the road from any direction?\",\"options\":[\"Because it undergoes regular reflection\",\"Because it acts like a plane mirror\",\"Because it reflects light in many different directions\",\"Because it absorbs all the sunlight falling on it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A stone has a rough surface that reflects sunlight in many different directions, making it visible from any direction.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a light ray hits a smooth surface at an angle of incidence of 35 degrees, what will be the angle of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"70 degrees\",\"35 degrees\",\"0 degrees\",\"90 degrees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the Laws of Reflection, the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection are always equal.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the Laws of Reflection during irregular reflection?\",\"options\":[\"Laws of reflection are not followed\",\"Laws of reflection are followed only at some points\",\"Laws of reflection are followed at every single point\",\"Only the first law of reflection is followed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that even in irregular reflection, the laws of reflection are followed at every single point.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to parallel light rays when they fall on a rough surface?\",\"options\":[\"They remain parallel to each other\",\"They reflect in many different directions\",\"They stop reflecting completely\",\"They all travel in one single direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Light rays parallel to each other, falling on a rough surface, reflect in many different directions, which is called irregular reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the characteristic of the reflected rays on the \'Smooth Surface\' compared to the \'Rough Surface\'?\",\"options\":[\"They are scattered in different directions\",\"They are parallel to each other\",\"They do not follow the laws of reflection\",\"They cross each other at the normal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows that on a smooth surface, the reflected rays remain parallel to each other, unlike on the rough surface.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why do the reflected rays on the \'Rough Surface\' go in different directions even though the incident rays are parallel?\",\"options\":[\"The laws of reflection are broken on rough surfaces\",\"The angle of incidence is different for each ray due to the uneven surface\",\"Rough surfaces absorb the light rays instead of reflecting them\",\"The normal is the same for all points on a rough surface\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"On a rough surface, the orientation of the surface (and thus the normal) changes at every point, leading to different directions for reflected rays.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"We are able to see most of the objects around us primarily because of which phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"Regular reflection\",\"Irregular reflection\",\"Refraction of light\",\"Absence of reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that we are able to see most of the objects around us because of irregular reflection from their surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the reflected ray positioned in relation to the incident ray?\",\"options\":[\"On the same side of the normal\",\"On the opposite side of the normal\",\"Perpendicular to the normal\",\"Behind the reflecting surface\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first law of reflection states that the incident ray and the reflected ray are on opposite sides of the normal.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How can a \'beam of light\' be cast on a wall using a mirror in sunlight?\",\"options\":[\"By using irregular reflection from the mirror\",\"Because the mirror reflects rays in many different directions\",\"Because all reflected rays from the smooth mirror are parallel and travel in one direction\",\"By holding the mirror in a dark room\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A mirror is smooth and flat, so all reflected rays are parallel and travel in one direction, forming a beam.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student uses a sheet of plain white paper to reflect light. The light spreads out and does not form a sharp beam. What can you conclude about the paper\'s surface?\",\"options\":[\"It is perfectly smooth like a mirror\",\"It is a rough surface causing irregular reflection\",\"It does not follow the laws of reflection\",\"It is transparent to light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When light spreads out in many directions, it indicates irregular reflection caused by a rough surface.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between seeing an image in a mirror and seeing a tree outside?\",\"options\":[\"A tree does not reflect light, but a mirror does\",\"A mirror follows the laws of reflection, but a tree does not\",\"A mirror requires a specific viewing direction, while a tree can be seen from any direction\",\"A tree undergoes regular reflection, while a mirror undergoes irregular reflection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Regular reflection in a mirror requires a specific viewing angle, whereas irregular reflection from a tree allows it to be seen from any direction.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these surfaces would most likely produce a regular reflection?\",\"options\":[\"A piece of stone\",\"A wooden table\",\"A still water surface\",\"A brick wall\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A smooth and flat surface like still water acts like a mirror and produces regular reflection, unlike the other rough surfaces mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular Reflection\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Light is a form of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Matter\",\"Energy\",\"Force\",\"Mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that light is a form of energy.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a natural source of light mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Bulb\",\"Torch\",\"Star\",\"Candle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists stars as an example of natural sources of light.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument works based on the straight-line propagation of light?\",\"options\":[\"Microscope\",\"Telescope\",\"Periscope\",\"Pinhole Camera\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Pinhole Camera is an instrument based on the straight-line propagation of light.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is required for light to travel from one place to another?\",\"options\":[\"Air\",\"Water\",\"No medium is required\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that light does not require a medium for propagation.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the phenomenon called when light rays fall on an object and turn back from its surface?\",\"options\":[\"Refraction\",\"Reflection\",\"Propagation\",\"Absorption\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The turning back of light rays from a surface is defined as the reflection of light.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"We are able to see the objects around us because of which type of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"Regular reflection\",\"Irregular reflection\",\"Straight reflection\",\"Parallel reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that we see objects due to irregular reflection from rough surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle of incidence of a light ray is 40 degrees, what will be the measure of the angle of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"20 degrees\",\"80 degrees\",\"40 degrees\",\"90 degrees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the laws of reflection, the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection are always equal.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following objects will show regular reflection of light?\",\"options\":[\"A wooden table\",\"A piece of cloth\",\"New, shiny metal utensils\",\"A stone wall\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that reflection from a mirror or new, shiny metal utensils is regular reflection.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the laws of reflection, where do the incident ray and the reflected ray lie in relation to the normal?\",\"options\":[\"On the same side of the normal\",\"On opposite sides of the normal\",\"Parallel to the normal\",\"Behind the normal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first law of reflection stated in the text is that the incident ray and reflected ray are on opposite sides of the normal.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal?\",\"options\":[\"They all lie in different planes\",\"They all lie in the same plane\",\"Only the incident ray and normal lie in the same plane\",\"They never meet at a single point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The third law of reflection states that the incident ray, reflected ray, and the normal all lie in the same plane.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a rough surface result in irregular reflection?\",\"options\":[\"Because it absorbs all light\",\"Because it is not a natural source of light\",\"Because the surface is not smooth like a mirror\",\"Because it blocks the straight-line propagation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links irregular reflection to \'rough surfaces\', implying the lack of smoothness causes the irregularity.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which set contains only man-made sources of light?\",\"options\":[\"Sun and Bulbs\",\"Stars and Torches\",\"Bulbs and Torches\",\"Sun and Stars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text categorizes bulbs and torches as man-made sources.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29');
INSERT INTO `ep_topic_packs` VALUES (21863,36,6,'Science','en','Beyond the Sky','[\"The sky is the part of the atmosphere that we can see from the Earth’s surface.\",\"The atmosphere is a layer of gases around Earth; it becomes thinner at higher altitudes and almost none beyond about 100 km.\",\"Sunlight scatters in the atmosphere; blue light scatters more, so the sky looks blue during the day.\",\"Space is the region beyond the atmosphere where there are no gases to scatter light.\",\"Celestial objects are things that are outside the Earth’s atmosphere – the Sun, Moon, stars, planets, comets, satellites, etc.\",\"A constellation is a group of stars that form a recognizable pattern; the pattern stays the same but the stars move across the sky each night.\",\"Comets are made of dust, frozen gases and ice; when they approach the Sun the ice turns to gas and creates a glowing tail.\",\"A meteor is a rock that burns up in the atmosphere; if it reaches the ground it is called a meteorite.\",\"The Earth is a sphere; the northern hemisphere is above the equator, the southern below; the pole star is visible only from the northern hemisphere.\",\"The Sun is a star; it is about 14 million km from Earth and is much smaller and dimmer than most other stars in the Milky Way.\",\"The Milky Way (Akashganga) is a galaxy that contains billions of stars arranged in a spiral pattern.\",\"The Solar System is a small part of the Milky Way; it contains the Sun, eight planets, moons, asteroids and comets.\",\"ISRO (Indian Space Research Organisation) sends spacecraft to study the Moon, the Sun and Earth (e.g., Chandrayaan‑1, Mangalyaan, Aditya‑L1, NISAR).\",\"Light travels about 300 000 km in one second; it takes about 13 000 km to travel from Mumbai to New York, showing how big Earth is.\",\"Observing the sky is best from places with little light pollution, using binoculars or a telescope, and starting with western stars after sunset.\",\"Blind beliefs about comets or meteors are not based on science; they can cause unnecessary fear.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Around how many years ago were humans unaware of shelter, clothing, and cooked food?\",\"options\":[\"5-10 thousand years ago\",\"10-12 thousand years ago\",\"15-20 thousand years ago\",\"25-30 thousand years ago\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that around 15-20 thousand years ago, humans were not aware of shelter, clothing, and eating cooked food.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where did early humans rest when they were not wandering in search of food?\",\"options\":[\"In wooden huts\",\"In caves\",\"Under tall trees\",\"In stone houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, early humans wandered in jungles and rested in caves.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did early humans observe specific groups of stars and their changing positions?\",\"options\":[\"To count the number of stars\",\"To predict monsoon and winter seasons\",\"To find sources of water\",\"To hunt animals at night\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions they observed star groups to predict the monsoon and winter seasons.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did early humans identify directions while wandering in the jungle at night?\",\"options\":[\"By following animal tracks\",\"By looking at the cave paintings\",\"By looking at the stars\",\"By observing the wind flow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that while wandering at night, they identified directions by looking at the stars.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an early human noticed a group of stars shifting its position over several months, what were they likely trying to determine?\",\"options\":[\"The arrival of a new season\",\"The location of a nearby river\",\"The movement of wild animals\",\"The best time to draw cave paintings\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Observing changing positions of stars helped them predict seasons like monsoon and winter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which celestial objects did early humans observe that would appear and then disappear suddenly?\",\"options\":[\"The Moon\",\"Glittering stars\",\"Comets\",\"Planets\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that they observed comets which disappeared suddenly.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what was a major consequence of comets colliding with the Earth\'s surface in ancient times?\",\"options\":[\"Creation of new stars\",\"Extinction of humans and animals\",\"Discovery of fire\",\"Change in the direction of stars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the \'Extinction of Humans and Animals due to collision of comets against Earth\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What did early humans use as a canvas to record events like comet collisions?\",\"options\":[\"Animal skins\",\"Tree bark\",\"Stones of the caves\",\"Clay tablets\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states they drew paintings of these events on the stones of the caves.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the appearance and activity of the people suggest about their lifestyle?\",\"options\":[\"They lived in well-built shelters.\",\"Their life was similar to that of wild animals.\",\"They were experts in farming and agriculture.\",\"They wore stitched clothing for protection.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows humans without modern shelter or clothing, which the text describes as being similar to wild animals.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/080_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what evidence is shown that early humans recorded their observations of the sky and nature?\",\"options\":[\"A written diary found in the cave\",\"Paintings on the cave walls\",\"Tools made from fallen comets\",\"Maps drawn on the ground\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows cave paintings, which the text explains were used to record events like comet collisions.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/080_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best explains why early humans were \'amazed\' by the sky?\",\"options\":[\"Because the sky was always dark\",\"Because they used stars to hunt\",\"Because of the glittering stars and sudden comets\",\"Because they wanted to travel to the moon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links their amazement to the glittering stars and the observation of comets.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can we conclude about early humans from the fact that they predicted seasons using stars?\",\"options\":[\"They had no interest in nature.\",\"They possessed basic astronomical knowledge.\",\"They only traveled during the winter.\",\"They lived in groups to stay warm.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using star positions to predict seasons shows they had developed knowledge of celestial movements.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the thick envelope of air around the earth called?\",\"options\":[\"Space\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Vacuum\",\"Celestial region\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the thick envelope of air around the earth as the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the sky appear blue during the day?\",\"options\":[\"Because the air is very dense at sea level\",\"Because water vapour turns into blue clouds\",\"Because sunlight scatters more blue colour when falling on particles\",\"Because the space beyond the sky is blue\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sunlight falls on gas, dust, and water vapour particles, scattering blue colour more, making the sky appear blue.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"At what height from the surface of the earth does air become very rare and eventually disappear?\",\"options\":[\"Around 10 km\",\"Around 20 km\",\"Around 100 km\",\"Around 450 km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that at around 100 km from the surface, the air becomes very rare and beyond that, there is no air.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the main difference between \'Sky\' and \'Space\'?\",\"options\":[\"Sky is blue while Space is always white\",\"Sky contains the atmosphere while Space is the empty region beyond it\",\"Sky is endless while Space ends at 100 km\",\"Sky contains planets while Space contains clouds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines sky as the region where the atmosphere extends, and space as the empty region beyond the sky.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If clouds are found around 6 km from the surface, how much higher is the peak of Mount Everest compared to the clouds?\",\"options\":[\"2.8 km\",\"8.8 km\",\"14.8 km\",\"4.0 km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mount Everest is 8.8 km and clouds are at 6 km; the difference is 8.8 - 6 = 2.8 km.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What would an observer see looking at the sky from a region where there is no atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"A bright blue sky\",\"A sky filled with thick dust\",\"The sky would not appear blue\",\"A sky filled with water vapour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"No atmosphere means no particles to scatter sunlight, hence the sky does not appear blue.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A satellite is orbiting the earth at an altitude of 400 km. Based on the text, what type of satellite is it?\",\"options\":[\"Geostationary satellite\",\"Aditya L-1 spacecraft\",\"Navigational satellite\",\"Weather kite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Navigational satellites as being around 300 to 450 km.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the density of air on high mountains like the Himalayas?\",\"options\":[\"The air is denser than at sea level\",\"The air is rarer than at sea level\",\"There is no air on high mountains\",\"The air contains only dust particles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that air is rarer on high mountains like the Himalayas compared to sea level.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"At what approximate distance from Earth are Geostationary satellites located?\",\"options\":[\"Around 450 km\",\"Around 20 km\",\"Around 36,000 km\",\"Around 15-lakh km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states Geostationary Satellites are around 36,000 km.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which objects are shown flying within the lower, light-blue shaded region of the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Space station and Navigational satellites\",\"Passenger aircraft and Fighter planes\",\"Geostationary satellites and Spacecraft\",\"Only the Space station\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the aircraft in the lower blue atmosphere, while satellites are in the darker upper region.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/082_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the transition from blue to black color most likely represent?\",\"options\":[\"The change from day to night\",\"The boundary where the sky ends and space begins\",\"The difference between clouds and mountains\",\"The path of a fighter plane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The transition represents the end of the atmosphere (sky) and the beginning of the empty region (space).\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/082_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How much further away is the Aditya L1 spacecraft compared to a Geostationary satellite?\",\"options\":[\"14,64,000 km\",\"15,36,000 km\",\"36,000 km\",\"15,00,000 km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Aditya L1 (15,00,000 km) minus Geostationary (36,000 km) equals 14,64,000 km.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a Jet aircraft NOT be called a spacecraft?\",\"options\":[\"Because it flies at 10 km, which is within the atmosphere (sky)\",\"Because it is smaller than a satellite\",\"Because it only flies over the Himalayas\",\"Because it does not carry passengers\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Spacecraft travel into space, whereas Jet aircraft fly at altitudes (like 10-20km) well within the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, where does lightning during the monsoon occur?\",\"options\":[\"In space\",\"In the sky\",\"Beyond 100 km\",\"Near Geostationary satellites\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lightning occurs during monsoon, and clouds (where lightning happens) are at 6 km, which is in the sky.\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is described as an \'endless\' region?\",\"options\":[\"The Atmosphere\",\"The Sky\",\"Space\",\"Mount Everest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Space is endless.\'\",\"topic\":\"Sky and Space\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did the use of telescopes for observing stars and planets begin?\",\"options\":[\"1509 A.D.\",\"1609 A.D.\",\"1709 A.D.\",\"1809 A.D.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the use of telescopes started around 400 years ago in 1609 A.D.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these celestial features was discovered using earlier telescopes?\",\"options\":[\"Conditions on unknown planets\",\"Events billions of kilometers away\",\"Craters on the moon\",\"Infrared rays from space\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that using earlier telescopes, information was obtained about craters on the moon, sunspots, and Jupiter\'s satellites.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Before telescopes were invented, how did humans gather information about the sky?\",\"options\":[\"By using spacecrafts\",\"By observing with the naked eye\",\"By using X-ray photography\",\"By using powerful lenses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in earlier times, people learnt information just by observing with their naked eye.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many satellites of Jupiter were identified using earlier telescopes?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Six\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions that earlier telescopes provided information about the \'four satellites of Jupiter\'.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was known to humans even before the telescope was invented?\",\"options\":[\"The existence of sunspots\",\"The occurrence of solar and lunar eclipses\",\"The conditions on distant planets\",\"The craters on the moon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists solar and lunar eclipses as things people knew about before telescopes through naked-eye observation and calculations.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a modern instrument is designed to gather information from billions of kilometers away, which rays might it use according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sound waves\",\"Visible light only\",\"X-rays, Ultra-violet and Infrared rays\",\"Microwaves and Radio waves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that instruments gathering information from billions of kilometers away were designed using X-rays, Ultra-violet, and Infrared rays.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main difference between earlier telescopes and the telescopes made later by scientists?\",\"options\":[\"Later telescopes were less powerful\",\"Later telescopes could only see the moon\",\"Later telescopes were more powerful and collected information about unknown planets\",\"Later telescopes were used only for naked eye observation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that later, scientists made more powerful telescopes that collected information about unknown planets and their conditions.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what has been the primary source of information about far away planets by traveling to them?\",\"options\":[\"Constellations\",\"Spacecrafts\",\"Sunspots\",\"Calculations\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that spacecrafts which travelled to far away planets sent information about them.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the timeline provided in the text, for approximately how many years have humans been using telescopes?\",\"options\":[\"100 years\",\"200 years\",\"400 years\",\"1600 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the use of telescopes started around 400 years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was NOT possible for humans to know before 1609 A.D.?\",\"options\":[\"When a solar eclipse would occur\",\"The time taken by planets to revolve around the sun\",\"The detailed appearance of sunspots\",\"Which constellations are visible in the sky\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sunspots were discovered using earlier telescopes, meaning they were not known through naked-eye observation before 1609 A.D.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why has our knowledge about space increased significantly in the last hundred years?\",\"options\":[\"Because we stopped using calculations\",\"Because of the study involving advanced instruments and spacecrafts\",\"Because planets have moved closer to the Earth\",\"Because we only rely on naked eye observations now\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that because of the study using advanced instruments and spacecrafts, we have gathered a lot of new information in the last hundred years.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What did people use to make predictions about the sky before the invention of telescopes?\",\"options\":[\"Photographs\",\"X-ray machines\",\"Calculations based on naked eye observations\",\"Infrared sensors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says people made predictions and calculations based on information learnt by observing with their naked eye.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Telescopes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the specific groups or collections of stars in the sky called?\",\"options\":[\"Planets\",\"Constellations or nakshatra\",\"Solar systems\",\"Comets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that groups or collections of stars are called constellations or nakshatra.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many planets are mentioned as revolving around the sun in our system?\",\"options\":[\"Seven\",\"Nine\",\"Eight\",\"Ten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, there are only eight planets revolving around the sun.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction do groups of stars appear to change their places over time?\",\"options\":[\"North to South\",\"West to East\",\"South to North\",\"East to West\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that these groups of stars keep changing their places from east to west.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a constellation moves across the sky, what aspect of it remains the same?\",\"options\":[\"Its position in the sky\",\"The figure of the group\",\"Its distance from the equator\",\"Its brightness compared to planets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the figures of these groups of stars remain the same but their position changes.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the central line that divides the Earth into two halves?\",\"options\":[\"Axis\",\"Orbit\",\"Equator\",\"Horizon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the line dividing the sphere as the \'Equator\'.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/084_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the white lines connecting the bright dots represent?\",\"options\":[\"Flight paths of planets\",\"The shape or figure of a constellation\",\"Lightning in the night sky\",\"Boundaries of the atmosphere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows stars connected to form specific figures, which the text defines as constellations.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to agricultural proverbs, what does the word \'Moti\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"A precious stone found in the sea\",\"A specific bright star\",\"A rich crop yield\",\"The name of a planet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in the context of these proverbs, \'Moti\' means a rich crop.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it impossible to see all the constellations in the sky at one time?\",\"options\":[\"Because stars are too dim to see\",\"Because the Earth is a huge sphere and we only see a little part of the sky above us\",\"Because planets block the view of the stars\",\"Because constellations only appear during the monsoon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because the Earth is a sphere, we can only see a little part of the sky above our head at once.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the part of the Earth that lies above the equator?\",\"options\":[\"Southern hemisphere\",\"Eastern hemisphere\",\"Northern hemisphere\",\"Western hemisphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the part of the earth that lies above the equator as the northern hemisphere.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific star is mentioned as being invisible to people in the southern hemisphere?\",\"options\":[\"The Sun\",\"The Pole star\",\"The Swati star\",\"The Pushya star\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the pole star present in the northern direction cannot be seen from the southern hemisphere.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the proverb provided, which constellation\'s period is associated with timely and beneficial rain?\",\"options\":[\"Pushya\",\"Swati\",\"Moti\",\"Equator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that timely and beneficial rain happens especially during the Swati constellations period.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How do stars (other than the eight planets) differ from planets according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are not visible at night\",\"They are self-luminous\",\"They do not form groups\",\"They move from west to east\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes stars as being self-luminous like the sun, unlike the planets.\",\"topic\":\"Constellations or Nakshatra\",\"part\":