ion\",\"Upside down position\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to tie the bar magnet so that it hangs freely in a horizontal position.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Magnets\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what will happen to the magnet after it stops moving?\",\"options\":[\"It will point in the East-West direction\",\"It will point in the North-South direction\",\"It will keep spinning forever\",\"It will point towards the wooden stand\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a freely suspended magnet always aligns itself in the north-south direction.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Magnets\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/104_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you push the coloured end of a freely suspended magnet to point towards the East, what will happen when it becomes stable again?\",\"options\":[\"It will remain pointing towards the East\",\"It will point towards the West\",\"It will return to the North direction\",\"It will point towards the ground\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that every time the magnet gets stable, it points in one direction only, which is North.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Magnets\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name given to the end of the magnet that points towards the south?\",\"options\":[\"North pole\",\"South pole\",\"East pole\",\"West pole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the end which points out in the south as the south pole of the magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Magnets\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is being demonstrated by moving a second magnet near the suspended one?\",\"options\":[\"How to paint a magnet\",\"How to change the north-south position using magnetic force\",\"How to weigh a magnet using a thread\",\"How to make a magnet lose its strength\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity shows that the position of a suspended magnet can be changed by