e direction?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Steel\",\"Plastic\",\"Cobalt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A plastic box is non-magnetic and will not interfere with the magnetic needle, unlike iron.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Compass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Ramesh made a compass using an iron box. Why will his compass NOT show the accurate direction?\",\"options\":[\"Iron is too heavy to carry\",\"Iron is a magnetic material that will affect the needle\'s movement\",\"Iron boxes cannot be used in the sea\",\"Iron boxes do not have enough space for the needle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since iron is magnetic, it will attract the needle and prevent it from pointing to the true North-South direction.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Compass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a magnetic compass particularly useful in places like deserts and seas?\",\"options\":[\"Because these places have no landmarks to help identify directions\",\"Because magnets only work in hot or wet climates\",\"Because iron boxes are not available in these places\",\"Because the needle rotates faster in these areas\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In vast areas like seas and deserts where there are no visible landmarks, a compass is essential to find directions.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Compass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a magnetic substance?\",\"options\":[\"Glass\",\"Wood\",\"Cobalt\",\"Plastic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists Iron, Cobalt, and Nickel as magnetic substances.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are substances that are not attracted towards a magnet called?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic substances\",\"Non-magnetic substances\",\"Artificial magnets\",\"Magnetic poles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Substances not attracted to a magnet are defined as non-magnetic substances.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which part of a magnet is the magnetic force the greatest?\",\"options\":[\"The middle part\",\"The entire surface\",\"The poles\",\"Only the North pole\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the parts of the magnet with greater magnetic force are the poles.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when two \'like\' poles of a magnet are brought near each other?\",\"options\":[\"They attract each other\",\"They push each other away\",\"They break into pieces\",\"They lose their magnetic force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that like poles of a magnet show repulsion, meaning they push each other away.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which device is specifically used to find out directions?\",\"options\":[\"A plastic ruler\",\"A magnetic compass\",\"An iron rod\",\"A wooden block\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a magnetic compass is used to find out directions.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student has a magnet and wants to separate the North pole from the South pole. Is this possible?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, by breaking the magnet in half\",\"Yes, by heating the magnet\",\"No, the poles cannot be separated\",\"No, because magnets only have one pole\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text clearly states that the north and south poles of a magnet cannot be separated from each other.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a North pole of one magnet is brought near the South pole of another magnet, what will occur?\",\"options\":[\"Repulsion\",\"Attraction\",\"No reaction\",\"The poles will disappear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Unlike poles (North and South) show attraction according to the text.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are magnets made in different shapes like bars or horseshoes?\",\"options\":[\"To make them look colorful\",\"Because they are found that way in nature\",\"They are made artificially as per the need\",\"To make them non-magnetic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that magnets with different shapes are made artificially as per the need.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A suspended magnet is left to swing freely. In which direction will it always settle?\",\"options\":[\"East-West\",\"North-South\",\"Up-Down\",\"Any random direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The principle of a magnetic compass is that a suspended magnet always settles in the north-south direction.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these materials would be suitable to make a non-magnetic toy?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Nickel\",\"Cobalt\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Wood is listed as an example of a non-magnetic substance.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which principle allows a magnetic compass to work?\",\"options\":[\"Like poles repel each other\",\"A suspended magnet settles in a specific direction\",\"Magnetic force is strongest at the poles\",\"Poles cannot be separated\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the compass works on the principle that a suspended magnet always settles in north-south direction.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a substance that is attracted to a magnet, which of the following could it be?\",\"options\":[\"Glass\",\"Plastic\",\"Iron\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Iron is identified in the text as a magnetic substance, meaning it is attracted towards a magnet.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21866,36,6,'Science','en','Force and Pressure','[\"Force is the effort applied to move or stop an object.\",\"Muscular force is produced by muscles and bones.\",\"Mechanical force is produced by machines such as wheels, levers and engines.\",\"Earth’s gravitational force pulls all objects toward the Earth.\",\"Magnetic force attracts iron and other magnetic materials to a magnet.\",\"Frictional force opposes motion and reduces speed.\",\"Electrostatic force is produced by static electric charges on rubbed surfaces.\",\"Balanced forces are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction; the object stays at rest or moves with constant speed.\",\"Unbalanced forces are unequal; the object changes its motion.\",\"Pressure is the force applied per unit area: P = F\\/A.\",\"For the same force, smaller contact area gives higher pressure.\",\"Atmospheric pressure is the weight of the air above us; it decreases with height.\",\"Wind and storms are caused by differences in atmospheric pressure.\",\"Pressure is measured in pascals (Pa) = N\\/m².\",\"Friction can be reduced by lubricants, making machines run faster and last longer.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the effort applied to push, pull, stop, or throw an object called?\",\"options\":[\"Energy\",\"Force\",\"Motion\",\"Speed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the effort applied to perform activities like pushing, pulling, or stopping as \'Force\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what happens to a stationary object when force is applied to it?\",\"options\":[\"It stays at rest\",\"It changes its place\",\"It disappears\",\"It loses its weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if force is applied on a stationary object, it changes its place.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is a \'moving object\' defined in the given text?\",\"options\":[\"An object that is very heavy\",\"An object that stays in one position\",\"An object that keeps changing its position continuously\",\"An object that is being picked up\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a moving object as one that keeps changing its position continuously.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following activities mentioned in the text requires the application of force?\",\"options\":[\"Thinking about a bag\",\"Looking at a switch\",\"Pressing a light switch\",\"Watching a box\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists \'pressing light switch\' as an activity where we need to apply force.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is required to bring a moving object to rest?\",\"options\":[\"Continuous position change\",\"Force\",\"Stationary effort\",\"Picking it up\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a force is needed to bring a moving object to rest.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is cleaning a desk, which of the following is true based on the textbook description?\",\"options\":[\"No effort is being used\",\"The desk is changing its position continuously by itself\",\"The student is applying force\",\"The desk is at rest and cannot move\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'cleaning\' as an activity where we need to apply force.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a purpose for applying force in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To throw an object\",\"To pick an object\",\"To give pressure on an object\",\"To make an object disappear\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions push, pull, stop, throw, pick, and pressure, but not making an object disappear.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When opening a compass box, what are you doing according to the lesson?\",\"options\":[\"Applying force\",\"Bringing it to rest\",\"Keeping it stationary\",\"Reducing pressure\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Opening a compass box is listed as an activity where we need to apply force.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what must the person apply to make the stationary bicycle start moving?\",\"options\":[\"Force\",\"Rest\",\"Continuous position\",\"Stationary effort\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text and figure caption indicate that a force is needed to make a stationary object move.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/110_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure and its caption, what is necessary to stop the bicycle while it is in motion?\",\"options\":[\"Wait for it to change place\",\"Apply force\",\"Keep it moving continuously\",\"Pick it up\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The caption for the figure states that a force is also needed to bring a moving object to rest.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/110_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The force applied with the help of muscles is known as which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Mechanical force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Magnetic force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines the force applied with the help of muscles as muscular force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the force applied by means of a machine called?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force\",\"Chemical force\",\"Mechanical force\",\"Biological force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the force applied by means of a machine is called mechanical force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Movement in the human body occurs with the help of which two parts while performing tasks?\",\"options\":[\"Skin and hair\",\"Bones and muscles\",\"Nails and teeth\",\"Blood and nerves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that movement occurs with the help of bones and muscles in the body.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides humans, which other living beings use muscular force to perform activities?\",\"options\":[\"Plants\",\"Animals\",\"Microbes\",\"Fungi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that like humans, animals also use muscular force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of force is being used by the bullocks to plow the field and the players to play the game?\",\"options\":[\"Mechanical force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Electrical force\",\"Magnetic force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows activities like playing and agricultural work done by animals\\/humans, which use muscular force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/111_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of force is primarily applied by the equipments shown, such as the mixer and the motorcycle?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force\",\"Mechanical force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Static force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The equipments shown are machines, and force applied by machines is mechanical force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/111_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding the use of machines?\",\"options\":[\"Machines never require muscular force.\",\"Machines only work with gravitational force.\",\"Muscular force is still required while working with some machines.\",\"Machines only use bones for movement.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that even while working with some machines, muscular force is still required.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following activities is an example of muscular force mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A mixer grinding spices\",\"A motorcycle running on the road\",\"Playing games\",\"A thresher machine working in a field\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists playing games as an activity where movement occurs via muscular force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a farmer is using a tractor instead of bullocks to plow a field, what change in the type of force is primarily occurring?\",\"options\":[\"From mechanical to muscular force\",\"From muscular to mechanical force\",\"From muscular to gravitational force\",\"From magnetic to mechanical force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bullocks use muscular force, while a tractor (a machine) uses mechanical force.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the force used by a cheetah chasing a deer classified as muscular force?\",\"options\":[\"Because it involves the use of a machine.\",\"Because it occurs with the help of bones and muscles.\",\"Because it is a type of agricultural activity.\",\"Because it does not involve any movement.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Muscular force is defined as force applied with the help of muscles, which is how animals move.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular and Mechanical Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the force called when the Earth attracts all objects towards itself?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Mechanical force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Muscular force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the force by which Earth attracts all objects towards itself is called Earth’s gravitational force.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these happens to a ball released from the hand?\",\"options\":[\"It flies upwards\",\"It falls to the ground\",\"It stays stationary in the air\",\"It moves in a circle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a ball released from the hand falls to the ground.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the speed of an object thrown upwards due to gravitational force?\",\"options\":[\"It increases gradually\",\"It remains the same\",\"It decreases gradually\",\"It stops immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to gravitational force, the speed of an object thrown upwards decreases gradually.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what force is responsible for the fruits lying on the ground?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force\",\"Earth\'s gravitational force\",\"Mechanical force\",\"Frictional force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that fruits from trees fall to the ground because Earth attracts all objects towards itself via gravitational force.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/111_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you throw a small piece of chalk in an upward direction, what will be the final result?\",\"options\":[\"It will continue to go up forever\",\"It will disappear in the sky\",\"It will come down again towards Earth\",\"It will stay fixed at a height\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks why objects thrown upwards come down again and explains it is due to Earth\'s gravitational force.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A kite is flying in the sky and its string gets cut. Based on the textbook topic, what will happen to the kite?\",\"options\":[\"It will fall to the ground\",\"It will move towards the sun\",\"It will turn into a ball\",\"It will stop moving entirely\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'a cut kite\' as an example of an object that falls to the ground due to gravity.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Does the gravitational force act on a stone that is just sitting still on the ground?\",\"options\":[\"No, it only acts on falling objects\",\"No, it only acts on objects thrown up\",\"Yes, it acts even on stationary objects\",\"Yes, but only if the stone is very large\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'The gravitational force acts even on the stationary objects on the surface or ground.\'\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if a fruit is still hanging on the tree and not moving, which statement is true according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"No force is acting on the fruit\",\"Gravitational force is acting on the fruit\",\"Only magnetic force is acting on the fruit\",\"Gravity only acts once the fruit starts falling\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that gravitational force acts even on stationary objects on the surface or ground.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/111_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does an eraser thrown upwards eventually come down?\",\"options\":[\"Because its speed increases as it goes up\",\"Because the Earth attracts it towards itself\",\"Because the air pushes it downwards\",\"Because it loses its mass in the air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that objects come down because Earth attracts all objects around it towards itself.\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a correct observation about Earth\'s gravitational force mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It only attracts fruits and balls\",\"It makes objects move faster as they go up\",\"It attracts all objects around the Earth towards itself\",\"It only works on objects that are released from the hand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'Earth attracts all the objects around it, towards itself.\'\",\"topic\":\"Gravitational Force\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who wrote the book titled \'Siddhant Shiromani\'?\",\"options\":[\"Sir Isaac Newton\",\"Bhaskaracharya (II)\",\"Aryabhata\",\"Galileo Galilei\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Indian mathematician Bhaskaracharya (II) wrote the book Siddhant Shiromani.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many years ago did Bhaskaracharya (II) live?\",\"options\":[\"350 years ago\",\"600 years ago\",\"950 years ago\",\"1200 years ago\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Bhaskaracharya (II) lived around 950 years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to \'Siddhant Shiromani\', what does the Earth do to objects?\",\"options\":[\"It pushes objects away from its surface\",\"It makes objects float in the air\",\"It pulls objects towards itself\",\"It makes objects rotate in circles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Bhaskaracharya wrote in his book that the earth pulls objects towards itself.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific word did Bhaskaracharya (II) NOT use in his study of the force of attraction?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\",\"Force\",\"Attraction\",\"Gravity\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Bhaskaracharya did not use the word gravity.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Sir Isaac Newton was a scientist belonging to which country?\",\"options\":[\"India\",\"Germany\",\"Britain\",\"America\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes Sir Isaac Newton as a renowned British scientist.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If Newton lived about 350 years ago and Bhaskaracharya lived 600 years before him, in what year (relative to now) did Bhaskaracharya live?\",\"options\":[\"250 years ago\",\"600 years ago\",\"950 years ago\",\"1000 years ago\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"350 years (Newton) + 600 years (difference) = 950 years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which of the following fields did Sir Isaac Newton conduct his experiments?\",\"options\":[\"Biology and Chemistry\",\"Astronomy and Mathematics\",\"History and Geography\",\"Botany and Zoology\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Newton conducted experiments in astronomy, mathematics, and physics.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which two topics are mentioned as important discoveries made by Newton?\",\"options\":[\"Light and Sound\",\"Electricity and Magnetism\",\"Motion and Gravity\",\"Heat and Pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Newton made important discoveries in topics such as motion and gravity.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how does the timing of Bhaskaracharya\'s work compare to Newton\'s?\",\"options\":[\"Bhaskaracharya lived 350 years after Newton\",\"Bhaskaracharya lived 600 years before Newton\",\"They both lived at the same time\",\"Newton lived 950 years before Bhaskaracharya\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Bhaskaracharya lived about 600 years before Newton.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes Bhaskaracharya\'s contribution to science according to the passage?\",\"options\":[\"He was the first to use the word gravity in a book\",\"He discovered the laws of motion 350 years ago\",\"He studied the force of attraction long before Newton\",\"He was a British scientist who studied astronomy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that Bhaskaracharya had already developed the idea and studied the force of attraction 600 years before Newton.\",\"topic\":\"Bhaskaracharya and Newton\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"The force exerted by a magnet is called ____.\",\"options\":[\"muscular force\",\"mechanical force\",\"magnetic force\",\"gravitational force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines the force exerted by a magnet as magnetic force.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these materials is attracted towards magnets?\",\"options\":[\"Wood\",\"Iron\",\"Plastic\",\"Rubber\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that it is observed that iron objects are attracted towards magnets.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific force that causes the nails to stick to the red and white object?\",\"options\":[\"Frictional force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Electrostatic force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a magnet attracting nails, which is an example of magnetic force.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/112_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these appliances uses magnetic force to keep its door closed tightly?\",\"options\":[\"Washing machine\",\"Electric iron\",\"Refrigerator\",\"Microwave oven\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the refrigerator door sticks tightly when closed due to magnetic force.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is required to pull iron objects away once they are attracted to a magnet?\",\"options\":[\"Heat\",\"Force\",\"Light\",\"Electricity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Force is needed to pull these objects\' away from the magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if we replace the nails with erasers and pencils, what will happen?\",\"options\":[\"They will stick to the magnet\",\"They will be repelled by the magnet\",\"They will not be attracted to the magnet\",\"They will break into pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies only iron objects are attracted; pencils and erasers are not mentioned as magnetic materials.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/112_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student brings a magnet near a pile of pins and a pen, which object will move towards the magnet?\",\"options\":[\"The pen\",\"The pins\",\"Both the pen and pins\",\"Neither the pen nor pins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pins are usually made of iron\\/steel and are attracted to magnets, unlike plastic pens.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where is magnetic force commonly used according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"In many appliances\",\"Only in laboratories\",\"Only in schools\",\"In wooden furniture\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Magnetic force is used in many appliances.\'\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a refrigerator door require a \'little extra force\' to open?\",\"options\":[\"Because the door is very heavy\",\"Because the hinges are rusty\",\"Because magnetic force holds it shut\",\"Because of the air pressure inside\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The door sticks tightly due to magnetic force, requiring extra force to overcome that attraction.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What can be concluded about the objects that a magnet pulls towards itself?\",\"options\":[\"They are all made of wood\",\"They are all made of iron\",\"They are all very light in weight\",\"They are all made of plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically observes that iron objects are attracted towards magnets.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic Force\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the force that opposes the motion of an object called?\",\"options\":[\"Frictional force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Electrostatic force\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines the force that opposes motion as \'Frictional force\'.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is used to rub wooden furniture to make its surface smooth?\",\"options\":[\"Smooth silk cloth\",\"Plastic sheet\",\"Rough sandpaper\",\"Oiled paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, rough sandpaper or polish paper is used to make wooden furniture smooth.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what causes the speed of the moving coins to decrease as they slide across the board?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the coins\",\"Friction between the surface and the coins\",\"The air pressure in the room\",\"Magnetic pull of the board\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that friction between the surface of the carrom board and the coins reduces their speed.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/112_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the speed of a vehicle like a cycle or bus when brakes are applied?\",\"options\":[\"The speed increases\",\"The speed remains constant\",\"The speed decreases\",\"The vehicle changes direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook mentions that the speed of vehicles decreases on applying brakes.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do meteors burn up before they reach the ground?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the heat of the sun\",\"Due to friction with atmospheric air\",\"Because they are made of fire\",\"Due to the lack of oxygen in space\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that burning of a meteor is due to friction with atmospheric air at high speed.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the spacecraft shown disintegrated during re-entry into the atmosphere primarily due to what?\",\"options\":[\"A collision with a satellite\",\"Tremendous friction with the air\",\"Lack of fuel in the engines\",\"Extreme cold in the upper atmosphere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the mishap of the Columbia happened because of tremendous friction upon re-entering the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/113_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true regarding friction on different surfaces?\",\"options\":[\"Friction is the same on all surfaces\",\"Friction between smooth surfaces is greater\",\"Friction between rough surfaces is greater\",\"Smooth surfaces have no friction at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook explicitly states that friction between rough surfaces is greater than friction between smooth surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is used in machines to reduce friction while the wheels rotate?\",\"options\":[\"Sandpaper\",\"Lubricants and grease\",\"Rough polish paper\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that substances like lubricants and grease are used to reduce friction in machine wheels.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who served as a mission specialist on the Columbia spacecraft mission that set out on 16th January 2003?\",\"options\":[\"Sunita Williams\",\"Dr. Kalpana Chawla\",\"Rakesh Sharma\",\"Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Dr. Kalpana Chawla as a mission specialist and head of a division for the Columbia mission.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a marble is struck with the same force on a smooth floor and a rough tile, why does it cover different distances?\",\"options\":[\"The marble changes its weight\",\"The force of gravity changes\",\"The amount of friction is different\",\"The marble becomes smoother on the tile\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The distance is different because friction varies between smooth and rough surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is a direct benefit of decreasing frictional force in machine wheels?\",\"options\":[\"Wear and tear decreases\",\"The machine becomes heavier\",\"The speed of the wheels decreases\",\"The machine produces more heat\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that as frictional force decreases, wear and tear decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Both writing with a pencil and erasing it are possible because of which force?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Frictional force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Mechanical force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that writing with a pencil and erasing it both happen because of friction.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which organization created the reusable spacecraft named Columbia?\",\"options\":[\"ISRO\",\"NASA\",\"ESA\",\"SpaceX\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that NASA made the spacecraft named Columbia.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the Columbia spacecraft face a crisis during its final return journey?\",\"options\":[\"It ran out of oxygen\",\"A small piece of its heat shield broke off\",\"It was hit by a meteor shower\",\"The astronauts made a calculation error\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains the crisis happened because a small piece of its heat shield broke off during flight.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the heat produced when the friction between a returning spacecraft and the air is tremendous?\",\"options\":[\"The heat produced is very low\",\"The heat produced is very high\",\"No heat is produced\",\"The spacecraft cools down\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when friction is tremendous, the heat produced by this friction is also very high.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional Force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the force produced due to static electric charges called?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Electrostatic force\",\"Mechanical force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the force produced due to static electric charges as \'Electrostatic force\'.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these materials is mentioned in the text as being able to produce a temporary electric charge due to friction?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Ebonite\",\"Copper\",\"Aluminium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists glass, plastic, rubber, and ebonite as materials where electric charge is produced due to friction.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the electric charge produced on the surface of a rubbed plastic ruler?\",\"options\":[\"It flows away immediately\",\"It turns into magnetic force\",\"It remains on the surface for some time\",\"It disappears as soon as rubbing stops\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, this electric charge remains on that surface for some time and does not flow away.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the rainy season, what causes the crackling sound when removing nylon clothes?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic attraction\",\"Static electricity\",\"Air pressure\",\"Gravitational pull\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that hearing a crackling sound or seeing sparks when removing nylon clothes happens due to static electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an inflated balloon is rubbed on dry hair and brought near small pieces of paper, what will be the result?\",\"options\":[\"The paper pieces will be repelled\",\"The paper pieces will be attracted\",\"The balloon will burst\",\"Nothing will happen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests using an inflated balloon instead of a comb to observe the attraction of paper pieces due to static charge.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How is a temporary electric charge produced on the surface of materials like glass or rubber?\",\"options\":[\"By heating them\",\"By washing them\",\"Due to friction\",\"Due to gravity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to friction, a temporary electric charge is produced on the rubbed surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are light objects like paper pieces and threads attracted to a rubbed plastic comb?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the weight of the comb\",\"Because of static electric charges\",\"Because of the moisture in the air\",\"Because the comb becomes a magnet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that light objects are attracted because of static electric charges.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To produce an electrostatic force using a ruler, what condition should the hair be in?\",\"options\":[\"Wet hair\",\"Oily hair\",\"Dry hair\",\"Soapy hair\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Try this\' section specifically instructs to rub the ruler on \'dry hair\'.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what action was performed on the comb to enable it to attract the paper pieces?\",\"options\":[\"It was dipped in water\",\"It was rubbed on dry hair\",\"It was heated over a flame\",\"It was kept in the dark\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity associated with the image explains that the comb must be rubbed on dry hair to attract paper pieces.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which force is responsible for the movement of paper pieces towards the comb in the given figure?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force\",\"Mechanical force\",\"Electrostatic force\",\"Gravitational force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the experiment for \'Electrostatic force\' as described in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Besides paper pieces, which other light object is mentioned as being attracted by static electric charges?\",\"options\":[\"Iron nails\",\"Threads\",\"Small stones\",\"Glass beads\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'light objects like paper pieces and threads are attracted\'.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why is the electric charge on the comb described as \'static\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is very powerful\",\"Because it does not flow away\",\"Because it only works on plastic\",\"Because it lasts forever\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the charge is static because \'It does not flow away\' and \'remains on that surface for some time\'.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Force\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are forces called when they act in opposite directions and are not equal?\",\"options\":[\"Balanced forces\",\"Unbalanced forces\",\"Stable forces\",\"Zero forces\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when forces acting in opposite directions are not equal, they are called ‘Unbalanced Forces’.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When the combined effect of forces acting in opposite directions is zero, the forces are called ____.\",\"options\":[\"Unbalanced forces\",\"Greater forces\",\"Balanced forces\",\"Opposite forces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when the combined effect of forces becomes zero, such forces are called Balanced forces.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a game of tug of war, the rope does not move to either side when the forces applied by both teams are ____.\",\"options\":[\"Unequal\",\"Equal\",\"Unbalanced\",\"Greater\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that the rope remains stable when equal forces act in opposite directions.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the position of an object when balanced forces act on it?\",\"options\":[\"It moves towards the greater force\",\"It moves in both directions\",\"It remains in a stable position\",\"It moves towards the smaller force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that under balanced forces, the object (like the cloth) remains in a stable position.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When forces acting in opposite directions are not equal, which force\'s effect is seen?\",\"options\":[\"The smaller force\",\"The greater force\",\"The zero force\",\"The balanced force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when forces are not equal, the effect of the greater force is seen.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the combined effect of forces when two puppies pull a cloth with equal strength in opposite directions?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes zero\",\"It becomes double\",\"It becomes unbalanced\",\"It becomes greater\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when equal forces act in opposite directions, the combined effect of forces becomes zero.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In a tug of war match, Team A wins by pulling the rope towards their side. What type of forces were acting at the moment they won?\",\"options\":[\"Balanced forces\",\"Zero forces\",\"Unbalanced forces\",\"Stable forces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A team wins when the forces are not equal, which are defined as unbalanced forces.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a piece of cloth is being pulled and it moves towards the right puppy, what can we conclude about the force applied by the left puppy?\",\"options\":[\"It is equal to the right puppy\'s force\",\"It is greater than the right puppy\'s force\",\"It is less than the right puppy\'s force\",\"It is zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The effect of the greater force is seen; if it moves right, the right force is greater and the left is less.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of unbalanced forces according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The combined effect is zero\",\"The object remains in a stable position\",\"Forces acting in opposite directions are equal\",\"Forces acting in opposite directions are not equal\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines unbalanced forces as forces acting in opposite directions that are not equal.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the combined effect of forces zero in a balanced force situation?\",\"options\":[\"Because the forces act in the same direction\",\"Because equal forces act in opposite directions\",\"Because there is no force being applied\",\"Because only one side is applying force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that equal forces acting in opposite directions result in a combined effect of zero.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if both animals push each other with equal strength in opposite directions, what will happen to their position?\",\"options\":[\"They will move to the left\",\"They will move to the right\",\"They will remain in a stable position\",\"They will both fall down\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on the text, when equal forces act in opposite directions, the object remains in a stable position.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/115_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the white animal manages to push the black animal backwards, what does this indicate about the forces?\",\"options\":[\"The forces are balanced\",\"The forces are unbalanced\",\"The combined effect is zero\",\"The black animal is applying more force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Movement occurs in the direction of the greater force when forces are not equal (unbalanced).\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Forces\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/115_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the force exerted on a unit area called?\",\"options\":[\"Friction\",\"Pressure\",\"Gravity\",\"Velocity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines the force exerted on a unit area as \'Pressure\'.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following formulas is used to calculate Pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure = Force × Area\",\"Pressure = Area \\/ Force\",\"Pressure = Force \\/ Area\",\"Pressure = Force + Area\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the formula used to measure pressure is Pressure = Force \\/ Area.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The unit newton\\/metre² is also known by which other name?\",\"options\":[\"Joule\",\"Watt\",\"Pascal\",\"Kelvin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that the unit newton\\/metre² is also known as \'pascal\'.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the pressure formula, \'newton\' is the unit used for which quantity?\",\"options\":[\"Area\",\"Force\",\"Volume\",\"Mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Newton is the unit of force.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the pressure if the area over which a force is applied increases?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure increases\",\"Pressure decreases\",\"Pressure remains the same\",\"Pressure becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the pressure is less if the area is more.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a sharp nail penetrate wood easily compared to a blunt nail?\",\"options\":[\"The force acts on a very small area\",\"The force acts on a very large area\",\"The sharp nail has more mass\",\"The blunt nail has less force\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"When the tip is sharp, the effect of the applied force acts on a very small area, increasing pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, in which of the following is it necessary to measure pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Solar panels\",\"Air inside vehicle tyres\",\"Length of a road\",\"Weight of a brick\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that measurement of pressure is necessary at large water reservoirs, pumps, and the air inside vehicle tyres.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a performer stand on a board fixed with many sharp nails without them piercing his feet?\",\"options\":[\"The nails are not actually sharp\",\"The force is distributed over a larger area\",\"The performer has very hard skin\",\"The force is concentrated on a single point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When there are many nails, the applied force (weight) is distributed over a larger area, reducing the pressure on each nail.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a force of 20 newtons is applied on an area of 4 metre², what will be the pressure?\",\"options\":[\"80 pascal\",\"24 pascal\",\"5 pascal\",\"16 pascal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Pressure = Force \\/ Area, 20 \\/ 4 = 5 pascal.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, in which position does the brick sink deeper into the wet soil?\",\"options\":[\"Horizontal position\",\"Vertical position\",\"Both positions sink equally\",\"The brick does not sink at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the vertical position, the contact area is smaller, leading to higher pressure and deeper sinking.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/116_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment in the given figure, what can be concluded about the contact area and sinking?\",\"options\":[\"The brick sinks less when the contact area is larger\",\"The brick sinks more when the contact area is larger\",\"Contact area has no effect on sinking\",\"A horizontal brick exerts more pressure than a vertical one\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'When it sank less, was the contact area... larger than before?\' and concludes pressure is less if area is more.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/116_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a performer attempts to stand on just a single sharp nail instead of many, what is the most likely result based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"The nail will not pierce the foot because the force is the same\",\"The nail will pierce the foot because the force acts on a very small area\",\"The pressure will decrease because there is only one nail\",\"The area will increase, making it safer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Standing on a single nail concentrates the force on a very small area, creating enough pressure to pierce the foot.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A force is applied to two different surfaces. Surface A has an area of 1 m² and Surface B has an area of 5 m². Which surface will experience higher pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Surface A\",\"Surface B\",\"Both will have equal pressure\",\"Pressure cannot be determined\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Pressure is more if the area is less; since 1 m² is less than 5 m², Surface A experiences more pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements best evaluates the relationship between force, area, and pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure is directly proportional to area\",\"For the same force, reducing the area increases the pressure\",\"For the same area, reducing the force increases the pressure\",\"Area and pressure always increase together\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'The pressure is more if the area is less\', implying an inverse relationship for a given force.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the unit of pressure named \'pascal\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is the Latin word for force\",\"It is named in honour of a scientist\",\"It is the name of the instrument used to measure it\",\"It represents the area of a circle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the units newton and pascal are named in the honour of those scientists.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the envelope of air around the earth called?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrosphere\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Lithosphere\",\"Biosphere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the envelope of air around the earth is called the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the air pressure as we go higher from the earth\'s surface?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It decreases\",\"It first increases then decreases\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, as we go higher, the atmosphere becomes thinner, so the air pressure decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does air flow when there is a difference in air pressure between two places?\",\"options\":[\"From lower pressure to higher pressure\",\"From higher pressure to lower pressure\",\"From north to south only\",\"It stops flowing completely\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that air flows from the region of higher pressure to the region of lower pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Large storms occur due to the difference in ________ between two places.\",\"options\":[\"Water levels\",\"Temperature only\",\"Air pressure\",\"Soil type\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that large storms occur due to the difference in air pressure between two places.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the air pressure inside the straw when the girl pulls the air?\",\"options\":[\"The air pressure increases\",\"The air pressure decreases\",\"The air pressure remains constant\",\"The air pressure becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when we pull air from the straw, the air pressure inside the straw decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/116_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why is the empty bottle first kept in warm water with its lid open?\",\"options\":[\"To fill the bottle with water\",\"To make the bottle stronger\",\"To allow some air to move out of the bottle\",\"To clean the bottle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says that keeping the open bottle in warm water causes some air from the bottle to move out.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/116_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When drinking water using a straw, what pushes the water into the straw?\",\"options\":[\"The force of gravity\",\"The atmospheric pressure on the water surface\",\"The magnetic force of the earth\",\"The temperature of the water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that since the atmospheric pressure on the water surface is higher, the water is pushed into the straw.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the atmosphere exert less pressure at higher altitudes?\",\"options\":[\"Because the air becomes thicker\",\"Because the air becomes thinner\",\"Because the temperature is very high\",\"Because there is no gravity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that as we go higher, the atmosphere becomes thinner, causing air pressure to decrease.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what can be measured on the surface of the earth?\",\"options\":[\"Only the speed of air\",\"The thickness of the earth\'s crust\",\"The pressure exerted by the atmosphere\",\"The depth of the oceans\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the pressure exerted by the atmosphere on the surface of earth can be measured.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the bottle experiment, what is the necessary condition before putting the bottle into cold water?\",\"options\":[\"The lid must be open\",\"The lid must be closed tightly\",\"The bottle must be filled with water\",\"The bottle must be kept in the sun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to close the lid of the bottle tightly after air moves out and before putting it in cold water.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the air pressure in a valley is higher than on a nearby mountain peak, how will the air move?\",\"options\":[\"From the mountain peak to the valley\",\"From the valley to the mountain peak\",\"The air will not move at all\",\"The air will move in circles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Air flows from regions of higher pressure (valley) to regions of lower pressure (mountain peak).\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the straw example, what is the primary cause for the movement of liquids against gravity in this context?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force of the tongue\",\"Creation of a pressure difference\",\"Chemical reaction in the water\",\"Suction of the water molecules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The movement is caused by the difference between the lower pressure inside the straw and the higher atmospheric pressure outside.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are the efforts taken to push or pull an object called?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure\",\"Force\",\"Motion\",\"Friction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the efforts taken to push or pull an object are called force.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of force is applied with the help of muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Mechanical force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Electrostatic force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The force applied with the help of muscles is called muscular force.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What effect does frictional force have on the motion of an object?\",\"options\":[\"It increases the speed\",\"It opposes motion and decreases speed\",\"It changes the direction of motion\",\"It keeps the speed constant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Frictional force opposes motion and causes the speed to decrease.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"On which of the following substances can static electric charges develop due to friction?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Copper\",\"Ebonite\",\"Aluminum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that static electric charges develop on substances like plastic, rubber, and ebonite due to friction.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do objects fall towards the Earth when released?\",\"options\":[\"Due to magnetic force\",\"Due to atmospheric pressure\",\"Due to Earth\'s gravitational force\",\"Due to mechanical force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Earth attracts all objects around it towards itself through gravitational force.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If two equal forces act on a box from opposite sides, what will happen to the box?\",\"options\":[\"It will move towards the left\",\"It will move towards the right\",\"It will remain at rest\",\"It will start spinning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"When two equal forces act in opposite directions, the object remains at rest in a balanced state.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of pressure according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Force acting on a unit area\",\"The push or pull on an object\",\"The attraction of the Earth\",\"The speed of an object\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The force acting on a unit area is called pressure.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the same amount of force is applied, how does pressure change when the area of contact is made smaller?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure decreases\",\"Pressure increases\",\"Pressure remains the same\",\"Pressure becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"For the same applied force, pressure increases when the area of contact is smaller.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when the forces acting on an object are unbalanced?\",\"options\":[\"The object remains at rest\",\"The effect of the smaller force is seen\",\"The effect of the greater force is seen\",\"The forces cancel each other out\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the case of unbalanced forces, the effect of the greater force is seen.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name for the pressure exerted by the air?\",\"options\":[\"Static pressure\",\"Mechanical pressure\",\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"Gravitational pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The pressure exerted by the air is called atmospheric pressure.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary cause for the formation of wind and storms?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force of the Earth\",\"Difference in atmospheric pressure between two places\",\"Static electric charges in the air\",\"Friction between clouds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wind flows and storms are formed when there is a difference in atmospheric pressure between two places.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which force is specifically described as being applied by means of a machine?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force\",\"Mechanical force\",\"Electrostatic force\",\"Magnetic force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The force applied by means of a machine is called mechanical force.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21867,36,6,'Science','en','Motion and Types of Motion','[\"- An object is in motion when its position keeps changing continuously.\",\"- If the change of position is in a straight line, it is called linear motion.\",\"- Circular motion is when an object moves along a circular path, e.g., a wheel or a merry‑go‑round.\",\"- Oscillatory motion is back‑and‑forth or up‑and‑down on the same path, like a pendulum or a vibrating string.\",\"- Random motion is movement in many directions without a fixed pattern, e.g., ants or butterflies.\",\"- Non‑linear motion includes circular, oscillatory and random motions – they are not straight lines.\",\"- To decide if an object is moving, compare its position with a fixed reference point (e.g., a stationary wall).\",\"- Inside a moving bus, you feel objects are still, but from outside they are moving – motion is relative.\",\"- The Earth rotates on its axis and revolves around the Sun, so everything on Earth is in motion.\",\"- Examples: a car on a road (linear), a spinning fan (circular), a swinging pendulum (oscillatory), ants on a sugar cube (random).\"]','[{\"q\":\"When the position of an object keeps changing, the object is said to be in ________.\",\"options\":[\"rest\",\"motion\",\"station\",\"reverse\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines motion as the state when the position of an object keeps changing.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Initially, when the ball is in the bowler’s hand before running up, what is its state?\",\"options\":[\"It is in motion\",\"It is changing direction\",\"It is at rest\",\"It is moving backwards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that initially, when the ball is in the bowler\'s hand, it is at rest.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the ball is shown in the air after being bowled. This indicates that the ball\'s ________ is changing.\",\"options\":[\"color\",\"weight\",\"position\",\"shape\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when a bowler bowls, the position of the ball changes, which is seen in the image as it travels.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/120_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the ball when the batsman hits it with a bat?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes steady\",\"It goes in a different direction\",\"It loses its motion\",\"It returns to the bowler\'s hand\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when the batsman hits the ball, it goes in a different direction and its position changes again.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When the wicket keeper catches the ball and holds it, the ball becomes ________ in his hands.\",\"options\":[\"in motion\",\"steady\",\"backwards\",\"changing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when the ball goes into the wicket keeper\'s hands, it becomes steady.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you are traveling in a bus, why does your bag reach the destination even if it stays near your feet?\",\"options\":[\"Because the bag is at rest relative to the road\",\"Because the bag is moving backwards\",\"Because the bag gets the same motion as the bus\",\"Because the bag changes its position inside the bus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that since the bus is in motion, the bag also gets the same motion.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to a passenger sitting inside a moving bus, the person sitting next to them is ________.\",\"options\":[\"in motion\",\"moving backwards\",\"in the same position\",\"changing direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says that throughout the journey, the passenger next to you is in the same position in the bus.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When looking out of a moving bus window, how do the trees and houses along the road appear to move?\",\"options\":[\"Forwards\",\"Backwards\",\"Upwards\",\"They do not appear to move\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that trees and houses along the road appear to move backwards when looking out of a moving bus.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Whether an object is in motion or not is decided based on ________.\",\"options\":[\"the weight of the object\",\"the speed of the object only\",\"from where and how we observe it\",\"the direction of the wind\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that motion is decided based on the point and method of observation.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A person standing on the road sees a bus passing by. For this person, the bag inside the bus is ________.\",\"options\":[\"steady\",\"stationary\",\"in motion\",\"at rest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a person watching from outside would think the position of the bag has changed because the bus is in motion.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the ball is moving towards the batsman, which player will see the ball\'s position changing?\",\"options\":[\"Only the bowler\",\"Only the batsman\",\"Both the bowler and the batsman\",\"Neither the bowler nor the batsman\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the ball is changing its position relative to the field, all players observing it will see it in motion.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/120_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If an object is said to be \'steady\' according to an observer, what does it mean?\",\"options\":[\"Its position is changing rapidly\",\"It is moving backwards\",\"It did not change its position relative to that observer\",\"It is moving in a different direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the example of a bag in a bus to show that if it doesn\'t change position, it is steady according to the passenger.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a requirement for an object to be considered in motion?\",\"options\":[\"It must change its color\",\"Its position must keep changing\",\"It must be hit by a bat\",\"It must move backwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines motion specifically as the continuous change in the position of an object.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why might one person see an object as moving while another sees it as steady?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are observing from different places\",\"Because the object is moving and steady at the same time\",\"Because one person is not looking correctly\",\"Because the object changes its state every second\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that motion depends on from where and how we observe it, leading to different perceptions.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are sitting in a moving bus and looking at your own bag at your feet, the bag is ________ relative to you.\",\"options\":[\"moving backwards\",\"in motion\",\"steady\",\"changing position\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the bag did not change its position and was steady according to the passenger inside the bus.\",\"topic\":\"Motion\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the position of a fixed object called when used to determine motion?\",\"options\":[\"Moving point\",\"Reference point\",\"Turning point\",\"Stationary point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the position of a fixed object is called the ‘Reference point’.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To decide whether an object is in motion, we must observe it with respect to which type of object?\",\"options\":[\"A moving object\",\"A fast object\",\"A steady object\",\"A rotating object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, to decide if an object is in motion, we must observe it with respect to a steady object.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is needed to decide whether an object is at rest or in motion?\",\"options\":[\"A fixed reference point\",\"A magnifying glass\",\"A circular path\",\"A moving observer\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a fixed reference point is needed to decide if an object is at rest or in motion.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects are mentioned in the text as appearing to be at rest?\",\"options\":[\"Clouds and birds\",\"Cars and buses\",\"Trees and electric poles\",\"Sun and Moon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that we feel trees and electric poles around us are at rest.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is everything on Earth technically in motion even if it appears stationary to us?\",\"options\":[\"Because the wind is always blowing\",\"Because the Earth rotates and revolves\",\"Because the reference point is always changing\",\"Because objects are made of moving atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the Earth rotates around its axis and revolves around the sun, meaning everything on it is moving.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are standing near an electric pole, how can you use it as a reference point to know a car is moving?\",\"options\":[\"By checking if the car changes its position relative to the pole\",\"By checking if the pole starts moving with the car\",\"By measuring the height of the pole\",\"By observing the color of the car and the pole\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Motion is decided by observing an object\'s position with respect to a steady object (the reference point).\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The Earth keeps rotating continuously around its own ______.\",\"options\":[\"Reference point\",\"Orbit\",\"Axis\",\"Pole\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the earth itself keeps rotating around its own axis continuously.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we use the Sun as a reference point, what can we say about a tree on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"The tree is at rest\",\"The tree is moving\",\"The tree is the reference point\",\"The tree is stationary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the Earth revolves around the Sun, everything on Earth (including the tree) is moving relative to the Sun.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How do we decide which objects around us are stationary?\",\"options\":[\"By seeing if they change their position relative to a fixed point\",\"By checking if they are rotating around the sun\",\"By measuring their speed with a clock\",\"By looking at them without any reference point\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Stationary objects are those that do not change position relative to a fixed reference point.\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why is the \'Reference point\' essential for defining motion?\",\"options\":[\"Because motion is only a feeling\",\"Because all objects are actually at rest\",\"Because motion is relative to a fixed position\",\"Because fixed objects do not exist on Earth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that motion or rest can only be decided by comparing an object to a fixed position (reference point).\",\"topic\":\"Reference point\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the motion of an object called if its position keeps changing in a straight line?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Curved motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the position of an object keeps changing in a straight line, it is called linear motion.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of linear motion mentioned in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"A merry-go-round in a fair\",\"A slingshot being whirled\",\"A fruit fallen from a tree\",\"Ants running in all directions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions a fruit fallen from the tree as an example of motion in a straight line (linear motion).\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A moving object keeps changing its _________ continuously.\",\"options\":[\"Shape\",\"Color\",\"Position\",\"Size\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text begins by stating that a moving object keeps changing its position continuously.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of motion is performed by an object released from a height in a downward direction?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Stationary motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, an object released from a height in a downward direction moves in a straight line, which is linear motion.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When an object moves along a circular path, its motion is known as ________.\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Straight motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Random motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines motion along a circular path as circular motion.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of motion is demonstrated by the object being whirled by the man?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Stationary motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show that a slingshot moves along a circular path, which is circular motion.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/121_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the large wheel in the fair moves in which type of motion?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Straight line motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a merry-go-round (giant wheel) in a fair moves along a circular path, called circular motion.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/121_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a car is driving on a perfectly straight highway, what type of motion is it performing?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Zig-zag motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Motion in a straight line is defined as linear motion in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what happens to the motion of ants when salt water is sprayed on them?\",\"options\":[\"They move in a straight line\",\"They move in a circular path\",\"They start running in all directions randomly\",\"They stop moving immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when salt water is sprayed, ants start running in all directions randomly.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects follows a circular path according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A falling fruit\",\"A train on straight rails\",\"A merry-go-round\",\"An object dropped from a height\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the merry-go-round as an object that moves along a circular path.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the motion of ants running in all directions NOT considered linear motion?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are moving too fast\",\"Because they are not moving in a straight line\",\"Because they are moving in a circular path\",\"Because they are not changing position\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Linear motion requires the position to change in a straight line; running in all directions is not a straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of motion is characterized by the path of the object rather than its speed?\",\"options\":[\"Only linear motion\",\"Only circular motion\",\"Both linear and circular motion\",\"Neither linear nor circular motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines both linear and circular motion based on the path (straight line vs. circular path) the object takes.\",\"topic\":\"Types of motion\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"While stitching, the cloth moves continuously forward in a straight line. This is an example of which type of motion?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Zig-zag motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the cloth moves forward in a straight line, which shows linear motion.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of motion is observed in the wheel on one side of the sewing machine?\",\"options\":[\"Linear\",\"Circular\",\"Up and down\",\"Stationary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the motion of the wheel on one side of the sewing machine is circular.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the needle of the sewing machine move while stitching clothes?\",\"options\":[\"It moves in a circular path\",\"It moves forward in a straight line\",\"It moves up and down continuously\",\"It stays completely still\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the needle of the machine moves up and down continuously.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what kind of movement is seen in the foot pedal of the sewing machine?\",\"options\":[\"Circular movement\",\"Up and down movement\",\"Linear movement\",\"Side to side movement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the foot pedal moves up and down.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a tailor is stitching a long straight seam, the movement of the cloth represents which concept from the text?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\",\"Random motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Stitching a straight seam involves the cloth moving in a straight line, which is linear motion.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of motion is performed by the large wheel located under the table when the machine is operated?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Up and down motion\",\"No motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the end of the rod near the axis of the large wheel moves in a circular path, implying the wheel rotates circularly.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/122_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When observing the rod connecting the foot pedal to the large wheel, what can be concluded about the movement at its two ends?\",\"options\":[\"The movement at both ends is exactly the same\",\"Both ends move in a straight line\",\"The movement at both ends is not the same\",\"Both ends move in a circular path\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if the movement at both ends is the same and clarifies that one end moves up and down while the other moves in a circular path.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific part of the machine moves up and down continuously to pierce the cloth?\",\"options\":[\"The large wheel\",\"The foot pedal\",\"The needle\",\"The side wheel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the needle moves up and down continuously while stitching.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/122_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the observations in the text, which part moves in a circular path near the axis of the large wheel?\",\"options\":[\"The cloth\",\"The needle\",\"The end of the connecting rod\",\"The foot pedal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the end of the rod near the axis of the large wheel moves in a circular path.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The text describes the cloth moving continuously forward in a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Circular path\",\"Straight line\",\"Zig-zag path\",\"Curved line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the cloth moves continuously forward in a straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Observe\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the motion of an object that moves continuously back and forth on the same path called?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\",\"Random motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines oscillatory motion as the motion of an object that moves continuously up and down or back and forth on the same path.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these musical instruments is mentioned in the text as having stretched strings that show oscillatory motion?\",\"options\":[\"Flute\",\"Tabla\",\"Harmonium\",\"Tambora\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions sitar, tambora, and veena as instruments with stretched strings.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the stretched strings of a sitar or veena when they are plucked?\",\"options\":[\"They break\",\"They vibrate\",\"They rotate\",\"They move in a straight line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, these strings vibrate when plucked, which is a form of oscillatory motion.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the movement of a swing described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It moves in a complete circle\",\"It moves only in one direction\",\"It goes from one end to another and then back and forth again\",\"It moves in a zig-zag way\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the swing\'s movement as going from one end to another and back and forth again.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In oscillatory motion, the object must move continuously on ________.\",\"options\":[\"Different paths\",\"The same path\",\"A circular path\",\"A downward path only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition states that the object moves back and forth or up and down on the same path.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the motion of a plucked string considered oscillatory motion?\",\"options\":[\"Because it makes a sound\",\"Because it is made of metal\",\"Because it vibrates back and forth\",\"Because it stays in one place\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Vibration is a back and forth motion on the same path, which fits the definition of oscillatory motion.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of motion is the girl on the swing performing?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Random motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a girl on a swing, and the text identifies the movement of a swing as oscillatory motion.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/122_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a weight is hung from a spring and it moves continuously up and down on the same path, what type of motion is it showing?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\",\"Stationary motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Any continuous up and down movement on the same path is defined as oscillatory motion in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, which phrase from the text best describes the path the swing takes?\",\"options\":[\"Moves in a straight line forever\",\"Goes from one end to another and back again\",\"Moves only in a downward direction\",\"Stays at one end without moving back\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the swing movement which the text describes as going from one end to another and back again.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/122_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common characteristic between the swing and the strings of a veena according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Both are made of wood\",\"Both move in a circular direction\",\"Both exhibit oscillatory motion\",\"Both move only when the wind blows\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text classifies both the movement of the swing and the vibration of the strings as oscillatory motion.\",\"topic\":\"Oscillatory motion\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the motion of objects moving in different directions called?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Uniform motion\",\"Periodic motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the motion of objects moving in different directions as \'Random motion\'.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In what manner do butterflies move while flying around flowers in a garden?\",\"options\":[\"In a straight line\",\"In a circular path\",\"In a zigzag manner\",\"In a stationary manner\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that while flying, butterflies move in different directions in a zigzag manner.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are random, circular, and oscillatory motions classified as \'Nonlinear motions\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are very fast\",\"Because they are not in one line\",\"Because they are continuously straight\",\"Because they only happen in gardens\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that these motions are nonlinear because they are not in one line.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these motions is described as being neither continuously straight nor circular?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Uniform motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the motion of butterflies (random motion) is neither continuously straight nor circular.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a player is running across a field changing directions constantly to dodge others, what type of motion is this according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Circular motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Motion in different directions is defined as random motion.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following groups consists only of \'Nonlinear motions\' as per the textbook description?\",\"options\":[\"Linear and Random\",\"Circular and Linear\",\"Random and Oscillatory\",\"Straight and Zigzag\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists random, circular, and oscillatory motions as examples of nonlinear motions.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definition provided, if an object\'s motion is not in one line, it must be categorized as:\",\"options\":[\"Linear\",\"Nonlinear\",\"Uniform\",\"Stationary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that motions not in one line are \'Nonlinear motions\'.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where are butterflies typically seen flying in the example provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"In a forest\",\"In a garden\",\"In a house\",\"In a desert\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions seeing butterflies flying around flowers in a garden.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the motion of the insects shown flying around the flowers is an example of:\",\"options\":[\"Linear motion\",\"Random motion\",\"Uniform motion\",\"Oscillatory motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows butterflies, which the text uses as an example of random motion.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/122_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, the path of the butterflies is described in the text as:\",\"options\":[\"Continuously straight\",\"Always circular\",\"Zigzag\",\"In one line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the flying motion of the butterflies shown in the figure as zigzag.\",\"topic\":\"Random and Nonlinear motions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/122_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, what should be placed on the part of the metal strip that is on the table?\",\"options\":[\"Light paper sheets\",\"Heavy objects like notebooks or bricks\",\"A glass of water\",\"Nothing should be placed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to place heavy objects like notebooks or bricks on the part of the strip which is on the table.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the hanging part of the strip after it is gently pushed downward?\",\"options\":[\"It stays in the downward position\",\"It falls off the table immediately\",\"It keeps vibrating for some time\",\"It starts spinning in circles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that for some time, that part of the strip will keep vibrating.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To perform this experiment correctly, how should the metal strip be positioned on the table?\",\"options\":[\"Entirely on the table surface\",\"Entirely hanging outside the table\",\"Half on the table and half outside the table\",\"Standing vertically against the table edge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The instructions state to place the strip so that half of it is on the table and half is outside.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary to place heavy objects on the part of the strip that is on the table?\",\"options\":[\"To make the strip look colorful\",\"To hold the strip firmly so the other end can vibrate\",\"To measure the weight of the notebooks\",\"To stop the strip from vibrating\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Heavy objects are needed to anchor the strip to the table while the free end moves.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does the text instruct you to push the hanging part of the strip?\",\"options\":[\"Upward\",\"Downward\",\"Towards the left\",\"Towards the right\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'gently push the part of the strip hanging outside the table downward\'.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal of observing the movement of the strip in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"To see how long the strip is\",\"To check the strength of the table\",\"To decide what type of motion it has\",\"To count the number of notebooks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The final instruction is to observe the movement and decide what type of motion it has.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the dashed lines and the curved arrow near the end of the strip represent?\",\"options\":[\"The strip is breaking into pieces\",\"The strip is vibrating up and down\",\"The strip is being pushed into the table\",\"The strip is growing longer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The dashed lines show the path of the strip as it vibrates up and down after being pushed.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what specific objects are shown being used to hold the strip down?\",\"options\":[\"Bricks\",\"A stack of books\",\"A school bag\",\"A metal weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration clearly shows a stack of four books placed on the strip.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which term from the text describes the specific back-and-forth movement of the metal strip?\",\"options\":[\"Rolling\",\"Vibrating\",\"Linear\",\"Circular\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the word \'vibrating\' to describe the movement of the strip.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment described, what must a student analyze to identify the type of motion?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the bricks used\",\"The movement of the strip\",\"The material of the table\",\"The thickness of the notebooks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To decide the type of motion, the student is instructed to observe the movement of the strip.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"When the position of an object keeps changing, the object is said to be in what state?\",\"options\":[\"Rest\",\"Motion\",\"Stationary\",\"Fixed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the position of an object keeps changing, it is said to be in motion.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which two factors are important while observing a change in the position of an object?\",\"options\":[\"The object\'s weight and height\",\"The speed and the time taken\",\"The observer’s position and the reference point\",\"The color and shape of the object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the observer’s position and the reference point are important while observing a change in position.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A person sitting inside a moving bus feels that the position of other passengers has not changed. Why is this?\",\"options\":[\"Because the bus is not actually moving\",\"Because the observer is inside the same frame of reference as the passengers\",\"Because the passengers are sleeping\",\"Because the bus is moving too fast to see\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a person inside the bus feels the position of passengers has not changed because of their relative position.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does a person standing outside a moving bus notice about the passengers inside?\",\"options\":[\"Their position is not changing\",\"They are stationary\",\"Their position is changing\",\"They are invisible\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a person outside the bus notices that the position of the objects\\/passengers inside is changing.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example of a bicycle in motion, which parts can be observed to identify different types of motion?\",\"options\":[\"Only the seat and handle\",\"The wheels, paddle and chain\",\"Only the bell\",\"Only the frame\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to identify motion in the wheels, paddle, and chain of a bicycle.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding motion in some events?\",\"options\":[\"Only one type of motion can exist at a time\",\"Motion is impossible to observe from outside\",\"Different types of motion are seen at the same time\",\"Position never changes in motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in some events, different types of motion are seen at the same time.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you are standing on a platform and a train passes by, why do you say the train is in motion?\",\"options\":[\"Because you are also moving\",\"Because your reference point shows the train\'s position is changing\",\"Because the train is very loud\",\"Because the train has wheels\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Motion is defined by the change in position relative to an observer and a reference point.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the definition of motion?\",\"options\":[\"Staying in one place\",\"The continuous change in the position of an object\",\"The speed at which an object travels\",\"The force applied to an object\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines motion as the state when the position of an object keeps changing.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why might two different observers disagree on whether an object is moving?\",\"options\":[\"Because one observer is wrong\",\"Because they have different heights\",\"Because their positions and reference points are different\",\"Because the object moves too slowly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that the observer\'s position and reference point are crucial in observing motion.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of a moving bicycle would likely show a different type of motion compared to the chain?\",\"options\":[\"The wheels\",\"The stationary bell\",\"The fixed seat\",\"The carrier\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text implies that wheels, paddles, and chains may exhibit different types of motion simultaneously.\",\"topic\":\"What We Have Learnt?\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21868,36,6,'Science','en','Gifts of Nature','[\"- Soil is a natural resource that can be fertile or infertile.\",\"- Red soil is rich in iron and can be used to produce iron and steel.\",\"- Bauxite soil contains aluminium and is exported as a valuable resource.\",\"- Clay, china clay and multani mati are used in making terracotta, idols and cosmetics.\",\"- Soil texture (coarse, moderate, fine) affects its use for bricks, idols and agriculture.\",\"- Water‑holding capacity is the amount of water soil can retain; it depends on soil texture and organic matter.\",\"- Water is a renewable resource available as surface water, groundwater, rainwater, etc.\",\"- Hard water contains dissolved salts that prevent soap from lathering; soft water does not.\",\"- Sunlight is a renewable source of energy called solar energy; it can be used to generate electricity, heat water and cook food.\",\"- Wind energy is produced by windmills and can generate electricity.\",\"- Forests provide wood, paper, medicinal plants and other useful products.\",\"- Fossil fuels (coal, crude oil, natural gas) are non‑renewable and formed over millions of years.\",\"- Renewable resources can be replenished quickly; non‑renewable resources take millions of years to form.\",\"- Sustainable resources are those that can be used without depleting them.\",\"- Protecting trees and forests helps maintain rainfall, habitat and reduces soil erosion.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In which of these states are hills of red rocks and red soil commonly found?\",\"options\":[\"Punjab and Haryana\",\"Karnataka and Maharashtra\",\"Gujarat and Rajasthan\",\"Assam and Sikkim\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that many hills of red rocks and large areas of red soil are seen in Karnataka, Odisha, and Maharashtra.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the red soil found in the hills of Maharashtra considered infertile for farming?\",\"options\":[\"It contains too much water\",\"It is too soft for roots\",\"Crops do not grow well in it\",\"It is used only for making idols\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the red soil formed from these rocks is completely infertile for farming because crops do not grow well in it.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What gives the red soil and rocks their characteristic red colour?\",\"options\":[\"Minerals of aluminium\",\"Minerals of iron\",\"China clay\",\"Multani mati\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the red colour is due to the minerals of iron present in the soil and rocks.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal is primarily obtained by processing the red soil mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Aluminium\",\"Iron and steel\",\"Copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, by processing this soil, iron and steel can be obtained from it.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do many countries buy minerals of iron from India every year?\",\"options\":[\"They want to make red paint\",\"They have no steel industries\",\"They have steel industries but no iron minerals\",\"They use it to improve their farming soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that these countries have big steel making industries but do not have minerals of iron, so they buy from India.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which mineral is found in the red rocks of the Konkan, Kolhapur, and Ratnagiri regions?\",\"options\":[\"Bauxite\",\"China clay\",\"Multani mati\",\"Terracotta\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the coastal region of Konkan, Kolhapur, and Ratnagiri, mineral bauxite is present in the soil and rocks.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal is obtained from the mineral Bauxite?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Steel\",\"Aluminium\",\"Copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that aluminium metal is obtained from bauxite.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the Marathi proverb ‘मातीमोल होणे’ as used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To become very expensive\",\"To become worthless\",\"To turn into gold\",\"To be useful for farming\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the proverb ‘मातीमोल होणे’ as meaning ‘to become worthless’.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which state is \'multani mati\', used in cosmetics, primarily found?\",\"options\":[\"Bihar\",\"Kerala\",\"Maharashtra\",\"Rajasthan\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that multani mati in cosmetics is found in Rajasthan.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific use of the clay called \'terracotta\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Producing aluminium\",\"Making decorative items and idols\",\"Growing crops in hills\",\"Making steel in industries\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that decorative items and idols are made from clay called terracotta.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are soil deposits like bauxite and iron minerals called \'Gifts of nature\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are sold for free\",\"Because humans have not created this wealth\",\"Because they are used to make gifts\",\"Because they only exist in India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that humans have not created this wealth; it is a gift given by nature, hence the name.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist finds a deposit of China clay. Based on the text, in which states is this most likely located?\",\"options\":[\"Maharashtra and Karnataka\",\"Rajasthan and Odisha\",\"Bihar and Kerala\",\"Konkan and Ratnagiri\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that china clay is found in the soil of Bihar and Kerala.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the trade of iron minerals affect India\'s economy?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the country\'s soil more fertile\",\"It earns the country several crores of rupees every year\",\"It reduces the amount of steel available in India\",\"It prevents other countries from building industries\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that our country earns several crores of rupees every year from this trade.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the trade of iron minerals expected to continue for the next hundred or two hundred years?\",\"options\":[\"Because the price of iron never changes\",\"Because other countries will never find iron\",\"Because India has large reserves of these minerals\",\"Because red soil cannot be used for anything else\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the trade will continue because we have large reserves of these minerals.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of natural wealth that is specifically good for farming?\",\"options\":[\"Red soil from hills\",\"Bauxite hills\",\"Fertile soil from river deposits\",\"Terracotta clay\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that fertile black soil and fertile soil formed from river deposits brought by floods are also natural wealth.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Soil as Wealth\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the texture of soil formed from river deposits described as in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Rough like sand\",\"Soft like flour\",\"Coarse like rangoli\",\"Hard like stone\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that soil formed from river deposits is soft like flour.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which crop is specifically mentioned as often being grown in light whitish soil?\",\"options\":[\"Wheat\",\"Rice\",\"Chilli\",\"Sugarcane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if the reader has noticed that chilli is often grown in fields with light whitish soil.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On what basis is soil classified as very coarse, moderately coarse, or fine?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the soil\",\"The size and proportion of sand-like particles\",\"The depth of the soil in the field\",\"The type of crops grown in it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, soil classification into coarse or fine is based on the size and proportion of sand-like particles.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Water-holding capacity\' of the soil refer to?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the soil when dry\",\"The speed at which water flows through the soil\",\"The ability of the soil to retain moisture\",\"The color of the soil after it rains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the ability of soil to retain moisture as its \'Water-holding capacity\'.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you rub a soil sample between your fingers and it feels rough like rangoli, which property are you identifying?\",\"options\":[\"Soil color\",\"Soil texture\",\"Soil fertility\",\"Soil temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that feeling the soil to see if it is rough or soft is called soil texture.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following determines how much water the soil can hold?\",\"options\":[\"The amount of decayed leaves, dung, and sand-like particles\",\"The number of measuring cylinders used in the experiment\",\"The type of idols made from the soil\",\"The color of the Multani mati\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the amount of decayed leaves, dung, and sand-like particles determines the water-holding capacity.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of clay is mentioned in the text alongside terracotta and shadu clay?\",\"options\":[\"Red clay\",\"China clay\",\"Black clay\",\"River clay\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions items made from terracotta, china clay, and shadu clay.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which measuring cylinder contains the most \'collected water\' after the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Cylinder A\",\"Cylinder B\",\"Cylinder C\",\"All cylinders have equal water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows that cylinder C (containing sand) has the highest level of collected water compared to A and B.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/127_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment shown in the figure, which soil sample has the highest \'Water-holding capacity\'?\",\"options\":[\"Sample A (Clayey soil)\",\"Sample B (Sandy soil)\",\"Sample C (Sand)\",\"They all have the same capacity\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sample A has the least water in the cylinder, meaning it retained (held) the most water.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"36\\/127_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the ability to retain moisture considered an important property for farming?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the soil look like flour\",\"It helps in classifying soil by color\",\"It ensures water is available for crops in the field\",\"It is necessary for making bricks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that for farming, the ability of soil to retain moisture is an important property.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a soil sample with a very high proportion of large sand-like particles. How should this soil be classified?\",\"options\":[\"Fine soil\",\"Soft soil\",\"Very coarse soil\",\"Whitish soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Soil with a high proportion of large sand-like particles is classified as very coarse.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of using filter paper in the experiment described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To change the color of the soil\",\"To hold the soil while allowing water to pass through\",\"To measure the weight of the soil\",\"To increase the water-holding capacity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Filter paper is used in the funnels to hold the soil samples (A, B, C) while water is poured through them.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Apart from farming, for which other activity is the soil\'s ability to retain moisture mentioned as important?\",\"options\":[\"Making tea\",\"Construction\",\"Filtering sand\",\"Measuring cylinders\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that for farming and construction, the ability of soil to retain moisture is an important property.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the material remaining in a tea strainer when you press it with a spoon, as per the \'Think\' section?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into sand\",\"It changes its color to white\",\"Liquid (tea) comes out of it\",\"It becomes very coarse\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Pressing the remaining material in a strainer demonstrates that it holds liquid, similar to soil moisture.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a soil sample is described as \'fine\', what does this tell us about its texture?\",\"options\":[\"It has a red color\",\"It has very small particles\",\"It is used only for construction\",\"It cannot hold any water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fine texture indicates that the particles in the soil are very small compared to coarse soil.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Texture and Properties\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many litres of water are required to produce one kilogram of paper?\",\"options\":[\"100 to 200 litres\",\"340 to 500 litres\",\"500 to 800 litres\",\"1000 to 1200 litres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that around 340 to 500 litres of water are required to make one kilogram of paper.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of sweating in living organisms as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To transport nutrients\",\"To help in digestion\",\"To regulate body temperature\",\"To prepare food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, sweating helps to regulate the body temperature in living organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a factory needs 400 litres of water to produce 1 kg of paper, how much water would be needed to produce 5 kg of paper?\",\"options\":[\"800 litres\",\"1200 litres\",\"2000 litres\",\"4000 litres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If 1 kg requires 400 litres, then 5 kg requires 400 x 5 = 2000 litres.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which state are ancient stepwells known as \'Vav\' found?\",\"options\":[\"Maharashtra\",\"Rajasthan\",\"Gujarat\",\"Madhya Pradesh\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in Gujarat, stepwells are known as \'Vav\'.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for water in which soap does not form lather?\",\"options\":[\"Soft water\",\"Hard water\",\"Surface water\",\"Groundwater\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water in which soap does not form lather is called \'Hard water\'.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does water from some wells taste salty while rainwater does not?\",\"options\":[\"Well water is closer to the sea\",\"Rainwater contains dissolved minerals\",\"Different types of salts dissolve in water as it seeps into the ground\",\"Soap does not form lather in rainwater\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As water seeps into the ground to become groundwater, many different types of salts dissolve in it, making it salty.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A person finds that clothes are not being cleaned properly even after using a lot of soap. What can be inferred about the water being used?\",\"options\":[\"It is soft water\",\"It is rainwater\",\"It is hard water\",\"It is surface water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that clothes are not cleaned properly with hard water because soap does not form lather in it.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is the term for water that reaches wells and bore wells after seeping into the ground?\",\"options\":[\"Surface water\",\"Groundwater\",\"Spring water\",\"Sea water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water found below the ground that reaches wells and bore wells is called \'Groundwater\'.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of making arrangements for rainwater to seep into the ground instead of just storing it in tanks?\",\"options\":[\"To make the water salty\",\"To increase the groundwater level\",\"To prevent the formation of lather\",\"To clean the building rooftops\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Releasing rainwater into the ground helps increase the groundwater level, which in turn increases water in nearby wells.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Plants require water specifically for which of the following biological processes?\",\"options\":[\"To produce sweat\",\"To prepare food\",\"To dissolve soap\",\"To create wood pulp\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that plants use water to prepare food.\",\"topic\":\"Water - A Valuable Resource\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is water considered a \'valuable natural resource\' in the context of industrial production?\",\"options\":[\"It is only used for drinking in factories\",\"It is needed for processing wood pulp and making medicines\",\"It is always available in large quantities in the sea