\":\"According to the poem, what is every name called?\",\"options\":[\"A noun\",\"A pronoun\",\"A verb\",\"An adjective\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'Every name is called a noun\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of speech stands in place of a noun?\",\"options\":[\"Adverb\",\"Conjunction\",\"Pronoun\",\"Preposition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'In place of noun the pronoun stands\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of an adjective as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It shows relation\",\"It describes a thing\",\"It joins sentences\",\"It tells how things are done\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'The adjective describes a thing\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of speech tells us \'how things are done\'?\",\"options\":[\"Verb\",\"Adjective\",\"Adverb\",\"Interjection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'How things are done the adverbs tell\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the example \'magic wand\', which word acts as the adjective?\",\"options\":[\"magic\",\"wand\",\"magic wand\",\"none of these\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem uses \'magic wand\' as an example of an adjective describing a thing.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a word shows relation, such as \'in\' or \'at\', what is it called?\",\"options\":[\"Conjunction\",\"Preposition\",\"Pronoun\",\"Verb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'The preposition shows relation, As in the street or at the station.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific role of conjunctions according to the poem?\",\"options\":[\"To describe magic wands\",\"To show action and things done\",\"To join sentences, words, or phrases\",\"To cry out with an exclamation mark\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'Conjunctions join, in many ways, Sentences, words, or phrase and phrase.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which punctuation mark is specifically mentioned as being needed by an interjection?\",\"options\":[\"Full stop\",\"Question mark\",\"Comma\",\"Exclamation mark\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'The interjection cries out, “Hark! I need an exclamation mark!\'\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The words \'read\', \'write\', \'jump\', and \'run\' are examples of which part of speech?\",\"options\":[\"Nouns\",\"Verbs\",\"Adverbs\",\"Pronouns\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem lists \'To read and write, to jump and run\' as examples of verbs meaning action.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the poem\'s examples, which of these pairs correctly matches the part of speech with its example?\",\"options\":[\"Noun - he and she\",\"Adverb - quickly and slowly\",\"Preposition - magic and bridal\",\"Verb - street and town\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem provides \'quickly, slowly\' as examples for adverbs.\",\"topic\":\"The Parts of Speech\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21894,41,7,'English','en','1.3 Little Girls Wiser than Old People','[\"- The story is about two girls, Akulka and Malasha, who play in a puddle on Easter day.\",\"- Easter is a Christian festival celebrated on a Sunday.\",\"- The girls show their new clothes to each other and play in the puddle.\",\"- Akulka is older and warns Malasha not to splash water.\",\"- Malasha splashes water, gets the girls\' clothes dirty, and the girls quarrel.\",\"- Their mothers and neighbours get angry and a fight starts among the villagers.\",\"- An old woman intervenes, telling the men that the girls are wiser and should not fight.\",\"- The men feel ashamed and stop fighting.\",\"- The story shows that children can be wiser than adults in some situations.\",\"- Key vocabulary: puddle, kerchiefs, bespattered, rills, nape of the neck, accursed one, knocked off her feet.\",\"- The story encourages discussion about children’s wisdom and adults’ involvement in quarrels.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the full name of Leo Tolstoy as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Count Lev Nikolayevich Tolstoy\",\"Count Leo Nikolayevich\",\"Lev Tolstoy Nikolayevich\",\"Nikolayevich Lev Tolstoy\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states his full name is Count Lev Nikolayevich Tolstoy.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of literature did Leo Tolstoy NOT author according to the introduction?\",\"options\":[\"Novels\",\"Short stories\",\"Philosophical works\",\"Science textbooks\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions novels, short stories, and philosophical works, but not science textbooks.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Whose ideas on non-violence influenced Mahatma Gandhi?\",\"options\":[\"Martin Luther King, Jr.\",\"Leo Tolstoy\",\"Count Lev Nikolayevich\",\"The children in the story\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Tolstoy\'s ideas on non-violence influenced great men like Mahatma Gandhi.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, for whom did Tolstoy write many stories?\",\"options\":[\"Only for philosophers\",\"Only for grown-ups\",\"For children\",\"Only for kings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Tolstoy wrote many stories for children.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the two characters in the circular frame wearing on their heads?\",\"options\":[\"Hats\",\"Headscarves\",\"Crowns\",\"Ribbons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration in the figure shows two girls wearing headscarves.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/020_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to study the influence of non-violence on Martin Luther King, Jr., which author\'s work should they read based on this text?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Leo Tolstoy\",\"Count Nikolayevich\",\"The girls in the picture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Tolstoy\'s ideas influenced Martin Luther King, Jr.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the introduction, what can we conclude about the story \'Little Girls Wiser than Old People\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is a novel for adults only.\",\"It is a philosophical work for kings.\",\"It is a story for children that also has a message for adults.\",\"It is a short story written by Mahatma Gandhi.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says this is one of the stories for children, but it has a message for grown-ups too.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the mood or expression of the two girls depicted in the drawing?\",\"options\":[\"They look angry and upset.\",\"They look happy and smiling.\",\"They look tired and sleepy.\",\"They look scared and worried.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The drawing shows both girls with smiling faces and a happy expression.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/020_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these famous leaders was influenced by Tolstoy\'s ideas on non-violence?\",\"options\":[\"Martin Luther King, Jr.\",\"Abraham Lincoln\",\"George Washington\",\"Nelson Mandela\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Martin Luther King, Jr. as one of the great men influenced by Tolstoy.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the author mention that the story has a message for \'grown-ups, too\'?\",\"options\":[\"To show that the story is too difficult for children.\",\"To indicate that the story contains universal wisdom relevant to all ages.\",\"Because grown-ups are the ones who buy the books.\",\"Because the story is actually a long novel.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It implies that while written for children, the themes are significant enough for adults to learn from.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many members should each group have for the activity?\",\"options\":[\"2-4 members\",\"4-6 members\",\"6-8 members\",\"10 members\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to \'Form groups of 4-6\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum number of reasons for fights each member should write?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Five\",\"Ten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says each member should write \'at least five reasons\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which language must the final lists of reasons be written?\",\"options\":[\"Mother tongue only\",\"English only\",\"Both English and mother tongue\",\"Any language of choice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that \'the lists should be in English\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step mentioned in the \'Warming up!\' activity?\",\"options\":[\"Write five reasons\",\"Classify reasons\",\"Form groups of 4-6\",\"Read out the list\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity begins with the instruction to \'Form groups of 4-6\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which example is provided in the text as a reason why fights occur?\",\"options\":[\"Two children want the same toy\",\"Children playing in the rain\",\"Students forgetting their homework\",\"Teachers giving too much work\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text gives the example: \'Two children want the same toy.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two categories used to classify the reasons for fights?\",\"options\":[\"Good and Bad\",\"Long and Short\",\"Trivial and Serious\",\"True and False\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to \'Classify the reasons into \'trivial\' and \'serious\' ones\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What should other groups do while Group 1 is reading out their list?\",\"options\":[\"Write their own new reasons\",\"Mark common items and note new ones\",\"Ignore the list and talk\",\"Translate the list into their mother tongue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states other groups should \'compare it with their list, mark the common items, and note down the new ones\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student writes \'Toto forgot my birthday\', how should it be classified according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Serious\",\"Trivial\",\"Common\",\"New\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly labels \'Toto forgot my birthday\' as \'Trivial\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student writes \'Bittu\'s friend tells lies\', which category does it fall into based on the examples?\",\"options\":[\"Trivial\",\"Serious\",\"Funny\",\"Unimportant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly labels \'Bittu\'s friend tells lies\' as \'Serious!\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of putting together reasons from all group members?\",\"options\":[\"To see who wrote the most\",\"To make a longer list\",\"To finish the work faster\",\"To practice handwriting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to put reasons together \'to make a longer list\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is allowed during the discussion of reasons, but not for the final list?\",\"options\":[\"Using a dictionary\",\"Writing in pencil\",\"Using the mother tongue\",\"Drawing pictures\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says children may discuss in their \'mother tongue\', but lists must be in English.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the classification table, what is the final goal of the activity after collecting reasons?\",\"options\":[\"To memorize the reasons\",\"To decide who is right or wrong\",\"To categorize reasons by their level of importance\",\"To stop all fights in the class\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity concludes with classifying reasons into \'trivial\' and \'serious\', which relates to their importance.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"At what time of the year does the story take place?\",\"options\":[\"Late winter\",\"Early Easter\",\"Mid-summer\",\"Christmas eve\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'It was an early Easter.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What color was the little girl\'s dress?\",\"options\":[\"Yellow with a design\",\"Red\",\"Blue\",\"Green\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'The little girl had a blue dress.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the large puddle originate from?\",\"options\":[\"A nearby river\",\"A manure pile\",\"A broken pipe\",\"The church roof\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the puddle had run down from a manure pile into a lane.\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did both girls have wrapped around their heads?\",\"options\":[\"Blue hats\",\"Yellow ribbons\",\"Red kerchiefs\",\"White scarves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Both had their heads wrapped in red kerchiefs.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the girls do immediately before going to the puddle?\",\"options\":[\"They finished their lunch\",\"They attended prayers in the Church\",\"They helped their mothers with clothes\",\"They played in the snow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'After prayers in the Church, the two girls went to the puddle.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, who is Malasha?\",\"options\":[\"The older girl in the yellow dress\",\"The mother of the girls\",\"The little girl in the blue dress\",\"A neighbor watching the girls\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Akulka addresses the little girl as Malasha when warning her about her shoes.\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the girls want to go to the puddle?\",\"options\":[\"To wash their new clothes\",\"To show their garments and plash in the water\",\"To fetch water for the farm\",\"To look for their lost shoes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says they showed their new garments to each other and wanted to plash in the water.\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'rills\' most likely mean in the context of the melting snow?\",\"options\":[\"Small streams of water\",\"Large blocks of ice\",\"Piles of manure\",\"New clothes\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the context of snow melting and running down the village, rills refers to small streams.\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Akulka, what would happen if Malasha went into the puddle with her shoes on?\",\"options\":[\"She would get sick\",\"Her shoes would get stuck\",\"Her mother would scold her\",\"The puddle would get deeper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Akulka warns, \'Don\'t go, Malasha, your mother will scold you.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What specific action did the girls take to protect their clothes while walking through the puddle?\",\"options\":[\"They wore long boots\",\"They took off their dresses\",\"They raised their skirts\",\"They walked on their tiptoes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the girls \'raised their skirts, and walked through the puddle.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the author show that Akulka is more responsible or experienced than Malasha?\",\"options\":[\"By describing her yellow dress as more expensive\",\"By showing her warning Malasha about being scolded and suggesting they remove shoes\",\"By making her walk through the puddle first to test the depth\",\"By having her stay out of the puddle entirely\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Akulka takes the lead in advising Malasha to remove shoes to avoid trouble with her mother.\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was Malasha\'s physical experience when she first entered the water?\",\"options\":[\"The water was freezing cold\",\"The water reached up to her knees\",\"The water reached up to her ankles and she felt afraid\",\"The water was very shallow and she started laughing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Malasha stepped in up to her ankles, and said: \\\"It is deep, Akulka, I am afraid.\\\"\'\",\"topic\":\"The Meeting at the Puddle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What specific instruction did Akulka give to Malasha before the incident?\",\"options\":[\"To run through the water\",\"To walk softly and not splash\",\"To jump over the puddle\",\"To go back home immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Akulka said, \'Malasha, look out, and do not splash it up, but walk softly.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'bespattered\' mean as used in the context of Akulka\'s dress?\",\"options\":[\"To clean thoroughly\",\"To tear into pieces\",\"To splash with small drops of liquid or dirt\",\"To dry in the sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the text, Malasha\'s action resulted in splashing mud\\/water onto Akulka\'s dress, nose, and eyes.\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Malasha had \'walked softly\' as requested, what would have been the most likely outcome according to the text\'s logic?\",\"options\":[\"Akulka would have still grown angry\",\"Akulka\'s new dress would have remained clean\",\"Malasha would have run home anyway\",\"The girls would have struck each other\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The conflict started specifically because Malasha \'plumped her foot\' and splashed Akulka; walking softly was intended to prevent this.\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Malasha jump out of the puddle and run home?\",\"options\":[\"She was finished playing\",\"She saw the trouble she caused and was frightened\",\"She wanted to show her mother her wet feet\",\"Akulka told her to go home\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Malasha was frightened and, seeing what trouble she had caused, jumped out of the puddle and ran home.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which parts of Akulka were splashed besides her dress?\",\"options\":[\"Her hands and feet\",\"Her hair and ears\",\"Her nose and eyes\",\"Her arms and neck\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the splash hit \'not only her dress, but also her nose and eyes.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was Akulka\'s immediate reaction upon seeing the spots on her dress?\",\"options\":[\"She started crying\",\"She laughed at Malasha\",\"She grew angry and scolded Malasha\",\"She went home to change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'When Akulka saw the spots on her dress, she grew angry at Malasha, and scolded her...\'\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which action by Akulka indicates that she intended to physically punish Malasha?\",\"options\":[\"She looked at the spots\",\"She walked softly\",\"She ran after Malasha and wanted to strike her\",\"She stood still in the puddle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Akulka \'ran after her, and wanted to strike her.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the sequence of events, who is responsible for the \'spots\' appearing on the dress?\",\"options\":[\"Akulka\",\"Malasha\",\"Both girls equally\",\"The old people\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes Malasha plumping her foot into the water, which bespattered Akulka\'s dress.\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what visual evidence confirms that Akulka is \'angry\' as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"She is wearing a red headscarf\",\"She has a frowning expression and her hands are on her hips\",\"She is standing in the water\",\"She is wearing a yellow dress\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows the girl in the yellow dress (Akulka) with a clear angry facial expression and aggressive posture.\",\"topic\":\"The Incident\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/022_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how does the illustrator depict the moment Malasha \'bespattered\' Akulka?\",\"options\":[\"By showing the girls hugging\",\"By showing the girls running towards the houses\",\"By showing dark spots of mud flying from the water onto Akulka\'s yellow dress\",\"By showing the girls washing their clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The drawing shows dark droplets moving from the puddle toward Akulka\'s dress, representing the splash.\",\"to