r off into the street\",\"To find a lost toy\",\"To clean the lane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains she scratched the ground so as to let the water off into the street.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What were the women doing while Akulka was at the puddle?\",\"options\":[\"Helping the girls\",\"Exchanging words\",\"Cleaning their dresses\",\"Talking to the old woman\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'While the women exchanged words, Akulka wiped off her dress, and went back to the puddle\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'paid no attention to the old woman\' imply about the people mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"They were listening carefully\",\"They were ignoring her\",\"They were afraid of her\",\"They were helping her\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Paying no attention means to ignore someone or not notice them.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the context of Malasha\'s actions, what is a \'rill\'?\",\"options\":[\"A large hole\",\"A small stream of water\",\"A pile of dirt\",\"A wooden fence\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A rill is a small stream, which Malasha widened to help the water flow.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Malasha \'widened\' the rill, what did she do to its size?\",\"options\":[\"She made it longer\",\"She made it narrower\",\"She made it broader\",\"She made it deeper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To widen something means to make it broader or wider in space.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the behavior of Akulka and Malasha contrast with the behavior of the women in this passage?\",\"options\":[\"The girls were fighting while the women were working\",\"The girls were working together while the women were arguing\",\"The girls were ignoring the water while the women were cleaning it\",\"The girls were angry while the women were peaceful\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The women were \'exchanging words\' (arguing) while the girls were helping each other manage the water.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the old woman\'s situation from the first sentence?\",\"options\":[\"She was leading the group\",\"She was being respected by everyone\",\"She was in danger of being pushed over\",\"She was successfully stopping the fight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says they \'almost knocked her off her feet\', suggesting she was physically unstable or ignored.\",\"topic\":\"The Girls at the Puddle\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'One word brought back another\' suggest about the conversation?\",\"options\":[\"The people were having a friendly chat\",\"The argument was escalating with each reply\",\"They were reciting a poem together\",\"They were teaching each other new words\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The phrase implies that each spoken word provoked a sharper response, leading to a growing quarrel.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the story, what does the word \'holiday\' most likely refer to?\",\"options\":[\"A day of hard labor in the fields\",\"A regular school day\",\"A special day of celebration or rest\",\"A time for fighting and arguments\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the textbook context, a holiday is a day of celebration where people are dressed up and not working.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What physical action is described by the phrase \'knocked her off her feet\'?\",\"options\":[\"She was picked up gently\",\"She was pushed or hit so hard that she fell\",\"She started dancing suddenly\",\"She ran away very fast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To be knocked off one\'s feet means to be struck or pushed causing a fall.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what was the old woman trying to do during the conflict?\",\"options\":[\"She was trying to start a new fight\",\"She was trying to stop the people from fighting\",\"She was trying to play with the girls\",\"She was trying to clean the muddy water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The old woman acts as a peacemaker, attempting to reason with the fighting adults.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the girls, Akulka and Malasha, do while the adults were still quarreling?\",\"options\":[\"They joined the fight with the men\",\"They went home to their mothers\",\"They forgot their quarrel and began playing together\",\"They started crying louder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that the girls moved on from their disagreement much faster than the adults.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the men feel ashamed at the end of the incident?\",\"options\":[\"Because they lost the physical fight\",\"Because the old woman hit them\",\"Because the girls had made peace while the men were still fighting\",\"Because it started raining on their holiday clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The men realized their behavior was foolish compared to the children who had already reconciled.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a child is \'crying\' after a scuffle in this story, what does it most likely indicate?\",\"options\":[\"They are very happy\",\"They are hurt or upset by an action\",\"They are singing a song\",\"They are trying to sleep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Crying in this context is a reaction to the physical or emotional distress of the quarrel.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which two characters are the \'Little Girls\' mentioned in the chapter title?\",\"options\":[\"The old woman and Akulka\",\"Malasha and the old woman\",\"Akulka and Malasha\",\"The mothers of the two girls\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Akulka and Malasha are the two central child characters who have the initial quarrel.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the girls\' behavior of playing together again suggest about their character?\",\"options\":[\"They are forgetful and silly\",\"They are more forgiving and wiser than the adults\",\"They are afraid of the men\",\"They want to cause more trouble\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Their ability to forgive and play again shows a wisdom that the fighting adults lacked.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the prompt \'Did the people stop fighting?\', what can be inferred about the adults\' initial reaction?\",\"options\":[\"They immediately listened to the old woman\",\"They ignored the children and continued their dispute\",\"They went to church together\",\"They apologized to each other right away\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies the adults were caught up in the heat of the moment and did not stop immediately.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Comprehension\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who was trying to separate the fighting men?\",\"options\":[\"Malasha\",\"Akulka\",\"The old woman\",\"Count Leo Tolstoy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the old woman was trying to separate the men while the water went down the rill.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What were the girls laughing at as they ran?\",\"options\":[\"The fighting men\",\"A chip bobbing in the rill\",\"The old woman\'s words\",\"Each other\'s clothes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the girls were running and laughing at a chip which was bobbing up and down the rill.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who shouted \'Look out, Malasha, look out!\'?\",\"options\":[\"The old woman\",\"The peasants\",\"Akulka\",\"Leo Wiener\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly attributes this shout to Akulka.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the men go after they felt ashamed and laughed at themselves?\",\"options\":[\"To the rill\",\"To the market\",\"To their farms\",\"To the old woman\'s house\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the men scattered to their farms at the end.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Malasha unable to speak when Akulka shouted to her?\",\"options\":[\"She was too angry to talk\",\"She could not speak for laughter\",\"She was out of breath from running\",\"She was afraid of the peasants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says Malasha wanted to say something but could not speak for laughter.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the old woman mean when she says the girls have \'long ago forgotten it\'?\",\"options\":[\"The girls have lost their memory\",\"The girls have moved on from their quarrel and are playing\",\"The girls do not recognize their parents\",\"The girls have forgotten how to fight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"She points out that while the men are still fighting, the children have already reconciled and are playing nicely.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is a \'rill\'?\",\"options\":[\"A large wooden fence\",\"A small stream of water\",\"A type of farm tool\",\"A musical instrument\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The context of water going down it and a chip bobbing in it indicates it is a small stream.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the men feel ashamed at the end of the story?\",\"options\":[\"Because the old woman scolded them loudly\",\"Because they lost the fight\",\"Because they realized they were fighting while the children had made peace\",\"Because they forgot to go to their farms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The men felt ashamed when they saw the girls playing nicely together despite the men fighting on their account.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the girls were running on opposite sides of the rill, what were they doing?\",\"options\":[\"Running away from each other\",\"Running along the banks of the same stream\",\"Running in different villages\",\"Running toward the old woman to stop her\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Running \'one from one side... the other from the other side\' of the rill implies they were flanking the stream.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which action by the girls best demonstrates that they are \'wiser\' than the peasants?\",\"options\":[\"Running straight into the crowd\",\"Shouting at each other by the rill\",\"Playing nicely together after a quarrel\",\"Laughing at a bobbing chip\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The old woman identifies their ability to forget the fight and play together as the sign of their wisdom.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the irony in the peasants\' behavior compared to the girls\' behavior?\",\"options\":[\"The adults are fighting over a problem the children have already solved\",\"The children are fighting while the adults are playing\",\"The old woman is younger than the peasants\",\"The water is flowing uphill instead of downhill\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The irony lies in the fact that the \'mature\' adults are violent while the \'immature\' children have reconciled.\",\"topic\":\"The Resolution\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the ultima