2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Enact the whole story without using words\'.\",\"topic\":\"Word Categories\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal of the TOW activity according to the instructions?\",\"options\":[\"To write long essays\",\"To give as many examples as possible within a time limit\",\"To draw pictures of animals\",\"To memorize the whole story\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The instruction says \'Give as many examples of the following as possible within two minutes\'.\",\"topic\":\"Word Categories\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student provides the words \'Violin\' and \'Guitar\', which TOW category are they addressing?\",\"options\":[\"Animals\",\"Body parts\",\"Vegetables\",\"Musical instruments\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Violin and Guitar are examples of Musical instruments, which is one of the listed categories.\",\"topic\":\"Word Categories\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these words would correctly fit into the \'Vegetables\' category mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sparrow\",\"Spinach\",\"Harmonium\",\"Elbow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Spinach is a vegetable, while the others belong to Birds, Musical instruments, and Body parts respectively.\",\"topic\":\"Word Categories\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The discussion questions in the \'Things to do\' section focus on the relationship between which two groups?\",\"options\":[\"Teachers and students\",\"Animals and birds\",\"Children and grown ups\",\"Musicians and actors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The discussion points ask if children are wiser than grown ups and if grown ups should involve themselves in children\'s quarrels.\",\"topic\":\"Word Categories\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the textbook likely include \'Two minutes of Oral Work\' with specific categories like \'Body parts\' and \'Birds\'?\",\"options\":[\"To test the student\'s ability to act without words\",\"To categorize and recall vocabulary quickly\",\"To teach students how to play musical instruments\",\"To explain why grown ups quarrel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity requires students to quickly categorize and recall words, testing their vocabulary range.\",\"topic\":\"Word Categories\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the timing guideline, how many minutes should be used for each word?\",\"options\":[\"One minute\",\"Two minutes\",\"Three minutes\",\"Four minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to \'Use two minutes per word.\'\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the instruction \'Use two minutes per word\', what does the word \'per\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"For every single\",\"Only for the first\",\"For all words combined\",\"Excluding the last\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"\'Per word\' indicates the time allocated for each individual word.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student has to process 5 words, how much total time should they take based on the guideline?\",\"options\":[\"5 minutes\",\"7 minutes\",\"10 minutes\",\"15 minutes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"5 words multiplied by 2 minutes per word equals 10 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a task involving 10 words is completed in exactly 20 minutes, how was the guideline followed?\",\"options\":[\"The guideline was followed exactly\",\"The student worked too fast\",\"The student worked too slow\",\"The guideline was ignored\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"10 words at 2 minutes each equals 20 minutes, matching the guideline.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit of time is specified in the timing guideline?\",\"options\":[\"Seconds\",\"Hours\",\"Minutes\",\"Days\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'minutes\' as the unit of time.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the guideline specifically referring to when it mentions \'two minutes\'?\",\"options\":[\"The time for a whole page\",\"The time for a single word\",\"The time for a whole sentence\",\"The time for the entire chapter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The instruction specifies the time \'per word\'.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student has 12 minutes to complete their work. According to the rule, how many words should they finish?\",\"options\":[\"2 words\",\"4 words\",\"6 words\",\"12 words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"12 minutes divided by 2 minutes per word equals 6 words.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the instruction was changed to \'double the current time per word\', how many minutes would be spent on one word?\",\"options\":[\"2 minutes\",\"4 minutes\",\"1 minute\",\"8 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Doubling the current 2 minutes results in 4 minutes per word.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a student completes 3 words in 3 minutes, are they following the guideline?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, they are exactly on time\",\"No, they are slower than required\",\"No, they are faster than the two-minute rule\",\"Yes, because they finished the words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The rule requires 6 minutes for 3 words; 3 minutes is too fast.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Complete the instruction from the text: \'Use two minutes per ___.\'\",\"options\":[\"letter\",\"sentence\",\"word\",\"paragraph\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook text specifies \'word\' as the unit.\",\"topic\":\"Timing Guidelines\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"remember\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21895,41,7,'English','en','1.4 Journey to the West','[\"Journey to the West is a famous Chinese epic written by Wu Cheng’en in the 16th century.\",\"It tells the story of the monk Yuan Chwang (also known as Xuanzang) who travelled from China to India to bring Buddhist scriptures back home.\",\"Yuan Chwang’s journey took 17 years and crossed deserts, mountains and the Silk Road.\",\"The monk was helped by three disciples: Sun Wukong (the Monkey King), Zhu Bajie (Pigsy) and Sha Wujing (the Sand Priest).\",\"Sun Wukong can transform into 72 different forms and has super‑powers like flying, walking through fire and having x‑ray vision.\",\"The novel mixes historical facts with folklore, making it both a historical record and a fictional adventure.\",\"The word ‘west’ is relative; for people in China, the west is the direction of India and other western countries.\",\"Yuan Chwang’s dream was to bring Buddhist scriptures to China, a pilgrimage for him.\",\"The story teaches about perseverance, faith, and the use of wisdom to overcome obstacles.\",\"The novel is still popular in China and has inspired many films and translations worldwide.\",\"Key terms: epic (long poem about a hero), pilgrimage (religious journey), supernatural (beyond natural laws), demon (evil spirit).\",\"Sun Wukong’s special powers include 72 transformations, a magical staff that changes size, and the ability to travel thousands of kilometres in one somersault.\",\"The disciples were not saints; they were mischievous but helped Yuan Chwang to return to heaven.\",\"The novel’s title refers to the direction of the journey, not the western world as we think of it today.\",\"The story shows that even powerful beings can be guided by compassion and duty.\",\"The journey involved crossing the Gobi Desert, the Tian Shan mountains and the Silk Road through modern Kyrgyzstan, Uzbekistan, Afghanistan and Pakistan.\",\"Yuan Chwang’s return journey was equally difficult but successful.\",\"The novel is a blend of history, mythology and moral lessons.\",\"Students can compare the journey with modern travel methods like cars, trains and planes.\",\"The story encourages reading both the historical account and the fantastical tales to understand both sides.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the famous traveller mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Marco Polo\",\"Yuan Chwang\",\"Fa Hien\",\"Columbus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the famous seventh century traveller Yuan Chwang.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which century did Yuan Chwang make his journey?\",\"options\":[\"Fifth century\",\"Sixth century\",\"Seventh century\",\"Eighth century\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Yuan Chwang was a famous seventh century traveller.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what do people naturally think of when they hear \'journey to the west\'?\",\"options\":[\"A journey to China\",\"A journey to India\",\"A journey to countries like England and America\",\"A journey to the mountains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says we naturally start thinking about a journey to western countries like England and America.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The text describes the term \'west\' as being:\",\"options\":[\"A fixed location\",\"A relative term\",\"A country\'s name\",\"An absolute direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically states, \'But \\\"west\\\" is a relative term.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is in a country to the east of India, how would they describe India\'s location?\",\"options\":[\"As the East\",\"As the West\",\"As the North\",\"As the South\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'For countries to our east, WE are the west!\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"From which country did Yuan Chwang start his journey to India?\",\"options\":[\"England\",\"America\",\"China\",\"Japan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the journey was actually a journey from China to our own country (India).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Yuan Chwang is described in the text as a famous _____.\",\"options\":[\"King\",\"Soldier\",\"Traveller\",\"Scientist\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to him as the \'famous seventh century traveller\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why might the title \'Journey to the West\' be confusing for an Indian reader at first?\",\"options\":[\"Because the journey is actually to India, which we don\'t usually call \'the west\'\",\"Because the journey was made by a Chinese person\",\"Because the journey happened a long time ago\",\"Because the journey was made by sea\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that while we think of England\\/America as \'west\', this journey was actually to India.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best explains why India is \'west\' for China?\",\"options\":[\"India is located to the east of China\",\"India is located to the west of China\",\"India and China are in the same place\",\"China is a western country\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies relativity; since the journey from China to India is called a \'journey to the west\', India must be west of China.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement about the word \'West\' is true?\",\"options\":[\"It only refers to England and America\",\"It is a term that never changes\",\"Its meaning depends on your current location\",\"It is a term used only by travellers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that \'west\' is relative, meaning it changes based on where you are (e.g., India is west for those further east).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How did people travel from China to India in the days of Yuan Chwang?\",\"options\":[\"By ship or boat\",\"On foot or horseback\",\"By train or bus\",\"By air or sea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that one had to travel thousands of miles on foot or horseback.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'scriptures\' as defined in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Travel diaries\",\"Maps of the region\",\"Holy, sacred books or writings\",\"Stories of ancient kings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines scriptures as holy, sacred books or writings.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was Yuan Chwang\'s specific dream mentioned in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"To become a famous explorer\",\"To take Buddhist scriptures from India to China\",\"To build new roads across the mountains\",\"To defeat the bandits and robbers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says Yuan Chwang had a dream to take Buddhist scriptures from India to his homeland in China.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a \'pilgrimage\'?\",\"options\":[\"A journey for a religious purpose\",\"A business trip to a foreign land\",\"A vacation to the mountains\",\"A search for hidden treasure\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines a pilgrimage as a journey for a religious purpose.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was NOT mentioned as a physical challenge of the journey?\",\"options\":[\"Crossing deserts\",\"Crossing snow-clad mountains\",\"Crossing deep oceans\",\"Crossing strange, unfamiliar regions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions deserts, snow-clad mountains, and unfamiliar regions, but not oceans.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a traveler in those days chose to use the roads, what danger did they face?\",\"options\":[\"Heavy traffic\",\"Bandits and robbers\",\"Lack of food\",\"Wild animals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that bandits and robbers roamed the roads.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A traveler crossing \'snow-clad mountains\' would most likely need to prepare for which condition?\",\"options\":[\"Extreme heat and sandstorms\",\"Very cold temperatures and snow\",\"Heavy rainfall and floods\",\"Strong winds in the desert\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Snow-clad means covered in snow, implying cold conditions.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the author ask the question \'who would do it?\' after describing the journey?\",\"options\":[\"To ask for a list of travelers\",\"To emphasize how difficult and dangerous the journey was\",\"To show that the journey was very popular\",\"To suggest that the journey was impossible\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The question highlights the extreme hardships that would discourage most people.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase from the text indicates that Yuan Chwang was entering areas he did not know?\",\"options\":[\"Thousands of miles\",\"Strange, unfamiliar regions\",\"Homeland in China\",\"Holy, sacred books\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Unfamiliar regions\' refers to places that are not known or recognized by the traveler.\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what was the primary motivation that drove Yuan Chwang to face such dangers?\",\"options\":[\"The desire for wealth and gold\",\"His religious dream and purpose\",\"The need to find a shorter route\",\"A command from the King of China\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains he had a dream to bring scriptures, describing it as a pilgrimage (religious purpose).\",\"topic\":\"Yuan Chwang\'s Dream\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"For how many years did Yuan Chwang keep on travelling?\",\"options\":[\"Seven years\",\"Ten years\",\"Seventeen years\",\"Twenty years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states he kept on travelling for seventeen long years.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which desert did Yuan Chwang travel across during his journey?\",\"options\":[\"Sahara Desert\",\"Gobi Desert\",\"Thar Desert\",\"Arabian Desert\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he travelled across the Gobi Desert.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Yuan Chwang undertake this long and difficult journey?\",\"options\":[\"To become a king\",\"To follow his dream\",\"To trade silk\",\"To escape his country\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he undertook the journey to follow his dream.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Along which mountain range did Yuan Chwang travel?\",\"options\":[\"The Himalayas\",\"The Alps\",\"The Tian Shan mountain range\",\"The Rocky Mountains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies he travelled along the Tian Shan mountain range.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these modern-day regions did Yuan Chwang pass through before reaching India?\",\"options\":[\"Kyrgyzstan\",\"Egypt\",\"Thailand\",\"Japan\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists Kyrgyzstan as one of the regions he passed through before India.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What did Yuan Chwang gather when he reached India?\",\"options\":[\"Gold and silver\",\"Important manuscripts\",\"Rare plants\",\"Silk and spices\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in India, he gathered many important manuscripts.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the name of the famous road Yuan Chwang travelled along?\",\"options\":[\"The Golden Road\",\"The Cotton Road\",\"The Silk Road\",\"The Spice Road\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he travelled on and on along the Silk Road.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what was the sequence of his journey?\",\"options\":[\"India, then Gobi Desert, then Silk Road\",\"Gobi Desert, then Tian Shan range, then Silk Road\",\"Silk Road, then Gobi Desert, then Tian Shan range\",\"Pakistan, then Gobi Desert, then India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the order as Gobi Desert, then Tian Shan mountain range, then the Silk Road.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The phrase \'suffering many hardships\' suggests that the journey was:\",\"options\":[\"Very comfortable and easy\",\"Short and quick\",\"Full of difficulties and pain\",\"Fun and exciting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Hardships refer to the difficulties he faced during his seventeen-year journey.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is tracing Yuan Chwang\'s route on a map, which country would be his final destination mentioned in this text?\",\"options\":[\"Uzbekistan\",\"Afghanistan\",\"Pakistan\",\"India\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text describes the regions he passed through \'before he came to India\', making India the destination.\",\"topic\":\"The Long and Difficult Journey\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where did Yuan Chwang reach safely after his difficult journey?\",\"options\":[\"India\",\"China\",\"Japan\",\"Tibet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Yuan Chwang managed to reach China safely.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Yuan Chwang bring back with him to China?\",\"options\":[\"Gold and jewels\",\"Maps of the west\",\"Scriptures\",\"Spices and silk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he reached China safely along with the scriptures.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did people believe that gods and supernatural powers had helped Yuan Chwang?\",\"options\":[\"Because he was a god himself\",\"Because his achievement was almost unbelievable\",\"Because he told everyone he saw gods\",\"Because the journey was very short\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says his achievement was so great and unbelievable that people thought supernatural powers helped him.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the direct result of people believing that supernatural powers helped Yuan Chwang?\",\"options\":[\"A new law was passed\",\"He was made the King\",\"Many interesting folk tales were created\",\"People stopped traveling to the west\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the belief in supernatural help \'gave rise to many interesting folk tales\'.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When was the epic novel \'Journey to the West\' written?\",\"options\":[\"Before Yuan Chwang started his journey\",\"During the journey\",\"Immediately after he returned\",\"Centuries after the pilgrimage\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the novel was written \'Centuries later\'.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how did the author of \'Journey to the West\' create the novel?\",\"options\":[\"By interviewing Yuan Chwang\",\"By making use of many folk tales\",\"By imagining everything from scratch\",\"By translating Indian books\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the novel \'made use of many of the folk tales\'.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which character represents the pilgrim Yuan Chwang returning with the scriptures?\",\"options\":[\"The monkey-like figure with a staff\",\"The man riding the white horse\",\"The man carrying the luggage\",\"The pig-like figure at the front\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The central figure on the horse represents the monk Yuan Chwang on his pilgrimage.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/029_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the presence of the monkey, pig, and other unique characters illustrates which specific idea from the text?\",\"options\":[\"The way back was difficult\",\"The journey to China\",\"Supernatural powers helping in the quest\",\"The writing of the epic novel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The supernatural-looking companions represent the \'supernatural powers\' the text says people believed helped him.\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/029_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text say about the popularity of the novel and its stories today?\",\"options\":[\"They are no longer read\",\"They are only popular in China\",\"They are still very popular\",\"They are considered too difficult to read\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes by saying \'The novel and the stories in it are still very popular.\'\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text compare the journey back home to the journey going there?\",\"options\":[\"The way back was much easier\",\"The way back was equally difficult\",\"The way back was much longer\",\"The way back was very boring\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text starts by saying \'The way back home was also equally difficult.\'\",\"topic\":\"Return and Folk Tales\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who is described as the goddess of mercy in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sun Wukong\",\"Guanyin deity\",\"Zu Bajji\",\"Sha Wujing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the Guanyin deity is the goddess of mercy.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'demons\' as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Holy monks\",\"Goddesses\",\"Evil spirits\",\"Protectors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'demons\' as \'evil spirits\' in the vocabulary notes.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which character is also known by the name \'Pigsy\'?\",\"options\":[\"Sun Wukong\",\"Sha Wujing\",\"Zu Bajji\",\"Guanyin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'Zu Bajji or Pigsy\' as one of the three special people.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What do the demons and evil powers want to do to the holy monk?\",\"options\":[\"Protect him from danger\",\"Kill and gobble him up\",\"Help him on his journey\",\"Make him their king\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that many demons and evil powers are eager to kill and gobble up the holy monk.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what does the word \'vanquish\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"To run away\",\"To surrender\",\"To conquer or defeat\",\"To protect\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'vanquish\' as \'conquer, defeat\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many special people did the Guanyin deity assign to protect the monk?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states she assigned the task to \'three special people\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a character is referred to as the \'Sand Priest\', which name are they associated with in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sun Wukong\",\"Zu Bajji\",\"Sha Wujing\",\"Guanyin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'Sha Wujing or Sand Priest\' as one of the assigned protectors.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which role does Sun Wukong play in the journey according to the passage?\",\"options\":[\"The holy monk\",\"The goddess of mercy\",\"One of the protectors assigned to save the monk\",\"An evil spirit trying to kill the monk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sun Wukong is one of the three people assigned the task of saving the monk and vanquishing demons.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the Guanyin deity need to assign protectors to the holy monk?\",\"options\":[\"Because the monk was lost in the West\",\"Because the monk wanted to learn how to fight\",\"Because many demons were eager to kill the monk\",\"Because the monk was looking for the goddess\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies protectors were needed because demons and evil powers were eager to kill and gobble up the monk.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary relationship between the Guanyin deity and the three special people?\",\"options\":[\"She is their enemy\",\"She assigned them a specific task to perform\",\"She is one of the demons they must vanquish\",\"They are her teachers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Guanyin deity \'has assigned this task to three special people\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Novel and the Protectors\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how are the three characters described regarding their behavior?\",\"options\":[\"They are holy saints.\",\"They are known troublemakers.\",\"They are kind deities.\",\"They have no special powers.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the characters are \'known trouble makers\' and \'no saints\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who was responsible for throwing the three characters out of heaven?\",\"options\":[\"The kind deity Guanyin\",\"The tricksters\",\"The gods\",\"The saints\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'the gods have thrown them out of heaven\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of a \'cudgel\' as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A long sharp sword\",\"A magic spell\",\"A short heavy stick\",\"A pair of wings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a cudgel as \'a short heavy stick\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why were the three characters thrown out of heaven by the gods?\",\"options\":[\"Because they were saints\",\"Because they were troublemakers\",\"Because they had no powers\",\"Because they helped Guanyin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links their expulsion to the fact that they are known troublemakers.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which word best describes the deity Guanyin based on her actions in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Angry\",\"Strict\",\"Kind\",\"Fearful\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to her as \'the kind deity Guanyin\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the ability \'x-ray vision\' allow a character to do?\",\"options\":[\"To fly through the air\",\"To see through something\",\"To play tricks on others\",\"To jump very high\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines x-ray vision as \'the ability to see through something\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a character performs a somersault \'at will\', when does it happen?\",\"options\":[\"Only when the gods allow it\",\"Whenever the character wants or likes\",\"Only when they are in heaven\",\"Every morning at sunrise\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'at will\' as \'whenever one wants or likes\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a character is described as a \'trickster\', what action are they most likely to perform?\",\"options\":[\"Helping a saint\",\"Playing a trick\",\"Building a heaven\",\"Throwing away a cudgel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a trickster as \'one who plays tricks\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what can we conclude about the relationship between supernatural powers and being a saint?\",\"options\":[\"Only saints have supernatural powers.\",\"Having supernatural powers does not automatically make one a saint.\",\"Supernatural powers are only given to those in heaven.\",\"Troublemakers are not allowed to have supernatural powers.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the characters \'are no saints\' even though \'they all have supernatural powers\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the significance of Guanyin offering something to the characters despite their past?\",\"options\":[\"It shows she wants to be a troublemaker too.\",\"It shows her kindness in giving them an opportunity.\",\"It shows she is afraid of their supernatural powers.\",\"It shows the gods made a mistake.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies her as \'kind\', implying her offer is an act of benevolence toward the outcasts.\",\"topic\":\"The Three Characters\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the holy monk going on his pilgrimage?\",\"options\":[\"China\",\"India\",\"Heaven\",\"The West\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the pilgrimage is to India, which is referred to as the holy land.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many characters agreed to be Yuan Chwang\'s disciples?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'the three characters have agreed to be Yuan Chwang\'s disciples\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the condition for the characters to return to heaven?\",\"options\":[\"They must live in India forever.\",\"They must fight all the enemies in heaven.\",\"They must help the holy monk and use their powers for good work.\",\"They must find a new holy land.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if they help the holy monk and use their powers for good work, they may return to heaven.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text describe the nature of the journey to India?\",\"options\":[\"Easy and short\",\"Difficult\",\"Boring and long\",\"Impossible to complete\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly describes the journey as \'difficult\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what would happen if the disciples refused to use their powers for good work?\",\"options\":[\"They would be rewarded in India.\",\"They would not be allowed to return to heaven.\",\"They would become holy monks themselves.\",\"They would have to stay in heaven.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that returning to heaven is dependent on using their powers for good work.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these words is NOT used in the text to describe the adventures on the way to India?\",\"options\":[\"Thrilling\",\"Exciting\",\"Humorous\",\"Scary\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions thrilling, exciting, and humorous adventures, but does not use the word \'scary\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the \'holy monk\' mentioned in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"One of the three disciples\",\"Yuan Chwang\",\"A king from India\",\"A god from heaven\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Yuan Chwang as the holy monk whom the disciples are helping.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the disciples expected to use for the \'good work\'?\",\"options\":[\"Their wealth\",\"Their powers\",\"Their weapons\",\"Their maps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that they should \'use your powers for this good work\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is another name for India?\",\"options\":[\"The land of heaven\",\"The holy land\",\"The land of disciples\",\"The land of adventures\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to India as \'the holy land\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary motivation for the three characters to help Yuan Chwang?\",\"options\":[\"They want to see the world.\",\"They want to become famous in India.\",\"They want the chance to return to heaven.\",\"They want to learn new powers.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states they agreed to help so that they \'may return to heaven\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Disciples\' Mission\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where was Sun Wukong born?\",\"options\":[\"In a golden palace\",\"On the Flower Fruit Mountain\",\"Under the deep ocean\",\"Inside a pillar supporting the sky\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he was born when winds blew over a huge stone egg on the Flower Fruit Mountain.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many different ways can Sun Wukong transform himself?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"12\",\"72\",\"100\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions he is able to transform himself in 72 different ways.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific name of Sun Wukong\'s magical weapon?\",\"options\":[\"The Diamond Pillar\",\"The X-ray Staff\",\"The As-you-wish-gold-banded cudgel\",\"The Flower Fruit Sword\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies his magical weapon as the \'As-you-wish-gold-banded cudgel\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does Sun Wukong do with his cudgel when it becomes as tiny as a needle?\",\"options\":[\"He swallows it\",\"He puts it in his pocket\",\"He tucks it behind his ear\",\"He hides it in his hair\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says when it becomes tiny as a needle, the Monkey King tucks it away behind his ear.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it stated that no one and nothing can injure Sun Wukong?\",\"options\":[\"Because he can fly away very fast\",\"Because his body is as hard as a diamond\",\"Because he can walk through fire\",\"Because he has 72 transformations\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that his body is as hard as a diamond, so nothing can injure him.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unique capability of Sun Wukong\'s hair strands?\",\"options\":[\"They can grow into giant pillars\",\"They can be used to see through walls\",\"They can transform into any living or non-living thing\",\"They are as hard as diamonds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he can transform each hair strand into any living or non-living thing of his choice.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Sun Wukong needs to travel a distance of 5,000 kilometres quickly, which power would be most useful?\",\"options\":[\"Walking through fire\",\"Using his x-ray vision\",\"Performing a somersault\",\"Transforming into a needle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he can travel thousands of kilometres in just one somersault.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an enemy tries to hide behind a thick wooden door, how would Sun Wukong find them?\",\"options\":[\"By using his \'golden-gaze-fiery eyes\' with x-ray vision\",\"By transforming the door into a hair strand\",\"By growing his cudgel into a pillar\",\"By walking through the fire\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"His \'golden-gaze-fiery eyes\' have x-ray vision, allowing him to see things others cannot.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the versatility of the \'As-you-wish-gold-banded cudgel\'?\",\"options\":[\"It can only be used as a weapon for hitting enemies\",\"It changes size from a sky-supporting pillar to a tiny needle\",\"It is only useful when Sun Wukong is under water\",\"It transforms into 72 different living things\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes it growing as big as a pillar or becoming as tiny as a needle.\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what makes Sun Wukong\'s vision superior to others?\",\"options\":[\"He can see in 72 different directions at once\",\"He can see through fire and water easily\",\"His x-ray vision prevents anyone from deceiving him\",\"His eyes are made of hard diamonds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states his x-ray vision means \'No one can deceive him.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Extraordinary Sun Wukong\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Sun Wukong needs to create a distraction by making a swarm of bees. How could he do this according to his powers?\",\"options\":[\"By using his