bulary and Discussion (Mouse)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When the town mouse \'turned up his long nose\' at the country food, what was he most likely feeling?\",\"options\":[\"Great hunger and excitement\",\"Dislike or a feeling of superiority\",\"Fear of the rustic surroundings\",\"Confusion about the type of food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To \'turn up one\'s nose\' is an idiom indicating dislike or looking down on something.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Discussion (Mouse)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the discussion section, what does the country mouse\'s use of \'only!\' suggest about his view of town life?\",\"options\":[\"He thinks town life is better than country life\",\"He is surprised by how cheap town life is\",\"He feels the benefits of town life are not worth the danger\",\"He wants to eat more town food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The use of \'only!\' in this context suggests that the country mouse finds the town life\'s risks outweigh its rewards.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary and Discussion (Mouse)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what do verbs (action words) show?\",\"options\":[\"The place where an action happens\",\"The time at which the action happens\",\"The person who does the action\",\"The speed of the action\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that verbs have different forms to show the time at which the action happens.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which endings are usually found at the end of past tense verb forms?\",\"options\":[\"\'-s\', \'-es\', or \'-ies\'\",\"\'-ing\' or \'-ong\'\",\"\'-d\', \'-ed\', or \'-ied\'\",\"\'-will\' or \'-shall\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that past tense forms usually have ‘-d’, ‘-ed’, or ‘-ied’ at the end.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which words are used to indicate the future tense?\",\"options\":[\"is and are\",\"was and were\",\"will and shall\",\"do and does\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the future is indicated by ‘will’ and ‘shall’.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to present tense verbs when used with the pronouns \'I, we, you and they\'?\",\"options\":[\"They always end in \'-s\'\",\"They change to past tense\",\"There is no change in them\",\"They must add \'-ed\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says present tense forms used with I, we, you and they do not have ‘-s’, ‘-es’ or ‘-ies’, there is no change in them.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the example \'He lives in a hole\', what tense is being used?\",\"options\":[\"Past tense\",\"Present tense\",\"Future tense\",\"Irregular tense\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'Present - He lives in a hole\' as a specific example.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the present tense form usually changed for a third person singular subject?\",\"options\":[\"By adding \'-d\' or \'-ed\'\",\"By adding \'-s\', \'-es\' or \'-ies\'\",\"By adding \'will\'\",\"By removing the ending\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that some present tense forms (third person singular) have -s, -es or -ies at the end.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the present tense is \'like\\/likes\', what is the correct past tense form according to the table?\",\"options\":[\"liking\",\"will like\",\"liked\",\"likes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the text shows \'liked\' as the past tense for \'like\\/likes\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Complete the sequence based on the irregular verbs table: flew (Past), fly\\/flies (Present), _______ (Future).\",\"options\":[\"flying\",\"will fly\",\"flied\",\"shall flying\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern in the table, the future tense is formed by adding \'will\' to the present form.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these sentences correctly uses the present tense as shown in the text\'s examples?\",\"options\":[\"Last year I wrote poems.\",\"Now I write short stories.\",\"In future, I will write novels.\",\"He lived in a hole.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'Now I write short stories\' as an example of the present tense.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following verbs is classified in the text as having an \'irregular\' past tense form?\",\"options\":[\"liked\",\"scampered\",\"went\",\"laughed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text includes \'went\' in a table specifically for verbs with \'irregular\' past tense forms.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table of irregular verbs, what is the past tense of \'give\'?\",\"options\":[\"gives\",\"giving\",\"gave\",\"will give\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table explicitly lists \'gave\' as the past tense form.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which pair correctly matches the Present to the Past tense for an irregular verb mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"go -> went\",\"like -> liked\",\"scamper -> scampered\",\"laugh -> laughed\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'go\\/goes\' to \'went\' as an irregular form, whereas the others follow the regular \'-ed\' pattern.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the topic text, what is the main purpose of a \'menu card\' in a hotel?\",\"options\":[\"To show pictures of the chef\",\"To list available food items and their prices\",\"To provide the history of the hotel building\",\"To give directions to the nearest hospital\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A menu card is used to inform customers about the food choices and prices available at a restaurant.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many people does the customer need a table for?\",\"options\":[\"Four\",\"Two\",\"Six\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The customer in the dialogue explicitly states, \'We need a table for six.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/053_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these cities is NOT mentioned in the text as a place to imagine opening a hotel?\",\"options\":[\"Nagpur\",\"London\",\"Pune\",\"Dahiwadi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Dahiwadi, Nagpur, Bengaluru, and London; Pune is not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the price of a \'Delux Thali\'?\",\"options\":[\"160\\/-\",\"230\\/-\",\"90\\/-\",\"50\\/-\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The menu card shows the price for a Delux Thali as 230\\/-.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the dialogue between the Maharashtrian family, what kind of food does the Great Grandma prefer?\",\"options\":[\"Something very spicy\",\"Something crunchy\",\"Something soft\",\"Something cold\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Great Grandma says, \'Something soft for me.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you open a hotel in London, which language would be most essential for your menu card according to the activity context?\",\"options\":[\"Marathi\",\"Kannada\",\"English\",\"Hindi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"London is an English-speaking city, making English the most appropriate language for the menu.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the menu card shown, which sweet item is specifically marked as \'Seasonal\'?\",\"options\":[\"Gulab jamun\",\"Basundi\",\"Aamras\",\"Puran Poli\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Under the Sweets section, \'Aamras\' has \'(Seasonal)\' written next to it.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What requirement does Grandma have for the food she wants to order?\",\"options\":[\"It should be very oily\",\"It should not be very spicy\",\"It must be a sweet dish\",\"It must be from the Upwas Special section\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Grandma says, \'And not very spicy.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the menu card, calculate the total cost for one \'Sabudana Wada\' and one \'Gulab jamun\'.\",\"options\":[\"80\\/-\",\"70\\/-\",\"90\\/-\",\"100\\/-\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sabudana Wada is 50\\/- and Gulab jamun is 30\\/-. 50 + 30 = 80.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text suggest that hotel staff need to know specific languages?\",\"options\":[\"To talk to their family members\",\"To read history books\",\"To communicate effectively with customers in that location\",\"To write poems about food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Staff need to know languages to interact with the local and visiting customers at the hotel\'s location.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which item is included in a \'Regular Thali\' but NOT listed in the \'Marathi Zatka\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Butter\",\"Dahi\",\"Matki Usal\",\"Zunka Bhakar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dahi is listed in the Regular Thali description, whereas the other options are either in Marathi Zatka or not in the Thali.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the dialogue, who is the Hotel Staff addressing when they say \'Good evening Sir\'?\",\"options\":[\"The Australian Customer\",\"The Hotel Manager\",\"The Customer asking for a table\",\"The Great Grandma\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The dialogue shows the Hotel Staff greeting the customer who then asks for a table for six.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/053_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a hotel is opened in Bengaluru, which local language script would likely be used alongside English?\",\"options\":[\"Devanagari (Marathi)\",\"Kannada\",\"Bengali\",\"Tamil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bengaluru is in Karnataka, where Kannada is the local language and script.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the price difference between a \'Delux Thali\' and a \'Regular Thali\'?\",\"options\":[\"50\\/-\",\"60\\/-\",\"70\\/-\",\"80\\/-\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Delux Thali (230) minus Regular Thali (160) equals 70.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an \'Upwas Special\' item according to the menu?\",\"options\":[\"Bharali Wangi\",\"Sabudana Khichadi\",\"Kothimbir Wadi\",\"Masala Bhat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sabudana Khichadi is listed under the \'Upwas Special\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/052_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21900,41,7,'English','en','2.3 Teeny-tiny','[\"Adjectives describe nouns and show size, colour, shape, age, quality, material, etc.\",\"Teen‑tiny means very small; it is a compound adjective used in informal stories.\",\"In the story, the word ‘teeny‑tiny’ is repeated to create rhythm and humour.\",\"Adjectives can be single or paired, e.g., ‘tiny‑tiny’, ‘small‑small’, ‘little‑little’.\",\"The story shows how a repeated adjective can change the tone of a sentence.\",\"Main verbs are ‘be’, ‘have’, ‘do’; they can also act as auxiliary (helping) verbs.\",\"Auxiliary ‘do’ is used to form questions, negatives and emphatic statements.\",\"When ‘do’ is used with a main verb, it helps to ask questions like ‘Do you know?’ or ‘Did she see?’\",\"Negative sentences with ‘do’ look like ‘I do not like’ or ‘They did not go’.\",\"The story’s repeated use of ‘teeny‑tiny’ demonstrates how adjectives can be exaggerated for effect.\",\"In English, adjectives can be used before nouns (attributive) or after linking verbs (predicative).\",\"Compound adjectives often use a hyphen, e.g., ‘teeny‑tiny’, ‘big‑big’.\",\"The story’s structure shows a simple past narrative with repeated actions.\",\"The teacher’s activity asks students to replace ‘teeny‑tiny’ with other adjectives and see if the story still works.\",\"The story also illustrates how adjectives can be used to describe objects, places, and feelings.\",\"The story’s ending shows a change from fear to confidence, reflected in the voice’s volume.\",\"The chapter encourages creative writing by asking students to write their own repetitive stories.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where did the teeny-tiny woman live according to the story?\",\"options\":[\"In a teeny-tiny castle\",\"In a teeny-tiny village\",\"In a teeny-tiny churchyard\",\"In a teeny-tiny forest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the woman lived in a teeny-tiny house in a teeny-tiny village.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the teeny-tiny woman tie before going out for a walk?\",\"options\":[\"Her teeny-tiny shoes\",\"Her teeny-tiny apron\",\"Her teeny-tiny scarf\",\"Her teeny-tiny hat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions she tied her teeny-tiny scarf before going out.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what activity is the teeny-tiny woman performing?\",\"options\":[\"Opening a teeny-tiny gate\",\"Taking a teeny-tiny walk\",\"Looking for a teeny-tiny bone\",\"Going up teeny-tiny stairs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the woman outdoors, which corresponds to the text describing her taking a walk.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/055_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the teeny-tiny woman find the teeny-tiny bone?\",\"options\":[\"In her teeny-tiny pocket\",\"On a teeny-tiny grave\",\"In a teeny-tiny cupboard\",\"Under the teeny-tiny clothes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says she saw a teeny-tiny bone on a teeny-tiny grave.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the woman\'s original plan for the teeny-tiny bone?\",\"options\":[\"To bury it again\",\"To hide it in the cupboard\",\"To make some teeny-tiny soup\",\"To give it to a teeny-tiny dog\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"She said to herself that the bone would make her some teeny-tiny soup for her supper.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the woman put the bone immediately after finding it?\",\"options\":[\"In her teeny-tiny cupboard\",\"Under her teeny-tiny bed\",\"In her teeny-tiny pocket\",\"On her teeny-tiny stairs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states she put the teeny-tiny bone into her teeny-tiny pocket.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the teeny-tiny woman go to her teeny-tiny bed when she got home?\",\"options\":[\"She was a teeny-tiny bit tired\",\"She was a teeny-tiny bit frightened\",\"She wanted to hide the bone\",\"It was time for her teeny-tiny supper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says when she got home, she was a teeny-tiny bit tired, so she went to her bed.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the woman doing to show she is \'a teeny-tiny bit frightened\'?\",\"options\":[\"She is running out of the house\",\"She is shouting at the cupboard\",\"She is hiding her head under the clothes\",\"She is putting the bone in the cupboard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the woman in bed pulling the covers over her face, which the text describes as hiding under the clothes because she was frightened.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/056_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What woke the woman up after she had been to sleep for a teeny-tiny time?\",\"options\":[\"A teeny-tiny dog barking\",\"A teeny-tiny voice from the cupboard\",\"A teeny-tiny knock on the door\",\"The teeny-tiny bone falling\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"She was awakened by a teeny-tiny voice from the teeny-tiny cupboard.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did the voice change each time it asked for the bone?\",\"options\":[\"It became a teeny-tiny bit softer\",\"It became a teeny-tiny bit faster\",\"It became a teeny-tiny bit louder\",\"It became a teeny-tiny bit kinder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the voice cried out \'a teeny-tiny louder\' each subsequent time.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the story, what does the word \'supper\' most likely mean?\",\"options\":[\"A morning walk\",\"A piece of clothing\",\"An evening meal\",\"A place to store bones\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The woman mentions making soup for her supper, which implies a meal.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What did the woman do the second time she heard the voice?\",\"options\":[\"She hid her head a teeny-tiny further under the clothes\",\"She jumped out of her teeny-tiny bed\",\"She ran to the teeny-tiny cupboard\",\"She ate her teeny-tiny soup\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says she hid her head a teeny-tiny further under the teeny-tiny clothes the second time.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which word best describes the woman\'s final action in the story?\",\"options\":[\"Cowardly\",\"Bold\",\"Quiet\",\"Sleepy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Though frightened, she finally put her head out and spoke in her loudest voice, showing a moment of boldness.\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What were the exact words the voice said from the cupboard?\",\"options\":[\"\'Where is my bone?\'\",\"\'I want my bone!\'\",\"\'Give me my bone!\'\",\"\'Take the bone!\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text quotes the voice saying \'Give me my bone!\'\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the woman\'s final response to the voice?\",\"options\":[\"She whispered \'No\'\",\"She shouted \'TAKE IT!\'\",\"She said \'It is mine\'\",\"She stayed silent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The story ends with the woman saying in her loudest teeny-tiny voice, \'TAKE IT!\'\",\"topic\":\"Teeny-tiny\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a \'churchyard\'?\",\"options\":[\"A place where people go to pray\",\"An enclosed area around a church where people are buried\",\"A garden belonging to the teeny-tiny woman\",\"A place where the last meal of the day is eaten\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a churchyard as an enclosed area around a church, where people are buried.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of \'supper\' as given in the topic text?\",\"options\":[\"The first meal of the day\",\"A light snack in the afternoon\",\"The last meal of the day\",\"A meal eaten at a churchyard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines supper as the \'last meal of the day\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'She had a dog\', what type of verb is \'had\'?\",\"options\":[\"Auxiliary verb\",\"Helping verb\",\"Main verb\",\"Action verb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in sentence 4 (\'She had a dog\'), \'had\' is used as a main verb.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is a \'grave\' according to the vocabulary list?\",\"options\":[\"A place where a person is buried\",\"A teeny-tiny gate\",\"A type of helping verb\",\"A large church building\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines a grave as a place where a person is buried.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the teeny-tiny voice’s speech given in a larger type every time?\",\"options\":[\"To show the voice is getting louder\",\"Because the woman is teeny-tiny\",\"To indicate it is the main verb\",\"To show the person is buried in a grave\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The larger type implies an increase in volume or intensity of the voice\'s speech.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When are \'be\', \'have\', and \'do\' called auxiliary verbs?\",\"options\":[\"When they are used alone in a sentence\",\"When they are used at the beginning of a sentence\",\"When they are used with other main verbs to show certain things\",\"When they are used to describe a teeny-tiny woman\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states they are called helping or auxiliary verbs when used with other main verbs.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'She does all the work\', how is the verb \'does\' categorized?\",\"options\":[\"As a helping verb\",\"As an auxiliary verb\",\"As a main verb\",\"As a teeny-tiny verb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'does\' in sentence 5 as being used as a main verb.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following verbs can be used as both main verbs and helping verbs?\",\"options\":[\"come, open, work\",\"be, have, do\",\"tired, dog, gate\",\"buried, meal, area\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'be\', \'have\', and \'do\' can be used as both main and helping verbs.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'She was a teeny-tiny bit tired\', what is the function of \'was\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is an auxiliary verb\",\"It is a main verb\",\"It is a helping verb\",\"It is a teeny-tiny adjective\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sentence 3 in the text uses \'was\' as a main verb.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is another term used in the text for \'helping verbs\'?\",\"options\":[\"Main verbs\",\"Auxiliary verbs\",\"Teeny-tiny verbs\",\"Action verbs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'auxiliary\' as a synonym for \'helping\' verbs.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what determines if \'be\' is a main verb or an auxiliary verb?\",\"options\":[\"Whether it is used with another main verb\",\"Whether it is in a teeny-tiny sentence\",\"Whether it refers to a churchyard\",\"Whether it is written in larger type\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that these verbs are auxiliary when used with other main verbs.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If \'do\' is used with another main verb, what is its role according to the Language Study section?\",\"options\":[\"It remains a main verb\",\"It becomes a teeny-tiny verb\",\"It is called a helping or auxiliary verb\",\"It becomes a noun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when \'do\' is used with other main verbs, it is called a helping or auxiliary verb.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are the two primary uses of the auxiliary verb \'Do\'?\",\"options\":[\"To form questions and negative sentences\",\"To describe nouns and pronouns\",\"To join two different sentences\",\"To end a sentence with a full stop\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states \'Do\' is used to form questions and to form negative sentences.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a \'Yes\\/no\' question provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"What did she say?\",\"What does he want?\",\"Did you see the bird?\",\"They did not do it.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Did you see the bird?\' under the category of Yes\\/no questions.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the question \'What does he want?\', which word is the auxiliary verb?\",\"options\":[\"What\",\"does\",\"he\",\"want\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the context of the topic \'Auxiliary Verb: Do\', \'does\' is the form of \'do\' used to help the main verb \'want\'.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which category does the question \'What did she say?\' belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Yes\\/no question\",\"Negative sentence\",\"Wh- question\",\"Main verb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly categorizes \'What did she say?\' as a Wh- question.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a negative sentence using a form of \'do\' as an auxiliary verb?\",\"options\":[\"They are not serious about their studies.\",\"She does not like flowers.\",\"Do you fly?\",\"Do you know the answer?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'She does not like flowers\' uses \'does\' to form a negative sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'They did not do it\', what is the function of the word \'did\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is the main verb.\",\"It is an auxiliary verb used to form a negative.\",\"It is an adjective describing \'it\'.\",\"It is a question word.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In this sentence, \'did\' acts as the auxiliary verb to form the negative, while \'do\' is the main verb.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the examples \'Do you swim?\' and \'Do you fly?\', what can be concluded about the main verb in a question?\",\"options\":[\"The main verb can be removed.\",\"The main verb must be an auxiliary verb.\",\"The main verb is necessary and follows the auxiliary verb.\",\"The main verb is always \'do\'.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if we can drop the main verb, implying it is essential to the structure of the question.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which sentence shows the contracted form of \'do not\' as used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"We don’t like to study.\",\"We did not eat it.\",\"She does not like flowers.\",\"They did not do it.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"\'Don\'t\' is the contracted form of \'do not\' used in the example \'We don’t like to study.\'\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a negative command or instruction mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Do you ever think?\",\"Don’t be so negative.\",\"What does he want?\",\"Did you see the bird?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Don’t be so negative\' is listed as a negative sentence in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between \'do\' and the main verb in the sentence \'We did not eat it\'?\",\"options\":[\"\'did\' is the main verb and \'eat\' is the auxiliary.\",\"\'did\' is the auxiliary verb used to help the main verb \'eat\'.\",\"Both \'did\' and \'eat\' are auxiliary verbs.\",\"Neither \'did\' nor \'eat\' are verbs.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that \'Do\' (in its past form \'did\') is used with the main verb (\'eat\') to form negative sentences.\",\"topic\":\"Auxiliary Verb : Do\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21901,41,7,'English','en','2.4 Putting together a Class Magazine','[\"- A class magazine is a small book made by students for their class.\",\"- It contains different sections like stories, poems, pictures, puzzles, jokes, news, recipes, advice, interviews, craft ideas and more.\",\"- Each section has a title and a few pages of content.\",\"- Students collect real magazine samples to see what kinds of material are used.\",\"- They choose three favourite types of material and collect one example of each for a class project.\",\"- A class magazine can be printed on plain paper or coloured paper and may include drawings, photographs and cut‑outs.\",\"- Instructions for making paper items (birds, boats, masks) are written step by step: fold, cut, turn, join, crease, etc.\",\"- Sentences in English can be of four types: statement, question, command, exclamation.\",\"- Negative sentences use words like not, no, never, not to change the meaning of a command or statement.\",\"- Positive sentences can be made from negative ones by removing the negative word or using a synonym.\",\"- Students practice turning commands into negative commands and negative statements into positive statements.\",\"- Oral work: think of negative words or commands quickly.\",\"- A class magazine should be interesting, useful and fun for classmates.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, who are the characters preparing a class magazine?\",\"options\":[\"The teacher and the students\",\"Tara and her friends\",\"The school principal\",\"A group of professional writers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions reading to find out how Tara and her friends prepare the magazine.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the text suggest people usually buy magazines from?\",\"options\":[\"A library\",\"A school\",\"A shop\",\"An office\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if you would like to make your own magazine instead of buying one from a shop.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides children\'s magazines, where else does the text suggest looking for children\'s sections?\",\"options\":[\"Textbooks and workbooks\",\"Novels and storybooks\",\"Periodicals and newspapers\",\"Dictionaries and encyclopedias\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to collect specimens from children’s sections in other periodicals, newspapers, etc.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is listed as a type of puzzle to include in a magazine?\",\"options\":[\"Number puzzles\",\"Logic puzzles\",\"Word puzzles\",\"Jigsaw puzzles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Word puzzles is explicitly listed in the bulleted points of materials.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of collecting specimens of children\'s magazines according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"To sell them to a shop\",\"To see which materials are included in them\",\"To practice reading aloud\",\"To learn how to draw\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to collect specimens to \'See which of the following materials are included in the magazines.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to include a set of instructions for making a paper bird, which category does it fall under?\",\"options\":[\"Informative articles\",\"Friendly advice\",\"Craft ideas\",\"News items\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Instructions for making things are categorized under \'craft ideas\' in the list.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which category would a section titled \'How to improve your memory for exams\' belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Tips on how to study\",\"Collection of interesting facts\",\"Interviews\",\"Riddles\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The list includes \'tips on how to study\', which covers exam preparation advice.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text ask you to do as a \'class project\'?\",\"options\":[\"Write a long story\",\"Collect one specimen of each material listed\",\"Interview the principal\",\"Draw pictures for every page\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Under \'Warming up!\', the second bullet point is to \'Collect one specimen of each of the above as a class project.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the list of materials for a magazine?\",\"options\":[\"Board games\",\"Recipes\",\"Movie reviews\",\"Picture stories\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Movie reviews are not mentioned in the provided list of materials.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you find a section in a magazine where a famous person answers questions, what is it called?\",\"options\":[\"A play\",\"An interview\",\"A news item\",\"A riddle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A question-and-answer format with a person is categorized as an \'interview\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions that magazines are read by which groups of people?\",\"options\":[\"Only children\",\"Only grown-up people\",\"Both grown-up people and children\",\"Only teachers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states many magazines are for grown-up people, but some are also for children.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first activity suggested under the \'Warming up!\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Making a list of jokes\",\"Collecting specimens of three favorite materials\",\"Drawing a cover page\",\"Writing a poem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first bullet point under \'Warming up!\' asks which materials you like most and to collect specimens of three favorites.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are Tara and her friends busy producing?\",\"options\":[\"A school textbook\",\"A class magazine\",\"A drawing competition\",\"A storybook\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Tara and her friends are busy producing their \'Class Magazine\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In what form do the students want to display the magazine on the classroom walls?\",\"options\":[\"A single large book\",\"A digital slideshow\",\"A collage\",\"A series of posters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions they want to display it on the classroom walls in the form of a collage.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why did the children cut out pieces of the articles?\",\"options\":[\"To throw them away\",\"To make them easier to read\",\"To display them as a collage\",\"To hide the mistakes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains they cut out pieces because they want to display the magazine in the form of a collage.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What problem did Tara face while putting the magazine together?\",\"options\":[\"No one sent any articles\",\"The pieces of the articles got mixed up\",\"They ran out of coloured paper\",\"The teacher did not like the designs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'sadly, the pieces have all got mixed up\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these was NOT used by the children to decorate the magazine?\",\"options\":[\"Photos\",\"Pictures\",\"Designs\",\"Paintings\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions colours, photos, pictures, and designs, but does not specifically list paintings.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the intended final step for the cut-out pieces before they are displayed?\",\"options\":[\"Writing them in a beautiful hand\",\"Arranging them on coloured paper with illustrations\",\"Sending them back to the children\",\"Cutting them into smaller shapes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says they will arrange the pieces on coloured paper with appropriate illustrations.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these is an ingredient listed for the \'Green Chutney\' recipe?\",\"options\":[\"Guava\",\"Chat masala\",\"Coconut\",\"Black craft paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The snippet for Green Chutney in the image lists \'A medium piece of coconut\' as an ingredient.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/060_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, who wrote the snippet about losing a friendship?\",\"options\":[\"Didi\",\"Needy Student\",\"Needy Friend\",\"Jay\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The image shows the note about losing friendships is signed by \'- Needy Friend\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/060_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How did \'many children\' participate in the magazine project according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"By cutting the paper\",\"By sending their contributions\",\"By cleaning the classroom\",\"By buying the magazine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'Many children have sent their contributions to the magazine\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the titles in the given figure, which one is most likely a craft project?\",\"options\":[\"Green Chutney\",\"Fruit Chat\",\"Paper Penguin\",\"Needy Student\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The title \'Paper Penguin\' corresponds to the instructions about cutting shapes from craft paper.\",\"topic\":\"The Class Magazine Project\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/060_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What color of craft paper is required to make the head and body of the Paper Penguin?\",\"options\":[\"White\",\"Blue\",\"Black\",\"Green\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies cutting out a head and body shape from black craft paper.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Green Chutney recipe, how many green chillies are needed?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The ingredients list for Green Chutney mentions \'2 green chillies\'.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the method for making Fruit Chat?\",\"options\":[\"Put ingredients in a bowl\",\"Wash the guava and cut it\",\"Sprinkle chat masala\",\"Mix the ingredients well\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Step 1 of the Fruit Chat method is \'Wash the guava and cut it into big pieces.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the person seeking advice because they have too much homework?\",\"options\":[\"Needy Friend\",\"Jay\",\"Needy Student\",\"Didi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The letter about getting too much homework is signed by \'Needy Student\'.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does Didi suggest is the best way to ensure you have time to play?\",\"options\":[\"Stop doing homework\",\"Use your time wisely\",\"Do homework on the floor\",\"Ask a friend for help\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Didi states, \'To give yourself the most time to play, it’s important to use your time wisely.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What should be shown in the background of the Paper Penguin craft?\",\"options\":[\"Trees and flowers\",\"Water and fish\",\"Sky and ice\",\"Mountains and sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Draw a proper background to show the sky and the ice.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific problem mentioned by \'Needy Friend\'?\",\"options\":[\"Too much homework\",\"Loss of friendships\",\"Difficulty with math\",\"Lack of ingredients\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Needy Friend mentions losing a best friend and another friendship a few months ago.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the Fruit Chat recipe, what is used to season the fruit after putting it in the bowl?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar and salt\",\"Green chillies\",\"Coriander leaves\",\"Chat masala\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Step 3 of the Fruit Chat method says to \'Sprinkle chat masala and mix the ingredients well.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are following the Paper Penguin instructions, where should you paste the different parts?\",\"options\":[\"On black craft paper\",\"On card paper\",\"On a glass bowl\",\"On the floor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Paste the different parts together on card paper.\'\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which ingredient from the Green Chutney list provides a sweet taste?\",\"options\":[\"Coconut\",\"Coriander leaves\",\"1\\/2 spoonful of sugar\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sugar is the ingredient listed that provides sweetness.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which snippet is intended to be a joke?\",\"options\":[\"The instructions for the penguin\",\"The ingredients for chutney\",\"The conversation between Teacher and Jay\",\"The advice from Didi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The dialogue between the Teacher and Jay about doing math on the floor is a humorous anecdote or joke.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which components are required to complete the Paper Penguin craft according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Head, body, two wings, card paper, and decorations\",\"Guava, chat masala, and a glass bowl\",\"Coconut, chillies, and sugar\",\"Math homework and a floor\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Paper Penguin section lists head, body, wings, card paper, and decorations like markers and stickers.\",\"topic\":\"Mixed-up Pieces\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In what color boxes are the names of the magazine sections given?\",\"options\":[\"Red boxes\",\"Green boxes\",\"Blue boxes\",\"Yellow boxes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the names of the sections are given in blue boxes.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who would especially love to read the Class Magazine you create?\",\"options\":[\"The Principal\",\"Tara\",\"The Librarian\",\"The Class Monitor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that all friends and especially Tara would love to read the magazine.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where should you find the names of the items and the remaining pieces?\",\"options\":[\"In the library\",\"On the board\",\"In the blue boxes\",\"From the teacher\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to find the names of the items and remaining pieces from those shown on the board.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do with the items and pieces after finding them?\",\"options\":[\"Color them blue\",\"Give them to Tara\",\"Put them in a proper sequence\",\"Hide them from friends\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically asks to put the items and pieces in a proper sequence.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is necessary to ensure that others want to read your magazine?\",\"options\":[\"Add interesting stuff\",\"Use only blue ink\",\"Keep it very short\",\"Make it very difficult\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'If you want the others to read it, be sure to add interesting stuff.\'\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When planning your own Class Magazine, what can you do regarding the sections?\",\"options\":[\"Only use the blue boxes\",\"Add other interesting sections\",\"Remove all existing sections\",\"Use only one section\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests that you may add other interesting sections to your own class magazine.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what was the initial problem Tara and her friends faced?\",\"options\":[\"They lost their magazine\",\"They had no paper\",\"There was confusion to clear\",\"They had no blue boxes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks, \'Have you helped Tara and friends to clear the confusion?\', implying there was confusion initially.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal of the activity described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To draw blue boxes\",\"To clear confusion and plan a Class Magazine\",\"To find Tara\'s lost items\",\"To clean the classroom board\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text focuses on clearing confusion and planning a magazine for your own class.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which action is most important for organizing the magazine content logically?\",\"options\":[\"Finding the board\",\"Putting items in a proper sequence\",\"Naming the friends\",\"Reading the blue boxes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Putting items in a proper sequence is the key step for organization mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions \'remaining pieces\' are shown on the:\",\"options\":[\"Blue boxes\",\"Magazine cover\",\"Board\",\"Notebook\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states to find the names of the items and the remaining pieces from those shown on the board.\",\"topic\":\"Planning your Class Magazine\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many types of sentences were introduced to the students last year?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'You have seen last year that there are four types of sentences\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed in the text as a negative word?\",\"options\":[\"not\",\"never\",\"no\",\"always\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'not, no, never\' as negative words.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What term does the textbook use as a synonym for \'positive\' statements?\",\"options\":[\"Exclamatory\",\"Affirmative\",\"Negative\",\"Command\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the phrase \'positive (affirmative) ones\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what makes a sentence have a negative meaning?\",\"options\":[\"It ends with a question mark\",\"It contains words like not, no, or never\",\"It is a very long sentence\",\"It starts with a verb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that sentences containing negative words like not, no, never have a negative meaning.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Following the example in the text, what is the negative form of the command \'Wash the guava\'?\",\"options\":[\"No wash the guava.\",\"Wash not the guava.\",\"Don\'t wash the guava.\",\"Never wash guava.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the example \'Don\'t put all the ingredients together\', the negative of \'Wash the guava\' is \'Don\'t wash the guava\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific time limit set for the \'Oral Work\' activity?\",\"options\":[\"One minute\",\"Two minutes\",\"Five minutes\",\"Ten minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure title is \'Two minutes of Oral Work\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/063_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Using the pattern for \'Grind them in a mixer\', how would you write this as a negative command?\",\"options\":[\"Not grind them in a mixer.\",\"Grind not them in a mixer.\",\"Don\'t grind them in a mixer.\",\"Grind them no in a mixer.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text teaches to add \'Don\'t\' to the beginning of a command to make it negative.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which verb is provided to help students think of commands?\",\"options\":[\"cook\",\"study\",\"write\",\"read\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure lists the verbs: cross, help, wash, study, take.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/063_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How should the negative statement \'The other did not know what to do\' be changed to positive using the word \'knew\'?\",\"options\":[\"The other did knew what to do.\",\"The other knew what to do.\",\"The other not knew what to do.\",\"The other know what to do.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To make it positive and grammatically correct as per the instruction \'Use knew\', the sentence becomes \'The other knew what to do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When changing \'It made no difference to us\' to a positive statement using \'a difference\', which is correct?\",\"options\":[\"It made a difference to us.\",\"It did a difference to us.\",\"It not made a difference to us.\",\"It makes a difference to us.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Removing the negative word \'no\' and replacing it with \'a\' results in \'It made a difference to us\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the positive form of the statement \'I do not like that music at my dinner\'?\",\"options\":[\"I no like that music at my dinner.\",\"I not like that music at my dinner.\",\"I like that music at my dinner.\",\"I liked that music at my dinner.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Removing the negative auxiliary \'do not\' makes the statement positive: \'I like that music at my dinner\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the positive version of \'I cannot understand\'?\",\"options\":[\"I can understand.\",\"I could understand.\",\"I understand.\",\"I am understanding.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides this exact example: \'Positive : I can understand.\'\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What does the acronym \'TOW\' stand for in this textbook exercise?\",\"options\":[\"Two minutes of Oral Work\",\"Task of Writing\",\"Time of Words\",\"Ten Oral Words\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines TOW as \'Two minutes of Oral Work\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the TOW instructions, how much time is given to think of commands for a set of verbs?\",\"options\":[\"Five minutes\",\"Two minutes\",\"Ten minutes\",\"One minute\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The title \'Two minutes of Oral Work\' indicates the time limit for the activity.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following verbs is included in the list for creating commands?\",\"options\":[\"write\",\"speak\",\"study\",\"read\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'study\' as one of the verbs to be used for commands.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the first TOW activity, which of these is a valid command starting with the verb \'cross\'?\",\"options\":[\"Cross the road at the zebra crossing.\",\"He is crossing the bridge.\",\"A big red cross.\",\"They crossed the finish line.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A command is an imperative sentence; \'Cross the road\' fits the instruction to start with the verb.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The second TOW activity asks students to think of as many ________ as possible.\",\"options\":[\"positive words\",\"negative words\",\"action words\",\"naming words\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The second TOW activity specifically asks for \'negative words\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which example of a negative word is provided in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"never\",\"nothing\",\"no one\",\"none\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'no one\' as the specific example for negative words.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is using the verb \'wash\' for the TOW activity, which sentence follows the instruction?\",\"options\":[\"I like to wash my car.\",\"Wash your hands before eating.\",\"She is washing the fruit.\",\"The wash is clean.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity requires a command beginning with the verb \'wash\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which verb from the provided list would be most appropriate for a command regarding a library or a book?\",\"options\":[\"cross\",\"wash\",\"study\",\"help\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Among the choices, \'study\' is the most relevant verb for academic or book-related commands.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following words would be a correct addition to the second TOW activity?\",\"options\":[\"always\",\"nobody\",\"someone\",\"everywhere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Nobody\' is a negative word, fitting the category of the example \'no one\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the first TOW activity, which verb is NOT listed among the five verbs provided?\",\"options\":[\"take\",\"help\",\"wash\",\"clean\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The verbs listed are cross, help, wash, study, and take; \'clean\' is not in the list.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21902,41,7,'English','en','2.5 Windy Lines','[\"Wind is a natural phenomenon caused by the movement of air from high pressure to low pressure areas.\",\"Wind speed is measured in meters per second (m\\/s) or kilometers per hour (km\\/h).\",\"The direction of wind is given by the direction from which it blows, e.g., north wind means wind comes from the north.\",\"Wind can be described as gentle, breezy, gusty, or stormy depending on its speed and effect.\",\"A gust is a sudden, brief increase in wind speed.\",\"Wind can carry dust, pollen, and even sound waves, making it audible in some places.\",\"Wind plays a key role in weather patterns and climate by redistributing heat and moisture around the Earth.\",\"Wind is used for practical purposes such as windmills for generating electricity and sailing ships for propulsion.\",\"The wind can be felt as a change in temperature, especially when it moves from a warm area to a cool one.\",\"Wind can cause erosion by blowing away loose soil and sand, shaping landscapes over time.\",\"Wind is a part of many literary and poetic images, often symbolizing freedom, change, or the unseen forces of nature.\",\"The speed of wind is often indicated by the Beaufort scale, ranging from 0 (calm) to 12 (hurricane).\",\"Wind can be measured using anemometers, which record the speed of air flow.\",\"Wind direction is measured using a weather vane or a wind compass.\",\"Wind can affect human activities such as agriculture, aviation, and outdoor sports.\",\"Wind can create noise, especially when it passes through structures or over obstacles.\",\"Wind is a key factor in the formation of clouds and precipitation.\",\"Wind can be harnessed for renewable energy through wind turbines.\",\"Wind can also be a natural hazard, causing damage during storms and hurricanes.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the primary subject of the quotations mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Rain\",\"Wind\",\"The Sun\",\"The Moon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that this is a collection of quotations on the subject of wind.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who are the authors of the quotations mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Students\",\"Famous authors\",\"Teachers\",\"Scientists\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the quotations are from famous authors.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text suggest you can make collections of?\",\"options\":[\"Scientific facts\",\"Famous or memorable lines\",\"Mathematical formulas\",\"Daily news reports\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests making collections of famous or memorable lines on different topics.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these topics is specifically mentioned as a suggestion for a new collection?\",\"options\":[\"Mountains\",\"Rainbow\",\"Forests\",\"Rivers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rainbow is listed in the text as one of the topics for making collections.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the internet, who is mentioned as a source of help for this activity?\",\"options\":[\"Your friends\",\"Your parents\",\"Your teacher\",\"A librarian\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says you can make collections with the help of the internet and your teacher.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to find lines about \'Home\', what resource should they use according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only textbooks\",\"The internet and their teacher\",\"Television news\",\"Sports magazines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests using the internet and the teacher to find lines on topics like \'home\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How can a student creatively present their collection of lines?\",\"options\":[\"In a plain notebook\",\"Against beautiful backgrounds and greeting cards\",\"In a newspaper advertisement\",\"On a simple blackboard\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions putting the lines against beautiful backgrounds and greeting cards.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common purpose of the topics like \'morning\', \'night\', and \'school\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are all weather patterns\",\"They are suggested topics for quote collections\",\"They are names of famous authors\",\"They are titles of greeting cards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists these as different topics on which students can collect memorable lines.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What should be the visual setting for the collected famous lines?\",\"options\":[\"Plain white paper\",\"Beautiful backgrounds\",\"Dark, solid colors\",\"Graphs and charts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests putting the lines against beautiful backgrounds.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the main goal of the activity described?\",\"options\":[\"To learn how to browse the internet\",\"To memorize facts about the wind\",\"To create artistic collections of themed quotations\",\"To write original poems about the moon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity focuses on collecting and presenting memorable lines on various themes artistically.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Activity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the lines by A.A. Milne, what is it that \'Nobody knows\'?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the wind\",\"Where the wind comes from and goes\",\"The name of the wind\",\"How fast the wind blows\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Nobody knows, Where the wind comes from, Where the wind goes.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the poem by R. L. Stevenson, which two adjectives are used to describe the wind?\",\"options\":[\"Warm and gentle\",\"Soft and young\",\"Strong and cold\",\"Loud and old\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem addresses the wind as \'O you that are so strong and cold\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What question does the speaker ask about the wind\'s age in \'The Wind\'?\",\"options\":[\"Are you a thousand years old?\",\"Are you young or old?\",\"When were you born?\",\"How long have you been blowing?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poet asks, \'O blower, are you young or old?\'\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the wind do \'all day long\' according to R. L. Stevenson?\",\"options\":[\"It hides in the trees\",\"It sleeps in the field\",\"It keeps a-blowing\",\"It dances with the child\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'O wind, a-blowing all day long\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The speaker in R. L. Stevenson\'s poem compares the wind to a \'stronger child\'. What does this imply about the wind?\",\"options\":[\"The wind is very small\",\"The wind has a playful but powerful nature\",\"The wind goes to school\",\"The wind is weaker than the speaker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Comparing the wind to a \'stronger child\' suggests it has energy and power similar to a person but on a larger scale.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these sounds is the wind described as making?\",\"options\":[\"A quiet whisper\",\"A loud song\",\"A gentle hum\",\"A scary growl\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'O wind, that sings so loud a song!\'\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to find the source of the wind based on A.A. Milne\'s poem, what would the result be?\",\"options\":[\"They would find it on a hill\",\"They would find it in the field\",\"No one can tell them where it is\",\"They would find it in the trees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem starts with \'No one can tell me, Nobody knows... Where the wind comes from\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The speaker asks if the wind is a \'beast of field and tree\'. What is the speaker trying to understand?\",\"options\":[\"If the wind is an animal\",\"The physical form or identity of the wind\",\"If the wind eats grass\",\"If the wind lives in a house\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The speaker is using metaphors (beast vs child) to try and identify what the wind actually is.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common theme between the excerpts by A.A. Milne and R. L. Stevenson?\",\"options\":[\"The wind is dangerous\",\"The wind is a mystery that is hard to define\",\"The wind only blows on hills\",\"The wind is always warm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Both poets express uncertainty (\'Nobody knows\', \'Are you a beast... or just a child?\') about the wind\'s nature and origin.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the poet R. L. Stevenson call the wind a \'blower\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it makes a loud song\",\"Because its primary action is the movement of air\",\"Because it is cold\",\"Because it is a beast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The term \'blower\' refers to the wind\'s characteristic action of blowing air.\",\"topic\":\"The Wind\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the poem that asks, \'Who has seen the wind?\'\",\"options\":[\"Sarojini Naidu\",\"Christina Rossetti\",\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"The Wakening Skies\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly attributes the poem \'Who has seen the wind?\' to Christina Rossetti.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Christina Rossetti, what happens when the wind is \'passing through\'?\",\"options\":[\"The trees bow their heads\",\"The leaves hang trembling\",\"The skies pray to the light\",\"The wind cries like a child\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'But when the leaves hang trembling, The wind is passing through.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the lines from \'The Coromandel Fisher\', what are the \'wakening skies\' doing?\",\"options\":[\"Crying all night\",\"Sleeping in the arms of dawn\",\"Praying to the morning light\",\"Bowing down their heads\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'the wakening skies pray to the morning light\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What action of the trees indicates that the wind is \'passing by\'?\",\"options\":[\"They grow taller\",\"Their leaves turn green\",\"They bow down their heads\",\"They wake up the brothers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'But when the trees bow down their heads, The wind is passing by.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you observe a plant\'s leaves shaking slightly without seeing anything move them, which line best describes this?\",\"options\":[\"The wind lies asleep\",\"The leaves hang trembling\",\"The skies pray to the light\",\"Rise, brothers, rise\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Trembling leaves are the physical sign of the invisible wind passing through.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To what does Sarojini Naidu compare the wind lying asleep in the arms of the dawn?\",\"options\":[\"A praying sky\",\"A trembling leaf\",\"A child that has cried all night\",\"A brother rising for work\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the simile \'like a child that has cried all night\' to describe the wind.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text from \'The Coromandel Fisher\', what time of day is it?\",\"options\":[\"Sunset\",\"Midnight\",\"Afternoon\",\"Morning\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The mention of \'wakening skies\', \'morning light\', and \'dawn\' indicates it is morning.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the poet say \'Neither I nor you\' have seen the wind?\",\"options\":[\"Because the wind is invisible to the human eye\",\"Because the wind only blows when people are asleep\",\"Because the wind moves too fast to be seen\",\"Because the trees hide the wind from view\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem emphasizes that while we see the effects of the wind, the wind itself is not seen by anyone.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of \'The Coromandel Fisher\', who is the speaker most likely calling to \'Rise\'?\",\"options\":[\"The wind\",\"The children\",\"The fishermen\",\"The trees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The title \'The Coromandel Fisher\' and the address to \'brothers\' implies the speaker is calling fellow fishermen.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase suggests that the wind was likely very loud or stormy before the dawn arrived?\",\"options\":[\"The wind is passing through\",\"Like a child that has cried all night\",\"The trees bow down their heads\",\"Pray to the morning light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The comparison to a child who \'cried all night\' suggests the wind was active or turbulent during the night.\",\"topic\":\"The Coromandel Fisher\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the line \'You throw the sand against the wind\'?\",\"options\":[\"P. B. Shelley\",\"William Blake\",\"William Wordsworth\",\"Robert Frost\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly attributes the quote about throwing sand to William Blake.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to William Blake, what happens when you throw sand against the wind?\",\"options\":[\"The sand disappears into the sky\",\"The sand falls straight down\",\"The wind blows it back again\",\"The wind stops blowing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if you throw sand against the wind, the wind blows it back again.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which poet wrote the line \'... O Wind, If winter comes, can spring be far behind?\'\",\"options\":[\"William Blake\",\"P. B. Shelley\",\"John Keats\",\"Lord Byron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies P. B. Shelley as the author of the quote regarding winter and spring.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In P. B. Shelley\'s quote, which season is expected to follow winter?\",\"options\":[\"Autumn\",\"Summer\",\"Monsoon\",\"Spring\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The quote asks if \'spring\' can be far behind when winter comes, implying it follows winter.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the wind do to the sand in the quote by William Blake?\",\"options\":[\"It carries it to a new land\",\"It turns it into dust\",\"It returns it to the person who threw it\",\"It makes the sand heavier\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The phrase \'blows it back again\' indicates the wind returns the sand to its source.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The question \'can spring be far behind?\' suggests that spring is:\",\"options\":[\"Never going to arrive\",\"Coming very soon after winter\",\"A very long way off\",\"Only a dream\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rhetorical question implies that spring is inevitably close once winter arrives.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person tries to fight against a powerful natural force, which quote best describes the likely outcome?\",\"options\":[\"The quote by P. B. Shelley\",\"The quote by William Blake\",\"Both quotes equally\",\"Neither of the quotes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Blake\'s quote about sand being blown back illustrates the futility or consequence of acting against a force like the wind.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If \'winter\' in Shelley\'s quote represents a difficult time in life, what would \'spring\' most likely represent?\",\"options\":[\"A time of even more trouble\",\"A time of hope and new beginnings\",\"The end of the world\",\"A permanent state of coldness\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the cycle of seasons, spring represents renewal, which applies to hope following a difficult \'winter\' period.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the line \'... O Wind, If winter comes...\', the poet is addressing the wind directly. What does this indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The poet is afraid of the wind\",\"The poet is talking to the wind as if it can hear\",\"The poet is trying to stop the wind\",\"The poet is describing a scientific fact\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using \'O Wind\' is a poetic way of addressing the wind directly, personifying it.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference in the way the wind is presented in the two quotes?\",\"options\":[\"Blake shows the wind reacting to an action, while Shelley asks the wind a hopeful question\",\"Blake likes the wind, but Shelley hates the wind\",\"Blake\'s wind is hot, while Shelley\'s wind is cold\",\"There is no difference; both poets say the wind is dangerous\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Blake describes a physical reaction (blowing sand back), whereas Shelley uses the wind to reflect on the cycle of seasons and hope.\",\"topic\":\"If winter comes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21903,41,7,'English','en','2.6 Great scientists have a questioning mind','[\"Great scientists are curious and ask questions like ‘Why?’ and ‘Can I improve it?’\",\"C.V. Raman was born in Tiruchirapalli, India, in 1888\",\"He studied optical science and acoustics\",\"Raman noticed the sky and sea are blue and wondered why\",\"He hypothesised light scattering by water molecules\",\"He performed experiments on a ship in Calcutta to test this\",\"The experiment led to the discovery of the Raman Effect in 1928\",\"The Raman Effect explains how light changes frequency when it scatters off molecules\",\"Raman received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1930, the first Asian laureate\",\"28 February, the day of the discovery, is National Science Day in India\",\"Raman believed science should be done with independent thinking, not just expensive equipment\",\"He said a good scientist searches nature for answers\",\"The Raman Effect helps identify chemical compounds by their molecular fingerprints\",\"Raman’s work shows how everyday observations can lead to major scientific breakthroughs\"]','[{\"q\":\"During which period did Dr. A. P. J. Abdul Kalam serve as the President of India?\",\"options\":[\"1997 to 2002\",\"2002 to 2007\",\"2005 to 2010\",\"2000 to 2005\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Dr. A. P. J. Abdul Kalam was the President of India from 2002 to 2007.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which prestigious award was given to Dr. Kalam for his contribution to scientific research and defense technology?\",\"options\":[\"Padma Bhushan\",\"Padma Vibhushan\",\"Bharat Ratna\",\"Nobel Prize\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he was awarded Bharat Ratna for his scientific research and modernization of defense technology.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the other great Indian scientist mentioned by Dr. Kalam in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Homi Bhabha\",\"Vikram Sarabhai\",\"C.V. Raman\",\"Jagadish Chandra Bose\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The passage states that Dr. Kalam talks about another great Indian scientist, C.V. Raman.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a primary requirement for a political leader?\",\"options\":[\"To travel the world\",\"To understand people\'s problems\",\"To be a scientist\",\"To win every election\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Leaders have to understand people’s problems.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text describe teachers as a specific category of favorite people?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are so knowledgeable\",\"Because they give homework\",\"Because they travel a lot\",\"Because they work in labs\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Teachers are so knowledgeable. I want to be a teacher.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What distinguishes a \'progressive farmer\' from a regular farmer according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They only grow fruits\",\"They use new methods\",\"They do not work hard\",\"They live in the city\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'I will use new methods. I will be a progressive farmer.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student enjoys doing experiments in the kitchen, which profession does the text suggest for them?\",\"options\":[\"Scientist\",\"Chef\",\"Artist\",\"Lawyer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Chefs do so many experiments in the kitchen.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the main purpose of a farmer\'s work?\",\"options\":[\"To study the soil\",\"To grow food crops for all\",\"To sell new machines\",\"To build houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Farmers grow food crops for all.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following categories is NOT mentioned in the list of professions provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Journalist\",\"Architect\",\"Pilot\",\"Social worker\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"While journalist, architect, and social worker are in the list, \'pilot\' is not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text link the profession of a Chef to \'Art\'?\",\"options\":[\"Chefs paint their food\",\"Cooking is described as an art\",\"Chefs work in art galleries\",\"Chefs must be musicians\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'Cooking is an art.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the intended outcome of the group discussion mentioned at the end of the Warming up! section?\",\"options\":[\"To list all scientists in India\",\"To discuss what you want to be and how to achieve it\",\"To memorize Dr. Kalam\'s books\",\"To learn how to cook\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs groups to \'Discuss what you want to be and what you have to do to achieve your objective.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which quality is common to both Leaders and Progressive Farmers as implied by the text\'s descriptions of their work?\",\"options\":[\"They both work in the kitchen\",\"They both require specific actions\\/methods to achieve goals\",\"They both work only for children\",\"They both must be scientists\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links leaders to understanding problems\\/working hard and farmers to using new methods, implying both require specific effort and strategy.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which city was C. V. Raman born?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Tiruchirapalli\",\"Berlin\",\"Chennai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Raman was born in Tiruchirapalli in southern India.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was C. V. Raman born?\",\"options\":[\"1850\",\"1905\",\"1888\",\"1947\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the year of his birth as 1888.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which famous scientist lived in the same era as C. V. Raman?\",\"options\":[\"Newton\",\"Einstein\",\"Galileo\",\"Edison\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Raman lived in the same era as Einstein.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what makes a great scientific mind \'restless\'?\",\"options\":[\"A lack of sleep\",\"Traveling long distances\",\"Constantly asking questions\",\"Studying in Germany\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says great scientific minds are restless with questions like \'Why does this happen?\'.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How far east of Germany was the scientist C. V. Raman located?\",\"options\":[\"500 kilometres\",\"1888 kilometres\",\"5000 kilometres\",\"1000 kilometres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he lived about 5000 kilometres east of Germany.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which two specific branches of science was Raman deeply interested in from the start?\",\"options\":[\"Biology and Chemistry\",\"Optical science and acoustics\",\"Geology and Astronomy\",\"Medicine and Botany\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions his interest in optical science and acoustics.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Raman discover about the mridangam and the tabla?\",\"options\":[\"They were difficult to play\",\"They were made of special wood\",\"They produced more melodious sounds than other percussion instruments\",\"They were invented in southern India\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he discovered these instruments produced more melodious sounds than any other percussion instrument.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student looks at a new gadget and asks, \'What more can this do?\', which trait from the text are they demonstrating?\",\"options\":[\"A bright student\'s memory\",\"A questioning scientific mind\",\"An interest in acoustics\",\"A fascination with colourful things\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'What more can this do?\' as a question asked by restless scientific minds.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following would Raman likely have studied to understand the beauty of a butterfly or a gem?\",\"options\":[\"Acoustics\",\"Sound-related physics\",\"Optical science\",\"Percussion instruments\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since he was fascinated by colourful things, optical science (the study of light\\/vision) is the relevant field mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Raman became a scholar in which specific field of physics?\",\"options\":[\"Nuclear physics\",\"Sound and sound-related physics\",\"Space physics\",\"Heat and Thermodynamics\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he became a scholar in sound and sound-related physics.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the most likely connection between Raman\'s interests and his discovery about musical instruments?\",\"options\":[\"His birth in Tiruchirapalli made him a musician\",\"His interest in acoustics led to his discovery about the tabla and mridangam\",\"His fascination with gems helped him understand sound\",\"His travel to Germany taught him about percussion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests his interest in acoustics is \'what perhaps led him to discover\' the melodious nature of the instruments.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase from the text best describes Raman\'s attitude towards the world around him?\",\"options\":[\"He was a bright student\",\"He lived 5000 kilometres away\",\"He kept seeking knowledge about everything around him\",\"He was a great scientist in India\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This phrase summarizes his active pursuit of understanding, which is the core theme of a questioning mind.\",\"topic\":\"Early Life of C. V. Raman\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where was C. V. Raman traveling to when he observed the blue waters of the ocean?\",\"options\":[\"London\",\"Calcutta\",\"New York\",\"Paris\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the ship sailed from the port of London towards Calcutta.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the subject of the lecture C. V. Raman delivered in London?\",\"options\":[\"The science of light\",\"The scattering of water molecules\",\"The acoustics of the violin\",\"The discovery of the Nobel Prize\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he had delivered a lecture on the acoustics of the violin in London.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the text, what is \'optics\' defined as?\",\"options\":[\"The study of sound\",\"The science of light\",\"The study of water molecules\",\"The science of sailing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines optics as \'the science of light\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did C. V. Raman win the Nobel Prize for Physics?\",\"options\":[\"1928\",\"1930\",\"1920\",\"1935\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the discovery won him the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1930.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific observation triggered a series of questions in Raman\'s mind?\",\"options\":[\"The ship was moving too fast\",\"The sky and the ocean were the same shade of blue\",\"The water molecules were visible to the naked eye\",\"The acoustics of the ship were similar to a violin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He noticed the sky was of the same shade as the ocean, leading him to wonder why both were blue.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was Raman\'s initial theory regarding the blue color of the sea?\",\"options\":[\"It was a reflection of the ship\'s hull\",\"It was caused by the acoustics of the water\",\"It was due to the scattering of light by water molecules\",\"It was an optical illusion caused by the sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"His mind suggested the reason could be the scattering of light by water molecules.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did C. V. Raman conduct experiments immediately after reaching Calcutta?\",\"options\":[\"To prepare for his next lecture in London\",\"To prove his theory about the scattering of light scientifically\",\"To win the Nobel Prize for Asia\",\"To study the acoustics of the port\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says he went to conduct experiments to prove his theory which was yet to be proved scientifically.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was unique about C. V. Raman winning the Nobel Prize for Physics?\",\"options\":[\"He was the youngest scientist to win it\",\"It was the first time the prize was awarded to an Asian\",\"He won it for a lecture he gave on a ship\",\"It was the only prize ever given for optics\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that it was the first time this prize was awarded to an Asian.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to follow in Raman\'s footsteps by studying the \'science of light\', which field should they choose?\",\"options\":[\"Acoustics\",\"Optics\",\"Marine Biology\",\"Aeronautics\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies optics as the science of light, which was Raman\'s field of research.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why is February 28 celebrated as National Science Day in India?\",\"options\":[\"It is the day C. V. Raman was born\",\"It is the day he won the Nobel Prize\",\"It is the day he discovered the Raman Effect\",\"It is the day he sailed from London\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the day he discovered the Raman Effect, 28 February, was declared as National Science Day.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the sequence of Raman\'s discovery process?\",\"options\":[\"Experimentation -> Nobel Prize -> Questioning\",\"Observation -> Questioning -> Experimentation -> Discovery\",\"Lecture -> Discovery -> Observation -> Experimentation\",\"Winning a Prize -> Questioning -> Sailing -> Discovery\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He observed the blue water, questioned why, conducted experiments in Calcutta, and then discovered the Raman Effect.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text imply about the scientific method used by C. V. Raman?\",\"options\":[\"A theory is considered proven as soon as a scientist thinks of it\",\"Lectures are more important than experiments for scientific discovery\",\"A scientific theory requires experimental proof before it is accepted\",\"Discoveries are only made while traveling on ships\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes his theory \'was yet to be proved scientifically\' until he conducted experiments to prove it.\",\"topic\":\"The Discovery of the Raman Effect\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was C.V. Raman elected as a Fellow of the Royal Society?\",\"options\":[\"1920\",\"1922\",\"1924\",\"1930\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he was elected Fellow of the Royal Society in 1924.\",\"topic\":\"Recognition and Philosophy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the approximate cost of the equipment Raman used to prove his theory?\",\"options\":[\"Two thousand rupees\",\"Two hundred rupees\",\"Twenty thousand rupees\",\"Free of cost\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the equipment cost a measly two hundred rupees.\",\"topic\":\"Recognition and Philosophy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Before 1924, in which specific field of science had Raman\'s contribution been acknowledged?\",\"options\":[\"Acoustics\",\"Optics\",\"Biology\",\"Chemistry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions his contribution to the science of optics had been acknowledged even before 1924.\",\"topic\":\"Recognition and Philosophy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The discovery of the \'Raman Effect\' helped in determining the internal structures of how many chemical compounds?\",\"options\":[\"200\",\"1000\",\"2000\",\"5000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the discovery helped in determining the internal structures of some 2000 chemical compounds.\",\"topic\":\"Recognition and Philosophy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Raman, where should scientists look to find answers to scientific problems?\",\"optio