ants to go back to school.\",\"She wants to make a career in politics.\",\"She wants to direct her own movies.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'sources close to her reveal that now she wants to make a career in politics\'.\",\"topic\":\"RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which word in the text is a synonym for \'disclosed\' or \'made known\'?\",\"options\":[\"Refused\",\"Believed\",\"Revealed\",\"Completed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To \'reveal\' means to make something known that was previously secret.\",\"topic\":\"RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, on what date was this news report published?\",\"options\":[\"Sept. 1\",\"Sept. 9\",\"Oct. 9\",\"Aug. 9\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The dateline in the figure clearly shows \'Mumbai, Sept. 9\'.\",\"topic\":\"RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/080_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, what is the main headline of this news analysis?\",\"options\":[\"RITIKA JOINS POLITICS\",\"RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING\",\"RITIKA\'S BIG BUDGET MOVIE\",\"FAMOUS ACTRESS IN MUMBAI\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The bold text at the top of the figure is the headline: \'RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING\'.\",\"topic\":\"RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/080_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Who is Stephen Hawking according to the news report?\",\"options\":[\"A famous biology professor\",\"A famous physics professor\",\"A famous newspaper editor\",\"A famous history teacher\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states he is a \'famous physics professor\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which newspaper did Stephen Hawking recently write about these challenges?\",\"options\":[\"The London Times\",\"The Daily News\",\"The Guardian\",\"The History Journal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Hawking wrote recently in \'the Guardian newspaper\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From which city was this news report filed?\",\"options\":[\"New York\",\"London\",\"Paris\",\"Tokyo\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The news report begins with the location \'London\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'mankind\' as used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only men in the world\",\"Kind people in history\",\"The human race as a whole\",\"People who study physics\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In this context, mankind refers to all human beings facing global challenges.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned by Hawking as a huge challenge facing the world today?\",\"options\":[\"Climate change\",\"Overpopulation\",\"Epidemic diseases\",\"Volcanic eruptions\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists climate change, overpopulation, and epidemic diseases, but not volcanic eruptions.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the profession of the man shown in the photograph?\",\"options\":[\"Doctor\",\"Physics professor\",\"Newspaper reporter\",\"Astronaut\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text next to the photo in the figure identifies Stephen Hawking as a \'famous physics professor\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does Hawking say about our current ability to leave Earth?\",\"options\":[\"We have already built colonies on other planets\",\"We have the technology to escape the planet easily\",\"We have not yet developed the ability to escape it\",\"We will never be able to leave the planet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states we \'have not yet developed the ability to escape it\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what must we do because we only have one planet right now?\",\"options\":[\"Work together to protect it\",\"Build more technology to destroy it\",\"Move to the stars immediately\",\"Stop reading the newspaper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says \'we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When does Hawking suggest humans might have built colonies amid the stars?\",\"options\":[\"In a few years from now\",\"In a few hundred years from now\",\"In the next ten years\",\"It has already happened\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars\'.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase from the news refers specifically to the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"The Guardian\",\"Colonies amid the stars\",\"The planet on which we live\",\"Most dangerous time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The phrase \'the planet on which we live\' is used to describe Earth.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does Hawking believe today is the \'most dangerous time\' in history?\",\"options\":[\"Because we have no technology at all\",\"Because we can destroy our planet but cannot leave it yet\",\"Because there are too many newspapers like the Guardian\",\"Because we have already moved to the stars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The danger lies in having the technology to destroy the planet without having the ability to escape it.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how does the headline relate to the text provided?\",\"options\":[\"It describes a happy event in London\",\"It summarizes the serious warning given by the professor\",\"It explains how to build colonies in space\",\"It lists the names of different newspapers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The headline \'MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\' summarizes the warning Hawking gives in the text.\",\"topic\":\"MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which word is NOT listed in the \'Two minutes of Oral Work\' to find related words for?\",\"options\":[\"news\",\"TV\",\"radio\",\"internet\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists news, TV, radio, and mobile as the words for oral work.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how are types of news decided on a newspaper page?\",\"options\":[\"By the price of the news\",\"By region and nature of the news\",\"By the number of words\",\"By the name of the reporter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that types are decided according to region and the nature of the news.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a category of news based on \'region\' as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Political\",\"Financial\",\"World\",\"Cultural\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists city, state, nation, and world as categories based on region.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is doing oral work for the word \'mobile\', which related word would be most appropriate?\",\"options\":[\"Smartphone\",\"Bicycle\",\"Television\",\"Newspaper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A smartphone is a word directly related to \'mobile\' in the context of communication technology.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which category describes the \'nature\' of news regarding music, dance, or traditions?\",\"options\":[\"Financial\",\"Political\",\"Cultural\",\"Science related\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cultural news covers the nature of arts and traditions mentioned in the text\'s classification.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific task for Activity 1 regarding pages 70 to 72?\",\"options\":[\"Copy all the news items\",\"Decide the types to which each news item belongs\",\"Draw pictures for the news\",\"Count the number of words\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly asks students to read the items and decide their types.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a category based on the \'nature\' of news according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"City\",\"State\",\"Financial\",\"Nation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Financial is listed under the nature of news, whereas the others are regional.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a news item is about a new discovery in space, which \'nature\' category from the text does it fit?\",\"options\":[\"Political\",\"Science related\",\"Financial\",\"Cultural\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Space discoveries fall under the \'science related\' nature of news mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does Activity 2 ask the students to identify in a newspaper?\",\"options\":[\"The cost of the newspaper\",\"The names of the editors\",\"Items other than news\",\"The date of publication\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activity 2 asks: \'What other items do you find in a newspaper besides news?\'\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about newspaper organization from Activity 1?\",\"options\":[\"News is placed randomly on any page\",\"Each page usually carries only a certain type of news\",\"Newspapers only contain international news\",\"Newspapers do not have specific sections\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that each page usually carries only a certain type of news based on region or nature.\",\"topic\":\"Oral Work and Activity\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the tiny settlement of Ralewadi located according to the news report?\",\"options\":[\"In the Himalayas\",\"Near Ambegaon\",\"Inside the Civil Hospital\",\"In a big city\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Ralewadi is a \'tiny settlement near Ambegaon\'.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the cost of one packet of Miribaba’s \'special\' powder?\",\"options\":[\"Rs 10\",\"Rs 25\",\"Rs 50\",\"It is free of cost\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions, \'He offers it at Rs 25 a packet.\'\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Miribaba, where did he develop the powder from?\",\"options\":[\"A local forest in Kavathe\",\"A special herb in the Himalayas\",\"Ambegaon Civil Hospital\",\"A secret laboratory\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Miribaba claims he developed the powder from a \'special herb in the Himalayas\'.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the news, what is the meaning of the word \'flock\'?\",\"options\":[\"To fly away like birds\",\"To move or gather in large numbers\",\"To stay away from a place\",\"To sleep in the sun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In this context, \'flock\' refers to the large crowds of people gathering to see Miribaba.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does Miribaba refuse to give the exact name and source of the herb?\",\"options\":[\"He has forgotten the name\",\"The herb has no name\",\"He says it was revealed to him\",\"He is not allowed to speak\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says he is unwilling to give the name and only says \'it was revealed to him\'.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does Miribaba do to every packet before selling it?\",\"options\":[\"He signs it\",\"He labels it with a name\",\"He blesses it\",\"He tests it in a lab\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Each packet is specially ‘blessed’ by him.\'\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a family buys 5 packets of Miribaba\'s powder, how much money will they spend in total?\",\"options\":[\"Rs 100\",\"Rs 125\",\"Rs 150\",\"Rs 75\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One packet costs Rs 25, so 5 packets cost 5 x 25 = 125.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What evidence does Dr. Karnik provide that contradicts Miribaba\'s claims?\",\"options\":[\"The powder is made of sugar\",\"The number of patients in the hospital has increased\",\"The herb does not grow in the Himalayas\",\"Miribaba is not a real doctor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dr. Karnik notes that hospital patient numbers have increased, suggesting the powder isn\'t curing people.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is there a question mark in the heading \'QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\'\",\"options\":[\"Because the reporter is asking for the price\",\"Because the claim of a \'quick cure\' is doubtful or unproven\",\"Because the editor forgot to remove it\",\"Because Miribaba asked a question\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The question mark indicates that the claim made in the headline is not a confirmed fact.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the news report is based on verifiable medical data?\",\"options\":[\"The claims made by Miribaba\'s followers\",\"Miribaba\'s statement about the Himalayan herb\",\"Dr. Karnik\'s report on the number of hospital patients\",\"The idea that the herb was \'revealed\' to Miribaba\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dr. Karnik\'s observation of patient numbers at the Civil Hospital is a verifiable fact compared to personal claims.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the news, what is the most likely reason people are waiting in \'long winding queues\'?\",\"options\":[\"They want to enjoy the hot sun\",\"They are hoping for a miracle cure for their diseases\",\"They are waiting to meet Dr. Karnik\",\"They are protesting against Miribaba\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"People flock to get the powder because Miribaba claims it \'cures all sorts of diseases\'.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the reliability of the followers\' claims?\",\"options\":[\"They are reliable because thousands of people are there\",\"They are reliable because the powder is blessed\",\"They may not be reliable as they are personal claims without medical proof\",\"They are reliable because the herb is from the Himalayas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Followers\' claims are anecdotal and lack scientific evidence, especially when contrasted with the hospital\'s data.\",\"topic\":\"QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21907,41,7,'English','en','3.4 Please don’t read this poem !','[\"1. The poem is a playful warning that goes against the usual rule of reading.\",\"2. The author, Kenn Nesbitt, uses humour to make the reader curious.\",\"3. The poem’s title and first line are a direct command: ‘Please don’t read this poem.’\",\"4. The poem uses simple, everyday language and rhyming couplets.\",\"5. The poem contains many short, imperative sentences that create a sense of urgency.\",\"6. The poem’s structure is a series of short stanzas, each ending with a warning or threat.\",\"7. The poem uses repetition of the word ‘please’ to emphasize the request.\",\"8. The poem’s rhymes are mostly end‑rhymes: ‘poem\\/come’, ‘read\\/need’, ‘line\\/shine’ etc.\",\"9. The poem’s tone is playful but also a bit dramatic, as if the speaker is a child’s teacher.\",\"10. The poem invites the reader to think about why we read and what we do with what we read.\",\"11. The poem can be used to practise intonation, rhyme, and understanding of the speaker’s point of view.\",\"12. The poem can be used to practise making inferences and drawing conclusions.\",\"13. The poem can be used to practise recognising and using contracted forms (e.g. ‘don’t’, ‘isn’t’, ‘you’re’).\",\"14. The poem can be used to practise writing short dialogues and adding lines to a text.\",\"15. The poem can be used to practise identifying right or wrong behaviour in reading situations.\",\"16. The poem can be used to practise recognising and using ‘be’ and ‘have’ in questions.\",\"17. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘why’ and ‘what’ questions.\",\"18. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘where’ and ‘are’ questions.\",\"19. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘have’ and ‘has’ questions.\",\"20. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘are’ and ‘is’ questions.\",\"21. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘is’ and ‘are’ questions.\",\"22. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘is’ and ‘are’ questions.\",\"23. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘is’ and ‘are’ questions.\",\"24. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘is’ and ‘are’ questions.\",\"25. The poem can be used to practise writing short sentences with ‘is’ and ‘are’ questions.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the first stanza, who is the poem meant for?\",\"options\":[\"The reader\",\"The speaker\'s friends\",\"Only the speaker\",\"The police\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first stanza states, \'It\'s only meant for me.\'\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the speaker suggest the reader should do instead of reading the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Go outside and play\",\"Call a cop\",\"Write their own poem\",\"Go to sleep\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The second stanza says, \'I\'m sure you\'d rather just go outside and play.\'\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What threat does the speaker make if the reader doesn\'t quit reading immediately?\",\"options\":[\"They will tell the reader\'s family\",\"They will call a cop\",\"They will take the poem away\",\"They will stop writing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The fifth stanza states, \'If you don\'t quit this instant, I swear I\'ll call a cop.\'\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the speaker mean by the phrase \'move along now\' in the first stanza?\",\"options\":[\"Keep reading faster\",\"Leave the area or go away\",\"Dance to the rhythm\",\"Change the page\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In this context, \'move along\' means to stop looking and leave.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the speaker say \'That isn\'t very nice\' in the third stanza?\",\"options\":[\"Because the reader is being loud\",\"Because the reader is still reading despite being asked to stop\",\"Because the poem is sad\",\"Because the reader is playing outside\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The speaker is upset that the reader is ignoring the request to stop reading.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What will happen to the reader\'s relationship with their family if they are locked up?\",\"options\":[\"Their family will visit them every day\",\"Their family will pay the bail\",\"Their family will likely forget they exist\",\"Their family will read the poem too\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The seventh stanza says, \'Your family, too, will likely forget that you exist.\'\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the character\'s raised finger and facial expression most closely match which instruction from the poem?\",\"options\":[\"\'just go outside and play\'\",\"\'slowly back away\'\",\"\'Do not read one more line\'\",\"\'Tell all your friends\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The character\'s stern gesture in the image represents a firm warning or command to stop reading.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/084_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a reader is described as \'frail\' after being in jail for a long time, how would they look?\",\"options\":[\"Strong and healthy\",\"Weak and delicate\",\"Angry and loud\",\"Young and energetic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Frail means physically weak, especially due to age or illness.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the character looks distressed and is holding out a hand. Which line from the text explains this feeling of urgency?\",\"options\":[\"\'There\'s nothing here to see\'\",\"\'It\'s too late now, amigo\'\",\"\'I\'ve asked you once politely\'\",\"\'He\'ll lock you up in jail\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The character\'s expression of panic matches the realization that the reader has already finished most of the poem.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/085_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the \'only one solution\' the speaker provides at the end of the poem?\",\"options\":[\"To delete the poem\",\"To apologize to the speaker\",\"To tell others not to read the poem\",\"To go back in time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The final stanza says the solution is to \'Tell all your friends and family they shouldn\'t read it too.\'\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the speaker\'s tone change from the beginning of the poem to the middle (stanza 5)?\",\"options\":[\"From angry to happy\",\"From polite request to serious threat\",\"From joking to crying\",\"From bored to excited\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The speaker starts by saying \'please\' but eventually threatens to call a cop and send the reader to jail.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the irony behind the speaker\'s \'solution\' in the final stanza?\",\"options\":[\"It makes more people curious to read the poem\",\"It helps the reader get out of jail\",\"It makes the speaker stop writing\",\"It proves the reader was right\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"By telling others not to read it, the reader is actually spreading the word about the poem, just as the title did.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the poem \'Please don’t read this poem!\'?\",\"options\":[\"Kenn Nesbitt\",\"William Wordsworth\",\"Ruskin Bond\",\"Robert Frost\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions Kenn Nesbitt as the poet.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the vocabulary list provided, what does the word \'amigo\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Enemy\",\"Teacher\",\"Friend\",\"Policeman\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'amigo\' as \'friend\'.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'frail\' as used in the poem\'s context?\",\"options\":[\"Strong\",\"Weak\",\"Angry\",\"Fast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The vocabulary list defines \'frail\' as \'weak\'.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Think and answer\' section, who is the poet addressing when he says not to read the poem?\",\"options\":[\"The reader\",\"The police\",\"His family\",\"His teacher\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poet is speaking directly to the person currently reading the poem.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'cop\' refer to in the poem\'s vocabulary?\",\"options\":[\"A doctor\",\"A pilot\",\"A policeman\",\"A soldier\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'cop\' as \'policemen\'.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When the poet says, \'Tell all your friends and family they shouldn’t read it too\', what is his likely intention?\",\"options\":[\"To hide the poem forever\",\"To make them curious so they will want to read it\",\"Because the poem is very sad\",\"Because the poem is a secret\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Telling people not to do something often makes them more curious to do it.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student feels curious after reading the line \'Please don’t read this poem\', which question from the text does this address?\",\"options\":[\"Question 3(a)\",\"Question 3(b)\",\"Question 3(e)\",\"Question 1\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Question 3(b) asks what you felt when you read that specific line.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The line \'Put the poem down now and slowly back away\' is most similar to a situation involving:\",\"options\":[\"A dangerous object or threat\",\"A birthday party\",\"A library study session\",\"A peaceful sleep\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The phrasing \'slowly back away\' is typically used in dangerous situations, creating a humorous effect here.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Think and answer\' section, does the poet genuinely want the reader to stop reading?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, he is very serious\",\"No, he is using a playful way to engage the reader\",\"Yes, he wants to protect the reader\",\"No, he forgot why he wrote it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The tone of the questions suggests the poet is being playful rather than serious about stopping the reader.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of the instructions given in the \'Think and answer\' section?\",\"options\":[\"To test the reader\'s ability to follow orders\",\"To encourage the reader to reflect on the poet\'s tone and intent\",\"To teach the reader how to become a policeman\",\"To explain the history of poetry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The questions are designed to make the reader think about the feelings and hidden meanings behind the poet\'s words.\",\"topic\":\"Think and answer\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used in the text for listening to other people’s conversation without their knowledge?\",\"options\":[\"Eavesdropping\",\"Interfering\",\"Gossiping\",\"Overhearing\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines eavesdropping as listening to other people’s conversation without their knowledge.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the list, which of these is considered \'Wrong\' regarding someone\'s personal writing?\",\"options\":[\"Borrowing a textbook\",\"Reading someone else’s diary\",\"Buying a new notebook\",\"Writing in your own journal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Item (b) in the text identifies reading someone else\'s diary as a \'Wrong\' action.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what should you avoid doing when someone is typing a password?\",\"options\":[\"Looking at the screen only\",\"Talking to the person\",\"Looking at the keyboard\",\"Closing your eyes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Item (c) states that looking at the keyboard when someone is typing a password is wrong.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you find a sealed letter addressed to your neighbor, what action would be \'Wrong\' based on item (a)?\",\"options\":[\"Giving it to the neighbor\",\"Leaving it on the doorstep\",\"Reading the letter\",\"Returning it to the postman\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Item (a) classifies reading someone else’s letter as a \'Wrong\' action.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following personal details is NOT mentioned in item (e) as something you shouldn\'t get without knowledge?\",\"options\":[\"Address\",\"Phone number\",\"Email ID\",\"Date of birth\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Item (e) mentions address, phone number, and email ID, but does not mention date of birth.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common factor in all the \'Wrong\' actions listed in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They all involve using a computer\",\"They all involve physical violence\",\"They all involve invading someone\'s privacy without their knowledge\",\"They all involve stealing money\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"All points (a-e) describe actions where a person\'s private information or space is accessed without their consent or knowledge.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"You are standing behind a friend who is logging into their email. To do the \'Right\' thing according to point (c), you should:\",\"options\":[\"Watch the keys they press\",\"Look away from the keyboard\",\"Try to guess the password\",\"Ask them to say the password aloud\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since looking at the keyboard is \'Wrong\', the correct action is to look away to respect their privacy.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'Eavesdropping\' categorized as a \'Wrong\' action in this context?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is hard to hear clearly\",\"Because it is a violation of a private conversation\",\"Because it makes the listener tired\",\"Because conversations are usually boring\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Eavesdropping is wrong because it involves listening to others without their knowledge, which is a breach of privacy.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase in the text emphasizes that these actions are done secretly?\",\"options\":[\"Decide whether\",\"Give reasons\",\"Without their knowledge\",\"Someone else\'s\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The phrase \'without their knowledge\' is used in points (d) and (e) to indicate the secret nature of these wrong actions.\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student tries to find a teacher\'s home address by looking through the teacher\'s private bag, which point are they violating?\",\"options\":[\"Point (a) only\",\"Point (c) only\",\"Point (e)\",\"None of the points\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Trying to get someone\'s address without their knowledge is specifically listed in point (e).\",\"topic\":\"Right or Wrong\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which auxiliary verbs form questions without the help of \'do\'?\",\"options\":[\"go and write\",\"be and have\",\"read and give\",\"make and go\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the auxiliary verbs \'be\' and \'have\' form questions without the help of \'do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a correct question using the \'be\' auxiliary as shown in the examples?\",\"options\":[\"Do you writing a story?\",\"Are you writing a story?\",\"Have you writing a story?\",\"Is you writing a story?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'Are you writing a story?\' as a correct example of a question using \'be\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the question \'Has he given you a book?\', which auxiliary verb is used?\",\"options\":[\"do\",\"be\",\"have\",\"give\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Has\' is a form of the auxiliary verb \'have\' mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the correct question form for the auxiliary \'be\' from the following options:\",\"options\":[\"Were they reading their homework?\",\"Did they reading their homework?\",\"Have they reading their homework?\",\"Do they reading their homework?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Were they reading their homework?\' as a valid question using the auxiliary \'be\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To form a question from the statement \'I have made a mistake\', how should it be written according to the rules?\",\"options\":[\"Do I have made a mistake?\",\"Have I made a mistake?\",\"Am I made a mistake?\",\"Did I made a mistake?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows \'Have I made a mistake?\' as the correct way to form a question using \'have\' without \'do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which form of \'be\' is used to ask a question about \'she\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Are\",\"Were\",\"Is\",\"Have\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'Is\' in the example \'Is she going home?\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the Language Study section, what is the main difference when forming questions with \'be\' and \'have\' compared to other verbs?\",\"options\":[\"They always use the word \'do\'\",\"They do not use the help of \'do\'\",\"They cannot be used to ask questions\",\"They must always come at the end of the sentence\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states these auxiliaries form questions without the help of \'do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to ask someone if they have finished a task, which structure follows the example \'Have you written this?\'\",\"options\":[\"Have you finished this?\",\"Do you have finished this?\",\"Are you finished this?\",\"Were you finished this?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern \'Have you written this?\', the correct auxiliary to use is \'have\' without \'do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following questions is grammatically INCORRECT based on the rule that \'be\' and \'have\' don\'t need \'do\'?\",\"options\":[\"Is she going home?\",\"Have I made a mistake?\",\"Do have you written this?\",\"Are you writing a story?\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'have\' forms questions without \'do\', so \'Do have you...\' is incorrect.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which auxiliary verb category does the question \'Were they reading their homework?\' belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Have\",\"Do\",\"Be\",\"Write\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Were they reading their homework?\' under the \'Be\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Yes\\/No questions\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'Where are you going?\', which word is the auxiliary verb from the \'Be\' group?\",\"options\":[\"Where\",\"are\",\"you\",\"going\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the \'Be\' category examples, \'are\' is the auxiliary verb used with \'you\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which question word is used in the sentence \'What is she painting?\'?\",\"options\":[\"Where\",\"Why\",\"What\",\"How\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists \'What is she painting?\' under the \'Be\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Have\' section, which sentence uses the verb \'put\'?\",\"options\":[\"What have you drawn?\",\"Why has he come?\",\"Where have you put it?\",\"Where are you going?\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Where have you put it?\' as an example under the \'Have\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these sentences belongs to the \'Be\' category according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Why are you laughing?\",\"Why has he come?\",\"What have you drawn?\",\"Where have you put it?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"\'Why are you laughing?\' uses the auxiliary \'are\' which belongs to the \'Be\' group.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Have\' category, which auxiliary verb is used when the subject is \'he\'?\",\"options\":[\"have\",\"are\",\"is\",\"has\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text shows \'Why has he come?\', indicating \'has\' is used with \'he\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What action is being performed in the sentence \'What is she painting?\'?\",\"options\":[\"laughing\",\"painting\",\"going\",\"drawing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The main verb in the sentence \'What is she painting?\' is \'painting\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Following the pattern of \'What have you drawn?\', how would you ask a question using \'put\' and \'you\'?\",\"options\":[\"What are you put?\",\"Where have you put it?\",\"Why is you put?\",\"Where has you put it?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'Where have you put it?\' as the correct structure for the verb \'put\' with \'you\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you follow the pattern of \'Why has he come?\', which auxiliary verb would you use for a single person (he\\/she) in the \'Have\' group?\",\"options\":[\"are\",\"have\",\"has\",\"is\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'has\' for the subject \'he\' in the \'Have\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common ending for the main verbs in the \'Be\' category examples (going, painting, laughing)?\",\"options\":[\"-ed\",\"-en\",\"-ing\",\"-s\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"All main verbs in the \'Be\' section (going, painting, laughing) end in \'-ing\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the two groups, which auxiliary verb is paired with \'you\' in the \'Have\' group examples?\",\"options\":[\"are\",\"is\",\"has\",\"have\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In the \'Have\' group, the examples \'What have you drawn?\' and \'Where have you put it?\' both use \'have\' with \'you\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21908,41,7,'English','en','3.5 The Red-headed League','[\"- The Red‑headed League is a Sherlock Holmes story about a clever plan to steal gold from a bank.\",\"- The story is told by Dr. Watson, a friend of Holmes.\",\"- Mr. Jabez Wilson is a pawn‑broker who gets hired by the League to stay in his shop every day.\",\"- The League’s job is to keep Mr. Wilson away from his shop so that criminals can dig a tunnel from his house to the bank’s cellar.\",\"- The criminals are John Clay (the assistant) and Mr. Duncan Ross, both red‑haired men.\",\"- Holmes discovers the plan by noticing Clay’s odd habits and the strange advertisement.\",\"- Holmes and his friends set up a trap in the bank’s cellar and catch the criminals.\",\"- The League’s advertisement offers a job to “red‑headed men” for a nominal salary.\",\"- The story shows how observation, deduction and planning can solve a mystery.\",\"- Key vocabulary: pawn‑broker, nominal, red‑headed, tunnel, cellar, foiled, cunning.\",\"- The story illustrates the importance of paying attention to small details.\",\"- Holmes uses questioning and evidence to prove the criminals’ guilt.\",\"- The League’s purpose was to create a distraction and a cover for the robbery.\",\"- The mystery ends when Holmes arrests Clay and saves the gold.\",\"- The story is a good example of detective fiction with a twist of humour.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the narrator of the story \'The Red-headed League\'?\",\"options\":[\"Sherlock Holmes\",\"Mr. Jabez Wilson\",\"Dr. Watson\",\"Vincent Spaulding\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the story is told by Sherlock Holmes\' friend, Dr. Watson.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the occupation of Mr. Jabez Wilson?\",\"options\":[\"Photographer\",\"Pawn-broker\",\"Newspaper editor\",\"Detective\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'He was a pawn-broker.\'\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the story, what does the word \'fiery\' describe about Mr. Wilson?\",\"options\":[\"His angry temper\",\"His fast way of speaking\",\"The bright red color of his hair\",\"His passion for photography\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes him as an elderly man with \'fiery red hair,\' and the glossary defines fiery as \'like fire, bright red.\'\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How much salary was offered per week for a member of the Red-headed League?\",\"options\":[\"Two pounds\",\"Four pounds\",\"Seven pounds\",\"Eleven pounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The advertisement in the text says, \'the salary is four pounds a week\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Mr. Wilson satisfied with his assistant, Vincent Spaulding?\",\"options\":[\"Because he was a professional photographer\",\"Because he was smart and worked for half the normal wages\",\"Because he was a member of the Red-headed League\",\"Because he was an elderly man like Mr. Wilson\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Spaulding was smart, efficient, and worked for only half the normal wages.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the advertisement, if a red-headed man wanted to apply for the vacancy, when should he visit the office?\",\"options\":[\"On Monday at eleven o’clock\",\"On Monday at seven o’clock\",\"Every day for two months\",\"Only on weekends\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The advertisement states: \'Red-headed men may apply in person on Monday, at eleven o’clock\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What specific habit of Vincent Spaulding did Mr. Wilson consider a \'fault\'?\",\"options\":[\"He asked for too much money\",\"He was not efficient at work\",\"He frequently went to the cellar to develop photographs\",\"He spent all his time reading newspapers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that his fault was leaving work to go down into the cellar to develop photographs.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of \'nominal services\' as mentioned in the advertisement?\",\"options\":[\"Very difficult and dangerous work\",\"Work that requires high technical skills\",\"Easy or simple tasks\",\"Work that involves traveling to Fleet Street\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'nominal\' as \'easy, simple\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where was the office of the Red-headed League located?\",\"options\":[\"Baker Street\",\"7 Fleet Street\",\"The cellar of the pawn-shop\",\"Duncan Ross\'s house\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The advertisement gives the address as \'the office of the League, 7 Fleet Street\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a customer brings a valuable gold watch to Mr. Wilson to get a loan, what is Mr. Wilson acting as?\",\"options\":[\"A detective\",\"A pawn-broker\",\"A photographer\",\"A league member\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A pawn-broker is defined as a person who lends money at interest when valuable things are kept with him.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the location of the \'cellar\' significant to Vincent Spaulding\'s hobby?\",\"options\":[\"It is where the Red-headed League office is\",\"It is a dark place suitable for developing photographs\",\"It is where he meets Duncan Ross\",\"It is where he reads the newspaper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links going down into the cellar with his hobby of developing photographs.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which detail about Vincent Spaulding might be considered suspicious for a highly \'smart and efficient\' worker?\",\"options\":[\"He has a hobby of photography\",\"He works for half the normal wages\",\"He showed the advertisement to his boss\",\"He knows Duncan Ross\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It is unusual for a smart and efficient worker to accept only half the standard pay, suggesting a hidden motive.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If a red-headed man is told to \'apply in person\' for the job, what action must he take?\",\"options\":[\"Send a letter to Duncan Ross\",\"Call Sherlock Holmes on the phone\",\"Physically go to the office at 7 Fleet Street himself\",\"Ask his assistant to go on his behalf\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Applying \'in person\' means the individual must attend the location themselves.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which character is the one described in the text as having \'fiery red hair\'?\",\"options\":[\"The man standing and leaning on the table\",\"The man sitting in the chair\",\"The man standing in the background with a mustache\",\"None of the men shown\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The man sitting in the chair is depicted with bright red hair, matching the description of Mr. Jabez Wilson.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/088_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the sitting man holding that contains the advertisement he showed to Holmes?\",\"options\":[\"A photograph\",\"A magnifying glass\",\"A newspaper\",\"A pawn-ticket\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Mr. Wilson holding a newspaper, which the text says contained the advertisement for the Red-headed League.\",\"topic\":\"Part I : Mr Wilson\'s Story\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/088_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Who founded the famous Red-headed League according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Mr. Wilson\",\"Mr. Duncan Ross\",\"Mr. Ezekiah Hopkins\",\"Spaulding\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that an American millionaire, Mr. Ezekiah Hopkins, had founded the Red-headed League.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'founded\' as used in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Discovered\",\"Started\",\"Searched\",\"Ended\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines \'founded\' as \'started\' in the glossary section.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the representative of the Red-headed League sitting in the office?\",\"options\":[\"Mr. Ezekiah Hopkins\",\"Mr. Wilson\",\"Spaulding\",\"Mr. Duncan Ross\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the red-headed man behind the table was Mr. Duncan Ross, a representative of the League.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the fixed weekly pay for the job at the Red-headed League?\",\"options\":[\"Two pounds\",\"Four pounds\",\"Ten pounds\",\"Fourteen pounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'The pay was fixed at four pounds a week.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Fleet Street crowded with red-headed people?\",\"options\":[\"They were celebrating a festival\",\"They were looking for Mr. Wilson\",\"They were responding to the advertisement\",\"They were members of a parade\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The crowd was there because Spaulding showed the advertisement to Mr. Wilson and they went to the address given, implying others did too.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Mr. Wilson initially want to go back and not apply for the job?\",\"options\":[\"He did not like the office\",\"He thought the pay was too low\",\"He saw too much competition from the crowd\",\"He was afraid of Mr. Duncan Ross\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says Mr. Wilson thought that with so much competition he would not get the job.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the most important condition Mr. Wilson had to follow to keep his job?\",\"options\":[\"He must copy the Encyclopedia quickly\",\"He must not leave the office during work hours\",\"He must bring his own chair\",\"He must wear a red wig\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mr. Duncan Ross explained that if he left the office, he would lose the job.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did Spaulding help Mr. Wilson decide to take the job?\",\"options\":[\"He offered to pay Mr. Wilson more money\",\"He promised to do the copying work for him\",\"He assured Mr. Wilson he would look after his business\",\"He gave Mr. Wilson his own red hair\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Spaulding assured Mr. Wilson that he would look after Mr. Wilson’s business in his absence.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Mr. Wilson works from ten o\'clock to two o\'clock, how many hours does he spend in the office daily?\",\"options\":[\"Two hours\",\"Three hours\",\"Four hours\",\"Five hours\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The duration from 10:00 to 2:00 is exactly four hours.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which task was Mr. Wilson specifically assigned to do at the office?\",\"options\":[\"Interview other red-headed people\",\"Clean the office chairs and table\",\"Copy out the Encyclopedia Britannica\",\"Write letters to Ezekiah Hopkins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the work was to \'copy out the Encyclopedia Britannica\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which letter of the alphabet did Mr. Wilson start his work with?\",\"options\":[\"Letter \'R\' for Red\",\"Letter \'E\' for Encyclopedia\",\"Letter \'A\'\",\"Letter \'W\' for Wilson\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mr. Ross told Mr. Wilson to start with the letter ‘A’.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the work described as \'light\' in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"Because the office was very bright\",\"Because it only involved copying text for a few hours\",\"Because Mr. Wilson did not have to carry heavy objects\",\"Because the pay was very high\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The work was considered light because it was simply copying a book for four hours a day.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does Mr. Duncan Ross\'s action of locking the door at two o\'clock suggest?\",\"options\":[\"He wanted to trap Mr. Wilson inside\",\"The workday was strictly over at that time\",\"He was afraid of thieves\",\"He wanted Mr. Wilson to work overtime\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Locking the door at two o\'clock signifies the end of the required work hours (ten to two).\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which question asks about the reason for Mr. Wilson\'s choice to take the position?\",\"options\":[\"The first question\",\"The second question\",\"The third question\",\"The fourth question\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The third question in the figure is \'Why did Mr Wilson accept the job?\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/089_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which question asks for the reader\'s personal opinion rather than a fact from the story?\",\"options\":[\"The first question\",\"The second question\",\"The third question\",\"The fourth question\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The fourth question \'Would you like to do such a job? Why?\' asks for the student\'s personal view.\",\"topic\":\"The Application and the Job\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/089_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"For how many weeks did Mr. Wilson work at the Red-headed League before it ended?\",\"options\":[\"Two weeks\",\"Four weeks\",\"Eight weeks\",\"Ten weeks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Eight weeks had passed like this...\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the specific date mentioned on the note nailed to the door?\",\"options\":[\"October 9, 1880\",\"August 9, 1880\",\"October 10, 1880\",\"September 9, 1880\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The note said, \'The Red-headed League is dissolved. Oct 9, 1880.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was Mr. Wilson\'s task while working for the League?\",\"options\":[\"Writing letters to Mr. Duncan Ross\",\"Copying from an encyclopedia\",\"Guarding the office rooms\",\"Interviewing other red-headed men\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mr. Wilson mentions writing about \'Abbots, Archery etc.\', which are entries in an encyclopedia.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Mr. Wilson, what physical mark does his assistant Spaulding have?\",\"options\":[\"A scar on his chin\",\"A black patch over one eye\",\"A white splash of acid on his forehead\",\"A red birthmark on his cheek\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mr. Wilson describes him as having \'a white splash of acid on his forehead.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why were the enquiries at the nearby offices unsuccessful?\",\"options\":[\"The offices were all closed for the weekend\",\"The rooms had been rented under a false name\",\"The neighbors were part of the League\",\"Mr. Wilson went to the wrong building\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'The rooms had been rented for a false name.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Mr. Wilson visited Holmes on a Saturday, by which day did Holmes hope to reach a conclusion?\",\"options\":[\"Sunday\",\"Monday\",\"Tuesday\",\"The following Saturday\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes says, \'Today is Saturday, and by Monday we may come to a conclusion.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which word would most likely appear after \'Archery\' in the encyclopedia Mr. Wilson was copying?\",\"options\":[\"Abbot\",\"Architecture\",\"Apple\",\"Axe\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on alphabetical order, \'Architecture\' follows \'Archery\' more closely than the other options in an encyclopedia.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to Mr. Duncan Ross\'s visits over the eight-week period?\",\"options\":[\"He came every single day at ten o\'clock\",\"He only came on Saturdays to pay the salary\",\"He came once in a while and then not at all\",\"He stayed in the office all day with Mr. Wilson\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Mr Duncan Ross came in only once in a while and after a time, he did not come in at all\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'dissolved\' mean in the context of the Red-headed League?\",\"options\":[\"The office was washed away\",\"The organization was officially ended\",\"The members moved to a new building\",\"The salary was increased\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In this context, \'dissolved\' means the business or group has come to an end.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who first brought the advertisement of the Red-headed League to Mr. Wilson\'s attention?\",\"options\":[\"Mr. Duncan Ross\",\"Sherlock Holmes\",\"His assistant, Spaulding\",\"A neighbor at the nearby office\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes asks about \'this assistant of yours who first called your attention to the advertisement\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Mr. Wilson decide to visit Sherlock Holmes?\",\"options\":[\"To ask for a new job\",\"To report a stolen salary\",\"To find out who the League was and why they played a prank\",\"To show him his encyclopedia notes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mr. Wilson says, \'I want to find out about the League... who they are and why they played this prank upon me.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How did Mr. Wilson feel when he saw the note on the door?\",\"options\":[\"Relieved\",\"Shocked\",\"Angry\",\"Happy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says, \'I was shocked. I did not know what to do\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred from Holmes saying \'I thought as much\' after hearing the description of the assistant?\",\"options\":[\"Holmes had never heard of the assistant before\",\"Holmes suspected the assistant\'s identity or involvement\",\"Holmes thought the assistant was a very handsome man\",\"Holmes was bored by the description\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The phrase \'I thought as much\' suggests Holmes had a prior suspicion that was confirmed by the description.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the primary reason Spaulding could not help Mr. Wilson regarding the League\'s closure?\",\"options\":[\"He was not at home\",\"He was the one who locked the door\",\"He simply could not help in any way\",\"He had already found a new job\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'I went home and asked my assistant Spaulding for advice. But he could not help me in any way.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which detail about the assistant\'s appearance is emphasized as a specific identifying feature?\",\"options\":[\"His height\",\"His lack of facial hair\",\"The white splash of acid on his forehead\",\"His stout build\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"While all are mentioned, the acid splash is a unique, distinguishing mark that Holmes notes.\",\"topic\":\"The Mystery of the Dissolution\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which auxiliary verbs form negatives without the help of \'do\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"go and see\",\"be and have\",\"sleep and finish\",\"do and not\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the auxiliary verbs \'be\' and \'have\' form negatives without the help of \'do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the short form of \'was not\' used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"wasnt\",\"was\'not\",\"wasn\'t\",\"wase\'nt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'wasn\'t\' as the negative form for \'was not\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When \'be\' is used as a main verb, how are questions formed?\",\"options\":[\"By using the auxiliary \'do\'\",\"By adding \'not\' at the end\",\"Without the help of \'do\'\",\"By changing \'be\' to \'have\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when \'be\' is used as a main verb, \'do\' is not needed to form questions.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a sentence if a form of \'be\' or \'have\' is missing when forming a negative?\",\"options\":[\"The sentence remains correct\",\"The sentence will be incorrect\",\"The sentence becomes a question\",\"The sentence uses \'do\' instead\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that without a form of \'be\' or \'have\', the sentence will be incorrect.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a correct negative sentence using \'have\' as an auxiliary?\",\"options\":[\"I not seen it.\",\"I do not seen it.\",\"I have not seen it.\",\"I haven\'t see it.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text gives \'I have not seen it\' as a correct example of a negative with \'have\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which is a correct way to ask a question using the main verb \'be\'?\",\"options\":[\"Do you be hungry?\",\"Are you hungry?\",\"You are hungry?\",\"Does he be sad?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'Are you hungry?\' as an example of a question with the main verb \'be\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a valid negative form for the main verb \'have\' according to the examples?\",\"options\":[\"I not have money.\",\"I don\'t have money.\",\"I no have money.\",\"I not money.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'I don\'t have money\' as a correct negative form for the main verb \'have\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the question \'Have you wings?\', which rule is being applied?\",\"options\":[\"Using \'do\' to form a question\",\"Using \'have\' as a main verb without \'do\'\",\"Using \'be\' as an auxiliary verb\",\"Using \'not\' to form a question\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows \'Have you wings?\' as a question where the main verb \'have\' does not use \'do\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which auxiliary can be used specifically with the main verb \'have\'?\",\"options\":[\"be\",\"do\",\"am\",\"was\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'With the main verb have, auxiliary do can be used.\'\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which sentence correctly demonstrates the use of \'not\' with the auxiliary \'have\'?\",\"options\":[\"He has not finished.\",\"He not has finished.\",\"He hasn\'t finish.\",\"He not finished.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'He has not finished\' as a correct example.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the questions \'Have you a pen?\' and \'Do you have a pen?\'. What does the text say about them?\",\"options\":[\"Only the first one is correct.\",\"Only the second one is correct.\",\"Both are correct ways to use the main verb \'have\'.\",\"Neither is correct for Standard English.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists both \'Have you a pen?\' and \'Do you have a pen?\' as valid examples for the main verb \'have\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the incorrect sentence based on the rules provided in the text.\",\"options\":[\"I am not angry.\",\"I am not going.\",\"I not going.\",\"She wasn\'t sleeping.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that without a form of \'be\' or \'have\', the sentence will be incorrect; \'I not going\' lacks the verb \'am\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What does Dr. Watson mean when he says, \'I make nothing of it\'?\",\"options\":[\"He did not see anything.\",\"He does not understand the situation.\",\"He thinks the case is very easy.\",\"He has no interest in the case.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'I make nothing of it\' as \'I do not understand it\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where was the bank located in relation to Mr. Wilson’s shop?\",\"options\":[\"Right across the street\",\"Inside the same building\",\"Just behind the shop\",\"At the corner of the Strand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there was \'a bank just behind Mr Wilson’s shop\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Mr. Holmes do two or three times while standing outside the shop?\",\"options\":[\"He shouted for help.\",\"He thumped upon the pavement.\",\"He looked through the window.\",\"He measured the road.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Holmes \'thumped upon the pavement two or three times\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Holmes really knock on Mr. Wilson’s door and ask for directions?\",\"options\":[\"He was actually lost and needed help.\",\"He wanted to see the assistant\'s face.\",\"He wanted to see the knees of the assistant\'s trousers.\",\"He wanted to check if Mr. Wilson was home.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes explicitly tells Watson he wanted to see \'the knees of his trousers\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"On which day of the week did these events take place?\",\"options\":[\"Saturday\",\"Sunday\",\"Monday\",\"Friday\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Holmes says, \'Today is Friday. The offices and banks will be closed on the weekend.\'\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Holmes believe the crime would likely happen over the weekend?\",\"options\":[\"Because the police do not work on weekends.\",\"Because the offices and banks would be closed.\",\"Because the criminal is busy on weekdays.\",\"Because Mr. Wilson would be away on a holiday.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes notes that banks and offices are closed on the weekend, making it a suitable time for a crime.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What time was Watson supposed to meet Holmes at Baker Street?\",\"options\":[\"8:00 PM\",\"9:00 PM\",\"10:00 PM\",\"12:00 AM\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes tells Watson, \'Come to Baker Street at 10.00\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific item did Holmes ask Watson to bring with him at night?\",\"options\":[\"A magnifying glass\",\"A lantern\",\"A revolver\",\"A notebook\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes tells Watson to \'bring your revolver\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who is Mr. Jones in this account?\",\"options\":[\"A smart criminal\",\"A man from Scotland Yard\",\"Mr. Wilson\'s assistant\",\"A banker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text introduces him as \'Mr Jones of Scotland Yard\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is Mr. Merryweather’s profession?\",\"options\":[\"Police officer\",\"Shop assistant\",\"Banker\",\"Detective\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes Mr. Merryweather as \'a banker\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which sequence correctly describes the path to the cellar?\",\"options\":[\"Iron gate -> narrow passage -> stone steps -> dark passage\",\"Stone steps -> iron gate -> dark passage -> narrow passage\",\"Narrow passage -> dark passage -> iron gate -> stone steps\",\"Iron gate -> stone steps -> dark passage -> narrow passage\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text describes the path: iron gate, then narrow passage, then stone steps, then dark passage.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How did the passage leading to the cellar smell?\",\"options\":[\"Like old paper\",\"Like fresh flowers\",\"Earth-smelling\",\"Like smoke\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes it as a \'dark, earth-smelling passage\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a \'cellar\'?\",\"options\":[\"A room for sleeping\",\"A place where two roads cross\",\"A room used for storage under a building\",\"A famous place in London\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The glossary in the text defines cellar as \'a room used for storage under a building\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was the cellar full of when the group entered?\",\"options\":[\"Gold coins\",\"Big boxes\",\"Old furniture\",\"Tunnels\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the huge cellar as \'full of big boxes\'.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Holmes\'s actions, what can we infer about the assistant\'s trousers?\",\"options\":[\"They were brand new.\",\"They were the wrong size.\",\"They provided a clue about the crime being planned.\",\"They were the same color as the pavement.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes specifically wanted to see the knees to confirm his expectations regarding the \'serious\' matter and the crime being planned.\",\"topic\":\"Part II : Dr Watson\'s Account\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who is Mr. Merryweather according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A criminal assistant\",\"The chairman of the bank\",\"A police inspector\",\"Sherlock Holmes\'s assistant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Mr Merryweather is the chairman of the bank\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From where did the bank borrow a huge quantity of gold?\",\"options\":[\"The Bank of England\",\"The Bank of London\",\"The Bank of France\",\"Mr. Wilson\'s house\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the bank had borrowed a huge quantity of gold from the Bank of France.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'take the criminals by surprise\' mean in this context?\",\"options\":[\"To give the criminals a gift\",\"To warn the criminals before arresting them\",\"To catch the criminals without giving them a warning\",\"To let the criminals escape through the cellar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines it as \'catch the criminals without giving them a warning\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the only escape route for the criminals from the cellar?\",\"options\":[\"Through the main bank door\",\"Back through Mr. Wilson’s house\",\"Through the Bank of France\",\"Through the cellar window\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes whispered that the only escape route is back through Mr. Wilson\'s house.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who was waiting at Mr. Wilson\'s door to catch the criminals?\",\"options\":[\"Sherlock Holmes and Watson\",\"Mr. Merryweather\",\"An inspector and two officers\",\"Mr. Duncan Ross\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mr. Jones states he has an inspector and two officers waiting at the door.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'stopped all the holes\' mean in the plan to catch the criminals?\",\"options\":[\"Filled the cellar with gold\",\"Repaired the floor of the bank\",\"Closed all the routes by which the criminal may escape\",\"Turned off all the lights in the cellar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines it as \'closed all the routes by which (the criminal) may escape \\/ run away\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How long did the group wait silently in the cellar?\",\"options\":[\"Less than thirty minutes\",\"Exactly one hour\",\"More than an hour\",\"All through the night\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'We waited silently for more than an hour.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the first sign that the criminals were entering the cellar?\",\"options\":[\"A loud bang on the door\",\"A point of bright light appeared in the floor\",\"A man shouted from the hole\",\"The sound of a revolver firing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the sequence starting with \'a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the criminal \'Clay\' disguised as earlier in the story?\",\"options\":[\"Mr. Merryweather\",\"Mr. Jones\",\"Spaulding, the assistant\",\"The Inspector\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the man as \'Spaulding, the assistant alias the criminal Clay\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How did Sherlock Holmes prevent Clay from using his revolver?\",\"options\":[\"He shot Clay first\",\"He hit Clay on his wrist\",\"He grabbed the revolver from Clay\'s hand\",\"He called the policemen to take it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Clay\'s friend unable to escape even after diving back down the hole?\",\"options\":[\"The hole was too small\",\"He got lost in the dark\",\"Three men were waiting for him at the other end\",\"Sherlock Holmes caught him by the collar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes tells Clay, \'He cannot escape. There are three men waiting for him at the other end!\'\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If Mr. Jones had not brought the officers to Mr. Wilson\'s house, what would have likely happened?\",\"options\":[\"The gold would have disappeared\",\"Clay\'s friend would have escaped through the other end\",\"Holmes would not have entered the cellar\",\"Mr. Merryweather would have been arrested\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The officers were there to \'stop all the holes\'; without them, the escape route would be open.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the criminal coming out of?\",\"options\":[\"A wooden crate\",\"A square hole in the floor\",\"A doorway behind the boxes\",\"A ventilation shaft\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a man emerging from a square opening in the floor, which the text describes as a square hole.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/093_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the men in the cellar hiding behind while they wait?\",\"options\":[\"Stone walls\",\"Large stacked boxes\",\"Iron pillars\",\"Bank counters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the men positioned behind large yellow boxes\\/crates in the cellar.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/093_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what was inside the boxes shown in the cellar?\",\"options\":[\"Bank documents\",\"Clothes for the Red-headed League\",\"A huge quantity of gold\",\"Tools for digging tunnels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'the boxes in the cellar were full of gold\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Capture in the Cellar\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who thanked Mr. Holmes for preventing the bank robbery?\",\"options\":[\"Mr. Wilson\",\"Mr. Merryweather\",\"John Clay\",\"Dr. Watson\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text begins with Mr. Merryweather thanking Mr. Holmes for foiling the robbery.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'foiled\' mean in the context of the story?\",\"options\":[\"Encouraged\",\"Assisted\",\"Prevented or laid waste\",\"Wrapped in metal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'foiled\' as \'laid waste, prevented\' in the vocabulary notes.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the criminals plan to dig the underground tunnel from?\",\"options\":[\"The bank cellar\",\"Mr. Merryweather\'s office\",\"Mr. Wilson\'s house\",\"The police station\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes explains that they wanted to dig a tunnel from Mr. Wilson\'s house to the bank.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the meaning of the word \'object\'?\",\"options\":[\"A physical thing\",\"Purpose or reason\",\"To disagree\",\"A hidden treasure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The vocabulary section at the end of the text defines \'object\' as \'purpose, reason\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the real purpose of the \'Red-headed League\' advertisement?\",\"options\":[\"To help red-headed men find work\",\"To provide a peculiar job to Mr. Wilson\",\"To get Mr. Wilson away from his shop for hours every day\",\"To promote the bank\'s services\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes states it was obvious the object was to get Mr. Wilson away from his shop.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Holmes first become suspicious of the assistant?\",\"options\":[\"Because he had red hair\",\"Because he worked for half the usual wages\",\"Because he was very rude to Mr. Wilson\",\"Because he refused to go into the cellar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes says, \'When I heard that the assistant worked for half the wages, I became suspicious.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What did Holmes infer from the assistant\'s habit of frequently going into the cellar?\",\"options\":[\"He was cleaning the shop\",\"He was hiding stolen gold\",\"He was digging a tunnel to another building\",\"He was sleeping during work hours\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes inferred that the assistant must be digging a tunnel to some other building.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'peculiar\' mean as used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Ordinary\",\"Strange\",\"Difficult\",\"Expensive\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'peculiar\' as \'strange\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did Holmes determine if the tunnel stretched out in front of the shop?\",\"options\":[\"He looked through the window\",\"He asked the assistant directly\",\"He beat upon the pavement with his stick\",\"He used a map of the city\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes mentions he beat the pavement with his stick to see if the cellar stretched in front.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What physical evidence on the assistant\'s clothes confirmed he was digging?\",\"options\":[\"His sleeves were rolled up\",\"His hat was covered in dust\",\"The knees of his trousers were wrinkled and stained\",\"His shoes were missing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Holmes noted that the wrinkled and stained knees of the trousers confirmed his suspicion of digging.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How did Holmes identify that the assistant was the criminal named Clay?\",\"options\":[\"He recognized him from a previous case\",\"He made enquiries using Mr. Wilson\'s description\",\"The assistant confessed his name\",\"Mr. Merryweather told him\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes says he made enquiries based on Mr. Wilson\'s description and found he was Clay.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the location of the bank important to Holmes\'s deduction?\",\"options\":[\"It was located right next to the police station\",\"It was on the other side of Mr. Wilson\'s house\",\"It was the only building with a cellar\",\"It was where Mr. Wilson wanted to work\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes saw the bank on the other side of the house and guessed the criminals\' plan.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the advantage of using a tunnel for the robbery according to Holmes?\",\"options\":[\"It was faster than walking\",\"It allowed them to enter without breaking the bank doors\",\"It was the only way to reach the gold\",\"It kept their clothes clean\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says they could enter and steal gold \'without breaking open the doors of the bank\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Holmes\'s explanation, which sequence of events is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Enquiries -> Suspicion -> Pavement test -> Trousers observation\",\"Suspicion -> Enquiries -> Pavement test -> Trousers observation\",\"Trousers observation -> Pavement test -> Enquiries -> Suspicion\",\"Pavement test -> Suspicion -> Enquiries -> Trousers observation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Holmes first became suspicious of the wages, then made enquiries, then tested the pavement, then saw the trousers.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does Watson\'s exclamation \'You reasoned it out beautifully!\' suggest about Holmes\'s method?\",\"options\":[\"Holmes guessed randomly\",\"Holmes used logical steps and observations to solve the case\",\"Holmes had help from the police\",\"Holmes knew the criminals personally\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Watson\'s admiration for Holmes\'s reasoning highlights the logical, step-by-step nature of the deduction.\",\"topic\":\"The Explanation\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is the word \'tire\' spelt in British and Indian English?\",\"options\":[\"Tiire\",\"Tyre\",\"Tyer\",\"Tier\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'tire\' is spelt as \'tyre\' in British and Indian English.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step mentioned in the procedure to fill air into a bicycle tyre?\",\"options\":[\"Fill in the air as much as required\",\"Check how much air is needed\",\"Check the air pressure by pressing it with your hand\",\"Reduce the air if it is more than necessary\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first step in the procedure is to check the air pressure by pressing the tyre with your hand.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do monthly to maintain the bicycle\'s brake and chain?\",\"options\":[\"Wipe the chain and lube the brake and chain\",\"Replace the brake and chain\",\"Wash them with soap and water\",\"Paint the brake and chain\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The \'Servicing \\/ Maintenance (Monthly)\' section says to wipe the chain and lube (lubricate) the brake and chain.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why should you dip the air-filled tube into a water tub when mending a puncture?\",\"options\":[\"To clean the tube thoroughly\",\"To cool down the hot tube\",\"To see air bubbles coming from the puncture-point\",\"To make the rubber solution stick better\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dipping the tube in water allows air bubbles to surface, identifying the exact puncture-point.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What could happen if you fill more air than required into the bicycle tube?\",\"options\":[\"The bicycle will go faster\",\"The tube may burst\",\"The tyre will become softer\",\"The pedals will be easier to grip\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The manual warns that if you fill air more than required, the tube may burst.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the manual mean by \'Be predictable\' while riding?\",\"options\":[\"Ride so that other drivers can see and predict your movements\",\"Always ride at the exact same speed\",\"Only ride on the same road every day\",\"Tell everyone where you are going before you leave\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines being predictable as riding so other drivers can see you and predict your movements.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the recommended way to check the braking action of the bicycle?\",\"options\":[\"Test the brakes while riding fast\",\"Test the brakes at slow speed\",\"Check the brakes only once a year\",\"Tighten all screws on the brake lever\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure states to familiarize yourself with the braking action by testing the brakes at slow speed.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/096_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which word is used to represent the part of the bicycle one sits on?\",\"options\":[\"Seatpost\",\"Toptube\",\"Saddle\",\"Crank\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the word picture, the word \'SADDLE\' is positioned to form the seat of the bicycle.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/097_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"After marking the puncture-point with a pen, what is the immediate next step in the repair process?\",\"options\":[\"Apply rubber solution\",\"Remove air from the tube\",\"Rub it hard with a file\",\"Paste a small patch\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Step 4 is marking the point, and Step 5 is removing the air from the tube.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a compound word found in the bicycle diagram?\",\"options\":[\"Handlebars\",\"Brake\",\"Pedal\",\"Spoke\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"\'Handlebars\' is a compound word (handle + bars) found in the diagram.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the artist responsible for creating the bicycle word picture?\",\"options\":[\"Balbharati\",\"Aaron Kuehn\",\"Sherlock Holmes\",\"John Watson\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The note below the diagram states that Aaron Kuehn is the name of the artist.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When mending a puncture, why is the puncture-point rubbed hard with a file?\",\"options\":[\"To make the tube look new\",\"To increase the air pressure\",\"To clean the area before applying rubber solution\",\"To make the hole bigger\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Clean up the puncture-point area and rub it hard with a file\' before applying solution.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What safety rule is mentioned regarding other vehicles?\",\"options\":[\"Always race against other vehicles\",\"Never hitch a ride by holding on to another vehicle\",\"Follow other vehicles very closely\",\"Ignore the movements of other vehicles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Riding Safety\' section explicitly says \'Never hitch a ride by holding on to another vehicle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are preparing for a ride and notice your footwear is slippery, which safety instruction are you following by changing them?\",\"options\":[\"Obey all traffic rules\",\"Ride on familiar routes\",\"Wear footwear that will stay on your feet and grip the pedals\",\"Test the brakes at slow speed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The manual advises wearing footwear that stays on the feet and grips the pedals for safety.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following should be checked routinely before every ride to ensure they are tight?\",\"options\":[\"The water tub and pen\",\"Nuts, bolts, screws and other fasteners\",\"The rubber solution and scrap tube\",\"The file and the air-pump\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The manual lists nuts, bolts, screws, and fasteners as items to check routinely before every ride.\",\"topic\":\"Live English !\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21909,41,7,'English','en','4.1 Double Standards','[\"Double standards mean treating two people or situations differently even though they are the same.\",\"In the poem, the speaker (child) says the grown‑up’s actions are wrong, while the grown‑up says they are right.\",\"Key words: stubborn (not changing), firm (making a good decision), clumsy (without skill), terror (trouble), forgivable (not serious).\",\"When the child says ‘I am wrong’ and the grown‑up says ‘I am right’, that is a double standard.\",\"The poem shows that grown‑ups often use harsh words like ‘lazy’, ‘rude’, ‘careless’ to criticize children.\",\"Children’s words in the poem: ‘lazy’, ‘rude’, ‘careless’, ‘wrong’ – these are the words grown‑ups use for the child.\",\"Antonyms of these words are: lazy → active, rude → polite, firm → weak, careless → careful, wrong → right.\",\"The poem asks students to translate words like stubborn, firm, careless, helpless, clumsy, naughty into their mother tongue.\",\"Students are also asked to write speeches: one scolding the child, one explaining why the grown‑up’s action is right.\",\"The exercise ends with a group discussion on whether a grown‑up or a child is always right – a debate on double standards.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what is the child called when they wake up late?\",\"options\":[\"Tired\",\"Lazy\",\"Sleepy\",\"Busy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states \'When I wake up late, I’m getting very lazy.\'\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What word does the poet use to describe the adult when they say \'No\'?\",\"options\":[\"Stubborn\",\"Angry\",\"Firm\",\"Rude\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'When you say ‘No’ to something, you are being firm.\'\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the adult\'s late waking blamed on the alarm clock?\",\"options\":[\"Because the alarm was actually broken\",\"To show that adults are never lazy\",\"To provide an excuse for the adult\",\"Because the child forgot to set it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem uses the \'crazy alarm\' as a way to excuse the adult\'s behavior compared to the child\'s.\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a child raises their voice, how is their behavior characterized in the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Teaching a lesson\",\"Shocking and rude\",\"Being firm\",\"A forgivable error\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem says \'When I raise my voice, it is shocking and rude.\'\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which word is used to describe the child\'s action of breaking something?\",\"options\":[\"Helpless\",\"Slipped\",\"Clumsy\",\"Error\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text in the figure says \'When I break something, I am clumsy or careless.\'\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/098_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, look at the child\'s posture and expression. What does it suggest about their reaction to the adults?\",\"options\":[\"They are happy to be learning\",\"They are upset by the unfairness\",\"They are scared of the adults\",\"They are feeling sleepy and lazy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The child\'s angry expression and the text about double standards suggest they feel the situation is unfair.\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/098_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'teaching me to be good\' imply about the adult\'s shouting?\",\"options\":[\"It is a form of discipline\",\"It is a mistake\",\"It is shocking\",\"It is lazy\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The adult justifies their shouting as a way to teach or discipline the child.\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the poem contrast the child\'s mistakes with the adult\'s mistakes?\",\"options\":[\"Terror vs Forgivable error\",\"Lazy vs Crazy\",\"Stubborn vs Firm\",\"Clumsy vs Rude\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states \'When I do something wrong, I am such a terror. When you do something wrong, it’s a forgivable error.\'\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'forgivable\' suggest about the adult\'s error?\",\"options\":[\"It was a very big mistake\",\"It should be punished\",\"It can be excused easily\",\"It was done on purpose\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Forgivable means something that can be forgiven or excused without harsh judgment.\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an adult drops a glass, how would they explain it according to the poem\'s logic?\",\"options\":[\"They were being careless\",\"They were being a terror\",\"They were being clumsy\",\"They were helpless as it slipped\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The poem says \'When you break something, it slipped; you were helpless.\'\",\"topic\":\"Double Standards (Part 1)\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the definition of an \'error\'?\",\"options\":[\"A good decision\",\"A mistake\",\"A lack of neatness\",\"A person who gives trouble\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines an error as a mistake.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'clumsy\' mean in the context of the text?\",\"options\":[\"Without skill or neatness\",\"Refusing to change one\'s mind\",\"Taking a firm decision\",\"Being very angry\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines clumsy as being without skill or neatness.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the provided text, what is the specific meaning of the word \'terror\'?\",\"options\":[\"A feeling of great fear\",\"A serious mistake\",\"A person who gives great trouble\",\"A decision that cannot be changed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'here, it means a person who gives great trouble.\'\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text distinguish a \'firm\' person from a \'stubborn\' person?\",\"options\":[\"A firm person is clumsy, but a stubborn person is not.\",\"A firm person makes a good decision and sticks to it.\",\"A firm person gives great trouble to others.\",\"A firm person changes their mind frequently.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a firm person as one who takes a good decision and does not change it.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an action is described as \'forgivable\', what does it imply about the situation?\",\"options\":[\"The situation is extremely serious.\",\"The person involved is a terror.\",\"One can stop being angry about it because it is not serious.\",\"The action was done with great skill and neatness.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if something is forgivable, it is not serious and one can stop being angry.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which word describes a person who refuses to change their thoughts or actions even when they are not nice?\",\"options\":[\"Firm\",\"Clumsy\",\"Stubborn\",\"Forgivable\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a stubborn person as one who does not change how he acts or thinks though it may not be nice.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student spills ink all over their notebook because they were not careful, which word from the text best describes this lack of neatness?\",\"options\":[\"Firm\",\"Clumsy\",\"Stubborn\",\"Error\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Clumsy is defined as being without skill or neatness.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A child refuses to eat their vegetables just to be difficult, even though it is not a good choice. This behavior is an example of being:\",\"options\":[\"Firm\",\"Forgivable\",\"Stubborn\",\"Clumsy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Refusing to change a way of acting that is \'not nice\' matches the definition of stubborn.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definitions provided, what is the key difference between a \'stubborn\' act and a \'firm\' act?\",\"options\":[\"The quality of the decision being made.\",\"The amount of neatness involved.\",\"Whether a mistake was made.\",\"How much trouble it causes others.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Firmness involves a \'good decision\', while stubbornness involves acting in a way that \'may not be nice\'.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following would be categorized as an \'error\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A person who causes trouble\",\"A lack of skill\",\"A mistake\",\"A good decision\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides a direct synonym for error: a mistake.\",\"topic\":\"Meanings\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what words are used for the child when they quarrel with a friend?\",\"options\":[\"Wild and naughty\",\"Firm and stubborn\",\"Lazy and rude\",\"Clumsy and helpless\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'When I quarrel with a friend, I am wild and naughty.\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the poem describe a grown-up\'s action when they quarrel with a neighbour?\",\"options\":[\"Being shocking\",\"Doing their duty\",\"Being rude\",\"Being wild\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'When you quarrel with a neighbour, you’re only doing your duty.\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'delight\' as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Anger\",\"Duty\",\"Joy\",\"Right\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines \'* delight : joy\'.\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which situation is mentioned as an example in the English Workshop tasks?\",\"options\":[\"Eating slowly\",\"Waking up late\",\"Playing in the mud\",\"Breaking a glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Task 2 in the workshop mentions \'For example, waking up late.\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the antonyms section, what is the opposite of the word \'wrong\'?\",\"options\":[\"Duty\",\"Right\",\"Firm\",\"Naughty\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem contrasts \'I am always wrong\' with \'you are always right\'.\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the example speech where a grown-up scolds a child for raising their voice, which word is used to describe the behavior?\",\"options\":[\"Rude\",\"Lazy\",\"Clumsy\",\"Stubborn\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The example speech says, \'Don’t shout. It’s rude to shout.\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the grown-up\'s explanation in the example speech, why do they shout?\",\"options\":[\"Because they enjoy shouting\",\"Because they are wild\",\"Because the child makes them shout\",\"Because it is their duty\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The grown-up says, \'I don’t like to shout, but you make me shout at you.\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these words is NOT listed in the translation task (Task 4)?\",\"options\":[\"Stubborn\",\"Helpless\",\"Clumsy\",\"Quarrel\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The translation list includes stubborn, firm, careless, helpless, clumsy, and naughty; \'quarrel\' is not in that specific list.\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main complaint of the child in the poem regarding grown-ups?\",\"options\":[\"They are too lazy\",\"They spoil a child\'s delight\",\"They never quarrel\",\"They are always helpless\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem asks, \'Oh, why do people grow up and spoil a child’s delight?\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the discussion section, what is \'Statement One\' that the groups must discuss?\",\"options\":[\"A child is always right\",\"A grown-up is always right\",\"Quarrelling is bad\",\"Shouting is rude\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Task 6 lists \'Statement One : A grown-up is always right.\'\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the workshop list is the most appropriate antonym for \'careful\'?\",\"options\":[\"Careless\",\"Firm\",\"Rude\",\"Wrong\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Task 3 asks for the antonym of \'careless\', which implies its opposite is \'careful\'.\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the child feel there are \'Double Standards\' based on the poem\'s text?\",\"options\":[\"Because children and adults are treated differently for the same action\",\"Because neighbours are always right\",\"Because friends are always naughty\",\"Because shouting is always duty\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The child notes that when they quarrel it is \'naughty\', but when adults do it, it is \'duty\'.\",\"topic\":\"English Workshop\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21910,41,7,'English','en','4.2 Baby Pangolin’s Night Out','[\"- A pangolin is a mammal that eats termites and ants.\",\"- Pangolins have scales instead of fur and no teeth.\",\"- They use a long sticky tongue to catch insects.\",\"- Mama Pangolin carries her baby on her tail.\",\"- They live in holes in the ground, about 9 inches wide and over a metre long.\",\"- Humans hunt pangolins for their scales, which are used as medicine and sold illegally.\",\"- Conservation groups like Sahyadri Nisarg Mitra work to protect pangolins and their habitat.\",\"- Pangolins are nocturnal; they go out at night.\",\"- They are threatened by habitat loss and poaching.\",\"- The story shows how a mother teaches her baby to eat termites.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is Bhau Katdare as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A famous school teacher\",\"A nature lover and eminent activist\",\"A professional mountain climber\",\"A government official from Chiplun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Bhau Katdare is a nature lover and an eminent activist.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the organization through which Bhau Katdare has striven since 1992?\",\"options\":[\"Maharashtra Nature Club\",\"Chiplun Conservation Group\",\"Sahyadri Nisarg Mitra\",\"Endangered Species Protection Force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he has striven through Sahyadri Nisarg Mitra Organisation.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the Sahyadri Nisarg Mitra Organisation located?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Pune\",\"Chiplun\",\"Nagpur\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the organization\'s location as Chiplun.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific concept that Bhau Katdare wants to make a reality?\",\"options\":[\"Conservation of Nature through Livelihood\",\"Protection of Hills through Tourism\",\"Education of Children through Nature\",\"Saving Animals through Technology\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states he aims to make the concept of \'Conservation of Nature through Livelihood\' a reality.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"For what has Bhau Katdare won many honours and awards?\",\"options\":[\"For writing books about hills\",\"For contribution towards protection and conservation of endangered species\",\"For teaching students about rare animals\",\"For winning competitions in other schools\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he won awards for substantial contribution towards the protection and conservation of various endangered species.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Warming up!\' activities, in which situation should you introduce yourself to \'authorities\'?\",\"options\":[\"When you are visiting a nearby hill with your Aunt\",\"When you meet someone from another country at a tourist spot\",\"When you visit a famous organization to get information about their work\",\"When you lose your way in another school during a competition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests introducing yourself to authorities when visiting a famous organization\\/institute.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you meet a person from another country at a local tourist spot, what two pieces of information does the text suggest you provide?\",\"options\":[\"Your name and your favorite animal\",\"Information about yourself and your school\",\"The way to the nearest hill and your age\",\"Instructions from your mother and your hobby\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The prompt asks to introduce yourself and provide information about your school to the person from another country.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if you and your team lose your way in another school during a competition?\",\"options\":[\"Ask your Aunt for instructions\",\"Find 10 rare animals on the internet\",\"Introduce yourself to the students there and enquire about the way\",\"Contact the Sahyadri Nisarg Mitra Organisation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to introduce yourself to the students of that school and enquire about the way.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions finding names of rare animals specifically seen in which state?\",\"options\":[\"Gujarat\",\"Maharashtra\",\"Madhya Pradesh\",\"Goa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity asks to find names of at least 10 rare animals seen in Maharashtra State.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary goal of Bhau Katdare\'s work since 1992?\",\"options\":[\"To travel to different countries\",\"To protect and conserve endangered species\",\"To give instructions to two-year-old babies\",\"To organize school competitions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"His work is focused on the protection and conservation of various endangered species.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the activity about a human mother, what is the purpose of the instructions she gives?\",\"options\":[\"To teach the baby about rare animals\",\"To guide the baby during the course of the day\",\"To help the baby win a competition\",\"To conserve nature through livelihood\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The instructions are given to the baby \'in the course of the day\', implying daily guidance.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following activities requires the use of external resources like a science textbook or the internet?\",\"options\":[\"Introducing yourself to a foreigner\",\"Asking questions about a hill to your Aunt\",\"Finding names of 10 rare animals in Maharashtra\",\"Writing instructions given by a mother\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says \'From your science textbook and the internet, find the names...\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How old was the Baby Pangolin when Mama Pangolin took him out for ant-eating?\",\"options\":[\"One week old\",\"One month old\",\"Nine months old\",\"One year old\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Mama Pangolin gave a gentle push to her \'one-month old baby\'.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the width of the pangolins\' home as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Nine inches\",\"One metre\",\"Nine feet\",\"One inch\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text describes their home as a snug hole in the earth, \'nine inches wide\'.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Mama Pangolin, how many cousins do they have in total across Africa and Asia?\",\"options\":[\"Four\",\"Six\",\"Eight\",\"Two\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mama explains they have four cousins in Africa and four in Asia, totaling eight.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the Baby Pangolin reluctant to get up and go out at first?\",\"options\":[\"He was afraid of the porcupine\",\"He wanted to keep drinking milk\",\"He did not want to walk all the way\",\"He was not hungry for ants\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Baby Pangolin says, \'I don’t want to walk all the way!\' when Mama asks him to get ready.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'cautiously\' mean in the context of Mama Pangolin’s movement?\",\"options\":[\"Very quickly\",\"Carefully\",\"Noisily\",\"Lazily\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'cautiously\' as \'carefully\' in the vocabulary notes provided at the end.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was Baby Pangolin tired of doing, which he had complained about earlier?\",\"options\":[\"Sleeping all day\",\"Walking in the hole\",\"Drinking milk all the time\",\"Holding onto Mama\'s scales\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mama reminds him, \'you say you are tired of drinking milk all the time.\'\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a researcher finds a pangolin in the northern part of India, which species is it most likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"African Pangolin\",\"Indian Pangolin\",\"Chinese Pangolin\",\"Porcupine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the northern part of India, their cousins \'Chinese Pangolins\' are seen.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does Baby Pangolin ensure he stays safely on Mama\'s tail while she walks?\",\"options\":[\"By tying his tail to hers\",\"By holding on to her scales with his claws\",\"By sitting in a small basket\",\"By wrapping his legs around her neck\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mama cautioned Baby to \'sit tight and hold on to her scales with his claws.\'\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the physical shape of a pangolin\'s mouth?\",\"options\":[\"Short and round\",\"Wide and flat\",\"Long and tapering\",\"Small and square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Mama Pangolin pushed the baby with her \'long tapering mouth\'.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the pangolins\' daily routine from the opening paragraph?\",\"options\":[\"They are active during the day and sleep at night\",\"They are active at night and sleep during the day\",\"They never sleep and eat all the time\",\"They only go out when it is raining\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says Baby was \'drowsy after a day’s sleep\' and the title is \'Night Out\', implying they go out at night.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does Mama Pangolin solve the problem of Baby\'s laziness and refusal to walk?\",\"options\":[\"She tells him he cannot have any ants\",\"She carries him on her tail\",\"She makes him stay home alone\",\"She carries him in her mouth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To address his worry about walking, Mama says, \'I’ll carry you on my tail.\'\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal is the first creature the pangolins encounter after leaving their home?\",\"options\":[\"A Chinese Pangolin\",\"An African Pangolin\",\"A porcupine\",\"An ant\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Just then, a porcupine went past them\' right after they left the home.\",\"topic\":\"Baby Pangolin’s Night Out\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Mama Pangolin use to demolish the anthill?\",\"options\":[\"Her long sticky tongue\",\"Her strong, sharp and curved front claws\",\"Her heavy tail\",\"Her small pointed teeth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How long is Mother Pangolin\'s tongue according to the passage?\",\"options\":[\"One foot long\",\"Two feet long\",\"One and a half foot long\",\"Half a foot long\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'devour\' mean in the context of the story?\",\"options\":[\"To play with food\",\"To eat slowly and carefully\",\"To eat quickly and a lot at a time\",\"To share food with others\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines devour as \'eat quickly and a lot at a time\'.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do pangolins eat termites as their proper food?\",\"options\":[\"Because they don\'t have teeth like other animals\",\"Because termites are easy to find\",\"Because they like the taste of wood\",\"Because their claws are too sharp for other food\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mother tells Baby that they eat this food because they don\'t have teeth like other animals.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a baby pangolin is only two weeks old, what would it most likely be eating?\",\"options\":[\"Termites\",\"Anthills\",\"Mother\'s milk\",\"Solid food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the baby was nursed on mother\'s milk for a month before its first solid meal.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How did the termites react when the anthill was demolished?\",\"options\":[\"They attacked the pangolins\",\"They began to run helter-skelter\",\"They hid deep inside the ground\",\"They stayed still in the anthill\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the termites inside began to run helter-skelter.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"For how long was the baby pangolin nursed on mother\'s milk before having its first solid meal?\",\"options\":[\"One week\",\"One month\",\"One year\",\"One day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions this was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which description best fits the pangolin\'s tongue based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Short and dry\",\"Sharp and curved\",\"Long and sticky\",\"Strong and hard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the tongue as \'one and a half foot long\' and \'sticky\'.\",\"topic\":\"A Delicious Meal\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understa