111\",\"6345\",\"2619\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Train 6345: 22.55 (Madgaon) - 18.45 (Ratnagiri) = 4 hours and 10 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"Activity : Live English!\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which station is the last one listed with a specific kilometer distance in the figure?\",\"options\":[\"Mangalore\",\"Suratkal\",\"Udupi\",\"Mulki\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Suratkal is the last station shown with a distance (1028 km) in the km column.\",\"topic\":\"Activity : Live English!\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the upward-pointing arrow next to train numbers 0104, 0112, 6346, and 2620 indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The train is going towards Mumbai\",\"The train is going towards Mangaluru\",\"The train is climbing a hill\",\"The train is faster than others\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The arrows indicate the direction of travel; these trains move from the bottom stations (like Madgaon\\/Mangalore) toward Mumbai.\",\"topic\":\"Activity : Live English!\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are verbs that do not need objects known as?\",\"options\":[\"Transitive verbs\",\"Intransitive verbs\",\"Action verbs\",\"Subjective verbs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that verbs which do not have any objects are known as intransitive verbs.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'She answers all the questions\', what is the grammatical role of \'all the questions\'?\",\"options\":[\"Subject\",\"Verb\",\"Object\",\"Adjective\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text labels \'She\' as the subject, \'answers\' as the verb, and \'all the questions\' as the object.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following verbs is categorized as \'intransitive\' in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"writing\",\"answers\",\"ate\",\"smiles\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'smiles\' (smile) as a verb that does not have an object and is therefore intransitive.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common structure of the transitive verb sentences provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Subject + Verb\",\"Verb + Object\",\"Subject + Verb + Object\",\"Subject + Object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the structure \'subject verb object\' for all transitive verb examples.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'The shell hit the animal\', which word is the object?\",\"options\":[\"The shell\",\"hit\",\"the animal\",\"animal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In this sentence, \'the animal\' is the entity receiving the action of the verb \'hit\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the verb in the sentence \'Birds fly\' considered intransitive?\",\"options\":[\"It has a plural subject.\",\"It does not have an object.\",\"It describes a physical action.\",\"It is a very short sentence.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that verbs like \'fly\' are intransitive because they do not have any objects.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these sentences contains a transitive verb?\",\"options\":[\"The egg fell down.\",\"The balloon rose in the sky.\",\"The dog ate the biscuit.\",\"We stood mute and motionless.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'ate\' in \'The dog ate the biscuit\' as a transitive verb because it has an object.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the subject in the sentence \'I was observing this phenomenal creature\'.\",\"options\":[\"I\",\"observing\",\"phenomenal\",\"creature\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the sentence structure provided by the text, the person or thing performing the action is the subject.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the verbs listed for the \'Two minutes of Oral Work\'?\",\"options\":[\"make\",\"catch\",\"jump\",\"keep\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The list includes make, catch, grow, reach, take, get, go, and keep; \'jump\' is not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of these phrases is NOT shown as a word that goes with the central verb?\",\"options\":[\"by bus\",\"to sleep\",\"to school\",\"mad\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows \'by bus\', \'to sleep\', and \'mad\', but \'to school\' is not present in the diagram.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/114_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which pair of opposite directions is associated with the verb \'go\'?\",\"options\":[\"up \\/ down\",\"right \\/ left\",\"in \\/ out\",\"fast \\/ slow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure explicitly lists \'right \\/ left\' as words that go with the verb \'go\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"41\\/114_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Evaluate the sentence \'The balloon rose in the sky\' based on the text. Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[\"It has a transitive verb because \'in the sky\' is the object.\",\"It has an intransitive verb because it does not have an object.\",\"It has no subject.\",\"It is a transitive sentence because it has a verb.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'rose\' as an intransitive verb that does not have an object.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21912,41,7,'English','en','4.4 A Parody','[\"A parody is a humorous imitation of a writer’s style or a well‑known text.\",\"The word *doth* is an old form of *does* used in poetry.\",\"Parodies often keep the rhythm or structure of the original but change the meaning for comic effect.\",\"In a sentence, a transitive verb takes a direct object (O1) and may also have an indirect object (O2).\",\"An intransitive verb does not take an object; it can stand alone or with a prepositional phrase.\",\"Examples: *She paid fifty rupees to the shopkeeper* – *paid* is transitive, *to the shopkeeper* is indirect object.\",\"*Birds fly* – *fly* is intransitive; *She flew the kite* – *flew* is transitive.\",\"Transitive verbs can be identified by asking “what?” or “whom?” after the verb.\",\"Intransitive verbs answer “how?” or “where?” but not “what?” or “whom?”.\",\"In the book, verbs like *woke*, *smelled*, *rolled*, *rang*, *bounced*, *jumped*, *caught* are used in both transitive and intransitive forms.\",\"A verb is transitive if it can be followed by a noun phrase that is the direct object.\",\"A verb is intransitive if it cannot be followed by a noun phrase that is the direct object.\",\"When a verb has two objects, the first is the direct object and the second is the indirect object.\",\"The book uses the example *Mrs Desai teaches us English* – *teaches* is transitive, *us* is indirect object.\",\"The book also shows *She paid fifty rupees to the shopkeeper* – *paid* is transitive, *fifty rupees* is direct object, *to the shopkeeper* is indirect object.\",\"In the oral work, students choose words from two columns and make sentences using one word from each column.\",\"The structure of a sentence is: subject – verb – object1 – object2.\",\"The book encourages students to translate poems into their mother tongue to practice language skills.\",\"Parody can be used to teach humor and creativity in writing.\",\"Understanding transitive vs. intransitive verbs helps in constructing correct sentences.\",\"The book includes both descriptive and problem‑solving exercises to reinforce learning.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the poem \'How doth the little crocodile\'?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Watts\",\"Lewis Carroll\",\"William Wordsworth\",\"Robert Frost\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly attributes the crocodile poem to Lewis Carroll.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From which river does the crocodile pour water onto his scales?\",\"options\":[\"The Amazon\",\"The Ganges\",\"The Nile\",\"The Thames\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions the crocodile pours the \'waters of the Nile\'.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the first poem, what does the bee gather all day?\",\"options\":[\"Wax\",\"Water\",\"Honey\",\"Flowers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the bee \'gathers honey all the day\'.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the bee use to build its cell and store food?\",\"options\":[\"Mud\",\"Wax\",\"Leaves\",\"Honey\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem describes how the bee \'neatly spreads her wax\' to build her cell.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the \'sweet food\' that the bee labors to store?\",\"options\":[\"Nectar\",\"Sugar\",\"Pollen\",\"Honey\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In the context of the bee gathering honey, the \'sweet food\' refers to honey.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the poem by Lewis Carroll, how does the crocodile appear to be feeling?\",\"options\":[\"Angry\",\"Cheerful\",\"Sad\",\"Tired\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'How cheerfully he seems to grin\'.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is described as \'improving each shining hour\' like the bee, what are they doing?\",\"options\":[\"Sleeping during the day\",\"Making good use of their time\",\"Playing with flowers\",\"Cleaning their room\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To \'improve each shining hour\' means to use time productively.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the crocodile\'s \'golden scale\', what is the effect of the Nile water on him?\",\"options\":[\"It makes him look dirty\",\"It makes him look bright and shiny\",\"It makes him look scary\",\"It makes him turn yellow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pouring water on \'golden scales\' suggests making them look bright and improved.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the actual purpose of the crocodile\'s \'gently smiling jaws\'?\",\"options\":[\"To talk to the fishes\",\"To show he is happy\",\"To catch and eat the fishes\",\"To drink the Nile water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Welcoming fishes into \'smiling jaws\' is a predatory action described in a deceptive way.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which word in the second poem suggests that the crocodile\'s friendly appearance might be deceptive?\",\"options\":[\"Little\",\"Seems\",\"Golden\",\"Gently\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The word \'seems\' indicates that his cheerful grin might not reflect his true intentions.\",\"topic\":\"How doth the little crocodile\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a parody?\",\"options\":[\"A serious poem about nature\",\"A playful, comic imitation of a writer’s style\",\"A long story about history\",\"A collection of difficult words\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a parody as a playful, comic imitation of a writer’s style.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the old form \'doth\'?\",\"options\":[\"did\",\"done\",\"does\",\"doing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'doth\' is the old form of \'does\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A parody is described as being like a ______.\",\"options\":[\"verbal cartoon\",\"silent movie\",\"painted portrait\",\"musical song\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a parody is like a verbal cartoon.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of verbs can sometimes have two objects?\",\"options\":[\"Intransitive verbs\",\"Helping verbs\",\"Transitive verbs\",\"Action verbs only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Some transitive verbs can have two objects\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the study of grammar provided, what does O₁ represent?\",\"options\":[\"The indirect object\",\"The direct object\",\"The main verb\",\"The subject\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that Object 1 (O₁) is the direct object.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is Object 2 (O₂) called in a sentence with two objects?\",\"options\":[\"Direct object\",\"Subject object\",\"Indirect object\",\"Transitive object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that Object 2 (O₂) is the indirect object.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'They gave him a book\', which word is the direct object (O₁)?\",\"options\":[\"They\",\"gave\",\"him\",\"a book\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Following the example \'subject verb object1, object2\', \'a book\' is O₁.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'Grandmother told us a story\', which word is the indirect object (O₂)?\",\"options\":[\"Grandmother\",\"told\",\"us\",\"a story\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the provided example, \'us\' is labeled as the second object (O₂), which is the indirect object.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the subject in the sentence: \'Asmita wrote a letter to her Aunt.\'\",\"options\":[\"Asmita\",\"wrote\",\"a letter\",\"her Aunt\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the sentence structure provided in the text, the first part is the subject.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the patterns shown, which part of the sentence \'Asmita wrote a letter to her Aunt\' is the direct object (O₁)?\",\"options\":[\"Asmita\",\"wrote\",\"a letter\",\"her Aunt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the example \'Asmita wrote a letter to her Aunt\', \'a letter\' is positioned as the first object (O₁).\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Two Objects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'Birds fly\', which word is the verb?\",\"options\":[\"Birds\",\"fly\",\"The\",\"None of these\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the sentence \'Birds fly\', the action word \'fly\' is the verb.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'We fly kites\', what is the word \'kites\'?\",\"options\":[\"The subject\",\"The verb\",\"The object\",\"An adjective\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In \'We fly kites\', \'kites\' is the object receiving the action of the verb.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what can we learn about verbs?\",\"options\":[\"A verb is always transitive.\",\"A verb is always intransitive.\",\"The same verb can be transitive in some sentences and intransitive in others.\",\"Verbs cannot change their type.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the same verb can be both transitive and intransitive depending on the sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'The saucer broke\', the verb \'broke\' is used as a\\/an:\",\"options\":[\"Transitive verb\",\"Intransitive verb\",\"Noun\",\"Subject\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In \'The saucer broke\', there is no object receiving the action, making it intransitive.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these sentences uses the verb \'boiled\' transitively?\",\"options\":[\"The water boiled.\",\"She boiled the water.\",\"The water was hot.\",\"Boiling water is dangerous.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In \'She boiled the water\', the verb \'boiled\' has an object \'the water\', making it transitive.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the sentence where the verb \'fly\' is intransitive.\",\"options\":[\"We fly kites.\",\"Birds fly.\",\"They fly airplanes.\",\"I fly a drone.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In \'Birds fly\', the verb does not have an object, so it is intransitive.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare \'Neha broke the saucer\' and \'The saucer broke\'. What is the difference in the use of the verb \'broke\'?\",\"options\":[\"In the first it is intransitive, in the second it is transitive.\",\"In both sentences, the verb is transitive.\",\"In both sentences, the verb is intransitive.\",\"In the first it is transitive, in the second it is intransitive.\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The first sentence has an object (the saucer), while the second does not.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the verb in \'The water boiled\' considered intransitive?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has no object.\",\"Because \'water\' is the object.\",\"Because \'boiled\' is a past tense verb.\",\"Because the sentence is short.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A verb is intransitive when the action does not pass over to an object.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the examples, who is the subject in the sentence \'Neha broke the saucer\'?\",\"options\":[\"Neha\",\"broke\",\"the\",\"saucer\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Neha is the person performing the action in the sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines if the verb \'boiled\' is transitive or intransitive in the given text?\",\"options\":[\"The spelling of the verb.\",\"Whether the verb is followed by an object like \'the water\'.\",\"The person who is speaking.\",\"The length of the sentence.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The presence or absence of an object determines the type of verb usage.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29');
INSERT INTO `ep_topic_packs` VALUES (21913,41,7,'English','en','4.5 From The Selfish Giant','[\"The Selfish Giant is a short story by Oscar Wilde.\",\"The giant owns a beautiful garden but keeps children out.\",\"He builds a wall and puts up a notice: \\\"Trespassers will be prosecuted.\\\"\",\"The children play elsewhere and miss the garden.\",\"Winter, snow, frost, north wind, and hail stay in the garden because no children are there.\",\"Spring, summer, and autumn do not visit the garden because the giant is selfish.\",\"The giant eventually sees the children again and feels remorse.\",\"He removes the wall and invites the children to play.\",\"The story shows the moral that selfishness keeps joy away and sharing brings happiness.\",\"Personification is used: seasons and weather are described as characters.\",\"The giant’s heart melts when he sees the children again, symbolising change of heart.\",\"The story ends with the giant playing with the children in the garden.\",\"Key vocabulary: trespassers, prosecute, selfish, winter, spring, autumn, summer, frost, snow, north wind, hail, linnet.\",\"Key ideas: sharing, kindness, consequences of selfishness, transformation.\",\"The story is often read in class to discuss morals and character development.\",\"The giant’s garden is a metaphor for a heart that can be opened or closed.\",\"The story is part of a longer tale; students can read the full version for deeper understanding.\",\"The play activity helps students practise dialogue and stage directions.\",\"The exercise asks students to list weather words and group them.\",\"The exercise also asks students to write a thank‑you note to the giant.\",\"The exercise encourages discussion about the narrator and personification.\",\"The story teaches that kindness brings spring and joy into a life.\",\"The giant’s wall is a symbol of barriers people build when they are selfish.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was Oscar Wilde\'s nationality according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"English\",\"Irish\",\"American\",\"Scottish\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states Oscar Wilde was an Irish playwright, novelist, essayist and a poet.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a profession of Oscar Wilde?\",\"options\":[\"Playwright\",\"Novelist\",\"Scientist\",\"Poet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he was a playwright, novelist, essayist, and poet, but not a scientist.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What genre of story is \'The Selfish Giant\'?\",\"options\":[\"Historical biography\",\"Short fantasy story\",\"Scientific report\",\"Mystery novel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes \'The Selfish Giant\' as a short fantasy story for children.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the Giant build a wall around his garden?\",\"options\":[\"To protect the flowers from the wind\",\"To keep children out\",\"To mark his territory for the neighbors\",\"To make the garden look beautiful\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the story revolves around a giant who builds a wall to keep children out of his garden.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Giant learn from the innocence of the children?\",\"options\":[\"How to build stronger walls\",\"Compassion\",\"How to grow a better garden\",\"Selfishness\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the Giant learns compassion from the innocence of the children.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Warming up!\' activity, what are students asked to make a list of?\",\"options\":[\"Names of different giants\",\"Things they share and things they do not share\",\"Books written by Oscar Wilde\",\"Different types of garden plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions say to \'Make a list of things that you usually share with others and another of things which you do not usually share.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, what is the alternative to practicing alone?\",\"options\":[\"Sharing your tiffin\",\"Playing in a team\",\"Telling news to others\",\"Teaching others\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text pairs \'You play in a team\' with \'You practise alone\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you follow the activity\'s suggestion, what should you do after learning to make something new?\",\"options\":[\"Keep it a secret\",\"Teach it to others\",\"Write it in a diary\",\"Sell the idea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks how you feel when \'You learn to make something new. You teach it to others.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the central character transformation described in the summary of the story?\",\"options\":[\"A child becoming a giant\",\"A giant learning compassion\",\"A poet becoming a novelist\",\"A garden turning into a wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes a giant who builds a wall but eventually learns compassion.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The activity contrasts \'eating all by yourself\' with which action?\",\"options\":[\"Sharing your tiffin with others\",\"Playing in a team\",\"Telling news to others\",\"Reading something nice\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'You share your tiffin with others\' as the opposite of \'You eat all by yourself.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity prompts, what is the main theme being explored before reading the story?\",\"options\":[\"The life of Irish authors\",\"The psychology of building walls\",\"The experience of sharing versus keeping things to oneself\",\"The history of fantasy literature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity focuses on sharing tiffin, news, skills, and play, reflecting the theme of the story.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Oscar Wilde was an essayist and a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Musician\",\"Painter\",\"Poet\",\"Dancer\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first sentence of the text lists \'poet\' as one of his roles.\",\"topic\":\"Warming up!\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the children go to play every afternoon after school?\",\"options\":[\"In the school playground\",\"In the Cornish Ogre\'s garden\",\"In the giant’s garden\",\"In the village park\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that every afternoon, children used to go and play in the giant’s garden.\",\"topic\":\"The Giant\'s Garden\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many peach trees were there in the giant\'s garden?\",\"options\":[\"Seven\",\"Ten\",\"Twelve\",\"Fifteen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions there were twelve peach trees in the garden.\",\"topic\":\"The Giant\'s Garden\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to the peach trees during the springtime?\",\"options\":[\"They bore rich fruit\",\"They broke out into pink and pearl blossoms\",\"They lost all their leaves\",\"They turned into stars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in the springtime the trees broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl.\",\"topic\":\"The Giant\'s Garden\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the children often stop their games in the garden?\",\"options\":[\"To listen to the birds singing\",\"To pick the beautiful flowers\",\"To hide from the giant\",\"To eat the rich fruit\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says the birds sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games to listen to them.\",\"topic\":\"The Giant\'s Garden\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a child visited the garden in the autumn, what would 