er do after seeing the notice-board?\",\"options\":[\"It bloomed brighter\",\"It called the Spring\",\"It slipped back into the ground to sleep\",\"It asked the North Wind to leave\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the flower was so sorry for the children that it slipped its head back into the ground and went to sleep.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which two characters were pleased that Spring had forgotten the garden?\",\"options\":[\"The Giant and the Children\",\"The Snow and the Frost\",\"The North Wind and the Hail\",\"The Autumn and the Summer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the only people who were pleased were the Snow and the Frost.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How did the Frost change the appearance of the trees?\",\"options\":[\"He made them lose their leaves\",\"He painted them silver\",\"He broke their branches\",\"He covered them in white cloaks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the Frost painted all the trees silver.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the North Wind wearing when he arrived at the garden?\",\"options\":[\"A grey suit\",\"A silver coat\",\"Furs\",\"A white cloak\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the North Wind as being \'wrapped in furs\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What damage did the North Wind cause in the garden?\",\"options\":[\"He broke the slates on the roof\",\"He killed the blossoms\",\"He blew the chimney-pots down\",\"He froze the golden fruit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the North Wind roared all day and blew the chimney-pots down.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"For how long did the Hail rattle on the roof of the castle each day?\",\"options\":[\"All day long\",\"One hour\",\"Three hours\",\"Until the sun set\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the Hail rattled on the roof for three hours every day.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The author describes the Hail as having breath like ice. Which literary device is being used here?\",\"options\":[\"Alliteration\",\"Personification\",\"Rhyme\",\"Metaphor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that describing elements of nature as persons is known as personification.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the Autumn refuse to give golden fruit to the Giant\'s garden?\",\"options\":[\"Because it was too cold\",\"Because the Giant was too selfish\",\"Because the Hail broke the trees\",\"Because the Snow covered the ground\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Autumn says, \'He is too selfish,\' and therefore gives no fruit to his garden.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the Giant\'s reaction as he looked out at his cold, white garden?\",\"options\":[\"He was happy to have the Snow\",\"He was angry at the North Wind\",\"He could not understand why Spring was late\",\"He decided to invite the children back\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the Giant sat in the window and said, \'I cannot understand why the Spring is so late in coming\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the descriptions of the North Wind and Hail, how are these elements portrayed?\",\"options\":[\"As gentle and quiet visitors\",\"As helpful friends of the Giant\",\"As destructive and harsh characters\",\"As sad and lonely spirits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The North Wind blows down chimney-pots and the Hail breaks roof slates, showing their destructive nature.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the Snow is described as wearing a \'great white cloak\', what is this cloak actually representing in nature?\",\"options\":[\"A layer of ice\",\"A layer of fallen snow on the ground\",\"The clouds in the sky\",\"The silver frost on trees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the context of personification, the \'cloak\' represents the snow covering the grass.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason the garden remains in a state of \'Eternal Winter\'?\",\"options\":[\"The North Wind refused to leave\",\"The Giant\'s selfishness drove away the Spring\",\"The Hail broke the castle roof\",\"The children preferred playing on the road\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the absence of Spring and Autumn\'s fruit directly to the Giant being \'too selfish\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which element is described as being \'dressed in grey\'?\",\"options\":[\"The Snow\",\"The Frost\",\"The North Wind\",\"The Hail\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Hail \'was dressed in grey\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Eternal Winter\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the Giant first think the lovely music was?\",\"options\":[\"A little linnet singing\",\"The king’s musicians passing by\",\"The North Wind roaring\",\"Children laughing in the garden\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the Giant thought it must be the king’s musicians passing by because the music sounded so sweet.\",\"topic\":\"The Return of Spring\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was actually making the \'lovely music\' outside the Giant\'s window?\",\"options\":[\"A nightingale\",\"A group of children\",\"A little linnet\",\"The waving trees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text clarifies that it was really only a little linnet singing outside his window.\",\"topic\":\"The Return of Spring\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the bird\'s song seem like the most beautiful music in the world to the Giant?\",\"o