,\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that they get camouflaged because their colours blend with those of their surroundings.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is Darwin\'s first principle?\",\"options\":[\"Theory of natural selection\",\"Theory of sudden change\",\"Theory of survival of the fittest\",\"Theory of geographical diversity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the theory of survival of the fittest is Darwin\'s first principle.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Adaptation is described in the text as a process that is ______.\",\"options\":[\"Sudden and fast\",\"Gradual and continuous\",\"Random and temporary\",\"Instant and fixed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Always remember\' section specifies that adaptation is not sudden; it is gradual and continuous.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a butterfly is found amidst colourful flowers and cannot be easily seen, this is an example of ______.\",\"options\":[\"Classification\",\"Natural selection\",\"Camouflage\",\"Evolution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that organisms get camouflaged amidst parts of plants like flowers when their colours blend.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a new beneficial characteristic that helps an organism survive, according to Darwin\'s second principle?\",\"options\":[\"It disappears in the next generation\",\"It is preserved in the next generation\",\"It changes into a different trait\",\"It stays only with the parent organism\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Darwin\'s second principle states that if a beneficial change helps survival, it is preserved in the next generation.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The differences in structure between present-day animals and those from thousands of years ago are called ______.\",\"options\":[\"Mutations\",\"Habitats\",\"Adaptations\",\"Diversity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines these long-term structural differences as adaptations that occurred according to prevailing conditions.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the definition of adaptation, changes occur in various organs and ______ of organisms.\",\"options\":[\"Geographical conditions\",\"Life-processes\",\"Social structures\",\"Physical boundaries\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines adaptations as changes in various organs and life-processes of organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Darwin\'s theory of \'natural selection\' is also known as his ______.\",\"options\":[\"First principle\",\"Second principle\",\"Third principle\",\"Final principle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the theory of natural selection is Darwin\'s second principle.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is our duty regarding the diversity of the living world?\",\"options\":[\"To study it only\",\"To change it\",\"To conserve it\",\"To ignore it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says, \'It is our duty to conserve this diversity.\'\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the scientist shown in the given figure.\",\"options\":[\"Albert Einstein\",\"Charles Darwin\",\"Louis Pasteur\",\"Isaac Newton\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure is labeled under \'Great Scientists\' and the text discusses Charles Darwin\'s theories.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/018_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The scientist in the given figure suggested that only those organisms are likely to survive which can ______.\",\"options\":[\"Best adapt themselves to a changing environment\",\"Grow larger than their enemies\",\"Live for thousands of years\",\"Move to a different habitat\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the figure of Charles Darwin states he suggested survival for those who best adapt to a changing environment.\",\"topic\":\"Blending and Darwin\'s Theory\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/018_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Who introduced the system of binomial nomenclature?\",\"options\":[\"Charles Darwin\",\"Carl Linnaeus\",\"Aristotle\",\"Gregor Mendel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that binomial nomenclature was introduced by Carl Linnaeus.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason why living things are classified into groups?\",\"options\":[\"To give them shorter names\",\"To make them look organized in books\",\"Because it is difficult to study and remember all organisms at once\",\"To ensure all animals have the same habitat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Classification helps because it is difficult to study and remember all the organisms in the diverse living world at the same time.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If there are four students named \'Kiran\' in a class, how does the textbook suggest we identify a specific one without confusion?\",\"options\":[\"By using their roll number\",\"By using their first name and last name\",\"By their height and weight\",\"By their favorite subject\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook uses the analogy of telling the full name (first and last name) to ensure there is no confusion, similar to binomial nomenclature.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientific name consists of two parts. What does the first part represent?\",\"options\":[\"Species\",\"Family\",\"Order\",\"Genus\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a scientific name consists of two parts – the first part is \'genus\'.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the hierarchy of classification for Mango, which Phylum does it belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Plantae\",\"Anthophyta\",\"Dicotyledonae\",\"Sapindales\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The hierarchy table for Mango lists \'Anthophyta\' under the category of Phylum.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the defining characteristic of all organisms belonging to the same \'species\'?\",\"options\":[\"They must have the exact same color and height\",\"They must live in the same geographical habitat\",\"They can reproduce among themselves and form new individuals like themselves\",\"They must have the same eating habits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines species as organisms so similar that they can reproduce among themselves and form new individuals like themselves.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the hierarchy table provided for Humans, to which \'Family\' do we belong?\",\"options\":[\"Mammalia\",\"Primates\",\"Hominidae\",\"Homo\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The hierarchy table for Humans lists \'Hominidae\' as the Family.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the scientific name \'Mangifera indica\', which word represents the species?\",\"options\":[\"Mangifera\",\"indica\",\"Mango\",\"Plantae\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In binomial nomenclature, the second part is the species; for Mango, the table shows the species is \'indica\'.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is \'World Frog Protection Day\' observed according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"17th April\",\"29th April\",\"5th June\",\"22nd March\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that 17th April is observed as ‘World Frog Protection Day’.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The hierarchy of classification forms groups and sub-groups based on which criteria?\",\"options\":[\"Only the color of the organism\",\"Basic similarities and differences\",\"The alphabetical order of their names\",\"The age of the scientist who discovered them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that groups and sub-groups are formed depending upon basic similarities and differences.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a new plant is discovered that belongs to the same genus as Jowar, what would be the first part of its scientific name?\",\"options\":[\"bicolor\",\"Sorghum\",\"Jowar\",\"Plantae\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the genus of Jowar is Sorghum (from Sorghum bicolor), any plant in the same genus would share that first name.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific law prohibits the killing or harming of frogs?\",\"options\":[\"The Animal Welfare Act\",\"The Forest Conservation Act\",\"The Wild Life Protection Act\",\"The Environmental Protection Act\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that killing or harming frogs is prohibited by the Wild Life Protection Act.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the scientific name assigned to the organism \'Cow\'?\",\"options\":[\"Canis lupus familiaris\",\"Hibiscus rosa-sinensis\",\"Sorghum bicolor\",\"Bos taurus\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists \'Bos taurus\' as the scientific name for Cow.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/019_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the organism shown is protected by law. How are all domestic cats in the world related to each other in a similar classification context?\",\"options\":[\"They belong to different kingdoms\",\"They belong to the same species\",\"They have different scientific names based on color\",\"They belong to different genera\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses cats as an example to explain that all domestic cats in the world belong to the same species.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/019_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why are organisms like hen, cow, and dog grouped into Kingdom Animalia while mango and wheat are in Kingdom Plantae?\",\"options\":[\"Based on their binomial names\",\"Based on the guidelines of the International Code of Nomenclature\",\"Based on the hierarchy of classification starting with basic similarities\",\"Because they were classified by different scientists\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The hierarchy starts with Kingdom Animalia or Plantae based on basic similarities and differences.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of living organisms\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21987,46,7,'Science','en','Plants : Structure and Function','[\"Roots are the part of a plant that grows below the soil and absorb water and minerals.\",\"The main types of roots are tap roots (single thick root) and fibrous roots (many thin roots).\",\"Tap roots grow straight down and are common in dicotyledonous plants like peas and beans.\",\"Fibrous roots spread out horizontally and are common in monocotyledonous plants like grasses and corn.\",\"Roots have root hairs near the tips that increase surface area for absorption.\",\"The root tip is protected by a root cap, a thin, cap‑like structure.\",\"Adventitious roots grow from stems or leaves and can be aerial or breathing roots.\",\"The stem is the main support of a plant and bears leaves, flowers and fruits.\",\"Nodes are points on the stem where leaves or branches emerge; internodes are the segments between nodes.\",\"Leaves are produced from nodes and have a blade (lamina) and a petiole (stalk).\",\"Leaf margins can be entire, dentate (toothed) or lobed.\",\"Simple leaves have one undivided blade; compound leaves have several leaflets.\",\"Leaf venation can be parallel (as in maize) or reticulate (as in peepal).\",\"Flowers have a calyx (sepals), corolla (petals), androecium (male part) and gynoecium (female part).\",\"Pollination is the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma, leading to fertilisation.\",\"Fruits develop from the ovary after fertilisation and contain seeds.\",\"Seeds can be dicotyledonous (two seed leaves) or monocotyledonous (one seed leaf).\",\"Plants use roots for anchorage, absorption, storage and sometimes for special functions like breathing or support.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which part of the plant grows from inside the seed towards the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Plumule\",\"Radicle\",\"Stem\",\"Leaf\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the part that grows from inside the seed towards the soil is called the radicle.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the part that grows above the soil during seed germination?\",\"options\":[\"Root\",\"Radicle\",\"Plumule\",\"Taper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the part that grows above the soil is called the plumule.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following are mentioned as plant organs used to identify plants?\",\"options\":[\"Only roots and stems\",\"Only leaves and flowers\",\"Root, stem, leaves, flowers, and fruits\",\"Soil, water, and seeds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the root, stem, leaves, flowers, and fruits as various parts (organs) of plants.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the root change in shape as it grows deeper into the ground?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes thicker at the end\",\"It stays the same thickness throughout\",\"It gradually tapers to a pointed end\",\"It becomes wider and flat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the root as thick near the ground and gradually tapering to a pointed end.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the primary purpose of the part of the plant growing below the soil?\",\"options\":[\"To produce flowers\",\"For support\",\"To grow leaves\",\"To attract insects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the root as the part of the plant growing below the soil for support.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which label identifies the part that will eventually form the root system?\",\"options\":[\"Plumule\",\"Radicle\",\"Seed coat\",\"Soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the radicle growing downwards, which the text explains forms the root.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/020_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to observe the formation of a root, how many days should they wait after placing the seeds in the flask?\",\"options\":[\"One hour\",\"Two or three days\",\"Two weeks\",\"One month\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment instructions say to observe and note the changes in the seeds in two or three days.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student is setting up the experiment mentioned in the text. Where should they place the soaked moth beans for clear observation?\",\"options\":[\"Inside the crumpled paper\",\"At the bottom of the flask under the paper\",\"Between the paper and the glass wall\",\"Outside the flask on the table\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to place the seeds between the paper and the glass wall for observation.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of root formation, which part of the embryo must be healthy for a plant to develop its underground support system?\",\"options\":[\"The plumule\",\"The leaves\",\"The radicle\",\"The fruit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the root forms from the radicle, so the radicle is essential for the root system.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the main difference between the growth direction of the Plumule and the Radicle?\",\"options\":[\"Both grow downwards into the soil\",\"The Plumule grows upwards while the Radicle grows downwards\",\"Both grow upwards towards the light\",\"The Plumule grows sideways and the Radicle grows upwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show the plumule growing above the soil (upwards) and the radicle growing towards the soil (downwards).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/020_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a \'moistened\' ball of paper used in the conical flask experiment instead of a dry one?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the glass wall clean\",\"To provide the water needed for the seeds to germinate\",\"To make the paper stay at the bottom\",\"To prevent the seeds from touching the glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Moisture is necessary for seeds to grow; a dry paper would not support the formation of the radicle and plumule.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best summarizes the formation of a root as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It grows from the plumule above the soil\",\"It grows from the radicle and tapers to a point\",\"It grows from the leaves into the ground\",\"It grows from the stem for support\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains the root forms from the radicle, grows into the ground, and tapers to a pointed end.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction & Formation of Root\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are the hair-like processes found near the root tips called?\",\"options\":[\"Secondary roots\",\"Root hairs\",\"Root caps\",\"Fibrous roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that roots bear hair-like processes near the root tips called root hairs.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the root is responsible for protecting the root-tip from injuries?\",\"options\":[\"Root hair\",\"Primary root\",\"Root cap\",\"Secondary root\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the root-cap protects the root-tip from injuries.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Roots that grow obliquely and spread far and wide in the soil to support the plant are known as...\",\"options\":[\"Fibrous roots\",\"Thread-like roots\",\"Tap roots\",\"Monocot roots\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines tap roots as roots where secondary roots grow obliquely and spread far and wide to support the plant.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"From which part of the plant do fibrous roots arise?\",\"options\":[\"The leaves\",\"The root tip\",\"The stem\",\"The secondary roots\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, thread-like or fibre-like roots arising from the stem are called fibrous roots.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist is studying a monocotyledonous plant, what type of root system will they observe?\",\"options\":[\"Tap roots\",\"Fibrous roots\",\"Primary roots only\",\"Oblique roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that monocotyledonous plants have fibrous roots.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the onion experiment described in the text, what will be the appearance of the roots after eight days?\",\"options\":[\"Thick and growing obliquely\",\"A single primary root with secondary branches\",\"Thread-like or fibre-like\",\"Covered in large protective caps\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The onion experiment is used to demonstrate fibrous roots, which are described as thread-like or fibre-like.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the root tip described as a delicate region in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is covered by a cap\",\"Because it is the region of growth of the root\",\"Because it contains secondary roots\",\"Because it grows far and wide in the soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the root tip is delicate because it is the region of the growth of the root.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is a major functional difference between the root tip and the root cap?\",\"options\":[\"The tip provides support while the cap absorbs water\",\"The tip grows obliquely while the cap grows straight\",\"The tip is for growth while the cap is for protection\",\"The tip bears secondary roots while the cap bears root hairs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the root tip as the region of growth and the root cap as the structure that protects it.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of plants typically possess a tap root system?\",\"options\":[\"Monocotyledonous plants\",\"Dicotyledonous plants\",\"Only onion plants\",\"Plants with thread-like roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that dicotyledonous plants have tap roots.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific role of secondary roots mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To protect the root tip from injury\",\"To grow into thread-like structures from the stem\",\"To grow obliquely and help support the plant\",\"To produce root hairs for the onion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says secondary roots grow obliquely and spread far and wide to support the plant.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which labeled part branches out from the main primary root to spread through the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Root cap\",\"Root hair\",\"Secondary root\",\"Root tip\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In figure 2.2, the secondary roots are shown branching out from the central primary root.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/020_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of root system is shown developing from the base of the onion?\",\"options\":[\"Tap roots\",\"Fibrous roots\",\"Primary roots\",\"Oblique roots\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Figure 2.3 is titled \'Fibrous roots\' and shows the thread-like roots of an onion.\",\"topic\":\"Tap Roots and Fibrous Roots\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/020_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What are the roots that grow from the stem just above the soil in plants like maize and sugarcane called?\",\"options\":[\"Tap roots\",\"Fibrous roots\",\"Adventitious roots\",\"Primary roots\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that roots growing from the stem just above the soil are called adventitious roots.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these plants possess two types of roots: underground roots and roots growing from the stem?\",\"options\":[\"Pea\",\"Mustard\",\"Sorghum\",\"Coriander\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, plants like maize, sugarcane, and sorghum have two types of roots.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the technical name for \'breathing roots\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Stilt-roots\",\"Pneumatophores\",\"Prop roots\",\"Runners\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions breathing roots as pneumatophores.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the maize stalk, what is the specific function of the roots emerging from the stem above the ground?\",\"options\":[\"Only to absorb water\",\"To provide additional support to the plant\",\"To produce seeds for the plant\",\"To store food for the winter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Roots like stilt-roots or adventitious roots in maize perform the additional function of supporting the plant.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/021_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, where do the additional supporting roots of the maize plant originate?\",\"options\":[\"From the leaves\",\"From the root-tip\",\"From the stem just above the soil\",\"From the underground tap root\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that these roots grow from the stem just above the soil level.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/021_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What are the roots that emerge from the trunk and branches of a banyan tree and grow towards the soil called?\",\"options\":[\"Runners\",\"Stilt-roots\",\"Prop roots\",\"Fibrous roots\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines roots emerging from the trunk and branches of a banyan tree as prop roots.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the 250-year-old banyan tree that appears like a small forest located?\",\"options\":[\"Indian Botanical Garden, Kolkata\",\"National Park, Mumbai\",\"Agricultural University, Pune\",\"Central Forest, Delhi\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions a 250-year-old banyan tree in the Indian Botanical Garden in Kolkata.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the underground parts of plants like radish, carrot, and sweet potato thick, fleshy, and swollen?\",\"options\":[\"They are infected by soil bacteria\",\"They are modified to store food\",\"They are absorbing too much water\",\"They are protecting the root-tip\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"These are modified roots that become thick and fleshy to perform additional functions like food storage.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a normal function of roots as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Absorption of water and minerals\",\"Anchoring the plant\",\"Supporting the plant\",\"Photosynthesis\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Normal functions listed are absorption of water\\/minerals, anchoring, and supporting; photosynthesis is not mentioned as a root function.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with the glass jar and plantlet, why is 5ml of oil added to the water?\",\"options\":[\"To provide nutrients to the roots\",\"To prevent the water from evaporating\",\"To make the roots grow faster\",\"To change the color of the water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oil is added to ensure that any decrease in water level is due to root absorption, not evaporation.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason roots show modifications like becoming aerial roots or stilt-roots?\",\"options\":[\"To look different from other plants\",\"To perform additional functions beyond normal ones\",\"To replace the stem of the plant\",\"To help the plant grow flowers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states roots show modifications to perform additional functions.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a large tree like a banyan had only fibrous roots instead of a strong root system, what would likely happen?\",\"options\":[\"It would grow much taller\",\"It would not be able to stand firmly and might fall\",\"It would produce more fruit\",\"It would need less water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fibrous roots are shallow and wouldn\'t provide the necessary anchoring and support for a massive tree.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Radish, carrot, and beet are examples of which plant part?\",\"options\":[\"Stems\",\"Leaves\",\"Roots\",\"Fruits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks why these underground parts are thick and then asks \'Which part of the plant are they?\', implying they are roots.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a modified root mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Coriander root\",\"Mustard root\",\"Runners\",\"Jowar tap root\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists aerial roots, stilt-roots, runners, and breathing roots as examples of modified roots.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text\'s description of root functions, what would happen if the root-tip of a plant is injured?\",\"options\":[\"The plant will grow faster\",\"The growth of the root will be affected\",\"The leaves will turn blue\",\"The stem will turn into a root\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The root-tip is responsible for growth; injury to it would hinder the root\'s ability to grow further.\",\"topic\":\"Modified and Adventitious Roots\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"From which part of the sprouting seed does the stem grow above the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Radicle\",\"Plumule\",\"Node\",\"Internode\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the stem grows above the soil from the plumule of the sprouting seed.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the tip or the apical end of the stem called?\",\"options\":[\"Node\",\"Internode\",\"Bud\",\"Plumule\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the tip or the apical end of the stem is called a bud.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On which specific part of the stem do leaves come out?\",\"options\":[\"Internodes\",\"Nodes\",\"Apical end\",\"Plumule\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that leaves come out at the nodes.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is an \'internode\' defined in the context of a plant stem?\",\"options\":[\"The part where leaves grow\",\"The growing tip of the stem\",\"The part of the stem between two nodes\",\"The part that grows below the soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an internode as the part of the stem between two nodes.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part is indicated as the \'Growing end\' of the stem?\",\"options\":[\"Node\",\"Internode\",\"Bud\",\"Leaf\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the apical end\\/growing end as a Bud.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/022_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the length of the stem as the sprout grows?\",\"options\":[\"It gradually decreases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It gradually increases\",\"It stops growing after the plumule appears\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that as the sprout grows, the length of the stem gradually increases, too.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is measuring the distance between two points on a sugarcane stalk where leaves were attached, which part are they measuring?\",\"options\":[\"The node\",\"The internode\",\"The bud\",\"The plumule\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since leaves come out at nodes, the distance between two such points is the internode.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to record the \'Thickness of node\' for a Fenugreek plant as per the chart. Where should they place their measuring tape?\",\"options\":[\"At the very tip of the stem\",\"Between two leaves\",\"At the point where a leaf comes out\",\"On the root of the plant\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nodes are the parts where leaves come out, so the thickness must be measured at that point.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many distinct \'Nodes\' are identified by the label lines on the main stem?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The label \'Nodes\' in the diagram has two lines pointing to two different node locations on the stem.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/022_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text and diagram, which part is responsible for the primary upward growth of the stem?\",\"options\":[\"The internode\",\"The node\",\"The bud at the apical end\",\"The leaf\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the apical end (tip) as the bud, and the diagram labels this as the \'Growing end\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Stem\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where do leaves grow from on a stem?\",\"options\":[\"Nodes\",\"Internodes\",\"Leaf apex\",\"Leaf-base\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, leaves grow from the nodes on a stem.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the broad, spread-out part of the leaf called?\",\"options\":[\"Petiole\",\"Leaf-base\",\"Leaf-blade or lamina\",\"Stipule\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the broad, spread-out part of the leaf as the leaf-blade or lamina.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the small leaf-like structures sometimes present near the leaf-base called?\",\"options\":[\"Leaflets\",\"Stipules\",\"Petioles\",\"Nodes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Small leaf-like structures near the leaf-base are called stipules.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a leaf has a single undivided leaf blade and a single mid-rib, it is classified as a:\",\"options\":[\"Compound leaf\",\"Simple leaf\",\"Palmate leaf\",\"Linear leaf\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Leaves with a single undivided leaf blade and a single mid-rib are called simple leaves.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these terms is used to describe the shape of a leaf?\",\"options\":[\"Dentate\",\"Lobed\",\"Obovate\",\"Tapering\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that according to shape, leaves are rounded (obovate), palmate, lanceolate, or linear.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which arrangement shows leaves growing in a circular pattern around the stem at a single point?\",\"options\":[\"Alternate\",\"Decussate\",\"Opposite\",\"Whorled\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure labeled \'Whorled\' shows multiple leaves emerging from the same point on the stem.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/023_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the leaf can be described as tapering, pointed, or rounded?\",\"options\":[\"Leaf margin\",\"Leaf apex\",\"Leaf-base\",\"Petiole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The tip of the leaf is called the leaf apex, and it may be tapering, pointed, or rounded.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a leaf that is divided into many small parts called leaflets. How should this leaf be categorized?\",\"options\":[\"Simple leaf\",\"Compound leaf\",\"Entire leaf\",\"Stipule leaf\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When a leaf-blade is divided into many small parts called leaflets, it is a compound leaf.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant\'s leaf is attached directly to the stem without a stalk, which part does it lack?\",\"options\":[\"Lamina\",\"Apex\",\"Petiole\",\"Mid-rib\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The stalk of a leaf is called a petiole; some plants do not have one.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, how does the \'Alternate\' arrangement differ from the \'Opposite\' arrangement?\",\"options\":[\"Alternate has two leaves at each node, while Opposite has one.\",\"Alternate has leaves growing in a circle, while Opposite has them in pairs.\",\"Alternate has a single leaf at each node on different sides, while Opposite has two leaves facing each other at the same node.\",\"Alternate has no petiole, while Opposite has a long petiole.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows \'Alternate\' with one leaf per node alternating sides, and \'Opposite\' with two leaves at the same node.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/023_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary structural difference between a simple leaf and a compound leaf?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the lamina\",\"The presence of a leaf-base\",\"The division of the leaf-blade\",\"The shape of the leaf apex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes them by whether the leaf-blade is undivided (simple) or divided into leaflets (compound).\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which set of terms specifically describes the types of leaf margins mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Tapering, pointed, rounded\",\"Entire, dentate, lobed\",\"Alternate, opposite, whorled\",\"Obovate, palmate, linear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that leaf margins may be entire, dentate, or lobed.\",\"topic\":\"The Leaf\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What type of venation is found in a peepal leaf-blade?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel venation\",\"Reticulate venation\",\"Linear venation\",\"Circular venation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the peepal leaf-blade has reticulate venation because its secondary veins form a network.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which plant do the veins run parallel from the leaf-base to the leaf-apex?\",\"options\":[\"Peepal\",\"Rose\",\"Maize\",\"Banyan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in leaves of maize, all the veins are parallel, running from the leaf-base to the leaf-apex.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the network formed by secondary veins in a peepal leaf called?\",\"options\":[\"Apex\",\"Petiole\",\"Reticulum\",\"Stipule\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that secondary veins are branched and form a network or reticulum.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To make a lace-like greeting card, how many days should a fallen peepal leaf be soaked in water?\",\"options\":[\"2-5 days\",\"5-10 days\",\"15-20 days\",\"30-40 days\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'A little fun!\' section suggests soaking a fallen peepal leaf in water for 15-20 days.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the single mid-vein lie in a peepal leaf?\",\"options\":[\"Along the leaf margin\",\"Along the mid-line of the leaf blade\",\"At the leaf apex\",\"Only at the leaf base\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the single mid-vein lies along the mid-line of the leaf blade.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of maize, what can be inferred about its venation?\",\"options\":[\"It has a branched network of veins.\",\"It is divided into two equal parts by a mid-vein.\",\"It has parallel venation.\",\"It has a lace-like reticulum.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the maize leaf-blade has parallel venation.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do secondary veins in a peepal leaf originate?\",\"options\":[\"They arise from the leaf-base.\",\"They arise from the leaf-apex.\",\"They arise from the mid-vein.\",\"They arise from the stipules.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that secondary veins arise from the mid-vein in a peepal leaf.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of venation is shown in the bottom image of the leaf?\",\"options\":[\"Reticulate venation\",\"Parallel venation\",\"Network venation\",\"Branched venation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The bottom image shows veins running vertically and parallel to each other, which the text identifies as parallel venation.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/023_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of the following is a characteristic to be observed for the plant \'Canna\'?\",\"options\":[\"Color of the flower\",\"Height of the plant\",\"Shape of leaf-blade\",\"Type of root system\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chart in the figure includes \'Shape of leaf-blade\' as one of the columns to be filled for Canna.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/023_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student observes a leaf where the mid-vein divides the leaf into two equal parts and secondary veins form a network, which plant is it most likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"Maize\",\"Grass\",\"Peepal\",\"Sugar cane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The description matches the peepal leaf characteristics provided in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary structural difference between the venation of peepal and maize leaves?\",\"options\":[\"Peepal has no mid-vein, while maize does.\",\"Peepal has a network of veins, while maize has parallel veins.\",\"Maize has branched secondary veins, while peepal does not.\",\"Peepal veins run from base to apex, while maize veins form a reticulum.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text contrasts the network (reticulum) of peepal with the parallel veins of maize.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the leaf is responsible for dividing the peepal leaf into two equal parts?\",\"options\":[\"Leaf apex\",\"Leaf base\",\"Secondary veins\",\"Mid-vein\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states the peepal leaf is divided into two equal parts by a single mid-vein.\",\"topic\":\"Venation in Leaves\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the long or short stalk that attaches a flower to the stem?\",\"options\":[\"Receptacle\",\"Pedicel\",\"Stamen\",\"Carpel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that flowers may have a long or a short stalk called a pedicel, which is attached to the stem.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the flower is described as the male reproductive part consisting of stamens?\",\"options\":[\"Calyx\",\"Corolla\",\"Androecium\",\"Gynoecium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the androecium as the male reproductive part of the flower, consisting of stamens.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the receptacle in a flower?\",\"options\":[\"To produce pollen grains\",\"To protect the flower in the bud condition\",\"To support the petals and other parts of the flower\",\"To attract insects with its green colour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, petals and other parts of the flower are supported on the receptacle.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the pedicel attached at its two ends?\",\"options\":[\"One end to the stem and the other to the receptacle\",\"One end to the petal and the other to the sepal\",\"One end to the anther and the other to the filament\",\"One end to the stigma and the other to the ovary\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions one end of the pedicel is attached to the stem, and the other end is the expanded receptacle.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are observing the bright red, attractive parts of a Hibiscus flower, which floral part are you looking at?\",\"options\":[\"Calyx\",\"Corolla\",\"Androecium\",\"Gynoecium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The corolla is made up of colourful parts called petals, which are the attractive parts of flowers like Hibiscus.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a structure in a flower that includes a stigma, a style, and an ovary. Which part of the flower has the student identified?\",\"options\":[\"Stamen\",\"Androecium\",\"Gynoecium\",\"Calyx\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the gynoecium is made up of carpels, and a carpel consists of stigma, style, and ovary.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the textbook description, how does the androecium differ from the gynoecium?\",\"options\":[\"The androecium is colorful, while the gynoecium is green.\",\"The androecium is the male part, while the gynoecium is the female part.\",\"The androecium consists of carpels, while the gynoecium consists of stamens.\",\"The androecium protects the bud, while the gynoecium supports the petals.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the androecium as the male reproductive part and the gynoecium as the female reproductive part.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary role of the sepals when a flower is still in the bud condition?\",\"options\":[\"To produce seeds for reproduction\",\"To provide a smell to attract insects\",\"To cover and protect the petals\",\"To expand and form the receptacle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in the bud condition, petals are covered by leaf-like parts called sepals.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following parts are found within a single carpel?\",\"options\":[\"Anther and filament\",\"Sepals and petals\",\"Stigma, style, and ovary\",\"Pedicel and receptacle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a carpel consists of stigma, style, and ovary.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Each stamen in the androecium is composed of which two structures?\",\"options\":[\"Stigma and style\",\"Anther and filament\",\"Petal and sepal\",\"Ovary and carpel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that each stamen is made up of anther and filament.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which labeled part represents the uppermost portion of the female reproductive system?\",\"options\":[\"Anther\",\"Petal\",\"Stigma\",\"Pedicel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the top-most part of the central floral structure as the Stigma, which is part of the gynoecium.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/024_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which label identifies the stalk that supports the entire flower structure?\",\"options\":[\"Stigma\",\"Anther\",\"Petal\",\"Pedicel\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the base stalk of the flower as the Pedicel.\",\"topic\":\"The Flower\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/024_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21988,46,7,'Science','en','Properties of Natural Resources','[\"Air is a mixture of gases (nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide) and tiny particles of dust and water vapour.\",\"Air is a homogeneous mixture – its composition is the same everywhere in a small region.\",\"The weight of air can be felt when a stick with balloons is tilted; the stick moves because air has mass.\",\"Atmospheric pressure is the force exerted by air on a surface; it is about 1.01×10^5 N\\/m² at sea level.\",\"Pressure decreases with height above sea level.\",\"Bernoulli’s principle: when the speed of a fluid (air) increases, its pressure decreases, and vice versa.\",\"A pressure difference makes air flow from high pressure to low pressure – this is wind or breeze.\",\"When the temperature of air rises, its density falls and the pressure at a given height falls.\",\"When the temperature of air falls, its density rises and the pressure at a given height rises.\",\"Water vapour in air can condense into droplets when the air cools – this is dew.\",\"Ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats; this is because water expands when it freezes below 4 °C (anomalous behaviour).\",\"Water expands by about 9 % when it freezes; its density at 4 °C is 1 g\\/cm³, the maximum density.\",\"Water is a universal solvent – it can dissolve many substances because of its polarity.\",\"Soil colour depends on organic matter and mineral content; it helps indicate soil fertility and drainage.\",\"Sandy soil drains quickly but holds little nutrients; clay soil holds water but is hard to plough; silt soil is intermediate.\",\"The pH of soil indicates acidity (pH<6.5), neutrality (6.5–7.5) or alkalinity (pH>7.5).\",\"Good soil structure gives roots oxygen, good drainage and allows roots to grow well.\",\"Sound needs a medium; in space there is no air, so sound cannot travel.\",\"The density of seawater is higher than that of rainwater because salt increases its mass for the same volume.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, air is called a ______ mixture.\",\"options\":[\"Heterogeneous\",\"Homogeneous\",\"Pure\",\"Solid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly asks why air is called a homogeneous mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Even though we cannot see air, how do we know it is all around us?\",\"options\":[\"We can smell it\",\"We can feel its presence\",\"We can see its color\",\"We can hear it constantly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'We cannot see it, yet we feel the presence of air.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When we inhale, through which part of the body is air taken in?\",\"options\":[\"Mouth\",\"Ears\",\"Nose\",\"Eyes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'When we inhale, air is taken in through the nose.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why does the stick tilt downwards on the side of the larger balloon?\",\"options\":[\"Because the balloon is green\",\"Because air has weight\",\"Because the stick is made of plastic\",\"Because the thread is tied at the centre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the stick goes down on the side of the inflated balloon because air has weight.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/026_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the horizontal stick when one of the two balloons tied to it is inflated and re-tied?\",\"options\":[\"The stick remains horizontal\",\"The stick goes up on the side of the inflated balloon\",\"The stick goes down on the side of the inflated balloon\",\"The stick breaks into two pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'The stick goes down on the side of the inflated balloon.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to prove that air has weight using the experiment described, what is a necessary step?\",\"options\":[\"Use balloons of different colors\",\"Inflate one balloon while leaving the other as it is\",\"Tie the thread at one end of the stick\",\"Use a stick that is already tilted\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To show the difference in weight, one balloon must be inflated to add air, as per the \'Try this\' section.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Air is a mixture of gases; therefore, like all other matter, it has ______.\",\"options\":[\"Only weight\",\"Only mass\",\"Mass and weight\",\"No mass or weight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Therefore, like all other matter, it has mass and weight.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, what would happen to the stick if the air inside the inflated balloon was completely let out?\",\"options\":[\"The stick would tilt further down\",\"The stick would return to a horizontal position\",\"The stick would spin around the thread\",\"The stick would become vertical\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If the air (which has weight) is removed, the balance between the two identical balloons would be restored to horizontal.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/026_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text suggest blowing on your hand through your lips?\",\"options\":[\"To clean your hand\",\"To feel the presence of air\",\"To see the gases in the air\",\"To measure the weight of air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'If we blow on our hand through our lips, we can feel the air.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the balloon experiment, what is the purpose of tying the thread at the centre of the stick?\",\"options\":[\"To make the stick look like a scale\",\"To ensure the stick remains horizontal initially\",\"To make the balloons stay at the ends\",\"To prevent the stick from breaking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to tie the thread at the centre and hang it \'so that it remains horizontal\' as a starting point.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a property of air mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It is a solid mixture\",\"It is invisible but has weight\",\"It can only be felt when inhaling\",\"It has no mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text establishes that we cannot see it (invisible) and it has weight.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Air is described in the text as a mixture of ______.\",\"options\":[\"Liquids\",\"Solids\",\"Gases\",\"Dust only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Air is a mixture of gases.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of air\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Thermometer\",\"Barometer\",\"Speedometer\",\"Lactometer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that atmospheric pressure can be measured with the help of a barometer.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Under ordinary conditions, what is the atmospheric pressure at sea level?\",\"options\":[\"About 1,01,400 Newtons per square metre\",\"About 10,140 Newtons per square metre\",\"About 1,014 Newtons per square metre\",\"About 1,01,400 Newtons per square centimetre\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the value as 1,01,400 Newtons per square metre at sea level.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the pressure of air on a body according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the syringe\",\"The movement of the piston\",\"Molecules of gases striking the body\",\"The pull of the earth\'s gravity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when molecules of gases in constant motion strike a body, they create pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the air inside the syringe when the piston is pulled while the inlet hole is closed?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes compressed\",\"It becomes rarified\",\"It escapes through the piston\",\"It turns into a liquid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the hole is closed and the piston is pulled, the air becomes rarified.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person climbs a high mountain, what change will they observe in the atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"It will increase\",\"It will remain the same\",\"It will decrease\",\"It will first increase then decrease\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that as we go higher above sea level, atmospheric pressure decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the piston immediately pushed in when it is released after being pulled with the inlet hole closed?\",\"options\":[\"Because the inside pressure is higher than the outside pressure\",\"Because the outside air pressure is very high compared to the inside\",\"Because the syringe is made of plastic\",\"Because the thumb attracts the piston\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains the outside air pressure is very high compared to the lowered pressure inside, pushing the piston in.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the part that is pulled to change the volume of air inside the syringe?\",\"options\":[\"Hole\",\"Needle\",\"Piston\",\"Tube\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the movable part as the \'Piston\'.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/026_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the effect on the air pressure inside the syringe when the action shown is performed?\",\"options\":[\"The pressure increases\",\"The pressure is lowered\",\"The pressure remains equal to sea level\",\"The pressure becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the piston being pulled with the hole closed, which the text says lowers the air pressure inside.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/026_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred from the experiment when the syringe is held in vertical, horizontal, or inclined positions?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure is higher in the vertical position\",\"Pressure is lower in the horizontal position\",\"Atmospheric pressure is the same in all directions\",\"Atmospheric pressure only acts downwards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that since the piston goes in to the same extent in all cases, pressure is the same in all directions.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In what state are the molecules of the gases in the air?\",\"options\":[\"Stationary\",\"In constant motion\",\"Moving only downwards\",\"Vibrating in fixed positions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the molecules of the gases in the air are in constant motion.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the force required to pull the piston change when the inlet hole is closed compared to when it is open?\",\"options\":[\"More force is required\",\"Less force is required\",\"The same force is required\",\"No force is required\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks if the force is more or less and explains that pulling it with the hole closed creates a pressure difference that resists the pull.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit is used in the text to express the value of atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Kilograms per square metre\",\"Newtons per square metre\",\"Pounds per square inch\",\"Grams per cubic centimetre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the unit \'Newtons per square metre\' for atmospheric pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the air in the syringe becomes \'rarified\', what does this mean for the space available to the air?\",\"options\":[\"Less space is available\",\"More space becomes available\",\"The space remains the same\",\"There is no space at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that on pulling the piston, more space becomes available to the air, making it rarified.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist takes a syringe to a place with no air molecules, what would be the atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"It would be 1,01,400 N\\/m2\",\"It would be very high\",\"There would be no atmospheric pressure\",\"It would be the same as sea level\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the text defines atmospheric pressure as molecules striking a body, no molecules would mean no pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the piston if you pull it while the inlet hole is open and then let it go?\",\"options\":[\"It is immediately pushed in\",\"It remains in that position\",\"It flies out of the syringe\",\"It vibrates rapidly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the piston is pulled out (without closing the hole), it remains in that position even if you let it go.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the Swedish scientist who put forth the principle that the pressure of air decreases when its velocity increases?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Newton\",\"Daniel Bernoulli\",\"Albert Einstein\",\"Galileo Galilei\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in 1726, the Swedish scientist Daniel Bernoulli put forth this important principle.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did Daniel Bernoulli put forth his principle regarding air pressure and velocity?\",\"options\":[\"1826\",\"1726\",\"1626\",\"1926\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Daniel Bernoulli put forth the principle in 1726.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Bernoulli’s principle, what happens to the pressure of air when its velocity increases?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It decreases\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Bernoulli\'s principle states that the pressure of air decreases when its velocity increases.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a body is moving through air, in which direction does the air pressure decrease?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel to the direction of motion\",\"Opposite to the direction of motion\",\"Perpendicular to the direction of motion\",\"Towards the center of the body\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that air pressure decreases perpendicular to the direction of the motion of a body.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the straw fountain experiment, what happens to the air at the open end of the upright straw when you blow through the small piece?\",\"options\":[\"The air is pulled into the straw\",\"The air is pushed away\",\"The air becomes hot\",\"The air stops moving\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that on blowing through the straw, the air at its open end is pushed away.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how is the small piece of straw positioned relative to the first upright straw?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel\",\"Perpendicular\",\"Inside the first straw\",\"At a 45-degree angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show the small piece of straw held perpendicular to the first straw.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/027_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the straw fountain experiment, why does the water move in the upward direction?\",\"options\":[\"Because the pressure at the top becomes higher than atmospheric pressure\",\"Because the pressure at the top becomes less than atmospheric pressure\",\"Because of the heat from the breath\",\"Because the straw is made of plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water is pushed upward because the pressure near the upper end becomes less than atmospheric pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what determines the height of the water fountain in the straw experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the straw\",\"The color of the cup\",\"How forcefully you blow\",\"The temperature of the water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'The more forcefully you blow the higher will be the fountain.\'\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Air in the surroundings flows rapidly from ________ pressure to ________ pressure.\",\"options\":[\"Lower, higher\",\"Higher, lower\",\"Atmospheric, zero\",\"Zero, atmospheric\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that air flows rapidly from higher pressure to the lower pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what causes the \'Fountain of water\' to form?\",\"options\":[\"Gravity pulling the water up\",\"Increased air pressure at the top of the straw\",\"Decreased air pressure at the top of the straw due to blowing\",\"The water level in the cup rising naturally\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Blowing air across the top increases velocity, which decreases pressure, causing water to rise and form a fountain.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/027_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the velocity of air decreases, what happens to its pressure according to Bernoulli?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases\",\"It increases\",\"It disappears\",\"It stays constant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bernoulli\'s principle states that the pressure of air increases when its velocity decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nationality of the scientist Daniel Bernoulli?\",\"options\":[\"German\",\"Swedish\",\"British\",\"American\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Daniel Bernoulli as a Swedish scientist.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment described, what would happen if you blew very softly through the straw?\",\"options\":[\"The fountain would be very high\",\"The fountain would be lower\",\"The water would turn into ice\",\"The cup would break\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since blowing more forcefully makes it higher, blowing softly would result in a lower fountain.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which principle explains the working of the straw fountain?\",\"options\":[\"Newton\'s Law\",\"Archimedes\' Principle\",\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"Pascal\'s Law\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'This straw fountain works on Bernoulli’s principle.\'\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between air velocity and air pressure as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are directly proportional\",\"They are inversely related (one increases as the other decreases)\",\"They have no relationship\",\"They both always increase together\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The principle states pressure decreases when velocity increases and vice versa, indicating an inverse relationship.\",\"topic\":\"Bernoulli’s principle\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the air to start flowing from one place to another?\",\"options\":[\"Difference in air temperature\",\"Difference in air pressure\",\"Difference in humidity levels\",\"Difference in light intensity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that air starts flowing from a place of higher pressure to a place of lower pressure, creating wind.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the water vapour in the air when it surrounds a glass filled with ice-cubes?\",\"options\":[\"It evaporates into the atmosphere\",\"It turns into smoke and dust\",\"It condenses and transforms into water\",\"It increases the air pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As the water vapour in the air cools down due to the ice, it condenses and transforms into water droplets on the glass.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The level of humidity in the air is primarily determined by its capacity to hold what?\",\"options\":[\"Dust particles\",\"Oxygen gas\",\"Water vapour\",\"Light rays\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the level of humidity is determined by the air\'s capacity to hold water vapour.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the air feel dry in the afternoon even if the moisture content remains the same?\",\"options\":[\"The air pressure decreases significantly\",\"The capacity of the air to hold moisture increases with temperature\",\"The water vapour turns into dew\",\"The scattering of light removes the moisture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the afternoon, high temperatures increase the air\'s capacity to hold moisture, making the proportion of moisture feel less compared to its full capacity.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are living in a coastal area during the monsoon, what are you most likely to feel?\",\"options\":[\"Extreme dryness\",\"Low air pressure\",\"Dampness or humidity\",\"Lack of scattering of light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the proportion of water vapour is high in coastal areas and during monsoons, resulting in a feeling of dampness.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why do wet clothes take longer to dry in the rainy season than in summer?\",\"options\":[\"The air pressure is too high in the rains\",\"There is no scattering of light in the rains\",\"The air already has a high proportion of water vapour\",\"The air temperature is at its highest during rains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the rains, the proportion of water vapour in the air is high, which reduces the rate at which wet clothes can release moisture.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the natural phenomenon called when minute particles in the air spread light in all directions?\",\"options\":[\"Condensation of light\",\"Scattering of light\",\"Humidity of light\",\"Pressure of light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the spreading of light in all directions by minute particles as the scattering of light.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What occurs when the air temperature is low at dawn and its capacity to hold vapour is exceeded?\",\"options\":[\"The air pressure increases\",\"Excess vapour transforms into dew\",\"The air becomes extremely dry\",\"Light rays stop scattering\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"At dawn, low temperatures reduce the air\'s capacity to hold vapour, causing excess vapour to transform into water droplets called dew.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student observes water droplets on the outside of a cold soda can. Which concept from the text explains this?\",\"options\":[\"Scattering of light\",\"Air pressure difference\",\"Condensation of water vapour\",\"Mass and weight of air\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This is an application of the ice-cube experiment where cooling air causes water vapour to condense into droplets.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, air is a mixture of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Only oxygen and nitrogen\",\"Gases, dust, smoke, and moisture\",\"Water droplets and ice cubes\",\"Light rays and pressure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states air is a mixture of very fine particles of some gases, dust, smoke, and moisture.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If place A has higher air pressure than place B, what will happen?\",\"options\":[\"Air will flow from B to A\",\"Air will flow from A to B\",\"Humidity will increase at place A\",\"Light will scatter only at place B\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Air always flows from a place of higher pressure to a place of lower pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of air is demonstrated when a balloon changes shape and size upon being filled?\",\"options\":[\"Air has moisture\",\"Air scatters light\",\"Air occupies space and has volume\",\"Air flows from high to low pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Filling a balloon shows that air occupies space and has a certain volume.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the relationship: How does the capacity of air to hold water change as temperature decreases?\",\"options\":[\"The capacity increases\",\"The capacity decreases\",\"The capacity remains constant\",\"The capacity becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the temperature is low (like at dawn), the capacity to hold vapour is less.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed in the text as a property of air?\",\"options\":[\"Occupying space\",\"Having mass and weight\",\"Having a certain volume\",\"Being a pure substance\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies air as a mixture and lists space, volume, mass, and weight as its properties.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of \'minute particles\' in the phenomenon of scattering of light?\",\"options\":[\"They absorb all light rays\",\"They cool the air down to form dew\",\"They spread the light rays in all directions\",\"They increase the air pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when rays of light fall on these minute particles, the particles spread the light in all directions.\",\"topic\":\"Humidity and Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In what form does the earth reflect the energy it receives from the sun?\",\"options\":[\"Light\",\"Heat\",\"Sound\",\"Electricity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that energy received from the sun is reflected by the earth in the form of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these constituents of air absorb a part of the heat reflected by the earth?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen and Oxygen\",\"Argon and Helium\",\"Water vapour and Carbon dioxide\",\"Dust and Smoke\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that water vapour and carbon dioxide absorb a part of the reflected heat.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What would be the condition of the earth\'s average surface temperature if there were no air?\",\"options\":[\"It would be very high\",\"It would be very low\",\"It would remain the same\",\"It would fluctuate every hour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, without air, the average temperature of the earth\'s surface would have been very low.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the density of air change in the winter season?\",\"options\":[\"The density decreases\",\"The density remains constant\",\"The density increases\",\"The density becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that in winter, the density of the air increases.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we hear the whistle of a distant train clearly early in the morning in winter?\",\"options\":[\"Because the air is dry\",\"Because the density of air increases\",\"Because there is no wind\",\"Because the sun is not out\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The increase in air density during winter allows us to hear distant sounds like a train whistle more clearly.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary role of air in the context of sound?\",\"options\":[\"It creates sound\",\"It absorbs all sound\",\"It acts as a medium for transmission\",\"It reflects sound back to space\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that air is useful as a medium for the transmission of sound.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the density of air to change according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Change in its temperature\",\"Change in its color\",\"Change in the amount of nitrogen\",\"Change in the speed of the earth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the density of air changes due to change in its temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Sound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based 