ending on the shape of the bottle.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mass is the store of matter; while volume and density change during freezing, the amount of matter (mass) remains the same.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of water\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why do some articles like the sharpener and pencil stay at the top while others stay at the bottom?\",\"options\":[\"Articles at the top are heavier than water.\",\"Articles at the bottom are lighter than water.\",\"Articles at the top are lighter than water and float.\",\"Articles at the bottom have less mass than those at the top.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows objects floating and sinking; objects that float are lighter (less dense) than water, as explained in the text regarding ice.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of water\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/030_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, which of the following items is clearly shown floating on the surface of the water?\",\"options\":[\"A metal screw\",\"A plastic ruler\",\"A heavy stone\",\"A glass marble\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows a ruler, pencil, and sharpener floating on the surface of the water.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of water\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/030_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"At what temperature is the density of water found to be maximum?\",\"options\":[\"0° C\",\"4° C\",\"10° C\",\"100° C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the density of water is maximum at 4° C.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Usually, when the temperature of a substance is lowered, what happens to its volume?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It decreases\",\"It first increases then decreases\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that usually, when the temperature of a substance is lowered, its volume decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the expansion of water when its temperature falls below 4° C called?\",\"options\":[\"Normal expansion\",\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"Thermal conduction\",\"Density increase\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the expansion of water below 4° C as the anomalous behaviour of water.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the general rule, what happens to the density of most substances when their temperature is lowered?\",\"options\":[\"Density decreases\",\"Density remains constant\",\"Density increases\",\"Density becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that usually, when the temperature of a substance is lowered, its density increases.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does water behave differently from most liquids when cooled below 4° C?\",\"options\":[\"Its density starts increasing\",\"Its density starts decreasing\",\"Its volume starts decreasing\",\"Its temperature stops falling\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that unlike most liquids, if water\'s temperature falls below 4° C, its density starts decreasing.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the temperature of water is lowered from 4° C to 2° C, what change occurs in its volume?\",\"options\":[\"Volume decreases\",\"Volume remains unchanged\",\"Volume increases\",\"Volume becomes half\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the temperature of water is lowered below 4° C, its volume increases.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When water at normal temperature starts cooling, how does its density change initially?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases immediately\",\"It increases like that of most liquids\",\"It remains constant until 0° C\",\"It fluctuates randomly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says that when water at normal temperature starts cooling, its density increases like that of most liquids until it reaches 4° C.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the temperature of the water layer at the very bottom of the lake?\",\"options\":[\"0° C\",\"1° C\",\"2° C\",\"4° C\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the temperature layers of the water, with 4° C being the layer at the bottom.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/030_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you keep a glass of water in a freezer, from which part does the water first start freezing according to the observation mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"From the bottom\",\"From the center\",\"From the top surface\",\"From the sides only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on the anomalous behaviour and the figure showing ice at the top, water starts freezing from the top surface.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how are the fish able to stay alive even though there is ice on the surface?\",\"options\":[\"They are frozen inside the ice\",\"The water at the bottom remains liquid at 4° C\",\"They do not need water to survive\",\"The ice is warm enough for them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows fish swimming in the bottom layer of water which remains at 4° C, while only the surface is frozen.\",\"topic\":\"Anomalous behaviour of water\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/030_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist observes a liquid whose volume increases as it cools from 3° C to 1° C. Which property is being demonstrated?\",\"options\":[\"Regular contraction\",\"Anomalous behaviour\",\"Evaporation\",\"Condensation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The increase in volume during cooling (below 4° C) is specifically id