ue to the dissolved salt.\",\"topic\":\"Solute, Solvent and Solution\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/031_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, what can be inferred about the liquid in container B compared to container A?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid B is pure water\",\"Liquid B has a lower density than liquid A\",\"Liquid B is a salt solution with higher density\",\"Liquid B is used for generating electricity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Container B is labeled \'Salt solution\' and shows the potato floating, which the text attributes to increased density.\",\"topic\":\"Solute, Solvent and Solution\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/031_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is water referred to as a \'universal solvent\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is used to generate electricity\",\"Because it is useful for cleaning purposes\",\"Because many substances are soluble in it\",\"Because it controls the temperature of engines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states water is a universal solvent because many substances are soluble in it.\",\"topic\":\"Solute, Solvent and Solution\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to salt particles when they dissolve in water?\",\"options\":[\"They become larger and sink to the bottom\",\"They spread and eventually become too small to be seen\",\"They change into water molecules\",\"They turn into a gas and disappear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes dissolving as particles spreading and becoming so small that they cannot be seen.\",\"topic\":\"Solute, Solvent and Solution\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it easier to swim in the sea than in a well or a lake?\",\"options\":[\"Sea water is a better coolant\",\"Sea water is used for water transport\",\"Sea water has a higher density due to salts\",\"Sea water is more fluid than well water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the floating of a potato in salt water