s and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides organic ingredients and texture, which chemical ingredients influence the colour of the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen and Phosphorus\",\"Iron and Lime\",\"Copper and Zinc\",\"Sulfur and Potassium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the colour of the soil depends on chemical ingredients like iron and lime.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the colour of the soil typically change with respect to the land surface?\",\"options\":[\"It gets lighter near the surface\",\"It stays the same at all layers\",\"It is darker near the surface than the lower layers\",\"It is only black at the lower layers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that the colour of soil near the land surface is darker than the colour of the lower layers.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does sandy soil have a very low capacity to supply nutrients to plants?\",\"options\":[\"The particles are too small to hold nutrients\",\"The quartz particles do not dissolve in water\",\"The soil contains too much organic material\",\"The soil is too difficult to plough\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that sandy soil particles (quartz) do not dissolve in water, resulting in a low capacity to supply nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of soil is determined by the proportion of particles of various sizes in it?\",\"options\":[\"Temperature\",\"Texture\",\"Weight\",\"Depth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines soil texture as being based on the proportion of particles of various sizes in the soil.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer finds that a specific soil is very smooth to touch and holds a lot of water. Which type of soil is this?\",\"options\":[\"Sandy soil\",\"Silt soil\",\"Clay soil\",\"Sedimentary soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes clay soil as feeling smooth to touch and having a high water holding capacity.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a construction project requires soil through which water drains very rapidly, which soil type should be selected?\",\"options\":[\"Clay soil\",\"Silt soil\",\"Sandy soil\",\"Sedimentary soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that water drains rapidly through sandy soil.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the descriptions provided, what is the correct order of soil types from largest particle size to smallest?\",\"options\":[\"Clay > Silt > Sandy\",\"Silt > Sandy > Clay\",\"Sandy > Silt > Clay\",\"Sandy > Clay > Silt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sandy soil has large particles, silt has medium-sized, and clay has the maximum small particles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the ploughability of the three soil types, which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Silt soil is easier to plough than sandy soil\",\"Clay soil is the easiest to plough\",\"Silt soil is more ploughable than clay soil but less than sandy soil\",\"Sandy soil is the most difficult to plough\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states silt soil is not as ploughable as sandy soil but much more ploughable than clay soil.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the colour of soil considered an important property for classification?\",\"options\":[\"It directly changes the mineral composition\",\"It indicates properties like fertility and drainage\",\"It determines the size of the particles\",\"It makes the soil easier to plough\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that properties like fertility, drainage, and water holding capacity become clear from the soil\'s colour.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the text as a possible colour of soil?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Gray\",\"Yellow\",\"Green\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists black, red, copper, yellow, and gray as soil colours; green is not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which cylinder represents the experiment with \'sand\' (a) where the most water has been collected?\",\"options\":[\"Cylinder a\",\"Cylinder b\",\"Cylinder c\",\"None of the cylinders\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In figure 3.11, cylinder \'a\' shows the highest level of collected water, corresponding to sand which has rapid drainage.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/032_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which type of soil is shown in the middle and contains a large proportion of organic materials?\",\"options\":[\"Sandy soil\",\"Silt soil\",\"Clay soil\",\"Quartz soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Figure 3.12 shows silt soil in the middle; the text notes it contains a large proportion of organic materials.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/032_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the relationship between particle size and water collection based on the text and the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Smaller particles allow more water to collect in the cylinder\",\"Larger particles result in less water being collected\",\"Smaller particles hold more water, resulting in less water collected in the cylinder\",\"Particle size has no effect on water collection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Clay (small particles) has high water holding capacity, so less water drains into the cylinder compared to sand (large particles).\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Texture of soil\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The structure of soil depends upon the different shapes of the ______ in it.\",\"options\":[\"minerals\",\"particles\",\"colors\",\"layers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the structure of soil depends upon the different shapes of the particles in it.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure showing various forms of soil, which of these is NOT a structure mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Columnar\",\"Laminar\",\"Granular\",\"Triangular\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions columnar, laminar, granular, and block-shaped structures, but not triangular.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/033_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary advantage of a good soil structure for plant growth?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the soil look white\",\"It allows roots to get a sufficient supply of oxygen\",\"It prevents the soil from being baked\",\"It turns the soil into Kaolinite\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, one advantage of good soil structure is that roots get a sufficient supply of oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property allows soil to be given any required shape to make articles?\",\"options\":[\"Fertility\",\"Drainage\",\"Plasticity\",\"Conservation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The property of soil being able to be given any required shape is called plasticity.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which article is a common example of using soil\'s plasticity to create a container for water or plants?\",\"options\":[\"A metal spoon\",\"An earthen pot\",\"A plastic bottle\",\"A glass jar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows earthen pots and bricks as articles made from soil due to its plasticity.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/033_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of soil is specifically used in the manufacturing of cosmetics?\",\"options\":[\"China clay\",\"Shadu soil\",\"Terracotta soil\",\"Multani soil\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Multani soil is used in cosmetics.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"China clay is an industrial mineral known by which name?\",\"options\":[\"Kaolinite\",\"Terracotta\",\"Laminar\",\"Granular\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that China clay is an industrial mineral of the type \'Kaolinite\'.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is China clay used to make crockery instead of ordinary soil?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is red in colour\",\"Because it acquires a glaze and hardness upon heating\",\"Because it is used for making statues\",\"Because it has poor water drainage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that upon heating, China clay acquires a glaze and hardness, making it suitable for crockery.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to make a decorative red pot for a garden. Which soil should they use?\",\"options\":[\"Shadu soil\",\"Multani soil\",\"Terracotta soil\",\"China clay\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Terracotta soil is red in colour and used for decorative articles and pots for growing plants.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does soil help us get water for use throughout the year?\",\"options\":[\"By turning into bricks\",\"Through water conservation in bunds and lakes\",\"By increasing its plasticity\",\"By becoming white in colour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Soil holds water, and by means of bunds and lakes, we can get water for use throughout the year.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following would be the most likely result if a soil has very poor water drainage?\",\"options\":[\"The soil will become China clay\",\"The roots of plants will not grow well\",\"The soil will turn red\",\"The soil will lose its plasticity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that good drainage helps roots grow well; therefore, poor drainage would hinder growth.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which soil type is most suitable for making laboratory apparatus and bathroom tiles?\",\"options\":[\"Terracotta soil\",\"Shadu soil\",\"China clay\",\"Multani soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"China clay is used to make crockery, bathroom tiles, and laboratory apparatus.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between soil structure and fertility according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Fertility depends upon soil structure\",\"Structure depends upon fertility\",\"They are not related\",\"Only red soil is fertile\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states directly that the fertility of soil depends upon soil structure.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to articles made of soil to make them hard?\",\"options\":[\"They are painted\",\"They are washed with water\",\"They are baked\",\"They are mixed with Multani soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that articles made from soil can be baked to make them hard.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which soil is whitish in colour and specifically mentioned for making statues and idols?\",\"options\":[\"China clay\",\"Shadu soil\",\"Terracotta soil\",\"Multani soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shadu soil is whitish in colour and used for making statues and idols.\",\"topic\":\"Soil structure and Uses\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is \'World Soil Day\' celebrated every year to make efforts for soil conservation?\",\"options\":[\"5th June\",\"5th December\",\"22nd April\",\"15th August\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, World Soil Day is celebrated on 5th December.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist put forth the concept of pH based on the concentration of hydrogen ions?\",\"options\":[\"Newton\",\"Sorensen\",\"Louis Pasteur\",\"C.V. Raman\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Danish scientist Sorensen introduced the concept of pH.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"For how many days should a soil sample be dried in the shade before it is sifted for testing?\",\"options\":[\"1 to 2 days\",\"20 to 30 days\",\"8 to 10 days\",\"5 to 6 days\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the soil sample is dried in an open space in the shade for eight to ten days.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of conducting a \'soil testing\' procedure?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the price of the land\",\"To find deficiencies and decide measures to remove them\",\"To check the depth of the water table\",\"To count the number of earthworms in the soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Soil is tested to find out if there is a deficiency of any ingredients and to decide measures to remove it.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which two tests are mentioned as particularly useful in finding out the characteristics of soil?\",\"options\":[\"Color and smell tests\",\"Weight and volume tests\",\"pH and electrical conductivity tests\",\"Moisture and temperature tests\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that pH and electrical conductivity tests are particularly useful.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what does the pH of soil depend upon?\",\"options\":[\"The amount of oxygen in the soil\",\"The concentration of hydrogen ions\",\"The number of rocks in the soil\",\"The speed of wind over the field\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sorensen\'s concept of pH is based on the concentration of hydrogen ions.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a soil sample is tested and found to have a pH of 8.2, how would it be classified?\",\"options\":[\"Acidic soil\",\"Neutral soil\",\"Alkaline soil\",\"Organic soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Soil with a pH higher than 7.5 is classified as alkaline soil.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To determine the pH of soil, what is the required proportion of soil to water in the mixture?\",\"options\":[\"1:1\",\"1:2\",\"2:1\",\"1:5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states a mixture of water and soil in the proportion 1:2 is taken for testing.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer has just harvested wheat, which has decreased the fertility of his land. Which crop should he plant next to restore it?\",\"options\":[\"More wheat\",\"Rice\",\"Moong\",\"Sugarcane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Leguminous crops like moong are recommended to restore fertility after harvesting wheat.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a direct cause of diminished soil fertility according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Proper drainage of water\",\"High proportion of organic matter\",\"Continuous use of saline water\",\"Rotation of crops\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Continuous use of saline water is listed as one of the causes of diminished soil fertility.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the consequence of using chemical fertilizers excessively on land?\",\"options\":[\"The soil texture is improved\",\"The soil texture is disturbed and becomes unsuitable for sowing\",\"The pH of the soil always becomes exactly 7.0\",\"The organic matter in the soil increases rapidly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Excessive use of chemical fertilizers disturbs soil texture and makes land unsuitable for sowing.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"At which pH level does soil fertility begin to diminish?\",\"options\":[\"pH between 6.5 and 7.5\",\"pH exactly 7.0\",\"pH less than 6 or higher than 8\",\"pH between 7.0 and 7.5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Soil pH less than 6 or higher than 8\' as a cause of diminished fertility.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the pH range for \'Neutral soil\'?\",\"options\":[\"Less than 6.5\",\"6.5 - 7.5\",\"Higher than 7.5\",\"Exactly 8.0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines neutral soil as having a pH of 6.5-7.5.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the cultivation of leguminous crops like peanut and soyabean recommended after wheat?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the pH above 8\",\"To restore the fertility of the soil\",\"To reduce the organic matter in the soil\",\"To stop the drainage of water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Leguminous crops are cultivated to restore the fertility of the soil after it decreases due to wheat harvesting.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these practices would a scientist likely advise against to maintain soil health?\",\"options\":[\"Rotating crops in the field\",\"Using proper drainage systems\",\"Repeated cultivation of the same crop\",\"Adding organic matter to the soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Repeated cultivation of the same crop is listed as a cause of diminished soil fertility.\",\"topic\":\"Soil testing and Fertility\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21989,46,7,'Science','en','Nutrition in Living Organisms','[\"Nutrition is the process of taking in food and using it for energy, growth and repair.\",\"Living organisms need nutrients to build tissues, produce energy and maintain health.\",\"Nutrients are classified into macronutrients (carbohydrates, proteins, fats) and micronutrients (minerals, vitamins).\",\"Carbohydrates are the main energy source; proteins build and repair tissues; fats store energy and protect organs.\",\"Minerals such as nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, magnesium, iron, manganese, zinc are essential for plant growth.\",\"Vitamins are organic compounds that act as co‑enzymes in many biochemical reactions.\",\"Autotrophic nutrition: plants and some bacteria produce their own food through photosynthesis.\",\"Heterotrophic nutrition: animals, fungi, many bacteria obtain food from other organisms.\",\"Photosynthesis uses light energy, water, CO₂ and chlorophyll to produce glucose and oxygen: 6CO₂+6H₂O+light→C₆H₁₂O₆+6O₂.\",\"Nitrogen fixation converts atmospheric N₂ into usable forms; done by root‑nodules (rhizobium) and free‑living bacteria (azotobacter).\",\"Symbiotic nutrition: mutualistic relationships like lichens (fungus + algae) or mycorrhizae (fungus + plant roots).\",\"Parasitic plants (e.g., cuscuta, loranthus) attach to host plants to obtain water and nutrients.\",\"Insectivorous plants (e.g., Drosera) trap insects to supplement nitrogen in poor soils.\",\"Saprophytic organisms (fungi, bacteria) decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients.\",\"Animals ingest food by chewing, sucking, scraping, etc.; digestion breaks food into simple molecules for absorption.\",\"Deficiency of nitrogen causes stunted growth and yellowing; phosphorus deficiency leads to poor root development; potassium deficiency weakens stems.\",\"Plants show specific symptoms of mineral deficiency: yellowing of leaves, early leaf fall, weak stems, etc.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are the substances called that are digested and assimilated for obtaining energy and for growth?\",\"options\":[\"Macro-nutrients\",\"Micro-nutrients\",\"Foodstuffs\",\"Minerals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines foodstuffs as substances digested and assimilated for energy, growth, and health.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Into how many types are nutrients classified according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Nutrients are classified into two types: macro-nutrients and micro-nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is classified as a macro-nutrient?\",\"options\":[\"Vitamins\",\"Minerals\",\"Proteins\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that carbohydrates, proteins, and fats are macro-nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are minerals and vitamins called micro-nutrients?\",\"options\":[\"They are not important for health\",\"They are required in very small quantity\",\"They are only found in small plants\",\"They do not provide any energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Nutrients required in very small quantity are called micro-nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of taking in and using food by living organisms called?\",\"options\":[\"Digestion\",\"Assimilation\",\"Nutrition\",\"Growth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines nutrition as the process of taking in and using food.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a specific need for nutrition mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To increase body temperature\",\"To repair damaged cells and tissues\",\"To help in breathing\",\"To improve eyesight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One of the needs for nutrition listed is to repair damaged cells and tissues.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an organism can produce its own food and nourish itself, what type of nutrition is it using?\",\"options\":[\"Heterotrophic nutrition\",\"Macro-nutrition\",\"Micro-nutrition\",\"Autotrophic nutrition\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines autotrophic nutrition as the ability of organisms to produce their own food.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student eats a diet rich in carbohydrates and fats. Which category of nutrients is the student primarily consuming?\",\"options\":[\"Micro-nutrients\",\"Macro-nutrients\",\"Minerals\",\"Vitamins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carbohydrates and fats are specifically listed as macro-nutrients in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition?\",\"options\":[\"The quantity of food consumed\",\"The type of minerals required\",\"Whether the organism produces its own food or depends on others\",\"Whether the organism is a plant or a mineral\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Autotrophs produce their own food, while heterotrophs depend on other organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which function of nutrition directly helps the body when it is attacked by a virus or bacteria?\",\"options\":[\"Growth of the body\",\"To fight diseases\",\"To supply energy for work\",\"Development of the body\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'To fight diseases\' as one of the four needs for nutrition.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which group contains only macro-nutrients?\",\"options\":[\"Carbohydrates, Proteins, Fats\",\"Minerals, Vitamins, Proteins\",\"Fats, Minerals, Carbohydrates\",\"Vitamins, Fats, Proteins\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly groups carbohydrates, proteins, and fats as macro-nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"An organism that depends on other plants or animals for food is following which type of nutrition?\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic nutrition\",\"Self-sufficient nutrition\",\"Heterotrophic nutrition\",\"Macro-nutrition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Organisms depending on others for food are described as having heterotrophic nutrition.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process by which plants produce their own food using sunlight and chlorophyll called?\",\"options\":[\"Respiration\",\"Photosynthesis\",\"Transpiration\",\"Absorption\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the process of making food using sunlight, chlorophyll, water, and carbon dioxide is called photosynthesis.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance present in the leaves is responsible for absorbing sunlight?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Glucose\",\"Chlorophyll\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that chlorophyll present in the leaves absorbs sunlight.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Through which microscopic openings do leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air?\",\"options\":[\"Chloroplasts\",\"Roots\",\"Stomata\",\"Stems\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines stomata as microscopic openings in the leaves through which plants take in CO2.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the specific organelle shown in the magnified red circle?\",\"options\":[\"Stomata\",\"Chloroplast\",\"Root hair\",\"Stem cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The label in the figure identifies the magnified organelle as a Chloroplast.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/037_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure and the text, what is the primary function of the chlorophyll contained within this organelle?\",\"options\":[\"To transport water to the stem\",\"To absorb sunlight for food production\",\"To release carbon dioxide into the air\",\"To absorb minerals from the soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, which absorbs sunlight to help convert CO2 and water into food.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/037_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Plants convert light energy into which form of energy to store it as food?\",\"options\":[\"Mechanical energy\",\"Chemical energy\",\"Electrical energy\",\"Heat energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that plants convert light energy into chemical energy and store it in the form of food.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for the food (glucose) produced during photosynthesis as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"6CO₂\",\"6H₂O\",\"C₆H₁₂O₆\",\"6O₂\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chemical equation in the text identifies glucose as C6H12O6.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides leaves, which other part of a plant can perform photosynthesis if it contains chlorophyll?\",\"options\":[\"Roots\",\"Green stems\",\"Dry bark\",\"Flowers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that photosynthesis takes place in green stems too, as they contain chlorophyll.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant\'s stomata are completely blocked, which part of the photosynthesis equation will be directly prevented from entering the leaf?\",\"options\":[\"Sunlight\",\"Water (H₂O)\",\"Carbon dioxide (CO₂)\",\"Chlorophyll\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Stomata are the openings through which CO2 is taken in from the air; blocking them prevents CO2 entry.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A gardener applies water and salts to the soil. Which plant part is responsible for the initial absorption of these materials for photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Leaves\",\"Roots\",\"Stems\",\"Chloroplasts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that water, minerals, and salts are absorbed by roots from the soil.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the photosynthesis equation provided, what are the two end-products of the process?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide and Water\",\"Sunlight and Chlorophyll\",\"Glucose and Oxygen\",\"Minerals and Salts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The equation shows Carbon dioxide + water results in food (glucose) + oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the role of the stem in the nutrition of autotrophic plants?\",\"options\":[\"It absorbs carbon dioxide from the air.\",\"It captures sunlight using chlorophyll.\",\"It transports water and minerals from roots to leaves.\",\"It is the only part where glucose is produced.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the stem transports water, minerals, and salts up to the leaves.\",\"topic\":\"Autotrophic plants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which two components make up the transport system of plants?\",\"options\":[\"Roots and leaves\",\"Xylem and phloem\",\"Stems and flowers\",\"Glucose and minerals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the transport system of plants consists of the xylem and the phloem.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific function of the xylem in the plant transport system?\",\"options\":[\"To transport food from the leaves\",\"To store glucose in the roots\",\"To transport minerals and water from the root\",\"To produce food through photosynthesis\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the xylem transports minerals and water from the root to all aerial parts.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is mentioned as an example of the food transported by the phloem?\",\"options\":[\"Ink\",\"Minerals\",\"Water\",\"Glucose\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions phloem transports food, giving glucose as an example.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From which part of the plant does the phloem start transporting food?\",\"options\":[\"The roots\",\"The leaves\",\"The stem\",\"The flowers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that phloem transports food from the leaves to other parts of the plant.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the food once it is transported to various parts of the plant by the phloem?\",\"options\":[\"It is excreted immediately\",\"It is converted into minerals\",\"It is either consumed or stored\",\"It is evaporated into the air\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that food transported to other parts is either consumed or stored.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Despite having a transport system, which systems are absent in plants?\",\"options\":[\"Circulatory and respiratory systems\",\"Nervous and muscular systems\",\"Digestive and excretory systems\",\"Reproductive and skeletal systems\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that plants do not have a separate digestive or excretory system.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with the pumpkin stem, why is ink added to the water?\",\"options\":[\"To provide nutrients to the leaves\",\"To help the plant grow faster\",\"To observe the changes and track the transport of water\",\"To prevent the stem from rotting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ink is used as a visual marker to observe how the transport system moves liquid through the stem.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to demonstrate the function of the xylem, where should they look for the transported material?\",\"options\":[\"Only in the roots\",\"In the aerial parts of the plant\",\"Only in the soil\",\"Inside the seeds only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The xylem carries water and minerals from the root to all aerial parts of the plant.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the plant is responsible for moving minerals to the leaves?\",\"options\":[\"Phloem\",\"Xylem\",\"Pumpkin stem\",\"Conical flask\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The xylem is the specific tissue that transports minerals from the root to aerial parts like leaves.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the transport system description, what is the primary difference between xylem and phloem movement?\",\"options\":[\"Xylem moves food, while phloem moves water\",\"Xylem moves minerals upward from roots, while phloem moves food from leaves\",\"Xylem stores food, while phloem consumes it\",\"Xylem is for excretion, while phloem is for digestion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes xylem moving water\\/minerals from roots and phloem moving food from leaves.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a pumpkin stem is placed in red ink water, and the leaves eventually show red streaks, which tissue is responsible for this observation?\",\"options\":[\"The digestive system\",\"The phloem\",\"The xylem\",\"The excretory system\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the ink is in the water, and xylem transports water to aerial parts (leaves), the xylem is responsible.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the phloem\'s role essential for the parts of the plant that do not have leaves?\",\"options\":[\"Because those parts need to excrete glucose\",\"Because those parts must receive food produced by the leaves\",\"Because those parts need to transport water to the roots\",\"Because those parts are responsible for chemosynthesis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Phloem transports food from the leaves to other parts where it is consumed or stored, supporting non-photosynthetic parts.\",\"topic\":\"Transport system in plants\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these elements is found in proteins but is not a component of carbohydrates?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, carbohydrates are made of C, H, and O, while proteins contain C, H, O, and Nitrogen.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is meant by the \'fixation\' of nitrogen in plants?\",\"options\":[\"Releasing nitrogen gas into the air\",\"Converting gaseous nitrogen into compounds\",\"Breaking down proteins into nitrogen\",\"Mixing nitrogen with carbohydrates\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines fixation as the process where nitrogen needs to be converted into compounds because plants cannot utilize gaseous nitrogen.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which micro-organism is specifically found in the root-nodules of leguminous plants?\",\"options\":[\"Azotobacter\",\"Rhizobium\",\"Nitric oxide\",\"Nitrogen dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that root-nodules of leguminous plants contain the Rhizobium micro-organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the swellings or small structures seen on the roots of the plant?\",\"options\":[\"Root-nodules\",\"Soil minerals\",\"Nitrogen salts\",\"Rainwater droplets\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the roots of a leguminous plant, which the text describes as containing root-nodules.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/038_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which micro-organism is actively absorbing atmospheric nitrogen within these root structures?\",\"options\":[\"Azotobacter\",\"Amoeba\",\"Rhizobium\",\"Yeast\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows root-nodules of a leguminous plant, and the text says Rhizobium in these nodules absorbs atmospheric nitrogen.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/038_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which micro-organism performs nitrogen fixation while being present directly in the soil rather than inside root nodules?\",\"options\":[\"Rhizobium\",\"Azotobacter\",\"Nitric acid\",\"Leguminous plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that micro-organisms like Azotobacter are present in soil and convert atmospheric nitrogen into nitrates.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"During atmospheric fixation, what is the first compound formed when nitrogen and oxygen react due to lightning?\",\"options\":[\"Nitric acid (HNO₃)\",\"Nitrogen dioxide (NO₂)\",\"Nitric oxide (NO)\",\"Nitrogen salts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that lightning causes nitrogen and oxygen to react to form nitric oxide (NO) first.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) when it comes into contact with rainwater during the rainy season?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into gaseous nitrogen\",\"It is converted into nitric acid (HNO₃)\",\"It reacts with minerals to form carbon\",\"It is absorbed directly by the leaves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that nitrogen dioxide dissolves in rainwater and is converted into nitric acid (HNO₃).\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a soil is rich in minerals, what is the final form of nitrogen that plants use for growth after atmospheric fixation?\",\"options\":[\"Gaseous nitrogen\",\"Nitric oxide gas\",\"Nitrogen salts\",\"Nitrogen dioxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that nitric acid reacts with minerals in the soil to convert into salts, which plants use for growth.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly describes the difference between biological and atmospheric nitrogen fixation?\",\"options\":[\"Biological fixation uses lightning, while atmospheric uses micro-organisms.\",\"Biological fixation produces carbohydrates, while atmospheric produces proteins.\",\"Biological fixation involves micro-organisms, while atmospheric involves lightning and rain.\",\"Biological fixation occurs in the air, while atmospheric occurs only in root-nodules.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text categorizes fixation into biological (micro-organisms like Rhizobium\\/Azotobacter) and atmospheric (lightning\\/rain).\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sequence of compounds formed during atmospheric nitrogen fixation?\",\"options\":[\"NO -> NO₂ -> HNO₃ -> Salts\",\"HNO₃ -> NO₂ -> NO -> Salts\",\"Salts -> HNO₃ -> NO₂ -> NO\",\"NO₂ -> NO -> HNO₃ -> Salts\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Following the text: Nitrogen + Oxygen -> Nitric oxide (NO) -> Nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) -> Nitric acid (HNO₃) -> Salts.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer notices that his leguminous crops are growing well. Which process is primarily responsible for providing the nitrogen needed for their protein synthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Photosynthesis of carbohydrates\",\"Biological fixation by Rhizobium\",\"Conversion of nitric acid into oxygen\",\"Absorption of gaseous nitrogen by leaves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Leguminous plants have Rhizobium in their root nodules which provides nitrogen through biological fixation for protein synthesis.\",\"topic\":\"Nitrogen Fixation\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the type of nutrition where two or more different types of plants live together to fulfill their needs with each others\' help?\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic nutrition\",\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"Parasitic nutrition\",\"Insectivorous nutrition\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines symbiotic nutrition as two or more plants living together to fulfill needs like nutrition, protection, and support with each others\' help.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When certain fungi grow around the roots of other plants, what do the fungi supply to these plants?\",\"options\":[\"Nutrients\",\"Food\",\"Minerals and water\",\"Shelter and protection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, fungi growing around roots supply minerals and water to the plants.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the symbiotic relationship between algae and fungi, what does the fungus provide to the algae?\",\"options\":[\"Food and nutrients\",\"Water, minerals, and shelter\",\"Only sunlight\",\"Support and oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that fungi provide water, minerals, as well as shelter to algae.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a specific example of symbiosis between algae and fungi mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Mushroom\",\"Lichen\",\"Root nodules\",\"Moss\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions Lichen as an example of symbiosis between algae and fungi.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which component of this organism is responsible for providing food to the other?\",\"options\":[\"The fungus\",\"The roots\",\"The algae\",\"The soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a Lichen; the text explains that in this symbiosis, the algae provide food to the fungi.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/038_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Besides nutrition and support, what is another need fulfilled through symbiotic nutrition according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Reproduction\",\"Photosynthesis\",\"Protection\",\"Locomotion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that plants live together to fulfill needs of nutrition, protection, and support.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The organism shown in the given figure is formed by the symbiotic relationship between:\",\"options\":[\"Algae and fungi\",\"Fungi and plant roots\",\"Two different types of algae\",\"Bacteria and fungi\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a Lichen, which the text defines as a symbiosis between algae and fungi.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/038_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant is supplying nutrients to a fungus growing around its roots, what is the fungus likely doing for the plant?\",\"options\":[\"Providing food through photosynthesis\",\"Providing minerals and water\",\"Providing shelter and support\",\"Providing protection from sunlight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when plants supply nutrients to fungi, the fungi in turn supply minerals and water.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the relationship between algae and fungi in a lichen considered \'symbiotic\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because the fungus eats the algae\",\"Because they live together and help fulfill each other\'s needs\",\"Because they compete for the same food\",\"Because the algae lives as a parasite on the fungus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Symbiosis involves living together to fulfill needs with each others\' help, which algae and fungi do in a lichen.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"An organism provides shelter and minerals to its partner, while the partner provides it with food. This is an example of:\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic nutrition\",\"Saprophytic nutrition\",\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"Root nutrition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This mutual exchange of needs (shelter\\/minerals for food) describes the symbiotic nutrition process mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Symbiotic nutrition\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What essential substance is missing in heterotrophic plants that prevents them from making their own food?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Minerals\",\"Chlorophyll\",\"Oxygen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that heterotrophic plants do not contain chlorophyll.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the yellow, wire-like, leafless climber plant mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Loranthus\",\"Cuscuta\",\"Hibiscus\",\"Rose\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the yellow, wire-like, leafless climber as Cuscuta.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Plants that grow on the body of other plants to obtain food are known as ______ plants.\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic\",\"Insectivorous\",\"Parasitic\",\"Saprophytic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines plants that grow on other plants for food as parasitic plants.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is Cuscuta classified as a \'completely\' parasitic plant?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is yellow in color\",\"Because it is a climber\",\"Because it lacks chlorophyll entirely\",\"Because it grows on big trees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, due to the absence of chlorophyll, Cuscuta is completely dependent on the host plant.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which plant is shown growing as a parasite on the branches of a tree?\",\"options\":[\"Cuscuta\",\"Loranthus\",\"Lichen\",\"Mushroom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The caption for the figure explicitly identifies the plant as Loranthus.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/039_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the green leaves visible in the given figure, which life process can this plant perform on its own?\",\"options\":[\"Absorption of minerals from soil\",\"Photosynthesis\",\"Absorption of water from soil\",\"Complete independent growth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that Loranthus has parts that carry out photosynthesis, which is supported by the green leaves shown in the figure.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/039_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"From where does a parasitic plant like Loranthus obtain its minerals and water?\",\"options\":[\"From the soil\",\"From the air\",\"From the host plant\",\"From the rain\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Parasitic plants grow on the body of other plants (host plants) to obtain their requirements.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant has no leaves and is yellow in color, how would you categorize its nutrition based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Partially parasitic\",\"Completely parasitic\",\"Autotrophic\",\"Symbiotic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the leafless, yellow Cuscuta as a completely parasitic plant.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between Loranthus and Cuscuta based on the information provided?\",\"options\":[\"Loranthus is a climber, while Cuscuta is a tree\",\"Cuscuta is completely parasitic, while Loranthus is partially parasitic\",\"Loranthus lacks chlorophyll, while Cuscuta has it\",\"Cuscuta grows on the ground, while Loranthus grows on trees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes Cuscuta as completely parasitic and asks why Loranthus is known as partially parasitic.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a characteristic of heterotrophic plants mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They produce their own chlorophyll\",\"They do not contain chlorophyll\",\"They always grow in the soil\",\"They provide food to other trees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The very first sentence of the text states that heterotrophic plants do not contain chlorophyll.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is Loranthus specifically called a \'partially\' parasitic plant rather than a \'completely\' parasitic one?\",\"options\":[\"It does not need water\",\"It can produce its own food through photosynthesis\",\"It does not grow on a host plant\",\"It is not a climber\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Loranthus is partially parasitic because it has green parts to carry out photosynthesis but relies on the host for water and minerals.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a plant like Cuscuta because it lacks chlorophyll?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes a host for other plants\",\"It becomes completely dependent on the host plant\",\"It starts absorbing food from the air\",\"It develops green leaves later\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the absence of chlorophyll, Cuscuta is completely dependent on the host plant.\",\"topic\":\"Heterotrophic plants\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which type of environment do insectivorous plants generally grow?\",\"options\":[\"Soil rich in minerals\",\"Soil or water deficient in nitrogen compounds\",\"Dry sandy deserts\",\"Deep ocean floors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that insectivorous plants generally grow in soil or water deficient in nitrogen compounds.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist identified the plant Drosera burmanii in Sri Lanka in 1737?\",\"options\":[\"Charles Darwin\",\"Louis Pasteur\",\"Johannes Burman\",\"Robert Hooke\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the scientist Johannes Burman identified this plant in Sri Lanka in 1737.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of the sticky substance found at the tips of the hairs on Drosera burmanii leaves?\",\"options\":[\"To attract insects\",\"To perform photosynthesis\",\"To store water\",\"To protect the plant from sunlight\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Droplets of a sticky substance found at the tips of the hairs are used to attract insects.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the pitcher plant feed on insects even though it can produce food by photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Because it cannot absorb water\",\"Because it needs to stay cool\",\"Because it lacks chlorophyll\",\"To obtain necessary nitrogen compounds\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Insectivorous plants like the pitcher plant feed on insects to obtain nutrients, specifically nitrogen, which is deficient in their soil.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant is observed obtaining its food from the dead and decaying body of another organism, it is classified as a:\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic plant\",\"Parasitic plant\",\"Saprophytic plant\",\"Insectivorous plant\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plants which obtain food from dead and decaying bodies are called saprophytic plants.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What do fungi secrete on dead remains to breakdown the carbon compounds they contain?\",\"options\":[\"Sticky droplets\",\"Digestive enzymes\",\"Chlorophyll\",\"Nitrogen compounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fungi secrete digestive enzymes on dead remains to digest or breakdown carbon compounds.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following fungi is specifically mentioned as being used in fermentation processes?\",\"options\":[\"Yeast\",\"Drosera\",\"Pitcher plant\",\"Mushroom\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that yeast is used in fermentation processes and for making bread.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Mushrooms are considered a beneficial food source because they are rich in:\",\"options\":[\"Sugar and fats\",\"Nitrogen and sticky substances\",\"Carbon compounds only\",\"Iron and vitamins\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, mushrooms are a rich source of iron and vitamins.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A baker wants to make a loaf of bread. Based on the text, which organism should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Drosera burmanii\",\"Yeast\",\"A pitcher plant\",\"Medicinal fungi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Yeast is used for making bread as per the \'Always remember\' section.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the information provided, which statement correctly describes the diverse roles of fungi?\",\"options\":[\"All fungi are poisonous and cause diseases\",\"All fungi are used only for making bread\",\"Some fungi cause illness while others have medicinal properties\",\"Fungi are the only plants that perform photosynthesis\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that some fungi cause diseases while some have medicinal properties.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the characteristic appearance of the plant body?\",\"options\":[\"Flower-like appearance\",\"Tall tree-like structure\",\"Thick woody stem\",\"Leafless vine\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text describing the plant in the figure states the plant body has a flower-like appearance.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/039_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of nutrition is shown by the organism growing on the decaying matter?\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic\",\"Parasitic\",\"Insectivorous\",\"Saprophytic\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a saprophytic plant (fungi) which obtains food from dead and decaying bodies.\",\"topic\":\"Insectivorous and Saprophytic plants\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/039_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which nutrient is an important component of proteins, chlorophyll, and cytoplasm in plants?\",\"options\":[\"Phosphorus\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Potassium\",\"Zinc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Nitrogen is an important component of proteins, chlorophyll, and cytoplasm.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of Manganese in a plant\'s growth?\",\"options\":[\"Conversion of light energy\",\"Production of chlorophyll\",\"Production of main plant hormones\",\"Metabolic activities\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Manganese is responsible for the production of main plant hormones.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which nutrient deficiency leads to early leaf-fall and late flowering?\",\"options\":[\"Phosphorus\",\"Magnesium\",\"Iron\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Early leaf-fall and late flowering are specific deficiency effects of Phosphorus.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which nutrient is necessary for the metabolic activities of a plant?\",\"options\":[\"Zinc\",\"Manganese\",\"Potassium\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Potassium is listed as being necessary for metabolic activities.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant is unable to convert light energy into chemical energy, which nutrient is likely deficient?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen\",\"Phosphorus\",\"Magnesium\",\"Potassium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The function of Phosphorus is the conversion of light energy into chemical energy.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a deficiency in Zinc affect the growth of a plant?\",\"options\":[\"It causes spotted leaves\",\"It leads to a weak stem\",\"It causes retarded growth and yellowing of leaves\",\"It results in late flowering\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists retarded growth and yellowing of leaves as effects of Zinc deficiency.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is a common deficiency effect shared by Nitrogen, Magnesium, Iron, and Zinc?\",\"options\":[\"Spotted leaves\",\"Yellowing of leaves\",\"Slow growth of roots\",\"Weak stem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Yellowing of leaves is a common deficiency effect for Nitrogen, Magnesium, Iron, and Zinc.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is Magnesium considered essential for the green color of plants?\",\"options\":[\"It produces plant hormones\",\"It is needed for the production of chlorophyll\",\"It helps in root growth\",\"It converts light energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Magnesium is essential because its function is the production of chlorophyll, which gives plants their green color.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer observes that his crops have very weak stems and the leaves are wilting. Which nutrient should he provide to the plants?\",\"options\":[\"Phosphorus\",\"Potassium\",\"Manganese\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Weak stems and wilting of leaves are symptoms of Potassium deficiency.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A gardener notices that a plant\'s roots are growing very slowly and the leaves are falling off earlier than usual. Which nutrient deficiency is the plant suffering from?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen\",\"Zinc\",\"Phosphorus\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Slow growth of roots and early leaf-fall are effects of Phosphorus deficiency.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which nutrient deficiency is uniquely identified by the appearance of \'spotted leaves\'?\",\"options\":[\"Zinc\",\"Manganese\",\"Magnesium\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the table, Manganese is the only nutrient listed that causes spotted leaves when deficient.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/040_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table in the given figure, which two nutrients have the exact same listed function regarding chlorophyll?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen and Iron\",\"Magnesium and Manganese\",\"Magnesium and Iron\",\"Nitrogen and Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows that both Magnesium and Iron have the function \'Production of chlorophyll\'.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrient Deficiency in Plants\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/040_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the process of nutrition in animals?\",\"options\":[\"Digestion\",\"Ingestion\",\"Absorption\",\"Assimilation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, ingestion is the first step where food is taken into the body.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which process involves the conversion of food into simple soluble forms?\",\"options\":[\"Egestion\",\"Assimilation\",\"Digestion\",\"Ingestion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Digestion is defined in the text as the conversion of food into simple soluble forms.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary for food to be converted into soluble forms in the body?\",\"options\":[\"To make the food taste better\",\"To allow it to mix easily with blood\",\"To help the animal move faster\",\"To prevent the need for egestion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states food needs to convert into soluble forms so that it can easily mix in blood.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific name of the red object that the organism is approaching and eventually taking inside?\",\"options\":[\"Nucleus\",\"Pseudopodia\",\"Food particle\",\"Enzyme\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The labels in the figure identify the red object being taken in as a \'Food particle\'.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/041_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, how does this unicellular organism take in its food despite having no mouth?\",\"options\":[\"It uses its nucleus to grab the food\",\"It surrounds the food particle from all sides\",\"It waits for the food to dissolve in water\",\"It uses a tube-like proboscis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that Amoeba surrounds the food particle from all sides to take it into the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/041_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these processes describes the utilization of absorbed food for energy production and growth?\",\"options\":[\"Assimilation\",\"Absorption\",\"Digestion\",\"Egestion\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Assimilation is the utilization of absorbed food by cells and tissues for energy, growth, and repair.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following organisms performs all steps of nutrition within a single cell?\",\"options\":[\"Cockroach\",\"Butterfly\",\"Amoeba\",\"Grasshopper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in unicellular animals like Amoeba, all steps of nutrition occur within their unicellular body.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A butterfly uses a tube-like ______ to suck its food.\",\"options\":[\"Pseudopodia\",\"Proboscis\",\"Jaw\",\"Needle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that butterflies suck food with a tube-like proboscis.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which insects have jaw-like mouth-parts used for nibbling?\",\"options\":[\"Mosquitoes and bedbugs\",\"Butterflies and moths\",\"Cockroaches and grasshoppers\",\"Amoeba and Euglena\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that insects like the cockroach and grasshopper which nibble have jaw-like mouth-parts.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the undigested food in an Amoeba?\",\"options\":[\"It is converted into energy immediately\",\"It is left behind as the animal moves away\",\"It is absorbed into the blood\",\"It is swallowed by the nucleus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that undigested food is left behind as the amoeba moves further with the help of pseudopodia.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a mosquito needs to feed on blood, which mouth-parts does it use according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Jaw-like parts for chewing\",\"A tube-like proboscis only\",\"A needle-like part to pierce and a tube-like part to suck\",\"Pseudopodia to surround the blood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions mosquitoes use a needle-like mouth part to pierce and a tube-like mouth part to suck blood.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The transfer of soluble food to the blood is known as ______.\",\"options\":[\"Absorption\",\"Assimilation\",\"Ingestion\",\"Digestion\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Absorption is defined as the transfer of soluble food to the blood.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a method of ingestion mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Photosynthesis\",\"Scraping\",\"Circulation\",\"Respiration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists swallowing, chewing, sucking, scraping, and gnawing as methods of ingestion.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the correct sequence of the steps of nutrition.\",\"options\":[\"Ingestion -> Absorption -> Digestion -> Assimilation -> Egestion\",\"Ingestion -> Digestion -> Absorption -> Assimilation -> Egestion\",\"Digestion -> Ingestion -> Absorption -> Egestion -> Assimilation\",\"Absorption -> Digestion -> Ingestion -> Assimilation -> Egestion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists the steps in order: 1. Ingestion, 2. Digestion, 3. Absorption, 4. Assimilation, 5. Egestion.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a unicellular animal mentioned alongside Amoeba and Euglena?\",\"options\":[\"Grasshopper\",\"Paramoecium\",\"Bedbug\",\"Cockroach\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists Amoeba, Euglena, and Paramoecium as examples of unicellular animals.\",\"topic\":\"Nutrition in animals\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Animals that use plants directly as their food are classified as which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Herbivores\",\"Carnivores\",\"Scavengers\",\"Decomposers\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, herbivores use plants directly as their food.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of an omnivore mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Vulture\",\"Hyena\",\"Chimpanzee\",\"Anteater\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists monkey, chimpanzee, and human as examples of omnivores.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are carnivores considered to be indirectly dependent on plants for their food?\",\"options\":[\"They eat seeds and fruits occasionally\",\"They feed on herbivores which eat plants\",\"They decompose dead plant matter\",\"They help in the growth of new plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carnivores feed on other animals (like herbivores) that eat plants, making them indirectly dependent on plants.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function performed by scavengers and decomposers in the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Providing food for herbivores\",\"Hunting live prey to control population\",\"Cleaning and conserving the environment\",\"Directly producing energy from sunlight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that scavengers and decomposers perform the function of cleaning and conserving the environment.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A bird that feeds exclusively on seeds would be specifically classified as a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Frugivore\",\"Granivore\",\"Insectivore\",\"Predator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines granivores as seed-eaters.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an organism obtains its food by breaking down the dead bodies of other organisms, it is classified as a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Scavenger\",\"Herbivore\",\"Decomposer\",\"Omnivore\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Decomposers are microbes that obtain food by decomposing dead bodies or materials.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what distinguishes a scavenger from a decomposer?\",\"options\":[\"Scavengers eat plants while decomposers eat animals\",\"Scavengers obtain food from dead bodies while decomposers break them down\",\"Scavengers are microbes while decomposers are large birds\",\"Scavengers are predators while decomposers are insectivores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Scavengers eat dead bodies (like vultures), while decomposers are microbes that decompose the material.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which category of carnivores specifically refers to animals that feed on insects?\",\"options\":[\"Predators\",\"Granivores\",\"Frugivores\",\"Insectivores\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines insectivores as animals that feed on insects.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which geographical region is the bobcat or \'udmanjar\' naturally found?\",\"options\":[\"Central America\",\"South America\",\"India\",\"Africa\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the bobcat or udmanjar is found in India.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which animal can be classified as a scavenger based on its feeding habit shown?\",\"options\":[\"The Lion\",\"The Deer\",\"The Vulture\",\"The Chameleon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The vulture is shown near a carcass and is listed in the text as a scavenger.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/041_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the animal shown is an anteater. According to the text, where is this animal found?\",\"options\":[\"India\",\"Central and South America\",\"Only in North America\",\"Africa and Asia\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the figure states the anteater is found in Central and South America.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/042_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How would you classify the human being shown in the given figure based on the text\'s classification of food habits?\",\"options\":[\"Herbivore\",\"Carnivore\",\"Omnivore\",\"Scavenger\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists humans as examples of omnivores because they obtain food from both plants and animals.\",\"topic\":\"Classification by food habits\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/041_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the mode of nutrition where animals absorb liquid organic material from dead bodies or the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic nutrition\",\"Saprozoic nutrition\",\"Ectoparasitic nutrition\",\"Endoparasitic nutrition\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines saprozoic nutrition as obtaining nutrients by absorbing liquid organic material from dead bodies or the environment.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following animals is an example of a saprozoic organism according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Tick\",\"Tapeworm\",\"Housefly\",\"Bedbug\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists houseflies, ants, and spiders as examples of saprozoic nutrition.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Animals that live on the body surface of other animals and suck their blood are called:\",\"options\":[\"Endoparasites\",\"Saprozoic animals\",\"Ectoparasites\",\"Unicellular animals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines ectoparasites as animals that live on the body surface and obtain food by sucking blood.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do endoparasites like roundworms live to obtain their food?\",\"options\":[\"On the skin of other animals\",\"In the environment\",\"Inside the body of other animals\",\"On dead bodies of animals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that endoparasites like tapeworms and roundworms live inside the body of other animals.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a common characteristic of both lice and ticks?\",\"options\":[\"They live inside the intestine\",\"They are ectoparasites\",\"They absorb liquid from dead bodies\",\"They are unicellular animals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Louse and ticks are listed together as examples of ectoparasites that live on the body surface.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what do saprozoic animals like spiders absorb for their nutrition?\",\"options\":[\"Solid food particles\",\"Blood from living animals\",\"Liquid organic material\",\"Nutrients from green plants\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states saprozoic animals obtain nutrients by absorbing liquid organic material.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a small animal is found attached to the skin of a dog and is sucking its blood, what type of nutrition is it showing?\",\"options\":[\"Saprozoic nutrition\",\"Ectoparasitic nutrition\",\"Endoparasitic nutrition\",\"Autotrophic nutrition\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Living on the body surface and sucking blood is defined as ectoparasitic nutrition.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a worm living inside the intestine of a buffalo. How should this worm be classified?\",\"options\":[\"As an ectoparasite\",\"As a saprozoic insect\",\"As an endoparasite\",\"As a unicellular animal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Animals living inside the body of other animals for food are known as endoparasites.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary difference between the feeding habits of a housefly and a bedbug based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"The housefly lives inside the host, while the bedbug lives outside.\",\"The housefly absorbs liquid from dead matter, while the bedbug sucks blood from a living host.\",\"The housefly is a parasite, while the bedbug is saprozoic.\",\"The housefly is an ectoparasite, while the bedbug is an endoparasite.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Houseflies are saprozoic (dead matter\\/environment), while bedbugs are ectoparasites (suck blood from living animals).\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are tapeworms and roundworms grouped together in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they both live on the surface of the skin.\",\"Because they both absorb liquid from the environment.\",\"Because they both live inside the body of other animals.\",\"Because they are both unicellular insects.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both are examples of endoparasites that live inside the body of other animals.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT an example of an ectoparasite mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Louse\",\"Tick\",\"Bedbug\",\"Ant\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists ants as an example of saprozoic nutrition, not ectoparasitic nutrition.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definitions provided, what is the essential requirement for parasitic nutrition?\",\"options\":[\"The animal must be unicellular.\",\"The animal must depend on another animal for food.\",\"The animal must only eat dead bodies.\",\"The animal must live in the environment.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in parasitic nutrition, animals depend upon other animals for food.\",\"topic\":\"Saprozoic and Parasitic nutrition\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21990,46,7,'Science','en','Food Safety','[\"Food contains carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water – all needed for growth.\",\"Carbohydrates give energy; proteins build and repair tissues; fats store energy and protect organs.\",\"Vitamins are organic compounds needed in small amounts; minerals are inorganic elements like iron and calcium.\",\"Fibre helps digestion and keeps the gut healthy.\",\"Water keeps body temperature normal and transports nutrients.\",\"Food spoilage is a change in colour, odour, texture or taste caused by microbes, insects, chemicals or improper handling.\",\"Spoiled food can make us sick – symptoms include nausea, vomiting and stomach pain.\",\"Food preservation slows down or stops spoilage by using methods like freezing, drying, boiling, smoking, adding preservatives or irradiation.\",\"Pasteurisation heats food (e.g., milk) to 72 °C for 15 s to kill harmful microbes while keeping taste.\",\"Adulteration is adding harmful or useless substances (e.g., sand, iron filings) or removing useful parts (e.g., fat from milk).\",\"To detect adulteration, simple tests include adding water to milk and looking for separation, using a magnet to find iron filings, or adding acid to see colour change.\",\"Proper storage (cool, dry, sealed) and checking expiry dates help keep food safe.\",\"Avoid over‑cooking and over‑serving; use leftovers wisely to reduce waste.\",\"When buying food, buy only what you need and store it properly to prevent spoilage.\",\"Food safety is important for everyone – young, old, rich or poor – because contaminated food can cause illness or death.\",\"The Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) sets rules for safe food handling and storage.\",\"Freezing keeps food safe by stopping microbial growth; drying removes water that microbes need.\",\"Smoking adds flavour and preserves food by depositing chemicals that inhibit microbes.\",\"Irradiation uses high‑energy rays to kill microbes and insects in food.\",\"Chemical preservatives like sodium benzoate, citric acid and vinegar keep food safe for a long time.\",\"Natural preservatives include salt, sugar and oil, which reduce water activity and stop microbial growth.\",\"Food safety education helps us make better choices and protect our health.\",\"Food waste can be reduced by planning meals, using leftovers, and storing food correctly.\",\"When food is stored at 5 °C, it stays safe for a short period; longer storage needs refrigeration or freezing.\",\"A balanced diet with fresh fruits and vegetables reduces the risk of food‑borne diseases.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the text as a constituent of food?\",\"options\":[\"Carbohydrates\",\"Proteins\",\"Oxygen\",\"Fibre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists carbohydrates, fats, proteins, vitamins, minerals, fibre, and water as food constituents; oxygen is not listed.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why are all constituents of food necessary?\",\"options\":[\"To make the food taste better\",\"For the proper growth of the body\",\"To change the color of the food\",\"To make the food cook faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that all constituents are necessary for the proper growth of the body.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these food materials are mentioned as sources of nutrients in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Jowar, wheat, and pulses\",\"Sugar and salt\",\"Processed snacks\",\"Carbonated drinks\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions wheat, jowar, pulses, rice, vegetables, and fruits as sources of nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text suggest could happen to food materials that might be harmful?\",\"options\":[\"They can become more nutritious\",\"They can be infested by insects or spoiled\",\"They can turn into water\",\"They can grow into new plants automatically\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks what will happen if food materials are infested by insects or spoiled in any way.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which category of food provides us with the constituents needed for growth?\",\"options\":[\"Only grains like wheat and rice\",\"Only vegetables and fruits\",\"A variety of materials including grains, pulses, and vegetables\",\"Only water and fibre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists a wide range of food materials (wheat, jowar, pulses, rice, vegetables, fruits) that provide necessary nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person consumes fruits and vegetables, which specific constituents mentioned in the text are they likely to obtain?\",\"options\":[\"Only fats\",\"Vitamins, minerals, and fibre\",\"Only proteins\",\"Only water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fruits and vegetables are primary sources for vitamins, minerals, and fibre among the listed constituents.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student eats a meal consisting of rice and dal (pulses). According to the text and the recall chart, which two major nutrients are they primarily consuming?\",\"options\":[\"Fats and Fibre\",\"Vitamins and Minerals\",\"Carbohydrates and Proteins\",\"Water and Oil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rice (Sr. No. 1) provides carbohydrates and pulses (Sr. No. 2) provide proteins.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a food material is \'infested by insects\' as mentioned in the text, what is the most likely consequence for the body\'s growth?\",\"options\":[\"Growth will be faster\",\"The nutrients may be lost or harmful, affecting proper growth\",\"The food will provide more energy\",\"The body will stop needing water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Spoiled or infested food loses its nutritional value and can be harmful, hindering the \'proper growth\' mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which nutrient should be correctly filled in the \'Nutrients obtained\' column for Sr. No. 1?\",\"options\":[\"Proteins\",\"Fats\",\"Carbohydrates\",\"Vitamins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Jowar, wheat, millet, and rice are primary sources of carbohydrates.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/044_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which food source corresponds to the nutrient \'Proteins\' in Sr. No. 2?\",\"options\":[\"Oil, ghee, etc.\",\"Beans and pulses\",\"Fruits and vegetables\",\"Jowar and wheat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Beans and pulses are the standard food sources for obtaining proteins.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/044_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what color do the skins of some fruits turn when they spoil?\",\"options\":[\"Bright red\",\"Black\",\"Pale yellow\",\"Green\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that sometimes fruits or their skins turn black when they are spoiled.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic sign of spoiled food mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Increase in weight\",\"Change in texture\",\"Decrease in price\",\"Change in shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines food as spoiled if there is a change in its colour, odour, texture, quality, taste, and nutrient content.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person stores food grains in a damp place, what is the most likely result according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The nutrient content increases\",\"The food becomes easier to cook\",\"The quality of the food materials deteriorates\",\"The food stays fresh for a longer time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if food is improperly stored, say in a damp place, the quality of the food materials deteriorates.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it essential to consume foodstuffs that are of the best quality?\",\"options\":[\"To save money on medicines\",\"To ensure the food tastes very sweet\",\"To avoid falling prey to disease or illness\",\"To make the food look attractive\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that we must consume best quality food, otherwise, we may fall prey to disease or illness.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Besides animal sources, from which other source must foodstuffs be of the best quality?\",\"options\":[\"Plant source\",\"Mineral source\",\"Synthetic source\",\"Chemical source\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that foodstuffs we consume, whether of animal or plant source, must be of the best quality.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the nutrient content of food when it undergoes spoilage?\",\"options\":[\"It remains unchanged\",\"It is doubled\",\"There is a loss of nutrient content\",\"It turns into minerals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that food is said to be spoiled if there is a loss of its nutrient content.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Over-cooking food is categorized in the text as an example of spoilage due to which factor?\",\"options\":[\"Transportation\",\"Wrong handling\",\"Natural aging\",\"Plant source defects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says naturally available food materials are spoiled by wrong handling as when they are over-cooked.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Food spoilage that occurs while moving goods from the farm to the market is known as spoilage during...\",\"options\":[\"Storage\",\"Cooking\",\"Transportation\",\"Handling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that food materials may also be spoiled during transportation.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what visible factor is a major cause of food spoilage at this street stall?\",\"options\":[\"The use of large metal pans\",\"Food being kept uncovered and exposed to flies\",\"The vendor wearing a purple shirt\",\"The customer standing too close\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows food items kept in the open with flies hovering around, which relates to \'wrong handling\' and \'improper storage\' mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/044_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which condition of the selling area could lead to the deterioration of the quality of the vegetables?\",\"options\":[\"The seller using a weighing scale\",\"The seller wearing a blue saree\",\"Selling produce on the ground near waste and litter\",\"The customer carrying a yellow bag\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows produce on the ground with litter around, which is an example of \'improper storage\' and \'wrong handling\' leading to spoilage.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/044_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what specific storage problem is shown that leads to food spoilage?\",\"options\":[\"The sacks are too heavy to lift\",\"The sacks are made of cloth\",\"Improper storage resulting in a torn sack and spilled grain\",\"The sacks are stacked in a pyramid shape\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows grain leaking from a torn sack on the ground, illustrating \'improper storage\' mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/044_105.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the result when naturally available food materials are stored in a damp place?\",\"options\":[\"Their taste improves\",\"Their quality deteriorates\",\"Their color becomes brighter\",\"Their nutrient content increases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when food is improperly stored, say in a damp place, the quality of the food materials deteriorates.\",\"topic\":\"Food spoilage\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what happens to peanuts when they undergo changes due to factors within them?\",\"options\":[\"They turn black\",\"They become sour\",\"They become rancid\",\"They become alkaline\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that peanuts become rancid due to factors within the food materials.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a specific sign of spoilage in meat mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It turns black\",\"It becomes sour\",\"It becomes rancid\",\"It turns into a fruit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that meat becomes sour as a change occurring in food materials.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Food grains produced in farms are often damaged due to which of the following reasons?\",\"options\":[\"Internal chemical reactions\",\"Natural sweetness of grains\",\"Improper handling and storage\",\"Contact with acidic fruits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that food grains are damaged due to improper handling, storage, and transportation.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do foodstuffs like meat and milk sometimes get spoiled when they come in contact with metals?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are either acidic or alkaline\",\"Because they are always stored in farms\",\"Because they contain insects\",\"Because they turn black naturally\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that meat and milk are acidic or alkaline and may spoil due to chemical reactions with metals.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a fruit has turned black, what is the primary cause according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Improper transportation only\",\"Factors within the food material\",\"Contact with metal containers\",\"Lack of water during growth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that fruits turning black is a change that occurs due to factors within the food materials.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Contamination of food by micro-organisms or insects can occur from which surrounding sources?\",\"options\":[\"Only from metal containers\",\"Only from improper handling\",\"Air, water or soil\",\"Internal acidity of the food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that foodstuffs may be spoiled due to contamination by micro-organisms or insects from the surrounding air, water or soil.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is categorized as a \'chemical reaction\' causing food spoilage in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Peanuts becoming rancid\",\"Fruits turning black\",\"Spoilage due to contact with metals\",\"Damage during transportation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically links chemical reactions to the contact of acidic or alkaline foods with metals.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When discussing street foods like pav-bhaji or pizzas, which factor is highlighted as a concern for food safety?\",\"options\":[\"The cost of the ingredients\",\"The color of the serving plates\",\"Whether they were served hygienically\",\"The distance from the farm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks the reader to consider if these foods were displayed and served hygienically.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer notices his harvested grain is rotting because it was left in a damp, open field. This spoilage is best described as being caused by:\",\"options\":[\"Internal factors of the grain\",\"Improper storage and handling\",\"Acidity of the grain\",\"Reaction with metal tools\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Leaving grain in a damp field is an example of improper storage, which the text lists as a reason for damage.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these factors causing food spoilage is considered an \'internal factor\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Improper transportation\",\"Insects from the soil\",\"Changes that make fruits turn black\",\"Contamination from water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies fruits turning black as a change occurring due to factors within the food materials.\",\"topic\":\"Factors that cause spoilage of food\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which two popular Indian street foods are shown as examples of \'our favourites\'?\",\"options\":[\"Pizza and Burger\",\"Vada-pav and Pani-puri\",\"Pav-bhaji and Shev-puri\",\"Samosa and Kachori\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows a Vada-pav (left) and a plate of Pani-puri (right), which are mentioned in the text.\",\"topi