nderstand\"},{\"q\":\"What type of wastage occurs due to the custom of offering too much food to guests at traditional feasts?\",\"options\":[\"Qualitative wastage\",\"Quantitative wastage\",\"Chemical wastage\",\"Biological wastage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Serving too much food at banquets is categorized under quantitative wastage in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person washes vegetables after cutting them, what kind of wastage are they causing?\",\"options\":[\"Quantitative wastage\",\"Qualitative wastage\",\"Distribution wastage\",\"Storage wastage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Washing vegetables after cutting them is listed as a cause of qualitative wastage.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a cause of qualitative wastage of food?\",\"options\":[\"Inadequate threshing\",\"Wrong methods of distribution\",\"Over-cooking of food\",\"Improper storage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Over-cooking is specifically mentioned as a cause of qualitative wastage.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Mis-handling of which specific fruits is mentioned as a cause of qualitative wastage?\",\"options\":[\"Apples and bananas\",\"Grapes and mangoes\",\"Oranges and lemons\",\"Papayas and guavas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the mis-handling of grapes and mangoes.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does mis-calculating transport time from producers to consumers affect food?\",\"options\":[\"It increases the quantity of food\",\"It results in qualitative wastage\",\"It helps in better distribution\",\"It prevents hunger\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mis-calculation of transport time is listed as a cause of qualitative wastage.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Inadequate threshing is categorized under which type of food wastage?\",\"options\":[\"Qualitative wastage\",\"Quantitative wastage\",\"Nutritional wastage\",\"Preservation wastage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Inadequate threshing is listed as a reason for quantitative wastage of food.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these actions would lead to qualitative wastage rather than quantitative?\",\"options\":[\"Hand sowing of seeds\",\"Improper storage\",\"Excessive use of food preservatives\",\"Wrong methods of distribution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Excessive use of food preservatives affects the quality, whereas the others affect the quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the consequence of food being wasted at banquets according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It helps the farmers earn more\",\"It could have met the need of many others\",\"It improves the quality of the next meal\",\"It reduces the need for transport\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that had the food not been wasted at feasts, it could have met the need of many others.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT listed as a cause of qualitative wastage?\",\"options\":[\"Over-cooking\",\"Mis-handling fruits\",\"Washing vegetables after cutting\",\"Wrong methods of distribution\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Wrong methods of distribution is listed under quantitative wastage, not qualitative.\",\"topic\":\"Food wastage\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is \'World Food Day\' celebrated every year?\",\"options\":[\"16th September\",\"16th October\",\"15th August\",\"26th January\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text and the figure, World Food Day is observed on 16th October.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for checking the expiry date on bottled or canned food?\",\"options\":[\"To check the price of the food\",\"To see the name of the company\",\"To ensure the food is used within a safe time\",\"To find out the weight of the packet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states we should check the expiry date and use the food within that time to prevent spoilage.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what should be done with left-over food instead of throwing it away?\",\"options\":[\"Keep it in the open for a long time\",\"Re-use it in a proper way\",\"Mix it with fresh grains\",\"Give it to someone immediately without heating\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises to avoid throwing away left-over food and to re-use it in a proper way.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text advise us to avoid making excessive purchases of grains and vegetables?\",\"options\":[\"To save space in the kitchen\",\"To prevent food wastage and spoilage\",\"Because the shops might run out of stock\",\"To increase the price of the food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Buying only what is needed helps avoid the temptation of excessive purchases which leads to wastage.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is specifically mentioned as a \'perishable foodstuff\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Dried spices\",\"Salt\",\"Milk\",\"Sugar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists vegetables, fruits, and milk as examples of perishable foodstuffs.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What habit regarding serving food is suggested to prevent wastage?\",\"options\":[\"Serve more than you can eat\",\"Serve yourself only as much as you can eat\",\"Always leave some food on the plate\",\"Serve only one type of food at a time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Serving only the amount one can consume ensures that no food is left to be thrown away.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of wedding feasts, which grain is mentioned as being wasted when used as \'akshata\'?\",\"options\":[\"Wheat grains\",\"Bajra grains\",\"Rice grains\",\"Jowar grains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to discuss how to avoid the use of rice grains as \'akshata\' to prevent wastage.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which cooking habit helps in preventing the loss of food quality and wastage?\",\"options\":[\"Cooking food on very high flame\",\"Avoiding over-cooking food\",\"Adding extra water to all dishes\",\"Cooking large quantities every day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Avoiding over-cooking is listed as a step to prevent food wastage and spoilage.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific objective mentioned regarding food security?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the price of food\",\"To work towards food security\",\"To celebrate different cuisines\",\"To sell more bottled food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure explicitly states the goal is \'To work towards food security and avoid food wastage\'.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/046_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which day is dedicated to avoiding food wastage globally?\",\"options\":[\"World Environment Day\",\"World Health Day\",\"World Food Day\",\"World Science Day\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure identifies 16th October as \'World Food Day\'.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/046_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How does storing grains and perishables using \'proper methods\' contribute to food safety?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the food taste better\",\"It prevents the food from spoiling quickly\",\"It changes the color of the vegetables\",\"It reduces the weight of the grains\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Proper storage methods are essential to prevent the spoilage of both grains and perishable items.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these actions is a direct way to ensure you \'eat all the food that you have served yourself\'?\",\"options\":[\"Buying excessive amounts of milk\",\"Checking the expiry date after eating\",\"Serving small portions initially\",\"Over-cooking the vegetables\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Serving only as much as you can eat is the practical step to ensure all served food is consumed.\",\"topic\":\"Preventing food wastage and spoilage\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the prevention of food spoilage by microbial growth and infestation by pests called?\",\"options\":[\"Food preservation\",\"Food protection\",\"Food standardization\",\"Food distribution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines food protection as the prevention of food spoilage by microbial growth and infestation by pests.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the Food Safety and Standardization Act of India established?\",\"options\":[\"2004\",\"2005\",\"2006\",\"2007\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that FSSAI has been established as per the Food Safety and Standardization Act-2006.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which government organization controls the production and distribution of food and drugs by their standardization?\",\"options\":[\"Agricultural Department\",\"Health Ministry\",\"Food and Drug Administration\",\"Pest Control Board\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is the organization that controls production and distribution through standardization.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of methods like freezing, drying, and boiling?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the weight of the food\",\"To prevent the growth of micro-organisms\",\"To change the color of the food\",\"To increase the cost of the food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"These methods prevent the growth of micro-organisms in food and save it from spoiling.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what does \'food preservation\' involve to prevent spoilage due to internal factors?\",\"options\":[\"Use of various preservatives\",\"Exposure to open air\",\"Infestation by pests\",\"Standardization by FDA\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Food preservation is defined as the use of various preservatives to prevent spoilage due to internal factors.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the abbreviation FSSAI stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Food Security and Safety Association of India\",\"Food Safety and Standardization Act of India\",\"Federal Standard for Safety and Agriculture in India\",\"Food Storage and Security Agency of India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies FSSAI as the Food Safety and Standardization Act of India.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a food manufacturer wants to check the official guidelines for food safety in Maharashtra, which website should they visit?\",\"options\":[\"www.fssai.gov.in\",\"www.fda.maharashtra.gov.in\",\"www.foodsafety.com\",\"www.maharashtra.gov.in\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides www.fda.maharashtra.gov.in as a relevant web address for the FDA in Maharashtra.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer uses a method to prevent pests from attacking his grain. Which process is he specifically practicing?\",\"options\":[\"Food preservation\",\"Food protection\",\"Food standardization\",\"Food distribution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Prevention of infestation by pests is specifically defined as food protection.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these methods mentioned in the text would be most effective for preventing spoilage by removing moisture?\",\"options\":[\"Freezing\",\"Drying\",\"Boiling\",\"Air-tight packing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Drying is a method of safe storage that removes moisture to prevent microbial growth.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does air-tight packing help in saving food from spoiling?\",\"options\":[\"It adds preservatives to the food\",\"It prevents the growth of micro-organisms\",\"It changes the internal factors of food\",\"It standardizes the food production\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Air-tight packing is one of the methods mentioned that prevents the growth of micro-organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definitions provided, what is the main difference between food protection and food preservation?\",\"options\":[\"Protection uses chemicals, while preservation uses heat\",\"Protection deals with external factors like pests, while preservation deals with internal factors\",\"Protection is for drugs, while preservation is for food\",\"Protection is done by FSSAI, while preservation is done by FDA\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Protection focuses on microbial growth and pests, whereas preservation focuses on internal factors using preservatives.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the role of the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) considered essential in the food industry?\",\"options\":[\"It provides free food to the public\",\"It prevents the growth of micro-organisms by boiling\",\"It ensures safety by controlling production and distribution through standardization\",\"It only manages the web addresses of food companies\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The FDA is essential because it controls production and distribution of food and drugs by their standardization.\",\"topic\":\"Food storage and preservation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Why does food remain in good condition for a longer duration when kept in a refrigerator?\",\"options\":[\"It prevents the food from coming in contact with air\",\"Biological and chemical reactions are slowed down at low temperature\",\"The cold temperature adds natural preservatives to the food\",\"It removes all moisture from the food materials\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, biological and chemical reactions in food materials are slowed down at low temperature, keeping food in good condition.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas is filled in air-tight packets of potato wafers to prevent the growth of fungus and insects?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Hydrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Nitrogen gas is filled in air-tight packets of potato wafers and other food products.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is categorized as a natural preservative in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium benzoate\",\"Citric acid\",\"Acetic acid\",\"Oil\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists salt, sugar, and oil as naturally available materials used as preservatives.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which chemical preservative is specifically mentioned as being used in sauces, jellies, and jams?\",\"options\":[\"Aluminium phosphide\",\"Sodium benzoate\",\"Malathion\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sodium benzoate is listed as an example of a chemical preservative used in sauces, jellies, and jams.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Smoking\' method of food preservation, which chemical is used?\",\"options\":[\"Malathion\",\"Sodium benzoate\",\"Aluminium phosphide\",\"Acetic acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Aluminium phosphide is used in the smoking method.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of spraying Malathion on gunny bags containing foodgrains?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the ripening period of grains\",\"To act as a chemical preservative for the grains\",\"To serve as an insecticide to protect the grains\",\"To slow down the sprouting of the grains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Malathion is mentioned under the heading \'Use of insecticides\' to be sprayed on gunny bags.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does irradiation increase the shelf-life of fruits?\",\"options\":[\"By increasing the ripening period\",\"By decreasing the ripening period\",\"By cooling the fruits quickly\",\"By adding salt and sugar to the skin\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the shelf-life of fruits increases due to an increase in the ripening period during irradiation.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a trader wants to prevent potatoes and onions from sprouting during storage, which method should be applied?\",\"options\":[\"Pasteurisation\",\"Smoking\",\"Irradiation\",\"Use of inert gas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the shelf-life of potatoes and onions increases due to slowed-down sprouting in the irradiation method.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the pasteurisation method described, milk should be heated to what temperature?\",\"options\":[\"60°C\",\"80°C\",\"100°C\",\"120°C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies heating milk at 80°C for 15 minutes during pasteurisation.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for cooling milk quickly after heating it in the pasteurisation process?\",\"options\":[\"To improve the taste of the milk\",\"To increase the fat content\",\"To destroy the microbes present in the milk\",\"To prevent the milk from evaporating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that heating and then cooling quickly destroys the microbes present in the milk.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a chemical preservative used specifically for packaged ready-to-cook vegetables?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrate and nitrite salts\",\"Aluminium phosphide\",\"Malathion\",\"Sugar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists nitrate and nitrite salts as chemical preservatives used in packaged ready-to-cook vegetables.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following radiations is NOT mentioned in the text as being used for food irradiation?\",\"options\":[\"X-rays\",\"Gamma-rays\",\"High energy electrons\",\"Infrared rays\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions high energy electrons, X-rays, and gamma-rays, but does not mention infrared rays.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which method of food protection is most suitable for preserving foodgrains in large gunny bags?\",\"options\":[\"Freezing\",\"Use of insecticides\",\"Pasteurisation\",\"Use of preservatives\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Malathion (an insecticide) is sprayed on gunny bags containing foodgrains.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which method is associated with the use of Aluminium phosphide?\",\"options\":[\"Use of insecticides\",\"Irradiation\",\"Smoking\",\"Use of inert gas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the diagram, the box for \'Smoking\' mentions that Aluminium phosphide is used in this method.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/047_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific effect of \'Freezing\' on food materials?\",\"options\":[\"It destroys microbes by heating\",\"It prevents growth of fungus using Nitrogen\",\"Biological and chemical reactions are slowed down\",\"It increases the ripening period of fruits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram states under \'Freezing\' that biological and chemical reactions in food materials are slowed down.\",\"topic\":\"Methods of Food Protection\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/047_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Where in Maharashtra has an irradiation plant been installed specifically for onions and potatoes?\",\"options\":[\"Navi Mumbai\",\"Lasalgaon\",\"Mumbai\",\"Pune\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that irradiation plants have been installed at Lasalgaon for onions and potatoes.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist developed the technique of preserving food quality by raising its temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Albert Einstein\",\"Louis Pasteur\",\"Alexander Fleming\",\"Robert Hooke\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Louis Pasteur as the microbiologist who developed the technique of raising temperature to prevent microbial growth.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of raising the temperature of food in the technique developed by Louis Pasteur?\",\"options\":[\"To improve the taste of the food\",\"To increase the fat content\",\"To prevent microbial growth\",\"To add artificial colors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Louis Pasteur\'s technique preserves food quality by raising the temperature to prevent microbial growth.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of food adulteration through the removal of an important component?\",\"options\":[\"Adding small stones to grains\",\"Mixing urea in milk\",\"Removing the fat content of milk\",\"Mixing sawdust in chilly powder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the removal of fat content of milk as an example of adulteration by removing important components.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what could be a long-term health effect of consuming certain food adulterants?\",\"options\":[\"Improved digestion\",\"Cancer\",\"Increased energy\",\"Better organ functioning\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that some adulterants may affect organ functioning over a long period or even cause cancer.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the removal of the essence from cloves or cardamoms considered a form of food adulteration?\",\"options\":[\"Because it makes the food cheaper\",\"Because it involves adding harmful colors\",\"Because it is the removal of an important component of the food\",\"Because it prevents the food from spoiling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text classifies the removal of essence from cloves and cardamoms under \'Removal of some important components of food\'.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Maharashtra, irradiation plants for spices and condiments are located at which place?\",\"options\":[\"Lasalgaon\",\"Nagpur\",\"Navi Mumbai\",\"Nashik\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that irradiation plants for spices and condiments are installed at Navi Mumbai.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following materials is mentioned in the text as a harmful material mixed with food for adulteration?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar crystals\",\"Iron filings\",\"Vitamin powder\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists iron filings as one of the harmful materials used for adulteration.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person experiences abdominal discomfort after eating, what does the text suggest might be the cause related to food quality?\",\"options\":[\"Eating too much nutritive food\",\"Irradiation of potatoes\",\"Food adulterants\",\"Pasteurization of milk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that some adulterants cause abdominal discomfort or poisoning.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement best describes who is affected by food adulteration?\",\"options\":[\"Only poor people are affected\",\"Only young children are at risk\",\"The health of all people, regardless of age or wealth, is endangered\",\"Only rich people are affected by high-quality adulterants\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly says the health of all people, young and old, poor or rich, is endangered by food adulteration.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which substance is listed as the adulterant for the foodstuff at Serial Number 3?\",\"options\":[\"Urea\",\"Iron filings\",\"Seeds of papaya\",\"Dung of horse\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the table provided in the figure, \'Seeds of papaya\' is written in the Adulterant column for Sr. No. 3.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/048_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table in the given figure, which foodstuff is listed first (Sr. No. 1) to discuss its adulteration?\",\"options\":[\"Ice cream\",\"Red chilly powder\",\"Milk\",\"Cloves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows \'Milk\' as the foodstuff at Serial Number 1.\",\"topic\":\"Food Adulteration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/048_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did the Indian Parliament pass the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act?\",\"options\":[\"1947\",\"1954\",\"1976\",\"1990\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the Parliament passed the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act in 1954.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the maximum punishment provided by the 1976 amendment for adulterating food with harmful substances?\",\"options\":[\"A small fine\",\"One year in prison\",\"Life imprisonment\",\"Seizing the shop\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The 1976 amendment provides for \'life imprisonment\' for those involved in harmful food adulteration.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which chemical is specifically mentioned as being used to make bananas look bright yellow and attractive?\",\"options\":[\"Urea\",\"Calcium carbide\",\"Phosphoric acid\",\"Metanyl yellow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that calcium carbide and other chemicals are used to make fruits like bananas look bright yellow.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do milk vendors sometimes add urea to milk?\",\"options\":[\"To make it taste sweet\",\"To increase the quantity of milk\",\"To make it appear to have higher fat content\",\"To preserve it for a longer time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Milk vendors add urea so that the milk appears to have a higher fat content.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these acids is frequently mixed with cold drinks according to the \'Always remember\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Citric acid\",\"Phosphoric acid\",\"Acetic acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that harmful chemicals like carbonated soda and phosphoric acid are mixed with cold drinks.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"While testing milk, what indicates that water has been added to it?\",\"options\":[\"The drop turns red on the slide\",\"The spread-mark does not appear milky white\",\"The drop sticks to the glass slide\",\"The drop disappears immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If the spread-mark of milk on an inclined glass slide does not appear milky white, water must have been added.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the mixture of turmeric powder and water when concentrated HCl is added if metanyl yellow is present?\",\"options\":[\"It turns blue and then colorless\",\"It turns red and the color disappears\",\"It turns red and the red color does not disappear\",\"It turns green immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The mixture becomes red on addition of conc. HCl and the red colour does not disappear if metanyl yellow is present.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of passing a magnet through rava?\",\"options\":[\"To clean the rava\",\"To detect fine iron filings\",\"To detect brick dust\",\"To make the rava fine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A magnet is used to detect iron filings because they will adhere to the magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do some shopkeepers change the \'Expiry date\' of food packets?\",\"options\":[\"To follow government rules\",\"To avoid a financial loss\",\"To make the food taste better\",\"To help the customers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shopkeepers change the expiry date to avoid financial loss from unsold, expired stock.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student performs a test on red chilly powder by stirring it in a beaker of water. A red layer settles at the bottom. What does this conclude?\",\"options\":[\"The chilly powder is pure\",\"It is adulterated with water\",\"It is adulterated with brick dust\",\"It is adulterated with iron filings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A red layer settled at the bottom of the beaker indicates adulteration with brick dust.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are checking for adulteration in rava, which property of the adulterant are you using for the test?\",\"options\":[\"Solubility in water\",\"Change in color with acid\",\"Magnetic property\",\"Weight of the particles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The test for rava uses a magnet, which relies on the magnetic property of iron filings.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which information is NOT mentioned in the text as a requirement for food packaging labels?\",\"options\":[\"Manufacturing date\",\"Expiry date\",\"Instructions for storage\",\"Price of the product\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists manufacturing date, expiry date, and storage instructions, but does not mention price in this specific context.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A housewife adds conc. HCl to a sample of turmeric water and the red color disappears. What can she conclude about the turmeric?\",\"options\":[\"It contains metanyl yellow\",\"It is pure (metanyl yellow is absent)\",\"It contains brick dust\",\"It contains urea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the red color *does not* disappear if metanyl yellow is present; therefore, if it disappears, the adulterant is absent.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the directions in the Act, why is the choice of \'packing material\' important for food and medicines?\",\"options\":[\"It should make the product look attractive\",\"It should not endanger the food or medicines\",\"It should be easy to open\",\"It should be very colorful\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Act states that packing material should not endanger the food or medicines.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following practices is described as a way to make fruits \'more attractive and tasty\' but is actually a form of adulteration?\",\"options\":[\"Storing them in air-tight containers\",\"Injecting chemicals into them\",\"Washing them with water\",\"Labeling them with manufacturing dates\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that chemicals are injected into fruits to make them more attractive and tasty.\",\"topic\":\"Detecting food adulteration\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21991,46,7,'Science','en','Measurement of Physical Quantities','[\"Mass is a scalar quantity – it has only magnitude, no direction.\",\"Weight is a vector quantity – it has magnitude and direction (downward).\",\"Weight depends on gravity; it changes with altitude and latitude.\",\"Length, time, mass, temperature, volume, speed, density are physical quantities.\",\"Units express the magnitude of a quantity: metre (m) for length, kilogram (kg) for mass, second (s) for time, Kelvin (K) for temperature, litre (L) for volume.\",\"Standard units are fixed by international agreements (e.g., SI system).\",\"The metre was originally defined by a platinum‑iridium bar; now by the speed of light.\",\"The kilogram is defined by a platinum‑iridium prototype kept in Paris.\",\"The second is defined by the frequency of a cesium‑133 atom (atomic clock).\",\"The Kelvin is defined by the triple point of water.\",\"The ampere is defined by the force between two conductors; the candela by luminous intensity.\",\"Measurement accuracy depends on the purpose; use appropriate instruments.\",\"Errors arise from using wrong devices, improper use, and lack of standardisation.\",\"Standardisation ensures fair trade and scientific consistency.\",\"In everyday life we use hand‑span, foot, yard, etc., but these are not reliable units.\",\"The International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM) maintains standards worldwide.\",\"The speed of light (≈3.0×10^8 m\\/s) is used to define the metre.\",\"The concept of scalar vs. vector is important for understanding physical laws.\",\"Weight varies on different planets because gravitational acceleration differs.\",\"Mass remains constant anywhere because it is the amount of matter.\",\"Standard units are crucial for accurate measurement in science and commerce.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Quantities such as mass, weight, distance, speed, temperature, and volume are collectively called what?\",\"options\":[\"Physical quantities\",\"Chemical quantities\",\"Biological quantities\",\"Mathematical quantities\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that quantities like mass, weight, distance, etc., are called physical quantities.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What two components are used to express the magnitude of a physical quantity?\",\"options\":[\"A name and a number\",\"A value and a unit\",\"A symbol and a formula\",\"A weight and a volume\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a value and a unit are used to express the magnitude of a physical quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example \'Swarali walks two kilometres\', what does the word \'two\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The unit\",\"The physical quantity\",\"The value\",\"The distance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that in the example \'two kilometres\', \'two\' is the value.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a physical quantity used to measure the amount of space a liquid occupies?\",\"options\":[\"Density\",\"Speed\",\"Volume\",\"Temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists volume as a physical quantity, which is commonly used for liquids.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a shopkeeper measures the \'weight of vegetables\', what is he determining?\",\"options\":[\"A physical quantity\",\"A chemical property\",\"Only a unit\",\"Only a value\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that measuring the weight of vegetables is an example of measuring a physical quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A value and a unit together express the ________ of a physical quantity.\",\"options\":[\"Direction\",\"Magnitude\",\"Quality\",\"Density\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a value and a unit are used to express the magnitude of a physical quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the statement \'Swarali walks two kilometres everyday\', what is the physical quantity being described?\",\"options\":[\"Swarali\",\"Everyday\",\"Two\",\"Distance\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies distance as the physical quantity in the example of Swarali walking.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a doctor checks the \'temperature of the body\', which physical quantity is being measured?\",\"options\":[\"Temperature\",\"Volume\",\"Mass\",\"Speed\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists temperature as a physical quantity measured in day-to-day life.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these pairs correctly identifies the magnitude components in the phrase \'5 kilograms\'?\",\"options\":[\"5 is the unit, kilogram is the value\",\"5 is the value, kilogram is the unit\",\"5 is the physical quantity, kilogram is the magnitude\",\"5 is the magnitude, kilogram is the value\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Following the text\'s logic (two kilometres), the number is the value and the measurement name is the unit.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a physical quantity related to the movement of vehicles?\",\"options\":[\"Density\",\"Speed\",\"Volume\",\"Weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the speed of vehicles as a physical quantity measured in day-to-day life.\",\"topic\":\"Physical quantity\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A quantity that can be completely expressed by its magnitude alone is called a ________ quantity.\",\"options\":[\"Vector\",\"Scalar\",\"Directional\",\"Complex\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a quantity expressed by magnitude alone is a scalar quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a scalar quantity mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Displacement\",\"Velocity\",\"Density\",\"Direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists density as a quantity that is expressed by magnitude alone.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A vector quantity is expressed completely only when magnitude and ________ are both given.\",\"options\":[\"Time\",\"Unit\",\"Direction\",\"Mass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a vector quantity as one requiring both magnitude and direction.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'magnitude\' refer to in the context of expressing a scalar quantity?\",\"options\":[\"The direction of the quantity\",\"A value with a unit\",\"The speed of an object\",\"The location of an object\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that magnitude is a value with a unit.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the temperature of a person (e.g., 101° Fahrenheit) considered a scalar quantity?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has a direction\",\"Because it is measured in degrees\",\"Because it is expressed by magnitude alone\",\"Because it changes over time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Temperature is listed as a scalar quantity because only magnitude is used to express it.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these pairs consists only of vector quantities as per the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Mass and Area\",\"Displacement and Velocity\",\"Length and Time\",\"Breadth and Work\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly names displacement and velocity as vector quantities.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An aeroplane is flying at a velocity of 500 km\\/hr towards Mumbai. Which part of this description makes it a vector quantity?\",\"options\":[\"The speed of 500 km\\/hr\",\"The mention of an aeroplane\",\"The inclusion of the direction \'towards Mumbai\'\",\"The unit km\\/hr\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A vector quantity must include direction; \'towards Mumbai\' provides that direction.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a tunnel is described as being \'two kilometres\' long, what type of quantity is being expressed?\",\"options\":[\"Vector quantity\",\"Scalar quantity\",\"Velocity quantity\",\"Displacement quantity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Length (two kilometres) is expressed by magnitude alone, making it a scalar quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the statement that describes a vector quantity based on the textbook examples.\",\"options\":[\"The area of a field is 5 acres.\",\"A car moved 20 kilometres towards the north.\",\"The density of the liquid is high.\",\"The work was completed in two hours.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Displacement (20 km towards north) involves both magnitude and direction, fitting the definition of a vector.\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the fundamental difference between the description of \'length\' and \'displacement\' as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Length requires direction, but displacement does not.\",\"Length is a vector, while displacement is a scalar.\",\"Displacement requires direction to be complete, while length does not.\",\"There is no difference; both are scalar quantities.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text classifies length as scalar (magnitude alone) and displacement as vector (magnitude and direction).\",\"topic\":\"Scalar and Vector quantity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for the amount of material present in a substance?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Mass\",\"Inertia\",\"Gravity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines mass as the amount of material present in a substance.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following are the units of mass mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Newton and Kilogram\",\"Metre and Centimetre\",\"Gram and Kilogram\",\"Litre and Millilitre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that gram and kilogram are the units of mass.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the gravitational force that acts on a mass called?\",\"options\":[\"Inertia\",\"Scalar quantity\",\"Weight\",\"Material\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines weight as the gravitational force that acts on a mass.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Mass is a ______ quantity, while weight is a ______ quantity.\",\"options\":[\"vector, scalar\",\"scalar, vector\",\"natural, artificial\",\"constant, fixed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, mass is a scalar quantity and weight is a vector quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the natural tendency of an object to resist a change in its state called?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Mass\",\"Inertia\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines inertia as the natural tendency of mass to resist a change in its state.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a shopkeeper uses a two-pan common balance, what is actually being compared?\",\"options\":[\"Two weights\",\"Two volumes\",\"Two masses\",\"Two gravitational forces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when we use the two-pan common balance in a shop, we compare two masses.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does weight change at different places on the earth?\",\"options\":[\"Because the amount of material changes\",\"Because the gravitational force is different at different places\",\"Because the inertia of the object changes\",\"Because mass is a vector quantity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Weight is the gravitational force, which the text notes is different at different places on the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the qualitative measure of the inertia of an object?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Mass\",\"Gravity\",\"Size\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that mass is the qualitative measure of the inertia of an object.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an object is moved from one city to another, which property will remain unchanged?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Mass\",\"Vector direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that mass does not change from place to place anywhere in the world.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Object A has a larger mass than Object B, what can be concluded about their inertia?\",\"options\":[\"Object B has greater inertia\",\"Object A has greater inertia\",\"Both have the same inertia\",\"Neither object has inertia\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the larger the mass, the greater is the inertia.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly describes the direction of weight?\",\"options\":[\"It acts away from the earth\'s surface\",\"It acts towards the centre of the earth\",\"It has no specific direction because it is scalar\",\"It acts in the direction of the object\'s movement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines weight as the force by which the earth attracts an object towards its centre.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student claims that mass and weight are the same because both are measured in kilograms in shops. Why is this claim scientifically incorrect?\",\"options\":[\"Because mass is a vector and weight is a scalar\",\"Because kilograms are units of mass, and weight is a different quantity (force)\",\"Because mass changes from place to place but weight does not\",\"Because weight is the amount of material in a substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text clarifies that what we measure in grams\\/kilograms is mass, and that mass and weight are different quantities.\",\"topic\":\"Mass and Weight\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the weight of an object found to be maximum on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"At the equator\",\"At the poles\",\"At sea-level\",\"At high altitudes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the weight of an object is maximum at the poles.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the weight of an object found to be minimum on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"At the poles\",\"At the equator\",\"At sea-level\",\"At the North Pole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the weight of an object is minimum at the equator.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a physical quantity in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Velocity\",\"Temperature\",\"Mass\",\"Volume\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists mass, weight, distance, velocity, and temperature, but not volume.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the weight of an object at a high altitude compare to its weight at sea-level?\",\"options\":[\"It is more\",\"It is the same\",\"It is less\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the weight of an object at a high altitude is less than its weight at sea-level.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are different units used to measure different physical quantities?\",\"options\":[\"Because the quantities are different from each other\",\"Because units are difficult to remember\",\"Because all quantities have the same properties\",\"Because it is a rule of the poles\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that as physical quantities are different from each other, a specific unit is used for each.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Is it possible to use one and the same unit to measure mass, weight, distance, and temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, for convenience\",\"No, because these quantities are different\",\"Yes, but only at the poles\",\"No, because they are all the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that different units must be used because these physical quantities are different from each other.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an object is moved from the equator to the North Pole, what will happen to its weight?\",\"options\":[\"It will decrease\",\"It will increase\",\"It will remain the same\",\"It will become less than at sea-level\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since weight is minimum at the equator and maximum at the poles, moving to the pole increases weight.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist measures the weight of a box at sea-level and then on top of a high mountain. What will the result show?\",\"options\":[\"Weight is higher on the mountain\",\"Weight is lower on the mountain\",\"Weight is the same in both places\",\"Weight is maximum on the mountain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states weight is less at high altitudes compared to sea-level.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what determines the choice of a specific unit for measurement?\",\"options\":[\"The altitude of the location\",\"The distance from the poles\",\"The specific nature of the physical quantity\",\"The weight of the object at the equator\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states specific units are used because physical quantities are different from each other.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which conclusion can be drawn about weight from the \'Use your brain power\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Weight is a constant value everywhere\",\"Weight changes depending on the location and altitude\",\"Weight and mass use the same unit\",\"Weight is maximum at the equator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows weight varies between poles\\/equator and sea-level\\/high altitude.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why does our body have weight?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the gravitational force of the earth\",\"Because of the length of our hand-span\",\"Because our mass changes at different places\",\"Because of the standardized units of measurement\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that our body has weight because of the gravitational force of the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specified unit for measuring length mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Hand-span\",\"Gram\",\"Metre\",\"String\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the metre (m) is the specified unit for measuring length.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the gravitational force of the moon compare to that of the earth?\",\"options\":[\"It is exactly the same\",\"It is more than the earth\'s\",\"It is less than the earth\'s\",\"It is double the earth\'s\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the gravitational force of the moon is less than that of the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to our mass when we move from the earth to the moon?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes less\",\"It becomes more\",\"It remains the same\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that our mass is the same at both the earth and the moon.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If several students measure the same bench using their hand-spans, why might they get different results?\",\"options\":[\"The bench changes its length for each student\",\"The span of a hand is not the same for every person\",\"The gravitational force changes inside the classroom\",\"The students are using standardized units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that hand-spans vary, so the measure obtained by each person will be different.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are standardized measures used for measuring things called?\",\"options\":[\"Physical quantities\",\"Hand-spans\",\"Standard units\",\"Gravitational forces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines standardized measures as standard units.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student cuts a piece of string that is \'four hand-spans\' long. Why is this measurement NOT considered accurate for everyone?\",\"options\":[\"Because hand-spans are too difficult to count\",\"Because the string might stretch\",\"Because different students have different hand-span lengths\",\"Because string is not a physical quantity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment in the text shows that pieces cut by different students using hand-spans will not be the same length.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What has been accepted as the standard for 1.0 metre?\",\"options\":[\"The length of an adult\'s hand-span\",\"A certain distance accepted as the standard\",\"The distance between the earth and the moon\",\"The length of a ball of string\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a certain distance has been accepted as the standard for 1.0 metre.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a \'hand-span\' not suitable to be a standard unit for measuring cloth?\",\"options\":[\"It is too small to measure cloth\",\"Cloth cannot be measured in spans\",\"The length measured by different people will be different\",\"Hand-spans only work for measuring benches\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if hand-spans were used, the lengths measured by each person would come out to be different.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you need to measure a physical quantity accurately, what must you use?\",\"options\":[\"The unit specified for that quantity\",\"Any convenient body part like a hand-span\",\"A ball of string\",\"The gravitational force of the moon\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that to measure any quantity accurately, we use the unit specified for it.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a physical quantity that requires accurate measurement?\",\"options\":[\"Hand-span\",\"Length\",\"Standard unit\",\"Metre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions length as an example of a physical quantity that we have to measure accurately using a specified unit.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best summarizes the relationship between weight and location mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Weight is the same everywhere in the universe\",\"Weight depends on the gravitational force of the location\",\"Weight is a standardized unit like the metre\",\"Weight is determined by the span of one\'s hand\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text illustrates this by comparing weight on Earth and the Moon based on their differing gravitational forces.\",\"topic\":\"Standardized measurement\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the MKS system of measurement, what does the letter \'M\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Metre\",\"Mass\",\"Mile\",\"Millimetre\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the MKS system, distance is measured in metres, mass in kilograms, and time in seconds.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit is used to measure mass in the CGS system?\",\"options\":[\"Kilogram\",\"Milligram\",\"Gram\",\"Quintal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The CGS system uses centimetres for distance, grams for mass, and seconds for time.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many fundamental units is the International System of Units (SI) based on?\",\"options\":[\"Three\",\"Five\",\"Seven\",\"Nine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the International system of units (SI) is based on seven fundamental units.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these quantities has the same unit of measurement in both MKS and CGS systems?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Distance\",\"Time\",\"Speed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Time is measured in seconds in both the MKS and CGS systems.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is the quantity \'speed\' calculated?\",\"options\":[\"Product of distance and time\",\"Ratio of distance and time\",\"Sum of distance and mass\",\"Difference between mass and time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines speed as the ratio of the quantities distance and time.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is a necessary characteristic of a standard fundamental unit?\",\"options\":[\"It must change with time\",\"It must be variable\",\"It must not be variable\",\"It must be different for every country\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A standard fundamental unit must be available to all and it must not be variable.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are measuring the distance between two points using the CGS system, which unit will you use?\",\"options\":[\"Metre\",\"Centimetre\",\"Kilometre\",\"Millimetre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the CGS system, distance is measured in centimetres.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To work out the area of a classroom, which fundamental quantity is primarily taken into account?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Time\",\"Length\",\"Speed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Calculating area involves taking the quantity of length (distance) into account.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student needs to record the mass of an object in SI units. Which symbol should they use?\",\"options\":[\"m\",\"s\",\"kg\",\"g\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the SI system, the symbol for the unit of mass (kilogram) is \'kg\'.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it sufficient to select only a few quantities as \'fundamental quantities\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because other quantities are not important\",\"Because a majority of other quantities are related to them\",\"Because these are the only quantities that can be measured\",\"Because they are the only units in the metric system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Many physical quantities are related to each other, so standardizing a few fundamental ones allows us to measure all others.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding the International System of Units?\",\"options\":[\"It is also called the metric system\",\"It is only used in the MKS system\",\"It is based on the CGS system\",\"It uses grams as a fundamental unit of mass\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The International system of units (SI) is also called the metric system.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the SI unit table, which unit-symbol pair is correctly matched?\",\"options\":[\"Length - metre - l\",\"Mass - kilogram - kg\",\"Time - second - sec\",\"Mass - gram - g\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the SI table, Mass has the unit \'kilogram\' and the symbol \'kg\'.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If speed is the ratio of distance to time, what would be the unit of speed in the MKS system?\",\"options\":[\"Kilogram\\/second\",\"Metre\\/second\",\"Centimetre\\/second\",\"Metre\\/gram\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since MKS uses metres for distance and seconds for time, the ratio (speed) is metre\\/second.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the MKS system, which three quantities are accepted as the fundamental quantities used to measure all others?\",\"options\":[\"Distance, Mass, Speed\",\"Distance, Time, Area\",\"Distance, Mass, Time\",\"Mass, Time, Speed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the MKS system, distance, mass, and time are accepted as the fundamental quantities.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the SI symbol for the unit of length?\",\"options\":[\"mt\",\"m\",\"L\",\"cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table for the International system of units shows the symbol for metre is \'m\'.\",\"topic\":\"Systems of measurement\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What material is used to make the standard solid cylinder for mass?\",\"options\":[\"Gold-silver alloy\",\"Platinum-iridium alloy\",\"Iron-steel alloy\",\"Copper-nickel alloy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the standard of mass is a solid cylinder made of a platinum-iridium alloy.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the International Bureau of Weights and Measures located?\",\"options\":[\"London\",\"New York\",\"Paris\",\"Berlin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the International Bureau of Weights and Measures is at Paris.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the standard of mass kept at the International Bureau of Weights and Measures called?\",\"options\":[\"One gram\",\"One pound\",\"One kilogram\",\"One tonne\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As per international agreement, this specific mass is called one kilogram.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the standard metre defined using the platinum-iridium bar?\",\"options\":[\"The total length of the bar\",\"The distance between two fine lines engraved on the bar\",\"The thickness of the bar\",\"The weight of the bar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The distance between two fine lines engraved on the platinum-iridium bar is accepted as the standard metre.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the basis for measuring the time taken for one day (24 hours)?\",\"options\":[\"One revolution of the moon\",\"One revolution of the earth\",\"The speed of light\",\"The vibration of atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The time required for one revolution of the earth is measured and taken as 24 hours.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is distributed to standardizing laboratories all over the world to ensure uniform measurement?\",\"options\":[\"The original prototypes\",\"Accurate copies of the prototypes\",\"Raw platinum-iridium metal\",\"Instruction manuals only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Official accurate copies of the prototypes are made and distributed to standardizing laboratories all over the world.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To standardize one second, how many seconds are considered to be in one minute?\",\"options\":[\"24 seconds\",\"100 seconds\",\"60 seconds\",\"3600 seconds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that to standardize one second, one minute is considered to have 60 seconds.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a laboratory in India needs to verify a 1 kg weight, what should it use for comparison according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A common stone found locally\",\"An official accurate copy of the prototype\",\"A calculation based on the earth\'s revolution\",\"The distance between two lines on any metal bar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Official accurate copies of the prototype are kept in standardizing laboratories worldwide for this purpose.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the standards provided, how many seconds are there in one hour?\",\"options\":[\"60 seconds\",\"120 seconds\",\"3600 seconds\",\"86400 seconds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If one hour has 60 minutes and one minute has 60 seconds, then 60 x 60 = 3600 seconds.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which physical quantity\'s standard is defined by a solid cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"Length\",\"Time\",\"Mass\",\"Velocity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes a solid cylinder made of platinum-iridium as the standard of mass.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the earth\'s revolution and the standard of time?\",\"options\":[\"The revolution time is divided into hours, minutes, and seconds to create a standard.\",\"The revolution time is ignored in favor of atomic clocks.\",\"One revolution is equal to exactly one second.\",\"The revolution time is used to measure the standard metre.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The time for one revolution is taken as 24 hours, which is then divided into minutes and seconds to standardize time.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are two fine lines engraved on the platinum-iridium bar instead of just using the bar\'s ends?\",\"options\":[\"To make the bar look decorative\",\"To ensure the distance (standard metre) is precise and clearly marked\",\"To prevent the bar from breaking\",\"To mark the weight of the bar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The lines are engraved to define the exact distance accepted as the standard metre.\",\"topic\":\"Standards of fundamental quantities\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In ancient Egypt, what was the distance from a man’s elbow to the tip of his middle finger called?\",\"options\":[\"Gunj\",\"Cubit\",\"Span\",\"Hand\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a \'cubit\' as the distance from a man’s elbow to the tip of his middle finger in ancient Egypt.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit was used to weigh gold in olden times according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Gram\",\"Cubit\",\"Gunj\",\"Hour glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in olden times, gold was weighed in a unit called gunj.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which device was used for measuring time in ancient times?\",\"options\":[\"Sundial\",\"Wristwatch\",\"Hour glass\",\"Pendulum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that an hour glass was used for measuring time.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the measure of a cubit differ from person to person?\",\"options\":[\"Because people used different tools\",\"Because the length of body parts varies between individuals\",\"Because the king changed the rules\",\"Because the middle finger was not used\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since a cubit was based on a man\'s arm length, it differed because body sizes vary between people.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was considered as the \'standard\' cubit in ancient Egypt?\",\"options\":[\"The cubit of the king\",\"The cubit of the tallest man\",\"The cubit of the priest\",\"The cubit of the merchant\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the cubit of the king was considered as the standard to avoid differences.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What did man first use when he felt the need to measure things?\",\"options\":[\"Metal scales\",\"Parts of his own body\",\"Stones and pebbles\",\"Wooden sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that man started by using the parts of his own body for the purpose of measurement.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If two different merchants in Egypt used their own arms to measure cloth, why would a customer be confused?\",\"options\":[\"The cloth would be the same length\",\"The length of the cloth would vary based on each merchant\'s arm size\",\"The merchants would use gunj instead\",\"The hour glass would run out of time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Because body parts differ per person, using individual cubits results in inconsistent measurements.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you were a jeweler in olden times, which unit would you use to measure the weight of a gold ring?\",\"options\":[\"Cubit\",\"Hour glass\",\"Gunj\",\"Elbow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies gunj as the unit used for weighing gold in olden times.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary difference between a \'cubit\' and the \'king\'s cubit\' as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"One is for time and the other is for weight\",\"The cubit is a personal measure, while the king\'s cubit is a standard measure\",\"The king\'s cubit was measured using an hour glass\",\"There is no difference between them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A regular cubit varied by person, but the king\'s cubit was established as a standard to ensure consistency.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why was it necessary to establish a \'standard\' measurement like the king\'s cubit?\",\"options\":[\"Because body parts were too difficult to use\",\"To ensure that the measurement remained the same for everyone\",\"To make sure the king got more gold\",\"Because the hour glass was not accurate enough\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Standards were created because individual measurements (like body parts) differed, causing inconsistency.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What determines how accurate a measurement must be?\",\"options\":[\"The cost of the device\",\"The purpose of the measurement\",\"The time of day\",\"The weight of the object\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, how accurate a measurement must be depends upon its purpose.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the measurement of substances like gold done meticulously and accurately?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are available in large quantities\",\"Because they are precious and used in small quantities\",\"Because they are very heavy\",\"Because they change color easily\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Substances that are precious, of great importance, and used in very small quantities require meticulous measurement.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a quantity where technology now allows us to measure very small magnitudes for sports competitions?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Temperature\",\"Time\",\"Volume\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that technology allows measuring small magnitudes of distance and time in connection with important sports competitions.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is mentioned as a major cause of error in measurement?\",\"options\":[\"Using a metal weight\",\"Not using the device properly\",\"Checking the balance pointer\",\"Using a standard measure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Not using the device properly\' as one of the two major causes of errors.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of checking a measure against a standard measure from time to time called?\",\"options\":[\"Calculation\",\"Standardization\",\"Modification\",\"Magnification\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Checking a measure against a standard measure to ensure accuracy is called standardization.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To ensure you receive the correct amount of fuel at a petrol pump, what is necessary?\",\"options\":[\"To check it against a standard measure\",\"To use a stopwatch\",\"To weigh the petrol on a balance\",\"To check the temperature of the petrol\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Standardization against a standard measure is necessary to ensure the petrol pump indicates the correct volume.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What should a consumer look for on a balance to ensure it is officially approved?\",\"options\":[\"The brand name of the shop\",\"The color of the pans\",\"The stamp of standardization\",\"The price of the balance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Consumers should check if the balance carries the stamp of standardization by the department of weights and measures.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The Weights and Measures sub-division is a part of which government division?\",\"options\":[\"Department of Sports\",\"Food, Urban Supply and Consumer Protection Division\",\"Health and Family Welfare Division\",\"Science and Technology Division\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states it functions as part of the Food, Urban Supply and Consumer Protection Division.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a specific function of the Weights and Measures sub-division?\",\"options\":[\"Selling groceries to consumers\",\"Granting licences for the production of weights\",\"Organizing sports competitions\",\"Mining precious metals like gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One function of the sub-division is to grant licences for the production, sales, and repairs of weights and measures.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When checking a balance in the market, what should be the position of the pointer for it to be correct?\",\"options\":[\"Tilted to the left\",\"Tilted to the right\",\"Upright\",\"Hidden under the pan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text advises checking if the pointer of the balance is upright.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it legally binding to use standard weights and measures?\",\"options\":[\"To make measurements faster\",\"To prevent consumers from being cheated\",\"To reduce the cost of goods\",\"To help shopkeepers save time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The sub-division functions to prevent consumers from being cheated, making the use of standard weights binding by law.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which physical quantity\'s measurement is specifically mentioned in the context of body health?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Distance\",\"Temperature\",\"Time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions measuring body temperature as an example of measuring small magnitudes accurately.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a shopkeeper uses a wooden block instead of a metal weight, which requirement for a proper balance is being violated?\",\"options\":[\"The weight must be made of metal\",\"The balance must be stable\",\"The pointer must be upright\",\"The pan must not be tampered with\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly asks to check \'Is the weight made of metal?\'\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do officers of the Weights and Measures sub-division visit different places?\",\"options\":[\"To sell new balances to shopkeepers\",\"To ascertain whether the weights and measures used are proper\",\"To collect taxes on groceries\",\"To participate in sports competitions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Officers visit places to ascertain whether the weights and measures being used are proper or not.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What should be checked regarding the underside of a balance pan?\",\"options\":[\"If it is clean\",\"If it has been tampered with\",\"If it is made of plastic\",\"If it has the shop\'s name\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to check if the underside of the pan of the balance has been tampered with.\",\"topic\":\"Importance of accurate measurement\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the full form of the term \'TMC\' used in the context of dam water measurement?\",\"options\":[\"Ten Million Cubic feet\",\"Thousand Million Cubic feet\",\"Total Million Cubic feet\",\"Triple Million Cubic feet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As per the text, 1 TMC means one thousand million cubic feet.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, how many litres are there in 1 cubic foot?\",\"options\":[\"28.317 litres\",\"283.17 litres\",\"2.8317 litres\",\"2831.7 litres\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1 cubic foot means 28.317 litres.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"During which season is news regarding the water stock in dams most commonly reported?\",\"options\":[\"Summer season\",\"Winter season\",\"Rainy season\",\"Spring season\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions hearing, seeing, and reading news about dam water during the rainy season.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many thousand million litres are equal to 1 TMC?\",\"options\":[\"1000 thousand million litres\",\"28.317 thousand million litres\",\"1 thousand million litres\",\"283.17 thousand million litres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1 TMC is about 28.317 thousand million litres.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a dam has a capacity of 1000 million cubic feet, what is its capacity in TMC?\",\"options\":[\"10 TMC\",\"100 TMC\",\"1 TMC\",\"0.1 TMC\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 1 TMC is defined as one thousand million cubic feet, 1000 million cubic feet equals 1 TMC.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To calculate the volume of 2 cubic feet in litres, which value from the text should be multiplied by 2?\",\"options\":[\"28316846592\",\"28.317\",\"1000\",\"28.317 thousand million\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 1 cubic foot is 28.317 litres, 2 cubic feet would be 2 * 28.317 litres.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following months are specifically mentioned for collecting information about water discharge from dams?\",\"options\":[\"January, February and March\",\"April, May and June\",\"August, September and October\",\"November and December\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests collecting information about discharge during the months of August, September, and October.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is 1 TMC approximately equal to 28.317 thousand million litres?\",\"options\":[\"Because 1 TMC is 1000 million cubic feet and each cubic foot is 28.317 litres\",\"Because 1 TMC is exactly 1 million cubic feet\",\"Because 1 cubic foot is equal to 1000 litres\",\"Because dams only hold water in thousand million litre units\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The conversion is derived by multiplying the 1000 million cubic feet by the 28.317 litres per cubic foot.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the exact number of litres in 1 TMC as printed in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"28316846592 litres\",\"28317000000 litres\",\"28.317 litres\",\"1000000000 litres\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides the exact figure 28316846592 litres for 1 TMC.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the numerical data provided, which statement best evaluates the scale of 1 TMC?\",\"options\":[\"It is a small unit used for measuring household water usage.\",\"It is a massive unit representing billions of litres used for large dam capacities.\",\"It is a unit used only to measure the height of rainfall in millimetres.\",\"It is equivalent to exactly one thousand litres of water.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The large number (over 28 billion litres) indicates it is a massive unit suitable for dam capacities.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know? (TMC)\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21992,46,7,'Science','en','Motion, Force and Work','[\"Motion is a change in position of a body over time.\",\"A force applied to a body causes a change in its motion.\",\"Distance is the total length travelled, a scalar quantity measured in metres.\",\"Displacement is the shortest straight‑line distance between start and end points, a vector quantity measured in metres.\",\"Speed is the magnitude of motion, calculated as distance divided by time (m\\/s).\",\"Velocity is speed with a direction, calculated as displacement divided by time (m\\/s).\",\"Average velocity is total displacement divided by total time; instantaneous velocity is the velocity at a particular instant.\",\"Acceleration is the change in velocity per unit time (m\\/s²).\",\"Force is a push or pull that can change the velocity of a body; it is a vector quantity measured in Newtons (N).\",\"Work is done when a force causes a displacement; W = F × s (Joules).\",\"If no external force acts, a body moves with constant velocity (Newton’s First Law).\",\"The unit of work in SI is the Joule, where 1 J = 1 N × 1 m.\",\"Work can be positive, negative or zero depending on the direction of force relative to displacement.\",\"Energy is the capacity to do work; kinetic energy is associated with motion.\",\"In a frictionless surface, a body will continue moving with constant velocity if no net force acts.\",\"The magnitude of work depends on both the force applied and the distance over which it acts.\",\"When a force is applied in the same direction as displacement, work is positive.\",\"When a force is applied opposite to displacement, work is negative.\",\"When a force is perpendicular to displacement, no work is done.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the length of the route actually traversed by a moving body, irrespective of the direction, called?\",\"options\":[\"Displacement\",\"Distance\",\"Velocity\",\"Force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines distance as the length of the route actually traversed by a moving body, irrespective of the direction.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a vector quantity according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Distance\",\"Speed\",\"Displacement\",\"Time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in displacement, both distance and direction are taken into account, making it a vector quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unit of measurement for both distance and displacement in the SI system?\",\"options\":[\"Centimetre\",\"Kilometre\",\"Metre\",\"Millimetre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the unit of measurement of distance and displacement is the metre in the SI system.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what occurs when a force acts on a body?\",\"options\":[\"A change in motion or shape\",\"A change in mass\",\"A change in direction only\",\"No change occurs\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a change occurs in the motion or the shape of a body when a force acts on it.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Displacement is defined as the _______ distance traversed in a particular direction.\",\"options\":[\"Maximum\",\"Total\",\"Minimum\",\"Average\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines displacement as the minimum distance traversed in a particular direction between two points.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the example of Ranjit\'s journey, which line represents his displacement from house A to school D?\",\"options\":[\"The path AB\",\"The path BC\",\"The path CD\",\"The straight line AD\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explains that Ranjit\'s displacement from his house to the school is the straight line AD.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Ranjit walks 500 metres from A to B, 300 metres from B to C, and 400 metres from C to D, what is the total distance he traversed?\",\"options\":[\"500 metres\",\"1200 metres\",\"700 metres\",\"900 metres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Distance is the sum of the actual path traversed: AB + BC + CD (500 + 300 + 400 = 1200).\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the straight-line distance between Ranjit\'s house (A) and school (D) is 800 metres, what is his displacement after he reaches school?\",\"options\":[\"1200 metres\",\"800 metres\",\"400 metres\",\"0 metres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Displacement is the minimum distance along a straight line from the original point to the final point.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is distance considered a scalar quantity?\",\"options\":[\"Because it takes direction into account\",\"Because it is measured in metres\",\"Because it is the length of the route irrespective of direction\",\"Because it is always a straight line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Distance is a scalar quantity because it is the length of the route actually traversed irrespective of the direction.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these statements correctly distinguishes distance from displacement?\",\"options\":[\"Distance is always shorter than displacement\",\"Displacement requires direction, while distance does not\",\"Distance is a vector, while displacement is a scalar\",\"They use different units of measurement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that displacement takes direction into account (vector), while distance does not (scalar).\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which systems of measurement is the \'metre\' used for distance and displacement?\",\"options\":[\"Only in the SI system\",\"Only in the MKS system\",\"In both SI and MKS systems\",\"Only in the CGS system\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the unit is the metre in the SI as well as in the MKS system.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of Ranjit\'s path, which value will always be the \'minimum\' between two points?\",\"options\":[\"The distance AB+BC+CD\",\"The actual route traversed\",\"The displacement AD\",\"The scalar quantity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines displacement as the minimum distance along a straight line between the starting and ending points.\",\"topic\":\"Distance and displacement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of velocity according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The total distance covered by a body\",\"The distance traversed by a body in a specific direction in unit time\",\"The time taken to cover a specific distance\",\"The change in speed over a period of time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines velocity as the distance traversed by a body in a specific direction in unit time.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which formula is used to calculate velocity?\",\"options\":[\"Velocity = Distance \\/ Time\",\"Velocity = Speed × Time\",\"Velocity = Displacement \\/ Period of time required for the displacement\",\"Velocity = Displacement × Period of time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook explicitly states the formula: Velocity = Displacement \\/ Period of time required for the displacement.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To predict whether a storm will reach a particular place, which of the following is essential to mention?\",\"options\":[\"Only the speed of the storm\",\"The direction of the storm\",\"The color of the storm clouds\",\"The distance already covered by the storm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that to predict if a storm will reach a place, a mention of its direction is essential.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When stating that a car has a speed of 40 km per hour, what information is NOT needed?\",\"options\":[\"The distance\",\"The time\",\"The direction\",\"The magnitude\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when we say the speed of a car is 40 km per hour, there is no need to specify the direction.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a body undergoes a displacement of 100 meters in 10 seconds, what is its velocity?\",\"options\":[\"1000 meters per second\",\"90 meters per second\",\"10 meters per second\",\"110 meters per second\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Velocity = Displacement \\/ Time, 100 \\/ 10 = 10 meters per second.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary difference between speed and velocity?\",\"options\":[\"Speed uses displacement while velocity uses distance\",\"Velocity requires a mention of direction, while speed does not\",\"Speed is measured in hours and velocity in seconds\",\"Velocity is only used for storms and speed is only used for cars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text illustrates that speed (like a car\'s speed) doesn\'t need direction, but velocity (like a storm\'s movement) involves a specific direction.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for velocity, what does the \'Period of time\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"The time required for the displacement\",\"The total age of the body\",\"The time it takes to stop\",\"The time spent at rest\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The formula specifies the denominator is the \'Period of time required for the displacement\'.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'direction\' mentioned in the context of a storm in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To calculate the total distance of the storm\",\"To predict if the storm will reach a specific location\",\"To measure the speed of the wind\",\"To determine the time the storm started\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states direction is essential to predict whether a storm will reach a particular place or not.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Calculate the velocity of a vehicle that travels a displacement of 60 km in 2 hours.\",\"options\":[\"120 km\\/hr\",\"58 km\\/hr\",\"30 km\\/hr\",\"62 km\\/hr\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Applying the formula Velocity = Displacement \\/ Time: 60 km \\/ 2 hours = 30 km\\/hr.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best completes the relationship: Speed is to Magnitude as Velocity is to ______?\",\"options\":[\"Distance only\",\"Time only\",\"Magnitude and Direction\",\"Displacement only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies velocity includes distance in a specific direction (magnitude + direction), whereas speed (40 km\\/h) does not need direction.\",\"topic\":\"Speed and velocity\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for Speed as per the given text?\",\"options\":[\"Speed = Displacement \\/ Time period\",\"Speed = Distance \\/ Time period\",\"Speed = Distance × Time period\",\"Speed = Time period \\/ Distance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Speed as Distance divided by Time period.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s find the unit\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which quantity is used in the numerator to calculate Velocity?\",\"options\":[\"Distance\",\"Time period\",\"Speed\",\"Displacement\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the formula in the text, Velocity = Displacement \\/ Time period.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s find the unit\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unit of speed or velocity mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"metres\\/second (m\\/s)\",\"kilometres\\/hour (km\\/h)\",\"seconds\\/metre (s\\/m)\",\"metres × second (m-s)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the unit of speed or velocity is written as metres\\/second (m\\/s).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s find the unit\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To find the unit of speed, what must be inserted into the formula instead of quantities?\",\"options\":[\"The names of the objects\",\"The units of the quantities\",\"The total distance only\",\"The final speed value\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Insert the units instead of quantities in the formulae\' to get the unit of speed.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s find the unit\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the calculation of both speed and velocity, which quantity remains in the denominator?\",\"options\":[\"Distance\",\"Displacement\",\"Time period\",\"Metres\"],\"answer\"