y turning the steering wheel\",\"By using the accelerator\",\"By opening the doors\",\"By changing the tires\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the driver increases or decreases velocity by means of the accelerator.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a football player kicks a moving ball to change its direction, what happens to the ball?\",\"options\":[\"Its mass changes\",\"Acceleration takes place\",\"The time stops\",\"The distance becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the direction changes, the velocity changes, meaning acceleration takes place.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the velocity of a toy car when it collides into a wall?\",\"options\":[\"Its velocity changes\",\"Its spring winds up\",\"It moves faster\",\"It changes color\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the toy car collides into a wall, it stops, meaning its velocity changes.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a vehicle\'s velocity changes, the acceleration is calculated by dividing the change in velocity by ____.\",\"options\":[\"Distance covered\",\"Time taken for change\",\"Initial velocity\",\"Final velocity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The formula provided in the text is Acceleration = Change in velocity \\/ Time taken for change.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A toy car is moving in a straight line. If it is hit on one side, what specifically changes according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only its speed\",\"Its direction\",\"Its weight\",\"Its spring length\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that when hit on one side, the toy car changes direction and keeps going.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the truck example, the velocity for distance BC is 30 km\\/hr and for CD is 40 km\\/hr. This implies:\",\"options\":[\"Velocity for CD is less than BC\",\"Velocity for CD is greater than BC\",\"Velocity is constant throughout\",\"There is no acceleration between BC and CD\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the velocity for the distance CD is greater than the velocity for the distance BC.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the velocity of the toy car change when it hits a wall or is hit on the side?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is made of plastic\",\"Because it comes into contact with something external\",\"Because the spring is fully wound\",\"Because it is on a flat floor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the change takes place because the car comes into contact with something external to it.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definition of force in the text, what is required for a body to undergo acceleration?\",\"options\":[\"It must be in a vacuum\",\"Force must act on the body\",\"It must move at a constant speed\",\"It must move in a straight line only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the interaction that brings about acceleration is called force, and force acts on a body.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which formula correctly represents the relationship described in the text for acceleration?\",\"options\":[\"Acceleration = Velocity × Time\",\"Acceleration = Change in velocity \\/ Time taken for change\",\"Acceleration = Distance \\/ Time\",\"Acceleration = Force \\/ Velocity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This is the exact formula provided in the textbook text.\",\"topic\":\"Acceleration\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist was the first to study force and the resulting acceleration?\",\"options\":[\"Sir Isaac Newton\",\"Albert Einstein\",\"Galileo Galilei\",\"C. V. Raman\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that Sir Isaac Newton was the first to study force and the resulting acceleration.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a carrom coin\'s motion when talcum powder is applied to the board?\",\"options\":[\"It stops immediately\",\"It moves for a shorter time\",\"It keeps moving for a longer time\",\"Its direction changes randomly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that talcum powder reduces friction, allowing the coin to move for a longer time.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Newton\'s First Law of Motion, what happens to a moving body if no force acts on it?\",\"options\":[\"It comes to an immediate stop\",\"It continues moving with the same velocity and direction\",\"Its velocity increases gradually\",\"It starts moving in circles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Newton\'s First Law states that if no force acts on a body in motion, it continues moving with the same velocity and in the same direction.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a 1 kg standard weight is pulled on a frictionless surface with an acceleration of 1 m\\/s², what is the force applied?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 Newton\",\"1 Newton\",\"2 Newtons\",\"10 Newtons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines 1 Newton (1N) as the force applied to a 1 kg weight to produce an acceleration of 1 m\\/s².\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a rolling glass marble eventually stop on a smooth tabletop?\",\"options\":[\"Due to its own weight\",\"Due to the force of friction\",\"Because it runs out of energy\",\"Because the tabletop is too big\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the velocity of the coin (or marble) decreases and it stops due to the force of friction.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, a question is asked about force. Based on the text and the concept of acceleration, what is the correct conclusion?\",\"options\":[\"Force is a scalar quantity\",\"Force is a vector quantity\",\"Force has no direction\",\"Force is not related to acceleration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since force produces acceleration (a vector), force itself is also a vector quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/059_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a body is stationary and no force is acting on it, what will happen according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It will start moving slowly\",\"It will remain stationary\",\"It will accelerate\",\"It will change its shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Newton\'s First Law states that if a body is stationary and no force acts on it, it will remain stationary.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is force measured according to the textbook description?\",\"options\":[\"By the distance the object travels\",\"By the time it takes to stop\",\"By the acceleration that it produces\",\"By the amount of talcum powder used\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'Force is measured by the acceleration that it produces.\'\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you pull a 1 kg weight with an acceleration of 2 m\\/s², how much force have you applied?\",\"options\":[\"1N\",\"2N\",\"3N\",\"4N\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that pulling a 1 kg weight with an acceleration of 2 m\\/s² means applying a force of 2N.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred if a moving body keeps moving with a constant velocity?\",\"options\":[\"A large force is being applied\",\"The force of friction is very high\",\"No force of friction is acting on it\",\"The body is accelerating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if no force of friction is acting on a moving body, it will keep on moving with a constant velocity.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the text mentions that acceleration is a vector quantity. Which other quantity mentioned in the topic text is also measured by the acceleration it produces?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Friction\",\"Force\",\"Time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that force is measured by the acceleration it produces, making it a vector quantity as well.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/059_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'no acceleration\' mean in terms of a body\'s velocity?\",\"options\":[\"The velocity is increasing\",\"The velocity is decreasing\",\"The velocity does not change\",\"The body is moving in a circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'if no force is acting on a body, its velocity does not change, i.e. the body does not accelerate.\'\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To perform the experiment of pulling a 1 kg weight to measure force, what kind of surface is required to define 1 Newton accurately?\",\"options\":[\"A rough wooden surface\",\"A surface with no friction\",\"A carpeted floor\",\"A wet surface\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies placing the weight on a \'surface with no friction\' to define 1 Newton.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a striker hits a carrom coin and it stops after a short distance, what is the primary reason for this?\",\"options\":[\"The coin is too heavy\",\"The striker was not strong enough\",\"The force of friction between the board and coin\",\"The talcum powder made it stop\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the velocity of the coin decreases due to the force of friction, causing it to stop.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the relationship between force and acceleration for a fixed mass as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Force is independent of acceleration\",\"Higher acceleration requires higher force\",\"Lower acceleration requires higher force\",\"Force only exists when there is no acceleration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 1 m\\/s² acceleration requires 1N, and 2 m\\/s² requires 2N, showing that higher acceleration requires more force.\",\"topic\":\"Force and acceleration\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as the ability to do work?\",\"options\":[\"Force\",\"Displacement\",\"Energy\",\"Motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the ability to do work is called energy.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unit of force in the SI system?\",\"options\":[\"Joule\",\"Erg\",\"Newton\",\"Metre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in the SI system, the unit of force is Newton (N).\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a force is applied to a body and it results in the body moving forward, what can we say has happened?\",\"options\":[\"Energy is lost\",\"The force has done some work\",\"The displacement is zero\",\"The body has no energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that due to displacement, we say that the force has done some work.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Calculate the work done if a force of 10 N displaces an object by 5 metres in the direction of the force.\",\"options\":[\"2 J\",\"15 J\",\"50 J\",\"0.5 J\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula W = F × s, Work = 10 N × 5 m = 50 J.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the SI system, what is the unit of displacement used to calculate work?\",\"options\":[\"Centimetre\",\"Metre\",\"Kilometre\",\"Newton\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in the SI system, the unit of displacement is metre (m).\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the CGS system, the unit of work is called the ______.\",\"options\":[\"Joule\",\"Newton\",\"Metre\",\"Erg\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that in the CGS system the unit of work is erg.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly expresses the relationship between Work (W), Force (F), and Displacement (s)?\",\"options\":[\"W = F \\/ s\",\"W = F + s\",\"W = F × s\",\"W = s \\/ F\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The formula provided in the text is Work (W) = force (F) × displacement (s).\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student pushes a heavy wall with a force of 50 N but the wall does not move (displacement is 0), how much work is done?\",\"options\":[\"50 J\",\"500 J\",\"0 J\",\"5 J\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since Work = Force × Displacement, if displacement is 0, the work done is 0 J.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how can the force applied to the wooden block be increased?\",\"options\":[\"By adding more weight to the string\",\"By removing the pulley\",\"By shortening the string\",\"By moving the table\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks how the force can be increased in the context of the weight tied to the string, implying adding more weight increases the pulling force.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/059_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the information in the given figure, which conditions must be met to say that 1 Joule of work is done?\",\"options\":[\"1N force and 1m displacement in the direction of force\",\"1N force and 1cm displacement in the direction of force\",\"1 erg force and 1m displacement in the direction of force\",\"1N force and no displacement\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure defines 1 Joule as the work done when a force of 1N displaces a block by 1 metre in the direction of the force.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/059_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the example of the wooden block, the work is calculated based on displacement that takes place in which direction?\",\"options\":[\"Opposite to the force\",\"Perpendicular to the force\",\"In the direction of the force\",\"Towards the pulley only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that displacement takes place in the direction of the force.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, if the force is increased by adding more weight in the pulley experiment, what is expected to happen to the work done, assuming displacement occurs?\",\"options\":[\"Work done will decrease\",\"Work done will increase\",\"Work done will remain zero\",\"Work done will only depend on the pulley\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since Work = Force × Displacement, increasing the force while displacement occurs will result in more work being done.\",\"topic\":\"Force, displacement and work\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21993,46,7,'Science','en','Static Electricity','[\"Electric charge is a property of tiny particles inside all objects.\",\"Each atom has a positive nucleus and negative electrons; normally they balance so the atom is neutral.\",\"When two objects are rubbed, electrons move from one to the other, leaving one positively charged and the other negatively charged.\",\"A positively charged object attracts negatively charged objects and repels other positive ones; the opposite holds for negative charges.\",\"Like charges repel, unlike charges attract – this is called repulsion and attraction.\",\"Static electricity is the build‑up of charge that stays on an object for a short time.\",\"Frictional electricity is produced when two different materials rub against each other.\",\"Induction is when a charged object causes a nearby neutral object to become charged without touching it.\",\"A charged object can attract an uncharged object because the uncharged one becomes polarized.\",\"A simple electroscope (copper rod with gold leaves) shows charge by the leaves opening apart.\",\"Lightning is a huge discharge of static electricity between clouds and the ground.\",\"A lightning conductor (copper rod with a pointed top) directs the charge safely into the earth.\",\"The upper end of a lightning conductor is pointed to concentrate the electric field and attract the charge.\",\"Coal and salt in the ground improve conductivity, spreading the charge into the earth.\",\"When a charged comb is brought near flowing water, the water becomes temporarily charged and is attracted to the comb.\",\"When a charged balloon touches an aluminium ball, both become charged in the same way (contact).\",\"When a charged balloon is brought near an aluminium ball, the ball becomes oppositely charged by induction and they attract.\",\"When a charged balloon touches an aluminium ball, the ball becomes similarly charged and they repel.\",\"The earth is a huge reservoir of charge; a lightning strike transfers charge from the cloud to the earth.\",\"Electrostatic experiments should be done in dry conditions because moisture dissipates the charge quickly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are all substances made of?\",\"options\":[\"Large visible grains\",\"Very tiny particles\",\"Only positive charges\",\"Metal objects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all substances are made of very tiny particles.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two types of electric charges mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"North and South\",\"Hot and Cold\",\"Positive (+) and Negative (-)\",\"Dry and Wet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the two opposite types of charges as positive (+) and negative (-).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Electric charge is described as an ________ property of the tiny particles that make up substances.\",\"options\":[\"External\",\"Intrinsic\",\"Accidental\",\"Unnecessary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that electric charge is an intrinsic property of these particles.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is electric charge usually in a \'hidden state\' in most substances?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is only found in metal objects\",\"Because the charges are too small to see\",\"Because opposite types of charges are present in equal numbers\",\"Because charges only exist in the dark\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains it is in a hidden state because two opposite types of charges are present in equal numbers.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When is an object considered to be \'neutral\'?\",\"options\":[\"When it has only positive charges\",\"When it has only negative charges\",\"When it is made of plastic or polyester\",\"When the positive and negative charges are balanced\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when the positive and negative charges are balanced, the object is neutral.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does it mean when an object is said to be \'charged\'?\",\"options\":[\"The charges on the object are not balanced\",\"The object is made of metal\",\"The object has no particles\",\"The object is in a hidden state\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that if charges are not balanced, the object is said to be \'charged\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a plastic ruler is rubbed on dry hair, what effect will it have on small pieces of paper?\",\"options\":[\"It will push the paper away\",\"It will attract the pieces of paper\",\"It will make the paper change color\",\"It will have no effect on the paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a plastic ruler rubbed on dry hair attracts pieces of paper.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following actions might result in seeing a spark in the dark?\",\"options\":[\"Passing a polyester curtain slowly\",\"Rubbing a blanket with hands and taking it near a metal object\",\"Holding a plastic ruler near dry hair\",\"Counting the particles in a substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text gives the example of rubbing a blanket and taking it near a metal object to see a spark in the dark.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student passes by a polyester curtain repeatedly and notices it moves toward them. What is the cause of this effect?\",\"options\":[\"The wind from the student\'s movement\",\"The curtain is made of metal\",\"Electric charge\",\"The curtain is neutral\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the polyester curtain example to illustrate the presence of electric charge.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an object has a \'net charge\', what can we conclude about its internal state?\",\"options\":[\"The positive and negative charges are equal\",\"The positive and negative charges are not balanced\",\"The object contains no tiny particles\",\"The object is in a hidden state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that a net charge exists only when charges are not balanced.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement is true regarding the location of electric charge?\",\"options\":[\"It is only found in plastic and polyester\",\"It is only found in metal objects\",\"It is stored in all objects, including our own bodies\",\"It is only present when we see a spark\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that electric charge is held in abundance by all objects in our surroundings and even our own bodies.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between \'tiny particles\' and \'electric charge\' as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Particles create charges only when rubbed\",\"Electric charge is a fundamental property of these particles\",\"Particles and charges are two completely different things\",\"Only metal particles have electric charges\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that electric charge is an intrinsic property of the tiny particles that make up all substances.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall and Electric charge\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the scientist that named the electric charges positive and negative?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Newton\",\"Benjamin Franklin\",\"Albert Einstein\",\"Thomas Edison\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the scientist Benjamin Franklin named the electric charges positive charge (+) and negative charge (-).\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when one end of a glass rod is rubbed against a silk cloth?\",\"options\":[\"The glass rod loses all its mass\",\"A small charge gets transferred from one object to the other\",\"The silk cloth becomes magnetic\",\"Both objects lose their electric properties\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, rubbing causes a small charge to get transferred from one object to the other.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the phenomenon called when two rods carrying similar charges push each other away?\",\"options\":[\"Attraction\",\"Conduction\",\"Repulsion\",\"Friction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the pushing away of two rods carrying similar charges as repulsion.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment, what material is the plastic rod rubbed against to charge it?\",\"options\":[\"Silk cloth\",\"Cotton cloth\",\"Woollen cloth\",\"Nylon cloth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies taking a plastic rod and rubbing one of its ends against a woollen cloth.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the interaction between the two rods in part A represent?\",\"options\":[\"Attraction\",\"Repulsion\",\"Neutralization\",\"Magnetism\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In part A, the arrows show the two glass rods pushing each other away, which is repulsion.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/061_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of charge is shown on the suspended glass rod in both experiments A and B?\",\"options\":[\"Negative charge (-)\",\"Neutral charge (0)\",\"Positive charge (+)\",\"No charge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows \'+\' signs on the glass rod, which represents a positive charge.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/061_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result when a charged plastic rod is brought near a suspended charged glass rod?\",\"options\":[\"They push each other away\",\"They are pulled towards each other\",\"They remain stationary\",\"They change their color\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The second experiment shows that the glass rod and plastic rod are pulled towards each other because they carry opposite charges.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If two objects are rubbed together and both become charged, how do they interact if they carry opposite types of charges?\",\"options\":[\"They repel each other\",\"They attract each other\",\"They do not interact\",\"They push each other away\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that rods carrying opposite types of charges get pulled towards each other, which is called attraction.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to bring two glass rods that were both rubbed with silk near each other, what would happen?\",\"options\":[\"They would attract each other\",\"They would push each other away\",\"They would stick together\",\"Nothing would happen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since both are glass rods rubbed with silk, they carry similar charges and will push each other away (repulsion).\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do the glass rod and the plastic rod behave differently when brought near the suspended rod in the experiments?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are made of different lengths\",\"Because they carry similar charges\",\"Because they carry opposite types of charges\",\"Because one rod is not charged at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the glass rod and plastic rod carry opposite types of charges, leading to attraction.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the necessary condition for two objects to attract each other?\",\"options\":[\"They must carry similar charges\",\"They must carry positive charges only\",\"They must carry opposite types of charges\",\"They must not be rubbed at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that rods carrying opposite types of charges get pulled towards each other (attraction).\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the objects immediately after rubbing a glass rod with silk cloth?\",\"options\":[\"Only the rod becomes charged\",\"Only the cloth becomes charged\",\"Both the rod and the cloth become somewhat charged\",\"Neither object becomes charged\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to rubbing, a charge is transferred, and as a result, both objects become somewhat charged.\",\"topic\":\"Repulsion and attraction\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are all substances ultimately made up of?\",\"options\":[\"Small pieces of paper\",\"Very tiny atoms\",\"Electric charges\",\"Nylon and cotton\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all substances are made up of particles, and these particles are ultimately made up of very tiny atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of charge is described as stationary within an atom?\",\"options\":[\"Negative charge\",\"Neutral charge\",\"Positive charge\",\"Moving charge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that each atom contains a stationary positive charge.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is an atom considered to be electrically neutral?\",\"options\":[\"It contains no charges at all\",\"The positive and negative charges are perfectly balanced\",\"It only contains stationary charges\",\"It is made up of very tiny particles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"An atom is neutral because its positive and negative charges are perfectly balanced.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the charges when certain objects are rubbed against each other?\",\"options\":[\"Positive charges move to the other object\",\"Negative charges are destroyed\",\"Negative charges move from one object to the other\",\"Both positive and negative charges move\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"When objects are rubbed, negative charges particles on one object go to the other object.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An object becomes negatively charged when it has an ______ of negatively charged particles.\",\"options\":[\"Deficiency\",\"Equality\",\"Excess\",\"Absence\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states an object becomes negatively charged due to an excess of negatively charged particles.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If an object loses some of its negative charges, what kind of charge does it acquire?\",\"options\":[\"Positive charge\",\"Negative charge\",\"Neutral charge\",\"No charge\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The object from which negative particles go away becomes positively charged due to a deficiency of negative particles.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the movement of the \'-\' signs indicate?\",\"options\":[\"Positive charges are being created\",\"Negative charges are being transferred due to friction\",\"The objects are becoming electrically neutral\",\"Stationary charges are moving randomly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows negative charges (\'-\') moving from one object to another during the process of friction.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/062_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the result of the process labeled \'Friction\'?\",\"options\":[\"Both objects remain neutral\",\"Only the orange object becomes charged\",\"The balance of electrically neutral atoms gets disturbed\",\"Positive charges are transferred to the grey object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Friction (rubbing) disturbs the balance of neutral atoms by transferring negative charges.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/062_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following materials is listed in the \'Try this\' section to be used for rubbing?\",\"options\":[\"Iron rod\",\"Silk cloth\",\"Glass slab\",\"Rubber band\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The materials listed for the procedure include paper, polythene, nylon cloth, cotton cloth, and silk cloth.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a ball pen refill is rubbed against a material and then attracts pieces of paper, what can be concluded?\",\"options\":[\"The refill is broken\",\"The refill has become electrically charged\",\"The refill is still neutral\",\"The paper pieces are magnetic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Attracting paper pieces is the observation used to determine if an object has become charged.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for the origin of an electric charge on an object?\",\"options\":[\"The creation of new atoms\",\"The movement of stationary positive charges\",\"The disturbance of the balance of neutral atoms\",\"The cooling of the object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Objects become charged when the balance of electrically neutral atoms gets disturbed.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an atom has 10 positive charges and 10 negative charges, what is its electrical state?\",\"options\":[\"Positively charged\",\"Negatively charged\",\"Electrically neutral\",\"Highly unstable\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"An atom is neutral when it has equal amounts of positive and negative charge.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what happens to the two objects being rubbed together?\",\"options\":[\"Both become positively charged\",\"Both become negatively charged\",\"One becomes positively charged and the other negatively charged\",\"They both lose their atoms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that of the two objects being rubbed, one becomes positively charged and the other, negatively charged.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the condition for an atom to become positively charged?\",\"options\":[\"Its negative charge increases\",\"Its negative charge decreases\",\"Its positive charge decreases\",\"It gains more atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Always remember\' section states that if the negative charge decreases, the atom becomes positively charged.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text ask \'Do all objects get charged by rubbing?\' in the brain power section?\",\"options\":[\"To suggest that rubbing always charges every material\",\"To imply that only metals can be charged\",\"To encourage investigation into whether different materials respond differently to rubbing\",\"To show that atoms are not present in all objects\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This question prompts students to evaluate if the results of the experiment (like the steel spoon or copper strip) differ from the balloon or refill.\",\"topic\":\"Origin of an electric charge\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who named the property of amber to attract things as \'electricity\' in 1646 A.D.?\",\"options\":[\"Thales\",\"Thomas Browne\",\"Elektron\",\"Aristotle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Thomas Browne named this property \'electricity\' in 1646 A.D.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the Greek word for amber?\",\"options\":[\"Electricity\",\"Static\",\"Elektron\",\"Friction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that amber is called \'elektron\' in the Greek language.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is frictional electricity also referred to as \'static electricity\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it moves through the object\",\"Because it is produced only at the place of friction\",\"Because it lasts for a very long time\",\"Because it is produced by moist air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It is called static electricity because the charge is produced only at the place of friction and does not move.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to demonstrate static electricity. Which weather condition would be most successful according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Humid summer\",\"Rainy evening\",\"Dry winter day\",\"Misty morning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests performing experiments in dry weather, particularly in winter, to avoid charge absorption.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to static electricity charges when the air is moist?\",\"options\":[\"They become stronger\",\"They are absorbed in the air\",\"They move to the woollen cloth\",\"They turn into yellow amber\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the charges of static electricity are absorbed in moist air.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what did Thales rub the amber rod against to attract feathers?\",\"options\":[\"Silk cloth\",\"Cotton cloth\",\"Woollen cloth\",\"Plastic sheet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Thales found that feathers are attracted to amber which had been rubbed against a woollen cloth.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is frictional electricity defined in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Charge produced by heat\",\"Charge generated by friction\",\"Charge that flows through wires\",\"Charge found in feathers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines frictional electricity as the electric charge generated by friction.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you rub a plastic rod on a piece of cloth, where will the static charge be located on the rod?\",\"options\":[\"Throughout the entire rod\",\"Only at the place of friction\",\"In the air around the rod\",\"Only at the ends of the rod\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that this charge is produced only at the place of friction.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Evaluate why static electricity experiments are difficult to perform during the monsoon season.\",\"options\":[\"Because the air is too cold\",\"Because the air is moist and absorbs the charges\",\"Because amber does not work in rain\",\"Because friction cannot occur in wet weather\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Static charges are absorbed in moist air, which is prevalent during the monsoon, making experiments fail.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the origin of the term \'electricity\'. Why was this specific name chosen?\",\"options\":[\"It was the name of the scientist who discovered it\",\"It is derived from the Greek word for amber, \'elektron\'\",\"It refers to the woollen cloth used in the experiment\",\"It describes the short duration of the charge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The property was named electricity because amber (which showed the property) is called \'elektron\' in Greek.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, identify the Greek scientist who observed the properties of amber 2500 years ago.\",\"options\":[\"Thomas Browne\",\"Thales\",\"Aristotle\",\"Socrates\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labeled \'Thales\' represents the Greek scientist mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/063_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, identify the person who named the property of attraction \'electricity\' in 1646 A.D.\",\"options\":[\"Thales\",\"Thomas Browne\",\"Isaac Newton\",\"Albert Einstein\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labeled \'Thomas Browne\' represents the person who coined the term \'electricity\'.\",\"topic\":\"Frictional electricity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/063_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is used as a test for identifying an electrically charged object?\",\"options\":[\"Attraction\",\"Repulsion\",\"Rubbing\",\"Heating\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that repulsion is used as a test for identifying an electrically charged object.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when two straws carrying similar charges are brought near each other?\",\"options\":[\"They attract each other\",\"They do not move\",\"They repel each other\",\"They become uncharged\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, there is repulsion between like (similar) electric charges.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is mentioned in the apparatus to be used for rubbing the straws?\",\"options\":[\"Silk cloth\",\"Plastic sheet\",\"Woollen cloth\",\"Cotton towel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The apparatus list specifically mentions woollen cloth (socks or gloves).\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the effect of unlike electric charges on each other?\",\"options\":[\"Repulsion\",\"Attraction\",\"No effect\",\"Neutralization\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that there is attraction between unlike electric charges.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the straw on the bottle when a straw with a similar charge is brought near it?\",\"options\":[\"It moves towards the other straw\",\"It moves away from the other straw\",\"It remains completely still\",\"It falls off the bottle immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the straw moving (repelling) because like charges repel each other.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/063_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table in the given figure, what is the observation when a charged straw is taken near an uncharged straw?\",\"options\":[\"Repulsion\",\"No change\",\"Attraction\",\"Vibration\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that electrically charged objects attract uncharged objects.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/063_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you rub two straws with the same woollen cloth at the same time, what will happen if they are brought near each other?\",\"options\":[\"They will attract because they are both straws\",\"They will repel because they carry similar charges\",\"They will attract because the cloth is woollen\",\"Nothing will happen because they are made of plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rubbing both with the same material gives them similar charges, leading to repulsion.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student rubs a straw with a woollen glove. If the glove is brought near the straw, what will be the observation?\",\"options\":[\"Repulsion\",\"Attraction\",\"The straw will melt\",\"No movement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The straw and the cloth used for rubbing carry opposite (unlike) charges, so they attract.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can attraction NOT be used as a sure test for identifying if an object is charged?\",\"options\":[\"Because charged objects attract uncharged objects too\",\"Because only similar charges attract\",\"Because attraction only happens with wool\",\"Because repulsion is stronger than attraction\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since a charged object attracts both oppositely charged and uncharged objects, attraction doesn\'t prove the second object is charged.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what can be inferred about the charges on the straw and the woollen cloth after rubbing them together?\",\"options\":[\"They have the same type of charge\",\"They have opposite (unlike) charges\",\"Only the straw gets charged\",\"Only the cloth gets charged\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes attraction between the straw and the cloth it was rubbed with, implying unlike charges.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a straw on a bottle moves away from a straw held in hand, what is the most accurate conclusion?\",\"options\":[\"One straw is uncharged\",\"Both straws carry similar charges\",\"Both straws carry opposite charges\",\"The bottle is electrically charged\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Repulsion only occurs between like (similar) charges.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the procedure where two straws are rubbed against woollen cloth at the same time, what is the \'Inference\' regarding their charges?\",\"options\":[\"They have unlike charges\",\"They have similar charges\",\"They have no charges\",\"They have lost their charges\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rubbing two identical items with the same material produces similar charges.\",\"topic\":\"Identifying a charged object\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is true about the charges on an uncharged or neutral object?\",\"options\":[\"It has only positive charges\",\"It has only negative charges\",\"The number of positive and negative charges are equal\",\"It has no charges at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the number of positive and negative charges are equal on an uncharged or neutral object.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the electric charge generated by induction when the charged object is moved away?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes permanent\",\"It stays only as long as the charged object is near\",\"It increases in strength\",\"It changes into a magnetic charge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the electric charge generated by induction stays only as long as the charged object is near to it.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the pieces of paper when the comb is rubbed against paper and then brought near them?\",\"options\":[\"They are repelled by the comb\",\"They get attracted to the comb\",\"They remain stationary\",\"They start spinning\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text describe charging by contact\\/induction where the comb attracts the pieces of paper.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When a negatively charged comb is brought near a trickle of water, why does the water get attracted?\",\"options\":[\"Because the water is initially negatively charged\",\"Due to the attraction between opposite charges\",\"Because the comb loses its charge to the air\",\"Due to the weight of the comb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that attraction occurs due to the opposite charges between the comb (negative) and the induced charge in the water (positive).\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the reason for a part of the flowing water becoming positively charged when a negatively charged comb is near it?\",\"options\":[\"It gains more positive particles from the comb\",\"It loses all its charges to the tap\",\"There is a deficiency of negative charge because negative particles are pushed away\",\"The water molecules turn into air\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that negative particles are pushed away, making that part positively charged due to a deficiency of negative charge.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the effect shown in the third stage (labeled \'c\')?\",\"options\":[\"Opposite charges attract each other\",\"The ball becomes neutral\",\"Like charges repel each other\",\"The balloon loses its charge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Figure C and the text show that after touching, both objects become similarly charged and like charges repel each other.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/065_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What observation is made when a spent tubelight is rubbed vigorously with a thin polythene bag in a dark place?\",\"options\":[\"The tubelight breaks\",\"The tubelight changes color permanently\",\"The tubelight glows or shows light activity\",\"The polythene bag melts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rubbing the tubelight with polythene generates static electricity, causing it to glow in the dark.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what phenomenon allows the balloon to remain in this position against the wall?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic attraction\",\"Gravitational pull\",\"Static electric charge and induction\",\"Chemical bonding\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A charged balloon sticks to a wall due to the generation of opposite charges by induction.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/064_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When an uncharged aluminium ball is touched by a negatively charged balloon, what happens to the ball?\",\"options\":[\"It remains uncharged\",\"It becomes positively charged\",\"It becomes similarly charged (negatively charged)\",\"It disappears\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that on touching, both the objects become similarly charged.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the flow of water when the charged comb is taken far away?\",\"options\":[\"It remains attracted to the spot where the comb was\",\"It becomes positively charged permanently\",\"It becomes uncharged again and stops getting attracted\",\"It starts repelling the tap\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when the comb is taken away, charges become equal again and the water becomes uncharged.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to charge a plastic comb by contact as per the first experiment, what must you do?\",\"options\":[\"Hold it near a magnet\",\"Rub it against paper\",\"Dip it in water\",\"Keep it in the dark\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first experiment in the text starts by rubbing a plastic comb against paper to charge it.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a method of charging an object mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Convection\",\"Induction\",\"Radiation\",\"Vibration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly discusses \'To charge an object by induction\'.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a negatively charged object is brought near a neutral object without touching, what charge is generated in the nearest part of the neutral object?\",\"options\":[\"Negative charge\",\"Positive charge\",\"No charge\",\"Neutral charge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that an opposite charge (positive) is generated in the other object by induction when a negatively charged object is near.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is used to rub the comb or balloon in the induction experiment involving water?\",\"options\":[\"Polythene bag\",\"Hair\",\"Aluminium\",\"Paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Rub a comb or a balloon on your hair\' for the induction experiment with water.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiments described, what is the result of \'like charges\' meeting?\",\"options\":[\"They attract each other\",\"They repel each other\",\"They neutralize each other\",\"They create a spark\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states in the aluminium ball experiment that \'the like charges repel each other\'.\",\"topic\":\"Generation of electric charge\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of a gold leaf electroscope?\",\"options\":[\"To generate electric charge\",\"To detect the electric charge on an object\",\"To store lightning energy\",\"To measure the weight of gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states it is a simple device to detect the electric charge on an object.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal is used for the rod in the gold leaf electroscope described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Silver\",\"Copper\",\"Aluminum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the electroscope consists of a copper rod.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the gold leaves when an uncharged object is brought near the metal disc?\",\"options\":[\"They open up\",\"They collapse\",\"They remain closed\",\"They turn into copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when an uncharged object is taken near the disc, the leaves remain closed.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do the gold leaves open up when a charged object is brought near the disc?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are attracted to the bottle\",\"Because they are made of gold\",\"Because they get the same charge and repel each other\",\"Because the copper rod becomes heavy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the leaves repel each other because both are generated by the same electric charge.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of touching the metal disc of a charged electroscope with your hand?\",\"options\":[\"The leaves open wider\",\"The leaves collapse\",\"The copper rod melts\",\"The bottle breaks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when we touch the disc with our hand, the leaves collapse.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When the electroscope is discharged by hand, where does the electric charge go?\",\"options\":[\"Into the air\",\"Into the gold leaves\",\"Into the earth through our body\",\"Into the glass bottle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the charge goes into the earth through our body, causing the leaves to discharge.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Benjamin Franklin\'s 1752 experiment, what material was used to make the kite?\",\"options\":[\"Paper and plastic\",\"Silk cloth, deodar wood, and metal wire\",\"Cotton cloth and iron wire\",\"Nylon and copper wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists silk cloth, deodar wood, and a metal wire as the materials for the kite.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who assisted Benjamin Franklin during his kite experiment?\",\"options\":[\"His son William\",\"A fellow scientist\",\"A copper smith\",\"His brother\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he conducted the experiment with his son William.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to the loose string of Franklin\'s kite when the metal wire touched the clouds?\",\"options\":[\"It caught fire\",\"It snapped into two\",\"It became taut\",\"It turned into silk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the loose string of the kite became taut when the electric charge reached it.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was seen when the kite string touched the earth during the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"A spark\",\"A flame\",\"Smoke\",\"Water droplets\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says a spark was seen as the string touched the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Benjamin Franklin prove through his kite experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Kites can fly in the rain\",\"Silk is a good conductor\",\"Lightning is a form of electric charge\",\"Deodar wood attracts lightning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that he showed lightning is a form of electric charge.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' section, what property must a metal have to be used as leaves in an electroscope?\",\"options\":[\"It must be very heavy\",\"It must be able to be made into very thin leaves\",\"It must be magnetic\",\"It must be transparent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to \'thin gold leaves\', implying the metal must be capable of being made very thin to move easily.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the metal disc located in the construction of the electroscope?\",\"options\":[\"Inside the bottle at the bottom\",\"At the upper end of the copper rod\",\"Attached to the gold leaves\",\"On the outside of the bottle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes a copper rod which has a metal disc at the upper end.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How was the metal wire positioned on Franklin\'s kite?\",\"options\":[\"Wrapped around the wood only\",\"One end at the top and the other joined to the string\",\"Inside the silk cloth\",\"Tied to the bottom of the string\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the wire was joined so one end was at the top and the other joined to the string.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the gold leaves of an electroscope are \'open\', what does this indicate about the object near the disc?\",\"options\":[\"The object is uncharged\",\"The object is made of gold\",\"The object is charged\",\"The object is made of wood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says when a charged object is taken near the disc, the leaves open up.\",\"topic\":\"Gold leaf electroscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21994,46,7,'Science','en','Heat','[\"Heat is a form of energy that can be transferred from a hot body to a cold body.\",\"Heat transfer occurs in three modes: conduction, convection and radiation.\",\"Conduction is the transfer of heat through a solid material by molecular contact.\",\"Conduction is fastest in metals like copper and slowest in insulators like plastic or wood.\",\"Convection is the transfer of heat by the movement of a fluid (liquid or gas).\",\"In convection hot fluid rises because it is less dense and cold fluid sinks.\",\"Radiation is the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves and does not need a medium.\",\"The Sun’s heat reaches Earth mainly by radiation.\",\"A thermometer measures temperature by the expansion of a liquid (mercury or alcohol).\",\"Heat causes solids to expand and liquids to contract or expand when heated.\",\"The expansion of metals is why railway tracks have gaps at the joints.\",\"A thermos flask uses a vacuum and a silvered inner surface to reduce heat loss by conduction, convection and radiation.\",\"White colour reflects more solar radiation, keeping objects cooler in summer.\",\"Black colour absorbs more radiation, keeping objects warmer in winter.\",\"Heat can be transferred by touching a hot object; the hotter the object, the faster the heat transfer.\",\"Heat energy can be stored in hot water or in a hot metal spoon.\",\"Heat transfer can be observed in everyday life: boiling water, ice melting, sunlight warming a room, etc.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which instrument is mentioned in the text as being used to measure the temperature of a substance?\",\"options\":[\"Barometer\",\"Thermometer\",\"Lactometer\",\"Speedometer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a thermometer is used to measure the temperature of a substance.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the primary cause for the changes in the state of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure\",\"Light\",\"Heat\",\"Sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that changes in the state of matter are caused by heat.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you rub your palms against each other and put them on your cheeks, what form of energy do you primarily feel?\",\"options\":[\"Electrical energy\",\"Sound energy\",\"Heat energy\",\"Light energy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rubbing palms is an activity mentioned to help understand the properties of heat energy.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the heat in boiling water slowly decrease once the container is taken off the flame?\",\"options\":[\"The water gains more heat from the air\",\"The heat is transferred away from the water\",\"The water turns into ice immediately\",\"The thermometer absorbs all the heat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks where the heat goes, implying it leaves the water and moves to the surroundings.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the moisture that collects on the outside of a cold glass come from?\",\"options\":[\"From the ice cubes inside the glass\",\"From the glass material itself\",\"From the moisture in the surrounding air\",\"From the water leaking through the glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that moisture in the air cools down and collects on the outside of the glass.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions that \'solar heat\' reaches the earth. What is the source of this heat?\",\"options\":[\"The Moon\",\"The Sun\",\"The Stars\",\"The Clouds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Solar heat refers to the heat coming from the sun.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the cause of the steam rising from the pots on the stove?\",\"options\":[\"The cooling of the air\",\"The application of heat energy\",\"The lack of water in the pots\",\"The pressure of the room\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Heat from the stove causes the water to boil and produce steam as shown in the picture.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/068_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, a device is placed in the person\'s mouth. What is this device measuring?\",\"options\":[\"Blood pressure\",\"Heart rate\",\"Body temperature\",\"Oxygen level\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a thermometer being used to measure the temperature of a person who appears unwell.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/068_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the moisture in the air when it touches the cold surface of the glass?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into ice cubes\",\"It heats up and disappears\",\"It cools down and collects as droplets\",\"It enters the glass to fill it up\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that moisture in the air cools down due to ice cubes and collects on the outside.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/068_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity of rubbing palms and the examples provided, which of the following is a characteristic of heat?\",\"options\":[\"It is a form of energy that can be felt\",\"It only exists in the sun\",\"It cannot change the state of matter\",\"It never moves from one place to another\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text describes heat as energy and shows through examples that it has effects and can be felt.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Heat is described in the introductory text as a form of ______.\",\"options\":[\"Matter\",\"Energy\",\"Solid\",\"Liquid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the examples as showing properties of \'heat energy\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the temperature of boiling water after the flame is turned off?\",\"options\":[\"It continues to rise\",\"It stays exactly the same\",\"It slowly decreases\",\"It drops to zero instantly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that heat in boiling water slowly decreases once it is taken off the flame.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as the flow of heat from one place to another?\",\"options\":[\"Storage of heat\",\"Transfer of heat\",\"Creation of heat\",\"Destruction of heat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that transfer of heat means the flow of heat from one place to another.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does heat naturally flow between two bodies?\",\"options\":[\"From a cold body to a hot body\",\"From a cold body to another cold body\",\"From a hot body to a cold body\",\"From a hot body to another hot body\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that heat is transferred from a hot body to a cold body.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a halwai wrap a cloth around the end of his slotted spoon while stirring boiling milk?\",\"options\":[\"To make the spoon look better\",\"To increase the weight of the spoon\",\"To prevent the heat from reaching his hand\",\"To cool down the boiling milk faster\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The halwai uses a cloth to protect his hand from the heat transferred through the spoon.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a cold object when it is placed in the vicinity of a hot object?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes colder\",\"It becomes warm\",\"Its temperature remains the same\",\"It starts to melt immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when we take a hot object in the vicinity of a cold one, the cold object becomes warm.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a hot object when it is placed near a cold object?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes hotter\",\"It stays at the same temperature\",\"It becomes cooler\",\"It changes its color\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when a hot object is in the vicinity of a cold one, the hot one becomes cooler.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why do we use a handkerchief while holding a steel glass of hot milk?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the milk hot for a longer time\",\"To prevent the glass from breaking\",\"To protect our hand from the heat of the glass\",\"To clean the glass while drinking\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A handkerchief acts as a barrier to the heat transferred from the hot milk\\/glass to our hand.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a cold object becomes warm near a hot object, what can be inferred about heat?\",\"options\":[\"Heat is being lost by the cold object\",\"Heat is transferred from the hot body to the cold body\",\"Heat is being created by the cold object\",\"Heat does not move between objects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says we can infer heat is transferred from a hot body to a cold body when the cold one warms up.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the practice of wearing woollen clothes in winter mentioned in the context of heat?\",\"options\":[\"Because woollen clothes generate heat\",\"Because woollen clothes help in the transfer of heat\",\"Because woollen clothes relate to how heat is managed by the body\",\"Because woollen clothes are only for fashion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks this to prompt thinking about how clothes prevent the transfer of body heat to the cold surroundings.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which observation in the text proves that heat has moved from one object to another?\",\"options\":[\"A hot object becoming hotter\",\"A cold object becoming cooler\",\"A cold object becoming warm in the vicinity of a hot one\",\"A cloth changing color when wrapped on a spoon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The change in temperature of the cold object indicates that heat has been transferred to it.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When a halwai stirs milk, the spoon becomes hot. What does this demonstrate about the spoon?\",\"options\":[\"The spoon is creating its own heat\",\"Heat is being transferred through the spoon\",\"The spoon is cooling the milk down\",\"The spoon is not affected by the boiling milk\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The need for a cloth shows that heat flows from the boiling milk through the spoon to the handle.\",\"topic\":\"Transfer of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a mode of heat transfer in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction\",\"Convection\",\"Radiation\",\"Insulation\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists conduction, convection, and radiation as the modes of heat transfer.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the conduction experiment, what was the approximate length of the stainless steel, aluminium, and copper bars?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"30 cm\",\"50 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies taking bars that are each about 30 cm long.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the transfer of heat from the hot part of an object to the cold part called?\",\"options\":[\"Convection\",\"Radiation\",\"Conduction\",\"Expansion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the transfer of heat from the hot part of an object to the cold part as conduction of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do the pins start falling from the end of the bar that is near the burner?\",\"options\":[\"Because the burner is magnetic\",\"Because heat transfers from the hot end to the cold end\",\"Because the wax is stronger at the cold end\",\"Because the bar is tilted downwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that pins fall starting from the end near the burner because heat transfer takes place from the hot end to the cold end.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the experiment show about the medium required for conduction?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction occurs only in a vacuum\",\"Conduction does not require any medium\",\"Conduction takes place through a solid substance\",\"Conduction only occurs in liquids\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that conduction takes place through a solid substance, showing that a medium is required.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, which property determines how heat is conducted through a substance?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the substance\",\"The property of that substance\",\"The color of the substance\",\"The length of the substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that conduction of heat through a substance depends on the property of that substance.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student places wax spots at 2 cm, 4 cm, and 6 cm from the heated end of a copper bar, which pin will fall last?\",\"options\":[\"The pin at 2 cm\",\"The pin at 4 cm\",\"The pin at 6 cm\",\"All pins will fall at the same time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since heat travels from the hot end to the cold end, the pin furthest from the heat source (6 cm) will receive heat last.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A copper bar and a steel bar are heated at one end. Which bar will have its pins fall off first?\",\"options\":[\"The steel bar\",\"The copper bar\",\"Both will fall at the same time\",\"Neither bar will lose pins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment observation states that the pins on the copper bar start falling first.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about copper compared to steel based on the experiment\'s results?\",\"options\":[\"Copper is a poorer conductor than steel\",\"Heat is conducted quickly through copper\",\"Steel conducts heat faster than copper\",\"Copper does not require a medium for conduction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that heat is conducted quickly through copper compared to steel.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which observation serves as evidence that heat moves progressively along the bar?\",\"options\":[\"The pins fall all at once\",\"The pins fall starting from the hot end to the cold end\",\"The pins fall starting from the cold end to the hot end\",\"The pins do not fall at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The sequential falling of pins from the hot end to the cold end demonstrates the progressive movement of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these factors was kept the same (constant) for all three bars to ensure a fair comparison?\",\"options\":[\"The material of the bars\",\"The time it took for pins to fall\",\"The length and shape of the bars\",\"The rate of heat conduction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies taking bars each about 30 cm long and having the same shape.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the pins on the steel bar fall \'comparatively late\', what does this suggest about steel\'s thermal properties?\",\"options\":[\"Steel is a better conductor than copper\",\"Steel is a slower conductor of heat than copper\",\"Steel does not conduct heat at all\",\"Steel is not a solid substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the pins fall later on steel than on copper, it indicates that heat travels through steel more slowly.\",\"topic\":\"Conduction of heat\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the process where heat is transferred by means of currents in a liquid?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction\",\"Radiation\",\"Convection\",\"Insulation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the transfer of heat by means of currents as convection of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is added to the water in the experiment to make the heat transfer streams visible?\",\"options\":[\"Common salt\",\"Potassium permanganate\",\"Sugar crystals\",\"Copper sulphate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Potassium permanganate crystals are used to create magenta coloured streams that can be identified easily.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which states of matter can convection occur according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Solids and liquids\",\"Liquids and gases\",\"Solids and gases\",\"Solids only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that convection can occur in liquid and gaseous substances.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the density of water near the bottom of the beaker when heating begins?\",\"options\":[\"Its density increases\",\"Its density remains the same\",\"Its density decreases\",\"Its density becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when heating begins, the water near the bottom becomes warm and its density decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to the convection process if the experiment were performed in a vacuum (no medium)?\",\"options\":[\"It would happen faster\",\"It would not occur at all\",\"It would happen at the same speed\",\"Only the color would change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Convection needs a medium\', so it cannot occur in a vacuum.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do the magenta streams move upwards from the bottom of the beaker?\",\"options\":[\"Because the water becomes heavier\",\"Because the water\'s density decreases upon warming\",\"Because the burner pushes the water up\",\"Because potassium permanganate is lighter than water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As water warms, its density decreases, causing it to move upwards.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What color are the streams observed in the water during the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Blue\",\"Red\",\"Magenta\",\"Yellow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that due to potassium permanganate, magenta coloured streams can be identified.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the convection process, what replaces the warm water that moves upwards?\",\"options\":[\"Air from the atmosphere\",\"Crystals of potassium permanganate\",\"Cold water coming from above\",\"Steam from the bottom\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that as warm water moves upwards, it is replaced by cold water coming from above.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student heats a beaker of water from the top instead of the bottom, how would it affect the convection currents described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Currents would move faster\",\"The warm water would stay at the top and not circulate downwards\",\"The density of the top water would increase\",\"The potassium permanganate would not dissolve\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Convection relies on warm, less dense water rising; if the top is already warm, the upward current mechanism is disrupted.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what is the relationship between the temperature of water and its movement?\",\"options\":[\"Higher temperature increases density, causing downward movement\",\"Lower temperature decreases density, causing upward movement\",\"Higher temperature decreases density, causing upward movement\",\"Temperature has no effect on the movement of water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Heating makes water warm, decreasing its density and causing it to move upwards.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the use of potassium permanganate considered a successful part of this experimental procedure?\",\"options\":[\"It heats the water faster than the burner\",\"It changes the density of the water\",\"It makes the invisible movement of water currents visible to the eye\",\"It prevents the water from boiling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that these streams \'can be identified easily\' due to the magenta color provided by the crystals.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are the specific currents called that transfer heat in this manner?\",\"options\":[\"Electric currents\",\"Convection currents\",\"Magnetic currents\",\"Tidal currents\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that heat is transferred by means of currents called convection currents.\",\"topic\":\"Convection of heat\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the density of water when it is heated according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It decreases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the density of water decreases due to heating.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the piece of ice wrapped in wire gauze before dropping it into the test tube?\",\"options\":[\"To prevent it from melting\",\"To make it float on the surface\",\"To ensure it sinks to the bottom\",\"To keep the water clean\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Wrapping ice in wire gauze ensures it stays at the bottom of the test tube for the experiment.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why does the ice at the bottom not melt even though the water at the top is boiling?\",\"options\":[\"The wire gauze acts as a heat insulator\",\"The burner is placed too far from the ice\",\"Hot water has lower density and does not sink to the bottom\",\"The test tube holder prevents heat from traveling downwards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because heated water is less dense, it stays at the top, preventing convection currents from reaching the bottom.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/070_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which process of heat transfer fails to occur when only the upper part of the test tube is heated?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction\",\"Convection\",\"Radiation\",\"Expansion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that because heated water cannot sink, the process of convection does not occur.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you wanted the ice at the bottom to melt quickly using convection, where should the heat be applied?\",\"options\":[\"At the top of the test tube\",\"At the bottom of the test tube\",\"On the test tube holder\",\"On the wire gauze directly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Convection occurs when heated (less dense) liquid rises from the bottom, so heating the bottom would melt the ice.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific part of the water has the lowest density?\",\"options\":[\"The water surrounding the ice\",\"The water in the middle of the tube\",\"The boiling water at the top\",\"The water near the test tube holder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states density decreases due to heating; therefore, the boiling water at the top is the least dense.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/070_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason the heated water cannot sink to the bottom of the test tube?\",\"options\":[\"Its density has decreased\",\"The wire gauze blocks it\",\"The test tube is at a slant\",\"The ice is too cold\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because the density of water decreases due to heating, it cannot sink.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what is the relationship between density and convection?\",\"options\":[\"Convection only happens when density increases\",\"Density changes are necessary for convection to occur\",\"Density has no relation to the process of convection\",\"Convection happens faster in high-density liquids\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that if heated (low density) water cannot move to cooler areas, convection does not occur.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which apparatus is used to hold the test tube at a slant during the heating process?\",\"options\":[\"Wire gauze\",\"Burner\",\"Test tube holder\",\"Candle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies using a test tube holder to hold the tube at a slant.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student repeats this experiment but removes the wire gauze, what will likely happen to the ice?\",\"options\":[\"It will sink and stay at the bottom\",\"It will float to the top and melt quickly\",\"It will turn into wire gauze\",\"It will not melt at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ice is naturally less dense than water and floats; without the gauze to weigh it down, it would float to the heated top.\",\"topic\":\"Density and convection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the transfer of heat that takes place in the absence of any medium called?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction\",\"Convection\",\"Radiation\",\"Insulation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the transfer of heat without a medium as radiation.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the sun\'s heat reach the Earth despite the vacuum of space?\",\"options\":[\"Through the air in space\",\"By the process of radiation\",\"By the process of conduction\",\"Through the earth\'s atmosphere only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that sun\'s heat reaches us by radiation because there is no air between the sun and the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which camera uses heat radiations to make surroundings visible at night?\",\"options\":[\"Digital camera\",\"Ultraviolet camera\",\"Infrared camera\",\"X-ray camera\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that an infrared camera uses radiations to make surroundings visible at night.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When heat rays fall on an object, what happens to the heat?\",\"options\":[\"It is entirely absorbed by the object\",\"It is entirely reflected by the object\",\"A part is absorbed and a part is reflected\",\"It disappears into the atmosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when heat rays fall on an object, a part is absorbed and a part is reflected.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment with two tins, which factor affects the ability of a substance to absorb heat radiation?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the substance\",\"The color of the substance\",\"The height of the substance\",\"The shape of the substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the ability to absorb heat radiation depends on its colour and intrinsic properties.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is an infrared camera useful for keeping a watch on the enemy at night?\",\"options\":[\"It produces its own light to see the enemy\",\"It uses radiations from objects like trees and stones to see\",\"It magnifies the moonlight to see clearly\",\"It detects the sound made by the enemy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that radiation takes place from many objects in nature, which the infrared camera uses to make surroundings visible.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects in nature radiates heat according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only the sun\",\"Only bonfires and candles\",\"Trees, mountains, and roads\",\"Only objects that are burning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that radiation of heat takes place from many objects in nature such as trees, mountains, stones and roads.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with two aluminium tins, why is one tin painted black?\",\"options\":[\"To make it look different from the other\",\"To prevent the tin from rusting\",\"To test how color affects heat absorption\",\"To keep the water inside clean\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment is designed to show that color (black paint) affects the temperature\\/absorption of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you hold your hands on the two sides of a candle, the heat you feel reaches you mainly by which process?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction\",\"Convection\",\"Radiation\",\"Refraction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the candle example to illustrate that heat reaches us mainly by radiation.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is true about the Earth\'s atmosphere according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It fills the entire space between the sun and earth\",\"It is a very thick layer reaching the sun\",\"It is only a thin layer of air close to the earth\",\"It prevents all radiation from reaching the earth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the atmosphere as only a thin layer of air close to the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The ability of a substance to absorb heat depends on its color and its ______ properties.\",\"options\":[\"External\",\"Intrinsic\",\"Magnetic\",\"Chemical\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that absorption depends on color and \'intrinsic properties\'.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with two tins kept in the sun, what is the expected outcome regarding the water temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Both will have the exact same temperature\",\"The water in the black tin will be warmer\",\"The water in the plain tin will be warmer\",\"The temperature of both will decrease\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since black absorbs more heat radiation (as implied by the experiment setup), the black tin\'s water temperature will differ (increase more).\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the medium required for radiation?\",\"options\":[\"It requires a solid medium\",\"It requires a liquid medium\",\"It requires a gaseous medium\",\"It does not require any medium\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines radiation as the transfer of heat that takes place without a medium.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how far is the sun from the earth according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Thousands of kilometres\",\"Millions of kilometres\",\"Hundreds of kilometres\",\"Billions of kilometres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'The sun is millions of kilometres away from us.\'\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If two objects are made of the same material but have different colors, what determines their different heat absorption?\",\"options\":[\"Their weight\",\"Their color\",\"Their distance from the ground\",\"Their age\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that color is a key factor in the ability of a substance to absorb heat radiation.\",\"topic\":\"Radiation\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, what is the recommended temperature of the hot water to be poured into the beaker?\",\"options\":[\"30°C-40°C\",\"60°C-70°C\",\"90°C-100°C\",\"10°C-20°C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies pouring hot water heated up to 60°-70°C into the beaker.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects mentioned in the experiment is a good conductor of heat?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic ruler\",\"Pencil\",\"Copper strip\",\"Glass beaker\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that heat is easily conducted through a copper strip.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for substances through which heat is NOT easily conducted?\",\"options\":[\"Good conductors\",\"Bad conductors\",\"Fast conductors\",\"Metal conductors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text classifies substances that do not conduct heat easily as bad conductors.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which item from a compass box is specifically mentioned to be tested in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Eraser\",\"Sharpener\",\"Divider\",\"Protractor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'a divider from your compass box\' as one of the objects to be placed in the beaker.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we hold a glass tumbler full of hot tea more easily than a steel cup?\",\"options\":[\"Glass is a good conductor of heat\",\"Steel is a bad conductor of heat\",\"Glass is a bad conductor of heat\",\"Steel is lighter than glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Glass does not conduct heat as easily as steel, making it easier to hold when filled with hot liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main inference drawn from touching the outer ends of the objects in the hot water?\",\"options\":[\"All substances conduct heat equally\",\"Only liquids can conduct heat\",\"Some substances are good conductors while others are bad conductors\",\"Heat only travels through plastic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment demonstrates that different materials have different heat conduction properties.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why does a copper pot feel hot very quickly when filled with hot water?\",\"options\":[\"Copper is a bad conductor\",\"Heat is easily conducted through copper\",\"Copper reflects all the heat\",\"Copper is a type of plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that heat is easily conducted through a copper pot.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are drinking hot tea, which container would be the most difficult to hold according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"China cup\",\"Glass tumbler\",\"Copper cup\",\"Plastic cup\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states we cannot easily hold a copper cup if hot tea is poured into it because it conducts heat.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' section, what color of clothes is preferred during winter?\",\"options\":[\"White clothes\",\"Dark or black clothes\",\"Light yellow clothes\",\"Transparent clothes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks why we use dark or black clothes in winter, implying they are the standard choice for that season.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to stay cool in the summer, which type of clothing should they choose based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Black clothes\",\"Dark blue clothes\",\"White clothes\",\"Thick woollen clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the use of white clothes in summer as a point for reflection\\/application.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A pencil and a divider are both placed in hot water. Why does the outer end of the divider feel hotter than the pencil?\",\"options\":[\"The divider is made of a good conductor, while the pencil (wood) is a bad conductor\",\"The pencil is longer than the divider\",\"The divider is a bad conductor of heat\",\"The pencil absorbs more water than the divider\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Metal (divider) conducts heat easily, whereas wood (pencil) does not, as per the conductor categories in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following materials from the experiment would be safest to use as a handle for a hot metal pot?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Steel\",\"Plastic\",\"Aluminum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since plastic is a bad conductor (as shown in the experiment), it would not transfer heat to the hand.\",\"topic\":\"Good and bad conductors of heat\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to metals when they are heated according to the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"They contract\",\"They expand\",\"They stay the same size\",\"They become lighter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that metals expand on heating.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a solid substance when the heat applied to it is removed?\",\"options\":[\"It expands further\",\"It melts completely\",\"It comes back to its original state\",\"It changes into a liquid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that solids come back to their original state if heat is removed.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the expansion of different solid substances?\",\"options\":[\"All solids expand by the same amount\",\"Solids do not expand at all\",\"The extent to which different solids expand is different\",\"Only metals can expand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the extent to which different solids expand is different.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the ball and ring experiment, why does the ball pass through the ring again after it cools down?\",\"options\":[\"Because the ring became larger\",\"Because the ball contracted on cooling\",\"Because the ball became heavier\",\"Because the metal turned into a liquid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Metals contract on cooling, allowing the ball to return to its original size and pass through the ring.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for leaving gaps at the joints of rails?\",\"options\":[\"To save the amount of metal used\",\"To allow the rails to expand due to heat\",\"To help the train wheels grip better\",\"To prevent the rails from rusting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gaps are left to accommodate the expansion of the metal rails when they get heated.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a metal ball just passes through a ring at room temperature, what will happen if the ball is heated for several minutes?\",\"options\":[\"It will pass through more easily\",\"It will melt and fall through\",\"It will not pass through the ring\",\"It will shrink and fall through\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Heating causes the metal ball to expand, making it too large to pass through the same ring.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the observation regarding the ball \'After heating\'?\",\"options\":[\"The ball has become smaller than the ring\",\"The ball is now larger than the opening of the ring\",\"The ring has expanded to be much larger than the ball\",\"The ball has changed its shape into a square\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the ball sitting on top of the ring after heating because it has expanded and can no longer pass through.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/071_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the water level in the hollow glass tube as the water in the flask is heated?\",\"options\":[\"The water level stays exactly the same\",\"The water level rises up the tube\",\"The water level disappears\",\"The water level falls to the bottom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the expansion of a liquid, where the volume increases and the level rises in the tube when heated.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the property of expansion, why are gaps also provided in cement concrete bridges?\",\"options\":[\"To allow for the expansion of the structure in heat\",\"To let water flow through the bridge\",\"To make the bridge lighter for cars\",\"To provide a place for birds to nest\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Like rails, cement concrete structures expand in heat and need gaps to prevent damage.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which general principle of solids is demonstrated by the ball and ring activity?\",\"options\":[\"Solids always melt when heated\",\"Solids expand on heating and contract on cooling\",\"Solids only change size when they are broken\",\"Solids never change their state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity is a classic demonstration that solids expand when heated and contract when they cool down.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion and contraction of solids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the volume of a liquid when it is heated?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases\",\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It first decreases then increases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when a liquid is heated its volume increases, which is called expansion.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment to observe liquid expansion, what is used to record the water level in the glass tube?\",\"options\":[\"Graph paper\",\"Thermometer\",\"Measuring ruler\",\"Wire gauze\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure states to record the water level in the glass tube with the help of a ruler.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a liquid is heated, what change occurs at the particle level?\",\"options\":[\"The particles become smaller\",\"The particles disappear\",\"The distances between particles increase\",\"The distances between particles decrease\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when a liquid is heated, the distances between its particles increase.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for the decrease in volume of a gas when heat is removed?\",\"options\":[\"Expansion\",\"Contraction\",\"Evaporation\",\"Condensation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"On removing heat, the volume of the gas decreases, which is called contraction of the gas.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student heats water in a conical flask and records the level in a glass tube. What will happen to the level when heating is stopped?\",\"options\":[\"The level will continue to rise\",\"The level will stay at the highest point\",\"The level will fall as the liquid contracts\",\"The level will overflow immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the temperature falls, the liquid contracts, meaning the level will drop.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a balloon is fixed to the mouth of a glass bottle and the bottle is placed in hot water, why does the balloon change?\",\"options\":[\"The glass bottle expands\",\"The volume of gas inside increases\",\"The balloon material shrinks\",\"Water enters the balloon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The volume of a gas increases on heating, causing the balloon to expand.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the liquid expansion experiment, how often should the water level readings be recorded?\",\"options\":[\"Every 10 minutes\",\"Every 5°C rise in temperature\",\"Every 2°C rise in temperature\",\"After the water starts boiling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies recording the water level after every 2°C rise in temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are mercury or alcohol used in thermometers based on the principles discussed in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they do not change volume\",\"Because they expand and contract due to heat\",\"Because they are the only liquids available\",\"Because they turn into gas very quickly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Thermometers work because these liquids expand and contract predictably with temperature changes.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to draw a graph based on the liquid expansion experiment, what would the graph show?\",\"options\":[\"Change in water level as temperature rises\",\"Change in the weight of the flask\",\"The time it takes for water to boil\",\"The color change of the water\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to draw a graph to show the change in water level as the temperature rises.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which apparatus is specifically required to hold the glass tube and thermometer securely in the conical flask?\",\"options\":[\"Wire gauze\",\"Two-holed rubber stopper\",\"Graph paper\",\"Burner\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A two-holed rubber stopper is used to insert the glass tube and thermometer into the flask.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the relationship between heat and gas volume?\",\"options\":[\"Heat has no effect on gas volume\",\"Removing heat increases gas volume\",\"Heating increases gas volume\",\"Heating decreases gas volume\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the volume of a gas increases on heating.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to the balloon on the bottle if it were moved from hot water to cold water?\",\"options\":[\"It would expand further\",\"It would contract\",\"It would burst\",\"It would stay the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"On removing heat (cooling), the volume of the gas decreases (contraction), so the balloon would contract.\",\"topic\":\"Expansion of liquids and gases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the scientist that made the first thermos flask in 1892?\",\"options\":[\"Sir Isaac Newton\",\"Sir James Dewar\",\"C.V. Raman\",\"Alexander Graham Bell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Sir James Dewar made the first thermos flask in 1892.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part is specifically designed to prevent heat transfer by radiation?\",\"options\":[\"Rubber support\",\"Metal\\/plastic jar\",\"Silvered surface of the glass\",\"Sponge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The silvered surface reflects heat, which prevents heat from being radiated out.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/073_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason that conduction and convection of heat do not occur between the two glass tubes?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the silver coating\",\"Because of the protective plastic jar\",\"Because of the vacuum created by removing air\",\"Because of the sponge fixed between the tubes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that neither conduction nor convection occurs because of the vacuum.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why does a hot substance in a thermos flask eventually cool down after two or three hours?\",\"options\":[\"The vacuum slowly fills with air\",\"Heat is lost from around the lid and through the glass\",\"The silver coating wears off quickly\",\"The outer plastic jar absorbs all the heat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions heat is lost from around the lid and by a small amount of conduction through the glass.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the scientist shown belongs to which country?\",\"options\":[\"England\",\"America\",\"Scotland\",\"Germany\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes Sir James Dewar as a Scottish scientist.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/072_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which materials are fixed between the outer jar and the flask to protect it from breaking?\",\"options\":[\"Metal springs and glass\",\"Silver and air\",\"Sponge or rubber\",\"Water and ice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that pieces of sponge or rubber are fixed between the outer jar and the flask for protection.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the vacuum between the two glass tubes is compromised and air enters, what will happen to a hot liquid inside?\",\"options\":[\"It will stay hot for a longer time\",\"It will cool down faster due to conduction and convection\",\"The silver coating will become shinier\",\"The radiation of heat will completely stop\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Without a vacuum, air would allow heat transfer by conduction and convection, causing the liquid to cool faster.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the basic structure of the inner part of a Dewar flask?\",\"options\":[\"A single thick metal tube\",\"Two glass tubes, one inside the other\",\"A glass tube inside a solid rubber block\",\"Three layers of plastic with air between them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes it as a double-walled flask consisting of two glass tubes, one inside the other.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the shiny silver coating on the surfaces of the tubes?\",\"options\":[\"To make the flask look attractive\",\"To reflect the heat and prevent radiation\",\"To strengthen the glass tubes\",\"To prevent the sponge from touching the glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the heat going out gets reflected due to the shining surface, hence heat is not radiated.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A thermos flask is used to keep a sherbet cold. How does the structure help in this?\",\"options\":[\"The vacuum allows outside heat to enter easily\",\"The silver coating absorbs the coldness\",\"The vacuum and shiny surface prevent outside heat from being transferred inside\",\"The sponge generates cold energy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The same mechanisms that keep heat in also prevent external heat from entering the flask.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which component acts as the outermost layer of the thermos flask to provide a protective covering?\",\"options\":[\"The silver coating\",\"The vacuum\",\"The metal or plastic jar\",\"The inner glass tube\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there is a protective jar of metal or plastic outside the tubes.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the flask\'s function, which mode of heat transfer is specifically blocked by the \'shining surface\'?\",\"options\":[\"Conduction\",\"Convection\",\"Radiation\",\"Evaporation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the shining surface, heat is not radiated.\",\"topic\":\"Thermos flask (Dewar Flask)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21995,46,7,'Science','en','Disaster Management','[\"Disasters are events that cause loss of life or property.\",\"There are two main types: natural (floods, earthquakes, volcanoes, tsunamis, storms, lightning, cloudbursts, famine) and man‑made (war, industrial accidents, pollution).\",\"Natural disasters happen because of changes in weather or Earth’s interior; man‑made disasters result from human actions.\",\"Famine is a severe shortage of food and water, often caused by drought but can be worsened by poor planning and overpopulation.\",\"Water scarcity can be reduced by water‑saving practices, tree planting, and proper water management.\",\"A cloudburst is a sudden, heavy rainfall (≥100 mm\\/h) over a small area, caused by rapid condensation of vapour.\",\"Lightning is a discharge of static electricity between clouds or between a cloud and the ground; 95 % strike the ground.\",\"Lightning can be dangerous because it heats air to 30 000 °C, causing a shock wave and fire.\",\"Volcanoes erupt when hot magma, gas and ash escape the Earth’s crust; they can create new land and pollute the air.\",\"Tsunami is a series of large waves generated by an underwater earthquake or volcanic eruption; they travel fast (800–900 km\\/h).\",\"Floods occur when water overflows its banks; protective measures include dams, percolation tanks and river‑bed flattening.\",\"Storms are intense weather systems with strong winds; safety measures include staying indoors and avoiding open ground.\",\"Safety tips for lightning: stay indoors, avoid trees, poles, wires, and water; keep 15 ft distance between people.\",\"Lightning conductors on buildings help direct the strike safely.\",\"Earthquakes can trigger tsunamis if they occur under the sea; the energy pushes water upward.\",\"Disaster management involves prediction, preparedness, response and recovery.\",\"Community awareness and proper planning can reduce the impact of disasters.\",\"The National Disaster Management Authority (NDMA) provides guidelines and information online.\",\"UNDP and other international bodies support disaster‑risk reduction globally.\",\"Remember: ‘No disaster is inevitable, but its impact can be minimized by knowledge and action.’\"]','[{\"q\":\"Into which two main categories are disasters classified in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Natural and manmade disasters\",\"Small and large disasters\",\"Predictable and unpredictable disasters\",\"Weather and climate disasters\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions studying two types of disasters: manmade and natural disasters.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the primary cause of natural disasters like cloudbursts and storms?\",\"options\":[\"Human negligence\",\"Changes in the weather\",\"Structural failures\",\"Accidental explosions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that natural disasters such as famine, lightning strikes, cloudbursts, storms, etc. occur due to changes in the weather.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed as a natural disaster in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Bridge collapse\",\"Plane crash\",\"Lightning strike\",\"Road accident\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists lightning strikes, famine, cloudbursts, and storms as examples of natural disasters.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What action is necessary to take in the case of disasters that cannot be prevented?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore the situation\",\"Take precautions\",\"Stop the weather changes\",\"Increase property damage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that while some disasters can be prevented, it is necessary to take precautions in the case of others.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What specific concern does a farmer have regarding his farm during the rainy season according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Preventing lightning from hitting the crops\",\"Preventing bunds from getting washed away\",\"Stopping the clouds from bursting\",\"Preventing a bridge collapse\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'What should be done to prevent the bunds on a farm from getting washed away in the rainy season?\' as a point of discussion.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between natural and manmade disasters as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are completely unrelated\",\"They are related to each other\",\"Natural disasters only cause manmade ones\",\"Manmade disasters prevent natural ones\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Natural and manmade disasters are related to each other.\'\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is a likely consequence of natural calamities?\",\"options\":[\"Improvement in weather conditions\",\"Prevention of all future disasters\",\"Damage to property\",\"Elimination of water-scarcity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'There is the possibility of damage to property in such natural calamities.\'\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a natural disaster occurring due to weather changes in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Famine\",\"Storms\",\"Cloudbursts\",\"Plane crash\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Famine, storms, and cloudbursts are listed as natural disasters due to weather, whereas a plane crash is not.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which news headline refers to a disaster that is typically classified as manmade?\",\"options\":[\"Tsunami smashes Asia\",\"Nepal quake kills over 1,000\",\"Mahad Bridge Collapse\",\"Varda Vadala\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A bridge collapse is generally a manmade disaster resulting from structural failure or human error, unlike tsunamis or earthquakes.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/074_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which news item describes a disaster caused by a sudden movement of the earth\'s crust?\",\"options\":[\"Truck accident\",\"Nepal quake\",\"Russia plane crash\",\"Phone trail points to local links\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The headline \'Nepal quake kills over 1,000\' refers to an earthquake, which is a natural disaster.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/074_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The text asks \'Why do we experience water-scarcity?\'. This question is most likely linked to which natural disaster mentioned later in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Lightning strikes\",\"Famine\",\"Cloudbursts\",\"Storms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water-scarcity is a core component of a famine, which is listed as a natural disaster in the text.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, who is responsible for the damage to property in natural calamities?\",\"options\":[\"The weather itself\",\"The text poses this as a question to be considered\",\"Only the farmers\",\"The people who build bridges\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'Who is to be held responsible for that?\' regarding property damage, indicating it is a point for reflection.\",\"topic\":\"10. Disaster Management\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the condition arising due to long term and severe scarcity of foodgrains and water called?\",\"options\":[\"Flood\",\"Famine\",\"Earthquake\",\"Storm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines famine as the condition arising from long term and severe scarcity of foodgrains and water.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is considered the main cause of famine?\",\"options\":[\"Earthquake\",\"War\",\"Drought\",\"Internal unrest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that of all causes, drought is the main cause of famine.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which continent has turned out to be the most famine affected continent in the world?\",\"options\":[\"Africa\",\"Europe\",\"Asia\",\"Australia\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Asia has turned out to be the most famine affected continent of the world.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is classified as a man-made cause of famine in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Locust attacks\",\"Cold waves\",\"Absence of transport routes\",\"Heavy rains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists absence of transport routes, war, and uncontrolled population growth as man-made causes.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do environmental changes like fog and cold waves contribute to famine?\",\"options\":[\"By causing internal unrest\",\"By damaging crops\",\"By blocking transport routes\",\"By increasing population\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions environmental changes like cold waves or fog as causes that lead to the damage of crops.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which types of regions do a majority of famines occur?\",\"options\":[\"High altitude mountain regions\",\"Drought prone and flood affected regions\",\"Dense forest regions\",\"Regions with heavy snowfall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a majority of famines occurred in drought prone and flood affected regions.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer\'s entire harvest is destroyed by a swarm of locusts. Under which category of famine causes does this fall?\",\"options\":[\"Man-made cause\",\"Natural phenomena\",\"Internal unrest\",\"Transport failure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists attack of pests and locusts as natural causes\\/phenomena leading to famine.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a country faces a severe lack of roads and railways during a food shortage, which specific cause of famine is being described?\",\"options\":[\"Environmental change\",\"Natural calamity\",\"Absence of transport routes\",\"Drought\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Absence of transport routes is a man-made cause that prevents food distribution, contributing to famine.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which two countries have experienced some of the most dreadful famines in the world?\",\"options\":[\"India and China\",\"India and Japan\",\"China and Russia\",\"USA and India\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that among the most dreadful famines in the world are those that affected India and China.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a natural cause of famine according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Earthquake\",\"Uncontrolled population growth\",\"Storms\",\"Pest attacks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Uncontrolled population growth is listed as a man-made cause, while the others are natural.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the relationship between human activities and famine?\",\"options\":[\"Famines are only caused by natural events.\",\"Human activities have no impact on famine conditions.\",\"Famine conditions can be created by human activities besides natural events.\",\"Only war causes famine in Asia.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that although the main cause is natural, famine conditions are also created by some human activities.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Besides foodgrains, the scarcity of which other essential resource defines a famine?\",\"options\":[\"Clothing\",\"Water\",\"Electricity\",\"Shelter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition of famine in the text includes the severe scarcity of both foodgrains and water.\",\"topic\":\"Famine\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why is water shortage increasing in modern times?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the lack of historical canal schemes\",\"Because the balance between rainfall and population is disturbed\",\"Because of the decrease in foodgrain production\",\"Due to the complete absence of rainfall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that water shortage is increasing as the balance between rainfall and population is disturbed.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which historical figure implemented a canal scheme for drinking water in Aurangabad that exists even today?\",\"options\":[\"Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj\",\"Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj\",\"Malik Ambar\",\"The Green Revolutionaries\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Malik Ambar implemented a canal scheme for drinking water in Aurangabad which exists today.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What has been a negative consequence of the green revolution despite the high rise in foodgrain production?\",\"options\":[\"Decrease in the use of pesticides\",\"Loss of environmental balance\",\"Increase in the number of canals\",\"Reduction in the population\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the balance of the environment has been lost due to the use of chemical fertilizers, pesticides, and weedicides during the green revolution.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which two historical leaders implemented water supply and storage schemes that are considered ideal even today?\",\"options\":[\"Malik Ambar and Shivaji Maharaj\",\"Malik Ambar and Shahu Maharaj\",\"Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj\",\"Malik Ambar and the Green Revolutionaries\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights the water supply and storage schemes of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj as ideal.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which numbered label refers to the wasteful use of a vital natural resource?\",\"options\":[\"Unlimited lifting of water\",\"Erosion of land\",\"Misuse of water\",\"Planting of trees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Label 5 in the figure explicitly mentions \'Misuse of water\' as a factor.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what action are the children performing that helps address the issues mentioned in the labels?\",\"options\":[\"Eroding the land further\",\"Lifting water unlimitedly\",\"Planting and watering a sapling\",\"Misusing drinking water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows children actively planting and watering a sapling, which is a positive action for the environment.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the text as a cause for environmental imbalance?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical fertilizers\",\"Pesticides\",\"Weedicides\",\"Canal schemes\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists chemical fertilizers, pesticides, and weedicides as causes for the loss of environmental balance; canal schemes are mentioned as solutions.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text suggest about the history of famines?\",\"options\":[\"They only started after the green revolution\",\"They are a completely new phenomenon\",\"They date back to historic times\",\"They were caused by canal schemes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Famines are not a new phenomenon\' and problems of food and water \'date back to historic times\'.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how can students contribute to society regarding future disasters?\",\"options\":[\"By increasing the use of chemical fertilizers\",\"By making water supply and storage schemes\",\"By ignoring historic water schemes\",\"By increasing the lifting of water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests students can make schemes like those of the past to face future crises and disasters.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor mentioned in the text relates to the physical degradation of soil contributing to famine?\",\"options\":[\"Unlimited lifting of water\",\"Erosion of land\",\"Green revolution\",\"Canal schemes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Point number 4 in the text and figure identifies \'Erosion of land\' as a factor.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text describe the water schemes of Shivaji Maharaj as \'ideal\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they used chemical fertilizers\",\"Because they were implemented in Aurangabad\",\"Because they are useful even in today\'s circumstances\",\"Because they caused the green revolution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states their schemes are \'ideal even in today\'s circumstances\'.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these represents a human-controlled factor that leads to water scarcity according to the list provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Natural rainfall patterns\",\"Unlimited lifting of water\",\"Historic time periods\",\"The existence of fodder\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Point 3 in the text identifies \'Unlimited lifting of water\' as a factor we are responsible for.\",\"topic\":\"Are we responsible for famine?\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is a recommended method for managing water to ease the severity of a famine?\",\"options\":[\"Increasing water consumption\",\"Planned usage and reuse of water\",\"Building more factories\",\"Ignoring water storage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that planned usage and reuse of water is a way to ease famine severity.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At which level should water conservation and harvesting be properly planned?\",\"options\":[\"International level\",\"National level\",\"Local level\",\"Global level\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions proper planning of water conservation and harvesting at the local level.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should be done regarding trees to help prevent famine?\",\"options\":[\"Large scale plantation of trees\",\"Increasing deforestation\",\"Cutting trees for rallies\",\"Using wood for fuel\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists large scale plantation of trees and prevention of deforestation as a measure.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to take weather forecasts into account?\",\"options\":[\"To stop planting trees\",\"To increase land erosion\",\"To make appropriate changes in plans\",\"To avoid reusing water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests making appropriate changes in plans by taking weather forecasts into account.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the slogan provided, what are the two direct benefits of large scale tree plantation?\",\"options\":[\"More rainfall and better crops\",\"No famine and no land erosion\",\"Less water and more wood\",\"Better weather and more rallies\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The slogan explicitly states: \'Large scale tree plantation – No famine, no land erosion!\'\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What activity is suggested to be done during Environment Awareness Rallies?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting down old trees\",\"Conserving water at home\",\"Using slogans related to tree plantation\",\"Predicting the weather\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests composing a variety of slogans and using them during Environment Awareness Rallies.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a village decides to collect every drop of rain in small ponds, which point from the text are they following?\",\"options\":[\"Prevention of deforestation\",\"Weather forecasting\",\"Water harvesting at the local level\",\"Large scale plantation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Collecting rain is a form of water harvesting, which the text recommends planning at the local level.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer decides to change the type of crop he is growing after hearing a report about a coming drought. This is an example of:\",\"options\":[\"Planned reuse of water\",\"Making changes based on weather forecasts\",\"Preventing land erosion\",\"Large scale plantation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Changing agricultural plans based on drought reports aligns with taking weather forecasts into account.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following actions directly addresses the \'prevention of deforestation\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Reusing kitchen water for gardens\",\"Stopping the illegal cutting of forests\",\"Checking the daily temperature\",\"Building local dams\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Deforestation refers to the clearing of forests; preventing it means stopping the cutting of trees.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text link tree plantation to the physical state of the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Trees cause land erosion\",\"Trees make the land dry\",\"Tree plantation prevents land erosion\",\"Trees have no effect on land erosion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The slogan \'Large scale tree plantation – No famine, no land erosion!\' links trees to the prevention of erosion.\",\"topic\":\"Easing the severity of a famine\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum rate of rainfall required for a weather event to be defined as a cloudburst?\",\"options\":[\"50 mm per hour\",\"100 mm per hour\",\"500 mm per hour\",\"950 mm per hour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a cloudburst is rain occurring at a rate of 100 mm per hour or more.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does rainwater sometimes vaporize and return to the clouds instead of reaching the land?\",\"options\":[\"Due to low pressure in the atmosphere\",\"Due to very high temperature near the land\",\"Due to the presence of dust particles\",\"Due to strong winds blowing upwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that high temperature near the land causes falling rain to vaporize and go back into the clouds.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"On which date did a significant cloudburst occur in Leh (Ladakh)?\",\"options\":[\"26th August 2005\",\"6th August 2010\",\"26th August 2010\",\"6th August 2005\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a cloudburst occurred at Leh (Ladakh) on 6th August 2010.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary cause of the sudden heavy rain during a cloudburst after the vapor amount in clouds becomes very high?\",\"options\":[\"Slow evaporation\",\"Rapid condensation\",\"Freezing of water droplets\",\"High wind speed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that due to rapid condensation, it rains suddenly over a specific and small region.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a specific region receives 250 mm of rain in 2 hours, how does this compare to the definition of a cloudburst?\",\"options\":[\"It is not a cloudburst because it is less than 950 mm.\",\"It is a cloudburst because the rate is 125 mm per hour.\",\"It is not a cloudburst because it lasted more than one hour.\",\"It is a cloudburst because it happened in a small region.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A cloudburst is 100 mm\\/hr or more; 250 mm in 2 hours equals 125 mm\\/hr, which exceeds the threshold.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During the extraordinary cloudburst in Mumbai on 26th August 2005, how much rainfall was recorded in inches?\",\"options\":[\"8 to 10 inches\",\"100 inches\",\"37 inches\",\"950 inches\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that on that day it rained about 950 mm, which is 37 inches.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the area affected by a cloudburst?\",\"options\":[\"An entire state or country\",\"A specific and small region\",\"Only coastal areas\",\"Only high-altitude mountain peaks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a cloudburst as rain occurring over a specific and small region.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the result of the 950 mm rainfall in Mumbai within 8 to 10 hours?\",\"options\":[\"The temperature increased significantly\",\"The clouds disappeared immediately\",\"The entire city was waterlogged and flooded\",\"The land became dry due to vaporization\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the rainfall caused Mumbai to be waterlogged and flooded.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' prompt, why is it dangerous to wait at a foothill during heavy rain?\",\"options\":[\"The temperature at the foothill is too high.\",\"Rainwater and debris can rush down the slopes rapidly.\",\"Clouds are more likely to vaporize at the foothill.\",\"The rate of condensation is lowest at the foothill.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a cloudburst or heavy rain, the sudden volume of water flows down slopes, making foothills dangerous for flooding or landslides.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which sequence correctly describes the process leading to a cloudburst as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"Rain -> Land -> Sea -> Cloudburst\",\"High temp near land -> Vaporization of rain -> High vapor in clouds -> Rapid condensation\",\"Rapid condensation -> High temp near land -> Vaporization -> Rain\",\"Cloudburst -> 100 mm rain -> High temp -> Vaporization\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes rain vaporizing due to heat, returning to clouds, increasing vapor, and then rapidly condensing.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to the water coming down from rain clouds before the Leh or Mumbai cloudbursts started?\",\"options\":[\"It turned into ice before reaching the land.\",\"It reached the land and caused immediate cooling.\",\"It vapourized due to high temperature near the land.\",\"It was blown away to a different region by wind.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that water vapourizes and goes back into the clouds due to high temperature near the land.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a cloudburst occurs at a rate of 100 mm per hour, how much rain would fall in 30 minutes?\",\"options\":[\"100 mm\",\"200 mm\",\"50 mm\",\"25 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"At a rate of 100 mm per hour, half an hour (30 minutes) would result in 50 mm of rain.\",\"topic\":\"Cloudburst\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did the Government of India establish the National Flood Commission?\",\"options\":[\"1967\",\"1976\",\"1986\",\"1996\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Government of India established the National Flood Commission in 1976.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the protective measures mentioned, where should small dams be constructed?\",\"options\":[\"In desert areas\",\"In coastal regions\",\"In mountainous regions\",\"In city centers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Construction of small dams in mountainous regions\' as the first protective measure.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a measure to control floods by managing the river\'s physical structure?\",\"options\":[\"Making river beds flat\",\"Making river beds deeper\",\"Making river beds narrower\",\"Making river beds curved\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"One of the protective measures listed is \'Making river beds flat\'.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal of the flood control plan prepared by the National Flood Commission?\",\"options\":[\"To increase rainfall\",\"To avoid large scale damage of property and loss of life\",\"To build new cities near rivers\",\"To stop the flow of all rivers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that large scale damage of property and loss of life can be avoided because of this plan.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How far down does the flood control plan reach according to the national level structure?\",\"options\":[\"Only to the state level\",\"Only to the district level\",\"Down to the village level\",\"Only to the city level\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a plan regarding flood control is kept ready from national level to village level.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which measure involves biological intervention to prevent floods?\",\"options\":[\"Connecting rivers\",\"Construction of percolation tanks\",\"Cultivation of new forests\",\"Making river beds flat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cultivation of new forests involves planting trees, which is a biological way to manage environment and water.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a village wants to implement a measure that helps water seep into the ground to reduce runoff, which should they choose?\",\"options\":[\"Connecting rivers\",\"Construction of percolation tanks\",\"Making river beds flat\",\"Building small dams\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Percolation tanks are designed to allow water to seep (percolate) into the ground.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A town is planning to manage excess water by transferring it to a water-deficient area. Which measure from the text applies here?\",\"options\":[\"Cultivation of new forests\",\"Connecting rivers\",\"Making river beds flat\",\"Construction of small dams\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Connecting rivers allows for the management and transfer of water between different river systems.\",\"topic\":\"Flood\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the National Flood Commission\'s plan considered effective for disaster management?\",\"options\":[\"It focuses only on mountainous regions\",\"It is only implemented at the national level\",\"It covers everything from national to village levels to avoid loss\",\"It prevents all rainfall from occurring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The plan\'s effectiveness comes from its comprehensive reach from national to 