topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which date is observed as the International Day for Disaster Reduction?\",\"options\":[\"13th October\",\"13th November\",\"2nd October\",\"5th June\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies 13th October as the International Day for Disaster Reduction.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is one of the main functions of the UNDP during a disaster?\",\"options\":[\"To predict the exact time of the next storm\",\"To build permanent houses for all citizens\",\"To send equipment, financial aid, and volunteers\",\"To stop the storm from occurring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that one of the main functions is to send equipment, financial aid, and volunteers to the place of disaster.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it recommended to regularly cut down tree branches that are likely to fall?\",\"options\":[\"To help the tree grow faster\",\"To prevent them from falling and causing damage during a storm\",\"To use the wood for fuel before the storm starts\",\"To make the surroundings look cleaner\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises cutting branches that are likely to fall and cause damage.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides medical teams, who else does the UNDP send to disaster-affected areas?\",\"options\":[\"Tourists\",\"Local shopkeepers\",\"Groups of other experts\",\"School students\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that international medical teams and groups of other experts are also sent.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are at home during a storm, what should you do with the utilities to ensure safety?\",\"options\":[\"Open all windows and turn on the fans\",\"Shut the gas regulator valves and turn off the electric supply\",\"Keep the television on to watch the news\",\"Increase the gas supply to keep the house warm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The safety measures suggest shutting gas valves and turning off the electric supply.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are away from home during a storm, what is the most important information to give your relatives?\",\"options\":[\"What you ate for lunch\",\"Your exact location\",\"The color of the clouds\",\"How many trees you see\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises informing close relatives and friends about your exact location if you are away from home.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what natural phenomenon is being depicted through the swirling motion of air and clouds?\",\"options\":[\"Earthquake\",\"Storm\",\"Forest fire\",\"Drought\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the formation of a storm as mentioned in the topic title and text.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/079_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure and the safety instructions in the text, what should you do if you are caught outside in the conditions shown?\",\"options\":[\"Stand under a tall tree\",\"Take shelter in a safer place\",\"Try to fly a kite in the wind\",\"Run towards the center of the swirl\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises taking shelter in a safer place if you are outside during a storm.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/079_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text suggest making telephone calls to warn others about the \'probable danger\'?\",\"options\":[\"To check if their phones are working\",\"To tell them to go to a safer place\",\"To invite them over for a party\",\"To ask them for financial aid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to make calls to warn relatives and tell them to go to a safer place.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the role of \'volunteers\' sent by the UNDP as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"People who are forced to work\",\"People who offer their services to help at the disaster site\",\"People who are victims of the disaster\",\"People who only provide financial aid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Volunteers are mentioned as part of the aid sent by UNDP to assist at the place of disaster.\",\"topic\":\"Storms and National Institutions\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21996,46,7,'Science','en','Cell Structure and Micro-organisms','[\"Cell is the smallest unit of life and the basic building block of all living organisms.\",\"All living things are made up of cells – unicellular or multicellular.\",\"The main parts of a cell are the cell wall (only in plant cells), cell membrane, cytoplasm and organelles.\",\"Organelles are specialised structures inside a cell: nucleus, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, mitochondria, lysosomes, vacuoles, chloroplasts (in plants) and ribosomes.\",\"The nucleus contains DNA and controls cell functions; it has a double membrane with pores.\",\"Mitochondria are the powerhouses of the cell – they produce energy (ATP).\",\"Chloroplasts in plant cells carry out photosynthesis, converting light energy into chemical energy.\",\"Vacuoles store water, nutrients and waste; plant cells usually have one large central vacuole.\",\"The cell membrane is a flexible, thin covering that controls what enters and leaves the cell.\",\"Prokaryotic cells (bacteria, viruses) lack membrane‑bound organelles; they have a plasma membrane, cytoplasm and nucleoid.\",\"Eukaryotic cells (plant and animal) have many organelles and a nucleus.\",\"Micro‑organisms are tiny living organisms that can only be seen with a microscope; they include bacteria, fungi, protozoa and viruses.\",\"Micro‑organisms are everywhere – in air, water, soil, food and even inside plants and animals.\",\"Some micro‑organisms are useful (fermentation, antibiotics, waste decomposition) and some are harmful (food poisoning, diseases).\",\"The human body temperature is about 37 °C, which is close to the optimum temperature (15–35 °C) for many micro‑organisms.\",\"Micro‑organisms can survive in extreme conditions by forming a protective covering (spores).\",\"Vaccines are made from harmless micro‑organisms or their parts and give immunity against specific diseases.\",\"Antibiotics are medicines produced by micro‑organisms that kill or stop the growth of harmful bacteria.\",\"Proper hygiene, food safety and waste disposal help prevent the spread of harmful micro‑organisms.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the fundamental structural and functional unit of living organisms?\",\"options\":[\"Tissue\",\"Cell\",\"Organ\",\"Organ system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of living organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is considered the primary characteristic of all living organisms?\",\"options\":[\"Movement\",\"Growth\",\"Cellular organization\",\"Nutrition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that cellular organization is the primary characteristic of all living organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the organizational levels mentioned in the text, what comes immediately after \'tissues\'?\",\"options\":[\"Cells\",\"Organs\",\"Organ systems\",\"Organism\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The hierarchy provided is: cells, tissues, organs, organ systems, organism.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do living organisms carry out all their different life processes?\",\"options\":[\"With the help of cells\",\"Only through organ systems\",\"By using external energy only\",\"Through tissues alone\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that it is only with the help of cells that living organisms carry out all their different life processes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the comparison of a book\'s structure to a living organism, which level of a book would most likely correspond to the \'cell\' level?\",\"options\":[\"Chapter\",\"Paragraph\",\"Sentence\",\"Letter or Word\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Since cells are the minute components and the basis of structure, they correspond to the smallest units like letters or words in a book\'s flow chart.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the life processes of unicellular organisms take place?\",\"options\":[\"In specialized organs\",\"Within the single cell\",\"In the organ systems\",\"Outside the organism\'s body\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since unicellular organisms consist of only one cell, all life processes must occur within that single cell.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following represents the correct increasing order of organizational levels in a living organism?\",\"options\":[\"Cells -> Organs -> Tissues -> Organism\",\"Tissues -> Cells -> Organs -> Organ systems\",\"Cells -> Tissues -> Organs -> Organ systems -> Organism\",\"Organism -> Organ systems -> Organs -> Tissues -> Cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the levels as: cells, tissues, organs, organ systems, organism.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the minute components of which the body of a living organism is made?\",\"options\":[\"Atoms\",\"Cells\",\"Molecules\",\"Grains\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies cells as the minute components of which the body of a living organism is made.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement is true regarding the number of cells in living organisms?\",\"options\":[\"All organisms have the same number of cells.\",\"The number of cells is the same in all animals.\",\"The number of cells is not the same in all living organisms.\",\"Only unicellular organisms have a fixed number of cells.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if the number is the same, implying variation, and identifies both unicellular and complex organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an organism is composed of multiple organ systems working together, what is the highest level of organization it has reached?\",\"options\":[\"Tissue\",\"Organ\",\"Organism\",\"Cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The final level in the organizational hierarchy provided in the text is the organism.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the cell referred to as the \'structural unit\' of life?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is very small\",\"Because it forms the basis of the structure of all living organisms\",\"Because it is only found in complex organisms\",\"Because it is visible to the naked eye\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that cells form the basis of the structure of all living organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which level of organization is missing in a unicellular organism compared to a human?\",\"options\":[\"Cell\",\"Life processes\",\"Tissues and Organs\",\"Structural organization\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Unicellular organisms perform all processes in one cell, so they do not have the higher levels like tissues, organs, or organ systems.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Cells\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the first scientist to observe hexagonal compartments in a cork section and name them \'cells\'?\",\"options\":[\"Anton van Leeuwenhoek\",\"Robert Hooke\",\"M. J. Schleiden\",\"Rudolph Virchow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In 1665, Robert Hooke observed cork under a microscope and named the compartments \'cells\'.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The term \'cell\' is derived from the Latin word \'cella\', which means:\",\"options\":[\"honeycomb\",\"hexagonal box\",\"small room\",\"living unit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'cella\' is a Latin word meaning \'small room\'.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which two scientists formulated the theory that all living organisms are made up of cells?\",\"options\":[\"Robert Hooke and Rudolph Virchow\",\"Anton van Leeuwenhoek and Robert Hooke\",\"M. J. Schleiden and Theodore Schwann\",\"Theodore Schwann and Rudolph Virchow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In 1838, Schleiden and Schwann formulated the cellular structure theory.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Rudolph Virchow\'s statement in 1885, how are new cells formed?\",\"options\":[\"From non-living matter\",\"From pre-existing cells\",\"From small rooms in cork\",\"From bacterial protozoa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rudolph Virchow stated that all cells are formed from pre-existing cells.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the specific contribution of Anton van Leeuwenhoek in 1673?\",\"options\":[\"He named the compartments \'cells\'.\",\"He stated that cells come from pre-existing cells.\",\"He was the first to observe live bacterial and protozoan cells.\",\"He formulated the theory of cellular structure.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Leeuwenhoek assembled lenses and was the first to observe live bacterial and protozoan cells.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a biological sample is 2 millimetres in length, what is its length in micrometres?\",\"options\":[\"20 micrometres\",\"200 micrometres\",\"2000 micrometres\",\"20000 micrometres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 1 millimetre = 1000 micrometres, 2 millimetres = 2000 micrometres.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A cell organelle measures 1 micrometre. How many nanometres is this equal to?\",\"options\":[\"10 nanometres\",\"100 nanometres\",\"1000 nanometres\",\"10000 nanometres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1 micrometre (or micron) = 1000 nanometres.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a compound microscope necessary for the observation of cells?\",\"options\":[\"Cells are extremely minute and cannot be seen with the naked eye.\",\"Cells only become visible when placed on a glass slide.\",\"Cells are only found in hexagonal compartments.\",\"Cells need to be kept in small rooms to be seen.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Cells are extremely minute in size and cannot be seen with the naked eye, requiring magnification.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Robert Hooke observed that the compartments in the cork section looked like those of a:\",\"options\":[\"Spider web\",\"Bee-hive\",\"Bird\'s nest\",\"Tree trunk\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hooke saw hexagonal compartments like those of a bee-hive.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following pairs of units are specifically mentioned in the text for measuring the size of cells?\",\"options\":[\"Centimetre and Millimetre\",\"Metre and Kilometre\",\"Micrometre and Nanometre\",\"Millimetre and Centimetre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies micrometre and nanometre as the units used for measuring cell sizes.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the compound microscope, which part is the flat platform where the slide is placed for observation?\",\"options\":[\"Base\",\"Tube\",\"Stage\",\"Eyepiece\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram labels the flat platform where the specimen goes as the \'Stage\'.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/082_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which component is located at the bottom to reflect or provide light for the specimen?\",\"options\":[\"Illuminator\",\"Objective lenses\",\"Fine focus knob\",\"Eyepiece\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The \'Illuminator\' is the part at the bottom used to provide light to the slide.\",\"topic\":\"Great Scientists and Measurement\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/082_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following solutions can be used to stain the onion skin membrane for observation under a microscope?\",\"options\":[\"Dilute solution of iodine\",\"Concentrated salt solution\",\"Sugar syrup\",\"Plain tap water\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using a dilute solution of iodine or eosin to observe the onion membrane.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, the shape of a cell is mainly related to its ________.\",\"options\":[\"Color\",\"Function\",\"Age\",\"Weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that cell shapes are mainly related to their function.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the maximum magnification power of an electron microscope as mentioned in the topic?\",\"options\":[\"10 times\",\"100 times\",\"Two million times\",\"Two billion times\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the electron microscope can magnify images up to (2 x 10⁹) two billion times.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the components inside a cell that carry out various life-processes called?\",\"options\":[\"Organs\",\"Organelles\",\"Forceps\",\"Membranes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines organelles as the components within a cell that carry out life-processes.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which cell is characterized by a long, branched structure used for communication?\",\"options\":[\"Red blood cells\",\"A fat cell\",\"A nerve cell (Neuron)\",\"Muscle cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the Nerve cell (Neuron) with a distinct long, branched structure.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to observe the cells of an onion, from which part of the onion segment should they separate the thin skin?\",\"options\":[\"The outer dry layer\",\"The concave surface\",\"The convex surface\",\"The root tip only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to carefully separate the thin skin from the concave surface of the onion segment.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which organism shows a rectangular shape with a spiral structure inside?\",\"options\":[\"Amoeba\",\"Spirogyra\",\"A sperm\",\"An egg cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates Spirogyra with a rectangular frame and internal spiral patterns.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is specifically required to study the detailed structure of cell organelles?\",\"options\":[\"Compound microscope\",\"Hand lens\",\"Electron microscope\",\"Telescope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that organelles are studied in detail with the help of the electron microscope.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During the onion cell experiment, why is it important to ensure the membrane does not get folded?\",\"options\":[\"To prevent the iodine from drying\",\"To keep the slide from breaking\",\"To allow for clear observation of the cell structure\",\"To make the cells change their shape\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Folding would overlap cells, making it impossible to see the individual cell structures clearly under the microscope.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a shape of cells mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Columnar\",\"Spiral\",\"Rectangular\",\"Triangular\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists circular, rod-shaped, columnar, spiral, oval, and rectangular, but does not mention triangular.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which microorganisms found in water were mentioned as having been observed in the previous year?\",\"options\":[\"Amoeba and Paramoecium\",\"Bacteria and Viruses\",\"Fungi and Algae\",\"Muscle cells and Nerve cells\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks if the student remembers observing amoeba and paramoecium found in water last year.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for the \'great variation\' in the shapes of cells observed in the text and figure?\",\"options\":[\"The type of microscope used to see them\",\"The specific life-processes and functions they perform\",\"The amount of water available to the cell\",\"The color of the stain applied to the slide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly links the variation in shapes to the functions the cells perform.\",\"topic\":\"Observation and Cell Size\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the outermost covering specifically found only in plant cells?\",\"options\":[\"Plasma membrane\",\"Cytoplasm\",\"Cell wall\",\"Nuclear membrane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the cell wall is the outermost covering and is present only in plant cells.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelle is known as the \'powerhouse of the cell\' because it produces energy?\",\"options\":[\"Golgi bodies\",\"Mitochondria\",\"Lysosomes\",\"Ribosomes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, mitochondria produce energy and are therefore called the powerhouses of the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the liquid part of the cell that occupies the space between the plasma membrane and the nucleus?\",\"options\":[\"Cytoplasm\",\"Chloroplast\",\"Vacuole\",\"Endoplasmic reticulum\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines cytoplasm as the liquid part in the cell present around the nucleus, occupying the space between the plasma membrane and nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of the nucleus in a eukaryotic cell?\",\"options\":[\"To produce energy\",\"To distribute proteins\",\"To control all functions of the cell\",\"To throw out waste products\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the nucleus controls all functions of the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do vacuoles in plant cells differ from those in animal cells?\",\"options\":[\"Plant cells have many small vacuoles.\",\"Animal cells have one large vacuole.\",\"Plant cells have a single large vacuole.\",\"Animal cells do not have any vacuoles.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that vacuoles in animal cells are small, whereas there is only one large vacuole in a plant cell.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelle is responsible for the proper distribution of proteins?\",\"options\":[\"Endoplasmic reticulum\",\"Golgi bodies\",\"Mitochondria\",\"Chloroplasts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the function of Golgi bodies is the proper distribution of proteins.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist observes a cell under a microscope and notices a cell wall, what can be concluded about the cell?\",\"options\":[\"It is an animal cell.\",\"It is a plant cell.\",\"It is a liquid cell.\",\"It is a flexible animal cell.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the cell wall is present only in plant cells.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A protein has just been modified by the endoplasmic reticulum. According to the text, where will it be sent next?\",\"options\":[\"To the nucleus\",\"To the mitochondria\",\"To the Golgi bodies\",\"To the chloroplasts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the endoplasmic reticulum makes changes in proteins and sends them to the Golgi bodies.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelle would be most active in a leaf cell during the process of photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Lysosomes\",\"Chloroplasts\",\"Vacuoles\",\"Ribosomes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that chloroplasts in plant cells carry out the function of photosynthesis.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the characteristics of the plasma membrane?\",\"options\":[\"Hard and rigid\",\"Porous and double-layered\",\"Thin, delicate and flexible\",\"Sprawling and net-like\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the plasma membrane as a kind of thin covering that is extremely delicate and flexible.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do plant cells have a definite shape while animal cells do not?\",\"options\":[\"Because plant cells have a large vacuole.\",\"Because plant cells have a cell wall.\",\"Because animal cells have a plasma membrane.\",\"Because plant cells contain chloroplasts.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states plant cells have a definite shape due to the presence of the cell wall around them.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What would be the most likely consequence if the vacuoles in a cell stopped functioning?\",\"options\":[\"The cell would stop producing energy.\",\"The cell would not be able to distribute proteins.\",\"Waste products would not be thrown out of the cell.\",\"The cell would lose its net-like structure.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that vacuoles help to throw out waste products of the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which two organelles are described as having double outer coverings?\",\"options\":[\"Nucleus and Lysosomes\",\"Mitochondria and Plastids\",\"Golgi bodies and Vacuoles\",\"Endoplasmic reticulum and Ribosomes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that mitochondria and plastids are organelles with double outer coverings.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The endoplasmic reticulum is described as a \'sprawling, net-like organelle\'. What is its primary role regarding proteins?\",\"options\":[\"Producing proteins from scratch\",\"Distributing proteins to the nucleus\",\"Making necessary changes in proteins produced by ribosomes\",\"Storing proteins in flat sacs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states its function is to make necessary changes in the proteins produced by ribosomes.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the nucleus, what feature allows materials to potentially move in and out of it?\",\"options\":[\"Its sprawling net-like shape\",\"The porous nature of its double membrane\",\"The liquid cytoplasm surrounding it\",\"Its position as the outermost covering\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there is a \'porous double membrane\' around the nucleus, which implies the ability for passage through pores.\",\"topic\":\"Cell Structure and Organelles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are organisms that cannot be seen with our eyes but can only be observed under a microscope called?\",\"options\":[\"Macro-organisms\",\"Micro-organisms\",\"Visible organisms\",\"Multi-cellular organisms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines micro-organisms as those which cannot be seen with our eyes but only under a microscope.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a solitary micro-organism?\",\"options\":[\"Fungus colony\",\"Bacteria colony\",\"Amoeba\",\"Bread mold\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that amoeba and paramoecium are solitary, meaning they live singly.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the smallest size an object can be for the human eye to still see it?\",\"options\":[\"10 micrometres\",\"50 micrometres\",\"100 micrometres\",\"1000 micrometres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that we cannot see objects smaller than 100 micrometres in size with our eyes.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate size of the Polio virus according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"28 micrometres\",\"28 nanometres\",\"100 micrometres\",\"1 to 3 micrometres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically lists the size of the Polio virus as 28 nanometres.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where can micro-organisms be found according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Only in water and soil\",\"Only in the bodies of animals\",\"In air, water, soil, food, sewage, and living bodies\",\"Only in garbage and dead remains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states micro-organisms are present all around us in air, water, soil, food, sewage, garbage, and bodies of plants and animals.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a magnification of 1000 X is not enough to see a microbe, which instrument should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Magnifying lens\",\"Compound microscope\",\"Electron microscope\",\"Simple telescope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if 1000 X magnification is insufficient, an electron microscope is needed.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with bread or bhakari, why is it kept in a closed box for 3-4 days?\",\"options\":[\"To keep it fresh\",\"To allow micro-organisms to grow for observation\",\"To prevent it from drying out\",\"To hide it from insects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment is designed to observe microbes that grow on the bread over time.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is recommended in the text to observe microbes under a compound microscope?\",\"options\":[\"Pure distilled water\",\"Dry bread crumbs\",\"Yoghurt or buttermilk\",\"Boiled milk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Try this\' section suggests taking a drop of yoghurt or buttermilk on a glass slide for observation.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the sizes mentioned in the text, which of these is the largest?\",\"options\":[\"Polio virus\",\"Bacteria of typhoid\",\"Paramoecium\",\"A single nanometre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Paramoecium is about 100 micrometres, while typhoid bacteria are 1-3 micrometres and the polio virus is 28 nanometres.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How do single-celled micro-organisms perform their life-processes?\",\"options\":[\"They do not perform life-processes\",\"Through various organs like humans\",\"Using organelles within the single cell\",\"By joining with other cells to form tissues\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that even in minute organisms, their organelles carry out all life-processes within the single cell.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the size of the bacteria that cause typhoid?\",\"options\":[\"100 micrometres\",\"28 nanometres\",\"1 to 3 micrometres\",\"Less than 1 nanometre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the bacteria of typhoid measure 1 to 3 micrometres.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definition of micro-organisms provided, which of these would NOT be classified as one?\",\"options\":[\"Amoeba\",\"Typhoid bacteria\",\"Snail\",\"Polio virus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to categorize organisms like snails and elephants separately from those only visible under a microscope.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a compound microscope insufficient for seeing a polio virus clearly?\",\"options\":[\"Because the virus is 28 nanometres, which is much smaller than 1 micrometre\",\"Because the virus only lives in sewage\",\"Because the virus is solitary\",\"Because the virus is larger than 100 micrometres\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text implies that very small microbes like viruses (28nm) require an electron microscope because 1000X magnification is insufficient.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'solitary\' mean when describing micro-organisms like Amoeba?\",\"options\":[\"They live in large groups\",\"They live inside other animals\",\"They live singly\",\"They live only in water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines solitary as \'that is, they live singly\'.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' question, why can the point of a needle accommodate micro-organisms?\",\"options\":[\"Because the needle point is very large\",\"Because micro-organisms are extremely minute and measure less than 100 micrometres\",\"Because micro-organisms only live on metal\",\"Because the needle point is made of glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Micro-organisms are so small (less than 100 micrometres) that even a tiny area like a needle point can hold many of them.\",\"topic\":\"Micro-organisms\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which national institute located in Pune is involved in research in the field of cell science and biotechnology?\",\"options\":[\"National Institute of Virology\",\"National Center for Cell Science (NCCS)\",\"Indian Institute of Science Education and Research\",\"National Chemical Laboratory\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the National Center for Cell Science (NCCS), Pune, is involved in research in cell science (cytology) and biotechnology.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the optimal temperature range for the growth of most micro-organisms as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"0° - 10°\",\"10° - 20°\",\"25° - 37°\",\"50° - 75°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies the temperature for growth as 25° - 37°.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of prokaryotic cells?\",\"options\":[\"They contain membrane-bound organelles.\",\"They are always multi-cellular.\",\"They do not have membrane-bound organelles.\",\"They lack a plasma membrane.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, prokaryotic cells do not have the membrane-bound organelles found in eukaryotic cells.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do micro-organisms protect themselves during extreme and adverse environmental conditions?\",\"options\":[\"They increase their growth rate.\",\"They form a thick covering around themselves.\",\"They move to a different medium like soil.\",\"They change their shape into algae.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that during adverse conditions, micro-organisms form a thick covering around themselves and stop their life-processes.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student observes a micro-organism that is unicellular and lacks membrane-bound organelles. Based on the text, what type of cell is this?\",\"options\":[\"Eukaryotic cell\",\"Multi-cellular cell\",\"Prokaryotic cell\",\"Fungal cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cells that are unicellular and lack membrane-bound organelles are called prokaryotic cells in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these micro-organisms is multi-cellular?\",\"options\":[\"Bacteria\",\"Viruses\",\"Fungus on bread\",\"Protozoa\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the fungus that grows on bread is multi-cellular, while bacteria and viruses are unicellular.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which labeled part represents the genetic material region typical of a prokaryotic cell?\",\"options\":[\"Capsule\",\"Plasma membrane\",\"Nucleoid\",\"Ribosomes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the central genetic region as the \'Nucleoid\', which the text identifies as one of the few components of a prokaryotic cell.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/086_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the life-processes of a micro-organism when it forms a protective covering during adverse conditions?\",\"options\":[\"They speed up to fight the conditions.\",\"They stop completely.\",\"They continue at the same rate.\",\"They change into reproduction processes.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that micro-organisms stop their life-processes when they form a thick covering during adverse conditions.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a sample of bacteria is placed in a completely dry and freezing environment, what is the most likely outcome based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"They will grow faster due to lack of competition.\",\"They will immediately die and disappear.\",\"They will form a thick covering and wait for favorable conditions.\",\"They will turn into algae to produce chlorophyll.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says microbes survive adverse conditions (like ice) by forming a thick covering and stopping life processes until favorable conditions return.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, micro-organisms are classified into groups like algae, fungi, and bacteria based on which two factors?\",\"options\":[\"Size and color\",\"Shape and life-processes\",\"Temperature and oxygen needs\",\"Soil and water content\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that according to shape and life-processes, micro-organisms are classified as algae, fungi, protozoa, bacteria and viruses.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure showing various microbes, what can be inferred about the physical appearance of micro-organisms?\",\"options\":[\"All micro-organisms have the same circular shape.\",\"They exhibit a wide variety of shapes and structures.\",\"Only bacteria have complex structures.\",\"Viruses are the largest organisms shown.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure \'A variety of microbes\' shows many different shapes (rods, spheres, spirals, etc.), supporting the text\'s mention of classification by shape.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/085_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as a component of a prokaryotic cell in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Plasma membrane\",\"Cytoplasm\",\"Nucleoid\",\"Mitochondria\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleoid are their \'only components\', implying they lack organelles like mitochondria.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What specific nutrient is mentioned in the text in relation to the growth of algae?\",\"options\":[\"Decaying matter\",\"Chlorophyll\",\"Sugar\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Under the \'Nutrition\' section, the text lists \'algae-chlorophyll\' as an example of specific nutrients.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text\'s description of growth conditions, which atmosphere is most suitable for microbial growth?\",\"options\":[\"Dry and cold\",\"Moist, humid, and warm\",\"Dry and hot\",\"Oxygen-free and frozen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists the atmosphere for growth as \'Moist, humid, warm\'.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Considering the survival of microbes in hot water springs and the ocean floor, what can be concluded about their adaptability?\",\"options\":[\"They can only grow in soil and water.\",\"They are fragile and die easily.\",\"They can survive even in extreme and adverse conditions.\",\"They require human intervention to survive.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that some micro-organisms survive even in extreme and adverse conditions like the ocean floor and hot water springs.\",\"topic\":\"Nature and Growth of Micro-organisms\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What do microbes in the soil convert organic garbage into?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic scrap\",\"Manure\",\"Glass pieces\",\"Toxic chemicals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, microbes decompose garbage to obtain food, converting it into manure of the best quality.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do micro-organisms decompose garbage present in the soil?\",\"options\":[\"To produce heat\",\"To obtain food material\",\"To increase the volume of soil\",\"To create plastic bags\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that microbes present in soil and dung decompose garbage to obtain food material.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these materials would remain unchanged if buried in an earthen pot for 4 weeks?\",\"options\":[\"Fruit peel\",\"Garden waste\",\"Scrap metal\",\"Vegetable stalks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that garbage in pot B (containing glass, metal, plastic) remains unchanged because microbes do not decompose these materials.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Microbes are released into sewage for which specific purpose?\",\"options\":[\"To make the sewage smell better\",\"To kill all living organisms in it\",\"To help in the quick decomposition of organic compounds\",\"To convert sewage into drinking water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that microbes are released into sewage to help in the quick decomposition of the organic compounds in it.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which part of the pea and bean plants are nitrogen-fixing micro-organisms found?\",\"options\":[\"Leaves\",\"Flowers\",\"Root nodules\",\"Stems\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that micro-organisms are present in the root nodules of leguminous plants like pea and bean.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen by microbes benefit pulses?\",\"options\":[\"It makes them change color\",\"It increases their protein content\",\"It makes them grow without water\",\"It reduces the number of seeds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that nitrogenous compounds increase soil fertility and the protein content of pulses.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ideal condition for milk to be converted into yoghurt within 8-10 hours?\",\"options\":[\"Boiling hot milk\",\"Ice-cold milk\",\"Lukewarm milk\",\"Milk kept in a freezer\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that yoghurt or buttermilk should be mixed with lukewarm milk to allow microbes to multiply and convert it to yoghurt.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which chemical process involves the conversion of one type of carbon compound into another by micro-organisms?\",\"options\":[\"Evaporation\",\"Fermentation\",\"Condensation\",\"Sedimentation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines fermentation as the chemical process of conversion of one type of carbon compound into another by the action of micro-organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What causes bread dough or idli batter to \'rise\' during fermentation?\",\"options\":[\"The addition of water\",\"The release of carbon dioxide and other gases\",\"The cooling of the dough\",\"The removal of oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that gases like carbon dioxide are released during fermentation, which cause an increase in volume (rising).\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a product of the fermentation process?\",\"options\":[\"Antibiotics\",\"Vitamins\",\"Plastic bags\",\"Citric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists yoghurt, alcohol, bread, acetic acid, citric acid, lactic acid, vitamins, and antibiotics as products of fermentation, but not plastic.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Besides gases, what else is generated during the process of fermentation?\",\"options\":[\"Light\",\"Heat\",\"Electricity\",\"Magnetism\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Heat is generated in this process\' referring to fermentation.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is yoghurt added to the batter when making rava-idli or bhature?\",\"options\":[\"To make the batter salty\",\"To prevent the batter from rising\",\"To start the process of fermentation\",\"To change the color of the batter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links yoghurt and microbes to the fermentation process which makes dough rise and food easy to digest.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do preparations like idli and dosa become easy to digest?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are cooked at high pressure\",\"Because microbes break down substances into new compounds\",\"Because they contain a lot of water\",\"Because they are made from grains only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that as microbes grow and multiply in batter, they break down substances producing new compounds, making them easy to digest.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment with Pot A and Pot B, why is it important to collect dry and wet waste separately?\",\"options\":[\"Because wet waste can be decomposed into useful manure\",\"Because dry waste like glass turns into soil\",\"Because microbes only live in dry waste\",\"Because wet waste cannot be recycled\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that organic (wet) waste is decomposed by microbes into manure, whereas dry waste like glass and plastic is not.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these plants would have nodules on their roots to help increase soil fertility?\",\"options\":[\"Mango\",\"Fenugreek\",\"Rose\",\"Wheat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions observing the roots of pea, bean, and fenugreek plants for nodules that help in nitrogen fixation.\",\"topic\":\"Useful micro-organisms\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What are medicines like penicillin, which destroy pathogens and retard their growth, called?\",\"options\":[\"Vaccines\",\"Antibiotics\",\"Fertilizers\",\"Biogas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines antibiotics as medicines that destroy pathogens and retard their growth.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these diseases was previously considered incurable but is now controlled by antibiotics?\",\"options\":[\"Common cold\",\"Headache\",\"Tuberculosis\",\"Body-ache\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions tuberculosis, typhoid, and cholera as diseases now under control because of antibiotics.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How can antibiotics be used to protect domestic animals from various diseases?\",\"options\":[\"By mixing them with their food\",\"By spraying them on the animal\'s skin\",\"By keeping the animals in a laboratory\",\"By using them to produce ropes\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states domestic animals can be protected by mixing antibiotics with their food.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of a vaccine produced in a laboratory?\",\"options\":[\"To clear oil slicks in the ocean\",\"To give immunity against a particular disease\",\"To produce biogas from farm waste\",\"To provide instant relief from a headache\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A vaccine is produced to give immunity against a particular disease.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A large oil spill occurs in a lake. Based on the text, how can microbes help solve this problem?\",\"options\":[\"By turning the oil into fertilizer\",\"By using the oil for their growth to clear the slick\",\"By producing penicillin in the water\",\"By tanning the oil like skin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Some microbes use oil for growth and are used to clear oil floating on water surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it strictly necessary to complete a course of antibiotics as prescribed by a doctor?\",\"options\":[\"Because antibiotics are produced in a laboratory\",\"Because they are only used for domestic animals\",\"Because they can be harmful if used without proper consultation\",\"Because they are used to produce ropes and strings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text warns that antibiotics can be harmful if used without consulting a physician or following the prescription.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the medicines shown (capsules and bottles) are examples of substances used to:\",\"options\":[\"Tan the skin of animals\",\"Destroy pathogens and retard their growth\",\"Produce biogas from wet garbage\",\"Clear oil slicks from the ocean\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows capsules and medicine bottles which the text identifies as antibiotics used to destroy pathogens.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/088_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure and the text, what action should be avoided regarding these medicines?\",\"options\":[\"Taking them to treat a common cold on our own\",\"Using them to control plant diseases\",\"Mixing them with domestic animal food\",\"Producing them with the help of microbes\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states we should not take medicines on our own for ailments like common colds.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/088_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Microbes are used in the production of ropes and strings from which plant?\",\"options\":[\"Penicillin\",\"Agave\",\"Tuberculosis\",\"Typhoid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions microbes are used in the production of ropes and strings from agave.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to our body\'s resistance when we are vaccinated against a disease?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases significantly\",\"It remains the same\",\"It increases against that disease\",\"It only works for body-aches\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Vaccination increases our immunity, which is resistance to a particular disease.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is collected to produce fertilizer in a biogas plant?\",\"options\":[\"Penicillin and vaccines\",\"Oil slicks and agave\",\"Farm waste and human urine\",\"Tanned skin and ropes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Farm waste, human urine, faeces, and wet garbage are used in biogas plants to produce fertilizer.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Antibiotics are produced with the help of:\",\"options\":[\"Agave plants\",\"Specific microbes\",\"Oil slicks\",\"Tanned skin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that antibiotics are produced with the help of specific microbes.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"For which of these ailments does the text suggest we should NOT self-medicate?\",\"options\":[\"Tuberculosis\",\"Cholera\",\"Headaches\",\"Typhoid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions we should not take medicines on our own for headaches.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how do microbes contribute to the management of farm waste?\",\"options\":[\"By turning it into antibiotics for plants\",\"By using it to produce biogas and fertilizer\",\"By converting it into ropes and strings\",\"By using it to clear oil floating on oceans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Farm waste is collected and used in a biogas plant to produce biogas and fertilizer.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the use of antibiotics without a physician\'s consultation considered harmful?\",\"options\":[\"Because they only work for domestic animals\",\"Because they are made from farm waste\",\"Because they can have negative effects if misused\",\"Because they increase the possibility of oil spills\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that while useful, antibiotics can be harmful if used without consulting a physician.\",\"topic\":\"Antibiotics and Vaccines\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are disease-producing micro-organisms called?\",\"options\":[\"Probiotics\",\"Pathogens\",\"Enterotoxins\",\"Antibiotics\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines pathogens as disease-producing micro-organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the normal body temperature of a healthy human being?\",\"options\":[\"35° C\",\"38° C\",\"37° C\",\"98° C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the body temperature of a healthy human being is about 37° C.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which toxic materials are released by microbes into food, causing food poisoning?\",\"options\":[\"Exotoxins\",\"Enterotoxins\",\"Cytotoxins\",\"Neurotoxins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, some microbes release toxic materials called enterotoxins into the food.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Swachha Bharat Abhiyan data, what percentage of all diseases occur due to uncleanliness?\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"60%\",\"70%\",\"80%\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that almost 80% of all diseases occur due to uncleanliness.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the body temperature rise when micro-organisms enter our body?\",\"options\":[\"To help micro-organisms grow faster\",\"Because the immune system starts acting to destroy micro-organisms\",\"Because the blood starts flowing backwards\",\"To cool down the internal organs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if micro-organisms enter our body, the immune system starts acting and body temperature rises to destroy them.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a disease of the respiratory tract mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Typhoid\",\"Amoebiasis\",\"Diphtheria\",\"Cholera\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists diphtheria, pneumonia, and tuberculosis as diseases of the respiratory tract.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do microbes causing diseases like malaria and Zika fever gain entry into the human body?\",\"options\":[\"Through contaminated water\",\"Through the bite of a female mosquito\",\"By breathing in infected air\",\"By eating spoiled meat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that these microbes gain entry into the human body through the bite of a female mosquito.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for the growth of fungi on gunny bags and leather articles?\",\"options\":[\"Extremely cold weather\",\"Lack of oxygen\",\"Humid atmosphere\",\"Direct sunlight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in a humid atmosphere, fungi and other micro-organisms grow on these articles.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person consumes food contaminated with sewage and houseflies, which disease are they most likely to contract?\",\"options\":[\"Pneumonia\",\"Common cold\",\"Typhoid\",\"Tuberculosis\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that consuming food contaminated with sewage can lead to diseases of the alimentary canal like typhoid.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a white scum on a jar of pickles that was left open for a long time. What should be done with this food?\",\"options\":[\"It should be eaten immediately\",\"It should be boiled and then eaten\",\"It should be discarded as it is spoilt\",\"Only the top layer should be removed and the rest eaten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that scum and black particles indicate spoilage, and eating spoiled food causes illness.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it recommended to hold a handkerchief over your mouth when you cough or sneeze?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the face warm\",\"To prevent pathogens from being released into the air\",\"To improve the smell of the air\",\"To prevent the entry of mosquitoes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states pathogens are released in the air when an infected person sneezes or coughs, so a handkerchief prevents this spread.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the optimum temperature for microbial growth (15°C to 35°C), why is a fever of 38°C or higher effective?\",\"options\":[\"It provides the perfect temperature for microbes to sleep\",\"It is within the optimum range for microbes\",\"It exceeds the optimum temperature range, helping to destroy microbes\",\"It lowers the body\'s energy levels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes the optimum growth range is 15°C-35°C; a fever (above 37°C) destroys them by moving outside this range.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following groups contains only plant products that can be damaged by fungi in humid conditions?\",\"options\":[\"Leather, Cotton, Paper\",\"Gunny bags, Cotton, Paper\",\"Rubber, Leather, Gunny bags\",\"Paper, Rubber, Wallets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies gunny bags, cotton, paper, and rubber as plant products, while leather is an animal product.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text advise that water in pots, tyres, and cans should be drained promptly?\",\"options\":[\"To save water for drinking\",\"To prevent the growth of fungi on rubber\",\"To prevent mosquitoes from reproducing in stagnant water\",\"To keep the surroundings dry for better walking\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions mosquitoes reproduce in stagnant water, so draining it prevents the spread of diseases like malaria.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the most effective way to prevent the spread of 80% of diseases according to the Swachha Bharat Abhiyan section?\",\"options\":[\"Taking medicine every day\",\"Polishing all leather articles\",\"Keeping surroundings clean and avoiding open defecation\",\"Increasing the body temperature to 37° C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 80% of diseases occur due to uncleanliness and suggests cleanliness and proper garbage disposal as ways to stop them.\",\"topic\":\"Harmful micro-organisms\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21997,46,7,'Science','en','The Muscular System and Digestive System in Human Beings','[\"Muscles are bundles of fibres that contract and relax to move the body.\",\"Skeletal muscles attach to bones via tendons and are under voluntary control.\",\"Cardiac (heart) muscles contract rhythmically and are involuntary.\",\"Smooth muscles line internal organs like stomach, intestines, blood vessels and work slowly and involuntarily.\",\"The muscular system works in pairs: when one muscle contracts, its antagonist relaxes.\",\"The largest muscle in the human body is the quadriceps femoris in the thigh.\",\"The smallest muscle is the stapedius in the ear, which helps control sound vibration.\",\"The digestive system converts food into nutrients that can be absorbed into the blood.\",\"Enzymes are proteins that speed up chemical reactions in digestion (e.g., amylase, pepsin).\",\"The stomach secretes hydrochloric acid and pepsin to break down proteins.\",\"Bile from the liver emulsifies fats in the small intestine.\",\"The small intestine is about 6 m long and absorbs most nutrients.\",\"The large intestine is about 1.5 m long and absorbs water and electrolytes.\",\"The appendix is a small pouch attached to the large intestine and has no known digestive function.\",\"Healthy habits like avoiding tobacco and alcohol keep the digestive system working well.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are muscles defined as in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"Bundles of bones\",\"Bundles of fibres\",\"Bundles of skin\",\"Bundles of nerves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that muscles are bundles of fibres that can contract and relax as required.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the body is responsible for giving it a specific shape and posture?\",\"options\":[\"Bones only\",\"Muscles\",\"Skin only\",\"Blood vessels\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, muscles give our body a specific shape and posture.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to muscles when different parts of our body move?\",\"options\":[\"They turn into bone\",\"They remain completely still\",\"They contract and relax\",\"They become thinner and longer only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that muscles contract and relax as different parts of our body move.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what action is being performed at the elbow?\",\"options\":[\"Straightening the arm\",\"Bending the arm\",\"Rotating the wrist\",\"Stretching the arm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the instruction to \'bend your arm at the elbow\'.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/091_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the \'fleshy part\' of the upper arm called?\",\"options\":[\"Bone\",\"Skin\",\"Muscle\",\"Joint\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the fleshy part consists of muscle.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to maintain a good sitting posture, which system is primarily involved according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Digestive system\",\"Muscular system\",\"Respiratory system\",\"Excretory system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that muscles give our body a specific shape and posture.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When you follow the activity of closing your fist tightly and bending your arm, what sensation do you feel in the upper arm?\",\"options\":[\"Softness\",\"Hardness\",\"Coldness\",\"No sensation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'Did you feel the hardness in the upper arm?\' as a result of the muscle contracting.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the bulging appearance of the upper arm is a result of the muscle ______.\",\"options\":[\"Relaxing\",\"Contracting\",\"Expanding\",\"Breaking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When the arm is bent, the muscle contracts, causing it to feel hard and appear prominent.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/091_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why are muscles able to facilitate movement in the body?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are made of hard calcium\",\"Because they are bundles of fibres that can contract and relax\",\"Because they are fixed to the skin only\",\"Because they do not change their shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that muscles are bundles of fibres that contract and relax as required for movement.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a characteristic of muscles?\",\"options\":[\"They are always in a relaxed state\",\"They are only found in the arms\",\"They contract and relax as required\",\"They have no role in body movement\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that muscles are bundles of fibres that can contract and relax as required.\",\"topic\":\"Muscular system\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"By what means are muscles firmly attached to the bones?\",\"options\":[\"Ligaments\",\"Tendons\",\"Skin\",\"Joints\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that muscles are firmly attached to bones by means of tendons.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens at the joint when muscles contract?\",\"options\":[\"The bones break\",\"The tendons disappear\",\"There is movement and bones move\",\"The joint becomes fixed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when muscles contract, there is movement at the joint and the bones move.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these activities is mentioned as a movement that demands great strength?\",\"options\":[\"Moving eyelids\",\"Laughing\",\"Talking\",\"Chopping wood with an axe\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text uses chopping wood with an axe as an example of a movement demanding great strength.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the state of the muscle when the arm is stretched out and there is \'No movement\'?\",\"options\":[\"Contraction of muscles\",\"Relaxation of muscles\",\"Tearing of muscles\",\"Vibration of muscles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the muscle in the straight arm as \'Relaxation of muscles\'.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/091_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, what must happen to the muscle for the arm to move and lift the weight?\",\"options\":[\"The muscle must relax\",\"The muscle must contract\",\"The muscle must detach from the bone\",\"The muscle must stay still\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows that \'movement\' occurs when there is \'Contraction of muscles\'.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/091_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a small movement mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Jumping\",\"Movement of eyelids\",\"Throwing\",\"Walking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the movements of eyelids as small movements.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When muscles contract, how do the bones move in relation to each other?\",\"options\":[\"They only move nearer to each other\",\"They only move away from each other\",\"They move either nearer to or away from each other\",\"They do not change their position\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states bones move either nearer to or away from each other during muscle contraction.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is jumping and throwing a ball, which part of the body is primarily responsible for these movements according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only the skin\",\"Only the bones\",\"The action of muscles\",\"The digestive system\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the action of muscles is necessary for all kinds of movements like jumping and throwing.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the mutual relationship between muscles and bones regarding movement?\",\"options\":[\"Muscles move independently of bones\",\"Bones move only when tendons relax\",\"Muscles are attached to bones and their contraction moves the bones\",\"Bones are attached to muscles to keep them still\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that muscles are attached to bones and their contraction causes the bones to move.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"For which of the following movements do we use muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Talking and laughing\",\"Walking and jumping\",\"Throwing\",\"All kinds of movements\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states the action of muscles is necessary for all kinds of movements.\",\"topic\":\"Muscles and bones\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many muscles are present in the human body?\",\"options\":[\"More than 200\",\"More than 400\",\"More than 600\",\"More than 800\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there are more than 600 muscles in the human body.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of the weight of a healthy adult human body is contributed by muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Almost 20%\",\"Almost 40%\",\"Almost 60%\",\"Almost 80%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, muscles contribute almost 40% of the weight of a healthy adult.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many muscles are responsible for the various expressions on the human face?\",\"options\":[\"About 10 muscles\",\"About 20 muscles\",\"About 30 muscles\",\"About 40 muscles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there are about 30 muscles in the human face.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these actions is controlled by voluntary muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Blood circulation\",\"Digestion\",\"Walking\",\"Breathing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Walking is a function that depends upon our will, making the muscles used voluntary.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary characteristic of involuntary muscles?\",\"options\":[\"They only exist in the arms and legs\",\"Their functions do not depend upon our will\",\"They are used for expressions like happiness\",\"They only work when we are awake\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Involuntary muscles carry out functions that do not depend upon our will.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which organs carry out their functions in a fixed manner using involuntary muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Hands and legs\",\"Eyes and mouth\",\"Stomach and heart\",\"Arms and fingers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that functions of organs like the stomach, intestine, and heart are carried out by involuntary muscles.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is writing an essay, which type of muscles are they primarily using to move their hand?\",\"options\":[\"Involuntary muscles\",\"Voluntary muscles\",\"Vital muscles\",\"Stomach muscles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Working with hands depends on our will, so it involves voluntary muscles.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of muscle action is responsible for the vital function of blood circulation while a person is asleep?\",\"options\":[\"Voluntary muscle action\",\"Involuntary muscle action\",\"Facial muscle action\",\"Manual muscle action\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Blood circulation is a vital function that does not depend on our will, hence it is involuntary.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A person shows a \'sad\' expression after losing a game. Which muscles are responsible for this movement?\",\"options\":[\"Muscles of the legs\",\"Muscles of the heart\",\"Muscles of the face\",\"Muscles of the intestine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Expressions like sadness are expressed by the movements of the muscles of the face.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the movement of the intestine classified differently from the movement of the arms?\",\"options\":[\"Intestine movement is voluntary while arm movement is vital\",\"Arm movement depends on our will, but intestine movement does not\",\"Intestines have 30 muscles while arms have 600\",\"Arms are encircled by small muscles but intestines are not\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Arms use voluntary muscles (dependent on will), while intestines use involuntary muscles (independent of will).\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a \'vital function\' essential for life?\",\"options\":[\"Eating a sandwich\",\"Walking to school\",\"Digestion\",\"Smiling at a friend\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists breathing, blood circulation, and digestion as vital functions.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be concluded about the muscles encircling our eyes, mouth, and nose?\",\"options\":[\"They are used to move our arms\",\"They are responsible for displaying emotions\",\"They are the largest muscles in the body\",\"They contribute 40% of our body weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states these small facial muscles express emotions like happiness, sadness, and fear.\",\"topic\":\"Voluntary and Involuntary muscles\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of muscles are attached to two different bones at their ends?\",\"options\":[\"Cardiac muscles\",\"Smooth muscles\",\"Skeletal muscles\",\"Involuntary muscles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the two ends of each skeletal muscle are attached to two different bones.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what approximate rate do cardiac muscles cause the heart to relax and contract?\",\"options\":[\"50 times per minute\",\"70 times per minute\",\"90 times per minute\",\"100 times per minute\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, cardiac muscles cause the heart to relax and contract at a rate of about 70 times per minute.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following organs contains smooth muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Heart\",\"Arms\",\"Uterus\",\"Legs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the uterus as an example of an organ containing smooth muscles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides movement, what is another important responsibility of skeletal muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Pumping blood to the body\",\"Giving shape to our body\",\"Carrying out slow vital functions\",\"Controlling the beating of the heart\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that skeletal muscles are responsible for holding bones together and giving shape to our body.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the movement of smooth muscles described in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Voluntary and fast\",\"Involuntary and slow\",\"Voluntary and slow\",\"Involuntary and fast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the movements of smooth muscles are involuntary and slow.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the \'beating\' of the heart consist of, according to the description of cardiac muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Only the relaxation of the heart\",\"Only the contraction of the heart\",\"The attachment of muscles to bones\",\"The contraction and relaxation of the heart\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines beating as the contraction and relaxation of the heart brought about by cardiac muscles.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you decide to bend your arm to pick up a book, which type of muscles are you primarily using?\",\"options\":[\"Smooth muscles\",\"Cardiac muscles\",\"Skeletal muscles\",\"Involuntary muscles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Muscles of the arms are skeletal muscles, and their movements are voluntary (controlled by our will).\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which muscle type ensures that vital functions like digestion in the stomach continue even when we are unaware of them?\",\"options\":[\"Skeletal muscles\",\"Smooth muscles\",\"Voluntary muscles\",\"Cardiac muscles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that smooth muscles carry out vital functions of which we remain unaware, such as those in the stomach.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which type of muscle is shown in the topmost magnified circle pointing to the shoulder and arm?\",\"options\":[\"Cardiac muscles\",\"Smooth muscles\",\"Skeletal muscles\",\"Intestinal muscles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows skeletal muscles in the arm\\/shoulder area, which are attached to bones.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/092_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the middle magnified circle points to the heart. What is the nature of the movement of the muscles found there?\",\"options\":[\"Voluntary\",\"Involuntary\",\"Slow and voluntary\",\"Attached to two bones\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The middle circle points to the heart, which contains cardiac muscles; the text states their movement is involuntary.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/092_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is a key difference between cardiac muscles and smooth muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Cardiac muscles are voluntary, while smooth muscles are involuntary.\",\"Cardiac muscles are only in the heart, while smooth muscles are in other internal organs.\",\"Cardiac muscles are slow, while smooth muscles are fast.\",\"Cardiac muscles are attached to bones, while smooth muscles are not.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies cardiac muscles are in the heart, while smooth muscles are in internal organs other than the heart.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text refer to smooth muscles as \'special muscles\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are the only muscles attached to bones.\",\"Because they allow us to move our limbs whenever we want.\",\"Because they carry out vital functions without our conscious awareness.\",\"Because they are the only muscles that can contract 70 times per minute.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes them as special because they carry out vital functions of which we remain unaware.\",\"topic\":\"Types of muscles\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the muscle located on the front of the bone in the upper arm?\",\"options\":[\"Triceps\",\"Biceps\",\"Cardiac muscle\",\"Stomach muscle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the muscle on the front of the bone in our upper arm is called the biceps.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which muscle is found at the back of the upper arm bone?\",\"options\":[\"Biceps\",\"Flexor\",\"Triceps\",\"Extensor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the muscle at the back of the upper arm is called the triceps.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How do muscles in the human body always work to perform functions?\",\"options\":[\"Individually\",\"In groups\",\"One at a time\",\"Only when we are sleeping\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that muscles in our body always work in groups.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When some muscles in a group contract, what do the other muscles in the same group do?\",\"options\":[\"They also contract\",\"They stop working\",\"They relax\",\"They disappear\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when some muscles contract, other muscles of the same group relax.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of the coordinated contraction and relaxation of muscles?\",\"options\":[\"To make the bones grow longer\",\"To help in the proper performance of body functions\",\"To change the color of the skin\",\"To increase the temperature of the elbow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states this coordination is how muscles help in the proper performance of various body functions.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you hold your arm straight at the elbow, what is the angle formed?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"90°\",\"180°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines holding the arm straight at the elbow as being at 180°.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Try this\' activity, what happens to the muscles when you touch your shoulder with your fingers?\",\"options\":[\"The muscles remain completely still\",\"Only the triceps works\",\"Muscles in the arm contract and relax\",\"The elbow stops functioning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity demonstrates that arm movements involve the contraction and relaxation of specific muscle groups.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you bend your arm at the elbow through 90°, which muscle group is primarily involved in this action?\",\"options\":[\"Leg muscles\",\"Upper arm muscles\",\"Cardiac muscles\",\"Stomach muscles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes bending the arm at the elbow as an action involving the biceps and triceps, which are upper arm muscles.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What would be the most likely consequence if the cardiac muscles stopped moving?\",\"options\":[\"The arm would not bend\",\"Digestion would speed up\",\"The heart would stop functioning\",\"The biceps would become stronger\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cardiac muscles are responsible for heart movement; if they don\'t move, the heart stops.\",\"topic\":\"How muscles perform their functions\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to food if it entered the stomach but the stomach muscles did not move?\",\"options\":[\"The food would be digested instantly\",\"The food would not be processed or moved further\",\"The food would turn into muscle\",\"The biceps would contract instead\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Stomach muscles are needed to move and process food; without their 