verts food\",\"Digestion only happens in the digestive glands\",\"The alimentary canal works independently of glands\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that at each stage there is a different organ which performs its specific role.\",\"topic\":\"The Digestive System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the \'soluble form\' of food mentioned in the definition of digestion significant?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the food taste better\",\"It allows the food to be absorbed into the blood\",\"It prevents the food from entering the stomach\",\"It shortens the length of the alimentary canal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines digestion as the conversion of food into a soluble form for its absorption into the blood.\",\"topic\":\"The Digestive System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the sequence provided in the text, which is the correct order of food passage?\",\"options\":[\"Mouth -> Oesophagus -> Pharynx -> Stomach\",\"Mouth -> Pharynx -> Oesophagus -> Stomach\",\"Pharynx -> Mouth -> Stomach -> Oesophagus\",\"Oesophagus -> Pharynx -> Mouth -> Stomach\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists the parts as mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, and then stomach.\",\"topic\":\"The Digestive System\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many types of teeth are mentioned in the human digestive system?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there are four types of teeth: incisors, canines, pre-molars, and molars.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the hard substance that covers each tooth called?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium\",\"Enamel\",\"Ptyalin\",\"Maltose\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, each tooth is covered by a hard substance called enamel.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Enamel is primarily composed of which substance?\",\"options\":[\"Proteins\",\"Starch\",\"Calcium salt\",\"Salivary amylase\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that enamel is made of a calcium salt.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the process of digestion begin in the human body?\",\"options\":[\"In the stomach\",\"In the mouth\",\"In the small intestine\",\"In the esophagus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the process of digestion begins with the function of the teeth in the mouth.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the alternative name for the enzyme ptyalin?\",\"options\":[\"Maltose\",\"Calcium salt\",\"Salivary amylase\",\"Catalyst protein\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that saliva contains an enzyme called ptyalin or salivary amylase.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific function of ptyalin in the digestive process?\",\"options\":[\"It converts starch into maltose\",\"It converts proteins into calcium\",\"It converts sugar into starch\",\"It hardens the enamel of the teeth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that ptyalin converts starch into a sugar called maltose.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Enzymes are described as acting as \'catalysts\'. What does this mean in the context of the text?\",\"options\":[\"They provide calcium for the teeth\",\"They are sugars used for energy\",\"They bring about specific chemical reactions\",\"They protect the enamel from damage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines enzymes as substances that bring about specific chemical reactions and act as catalysts.\",\"topic\":\"Teeth and Enzymes\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Chemically, enzymes are a specific type of which nutrient?\",\"options\":[\"Carbohydrates\",\"Fats\",\"Proteins\",\"Minerals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 