":\"The text lists hydrochloric acid, pepsin, and mucus as the three components of gastric juice.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person consumes a meal rich in fats, which digestive juice stored in the gall bladder will help in its digestion?\",\"options\":[\"Saliva\",\"Gastric juice\",\"Bile\",\"Pancreatic juice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that bile, stored in the gall bladder, helps in the digestion of fats.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which small organ is attached to the first part of the large intestine?\",\"options\":[\"Gall bladder\",\"Appendix\",\"Pancreas\",\"Duct\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show that a small part called the appendix is attached to the first part of the large intestine.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/095_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate length of the small intestine in an adult human?\",\"options\":[\"1.5 m\",\"3 m\",\"6 m\",\"10 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the small intestine is about 6m long.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which process occurs specifically in the large intestine?\",\"options\":[\"Digestion of proteins\",\"Absorption of nutrients into blood\",\"Absorption of water\",\"Churning of food into slurry\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that only water is absorbed in the large intestine.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the lengths provided in the text, how much longer is the small intestine compared to the large intestine?\",\"options\":[\"4.5 m\",\"7.5 m\",\"2.5 m\",\"5.5 m\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Small intestine (6m) minus Large intestine (1.5m) equals 4.5m.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does food become acidic once it enters the stomach?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the action of pepsin\",\"Due to the mixing of hydrochloric acid\",\"Due to the presence of bile salts\",\"Due to the saliva from the mouth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that food becomes acidic in the stomach because it is mixed with hydrochloric acid.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to the nutrients obtained from digestion if the small intestine stopped functioning?\",\"options\":[\"They would be stored in the liver immediately\",\"They would not be absorbed into the blood\",\"They would be converted into water\",\"They would be digested by the large intestine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that absorption of nutrients into the blood occurs in the small intestine.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which organ is responsible for pushing food from the pharynx towards the stomach?\",\"options\":[\"Trachea\",\"Oesophagus\",\"Small intestine\",\"Pancreas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the oesophagus as a tube that pushes food towards the stomach.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where are the salivary glands located according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Inside the stomach and near the liver\",\"In front of the ears, near the pharynx and below the tongue\",\"Behind the trachea and above the oesophagus\",\"Inside the small intestine and large intestine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the location of salivary glands as in front of the ears, near the pharynx, and below the tongue.\",\"topic\":\"Organs of the Digestive System\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which enzyme present in saliva is responsible for converting starch into maltose?\",\"options\":[\"Pepsin\",\"Ptyalin\",\"Trypsin\",\"Lipase\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the table, the salivary gland secretes ptyalin which converts starch into maltose.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of the Hydrochloric Acid secreted by the gastric wall?\",\"options\":[\"To make the food alkaline\",\"To break down fats\",\"To make the food acidic\",\"To convert starch into sugars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table states that Hydrochloric Acid in the stomach functions to make the food acidic.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which secretion is responsible for the emulsification of fats by converting large fat particles into small ones?\",\"options\":[\"Bile\",\"Mucous\",\"Amylase\",\"Pepsin\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The function of Bile, secreted by the liver, is the emulsification of fats.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which gland is associated with the organ \'Mouth\'?\",\"options\":[\"Gastric wall\",\"Liver\",\"Pancreas\",\"Salivary gland\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The first row of the table shows that the Salivary gland is the gland located in the mouth.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/096_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which two secretions are responsible for the final conversion of proteins into amino acids?\",\"options\":[\"Ptyalin and Bile\",\"Pepsin and Amylase\",\"Trypsin and Intestinal juice\",\"Lipase and Mucous\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows that both Trypsin (from the pancreas) and Intestinal juice convert proteins into amino acids.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/096_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is mucous secreted by the gastric wall in the stomach?\",\"options\":[\"To digest complex carbohydrates\",\"To protect the inner lining from hydrochloric acid\",\"To convert fats into fatty acids\",\"To make the food alkaline\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table lists the function of mucous as protecting the inner lining of the stomach from hydrochloric acid.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person\'s pancreas is not functioning correctly, which process would be directly affected?\",\"options\":[\"Conversion of starch into maltose\",\"Protection of the stomach lining\",\"Conversion of fats into fatty acids and glycerol\",\"Making food acidic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The pancreas secretes Lipase, which converts fats into fatty acids and glycerol.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which end product is specifically formed from complex carbohydrates in the small intestine but not mentioned for the pancreas?\",\"options\":[\"Maltose\",\"Glucose\",\"Amino acids\",\"Fatty acids\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table specifies that the small intestine converts complex carbohydrates into glucose, while the pancreas converts them into simple sugars.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which enzyme is secreted by the pancreas to specifically help in the breakdown of proteins?\",\"options\":[\"Amylase\",\"Lipase\",\"Trypsin\",\"Ptyalin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Trypsin is the pancreatic enzyme listed for converting proteins into amino acids.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A patient has difficulty digesting fats into fatty acids. Which two secretions from different organs could be responsible for this process?\",\"options\":[\"Saliva and Gastric juice\",\"Pancreatic juice and Intestinal juice\",\"Bile and Pepsin\",\"Ptyalin and Trypsin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Both Pancreatic juice (Lipase) and Intestinal juice are listed as converting fats into fatty acids and glycerol.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common purpose of Bile and the secretions of the Gastric wall regarding the nature of food?\",\"options\":[\"Both make food acidic\",\"Both make food alkaline\",\"They both change the pH (acidity\\/alkalinity) of the food\",\"They both convert proteins to amino acids\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Gastric juice makes food acidic while Bile makes it alkaline, both changing the food\'s chemical nature.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a secretion of the Pancreas that acts on complex carbohydrates?\",\"options\":[\"Trypsin\",\"Lipase\",\"Amylase\",\"Pepsin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Amylase is the pancreatic secretion listed for converting complex carbohydrates into simple sugars.\",\"topic\":\"Glands of the Digestive System\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, when can we say that we are in good health?\",\"options\":[\"When we exercise every day\",\"When all our organ systems function properly\",\"When we eat only vegetables\",\"When we have a strong personality\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that we are in good health when all our organ systems function properly.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed as a harmful habit that affects health adversely?\",\"options\":[\"Sleeping late at night\",\"Eating spicy food\",\"Chewing of tobacco\",\"Drinking less water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions chewing of tobacco, smoking, and drinking alcohol as harmful habits.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symptoms are mentioned as a result of consuming tobacco products?\",\"options\":[\"Fever, cough, and cold\",\"Vomiting, nausea, and headache\",\"Stomach ache and dizziness\",\"Blurred vision and fatigue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that tobacco consumption causes problems like vomiting, nausea, and headache.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when tobacco particles stick to the teeth and gums over time?\",\"options\":[\"They make the teeth stronger\",\"They cause the teeth to turn white\",\"They cause injury resulting in dysfunction\",\"They improve the sense of taste\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that tobacco particles stick to teeth and gums and slowly cause injury resulting in dysfunction.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the consequence of the inflammation of the pharynx and intestine due to tobacco use?\",\"options\":[\"It leads to better digestion\",\"It progresses into cancer\",\"It causes temporary weight loss\",\"It improves the immune system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that inflammation of the pharynx and intestine progresses into cancer leading to death.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a person feel pain when moving their jaws after prolonged tobacco use?\",\"options\":[\"Due to lack of calcium in the diet\",\"Due to swelling of the gums\",\"Due to excessive talking\",\"Due to the growth of new teeth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that injury to the mouth cavity causes swelling of the gums and pain when moving the jaws.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to fulfill their \'role\' in the classroom as mentioned in the text, what should they do?\",\"options\":[\"Conduct a science experiment\",\"Organize a sports competition\",\"Make slogans against tobacco consumption\",\"Write a story about healthy food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests making pictures and slogans against tobacco consumption as a part of \'My Role\'.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where should a student display slogans and pictures against smoking and drinking alcohol?\",\"options\":[\"Only in their personal notebook\",\"In the classroom and the neighbourhood\",\"Only at the local hospital\",\"Inside the school library books\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies displaying slogans in the classroom and the neighbourhood.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Who should a student make aware if they notice instances of tobacco use in their neighbourhood?\",\"options\":[\"The local police only\",\"Their friends and siblings\",\"Parents and teachers\",\"Shopkeepers in the market\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests making parents and teachers aware of such instances in the neighbourhood.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how does tobacco use affect the digestive system\'s organs like the alimentary canal?\",\"options\":[\"It helps the organs process food faster\",\"It prevents the organs from functioning properly\",\"It strengthens the lining of the canal\",\"It has no direct effect on the alimentary canal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if tobacco is consumed, the alimentary canal and other organs cannot function properly.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between physical health and personality development according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Physical health is unrelated to personality\",\"Personality development is more important than health\",\"Physical health is extremely important in personality development\",\"Health only affects physical growth, not personality\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The very first sentence of the text states that physical health is extremely important in personality development.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What activity is suggested to be performed during the school assembly to fight addiction?\",\"options\":[\"Singing a song about nature\",\"Taking an oath against addiction\",\"Performing a dance about health\",\"Reading the daily news\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions composing an oath against addiction and taking it in the class and during assembly.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Harmful Habits\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21998,46,7,'Science','en','Changes – Physical and Chemical','[\"- A *change* is a transformation of a material into a different state or substance.\",\"- **Natural change** happens by itself (e.g., ripening of fruit, rusting of iron).\",\"- **Man‑made change** is caused by human action (e.g., cooking, painting).\",\"- **Useful change** benefits us (e.g., sharpening a pencil).\",\"- **Harmful change** is damaging or useless (e.g., corrosion of metal).\",\"- **Fast (quick) change** occurs in a short time (bursting of a balloon).\",\"- **Slow change** takes a long time (ripening of fruit).\",\"- **Reversible change** can go back and forth (melting and solidifying wax).\",\"- **Irreversible change** cannot be undone (ripening of mango).\",\"- **Periodic change** repeats at regular intervals (day‑night cycle).\",\"- **Non‑periodic change** repeats irregularly or not at all (earthquake).\",\"- **Physical change** keeps the same substance (melting ice, boiling water).\",\"- **Chemical change** creates a new substance (rusting iron, burning wood).\",\"- **Corrosion** is a chemical change that weakens metals (rust, green patina).\",\"- **Evaporation** is a physical change where liquid turns to vapor (water in a bowl).\",\"- **Dissolving, boiling, melting** are all physical changes.\",\"- **Burning wood** is a chemical change that produces new substances (CO₂, ash).\",\"- **Reversible changes** can be repeated (melting wax, dissolving sugar).\",\"- **Irreversible changes** cannot be repeated (burning wood, rusting iron).\",\"- **Physical changes** do not alter the chemical composition of the material.\",\"- **Chemical changes** alter the chemical composition and produce new properties.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the term used in the text for changes that occur of their own accord?\",\"options\":[\"Man-made changes\",\"Natural changes\",\"Artificial changes\",\"Chemical changes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that changes occurring of their own accord or naturally are called natural changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is explicitly mentioned in the text as an example of a natural change?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting vegetables\",\"Lighting an electric bulb\",\"Ripening of a fruit\",\"Rusting of iron\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the ripening of a fruit and the spoiling of milk as examples of natural changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why is the \'spoiling of milk\' classified as a natural change?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a man-made process\",\"Because it occurs naturally\",\"Because it involves a change in color\",\"Because it is useful for humans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that changes like the spoiling of milk occur naturally and are therefore called natural changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these changes does NOT occur of its own accord?\",\"options\":[\"Raining\",\"A fruit falling from a tree\",\"Cutting vegetables\",\"Ripening of a fruit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cutting vegetables is a change initiated by humans, unlike raining or fruit ripening which occur naturally.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student observes a leaf turning yellow and falling from a tree, how would the text classify this change?\",\"options\":[\"Man-made change\",\"Natural change\",\"Artificial change\",\"Forced change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text classifies changes that occur naturally, like a fruit falling from a tree, as natural changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A person switches on a lamp to study. Based on the classification points in the text, this change is most likely:\",\"options\":[\"Natural\",\"Man-made\",\"Spontaneous\",\"Accidental\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lighting an electric bulb is a change that does not occur of its own accord, making it man-made.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which pair of changes consists only of examples the text uses to illustrate natural changes?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting vegetables and lighting a bulb\",\"Ripening of fruit and spoiling of milk\",\"Rusting of iron and cutting vegetables\",\"Lighting a bulb and raining\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions ripening of fruit and spoiling of milk as examples of natural changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary criterion mentioned in the text for classifying a change as \'natural\'?\",\"options\":[\"Whether the change is fast or slow\",\"Whether the change is useful or harmful\",\"Whether the change occurs of its own accord\",\"Whether the change involves a liquid or a solid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines natural changes as those that occur of their own accord or naturally.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT listed as a change to be classified in the introductory questions of the text?\",\"options\":[\"Rusting of iron\",\"Lighting an electric bulb\",\"Cooking rice\",\"Cutting vegetables\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions rusting, lighting a bulb, and cutting vegetables, but does not mention cooking rice in this section.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the text, what does \'of their own accord\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Happening by human effort\",\"Happening naturally without human intervention\",\"Happening only in a laboratory\",\"Happening very slowly over years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'of their own accord\' synonymously with \'naturally\' to describe natural changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to group \'raining\' and \'lighting a bulb\' into different categories based on the text, what would be the best labels?\",\"options\":[\"Fast and Slow\",\"Natural and Man-made\",\"Useful and Harmful\",\"Physical and Chemical\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text introduces the concept of natural changes (like raining) versus changes that are not natural (like lighting a bulb).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text categorize the \'rusting of iron\' and \'raining\' in the list of questions?\",\"options\":[\"As man-made changes\",\"As changes to be classified by the reader\",\"As changes that never happen\",\"As examples of spoiling milk\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks the reader to consider points for classifying these specific examples into groups.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Natural Changes\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are changes that are useful to us called?\",\"options\":[\"Harmful changes\",\"Useful changes\",\"Fast changes\",\"Slow changes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines changes that are useful to us as useful changes.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Changes that take place in a short period of time are known as _____.\",\"options\":[\"Slow changes\",\"Natural changes\",\"Fast changes\",\"Harmful changes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, changes that take place in a short period of time are called fast or quick changes.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a useful change mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Falling of a tree\",\"Bursting of a balloon\",\"Baking bread\",\"Stormy weather\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists baking bread as a useful change.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary factor used to distinguish between fast and slow changes?\",\"options\":[\"The usefulness of the change\",\"The duration or time taken\",\"Whether it is man-made\",\"Whether it is natural\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that changes are classified as fast or slow based on their duration, i.e., the time they take to happen.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are harmful changes defined in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Changes that take a long time\",\"Changes that are not useful or do us harm\",\"Changes that occur in nature\",\"Changes that happen quickly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines harmful changes as those that are not useful or changes that do us harm.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Compared to the ripening of a fruit, the duration of the bursting of a balloon is _____.\",\"options\":[\"Much longer\",\"Exactly the same\",\"Far shorter\",\"Slightly longer\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the duration of the bursting of a balloon is far shorter than that of the ripening of a fruit.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text\'s classification, what kind of change is the falling of a tree in a storm?\",\"options\":[\"Useful change\",\"Harmful change\",\"Quick change\",\"Man-made change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to classify the falling of a tree in a storm, which is harmful as it causes damage.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are converting milk into yoghurt, you are performing a _____.\",\"options\":[\"Harmful change\",\"Natural change\",\"Useful change\",\"Fast change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The conversion of milk into yoghurt is a beneficial process, making it a useful change.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a slow change as per the examples in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sharpening a pencil\",\"Bursting a balloon\",\"Ripening of a fruit\",\"Cooking food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the ripening of a fruit as an example of a change that takes place over a long period of time (slow change).\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is sharpening a pencil classified as a useful change?\",\"options\":[\"Because it happens very quickly\",\"Because it is a man-made material\",\"Because it serves a purpose and is useful to us\",\"Because it takes a long period of time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that changes like sharpening a pencil are useful to us and therefore called useful changes.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a change takes a long period of time to happen, it is classified as a _____.\",\"options\":[\"Quick change\",\"Slow change\",\"Harmful change\",\"Useful change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines changes that take place over a long period of time as slow changes.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which criteria must be met for a change to be classified as \'fast\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It must be a man-made change\",\"It must be a useful change\",\"It must take place in a short period of time\",\"It must involve a balloon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines fast\\/quick changes specifically by their duration, which is a short period of time.\",\"topic\":\"Fast and Slow Changes\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are changes that can occur in a forward and reverse direction again and again called?\",\"options\":[\"Periodic changes\",\"Irreversible changes\",\"Reversible changes\",\"Natural changes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines changes that can occur in forward and reverse directions repeatedly as reversible changes.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these is an example of an irreversible change?\",\"options\":[\"Melting wax\",\"Bending heated glass\",\"Burning wood\",\"Cooling liquid wax\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that wood cannot be obtained back from ash, making it an irreversible change.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What substance is formed when wood is burnt, according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Smoke\",\"Ash\",\"Wax\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that ash is formed on burning the wood.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a piece of glass bangle when its middle part is heated in a candle flame?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into ash\",\"It becomes soft\",\"It disappears\",\"It changes color permanently\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes that as the middle of the glass piece gets heated, it becomes soft.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the glass piece bend during the experiment described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the heat alone\",\"Due to finger-pressure on its ends\",\"Because it turns into wax\",\"Because it is a ripe mango\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the glass bends due to the finger-pressure on its ends after it becomes soft.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is true about obtaining solid wax from melted wax?\",\"options\":[\"It is impossible\",\"It can be done repeatedly\",\"It only happens once\",\"It turns into wood\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says melting wax and obtaining the original solid wax again is something we can do repeatedly.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following cannot be transformed back into its raw state?\",\"options\":[\"Melted wax\",\"A ripe mango\",\"Heated glass\",\"Softened glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that a ripe mango cannot be transformed back into a raw mango.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What must be done to the glass pieces to make them hard again after bending them into links?\",\"options\":[\"Keep them in the flame\",\"Apply more pressure\",\"Let the pieces cool\",\"Burn them into ash\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure says to bring the two ends together and let the piece cool to set the shape.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you wanted to restore a candle after it has melted, what type of change are you performing?\",\"options\":[\"Irreversible change\",\"Natural change\",\"Reversible change\",\"Chemical change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Obtaining original solid wax from melted wax is a reversible change because it can be done repeatedly.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to turn ash back into wood. Based on the text, why is this not possible?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a reversible change\",\"Because it is an irreversible change\",\"Because the ash is too hot\",\"Because the glass is not soft\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the example of wood and ash to explain what is meant by an irreversible change.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following pairs correctly matches a process with its change type according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Burning wood : Reversible\",\"Ripening mango : Reversible\",\"Melting wax : Reversible\",\"Bending glass : Irreversible\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Melting wax is described as a change that can occur in forward and reverse directions, making it reversible.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what is the necessary condition for the glass to become pliable enough to bend?\",\"options\":[\"It must be cooled\",\"It must be supported by fingers\",\"It must be heated in the middle\",\"It must be linked into a toran\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states to observe the glass as the middle gets heated and becomes soft, allowing it to bend.\",\"topic\":\"Reversible and Irreversible Changes\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Changes that occur again and again after a definite interval of time are called ____.\",\"options\":[\"Non-periodic changes\",\"Periodic changes\",\"Physical changes\",\"Chemical changes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines periodic changes as those that occur again and again after a definite interval of time.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a non-periodic change mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sunrise\",\"High tide in the sea\",\"A meteor streaking across the sky\",\"Change of seasons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists a meteor streaking across the sky as an example where we cannot say for sure when it will recur.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason flooding is considered a non-periodic change?\",\"options\":[\"It occurs again and again.\",\"It happens after a definite interval of time.\",\"The time interval for its recurrence is not fixed.\",\"It only happens during the rainy season.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Non-periodic changes are those where the time interval is not fixed even if they recur.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what can be said about the recurrence of a bird flying away from a tree?\",\"options\":[\"It will definitely recur every morning.\",\"It cannot be said for sure when it will recur.\",\"It recurs after a fixed interval of ten minutes.\",\"It is a periodic change.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the bird flying away as an example of a change where we cannot say for sure when it will recur.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The change of seasons from summer to rains to winter is an example of which type of change?\",\"options\":[\"Non-periodic change\",\"Periodic change\",\"Chemical change\",\"Irregular change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Seasons change in a definite cycle and interval, making them periodic changes.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which hands of a clock show periodic changes?\",\"options\":[\"Only the hour hand\",\"Only the minute hand\",\"Only the second hand\",\"The hour, minute, and second hands\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All hands of a clock move in a definite interval of time, making their movement periodic.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does a high tide in the sea differ from a meteor streaking across the sky?\",\"options\":[\"High tide is non-periodic, while a meteor is periodic.\",\"High tide is periodic, while a meteor is non-periodic.\",\"Both are periodic changes.\",\"Both are non-periodic changes.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"High tide occurs at definite intervals (periodic), whereas a meteor\'s recurrence is not fixed (non-periodic).\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a certain change recurs but the time interval between occurrences is not fixed, how is it classified?\",\"options\":[\"As a periodic change\",\"As a non-periodic change\",\"As a permanent change\",\"As a seasonal change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the time interval is not fixed, such changes are called non-periodic changes.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which date is designated as World Health Day?\",\"options\":[\"31st May\",\"7th April\",\"31st April\",\"7th May\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure explicitly states that 7th April is World Health Day.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/097_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the celebration of these specific days every year is an example of periodic change because ____.\",\"options\":[\"They only happen once in history.\",\"They occur again after a definite interval of one year.\",\"The dates change every year.\",\"They are non-periodic events.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since these days occur on the same date every year, they repeat at a definite interval, making them periodic.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/097_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these is a characteristic of periodic changes?\",\"options\":[\"They never recur.\",\"They occur at random times.\",\"They occur after a definite interval of time.\",\"The time interval is never fixed.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines periodic changes by their occurrence after a definite interval of time.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Between 6.00 am and 6.00 pm, how many times does the minute hand of a clock complete a periodic cycle?\",\"options\":[\"1 time\",\"6 times\",\"12 times\",\"24 times\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"From 6.00 am to 6.00 pm is 12 hours; the minute hand completes one periodic cycle every hour.\",\"topic\":\"Periodic and Non-periodic Changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is a change called when no new substance is formed?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical change\",\"Physical change\",\"Permanent change\",\"Biological change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a change where no new substance is formed is called a physical change.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of change results in the transformation of one substance into another with new and different properties?\",\"options\":[\"Physical change\",\"Temporary change\",\"Chemical change\",\"Natural change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a chemical change as one where a substance is transformed into another with new and different properties.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a physical change, what happens to the composition of the original substance?\",\"options\":[\"It changes completely\",\"It becomes new\",\"It remains unchanged\",\"It disappears\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in a physical change, the composition of the original substances remains unchanged.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the properties of the original substances during a physical change?\",\"options\":[\"They remain the same\",\"They become new\",\"They become different\",\"They are lost forever\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in examples of physical changes, the properties of the original substances remain the same.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance undergoes a change and its properties remain the same, what kind of change has occurred?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical change\",\"Physical change\",\"Permanent change\",\"Transformation change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the properties remain the same and no new substance is formed, it is a physical change.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a new substance with new properties is formed after a process, how would you classify this change?\",\"options\":[\"Physical change\",\"Unchanged change\",\"Chemical change\",\"Temporary change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The formation of a new substance with new properties is the definition of a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is a key difference between physical and chemical changes?\",\"options\":[\"Physical changes always involve heat\",\"Chemical changes result in new substances, while physical changes do not\",\"Physical changes are always permanent\",\"Chemical changes keep the original properties\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes them by the formation (chemical) or lack of formation (physical) of new substances.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text describe the properties of a substance after a chemical change compared to before?\",\"options\":[\"The properties remain exactly the same\",\"The composition remains unchanged\",\"The properties are new and different\",\"The original matter remains unchanged\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A chemical change results in a substance having new and different properties.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which question from the text helps identify if a chemical change has occurred?\",\"options\":[\"In which did the original matter remain unchanged?\",\"In which was a new substance with a new property formed?\",\"In which did the composition remain unchanged?\",\"In which did the properties remain the same?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The formation of a new substance with new properties is the defining question for a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, a change is \'physical\' if:\",\"options\":[\"The original matter undergoes a change\",\"One substance is transformed into another\",\"New properties are formed\",\"No new substance is formed\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that if no new substance is formed, it is called a physical change.\",\"topic\":\"Physical and Chemical Changes\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of formation of vapour from a liquid called?\",\"options\":[\"Melting\",\"Boiling\",\"Evaporation\",\"Dissolving\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines evaporation as the process of formation of vapour from a liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is possible due to the process of evaporation?\",\"options\":[\"Rusting of iron\",\"Formation of salt from seawater\",\"Ripening of a tomato\",\"Breaking of a glass object\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the formation of salt from seawater is possible due to evaporation.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, boiling and melting are examples of which type of change?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical change\",\"Physical change\",\"Natural change\",\"Biological change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that processes like dissolving, boiling, and melting are all examples of physical change.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the original properties of water during processes like boiling or melting?\",\"options\":[\"They change completely\",\"They do not change\",\"They become chemical properties\",\"They disappear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that in physical changes like the water cycle processes, the original properties do not change.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these activities is an example of a physical change mentioned in the \'Use your brain power\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Burning wood\",\"Rusting of iron\",\"Making a table from wood\",\"Ripening of a tomato\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Making a table from wood is a physical change as it only changes the shape of the wood, not its composition.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is classified as a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Breaking of a glass object\",\"Drying of clothes\",\"Dissolving salt in water\",\"Rusting of iron\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text asks to classify rusting of iron, which is a standard example of a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Drying of wet clothes is an everyday example of which process?\",\"options\":[\"Evaporation\",\"Melting\",\"Dissolving\",\"Boiling\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that drying of clothes is possible due to evaporation.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a glass object breaks, what type of change has occurred?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical change\",\"Biological change\",\"Physical change\",\"State change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Breaking a glass object is a physical change because the material remains glass.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these processes is a part of the water-cycle and also a physical change?\",\"options\":[\"Burning\",\"Evaporation\",\"Rusting\",\"Ripening\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions evaporation as a part of the water-cycle and a process where original properties don\'t change.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does burning wood differ from making a table from wood?\",\"options\":[\"Burning is physical, making a table is chemical\",\"Both are physical changes\",\"Burning is chemical, making a table is physical\",\"Both are chemical changes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Burning wood changes its chemical nature, while making a table only changes its physical form.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the ripening of a tomato considered a chemical change compared to melting ice?\",\"options\":[\"Because the color stays the same\",\"Because the internal properties and composition change\",\"Because it can be reversed easily\",\"Because it is a part of the water cycle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ripening involves a change in the chemical properties of the fruit, unlike melting which is a physical state change.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which process involves a substance disappearing into a liquid to form a solution, categorized as a physical change?\",\"options\":[\"Boiling\",\"Melting\",\"Dissolving\",\"Evaporation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists dissolving as an example of a physical change.\",\"topic\":\"Evaporation and State Changes\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the color of the layer formed on an iron article when it rusts?\",\"options\":[\"Blackish\",\"Greenish\",\"Reddish brown\",\"Silvery white\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when an iron article rusts, a reddish brown layer forms on it.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A greenish layer is seen to form on which type of metal article due to corrosion?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Zinc\",\"Copper\",\"Tin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a greenish layer is seen to form on a copper article.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to metal objects as a result of the process of corrosion?\",\"options\":[\"They become stronger\",\"They become weak\",\"They turn into sugar\",\"They become shiny\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that things become weak due to corrosion.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following factors is mentioned as a cause of corrosion?\",\"options\":[\"Heat from a burner\",\"Vapours of chemicals in the air\",\"The use of a tripod\",\"A blackish substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states corrosion is caused by oxygen, moisture, and vapours of chemicals in the air.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of giving a thin coat of zinc to iron articles called?\",\"options\":[\"Tinning\",\"Rusting\",\"Galvanization\",\"Evaporation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines galvanization as giving iron articles a thin coat of zinc to prevent corrosion.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment of heating sugar, what is observed at the bottom of the dish when heating is stopped?\",\"options\":[\"A greenish layer\",\"A reddish brown layer\",\"A blackish substance\",\"A thin coat of zinc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure says to stop heating when a blackish substance is seen at the bottom of the evaporating dish.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a worker needs to protect a copper or brass vessel from corrosion, which process should they use?\",\"options\":[\"Galvanization\",\"Tinning\",\"Heating on a tripod\",\"Adding moisture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that copper and brass articles are coated with tin, a process called tinning.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To prevent an iron bridge from weakening, a thin coat of zinc is applied. This is an application of:\",\"options\":[\"Tinning\",\"Corrosion\",\"Galvanization\",\"Evaporation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Applying a thin coat of zinc to iron to prevent corrosion is known as galvanization.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which three factors together cause the corrosion of metals?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar, heat, and burner\",\"Zinc, tin, and copper\",\"Oxygen, moisture, and chemical vapours\",\"Iron, brass, and tripod\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists oxygen, moisture, and vapours of chemicals in the air as the causes of corrosion.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Always remember\' section, what is the rule for classifying changes?\",\"options\":[\"Apply all criteria at once\",\"Take into account only one criterion at a time\",\"Only use the criterion of color\",\"Never apply different criteria to the same change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that while classifying changes, we take into account only one criterion at a time.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is tinning used specifically for copper and brass articles?\",\"options\":[\"To make them turn blackish\",\"To prevent corrosion\",\"To make them weak\",\"To turn them into iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tinning is mentioned in the context of preventing corrosion for copper and brass articles.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which apparatus is required to hold the evaporating dish during the sugar heating experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Zinc coat\",\"A copper article\",\"A tripod\",\"Moisture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The apparatus list and procedure mention placing the evaporating dish on a tripod.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(21999,46,7,'Science','en','Elements, Compounds and Mixtures','[\"- Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space.\",\"- All matter is made of tiny particles called atoms and molecules.\",\"- States of matter: solid, liquid, gas.\",\"- Change of state happens when temperature or pressure changes.\",\"- Properties of matter: mass, volume, colour, smell, taste, texture, density, hardness, melting point, boiling point, conductivity, solubility, etc.\",\"- Substance = matter made of one kind of atom or molecule.\",\"- Mixture = matter made of two or more substances that can be separated by physical means.\",\"- Element = substance whose molecules are made of only one type of atom (e.g., O, H, Fe).\",\"- Compound = substance formed by chemical combination of two or more elements (e.g., H₂O, CO₂).\",\"- Symbols of elements are one or two letters (H, O, Fe, Au).\",\"- Metals are good conductors, malleable, ductile; non‑metals are poor conductors, brittle; metalloids have intermediate properties.\",\"- Common separation methods: filtration, straining, sorting, winnowing, magnet, distillation, separating funnel, centrifugation, chromatography.\",\"- Distillation separates liquids with different boiling points.\",\"- Chromatography separates components based on solubility and adhesion to paper.\",\"- A compound has a fixed composition; a mixture does not.\",\"- The smallest particle of a compound is a molecule.\",\"- Elements cannot be created or destroyed (law of conservation of mass).\",\"- The symbol of an element is the first letter of its name, sometimes two letters if needed (Cl for chlorine).\",\"- Water is a compound (H₂O) and can be a solvent for many mixtures.\",\"- The mass of a substance is measured with a balance; volume is measured with a graduated cylinder or by displacement.\",\"- The density of a substance is its mass per unit volume; it helps identify substances.\",\"- The boiling point of a liquid is the temperature at which it turns into vapour.\",\"- The melting point of a solid is the temperature at which it turns into liquid.\",\"- The colour of a substance is a property of its molecules.\",\"- The taste and smell of food are properties of the molecules present.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In scientific language, what is the specific term used for that which an object is made of?\",\"options\":[\"Energy\",\"Matter\",\"Force\",\"Vacuum\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in scientific language, that which an object is made of is called matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which word is commonly used as a synonym for matter in day-to-day language?\",\"options\":[\"Weight\",\"Object\",\"Substance\",\"Element\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the term matter is also used as a synonym of substance.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the properties of matter when a substance like chalk is divided into very small particles?\",\"options\":[\"The properties change completely\",\"The properties remain the same\",\"The properties disappear\",\"The properties become invisible\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that even though they are divided into small particles, their properties remain the same.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of ink is specifically mentioned in the text to describe the matter it is made of?\",\"options\":[\"Fragrance\",\"Hardness\",\"Blue colour\",\"Sweetness\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the blue colour of ink as a property of the matter of which it is made.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Try this\' section, what property of perfume is noticed when the lid of the bottle is opened?\",\"options\":[\"Fragrance\",\"Blue colour\",\"Weight\",\"Hardness\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the fragrance of a perfume as a property of the matter it is made of.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Matter is present in various things in which of the following states?\",\"options\":[\"Only solid and liquid\",\"Only liquid and gaseous\",\"Solid, liquid, or gaseous\",\"Only solid and gaseous\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions matter present in various things in solid, liquid, or gaseous states.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the classification list provided in the text, which of the following is an example of matter in a gaseous state?\",\"options\":[\"Milk\",\"Soil\",\"Steam\",\"Gold\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the classification exercise, steam is listed as a substance to be identified by its state (gaseous).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances from the text is classified as being in a liquid state?\",\"options\":[\"Coal\",\"Milk\",\"Plank\",\"Concrete\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Milk is listed in the classification section as a substance in the liquid state.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you wipe a drop of ink with a handkerchief, the cloth turns blue. What does this demonstrate about the matter in the ink?\",\"options\":[\"Matter changes its properties when touched\",\"The properties of the matter remain the same even in small particles\",\"Matter only exists in solid form\",\"Matter loses its colour when divided\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the ink example to show that properties remain the same even when matter is divided into small particles.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following substances mentioned in the text is likely made from more than one kind of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Concrete\",\"Oxygen\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Concrete is a mixture of various materials, unlike gold which is a single kind of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is responsible for the specific properties of different things?\",\"options\":[\"The size of the object\",\"The matter present in them\",\"The person touching them\",\"The container they are kept in\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that it is the matter present in various things that is responsible for their properties.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is a property of the matter in chalk?\",\"options\":[\"Fragrance\",\"Blue colour\",\"White colour\",\"Transparency\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the white colour of a chalk as a property of its matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these articles of everyday use is specifically mentioned in the \'Can you tell?\' section as being made of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Electric wire\",\"Clouds\",\"Sunlight\",\"Sound\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Electric wire is listed in the text under articles of everyday use made of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When classifying \'an engraved idol\' and \'smoke\', how do they differ in their state of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Idol is liquid, smoke is solid\",\"Idol is solid, smoke is gaseous\",\"Idol is gaseous, smoke is liquid\",\"Both are in the same state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"An engraved idol is a solid, while smoke is a gaseous state of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of dividing a piece of chalk in the \'Try this\' experiment?\",\"options\":[\"To change its chemical composition\",\"To see if its properties change when it becomes small particles\",\"To turn it into a liquid state\",\"To measure its exact weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that even when divided into small particles, the properties of matter remain the same.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Matter\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In scientific language, matter that is made of only one constituent is called a ______.\",\"options\":[\"Mixture\",\"Substance\",\"Element\",\"Compound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines matter made of only one constituent as a \'substance\'.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following devices is mentioned in the text for measuring mass?\",\"options\":[\"Measuring tape\",\"Thermometer\",\"Common balance\",\"Spring balance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that mass can be measured with the help of devices like the common balance.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two important properties of matter identified in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Color and shape\",\"Mass and volume\",\"Hardness and weight\",\"Density and temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that mass and volume are two important properties of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a \'substance\' as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sherbet\",\"Soil\",\"Air\",\"Diamond\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists gold, diamond, water, and chalk as examples of substances.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a small stone is dropped into a glass full of water, what property of the stone is demonstrated?\",\"options\":[\"It has mass\",\"It occupies space\",\"It is a mixture\",\"It has no weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dropping a stone in water shows it occupies space (volume), which is a property of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the pan containing a big stone go down when compared to a pan with a small stone?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has more volume\",\"Because it has more mass\",\"Because it is a mixture\",\"Because it is a substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The pan goes down because the object has mass, and a bigger stone typically has more mass.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"From where do objects acquire the properties of mass and volume?\",\"options\":[\"From the air around them\",\"From the device used to measure them\",\"From the matter they are made of\",\"From the space they occupy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that objects acquire these properties from the matter that they are made of.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text define a \'mixture\'?\",\"options\":[\"Matter made of only one constituent\",\"Matter that has no mass\",\"Matter made of two or more substances\",\"Matter that does not occupy space\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines mixtures as matter made of two or more substances.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' section, which of the following is a mixture?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Iron\",\"Brass\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Brass is listed in the brain power section as a mixture (an alloy of metals).\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given a glass of sherbet, why is it classified as a mixture according to the scientific definition provided?\",\"options\":[\"It is found in nature in pure form\",\"It is made of only one constituent\",\"It is made of two or more substances\",\"It can be measured on a common balance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sherbet is a mixture because it consists of two or more substances like water, sugar, and lemon.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare \'water\' and \'air\' based on the text. Which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Water is a substance and air is a mixture\",\"Both are substances\",\"Both are mixtures\",\"Water is a mixture and air is a substance\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists water as a substance and air as a mixture in the brain power section.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be concluded about a piece of chalk based on the properties of matter described?\",\"options\":[\"It has mass but does not occupy space\",\"It occupies space but has no mass\",\"It has both mass and occupies space\",\"It is a mixture of many constituents\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As a substance (matter), chalk must have both mass and volume (occupy space).\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are the smallest particles of substances called, according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Atoms\",\"Molecules\",\"Vapour\",\"Droplets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the smallest particles of substances are molecules.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"By which process are the water droplets on the inside of a teapot lid formed?\",\"options\":[\"Evaporation\",\"Boiling\",\"Condensation\",\"Decomposition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that water droplets on the lid are formed by condensation of the vapour.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is water in the form of vapour invisible to the naked eye?\",\"options\":[\"It has no mass\",\"It is composed of extremely tiny particles\",\"It does not contain any atoms\",\"It is not a substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that vapour is composed of extremely tiny particles, and therefore, we cannot see them.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What defines a substance as an \'element\'?\",\"options\":[\"Its molecules are made of different types of atoms\",\"It can be decomposed into different substances\",\"Its molecules are made of one or more atoms which are exactly alike\",\"It is always in the form of a visible spray\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an element as a substance whose molecules are made of one or more atoms which are exactly alike.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist tries to perform a decomposition reaction on a pure element, what will be the result?\",\"options\":[\"Two new substances will be formed\",\"The element will turn into water vapour\",\"Different substances will not be obtained\",\"The atoms will become visible immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that we do not get different substances by the decomposition of an element.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many types of atoms are found in the smallest particles of a single element?\",\"options\":[\"Only one type\",\"At least two types\",\"Crores of different types\",\"Exactly three types\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the smallest particles of elements are made of only one type of atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Under what condition do atoms become visible to our eyes?\",\"options\":[\"When they are decomposed\",\"When they are heated in a teapot\",\"When crores of atoms come together\",\"When they are sprayed from a pump\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says that when crores of atoms come together, their total volume is large enough to be visible.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the spray pump experiment, what can be concluded about all substances?\",\"options\":[\"They are all made of water\",\"They are all made of extremely tiny particles\",\"They can all be seen as vapour\",\"They all have the same mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the spray pump observation to conclude that all substances are made of extremely tiny particles.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following properties are different for atoms of different elements?\",\"options\":[\"Only their color\",\"Only their state of matter\",\"Mass and volume\",\"Visibility and temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the mass and volume of atoms of different elements are different.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If an unknown substance is found to have molecules made of two different types of atoms, can it be called an element?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, because it is a substance\",\"No, because an element\'s atoms must be exactly alike\",\"Yes, because it is made of molecules\",\"No, because it cannot be seen with the naked eye\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an element as having atoms that are exactly alike; different atoms would disqualify it.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between atoms and the visibility of an element?\",\"options\":[\"Single atoms are always visible\",\"Volume must be large enough for visibility\",\"Atoms lose their mass when they become visible\",\"Only decomposed atoms are visible\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that crores of atoms together create a volume large enough to be visible.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the composition of water vapour as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It is a single solid block of matter\",\"It is composed of particles that are too small to see\",\"It is made of atoms that are all different from each other\",\"It is not made of particles until it condenses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text evaluates vapour as being composed of extremely tiny particles that are invisible until they collect.\",\"topic\":\"Elements\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How many elements have been discovered by scientists to date according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"92\",\"100\",\"118\",\"1803\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that to date, scientists have discovered 118 elements.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Out of the 118 discovered elements, how many occur naturally in nature?\",\"options\":[\"118\",\"92\",\"26\",\"1803\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that 92 elements occur in nature, while the remaining are man-made.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the scientist Democritus name the small particles of elements \'atom\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are very large\",\"Because \'atomos\' means indivisible in Greek\",\"Because they are made of oxygen\",\"Because they can be seen with naked eyes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Democritus used the name \'atom\' because in the Greek language \'atomos\' means indivisible.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the year 1803, which scientist proposed that atoms cannot be 