 Dalton (shown in the figure) used ☉ to represent Hydrogen.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/104_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the initial letter of two elements is the same, such as Carbon and Chlorine, how is the symbol for Chlorine written?\",\"options\":[\"Using the first three letters\",\"Using a pair of letters\",\"Using only the last letter\",\"Using a different language\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When the initial letter is the same, a pair of letters is used, such as Cl for chlorine.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If 92 elements are natural out of a total of 118, how many elements are man-made?\",\"options\":[\"118\",\"92\",\"26\",\"210\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"118 total elements minus 92 natural elements equals 26 man-made elements.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding atoms and molecules based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"They can be seen with naked eyes\",\"They cannot be seen with naked eyes\",\"Only atoms can be seen\",\"Only molecules can be seen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that just like atoms, molecules also cannot be seen with naked eyes.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between atoms and a molecule of oxygen as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"One atom forms one molecule\",\"Two atoms join to form one molecule\",\"Three atoms join to form one molecule\",\"Atoms and molecules are the same thing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says two atoms of oxygen are joined to form a molecule of oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the present method of symbols, what is the symbol for Potassium?\",\"options\":[\"P\",\"Po\",\"K\",\"Pt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The list of symbols in the text and table specifies K for Potassium.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a new element is discovered tomorrow, what will be the total number of elements discovered by scientists?\",\"options\":[\"118\",\"119\",\"93\",\"120\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 118 are currently discovered, the next discovery would make it 119.\",\"topic\":\"Discovery and Symbols of Elements\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Into which third group are elements classified, besides metals and non-metals?\",\"options\":[\"Alloys\",\"Metalloids\",\"Mixtures\",\"Compounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that elements that show some properties of metals and some of non-metals are called metalloids, which is the third group.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following elements is an example of a non-metal?\",\"options\":[\"Arsenic\",\"Silicon\",\"Phosphorus\",\"Tungsten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists phosphorus, sulphur, and chlorine as examples of non-metals.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What defines a \'metalloid\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Elements that occur only in a gaseous state\",\"Elements that are mixtures of two or more metals\",\"Elements that show some properties of metals and some of non-metals\",\"Elements that are derived from Latin names only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines metalloids as elements that show some properties of metals and some properties of non-metals.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these elements is classified as a metalloid?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur\",\"Selenium\",\"Chlorine\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Arsenic, silicon, and selenium are provided as examples of metalloids in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The symbol \'W\' for the element Tungsten is derived from which language?\",\"options\":[\"Latin\",\"English\",\"German\",\"Greek\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the symbol \'W\' is derived from its German name \'Wolfram\'.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the Latin name for gold from which the symbol \'Au\' is derived?\",\"options\":[\"Argentum\",\"Aurum\",\"Wolfram\",\"Brass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the symbol for gold (Au) is derived from its Latin name Aurum.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a manufacturer needs to prevent iron from rusting in air, what method does the text suggest?\",\"options\":[\"Using it in its pure form\",\"Converting it into a non-metal\",\"Mixing it with other elements to form an alloy\",\"Changing its state to a liquid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that properties of original metals can be modified by mixing one or more elements to form an alloy.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is a mixture of metals called?\",\"options\":[\"Metalloid\",\"Alloy\",\"Non-metal\",\"Pure element\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a mixture of metals as an alloy.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of an alloy?\",\"options\":[\"Pure gold\",\"Silicon\",\"Brass\",\"Phosphorus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Brass, steel, and twenty-two carat gold are listed as examples of alloys.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How are non-metals distinguished from metals in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are only found in a liquid state\",\"They do not show properties like malleability and conductivity\",\"They have symbols derived only from German names\",\"They are always harder than pure gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines non-metals as elements that do not show the properties of metals like malleability, ductility, and conductivity.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is 22-carat gold used for making ornaments instead of pure gold?\",\"options\":[\"Pure gold is a metalloid\",\"Pure gold is very soft and bends easily\",\"Pure gold is a gas at room temperature\",\"Pure gold does not have any lustre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that pure gold is very soft and bends easily, so its properties are modified (into an alloy).\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which states of matter can elements occur?\",\"options\":[\"Solid state only\",\"Solid and liquid states only\",\"Solid, liquid or gaseous state\",\"Gaseous state only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that elements occur in solid, liquid, or gaseous state.\",\"topic\":\"Classification of Elements\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When sugar is heated in a test tube, what is the black substance that remains behind?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Carbon\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that heating sugar leaves behind a black substance which is carbon.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of a compound according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A substance made of only one type of atom\",\"A substance formed by a chemical combination of two or more elements\",\"A substance that can be separated by simple heating\",\"A mixture of different types of molecules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a compound as a substance formed by a chemical combination of two or more elements.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"One molecule of water is made up of which atoms?\",\"options\":[\"One atom of hydrogen and two atoms of oxygen\",\"Two atoms of hydrogen and two atoms of oxygen\",\"Two atoms of hydrogen and one atom of oxygen\",\"One atom of carbon and two atoms of oxygen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that one molecule of water is made of two atoms of hydrogen and one atom of oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these elements is described as a \'combustible substance\' that burns?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Carbon\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Hydrogen is a combustible substance, that is, it burns.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the smallest particle of a compound called?\",\"options\":[\"Element\",\"Atom\",\"Molecule\",\"Symbol\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the smallest unit of a compound as a molecule.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A substance is considered a compound only if its molecules are made up of:\",\"options\":[\"Atoms of the same type\",\"Atoms of different types\",\"Only hydrogen and oxygen\",\"Single atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Always remember\' section states a substance is a compound only if its molecules are made of atoms of different types.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do the properties of a compound compare to the properties of its constituent elements?\",\"options\":[\"They are exactly the same\",\"They are a mixture of both\",\"They are different from those of the constituent elements\",\"They depend only on the element present in the largest amount\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the properties of a compound are different from those of the constituent elements.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does a molecular formula represent?\",\"options\":[\"The weight of the compound\",\"The state of the compound (solid or liquid)\",\"A short form of the compound\'s name using symbols and number of atoms\",\"The temperature at which a compound melts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a molecular formula as a short form of a compound\'s name written with symbols and the number of atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is a compound?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to identify compounds and mentions carbon dioxide as a substance made of two elements.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given the molecular formula H₂O, what does the \'2\' indicate?\",\"options\":[\"Two molecules of water\",\"Two atoms of hydrogen\",\"Two atoms of oxygen\",\"Two different elements\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In H₂O, the number indicates the count of the preceding element\'s atoms (Hydrogen).\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is used to extinguish a fire, even though its constituents help in burning?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen\",\"Oxygen\",\"Water\",\"Carbon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that water is used to extinguish fire despite being made of hydrogen and oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When sugar is heated and leaves behind carbon and water, what does this imply about sugar?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar is an element\",\"Sugar is a mixture of black and white substances\",\"Sugar is a compound made of more than one element\",\"Sugar is made only of carbon atoms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The breakdown of sugar into carbon and water shows it is a compound made of multiple elements.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is oxygen not considered a compound based on the \'Always remember\' rule?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a gas\",\"Because it helps in combustion\",\"Because its molecules are not made of atoms of different types\",\"Because it does not have a molecular formula\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A compound must have atoms of different types; oxygen molecules consist only of oxygen atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the roles of Hydrogen and Oxygen in combustion as described in the text.\",\"options\":[\"Both are used to extinguish fires\",\"Hydrogen burns while Oxygen helps combustion\",\"Oxygen burns while Hydrogen helps combustion\",\"Both are combustible substances\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states Hydrogen is combustible and Oxygen helps combustion.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What information is required to write the molecular formula of a compound?\",\"options\":[\"The color and taste of the elements\",\"The symbols of constituent elements and the number of their atoms\",\"The melting point of the compound\",\"The name of the scientist who discovered it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states a molecular formula is written with the help of symbols of constituent elements and the number of their atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Compounds\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is formed by mixing different elements or compounds without a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"A new element\",\"A mixture\",\"A compound\",\"A pure substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a mixture is formed by mixing different elements or compounds without a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for mixing unwanted substances in foodstuff?\",\"options\":[\"Sublimation\",\"Winnowing\",\"Adulteration\",\"Sifting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that mixing unwanted substances in any foodstuff is called adulteration.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a mixture, the proportion of various components is:\",\"options\":[\"Always fixed\",\"Not fixed\",\"Exactly equal\",\"Increasing over time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the proportion of various components in a mixture is not fixed.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a method used for separating components of a mixture as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Heating to melt\",\"Combing with a magnet\",\"Chemical bonding\",\"Adding new substances\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'combing with a magnet\' as one of the simple and easy methods for separation.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the taste of original ingredients like sugar and lemon not change when preparing a sherbet?\",\"options\":[\"Because a new substance is formed\",\"Because a chemical change takes place\",\"Because no chemical change takes place during the formation of a mixture\",\"Because the proportion of ingredients is fixed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that no chemical change takes place during the formation of mixtures, so the taste of ingredients remains.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the properties of matter when a mixture is formed?\",\"options\":[\"They are completely lost\",\"They are used for separating the components later\",\"They change into new properties\",\"They become fixed and unchangeable\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that properties of matter are used for separating the components of a mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When does a mixture no longer remain useful to us?\",\"options\":[\"When it is made of elements\",\"When it is made of compounds\",\"When an unwanted and harmful substance is mixed into it\",\"When the proportion of components is not fixed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says when an unwanted and harmful substance is mixed with another substance, the mixture no longer remains useful.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the properties of matter, what else can be used to separate components of a mixture?\",\"options\":[\"The effects of heat\",\"The addition of chemicals\",\"The formation of new substances\",\"The taste of the mixture\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the properties of matter and the effects of heat are used for separating components.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have a mixture of semolina, salt, and iron filings, which method would you use first to remove the iron filings?\",\"options\":[\"Winnowing\",\"Sublimation\",\"Combing with a magnet\",\"Straining\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Iron filings are magnetic, so combing with a magnet is the appropriate method mentioned in the text for such mixtures.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which method would be most suitable to separate a solid that turns directly into gas from a mixture?\",\"options\":[\"Sifting\",\"Sublimation\",\"Picking\",\"Sorting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sublimation is listed in the text as a method for separation, which applies to substances that change state from solid to gas.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how can we classify \'adulterated foodstuff\'?\",\"options\":[\"As a pure compound\",\"As a new element\",\"As a kind of mixture\",\"As a chemical reaction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'an adulterated foodstuff is also a kind of mixture\'.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the difference between a mixture and the formation of a new substance?\",\"options\":[\"Mixtures have a fixed proportion, while new substances do not\",\"Mixtures involve a chemical change, while new substances do not\",\"No new substance is formed in a mixture, unlike in a chemical change\",\"Mixtures cannot be separated by simple methods like sifting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in a mixture, no chemical change takes place and no new substance is formed.\",\"topic\":\"Mixtures\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the distillation experiment, where is the salt water initially placed?\",\"options\":[\"In a conical flask\",\"In a round-bottom flask\",\"In a beaker\",\"In the inclined tube\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, salt water is taken in a round-bottom flask for the distillation process.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the salt when all the water has collected in the conical flask?\",\"options\":[\"It evaporates with the water\",\"It turns into vapour\",\"It remains behind at the bottom of the round-bottom flask\",\"It flows into the inclined tube\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that salt remains behind at the bottom of the round-bottom flask after the water is collected.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the wire gauze in the distillation apparatus?\",\"options\":[\"To cool the flask\",\"To hold the conical flask\",\"To place the round-bottom flask on for heating\",\"To filter the salt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions heating the liquid in the flask placed on the wire gauze.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the droplets of water collect after passing through the inclined tube?\",\"options\":[\"In the round-bottom flask\",\"In the conical flask\",\"On the wire gauze\",\"In the salt solution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes droplets of water falling into the conical flask.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What process causes the water vapour to turn back into liquid water in the inclined tube?\",\"options\":[\"Boiling\",\"Vaporization\",\"Condensation\",\"Heating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that vapour condenses to form water when it passes through the tube surrounded by cold water.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the water in the round-bottom flask turn into vapour?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the cold water outside\",\"Because it boils on heating\",\"Because it is mixed with salt\",\"Because it flows into the conical flask\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the salt water boils on heating and the water in it vapourizes.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides separating salt from water, what is another use of the distillation method?\",\"options\":[\"Making water salty\",\"Heating wire gauze\",\"Purification of impure liquids\",\"Cooling the round-bottom flask\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that distillation is also used for purification of impure liquids.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student needs to separate a mixture of sugar and water to get pure water, which method from the text should they apply?\",\"options\":[\"Filtration\",\"Distillation\",\"Simple heating without a tube\",\"Mixing with cold water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since distillation separates a solid (salt) from a liquid (water) and purifies liquids, it can be applied to sugar-water mixtures.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist wants to purify a sample of muddy water. Based on the text, which apparatus part is essential for turning the steam back into liquid?\",\"options\":[\"The wire gauze\",\"The round-bottom flask\",\"The inclined tube surrounded by cold water\",\"The burner\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The inclined tube with surrounding cold water is where condensation happens, which is necessary to collect the liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which sequence correctly describes the change of states that water undergoes during distillation?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid -> Solid -> Liquid\",\"Liquid -> Vapour -> Liquid\",\"Vapour -> Liquid -> Vapour\",\"Solid -> Liquid -> Vapour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water starts as a liquid (salt water), turns to vapour (boiling), and then back to liquid (condensation).\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific role of the \'surrounding cold water\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To boil the salt water faster\",\"To keep the conical flask steady\",\"To provide a cooling effect for condensation\",\"To wash the inclined tube\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says vapour vapourizes (condenses) due to the surrounding cold water.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is distillation an effective way to separate salt from water?\",\"options\":[\"Because salt and water both turn into vapour at the same time\",\"Because water vapourizes on heating while salt remains behind\",\"Because salt turns into liquid and water stays solid\",\"Because the conical flask attracts salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The method works because water changes state to vapour and moves to the other flask, while salt does not vapourize and stays behind.\",\"topic\":\"Distillation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is formed when a mixture of two immiscible liquids is left undisturbed?\",\"options\":[\"A single uniform solution\",\"Two clearly seen layers\",\"A solid precipitate\",\"Bubbles of gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when left undisturbed, two layers are clearly seen to have formed.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a mixture of two immiscible liquids, where does the heavier liquid remain?\",\"options\":[\"It floats on the top\",\"It remains in the middle\",\"It remains below\",\"It evaporates quickly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the heavier of the liquids remains below.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the apparatus used in the procedure to separate kerosene and water?\",\"options\":[\"Filter paper\",\"Separating funnel\",\"Evaporating dish\",\"Distillation flask\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies pouring the mixture into a separating funnel.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does kerosene float on water when placed in a separating funnel?\",\"options\":[\"Because kerosene is heavier than water\",\"Because kerosene is a lighter liquid\",\"Because kerosene is a solid\",\"Because water is lighter than kerosene\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the lighter liquid floats on the heavier one, and kerosene floats on water.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of leaving the mixture in the funnel undisturbed for a while?\",\"options\":[\"To allow the liquids to mix better\",\"To increase the temperature of the liquids\",\"To allow the layers of water and kerosene to form\",\"To make the liquids evaporate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Leaving the mixture undisturbed allows the heavier liquid to settle below and the lighter one to float.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of liquids is primarily used to separate them using a separating funnel?\",\"options\":[\"Their difference in color\",\"Their difference in boiling points\",\"Their property of being immiscible and having different weights\",\"Their ability to dissolve in each other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The method relies on the liquids being immiscible and forming layers based on being heavier or lighter.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are using a separating funnel to separate a mixture, what should you do just before opening the stopcock?\",\"options\":[\"Shake the funnel vigorously\",\"Remove the stopper without shaking the funnel\",\"Add more kerosene to the mixture\",\"Close the stopcock tighter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure states to remove the stopper without shaking the funnel before opening the stopcock.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When should the stopcock be closed during the separation of water and kerosene?\",\"options\":[\"When the kerosene starts to flow into the beaker\",\"As soon as the stopcock is opened\",\"When all the water is collected in the beaker\",\"Before removing the stopper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to close the stopcock when all the water is collected in the beaker.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part is used to control the flow of the liquid into the beaker?\",\"options\":[\"Separating funnel\",\"Stopcock\",\"Stand\",\"Stopper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The stopcock is the valve shown in the diagram that regulates the liquid flow.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/108_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the position of the kerosene layer indicate compared to the water layer?\",\"options\":[\"Kerosene is heavier than water\",\"Kerosene is lighter than water\",\"Kerosene has the same weight as water\",\"Kerosene is more soluble than water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows kerosene floating on top, which the text defines as the position for the lighter liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/108_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen if the stopcock is left open after all the water has drained out?\",\"options\":[\"The kerosene would also flow into the beaker\",\"The water would flow back into the funnel\",\"The funnel would break\",\"The two liquids would mix again in the funnel\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"If the stopcock remains open, the next layer (kerosene) will follow the water into the beaker.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is this specific method unsuitable for a mixture of salt and water?\",\"options\":[\"Salt and water are immiscible\",\"Salt and water do not form two separate layers when left undisturbed\",\"Salt is lighter than water\",\"Water is heavier than salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This method requires immiscible liquids that form distinct layers; salt dissolves in water and does not form a separate liquid layer.\",\"topic\":\"Separation of Immiscible Liquids\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is mentioned in the text as a mixture of liquids and insoluble solids?\",\"options\":[\"Salt water\",\"Sugar syrup\",\"Buttermilk\",\"Lemonade\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists turbid water, ink, milk, buttermilk, and blood as mixtures of liquids and insoluble solids.\",\"topic\":\"Centrifugation\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a centrifuge machine, which part rotates like a fan at great speed?\",\"options\":[\"The test tube\",\"The disc\",\"The stand\",\"The handle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the centrifuge machine consists of a disc which rotates like a fan at great speed.\",\"topic\":\"Centrifugation\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do particles of milk or ink not settle even when the mixture is left undisturbed?\",\"op