gravity acting on the paper\",\"The direction in which the solvent (water) and components rise\",\"The direction in which the beaker should be tilted\",\"The direction of the air flow inside the beaker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The arrow indicates the upward movement of the solvent and the separated components on the filter paper.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/109_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the role of the \'Spot of Ink\' placed near the bottom of the filter paper?\",\"options\":[\"To act as a weight to keep the paper straight\",\"To dye the water in the beaker blue\",\"To serve as the mixture whose components are to be separated\",\"To mark the maximum height the water can reach\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The ink spot is the mixture containing different constituents that are separated as they rise.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/109_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these can be used as a substitute for filter paper in the chromatography experiment described?\",\"options\":[\"A plastic strip\",\"A glass rod\",\"A chalkstick\",\"A metal spoon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the experiment can be done using a chalkstick instead of the filter paper.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if the ink used in the experiment contains two or more constituents of different colours?\",\"options\":[\"They will all stay at the 2 cm mark\",\"They will rise to different heights and appear distinct\",\"They will mix with the water and disappear\",\"They will turn into a single black spot at the top\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that constituents of different colours rise to different heights and appear distinct from each other.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Chromatography relies on two properties of substances: the ability to move up with the solvent and the ability to:\",\"options\":[\"Dissolve the filter paper\",\"Stick to the stationary filter paper\",\"Evaporate into the beaker\",\"Change their colour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The two properties mentioned are the movement in the solvent and the ability to stick to the stationary filter paper.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist is trying to identify the different components of a complex liquid mixture in a lab. Based on the text, which method should they apply?\",\"options\":[\"Sieving\",\"Chromatography\",\"Centrifugation\",\"Handpicking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Chromatography is used in scientific laboratories for identifying and separating components of a mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a specific component in a mixture has a very high ability to stick to the filter paper but a low solubility in the solvent, where would it likely be found?\",\"options\":[\"At the very top of the filter paper\",\"Dissolved in the water in the beaker\",\"At a lower height on the filter paper\",\"On the lid of the beaker\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Components that stick more to the paper and move less with the solvent remain behind at lower heights.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text describes the two properties used in chromatography as being \'mutually opposite\'. What is the result of this opposition?\",\"options\":[\"All components rise to the exact same height\",\"The filter paper dissolves in the water\",\"Components remain behind at different heights\",\"The ink spot remains stationary at the bottom\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because these properties are different and opposite for different substances, components remain behind at different heights.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a lid placed on the beaker during the chromatography procedure?\",\"options\":[\"To prevent the filter paper from falling\",\"To keep the environment inside the beaker stable for the process\",\"To increase the weight of the beaker\",\"To hide the results of the experiment\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"While the text doesn\'t explicitly state \'why\', placing a lid is a procedural step to maintain the internal environment for the solvent to rise correctly.\",\"topic\":\"Chromatography\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22000,46,7,'Science','en','Materials we Use','[\"Natural materials are those found in nature, like wood, stone, and metal.\",\"Man‑made materials are produced by humans, e.g. plastics, synthetic detergents, and cement.\",\"Toothpaste contains calcium carbonate, calcium hydrogen phosphate, and fluoride; fluoride prevents tooth decay.\",\"Detergents are surface‑active substances that can mix oil and water; they form a milky mixture when added to oil‑water mix.\",\"Soap is a natural detergent made from fats and alkali; it is a sodium or potassium salt of fatty acids.\",\"Hard water contains calcium and magnesium ions; soap forms scum in hard water and does not lather well.\",\"Synthetic detergents are made from fats or kerosene and work well even in hard water.\",\"Cement is a dry, greenish‑grey powder made from lime, silica, alumina, iron oxide and gypsum; Portland cement is the most common type.\",\"Concrete is made by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel; it is used for roads and buildings because it is strong and durable.\",\"Surface activity is the ability of a substance to spread on a surface; it is responsible for lather formation in soaps and detergents.\",\"Key ingredients of toothpaste: abrasives (calcium carbonate) to clean, fluoride to strengthen enamel, and humectants to keep it moist.\",\"Key ingredients of cement: lime (calcium oxide), silica (silicon dioxide), alumina, iron oxide and gypsum.\",\"Natural detergents (soap nut, soap pod) contain saponin and are gentle on skin.\",\"Hard soap is a sodium salt of fatty acids; soft soap is a potassium salt of fatty acids.\",\"The first commercial toothpaste was made by Colgate in 1873.\",\"Synthetic detergents can be used in both soft and hard water.\",\"Cement production involves heating limestone to 1400°C to make clinker, then grinding with gypsum.\",\"Concrete’s hardness comes from the chemical reaction of cement with water (hydration).\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a man-made substance?\",\"options\":[\"A substance found directly in nature\",\"A new substance produced by chemical processing of natural substances\",\"A substance that cannot be changed by chemicals\",\"A substance used only in ancient times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a man-made substance as one produced by the chemical processing of natural substances.\",\"topic\":\"Toothpaste\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was used in olden times in India for cleaning teeth?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium carbonate\",\"Fluoride\",\"Neem twigs\",\"Calcium hydrogen phosphate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions neem twigs as one of the materials used in olden times in India.\",\"topic\":\"Toothpaste\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the principal ingredients of modern toothpaste?\",\"options\":[\"Ash and coal powder\",\"Acacia bark and salt\",\"Calcium carbonate and calcium hydrogen phosphate\",\"Pomegranate rind and fluoride\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that calcium carbonate and calcium hydrogen phosphate are the principal ingredients.\",\"topic\":\"Toothpaste\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of calcium carbonate and calcium hydrogen phosphate in toothpaste?\",\"options\":[\"To give the toothpaste a sweet taste\",\"To remove dirt and polish the teeth\",\"To change the color of the gums\",\"To produce 