0,\"explain\":\"The text states that one effect of surface activity is lather formation.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the detergent molecules shown holding on to?\",\"options\":[\"Only other detergent molecules\",\"Only the surface of the cloth\",\"Both a molecule of water and a molecule of oil\",\"Two molecules of water at both ends\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show detergent molecules holding a water molecule at one end and an oil molecule at the other.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/111_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure showing the action of detergents, where is the \'Molecule of oil\' initially located?\",\"options\":[\"Floating at the very top of the water\",\"Stuck to the surface of the cloth\",\"Inside the molecule of water\",\"Dissolved completely in the detergent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the molecule of oil attached to the \'Surface of cloth\'.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/111_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the oil layer when a bottle containing only water and oil is shaken and then allowed to settle?\",\"options\":[\"The oil disappears\",\"The oil sinks to the bottom\",\"The oil floats on the water again\",\"The oil and water become milky\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure states that after shaking water and oil, when the liquid settles, the oil will again be seen floating on the water.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does soap water spread easily on many types of surfaces?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is heavier than plain water\",\"Due to its property of holding on to both oil and water\",\"Because it turns into a gas quickly\",\"Because it contains no molecules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that due to the property of holding on to both oil and water, soap water spreads easily on surfaces.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following best evaluates why detergents are effective for cleaning soiled clothes?\",\"options\":[\"They make the water turn milky\",\"They are surface active and can remove oil sticking to greasy threads\",\"They prevent the formation of lather\",\"They make the clothes oily by adding gels and creams\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Detergents are surface active and remove the oily layer that sticks to the greasy threads of clothes.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why do our clothes become oily?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the detergent molecules\",\"Due to applying gels, creams, or oiling our hair\",\"Because of the water in the bottle\",\"Because of the formation of lather\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions our body and clothes become oily due to applying gels, creams, or oiling hair.\",\"topic\":\"How Detergents Work\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the chemical found in natural detergents like soap nut and soap pod?\",\"options\":[\"Saponin\",\"Sodium salt\",\"Potassium salt\",\"Wood ash\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that soap nut and soap pod contain a chemical named saponin.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In ancient times, which materials were used to prepare soap in the West?\",\"options\":[\"Soap nut and soap pod\",\"Animal fat and wood ash\",\"Sodium and potassium salts\",\"Saponin and silk threads\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in ancient days, soap was prepared using animal fat and wood ash.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is soft soap specifically used for bathing instead of hard soap?\",\"options\":[\"It is a sodium salt of fatty acids\",\"It is used for washing clothes\",\"It does not cause irritation of the skin\",\"It forms a scum in hard water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that soft soap is used for bathing because it does not cause irritation of the skin.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when soap is used in the hard water of a well or a tube-well?\",\"options\":[\"It produces a lot of lather\",\"It cleans clothes more effectively\",\"It forms a scum and loses its cleansing property\",\"It turns into a potassium salt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, soap does not give lather in hard water but forms a scum, losing its cleansing property.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person needs to wash delicate silk or woolen clothes, which detergent is recommended to avoid damage?\",\"options\":[\"Hard soap\",\"Sodium salt of fatty acids\",\"Natural detergents like soap nut\",\"Wood ash soap\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that soap nut and soap pod do not have any harmful effect on silk and woolen threads.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A manufacturer wants to produce soap specifically for washing clothes. Which chemical should they use as the primary ingredient?\",\"options\":[\"Potassium salt of fatty acids\",\"Saponin\",\"Sodium salt of fatty acids\",\"Animal fat only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines hard soap, used for washing clothes, as a sodium salt of fatty acids.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical composition described in the text, how does hard soap differ from soft soap?\",\"options\":[\"Hard soap is natural, while soft soap is man-made\",\"Hard soap is a sodium salt, while soft soap is a potassium salt\",\"Hard soap contains saponin, while soft soap does not\",\"Hard soap is used for bathing, while soft soap is for clothes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes hard soap as a sodium salt and soft soap as a potassium salt of fatty acids.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are soap nut and soap pod classified as \'natural\' detergents in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they were invented 2000 years ago\",\"Because they are prepared using wood ash\",\"Because they contain the naturally occurring chemical saponin\",\"Because they form scum in hard water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"They are natural detergents because they are plant-based materials containing the chemical saponin.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, approximately how many years ago was soap believed to have been invented?\",\"options\":[\"500 years ago\",\"1000 years ago\",\"2000 years ago\",\"5000 years ago\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states it is believed soap was invented in the west about 2000 years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic shared by both soap nut and soap pod?\",\"options\":[\"They are both potassium salts of fatty acids\",\"They both cause skin irritation\",\"They both have no harmful effect on human skin\",\"They are both used to make hard soap\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that soap nut and soap pod do not have any harmful effect on human skin.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which natural detergent is shown as a long, segmented, pod-like structure?\",\"options\":[\"Soap nut\",\"Soap pod\",\"Hard soap\",\"Soft soap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the long, segmented structure at the top as the \'Soap pod\'.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/111_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which detergent is shown as round, nut-like structures, some of which are open to show the seed inside?\",\"options\":[\"Soap pod\",\"Soap nut\",\"Wood ash\",\"Animal fat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the round structures at the bottom as \'Soap nut\'.\",\"topic\":\"Natural and Man-made Detergents\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/111_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What are the main raw materials used to obtain the long structural units in synthetic detergents?\",\"options\":[\"Wood and coal\",\"Fats or kerosene\",\"Sugar and salt\",\"Plastic and rubber\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that long structural units in synthetic detergents are obtained from raw materials which are mainly fats or kerosene.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are synthetic detergents considered more versatile than traditional soap regarding water types?\",\"options\":[\"They only work in hot water\",\"They can be used in hard water as well\",\"They do not work in cold water\",\"They require pure distilled water to function\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that synthetic detergents can be used in hard water as well.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a manufacturer wants to produce a detergent that also kills bacteria, which additive should they include based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Fine sand\",\"Germicides\",\"Alcohol\",\"Dyes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Germicides are listed as supporting additives that give detergents specific useful properties, such as killing germs.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what determines which supporting additives are mixed into a specific detergent?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the kerosene used\",\"The price of the raw fats\",\"The intended use of the detergent\",\"The amount of hard water available\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that additives are mixed \'as per the use of the detergent\'.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which of these products are synthetic detergents commonly found according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Food items\",\"Cosmetics\",\"Building bricks\",\"Writing paper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that synthetic detergents are used in many types of cosmetics.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of adding substances like perfumes and dyes to synthetic detergents?\",\"options\":[\"To make the detergent cheaper to produce\",\"To give the detergents certain useful properties\",\"To make the detergent dissolve slower in water\",\"To replace the need for chemical processes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that additives are mixed with detergents to give them certain useful properties.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, which of these is listed as a supporting additive mixed with detergents?\",\"options\":[\"Vegetable oil\",\"Anti-foaming agents\",\"Natural rubber\",\"Milk protein\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure lists \'anti-foaming agents\' as one of the supporting additives.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/111_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which additive is mentioned that would help keep the skin soft when using a detergent product?\",\"options\":[\"Alcohol\",\"Moisturizers\",\"Fine sand\",\"Dyes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure lists \'moisturizers\' as an additive, which are used to keep skin soft.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/111_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement best justifies why synthetic detergents have \'taken the place of soap\'?\",\"options\":[\"They are made only from natural kerosene\",\"They can be used in hard water and customized with various additives\",\"They are used exclusively in the cosmetic industry\",\"They are produced by a single, simple method\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Their ability to work in hard water and the addition of various properties through additives makes them a more versatile replacement for soap.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How are raw materials like fats or kerosene transformed into synthetic detergents?\",\"options\":[\"By freezing them at very low temperatures\",\"By subjecting them to a variety of chemical processes\",\"By simply mixing them with old soap\",\"By boiling them in hard water until they thicken\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that detergents are obtained by subjecting raw materials to a variety of chemical processes.\",\"topic\":\"Synthetic Detergent\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Chemically, soap is a sodium or potassium salt of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral acids\",\"Fatty acids\",\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Carbonic acid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that chemically, soap is a sodium or potassium salt of fatty acids.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How much coconut oil is required for the soap preparation procedure described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"15 ml\",\"50 ml\",\"60 ml\",\"200 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies taking 60 ml of coconut oil in a beaker.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which alkali is used in the experiment to react with coconut oil?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Sodium chloride\",\"Potassium chloride\",\"Magnesium hydroxide\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The material list and procedure mention using 15g of sodium hydroxide.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is formed when fat and alkali combine during the soap-making process?\",\"options\":[\"Pure sodium\",\"Coconut oil crystals\",\"Salts of fatty acids\",\"Sodium hydroxide solution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in the process, fat and alkali combine to form salts of fatty acids.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the procedure, for how long should the mixture be boiled while stirring?\",\"options\":[\"5-8 minutes\",\"10-12 minutes\",\"20-25 minutes\",\"30-40 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure instructs to heat the mixture and boil it for 10-12 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the soap immediately after the salt solution is added and stirred?\",\"options\":[\"It dissolves completely in water\",\"It sinks to the bottom of the beaker\",\"It floats on the water\",\"It turns into a gas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that after pouring the salt solution and stirring, the soap formed now floats on the water.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"At what stage is the perfume added to the soap?\",\"options\":[\"Before heating the oil\",\"While dissolving sodium hydroxide\",\"After separating the thick soap\",\"Before adding the salt solution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure says to separate the thick soap and then add the perfume to it.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To prevent the mixture from boiling over during heating, what action must be taken?\",\"options\":[\"Add more salt\",\"Stir it all the while\",\"Add more coconut oil\",\"Cover the beaker with a lid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises to stir the mixture all the while to take care that it does not boil over while heating.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to give a specific shape to the prepared soap, which item from the material list would you use?\",\"options\":[\"Beaker\",\"Tripod\",\"Mould\",\"Wire gauze\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using a mould to shape the bar of soap.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the purpose of the white container into which the mixture is being poured?\",\"options\":[\"To boil the mixture\",\"To dissolve the salt\",\"To shape the bar of soap\",\"To measure the oil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows soap being poured into a mould, which the text says is used to shape the bar of soap.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/112_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure and the procedure, what must have been added to the mixture just before it reached this pouring stage?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Perfume\",\"Wire gauze\",\"Burner\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the procedure, perfume is added after the soap thickens and before it is shaped in the mould.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/112_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a necessary reactant for the chemical reaction that produces soap according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Perfume\",\"Glass rod\",\"Fat\",\"Wire gauze\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that fat and alkali combine to form soap.\",\"topic\":\"Preparation of soap\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the physical appearance of cement as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A wet, black paste with coarse grains\",\"A dry, greenish grey powder with fine particles\",\"A white, crystalline solid that dissolves in water\",\"A reddish-brown oily liquid used for coating\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that cement is a dry, greenish grey powder with fine particles.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which British engineer developed the method of making aqueous cement in 1756?\",\"options\":[\"James Watt\",\"Isaac Newton\",\"John Smeaton\",\"Robert Hooke\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in 1756, the British engineer John Smeaton developed the method of making aqueous cement.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the percentage of lime (calcium oxide) in the raw material used for Portland cement?\",\"options\":[\"25%\",\"5%\",\"60%\",\"10%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Portland cement is made from raw material which consists of 60% lime.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'Portland cement\' given that specific name?\",\"options\":[\"It was first manufactured in a city called Portland\",\"The inventor\'s name was Portland\",\"It has a texture similar to stone quarried from the Isle of Portland\",\"It was exclusively used to build the port of England\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states it gets its name from the stone quarried from the Isle of Portland in England, which has a similar texture.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following materials are produced specifically from concrete?\",\"options\":[\"Volcanic ash and lime\",\"Sheets, blocks, pillars and pipes\",\"Iron oxide and magnesia\",\"Pure silica and alumina\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that sheets, blocks, pillars and pipes are made from concrete produced from cement.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did the ancient Romans create durable aqueous cement?\",\"options\":[\"By mixing volcanic ash in moistened lime\",\"By crushing stones from the Isle of Portland\",\"By mixing 60% silica with iron oxide\",\"By heating gypsum and magnesia together\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states Romans used to make aqueous cement by mixing volcanic ash in moistened lime.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A builder wants to prepare a batch of concrete for a construction project. Which mixture should they use?\",\"options\":[\"Cement, silica, and alumina\",\"Cement, water, sand and gravel\",\"Lime, iron oxide, and magnesia\",\"Volcanic ash and dry sand\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines concrete as being prepared by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a manufacturer is looking for \'silica\' to produce cement, which common material would they provide?\",\"options\":[\"Sand\",\"Volcanic ash\",\"Gravel\",\"Limestone\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly identifies silica as sand in the list of cement ingredients.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the composition of Portland cement, what is the combined percentage of lime and silica?\",\"options\":[\"65%\",\"75%\",\"85%\",\"90%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Portland cement contains 60% lime and 25% silica, which equals 85% total.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What was the historical consequence of the decline of the Roman Empire regarding construction technology?\",\"options\":[\"Concrete became the only material used for roads\",\"The art of making cement was forgotten\",\"John Smeaton immediately took over the production\",\"Volcanic ash was replaced by gypsum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that with the decline of the Roman empire, the art of making cement was also forgotten.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of Portland cement ingredients, which chemical name is correctly matched with its common name?\",\"options\":[\"Silica - Magnesium oxide\",\"Lime - Calcium oxide\",\"Alumina - Calcium sulphate\",\"Gypsum - Silicon dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies lime as calcium oxide.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which component makes up the smallest specific percentage (5%) mentioned in the Portland cement formula?\",\"options\":[\"Silica\",\"Lime\",\"Alumina\",\"Gypsum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that Portland cement consists of 5% alumina.\",\"topic\":\"Cement and Concrete\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29');
INSERT INTO `ep_topic_packs` VALUES (22001,46,7,'Science','en','Natural Resources','[\"Natural resources are substances obtained from nature that satisfy human needs, such as soil, stones, minerals, air, water, plants and animals.\",\"The Earth’s lithosphere is the outer solid shell made of land and the upper part of the crust where minerals and ores are found.\",\"Minerals are solid crystals with a definite chemical composition and crystal structure; ores are minerals that contain a valuable metal in sufficient quantity to be mined economically.\",\"Minerals are classified as non‑metallic (e.g., quartz, mica) and metallic (e.g., iron, copper).\",\"Minerals can be formed by crystallisation from magma, by precipitation from water, or by transformation of organic matter (e.g., coal, oil, gas).\",\"Coal is a fossil fuel formed from buried plant material under pressure and heat over millions of years.\",\"Mineral oil (petroleum) and natural gas are formed from the decomposition of ancient marine organisms under high pressure and temperature.\",\"Fossil fuels are non‑renewable energy sources that release carbon dioxide when burnt, contributing to climate change.\",\"Forests are large areas covered by trees and plants; they provide timber, food, medicine, and protect soil and water resources.\",\"Marine resources include dissolved minerals (e.g., sodium, potassium), marine life (fish, shellfish), and hydrocarbons (oil, gas) found in seawater and seabed.\",\"Energy can be harnessed from oceans by tidal power, wave power and ocean currents.\",\"Conservation of forests involves protecting young trees, planting more trees than are cut, and enforcing strict regulations.\",\"Medicinal plants such as neem, tulsi, and cinnamon have therapeutic uses like treating cough, fever and cancer.\",\"The economic condition of a nation depends on its natural resources because they provide raw materials, energy, and employment opportunities.\",\"Alternative fuels such as hydrogen, biofuels and methanol are being explored as fossil fuels become scarce.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the earth\'s lithosphere made up of according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Land and the hard crust beneath it\",\"Only water and air\",\"Plants and animals\",\"Only mineral oil and fuels\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the earth’s lithosphere is made up of land and the hard crust beneath it.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions that the lithosphere is \'not homogeneous\'. What does this imply about its composition?\",\"options\":[\"It is made of only one type of mineral\",\"It is made up of many types of rocks\",\"It is perfectly smooth and uniform\",\"It consists only of water and soil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the lithosphere is not homogeneous because it is made up of many types of rocks.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how are minerals formed?\",\"options\":[\"By human manufacturing in factories\",\"By various processes taking place in the environment\",\"By the purification of gangue\",\"By mixing sand and soil together\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that minerals are formed by various processes taking place in the environment.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance is obtained from nature and satisfies a daily need, what is it called?\",\"options\":[\"An element\",\"A natural resource\",\"A homogeneous rock\",\"Gangue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Natural resources are defined as substances from nature that satisfy a variety of our daily needs.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is specifically listed as a resource found in the earth\'s crust?\",\"options\":[\"Birds\",\"Mineral oil\",\"Clouds\",\"Sunlight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists mineral oil as one of the resources found in the earth\'s crust.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the two processes mentioned to obtain metals from their ore?\",\"options\":[\"Heating and cooling\",\"Extraction and purification\",\"Mixing and grinding\",\"Filtering and evaporation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure states that metals are obtained from their ore by extraction and purification.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific term used for the impurities of sand and soil found in an ore?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral\",\"Metal\",\"Gangue\",\"Crust\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure defines \'gangue\' as the impurities of sand and soil in an ore.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which question is raised regarding the classification of minerals?\",\"options\":[\"Why are all minerals not ores?\",\"How are minerals formed in the crust?\",\"What is the weight of a mineral?\",\"Where can we find mineral oil?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure asks the brain power question: \'Why are all minerals not ores?\'\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/114_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does mineral wealth have an important place among natural resources?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is the only resource in the lithosphere\",\"Because minerals are the only substances that satisfy daily needs\",\"Because it is a significant part of the resources found in the earth\'s crust\",\"Because minerals are not formed by environmental processes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that mineral wealth has an important place among natural resources found in the crust.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a natural resource in the introductory paragraph?\",\"options\":[\"Stones\",\"Air\",\"Plastic\",\"Animals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Soil, stones, minerals, air, water, plants and animals are listed; plastic is not a natural resource.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A mining engineer finds a rock containing iron and a lot of sand. To get pure iron, what must be removed according to the terminology in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The lithosphere\",\"The gangue\",\"The mineral oil\",\"The elements\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sand and soil impurities in an ore are called gangue and must be removed through purification.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If we categorize substances based on their location, which of these belongs to the \'earth\'s crust\' group?\",\"options\":[\"Atmospheric air\",\"Ores\",\"Rainwater\",\"Forest animals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically lists ores as resources in the earth\'s crust.\",\"topic\":\"Natural resources in the earth’s crust\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are the rocks on the earth mainly made of?\",\"options\":[\"Soil and sand\",\"Minerals\",\"Pure metals\",\"Gangue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that rocks on the earth are mainly made of minerals.\",\"topic\":\"Minerals and ores\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following metals occurs in the free state in nature?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Aluminium\",\"Bismuth\",\"Sodium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions gold, silver, copper, platinum, and bismuth as metals that occur in the free state.\",\"topic\":\"Minerals a