figure, where did the organisms that eventually formed mineral oil and natural gas live millions of years ago?\",\"options\":[\"In deep underground caves\",\"In the sea\",\"On high mountains\",\"In dense forests\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first panel of the figure shows sea creatures like fish and sharks in water millions of years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/117_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Coal is primarily considered a storehouse of which element?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Carbon\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that coal is, in a way, a storehouse of carbon.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following gaseous fuels are obtained from coal?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen and Hydrogen\",\"Methane and Ethane\",\"Producer gas and water gas\",\"LPG and CNG\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that producer gas and water gas are obtained from coal.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A brick kiln owner needs a fuel to bake bricks on a large scale. Based on the text, which resource would be most suitable?\",\"options\":[\"Natural gas\",\"Coal\",\"Mineral oil\",\"Water gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions coal is used for baking bricks in kilns on a large scale.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the process of coal formation described, what was the original source of the energy stored in coal?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\'s interior heat\",\"Ancient forests and plants\",\"Soil minerals\",\"High pressure from rocks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Coal was formed from the remains of plants that were buried millions of years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT listed as a use of coal in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Running railway engines\",\"Generating energy in thermal power plants\",\"Fuel for cooking\",\"Lubricating heavy machinery\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists power plants, boilers, railway engines, cooking, and brick kilns, but not lubrication.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In what physical states can fuels be found according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only solid and liquid\",\"Only liquid and gaseous\",\"Solid, liquid or gaseous\",\"Only solid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that fuels are found in the solid, liquid or gaseous state.\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we compare the formation of coal with the formation of mineral oil as shown in the text and figures, what is a major difference in their origin?\",\"options\":[\"Coal comes from plant remains, while mineral oil comes from marine organisms\",\"Coal takes millions of years, while mineral oil takes only a few days\",\"Coal requires heat, while mineral oil requires cold temperatures\",\"Coal is found on the surface, while mineral oil is found in the sky\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says coal is formed from plants; the figure for mineral oil shows sea creatures (marine organisms).\",\"topic\":\"Fuel and Coal\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"On which date was the Oil and Natural Gas Corporation (ONGC) established?\",\"options\":[\"15th August 1947\",\"14th August 1956\",\"26th January 1950\",\"1st May 1960\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, ONGC was established on 14th August 1956.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of India\'s crude oil is produced by ONGC?\",\"options\":[\"62%\",\"50%\",\"77%\",\"90%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that ONGC produces about 77% of the crude oil produced in India.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what depth is mineral oil mainly found underground?\",\"options\":[\"100 to 500 metres\",\"5000 to 7000 metres\",\"1000 to 3000 metres\",\"10 to 50 metres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions mineral oil is found at a depth of about 1000 to 3000 metres.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic colour of petroleum?\",\"options\":[\"Jet black\",\"Greenish brown\",\"Golden yellow\",\"Dark red\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that petroleum is greenish brown in colour.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is the main component of natural gas?\",\"options\":[\"Ethane (C2H6)\",\"Propane (C3H8)\",\"Butane (C4H10)\",\"Methane (CH4)\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the main component of natural gas is methane (CH4).\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How was mineral oil formed millions of years ago?\",\"options\":[\"By the freezing of sea water\",\"By the decomposition of organic substances buried underground\",\"By the melting of volcanic rocks\",\"By the evaporation of ancient lakes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mineral oil is formed by the decomposition of organic substances (remains of sea organisms) buried under layers of soil and sand.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is petroleum referred to as a mixture of \'hydrocarbon\' type compounds?\",\"options\":[\"It is made only of water and carbon\",\"It contains mainly compounds of hydrogen and carbon\",\"It is found only in hydrogen-rich soil\",\"It is produced by hydrating carbon rocks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hydrocarbons are compounds primarily made of hydrogen and carbon, which are the main components of petroleum.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which process is used to separate the various components of petroleum like diesel and kerosene?\",\"options\":[\"Filtration\",\"Evaporation\",\"Fractional distillation\",\"Sedimentation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states petroleum is refined by fractional distillation to separate its components.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to natural gas when a network of pipelines is not available for transport?\",\"options\":[\"It is released into the atmosphere\",\"It is converted into solid coal\",\"It is transformed into CNG or LNG under high pressure\",\"It is mixed with water to flow through open channels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the absence of pipelines, natural gas is transformed into CNG or LNG under high pressure to make transport possible.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A manufacturer wants to produce \'artificial fibres\'. Which of these substances from the text will they need as a raw material?\",\"options\":[\"Limestone\",\"Natural gas\",\"Petroleum\",\"Sandstone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that components obtained from petroleum are used for the production of artificial fibres.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a city switches all its buses to CNG, what would be a direct environmental benefit based on its characteristics?\",\"options\":[\"The buses will produce more water than fuel\",\"No solid waste will remain after combustion\",\"The buses will not require any oxygen to run\",\"The buses will produce large amounts of carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One characteristic of CNG mentioned is that no solid waste remains after combustion.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these products is NOT listed in the text as being obtained from petroleum refining?\",\"options\":[\"Aviation petrol\",\"Lubricating oil\",\"Naphtha\",\"Natural gas\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Aviation petrol, lubricating oil, and naphtha are listed as petroleum products; natural gas is found associated with it but is a separate fossil fuel.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are the two main factors that transformed the remains of dead organisms into mineral oil?\",\"options\":[\"High pressure and temperature\",\"Low pressure and sunlight\",\"Oxygen and nitrogen\",\"Water and sand\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to high pressure and temperature, the remains were transformed into mineral oil.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical formulas provided in the text, which gas contains the highest number of carbon atoms per molecule?\",\"options\":[\"Methane (CH4)\",\"Ethane (C2H6)\",\"Propane (C3H8)\",\"Butane (C4H10)\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Butane (C4H10) has 4 carbon atoms, which is more than Methane (1), Ethane (2), or Propane (3).\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is CNG considered a fuel that is easy to manage in vehicles compared to other fuels?\",\"options\":[\"It is the only fuel that does not catch fire\",\"Its combustion can be controlled easily\",\"It does not produce any carbon dioxide at all\",\"It is found in liquid form naturally\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One of the listed characteristics of CNG is that its combustion can be controlled easily.\",\"topic\":\"Mineral oil and Natural gas\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What are the preserved remains of dead organisms found in rocks called?\",\"options\":[\"Minerals\",\"Fossils\",\"Crystals\",\"Ores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, fossils are the preserved remains of dead organisms in rock.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is commonly referred to as \'black gold\' in the context of mineral resources?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral oil\",\"Natural gas\",\"Coal\",\"LPG\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the \'brain power\' question asking why coal is called \'black gold\'.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two main components of Liquified Petroleum Gas (LPG)?\",\"options\":[\"Methane and Ethane\",\"Propane and Butane\",\"Methanol and Ethanol\",\"Hydrogen and Oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that LPG contains mainly two components, propane and butane.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the other name for methanol mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Green alcohol\",\"Liquid gold\",\"Wood alcohol\",\"Petroleum gas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies methanol as \'wood alcohol\'.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is known as \'green alcohol\'?\",\"options\":[\"Ethanol\",\"Methanol\",\"Propane\",\"Butane\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies ethanol as \'green alcohol\'.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a small amount of \'ethyl mercaptan\' added to LPG?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the fuel efficiency\",\"To help detect gas leakage\",\"To keep the gas in a liquid state\",\"To change the color of the gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ethyl mercaptan has a strong odour that helps detect leakage immediately to avoid accidents.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to petroleum gas when it is subjected to high pressure during the refining process?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into a solid\",\"It evaporates completely\",\"It transforms into a liquid\",\"It turns into ethyl mercaptan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states petroleum gas is transformed into a liquid by subjecting it to high pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'energy crisis\' refer to in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The high cost of ethyl mercaptan\",\"The smell of leaking LPG cylinders\",\"The likelihood of fossil fuel reserves getting exhausted\",\"The process of refining crude petroleum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The likelihood of reserves getting exhausted due to high demand and limited supply is known as the energy crisis.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where can impressions of organisms sometimes be seen?\",\"options\":[\"Only in steel cylinders\",\"On the surfaces of coal and stones\",\"Inside liquid petroleum\",\"In alternative fuels like ethanol\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that impressions of organisms are sometimes seen on the surfaces of coal and stones.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you have 240 liters of petroleum gas, what will its volume be once it is liquified for storage in an LPG cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"240 liters\",\"120 liters\",\"10 liters\",\"1 liter\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states the volume is reduced to 1\\/240 of the original; 240 \\/ 240 = 1.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a 100 kg mixture of LPG, based on the ratio provided in the text, how many kilograms would be butane?\",\"options\":[\"30 kg\",\"70 kg\",\"50 kg\",\"240 kg\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The ratio of propane to butane is 30:70, meaning 70% of the mixture is butane.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A person notices a strong, characteristic smell near a gas stove. Which chemical is responsible for this warning sign?\",\"options\":[\"Propane\",\"Butane\",\"Ethyl mercaptan\",\"Methanol\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"LPG is odourless, so ethyl mercaptan is added specifically to provide a detectable smell for safety.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is LPG stored specifically in \'thick-walled steel cylinders\'?\",\"options\":[\"To prevent the ethyl mercaptan from evaporating\",\"To maintain the high pressure needed to keep the gas liquid\",\"To make the cylinder easier to carry\",\"To allow the gas to expand to 240 times its volume\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Thick walls are necessary to withstand the high pressure required to keep the gas in a liquid state.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are alternative fuels like hydrogen and biofuels becoming necessary?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are cheaper than coal\",\"Because fossil fuel reserves are limited while demand is increasing\",\"Because they can be stored in thin-walled plastic bottles\",\"Because they contain more butane than LPG\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the use of alternative fuels to the limited reserves of fossil fuels and the increasing demand.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary reason LPG is considered a transformed state of petroleum gas?\",\"options\":[\"It has a different chemical formula than propane\",\"It has been mixed with coal to become \'black gold\'\",\"Its volume has been significantly reduced under pressure\",\"It has been buried for billions of years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The transformation involves subjecting the gas to high pressure and reducing its volume to 1\\/240.\",\"topic\":\"Fossils and LPG\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of the total land of the world is covered by forests?\",\"options\":[\"About 20%\",\"About 30%\",\"About 40%\",\"About 50%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that about 30% of the total land of the world is covered by forests.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a forest?\",\"options\":[\"A small garden with decorative plants\",\"A farm used for growing only food crops\",\"An extensive area of land covered by a variety of plants\",\"A place where only microbes can survive\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a forest as an extensive area of land covered by a variety of plants.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a protective function of forests?\",\"options\":[\"Providing wood for furniture\",\"Producing fragrant essential oils\",\"Reducing the rate of evaporation\",\"Supplying honey and sealing wax\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Reducing the rate of evaporation is listed as one of the seven protective functions of forests.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which medicinal plant is used for the treatment of Malaria?\",\"options\":[\"Adulsa\",\"Periwinkle\",\"Cinchona\",\"Bel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Cinchona as the medicinal plant used for Malaria.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is suffering from a cough and cold, which plant mentioned in the text would be most helpful?\",\"options\":[\"Periwinkle\",\"Adulsa\",\"Cinnamon\",\"Cinchona\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Adulsa is specifically mentioned for the treatment of cough and cold.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following trees provides strong and durable wood suitable for construction work?\",\"options\":[\"Lemon grass\",\"Vanilla\",\"Teak\",\"Kewada\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions teak, mahogany, neem, acacia, and subabhul as sources of strong and durable wood.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How do forests help in preventing soil erosion?\",\"options\":[\"By increasing the rate of evaporation\",\"By reducing the velocity of water flowing over the land\",\"By providing aromatic substances\",\"By producing sealing wax\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Reducing the velocity of water flowing over the land helps prevent soil erosion.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a recommended method to conserve forests?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting down young trees for firewood\",\"Planting more trees than are cut down\",\"Ignoring regulations regarding forest use\",\"Clearing forests for farming implements\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests planting and looking after many more trees than are cut down as a conservation method.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"From which of these plants do we obtain fragrant essential oils?\",\"options\":[\"Acacia\",\"Subabhul\",\"Vetiver (Khas)\",\"Bel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists vetiver (Khas) as a source of fragrant essential oils.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Sandalwood and oil of eucalyptus are primarily used in the production of which items?\",\"options\":[\"Farming implements\",\"Soaps and incense sticks\",\"Rubber and gum\",\"Paper and fibres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that sandalwood and oil of eucalyptus are used for making soaps, cosmetics, and incense sticks.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a productive function of forests?\",\"options\":[\"Providing medicinal plants\",\"Supplying wood for furniture\",\"Protecting wildlife\",\"Yielding aromatic substances\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Protecting wildlife is a protective function, whereas the others are productive functions.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which medicinal plant is used in the treatment of cancer?\",\"options\":[\"Neem\",\"Periwinkle\",\"Cinnamon\",\"Adulsa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists Periwinkle as a medicinal plant used for cancer.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'percolation of water into the ground\' considered a protective function?\",\"options\":[\"It helps in making furniture\",\"It helps improve the quality of the environment by managing water resources\",\"It provides dyes and catechu\",\"It is used for making incense sticks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Percolation helps manage water and maintain environmental quality, which is a protective role.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which numbered point describes the role of forests in maintaining the balance of atmospheric gases?\",\"options\":[\"Point 2\",\"Point 4\",\"Point 5\",\"Point 7\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Point 7 in the chart specifically mentions maintaining the balance of atmospheric gases.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/119_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table in the given figure, which plant should be used to treat a patient suffering from nausea?\",\"options\":[\"Neem\",\"Cinnamon\",\"Cinchona\",\"Bel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists Cinnamon for the treatment of diarrhea and nausea.\",\"topic\":\"Forest resources\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/120_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What name is given to the variety of natural resources found in seawater, at the bottom of the sea, and beneath the seabed?\",\"options\":[\"Earth resources\",\"Marine resources\",\"Seabed minerals\",\"Aquatic bio-fuels\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines resources available from seas and oceans as marine resources.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the mineral oil well first drilled at Bombay High by the drill-ship \'Sagar Samrat\'?\",\"options\":[\"1964\",\"1984\",\"1974\",\"1954\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the mineral oil well was drilled by \'Sagar Samrat\' in 1974.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which mineral obtained from the ocean is used in the production of atomic energy?\",\"options\":[\"Magnesium\",\"Thorium\",\"Potassium\",\"Sodium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists Thorium as a mineral resource used in the production of atomic energy.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is energy obtained on a large scale from the oceans?\",\"options\":[\"By burning seawater\",\"From sea waves and ocean currents\",\"By extracting salt from the water\",\"From the heat of the seabed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that sea waves at high and low tide and ocean currents are used for power generation.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how does the value of real pearls compare to gold?\",\"options\":[\"They are cheaper than gold\",\"They have the same value as gold\",\"They are even costlier than gold\",\"They are not as useful as gold\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that real pearls are even costlier than gold.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which marine bio-resource is used for the production of antibiotics?\",\"options\":[\"Fungi\",\"Sea cucumbers\",\"Shark fish\",\"Shells\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that fungi are used for the production of antibiotics.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a factory needs to produce soap and glass, which marine mineral would be the main ingredient required?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium\",\"Sulphate\",\"Potassium\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Potassium is the main ingredient in the production of soap, glass, and fertilizer.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer wants to find a good manure that can also be used as poultry feed. Which marine resource should they choose?\",\"options\":[\"Sea cucumbers\",\"Bombay duck powder\",\"Shark oil\",\"Shell powder\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dried shrimp and Bombay duck powder are used as poultry feed and are good manure.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A textile manufacturer wants to produce artificial silk. Which ocean mineral is specifically mentioned for this purpose?\",\"options\":[\"Thorium\",\"Sodium\",\"Sulphate\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Sulphate is used in making artificial silk.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Natural gas obtained from the Bombay High oilfield is carried via a pipeline to which specific location?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Uran\",\"Sagar Samrat\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that natural gas from this well is carried via a pipeline to a place called Uran.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is categorized as a marine occupation in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Agriculture\",\"Salt farming\",\"Forestry\",\"Mining on land\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Salt farming is listed as one of the five marine occupations.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary to keep a control on the use of natural resources according to the \'Always remember\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are found everywhere\",\"To increase the cost of resources\",\"To maintain the balance in nature and prevent depletion\",\"Because oceans occupy less space than land\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that excessive use leads to depletion, so control is needed to maintain nature\'s balance.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which ocean resource is specifically identified as a medicine for treating cancer and tumours?\",\"options\":[\"Cod fish\",\"Sea cucumbers\",\"Pomfret\",\"Fungi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states sea cucumbers are used as medicine for treating cancer and tumours.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the dark, cylindrical organisms shown in the middle right are used for which purpose?\",\"options\":[\"Producing edible oil\",\"Treating cancer and tumours\",\"Manufacturing soap\",\"Making artificial silk\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows sea cucumbers, which the text says are used for treating cancer and tumours.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/121_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the fish shown at the very top (Pomfret) is primarily a source of which nutrients?\",\"options\":[\"Carbohydrates and Fats\",\"Proteins and Vitamins\",\"Antibiotics and Minerals\",\"Atomic energy and Oil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies fishes like pomfret as sources of proteins and vitamins.\",\"topic\":\"Ocean resources\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/121_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22002,46,7,'Science','en','Effects of Light','[\"Light is a form of energy that travels in waves.\",\"A beam of light can be seen when it is scattered by particles in the air or in a liquid.\",\"Scattering of light is the redirection of light rays by tiny particles or molecules.\",\"Blue light is scattered more than red light because its wavelength is shorter.\",\"The sky appears blue during the day because blue sunlight is scattered in all directions.\",\"At sunrise and sunset the light passes through a longer path in the atmosphere, so more blue light is scattered and the sky looks red or orange.\",\"A shadow is the dark area produced when an opaque object blocks light.\",\"The dark part of a shadow is called the umbra.\",\"The lighter part of a shadow, where only part of the light is blocked, is called the penumbra.\",\"A point light source produces a sharp, well‑defined shadow with a clear umbra.\",\"An extended light source produces a softer shadow with both a penumbra and an umbra.\",\"During a solar eclipse the moon passes between the sun and the earth, casting the moon’s shadow on the earth.\",\"During a lunar eclipse the earth passes between the sun and the moon, casting the earth’s shadow on the moon.\",\"The zero‑shadow day occurs when the sun is directly overhead and shadows disappear at noon.\",\"Eclipses can be total, partial or annular depending on how the shadows overlap.\",\"Observing a solar eclipse with the naked eye can damage the eyes; special glasses are required.\",\"The colour of a laser beam is visible only when it is scattered by particles.\",\"The colour of the sky is a result of the scattering of sunlight by air molecules and dust.\",\"The blue colour of the sky is due to Rayleigh scattering.\",\"The red colour of the sunset is due to the scattering of blue light and the direct transmission of red light.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is light composed of, as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A single white colour\",\"Only two primary colours\",\"Several colours\",\"No colours at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that light is composed of several colours, which is seen when it passes through a prism.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What do we actually see when a beam of sunlight enters a house through a small window?\",\"options\":[\"Tiny water droplets\",\"Tiny dust particles\",\"Small air bubbles\",\"Microscopic insects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that we see dust particles in a beam of sunlight entering through a window.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why are we able to see a beam of light?\",\"options\":[\"Because light is very bright\",\"Because we see tiny dust particles floating in it\",\"Because the light travels in a straight line\",\"Because the room is completely dark\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that we see tiny dust particles floating in the beam, and that is why we are able to see the beam.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is observed on the other side of a glass prism when sunrays pass through it?\",\"options\":[\"A dark shadow\",\"A band of colours\",\"A reflection of the window\",\"A beam of pure white light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to recall that a band of light (colours) is seen on the other side of a prism.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When driving through a thick fog, what action helps us see the beams of light?\",\"options\":[\"Opening the car windows\",\"Switching on the head lamps\",\"Turning off the engine\",\"Looking at the sky\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we switch on the head lamps of a car when driving through thick fog to see the beams.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to demonstrate that light is composed of several colours, which object mentioned in the text should they use?\",\"options\":[\"A small window\",\"A car head lamp\",\"A glass prism\",\"A satellite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the example of sunrays passing through a glass prism to show light is composed of several colours.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is common between seeing a beam of light in a window and seeing headlamp beams in fog?\",\"options\":[\"Both require a glass prism\",\"Both involve seeing particles in the path of light\",\"Both only happen at night\",\"Both show the earth is bluish\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In both cases, particles (dust or fog) make the beam of light visible to us.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions the Earth appears bluish from space. This phenomenon is an example of:\",\"options\":[\"A chemical reaction\",\"An effect of light\",\"A satellite error\",\"A change in Earth\'s shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists the bluish appearance of Earth as one of the \'effects\' of light.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what variety of shades are we likely to see according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only bright white light\",\"Shades of colour in the early morning and evening sky\",\"A band of colours from a prism\",\"Dust particles in a room\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions seeing a variety of shades of colour in the early morning and evening sky, which the figure illustrates.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how does the Earth appear in photographs taken from space by satellites?\",\"options\":[\"Greenish\",\"Reddish\",\"Bluish\",\"Yellowish\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that in photographs taken from space by satellites, the earth appears to be bluish.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/123_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following observations from the text best supports the idea that light interacts with matter to become visible?\",\"options\":[\"Light is composed of several colours\",\"Earth appears bluish from space\",\"Seeing dust particles floating in a beam of light\",\"A prism creates a band of light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that we see the beam because we see tiny dust particles floating in it.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"At what times of the day does the text mention seeing a variety of shades of colour in the sky?\",\"options\":[\"At noon and midnight\",\"Early morning and evening\",\"Only during the night\",\"Only during rainy afternoons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that we see a variety of shades of colour in the early morning and evening sky.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should be used to add milk or milk powder to the water in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"A spoon\",\"A dropper\",\"A glass rod\",\"A beaker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure mentions using a dropper to add a few drops of milk or milk powder to the water.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Under whose supervision should all experiments using laser rays be performed?\",\"options\":[\"Parents\",\"Friends\",\"Teacher\",\"Principal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that experiments using laser rays should be done under the supervision of the teacher.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the procedure after taking clear water in a glass beaker?\",\"options\":[\"Add milk powder\",\"Stir with a spoon\",\"Pass a beam of laser rays through it\",\"Make the water turbid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure starts by passing a beam of laser rays through clear water to check if the beam is seen.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the appearance of clear water when a few drops of milk are added?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes transparent\",\"It becomes turbid\",\"It becomes colorful\",\"It becomes oily\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that adding milk will cause the water to be seen as slightly turbid.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a light beam not seen when passed through plain water?\",\"options\":[\"Plain water absorbs all light\",\"Plain water reflects light back to the laser\",\"There are no particles to scatter the light rays\",\"The laser light is too weak\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Light is only seen when it hits particles and gets scattered; plain water lacks these particles.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What occurs when tiny laser rays hit the tiny particles of milk?\",\"options\":[\"The rays disappear\",\"The rays get scattered\",\"The rays turn into water\",\"The rays heat the milk\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that tiny rays hit the tiny particles of milk and get scattered.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, when do we perceive the light?\",\"options\":[\"When the laser is turned off\",\"When the water is perfectly clear\",\"When scattered rays enter our eyes\",\"When the milk powder dissolves completely\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'If these scattered rays enter our eyes, we perceive the light.\'\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to make a laser beam visible in a liquid, what must they ensure about the liquid?\",\"options\":[\"It must be completely clear\",\"It must be slightly turbid\",\"It must be boiling\",\"It must be in a metal container\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that the beam is clearly seen in slightly turbid water, not plain water.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student passes a laser through a beaker but cannot see the beam inside. What should they add to make it visible?\",\"options\":[\"More clear water\",\"A few drops of milk\",\"A larger laser pointer\",\"A clean glass rod\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Adding milk makes the water turbid, which scatters the light and makes the beam visible.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is represented by the red line seen inside the beaker?\",\"options\":[\"A glass rod\",\"A light beam showing the existence of light rays\",\"A crack in the glass beaker\",\"A stream of milk being poured\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a laser beam passing through the liquid, which the text identifies as a light beam showing the existence of light rays.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/123_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the liquid in the beaker were clear water instead of turbid water, how would the image change?\",\"options\":[\"The laser pointer would turn red\",\"The red line inside the beaker would not be visible\",\"The beaker would disappear\",\"The hand would not be seen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a light beam is not seen in plain water, so the red line representing the beam would disappear.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/123_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the milk particles and the visibility of the laser beam?\",\"options\":[\"Milk particles block the light completely\",\"Milk particles act as the source of light\",\"Milk particles scatter the light rays to make the beam visible\",\"Milk particles change the color of the laser rays\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that rays hit the milk particles and get scattered, allowing us to see the beam.\",\"topic\":\"Scattering of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the entire sky to appear bright when the sun rises?\",\"options\":[\"The reflection of light from the moon\",\"The scattering of sunlight by tiny particles in the air\",\"The heat generated by the sun\",\"The presence of clouds in the sky\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the sky appears bright due to the scattering of sunlight by dust and other tiny particles in the air.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following are mentioned as constituents of air that scatter sunlight?\",\"options\":[\"Large birds and insects\",\"Raindrops and hail\",\"Dust and other tiny particles\",\"Oxygen and Nitrogen gas only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions dust and other tiny particles as the constituents responsible for scattering.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What would be the appearance of the sky during the day if Earth had no atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"It would appear bright blue\",\"It would appear dark\",\"It would appear white\",\"It would appear yellow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that had there been no atmosphere on earth, the sky would have appeared dark during the day.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How has the observation of a dark sky in the absence of an atmosphere been verified?\",\"options\":[\"By using high-powered telescopes from Earth\",\"By mixing salt and sugar in water\",\"By observations from rockets and satellites\",\"By using a laser beam in a laboratory\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that this has been verified by observations from rockets and satellites which go out of the earth\'s atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Try this\' experiment, what is the purpose of mixing salt or detergent in water before using a laser beam?\",\"options\":[\"To change the color of the water\",\"To see if a laser beam is visible in them through scattering\",\"To make the water drinkable\",\"To dissolve the laser beam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment asks to observe whether a laser beam is seen in these mixtures, which tests for scattering properties.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an astronaut is standing on the Moon (which has no atmosphere), what would they observe about the sky during the day?\",\"options\":[\"The sky would be illuminated like on Earth\",\"The sky would appear dark\",\"The sky would be filled with bright clouds\",\"The sun would not be visible at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Applying the text\'s logic, no atmosphere means no scattering, resulting in a dark sky.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment described, if a laser beam is visible in a detergent solution but not in a salt solution, what can we conclude?\",\"options\":[\"Salt is brighter than detergent\",\"Detergent particles scatter light while salt particles (in that state) do not\",\"The laser beam only works in soapy water\",\"Salt water absorbs all the light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Visibility of the beam indicates scattering by the particles in the mixture.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the atmosphere and the visibility of the sun?\",\"options\":[\"The atmosphere makes the sun invisible\",\"Without an atmosphere, the sun would be directly seen against a dark sky\",\"The atmosphere creates the sun\'s light\",\"The atmosphere has no effect on how we see the sun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that without an atmosphere, the sun would be directly seen and the sky would be dark.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the illumination of our surroundings happen even if we are not looking directly at the sun?\",\"options\":[\"Because light travels in a straight line only\",\"Because of the scattering of light by air particles in all directions\",\"Because the ground produces its own light\",\"Because salt and sugar in the air reflect light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Illumination of the surroundings and the bright sky is due to the scattering of sunlight by particles in the air.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which conclusion can be drawn from the fact that rockets and satellites see a dark sky outside the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Light cannot travel through space\",\"Scattering of light requires a medium like the atmosphere\",\"The sun does not shine in space\",\"Rockets block the sunlight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The observation confirms that without the particles in the atmosphere to scatter light, the sky does not appear bright.\",\"topic\":\"१७.२ प्रकाशाचे गुणधर्म\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, what type of bulb should be used in the table lamp?\",\"options\":[\"A clear LED bulb\",\"A 60 or 100 W milky bulb\",\"A fluorescent tube light\",\"A high power halogen bulb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions using a 60 or 100 W milky bulb and states that an LED will not do.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the recommended diameter of the hole to be pricked in the black paper?\",\"options\":[\"5 to 10 mm\",\"1 to 2 cm\",\"1 to 2 mm\",\"0.5 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure instructions state to prick a hole of 1 to 2 mm diameter in the center of the paper.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is mentioned in the apparatus for pricking the hole in the black paper?\",\"options\":[\"A pair of scissors\",\"A packing needle\",\"A compass\",\"A sharp pencil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The apparatus list and procedure specify using a packing needle to prick the hole.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the purpose of covering the table lamp with black paper?\",\"options\":[\"To prevent the bulb from overheating\",\"To change the color of the light to black\",\"To allow light to pass only through a small hole\",\"To make the beaker appear darker\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The black paper covers the lamphouse so that light only emerges from the 1-2 mm hole pricked in it.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is milk or milk powder added to the clear water in the beaker?\",\"options\":[\"To change the temperature of the water\",\"To make the water turbid\",\"To make the water transparent\",\"To clean the beaker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that milk is added to make the water turbid, which is necessary for scattering to occur.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What color tinge is observed when the turbid water is viewed at an angle of 90°?\",\"options\":[\"Red tinge\",\"Yellow tinge\",\"Green tinge\",\"Blue tinge\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The experiment notes that a blue tinge is seen when observed along the 90° angle.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the dashed line pointing downwards at an angle from the center of the beaker represents which phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"Reflected white light\",\"Scattered blue light\",\"Absorbed heat\",\"Shadow of the beaker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram illustrates the scattering of light, and the text explains that the light seen at an angle is the scattered blue light.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What color does the hole appear when observed from the front after adding milk to the water?\",\"options\":[\"Reddish\",\"Bluish\",\"Pure white\",\"Pitch black\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that because blue light is scattered, the hole appears reddish from the front.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student performs the experiment in a brightly lit room instead of a dark room, what is the most likely result?\",\"options\":[\"The scattering effects will be easier to see\",\"The blue tinge will be much brighter\",\"The scattered light may not be clearly visible\",\"The water will turn red immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment specifies it should be done in a dark room to ensure the faint scattered light is visible.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a red-yellow light seen from the front of the beaker?\",\"options\":[\"Because the blue light has been scattered away\",\"Because the milk powder is naturally red\",\"Because the black paper filters out all other colors\",\"Because the water absorbs red light\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a red-yellow light is seen from the front because the blue light is scattered.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what can be concluded about the scattering of light in turbid water?\",\"options\":[\"Only red light is scattered at 90 degrees\",\"Blue light is scattered more easily than red-yellow light\",\"Scattering only happens if an LED bulb is used\",\"Clear water scatters light better than turbid water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The observation of a blue tinge at 90 degrees and reddish light in front indicates blue light is scattered out of the direct path.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Under which condition is the experiment on scattering of light instructed to be performed?\",\"options\":[\"In direct sunlight\",\"In a dark room\",\"In a large open field\",\"Under a bright fluorescent ceiling light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Important\' note at the end of the text states this experiment should be done in a dark room.\",\"topic\":\"Experiment: Scattering of light\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which gases in the atmosphere are mentioned as scattering sunlight?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen and Oxygen\",\"Hydrogen and Helium\",\"Carbon dioxide and Argon\",\"Neon and Xenon\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that sunlight is scattered by molecules of gases like nitrogen and oxygen in the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which color in sunlight is scattered the most by the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Red\",\"Yellow\",\"Blue\",\"Green\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the blue colour in the sunlight is scattered the most.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the sky appear blue to us?\",\"options\":[\"Because blue light is absorbed by the atmosphere\",\"Because blue light is scattered the most by gas molecules\",\"Because the sun only emits blue light during the day\",\"Because the ocean reflects blue light into the sky\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The sky appears blue because the blue colour in sunlight is scattered the most by atmospheric gases.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the scattering of red light compare to that of blue light?\",\"options\":[\"Red light is scattered more than blue light\",\"Red light is scattered less than blue light\",\"Red light is scattered equally to blue light\",\"Red light is not scattered at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that red light is scattered less than blue light.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment, what happens to the reddish color seen from the front when a few more drops of milk are added?\",\"options\":[\"It disappears immediately\",\"It turns into a blue color\",\"It becomes an intense red\",\"It turns into a pale yellow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, adding a few more drops makes the reddish colour seen from the front an intense red.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of adding \'many more\' drops of milk in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The reddish color becomes even brighter\",\"The reddish color is not seen\",\"The water becomes completely transparent\",\"The light turns green\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if many more drops are added, the reddish colour is not seen.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"At sunset, what change occurs in the path of sunlight reaching us?\",\"options\":[\"It travels a shorter distance through the atmosphere\",\"It travels a greater distance through the atmosphere\",\"It does not pass through the atmosphere at all\",\"It travels through a vacuum only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that at sunset, the light reaching us travels a greater distance through the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which colors reach us directly at sunset due to the scattering of other colors?\",\"options\":[\"Blue and Violet\",\"Green and Blue\",\"Red-yellow\",\"White and Silver\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that as a result of scattering, red-yellow light reaches us directly at sunset.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the sun appear red at sunset?\",\"options\":[\"Because blue light is scattered more due to the greater distance\",\"Because the sun actually changes its temperature\",\"Because the atmosphere disappears at night\",\"Because red light is scattered the most at that time\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Due to the greater distance at sunset, there is more scattering of blue, leaving red-yellow light to reach us.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary cause of light scattering mentioned in the topic?\",\"options\":[\"Reflection from clouds\",\"Molecules of gases like nitrogen and oxygen\",\"The distance between the Earth and Moon\",\"The presence of liquid water in the air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states sunlight is scattered by the molecules of gases like nitrogen and oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the distance light travels through the atmosphere increases, what happens to the blue light?\",\"options\":[\"More of the blue light is scattered\",\"Less of the blue light is scattered\",\"The blue light turns into red light\",\"The blue light reaches us more directly\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the greater distance, there is more scattering of the blue colour.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why does the sun look red at sunset but the sky looks blue during the day?\",\"options\":[\"Because the distance light travels through the atmosphere changes\",\"Because the sun moves closer to the Earth at sunset\",\"Because nitrogen disappears at sunset\",\"Because oxygen only scatters light in the morning\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text links the change in color to the greater distance light travels through the atmosphere at sunset compared to other times.\",\"topic\":\"Use your brain power !\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is a light source called when light is seen to emerge from a tiny hole in a cardboard?\",\"options\":[\"Extended source\",\"Point source\",\"Pinhole source\",\"Faint source\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a light source emerging from a tiny hole (O) is called a point source.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The dark part of a shadow where no light rays reach the screen is known as the...\",\"options\":[\"Penumbra\",\"Pinhole\",\"Umbra\",\"Extended shadow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the dark shadow where no rays reach is called the umbra.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the rays OP and OQ represent?\",\"options\":[\"Rays that pass by the ball to form the edges of the shadow\",\"Rays that are absorbed by the cardboard\",\"Rays that form the penumbra\",\"Rays that reflect back to the candle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show rays OP and OQ starting from point source O, passing by the ball to fall on points A and B.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/125_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the shadow if the cardboard with the pinhole is removed from in front of the candle?\",\"options\":[\"The shadow disappears completely\",\"The source becomes an extended source\",\"The shadow becomes darker and sharper\",\"Only the umbra is formed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if the cardboard with the pinhole is removed, we no longer have a point source; it is called an extended source.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part of the shadow is represented by the faint region BC?\",\"options\":[\"The umbra\",\"The point source\",\"The penumbra\",\"The cardboard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and figure label the faint part BC as the penumbra.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/125_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When an extended source is used, how many distinct parts are seen in the shadow of the ball?\",\"options\":[\"One part (only umbra)\",\"One part (only penumbra)\",\"Two parts (faint and dark)\",\"Three parts (umbra, penumbra, and light)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the extended source, we see two parts in the shadow - one faint and one dark.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the ball is bigger than the extended source, what happens to the umbra and penumbra as the screen is moved further away?\",\"options\":[\"They both become smaller\",\"They both become bigger and bigger\",\"The umbra disappears\",\"The penumbra disappears\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says that if the ball is bigger than the source, as the screen moves further, the umbra and penumbra become bigger and bigger.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the dark central part of the shadow as the screen is moved further away from the small ball?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes larger and larger\",\"It stays the same size\",\"It becomes smaller and smaller until it disappears\",\"It turns into a point source\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show that for a small object, as the screen moves further, the umbra becomes smaller and eventually disappears.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/125_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the dark part AD formed by an extended source?\",\"options\":[\"Penumbra\",\"Umbra\",\"Point source\",\"Screen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the dark part AD as the umbra.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the area between points A and B on the screen remain unlit in the point source experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Because the screen is too far\",\"Because no rays reach the screen between those points\",\"Because the light source is too weak\",\"Because the ball reflects all the light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that since no rays reach the screen between points A and B, that part remains unlit.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the faint part of a shadow called?\",\"options\":[\"Umbra\",\"Pinhole\",\"Penumbra\",\"Extended source\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the faint part of the shadow as the penumbra.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Under what condition does the umbra of an object eventually disappear as the screen is moved away?\",\"options\":[\"When the object is bigger than the light source\",\"When the object is smaller than the light source\",\"When a point source is used\",\"When the screen is moved closer to the object\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the ball is smaller in size than the light source, the umbra becomes smaller and disappears as the screen moves further.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which apparatus is used to create a point source from a candle in the described activity?\",\"options\":[\"A big ball\",\"A screen\",\"A cardboard with a tiny hole\",\"A torch\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes setting up a cardboard with a tiny hole (O) in front of the candle to create a point source.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nature of the shadow formed by a point source?\",\"options\":[\"It has both umbra and penumbra\",\"It is a dark shadow or umbra only\",\"It is a faint shadow or penumbra only\",\"It disappears when the screen is moved\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the shadow of a point source as the \'dark shadow or the umbra\' without mentioning a faint part.\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of light sources, what does \'O\' represent in the point source experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The object\",\"The umbra\",\"The tiny hole in the cardboard\",\"The screen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the tiny hole in the cardboard as (O).\",\"topic\":\"Shadows\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On which specific day can a solar eclipse be observed?\",\"options\":[\"Full moon night\",\"New moon day\",\"Every Monday\",\"Quarter moon day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a solar eclipse is seen only on a new moon day.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which harmful rays reach the Earth during a solar eclipse?\",\"options\":[\"X-rays\",\"Infra-red rays\",\"Ultra-violet rays\",\"Gamma rays\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that during a solar eclipse, ultra-violet rays which are harmful to us reach the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the condition for an eclipse to be said to have taken place?\",\"options\":[\"The sun and moon are at right angles\",\"The earth is closest to the sun\",\"The sun, moon, and earth come in a straight line\",\"The moon is farthest from the earth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"An eclipse occurs when the sun, the moon, and the earth come in a straight line.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the Solar Eclipse, which celestial body is positioned in the middle to cast a shadow?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\",\"Sun\",\"Moon\",\"Stars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the Moon between the Sun and the Earth during a solar eclipse.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/126_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is a \'total solar eclipse\'?\",\"options\":[\"When the moon is partially covered by earth\",\"When the solar disc is completely covered by the moon\",\"When the sun is seen for a few hours\",\"When the earth\'s shadow covers the sun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a total solar eclipse as when the solar disc is completely covered by the moon.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the names of the two types of shadows cast by the celestial bodies?\",\"options\":[\"Solar and Lunar\",\"Partial and Total\",\"Umbra and Penumbra\",\"Disc and Orbit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram labels the dark and faint parts of the shadow as Umbra and Penumbra.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/126_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes a lunar eclipse?\",\"options\":[\"The moon comes between the sun and the earth\",\"The sun comes between the earth and the moon\",\"The earth comes between the sun and the moon\",\"The earth, sun, and moon form a triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A lunar eclipse occurs when the earth comes between the sun and the moon.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it safe to watch a lunar eclipse with the naked eye but not a solar eclipse?\",\"options\":[\"Lunar eclipses happen at night\",\"Solar eclipses involve harmful ultra-violet rays\",\"The moon is smaller than the sun\",\"Lunar eclipses only last a few minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions ultra-violet rays are harmful during a solar eclipse, but says you can watch a lunar eclipse with the naked eye.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to observe a lunar eclipse. When should they look for it?\",\"options\":[\"On a new moon day\",\"During the afternoon\",\"On a full moon night\",\"At sunrise\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states a lunar eclipse is seen only on a full moon night.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when the shadow of the earth is cast only on a part of the moon?\",\"options\":[\"Total solar eclipse\",\"Partial lunar eclipse\",\"Total lunar eclipse\",\"Partial solar eclipse\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When the shadow of the earth is cast only on a part of the moon, it is a partial lunar eclipse.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are in a region on Earth where the moon\'s shadow is falling, what will you observe?\",\"options\":[\"A lunar eclipse\",\"The sun cannot be seen\",\"The moon becomes brighter\",\"A full moon night\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says a shadow of the moon is cast on the earth and the sun cannot be seen from that part.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What equipment is recommended for watching a solar eclipse?\",\"options\":[\"Telescope\",\"Magnifying glass\",\"Special type of goggles\",\"Sunglasses\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text recommends using a special type of goggles for watching a solar eclipse.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How long can a lunar eclipse be observed?\",\"options\":[\"A few seconds\",\"A few minutes\",\"A few hours\",\"An entire day\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a lunar eclipse can be seen over a period of a few hours.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen if the Earth, Moon, and Sun were never in a straight line?\",\"options\":[\"Eclipses would not occur\",\"It would always be a solar eclipse\",\"The moon would stop revolving\",\"The sun would stop shining\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since an eclipse is defined by these bodies coming into a straight line, no straight line means no eclipse.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary cause of a solar eclipse?\",\"options\":[\"The sun moving behind the moon\",\"The earth\'s shadow falling on the sun\",\"The moon\'s shadow falling on the earth\",\"The moon moving behind the earth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A solar eclipse happens when a shadow of the moon is cast on the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Eclipses\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the state called when a planet or a star passes behind the moon as seen from the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Eclipse\",\"Occultation\",\"Transit\",\"Shadow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines occultation as the state when a planet or a star passes behind the moon as seen from Earth.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the star called \'Rohini\' hidden behind the moon?\",\"options\":[\"2014\",\"2015\",\"2016\",\"2017\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in 2016 the star called \'Rohini\' was hidden behind the moon.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do eclipses occur often, according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"In the solar system\",\"Only on the Earth\",\"Only on the Moon\",\"In the Milky Way galaxy\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that eclipses often occur in the solar system.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Occultation is a common phenomenon that occurs with which celestial bodies?\",\"options\":[\"Only the Sun and the Moon\",\"Only other stars\",\"The sun, the moon or other stars\",\"Only planets and the sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states it is a common phenomenon that occurs with the sun, the moon or other stars.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a star after it is hidden behind the moon during an occultation?\",\"options\":[\"It disappears permanently\",\"It changes its color\",\"It appears on the other side of the moon\",\"It becomes a planet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the example of Rohini, after being hidden, the star appeared on the other side of the moon.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of an occultation mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The Earth passing behind the Sun\",\"The star Rohini being hidden behind the moon\",\"The moon passing behind the Earth\",\"The sun passing behind a planet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the 2016 event of star Rohini being hidden behind the moon as an example of occultation.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are observing a planet from Earth and it suddenly moves behind the moon, what phenomenon are you witnessing?\",\"options\":[\"A solar eclipse\",\"A lunar eclipse\",\"An occultation\",\"A reflection\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definition of a planet passing behind the moon as seen from Earth is called occultation.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what information should one find out regarding eclipses in the past?\",\"options\":[\"The names of scientists who saw them\",\"Relevant interesting information about them\",\"The distance of the stars involved\",\"The temperature of the moon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests finding out about various eclipses in the past and relevant interesting information about them.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text describe the frequency of occultation involving the sun, moon, or stars?\",\"options\":[\"It is a rare miracle\",\"It is a common phenomenon\",\"It happens once in a century\",\"It never happens in the solar system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly describes occultation as a \'common phenomenon\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What distinguishes an occultation from a general eclipse based on the specific definition provided?\",\"options\":[\"It must involve the star Rohini\",\"It must involve a body passing behind the moon as seen from Earth\",\"It only happens in the year 2016\",\"It only involves the sun and the moon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies occultation as the state when a planet or star passes behind the moon as seen from Earth.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name given to the day when the sun reaches exactly overhead at noon?\",\"options\":[\"Eclipse day\",\"Zero shadow day\",\"Tropic day\",\"Equinox day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines zero shadow day as the day on which the sun reaches exactly overhead.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what time of the day do shadows completely disappear on a zero shadow day?\",\"options\":[\"At sunrise\",\"At sunset\",\"At noon\",\"At midnight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, shadows completely disappear at noon on a zero shadow day.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The zero shadow day event can only be seen in the region between the Tropic of Cancer and the _______.\",\"options\":[\"Arctic Circle\",\"Antarctic Circle\",\"Equator\",\"Tropic of Capricorn\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states this event is visible between the Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A city is located at 15°N latitude. Based on the geographical limits mentioned in the text, is it possible for this city to experience a zero shadow day?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, because it is between 23.5°N and 23.5°S\",\"No, because it is not on the Equator\",\"Yes, but only during a solar eclipse\",\"No, because it is too far north\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"15°N falls within the range of 23.5°N to 23.5°S specified in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which season does the zero shadow day occur in the specified regions?\",\"options\":[\"Winter\",\"Monsoon\",\"Summer\",\"Autumn\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that this event occurs in summer on different days in different places.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do shadows disappear at noon on a zero shadow day?\",\"options\":[\"Because the sun is blocked by the moon\",\"Because the sun reaches exactly overhead\",\"Because the sun is at the lowest point in the sky\",\"Because it is a very cloudy day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shadows disappear because the light source (the sun) is directly above the object.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how is an eclipse scientifically described?\",\"options\":[\"A supernatural event\",\"A natural phenomenon\",\"A bad omen for society\",\"A religious miracle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text in the figure explicitly states that an eclipse is a natural phenomenon.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/127_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the message in the given figure, what action is necessary regarding superstitious beliefs in society?\",\"options\":[\"We should encourage them for safety\",\"We should ignore them completely\",\"Everyone should make efforts to rid society of them\",\"We should only believe them during eclipses\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure states it is necessary that everyone makes efforts to rid the society of superstitious beliefs.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/127_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific latitude of the Tropic of Cancer as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"0°\",\"23.5°S\",\"66.5°N\",\"23.5°N\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text provides the latitude for the Tropic of Cancer as 23.5°N.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is standing outside the region between 23.5°N and 23.5°S at noon, what will happen to their shadow?\",\"options\":[\"It will completely disappear\",\"It will be visible because the sun is not exactly overhead\",\"It will turn into a bright light\",\"It will only disappear in the summer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies zero shadow only occurs within the specified tropical region; outside this, the sun is not exactly overhead.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions that zero shadow day occurs on different days in different places. What does this suggest about the sun\'s position?\",\"options\":[\"The sun is overhead at all places at the same time\",\"The sun\'s overhead position changes throughout the summer\",\"The sun only reaches overhead at the Equator\",\"The sun never moves across the sky\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since it occurs on different days in different places, the sun reaches the \'exactly overhead\' point at different times for different latitudes.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a practice the text encourages us to discuss as a potential superstition?\",\"options\":[\"Observing the zero shadow day\",\"Tying lemon-chilli to a car\",\"Measuring the latitude of a place\",\"Studying natural phenomena\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Think and discuss\' section asks why it is not right to tie lemon-chilli to a car, linking it to superstitious beliefs.\",\"topic\":\"Zero shadow day\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22003,46,7,'Science','en','Sound : Production of Sound','[\"Sound is produced when an object vibrates and the vibration moves the surrounding medium (air, water, solids).\",\"The vibration causes the particles of the medium to oscillate, creating a wave that travels to our ears.\",\"A sound wave is a longitudinal wave: particles of the medium move back and forth in the same direction as the wave travels.\",\"The frequency of vibration (cycles per second) determines the pitch of the sound: higher frequency = higher pitch.\",\"The amplitude of vibration determines the intensity (loudness) of the sound: larger amplitude = louder sound.\",\"The time taken for one complete vibration is called the period (T). Frequency (f) is the number of vibrations per second: f = 1\\/T.\",\"The unit of frequency is Hertz (Hz). 1 Hz = one vibration per second.\",\"Sound needs a medium; in vacuum there is no sound because there are no particles to vibrate.\",\"The audible range for humans is about 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz. Below 20 Hz is infrasonic, above 20 000 Hz is ultrasonic.\",\"The decibel (dB) is a logarithmic unit for sound level. A 10 dB increase means the intensity is ten times greater.\",\"When the amplitude of a vibration is doubled, the intensity increases by a factor of four (10 dB).\",\"Tuning an instrument changes the tension of its strings or diaphragm, which changes the frequency of vibration.\",\"A pendulum’s period increases as its length increases; its frequency decreases with length.\",\"The sound produced by a string is higher when the tension is increased and lower when the tension is decreased.\",\"The vibration of a rubber band or a pendulum is an example of an oscillator and oscillatory motion.\",\"The sound produced by a vibrating object is heard when the vibration causes the surrounding medium to oscillate in the audible frequency range.\",\"In a musical instrument, the part that vibrates (strings, air column, membrane) is the sound source.\",\"The intensity of sound is proportional to the square of the amplitude of vibration.\",\"The pitch of a sound is directly related to its frequency.\",\"Infrasonic sounds (below 20 Hz) are used by animals like elephants for long‑range communication.\",\"Ultrasonic sounds (above 20 000 Hz) can be heard by some animals like dogs and bats.\",\"The human ear cannot detect sound in a vacuum because there is no medium to transmit vibrations.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the fundamental cause for the generation of sound?\",\"options\":[\"Heat energy\",\"Vibration of an object\",\"Movement of water\",\"Static electricity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that sound is generated due to the vibration of an object.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which of these examples can vibrations be explicitly felt by touching the object?\",\"options\":[\"A lightning strike\",\"Clapping hands\",\"Bursting a cracker\",\"Diaphragm of a musical instrument\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the diaphragm of a musical instrument as an example where vibrations are felt.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the molecules in the air when vibrations are produced?\",\"options\":[\"They are destroyed\",\"Vibrations are imparted to them\",\"They move in a vacuum\",\"They turn into water droplets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that vibrations are imparted to the molecules in the air to produce sound.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The text compares the movement of sound through air to which natural phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"Thunderclaps in the sky\",\"Waves generated in a lake by a stone\",\"A metal utensil falling\",\"A ringing mobile phone\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses the analogy of waves in a lake reaching the banks to explain how sound reaches our ears.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you place your palm on a mobile phone while it is ringing, what will you most likely experience?\",\"options\":[\"A sudden increase in temperature\",\"The physical sensation of vibrations\",\"The phone will stop ringing immediately\",\"The air molecules will stop moving\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Placing a palm on a ringing mobile is listed as an event where sound generation involves vibrations.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true regarding sound produced by bursting a firecracker?\",\"options\":[\"No vibrations are produced at all\",\"Vibrations are produced but not actually felt\",\"Vibrations are only produced in the air, not the cracker\",\"Vibrations are visible to the naked eye\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in examples like bursting a cracker, vibrations are not actually felt, but they are produced.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Through which medium do the vibrations reach our ears in the examples given in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Metal\",\"Air\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that vibrations reach our ears through the air.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a stone is thrown into calm water, where do the generated waves reach?\",\"options\":[\"The bottom of the lake\",\"The center of the lake\",\"The banks of the lake\",\"The surface of the stone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that waves reach up to the banks of the lake.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of swinging the clapper of a bell?\",\"options\":[\"The bell produces heat\",\"The bell rings\",\"The clapper breaks\",\"The air molecules disappear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists swinging the clapper of the bell and the bell ringing as an event that generates sound.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these actions involves using a pen cap to generate sound?\",\"options\":[\"Writing with it\",\"Whistling with it\",\"Dropping it on the floor\",\"Placing it on a speaker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Whistling using the cap of a pen is specifically mentioned as a sound-generating event.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing a \'thunderclap\' and a \'ringing bell\', what is the common scientific requirement for both to be heard?\",\"options\":[\"Both require the vibrations to be felt by hand\",\"Both require a metal surface\",\"Both require vibrations to be imparted to air molecules\",\"Both require the presence of water waves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that in all cases, vibrations are imparted to air molecules to produce sound.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the lake analogy, how does the text describe the process of hearing sound?\",\"options\":[\"Air molecules travel from the object to the ear\",\"Vibrations reach the ear through the air like waves\",\"The ear travels to the source of the vibration\",\"Sound is heard only when the object is touched\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says vibrations reach our ears through the air in a similar way to waves reaching the banks of a lake.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In scientific terms, what is the measure of the pitch of a sound?\",\"options\":[\"Amplitude\",\"Frequency\",\"Loudness\",\"Velocity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in scientific terms, frequency is a measure of pitch.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the tabla is adjusted to change its tension during tuning?\",\"options\":[\"The wooden body\",\"The pegs\",\"The diaphragm\",\"The metal rings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a tabla player tunes the tabla by adjusting the tension in the diaphragm.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct order of Indian musical notes from lower to higher pitch?\",\"options\":[\"Sa, Re, Ga, Ma, Pa, Dha, Ni\",\"Ni, Dha, Pa, Ma, Ga, Re, Sa\",\"Sa, Ga, Re, Ma, Dha, Pa, Ni\",\"Sa, Re, Ma, Ga, Pa, Ni, Dha\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists Sa, Re, Ga, Ma, Pa, Dha, Ni as notes of increasingly higher pitch.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does a singer ensure by adjusting the tension in the strings of a tanpura?\",\"options\":[\"The instrument looks polished\",\"The instrument will produce the required notes\",\"The instrument becomes louder\",\"The strings become longer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that adjusting the tension ensures the tanpura produces the required notes.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of \'tuning\' a musical instrument?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the volume of the sound\",\"To adjust how high or low the pitch of the notes will be\",\"To make the instrument easier to hold\",\"To change the rhythm of the song\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines tuning an instrument as adjusting how high or low the pitch of the notes produced will be.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the pitch of the note \'Ni\' compare to the note \'Sa\' in Indian music?\",\"options\":[\"Ni has a lower pitch than Sa\",\"Ni has the same pitch as Sa\",\"Ni has a higher pitch than Sa\",\"Ni has no frequency compared to Sa\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the notes Sa through Ni are of increasingly higher pitch.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a tabla player hammers the pegs to increase the tension in the diaphragm, what will happen to the sound?\",\"options\":[\"The pitch will decrease\",\"The pitch will remain exactly the same\",\"The pitch will increase because pitch depends on frequency\",\"The sound will stop completely\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tuning involves adjusting tension to change the pitch; increasing tension increases the pitch\\/frequency.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A harmonium player finds out the singer\'s key before starting. This action is taken to:\",\"options\":[\"Match the pitch of the harmonium with the singer\'s voice\",\"Make the singer sing at a higher volume\",\"Change the physical size of the harmonium\",\"Repair the keys of the harmonium\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Finding the key allows the accompanist to align the instrument\'s pitch with the singer\'s requirements.\",\"topic\":\"Tuning the instruments\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the 