oint away from the center, which property of her motion is increasing?\",\"options\":[\"Force of gravity\",\"Amplitude\",\"Original position\",\"Thickness of the rope\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Similar to the rubber band, the maximum distance from the original (center) position in an oscillation represents amplitude.\",\"topic\":\"Amplitude of vibration\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/130_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following sequences correctly describes the production of a loud sound?\",\"options\":[\"Small force -> small stretch -> small amplitude -> loud sound\",\"Great force -> small stretch -> large amplitude -> soft sound\",\"Great force -> further stretch -> increased amplitude -> louder sound\",\"Small force -> further stretch -> decreased amplitude -> softer sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links greater force to further stretch, increased amplitude, and louder sound.\",\"topic\":\"Amplitude of vibration\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When the rubber band is stretched from position A to position B, what physical change is observed in the rubber band\'s appearance?\",\"options\":[\"It is seen to be curved\",\"It becomes invisible\",\"It turns into a straight vertical line\",\"It changes into a square shape\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions that when stretched to position B, the rubber band is seen to be curved.\",\"topic\":\"Amplitude of vibration\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does applying a smaller force result in a softer sound in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Because the rubber band stops vibrating\",\"Because the amplitude of vibration is smaller\",\"Because the original position A changes\",\"Because the porcelain bowl absorbs the force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that with a smaller force, the rubber is stretched less, making the amplitude smaller and the sound softer.\",\"topic\":\"Amplitude of vibration\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name given to a small iron or wooden ball tied to a thread and suspended from a support?\",\"options\":[\"Vibrator\",\"Pendulum\",\"Elasticity\",\"Amplitude\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a pendulum as a small iron or wooden ball tied to a thread and suspended from a support.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the property called when a stretched rubber returns to its original state after being released?\",\"options\":[\"Gravitation\",\"Oscillation\",\"Elasticity\",\"Amplitude\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the property of a stretched rubber returning to its original state is called elasticity.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the approximate length of the thread used to make the pendulum?\",\"options\":[\"One meter\",\"Half a meter\",\"Two meters\",\"Ten centimeters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests taking a strong thread about half a meter long for the experiment.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these represents the \'amplitude\' of a pendulum\'s oscillation?\",\"options\":[\"The total distance from B to C\",\"The length of the thread\",\"The maximum distance from the original position A to position B or C\",\"The weight of the iron ball\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Amplitude is defined as the maximum distance between the original position A and the extreme position B or C.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which force is constantly at work while a pendulum is oscillating?\",\"options\":[\"Elasticity\",\"Earth\'s gravitation\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Static electricity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that all the while the pendulum oscillates, earth\'s gravitation is at work.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is at work when a stretched rubber band vibrates?\",\"options\":[\"Elasticity\",\"Only gravitation\",\"Magnetic pull\",\"Amplitude\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that when a stretched rubber band vibrates, elasticity is at work.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a pendulum moves from its central point A to an extreme point B, and the distance measured is 10 cm, what is the amplitude?\",\"options\":[\"5 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"0 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since AB is the amplitude, if the distance from A to B is 10 cm, the amplitude is 10 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student uses a rubber band instead of a thread for the pendulum, which property mentioned in the text would be most relevant to the band itself?\",\"options\":[\"Gravitation\",\"Elasticity\",\"Amplitude\",\"Oscillation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies elasticity as the property of rubber that allows it to return to its original state.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the pendulum, which part acts as the \'oscillator\'?\",\"options\":[\"Only the iron ball\",\"Only the support\",\"The suspended ball and thread assembly\",\"The distance AB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the ball tied to a thread and suspended from a support as \'such an oscillator\'.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes between the forces acting on the pendulum and the rubber band?\",\"options\":[\"Elasticity works on the pendulum, while gravitation works on the rubber band.\",\"Gravitation works on the pendulum, while elasticity works on the stretched rubber band.\",\"Only amplitude works on the pendulum.\",\"Neither gravitation nor elasticity is mentioned as a force.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies gravitation is at work for the pendulum and elasticity for the vibrating rubber band.\",\"topic\":\"Pendulum and Amplitude\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the time required by an oscillator to complete one oscillation called?\",\"options\":[\"Frequency\",\"Amplitude\",\"Time period\",\"Hertz\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the time required to complete one oscillation as the time period of the oscillator.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the number of oscillations completed by an oscillator in one second called?\",\"options\":[\"Time period\",\"Frequency\",\"Velocity\",\"Distance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the number of oscillations completed in one second is called the frequency.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit is used to measure the frequency of oscillation?\",\"options\":[\"Second\",\"Meter\",\"Hertz (Hz)\",\"Newton\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the unit of frequency is Hertz (Hz).\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the oscillator moves from B to A, A to C, C back to A, and A back to B, what has it completed?\",\"options\":[\"Half an oscillation\",\"One oscillation\",\"Two oscillations\",\"One second of motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the total motion B-A-C-A-B as one oscillation.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does a frequency of 1 Hz signify?\",\"options\":[\"One oscillation in one minute\",\"One oscillation in one second\",\"Ten oscillations in one second\",\"One oscillation in ten seconds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1 Hz means there is one oscillation in one second.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following formulas correctly represents the relationship between frequency (n) and time period (T)?\",\"options\":[\"n = T \\/ 1\",\"n = 1 \\/ T\",\"n = T + 1\",\"n = 1 - T\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook provides the formula Frequency (n) = 1 \\/ time period of oscillation (T).\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the time period (T) of an oscillator is 0.5 seconds, what is its frequency (n)?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 Hz\",\"1 Hz\",\"2 Hz\",\"5 Hz\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula n = 1\\/T, n = 1\\/0.5 = 2 Hz.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"An oscillator completes 500 oscillations in one second. What is its frequency?\",\"options\":[\"5 Hz\",\"50 Hz\",\"500 Hz\",\"0.5 Hz\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Frequency is the number of oscillations in one second; therefore, 500 oscillations per second is 500 Hz.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an oscillator has a frequency of 10 Hz, what is the time period (T) for one oscillation?\",\"options\":[\"10 seconds\",\"1 second\",\"0.1 second\",\"0.01 second\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since n = 1\\/T, then T = 1\\/n. T = 1\\/10 = 0.1 second.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the path B-A-C-A-B, which position represents the central position?\",\"options\":[\"Position B\",\"Position A\",\"Position C\",\"Positions B and C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the oscillator traverses from extreme position B to central position A.\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Compare two oscillators: Oscillator X has a frequency of 50 Hz and Oscillator Y has a frequency of 100 Hz. Which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Oscillator X completes more oscillations in one second.\",\"Oscillator Y takes more time to complete one oscillation.\",\"Oscillator Y completes more oscillations in one second.\",\"Both oscillators take the same time to complete one oscillation.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Frequency is the number of oscillations per second; 100 Hz (Y) is more than 50 Hz (X).\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the formula n = 1\\/T, what happens to the frequency if the time period of an oscillator is doubled?\",\"options\":[\"The frequency is doubled.\",\"The frequency is halved.\",\"The frequency remains the same.\",\"The frequency becomes zero.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since frequency is inversely proportional to the time period, doubling the time period (T) results in half the frequency (n).\",\"topic\":\"Time period and frequency\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the time period of oscillation (T) if the length of the pendulum is increased?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases\",\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the time period of oscillation increases if the length of the pendulum is increased.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, what is the correct formula to calculate the frequency (n) of the pendulum?\",\"options\":[\"n = T \\/ 20\",\"n = 1 \\/ T\",\"n = t \\/ 20\",\"n = T × 20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows the formula for frequency as n(Hz) = 1\\/T.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/132_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the purpose of the action shown where the free end of the ruler is pressed and released?\",\"options\":[\"To measure the weight of the ruler\",\"To observe vibration and sound production\",\"To break the plastic ruler\",\"To measure the length of the table\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the vibration of the ruler and the sound produced by plucking its free end.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/131_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a pendulum takes 40 seconds to complete 20 oscillations, what is its time period (T)?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 seconds\",\"2 seconds\",\"20 seconds\",\"800 seconds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula T = t\\/20 from the text, T = 40\\/20 = 2 seconds.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the frequency of a pendulum and its amplitude according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Frequency increases as amplitude increases\",\"Frequency decreases as amplitude increases\",\"Frequency remains the same regardless of amplitude\",\"Frequency becomes zero when amplitude is fixed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the frequency remains the same even if the amplitude decreases or increases.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When the length of the free part of the ruler is decreased little by little, what changes are observed?\",\"options\":[\"Only the color of the ruler changes\",\"The frequency and pitch of the sound change\",\"The ruler stops vibrating entirely\",\"The amplitude becomes infinite\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions taking note of the difference in frequency and pitch when the length of the free part is varied.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the time period (T) of a pendulum is 0.1 seconds, what is its frequency (n)?\",\"options\":[\"1 Hz\",\"10 Hz\",\"20 Hz\",\"0.1 Hz\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula n = 1\\/T, n = 1\\/0.1 = 10 Hz.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The time period of oscillation (T) of a pendulum depends on which factor?\",\"options\":[\"The material of the bob\",\"The amplitude of the swing\",\"The length of the pendulum\",\"The thickness of the thread\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the time period of oscillation (T) depends on the length of the pendulum.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the frequency (n) of a pendulum if its length is decreased?\",\"options\":[\"Frequency increases\",\"Frequency decreases\",\"Frequency remains constant\",\"Frequency becomes negative\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Decreasing length decreases the time period (T). Since n = 1\\/T, a smaller T results in a higher frequency.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, if a ruler is held with 25 cm off the table and plucked, and no sound is heard, what can be inferred?\",\"options\":[\"The ruler is not vibrating\",\"The frequency is affected by the length of the free part\",\"The table is absorbing all the sound\",\"The ruler is too strong to make sound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment asks to look for the reason, implying a correlation between the length of the free part and the sound generated.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To calculate the time period (T) in the pendulum experiment, the time for how many oscillations is recorded?\",\"options\":[\"1 oscillation\",\"10 oscillations\",\"20 oscillations\",\"100 oscillations\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to record how many seconds are required for 20 oscillations.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you keep the length of a pendulum fixed at 30 cm but change how far you pull it (amplitude), what will happen to the time required for 20 oscillations?\",\"options\":[\"It will increase significantly\",\"It will decrease significantly\",\"It will remain the same\",\"It will become zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since frequency and time period remain the same regardless of amplitude, the time for 20 oscillations stays the same.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following would result in a \'low frequency\' sound from a pendulum?\",\"options\":[\"A very short pendulum length\",\"A very long pendulum length\",\"A very large amplitude\",\"A very small amplitude\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A longer length increases the time period, which results in a lower frequency (n = 1\\/T).\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the length of the free part of the ruler and the sound generated?\",\"options\":[\"There is no correlation\",\"There is a correlation affecting frequency and pitch\",\"Only the volume changes, not the pitch\",\"The length only affects the color of the ruler\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to note the difference in frequency and pitch when the length is changed, indicating a correlation.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to increase the pitch (frequency) of the sound made by the ruler on the table, what should they do?\",\"options\":[\"Increase the length of the free part\",\"Decrease the length of the free part\",\"Press the ruler harder on the table\",\"Use a heavier ruler\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Decreasing the length of the vibrating part increases the frequency\\/pitch.\",\"topic\":\"Factors affecting frequency\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, what happens to the frequency of the wire when the tension is increased?\",\"options\":[\"Frequency decreases\",\"Frequency increases\",\"Frequency remains the same\",\"Frequency becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when tension in the wire is increased, the frequency is seen to increase.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used to describe the difference between a shrill sound and a less shrill sound?\",\"options\":[\"Loudness\",\"Amplitude\",\"High and low pitch\",\"Echo\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the difference in shrillness as the high and low pitch of sound.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment, what is the effect of reducing the tension in the wire?\",\"options\":[\"The sound becomes shriller\",\"The frequency increases\",\"The sound becomes less shrill\",\"The wire stops vibrating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when the tension is reduced, the sound is also less shrill.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following sounds has a higher pitch according to the examples provided?\",\"options\":[\"The roar of a lion\",\"The hum of a mosquito\",\"A drum beat\",\"A heavy bell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to compare the roar of a lion and the hum of a mosquito, where the mosquito\'s hum is the higher pitched (shriller) sound.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of inserting small rectangular blocks below the wedge-shaped block in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"To change the length of the wire\",\"To change the material of the wire\",\"To change the tension in the wire\",\"To stop the wire from vibrating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The blocks are used to observe whether there is any change in the tension in the wire without changing its length.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a wire produces a very shrill sound, what can be inferred about its frequency?\",\"options\":[\"It has a low frequency\",\"It has a high frequency\",\"Its frequency is zero\",\"Frequency cannot be determined\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links increased tension to both increased frequency and a shriller sound.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What should be the approximate length of the wooden board used in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"10 to 20 cm\",\"50 to 60 cm\",\"80 to 90 cm\",\"150 to 200 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies taking a wooden board 80 to 90 cm long.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student plucks a wire and then increases the tension. How will the new sound compare to the old one?\",\"options\":[\"It will be lower in pitch\",\"It will be less shrill\",\"It will be shriller\",\"It will have a lower frequency\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Increased tension results in a shriller sound (higher pitch).\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which relationship is correct regarding sound production in a stretched wire?\",\"options\":[\"Higher tension = Lower frequency\",\"Lower tension = Higher pitch\",\"Higher tension = Higher frequency\",\"Lower tension = Shriller sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when tension is increased, frequency is seen to increase.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of musical instruments like the sitar, what is the primary factor being adjusted to produce different pitches according to the experiment\'s logic?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the strings\",\"The tension in the strings\",\"The thickness of the wood\",\"The room temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment demonstrates that changing tension changes the pitch, which applies to instruments like the sitar.\",\"topic\":\"High and low pitch of sound\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If t