,\"Intensity of sound and Sound level\",\"Decibel and Hertz\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The very first sentence of the text states that loudness or softness is indicated by intensity of sound and sound level.\",\"topic\":\"Intensity of sound - sound level\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the decibel level recorded for heavy traffic?\",\"options\":[\"30 dB\",\"60 dB\",\"70 dB\",\"80 dB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists \'Heavy traffic – 70 dB\'.\",\"topic\":\"Intensity of sound - sound level\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/133_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which sound is louder than the start of ear-splitting sound?\",\"options\":[\"Ordinary factories\",\"Jet engine\",\"Heavy traffic\",\"Normal conversation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the start of ear-splitting sound is 120 dB, while a jet engine is higher at 130 dB.\",\"topic\":\"Intensity of sound - sound level\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/133_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why are workers near aeroplane engines at risk of temporary deafness according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because the frequency is less than 1000 Hz\",\"Because the sound level is below 100 dB\",\"Because jet engines produce sounds at 130 dB, which is above the 100 dB safety limit\",\"Because the amplitude of vibration is too low\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes jet engines are 130 dB and levels higher than 100 dB cause temporary deafness.\",\"topic\":\"Intensity of sound - sound level\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the decibel level for murmuring from a distance of 5 metres?\",\"options\":[\"0 dB\",\"10 dB\",\"30 dB\",\"60 dB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Murmuring from 5 metres away – 30 dB\'.\",\"topic\":\"Intensity of sound - sound level\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the frequency range of sound that is audible to human beings?\",\"options\":[\"Less than 20 Hz\",\"Between 20 Hz and 20,000 Hz\",\"More than 20,000 Hz\",\"Between 4 Hz and 20 Hz\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that the frequency of sound audible to human beings is between 20Hz and 20,000Hz.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A sound with a frequency less than 20 Hz is known as which type of sound?\",\"options\":[\"Audible sound\",\"Ultrasonic sound\",\"Infrasonic sound\",\"Resonant sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a sound with a frequency less than 20 Hz is called infrasonic sound.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following animals is mentioned as being able to produce infrasonic sound?\",\"options\":[\"Dog\",\"Bat\",\"Rhinoceros\",\"Dolphin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists whales, elephants, and rhinoceros as animals that produce infrasonic sound.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the pitch of a sound related to its frequency?\",\"options\":[\"Pitch is inversely related to frequency\",\"Pitch is directly related to frequency\",\"Pitch has no relation to frequency\",\"Pitch is only related to frequency in ultrasonic sounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Do you know?\' section states that the pitch of sound is directly related to the frequency of sound.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are we unable to hear the sound produced by the movement of our hands?\",\"options\":[\"The frequency is higher than 20,000 Hz\",\"The frequency is exactly 20 Hz\",\"The frequency is less than 20 Hz\",\"The movement does not produce any oscillations\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that movements like hand swings produce sound of frequency less than 20 Hz, which humans cannot hear.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Elephants are known to communicate with each other using infrasonic sound over distances of up to how many kilometers?\",\"options\":[\"2 km\",\"5 km\",\"10 km\",\"20 km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions evidence that elephants communicate over distances of up to 10 km using infrasonic sound.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If an oscillator makes 3 to 4 oscillations in one second, what can be concluded about the sound it produces?\",\"options\":[\"It is audible to humans\",\"It is ultrasonic sound\",\"It is infrasonic sound\",\"It has a frequency of 30 to 40 Hz\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"3-4 oscillations per second means 3-4 Hz, which is less than 20 Hz, making it infrasonic.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which technology is specifically used to locate the seabed or the position of a ship using sound?\",\"options\":[\"RADAR\",\"SONAR\",\"Microbes\",\"Oscillator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"SONAR (Sound Navigation And Ranging) is used to locate the seabed or the position of a ship.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of sound is used to clean delicate ornaments and tiny parts of a watch?\",\"options\":[\"Audible sound\",\"Infrasonic sound\",\"Ultrasonic sound\",\"Low pitch sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"One of the listed uses of ultrasonic sound is to clean delicate ornaments and tiny parts of a watch.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which animal\'s frequency range is shown to be entirely in the shaded \'Ultrasonic\' region?\",\"options\":[\"Blue Whale\",\"Violin\",\"Bat\",\"Flute\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The graph shows \'Bat\' and \'Dolphin\' within the shaded region labeled \'Ultrasonic\' (above 20,000 Hz).\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/135_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which frequency marks the transition from Infrasonic sound to the range Audible to Humans?\",\"options\":[\"4 Hz\",\"20 Hz\",\"256 Hz\",\"20,000 Hz\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a vertical line at 20 Hz separating the Infrasonic region from the \'Audible to Humans\' region.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/135_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"It is believed that some animals can receive signals of an impending earthquake using which type of sound?\",\"options\":[\"Infrasonic sound\",\"Audible sound\",\"Ultrasonic sound\",\"High pitch audible sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that dogs and other animals may receive ultrasonic sound signals in advance of an earthquake.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Ultrasonic sound is used in the medical field for which of the following purposes?\",\"options\":[\"To clean the skin\",\"To detect tumours in the brain\",\"To improve hearing in humans\",\"To increase the heartbeat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'To detect tumours in the brain\' as a use of ultrasonic sound.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Apart from medical and cleaning uses, ultrasonic sound is also used to kill which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Whales and elephants\",\"Certain microbes and insects\",\"Fish in the seabed\",\"Large animals during earthquakes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that ultrasonic sound is used to kill certain microbes and insects.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the hearing capabilities mentioned in the text, which animal can hear sounds that are at a higher frequency than what humans can hear?\",\"options\":[\"Elephant\",\"Rhinoceros\",\"Whale\",\"Dog\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states humans cannot hear sounds above 20,000 Hz, but a dog can hear such (ultrasonic) sounds.\",\"topic\":\"Audible, Infrasonic and Ultrasonic\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22004,46,7,'Science','en','Properties of a Magnetic Field','[\"- A magnet has two ends called the north pole and the south pole.\",\"- Like poles (north–north or south–south) repel each other.\",\"- Opposite poles (north–south) attract each other.\",\"- Magnets are made from alloys of iron, cobalt and nickel. Examples are Nipermag (iron, nickel, aluminium, titanium) and Alnico (aluminium, nickel, cobalt).\",\"- A freely suspended magnet always points north‑south because the Earth is a giant magnet with its magnetic north near the geographic south pole.\",\"- The Earth’s magnetic field is the region around it where magnetic forces act on a magnet or a magnetic needle.\",\"- Magnetic field lines are imaginary lines that start at the north pole and end at the south pole of a magnet.\",\"- Magnetic field lines never cross each other and are dense where the field is strong.\",\"- A magnet can attract objects at a distance; this is called magnetic force.\",\"- Magnetic force is a vector quantity (has magnitude and direction).\",\"- A magnetic field can pass through non‑magnetic materials like cardboard, water and plastic, but its intensity decreases with distance.\",\"- An electromagnet is a magnet made by passing electric current through a coil of wire around a ferromagnetic core.\",\"- Electromagnets are used in metal detectors, cranes, and many industrial machines.\",\"- The real test of a magnet is to see if it can repel another magnet or attract a compass needle.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, magnets are made from alloys of which three metals?\",\"options\":[\"Iron, cobalt and nickel\",\"Iron, copper and gold\",\"Nickel, silver and aluminium\",\"Cobalt, zinc and lead\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that magnets are made from alloys of iron, cobalt and nickel.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific alloy is used to make magnets and contains titanium?\",\"options\":[\"Alnico\",\"Steel\",\"Nipermag\",\"Bronze\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Nipermag is an alloy of iron, nickel, aluminium and titanium.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does a freely suspended magnet always settle?\",\"options\":[\"East-West direction\",\"North-South direction\",\"In any random direction\",\"Vertical direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It is a fundamental property that a freely suspended magnet settles in the North-South direction.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the names given to the two ends of a magnet?\",\"options\":[\"East and West poles\",\"Left and Right poles\",\"North and South poles\",\"Top and Bottom poles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The two ends of a magnet are named the North and South poles based on the direction they point.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a magnetic alloy consisting of aluminium, nickel, and cobalt?\",\"options\":[\"Nipermag\",\"Alnico\",\"Titanium-iron\",\"Cobalt-steel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines alnico as a magnetic alloy of aluminium, nickel and cobalt.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given the metals Aluminium, Nickel, and Cobalt to create a magnet, which alloy are you making?\",\"options\":[\"Nipermag\",\"Stainless Steel\",\"Alnico\",\"Brass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Alnico is specifically composed of aluminium, nickel, and cobalt.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A manufacturer wants to make a \'Nipermag\' magnet. Which metal must they include that is NOT found in Alnico?\",\"options\":[\"Nickel\",\"Aluminium\",\"Titanium\",\"Cobalt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nipermag contains titanium, whereas Alnico contains aluminium, nickel, and cobalt.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the ends of a magnet named \'North\' and \'South\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are made of different chemicals\",\"Because they settle in those geographic directions\",\"Because they attract different types of metals\",\"Because they were discovered in those hemispheres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The names are derived from the direction in which a freely suspended magnet settles.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these metals is a common component in both Nipermag and Alnico alloys?\",\"options\":[\"Titanium\",\"Cobalt\",\"Nickel\",\"Copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both Nipermag (Fe, Ni, Al, Ti) and Alnico (Al, Ni, Co) contain Nickel.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a magnet in a household appliance. Based on the text, which alloy was likely used if it contains Iron, Nickel, Aluminium, and Titanium?\",\"options\":[\"Alnico\",\"Nipermag\",\"Pure Cobalt\",\"Pure Iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The specific combination of Iron, Nickel, Aluminium, and Titanium forms Nipermag.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the method of magnetisation called when a single pole of a magnet is dragged from one end of a steel bar to the other repeatedly?\",\"options\":[\"Double-touch method\",\"Single touch method\",\"Electrical method\",\"Friction method\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the method of dragging one pole from end A to end B repeatedly as the single touch method.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the single touch method, what must be done after dragging the magnet to end \'B\' of the steel bar?\",\"options\":[\"Drag it back from B to A immediately\",\"Leave it at end B for five minutes\",\"Lift the magnet and return to end A\",\"Heat the steel bar at end B\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure states to lift the bar magnet and drag its pole from end A to end B again.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is used as the bar to be magnetised in the experiments described?\",\"options\":[\"Copper bar\",\"Aluminium bar\",\"Steel bar\",\"Plastic bar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The apparatus list and procedure specifically mention using a steel bar.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a characteristic of the magnetism created by the single touch method?\",\"options\":[\"It is of high strength\",\"It lasts for a very long time\",\"It is of low strength\",\"It is permanent and cannot be lost\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that magnetism created by the single touch method is of low strength and lasts for a short time.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the double-touch method, where are the poles of the two bar magnets initially placed?\",\"options\":[\"At the \'A\' end of the steel bar\",\"At the \'B\' end of the steel bar\",\"At the centre of the steel bar\",\"One at each end of the steel bar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure for the double-touch method says to place two opposite poles at the centre of the steel bar.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many times should the dragging process be repeated to magnetise the bar?\",\"options\":[\"2 to 5 times\",\"5 to 10 times\",\"15 to 20 times\",\"50 to 100 times\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both methods specify repeating the dragging process 15 to 20 times.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Compared to the single touch method, the magnetism generated by the double-touch method:\",\"options\":[\"Lasts for a shorter time\",\"Lasts longer\",\"Is of lower strength\",\"Disappears immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that magnetism generated by the double-touch method lasts longer compared to the single touch method.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student only has one bar magnet, which method of magnetisation can they perform?\",\"options\":[\"Single touch method\",\"Double-touch method\",\"Both methods\",\"Neither method\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The single touch method requires one bar magnet, while the double-touch method requires two.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is used to observe if the steel bar has successfully developed magnetism?\",\"options\":[\"Thread only\",\"Iron filings\",\"A second steel bar\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure suggests taking the steel bar near iron filings to observe the developed magnetism.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the double-touch method, how are the two magnets moved after being placed on the steel bar?\",\"options\":[\"They are dragged towards the centre\",\"They are dragged apart towards ends A and B\",\"They are both dragged towards end A\",\"They are both dragged towards end B\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to drag the poles apart, one to the \'A\' end and the other to the \'B\' end.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of hanging the steel bar freely by a thread after the magnetisation process?\",\"options\":[\"To see if it attracts the thread\",\"To test if it behaves like a magnet\",\"To increase its magnetic strength\",\"To clean the iron filings off\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hanging a bar freely is a standard way to observe its magnetic properties (like alignment).\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which poles must be placed at the centre of the steel bar during the double-touch method?\",\"options\":[\"Two North poles\",\"Two South poles\",\"Two opposite poles\",\"Any two poles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies placing \'two opposite poles of the two bar magnets\' at the centre.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetism\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist provided a scientific explanation for why a freely suspended magnet always settles in the north-south direction?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Newton\",\"William Gilbert\",\"Albert Einstein\",\"Galileo Galilei\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the scientist William Gilbert gave a scientific explanation based on experiment for this observation.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What shape did William Gilbert give to the naturally occurring magnetic rock for his experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Cylindrical\",\"Rectangular\",\"Round\",\"Triangular\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Gilbert gave a round shape to a naturally occurring magnetic rock to create a spherical magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does a freely suspended magnet always settle?\",\"options\":[\"East-West\",\"North-South\",\"North-West\",\"South-East\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the observation that a freely suspended magnet always settles in the north-south direction only.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was William Gilbert\'s major inference regarding the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"The Earth is a hollow sphere\",\"The Earth is a gigantic magnet\",\"The Earth has no magnetic properties\",\"The Earth revolves around a magnet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gilbert inferred from his experiments that the earth itself is a gigantic magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where is the magnetic south pole of the Earth located?\",\"options\":[\"Near the geographic South pole\",\"Near the geographic East pole\",\"Near the geographic North pole\",\"At the center of the Earth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the south pole of this magnet (Earth) must be near the geographic north pole of the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment described, which magnetic poles attract each other?\",\"options\":[\"North pole and North pole\",\"South pole and South pole\",\"Opposite poles\",\"Similar poles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes that the south pole of the magnetic sphere was attracted to the north pole of a bar magnet, showing opposite poles attract.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you bring the south pole of a bar magnet near Gilbert\'s spherical magnet, which pole of the sphere will be attracted to it?\",\"options\":[\"The south pole\",\"The north pole\",\"Both poles\",\"Neither pole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since opposite poles attract, the north pole of the spherical magnet would be attracted to the south pole of the bar magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a traveler is standing at the geographic North Pole, which pole of the Earth\'s internal magnet is directly beneath them?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic North pole\",\"Magnetic South pole\",\"Geographic South pole\",\"Magnetic East pole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the south pole of the Earth\'s gigantic magnet is near the geographic north pole.\",\"topic\":\"Earth : A gigantic magnet\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"