etic field intensity?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases\",\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the number of lines of force to the intensity; more lines (concentration) means higher intensity.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic field\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as apparatus for the experiments in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Bar magnet\",\"Iron filings\",\"Electric battery\",\"Cardboard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The apparatus list includes a bar magnet, pins, cardboard, iron filings, plastic bottle, bucket, and water, but not a battery.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic field\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who proposed the concept of magnetic lines of force to explain magnetic effects?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Newton\",\"Michael Faraday\",\"Albert Einstein\",\"Marie Curie\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Michael Faraday proposed the concept of lines of force to explain observed magnetic effects.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction do magnetic lines of force always run?\",\"options\":[\"From the south pole to the north pole\",\"From the center to the poles\",\"From the north pole to the south pole\",\"From the east pole to the west pole\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the second property, magnetic lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Michael Faraday introduce the concept of lines of force?\",\"options\":[\"To measure the weight of a magnet\",\"To explain magnetic attraction and repulsion\",\"To show how magnets are manufactured\",\"To prove that magnets are made of iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Faraday introduced the concept to explain the phenomena of magnetic attraction and repulsion.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the strength of the magnetic field at a particular point?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the magnet\",\"The color of the magnetic poles\",\"The number of magnetic lines at that point\",\"The temperature of the magnet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The sixth property states that the number of magnetic lines at a particular point determines the strength of the magnetic field there.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a physical characteristic of magnetic lines of force?\",\"options\":[\"They are visible to the naked eye\",\"They intersect each other at the center\",\"They are imaginary connecting lines\",\"They are made of thin metal wires\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first property defines magnetic lines of force as imaginary connecting lines.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If two magnetic lines of force are drawn close to each other, what will happen according to the properties mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"They will cross each other\",\"They will merge into one line\",\"They will not intersect each other\",\"They will disappear\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The fifth property explicitly states that magnetic lines of force do not intersect each other.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which property explains why opposite poles of two magnets pull towards each other?\",\"options\":[\"Lines of force are in a state of tension like a stretched spring\",\"Lines of force always run from south to north\",\"Lines of force intersect at the poles\",\"Lines of force are made of solid matter\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that lines of force in a stretched state pull the two poles towards each other, explaining attraction.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what happens when magnetic lines of force encounter each other?\",\"options\":[\"They attract each other\",\"They repel each other\",\"They cancel each other out\",\"They join to form a circle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The fourth property states that magnetic lines of force repel each other.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist observes a very high density of magnetic lines in a specific region, what can be inferred about the magnetic field?\",\"options\":[\"The magnetic field is very weak\",\"The magnetic field is very strong\",\"There is no magnetic field\",\"The magnet is broken\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the number of lines determines strength, a high number of lines indicates a strong magnetic field.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the top section of the given figure, what behavior of the lines of force explains the attraction between the N and S poles?\",\"options\":[\"The lines are breaking apart\",\"The lines are intersecting each other\",\"The lines are in a stretched state pulling the poles together\",\"The lines are running from South to North\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows lines connecting N and S poles which, according to the text, act like a stretched spring to pull poles together.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/139_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the bottom section of the given figure, what causes the lines of force to bend away from the center between the two North (N) poles?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic lines of force attract each other\",\"Magnetic lines of force repel each other\",\"Magnetic lines of force are intersecting\",\"The magnetic field is zero at the poles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows lines bending away because, as per the fourth property, magnetic lines of force repel each other, causing repulsion between like poles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/139_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Magnetic lines of force are described as being in a state of tension similar to which object?\",\"options\":[\"A rigid iron rod\",\"A piece of string\",\"A stretched spring\",\"A liquid flow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The third property states that magnetic lines of force are in a state of tension like a stretched spring.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of magnetic lines of force\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the intensity of a magnetic field when it passes through a cardboard or water?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It is found to decrease\",\"It disappears completely\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in each case (cardboard, bottle, or water), the intensity of the magnetic field is found to decrease.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what property of the magnetic field is being demonstrated?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic poles\",\"Penetrating ability\",\"Magnetic induction\",\"Magnetic repulsion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure title and the experiment show the magnetic field passing through the cardboard to attract pins.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/139_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with the plastic bottle, what is used to show that the magnetic field passes through water?\",\"options\":[\"A plastic lid\",\"A few pins\",\"A basin\",\"Sticking tape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure describes dropping a few pins in the water inside a plastic bottle to observe the magnet\'s effect.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of the following can a magnetic field pass through?\",\"options\":[\"Only air\",\"Only water\",\"Cardboard, a bottle, or water\",\"Only metallic objects\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a magnetic field can pass through a cardboard, a bottle, or water.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the magnet is moved slowly across the top of the cardboard, what will happen to the pins underneath?\",\"options\":[\"They will fall down immediately\",\"They will move along with the magnet\",\"They will stay in their original position\",\"They will lose their magnetism\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As the magnet moves, its field moves with it, causing the attracted pins to move accordingly through the cardboard.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/139_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the basin experiment, what is the purpose of placing the bar magnet on a plastic lid?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the magnet dry\",\"To make the magnet float on the water surface\",\"To increase the magnetic force\",\"To prevent the magnet from attracting the needle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure says to place the magnet on a plastic lid and float it on the surface of the water.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What must be done to the needle before sticking it to the cardboard in the basin experiment?\",\"options\":[\"It must be heated\",\"It must be painted\",\"It must be magnetised\",\"It must be cooled\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The procedure specifies to \'Magnetise a needle or pin\' before sticking it to the cardboard.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you increase the number of layers of cardboard between the magnet and the pins, what is the expected result?\",\"options\":[\"The pins will be attracted more strongly\",\"The intensity of the magnetic field will decrease further\",\"The magnetic field will become stronger\",\"The pins will turn into magnets faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that intensity decreases when passing through materials; adding more layers increases the barrier, further decreasing intensity.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which question from the \'Use your brain power\' section refers to the ability of magnets to exert force without touching an object?\",\"options\":[\"What is meant by magnetic force?\",\"How does a magnetic force act without direct contact?\",\"What is the difference between gravitational force and magnetic force?\",\"How do we magnetise a needle?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The second question in the \'Use your brain power\' section specifically asks about action without direct contact.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the basin experiment, what is the magnetized needle stuck to?\",\"options\":[\"A plastic bottle\",\"A piece of thick cardboard\",\"The bar magnet\",\"The bottom of the basin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Stick this needle firmly to a small piece of thick cardboard by means of a sticking tape.\'\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the observations in the text, what is the relationship between the distance\\/barrier and magnetic intensity?\",\"options\":[\"Barriers have no effect on intensity\",\"Intensity increases as it passes through more water\",\"Intensity decreases as it passes through substances\",\"Intensity only depends on the size of the pins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that while the field passes through materials, the intensity is found to decrease.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a common feature of the cardboard experiment and the bottle experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Both use a basin of water\",\"Both require magnetizing a needle\",\"Both demonstrate that magnetic fields can penetrate non-magnetic materials\",\"Both show that magnetic intensity increases through plastic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both experiments show the magnetic field passing through (penetrating) materials like cardboard, plastic, and water.\",\"topic\":\"Penetrating ability\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"The function of metal detectors is based on which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Permanent magnets\",\"Electromagnets\",\"Static electricity\",\"Gravitational force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the function of these machines (metal detectors) is based on electromagnets.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which industry are metal detectors used to detect iron or steel objects mixed unknowingly in products?\",\"options\":[\"Textile industry\",\"Food-processing industry\",\"Software industry\",\"Automobile industry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions metal detectors are used in the food-processing industry to detect iron\\/steel objects mixed in foodstuff.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why are metal detectors installed at airports and bus stations?\",\"options\":[\"To check the weight of passengers\",\"To detect precious stones\",\"For inspection of persons entering these places\",\"To generate electricity for the building\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states they are used for the inspection of persons entering very important places like airports and bus stations.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the field of geology, what is the purpose of using metal detectors?\",\"options\":[\"To measure the depth of the ocean\",\"To detect the presence and quantity of metals\",\"To predict earthquakes\",\"To study the movement of clouds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that in geology, these machines are used to detect the presence and quantity of metals.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A security guard needs to check a person for hidden metal objects at a temple entrance. Which device mentioned in the text should be used?\",\"options\":[\"A magnifying glass\",\"A metal detector\",\"A thermometer\",\"A barometer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Metal detectors are used for inspection of persons entering important places like temples.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it considered necessary to detect iron or steel objects in foodstuff?\",\"options\":[\"Because they make the food taste bitter\",\"Because they would be harmful to health\",\"Because they change the color of the food\",\"Because they make the food expire faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions these objects are detected because they would be harmful to health.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Besides airports and bus stations, which other specific buildings are mentioned as places where metal detectors are used?\",\"options\":[\"Certain temples\",\"Primary schools\",\"Local grocery shops\",\"Public parks\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists airports, bus stations, certain temples, and buildings as important places where they are used.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What information can a geologist obtain about metals using a metal detector?\",\"options\":[\"The age of the metal\",\"The price of the metal\",\"The quantity of the metal\",\"The melting point of the metal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states geologists use these machines to detect the presence and quantity of metals.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a food company finds steel shards in their flour, which machine helped them identify this hazard?\",\"options\":[\"A microscope\",\"A metal detector\",\"A weighing scale\",\"A centrifuge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Metal detectors are used in the food-processing industry to detect iron\\/steel objects.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a use of metal detectors mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Detecting precious articles\",\"Inspection of persons\",\"Measuring the temperature of metals\",\"Detecting metals in geology\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions detecting precious articles, inspection, and geology, but does not mention measuring temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the purpose of the red lines and arrows shown passing across the person in the large frame?\",\"options\":[\"To provide light for the person to see\",\"To represent the inspection process of the person\",\"To keep the person warm\",\"To measure the height of the person\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a person being inspected at a walk-through metal detector, with the lines indicating the scanning area.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/140_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which of the three shown devices would be most suitable for a geologist to use while searching for metals in the ground?\",\"options\":[\"The walk-through gate at the top right\",\"The small handheld wand at the top left\",\"The device with the long handle and circular base at the bottom\",\"The red sensors on the side of the frame\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The bottom device in the figure is a ground-search metal detector, which is designed for geological or outdoor searches.\",\"topic\":\"Metal detectors\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"46\\/140_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22005,46,7,'Science','en','In the World of Stars','[\"A galaxy is a huge system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity.\",\"The Milky Way is our galaxy; it contains hundreds of billions of stars.\",\"Stars form from nebulae – clouds of hydrogen and dust that collapse under gravity.\",\"When a cloud contracts, its core temperature rises until nuclear fusion starts, creating a new star.\",\"The life of a star is called its lifecycle; it changes slowly over millions of years.\",\"Low‑mass stars become red dwarfs and later white dwarfs after shedding outer layers as planetary nebulae.\",\"High‑mass stars explode as supernovae and may leave behind neutron stars or black holes.\",\"The Sun is a medium‑size star in the middle of its lifecycle, about 4.6 billion years old.\",\"Constellations are patterns of stars that appear close together from Earth’s point of view.\",\"The celestial sphere is an imaginary sphere around Earth on which all stars are projected.\",\"The horizon is the line where the sky meets the ground; it forms a circle when seen from a point on Earth.\",\"The zenith is the point on the celestial sphere directly above you; the nadir is directly below.\",\"Celestial poles are points where Earth’s rotation axis meets the celestial sphere; they are north and south poles of the sky.\",\"The meridian is a great circle passing through the celestial poles, your zenith and nadir.\",\"The celestial equator is the projection of Earth’s equator onto the celestial sphere; it divides the sky into northern and southern halves.\",\"The ecliptic is the apparent path of the Sun on the celestial sphere, 23.5° tilted to the celestial equator.\",\"The Sun appears to move east to west because Earth rotates west to east.\",\"The Pole Star (Polaris) is almost fixed in the sky and is used to find north in the northern hemisphere.\",\"Zodiac signs are 12 equal parts of the ecliptic, each 30° wide, named after constellations like Aries, Taurus, etc.\",\"Nakshatras are 27 lunar mansions in Indian astronomy; each covers about 13°20\' of the ecliptic.\",\"The brightest star in a constellation is often called its main star or reference point.\",\"The Sun’s apparent motion is called its apparent path; it is due to Earth’s orbit around the Sun.\",\"Observing the sky requires a clear view, a dark environment, and knowledge of constellations and celestial coordinates.\",\"Sky watching helps students learn about the motion of Earth and the Sun and the structure of the galaxy.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, stars are born out of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Galaxies\",\"Nebulae\",\"Planets\",\"Black holes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Stars are born out of nebulae.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two main components that make up a nebula?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen gas and ice\",\"Nitrogen gas and rocks\",\"Hydrogen gas and dust particles\",\"Helium gas and water vapor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines nebulae as clouds made up mainly of hydrogen gas and dust particles.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which force is responsible for attracting the particles in a nebula towards one another?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Force of gravity\",\"Electrostatic force\",\"Frictional force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that particles are attracted towards one another due to the force of gravity.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the shape of the cloud as it contracts and becomes dense?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes a flat disc\",\"It becomes a perfect cube\",\"It becomes spherical in shape\",\"It becomes an irregular ring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that as a result of contraction, the cloud becomes dense and spherical in shape.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the temperature to rise tremendously at the core of the contracting cloud?\",\"options\":[\"Decrease in gas pressure\",\"Increase in the pressure of the gas\",\"Expansion of the dust particles\",\"The presence of ice crystals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the pressure of the gas at the core increases, causing the temperature to rise tremendously.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A spherical cloud of hydrogen is officially called a \'star\' when which process begins?\",\"options\":[\"Dust starts to disappear\",\"Gravity stops acting\",\"Energy generation processes start\",\"The cloud starts to expand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a star as a spherical cloud where energy generation processes have started.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a nebula is described as a \'cloud of hydrogen\', what would happen if the force of gravity were removed?\",\"options\":[\"The cloud would contract faster\",\"The cloud would not contract or become dense\",\"The temperature would rise immediately\",\"Energy generation would start instantly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since gravity causes the particles to attract and contract, removing it would prevent the cloud from becoming dense.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is identified as a change that constitutes the \'lifecycle of stars\'?\",\"options\":[\"Instant explosion into a galaxy\",\"Movement from one galaxy to another\",\"Contraction, expansion, and rise in temperature\",\"Turning into a cloud of dust and oxygen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that processes such as contraction, expansion, and rise in temperature constitute the lifecycle of stars.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are different \'types\' of stars identified according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"By the galaxy they belong to\",\"By the amount of dust they contain\",\"By their forms at various stages of their lifecycle\",\"By their distance from the Earth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that different forms of stars at various stages during their lifecycle are identified as different types of stars.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which is the correct sequence of events leading to the birth of a star?\",\"options\":[\"Energy generation -> Contraction -> Nebula\",\"Nebula -> Contraction -> Temperature rise -> Energy generation\",\"Expansion -> Nebula -> Gravity -> Star\",\"Star -> Nebula -> Dust particles -> Gravity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the process starting from a nebula, followed by contraction, temperature rise, and finally energy generation.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the duration of the changes that occur in a star\'s lifecycle?\",\"options\":[\"They happen in a few seconds\",\"They occur over a very long period of time\",\"They happen only during the birth of the star\",\"They are instantaneous processes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that these changes \'occur over a very long period of time\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the relationship between gas pressure and temperature in the core of a nebula as described in the text.\",\"options\":[\"As pressure increases, temperature rises\",\"As pressure increases, temperature falls\",\"Pressure and temperature are unrelated\",\"Temperature rises only when pressure decreases\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the pressure of the gas increases, \'causing\' the temperature to rise tremendously.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the galaxy that our solar system belongs to?\",\"options\":[\"Milky Way\",\"Andromeda\",