e area\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states he could meet the needs of his family sufficiently, without any scarcity.\",\"topic\":\"Decision to Retire\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a worker is described as \'firm-minded\' like the mason, how are they likely to act when pressured to change a decision?\",\"options\":[\"They will easily change their mind\",\"They will stick to their decision regardless of persuasion\",\"They will ask others for advice before deciding\",\"They will become confused and upset\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The mason being \'firm-minded\' meant he refused to carry on despite the contractor\'s urging.\",\"topic\":\"Decision to Retire\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which quality mentioned in the text best explains why the contractor paid the mason \'handsomely\'?\",\"options\":[\"His desire to play with grandchildren\",\"His decision to retire suddenly\",\"His ability to fetch good business through his work\",\"His age and status as a grandfather\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the handsome salary to the fact that the mason fetched good business for the contractor.\",\"topic\":\"Decision to Retire\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the relationship between the mason\'s work ethic and his financial status?\",\"options\":[\"His lack of loyalty led to financial struggles\",\"His devotion and diligence resulted in financial stability for his family\",\"His retirement was caused by a sudden drop in salary\",\"The contractor exploited his hard work by paying him very little\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that his diligence and devotion led to a good salary and no scarcity for his family.\",\"topic\":\"Decision to Retire\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the contractor\'s reaction to the mason\'s retirement. Why was he \'disheartened\'?\",\"options\":[\"He was worried about the mason\'s health\",\"He would lose a worker who was highly productive and valuable\",\"He had to pay the mason a retirement bonus\",\"He wanted to retire himself\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The contractor was disheartened because the mason was an \'excellent workman\' who fetched good business.\",\"topic\":\"Decision to Retire\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What special favour did the contractor ask of the mason?\",\"options\":[\"To repair his own home\",\"To build one last house\",\"To find a new site for construction\",\"To train a new apprentice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the contractor convinced the mason to build one last house as a special favour.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where was the last house to be built?\",\"options\":[\"In a busy marketplace\",\"On a beautiful and convenient site\",\"Near the contractor\'s office\",\"In a remote village\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the house was to be built on a beautiful and convenient site.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How did the mason feel about building this final house?\",\"options\":[\"He was excited to showcase his skills\",\"He was grateful for the opportunity\",\"He agreed with great reluctance\",\"He was happy to work at a sought-after location\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the mason agreed to do so with great reluctance and was not happy.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'reluctance\' mean in the context of the story?\",\"options\":[\"Great enthusiasm\",\"Unwillingness\",\"Quickness\",\"Confidence\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the definition of reluctance as unwillingness.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the mason work \'roughly and carelessly\' on the last house?\",\"options\":[\"He lacked the proper tools\",\"He was not a skilled mason\",\"He could not put his mind and heart in his work\",\"The contractor did not provide a design\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the mason could not put his mind and heart in his work, leading to careless construction.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What was the quality of the building materials used for the last house?\",\"options\":[\"The best available in the market\",\"Low quality\",\"Average quality\",\"Imported materials\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that even the building material used was of low quality.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the house ready earlier than expected?\",\"options\":[\"The mason used modern machinery\",\"The mason wanted to complete the job in haste to retire\",\"The contractor helped with the construction\",\"The design was very simple\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he simply wanted to complete the job in haste and finally retire.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the finished state of the last house?\",\"options\":[\"Strong walls and excellent finish\",\"Exactly as beautiful as the design\",\"Roughly made structures and weak walls\",\"The best house the mason ever built\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the house as having roughly made structures, weak walls, and a poor finish.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What did the contractor have in his hand when he arrived at the site?\",\"options\":[\"A bag of money\",\"A file of legal documents\",\"A new set of building plans\",\"A gift box\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the contractor had a file of legal documents in his hand.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'beaming\' suggest about the contractor\'s mood?\",\"options\":[\"He was very angry\",\"He was confused\",\"He was smiling with great joy\",\"He was feeling tired\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines beaming as smiling with great joy.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was the \'shock\' that awaited the mason at the end of the story?\",\"options\":[\"The contractor refused to pay him\",\"The house collapsed immediately\",\"The house was actually a gift for the mason\",\"The mason was asked to rebuild the house\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The contractor reveals that the house was planned as a gift for the mason, which is the shock mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the contrast in the workmanship of this last house compared to the design?\",\"options\":[\"It was built faster than the design required\",\"It was built with better materials than designed\",\"It did not look as good as designed due to poor finish\",\"It was exactly as the design specified\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes the house did not look as good at all as designed because it was roughly made.\",\"topic\":\"The Contractor\'s Gift\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22062,52,8,'English','en','2.3 The Little River','[\"The Little River is a poem that describes a river in two seasons – summer and monsoon.\",\"In summer the river is only knee‑deep, easy to cross, and the banks are not steep.\",\"During monsoon (Ashadh) the river rises, becomes a spate, and the current grows strong.\",\"The poem uses figures of speech such as alliteration (repeated initial sounds) and onomatopoeia (words that imitate sounds).\",\"Alliteration examples: \\\"meanders along\\\", \\\"mystic moon\\\", \\\"mirthful murmur\\\" – the initial sound is repeated in each line.\",\"Onomatopoeia examples: \\\"babbling\\\", \\\"roar\\\", \\\"swish\\\", \\\"beep\\\" – words that mimic the sound of water or animals.\",\"Key words: meanders (flow with twists), slime (thick slippery substance), kash (tall grass), gamchha (cotton towel), in spate (sudden flood), babbling (talking rapidly), scour (scrub), rife (full of), wade (walk in shallow water).\",\"The poem shows how people use the river for bathing, washing clothes, and cooking, and how the river changes with seasons.\",\"The riddle at the end describes a river: it runs, murmurs, has a bed and a mouth, falls but cannot climb up.\",\"Students should remember that rivers can be clean or polluted; we must keep them clean by not dumping waste.\",\"Alliteration and onomatopoeia add musicality and vividness to poetry.\",\"The poem also highlights the importance of nature and the rhythm of rural life.\",\"In the poem, the river’s banks are described as not too steep, only knee‑deep, and having cool shade.\",\"The poem contrasts summer (calm, shallow) with monsoon (flood, strong current).\",\"The poem encourages us to appreciate the beauty of nature and to respect it.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the \'Chit-Chat\' section, what is one thing you might notice on the banks of a river during a picnic?\",\"options\":[\"Other people\",\"Tall skyscrapers\",\"Large deserts\",\"Snowy mountains\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly asks if you noticed other people by the river.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which season is matched with \'cold\' weather in the textbook exercise?\",\"options\":[\"Summer\",\"Spring\",\"Winter\",\"Autumn\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In section (1)(B), Winter is matched with (d) cold.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the weather matching exercise, which weather condition is associated with the \'Monsoon\'?\",\"options\":[\"Hot\",\"Rainy\",\"Windy\",\"Cool\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In section (1)(B), Monsoon is matched with (f) rainy.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which weather condition is described as \'pleasant\' in the matching exercise?\",\"options\":[\"Spring\",\"Summer\",\"Winter\",\"Pre-Winter\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In section (1)(B), Spring is matched with (c) pleasant.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student goes for a picnic in the month of May, which season from the table are they experiencing?\",\"options\":[\"Summer\",\"Winter\",\"Vasant\",\"Varsha\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table shows that Mid April to Mid June is Summer; May falls within this period.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a family visits a river in July, which Indian season name corresponds to this time according to the table?\",\"options\":[\"Varsha\",\"Hemant\",\"Autumn\",\"Vasant\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table shows that Mid June to Mid August is Varsha; July falls within this period.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the time intervals provided in the table, what is the duration of each Indian season listed?\",\"options\":[\"One month\",\"Two months\",\"Three months\",\"Four months\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Each interval (e.g., Mid February to Mid April) spans exactly two months.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which season is scheduled for the period between Mid August and Mid October according to the table?\",\"options\":[\"Autumn\",\"Summer\",\"Winter\",\"Vasant\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table explicitly lists Autumn for the period of Mid August to Mid October.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is a major environmental concern mentioned in the \'Chit-Chat\' section regarding rivers?\",\"options\":[\"Rivers getting polluted\",\"Rivers flowing too fast\",\"Rivers having too many fish\",\"Rivers being used for picnics\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text asks what should be done to make sure rivers do not get polluted.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the matching exercise, which season is characterized by \'windy\' weather?\",\"options\":[\"Summer\",\"Winter\",\"Autumn\",\"Spring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In section (1)(B), Autumn is matched with (e) windy.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which season is listed for the time period from Mid February to Mid April?\",\"options\":[\"Vasant\",\"Varsha\",\"Hemant\",\"Summer\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first row of the table in the figure shows Vasant for Mid February to Mid April.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/045_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the English name provided for the season \'Hemant\'?\",\"options\":[\"Winter\",\"Pre-winter\",\"Autumn\",\"Summer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The fifth row of the table in the figure lists \'Pre-winter\' as the English name for Hemant.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/045_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the device where words begin with the same sound in the same line?\",\"options\":[\"Prose\",\"Alliteration\",\"Rhyme\",\"Paragraph\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Alliteration as using words that begin with the same sound in the same time.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what do Figures of Speech add to a poem?\",\"options\":[\"Length\",\"Complexity\",\"Beauty\",\"Difficult words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Figures of Speech add to the beauty of a poem.\'\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are words that resemble a sound called?\",\"options\":[\"Sound words\",\"Alliterative words\",\"Onomatopoetic words\",\"Prose words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that words that resemble a sound are called Onomatopoetic words.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a set of similar devices that includes Alliteration?\",\"options\":[\"Figures of Speech\",\"Musical Notes\",\"Prose Writing\",\"Spelling Rules\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Alliteration belongs to a set of similar devices named Figures of Speech.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the device of Alliteration, what is considered more important than the first letter of the spelling?\",\"options\":[\"The last letter\",\"The number of letters\",\"The first sound\",\"The meaning of the word\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes: \'Here, the first sound is important, not the first letter in the spelling.\'\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do poets make poems different from prose according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"By using only long words\",\"By using many poetic devices\",\"By avoiding sounds\",\"By writing in paragraphs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states poets make poems musical and different from prose by using many poetic devices.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of Alliteration mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Bang\",\"Stepping stone\",\"Swish\",\"Roar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Stepping stone\' is listed as an example of words beginning with the same sound.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the text is an example of an Onomatopoetic word?\",\"options\":[\"Beautiful\",\"Knows\",\"Tweet\",\"Site\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'tweet\' as an example of a word that resembles a sound.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'No one knows\' considered an example of Alliteration in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because both words start with the letter \'N\'\",\"Because both words begin with the same sound\",\"Because it is a short phrase\",\"Because it resembles a sound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Even though the letters are different (N and K), they begin with the same sound, which is the rule for Alliteration.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a line in a poem contains the word \'bang\', which Figure of Speech is being used?\",\"options\":[\"Alliteration\",\"Prose\",\"Onomatopoeia\",\"Spelling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says if words resembling a sound occur in a line, the Figure of Speech is called Onomatopoeia.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary purpose of using devices like Alliteration and Onomatopoeia?\",\"options\":[\"To make the poem musical and beautiful\",\"To make the poem harder to read\",\"To teach new spellings\",\"To increase the word count\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions these devices make poems musical and add to their beauty.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the word game mentioned, which of these four-letter words can be formed using letters from \'ONOMATOPOEIA\'?\",\"options\":[\"TEAM\",\"NAME\",\"POET\",\"PANE\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The letters P, O, E, and T are all present in the word ONOMATOPOEIA.\",\"topic\":\"Poetic Devices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what is the depth of the river water during the summer season?\",\"options\":[\"Waist-deep\",\"Knee-deep\",\"Ankle-deep\",\"Neck-deep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'In summer, the water is only knee-deep\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which birds are mentioned as chirping near the white kash fields?\",\"options\":[\"Parrots\",\"Sparrows\",\"Mynahs\",\"Crows\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'Chirping mynahs through that site\' referring to the area with kash fields.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are cows and carts able to cross the river with ease in summer?\",\"options\":[\"Because the river is completely dry\",\"Because the water is shallow and the banks are not too steep\",\"Because there is a bridge over the river\",\"Because the sand is very hard\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem explains that water is knee-deep and the banks, though high, are not too steep, allowing easy crossing.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What do the village wives use to scour their dishes?\",\"options\":[\"Soap and water\",\"Mud from the woods\",\"River-sand\",\"Palm leaves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem says the wives return to household tasks \'Having used river-sand to scour each dish\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a villager wants to catch small fish at dusk, what item from the poem would they use?\",\"options\":[\"A fishing rod\",\"A large net\",\"A washcloth\",\"A bamboo basket\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions that they \'dip washcloths to trap small fish\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which month do the clouds gather and the river waters rise significantly?\",\"options\":[\"Chaitra\",\"Ashadh\",\"Vaishakh\",\"Shravan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem explicitly states, \'In Ashadh, clouds gather, the waters rise\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What change occurs in the river\'s current during the rainy season compared to summer?\",\"options\":[\"The current stops completely\",\"The current becomes clear and slow\",\"The current grows strong and becomes a muddy torrent\",\"The current flows backwards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'the current grows strong\' and describes it as a \'muddy torrent\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'the river’s in spate\' imply about the river\'s condition?\",\"options\":[\"The river has dried up\",\"The river is overflowing or at a very high level\",\"The river is frozen\",\"The river is very narrow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the context of rising waters and strong currents in Ashadh, \'in spate\' refers to a sudden flood or high water level.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the village priests live according to the poem?\",\"options\":[\"In the kash fields\",\"In the muddy torrent\",\"In the cool shade of mango and palm groves\",\"On the high banks of the river\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'Across lie groves of mango and palm; The village priests dwell in their cool shade\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the river in summer, which activity would be most practical for a traveler with a cart?\",\"options\":[\"Waiting for the current to slow down\",\"Crossing the river directly through the water\",\"Using a boat to transport the cart\",\"Avoiding the river due to slime\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the water is only knee-deep and there is no slime, crossing directly is the most practical action described.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which line from the poem is best illustrated by the child\'s action?\",\"options\":[\"\'trap small fish\'\",\"\'Splashing with their gamchhas as they wade\'\",\"\'scour each dish\'\",\"\'meanders along\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a child happily splashing in the water, which matches the description of boys and girls bathing and splashing.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/047_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the child\'s joyful expression most closely represents which sentiment from the final stanza?\",\"options\":[\"The fear of the muddy torrent\",\"The silence of the woods\",\"The village wakening to celebrate the rain\",\"The heat of the summer sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The child\'s happiness reflects the celebratory mood of the village when the rain arrives and the woods stir to life.\",\"topic\":\"The Little River\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/047_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'meanders\' as used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Flow with twists and turns\",\"Flow in a straight line\",\"Stop flowing completely\",\"Flow very slowly\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'meanders\' as flowing with twists and turns.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are \'gamchhas\' according to the glossary?\",\"options\":[\"Heavy woollen coats\",\"Thin cotton towels\",\"Large leather bags\",\"Silk headbands\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'gamchhas\' as thin cotton towels.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these best describes \'kash\'?\",\"options\":[\"A thick slippery substance\",\"A sudden flood in a river\",\"Tall grass with white feathery flowers\",\"A rapid flow of water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The glossary states that \'kash\' is tall grass with white feathery flowers.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does it mean to \'wade\' through the river?\",\"options\":[\"To swim in deep water\",\"To walk in water where it is not deep\",\"To cross the river using a boat\",\"To dive to the bottom of the river\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'wade\' as walking in a source of water where it is not deep.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a river is described as being \'in spate\', what is happening?\",\"options\":[\"The river has dried up\",\"The river is flowing very slowly\",\"There is a sudden flood in the river\",\"The river is full of slippery slime\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'In spate\' is defined in the glossary as a sudden flood in a river.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is asked to \'scour\' their desk, what action should they take based on the glossary?\",\"options\":[\"Paint it\",\"Scrub it\",\"Move it\",\"Break it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'scour\' as to scrub.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When the text mentions the river \'stirs to life\', what does this imply?\",\"options\":[\"The river becomes quiet and still\",\"The river becomes alive with sound and movement\",\"The river water turns into ice\",\"The river stops flowing due to heat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Stir to life\' means to become alive with sound and movement.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the glossary refers to a \'thick slippery substance\'?\",\"options\":[\"Torrent\",\"Slime\",\"Rife\",\"Kash\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'slime\' as a thick slippery substance.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'rife\' as provided in the glossary?\",\"options\":[\"Empty of\",\"Full of\",\"Far from\",\"Next to\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'rife\' as \'full of\'.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a \'torrent\' differ from a normal flow of water based on the definitions?\",\"options\":[\"It is much slower\",\"It is a rapid flow of water\",\"It is water that does not move\",\"It is water found only in \'gamchhas\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'torrent\' as a rapid flow of water.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary and Marginal Questions\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22063,52,8,'English','en','2.4 Excuses ! (A Skit)','[\"- Excuses are reasons given when we are late or miss something.\",\"- Punctuality means arriving on time; it shows respect and responsibility.\",\"- Direct speech records exact words spoken; indirect (reported) speech conveys the meaning without quoting.\",\"- In indirect speech, pronouns, tenses, and time words often change (e.g., tomorrow → next day).\",\"- Common excuses for being late include traffic, bad weather, illness, or family problems.\",\"- Teachers may use humour or jokes to make learning about excuses fun.\",\"- A skit is a short play that helps students practice speaking and listening.\",\"- When writing excuses, use polite words like “I’m sorry” and give a brief reason.\",\"- In a story‑writing contest, students must follow the given theme and time limit.\",\"- Good excuses are honest, brief, and show that the student cares about the situation.\",\"- Teachers can correct lateness by setting clear rules, giving reminders, and encouraging punctuality.\",\"- The chapter encourages students to think about why being on time matters in life and school.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What action was Asif performing when he entered the class?\",\"options\":[\"Running\",\"Walking\",\"Crawling\",\"Jumping\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Asif was \'crawling into the class\'.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the road sign say in Sharvari\'s joke?\",\"options\":[\"Stop\",\"Go Slow\",\"No Entry\",\"School Ahead\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sharvari mentions a road sign that says, \'Go Slow\'.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Chit-Chat section, what is considered \'important in life\'?\",\"options\":[\"Making jokes\",\"Punctuality\",\"Crawling\",\"Road signs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks, \'Why is punctuality so important in life?\'\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific warning did the teacher give Asif the previous day?\",\"options\":[\"Not to come to school\",\"Not to crawl in class\",\"Not to walk into the class late\",\"Not to follow road signs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Asif says, \'yesterday you warned me not to walk into the class late.\'\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Sharvari feel she was right to come to school late?\",\"options\":[\"She was tired of walking\",\"She was following the teacher\'s instruction to obey road signs\",\"She wanted to make the teacher laugh\",\"She forgot the way to school\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sharvari explains that the teacher instructed them to \'always follow what road signs say\'.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the teacher\'s tone when asking Sharvari if she feels \'ashamed\'?\",\"options\":[\"Happy\",\"Encouraging\",\"Disappointed or Stern\",\"Confused\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher is addressing Sharvari\'s regular lateness, implying a stern or disappointed tone.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the Chit-Chat, what are \'fake\' excuses?\",\"options\":[\"Excuses that are true\",\"Excuses that are made up or untrue\",\"Excuses given by teachers\",\"Excuses written in road signs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text contrasts \'true\' with \'fake\' excuses for late-coming.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text ask students to do with the excuse words in the jokes?\",\"options\":[\"Delete them\",\"Underline them\",\"Translate them\",\"Ignore them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks: \'Can you underline the words in the joke that were used as excuses...?\'\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to write a joke in the same format as the text, how should it be structured?\",\"options\":[\"As a long paragraph\",\"As a poem with rhyming words\",\"As a dialogue between Teacher and Student\",\"As a list of road signs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the jokes in a \'Teacher : [Name] : [Dialogue]\' format.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which question from the Chit-Chat helps a student reflect on their own school habits?\",\"options\":[\"Do you come to school on time, everyday?\",\"What does the road sign say?\",\"Why was Asif crawling?\",\"Who is the teacher?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first Chit-Chat question asks about the student\'s own punctuality.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the logical flaw in Asif\'s excuse?\",\"options\":[\"He forgot to come to school\",\"He misinterpreted \'not walking late\' as a command to crawl instead of being on time\",\"He didn\'t know how to crawl\",\"He was actually on time\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Asif took the word \'walk\' too literally to avoid the specific act, rather than the lateness itself.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does Sharvari use the teacher\'s own words against her?\",\"options\":[\"By saying the teacher is also late\",\"By claiming the teacher told her to stay home\",\"By using the teacher\'s instruction to follow signs as a reason for her delay\",\"By refusing to speak to the teacher\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sharvari justifies her lateness by citing the teacher\'s own instruction to follow road signs like \'Go Slow\'.\",\"topic\":\"Enjoy the jokes !\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is it called when the exact words of the speaker are reported?\",\"options\":[\"Indirect speech\",\"Direct speech\",\"Reported speech\",\"Narrative speech\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Direct speech as reporting the exact words of the speaker.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When only the contents of a speaker\'s words are reported instead of the exact words, it is called ______.\",\"options\":[\"Direct speech\",\"Formal speech\",\"Indirect speech\",\"Oral speech\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when exact words are not reported, but just its contents, it is Indirect speech.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the Direct speech example provided, which punctuation mark is used to enclose the speaker\'s exact words?\",\"options\":[\"Brackets\",\"Hyphens\",\"Quotation marks\",\"Apostrophes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The example shows Amar\'s words inside quotation marks: \\\"I shall come to meet you tomorrow, Sanket.\\\"\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the example, what does the word \'tomorrow\' change to in Indirect speech?\",\"options\":[\"The previous day\",\"The next day\",\"That day\",\"Today\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the Indirect speech example, \'tomorrow\' is replaced by \'the next day\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the transformation from Direct to Indirect speech in the text, what happens to the word \'shall\'?\",\"options\":[\"It remains \'shall\'\",\"It changes to \'should\'\",\"It changes to \'will\'\",\"It changes to \'would\'\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text shows \'I shall come\' changing to \'he would go\' in the Indirect version.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which conjunction is added in the Indirect speech example to connect the reporting verb to the reported content?\",\"options\":[\"because\",\"that\",\"if\",\"whether\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Indirect speech example uses \'that\' after \'told Sanket\'.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the example, if \'I\' refers to Amar in Direct speech, what does it change to in Indirect speech?\",\"options\":[\"you\",\"we\",\"he\",\"they\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the pronoun \'I\' changing to \'he\' in the Indirect speech sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the example, the reporting verb \'said\' in Direct speech is changed to which word in Indirect speech?\",\"options\":[\"asked\",\"told\",\"spoke\",\"shouted\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows \'Amar said\' changing to \'Amar told Sanket\' in Indirect speech.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the change in the verb \'come\' from the Direct speech example. What does it become in the Indirect speech example?\",\"options\":[\"came\",\"coming\",\"go\",\"gone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The example shows \'come to meet\' in Direct speech changing to \'go to meet\' in Indirect speech.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the Direct speech example, who is the listener being addressed by Amar?\",\"options\":[\"The teacher\",\"Sanket\",\"The reporter\",\"Amar himself\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Direct speech sentence ends with the name \'Sanket\', identifying the person being spoken to.\",\"topic\":\"Reported Speech\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who are the two characters present in the classroom scene?\",\"options\":[\"Ritesh and Dr. Jadhav\",\"Mrs. Sen and a shopkeeper\",\"Ritesh and Mrs. Sen\",\"Mrs. Sen and a thief\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the characters as Ritesh, a school student, and Mrs. Sen, his class teacher.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the shopkeeper shouting when he ran out of the shop?\",\"options\":[\"Help! Help!\",\"Stop him!\",\"Chor! Chor! catch him!\",\"Police! Police!\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to Ritesh, the shopkeeper ran out shouting, \'Chor! Chor! catch him!\'\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where was the man lying when Ritesh first saw him?\",\"options\":[\"In the middle of the market\",\"Near the pavement\",\"Inside a shop\",\"In a narrow lane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh states there was a person lying near the pavement groaning with pain.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the man near the pavement doing when Ritesh found him?\",\"options\":[\"He was shouting for help\",\"He was running with a packet\",\"He was groaning with pain\",\"He was calling Dr. Jadhav\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the person was groaning with pain near the pavement.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which period was about to start or had started when Ritesh arrived?\",\"options\":[\"First period\",\"Second period\",\"Third period\",\"Last period\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mrs. Sen sarcastically tells Ritesh he is \'too early for the second period.\'\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In what sense did Mrs. Sen say Ritesh was \'early\'?\",\"options\":[\"He arrived before school started\",\"He was early for the next day\",\"He was so late for the first period that he was early for the second\",\"He finished his homework early\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mrs. Sen uses sarcasm to point out that he has missed the first period entirely.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Ritesh deviate from his usual path to the market?\",\"options\":[\"He wanted to buy something\",\"He heard a scream from the main road\",\"He saw an earthquake happening\",\"He was chasing a thief\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh says he heard a scream from the main road and went to investigate if someone needed help.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where was Ritesh when he heard the scream?\",\"options\":[\"At the school gate\",\"Inside the market\",\"Halfway down the narrow lane\",\"At Dr. Jadhav\'s clinic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh specifies he was halfway down the narrow lane that leads to the market.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What did the man coming out of the shop have in his hand?\",\"options\":[\"A stick\",\"A packet\",\"A bag of money\",\"Nothing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh describes a man running out of the shop with a packet in his hand.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When Ritesh says \'I started well in time from home,\' what is he trying to imply?\",\"options\":[\"He left home late\",\"He intended to reach school on time\",\"He forgot his books at home\",\"He walked very slowly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Starting \'well in time\' means he left early enough to not be late under normal circumstances.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does Ritesh react to Mrs. Sen\'s sarcastic comment about being early?\",\"options\":[\"He laughs at the joke\",\"He gets angry\",\"He is confused and asks \'Pardon, Ma\'am?\'\",\"He ignores her and sits down\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh does not initially catch the sarcasm and asks for clarification.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which word best describes Mrs. Sen\'s attitude when she mentions an \'earthquake\'?\",\"options\":[\"Concerned\",\"Skeptical\",\"Terrified\",\"Believing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By suggesting an earthquake delayed him, she shows she expects a wild, unbelievable excuse.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was the \'most amazing thing\' that happened at the end of Ritesh\'s first excuse?\",\"options\":[\"The shopkeeper caught the thief\",\"The groaning man suddenly got up and ran with the thief\",\"Dr. Jadhav arrived at the scene\",\"The packet turned into gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh claims the man who was supposedly in pain suddenly got up and ran with the thief.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the sudden recovery of the \'groaning man\' suggest about the situation?\",\"options\":[\"He was miraculously healed\",\"He was likely an accomplice to the thief\",\"Ritesh is a doctor\",\"The man was scared of the shopkeeper\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The fact that he was \'groaning\' but then ran with the thief implies his injury was a fake part of a plan.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Mrs. Sen mention Dr. Jadhav before Ritesh could finish his story?\",\"options\":[\"She was sick\",\"She was trying to guess Ritesh\'s next predictable excuse\",\"Dr. Jadhav is Ritesh\'s father\",\"The man on the pavement asked for him\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"She anticipates that Ritesh will use a medical emergency as his next excuse for being late.\",\"topic\":\"The First Excuse\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to Ritesh\'s description, what color was the dog sitting on his bag?\",\"options\":[\"Black\",\"White\",\"Brown\",\"Grey\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh describes the dog as a \'huge, hairy, brown dog\' in the text.\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where did Ritesh realize he had left his school-bag?\",\"options\":[\"At the police station\",\"At the shop\",\"At the corner\",\"On the bus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh states, \'I realized that I had left my bag at the shop in the mad rush.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the shopkeeper provide to help Ritesh get his bag back?\",\"options\":[\"A stick\",\"A whistle\",\"Some biscuits\",\"A leash\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'Finally the shopkeeper gave me some biscuits.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Ritesh unable to take the people he was chasing to the police station?\",\"options\":[\"He forgot the way\",\"They vanished around the corner\",\"The police station was closed\",\"He had to go back for his bag\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh explains that they couldn\'t catch them because \'They just vanished around the corner.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'he wouldn\'t budge\', what does the word \'budge\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"To bark loudly\",\"To eat quickly\",\"To move or shift\",\"To sleep soundly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In this context, \'budge\' refers to the dog refusing to move off the bag.\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the shopkeeper surprised by the presence of the dog?\",\"options\":[\"The dog was very small\",\"The dog was eating his goods\",\"He didn\'t know where the dog had come from\",\"The dog was wearing a school-bag\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Even the shopkeeper was surprised. He didn\'t know where the dog had come from.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these methods did Ritesh and the shopkeeper try FIRST to move the dog before using food?\",\"options\":[\"Calling the police\",\"Whistling and clapping\",\"Throwing water\",\"Offering a toy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'whistling and clapping\' as things they tried to make the dog move.\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How did Ritesh finally succeed in getting the dog away from his bag?\",\"options\":[\"By scaring it with a stick\",\"By whistling loudly\",\"By luring it with biscuits\",\"By waiting for it to fall asleep\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh says, \'I offered the biscuits to the dog and lured him away from the bag.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How much time did Mrs. Sen calculate the dog incident took?\",\"options\":[\"Five minutes\",\"Ten minutes\",\"Fifteen minutes\",\"Twenty minutes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mrs. Sen says, \'OK. So let\'s say that accounts for fifteen minutes.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what physical characteristic of the dog mentioned in the text is clearly visible?\",\"options\":[\"It is wearing a collar\",\"It is hairy\",\"It is barking\",\"It is eating biscuits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the dog as \'hairy\', which is depicted by the texture lines on the dog in the drawing.\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the dog doing that confirms Ritesh\'s excuse?\",\"options\":[\"It is chasing Ritesh\",\"It is sitting\\/lying on the green bag\",\"It is running out of the shop\",\"It is biting the school-bag\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows the dog positioned directly on top of the school-bag, preventing access to it.\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/052_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What was the dog\'s vocal reaction when Ritesh tried to make it move?\",\"options\":[\"It barked loudly\",\"It whimpered\",\"It growled\",\"It stayed silent\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh specifically mentions, \'How that dog growled!\' when they tried to move it.\",\"topic\":\"The Dog and the Bag\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did Ritesh initially describe the dog to Mrs. Sen?\",\"options\":[\"Big, scary, and loud\",\"Poor, lonely, and small\",\"Happy, young, and playful\",\"Lost, hungry, and angry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh describes the dog as \'poor and lonely and small\' in the first line of the text.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Ritesh, who did he ask for help while looking for the dog\'s owner?\",\"options\":[\"The police and his friends\",\"His parents and neighbors\",\"Shopkeepers and passers-by\",\"The school principal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh mentions he began to enquire with other shopkeepers and passers-by.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What competition did Mrs. Sen suggest Ritesh should enter?\",\"options\":[\"A drawing competition\",\"A dog training competition\",\"A running race\",\"A story-writing competition\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Mrs. Sen asks Ritesh if he would like to send an entry for a story-writing competition.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did Ritesh do immediately after the puppy found its owner?\",\"options\":[\"He went back to the shopkeepers\",\"He ran straight to school\",\"He stayed to talk to the owner\",\"He looked for another puppy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh says, \'Then I turned and ran straight to school.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Ritesh correct his description of the dog from \'small\' to \'young\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it was actually big in size\",\"Because it was a very old dog\",\"Because he forgot what it looked like\",\"Because Mrs. Sen told him he was wrong\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ritesh clarifies that it was \'big in size but it was only a puppy, really.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Ritesh feel he couldn\'t leave the dog behind?\",\"options\":[\"He wanted to keep it as a pet\",\"He thought it was asking for his help\",\"The dog was blocking his way\",\"The shopkeepers told him to take it\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ritesh says, \'I thought it was asking for my help.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How did the owner of the puppy eventually get found?\",\"options\":[\"The shopkeepers identified the owner\",\"Ritesh called the owner on the phone\",\"The puppy saw the owner across the road and ran to them\",\"Mrs. Sen helped Ritesh find the owner\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh explains that the puppy saw someone across the road and bounded towards them.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What reason did Ritesh give for not wanting to enter the competition?\",\"options\":[\"He was too busy with schoolwork\",\"He didn\'t like his teacher\",\"He claimed he wasn\'t good at making up stories\",\"He wanted to spend time with the puppy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ritesh says, \'I wouldn\'t know what to write. I\'m not good at making up stories.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the text describes the puppy\'s movement when it saw its owner?\",\"options\":[\"Crawled\",\"Bounded\",\"Limped\",\"Walked\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the puppy \'just bounded towards the person.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Ritesh was \'enquiring\' with passers-by, what was he doing?\",\"options\":[\"Selling the puppy to them\",\"Asking them questions to get information\",\"Ignoring them while he walked\",\"Giving them directions to the school\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Enquiring means asking for information, which Ritesh did to find the owner.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is ironic about Ritesh saying \'I\'m not good at making up stories\'?\",\"options\":[\"He is actually a famous author\",\"He had just told a very elaborate story as an excuse for being late\",\"Mrs. Sen knows he is the best writer in class\",\"He has already won many writing competitions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The irony lies in the fact that Ritesh has been providing a long, unlikely story as an excuse for his lateness.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does Mrs. Sen perceive Ritesh\'s actions regarding the dog?\",\"options\":[\"She thinks he was wasting time\",\"She thinks he was being dishonest\",\"She refers to his actions as \'good deeds\'\",\"She thinks he should have brought the dog to school\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mrs. Sen says, \'Now that your good deeds are done...\', referring to his help with the dog.\",\"topic\":\"The Puppy and the Owner\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the phrase \'nothing short of\' as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"nothing less than\",\"something very small\",\"a short distance\",\"almost nothing\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, \'nothing short of\' means \'nothing less than\'.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To \'carry out a systematic search to discover truth about some incident\' is the definition of which word?\",\"options\":[\"account for\",\"investigate\",\"deal with\",\"budge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'investigate\' as carrying out a systematic search to discover the truth.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the vocabulary list, what is a \'pavement\'?\",\"options\":[\"The middle of a highway\",\"A track for racing cars\",\"A footpath of the sides of a road\",\"A wall made of stones\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'pavement\' specifically as a footpath on the sides of a road.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is \'groaning\', what kind of sounds are they making?\",\"options\":[\"Happy sounds\",\"Painful sounds\",\"Angry sounds\",\"Silent sounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'groaning\' means making painful sounds.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these phrases is synonymous with the word \'to handle\'?\",\"options\":[\"to deal with\",\"to budge\",\"to lure\",\"to vanish\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'to handle\' as the meaning for the phrase \'to deal with\'.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when something has \'vanished\'?\",\"options\":[\"It has grown larger\",\"It has moved slightly\",\"It has disappeared\",\"It has been found\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The vocabulary list defines \'vanished\' as \'disappeared\'.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a wild animal makes angry sounds at a traveler, which word from the text describes this action?\",\"options\":[\"groaned\",\"growled\",\"lured\",\"relieved\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'growled\' as angry sounds made by animals.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a heavy rock is stuck and does not \'move\' even when pushed, it does not:\",\"options\":[\"investigate\",\"vanish\",\"budge\",\"account for\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'budge\' as \'move\'.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When a person is \'attracted to do something\', often by a trick or temptation, they have been:\",\"options\":[\"relieved\",\"lured\",\"investigated\",\"dealt with\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'lured\' as being attracted to do something.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which word describes the feeling of being \'no longer worried\'?\",\"options\":[\"relieved\",\"groaning\",\"budge\",\"accounts for\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'relieved\' as being no longer worried.\",\"topic\":\"Vocabulary\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22064,52,8,'English','en','2.5 A Heroine of the Sea','[\"Anchor – a T‑shaped iron bar that holds a ship in place.\",\"Beam – the widest part of a ship, measured from side to side.\",\"Bow – the front end of a ship.\",\"Bilge\\/Keel – the bottom part of a ship, running along the centre.\",\"Bridge\\/Cockpit\\/Wheel House – the control cabin where the ship is steered.\",\"Crow’s Nest – a small tower or post at the top of a ship from where a sailor can look out.\",\"Hull – the main body of a ship that is above the water level.\",\"Part holes – small windows on the side of a ship.\",\"Grace Darling – a brave girl who rescued shipwreck survivors in 1838.\",\"Lighthouse – a tall tower with a light that warns ships of dangerous rocks.\",\"Shipwreck – a ship that is destroyed by hitting rocks or sinking.\",\"Rescue – to save people from danger.\",\"Oars – poles with blades used to row a boat.\",\"The story shows courage, self‑lessness and determination.\",\"Grace’s father was wise, educated and taught honesty and bravery.\",\"Grace was small but strong and believed she could row as well as a man.\",\"The rescue took place near the rocks on the east coast of England.\",\"Grace and her family became famous through newspapers and public praise.\",\"The story teaches that courage can come from anyone, regardless of gender or size.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where are the lonely lighthouses mentioned in the text located?\",\"options\":[\"Along the sandy beaches of France\",\"Around the rocky coast of England\",\"Near the busy ports of India\",\"On the high mountains of Scotland\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the lighthouses stand around the rocky coast of England.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did the shipwreck mentioned in the story take place?\",\"options\":[\"1883\",\"1938\",\"1838\",\"1738\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the shipwreck occurred one night in 1838.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of the lighthouse lights according to the passage?\",\"options\":[\"To help sailors find fish at night\",\"To light up the coast for tourists\",\"To warn sailors of dangerous rocks\",\"To signal the arrival of a storm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the lights warn sailors at night of dangerous rocks.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What do sailors do when they see the lighthouse lights?\",\"options\":[\"They bring their ships closer to the shore\",\"They take their ships far out to sea\",\"They stop their ships immediately\",\"They signal back to the lighthouse\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The passage states that when sailors see the lights, they take their ships far out to sea.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what causes ships to be driven onto the rocks despite the warnings?\",\"options\":[\"Mechanical failure of the ships\",\"Fierce storms\",\"Lack of oil in the lighthouses\",\"Errors made by the sailors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that \'sometimes fierce storms drive ships on to the rocks.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a sailor is navigating near the English coast at night and sees a bright light, what is he likely to conclude?\",\"options\":[\"There is a city nearby\",\"There are dangerous rocks ahead\",\"The sun is rising early\",\"Another ship is approaching\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that lighthouse lights warn