s\":[\"Naresh\",\"The Doctor\",\"Asif\",\"A trekking guide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The teacher says, \'Asif go and bring the First Aid Box.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what specific symbol is printed on the First Aid Box?\",\"options\":[\"A red heart\",\"A blue circle\",\"A green cross\",\"A yellow triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows a large green cross symbol on the front of the First Aid Box.\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What should be applied to the wound immediately before covering it with cotton?\",\"options\":[\"Distilled water\",\"Antiseptic cream\",\"A bandage\",\"Soap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The manual instructs to apply antiseptic cream on the wound and then cover it with cotton.\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final instruction given in the First Aid Manual?\",\"options\":[\"Wash the wound again\",\"Apply more cream\",\"Immediately consult the nearest doctor\",\"Go back to trekking\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The last point in the manual is to consult the nearest doctor after taking First Aid.\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which items are visible outside the box?\",\"options\":[\"Scissors and a thermometer\",\"Bottles and strips of tablets\",\"A roll of cotton and tape\",\"A stethoscope and gloves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows two bottles and two blue strips of tablets next to the box.\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the manual, what material is used to bind up the wound?\",\"options\":[\"A piece of cloth\",\"A bandage\",\"Sticky tape\",\"A plastic wrap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The manual specifically says to \'Bind up the wound with a bandage.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the teacher direct Asif to the First Aid Manual?\",\"options\":[\"Because Asif didn\'t know how to treat Naresh\",\"Because the manual was missing from the box\",\"To check if the manual was written in English\",\"To find the doctor\'s phone number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"When Asif asked how to treat Naresh with the kit, the teacher told him to go through the manual.\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these materials is specifically mentioned to cover the cream-applied wound?\",\"options\":[\"Bandage\",\"Gauze\",\"Cotton\",\"Paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The manual says \'Apply antiseptic cream on wound and cover it with cotton.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student gets a minor cut during a school trip, what is the most appropriate action based on the teacher\'s example?\",\"options\":[\"Wait until the trip is over to see a doctor\",\"Use the First Aid Kit and follow the manual\'s steps\",\"Only wash it with normal tap water\",\"Ignore the injury if it is not bleeding much\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The teacher demonstrates using the First Aid Box and following the manual for minor injuries.\",\"topic\":\"Live English\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following items is explicitly listed in the \'Warming Up!\' section as part of a First Aid Kit?\",\"options\":[\"Thermometer\",\"Antiseptic cream\",\"Stethoscope\",\"Painkiller spray\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Antiseptic cream is one of the eight items listed in the First Aid Kit section of the text.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Chit-Chat section, how many \'Wh\' words are typically used to frame questions?\",\"options\":[\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\",\"Six\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically asks, \'Which five Wh words are used to frame questions?\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the third step suggested in the First Aid Kit activity?\",\"options\":[\"Discuss uses in pairs\",\"Search on the Internet\",\"Make your own kit for home\",\"Share with the class\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The third numbered instruction in the First Aid Kit activity is \'Make your own First Aid Kit for your home.\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the Chit-Chat questions, what is the relationship between questions and critical thinking?\",\"options\":[\"Questions prevent critical thinking\",\"We cannot think critically without questions\",\"Critical thinking is only for childhood\",\"Questions are not useful in life\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'Do we think critically, without questions?\', implying that questions are necessary for critical thinking.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which question word should be used if the expected answer is \'Mumbai\' for the capital of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Who\",\"Where\",\"What\",\"Which\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the exercise, \'What\' is the proper question word to identify a specific thing or name like a capital city.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To get the answer \'On S.B. Road\' regarding a school\'s location, which word must be inserted?\",\"options\":[\"When\",\"Where\",\"How\",\"Whose\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Where\' is used to ask about place or location, such as \'On S.B. Road\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which question word is used to describe the manner of an action, such as an old man walking \'Slowly\'?\",\"options\":[\"Why\",\"What\",\"How\",\"Which\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'How\' is the question word used to describe the manner or way in which an action is performed.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the answer is \'Next Monday\', which question word correctly completes the sentence about the examination?\",\"options\":[\"When\",\"Where\",\"Who\",\"Why\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"\'When\' is the correct question word for time-related answers like \'Next Monday\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which question word is appropriate when the answer involves a reason, such as \'My bicycle got punctured\'?\",\"options\":[\"Whose\",\"Which\",\"Why\",\"Who\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Why\' is used to ask for a reason or cause.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'..... pen have you borrowed?\', what is the correct word if the answer is \'Aman\'s pen\'?\",\"options\":[\"Who\",\"Whose\",\"Which\",\"Where\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Whose\' is used to ask about possession, corresponding to the answer \'Aman\'s\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When the answer is a specific person like \'Mrs Ashwini Patil\', which question word is used to ask who teaches English?\",\"options\":[\"Who\",\"Whose\",\"Which\",\"What\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"\'Who\' is the question word used to identify a person.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which question word is used to identify a specific choice among many, such as a favorite \'Blue\' colour?\",\"options\":[\"What\",\"How\",\"Which\",\"Where\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Which\' is used to ask about a specific choice or preference from a group.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up !\",\"part\":22,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the main character described in the poem?\",\"options\":[\"A curious little boy\",\"A brave sailor\",\"A wise teacher\",\"A busy fisherman\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first line of the poem introduces \'a curious little boy\'.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'ebb\' mean according to the glossary?\",\"options\":[\"Tidewater moves away from the land\",\"Tidewater moves towards the land\",\"Water boiling in a kettle\",\"Wind blowing from a bellows\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'ebb\' as tidewater moving away from the land.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, where does the boy want to know seeds get into?\",\"options\":[\"The apple-core\",\"The kettle\'s spout\",\"The bellows\' nose\",\"The spider\'s web\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem asks \'How seeds get in the apple-core\'.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is \'marrow\' described as in the textbook notes?\",\"options\":[\"A soft fatty substance in bone cavities\",\"A type of seed found in apples\",\"A respiratory organ used by fish\",\"A toy gun used by children\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'marrow\' as a soft fatty substance in the cavities of bones.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the boy in the poem described as \'curious\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because he is always asking \'Why?\'\",\"Because he knows all the answers already\",\"Because he never speaks to anyone\",\"Because he likes to play with pop-guns\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The boy is called curious because he is constantly asking questions about everything he sees.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what does the boy want to know about lead and marbles?\",\"options\":[\"Why they sink\",\"Why they swim\",\"Why they shine\",\"Why they melt\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem states he wants to know \'Why lead and marbles sink\'.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of a \'kettle’s spout\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A pipe through which liquid can be poured\",\"A tool used for blowing air into a fire\",\"A part of a fish used for breathing\",\"The center part of a fruit like an apple\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines it as a pipe through which tea\\/liquid can be poured.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What two things does the boy want to know about the clouds?\",\"options\":[\"What makes them and why they cross the sky\",\"Why they are white and how they bring rain\",\"Why they disappear and where they go at night\",\"How they stay up and why they turn dark\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem says he wants to know \'what makes the clouds, and why they cross the sky\'.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the boy sees a piece of iron sinking in water, which other objects from the poem would he compare it to?\",\"options\":[\"Lead and marbles\",\"Wood and clouds\",\"Stars and sun\",\"Ice and dough\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The boy observes that lead and marbles sink, so he would relate iron sinking to them.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the boy were to observe steam rising from a boiling pot, which question from the poem would he likely relate it to?\",\"options\":[\"Why steam comes from the kettle’s spout\",\"Why wind comes from the bellows’ nose\",\"Why pop-guns should go pop\",\"Why coal should burn\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The boy asks about steam coming from a kettle, which is directly related to steam from a boiling pot.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is defined as a \'thick mixture of flour, water etc.\'?\",\"options\":[\"Dough\",\"Yeast\",\"Marrow\",\"Bellows\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text in the figure explicitly defines dough as a thick mixture of flour, water, etc.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/067_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the poet mean by saying some \'whys\' are \'not too hard to answer\'?\",\"options\":[\"Some questions have known reasons that can be explained\",\"The boy is not asking enough questions\",\"All questions in the world are impossible to answer\",\"The boy already knows the answers to his own questions\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It implies that for some of the boy\'s questions, humans have already found the scientific reasons.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The boy asks why \'wood should swim\' but \'lead and marbles sink\'. What is he trying to understand?\",\"options\":[\"The difference in how different objects behave in water\",\"Why wood is heavier than lead and marbles\",\"Why marbles are made out of wood\",\"Why lead is the best material for swimming\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He is contrasting the buoyancy of wood with the sinking of lead and marbles.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the poem, which question shows the boy\'s interest in the behavior of insects?\",\"options\":[\"Why spiders eat the flies\",\"Why bees should sting\",\"Why fish have gills\",\"Why flowers die\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Asking why spiders eat flies is a question about the predatory behavior of insects\\/arachnids.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text in the given figure, what is the dough ready for after it is mixed?\",\"options\":[\"To be baked into bread\",\"To be eaten raw immediately\",\"To be thrown away in the bin\",\"To be dried in the sun for days\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure text states that dough is \'ready to be baked into bread\'.\",\"topic\":\"“Why?”\",\"part\":23,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/067_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of \'Repetition\' as per the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"A figure of speech where words rhyme at the end of lines\",\"A figure of speech in which one or more words are repeated in the same sentence\",\"A figure of speech where opposite ideas are put together\",\"A figure of speech that compares two different things using \'like\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Repetition as a Figure of Speech where one or more words are repeated in the same sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'Son, son it gives me a great joy\', which word is repeated?\",\"options\":[\"great\",\"joy\",\"son\",\"gives\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The word \'son\' is used twice in the sentence, making it an example of Repetition.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the line \'Why this, Why that, Why then, Why now...\' called Repetition?\",\"options\":[\"Because it asks many questions\",\"Because the word \'Why\' is repeated multiple times\",\"Because the words rhyme with each other\",\"Because it describes a heroine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states it is Repetition because the word \'why\' is repeated in the lines.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many words can be repeated to form the Figure of Speech \'Repetition\'?\",\"options\":[\"Only one word exactly\",\"Only two words at the start\",\"One or more words\",\"Exactly six words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definition specifically states \'one or more words\' are repeated.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the textbook\'s definition, which of the following is an example of Repetition?\",\"options\":[\"The sea was very calm and quiet.\",\"Work, work, work is all he does.\",\"She ran as fast as a deer.\",\"The sun shines brightly in the sky.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Repeating the word \'work\' follows the definition of repeating one or more words in a sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a poet writes \'Rain, rain, go away\', which Figure of Speech are they using based on the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Rhyme\",\"Repetition\",\"Simile\",\"Alliteration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The repetition of the word \'rain\' fits the definition of Repetition provided in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the example \'Why this, Why that, Why then, Why now, Why not, Why by-and-by?\', how many times is the word \'Why\' repeated?\",\"options\":[\"4 times\",\"5 times\",\"6 times\",\"2 times\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Counting the occurrences in the provided text: 1 (this), 2 (that), 3 (then), 4 (now), 5 (not), 6 (by-and-by).\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of using \'Repetition\' in a sentence as suggested by the examples?\",\"options\":[\"To make the sentence shorter\",\"To emphasize a specific word or idea\",\"To ensure the sentence has a rhyming pair\",\"To change the meaning of the word\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Repeating words like \'Why\' or \'Son\' serves to emphasize those specific elements within the sentence structure.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best explains the difference between Rhyming Pairs and Repetition based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Rhyming pairs repeat the same word, while Repetition uses different words.\",\"Rhyming pairs involve similar sounds, while Repetition involves the exact same word(s) being used again.\",\"There is no difference between the two.\",\"Repetition only happens at the end of a sentence.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text treats rhyming pairs as a separate exercise (Task 2) from the repetition of the exact word \'Why\' (Task 3).\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the requirement for a sentence to be classified as \'Repetition\' according to the provided definition.\",\"options\":[\"The repeated words must be at the end of the sentence.\",\"The repeated words must be nouns.\",\"The repetition must occur within the same sentence.\",\"The words must be repeated in every sentence of the poem.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definition explicitly states the repetition happens \'in the same sentence\'.\",\"topic\":\"Definition of Repetition\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22065,52,8,'English','en','3.1 “Why?”','[\"Why questions help us find reasons and causes.\",\"Question words: Who, What, When, Where, Why, Which, Whose.\",\"Five ‘Wh’ words used in questions: Who, What, When, Where, Why.\",\"Questions help us learn, solve problems, and think critically.\",\"Answering questions gives us knowledge and understanding.\",\"We ask questions to satisfy curiosity and to learn new facts.\",\"Questions can be open-ended or closed-ended.\",\"Using the right question word gives the right answer.\",\"Questions are the first step in scientific inquiry.\",\"They help us explain why things happen in nature.\",\"They are useful in everyday life, at school, and in exams.\",\"Questions can be about people, places, things, or reasons.\",\"We use ‘Why’ to find the reason behind an event.\",\"‘How’ asks about the manner or process.\",\"‘What’ asks for identification or description.\",\"‘Where’ asks for location or place.\",\"‘When’ asks for time or sequence.\",\"‘Who’ asks for a person or people involved.\",\"‘Which’ asks to choose from options.\",\"Questions help us think critically and independently.\",\"They are essential tools for learning and discovery.\",\"They encourage us to ask more and learn more.\",\"They help us communicate and share knowledge.\",\"They are the basis of all learning activities.\",\"They help us solve problems and make decisions.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the web diagram in Task 4, what does the little boy want to know?\",\"options\":[\"The answers to his homework\",\"The reasons behind various things (Why...?)\",\"The names of his teachers\",\"The location of his school\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The web diagram explicitly shows the boy wants to know \'Why...?\', indicating his curiosity about reasons.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Task 5, which category does the word \'wind\' belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Animals\",\"Fruits\",\"Environment\",\"Insects\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'wind\' as a word from the poem related to the environment.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Task 5, \'spiders\' are categorized under which group of words from the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Environment\",\"Weather and Seasons\",\"Animals\\/fruits\\/insects\",\"School and Education\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly places \'spiders\' under the category of animals, fruits, or insects.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific objective of Task 6?\",\"options\":[\"To write a summary of a quiz contest\",\"To frame interview questions for a national quiz winner\",\"To participate in a national level quiz contest\",\"To describe a school assembly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Task 6 asks the student to frame 8 to 10 interview questions for a senior student who won a quiz.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are following the instructions in Task 6, how many interview questions should you prepare?\",\"options\":[\"Exactly 5\",\"At least 15\",\"8 to 10\",\"Only 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies framing \'8 to 10 Interview Questions\'.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these would be a suitable question for the interview mentioned in Task 6?\",\"options\":[\"What is your favorite color?\",\"How did you prepare for the national level Quiz Contest?\",\"Where did you buy your school bag?\",\"Why do spiders spin webs?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The interview is about the student\'s achievement in the quiz contest, so preparation questions are relevant.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Task 6, where is the interview supposed to take place?\",\"options\":[\"In the classroom\",\"At the student\'s home\",\"At the school assembly\",\"In the principal\'s office\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the interview will be conducted \'at the school assembly\'.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which question is most appropriate to ask a quiz winner to understand their \'achievement\' as per Task 6?\",\"options\":[\"What was the most challenging round in the contest?\",\"Do you like eating fruits?\",\"How many spiders did you see today?\",\"Is the wind blowing outside?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Asking about the challenges of the contest directly relates to the achievement mentioned in the task.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to frame questions specifically about the \'achievement\' in Task 6?\",\"options\":[\"To make the interview long and boring\",\"To focus on the student\'s success and inspire others\",\"To test the student\'s knowledge of spiders\",\"To fill up the blanks in the poem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Focusing on the achievement highlights the purpose of interviewing a winner at a school assembly.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these questions would be considered LEAST relevant for the interview described in Task 6?\",\"options\":[\"Who was your mentor for this contest?\",\"How did you feel when your name was announced as the winner?\",\"What advice do you have for other quiz aspirants?\",\"What is the scientific name of a spider?\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"While spiders are mentioned in Task 5, they are irrelevant to an interview about a national quiz achievement in Task 6.\",\"topic\":\"Interview Questions\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22066,52,8,'English','en','3.2 The Song of Songs','[\"1. A *Raag* (or *Raga*) is a set of notes used in Indian classical music, sung or played in a fixed combination of 5 to 6 basic notes.\",\"2. A *Raga* is performed at specific times or occasions, e.g., Raag Malkauns is often sung at late night.\",\"3. Musical instruments can be grouped as Indian (e.g., Tabla, Tanpura, Sitar) and Western (e.g., Drum, Piano, Violin).\",\"4. Folk music is simple, often passed down orally, and reflects local culture; classical music is structured, follows strict rules and is taught in schools.\",\"5. The story of Tansen and Emperor Akbar shows respect for a Guru and the idea that true music comes from the soul, not from external praise.\",\"6. In the story, Sant Haridas is a humble teacher who refuses to sing for the emperor, showing humility.\",\"7. The emperor Akbar is portrayed as a kind and open-minded ruler, different from other rulers who might be angry if a musician refuses.\",\"8. A *phrase* is a group of words that does not contain a subject or main verb; a *clause* contains a subject and a main verb.\",\"9. The sentence “The musician laid his tanpura on the carpet” is a phrase because it lacks a main verb for the subject.\",\"10. The sentence “There is someone who sings far better” contains a clause because it has a subject and a verb.\",\"11. True\\/False: “He had been Tansen’s music teacher and he lived the life of a hermit” – this is a clause.\",\"12. The story encourages students to ask questions, write sentences, and create plays based on the narrative.\",\"13. Vocabulary: *exquisite* (very fine), *exasperated* (irritated), *hypnotised* (captured attention), *divine* (like God).\",\"14. Matching exercise: Identify sentence types – exclamatory, interrogative, statement, imperative, negative.\",\"15. Activity: Collect stories of Akbar and Birbal to practice reading and presentation skills.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a \'Raag\' or \'Raga\' in Indian Classical music?\",\"options\":[\"A type of musical instrument\",\"A music piece with a fixed combination of notes\",\"A specific way of dancing to music\",\"A collection of folk songs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a \'Raag\' as a music piece in Indian Classical music sung or played in a fixed combination of notes.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many basic musical notes are typically combined to form a \'Raag\'?\",\"options\":[\"2 to 3\",\"7 to 8\",\"5 to 6\",\"10 to 12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a \'Raag\' is played in a fixed combination of 5 to 6 basic musical notes.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which instrument is specifically categorized under \'Indian\' musical instruments?\",\"options\":[\"Drum\",\"Guitar\",\"Tabla\",\"Piano\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram in the figure shows \'Tabla\' branching out from the \'Indian\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/069_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which instrument is categorized under \'Western\' musical instruments?\",\"options\":[\"Tabla\",\"Drum\",\"Sitar\",\"Flute\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram in the figure shows \'Drum\' branching out from the \'Western\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/069_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table provided in the text, when is \'Raag Malkauns\' best sung?\",\"options\":[\"Early morning\",\"Late night\",\"During the afternoon\",\"At sunset\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table explicitly lists \'late night\' as the occasion for Raag Malkauns.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What two types of music does the text ask the student to differentiate between?\",\"options\":[\"Pop music and Rock music\",\"Folk music and Classical music\",\"Jazz music and Blues music\",\"Devotional music and Film music\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Question 6 in the Chit-Chat section asks for the difference between folk music and classical music.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the definition of a Raag, how can it be performed?\",\"options\":[\"Only by singing\",\"Only by playing an instrument\",\"Either sung or played\",\"Only by writing the notes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a Raag as a music piece which is \'sung or played\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific purpose of the table mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To list the names of famous singers\",\"To map Ragas to their best-suited occasions\",\"To compare Indian and Western instruments\",\"To count the number of notes in a song\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table requires writing the names of Ragas and the occasion on which they are best sung.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to complete the table for Ragas, where does the text suggest they get the information?\",\"options\":[\"From a history book\",\"From any singer, musician, or the Internet\",\"From a math teacher\",\"From a sports magazine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to get the names of Ragas from \'any singer\\/musician or the Internet\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which question in the Chit-Chat section refers to learning music under a teacher?\",\"options\":[\"Question 1\",\"Question 3\",\"Question 5\",\"Question 6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Question 5 asks: \'Do you practise singing under the guidance of a Guru?\'\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the unique structure of a \'Raag\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The volume of the singer\",\"The type of instrument used\",\"A fixed combination of basic notes\",\"The length of the performance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states a Raag is defined by a \'fixed combination of 5 to 6 basic musical notes\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a direct instruction given in the \'Warming Up!\' section regarding musical instruments?\",\"options\":[\"Buy 5 to 6 instruments\",\"Write down names of Indian and Western instruments\",\"Learn to play the Tabla\",\"Repair old musical instruments\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'write down the names of 5 to 6 Indian musical instruments and those used in Western music\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up!\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific Raag did Tansen perform at the beginning of the story in the royal hall?\",\"options\":[\"Raag Malhar\",\"Raag Darbari\",\"Raag Deepak\",\"Raag Bhairavi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the exquisite strains of Raag Darbari still vibrated within the hall.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the music teacher Tansen spoke of to Emperor Akbar?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Haridas\",\"Ustad Mansur\",\"Swami Hariram\",\"Birbal\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly identifies Sant Haridas as the man Tansen had spoken of and his music teacher.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Emperor Akbar \'disbelieving\' when Tansen said someone sang better than him?\",\"options\":[\"He thought Tansen was lying to avoid singing.\",\"He believed Tansen had the most wonderful voice in the world.\",\"He had never heard of Sant Haridas before.\",\"He thought no one could be better than a court musician.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Akbar said, \'I think you have the most wonderful voice in the world!\' and was shocked to hear otherwise.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Under what condition did Tansen agree to take Akbar to see his guru?\",\"options\":[\"Akbar must bring expensive gifts for the guru.\",\"Akbar must not speak at all during the visit.\",\"Akbar must not come as the Emperor of Hindustan.\",\"Akbar must invite the guru back to the palace.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tansen said, \'Yes, Sire, provided you do not come as the Emperor of Hindustan.\'\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student makes a \'deliberate\' mistake like Tansen did, how are they acting?\",\"options\":[\"They are acting accidentally.\",\"They are acting out of ignorance.\",\"They are acting on purpose to achieve a result.\",\"They are acting because they are tired.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A deliberate mistake is one made intentionally, as Tansen did to provoke his guru to sing.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was Tansen\'s primary motivation for making a mistake while singing before his guru?\",\"options\":[\"He had forgotten the notes of the Raag.\",\"He wanted to show Akbar that he was not perfect.\",\"He wanted to trick his teacher into singing the correct note.\",\"He was nervous in the presence of his master.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tansen knew how to \'get round\' his guru by making a mistake so the guru would correct him by singing.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The text describes Sant Haridas living an \'austere life\'. What does this mean?\",\"options\":[\"A life of great wealth and luxury.\",\"A life of simple and strict self-discipline.\",\"A life spent traveling to different kingdoms.\",\"A life dedicated to serving the Emperor.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"An austere life, especially for a hermit, implies simplicity and lack of luxury.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the musician lay his tanpura after finishing his performance for Akbar?\",\"options\":[\"On a wooden stand\",\"On the carpet\",\"On the throne\",\"In its case\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the musician laid his tanpura on the carpet.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Akbar not get angry when Tansen told him the singer would not come to court?\",\"options\":[\"Because he was afraid of Tansen.\",\"Because he was a different kind of emperor who respected art.\",\"Because he didn\'t believe Tansen anyway.\",\"Because he was too tired to argue.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that while the reply would enrage others, Akbar was different and offered to go himself.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to Tansen, why is his Guru\'s music more beautiful and divine than his own?\",\"options\":[\"His Guru practices for more hours every day.\",\"His Guru uses a better quality tanpura.\",\"His Guru sings for God, while Tansen sings for a king.\",\"His Guru lives in the forest where the air is better.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tansen explains that he sings at the command of a king, but his Guru sings for the Master of kings—God.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"The music of Sant Haridas is compared to the \'fragrance of jasmine\'. What does this suggest about the music?\",\"options\":[\"It was strong and overpowering.\",\"It was sweet, pleasant, and filled the surroundings.\",\"It was only available during certain seasons.\",\"It made the listeners feel sleepy.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Comparing music to a fragrance suggests it is pleasing and spreads naturally through the air.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which title used by Tansen for Akbar indicates his role as the ruler of India?\",\"options\":[\"Ustadji\",\"Sire\",\"Shahenshah\",\"Guru\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shahenshah is the title Tansen uses to address Akbar as the Emperor of Hindustan.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How did Sant Haridas react when Tansen repeated the same musical mistake?\",\"options\":[\"He laughed at Tansen\'s poor memory.\",\"He became exasperated and took the tanpura himself.\",\"He told Tansen to leave his hut immediately.\",\"He asked Akbar to correct Tansen.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he was \'exasperated\' and took the tanpura from Tansen\'s hands to sing the right note.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what instrument is the person holding?\",\"options\":[\"Sitar\",\"Tanpura\",\"Tabla\",\"Flute\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the hermit (Sant Haridas) holding a tanpura, as described in the text.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/071_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is growing behind the person sitting on the ground?\",\"options\":[\"A large banyan tree\",\"A leafy plant or creeper\",\"A rose bush\",\"A stone wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows a green, leafy creeper\\/plant growing behind the seated figure.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/071_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'exquisite\' as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Very fine and pleasing\",\"Tired and sleepy\",\"Simple and plain\",\"Purely accidental\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'exquisite\' as very fine and pleasing.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the vocabulary list, what does it mean to be \'exhausted\'?\",\"options\":[\"To be angry\",\"To be tired\",\"To be simple\",\"To be divine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines \'exhausted\' as tired.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the text describes something that is \'of or like God\'?\",\"options\":[\"Austere\",\"Exquisite\",\"Divine\",\"Deliberate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'divine\' as of or like God.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If Emperor Akbar \'summoned\' a musician, what did he do?\",\"options\":[\"He punished the musician\",\"He called the musician\",\"He ignored the musician\",\"He convinced the musician\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'summoned\' as called.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'austere\' imply about a person\'s lifestyle?\",\"options\":[\"It is very fine and pleasing\",\"It is filled with anger\",\"It is simple\",\"It is captured by attention\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'austere\' as simple.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Tansen\'s music \'hypnotised\' the audience, what was the effect?\",\"options\":[\"It made them feel tired\",\"It captured their full attention\",\"It made them feel enraged\",\"It made them feel simple\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'hypnotised\' as captured full attention.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Tansen made a \'deliberate\' mistake. Based on the text, how was this mistake made?\",\"options\":[\"It was made by accident\",\"It was purely made\\/intentional\",\"It was a divine mistake\",\"It was a fine and pleasing mistake\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'deliberate\' as purely made.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a ruler tries to \'persuade\' a musician to come to the court, what is he doing?\",\"options\":[\"He is calling him immediately\",\"He is trying to convince him\",\"He is getting angry at him\",\"He is praising him\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'persuade\' as try to convince.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following words describes a feeling of being \'irritated\' or \'angry\'?\",\"options\":[\"Exquisite\",\"Austere\",\"Exasperated\",\"Divine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'exasperated\' as irritated or angry.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between being \'enraged\' and \'exasperated\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Enraged means simple, while exasperated means tired\",\"Enraged means filled with anger, while exasperated means irritated\\/angry\",\"Enraged means divine, while exasperated means fine\",\"Enraged means called, while exasperated means convinced\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'enraged\' as filled with anger and \'exasperated\' as irritated, angry.\",\"topic\":\"Difficult Words\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22067,52,8,'English','en','3.3 Truth','[\"Truth is the main theme of the poem.\",\"The poem shows how words can hurt as much as physical objects.\",\"Sticks and stones break bones, but words can break the mind.\",\"Words are described as ghosts, swords, and slanting weapons.\",\"Painful words leave scars on the mind and heart.\",\"The poem contrasts physical pain with emotional pain.\",\"The rhyme scheme of stanza 1 is ABAB.\",\"Stanza 2 and 3 have their own rhyme schemes to be found by the student.\",\"The poem encourages kindness to heal emotional wounds.\",\"The speaker is the poet himself, addressing the reader.\",\"The poem is a reminder to use kind words.\",\"Words can also heal when spoken with care.\",\"The poem uses figurative language like metaphor and simile.\",\"The poem is written in free verse with simple language.\",\"The poem has 4 stanzas.\",\"The title of the poem is ‘Truth’.\",\"The poet is Barrie Wade.\",\"The theme is the power of words.\",\"The poem uses imagery of stones, sticks, and words.\",\"The poem shows that words can leave lasting emotional scars.\",\"The poem suggests that kind words can relieve distress.\",\"The poem invites the reader to reflect on their own experiences.\",\"The poem uses rhyme to emphasize its message.\",\"The poem is a short, impactful piece about emotional truth.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the first question asked in the Chit-Chat section of the \'Warming Up\' activity?\",\"options\":[\"Why do we tell lies?\",\"Do we speak the truth all the time?\",\"How can words hurt someone?\",\"How do we break relationships?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Do we speak the truth all the time?\' as the first question in the Chit-Chat section.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the example in section 1(A), what is an event that makes a person very happy?\",\"options\":[\"Getting a new gift\",\"My best friend quarrelled with me\",\"Our team wins the match\",\"Speaking the truth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides \'Our team wins the match\' as the example for things that make you happy.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In section 1(B), why are certain things described as being \'hard to forget\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they were very funny\",\"Because they had upset you greatly\",\"Because they were about winning matches\",\"Because they were spoken in proper words\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that things are hard to forget \'because they had upset you greatly\'.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific example is given for an event that caused someone to be upset in section 1(B)?\",\"options\":[\"Losing a match\",\"Telling a lie\",\"My best friend quarrelled with me\",\"Using improper words\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'My best friend quarrelled with me\' as the example for section 1(B).\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The Chit-Chat section asks \'How do we break relationships?\'. Based on the other questions, what is a likely cause mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Winning a match\",\"Speaking the truth all the time\",\"The way words are used\",\"Forgetting things easily\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The context of the questions suggests that how we use words and speak affects our relationships.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student writes \'I received a gold medal\' in section 1(A), which example from the text are they following?\",\"options\":[\"The example of being upset\",\"The example of winning a match\",\"The example of telling a lie\",\"The example of breaking a relationship\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Section 1(A) is for things that make you happy, similar to the example \'Our team wins the match\'.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many items is a student expected to list for \'things that make you very happy\'?\",\"options\":[\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\",\"One\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Section 1(A) asks the student to \'Write down five things\'.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is writing about a time they were scolded by a teacher, which section does this best fit according to the provided examples?\",\"options\":[\"Chit-Chat question 1\",\"Section 1(A)\",\"Section 1(B)\",\"The title section\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Being scolded is an upsetting event, which fits the criteria for section 1(B).\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The question \'Why should we take care of using proper words while speaking?\' implies that words have the power to:\",\"options\":[\"Help us win matches\",\"Impact others negatively or positively\",\"Make us forget things\",\"Always tell the truth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The question suggests that the choice of words is important because of the effect they have on people.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common theme between the Chit-Chat questions and the activities in 1(A) and 1(B)?\",\"options\":[\"Sports and games\",\"Human emotions and communication\",\"The history of truth\",\"Rules for winning matches\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text focuses on truth, lies, hurting others with words, happiness, and being upset, all of which relate to emotions and communication.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the poet of the poem \'Truth\'?\",\"options\":[\"Barrie Wade\",\"Rudyard Kipling\",\"Robert Frost\",\"William Wordsworth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions \'Barrie Wade\' at the end of the poem.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what do stones and sticks break?\",\"options\":[\"Only skin\",\"The mind\",\"The heart\",\"Memories\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'Stones and sticks break only skin\'.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the first stanza, words are compared to _____ that haunt the poet.\",\"options\":[\"ghosts\",\"swords\",\"bats\",\"bricks\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'While words are ghosts that haunt me.\'\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the rhyme scheme of the first stanza of the poem?\",\"options\":[\"abab\",\"abcb\",\"aabb\",\"abcd\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'In the 1st stanza of the poem the Rhyme Scheme is abcb.\'\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to cuts and bruises over time according to the poem?\",\"options\":[\"They leave permanent scars\",\"They never go away\",\"They have healed\",\"They hurt the mind\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'Cuts and bruises now have healed\'.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the pain from words leave its scar?\",\"options\":[\"On the skin and bones\",\"On the hands and feet\",\"On the mind and heart\",\"On the bats and bricks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions, \'Pain from words has left its scar On mind and heart\'.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the pattern of lines that rhyme with other lines in a stanza called?\",\"options\":[\"Rhythm\",\"Rhyme scheme\",\"Stanza pattern\",\"Alphabetical order\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Rhyme scheme as a pattern of lines that rhyme other lines in the stanza.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The poet uses the term \'word-swords\' to show that words can:\",\"options\":[\"be used for protection\",\"heal the skin\",\"pierce and stick inside\",\"be easily forgotten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem links \'word-swords\' to the action \'To pierce and stick inside me\'.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the word \'WORDS\' is illustrated using cacti. What does this suggest about words based on the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Words are always green and fresh\",\"Words can be prickly and cause pain\",\"Words grow slowly in the desert\",\"Words are easy to touch\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The cactus thorns represent how words can \'hurt\', \'pierce\', and \'mortify\' as described in the poem.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the thorns on the letters represent which specific line from the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Sticks and stones may break my bones\",\"Cuts and bruises now have healed\",\"To pierce and stick inside me\",\"Bats and bricks may ache through bones\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The thorns on the cactus letters visually represent the action of piercing and sticking, as mentioned in the poem.\",\"topic\":\"Truth\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/076_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'haunt\' as given in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To forget something quickly\",\"To trouble or worry again and again\",\"To stay in one place\",\"To heal a wound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'haunt\' as to trouble or worry again and again.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what does it mean to \'mortify\' someone?\",\"options\":[\"To make one feel ashamed and embarrassed\",\"To make one feel proud and happy\",\"To cause physical pain\",\"To help someone lean\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'mortify\' as making one feel ashamed and embarrassed.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a \'scar\' defined as in the glossary?\",\"options\":[\"A fresh injury\",\"A sharp object\",\"A mark left by a wound\",\"A feeling of worry\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a \'scar\' is a mark left by a wound.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which word describes something that leans or slopes to one side?\",\"options\":[\"Pierce\",\"Slant\",\"Bruises\",\"Haunt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'slant\' as to lean or slope to one side.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The word \'pierce\' is used in the text to mean:\",\"options\":[\"To heal\",\"To slope\",\"To stab\",\"To worry\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'stab\' as the meaning for \'pierce\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are \'bruises\' according to the provided definitions?\",\"options\":[\"Marks left by old wounds\",\"Injuries or cuts on the skin\",\"Feelings of embarrassment\",\"Sloping lines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'bruises\' as injuries or cuts on the skin.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a memory continues to trouble a person repeatedly, which word from the text best describes this?\",\"options\":[\"Slant\",\"Mortify\",\"Haunt\",\"Pierce\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To \'haunt\' means to trouble or worry again and again.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is made to feel extremely ashamed in front of the class, they have been:\",\"options\":[\"Slanted\",\"Mortified\",\"Pierced\",\"Bruised\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To \'mortify\' means to make one feel ashamed and embarrassed.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the difference between a \'bruise\' and a \'scar\'?\",\"options\":[\"A bruise is a slope, while a scar is a stab\",\"A bruise is an injury on the skin, while a scar is the mark left after a wound\",\"A bruise is a worry, while a scar is an embarrassment\",\"There is no difference according to the text\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes bruises as injuries\\/cuts and scars as marks left by wounds.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The text asks a question regarding what words are compared to in which specific parts of the poem?\",\"options\":[\"The first two stanzas\",\"The last two stanzas\",\"The entire poem\",\"The third stanza only\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly asks, \'What are words compared to in the first two stanzas?\'\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22068,52,8,'English','en','3.4 The Two Gentlemen of Verona','[\"- The Two Gentlemen of Verona is a short story by A.J. Cronin about two brothers, Nicola and Jacopo, who work hard to pay for their sister’s tuberculosis treatment.\",\"- The narrator meets the brothers first when he sees them selling wild strawberries and later when they are shining shoes in a public square.\",\"- The brothers do odd jobs like selling newspapers, shining shoes, and helping the narrator in various ways.\",\"- They wish to visit the village Poleta to get a lift for their sister’s treatment.\",\"- The story shows that even after war and loss, the brothers remain determined and self‑less, illustrating the proverb ‘Where there is a will there’s a way.’\",\"- Key terms: narrator, odd jobs, tuberculosis, war, will, way, self‑less, dedication.\",\"- Important points for revision: focus on the brothers’ motivation, the narrator’s role, the setting (Alps, village), and the moral of the story.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a book review?\",\"options\":[\"A summary of a book written by the author\",\"An analysis of a newly published book by an expert\",\"A collection of stories and poems\",\"A list of names of great people\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a book review as an analysis of a newly published book by an expert.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where can book reviews be found according to the \'Warming Up\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Only in school textbooks\",\"Only in libraries\",\"In newspapers, magazines, and online\",\"In the conclusion of the book itself\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that book reviews are published in newspapers, magazines, and are available online.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific aspects does an expert express an opinion on in a book review?\",\"options\":[\"Price, weight, and size\",\"Contents, style, and merit\",\"Printing press and paper quality\",\"The number of pages and chapters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the expert expresses his\\/her opinion about the contents, style, and merit.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the \'Background or setting\' of a story refer to?\",\"options\":[\"The life history of the writer\",\"The physical weight of the book\",\"The time and place where the story occurs\",\"The list of characters in the book\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In literature, background or setting refers to the context, time, and location of the story.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which detail in a book review helps a reader understand the core message or summary?\",\"options\":[\"Illustrations\",\"Central idea\",\"Drawbacks\",\"Writer\'s name\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Central idea\\/summary of contents\' provides the core message of the book.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of a book review, what does \'merit\' most likely mean?\",\"options\":[\"The cost of the book\",\"The quality or worth of the book\",\"The speed of reading the book\",\"The font size used in the book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Merit refers to the excellence or worth of the book being analyzed.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are looking for the negative aspects of a book in a review, which section should you read?\",\"options\":[\"Strong aspects\",\"Style and language\",\"Drawbacks\",\"Main characters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Drawbacks refer to the limitations or negative points of the book.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to know if a book has pictures. Which detail from the checklist should they look for?\",\"options\":[\"Illustrations\",\"Background\",\"Conclusion\",\"Style\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Illustrations are the visual elements or pictures included in a book.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these would be considered a \'Strong aspect\' of a book?\",\"options\":[\"Grammatical errors\",\"A confusing plot\",\"Engaging and simple language\",\"Missing pages\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Strong aspects are positive features, such as engaging language.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'information about the writer\' included in the review details?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the page count of the review\",\"To provide context about the author\'s perspective or expertise\",\"To tell the reader where the author lives\",\"To list the author\'s favorite food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Knowing the writer helps the reader understand the background and authority of the person who wrote the book.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of the \'Conclusion\' in a book review?\",\"options\":[\"To introduce the main characters\",\"To provide a final judgment or recommendation\",\"To list the chapters of the book\",\"To describe the illustrations\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The conclusion summarizes the reviewer\'s final thoughts and overall opinion.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does a book review differ from a simple summary?\",\"options\":[\"A review only lists the characters\",\"A review includes an expert\'s opinion and analysis of merit\",\"A review is always written by the author\",\"A review does not mention the name of the book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Unlike a summary, a review involves analysis and the expression of an expert\'s opinion.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who are the two main characters that the story \'The Two Gentlemen of Verona\' revolves around?\",\"options\":[\"A.J. Cronin and a driver\",\"Nicola and Jacopo\",\"Poleta and Verona\",\"A nurse and a sister\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the story revolves around two boys named Nicola and Jacopo.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific medical condition does the boys\' sister suffer from?\",\"options\":[\"Tuberculosis of the lungs\",\"Pneumonia of the chest\",\"Tuberculosis of the spine\",\"A broken back from the war\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the sister suffers from tuberculosis of the spine.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do Nicola and Jacopo perform numerous jobs like selling fruit and shining shoes?\",\"options\":[\"To buy a new house in the village of Poleta\",\"To pay for their sister\'s medical treatment\",\"To save money to join the army like their father\",\"To travel across the foothills of the Alps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The boys earn money only to pay for their sister’s treatment.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where does the narrator first encounter the two brothers?\",\"options\":[\"In a public square in the town\",\"At a hospital in Poleta\",\"In a shelter during a storm\",\"At the foothills of the Alps\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The story begins with the narrator encountering them at the foothills of the Alps.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the narrator \'forbids\' the driver from stopping, based on the vocabulary definition, what is he doing?\",\"options\":[\"Allowing him to stop\",\"Disallowing him from stopping\",\"Encouraging him to stop\",\"Asking him to stop later\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the vocabulary list, \'forbids\' means \'disallows\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What qualities does the narrator observe in the boys when he sees them shining shoes?\",\"options\":[\"Greedy, loud, and aggressive\",\"Lazy, tired, and hopeless\",\"Innocent, serious, pleasant, and earnest\",\"Wealthy, arrogant, and noble\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The narrator finds them to be innocent, serious, pleasant, and earnest.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the meaning provided for the word \'subtle\'?\",\"options\":[\"Clearly visible\",\"Refined, nice\",\"Focus on a statement\",\"Reduced eagerness\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image defines \'subtle\' as \'refined, nice\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, what does it mean when someone\'s spirit is \'not shaken\'?\",\"options\":[\"They have lost all hope in life\",\"They have become very wealthy\",\"Their eagerness to do something is not reduced\",\"They have moved to a new shelter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The image defines \'not shaken their spirit\' as \'not reduced eagerness to do something\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What tragic event happened to the boys\' family before the story began?\",\"options\":[\"Their father died of tuberculosis\",\"Their father was killed in a war\",\"Their sister was lost in the Alps\",\"They were abandoned by their parents\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The nurse tells the narrator that their father was killed in a war.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the narrator choose to speak to the nurse instead of entering the room where the boys were?\",\"options\":[\"He was afraid of catching tuberculosis\",\"He did not want to intrude on their private moment\",\"He was in a hurry to get back to the cafe\",\"The boys had asked him to stay outside\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states he does not feel like intruding and thus asks the nurse for details.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which proverb is used in the text to describe the boys\' determination?\",\"options\":[\"Honesty is the best policy\",\"A friend in need is a friend indeed\",\"Where there is a will there\'s a way\",\"Slow and steady wins the race\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes by saying the story talks about \'Where there is a will there’s a way\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What were the boys doing when the narrator saw them on a stormy night?\",\"options\":[\"Selling wild strawberries\",\"Shining shoes in the square\",\"Selling newspapers\",\"Driving a car to Poleta\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions they are found selling newspapers one stormy night.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the author characterize the boys despite their difficult circumstances?\",\"options\":[\"As victims of a cruel fate\",\"As noble, gentle, and great human beings\",\"As children who have lost their way\",\"As businessmen focused only on profit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes them as noble and gentle and great human beings.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What does the boys\' willingness to work on a stormy night suggest about their situation?\",\"options\":[\"They enjoy being outside in the rain\",\"They are desperate to earn every possible penny for their sister\",\"They have no home to go to during storms\",\"They are trying to avoid the narrator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Their hard work in all conditions highlights their dedication to paying for their sister\'s treatment.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the fact that the boys \'literally starve\' themselves reveal about their character?\",\"options\":[\"They do not know how to buy food\",\"They are extremely selfless and dedicated to their sister\",\"They are trying to save money for a new car\",\"The war has made them forget how to eat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Starving themselves to pay for their sister\'s treatment shows extreme selflessness.\",\"topic\":\"The Two Gentlemen of Verona\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a \'Clause\'?\",\"options\":[\"A group of words without a verb\",\"A group of words that has a Subject and a Verb and is part of a larger sentence\",\"A single word that joins two sentences\",\"A sentence that does not make any sense\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a Clause as a group of words that has a Subject and a Verb and is part of a larger sentence.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What two essential components must a Clause have?\",\"options\":[\"An Adjective and a Noun\",\"A Subject and an Object\",\"A Subject and a Verb\",\"A Verb and an Adverb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a Clause is a group of words that has a Subject and a Verb.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for a \'Main Clause\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Subordinate Clause\",\"Dependent Clause\",\"Principal Clause\",\"Joining Clause\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the terms \'Main\\/Principal Clause\' interchangeably.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A clause that is not complete by itself and depends on another clause is called a:\",\"options\":[\"Principal Clause\",\"Main Clause\",\"Subordinate Clause\",\"Independent Clause\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a clause that is not complete by itself and depends on the main clause as a Dependant or Subordinate Clause.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'We returned home, when school was over\', why is \'We returned home\' called the Main Clause?\",\"options\":[\"Because it comes first in the sentence\",\"Because it makes complete sense on its own\",\"Because it contains the word \'when\'\",\"Because it is the shortest part of the sentence\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that \'We returned home\' is the Main Clause because it makes complete sense.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of a \'Subordinator\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It acts as the subject of the sentence\",\"It is the main verb of the principal clause\",\"It is the joining word for the clauses\",\"It makes the main clause incomplete\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the joining word (like \'when\') is called a Subordinator.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'They pay for their sisters treatment, who suffers from tuberculosis\', identify the Subordinator.\",\"options\":[\"They\",\"treatment\",\"who\",\"suffers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In this sentence, \'who\' is the joining word that introduces the subordinate clause.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the Main Clause in: \'They have shown that war has not shaken their spirit.\'\",\"options\":[\"that war has not shaken their spirit\",\"They have shown\",\"war has not shaken\",\"shaken their spirit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'They have shown\' is the principal part that can stand alone, while the rest depends on it.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'When he asks the nurse, she tells him their story\', which part is the Subordinate Clause?\",\"options\":[\"she tells him their story\",\"the nurse\",\"When he asks the nurse\",\"tells him their story\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'When he asks the nurse\' is incomplete by itself and starts with the subordinator \'When\'.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which word acts as the subordinator in the sentence: \'Although it doesn’t come in the narrator’s way, he takes them there.\'?\",\"options\":[\"narrator\",\"Although\",\"takes\",\"there\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Although\' is the joining word that introduces the dependent clause.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what makes a clause \'Dependent\'?\",\"options\":[\"It has no subject\",\"It has no verb\",\"It depends on the main clause to convey proper sense\",\"It is always placed at the end of a sentence\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states a Dependent clause depends on the main clause to convey a proper sense.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the sentence: \'When he asks the nurse, she tells him their story.\' Which clause is the Principal Clause?\",\"options\":[\"When he asks the nurse\",\"she tells him their story\",\"asks the nurse\",\"When he asks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'she tells him their story\' makes complete sense on its own, making it the Principal Clause.\",\"topic\":\"Language Study: Clauses\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is an acrostic?\",\"options\":[\"A poem where the last letters spell a word\",\"A poem where the first letter of each line spells out a word or message\",\"A slip of the tongue where letters are interchanged\",\"A story that uses rhyming sentences throughout\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an acrostic as a poem or writing format where the first letter (or syllable or word) of each line spells out a word or message.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Spoonerism\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"A style of rhyming poetry\",\"A challenge to write random words\",\"A slip of the tongue where first letters of words are interchanged\",\"A way to form words from top to bottom\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a spoonerism is a slip of the tongue where the first letters of two or more words get interchanged.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the most common form of an acrostic poem, how is the word or phrase spelled out?\",\"options\":[\"From bottom to top\",\"Using only the last letters of each line\",\"Using the first letters of each line\",\"Using only the middle words of each sentence\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the most common form is where the first letters of each line spell out the word or phrase.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the letters in a spoonerism?\",\"options\":[\"They are removed from the sentence\",\"The first letters of two or more words are interchanged\",\"They are capitalized at the start of every line\",\"They are used to form a vertical message\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in spoonerism, the first letters of two or more words get interchanged.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, looking at example (3), which word is used for the letter \'M\' in the acrostic for \'Family\'?\",\"options\":[\"Mother\",\"Message\",\"Manners\",\"Madam\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the image, the acrostic for \'Family\' uses \'Mother\' for the letter \'M\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, according to the text in example (1), what is the aim of the acrostic poetry style?\",\"options\":[\"To make the reader laugh with slips of the tongue\",\"To interchange the first letters of words\",\"To form a word from top to bottom\",\"To visit an internet link for examples\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text in the image for example (1) explicitly states: \'To form a word from top to bottom is the aim of this poetry style\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the spoonerism is \'Do you like to bead in red?\', what is the correct intended sentence?\",\"options\":[\"Do you like to read in bed?\",\"Do you like to lead in red?\",\"Do you like to bead in bed?\",\"Do you like to read in red?\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"By interchanging the \'b\' in bead and \'r\' in red, we get the correct phrase \'read in bed\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sentence for the spoonerism \'I can fee my soot prints\'?\",\"options\":[\"I can see my foot prints\",\"I can feel my soot prints\",\"I can see my soot prints\",\"I can fee my foot prints\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Interchanging the \'f\' in fee and \'s\' in soot results in \'see my foot prints\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The spoonerism \'Go and shake a tower\' is interchanged from which original phrase?\",\"options\":[\"Go and take a shower\",\"Go and make a power\",\"Go and bake a flower\",\"Go and take a tower\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides \'Go and take a shower\' as the original phrase for the spoonerism \'Go and shake a tower\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the original phrase for the spoonerism: \'You have very mad banners.\'\",\"options\":[\"You have very bad manners\",\"You have very sad banners\",\"You have very mad manners\",\"You have very bad banners\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'You have very bad manners\' as the original phrase for \'You have very mad banners\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is described as a \'slip of the tongue\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Acrostic\",\"Rhyme\",\"Spoonerism\",\"Theme\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'Spoonerism is a slip of the tongue.\'\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Following the rule of spoonerism, how would you change the phrase \'Crushing blow\'?\",\"options\":[\"Blushing crow\",\"Brushing clow\",\"Crushing flow\",\"Brushing blow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By interchanging the first sounds \'Cr\' and \'bl\', \'Crushing blow\' becomes \'Brushing clow\'.\",\"topic\":\"WORD PLAY\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where should you add the new word card in your collection?\",\"options\":[\"At the end of the collection\",\"In the proper place in alphabetical order\",\"In the order you learned them\",\"In a separate box for each day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to add the card to the collection at the proper place in alphabetical order.\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which short form should be used to mark an \'adjective\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"a.\",\"adj.\",\"ad.\",\"adject.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'adjectives (adj.)\' as one of the categories for classification.\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Into which language should you translate the sentence framed in Section C?\",\"options\":[\"Hindi\",\"Sanskrit\",\"Marathi\",\"Your mother tongue\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Section C specifically instructs to \'Translate the sentence from English into Marathi.\'\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who are the people you may talk to about your new word?\",\"options\":[\"Only your teacher\",\"Only your friends\",\"Teacher, friend, or elders\",\"Only your parents\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions you may talk about the word to your teacher, friend, or elders.\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if a word does not belong to the four specified classes (n., v., adj., adv.)?\",\"options\":[\"Mark it as \'other\'\",\"Ask the teacher for a new short form\",\"You need not mark it\",\"Look for a different word\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'If the word does not belong to any of these four classes you need not mark it.\'\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is a requirement for the question you frame in Section B?\",\"options\":[\"It must be a complex question\",\"You should know the appropriate answer to it\",\"It must be from the textbook\",\"It must be a question about a new word\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Section B states: \'You should also know the appropriate answer to it.\'\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Section C, what must be included in your English sentence?\",\"options\":[\"A simile\",\"A dictionary definition\",\"At least one word from your mother tongue or an Indian language\",\"A translation in brackets\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Section C requires the sentence to include at least one word from your mother tongue or any Indian language.\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a simile provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"as fast as a car\",\"as deep as a well\",\"as bright as a star\",\"as heavy as lead\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'as deep as a well\' as one of the examples for similes.\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are following Section C, which of these sentences is a correct example?\",\"options\":[\"I like to play cricket.\",\"The sky is very blue today.\",\"You need two teams to play kabaddi.\",\"She is as cold as ice.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The sentence \'You need two teams to play kabaddi\' uses an Indian word, which fits the Section C criteria.\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How should you mark the word \'mainland\' based on the example in Section A?\",\"options\":[\"mainland (v.)\",\"mainland (adj.)\",\"mainland (n.)\",\"mainland (adv.)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the example: \'mainland\' (n.).\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the instructions in Section B, how often should you use a new question?\",\"options\":[\"Once a week\",\"Every day\",\"Once a month\",\"Only during English class\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Section B states: \'You must use a new question every day.\'\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Section D, what is the final step after making up a simile comparison?\",\"options\":[\"Translate it into Marathi\",\"Try to use the simile in a complete sentence\",\"Write it on a word card\",\"Classify it as a noun or verb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Section D concludes with the instruction: \'Try to use the simile in a complete sentence.\'\",\"topic\":\"Daily Language Practice\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What specific problem did Pratima face during her English examination?\",\"options\":[\"She could not read big words.\",\"She faced the problem of meaning of unknown words.\",\"She forgot to bring her pen.\",\"She could not complete the paper on time.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the text, Pratima explicitly states, \'I faced the problem of meaning of unknown words.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Hamid, where can students find solutions to their problems with unknown words?\",\"options\":[\"By asking the teacher during the exam\",\"By referring to a good dictionary\",\"By reading the textbook again\",\"By discussing with friends during the exam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hamid says, \'Don\'t worry, we can find all solutions if we refer to a good dictionary.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did John find the unseen passage difficult to handle?\",\"options\":[\"He did not have enough time.\",\"He could not even read some big words in the passage.\",\"The handwriting was not clear.\",\"He forgot the grammar rules.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"John mentions, \'I could not even read some big words in the unseen passage.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the meaning, what other two things does Hamid suggest looking for in a dictionary?\",\"options\":[\"Spelling and history\",\"Pronunciation and use\",\"Synonyms and antonyms\",\"Pictures and diagrams\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hamid suggests, \'Let us go and see the meaning, pronunciation and use of the unknown words.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is at home and needs to find a word\'s meaning quickly without a computer, which device does Hamid suggest using?\",\"options\":[\"Television\",\"Radio\",\"Smart cell phone\",\"Landline phone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Hamid mentions that meanings can be searched from \'smart cell phones at home.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the phonetic transcription (pronunciation) provided for the word \'Work\'?\",\"options\":[\"\\/W3:k\\/\",\"\\/Work\\/\",\"\\/Wurk\\/\",\"\\/Wark\\/\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The image shows the phonetic transcription of \'Work\' as \\/W3:k\\/.\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/085_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the list \'We use dictionary to...\' in the given figure, which of these is a stated purpose?\",\"options\":[\"To translate full sentences\",\"To find out what part of speech a word is\",\"To find the history of a word\",\"To look up famous people\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The sidebar in the figure explicitly lists \'Find out what part of speech a word is\' as a use.\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/085_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the primary reason the students could not solve their word problems during the exam?\",\"options\":[\"They were not allowed to talk.\",\"They didn\'t bring a dictionary with them.\",\"The exam was too long.\",\"They didn\'t know how to use a dictionary.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Prashik points out the immediate problem: \'But we didn\'t bring dictionary with us.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity is suggested for students to perform using their parents\' devices?\",\"options\":[\"Play educational games\",\"Download an app of a good dictionary\",\"Watch videos on English grammar\",\"Call their friends to discuss the exam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity (3) suggests: \'Download an app of any good dictionary in your parents\' smart phone and try to use it.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What category of verbs are students specifically asked to research in an online dictionary?\",\"options\":[\"Regular Verbs\",\"Irregular Verbs\",\"Auxiliary Verbs\",\"Modal Verbs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity (2) asks students to \'Find out the pronunciation and usage of any ten Irregular Verbs.\'\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does Hamid mention both online and offline dictionaries?\",\"options\":[\"To show that dictionaries are expensive\",\"To provide options for searching meanings in different situations\",\"To explain the history of dictionaries\",\"To suggest that offline dictionaries are better\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hamid provides various methods (online, offline, PC, phone) to show that solutions are accessible in multiple ways.\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the conversation, what can be inferred about the \'unseen passage\' in the exam?\",\"options\":[\"It was the easiest part of the exam.\",\"It contained vocabulary that was challenging for multiple students.\",\"It was not part of the English exam.\",\"It only contained words the students already knew.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Utkarsh, Sidhanath, Pratima, and John all mention facing different difficulties (meaning, reading big words) specifically in the unseen passage.\",\"topic\":\"Live English: My Word-buddy\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22069,52,8,'English','en','4.1 The Vet','[\"1. A vet (veterinarian) is a doctor who treats animals and birds.\",\"2. Vets need wide knowledge of many animal species and their organs.\",\"3. Vets must understand why an animal behaves oddly (e.g., cat refusing milk).\",\"4. Vets diagnose illnesses that may be hard to spot because animals cannot speak.\",\"5. Vets often work with dangerous or wild animals, requiring special precautions.\",\"6. The poem in the chapter shows many funny illnesses of animals to illustrate vet challenges.\",\"7. A specialist doctor treats a specific part of the body: e.g., dentist – teeth, cardiologist – heart.\",\"8. Matching specialists: Dentist–teeth, Cardiologist–heart, Ophthalmologist–eye, Orthopedic–bone, Pediatrician–children, Neurologist–brain, Veterinarian–animals\\/birds.\",\"9. Pediatricians and veterinarians have tougher jobs because they deal with patients who cannot communicate symptoms.\",\"10. Vets must be knowledgeable about animal behavior, anatomy, and diseases.\",\"11. The poem uses rhyme to make learning fun and memorable.\",\"12. Vets must be careful with dangerous animals: use protective gear, keep distance, use sedation if needed.\",\"13. Vets may treat animals in zoos, farms, or in the wild.\",\"14. The poem asks students to identify illnesses and translate animal names.\",\"15. Understanding animal illnesses helps in early treatment and prevention.\",\"16. Vets often work long hours and need continuous learning.\",\"17. The poem’s rhyme scheme changes in later stanzas, showing variation in style.\",\"18. Vets are sometimes called “omniscient” because they know a lot about many animals.\",\"19. Students should practice matching specialists with body parts to reinforce learning.\",\"20. Vets use tools like stethoscopes, X‑ray machines, and blood tests, just like human doctors.\",\"21. Vets must also consider animal welfare and ethical treatment.\",\"22. Vets can specialize further (e.g., equine vet, avian vet).\",\"23. The poem encourages students to think about careers in veterinary science.\",\"24. Vets help keep animal populations healthy, which benefits ecosystems.\",\"25. Vets often collaborate with other scientists and conservationists.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the first stanza, what does a vet need to be successful and competent?\",\"options\":[\"A lot of money\",\"Exceedingly wide knowledge\",\"A large hospital\",\"Many assistants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem states that a successful vet needs knowledge exceedingly wide because every patient is different.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific problem mentioned regarding the parrot in the second stanza?\",\"options\":[\"It refuses to eat\",\"It is losing its feathers\",\"It does nothing but groan\",\"It cannot fly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks why the parrot does nothing but groan as one of the things a vet must know.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal is described as having trouble with its feet?\",\"options\":[\"The hippo\",\"The zebra\",\"The centipede\",\"The monkey\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions \'If the centipede has trouble with his feet\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What has the bear eaten too much of, according to the poem?\",\"options\":[\"Honey\",\"Buns\",\"Fish\",\"Paper-bags\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem says \'If the bear\'s too full of buns\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the vet need to know so much about the \'inside\' of his patients?\",\"options\":[\"Because all animals are the same\",\"Because each patient possesses a different inside\",\"Because animals have no insides\",\"Because the zoo is very large\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem explains that each patient the vet gets possesses a different inside.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the poet mean by calling the vet \'omniscient\'?\",\"options\":[\"The vet is very rich\",\"The vet is always tired\",\"The vet is all-knowing\",\"The vet is very fast\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Omniscient means knowing everything, which refers to the vast knowledge the vet is expected to have.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the giraffes to have stiff necks according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They slept poorly\",\"They had a tiff\",\"They ate too much\",\"They caught a cold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem says \'If giraffes have had a tiff and their necks are feeling stiff\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the poet \'profoundly pity\' the vet?\",\"options\":[\"Because he has no friends\",\"Because he must learn a great deal for his pay\",\"Because he has to live in a zoo\",\"Because he dislikes animals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poet pities the vet because of the immense amount of knowledge required for the salary earned.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a horse\'s coat is not \'shining like silk\', what does the poem imply?\",\"options\":[\"The horse needs a bath\",\"The horse is perfectly healthy\",\"The vet needs to know the reason for this health issue\",\"The horse is getting old\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The vet must know why the coat is not shining, implying it is a symptom of a health problem.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a canary bird is singing very faintly, what action is expected according to the poem\'s context?\",\"options\":[\"The keeper should sing louder\",\"The vet should be called to diagnose the problem\",\"The bird should be let out of the cage\",\"The bird should be given more buns\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem lists the canary\'s faint singing as one of the many problems the vet must address.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal\'s behavior is described as \'cannibal\' if it starts biting?\",\"options\":[\"The lion\",\"The cobra\",\"The crocodile\",\"The panther\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem mentions \'If the crocodile turns cannibal and bites\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference the poet highlights between a vet and a \'Doctor\' of man?\",\"options\":[\"Doctors earn less money\",\"Treating humans is an easier way to earn a living\",\"Vets only treat birds\",\"Doctors must know about all zoo animals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poet advises his son to treat humans because it is a \'far more advisable plan\' and an \'easier way\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'zoological crowd\' refer to in the final stanza?\",\"options\":[\"A group of people at the zoo\",\"The variety of animals the vet must treat\",\"The vet\'s assistants\",\"The children visiting the zoo\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the context of the poem, the \'zoological crowd\' refers to the wide range of animals mentioned previously.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the poem, why is the vet\'s job considered more difficult than a human doctor\'s?\",\"options\":[\"Animals are larger than humans\",\"Humans can talk and animals cannot\",\"The vet must understand the unique biology of many different species\",\"The vet has to work outdoors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem emphasizes that \'each of the patients... possesses a different inside\', unlike treating just one species (man).\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the poet\'s ultimate advice to his son regarding a career?\",\"options\":[\"Become a successful vet\",\"Study the insides of all animals\",\"Do not try to be a vet; treat humans instead\",\"Work at the zoo to earn more pay\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poet strongly advises his son to earn a living an easier way by calling himself \'Doctor\' and treating man.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the special term for a doctor who gives medicine or treatment to sick animals and birds?\",\"options\":[\"Dentist\",\"Pediatrician\",\"Veterinarian\",\"Neurologist\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a Veterinarian is a specialist who treats animals and birds.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the specialist list, which part of the body does a Cardiologist treat?\",\"options\":[\"Eye\",\"Heart\",\"Bone\",\"Brain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly matches the Cardiologist with the heart.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What do we call doctors who perform special advanced study of specific parts of the body?\",\"options\":[\"Generalists\",\"Specialists\",\"Nurses\",\"Veterinarians\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to doctors who do advanced study of specific body parts as specialists.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the job of a Veterinarian and a Pediatrician considered tougher than other specialists?\",\"options\":[\"They have to study more books\",\"They treat more patients every day\",\"It is difficult to diagnose their patients\' illnesses\",\"They only work in groups\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions they have a tougher job to diagnose their patients\' illnesses.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person has a fracture in their leg, which specialist from the list should they visit?\",\"options\":[\"Orthopedic\",\"Neurologist\",\"Dentist\",\"Opthalamalogist\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"An Orthopedic specialist treats bones, and a fracture is a bone issue.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which specialist would be consulted for a problem involving the spine?\",\"options\":[\"Cardiologist\",\"Pediatrician\",\"Neurologist\",\"Dentist\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states a Neurologist treats the brain and the spine.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specialist deals with the treatment of small kids?\",\"options\":[\"Veterinarian\",\"Pediatrician\",\"Orthopedic\",\"Opthalamalogist\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a Pediatrician as a doctor for small kids.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a pet parrot is not eating, which doctor should the owner take it to?\",\"options\":[\"Pediatrician\",\"Cardiologist\",\"Veterinarian\",\"Neurologist\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A Veterinarian treats animals and birds, including pets like parrots.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common difficulty shared by both Pediatricians and Veterinarians mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They both treat animals\",\"They both treat patients who cannot easily explain their problems\",\"They both treat only bones\",\"They both work only in hospitals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies their job is tougher because their patients (small kids and animals) cannot communicate symptoms clearly.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following specialists listed in the text does NOT treat human body parts?\",\"options\":[\"Dentist\",\"Opthalamalogist\",\"Veterinarian\",\"Orthopedic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"While the others treat human parts like teeth, eyes, and bones, the Veterinarian treats animals and birds.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specialist listed at number 1 responsible for treating?\",\"options\":[\"Bone\",\"Teeth\",\"Eye\",\"Heart\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the figure, number 1 is the Dentist, who treats teeth.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/086_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which letter in the second column corresponds to the specialist \'Orthopedic\'?\",\"options\":[\"a\",\"c\",\"d\",\"f\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Orthopedic specialist (number 4) matches with \'bone\', which is labeled as \'a\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/086_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the short form of the word \'veterinarian\'?\",\"options\":[\"vet\",\"vety\",\"vete\",\"vett\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the glossary, \'vet\' is the short form of veterinarian.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a \'centipede\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A doctor for birds\",\"A small, long thin creature with many legs\",\"A person who eats human flesh\",\"A sound of pain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines a centipede as a small, long thin creature with many legs.\",\"topic\":\"Glossary\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is described as \'competent\', what do they have?\",\"options\":[\"Very good knowledge and ability\",\"A sound of pain\",\"A quarrel with someone\",\"