Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which phrase is used in the text to show admiration, praise, or to congratulate someone?\",\"options\":[\"Come out of\",\"Ups and downs\",\"Hats off\",\"Week in and week out\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'Hats off\' as a phrase used to show admiration, praise, or to congratulate.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does Sindhu suggest a player should do after identifying their mistakes?\",\"options\":[\"Stop playing for a while\",\"Learn from them\",\"Ignore them and move on\",\"Complain to the coach\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sindhu mentions that one needs to \'identify and learn from your mistakes\'.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \'come out of\' mean in the context of dealing with failure?\",\"options\":[\"To exit a room\",\"To succeed in dealing with a problem and defeat it\",\"To travel to a new country\",\"To lose interest in a sport\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'come out of\' as succeeding in dealing with a problem and defeating it.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Sindhu, what is a constant reality in a player\'s career?\",\"options\":[\"Constant winning\",\"Only downward trends\",\"Ups and downs\",\"No emotional changes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sindhu states that \'ups and downs are always there\' in a player\'s journey.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does Sindhu mention that recovery \'takes time\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because players are lazy\",\"Because it is a psychological process to overcome failure\",\"Because tournaments are far apart\",\"Because physical injuries take long to heal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that coming out of the \'bad feeling\' of a loss is a process that requires time and coping.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student fails a test, which action would align with Sindhu\'s advice for \'dealing with loss\'?\",\"options\":[\"Giving up on the subject entirely\",\"Feeling bad and stopping all studies\",\"Practising harder and learning from the mistakes made\",\"Waiting for luck to change in the next test\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sindhu\'s advice is to come out of the bad feeling, practise harder, and learn from mistakes.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A player has just lost a major match. Based on the text, what is the most \'practical\' way for them to cope?\",\"options\":[\"Expect to win every week without exception\",\"Identify specific mistakes and work on them\",\"Avoid thinking about the loss entirely\",\"Immediately enter another tournament without practice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sindhu emphasizes identifying and learning from mistakes as a way to cope and improve.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the interview, what can be inferred about the mindset of a \'top player\'?\",\"options\":[\"They are not affected by losing\",\"They expect to lose most of their matches\",\"They have high expectations and feel the impact of early losses\",\"They do not believe in practicing after a loss\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says top players feel bad especially because of their status, indicating high expectations.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary message Sindhu conveys regarding professional sports?\",\"options\":[\"Winning is the only thing that matters\",\"Resilience and learning are necessary to handle inevitable losses\",\"Top players never lose in the first round\",\"Badminton is purely a physical game with no emotional aspect\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text focuses on coping with loss, learning from mistakes, and the reality of ups and downs.\",\"topic\":\"Dealing with Loss\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22071,52,8,'English','en','4.3 Golden Chain','[\"- Friendship is a golden chain that grows stronger with time.\",\"- A true friend is like a precious jewel, more valuable than gold.\",\"- Friendship cannot be bought or sold; it is priceless.\",\"- The poem uses figurative language: simile, metaphor, antithesis, alliteration.\",\"- Simile uses words like ‘like’ or ‘as’ to compare (e.g., ‘as bright as a star’).\",\"- Metaphor compares without using ‘like’ or ‘as’ (e.g., ‘Riya is the star of our class’).\",\"- Antithesis shows opposite ideas in the same line (e.g., ‘up and down the hill’).\",\"- Alliteration repeats the same consonant sound at the start of words (e.g., ‘bright bright’).\",\"- A golden chain symbolizes a strong, lasting bond.\",\"- A precious jewel symbolizes rarity and beauty.\",\"- A priceless gift symbolizes something that cannot be measured in money.\",\"- True friendship lasts as long as memory lives.\",\"- Friendship is a strong and blessed tie that binds kindred hearts.\",\"- Friendship is compared to an evergreen forest, an ever-flowing river, and a rainbow after heavy rains to show its enduring nature.\",\"- Acrostic poems use each letter of a name to start a line of praise.\",\"- True friendship is a gift that grows more precious each year.\",\"- The poem’s lines are examples of figurative language that enhance meaning.\",\"- Remember to read the poem carefully before answering questions.\",\"- The poem’s structure helps students identify different figures of speech.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the poet compare friendship to in the opening line of the poem?\",\"options\":[\"A silver ring\",\"A golden chain\",\"A diamond necklace\",\"A silk thread\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first line of the poem states, \'Friendship is a Golden chain\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the author of the poem \'Golden Chain\'?\",\"options\":[\"Robert Frost\",\"William Wordsworth\",\"Helen Steiner Rice\",\"Rabindranath Tagore\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The name of the poet mentioned at the end of the text is Helen Steiner Rice.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the poem, what are the \'links\' that make up the golden chain?\",\"options\":[\"Gold coins\",\"Precious jewels\",\"Our dear friends\",\"Happy memories\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The second line of the poem says, \'The links our friends so dear\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the chain of friendship \'clasped together\' according to the second stanza?\",\"options\":[\"With strong iron locks\",\"With a love that\'s deep and true\",\"With expensive golden wires\",\"With years of hard work\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem states it is \'clasped together firmly \\/ With a love that\'s deep and true\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which phrase from the final stanza is best illustrated by the children standing close with their arms around each other?\",\"options\":[\"Treasured more each year\",\"Binding kindred hearts together\",\"Can\'t be bought or sold\",\"Rare and precious jewel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The physical closeness and connection shown in the picture represent the \'binding\' of hearts mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/097_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the poem, what is the value of an \'understanding friend\' compared to gold?\",\"options\":[\"It is equal to the value of gold\",\"It is worth less than gold\",\"It is worth far more than gold\",\"It cannot be compared to gold at all\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem states, \'But to have an understanding friend \\/ Is worth far more than gold\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the happy expressions on the children\'s faces best represent which specific word from the third stanza?\",\"options\":[\"Time\",\"Memory\",\"Pleasure\",\"Destroy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The smiling faces reflect the \'pleasure\' and \'joy\' that friendship gives as described in the third stanza.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/097_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the poet say that \'Time can\'t destroy\' the beauty of friendship?\",\"options\":[\"Because gold never rusts\",\"Because it lives as long as memory lives\",\"Because friends never grow old\",\"Because it is kept in a safe place\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The poem says, \'Time can\'t destroy its beauty \\/ For as long as memory lives\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the poet mean by calling friendship a \'priceless gift\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is very expensive to maintain\",\"It has no value at all\",\"It cannot be bought or sold with money\",\"It is given only on birthdays\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains it is a \'priceless gift \\/ That can\'t be bought or sold\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the effect of \'years going passing by\' on the golden chain of friendship according to the poem?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the chain weak and old\",\"It erases the memories of friends\",\"It treasures the jewel more and binds hearts together\",\"It destroys the beauty of the links\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The poem says it is \'treasured more each year\' and binds hearts \'as the years go passing by\'.\",\"topic\":\"कविता\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the glossary provided, what is the meaning of the word \'kindred\'?\",\"options\":[\"Alike or similar\",\"Different or unique\",\"Held tightly\",\"Wiped away\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The glossary explicitly defines \'kindred\' as \'alike, similar\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'recollections\' refer to in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Planning for the future\",\"Remembering the past\",\"Holding something tightly\",\"Sharing secrets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'recollections\' as \'remembering the past\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the questions asked in the text, which possession is considered more precious than gold?\",\"options\":[\"Recollections\",\"Kindred spirits\",\"True friendship\",\"Secrets and sorrows\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks \'Which possession is more precious than gold?\', implying friendship is the answer in this context.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the glossary means to \'wipe away\'?\",\"options\":[\"Clasped\",\"Kindred\",\"Erase\",\"Recollections\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'erase\' as \'wipe away\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a friend is going through a difficult time, which \'Do\' mentioned in the text should you follow?\",\"options\":[\"Allow them to copy answers\",\"Help them in times of trouble\",\"Erase their recollections\",\"Keep them clasped tightly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Dos\' section in the diagram lists \'Help in times of trouble\' as a point for true friendship.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Chit-Chat\' section, which of the following is NOT mentioned as something we share with friends?\",\"options\":[\"Secrets\",\"Happiness\",\"Sorrows\",\"Gold\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions sharing secrets, happiness, and sorrows, but not gold.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many total points are requested to be written under the \'Dos\' category of \'True Friendship\'?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"5\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The instructions in the figure explicitly state \'Write 5 points each about Dos and Don\'ts\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/095_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Chit-Chat\' questions, what is a likely reason we spend a lot of time with friends?\",\"options\":[\"To erase our past\",\"To enjoy their company and share experiences\",\"To help them copy in exams\",\"To collect more gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The question \'Why do we spend a lot of time in the company of friends?\' implies the value of their company and sharing.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which word from the glossary describes the action of holding something very tightly?\",\"options\":[\"Kindred\",\"Erase\",\"Clasped\",\"Recollections\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The glossary defines \'clasped\' as \'held tightly\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of these is a valid question to ask when discussing the growth of a friendship?\",\"options\":[\"How much gold do you have?\",\"What is needed for friendship to grow?\",\"Can you erase my past?\",\"Are we kindred spirits?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This is one of the specific questions listed in the \'Chit-Chat\' section.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the example provided under the \'Don\'ts\' category refers to which specific activity?\",\"options\":[\"Sharing sorrows\",\"Helping in trouble\",\"Copying answers during exams\",\"Remembering the past\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure lists \'Allow you to copy answers during exams\' as a \'Don\'t\'.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/095_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student allows their friend to copy their homework, which category from the diagram are they violating?\",\"options\":[\"Dos\",\"Don\'ts\",\"Kindred\",\"Recollections\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Allowing a friend to copy (answers\\/work) is listed under the \'Don\'ts\' section of true friendship.\",\"topic\":\"Warming Up\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what figure of speech is used when a comparison is made using the words \'as...as\' or \'like\'?\",\"options\":[\"Metaphor\",\"Antithesis\",\"Simile\",\"Personification\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when a comparison occurs using words like \'as...as\' or \'like\', it is a Simile.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the definition of a Metaphor as per the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"A comparison using the word \'like\'\",\"An indirect or implied comparison without using \'as...as\'\",\"A line containing opposite ideas\",\"A direct comparison using \'just like\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Metaphor as a comparison that is indirectly understood or suggested without using words like \'as...as\' or \'just like\'.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which figure of speech occurs when opposite ideas are placed in the same line of a poem?\",\"options\":[\"Simile\",\"Metaphor\",\"Antithesis\",\"Alliteration\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when opposite ideas occur in the same line, the figure of speech is Antithesis.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the example \'tall just like a tree\', why is the figure of speech a Simile?\",\"options\":[\"Because it compares a person to a tree indirectly\",\"Because it uses the word \'like\' for comparison\",\"Because it contains opposite ideas\",\"Because it is a suggested comparison\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'tall just like a tree\' as a Simile because it uses the word \'like\' for comparison.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'Life is a game. Play it well\' classified as a Metaphor?\",\"options\":[\"It uses the word \'as\' to compare life to a game\",\"It uses the word \'like\' to compare life to a game\",\"The comparison is implied without using \'as\' or \'like\'\",\"It contains two opposite ideas in one line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It is a Metaphor because the comparison is suggested (implied) without using \'as\' or \'like\'.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these examples from the text illustrates Antithesis?\",\"options\":[\"The stormy sea, as dark as coal\",\"Time is money\",\"Up and down the hill, they flew\",\"The Rose is the king of flowers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Up and down\' are opposite ideas in the same line, making it an Antithesis.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the figure of speech in: \'Twinkle Twinkle Little Star, like a Diamond in the sky.\'\",\"options\":[\"Metaphor\",\"Simile\",\"Antithesis\",\"Rhyme\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The use of the word \'like\' to compare the star to a diamond makes it a Simile.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What figure of speech is found in the sentence \'Time is money\'?\",\"options\":[\"Simile\",\"Antithesis\",\"Metaphor\",\"Alliteration\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Time is money\' as an example where the comparison is implied without \'like\' or \'as\', making it a Metaphor.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the sentence \'Many are called, but few are chosen\', which two words create the Antithesis?\",\"options\":[\"Called and Chosen\",\"Many and Few\",\"But and Are\",\"Many and Called\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Antithesis involves opposite ideas; \'Many\' and \'Few\' are the opposites in this line.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the figure of speech in: \'The stormy sea, as dark as coal\'.\",\"options\":[\"Metaphor\",\"Antithesis\",\"Simile\",\"Personification\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The use of \'as dark as\' indicates a direct comparison, which is a Simile.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a Metaphor according to the exercise list in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Like a caged bird sadly sings\",\"The Rose is the king of flowers\",\"As bright as a star\",\"The stormy sea, as dark as coal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'The Rose is the king of flowers\' is an implied comparison without \'like\' or \'as\', making it a Metaphor.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between a Simile and a Metaphor based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Similes use opposite ideas while Metaphors do not\",\"Similes are implied while Metaphors use \'like\' or \'as\'\",\"Similes use \'like\' or \'as\' while Metaphors are implied comparisons\",\"Metaphors are only used in poems while Similes are used in prose\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes them by the presence (Simile) or absence (Metaphor) of words like \'as...as\' and \'like\'.\",\"topic\":\"Figures of Speech\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22072,52,8,'English','en','4.4 The Unsinkable Ship','[\"The Titanic was the largest luxury ship in 1912 and was called ‘Titanic’ meaning huge.\",\"It was called the unsinkable ship because builders believed it could never sink and only a few lifeboats were fitted.\",\"The ship set sail on 10 April 1912 from Southampton to New York and carried more than 2,000 passengers.\",\"On 14 April the ship struck an iceberg at 11:40 p.m. and water flooded the lower decks.\",\"Captain Smith sent radio distress calls at 12:15 a.m.; the Carpathia reached the Titanic after 3 hours and rescued about 700 people.\",\"Only 700 of the 2,200 passengers survived; about 1,500 people drowned.\",\"After the sinking, regulations were changed: all ships must have enough lifeboats for everyone and keep radios on at all times.\",\"In 1985 the wreck was found at a depth of about 4,000 m (13,000 ft) on the ocean floor.\",\"A simile uses ‘as … as’ or ‘like’ to compare two different things, e.g. ‘as tall as a building’.\",\"A compound word is formed by joining two words, e.g. ‘everything’ or ‘life‑skills’.\",\"Root words are the base part of a word, e.g. ‘sink’ in ‘unsinkable’.\",\"Prefixes add meaning before the root (anti‑, dis‑, non‑, re‑).\",\"Suffixes add meaning after the root (‑ful, ‑ous, ‑less, ‑ly).\",\"Simple sentence: one subject and one predicate (e.g. ‘They felt safe.’).\",\"Complex sentence: one main clause + subordinate clause (e.g. ‘Captain Smith knew that there were icebergs nearby.’).\",\"Compound sentence: two or more independent clauses joined by a coordinator (e.g. ‘He fought hard, but the ship changed direction slowly.’).\",\"The Titanic’s unsinkability claim led to complacency and fewer lifeboats, contributing to the disaster.\",\"The Carpathia’s late arrival was due to its distance and the fact that it was not on radio duty at the time.\"]','[{\"q\":\"On which date did the Titanic set sail from Southampton?\",\"options\":[\"10 May 1912\",\"10 April 1912\",\"15 April 1912\",\"10 April 1910\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the ship set sail on 10 April 1912.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'titanic\'?\",\"options\":[\"Beautiful\",\"Unsinkable\",\"Huge\",\"Fast\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the word \'titanic\' means \'huge\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'maiden voyage\' refer to in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"A voyage for women only\",\"The ship\'s final voyage\",\"A voyage across the Atlantic\",\"The ship\'s first voyage\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'maiden voyage\' as \'her first voyage\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many storeys high was the Titanic compared to a building?\",\"options\":[\"Ten storeys\",\"Twenty storeys\",\"Fifteen storeys\",\"Fifty storeys\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text compares the ship\'s height to a building with fifteen storeys.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many passengers were on board the Titanic?\",\"options\":[\"Exactly two thousand\",\"Less than two thousand\",\"More than two thousand\",\"More than five thousand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that she carried more than two thousand passengers.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these facilities was NOT mentioned as being inside the ship?\",\"options\":[\"Grand staircases\",\"Swimming pools\",\"Movie theaters\",\"Dining-rooms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions staircases, dining-rooms, shops, and swimming pools, but not movie theaters.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is an \'iceberg\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A large piece of a glacier on land\",\"A very large mass of ice floating in the sea\",\"A frozen river in the Atlantic\",\"A ship made of ice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an iceberg as a very large mass of ice floating in the sea.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the builders put only a few life-boats on the Titanic?\",\"options\":[\"There was no space for more\",\"They were too expensive\",\"They were sure the ship could never sink\",\"Life-boats were not invented yet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the builders were so sure the ship was unsinkable that they put only a few life-boats.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To which city was the Titanic traveling?\",\"options\":[\"Southampton\",\"London\",\"Paris\",\"New York\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states the ship set sail from Southampton to New York.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a traveler describes a ship as a \'floating town\', what are they implying based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"The ship is very small and crowded\",\"The ship is massive and contains many facilities\",\"The ship only carries people from one town\",\"The ship is made of wood like old houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The comparison to a \'floating town\' and a \'large hotel\' implies its massive size and variety of facilities.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the ship\'s interior, the Titanic was most similar to a:\",\"options\":[\"Small cottage\",\"Large hotel\",\"Military base\",\"Simple boat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states \'Inside, she was like a large hotel\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which ocean did the Titanic set out to cross?\",\"options\":[\"Pacific Ocean\",\"Indian Ocean\",\"Atlantic Ocean\",\"Arctic Ocean\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'She set out to cross the Atlantic Ocean\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Titanic: The Unsinkable Ship\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"On which date did the calm conditions of the Titanic\'s journey change?\",\"options\":[\"12 April\",\"14 April\",\"15 April\",\"11 April\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Then came the night of 14 April.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what specific time was the iceberg first spotted by the lookout?\",\"options\":[\"10.40 p.m.\",\"11.00 p.m.\",\"11.40 p.m.\",\"12.00 a.m.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'Suddenly at 11.40 p.m. the man saw something.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What were the weather and sea conditions on the night of the incident?\",\"options\":[\"Stormy sky and rough sea\",\"Clear sky and calm sea\",\"Cloudy sky and calm sea\",\"Foggy sky and rough sea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the night as, \'The sky was clear and the sea was calm.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Captain Smith order a man to keep a watch from the top of the ship?\",\"options\":[\"To look for other ships\",\"To enjoy the clear sky\",\"Because he knew icebergs were nearby\",\"To check the speed of the ship\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Captain Smith knew that there were icebergs nearby, so he told a man to keep a watch.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'steer\' mean in the context of Captain Smith\'s actions?\",\"options\":[\"To stop the ship\",\"To speed up the ship\",\"To turn the ship\",\"To sink the ship\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the vocabulary notes in the text, \'to steer : to turn\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happened immediately after the ship\'s side \'scraped\' against the iceberg?\",\"options\":[\"The ship stopped instantly\",\"A shower of ice fell on to the decks\",\"The passengers all woke up screaming\",\"The ship turned completely around\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says, \'her side scraped deep... A shower of ice fell on to the decks.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what does the word \'scraped\' imply about the contact between the ship and the ice?\",\"options\":[\"It barely touched the surface\",\"It bounced off the ice\",\"It scratched deep against a rough surface\",\"It broke the iceberg into pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'scraped\' as \'scratched deep against a rough surface\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the water first begin to pour into the Titanic?\",\"options\":[\"The captain\'s cabin\",\"The top decks\",\"The lower decks\",\"The engine room\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Water began to pour into the lower decks.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why was Captain Smith unable to avoid the iceberg entirely?\",\"options\":[\"The ship was moving too fast to stop\",\"The ship changed direction slowly\",\"The lookout forgot to yell\",\"The steering wheel was broken\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains, \'but the ship changed direction slowly and her side scraped deep...\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How did the majority of the passengers react at the exact moment of the collision?\",\"options\":[\"They rushed to the lifeboats\",\"They helped the Captain steer\",\"They heard nothing and continued to sleep\",\"They gathered on the deck to see the ice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Most passengers heard nothing and slept on.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What caused the man on watch to \'grow cold with fear\'?\",\"options\":[\"The freezing temperature of the night\",\"The sight of a huge iceberg in the ship\'s path\",\"The sound of the ship\'s engine failing\",\"The realization that the Captain was asleep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says he grew cold with fear because \'A huge iceberg stood right in the path of the Titanic.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which feeling best describes the passengers\' attitude before the night of 14 April?\",\"options\":[\"Terrified and anxious\",\"Bored and restless\",\"Proud and safe\",\"Angry and uncomfortable\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'The passengers were proud... They felt safe.\'\",\"topic\":\"The Night of 14 April\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"At what time did Captain Smith send the first urgent radio message for help?\",\"options\":[\"12:00 a.m.\",\"12:15 a.m.\",\"2:20 a.m.\",\"3:40 a.m.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that at 12.15 a.m., Captain Smith sent urgent messages on the ship’s radio asking for help.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many people were on board the Titanic when it began to sink?\",\"options\":[\"Exactly seven hundred\",\"Exactly fifteen hundred\",\"More than two thousand\",\"Less than a thousand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the Titanic had more than two thousand people on board.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On what date did the Titanic finally vanish into the sea?\",\"options\":[\"14 April\",\"15 April\",\"16 April\",\"12 April\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the ship vanished at 2.20 a.m. on 15 April.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of the word \'tilt\' as used in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"To sink straight down\",\"To move in a sloping, bent position\",\"To spin around rapidly\",\"To stay perfectly upright\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines tilt as \'move in a sloping, bent position\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why did \'The California\' not respond to the Titanic’s distress signals?\",\"options\":[\"She was too far away to help\",\"Her radio was turned off and the operator was asleep\",\"She did not have any life-boats\",\"She was also sinking in the ice-cold water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that \'The California\' did not receive messages because her radio was turned off and the operator was asleep.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the \'Carpathia\' unable to save the people who were in the water?\",\"options\":[\"It did not have enough space for them\",\"It arrived too late to save those in the water\",\"It could not find the location of the sinking\",\"Its crew was not trained for rescue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Carpathia was too late to save them as most had drowned or died of cold by 3:40 a.m.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How far away was \'The California\' from the Titanic?\",\"options\":[\"Ten miles\",\"Fifteen miles\",\"Twenty miles\",\"Thirty miles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'The California\' was only twenty miles away.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the Titanic vanished at 2:20 a.m. and the Carpathia arrived at 3:40 a.m., how much time passed between the sinking and the rescue ship\'s arrival?\",\"options\":[\"40 minutes\",\"1 hour and 20 minutes\",\"2 hours and 10 minutes\",\"3 hours\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Subtracting 2:20 from 3:40 gives 1 hour and 20 minutes.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what was a major factor that killed the people who jumped into the sea?\",\"options\":[\"Attacks by sea creatures\",\"The depth of the ocean\",\"The ice-cold temperature of the water\",\"Lack of life-jackets\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions people died of cold in the ice-cold water.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the problem with the life-boats on the Titanic?\",\"options\":[\"They were broken and could not float\",\"The crew forgot to lower them into the sea\",\"There was only enough space for a few hundred passengers\",\"They were too heavy to be moved\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there was only enough space in the boat for a few hundred passengers despite having over two thousand people.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the Carpathia\'s response to the distress call?\",\"options\":[\"They ignored the first few calls\",\"They sailed at full speed but were limited by their distance\",\"They waited for \'The California\' to respond first\",\"They arrived before the Titanic vanished\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says Carpathia sailed at full speed but took over three hours to reach because she was far away.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Apart from life-boats, what did the Carpathia\'s crew find floating on the water?\",\"options\":[\"Unused life-jackets\",\"Chairs and pieces of wood\",\"Food supplies from the kitchen\",\"Radio equipment\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the crew found chairs and pieces of wood floating on the water.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many people drowned when the Titanic sank?\",\"options\":[\"Seven hundred\",\"One thousand\",\"Fifteen hundred\",\"Two thousand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states \'About fifteen hundred people drowned\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the Carpathia took over three hours to reach the Titanic and arrived at 3:40 a.m., when did it likely receive the message and start moving?\",\"options\":[\"After 1:00 a.m.\",\"Before 12:40 a.m.\",\"Exactly at 2:20 a.m.\",\"At 3:00 a.m.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If it took over 3 hours to arrive at 3:40, it must have started before 12:40 a.m.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text imply about Captain Smith\'s fate?\",\"options\":[\"He was saved by the Carpathia\",\"He escaped in the first life-boat\",\"He stayed with the ship and died as it sank\",\"He swam to \'The California\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Captain Smith and most of his crew went down with the ship\'.\",\"topic\":\"The Sinking and Rescue Efforts\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In which year were the two new safety compulsions laid down for all ships?\",\"options\":[\"1912\",\"1913\",\"1985\",\"1998\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in 1913, it was decided that all ships must follow new safety rules.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what depth was the wreck of the Titanic found on the ocean floor?\",\"options\":[\"Around 1912 metres\",\"Around 1985 metres\",\"Around 4000 metres\",\"Around 13,000 metres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the ship was found at a depth of around 4000 metres (13,000 feet).\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who directed the famous film about the Titanic\'s maiden voyage in 1998?\",\"options\":[\"A group of scientists\",\"A submarine captain\",\"James Cameron\",\"A survivor of the ship\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly names James Cameron as the director of the 1998 film.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the new rule regarding life-boats after the Titanic disaster?\",\"options\":[\"Ships must carry life-boats only for women and children.\",\"Ships must have enough room in life-boats for everyone on board.\",\"Ships must carry fewer life-boats to save space.\",\"Life-boats must be kept in two pieces.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The 1913 decision required ships to have enough life-boat room for everyone on board.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What did scientists use to discover the wreck of the Titanic in 1985?\",\"options\":[\"A large fishing net\",\"A submarine\",\"A life-boat\",\"A radio transmitter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in 1985, scientists sent a submarine to look for the wreck.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In what state was the Titanic found when scientists reached the ocean floor?\",\"options\":[\"It was perfectly intact.\",\"It was lying in two pieces.\",\"It had vanished completely.\",\"It was floating near the surface.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the ship as lying in two pieces on the ocean floor.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do most people leave the cinema in tears after watching the 1998 film?\",\"options\":[\"Because the film is very long.\",\"Because the film is very sad.\",\"Because the film is about scientists.\",\"Because the film shows the submarine.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions it is a very sad film and almost everyone leaves in tears.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a ship has 1,200 people on board in the year 1914, how much life-boat space must it provide according to the new rules?\",\"options\":[\"Space for 600 people\",\"Space for 1,000 people\",\"Space for 1,200 people\",\"Space for 4,000 people\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The 1913 rule states there must be enough room for \'everyone on board\'.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the 1913 radio rule, if a ship is sailing at 3:00 AM, what must be true?\",\"options\":[\"The radio must be turned off to save power.\",\"The radio must be kept working.\",\"The radio should only be used if an iceberg is seen.\",\"The radio operator should be sleeping.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rule states ships must keep radios working every hour of the day and night.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was the immediate result of people \'thinking harder\' about passenger safety after the sinking?\",\"options\":[\"They stopped building ships.\",\"They made the Titanic a museum.\",\"They questioned why radios were turned off and life-boats were few.\",\"They sent a submarine immediately in 1912.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says people asked why ships carry few life-boats and turn off radios as a result of thinking harder about safety.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the actions taken by scientists after finding the wreck?\",\"options\":[\"They left the wreck untouched and only observed it.\",\"They brought the entire ship back to the surface.\",\"They took photographs and brought small parts to the surface.\",\"They repaired the two pieces underwater.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states they took photographs and brought small parts of the wreck to the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many years passed between the sinking of the Titanic and the discovery of its wreck by scientists?\",\"options\":[\"1 year\",\"72 years\",\"73 years\",\"86 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The ship sank in 1912 and the wreck was found in 1985 (1985 - 1912 = 73).\",\"topic\":\"Safety Rules and Later Discovery\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22073,52,8,'English','en','4.5 Festivals of North East India','[\"Festivals are special days celebrated with joy and traditions.\",\"In North East India, festivals are linked to agriculture and seasons.\",\"Assam’s chief festival is Bihu, celebrated three times a year for sowing, harvesting and reaping.\",\"Bihu dance is performed by young men and women with brisk steps and traditional instruments like dhol and pepa.\",\"Arunachal Pradesh’s new year festival is called Losar; people clean homes and discard old items to welcome the new year.\",\"Manipur’s Ningol Chakouba celebrates the bond between married girls and their parents with feasts and gifts.\",\"Mizoram’s Spring Festival or Chapchar Kut is celebrated with folk dances and traditional songs.\",\"Meghalaya’s Wangala is a harvest festival where men and women dance in parallel lines while beating drums.\",\"Tripura’s festivals involve dances that represent hunting, fishing and food‑gathering.\",\"Nagaland’s Moastu festival is celebrated after sowing season with colourful clothes and headgear.\",\"Festivals in the seven states often involve music, dance, food, and a sense of community.\",\"They help preserve oral history and cultural identity.\",\"Festivals also bring out natural talent and encourage artistic activities.\",\"Common features of festivals: music, dance, food, decorations, and communal participation.\",\"Festivals are important for thanking gods, seeking protection, and celebrating harvests.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which festival is mentioned in the text as being celebrated along with Makar Sankranti?\",\"options\":[\"Bihu\",\"Pongal\",\"Eid\",\"Onam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Makar Sankranti\\/Pongal\' as one of the festivals celebrated in our country.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Chit-Chat\' section, what is one of the things a student should consider regarding festivals?\",\"options\":[\"The cost of the festival\",\"The relation between seasons and festivals\",\"The history of the state\",\"The number of guests to invite\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Question 5 in the Chit-Chat section asks \'What is the relation between seasons and festivals?\'\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific verb is provided as an example related to \'Festivals\'?\",\"options\":[\"decorate\",\"worship\",\"celebrate\",\"invite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram for Festivals (Related Verbs) shows \'celebrate\' in one of the bubbles.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/107_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which adjective is used to describe \'Music\'?\",\"options\":[\"soft\",\"loud\",\"sweet\",\"noisy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram for Music (Related Adjectives) provides \'loud\' as the example word.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/107_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which noun is listed as a word related to \'Dance\'?\",\"options\":[\"rhythm\",\"costume\",\"steps\",\"stage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram for Dance (Related Nouns) shows \'steps\' as the example word.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"52\\/107_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The text asks to frame words for \'Music\' using adjectives. Which of the following is an appropriate adjective to add to that list?\",\"options\":[\"Sing\",\"Melodious\",\"Musician\",\"Rhythm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Melodious is an adjective that describes music, fitting the instruction to use adjectives.\",\"topic\":\"WARMING UP !\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT listed as a festival celebrated in our country in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Dussehra\",\"Ramzan\",\"Holi\",\"Halloween\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists Diwali, Holi, Ramzan, Dussehra, Christmas, and Makar Sankranti