e the length of segment OP?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"$\\\\sqrt{2}$\",\"$-\\\\sqrt{2}$\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since Q is marked by an arc with radius OP, the distance OQ must be equal to OP.\",\"topic\":\"Showing $\\\\sqrt{2}$ on a number line\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/015_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the length of OA is 1 unit and AP is 1 unit, what is the length of the hypotenuse OP in the right-angled triangle OAP?\",\"options\":[\"2 units\",\"1 unit\",\"$\\\\sqrt{2}$ units\",\"$\\\\sqrt{1}$ units\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"By taking square roots of OP² = 2, we get OP = $\\\\sqrt{2}$.\",\"topic\":\"Showing $\\\\sqrt{2}$ on a number line\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what type of number is $\\\\pi$?\",\"options\":[\"Rational number\",\"Irrational number\",\"Natural number\",\"Whole number\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that $\\\\pi$ is not a rational number, which means it is irrational.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the numbers called that can be shown by points on a number line?\",\"options\":[\"Only rational numbers\",\"Only irrational numbers\",\"Real numbers\",\"Natural numbers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines real numbers as the numbers which can be shown by points on a number line.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the values $\\\\frac{22}{7}$ and 3.14 used for $\\\\pi$ in calculations?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are exactly equal to $\\\\pi$\",\"Because they are irrational numbers\",\"Because they are very close to $\\\\pi$\",\"Because $\\\\pi$ is a rational number\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states these values are used for calculation purpose because they are very close to $\\\\pi$.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding rational and real numbers?\",\"options\":[\"Only some rational numbers are real numbers\",\"All rational numbers are real numbers\",\"No rational number is a real number\",\"Rational numbers cannot be shown on a number line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that since all rational numbers can be shown on a number line, all rational numbers are real numbers.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If $\\\\sqrt{2}$ is an irrational number, what type of number is $3\\\\sqrt{2}$?\",\"options\":[\"Rational number\",\"Irrational number\",\"Integer\",\"Natural number\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that numbers like $3\\\\sqrt{2}$ are also irrational numbers.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many irrational numbers are there on the number line?\",\"options\":[\"Only one\",\"Exactly two\",\"A limited number\",\"Infinitely many\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states there are infinitely many irrational numbers on the number line.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the logic in the text, if $3\\\\sqrt{2}$ were a rational number, then $\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ would have to be a:\",\"options\":[\"Rational number\",\"Irrational number\",\"Real number only\",\"Imaginary number\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text argues that if $3\\\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, then $\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ should also be a rational number.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a rational number used as an approximation for $\\\\pi$?\",\"options\":[\"$\\\\sqrt{2}$\",\"$\\\\pi$\",\"$\\\\frac{22}{7}$\",\"$\\\\sqrt{3}$\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions $\\\\frac{22}{7}$ and 3.14 are rational numbers used for calculation purpose.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following numbers is identified as irrational in the text?\",\"options\":[\"3.14\",\"$\\\\frac{22}{7}$\",\"$7+\\\\sqrt{2}$\",\"0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically notes that $7+\\\\sqrt{2}$ is an irrational number.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Besides $\\\\sqrt{2}$, which other irrational numbers does the text mention can be shown on a number line?\",\"options\":[\"Only $\\\\pi$\",\"$\\\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\\\sqrt{5}$\",\"Only $\\\\frac{22}{7}$\",\"Only 3.14\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Similarly we can show irrational numbers like $\\\\sqrt{3}$, $\\\\sqrt{5}$ ... on a number line.\'\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If we know $3-\\\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational, what can we conclude about its position on a number line?\",\"options\":[\"It cannot be shown on a number line\",\"It can be shown by a point on a number line\",\"It is not a real number\",\"It does not have a fixed value\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all real numbers (including irrational ones mentioned) can be shown by points on a number line.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the statement \'$\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ is a rational number\' considered not true in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because $\\\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational\",\"Because 3 is a rational number\",\"Because $\\\\pi$ is irrational\",\"Because 3.14 is rational\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text implies this is not true because $\\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ simplifies to $\\\\sqrt{2}$, which is known to be irrational.\",\"topic\":\"Note that -\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what type of triangle is obtained by drawing segment OR?\",\"options\":[\"Equilateral triangle\",\"Right angled triangle\",\"Isosceles triangle\",\"Obtuse angled triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Right angled Δ ORQ is obtained by drawing seg OR\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Through which point on the number line is the perpendicular line drawn?\",\"options\":[\"Point O\",\"Point R\",\"Point Q\",\"Point P\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'A line perpendicular to the number line is drawn through the point Q\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which theorem is used to calculate the length of segment OR?\",\"options\":[\"Archimedes theorem\",\"Thales theorem\",\"Pythagoras theorem\",\"Euclid\'s theorem\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'by Pythagoras theorem\' to calculate the length of OR.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If point R is at a \'unit distance\' from Q, what is the length of segment QR?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"1\",\"2\",\"√2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Unit distance means a distance of 1 unit, as shown in l(QR) = 1.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the value of [l(OQ)]² if l(OQ) = √2?\",\"options\":[\"√2\",\"4\",\"2\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The square of √2 is 2.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the Pythagoras theorem formula [l(OR)]² = [l(OQ)]² + [l(QR)]², which segment represents the hypotenuse?\",\"options\":[\"seg OR\",\"seg OQ\",\"seg QR\",\"The number line\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In a right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, what is the sum of [l(OQ)]² and [l(QR)]²?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"√3\",\"3\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The calculation is (√2)² + (1)² = 2 + 1 = 3.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final length of segment OR as calculated in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"√2\",\"1\",\"√3\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Since [l(OR)]² = 3, taking the square root gives l(OR) = √3.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If l(OQ) = √2, what number does point Q represent on the number line?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"√2\",\"√3\",\"0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The length from the origin O to point Q is √2, so Q represents the number √2.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly represents the step-by-step calculation of [l(OR)]²?\",\"options\":[\"(√2)² + 1² = 2 + 1 = 3\",\"(√2)² + 1² = 4 + 1 = 5\",\"2² + 1² = 4 + 1 = 5\",\"√2 + 1 = √3\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows the square of the sides being added: (√2)² is 2 and 1² is 1.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Pythagoras theorem\",\"part\":15,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which point is used as the centre to draw the arc?\",\"options\":[\"Point C\",\"Point R\",\"Point O\",\"Point $\\\\sqrt{3}$\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Draw an arc with centre O\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the radius of the arc that needs to be drawn?\",\"options\":[\"Radius OC\",\"Radius OR\",\"Radius $\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"Radius O\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions drawing an arc with \'radius OR\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which number is represented by point C on the line?\",\"options\":[\"$\\\\sqrt{2}$\",\"$\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"0\",\"1\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The point C shows the number $\\\\sqrt{3}$\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Point C is defined as the intersection of which two geometric elements?\",\"options\":[\"The line and the radius\",\"The centre and the arc\",\"The line and the arc\",\"The point O and point R\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Mark the point of intersection of the line and the arc as C\'.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If point C represents the number $\\\\sqrt{3}$ on the number line starting from O, what is the length of the segment OC?\",\"options\":[\"OR\",\"$\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"Both OR and $\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"Cannot be determined\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since C shows the number $\\\\sqrt{3}$ on the line, the distance from the origin O to C is $\\\\sqrt{3}$.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of drawing the arc with radius OR in this construction?\",\"options\":[\"To find the centre O\",\"To mark the point R on the line\",\"To transfer the distance OR onto the line to locate point C\",\"To measure the length of the line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The arc is used to intersect the line at point C, which then represents $\\\\sqrt{3}$.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to locate $\\\\sqrt{3}$ on a number line using this method, where should they place the metal point of the compass?\",\"options\":[\"At point R\",\"At point C\",\"At point O\",\"At $\\\\sqrt{3}$\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The compass point is placed at the centre of the arc, which is point O.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the radius OR is equal to $\\\\sqrt{3}$ units, what will be the distance of point C from point O?\",\"options\":[\"$\\\\sqrt{2}$ units\",\"$\\\\sqrt{3}$ units\",\"3 units\",\"1 unit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The arc transfers the radius length OR to the line as OC; if OR = $\\\\sqrt{3}$, then OC = $\\\\sqrt{3}$.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the construction steps, what can be concluded about the relationship between the lengths OR and OC?\",\"options\":[\"OR is longer than OC\",\"OC is longer than OR\",\"OR and OC are equal in length\",\"OR is half of OC\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since C lies on an arc with centre O and radius OR, the distance OC must equal the radius OR.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of showing $\\\\sqrt{3}$ on a number line, what does the intersection point C represent?\",\"options\":[\"The exact location of the irrational number $\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"The square of 3\",\"The radius of the circle\",\"The midpoint of OR\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that point C shows the number $\\\\sqrt{3}$ on the line.\",\"topic\":\"Exercise\",\"part\":17,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22114,55,8,'Maths','en','Parallel lines and transversals','[\"Parallel lines are two lines in the same plane that never meet, no matter how far they are extended.\",\"A line that cuts two or more lines is called a transversal.\",\"When a transversal cuts two parallel lines, it creates eight angles at the two intersection points.\",\"These eight angles can be grouped into four pairs: corresponding, alternate interior, alternate exterior, and consecutive interior (also called same-side interior).\",\"Corresponding angles are on the same side of the transversal and in the same relative position on each line; they are equal when the lines are parallel.\",\"Alternate interior angles lie on opposite sides of the transversal and inside the two lines; they are equal when the lines are parallel.\",\"Alternate exterior angles lie on opposite sides of the transversal and outside the two lines; they are equal when the lines are parallel.\",\"Consecutive interior angles are on the same side of the transversal and inside the two lines; they are supplementary (sum to 180°) when the lines are parallel.\",\"If any one pair of corresponding angles is equal, the two lines are parallel (converse).\",\"If any one pair of alternate interior angles is equal, the two lines are parallel (converse).\",\"If any one pair of consecutive interior angles is supplementary, the two lines are parallel (converse).\",\"The sum of the four interior angles around a point is 360°; this helps in solving for unknown angles.\",\"When two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, the angles on the same side of the transversal add up to 180° (consecutive interior).\",\"The measure of an angle formed by a transversal and a parallel line is the same as the angle formed by the transversal and the other parallel line at the corresponding position.\",\"In a diagram, if you know one angle, you can find the other seven using the properties above.\",\"The term “linear pair” refers to two adjacent angles that form a straight line and together measure 180°.\",\"When constructing a parallel line with a set‑square, the line drawn through a given point that keeps the same angle with a reference line is parallel.\",\"The distance between two parallel lines can be maintained by drawing perpendiculars from points on one line to the other and joining the feet of equal length.\",\"In geometry proofs, you often use the fact that corresponding angles are equal or consecutive interior angles are supplementary to establish parallelism.\",\"The symbol for parallel lines is ‘∥’ (e.g., line l ∥ line m).\",\"The symbol for a transversal cutting two lines is often shown as a dashed line crossing the two lines.\",\"When solving for an unknown angle, set up an equation using the fact that the sum of angles around a point or in a linear pair is 180° or 360°.\",\"Always check that the angles you identify belong to the correct pair (corresponding, alternate interior, etc.) before applying the property.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are lines in the same plane that do not intersect each other called?\",\"options\":[\"Intersecting lines\",\"Transversal lines\",\"Parallel lines\",\"Perpendicular lines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines parallel lines as lines in the same plane which do not intersect each other.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used to represent that \'line l and line m are parallel\'?\",\"options\":[\"line l = line m\",\"line l || line m\",\"line l + line m\",\"line l ⊥ line m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'line l and line m are parallel lines\' is written as \'line l || line m\'.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the definition, a line is a transversal if it intersects two given lines in how many points?\",\"options\":[\"One single point\",\"Two distinct points\",\"Three different points\",\"No points at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A line is called a transversal if it intersects two given lines in two distinct points.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the description provided, if line l intersects line m and line n, which line is identified as the transversal?\",\"options\":[\"line m\",\"line n\",\"line l\",\"Both line m and line n\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'line l is a transversal of line m and line n\'.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the essential condition for lines to be considered parallel besides not intersecting?\",\"options\":[\"They must be in the same plane\",\"They must be of the same length\",\"They must intersect a transversal\",\"They must be vertical\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines parallel lines as lines \'in the same plane\' which do not intersect.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If line p intersects line q and line r at the exact same single point, is line p a transversal of q and r?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, because it intersects both lines\",\"No, because it must intersect them in two distinct points\",\"Yes, because all three lines are in the same plane\",\"No, because the lines must be parallel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition requires the intersection to occur at two distinct points to be a transversal.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If line l || line m, what can be concluded about their intersection?\",\"options\":[\"They intersect at one point\",\"They intersect at two distinct points\",\"They do not intersect each other\",\"They are the same line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Parallel lines (||) are defined as lines that do not intersect each other.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Line x intersects line y at point A and line z at point B. If point A and point B are different locations, what is line x?\",\"options\":[\"A parallel line\",\"A transversal\",\"A plane\",\"A distinct line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since line x intersects two lines at two distinct points (A and B), it fits the definition of a transversal.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following must be true for line l to be a transversal of lines m and n?\",\"options\":[\"Line m must be parallel to line n\",\"Line l must not intersect line m\",\"Line l must intersect m and n at two different points\",\"Lines l, m, and n must never meet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines a transversal based on its intersection with two lines at two distinct points.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what distinguishes a transversal from the two lines it intersects?\",\"options\":[\"The transversal is longer than the other lines\",\"The transversal is the line that performs the intersection at distinct points\",\"The transversal must be parallel to one of the lines\",\"The transversal does not lie in the same plane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the line that \'intersects given two lines\' as the transversal.\",\"topic\":\"Transversal\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How many total angles are formed when a transversal intersects two distinct lines at two points?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"6\",\"8\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that at each of the two points of intersection, four angles are formed, making 8 angles in all.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the second arm of an angle formed by a transversal located if one arm is on the transversal?\",\"options\":[\"On the other transversal\",\"On one of the given lines\",\"Parallel to the transversal\",\"Perpendicular to the given lines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions each angle has one arm on the transversal and the other is on one of the given lines.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific name for a pair of angles on the same side of the transversal and inside the given lines?\",\"options\":[\"Corresponding angles\",\"Interior angles\",\"Alternate angles\",\"Exterior angles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition provided states that angles on the same side of the transversal and inside the given lines are interior angles.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a pair of corresponding angles, what is true about their arms on the transversal?\",\"options\":[\"They are in opposite directions\",\"They are perpendicular to each other\",\"They are in the same direction\",\"They do not lie on the transversal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines corresponding angles as having arms on the transversal in the same direction.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which characteristic defines a pair of alternate angles according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are on the same side of the transversal\",\"Their arms on the transversal show opposite directions\",\"They are both outside the given lines only\",\"They share the same vertex\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Alternate angles are defined as being on opposite sides of the transversal with arms on the transversal showing opposite directions.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many pairs of interior alternate angles are formed by a transversal?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states there are two pairs of interior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If ∠AMP and ∠MNR are a pair of corresponding angles, which of the following is also a pair of corresponding angles?\",\"options\":[\"∠PMN and ∠MNR\",\"∠AMQ and ∠MNS\",\"∠PMN and ∠MNS\",\"∠AMP and ∠TNS\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists (iii) ∠AMQ and ∠MNS as a pair of corresponding angles.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the pair that represents interior alternate angles from the given list.\",\"options\":[\"∠AMP and ∠MNR\",\"∠PMN and ∠MNR\",\"∠PMN and ∠MNS\",\"∠AMQ and ∠RNT\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies ∠PMN and ∠MNS as a pair of interior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these pairs is classified as exterior alternate angles in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"∠QMN and ∠RNM\",\"∠AMQ and ∠RNT\",\"∠QMN and ∠SNT\",\"∠PMN and ∠MNR\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists ∠AMQ and ∠RNT as a pair of exterior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary difference between a pair of interior angles and a pair of interior alternate angles?\",\"options\":[\"Interior angles are outside the lines, alternate are inside\",\"Interior angles are on the same side of the transversal, alternate are on opposite sides\",\"Interior angles have arms in the same direction, alternate have arms in opposite directions\",\"There is no difference; they are the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Based on the definitions, interior angles are on the same side, while alternate angles are on opposite sides of the transversal.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are looking for angles at the \'outer side of lines\' that are on opposite sides of the transversal, which group should you look in?\",\"options\":[\"Corresponding angles\",\"Interior angles\",\"Interior alternate angles\",\"Exterior alternate angles\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Exterior alternate angles are defined as being on the outer side of lines and on opposite sides of the transversal.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the grouping provided, which pair of angles shares the property of being on the same side of the transversal but differs in whether they are \'inside\' or \'one inside and one outside\' the lines?\",\"options\":[\"Interior angles and Corresponding angles\",\"Interior angles and Alternate angles\",\"Exterior alternate and Interior alternate\",\"Corresponding angles and Alternate angles\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Both interior and corresponding angles are on the same side of the transversal, but interior angles are both \'inside\', while corresponding angles follow the \'same direction\' rule.\",\"topic\":\"Angles made by a transversal\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How many angles are formed in total when two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal?\",\"options\":[\"Four\",\"Six\",\"Eight\",\"Ten\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, eight angles are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given activity, what type of pair do Part I and Part II form when placed together?\",\"options\":[\"Complementary pair\",\"Linear pair\",\"Vertically opposite pair\",\"Interior pair\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity notes that the angles shown by part I and part II form a linear pair.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, which of the following groups of angles are congruent because they all coincide with Part I?\",\"options\":[\"∠a, ∠c, ∠e, ∠g\",\"∠a, ∠b, ∠c, ∠d\",\"∠b, ∠d, ∠f, ∠h\",\"∠e, ∠f, ∠g, ∠h\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states ∠b ≅ ∠d ≅ ∠f ≅ ∠h because these angles coincide with part I.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a pair of interior alternate angles according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"∠a and ∠e\",\"∠d and ∠f\",\"∠a and ∠g\",\"∠c and ∠f\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists ∠d ≅ ∠f and ∠e ≅ ∠c as pairs of interior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the measure of interior angle ∠d is given, what is the sum of m∠d and m∠e?\",\"options\":[\"90°\",\"180°\",\"270°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The property of interior angles states that m∠d + m∠e = 180°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which angle is the corresponding angle for ∠c?\",\"options\":[\"∠a\",\"∠e\",\"∠g\",\"∠h\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the list of corresponding angles (∠c ≅ ∠g), ∠g is the corresponding angle for ∠c.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/019_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the relationship between the two shaded parts labeled I and II?\",\"options\":[\"They are vertically opposite angles\",\"They form a linear pair\",\"They are alternate angles\",\"They are corresponding angles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the angles shown by part I and part II form a linear pair.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/019_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which pair of angles is identified as exterior alternate angles in the text?\",\"options\":[\"∠b and ∠h\",\"∠d and ∠f\",\"∠a and ∠e\",\"∠c and ∠f\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies ∠a ≅ ∠g and ∠b ≅ ∠h as pairs of exterior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If m∠c = 100°, what is the measure of its interior angle partner ∠f?\",\"options\":[\"100°\",\"180°\",\"80°\",\"200°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since m∠c + m∠f = 180°, if m∠c is 100°, then m∠f must be 80°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If ∠a coincides with part II, which other angle will also coincide with part II?\",\"options\":[\"∠b\",\"∠d\",\"∠f\",\"∠g\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that ∠a, ∠c, ∠e, and ∠g all coincide with part II.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Let\'s discuss\' section, if the measure of one of the eight angles is given, how many remaining angles can be found?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Three\",\"Seven\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if we can find the measures of the \'remaining seven angles\' if one is given.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a pair of corresponding angles mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"∠a and ∠e\",\"∠b and ∠f\",\"∠d and ∠h\",\"∠d and ∠f\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"∠d and ∠f are interior alternate angles, not corresponding angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of angles - Activity\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the property of corresponding angles, each pair of corresponding angles formed by two parallel lines and their transversal is of ______.\",\"options\":[\"Supplementary angles\",\"Congruent angles\",\"Complementary angles\",\"Opposite angles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that each pair of corresponding angles formed by two parallel lines and their transversal is of congruent angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the property of interior angles formed by two parallel lines and their transversal?\",\"options\":[\"They are always congruent\",\"They are always acute\",\"They are supplementary angles\",\"They are exterior angles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that each pair of interior angles formed by two parallel lines and their transversal is of supplementary angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, what is the sum of the measures of a pair of interior angles?\",\"options\":[\"90°\",\"180°\",\"270°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Supplementary angles have a sum of 180°, as shown in the equations m∠PMN + m∠MNR = 180°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a pair of interior alternate angles as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"∠AMP and ∠MNR\",\"∠PMN and ∠MNS\",\"∠AMQ and ∠RNB\",\"∠QMN and ∠SNB\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists ∠PMN ≅ ∠MNS and ∠QMN ≅ ∠MNR as interior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If line PQ || line RS and line AB is a transversal, which angle is congruent to ∠AMP as an exterior alternate angle?\",\"options\":[\"∠MNR\",\"∠RNB\",\"∠SNB\",\"∠MNS\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies ∠AMP ≅ ∠SNB as a pair of exterior alternate angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the property of alternate angles, which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Only interior alternate angles are congruent\",\"Only exterior alternate angles are congruent\",\"Each pair of alternate angles is congruent\",\"Alternate angles are supplementary\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that each pair of alternate angles (both interior and exterior) is of congruent angles.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If m∠PMN = 70°, what is the measure of ∠MNR given that line PQ || line RS and line AB is a transversal?\",\"options\":[\"70°\",\"110°\",\"180°\",\"20°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since m∠PMN + m∠MNR = 180°, if m∠PMN = 70°, then m∠MNR = 180° - 70° = 110°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If m∠AMQ = 85°, what is the measure of ∠MNS based on the property of corresponding angles?\",\"options\":[\"95°\",\"180°\",\"85°\",\"170°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Corresponding angles are congruent, so m∠AMQ = m∠MNS = 85°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these mathematical statements correctly represents the property of interior angles for the given parallel lines?\",\"options\":[\"m∠PMN = m∠MNR\",\"m∠QMN + m∠MNS = 180°\",\"m∠AMQ + ∠MNS = 180°\",\"m∠AMP = m∠SNB\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the property of interior angles as m∠QMN + m∠MNS = 180°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which pair of angles are NOT congruent according to the properties described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"∠AMP and ∠MNR\",\"∠QMN and ∠MNR\",\"∠PMN and ∠MNR\",\"∠AMQ and ∠RNB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"∠PMN and ∠MNR are interior angles, which are supplementary (sum to 180°), not necessarily congruent.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which angle forms a pair of interior angles with ∠QMN?\",\"options\":[\"∠MNR\",\"∠MNS\",\"∠RNB\",\"∠SNB\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the figure and text, ∠QMN and ∠MNS are the interior angles on one side of the transversal.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/020_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if m∠QMN = 105°, what is the measure of ∠MNS?\",\"options\":[\"105°\",\"75°\",\"180°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since m∠QMN + m∠MNS = 180°, if m∠QMN = 105°, then m∠MNS = 180° - 105° = 75°.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of Parallel Lines\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/020_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), what is the relationship between ∠MNQ and ∠ONP?\",\"options\":[\"Interior angles\",\"Opposite angles\",\"Corresponding angles\",\"Angles in linear pair\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that m∠MNQ = m∠ONP = 70° because they are opposite angles.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), what is the sum of angles in a linear pair?\",\"options\":[\"90°\",\"180°\",\"270°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows m∠f + m∠e = 180° and identifies them as angles in linear pair.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Method II in Example (1), ∠MNQ and ∠NOB are which type of angles?\",\"options\":[\"Interior angles\",\"Opposite angles\",\"Corresponding angles\",\"Alternate angles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Method II explicitly labels m∠MNQ and m∠NOB as corresponding angles.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), which angle is congruent to ∠b?\",\"options\":[\"∠e\",\"∠l\",\"∠f\",\"∠m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The solution for Ex (2) states ∠b ≅ ∠f because they are corresponding angles.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), why does the sum of m∠AON and m∠ONP equal 180°?\",\"options\":[\"They are opposite angles\",\"They are corresponding angles\",\"They are interior angles\",\"They are vertically opposite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the relationship as \'Interior angles\' which sum to 180° for parallel lines.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), if m∠b = (x + 15)° and ∠b ≅ ∠f, what is the measure of ∠f?\",\"options\":[\"(2x + 15)°\",\"(x + 15)°\",\"180°\",\"50°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since ∠b ≅ ∠f, their measures are equal, so m∠f = m∠b = (x + 15)°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Method II of Example (1), how is m∠AON calculated after finding m∠NOB?\",\"options\":[\"By subtracting m∠NOB from 180°\",\"By adding 70° to 180°\",\"By multiplying m∠NOB by 2\",\"By finding the opposite angle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Method II uses the linear pair property: m∠AON + m∠NOB = 180°, so m∠AON = 180° - 70°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in solving for x in Example (2) after substituting the angle values?\",\"options\":[\"3x = 180°\",\"x + 15 = 180°\",\"x + 15 + 2x + 15 = 180°\",\"3x - 30 = 180°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the equation formed by adding the two expressions for the linear pair: x + 15 + 2x + 15 = 180°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), if m∠MNQ was 80° instead of 70°, what would be the measure of m∠ONP?\",\"options\":[\"100°\",\"80°\",\"110°\",\"70°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since they are opposite angles, they remain equal; if one is 80°, the other is 80°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), if the value of x is found to be 50°, what is the measure of ∠b?\",\"options\":[\"50°\",\"115°\",\"65°\",\"15°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"m∠b = (x + 15)°. Substituting x = 50, we get 50 + 15 = 65°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), Method I and Method II both result in m∠AON = 110°. What does this demonstrate?\",\"options\":[\"Only Method I is mathematically correct\",\"The problem can be solved using different geometric properties\",\"Interior angles are always 70°\",\"Corresponding angles are always 110°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes \'The above example can be solved by another method also\', showing multiple valid geometric paths to the same result.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the equation 3x + 30 = 180 from Example (2), what is the result of subtracting 30 from both sides?\",\"options\":[\"3x = 210\",\"x = 150\",\"3x = 150\",\"3x = 50\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: 3x = 180° - 30° = 150°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, what is the relationship between the angles in each pair of corresponding angles?\",\"options\":[\"They are congruent.\",\"They are supplementary.\",\"They are complementary.\",\"They are unequal.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, corresponding angles are congruent.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, the interior angles formed are ________.\",\"options\":[\"congruent\",\"supplementary\",\"complementary\",\"acute\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that interior angles formed by a transversal intersecting parallel lines are supplementary.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which condition must be met for two coplanar lines to be parallel when intersected by a transversal?\",\"options\":[\"A pair of corresponding angles is supplementary.\",\"A pair of alternate angles is congruent.\",\"A pair of interior angles is congruent.\",\"A pair of alternate angles is supplementary.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if a pair of alternate angles is congruent, then the lines are parallel.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a transversal intersects two coplanar lines and a pair of interior angles is supplementary, what can be concluded about the lines?\",\"options\":[\"The lines are perpendicular.\",\"The lines are parallel.\",\"The lines are intersecting.\",\"The lines are not in the same plane.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if a pair of interior angles is supplementary, then the lines are parallel.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A transversal intersects two parallel lines. If one angle of a pair of alternate angles measures 75°, what is the measure of the other angle in that pair?\",\"options\":[\"105°\",\"75°\",\"180°\",\"15°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since alternate angles are congruent for parallel lines, their measures are equal.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal. If the measure of one interior angle is 110°, what is the measure of the other interior angle in that pair?\",\"options\":[\"110°\",\"70°\",\"180°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Interior angles are supplementary (sum to 180°), so 180° - 110° = 70°.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a correct test for parallelism between two coplanar lines?\",\"options\":[\"Check if interior angles are congruent.\",\"Check if alternate angles are supplementary.\",\"Check if corresponding angles are congruent.\",\"Check if corresponding angles are supplementary.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists congruent corresponding angles as a condition that makes lines parallel.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which pair of angles is NOT necessarily congruent when parallel lines are intersected by a transversal?\",\"options\":[\"Corresponding angles\",\"Alternate angles\",\"Interior angles\",\"Vertically opposite angles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says corresponding and alternate angles are congruent, but interior angles are supplementary.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a transversal intersects two lines such that the corresponding angles are equal in measure, the lines are ________.\",\"options\":[\"Intersecting\",\"Parallel\",\"Perpendicular\",\"Divergent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Congruent (equal measure) corresponding angles indicate that the lines are parallel.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To prove two lines are parallel using interior angles, the sum of the measures of the pair of interior angles must be:\",\"options\":[\"90°\",\"180°\",\"270°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Supplementary angles sum to 180°, which is the requirement for the lines to be parallel.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is specifically mentioned for use in Method I to draw a parallel line?\",\"options\":[\"Compass\",\"Protractor\",\"Set-square\",\"Divider\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Method I uses set-squares to draw a line parallel to a given line.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Method II, what is the geometric relationship between segment PM and line l?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel\",\"Perpendicular\",\"Intersecting at 45 degrees\",\"Collinear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Step 2 of Method II explicitly says \'Draw a seg PM ⊥ line l\', where ⊥ denotes perpendicular.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Method I, where should point P be taken initially?\",\"options\":[\"On line l\",\"Outside line l\",\"At the end of line l\",\"On the set-square\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The construction objective is to draw a line through a point P \'outside the given line\'.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Method II, what condition must be met for the lengths of segments NQ and MP to ensure the lines are parallel?\",\"options\":[\"l(NQ) > l(MP)\",\"l(NQ) < l(MP)\",\"l(NQ) = l(MP)\",\"l(NQ) + l(MP) = 0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 4 of Method II requires drawing seg NQ such that l(NQ) = l(MP).\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is using Method II and draws l(MP) = 5 cm, what length should l(NQ) be to complete the construction correctly?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"7 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The construction requires l(NQ) to be equal to l(MP), which is 5 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Method I, after placing the set-squares and drawing a line along the edge of set-square B, what is the resulting line m in relation to line l?\",\"options\":[\"Perpendicular line\",\"Transversal line\",\"Parallel line\",\"Intersecting line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 5 of Method I states that \'Line m is parallel to line l\'.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which two points are used to define the position of the parallel line m?\",\"options\":[\"M and N\",\"P and M\",\"Q and N\",\"P and Q\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows line m passing through points P and Q, which are endpoints of the perpendiculars.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/023_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the small square symbols at points M and N indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The lines are parallel\",\"The segments are equal\",\"The segments PM and QN are perpendicular to line l\",\"The points are midpoints\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The square symbols represent right angles, indicating perpendicularity as described in Method II.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/023_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In Method II, if l(NQ) was not equal to l(MP), what would be the result for line m?\",\"options\":[\"It would still be parallel\",\"It would not be parallel to line l\",\"It would be perpendicular to line l\",\"It would be the same as line l\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The equality of the perpendicular segments ensures the line passing through P and Q remains at a constant distance from line l.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In Method I, which set-square has one edge close to point P?\",\"options\":[\"Set-square A\",\"Set-square B\",\"Both A and B\",\"Neither A nor B\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Step 3 of Method I states: \'One edge of set - square A is close to point P.\'\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Method II, what is the very first step of the construction process?\",\"options\":[\"Draw segment PM\",\"Take point N\",\"Draw line l\",\"Draw line m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 1 of Method II is \'Draw line l\'.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Method II, why is point N taken on line l?\",\"options\":[\"To draw a second perpendicular segment NQ\",\"To measure the total length of line l\",\"To mark the end of the line\",\"To place the set-square A\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Point N is required as the base point on line l to draw the second perpendicular segment NQ.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (I)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the construction of a parallel line to a given line l at a given distance?\",\"options\":[\"Draw line l\",\"Draw line PQ\",\"Take point P\",\"Draw a perpendicular\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the textbook, the first step is to draw line l.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many points are taken on the line l to begin the construction process?\",\"options\":[\"One point\",\"Two points\",\"Three points\",\"Four points\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Step 2 states to take two points A and B on the line l.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the construction method, what is drawn from points A and B to the line l?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel lines\",\"Intersecting lines\",\"Perpendiculars\",\"Transversals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 3 specifies drawing perpendiculars to the line l from points A and B.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To draw a line parallel to line l at a distance of 2.5 cm, where should points P and Q be marked?\",\"options\":[\"On line l\",\"On the perpendicular lines\",\"At any random position\",\"On the transversal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Step 4 states to take points P and Q on the perpendicular lines.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the required distance for the parallel line is 2.5 cm, what is the length of segments AP and BQ?\",\"options\":[\"5.0 cm\",\"1.25 cm\",\"2.5 cm\",\"0 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 4 specifies taking points P and Q at a distance of 2.5 cm from A and B respectively.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the small square symbols at points A and B represent?\",\"options\":[\"The lines are parallel\",\"The lines are perpendicular to line l\",\"The points are midpoints\",\"The distance is 2.5 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In geometry diagrams, the square symbol at an intersection indicates a right angle or perpendicularity.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/023_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which line is constructed to be parallel to line l?\",\"options\":[\"Line AP\",\"Line BQ\",\"Line PQ\",\"Line AB\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 6 confirms that line PQ is the line parallel to line l.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/023_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to draw a line parallel to line l at a distance of 4 cm, what should be the distance of point P from point A?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 cm\",\"4 cm\",\"8 cm\",\"2 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The distance of the point on the perpendicular must match the required distance of the parallel line.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to line PQ if point P was marked at 2.5 cm and point Q was marked at 3 cm from line l?\",\"options\":[\"Line PQ would still be parallel to line l\",\"Line PQ would not be parallel to line l\",\"Line PQ would be perpendicular to line l\",\"Line PQ would be the same as line l\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Parallel lines must maintain a constant distance; unequal distances would result in non-parallel lines.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which geometric concept is primarily used in this construction to ensure the lines are parallel at a specific distance?\",\"options\":[\"Drawing circles\",\"Measuring angles with a protractor\",\"Drawing perpendiculars of equal length\",\"Bisecting segments\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The method relies on drawing two perpendiculars and marking equal distances on them to define the parallel line.\",\"topic\":\"Construction (II)\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22115,55,8,'Maths','en','Indices and Cube root','[\"Indices are numbers written as a base raised to an exponent.\",\"The base is the number being multiplied; the exponent (index) tells how many times to multiply the base by itself.\",\"Example: 2³ = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 – base 2, exponent 3.\",\"Laws of indices: 1) a^m × a^n = a^(m+n) 2) (a^m)(b^m) = (ab)^m 3) (a^m)^n = a^(mn) 4) a^0 = 1 (if a≠0) 5) a^(-n) = 1\\/(a^n)\",\"If the exponent is a fraction p\\/q, a^(p\\/q) = (q√a)^p = (p√a)^q.\",\"The square of a number is written as a²; the square root is a^(1\\/2) or √a.\",\"The cube of a number is a³; the cube root is a^(1\\/3) or ∛a.\",\"A number can have two square roots: one positive, one negative (e.g., √16 = ±4).\",\"The cube root of a negative number is negative (∛(-8) = -2).\",\"The cube of a negative number is negative ( (-2)³ = -8).\",\"To find a cube root by factorisation, group prime factors in threes.\",\"The nth root of a^m can be written as a^(m\\/n).\",\"If m and n are integers, the laws of indices work the same as for positive exponents.\",\"When the exponent is rational, the number can have multiple meanings (e.g., 8^(2\\/3) can mean (∛8)² or (8²)^(1\\/3)).\",\"Practice converting between radical form and index form to strengthen understanding.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In the index form 2⁵, what is the number 2 called?\",\"options\":[\"Index\",\"Base\",\"Product\",\"Power\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in 2⁵, 2 is the base.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression 2⁵, what is the number 5 called?\",\"options\":[\"Base\",\"Multiple\",\"Index\",\"Factor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in 2⁵, 5 is the index.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the product 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 expressed in index form?\",\"options\":[\"5²\",\"2 × 5\",\"2⁵\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that the product of five 2s is expressed as 2⁵.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the laws of indices, what is the value of a⁰?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"a\",\"1\",\"None of these\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Law (iv) states that a⁰ = 1.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct law for (a × b)ᵐ?\",\"options\":[\"aᵐ + bᵐ\",\"aᵐ × bᵐ\",\"a × bᵐ\",\"(a+b)ᵐ\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Law (vi) states that (a × b)ᵐ = aᵐ × bᵐ.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which expression is equivalent to (a\\/b)⁻ᵐ according to the laws of indices?\",\"options\":[\"(a\\/b)ᵐ\",\"(b\\/a)⁻ᵐ\",\"(b\\/a)ᵐ\",\"1\\/(a\\/b)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Law (viii) states that (a\\/b)⁻ᵐ = (b\\/a)ᵐ.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the law aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, what is the result of 3⁴ × 3²?\",\"options\":[\"3⁸\",\"3⁶\",\"9⁶\",\"3²\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By adding the indices (4+2), we get 3⁶.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a⁻ᵐ = 1\\/aᵐ, then 5⁻³ is equal to:\",\"options\":[\"-5³\",\"1\\/5³\",\"1\\/3⁵\",\"-15\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Applying law (v), a negative index becomes a positive index in the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐ ˣ ⁿ, then (2³)⁴ is equal to which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"2⁷\",\"2¹²\",\"2³⁴\",\"6⁴\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By multiplying the indices (3 × 4), we get 2¹².\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the law aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, what is the result if m = n?\",\"options\":[\"a¹\",\"a⁰\",\"a²ᵐ\",\"a⁻¹\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If m = n, then m - n = 0, resulting in a⁰.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of indices\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"How is the cube root of 64 written in index form as per the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"64^3\",\"(64)^{1\\/3}\",\"64^2\",\"(64)^{1\\/2}\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the cube root of 64 can be written as (64)^{1\\/3}.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the laws of indices provided, what is the value of 5^0?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"5\",\"1\",\"-5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on the law a^0 = 1, the value of 5^0 is 1.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the law of indices for multiplication, what is the result of 3^5 × 3^2?\",\"options\":[\"3^{10}\",\"3^3\",\"3^7\",\"3^{2.5}\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"When multiplying indices with the same base, the powers are added: 5 + 2 = 7.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following symbols is used to represent the cube root of 64?\",\"options\":[\"√64\",\"∛64\",\"²√64\",\"4√64\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly shows the symbol ∛64 for the cube root of 64.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the division 3^7 ÷ 3^9, what is the power of the resulting number 3?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"-2\",\"16\",\"63\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the law a^m ÷ a^n = a^{m-n}, we get 7 - 9 = -2.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook examples, the expression (6\\/7)^3 is equivalent to which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"6^3 \\/ 7\",\"6 \\/ 7^3\",\"6^3 \\/ 7^3\",\"7^3 \\/ 6^3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The law (a\\/b)^m = a^m \\/ b^m implies that the power 3 applies to both the numerator and the denominator.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If (6\\/7)^{-3} = (7\\/6)^x, what is the value of x based on the laws of indices shown?\",\"options\":[\"-3\",\"1\",\"0\",\"3\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The law (a\\/b)^{-m} = (b\\/a)^m shows that the negative index becomes positive when the fraction is inverted.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The text shows that 4^3 = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64. What does this imply about the number 4?\",\"options\":[\"4 is the square of 64\",\"4 is the cube root of 64\",\"4 is the index of 64\",\"4 is the product of 64\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that since 4^3 = 64, the cube root of 64 is 4.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the expression (3^4)^5. What is the correct single index form for this expression?\",\"options\":[\"3^9\",\"3^{20}\",\"3^1\",\"3^{45}\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the law (a^m)^n = a^{mn}, we multiply the indices: 4 × 5 = 20.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How is 5^{-3} expressed as a fraction with a positive index in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/5^1\",\"1\\/5^{-3}\",\"1\\/5^3\",\"1\\/5^0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the law a^{-m} = 1\\/a^m, so 5^{-3} = 1\\/5^3.\",\"topic\":\"Meaning of cube root\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the nth root of \'a\' expressed in general index form?\",\"options\":[\"a^n\",\"a^(1\\/n)\",\"n^a\",\"1\\/a^n\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in general, the nth root of a is expressed as a^(1\\/n).\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the value of the 5th power of 3?\",\"options\":[\"15\",\"81\",\"243\",\"729\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 3^5 = 243.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the 5th power of 3 is 243, then what is the 5th root of 243?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"3\",\"243\",\"1\\/5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that conversely, the 5th root of 243 is 3.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the radical sign representation for the 5th root of 243?\",\"options\":[\"√243\",\"5√243\",\"³√243\",\"⁵√243\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text shows that the 5th root of 243 is expressed as (243)^(1\\/5) or ⁵√243.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How would you express the \'7th root of 128\' using index notation?\",\"options\":[\"128^7\",\"7^(1\\/128)\",\"128^(1\\/7)\",\"1\\/128^7\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Following the example in the text, the 7th root of 128 is 128^(1\\/7).\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the expression 900^(1\\/12) represent?\",\"options\":[\"12th power of 900\",\"900th root of 12\",\"12th root of 900\",\"Product of 900 and 1\\/12\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the example 900^(1\\/12) = 12th root of 900.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If (243)^(1\\/5) = 3, which mathematical statement correctly represents the inverse power relationship?\",\"options\":[\"3 × 5 = 243\",\"3^5 = 243\",\"243 × 1\\/5 = 3\",\"5^3 = 243\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the 5th power (3^5 = 243) and the 5th root (243^(1\\/5) = 3) as converse operations.\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the general expression a^(1\\/n), what does the number \'n\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The base\",\"The power\",\"The root\",\"The product\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the context of nth root, n represents the specific root being taken (e.g., 5th root, 7th root).\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the relationship shown in the text, if x² = 10, how can x be written as a root of 10?\",\"options\":[\"10^2\",\"10^(1\\/2)\",\"2^10\",\"10\\/2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that if x² = 10, then x is the square root (2nd root) of 10, written as 10^(1\\/2).\",\"topic\":\"Examples of nth root\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the 7th root of