"Estimation method\",\"Subtraction method\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that we can find the cube root using the factorisation method, similar to finding a square root.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the prime factorisation of 216 as given in the example?\",\"options\":[\"2 × 2 × 3 × 3\",\"2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3\",\"2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 3\",\"2 × 3 × 6 × 6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly shows $216 = 2 \\\\times 2 \\\\times 2 \\\\times 3 \\\\times 3 \\\\times 3$.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many times does each prime factor (2 and 3) appear in the factorisation of 216?\",\"options\":[\"Twice\",\"Thrice\",\"Four times\",\"Six times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states, \'Each of the factors 3 and 2, appears thrice.\'\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The factorisation method for finding a cube root is the same as the method used for finding which other value?\",\"options\":[\"Square root\",\"Cube\",\"Square\",\"Index\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions, \'Using the same method [as square root], we can find the cube root.\'\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are the factors of 216 grouped in the solution to find the cube root?\",\"options\":[\"(2 × 2) × (3 × 3)\",\"(3 × 2) × (3 × 2) × (3 × 2)\",\"(2 × 2 × 2) + (3 × 3 × 3)\",\"(6 × 6) × 6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the grouping as $216 = (3 \\\\times 2) \\\\times (3 \\\\times 2) \\\\times (3 \\\\times 2)$.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct mathematical notation for the cube root of 216 as shown in the text?\",\"options\":[\"$√216$\",\"$(216)^2$\",\"$(216)^{\\\\frac{1}{3}}$\",\"$(216)^3$\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the notation $(216)^{\\\\frac{1}{3}} = 6$.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If $216 = (3 × 2)^3$, what is the value of the cube root of 216?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"2\",\"5\",\"6\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Since $(3 \\\\times 2) = 6$, the cube root is 6.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the example, if a number is expressed as $x^3$, then its cube root is:\",\"options\":[\"$x$\",\"$3x$\",\"$x\\/3$\",\"$x^2$\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows $216 = 6^3$, therefore $\\\\sqrt[3]{216} = 6$.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression $\\\\sqrt[3]{216} = 6$, what does the symbol $\\\\sqrt[3]{\\\\quad}$ represent?\",\"options\":[\"Square root\",\"Cube root\",\"Cube\",\"Factorisation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses this symbol to denote the cube root of 216.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the grouping $(3 × 2) × (3 × 2) × (3 × 2)$ useful in finding the cube root?\",\"options\":[\"It shows that 216 is a multiple of 5\",\"It helps identify the square root\",\"It expresses the number as a product of three identical factors\",\"It reduces the number of prime factors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Grouping into three identical sets $(3 \\\\times 2)$ allows the number to be written as $6^3$, identifying 6 as the cube root.\",\"topic\":\"Worked Example: Cube Root\",\"part\":20,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the cube root of 1728 as shown in Ex. (3)?\",\"options\":[\"11\",\"12\",\"-11\",\"-12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the solution in Ex. (3), the cube root of 1728 is 12.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the cube root of -1331?\",\"options\":[\"11\",\"-11\",\"12\",\"-12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ex. (2) shows that the cube root of -1331 is -11.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Ex. (3), which factors are used to represent 1728 in the first step?\",\"options\":[\"11 × 11 × 11\",\"8 × 216\",\"12 × 144\",\"2 × 864\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The solution for Ex. (3) starts by factorising 1728 as 8 × 216.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the note in Ex. (3), what is the cube root of -1728?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"-12\",\"6\",\"-6\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The note explicitly states that the cube root of -1728 is -12.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which index law is used to simplify 2³ × 6³ into (2 × 6)³?\",\"options\":[\"aⁿ × aᵐ = aⁿ⁺ᵐ\",\"aⁿ \\/ aᵐ = aⁿ⁻ᵐ\",\"aⁿ × bⁿ = (a × b)ⁿ\",\"(aⁿ)ᵐ = aⁿᵐ\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the formula aⁿ × bⁿ = (a × b)ⁿ as the reason for the step.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is -1331 expressed as a product of three identical negative factors in Ex. (2)?\",\"options\":[\"(-11) × (-11) × (-11)\",\"-(11 × 11 × 11)\",\"(-11) × 11 × 11\",\"(-11) × (-11) × 11\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ex. (2) shows -1331 = (-11) × (-11) × (-11).\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 216 = 6 × 6 × 6, how is it represented in index form in Ex. (3)?\",\"options\":[\"6²\",\"6³\",\"6⁴\",\"2³\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows 6 × 6 × 6 is written as 6³.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Using the method in Ex. (3), if 1728 = 2³ × 6³, what is the final step to find ³√1728?\",\"options\":[\"2 + 6\",\"2 × 6\",\"2³ + 6³\",\"2⁶\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows ³√1728 = 2 × 6 = 12.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing Ex. (2) and Ex. (3), what is the relationship between the cube root of a positive number and its negative counterpart?\",\"options\":[\"They are the same.\",\"The cube root of the negative number is the negative of the cube root of the positive number.\",\"The cube root of a negative number is always positive.\",\"There is no relationship.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows ³√1331 = 11 and ³√-1331 = -11, and similarly for 1728.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the expression 1728 = 2³ × 6³, why can we write this as (2 × 6)³?\",\"options\":[\"Because the bases are the same.\",\"Because the indices (powers) are the same.\",\"Because 1728 is an even number.\",\"Because 2 and 6 are factors of 12.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The formula aⁿ × bⁿ = (a × b)ⁿ applies when the indices (n) are the same.\",\"topic\":\"Solution of Ex. (3)\",\"part\":24,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the cube root of 729 as mentioned in Practice Set 3.3?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"8\",\"9\",\"11\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to Q1(2) and Q3 of the text, the cube root of 729 is 9.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the cube root of 343?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"7\",\"9\",\"13\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In Q1(3), the student is asked to find the cube root of 343, which is 7.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the cube root of the negative number -512?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"-8\",\"12\",\"-12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Q1(4) asks for the cube root of -512, which is -8.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the solution for ³√0.125, how is the decimal 0.125 first expressed as a fraction?\",\"options\":[\"125\\/10\",\"125\\/100\",\"125\\/1000\",\"125\\/10000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the first step of the solution as ³√0.125 = ³√125\\/1000.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which rule of indices is cited in the solution for finding the cube root of 0.125?\",\"options\":[\"(a × b)ⁿ = aⁿ × bⁿ\",\"(a\\/b)ⁿ = aⁿ\\/bⁿ\",\"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ\",\"(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions (aⁿ\\/bⁿ = a\\/b)ⁿ as the rule used in the example.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Simplify the expression ³√27\\/125 as given in Practice Set 3.3 Q.2.\",\"options\":[\"9\\/25\",\"3\\/25\",\"3\\/5\",\"9\\/5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since ³√27 = 3 and ³√125 = 5, the simplified form is 3\\/5.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Find the cube root of 8000.\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"20\",\"200\",\"80\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The cube root of 8 is 2, and the cube root of 1000 is 10, so ³√8000 = 20.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the cube root of -2744?\",\"options\":[\"-12\",\"-14\",\"-16\",\"-18\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"By calculation based on Q1(5), the cube root of -2744 is -14.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To simplify ³√16\\/54, what is the most logical first step based on the numbers provided?\",\"options\":[\"Find the cube root of 16 directly\",\"Find the cube root of 54 directly\",\"Divide both numbers by 2 to get 8\\/27\",\"Multiply both numbers by 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"16 and 54 are not perfect cubes, but dividing them by 2 gives 8 and 27, which are perfect cubes.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If ³√729 = 9, then what is the value of ³√0.000729?\",\"options\":[\"0.9\",\"0.09\",\"0.009\",\"0.0009\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"³√0.000729 = ³√(729\\/1000000) = 9\\/100 = 0.09.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 3.3\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22116,55,8,'Maths','en','Altitudes and Medians of a triangle','[\"An altitude is a perpendicular line from a vertex to the opposite side.\",\"The three altitudes of any triangle meet at one point called the orthocentre (O).\",\"In a right‑angled triangle the orthocentre is the right‑angle vertex.\",\"In an obtuse triangle the orthocentre lies outside the triangle.\",\"In an acute triangle the orthocentre lies inside the triangle.\",\"A median joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.\",\"The three medians of a triangle are concurrent at a point called the centroid (G).\",\"The centroid always lies inside the triangle, no matter the type.\",\"The centroid divides each median in a 2:1 ratio (vertex to centroid : centroid to midpoint).\",\"The circumcentre is the centre of the circumscribed circle; the incentre is the centre of the incircle.\",\"The circumcentre, centroid and orthocentre are collinear for an isosceles triangle.\",\"For an equilateral triangle all four centres coincide.\",\"To find a midpoint, mark equal segments on the side and join the marks to the opposite vertex.\",\"To draw an altitude, use a set‑square to make a right angle with the base.\",\"To draw a median, first find the midpoint of the base, then draw a line to the opposite vertex.\",\"The orthocentre of a right triangle is the vertex of the right angle.\",\"The centroid divides each median into segments of ratio 2:1.\",\"The orthocentre of an obtuse triangle lies outside the triangle.\",\"The orthocentre of an acute triangle lies inside the triangle.\",\"The centroid of any triangle is always inside the triangle.\",\"The circumcentre of a right triangle is the midpoint of the hypotenuse.\",\"The incentre of a triangle is where the angle bisectors meet.\",\"The altitudes, medians, angle bisectors, perpendicular bisectors and circumcircle all have special points of concurrency.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the point of concurrence of the angle bisectors of a triangle called?\",\"options\":[\"Circumcentre\",\"Incentre\",\"Orthocentre\",\"Centroid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the point of concurrence of angle bisectors is called the incentre.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The point of concurrence of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides of a triangle is called the ________.\",\"options\":[\"Incentre\",\"Midpoint\",\"Circumcentre\",\"Vertex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the point of concurrence of perpendicular bisectors of sides is called the circumcentre.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what property do both the angle bisectors and the perpendicular bisectors of a triangle share?\",\"options\":[\"They are parallel\",\"They are concurrent\",\"They are unequal\",\"They are outside the triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that both angle bisectors and perpendicular bisectors of sides are concurrent.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the activity described, where should the point be taken relative to the line?\",\"options\":[\"On the line\",\"At the end of the line\",\"Outside the line\",\"Under the line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions specifically say to \'Take a point outside the line\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific geometric tool is mentioned in the activity to draw a perpendicular?\",\"options\":[\"Protractor\",\"Compass\",\"Set-square\",\"Divider\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity states to draw a perpendicular \'with the help of a set-square\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'concurrent\' mean in the context of the bisectors mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"They never meet\",\"They meet at a single point\",\"They are the same length\",\"They are perpendicular to each other\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Concurrence refers to the state where lines meet at a single point, known as the point of concurrence.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are following the activity, what is the first step you must take?\",\"options\":[\"Draw a set-square\",\"Draw a line\",\"Find the incentre\",\"Draw a circumcentre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity begins with the instruction: \'Draw a line\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To draw a perpendicular from a point outside a line using a set-square, how many lines are initially drawn before the perpendicular?\",\"options\":[\"Zero\",\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity starts by drawing one line and then taking a point outside it.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is required to identify the \'incentre\' of a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"Perpendicular bisectors of sides\",\"Bisectors of angles\",\"A set-square only\",\"A point outside the line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the incentre as the point of concurrence of the bisectors of angles of a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, if you have a line and a point outside it, what is the resulting geometric relationship created using the set-square?\",\"options\":[\"Parallelism\",\"Concurrence\",\"Perpendicularity\",\"Bisector\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity\'s goal is to \'Draw a perpendicular from the point to the line\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity: Draw a perpendicular\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the perpendicular segment drawn from a vertex of a triangle to its opposite side called?\",\"options\":[\"Median\",\"Altitude\",\"Hypotenuse\",\"Base\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines an altitude as the perpendicular segment drawn from a vertex to the opposite side.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In ∆ ABC, if seg AP is an altitude, which side is considered the base?\",\"options\":[\"Side AB\",\"Side AC\",\"Side BC\",\"Side AP\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, seg AP is an altitude on the base BC.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical tool is mentioned in the text to draw a perpendicular from a vertex to a side?\",\"options\":[\"Protractor\",\"Compass\",\"Set-square\",\"Scale\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states to draw a perpendicular from a vertex using a set-square.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the point of concurrence of the three altitudes of a triangle called?\",\"options\":[\"Centroid\",\"Orthocentre\",\"Incentre\",\"Circumcentre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the point of concurrence of altitudes is called the orthocentre.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which letter is used to denote the orthocentre of a triangle in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"C\",\"H\",\"P\",\"O\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the orthocentre is denoted by the letter \'O\'.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If seg YQ is an altitude on side XZ in ∆ XYZ, which of the following is true?\",\"options\":[\"seg YQ || seg XZ\",\"seg YQ ⊥ seg XZ\",\"seg YQ = seg XZ\",\"seg YQ + seg XZ = 180\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states \'seg YQ ⊥ seg XZ\' for the altitude on side XZ.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In ∆ XYZ, if seg ZP is the altitude, which side must be the base?\",\"options\":[\"Side XY\",\"Side YZ\",\"Side XZ\",\"Side ZP\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions considering side XY as a base to draw altitude ZP.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many altitudes can be drawn in a single triangle?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Infinite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes drawing altitudes from three different vertices (X, Y, and Z) for ∆ XYZ.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student draws a perpendicular from vertex X to side YZ and names the meeting point R, what is seg XR?\",\"options\":[\"The base\",\"The orthocentre\",\"The altitude\",\"The vertex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that seg XR is an altitude on side YZ.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'concurrent\' imply regarding the altitudes of a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"They are all of equal length\",\"They never meet each other\",\"They all pass through the same single point\",\"They are parallel to each other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Concurrency refers to the altitudes meeting at a single point called the orthocentre.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the construction steps for ∆ XYZ, if we change the base to side XZ, which vertex must the altitude be drawn from?\",\"options\":[\"Vertex X\",\"Vertex Y\",\"Vertex Z\",\"Point R\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Considering side XZ as a base, draw an altitude YQ\', which comes from vertex Y.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the relationship between the altitudes XR, YQ, and ZP in ∆ XYZ?\",\"options\":[\"They are sides of the triangle\",\"They intersect at point O\",\"They are perpendicular to each other\",\"They are drawn from the same vertex\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that the three altitudes are concurrent at the orthocentre \'O\'.\",\"topic\":\"Altitude\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the point of concurrence of the altitudes of a triangle called?\",\"options\":[\"Centroid\",\"Incentre\",\"Orthocentre\",\"Circumcentre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The topic title and activities describe the point of concurrence of altitudes as the orthocentre.\",\"topic\":\"Location of the orthocentre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Activity I, where is the orthocentre of a right-angled triangle located?\",\"options\":[\"In the interior of the triangle\",\"In the exterior of the triangle\",\"At the vertex of the right angle\",\"At the midpoint of the hypotenuse\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activity I states that in a right-angled triangle, the point of concurrence is the vertex of the right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Location of the orthocentre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Activity III, where does the orthocentre of an acute-angled triangle lie?\",\"options\":[\"In the interior of the triangle\",\"In the exterior of the triangle\",\"On one of the sides\",\"At one of the vertices\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Activity III guides the observation that the orthocentre of an acute-angled triangle is in its interior.\",\"topic\":\"Location of the orthocentre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity II, what must be drawn to observe the concurrence of altitudes in an obtuse-angled triangle?\",\"options\":[\"The medians of the triangle\",\"The lines containing the altitudes\",\"The perpendicular bisectors\",\"The angle bisectors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity II specifies drawing the lines containing the altitudes to see that they are concurrent.\",\"topic\":\"Location of the orthocentre\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a triangle has one angle measuring 120°, where will its orthocentre be located?\",\"options\":[\"In the interior of the triangle\",\"In the exterior of the triangle\",\"At the vertex of the 120° angle\",\"On the side opposite to the 120° angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A triangle with a 120° angle i