that the specific configuration (like the angle being included) matters.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is given that side AB = 4.5 cm, side BC = 3 cm, and ∠ A = 41° for two different triangles, can they definitely state the triangles are congruent?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, because of the SAS test\",\"Yes, because of the SSA test\",\"No, because the given angle is not included between the given sides\",\"No, because the side lengths are too small\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the 41° angle (∠ A) is not between the sides AB and BC, congruence is not guaranteed.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which test is used in the example to prove that △ ABD ≅ △ CBD?\",\"options\":[\"S-A-S test\",\"S-S-S test\",\"A-S-A test\",\"Hypotenuse-side test\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The solution explicitly states that the triangles are congruent by the S-S-S test because three pairs of sides are congruent.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which side is identified as the \'common\' side in the given solution?\",\"options\":[\"side AB\",\"side BC\",\"side BD\",\"side AD\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The solution states \'side BD is common\' for both △ ABD and △ CBD.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given information, side AB is congruent to which side?\",\"options\":[\"side AD\",\"side CB\",\"side CD\",\"side BD\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'side AB ≅ side CB ..... (given)\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are ∠ BAD and ∠ BCD congruent in the solution?\",\"options\":[\"They are given as congruent in the question\",\"They are common angles in both triangles\",\"They are corresponding angles of congruent triangles\",\"They are vertically opposite angles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists these angles under the reason \'corresponding angles of congruent triangles\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If side DA ≅ side DC, which pair of angles are corresponding and congruent based on the S-S-S test result?\",\"options\":[\"∠ ABD ≅ ∠ CBD\",\"∠ ADB ≅ ∠ CDB\",\"∠ BAD ≅ ∠ BCD\",\"All of these are correct\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists ∠ ADB ≅ ∠ CDB as one of the pairs of corresponding angles of the congruent triangles.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the solution, side DA is given as congruent to which side?\",\"options\":[\"side AB\",\"side DC\",\"side CB\",\"side BD\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'side DA ≅ side DC ..... (given)\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement is true about the triangles in the figure?\",\"options\":[\"They are congruent because all angles are equal\",\"They are congruent by the S-S-S test\",\"They are not congruent because only sides are given\",\"They are congruent by the A-A-A test\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The solution concludes \'∴ △ ABD ≅ △ CBD ..... (S-S-S test)\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the notation \'△ ABD ≅ △ CBD\', what does the symbol \'≅\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Similarity\",\"Parallelism\",\"Congruence\",\"Equality of area\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the context of the chapter \'Congruence of triangles\', the symbol ≅ denotes congruence.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many pairs of congruent angles are listed as a result of the triangles being congruent?\",\"options\":[\"One pair\",\"Two pairs\",\"Three pairs\",\"Four pairs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists three pairs: ∠ ABD ≅ ∠ CBD, ∠ ADB ≅ ∠ CDB, and ∠ BAD ≅ ∠ BCD.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which vertex of △ ABD corresponds to vertex C of △ CBD to maintain congruence?\",\"options\":[\"Vertex A\",\"Vertex B\",\"Vertex D\",\"Vertex S\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Based on the congruence △ ABD ≅ △ CBD, vertex A corresponds to vertex C.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22127,55,8,'Maths','en','Compound interest','[\"Compound interest is the interest calculated on the principal plus any interest that has already been added.\",\"The amount after each year becomes the new principal for the next year.\",\"Formula for amount after N years: A = P × (1 + R\\/100)^N where R is annual rate in %.\",\"Compound interest (CI) = A – P.\",\"If interest is compounded more frequently, divide the rate by the number of periods and multiply the number of periods by the number of years.\",\"For semi‑annual compounding use R\\/2 and 2N periods; for monthly use R\\/12 and 12N periods; for daily use R\\/365 and 365N periods.\",\"Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal: I = P × R × N \\/ 100.\",\"The difference between simple and compound interest grows with higher rates and longer durations.\",\"Depreciation or population growth uses the same formula with a negative or positive rate respectively.\",\"Always round to two decimal places when dealing with money or population counts.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the additional money paid by a person after a stipulated time when repaying a loan called?\",\"options\":[\"Principal\",\"Rate\",\"Interest\",\"Installment\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the additional money paid is called interest.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula I = PNR \\/ 100, what does the letter \'P\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Period\",\"Percent\",\"Payment\",\"Principal\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in the formula I = PNR \\/ 100, P stands for Principal.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following are mentioned as institutes from which a person can take a loan?\",\"options\":[\"Bank or patapedhi\",\"Post office or school\",\"Market or factory\",\"Insurance or treasury\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a person takes a loan from institutes like bank or patapedhi.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'N\' represent in the simple interest formula mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Number of months\",\"Number of years\",\"Net amount\",\"New rate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines N as the Number of years.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the rate of interest (R) expressed in the formula provided?\",\"options\":[\"per cent per month\",\"per cent per day\",\"at p.c.p.a.\",\"at total value\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states R is the Rate of interest at p.c.p.a.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to calculate simple interest, which formula should they use based on the \'Let\'s recall\' section?\",\"options\":[\"I = P + N + R\",\"I = PNR \\/ 100\",\"I = (P + N) \\/ R\",\"I = P \\/ (N * R)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula I = PNR \\/ 100 for finding the interest.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the formula I = PNR \\/ 100, if the Principal (P) increases while N and R remain constant, what happens to the Interest (I)?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It increases\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since P is in the numerator, an increase in P results in an increase in the calculated Interest (I).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what must be fixed when taking a loan from a bank or patapedhi?\",\"options\":[\"The number of people\",\"The rate of interest\",\"The location of the bank\",\"The type of currency\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states a person takes a loan with a \'fixed rate of interest\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person borrows ₹5000 (Principal) for 2 years (N) at a rate of 10 p.c.p.a. (R), which values would be substituted into the formula I = PNR \\/ 100?\",\"options\":[\"P=5000, N=2, R=10\",\"P=10, N=5000, R=2\",\"P=2, N=10, R=5000\",\"P=5000, N=10, R=2\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Following the definitions: P is the amount (5000), N is years (2), and R is the rate (10).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When a person repays a loan, the total money returned to the bank is:\",\"options\":[\"Less than the loan amount\",\"Only the additional money\",\"The loan amount plus the interest\",\"Only the principal amount\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says he repays the loan with \'some more money\' (interest), meaning the total is loan + interest.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula I = PNR \\/ 100, what does the letter \'P\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Period\",\"Principal\",\"Percent\",\"Payment\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, P stands for Principal, which is the initial loan amount (e.g., ₹10,000).\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula used in the text to calculate the interest (I) for one year?\",\"options\":[\"I = P + N + R\",\"I = PNR \\/ 10\",\"I = PNR \\/ 100\",\"I = P \\/ (N × R)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The student in the text uses the formula I = PNR \\/ 100 to calculate the interest.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for interest calculated on the amount formed by the principal and the interest of the previous year?\",\"options\":[\"Simple interest\",\"Fixed interest\",\"Compound interest\",\"Bank interest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines compound interest as the method where interest is charged on the amount formed by the principal and its interest.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the teacher, how often does the bank calculate the interest?\",\"options\":[\"After each month\",\"After each year\",\"After every two years\",\"Only at the end of the loan term\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The teacher states that the bank calculates the interest after each year.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a borrower fails to pay the interest for the first year, what does the bank consider as the loan for the second year?\",\"options\":[\"Only the original principal\",\"Only the interest of the first year\",\"The principal and the interest of the first year together\",\"Double the original principal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the bank considers the principal and the interest of the first year together as the loan for the second year.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the example given, if Sajjanrao takes a loan of ₹10,000 at 10 p.c.p.a. for 1 year, what is the interest (I)?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 100\",\"₹ 500\",\"₹ 1,000\",\"₹ 11,000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using I = 10000 × 10 × 1 \\/ 100, the interest is calculated as ₹ 1,000.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total amount Sajjanrao has to pay after one year including interest for his ₹10,000 loan?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 10,000\",\"₹ 11,000\",\"₹ 12,000\",\"₹ 9,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The total amount is the principal plus interest: 10,000 + 1,000 = ₹ 11,000.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Under the compound interest method, if the first year\'s principal is ₹10,000 and the interest is ₹1,000, what is the principal for the second year?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 10,000\",\"₹ 1,000\",\"₹ 11,000\",\"₹ 20,000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that for the second year, the principal is the amount of the first year (Principal + Interest).\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the \'amount\' of the first year in the context of this text?\",\"options\":[\"Principal minus Interest\",\"Principal of the first year together with its interest\",\"The rate of interest multiplied by time\",\"The total loan period\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the amount as the principal of the first year together with its interest.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why would the interest for the second year be higher than the interest for the first year in compound interest?\",\"options\":[\"Because the rate of interest (R) increases\",\"Because the time (N) increases to 2 years\",\"Because the interest is calculated on a larger principal (Amount of 1st year)\",\"Because the bank charges a penalty fee\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Interest is calculated on the \'amount\' of the first year, which is larger than the original principal.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a borrower pays the interest at the end of every year, does the principal for the next year change according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, it always increases\",\"No, it only changes if the borrower fails to pay the interest\",\"Yes, it decreases every year\",\"No, the bank never changes the principal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the principal changes for the second year \'If the borrower fails to pay the interest for one year\'.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the teacher\'s explanation, what is the fundamental difference between the first year and the second year in compound interest?\",\"options\":[\"The interest rate changes in the second year\",\"The formula for interest changes in the second year\",\"The principal for the second year includes the first year\'s interest\",\"The bank stops charging interest in the second year\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that for the second year, the principal is the amount (principal + interest) of the first year.\",\"topic\":\"Compound Interest\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"While finding compound interest every year, what is considered as the principal for the next year?\",\"options\":[\"The original principal\",\"The interest of the previous year\",\"The amount (A) of the previous year\",\"The average of principal and interest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the amount (A) of the previous year is the principal for the next year.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula A = P(1 + R\\/100)^N, what does the letter \'N\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Number of installments\",\"Duration in years\",\"Net interest rate\",\"New principal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that N represents the duration in years.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the teacher, which ratio remains constant for every year in compound interest?\",\"options\":[\"Interest \\/ Principal\",\"Amount \\/ Interest\",\"Principal \\/ Amount\",\"Amount \\/ Principal\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The teacher states, \'For every year the ratio Amount \\/ Principal is constant.\'\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the ratio Amount \\/ Principal is assumed to be \'M\', what is the formula for the amount after two years?\",\"options\":[\"P × M\",\"P × M^2\",\"P × 2M\",\"P + M^2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if the ratio is M, the amount after the second year is P × M^2.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the original principal P is ₹ 1000 and the ratio Amount \\/ Principal is 110 \\/ 100, what is the amount after the first year (A1)?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 1010\",\"₹ 1110\",\"₹ 1100\",\"₹ 1210\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A1 = P × 110\\/100. So, 1000 × 110\\/100 = 1100.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the derivation in the text, how is the amount of the second year (A2) related to the amount of the first year (A1)?\",\"options\":[\"A2 = A1 + (110 \\/ 100)\",\"A2 = A1 × (110 \\/ 100)\",\"A2 = A1 × P\",\"A2 = A1 \\/ (110 \\/ 100)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows A2 \\/ A1 = 110 \\/ 100, therefore A2 = A1 × 110 \\/ 100.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for the amount of ₹ 1 after 1 year if the interest rate is R p.c.p.a.?\",\"options\":[\"1 × (R \\/ 100)\",\"1 + R\",\"1 × (1 + R\\/100)\",\"1 × (100 \\/ R)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: the amount of ₹ 1 after 1 year = 1 × (1 + R\\/100).\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the teacher suggest it is convenient to find the amount (A) rather than the interest each year?\",\"options\":[\"Because interest is always zero\",\"Because the amount of the previous year becomes the principal for the next year\",\"Because the principal remains the same every year\",\"Because the rate of interest changes every year\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says it is convenient because the amount of the previous year is the principal for the next year.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the interest rate R is 10 p.c.p.a., what is the value of the term (1 + R\\/100) in the compound interest formula?\",\"options\":[\"1.01\",\"1.1\",\"11\",\"101\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"1 + 10\\/100 = 1 + 0.1 = 1.1.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the amount after N years is A = P × M^N, what does this imply about the relationship between the amount and the number of years?\",\"options\":[\"The amount increases linearly with N\",\"The amount is multiplied by the ratio M for every additional year\",\"The amount decreases as N increases\",\"The principal P changes to M every year\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that to find the amount, you multiply the principal by the ratio M as many times as the number of years.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the rate of interest (R) used in the calculation?\",\"options\":[\"10 p.c.p.a.\",\"12.5 p.c.p.a.\",\"125 p.c.p.a.\",\"8 p.c.p.a.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows R = 12.5 in the formula P(1 + 12.5\\/100)^3.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/100_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the expression (1125\\/1000) is simplified to which ratio before being cubed?\",\"options\":[\"8 \\/ 9\",\"1 \\/ 8\",\"9 \\/ 8\",\"5 \\/ 4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the calculation step where (1125\\/1000)^3 becomes (9\\/8)^3.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/100_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the ratio Amount \\/ Principal is given as 110 \\/ 100, what is the rate of interest (R) per annum?\",\"options\":[\"110%\",\"1%\",\"10%\",\"0.1%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since (100+R)\\/100 = 110\\/100, 100+R = 110, so R = 10.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the calculation shown in the text and figure, how is Compound Interest (I) calculated?\",\"options\":[\"I = Principal - Amount\",\"I = Amount + Principal\",\"I = Amount - Principal\",\"I = Principal × Rate × Duration\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure explicitly states: Compound Interest, I = Amount - Principal.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the duration is N years and the ratio is M, how does the formula P × M^N represent the process described by the student?\",\"options\":[\"It shows that interest is added only at the end of N years\",\"It represents multiplying the principal by the ratio M, N times\",\"It shows that the principal is divided by M each year\",\"It represents adding the ratio M to the principal N times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The student states: \'multiply the principal by those many times by this ratio\', which is mathematically P × M^N.\",\"topic\":\"Formula for Compound Interest\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the provided example, what is the principal amount (P) invested?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 3000\",\"₹ 4000\",\"₹ 1250\",\"₹ 5000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states \'Here, P = ₹ 4000\' in the solution of Ex. (1).\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the interest is calculated at an interval of six months, how is the rate (R) adjusted?\",\"options\":[\"It is taken as R\\/4\",\"It is taken as 2R\",\"It is taken as R\\/2\",\"It remains as R\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if interest is calculated six monthly, the rate is to be taken as R\\/2.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how do banks calculate compound interest \'now a days\'?\",\"options\":[\"Yearly\",\"Monthly\",\"Six monthly\",\"Daily\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Point 3 explicitly states \'Now a days banks calculate compound interest daily.\'\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When interest is calculated monthly, what is the duration in terms of stages for N years?\",\"options\":[\"N stages\",\"2N stages\",\"12 × N stages\",\"6 × N stages\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 2 states that for monthly calculation, the duration is taken as 12 × N stages of months.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person invests money for 3 years and the interest is calculated six monthly, how many stages will be considered?\",\"options\":[\"3 stages\",\"6 stages\",\"12 stages\",\"36 stages\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"For six monthly calculation, stages = 2N. Since N = 3, stages = 2 × 3 = 6.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the monthly compound interest calculation, what rate is used if the annual rate is R?\",\"options\":[\"R\\/2\",\"R\\/6\",\"R\\/12\",\"R\\/24\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 2 states that for monthly interest, banks take the interest rate as R\\/12.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the annual interest rate (R) is 10% and the interest is calculated six monthly, what rate is applied per stage?\",\"options\":[\"10%\",\"20%\",\"5%\",\"2.5%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The rate per stage is R\\/2. So, 10% \\/ 2 = 5%.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the duration considered as 2N stages when interest is calculated six monthly?\",\"options\":[\"Because there are two six-month periods in one year\",\"Because the rate is doubled\",\"Because the principal is halved\",\"Because the bank charges extra for six months\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text implies the conversion of years (N) to six-month intervals, and there are two such intervals per year.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing six-monthly and monthly compounding for a period of N years, which statement is true regarding the number of stages?\",\"options\":[\"Monthly compounding has fewer stages than six-monthly\",\"Monthly compounding has 6 times as many stages as six-monthly\",\"Six-monthly compounding has 2N stages while monthly has 12N stages\",\"Both have the same number of stages\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 1 defines six-monthly as 2N stages and Point 2 defines monthly as 12N stages.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Ex. (1), if the interest were to be calculated six monthly instead of yearly, what would be the new rate per stage?\",\"options\":[\"12 1\\/2 %\",\"25 %\",\"6 1\\/4 %\",\"3 1\\/8 %\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The original rate R is 12 1\\/2%. For six monthly, the rate is R\\/2. (12.5 \\/ 2) = 6.25 or 6 1\\/4 %.\",\"topic\":\"More information\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the reduction in the value of an article after using it for some time called?\",\"options\":[\"Appreciation\",\"Depreciation\",\"Profit\",\"Simple Interest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the reduction in price of an article after use as \'depreciation\'.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for compound interest, what sign is used for the rate of depreciation (R)?\",\"options\":[\"Positive\",\"Negative\",\"Neutral\",\"Zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that since the value of the article decreases, the rate of depreciation R is taken as negative.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a practical application of the compound interest formula mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Calculating the speed of a vehicle\",\"Measuring the height of a building\",\"Finding the increase in population\",\"Determining the weight of a machine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions increase in population as an example of a problem in other fields where the formula is useful.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the rate of depreciation (R) taken as negative in the compound interest formula?\",\"options\":[\"Because the time period is decreasing\",\"Because the value of the article decreases\",\"Because the cost price is unknown\",\"Because the population is increasing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that R is taken as negative because the value of the article decreases over time.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a machine\'s value reduces by a definite percentage every year, which formula is used to find its reduced price?\",\"options\":[\"Area of a rectangle\",\"Simple interest formula\",\"Compound interest formula\",\"Volume of a cylinder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that for finding the reduced price over a period of time, the formula for compound interest is used.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the price of a machine every year according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It increases by a definite percentage\",\"It remains the same as the cost price\",\"It reduces by a definite percentage\",\"It fluctuates randomly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that every year the price of a machine reduces by a definite percentage.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When an article is sold after use, how does its value typically compare to its cost price?\",\"options\":[\"It is higher than the cost price\",\"It reduces as compared to its cost price\",\"It is exactly double the cost price\",\"It remains unchanged\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if an article is sold after using it, its value reduces as compared to its cost price.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"To calculate the reduced price of a vehicle using the compound interest formula, what must be known?\",\"options\":[\"The color of the vehicle\",\"The rate of depreciation\",\"The name of the manufacturer\",\"The distance the vehicle traveled\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that to find the reduced price, the rate of depreciation should be known.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of population growth versus machine depreciation, how does the application of the formula differ?\",\"options\":[\"R is positive for population growth and negative for depreciation\",\"R is negative for population growth and positive for depreciation\",\"R is zero for both cases\",\"The formula cannot be used for population growth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since population increases, R would be positive, whereas for depreciation (decrease in value), the text says R is negative.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these represents the correct logic for calculating the value of a machine after 2 years of use?\",\"options\":[\"Add the depreciation rate to the cost price\",\"Use the compound interest formula with a negative R\",\"Multiply the cost price by the number of years\",\"Divide the cost price by the rate of interest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests using the compound interest formula with a negative rate for depreciation calculations.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the standard formula is A = P(1 + R\\/100)^n, how is it modified for a depreciation problem?\",\"options\":[\"A = P(1 + R\\/100)^n\",\"A = P(1 - R\\/100)^n\",\"A = P - (R × n)\",\"A = P \\/ (1 + R\\/100)^n\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Because R is taken as negative for depreciation, the term (1 + R\\/100) becomes (1 - R\\/100).\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of visiting a bank as suggested in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To learn how to print money\",\"To get information on different schemes and rate of interests\",\"To apply for a job as a manager\",\"To calculate the bank\'s total population\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests visiting a bank to get information regarding different schemes and rate of interests.\",\"topic\":\"Application of compound interest\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for compound interest, how many quantities are involved in total?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the formula for compound interest, four quantities A, P, N, and R incur.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the population growth example (Ex. 1), what was the population of the city in the year 2010?\",\"options\":[\"2,91,600\",\"2,50,000\",\"60,000\",\"9,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the solution of Ex. (1), P = Population in the year 2010 = 2,50,000.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When the value of an asset like a scooter falls over time, as described in Ex. (2), the rate \'R\' is referred to as the rate of:\",\"options\":[\"Increase\",\"Appreciation\",\"Depreciation\",\"Compounding\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In Ex. (2), R is defined as the \'Rate of depreciation\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula to calculate the Amount (A) when the Principal (P) and Interest (I) are known?\",\"options\":[\"A = P - I\",\"A = P + I\",\"A = P × I\",\"A = I - P\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In Ex. (4), the text shows A = P + I = 9000 + 1890.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the value of \'N\' (number of years) used in the calculation for the price of the scooter?\",\"options\":[\"1 year\",\"2 years\",\"3 years\",\"20 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the calculation shown in the figure, the power applied to the bracket is 2, representing N = 2 years.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/102_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific value is substituted for the Amount (A) to solve for the Principal (P)?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 5000\",\"₹ 10\",\"₹ 6655\",\"₹ 110\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the equation 6655 = P × (1 + 10\\/100)³, where 6655 is the Amount (A).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/102_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the solution of Ex. (3), if the amount is ₹ 6655, the rate is 10 p.c.p.a., and the time is 3 years, what was the principal?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 6000\",\"₹ 5000\",\"₹ 5500\",\"₹ 4500\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The solution for Ex. (3) concludes that the principal was ₹ 5000.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Ex. (4), the ratio 10890\\/9000 is simplified to 121\\/100. This is expressed as which power of (11\\/10)?\",\"options\":[\"(11\\/10)¹\",\"(11\\/10)²\",\"(11\\/10)³\",\"(11\\/10)⁴\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows 121\\/100 = (11\\/10)².\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the population of a city increases at a compounding rate, which variable in the formula represents the population after a certain number of years?\",\"options\":[\"P\",\"R\",\"N\",\"A\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In Ex. (1), A is defined as the population in the year 2012, which is the population after 2 years.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Ex. (2), what is the final price of the scooter after 2 years of depreciation?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 60000\",\"₹ 48000\",\"₹ 38400\",\"₹ 30000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The solution for Ex. (2) states that after two years the price will be Rs. 38400.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for compound interest, what does the letter \'N\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Number of years\",\"Net profit\",\"New principal\",\"Nominal rate\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the examples, N is consistently used to denote the number of years (e.g., N = 2 years).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Ex. (4), to find the number of years (N), what was the first step taken before substituting values into the compound interest formula?\",\"options\":[\"Calculate the rate\",\"Calculate the amount (A = P + I)\",\"Divide the principal by 100\",\"Subtract the interest from the principal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ex. (4) states: \'We will find the amount first. A = P + I\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a city\'s population grows from 2,50,000 to 2,91,600 in 2 years, what was the rate of increase per year?\",\"options\":[\"10%\",\"20%\",\"8%\",\"5%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ex. (1) specifies the rate of increase of population per year as 8%.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of Ex. (3), if the principal is ₹ 5000 and the amount is ₹ 6655, how much compound interest was earned?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 1655\",\"₹ 1155\",\"₹ 655\",\"₹ 500\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Interest = Amount - Principal = 6655 - 5000 = 1655.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the value of R taken as -20 in the calculation for the scooter\'s price in the textbook figure?\",\"options\":[\"Because the price is increasing\",\"Because the time is 2 years\",\"Because the value falls (depreciation)\",\"Because the principal is 60000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure for Ex. (2) uses (1 + -20\\/100) because the value falls by 20% every year.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Application)\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22128,55,8,'Maths','en','Area','[\"Area of a square = side × side.\",\"Area of a rectangle = length × breadth.\",\"Area of a right‑angled triangle = ½ × base × height (or ½ × product of the two sides that form the right angle).\",\"Area of any triangle = ½ × base × height.\",\"Area of a parallelogram = base × height.\",\"Area of a rhombus = ½ × product of its diagonals.\",\"Area of a trapezium = ½ × (sum of the two parallel sides) × height.\",\"Heron’s formula: For a triangle with sides a, b, c, let s = (a+b+c)\\/2. Then area = √[s(s‑a)(s‑b)(s‑c)].\",\"Circumference of a circle = 2πr. Area of a circle = πr².\",\"1 are = 100 m², 1 hectare = 10 000 m².\",\"Use graph paper to approximate the area of irregular figures by counting squares.\",\"When a circle is divided into many equal sectors and joined, it approximates a rectangle whose length is πr and width r, giving area πr².\",\"The height of a trapezium is the perpendicular distance between its parallel sides.\",\"A rhombus’s diagonals are perpendicular bisectors of each other.\",\"The area of a rhombus can also be found by ½ × d₁ × d₂.\",\"The area of a parallelogram can be found by choosing any side as base and the corresponding perpendicular distance as height.\",\"If the area of a parallelogram and one side are known, the height = area ÷ side.\",\"If the area of a parallelogram and the height are known, the base = area ÷ height.\",\"If the area of a rhombus and one diagonal are known, the other diagonal = (2 × area) ÷ given diagonal.\",\"If the area of a trapezium and its height are known, the sum of the parallel sides = (2 × area) ÷ height.\",\"If the area of a trapezium and the sum of the parallel sides are known, the height = (2 × area) ÷ sum.\",\"Use the Pythagorean theorem to find missing sides in right‑angled triangles within rhombus or trapezium problems.\",\"When dividing a circle into sectors, the number of sectors does not affect the area – it remains πr².\",\"The area of a circle can also be expressed as (circumference × radius)\\/2.\",\"For irregular polygons, divide into triangles and quadrilaterals whose areas can be calculated by the above formulas.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the formula for the Area of a square?\",\"options\":[\"4 × side\",\"side²\",\"2 × side\",\"side + side\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: Area of square = side².\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the sides of a closed polygon are given in kilometers (km), what will be the unit of its area?\",\"options\":[\"km\",\"sq m\",\"sq km\",\"cubic km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if sides are in km, the area is in sq km.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for the Area of a rectangle?\",\"options\":[\"length + breadth\",\"2 × (length + breadth)\",\"length × breadth\",\"length² × breadth²\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: Area of rectangle = length × breadth.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the area of a closed polygon measured in units like sq cm, sq m, or sq km?\",\"options\":[\"Because the area is measured by squares.\",\"Because polygons have four sides.\",\"Because area is the same as perimeter.\",\"Because all polygons are squares.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that areas are in square units \'because the area is measured by squares\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for the area of a right-angled triangle, which sides are multiplied?\",\"options\":[\"All three sides of the triangle\",\"The two longest sides\",\"The sides making the right angle\",\"The base and the hypotenuse\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the area as 1\\/2 × product of sides making right angle.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following represents the general formula for the Area of a triangle as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"base × height\",\"1\\/2 × base × height\",\"1\\/2 × (base + height)\",\"2 × base × height\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: Area of triangle = 1\\/2 × base × height.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a square has a side of 7 cm, what is its area?\",\"options\":[\"14 sq cm\",\"28 sq cm\",\"49 sq cm\",\"49 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using Area = side², 7 × 7 = 49 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A rectangle has a length of 10 m and a breadth of 5 m. Calculate its area.\",\"options\":[\"15 sq m\",\"50 sq m\",\"30 sq m\",\"50 m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using Area = length × breadth, 10 × 5 = 50 sq m.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A right-angled triangle has two sides making the right angle with lengths 6 cm and 8 cm. What is its area?\",\"options\":[\"48 sq cm\",\"24 sq cm\",\"14 sq cm\",\"24 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using Area = 1\\/2 × product of sides making right angle, 1\\/2 × 6 × 8 = 24 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a triangle has a base of 12 m and a height of 4 m, how does its area compare to a square with a side of 5 m?\",\"options\":[\"The triangle\'s area (24 sq m) is less than the square\'s area (25 sq m).\",\"The triangle\'s area (48 sq m) is greater than the square\'s area (25 sq m).\",\"Both have the same area of 25 sq m.\",\"The triangle\'s area (24 sq m) is greater than the square\'s area (20 sq m).\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Triangle area = 1\\/2 × 12 × 4 = 24 sq m. Square area = 5 × 5 = 25 sq m. 24 < 25.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, what is the formula for the Area of a parallelogram?\",\"options\":[\"Area = base + height\",\"Area = base × height\",\"Area = 2 × (base + height)\",\"Area = 1\\/2 × base × height\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: Area of parallelogram = base × height.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as the height of a parallelogram corresponding to a specific base?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the parallel sides\",\"The distance between the parallel sides\",\"The length of the diagonal\",\"The sum of all sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the distance between parallel sides is the height of the parallelogram corresponding to the base.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a right-angled triangle is cut from one side of a parallelogram and joined to the other side, what new figure is formed?\",\"options\":[\"A square\",\"A rhombus\",\"A rectangle\",\"A triangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity notes that the new figure formed by rearranging the parts of the parallelogram is a rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the areas of the original parallelogram and the newly formed rectangle equal?\",\"options\":[\"Because they have the same perimeter\",\"Because the rectangle is formed from the parallelogram\",\"Because all quadrilaterals have equal area\",\"Because they have the same diagonal length\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the rectangle is formed from the parallelogram, so areas of both the figures are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the base of a parallelogram is 12 cm and its height is 8 cm, what is its area?\",\"options\":[\"20 sq cm\",\"40 sq cm\",\"96 sq cm\",\"48 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Area = base × height: 12 × 8 = 96 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the area of a parallelogram is 56 sq cm and the base is 7 cm, what is its height?\",\"options\":[\"8 cm\",\"49 cm\",\"63 cm\",\"392 cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Height = Area \\/ base = 56 \\/ 7 = 8 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if side BC is considered as the base, which segment represents the height \'h\'?\",\"options\":[\"seg AB\",\"seg AR\",\"seg CD\",\"seg AC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text indicate that if side BC is the base, height h = l(AR).\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/105_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if seg AB is considered as the base, what is the corresponding height?\",\"options\":[\"l(BC)\",\"l(AR)\",\"l(DP)\",\"l(QC)\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states: if we consider seg AB as a base then corresponding height is l(QC) = k.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/105_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The base of a parallelogram corresponds to which part of the rectangle formed in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"The diagonal\",\"The breadth\",\"The length\",\"The perimeter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: Base of parallelogram is one side (length) of the rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If seg DP is perpendicular to side BC in parallelogram ABCD, which of the following is true?\",\"options\":[\"A(ABCD) = l(BC) × l(DP)\",\"A(ABCD) = l(AB) × l(DP)\",\"A(ABCD) = l(BC) + l(DP)\",\"A(ABCD) = l(AD) × l(AR)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Area is the product of the base (BC) and its corresponding height (DP).\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of a rhombus, which segments represent the diagonals?\",\"options\":[\"AB and BC\",\"AC and BD\",\"AP and PC\",\"BP and PD\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a rhombus where the lines connecting opposite vertices are AC and BD.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/106_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The height of a parallelogram corresponds to which part of the rectangle formed in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"The length\",\"The breadth\",\"The diagonal\",\"The area\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: its height is the other side (breadth) of the rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a parallelogram\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for the area of a rhombus as stated in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2 × product of lengths of the diagonals\",\"Product of lengths of the diagonals\",\"Side × Side\",\"2 × (length + breadth)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: Area of a rhombus = 1\\/2 × product of lengths of the diagonals.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for the area of a rhombus, what does the term \'product\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"Adding the lengths\",\"Subtracting the lengths\",\"Multiplying the lengths\",\"Dividing the lengths\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In mathematics, \'product\' refers to the result of multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the lengths of the diagonals of a rhombus are 11.2 cm and 7.5 cm, what is its area?\",\"options\":[\"84 sq cm\",\"18.7 sq cm\",\"42 sq cm\",\"37.5 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As shown in Ex. (1), Area = 1\\/2 × 11.2 × 7.5 = 5.6 × 7.5 = 42 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), if the area is 96 sq cm and one diagonal is 12 cm, what is the length of the other diagonal AC?\",\"options\":[\"8 cm\",\"16 cm\",\"12 cm\",\"24 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the calculation in Ex. (2), 96 = 6 × l(AC), which results in l(AC) = 16 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which segments represent the diagonals of the rhombus ABCD?\",\"options\":[\"Sides AB and BC\",\"Diagonals AC and BD\",\"Vertices A and B\",\"Segments AE and EC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows segments AC and BD connecting opposite vertices, which are the diagonals.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/107_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the square symbol at point E indicate about the diagonals AC and BD?\",\"options\":[\"They are parallel to each other\",\"They are of equal length\",\"They are perpendicular to each other\",\"They do not intersect\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The square symbol at the intersection point E represents a right angle, meaning the diagonals are perpendicular.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/107_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), how is the value \'6\' obtained in the step \'96 = 6 × l(AC)\'?\",\"options\":[\"By subtracting 6 from 12\",\"By dividing 96 by 16\",\"By calculating 1\\/2 of the diagonal length 12\",\"By multiplying 12 by 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The formula 1\\/2 × 12 × l(AC) simplifies to 6 × l(AC) because 1\\/2 of 12 is 6.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the product of the lengths of the diagonals of a rhombus is 192, what is the area of the rhombus?\",\"options\":[\"192 sq cm\",\"96 sq cm\",\"384 sq cm\",\"48 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula 1\\/2 × product, 1\\/2 × 192 = 96 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the solution in Example (2), what is the necessary first step to find the length of the side of the rhombus?\",\"options\":[\"Calculate the perimeter of the rhombus\",\"Find the length of the other diagonal\",\"Multiply the area by 2\",\"Divide the length of one diagonal by the area\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'So first find the length of diagonal AC\' to eventually find the side.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit of measurement is used for the area of the rhombus in the provided examples?\",\"options\":[\"cm\",\"sq cm\",\"cubic cm\",\"meter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'sq cm\' for all area results (42 sq cm and 96 sq cm).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example (2) - Part 1\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as the height of a trapezium in the given text?\",\"options\":[\"The length of the non-parallel sides\",\"The distance between the parallel sides\",\"The length of the diagonals\",\"The sum of all four sides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the height of the trapezium is the distance between the parallel sides.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the activity for trapezium ABCD, what type of quadrilateral is formed by the points ABQP?\",\"options\":[\"Rhombus\",\"Square\",\"Rectangle\",\"Parallelogram\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that \'Box ABQP is a rectangle.\'\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the property of the diagonals of a rhombus?\",\"options\":[\"They are parallel to each other\",\"They are perpendicular bisectors of each other\",\"They are always equal in length\",\"They are not related to each other\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Diagonals are perpendicular bisectors of each other.\'\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the derivation of the area of a trapezium, why is l(PQ) equal to l(AB)?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are parallel sides of the trapezium\",\"Because they are opposite sides of the rectangle ABQP\",\"Because they are hypotenuses of right-angled triangles\",\"Because they are heights of the trapezium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a rectangle, opposite sides are equal, and the text uses l(PQ) = l(AB) in the derivation.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Into how many parts is the area of trapezium ABCD divided in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"Two parts\",\"Three parts\",\"Four parts\",\"Five parts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'After drawing the perpendiculars in Box ABCD, its area is divided into 3 parts.\'\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which triangles are identified as right-angled triangles in the trapezium activity?\",\"options\":[\"Triangle ABC and Triangle ADC\",\"Triangle APD and Triangle BQC\",\"Triangle ABQ and Triangle DCP\",\"Triangle APB and Triangle DQC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Out of these Triangle APD and Triangle BQC are right angled triangles.\'\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the parallel sides of a trapezium are 12 cm and 16 cm, and the height is 10 cm, what is its area?\",\"options\":[\"280 sq cm\",\"140 sq cm\",\"192 sq cm\",\"96 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Area = 1\\/2 × (12 + 16) × 10 = 1\\/2 × 28 × 10 = 140 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the rhombus example in the text, if the diagonals are 12 cm and 16 cm, what is the length of one side?\",\"options\":[\"14 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"28 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using Pythagoras theorem as shown in the text: 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100, so side = 10 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the derivation, the expression 1\\/2 × h [l(DP) + l(PQ) + l(QC) + l(AB)] is simplified to what final formula?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2 × h × l(DC)\",\"1\\/2 × h × [l(DC) + l(AB)]\",\"h × [l(DC) + l(AB)]\",\"1\\/2 × [l(DC) + l(AB)]\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that Area = 1\\/2 × h [l(DC) + l(AB)].\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the rhombus example, if l(DE) = 1\\/2 l(DB), what is the measure of angle E?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'in Triangle ADE, m∠E = 90°\'.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the area of a trapezium is 50 sq cm and the sum of its parallel sides is 20 cm, what is its height?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"20 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"50 = 1\\/2 × 20 × h => 50 = 10 × h => h = 5 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following represents the correct formula for the Area of trapezium ABCD?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2 × (sum of the lengths of parallel sides) × h\",\"sum of the lengths of parallel sides × h\",\"1\\/2 × (product of the lengths of parallel sides) × h\",\"1\\/2 × (sum of the lengths of non-parallel sides) × h\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text concludes: A(Box ABCD) = 1\\/2 (sum of the lengths of parallel sides) × h.\",\"topic\":\"Area of a trapezium\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the basic formula to find the area of a triangle as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Area = $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ base $\\\\times$ height\",\"Area = base $\\\\times$ height\",\"Area = 2 $\\\\times$ base $\\\\times$ height\",\"Area = $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ (a + b + c)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that Area of a triangle = 1\\/2 base x height.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of finding the area of a triangle using side lengths, what does the letter \'s\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Side\",\"Sum\",\"Semiperimeter\",\"Square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines \'s\' as the semiperimeter of the triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, when do we use the semiperimeter to help find the area of a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"When the base is not given\",\"When the height is not given but lengths of three sides are given\",\"When the triangle is a right-angled triangle\",\"When the perimeter is already known\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says we find the area this way if height is not given but lengths of the three sides are given.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If $a, b,$ and $c$ are the lengths of the three sides of a triangle, what does the expression $(a + b + c)$ represent?\",\"options\":[\"The area of the triangle\",\"The height of the triangle\",\"The semiperimeter of the triangle\",\"The perimeter of the triangle\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The sum of all sides (a + b + c) is the perimeter; the semiperimeter is half of this sum.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a triangle has side lengths of 6 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm, what is the value of its semiperimeter (s)?\",\"options\":[\"24 cm\",\"12 cm\",\"14 cm\",\"48 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"s = 1\\/2 (6 + 8 + 10) = 1\\/2 (24) = 12 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Find the semiperimeter \'s\' for a triangle where the lengths of the sides are $a = 5, b = 7,$ and $c = 8$.\",\"options\":[\"20\",\"15\",\"10\",\"5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"s = 1\\/2 (5 + 7 + 8) = 1\\/2 (20) = 10.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which side length is represented by the letter \'a\'?\",\"options\":[\"Side BC\",\"Side AC\",\"Side AB\",\"The height\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text and figure define \'a\' as the length of side BC.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/109_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the lengths of the sides are $a, b,$ and $c$, which formula correctly calculates the semiperimeter?\",\"options\":[\"s = a + b + c\",\"s = $\\\\frac{a + b + c}{2}$\",\"s = $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\times a \\\\times b$\",\"s = a $\\\\times$ b $\\\\times$ c\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The semiperimeter is half the sum of the sides, which is (a + b + c) \\/ 2.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/109_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the semiperimeter (s) of a triangle is calculated to be 15 units, what must be the sum of the lengths of its three sides ($a + b + c$)?\",\"options\":[\"7.5 units\",\"15 units\",\"30 units\",\"45 units\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since s = 1\\/2 (a + b + c), then (a + b + c) = 2s = 2 x 15 = 30.\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct mathematical expression for semiperimeter as given in the text?\",\"options\":[\"s = $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ (a + b + c)\",\"s = (a + b + c)\",\"s = $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ (base $\\\\times$ height)\",\"s = $\\\\frac{a \\\\times b \\\\times c}{2}$\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines semiperimeter = s = 1\\/2 (a + b + c).\",\"topic\":\"Semiperimeter\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the formula $\\\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$ used to find the area of a triangle?\",\"options\":[\"Pythagoras Theorem\",\"Heron\'s Formula\",\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"Archimedes Formula\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that $\\\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$ is known as Heron\'s Formula.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example 1\",\"part\":14,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for the area of a triangle, what does the variable \'s\' represent?\",\"op