 figure in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"A ruler and a compass\",\"Graph paper and a pencil\",\"A protractor and a thread\",\"A measuring tape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the area of the irregular closed figure can be calculated approximately by using graph paper and drawing an outline with a pencil.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the area of one complete square on the graph paper mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 sq cm\",\"2 sq cm\",\"1 sq cm\",\"1.5 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the number of complete squares of area 1 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an irregular figure covers 15 complete squares on the graph paper, what is the area of those squares?\",\"options\":[\"7.5 sq cm\",\"15 sq cm\",\"30 sq cm\",\"10 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since each complete square is 1 sq cm, 15 complete squares equal 15 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, how is the area calculated for parts that are more than 1\\/2 sq cm but less than 1 sq cm?\",\"options\":[\"Each part is counted as 1\\/2 sq cm\",\"Each part is ignored\",\"Each part is counted as 1 sq cm\",\"Each part is counted as 0.75 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 11 parts having area more than 1\\/2 sq cm result in an area of 11 sq cm, meaning each is counted as 1.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the specific example provided in the text, how many parts had an area of exactly 1\\/2 sq cm?\",\"options\":[\"13\",\"11\",\"1\",\"0\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'number of parts having area 1\\/2 sq cm = 0\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step to find the area of an irregular object using this method?\",\"options\":[\"Count the number of complete squares\",\"Draw an outline of the object on graph paper\",\"Calculate the area of half squares\",\"Multiply the length by the breadth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to first \'Draw an outline of the given piece of object on the graph paper with pencil.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a figure has 4 parts that each have an area of exactly 1\\/2 sq cm, what would be the total area of these parts?\",\"options\":[\"4 sq cm\",\"1 sq cm\",\"2 sq cm\",\"0.5 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If the number of parts having area 1\\/2 sq cm is 4, then the area is 4 × 1\\/2 = 2 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the activity, the number of parts with area between 1\\/2 sq cm and 1 sq cm was 11. What was the area assigned to these parts?\",\"options\":[\"5.5 sq cm\",\"11 sq cm\",\"22 sq cm\",\"0 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'number of parts... = 11 ∴ Area of these parts = 11 sq cm\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the method described, why is the calculated area of the irregular figure considered \'approximate\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because graph paper is always inaccurate\",\"Because parts of squares are counted as whole units or specific fractions\",\"Because the pencil outline is too thick\",\"Because the number of complete squares is not counted\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The method involves counting parts of squares as 1 or 1\\/2, which leads to an approximation rather than an exact measurement.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a category mentioned in the text for counting squares\\/parts?\",\"options\":[\"Complete squares of area 1 sq cm\",\"Parts having area more than 1\\/2 sq cm but less than 1 sq cm\",\"Parts having area exactly 1\\/2 sq cm\",\"Parts having area exactly 1\\/4 sq cm\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists categories (1), (2), and (3), but does not mention parts having area exactly 1\\/4 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":26,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula to find the area of a circle?\",\"options\":[\"2πr\",\"πr²\",\"πd\",\"2πr²\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The area of a circle is calculated using the formula πr², where r is the radius.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the radius of a circle is 17.5 cm, what is its diameter?\",\"options\":[\"8.75 cm\",\"35 cm\",\"70 cm\",\"17.5 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Diameter is twice the radius (d = 2r), so 17.5 × 2 = 35 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Find the area of a circle whose radius is 28 cm. (Use π = 22\\/7)\",\"options\":[\"176 sq cm\",\"616 sq cm\",\"2464 sq cm\",\"1232 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Area = (22\\/7) × 28 × 28 = 22 × 4 × 28 = 2464 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Calculate the area of a circle with a radius of 10.5 cm.\",\"options\":[\"346.5 sq cm\",\"693 sq cm\",\"115.5 sq cm\",\"330 sq cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Area = (22\\/7) × 10.5 × 10.5 = 22 × 1.5 × 10.5 = 346.5 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the area of a circle is 12474 sq cm, what is its diameter?\",\"options\":[\"63 cm\",\"126 cm\",\"252 cm\",\"31.5 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"r² = Area\\/π = 12474 \\/ (22\\/7) = 3969; r = 63 cm. Diameter = 2 × 63 = 126 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A circular garden has a diameter of 42 m. What is the inner radius (r) of this garden?\",\"options\":[\"42 m\",\"84 m\",\"21 m\",\"3.5 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The radius is half of the diameter, so 42 \\/ 2 = 21 m.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A circular garden has a diameter of 42 m and a 3.5 m wide road around it. What is the outer radius (R) including the road?\",\"options\":[\"45.5 m\",\"24.5 m\",\"21 m\",\"38.5 m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Outer radius R = inner radius + road width = 21 + 3.5 = 24.5 m.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the circumference of a circle is 88 cm, what is its radius?\",\"options\":[\"7 cm\",\"14 cm\",\"28 cm\",\"44 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Circumference = 2πr; 88 = 2 × (22\\/7) × r; r = (88 × 7) \\/ 44 = 14 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Find the area of a circle whose circumference is 88 cm.\",\"options\":[\"616 sq cm\",\"154 sq cm\",\"308 sq cm\",\"44 sq cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"First find r = 14 cm from circumference, then Area = (22\\/7) × 14 × 14 = 616 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To find the area of a road around a circular garden, which of the following mathematical expressions is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Area of outer circle + Area of inner circle\",\"Circumference of outer circle - Circumference of inner circle\",\"Area of outer circle - Area of inner circle\",\"Area of outer circle × width of road\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The road is the space between two concentric circles, so its area is the difference between the outer and inner areas.\",\"topic\":\"Practice Set 15.6\",\"part\":27,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what area is assigned to parts that are less than 1\\/2 sq cm?\",\"options\":[\"1\\/2 sq cm\",\"1 sq cm\",\"0 sq cm\",\"0.5 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that parts having area less than 1\\/2 sq cm are not to be considered and their area is 0 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the calculation \'Total area = 13 + 11 + 0 + 0\', what is the resulting area?\",\"options\":[\"13 sq cm\",\"24 sq cm\",\"11 sq cm\",\"26 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the sum 13 + 11 + 0 + 0 = 24 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the radius of the circle to be drawn in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"28 cm\",\"2.8 cm\",\"28 mm\",\"14 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Activity section specifies drawing a circle of radius 28mm.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these figures is NOT mentioned to be drawn on the graph paper in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"Triangle\",\"Trapezium\",\"Circle\",\"Rectangle\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The activity mentions drawing a circle, a triangle, and a trapezium, but not a rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of using the formula for area in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"To find the radius\",\"To verify the answers found by counting squares\",\"To draw the graph paper\",\"To calculate the number of small squares\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Verify your answers using formula for area of these figures\'.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the approximation of area as the squares on the graph paper become smaller?\",\"options\":[\"The approximation becomes worse\",\"The approximation stays the same\",\"The approximation becomes better\",\"The area becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Observe that the smaller the squares of graph paper, better is the approximation of area.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a figure on graph paper has 5 parts that are each less than 1\\/2 sq cm, how much area do they contribute to the total?\",\"options\":[\"2.5 sq cm\",\"5 sq cm\",\"0.5 sq cm\",\"0 sq cm\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that parts less than 1\\/2 sq cm are not considered, meaning their area is 0.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the zeros included in the sum \'13 + 11 + 0 + 0\'?\",\"options\":[\"They represent parts with area less than 1\\/2 sq cm\",\"They represent the radius of the circle\",\"They represent the small squares of the graph\",\"They represent the formula for the trapezium\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that parts less than 1\\/2 sq cm are not considered, hence their area is 0.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, how do you find the area of the figures before verifying with a formula?\",\"options\":[\"By measuring with a ruler\",\"By counting the number of small squares on the graph paper\",\"By multiplying the radius by the height\",\"By drawing a trapezium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions say to \'Find the area of these figures by counting the number of small squares on the graph paper.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which condition leads to a \'better approximation\' of area when using graph paper?\",\"options\":[\"Using a larger radius for the circle\",\"Using a trapezium instead of a triangle\",\"Using smaller squares on the graph paper\",\"Ignoring parts larger than 1\\/2 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that smaller squares result in a better approximation of area.\",\"topic\":\"Activity\",\"part\":29,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which system of measurement has our nation adopted for recording land areas?\",\"options\":[\"Binary system\",\"Decimal system\",\"Imperial system\",\"Fractional system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that our nation has adopted the decimal system of measurement.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which department records the areas of lands in decimal units?\",\"options\":[\"Forest department\",\"Agriculture department\",\"Revenue department\",\"Survey department\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in the revenue department, areas of lands are recorded in decimal units.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many square meters (sq m) make 1 are?\",\"options\":[\"10 sq m\",\"1000 sq m\",\"100 sq m\",\"10,000 sq m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 100 sq m = 1 are.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"1 hectare is equal to how many ares?\",\"options\":[\"10 are\",\"100 are\",\"1000 are\",\"0.4 are\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that 100 are = 1 hectare.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the area of 1 hectare in square meters?\",\"options\":[\"1,000 sq m\",\"100 sq m\",\"10,000 sq m\",\"1,00,000 sq m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1 hectare = 10,000 sq m.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit of land measurement is approximately equal to 1 are?\",\"options\":[\"1 Acre\",\"1 Hectare\",\"1 Guntha\",\"100 Hectares\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that 1 Guntha land area is approximately 1 are.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a farmer has 500 square meters of land, how many \'ares\' does he own?\",\"options\":[\"5 are\",\"50 are\",\"0.5 are\",\"500 are\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since 100 sq m = 1 are, 500 sq m = 500 \\/ 100 = 5 are.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how many hectares is 1 acre of land?\",\"options\":[\"1.0 hectare\",\"0.1 hectare\",\"0.4 hectare\",\"4.0 hectare\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1 acre area is nearly 0.4 hectare.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a plot of land is 0.5 hectare, what is its area in square meters?\",\"options\":[\"500 sq m\",\"5,000 sq m\",\"50,000 sq m\",\"50 sq m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 1 hectare = 10,000 sq m, 0.5 hectare = 0.5 x 10,000 = 5,000 sq m.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the conversion factors provided, which of the following units represents the largest area?\",\"options\":[\"1 are\",\"1 Guntha\",\"1 hectare\",\"1 acre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"1 hectare is 100 ares, while 1 acre is only 0.4 hectare and 1 Guntha is approx 1 are.\",\"topic\":\"For more information\",\"part\":30,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22129,55,8,'Maths','en','Surface area and Volume','[\"Surface area is the total area covering the outside of a solid.\",\"Volume is the amount of space a solid occupies.\",\"A cuboid has 6 faces: top, bottom, 2 sides, front, back.\",\"Formula for volume of a cuboid: V = length × breadth × height.\",\"Formula for volume of a cube: V = side³.\",\"Surface area of a cuboid: SA = 2(lb + bh + lh).\",\"Surface area of a cube: SA = 6 × side².\",\"A cylinder has a curved surface and two circular bases.\",\"Curved surface area of a cylinder: CSA = 2πrh.\",\"Total surface area of a closed cylinder: TSA = 2πrh + 2πr².\",\"Volume of a cylinder: V = πr²h.\",\"1 cubic centimetre (cc) = 1 millilitre (ml).\",\"1 litre = 1000 cc = 1000 ml.\",\"To find the number of smaller solids that fit into a larger one, divide the volumes.\",\"Euler’s formula for polyhedra: F + V = E + 2.\",\"A prism’s volume = area of base × height.\",\"A right circular cone’s volume = ⅓πr²h (not covered here).\",\"When converting units, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm.\",\"Use π ≈ 3.14 for practical calculations.\",\"Always keep the units consistent when applying formulas.\",\"When a container is filled, its capacity equals its volume.\",\"The area of a rectangle is length × breadth.\",\"The area of a circle is πr².\",\"When a sheet is wrapped around a cylinder, the rectangle’s area equals the cylinder’s curved surface area.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the measure of the space occupied by a solid called?\",\"options\":[\"Surface area\",\"Volume\",\"Perimeter\",\"Length\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the measure of the space occupied by a solid is called the volume of the solid.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula for the total surface area of a cube?\",\"options\":[\"2(l + b + h)\",\"l^3\",\"6l^2\",\"4l^2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the total surface area of a cube = 6l^2.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many square centimeters are equal to 1 square meter?\",\"options\":[\"100 sq cm\",\"1,000 sq cm\",\"10,000 sq cm\",\"100,000 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As per the conversion provided, 1 sq m = 100 x 100 sq cm = 10,000 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a three-dimensional solid figure in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Cuboid\",\"Cylinder\",\"Cube\",\"Circle\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists cuboid, cube, and cylinder as three-dimensional solid figures.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the standard unit of volume defined as in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The space occupied by a cube with side 1 m\",\"The space occupied by a cube with side 1 cm\",\"The space occupied by a cylinder with height 1 cm\",\"The space occupied by a cuboid with length 1 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the space occupied by a cube with side 1 cm is a standard unit of volume.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is \'1 cubic centimeter\' written in short form?\",\"options\":[\"1 ccm\",\"1 sq cm\",\"1 cc\",\"1 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that 1 cubic centimeter is written in short as 1 cc or 1 cm^3.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 cm = 10 mm, then 1 sq cm is equal to how many sq mm?\",\"options\":[\"10 sq mm\",\"100 sq mm\",\"1,000 sq mm\",\"10^3 sq mm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, 1 sq cm = 10 x 10 sq mm = 100 sq mm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the formula 2(l x b + b x h + l x h) represent?\",\"options\":[\"Volume of a cuboid\",\"Total surface area of a cube\",\"Total surface area of a cuboid\",\"Volume of a cylinder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the total surface area of a cuboid = 2(l x b + b x h + l x h).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical expression is equivalent to 10,000 sq cm?\",\"options\":[\"10^2 sq cm\",\"10^3 sq cm\",\"10^4 sq cm\",\"10^5 sq cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 10,000 sq cm = 10^4 sq cm.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definition of volume, why is a cube with side 1 cm used as a standard unit?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a two-dimensional figure\",\"Because it represents the measure of space occupied by a specific solid\",\"Because it is easier to calculate surface area\",\"Because it has only length and breadth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines volume as the measure of space occupied, and uses the 1 cm side cube to define the standard unit of that space.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s learn.\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to Activity II, what is the formula for the volume of a cuboid?\",\"options\":[\"length + breadth + height\",\"length × breadth × height\",\"2 × (length + breadth)\",\"side × side × side\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Volume of a cuboid = length × breadth × height.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity III, if the side of a cube is \'l\', what is the formula for its volume?\",\"options\":[\"l × l\",\"3 × l\",\"l³\",\"l + l + l\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if the side of a cube is l, then volume of the cube = l × l × l = l³.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What unit is used to measure the volume of the cubes in the activities?\",\"options\":[\"sq cm\",\"cm\",\"cc\",\"m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'cc\' (cubic centimeters) to denote the volume of the cubes.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the volume formula \'l × b × h\', what does the letter \'h\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"heavy\",\"horizontal\",\"half\",\"height\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text specifies taking \'h\' for height in the volume formula.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity I, how many cubes of side 1 cm are arranged to form the cuboid?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"2\",\"6\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Activity I states: \'Arrange 6 such cubes as shown in figure.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Activity II, if a cuboid contains 12 cubes of volume 1 cc each, what is its total volume?\",\"options\":[\"7 cc\",\"12 cc\",\"24 cc\",\"3 cc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity II mentions that 12 cubes of volume 1 cc each result in a volume of 12 cc (3 × 2 × 2).\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity III, how many cubes of volume 1 cc are arranged to form a cube of side 2 cm?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Activity III states: \'In the adjoining figure 8 cubes of volume 1 cc are arranged.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the dimensions of the cuboid formed in Activity I?\",\"options\":[\"3 cm, 2 cm, 1 cm\",\"3 cm, 2 cm, 2 cm\",\"2 cm, 2 cm, 2 cm\",\"6 cm, 1 cm, 1 cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Activity I states: \'We get a cuboid of length 3 cm, breadth 2 cm and height 1 cm.\'\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the formula from Activity II, calculate the volume of a cuboid with length 4 cm, breadth 2 cm, and height 1 cm.\",\"options\":[\"7 cc\",\"6 cc\",\"8 cc\",\"4 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Volume = l × b × h = 4 × 2 × 1 = 8 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a cube has a side of 3 cm, what is its volume according to the formula in Activity III?\",\"options\":[\"9 cc\",\"27 cc\",\"6 cc\",\"12 cc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Volume of a cube = l³ = 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the volume of the cuboid in Activity I (6 cc) with the cube in Activity III (8 cc). Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[\"The cuboid has a larger volume.\",\"The cube has a larger volume.\",\"Both have the same volume.\",\"The cuboid has double the volume of the cube.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The cuboid in Activity I is 6 cc and the cube in Activity III is 8 cc; 8 is greater than 6.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you need to build a cuboid with a volume of 12 cc using 1 cc cubes, how many cubes will you need in total?\",\"options\":[\"6 cubes\",\"8 cubes\",\"12 cubes\",\"24 cubes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As seen in Activity II, a volume of 12 cc is made of 12 cubes of 1 cc each.\",\"topic\":\"Activity I, II and III\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as the space occupied by a liquid in a container?\",\"options\":[\"Mass of the liquid\",\"Volume of the liquid\",\"Density of the liquid\",\"Area of the liquid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the space occupied by a liquid in the container is its volume.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which units are commonly used for measuring the volume of liquid according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Metre and Centimetre\",\"Gram and Kilogram\",\"Millilitre and Litre\",\"Square cm and Square metre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions millilitre and litre as units used for measuring the volume of liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the volume of an empty cube with a side of 10 cm?\",\"options\":[\"100 cc\",\"10 cc\",\"1000 cc\",\"10000 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Volume = 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, 1 litre is equal to how many millilitres?\",\"options\":[\"10 ml\",\"100 ml\",\"1000 ml\",\"10000 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1 litre = 1000 millilitre.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a cube of side 10 cm is filled with water, what is the volume of that water in litres?\",\"options\":[\"10 litres\",\"1 litre\",\"0.1 litre\",\"100 litres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A cube of 1000 cc volume holds 1 litre of water.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between 1 cc and 1 ml?\",\"options\":[\"1 cc = 10 ml\",\"1 cc = 100 ml\",\"1 cc = 1 ml\",\"10 cc = 1 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that 1 litre = 1000 cc = 1000 ml, hence 1 cc = 1 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the volume of water filled in a cube of side 1 cm?\",\"options\":[\"1 litre\",\"10 ml\",\"1 ml\",\"100 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the volume of water filled in a cube of side 1 cm is 1 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a container has a volume of 1000 cc, how many millilitres of liquid can it hold?\",\"options\":[\"1 ml\",\"100 ml\",\"1000 ml\",\"10 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 1 cc = 1 ml, 1000 cc is equal to 1000 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the conversion 1 litre = 1000 cc, how many cubic centimetres (cc) are there in 2 litres?\",\"options\":[\"200 cc\",\"2000 cc\",\"20 cc\",\"20000 cc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If 1 litre is 1000 cc, then 2 litres is 2000 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following mathematical statements is derived from the text?\",\"options\":[\"1 litre = 100 cc\",\"1 cc = 1000 ml\",\"1 litre = 1000 cc = 1000 ml\",\"1 ml = 1000 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text directly provides the equality 1 litre = 1000 cc = 1000 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Volume of liquid\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Now I know\' section, what is the formula for the volume of a cuboid?\",\"options\":[\"side³\",\"length × breadth × height\",\"length + breadth + height\",\"2(lb + bh + hl)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: Volume of cuboid = length × breadth × height = l × b × h.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for the volume of a cube as given in the text?\",\"options\":[\"l × b × h\",\"l²\",\"side³\",\"4 × side\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: Volume of cube = side³ = l³.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Example (1), how many cubic centimeters (cc) are equal to 1 litre?\",\"options\":[\"100 cc\",\"10 cc\",\"10000 cc\",\"1000 cc\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions in Example (1) and (3) that 1000 cc = 1l.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), why is the volume of the tank calculated to find the water content?\",\"options\":[\"Because water has no volume\",\"The water contained in the tank is equal to the volume of the tank\",\"Because the tank is made of glass\",\"Because the height of the tank is 50 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The solution in Example (1) states: \'The water contained in the tank is equal to volume of the tank.\'\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), what is the first step to find how many boxes will fill the warehouse?\",\"options\":[\"Find the volume of a box\",\"Find the number of boxes\",\"Find the volume of the warehouse\",\"Convert litres to cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step (1) in the solution for Example (2) is \'Find volume of warehouse\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a cuboidal fish tank has a length of 1m, what value in centimeters is used for the volume calculation in Example (1)?\",\"options\":[\"1 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"100 cm\",\"1000 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example (1) shows: Length of tank = 1m = 100cm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), if the volume of a cube-shaped box is calculated as (40)³, what is the volume in cc?\",\"options\":[\"120 cc\",\"1600 cc\",\"64000 cc\",\"16000 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"40 × 40 × 40 = 64,000 cc (though the text shows the expanded multiplication, this is the application of the cube formula).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (3), if 5 litres of mixture is poured into a tray, what is the volume of the mixture in cc?\",\"options\":[\"50 cc\",\"500 cc\",\"5000 cc\",\"50000 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since 1 litre = 1000 cc, 5 litres = 5 × 1000 = 5000 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How is the \'Number of boxes\' determined in the warehouse example?\",\"options\":[\"Volume of warehouse + volume of a box\",\"Volume of warehouse × volume of a box\",\"(Volume of warehouse) \\/ (Volume of a box)\",\"Volume of warehouse - volume of a box\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step (3) of Example (2) defines the formula as: Number of boxes = (volume of warehouse)\\/(volume of a box).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (3), the length of the tray is calculated using the equation \'length × 40 × 2.5 = 5000\'. What is the result of 40 × 2.5?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"100\",\"1000\",\"42.5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows length = 5000 \\/ 100, implying 40 × 2.5 = 100.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final length of the tray calculated in Example (3)?\",\"options\":[\"40 cm\",\"2.5 cm\",\"50 cm\",\"100 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The solution concludes: ∴ length = 50 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), why are the warehouse dimensions (6m, 4m, 4m) converted to centimeters?\",\"options\":[\"Because volume must always be in cc\",\"To match the unit of the box side (40 cm) for calculation\",\"Because meters are too large for warehouses\",\"To make the number of boxes smaller\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dimensions are converted so that the volume of the warehouse and the volume of the box use the same units (cc).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a cuboid has dimensions 100 cm, 40 cm, and 50 cm, what is its volume in cc?\",\"options\":[\"20,000 cc\",\"2,00,000 cc\",\"2,000 cc\",\"190 cc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example (1) calculates 100 × 40 × 50 = 200000 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the relationship between a tray\'s capacity and the volume of the mixture it holds in Example (3)?\",\"options\":[\"The tray\'s capacity is half the volume of the mixture\",\"The tray\'s capacity is greater than the volume of the mixture\",\"The volume of the mixture equals the volume of the rectangular tray when filled to full capacity\",\"The mixture volume is unrelated to the tray\'s dimensions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step (3) in Example (3) states: Volume of rectangular tray = volume of mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), the warehouse dimensions are 6m, 4m, and 4m. What is the volume of the warehouse in cc?\",\"options\":[\"96 cc\",\"9,60,000 cc\",\"9,60,00,000 cc\",\"600 × 400 × 400 cc\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Step (1) of Example (2) shows Volume of warehouse = 600 × 400 × 400 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Cuboid and Cube)\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"When a paper wrapped around the curved surface of a cylinder is unwrapped, what geometric shape is formed?\",\"options\":[\"A circle\",\"A triangle\",\"A rectangle\",\"A square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the covered paper is unwrapped, it is a rectangle.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula for the curved surface area of a cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"πr²h\",\"2πrh\",\"2πr(h + r)\",\"πrh\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text derives the curved surface area as length × breadth = 2πr × h = 2πrh.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The length of the rectangular paper that exactly covers the curved surface of a cylinder is equal to which part of the cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"The height of the cylinder\",\"The radius of the cylinder\",\"The circumference of the circle (base)\",\"The area of the circular face\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Length = Circumference of the circle.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the derivation of the surface area, the breadth of the rectangular paper is equal to which dimension of the cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Height\",\"Circumference\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions taking a rectangular paper sheet whose breadth is equal to the height of the container.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which formula represents the total surface area of a closed cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"2πrh + πr²\",\"2πrh + 2πr²\",\"πr²h\",\"2πr + h\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states total surface area = curved surface area + 2 × area of circular faces = 2πrh + 2πr².\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a cylinder is closed, what is the shape of its upper and lower surfaces?\",\"options\":[\"Rectangular\",\"Circular\",\"Spherical\",\"Square\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the upper and the lower surface of the closed cylinder is circular.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the formula 2πr(h + r), what does \'r\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"The height of the cylinder\",\"The circumference of the base\",\"The radius of the circular base\",\"The area of the rectangle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the context of 2πr (circumference), \'r\' stands for the radius of the circle.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the circumference of the base of a cylinder is \'C\' and its height is \'h\', what is its curved surface area?\",\"options\":[\"C + h\",\"C \\/ h\",\"C × h\",\"2 × C × h\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines curved surface area as circumference of the base × height.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the formula for total surface area, 2πr(h + r), why is the term 2πr² included?\",\"options\":[\"To account for the curved side\",\"To account for the two circular faces (top and bottom)\",\"To account for the height of the cylinder\",\"To account for the length of the rectangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that total surface area includes 2 × area of circular faces, which is 2πr².\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A rectangular sheet is used to cover the curved surface of a cylinder. If the area of this rectangle is 100 sq cm, what is the curved surface area of the cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"50 sq cm\",\"100 sq cm\",\"200 sq cm\",\"400 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the area of this rectangle equals the area of curved surface of cylinder.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a simplified common factor form of the total surface area formula?\",\"options\":[\"2πrh(r)\",\"2πr(h + r)\",\"2π(rh + r)\",\"πr(2h + r)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes the formula as 2πr(h + r) by taking 2πr as a common factor from 2πrh + 2πr².\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are asked to calculate the surface area of only the \'sides\' of a cylindrical tin, which formula would you use?\",\"options\":[\"2πr(h + r)\",\"πr²\",\"2πrh\",\"2πr²\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'sides\' correspond to the curved surface area, which is 2πrh.\",\"topic\":\"Surface area of cylinder\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to Example (1), what is the formula for the total surface area of a cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"2πrh\",\"πr²h\",\"2πr(h + r)\",\"πr(h + r)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states the formula for total surface area of a cylinder is 2πr(h + r).\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), if the diameter of the base of the cylinder is 1 m, what is its radius?\",\"options\":[\"1 m\",\"0.5 m\",\"2 m\",\"1.5 m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the diameter is 1 m, therefore the radius is 0.5 m.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What value of π is used to solve Example (2) involving the zinc sheet?\",\"options\":[\"3.14\",\"22\\/7\",\"3.142\",\"22\\/7.5\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The solution for Example (2) shows the calculation using 22\\/7 for π.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), why is the area to be painted calculated as twice the total surface area?\",\"options\":[\"Because the tank has two lids.\",\"Because the tank is to be painted internally and externally.\",\"Because the height is twice the diameter.\",\"Because the rate of painting is doubled.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The tank is to be painted internally and externally. It means that the area to be painted is twice the total surface area.\'\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), which part of the cylinder does the sheet required for a single pipe represent?\",\"options\":[\"Total surface area\",\"Base area\",\"Curved surface area\",\"Volume\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The sheet required for a pipe = curved surface area of one pipe\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Example (2), the length of the pipe is equivalent to which dimension of the cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Circumference\",\"Height\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Length of pipe is same as the height of cylinder = h\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the calculation for Example (1), what is the value of (h + r) when height is 2 m and radius is 0.5 m?\",\"options\":[\"1.5 m\",\"2.0 m\",\"2.5 m\",\"3.0 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The calculation shown is (2.0 + 0.5) = 2.5.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Using the data in Example (2), what is the curved surface area of one pipe?\",\"options\":[\"330 sq cm\",\"660 sq cm\",\"150 sq cm\",\"990 sq cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text calculates the curved surface area as 2 × (22\\/7) × (35\\/10) × 30 = 660.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (1), if the total surface area is 7.85 sqm, what is the total area to be painted internally and externally?\",\"options\":[\"7.85 sqm\",\"14.70 sqm\",\"15.70 sqm\",\"16.70 sqm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text calculates the area to be painted as 2 × 7.85 = 15.70 sqm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How is the number of pipes determined from the zinc sheet in Example (2)?\",\"options\":[\"By multiplying Area of sheet by Curved surface area\",\"By dividing Area of sheet by Curved surface area\",\"By subtracting Curved surface area from Area of sheet\",\"By dividing Total surface area by Curved surface area\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The formula used in the text is: Number of pipes = (Area of sheet) \\/ (Curved surface area of a pipe).\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the rate of painting in Example (1) was ₹ 100 per sqm instead of ₹ 80, what would be the total expenditure for 15.70 sqm?\",\"options\":[\"₹ 1256\",\"₹ 1570\",\"₹ 785\",\"₹ 157\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Total expenditure = Area × Rate. 15.70 × 100 = 1570.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), if the area of the sheet was 66000 sq cm, how many pipes of 660 sq cm each could be made?\",\"options\":[\"10 pipes\",\"100 pipes\",\"150 pipes\",\"66 pipes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Number of pipes = 66000 \\/ 660 = 100.\",\"topic\":\"Solved examples (Surface area)\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common formula for the volume of any prism?\",\"options\":[\"2 × area of base × height\",\"area of base × height\",\"π × radius × height\",\"2π × radius × height\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a common formula for volume of any prism = area of base × height.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the shape of the base of a cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"Rectangular\",\"Square\",\"Circular\",\"Triangular\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the \'Base of a cylinder is circular.\'\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which formula is used to find the volume of a cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"2πrh\",\"2πr(h + r)\",\"πr²h\",\"πrh²\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: volume of a cylinder = πr²h.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the conversion given in the text, 1 litre is equal to how many milliliters (ml)?\",\"options\":[\"10 ml\",\"100 ml\",\"1000 ml\",\"10000 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: 1 litre = 1000 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To find how much water a cylindrical tank can hold, what must be calculated?\",\"options\":[\"Curved surface area\",\"Total surface area\",\"Volume of the tank\",\"Perimeter of the base\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'To find how much water a tank of cylindrical shape can hold, we have to find the volume of the tank.\'\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the volume of a cylinder calculated as πr²h?\",\"options\":[\"Because the base area is πr² and volume is base area × height\",\"Because the circumference is 2πr and volume is circumference × height\",\"Because the cylinder is a type of sphere\",\"Because the height is always equal to the radius\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since the base of a cylinder is circular (area = πr²) and volume = area of base × height, the formula becomes πr²h.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of Example (3), what does the \'thickness\' of the disc represent in the volume formula?\",\"options\":[\"The radius (r)\",\"The diameter (d)\",\"The height (h)\",\"The area (A)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example (3) states: \'Thickness of disc = height of disc = 0.2 cm\'.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the volume of a cylinder if its height is 10 cm and the radius of its base is 5 cm? (Use π = 3.14)\",\"options\":[\"157 cc\",\"314 cc\",\"785 cc\",\"1570 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As shown in Example (1): 3.14 × 5² × 10 = 3.14 × 25 × 10 = 785 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a cylindrical drum has a capacity of 70.4 litres, what is its volume in cubic centimeters (cc)?\",\"options\":[\"704 cc\",\"7040 cc\",\"70400 cc\",\"704000 cc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example (2) shows: 70.4 litre = 70.4 × 1000 cc = 70400 cc.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (3), if the diameter of the disc is 1.4 cm, what is the radius used for the volume calculation?\",\"options\":[\"1.4 cm\",\"0.7 cm\",\"2.8 cm\",\"0.2 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example (3) shows: Radius of the base of the disc = 1.4 ÷ 2 = 0.7 cm.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When melting a large solid cylinder to create smaller discs, which mathematical relationship is used to find the number of discs (n)?\",\"options\":[\"n = Volume of cylinder + Volume of one disc\",\"n = Volume of cylinder × Volume of one disc\",\"n = Volume of cylinder \\/ Volume of one disc\",\"n = Volume of one disc \\/ Volume of cylinder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example (3) shows the formula: n = Volume of cylinder \\/ Volume of one disc.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (2), if r² = 400, what is the radius (r) of the drum?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"40 cm\",\"200 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the solution for Example (2), r² = 400 leads to r = 20.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula for the Total surface area of a cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"2πrh\",\"πr²h\",\"2πr(h + r)\",\"πr(h + r)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Now I know\' section lists: Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(h + r).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example (3), the calculation \'42 × 42 × 160 \\/ 7 × 7 × 2\' is derived from which simplified expression?\",\"options\":[\"R²H \\/ r²h\",\"2πRH \\/ 2πrh\",\"πR² \\/ πr²\",\"(R+H) \\/ (r+h)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows n = R²H \\/ r²h, which leads to the numerical calculation 4.2×4.2×16 \\/ 0.7×0.7×0.2.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Example (3), how many discs can be made from the solid copper cylinder?\",\"options\":[\"288\",\"1440\",\"2880\",\"5760\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The final result of Example (3) is 2880 discs.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Examples (Volume of cylinder)\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who discovered the interesting formula regarding the faces, vertices, and edges of solid figures?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Newton\",\"Leonard Euler\",\"Pythagoras\",\"Aryabhata\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Leonard Euler, a great mathematician, discovered this formula.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct representation of Euler\'s formula as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"F + E = V + 2\",\"F + V = E + 2\",\"F - V = E + 2\",\"F + V + E = 2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides Euler\'s formula as F + V = E + 2.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of solid figures and Euler\'s formula, what does the letter \'V\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Volume\",\"Velocity\",\"Vertices\",\"Vertical\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the formula regarding faces (F), vertices (V), and edges (E).\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, at what stage of his life did Leonard Euler discover this formula?\",\"options\":[\"In his old age\",\"During his middle age\",\"At a very young age\",\"After he retired\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions he discovered the formula \'at a very young age\'.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a solid figure has 6 faces and 8 vertices, how many edges (E) must it have to satisfy Euler\'s formula?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"12\",\"14\",\"16\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using F + V = E + 2: 6 + 8 = E + 2; 14 = E + 2; therefore E = 12.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the formula F + V = E + 2, if a Hexagonal prism has 8 faces and 12 vertices, what is the count of its edges?\",\"options\":[\"18\",\"20\",\"22\",\"10\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using the formula: 8 + 12 = E + 2; 20 = E + 2; so E = 18.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which solid shape is explicitly shown to have 10 edges (E) in the table?\",\"options\":[\"Cube\",\"Cuboid\",\"Pentagonal pyramid\",\"Hexagonal prism\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows the value 10 in the \'Edges (E)\' row under the \'Pentagonal pyramid\' column.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, how many vertices (V) are recorded for the Hexagonal prism?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"8\",\"10\",\"12\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists 12 under the \'Vertices (V)\' row for the Hexagonal prism.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a Triangular pyramid has 4 faces and 4 vertices, which value of edges (E) correctly verifies Euler\'s formula?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"5\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"F + V = 4 + 4 = 8. Since F + V = E + 2, then 8 = E + 2, which means E = 6.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the relationship Euler discovered between the components of a solid figure?\",\"options\":[\"The sum of faces and vertices is two more than the number of edges.\",\"The number of edges is always equal to the sum of faces and vertices.\",\"The number of faces is always double the number of vertices.\",\"The sum of edges and vertices is two more than the number of faces.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"F + V = E + 2 means the sum of faces and vertices equals edges plus two.\",\"topic\":\"Euler\'s Formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22130,55,8,'Maths','en','Circle : Chord and Arc','[\"A chord is a straight line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle.\",\"The centre of the circle is the point equidistant from all points on the circle.\",\"The perpendicular drawn from the centre to a chord bisects the chord.\",\"If a chord is a diameter, the centre lies on the chord and the chord is the longest in the circle.\",\"A central angle is an angle whose vertex is the centre and whose sides are radii.\",\"The measure of a central angle equals the measure of the arc it intercepts.\",\"A minor arc is the shorter arc between two points; a major arc is the longer one.\",\"Corresponding arcs of a chord are the minor and major arcs intercepted by the chord.\",\"If two chords are equal in length, their corresponding arcs are equal in measure.\",\"The sum of the measures of a minor arc and its corresponding major arc is 360°.\",\"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord is also the perpendicular bisector of the chord.\",\"The length of a chord can be found using the Pythagorean theorem: if the distance from the centre to the chord is d and the radius is r, then half the chord length is √(r²−d²).\",\"The distance from the centre to a chord is the length of the perpendicular from the centre to the chord.\",\"If two arcs are congruent, the chords subtending them are congruent.\",\"If two chords are congruent, the arcs subtending them are congruent.\",\"The diameter is a special chord that passes through the centre.\",\"A circle with perpendicular diameters divides the circle into four equal arcs of 90° each.\",\"The measure of a central angle is expressed in degrees.\",\"The radius is the distance from the centre to any point on the circle.\",\"The chord length is 2×√(r²−d²) where d is the distance from the centre to the chord.\",\"The perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre of the circle.\",\"The measure of a minor arc plus the measure of its corresponding major arc equals 360°.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is Seg OD called with respect to the circle?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Chord\",\"Arc\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A segment joining the centre to any point on the circle is the radius.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, Seg AB passes through the centre O. What is Seg AB?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Minor arc\",\"Central angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A chord passing through the centre of the circle is called the diameter.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the circle shown, what is the technical name for Seg PQ?\",\"options\":[\"Radius\",\"Diameter\",\"Chord\",\"Central angle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A segment joining any two points on the circle is a chord.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which of the following is a central angle in the figure?\",\"options\":[\"∠PAQ\",\"∠DOB\",\"∠ADB\",\"∠AXD\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"An angle with its vertex at the centre of the circle is a central angle.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the measure of central angle ∠DOB is 70°, what is the measure of arc DB?\",\"options\":[\"35°\",\"70°\",\"140°\",\"290°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The measure of a minor arc is equal to the measure of its corresponding central angle.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the measure of minor arc DB is 100°, what is the measure of the major arc DAB?\",\"options\":[\"100°\",\"200°\",\"260°\",\"360°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"m(major arc) = 360° - m(corresponding minor arc).\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the figure and text, which of the following is a semicircular arc?\",\"options\":[\"arc AXD\",\"arc PQ\",\"arc APB\",\"arc PDQ\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A diameter divides the circle into two semicircular arcs; arc APB is one of them.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"55\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Complete the formula from the text: m(arc DB) = m∠______.\",\"options\":[\"DAB\",\"DOB\",\"ADB\",\"AXD\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The measure of a minor arc is equal to the measure of its central angle.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the textbook figure, arc PAB and arc PDQ are examples of which type of arc?\",\"options\":[\"Minor arc\",\"Major arc\",\"Semicircular arc\",\"Central arc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists arc PAB and arc PDQ under the category of Major arc.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If m(arc DAB) = 360° - m∠DOB, what does this calculation represent?\",\"options\":[\"Measure of a minor arc\",\"Measure of a diameter\",\"Measure of a major arc\",\"Measure of a semicircle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Subtracting the central angle (minor arc measure) from 360° gives the measure of the major arc.\",\"topic\":\"Let\'s recall.\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the property mentioned in the text, the perpendicular drawn from the centre of a circle to its chord ______ the chord.\",\"options\":[\"trisects\",\"bisects\",\"doubles\",\"intersects at one end\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the perpendicular drawn from the centre of a circle to its chord bisects the chord.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the activity, if OP is the perpendicular drawn from centre O to chord AB, which two segments are found to be equal?\",\"options\":[\"seg OA and seg OB\",\"seg OP and seg AB\",\"seg AP and seg PB\",\"seg OA and seg AP\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity asks to measure and verify that the two parts of the chord (AP and PB) are equal.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'bisect\' mean in the context of the chord property described?\",\"options\":[\"To cut into three equal parts\",\"To divide into two equal parts\",\"To make the chord longer\",\"To join the chord to the centre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bisecting a chord means dividing it into two equal parts, as verified by the divider in the activity.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is specifically mentioned in the activity to verify that the two parts of the chords are equal?\",\"options\":[\"Protractor\",\"Scale\",\"Divider\",\"Set-square\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says to \'Verify with a divider that the two parts of the chords are equal.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a perpendicular is drawn from the centre of a circle to a chord of length 10 cm, what will be the length of each part of the chord?\",\"options\":[\"10 cm\",\"20 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"2.5 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since the perpendicular bisects the chord, each part is half of the total length (10 \\/ 2 = 5).\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a circle with centre O, a perpendicular is drawn to chord XY at point M. If XM = 4 cm, what is the length of chord XY?\",\"options\":[\"4 cm\",\"2 cm\",\"8 cm\",\"12 cm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because the perpendicular bisects the chord, the total length is twice the length of one part (4 * 2 = 8).\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To verify the property of chords across different scenarios, the activity suggests drawing five circles with different ______.\",\"options\":[\"Centres\",\"Radii\",\"Chords\",\"Perpendiculars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Draw five circles with different radii.\'\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If seg AP = 6 cm and seg PB = 6 cm in a circle where P is a point on chord AB, what can be concluded about the segment drawn from the centre to point P if it is perpendicular?\",\"options\":[\"It is the radius of the circle\",\"It bisects the chord AB\",\"It is longer than the chord\",\"It does not touch the centre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The property states the perpendicular bisects the chord; since AP=PB, the chord is bisected.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a necessary condition for a segment from the centre to bisect a chord according to the learned property?\",\"options\":[\"The segment must be parallel to the chord\",\"The segment must be perpendicular to the chord\",\"The segment must be the diameter\",\"The segment must be shorter than the radius\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The property specifically refers to the \'perpendicular drawn from the centre\'.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student draws a perpendicular from the centre to a chord and finds the two parts are not equal. What does this imply based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"The property only works for diameters\",\"The drawing or measurement is inaccurate\",\"The circle must have a specific radius\",\"The chord must be vertical\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the property is a mathematical rule for all such cases, an inequality suggests a mistake in the activity\'s execution.\",\"topic\":\"Properties of chord of a circle\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main property discovered in Activity II regarding a circle and its chord?\",\"options\":[\"The chord is always equal to the radius of the circle.\",\"The segment joining the centre to the midpoint of its chord is perpendicular to the chord.\",\"The midpoint of a chord is always the centre of the circle.\",\"All chords in a circle are parallel to each other.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states this property as the result of the activity.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is mentioned in the activity to check if ∠APO or ∠BPO are right angles?\",\"options\":[\"Compass\",\"Divider\",\"Protractor\",\"Ruler\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using a set-square or protractor to check the angles.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If P is the midpoint of chord AB, which of these statements is correct regarding the lengths of the segments?\",\"options\":[\"AP = PB\",\"AP = 2 × AB\",\"PB = 2 × AB\",\"AP + PB = OP\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"By definition, a midpoint divides a segment into two equal parts.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the property, the segment joining the centre and the midpoint of a chord is _____ to the chord.\",\"options\":[\"parallel\",\"perpendicular\",\"congruent\",\"adjacent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that the segment is perpendicular to the chord.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If chord AB is 14 cm long and P is its midpoint, what is the length of segment AP?\",\"options\":[\"7 cm\",\"14 cm\",\"28 cm\",\"3.5 cm\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The midpoint P divides the 14 cm chord into two equal segments of 7 cm each.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If ∠BPO is a right angle as per the activity, what is its degree measure?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"90°\",\"180°\",\"270°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A right angle always measures exactly 90 degrees.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if P is the midpoint of chord AB, what is the measure of ∠BPO based on the property?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"120°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The property states the segment joining the centre to the midpoint is perpendicular, forming a 90° angle.\",\"topic\":\"Activity II\",\"p