orrect sequence of steps for the activity to observe Monerans from curd?\",\"options\":[\"Dilute curd, add water, observe under low power\",\"Take curd on slide, dilute, add cover slip, observe under high power\",\"Dry the curd, add cover slip, observe under high power\",\"Boil the curd, dilute, observe under high power\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity steps are: take a drop of curd, dilute with water, keep a cover slip, and observe under high power.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 1: Monera\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a primary characteristic of organisms in Kingdom Protista?\",\"options\":[\"They are multicellular organisms\",\"They are single-celled organisms\",\"They lack a nucleus\",\"They do not have a nuclear membrane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Protista are single-celled organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following organisms is an example of an autotrophic Protist?\",\"options\":[\"Amoeba\",\"Paramoecium\",\"Euglena\",\"Plasmodium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Euglena and Volvox as examples of autotrophs.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What structure is described as \'whip-like\' for locomotion in Protists?\",\"options\":[\"Pseudopodia\",\"Cilia\",\"Flagella\",\"Chloroplast\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies \'whip-like flagella\' are used for locomotion.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are Euglena and Volvox classified as autotrophs?\",\"options\":[\"They move using pseudopodia\",\"They contain chloroplasts\",\"They are multicellular\",\"They lack a nuclear membrane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions autotrophs like Euglena and Volvox contain chloroplasts.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What defines the nucleus of a Protist cell?\",\"options\":[\"It is scattered in the cytoplasm\",\"It is absent in single-celled organisms\",\"It is well-defined and enclosed in a nuclear membrane\",\"It is only present in heterotrophs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states Protista have a well-defined nucleus enclosed in a nuclear membrane.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these structures is described as \'hair-like\' for locomotion?\",\"options\":[\"Flagella\",\"Pseudopodia\",\"Cilia\",\"Tentacles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text refers to \'hair-like cilia\' for locomotion.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student observes a microbe in pond water that has an irregular shape and is motile, which organism is it most likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"Volvox\",\"Euglena\",\"Amoeba\",\"Paramoecium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity describes motile microbes in irregular shapes in pond water as amoebae.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist discovers a new Protist that uses whip-like structures to move. Based on the text, what is this structure called?\",\"options\":[\"Cilia\",\"Flagella\",\"Pseudopodia\",\"Chloroplast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies whip-like structures for locomotion as flagella.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following pairs are both categorized as heterotrophs in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Euglena and Volvox\",\"Amoeba and Euglena\",\"Amoeba and Paramoecium\",\"Volvox and Paramoecium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Amoeba and Paramoecium as examples of heterotrophs.\",\"topic\":\"Kingdom 2. Protista\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the characteristics provided, what is the fundamental difference between the nutrition of Volvox and Paramoecium?\",\"options\":[\"Volvox is single-celled, Paramoecium is n