r\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines bacteria as prokaryotic and protozoans as having eukaryotic cells.\",\"topic\":\"Bacteria and Protozoa\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where are free-living protozoans like Amoeba and Paramoecium typically found?\",\"options\":[\"Only in the body of other organisms\",\"In dirty water\",\"Only in sea water\",\"Inside bacterial colonies\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Amoeba and Paramoecium are free-living in dirty water.\",\"topic\":\"Bacteria and Protozoa\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do bacteria typically increase their population?\",\"options\":[\"By forming colonies only\",\"By autotrophic nutrition\",\"By simple binary fission\",\"By locomotion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that bacteria reproduce by simple binary fission.\",\"topic\":\"Bacteria and Protozoa\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following organisms is correctly matched with the disease it causes?\",\"options\":[\"Euglena - Malaria\",\"Plasmodium vivax - Malaria\",\"Entamoeba histolytica - Simple cell division\",\"Paramoecium - Amoebiasis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Plasmodium vivax causes malaria.\",\"topic\":\"Bacteria and Protozoa\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate size range of Fungi according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"10 nm to 100 nm\",\"10 µm to 100 µm\",\"1 µm to 10 µm\",\"100 µm to 500 µm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the size of fungi is approximately 10 µm to 100 µm.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a fungus mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Chlorella\",\"Chlamydomonas\",\"Baker\'s yeast\",\"Bacteriophage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Baker\'s yeast, Candida, and Mushroom are listed as examples of fungi.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does it mean when fungi are described as \'Saprotrophic\'?\",\"options\":[\"They produce their own food through photosynthesis.\",\"They absorb food from decaying organic matter.\",\"They live only inside living host cells.\",\"They are aquatic organisms that need chloroplasts.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Saprotrophic organisms absorb their food from decaying organic matter.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Fungi reproduce asexually by cell division or by which other process?\",\"options\":[\"Photosynthesis\",\"Budding\",\"Infection\",\"Chloroplast synthesis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions fungi reproduce sexually and asexually by cell division or by budding.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where are Algae primarily found according to their characteristics?\",\"options\":[\"On dead bodies of animals\",\"In dry desert soil\",\"In aquatic environments\",\"Inside human blood cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Algae are aquatic.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which cell organelle helps Algae carry out photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Mitochondria\",\"Protein coat\",\"Chloroplast\",\"DNA molecule\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Photosynthesis in algae is carried out with the help of chloroplast present in the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student observes a unicellular, eukaryotic, autotrophic organism in a pond, it is most likely to be classified as:\",\"options\":[\"Fungi\",\"Algae\",\"Virus\",\"Bacteria\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Algae are described as eukaryotic, unicellular, and autotrophic organisms found in water.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate size of viruses?\",\"options\":[\"10 µm to 100 µm\",\"10 nm to 100 nm\",\"1 mm to 10 mm\",\"100 nm to 500 nm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states viruses are approximately 10 nm to 100 nm in size.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A virus is described as a long molecule of DNA or RNA covered by a:\",\"options\":[\"Cellulose wall\",\"Chloroplast\",\"Protein coat\",\"Decaying matter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Virus structure consists of a long molecule of DNA or RNA covered by a protein coat.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the size of a virus compare to that of bacteria?\",\"options\":[\"Viruses are 10 to 100 times larger than bacteria.\",\"Viruses are the same size as bacteria.\",\"Viruses are 10 to 100 times smaller than bacteria.\",\"Viruses are 1000 times larger than bacteria.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states viruses are extremely minute, i.e., 10 to 100 times smaller than bacteria.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following viruses is known to infect cattle?\",\"options\":[\"Influenza virus\",\"Tobacco mosaic virus\",\"Picoma virus\",\"Bacteriophage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Cattle- picoma virus\' as an example.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is required to see viruses?\",\"options\":[\"Simple microscope\",\"Compound microscope\",\"Electron microscope\",\"Magnifying glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because they are extremely minute, viruses can be seen only with an electron microscope.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the host cell after a virus creates numerous replicas of itself?\",\"options\":[\"The host cell grows larger.\",\"The host cell is destroyed.\",\"The host cell becomes autotrophic.\",\"The host cell turns into a fungus.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"After creating replicas, viruses destroy the host cell and become free to infect new cells.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist finds a virus attacking a bacterial cell. What is this virus called?\",\"options\":[\"HIV\",\"Bacteriophage\",\"Wilt virus\",\"Candida\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Viruses that attack bacteria are called Bacteriophages.\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is a key nutritional difference between Fungi and Algae?\",\"options\":[\"Fungi are saprotrophic, while Algae are autotrophic.\",\"Fungi are autotrophic, while Algae are saprotrophic.\",\"Both Fungi and Algae are autotrophic.\",\"Both Fungi and Algae are saprotrophic.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Fungi absorb food from decaying matter (saprotrophic), whereas Algae perform photosynthesis (autotrophic).\",\"topic\":\"Fungi, Algae and Viruses\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22152,57,8,'Science','en','Health and Diseases','[\"Health is a state of complete physical, mental and social well‑being, not just absence of disease.\",\"A disease is a disturbance in normal physiological or psychological processes of the body.\",\"Diseases are classified by duration: acute (short) or chronic (long).\",\"They are also classified by cause: hereditary, acquired, infectious or non‑infectious.\",\"Infectious diseases spread through contaminated air, water, food or vectors such as mosquitoes, flies or animals.\",\"A pathogen is any microorganism (bacteria, virus, parasite) that can cause disease.\",\"Common infectious diseases: tuberculosis, hepatitis, malaria, dengue, cholera, typhoid, dysentery, swine flu, AIDS, rabies, elephantiasis, plague, chickenpox.\",\"Non‑infectious diseases include cancer, diabetes, heart disease, chronic respiratory disease, osteoporosis, etc.\",\"Key symptoms of dengue: acute fever, headache, vomiting, eye‑socket pain, low platelet count leading to internal bleeding.\",\"Symptoms of malaria: fever, chills, headache, body aches, nausea, vomiting, anemia.\",\"Symptoms of HIV\\/AIDS: weight loss, fever, night sweats, chronic infections, opportunistic infections.\",\"Symptoms of rabies: fever, hydrophobia (fear of water), aggression, confusion, paralysis.\",\"Preventive measures for dengue: eliminate standing water, use mosquito nets, insecticides, community clean‑up.\",\"Preventive measures for malaria: use insecticide‑treated nets, indoor residual spraying, early diagnosis and treatment.\",\"Preventive measures for swine flu: avoid contact with sick pigs, practice hand hygiene, vaccinate if available.\",\"Preventive measures for HIV: safe sex, avoid sharing needles, use condoms, get tested.\",\"Cancer prevention: avoid tobacco, limit alcohol, eat fruits and vegetables, exercise, maintain healthy weight.\",\"Diabetes prevention: balanced diet, regular exercise, avoid excess sugar, maintain healthy weight.\",\"Heart disease prevention: avoid smoking, control blood pressure, manage cholesterol, exercise regularly.\",\"Vaccination protects against many infectious diseases; schedule varies by age and region.\",\"Generic medicines are equivalent to branded drugs but cheaper; they help make treatment affordable.\",\"Lifestyle factors such as sleep, diet, exercise and stress management influence disease risk.\",\"Regular health check‑ups and early detection improve treatment outcomes.\",\"Public health measures: clean water, proper sanitation, waste disposal, vaccination drives, health education.\",\"Community involvement in mosquito control and waste management reduces disease spread.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, health is a state of complete physical, mental and ______ well-beingness.\",\"options\":[\"economical\",\"social\",\"environmental\",\"educational\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines health as a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-beingness.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as a \'condition of disturbances in physiological or psychological processes of body\'?\",\"options\":[\"Health\",\"Treatment\",\"Disease\",\"Medicine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines disease as the condition of disturbances in physiological or psychological processes of the body.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what specific activity is being performed to monitor the person\'s health?\",\"options\":[\"Checking blood pressure\",\"Checking fever\",\"Measuring height\",\"Testing eyesight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The caption for the figure (2.1) in the text is \'Checking Fever\'.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/016_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the definition provided, a person is considered healthy only when they have ______.\",\"options\":[\"no physical illness\",\"only mental peace\",\"complete physical, mental and social well-beingness\",\"regular access to medicines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Health is not merely the absence of disease but a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-beingness.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are diseases classified into different types in scientific study?\",\"options\":[\"To make them sound more complex\",\"To bring coordination in scientific study\",\"To increase the cost of medicines\",\"To prevent doctors from getting confused\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that diseases are classified to bring coordination in their scientific study.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is mentioned as a disease in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"Feverishness\",\"Down’s syndrome\",\"Headache\",\"Tiredness\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists diabetes, common cold, asthma, Down’s syndrome, and heart disease as examples.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the use of a thermometer indicates that the person is likely suffering from a disturbance in which process?\",\"options\":[\"Social process\",\"Physiological process\",\"Economic process\",\"Mental process\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fever is a disturbance in the physiological processes of the body.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/016_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is physically fit but is unable to interact properly with others in society, which aspect of their \'health\' is compromised according to the definition?\",\"options\":[\"Physical well-beingness\",\"Social well-beingness\",\"Physiological process\",\"Genetic well-beingness\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Health includes social well-beingness; if a person cannot interact socially, that aspect of health is affected.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The text mentions that \'each disease has its own specific ______.\'\",\"options\":[\"doctor\",\"medicine name\",\"symptoms\",\"cost\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Each disease has its own specific symptoms.\'\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student feels mentally stressed and anxious during exams but has no physical illness. According to the textbook\'s definition of health, is this student \'healthy\'?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, because there is no disease.\",\"Yes, because they can still go to school.\",\"No, because health includes mental well-beingness.\",\"No, because they are not taking medicines.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Health is a state of complete mental well-beingness; mental stress indicates a lack of complete health.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what distinguishes one disease from another?\",\"options\":[\"Only the name of the disease\",\"Only the age of the patient\",\"Reasons and symptoms\",\"The hospital where it is treated\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Reasons and symptoms of all these diseases are different.\'\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these categories of disturbances is specifically mentioned in the definition of disease?\",\"options\":[\"Physiological or psychological\",\"Social or economical\",\"Environmental or physical\",\"Nutritional or social\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The definition of disease refers to disturbances in physiological or psychological processes.\",\"topic\":\"Health and Disease\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are diseases that spread through contaminated air, water, food, or vectors called?\",\"options\":[\"Hereditary diseases\",\"Non-infectious diseases\",\"Infectious diseases\",\"Chronic diseases\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines infectious diseases as those spread through contaminated air, water, food, or vectors.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which pathogen is responsible for causing Tuberculosis?\",\"options\":[\"Salmonella typhi\",\"Mycobacterium tuberculosis\",\"Vibrio cholerae\",\"Entamoeba histolytica\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the pathogen for Tuberculosis.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to small droplets released by a patient in the air?\",\"options\":[\"They settle on the ground within few seconds\",\"They turn into large droplets immediately\",\"They are vaporised into fine mist\",\"They disappear instantly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that small droplets are vaporised into fine mist and remain in the air for minutes to hours.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A patient shows symptoms of anorexia, yellow urine, and grey stool. Which disease are they likely suffering from?\",\"options\":[\"Cholera\",\"Dysentery\",\"Typhoid\",\"Hepatitis\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Yellow urine and grey stool are specific symptoms listed for Hepatitis (Jaundice).\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a recommended treatment for Dysentery to prevent dehydration?\",\"options\":[\"BCG vaccine\",\"ORS consumption\",\"DOT medication\",\"Blood transfusion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists ORS consumption under prevention and treatment for Dysentery.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common mode of infection for Cholera, Typhoid, and Dysentery?\",\"options\":[\"Sharing of needles\",\"Spitting by patient\",\"Contaminated food and water\",\"Blood transfusion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows that contaminated food and water is the mode of infection for all three diseases.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which pathogen listed for Dysentery is a protozoan (implied by the name Entamoeba)?\",\"options\":[\"Shigella\",\"Bacilli\",\"Entamoeba histolytica\",\"Vibrio cholerae\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Entamoeba histolytica is listed as one of the pathogens for Dysentery.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the case of Typhoid, the fever can rise up to which temperature?\",\"options\":[\"100°F\",\"102°F\",\"104°F\",\"106°F\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions a fever up to 104°F as a symptom of Typhoid.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is \'proper disposal of sewage\' specifically listed as a prevention for Typhoid?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is spread through air\",\"Because the pathogen is carried through contaminated water and food affected by waste\",\"Because it prevents the growth of Mycobacterium\",\"Because it is a skin disease\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Typhoid is spread through contaminated food\\/water, making sewage management a critical preventive measure.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which disease requires the patient to be isolated to prevent the spread through air and spitting?\",\"options\":[\"Hepatitis\",\"Tuberculosis\",\"Cholera\",\"Dysentery\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Isolation of patient is listed under prevention and treatment for Tuberculosis.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Vibrio cholerae, the pathogen for Cholera, belongs to which category of organisms?\",\"options\":[\"Virus\",\"Bacterium\",\"Protozoa\",\"Fungi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions Vibrio cholerae (bacterium).\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symptom is common to both Hepatitis and Typhoid according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Grey stool\",\"Rash on abdomen\",\"Anorexia\",\"Cramps in legs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Anorexia is listed as a symptom for both Hepatitis and Typhoid.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How do large droplets released by a sick person behave in the air?\",\"options\":[\"They remain in the air for several hours\",\"They settle on the ground within few seconds\",\"They vaporise into mist immediately\",\"They travel long distances through wind\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that large droplets settle on the ground within few seconds.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which disease is associated with the symptom \'rash on abdomen\'?\",\"options\":[\"Tuberculosis\",\"Hepatitis\",\"Cholera\",\"Typhoid\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists \'rash on abdomen\' under the symptoms for Typhoid.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/017_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table in the given figure, which disease can be prevented by the BCG vaccine?\",\"options\":[\"Tuberculosis\",\"Hepatitis\",\"Dysentery\",\"Cholera\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table shows BCG vaccine under the Prevention and Treatment column for Tuberculosis.\",\"topic\":\"Infectious Diseases\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/017_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which species of mosquito is responsible for spreading Dengue?\",\"options\":[\"Anopheles\",\"Culex\",\"Aedes aegypti\",\"Plasmodium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, Dengue is spread by the Aedes aegypti mosquito.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Dengue is caused by the DEN-1 - 4 virus, which belongs to which specific type?\",\"options\":[\"Retrovirus\",\"Flavivirus\",\"Bacteriophage\",\"Influenza virus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that the Dengue virus belongs to the type-flavivirus.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is described as a \'very prominent symptom\' of Dengue in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Stomach ache\",\"Pains in eye socket\",\"High blood pressure\",\"Dry cough\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that pains in the eye socket is a very prominent symptom of Dengue.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the blood components during a Dengue infection that may lead to internal hemorrhage?\",\"options\":[\"Increase in red blood cells\",\"Decrease in white blood cells\",\"Decrease in platelet count\",\"Increase in hemoglobin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a decrease in platelet count may lead to internal hemorrhage in Dengue patients.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the primary reason the discarded tires are shown as a hazard?\",\"options\":[\"They cause air pollution\",\"They collect water where mosquitoes lay eggs\",\"They are made of toxic rubber\",\"They block the walking path\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates that water-logged places like tires allow mosquitoes to lay eggs and increase their population.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/018_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which stage of the mosquito\'s development is shown in the middle circle between the water and the adult mosquito?\",\"options\":[\"Egg\",\"Pupa\",\"Larva\",\"Caterpillar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the life cycle stages of a mosquito, where the middle stage in water is the larva.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/018_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which of these household items is NOT shown as a potential breeding site for mosquitoes?\",\"options\":[\"Flower vase\",\"Open bucket\",\"Discarded tin cans\",\"Closed cupboard\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a flower vase, bucket, and tin cans as breeding sites, but does not show a closed cupboard.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/017_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, in what type of water does the Aedes mosquito typically grow?\",\"options\":[\"Dirty sewage water\",\"Clean water\",\"Salt water\",\"Flowing river water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that Anopheles and Aedes grow in clean water.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which mosquito species is responsible for spreading Elephantiasis?\",\"options\":[\"Female Anopheles\",\"Aedes aegypti\",\"Female Culex\",\"Male Culex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that elephantiasis is spread by female Culex mosquitoes.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a locality has a lot of open, dirty sewage water, which disease is more likely to spread based on the mosquito\'s breeding habits?\",\"options\":[\"Malaria\",\"Dengue\",\"Elephantiasis\",\"Yellow Fever\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Culex (which spreads elephantiasis) grows in dirty, sewage water.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the breeding habits of Anopheles and Culex mosquitoes as mentioned in the text.\",\"options\":[\"Both grow in dirty water\",\"Anopheles grows in clean water, while Culex grows in dirty water\",\"Anopheles grows in dirty water, while Culex grows in clean water\",\"Both grow in clean water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Anopheles grows in clean water while Culex grows in dirty, sewage water.\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the textbook suggest that mosquito populations increase in water-logged places?\",\"options\":[\"Mosquitoes drink more water there\",\"Mosquitoes lay their eggs in these places\",\"Water-logged places are cooler for adult mosquitoes\",\"Mosquitoes find more food in water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Mosquitoes lay the eggs in water-logged places and thereby their population increases.\'\",\"topic\":\"Dengue\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific virus is responsible for causing Swine Flu?\",\"options\":[\"Influenza A (H1N1)\",\"H5N1 Virus\",\"Rhino Virus\",\"Corona Virus\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Swine flu is caused by the virus influenza A (H1N1).\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which country was the first case of Swine Flu reported in 2009?\",\"options\":[\"India\",\"USA\",\"Mexico\",\"China\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the \'Do you know?\' section, the first case of swine flu was reported in Mexico in 2009.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the National Institute of Virology (NIV) located?\",\"options\":[\"Delhi\",\"Mumbai\",\"Pune\",\"Mexico\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that diagnostic facilities are available at the National Institute of Virology (NIV) in Pune.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Through which of the following does the infection of Swine Flu occur?\",\"options\":[\"Contaminated water only\",\"Pigs and humans\",\"Mosquito bites\",\"Soil and plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that infection for swine flu occurs through pigs and humans.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a symptom of Swine Flu as mentioned in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"High blood pressure\",\"Difficulty in breathing\",\"Loss of vision\",\"Skin rashes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Difficulty in breathing is listed as one of the symptoms of Swine Flu.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary medium through which Swine Flu viruses spread from an infected person?\",\"options\":[\"Sweat and skin contact\",\"Blood transfusion\",\"Secretions of nose, throat, and saliva\",\"Hair and nails\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that viruses spread through secretions of the nose, throat, and saliva.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A person working closely with livestock, specifically pigs, should be cautious of Swine Flu because:\",\"options\":[\"Pigs are the only carriers of the virus\",\"Persons who come in contact with pigs can contract this disease\",\"The virus only spreads in muddy areas\",\"Pigs spread the virus through their skin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that persons who come in contact with pigs can contract this disease.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a patient in North India needs a diagnosis for Swine Flu, which institute in Delhi provides this facility?\",\"options\":[\"NIV\",\"NICD\",\"IIT\",\"AIIMS\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the National Institute of Communicable Diseases (NICD) in Delhi as a diagnostic facility.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the diagnostic procedure described, what must a doctor collect from a patient to confirm Swine Flu?\",\"options\":[\"A blood sample\",\"A bone marrow sample\",\"Liquid from the throat\",\"A urine sample\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that for diagnosis, liquid from the throat of the patient is sent to the laboratory.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which combination of symptoms would most likely lead a doctor to suspect Swine Flu according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sore throat and body pains\",\"Stomach ache and headache\",\"Itching and swelling\",\"Dizziness and nausea\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Sore throat, body pains\' and \'Difficulty in breathing\' as the symptoms.\",\"topic\":\"Swine Flu\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the full form of HIV as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Human Immunodeficiency Virus\",\"Human Immunity Virus\",\"Highly Infectious Virus\",\"Human Internal Virus\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that AIDS is caused by HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus).\",\"topic\":\"AIDS\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific test is used for the proper diagnosis of AIDS?\",\"options\":[\"Widal test\",\"ELISA test\",\"Biopsy\",\"Blood sugar test\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the ELISA test is used for the proper diagnosis of AIDS.\",\"topic\":\"AIDS\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which species was HIV first reported?\",\"options\":[\"African sp