ry, Open heart surgery, Heart transplant, Installation of stents, and Installation of pace maker; it does not mention blood donation.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/021_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which treatment involves the use of a specific electronic device to regulate the heart?\",\"options\":[\"Installation of stents\",\"Installation of pace maker\",\"By-Pass surgery\",\"Angioplasty\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure lists \'Installation of pace maker\' as one of the treatments for heart diseases.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/021_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you are performing C. O. L. S. on a patient, what is the correct rate of chest compressions per minute?\",\"options\":[\"60 to 80 strokes\",\"80 to 100 strokes\",\"100 to 120 strokes\",\"120 to 150 strokes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies the rate should be 100 to 120 strokes per minute.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When providing first aid for a suspected heart attack, how should the patient be positioned?\",\"options\":[\"Sitting upright on a soft chair\",\"Lying on the back on a hard surface\",\"Lying on the stomach on a bed\",\"Standing with support against a wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to keep the patient lying on the back in a horizontal position on a hard surface.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A person is found collapsed. After calling 108, what is the next immediate step to check the patient\'s state?\",\"options\":[\"Check consciousness by shaking the shoulders\",\"Start pressing the centre of the thorax\",\"Give the patient water to drink\",\"Perform a heart transplant\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Check the consciousness of patient by shaking the shoulders\' after calling for an ambulance.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which conclusion can be drawn about the treatment of diseases?\",\"options\":[\"Mantras are more effective than medicine\",\"Medical treatment is the proper way to cure diseases\",\"Magic tricks can help in emergency heart cases\",\"Scientific reasons are not necessary for diagnosis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that diseases can be cured by proper medical treatment and not by mantras or magic.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a \'hard surface\' specified for performing C. O. L. S. rather than a soft bed?\",\"options\":[\"To make the patient feel more uncomfortable\",\"To ensure the chest compressions are effective\",\"To prevent the patient from falling asleep\",\"To make it easier for the ambulance to arrive\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A hard surface is necessary so that the force of the compression actually presses the thorax rather than being absorbed by a soft mattress.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following groups contains only factors that increase the risk of heart attack according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Diabetes, heredity, mental stress\",\"Yoga, garlic, angioplasty\",\"Aloe vera, turmeric, ginger\",\"Ambulance, stents, pace maker\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Diabetes, heredity, and mental stress are listed as reasons (risk factors) for heart attack.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Diseases\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Generic medicines are also known as ______ medicines.\",\"options\":[\"Special\",\"General\",\"Branded\",\"Patent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that generic medicines are also known as general medicines.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Overdose of which type of medicine may damage the nervous system and liver?\",\"options\":[\"Antibiotics\",\"Vitamins\",\"Pain killers\",\"Generic medicines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, overdose of pain killers may damage the nervous system, excretory system, and liver.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the production cost of generic medicines very low?\",\"options\":[\"They use low-quality ingredients\",\"They are manufactured in small quantities\",\"There are fewer expenses on research\",\"They do not require any packaging\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions production cost is low due to fewer expenses on research as the formula is readily available.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the quality status of generic medicines compared to branded medicines?\",\"options\":[\"They are of lower quality\",\"They are at par in quality\",\"They are of superior quality\",\"Their quality is not tested\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that these medicines are at par in quality with branded medicines.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A person takes a medicine without a doctor\'s prescription and experiences a stomach ache. This is an example of ______.\",\"options\":[\"Proper use of medicine\",\"Misuse of medicines\",\"Generic medicine benefits\",\"Patent medicine research\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Taking medicines without a prescription is defined as misuse of medicines in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these mobile apps can be used to obtain generic medicines?\",\"options\":[\"Healthkart\",\"WhatsApp\",\"Facebook\",\"Instagram\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Healthkart and Jan Samadhan as apps to obtain generic medicines.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following symptoms is specifically associated with an overdose of antibiotics according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Liver damage\",\"Nervous system damage\",\"White patches on tongue\",\"Excretory system damage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists white patches on the tongue as a symptom of antibiotic overdose, while the others relate to painkillers.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Generic medicines are distributed without a patent because ______.\",\"options\":[\"They are not effective\",\"Their formula is already available\",\"They are only for poor people\",\"They are not manufactured in factories\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains they are distributed without a patent because their formula is readily available.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best explains why generic medicines are a suitable alternative for poor people?\",\"options\":[\"They do not require a doctor\'s prescription\",\"They have no side effects even if overdosed\",\"They are affordable due to low production costs\",\"They are only available through mobile apps\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text suggests generic medicines as an alternative for poor people who cannot afford costly medicines.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the nervous system and liver, which other system is damaged by an overdose of painkillers?\",\"options\":[\"Respiratory system\",\"Excretory system\",\"Circulatory system\",\"Skeletal system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that overdose of pain killers may damage the nervous system, excretory system, and liver.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the label indicates that these medicines belong to which category?\",\"options\":[\"Generic medicines\",\"Branded medicines\",\"Expensive medicines\",\"Patented medicines\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The label in the figure clearly identifies the items as \'Generic medicines\'.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/022_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to buy the type of medicines shown in the given figure using ICT, which app should they download?\",\"options\":[\"Jan Samadhan\",\"Google Maps\",\"YouTube\",\"Twitter\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Jan Samadhan as a mobile app used to obtain generic medicines, which are shown in the figure.\",\"topic\":\"Medicines and Generic Medicines\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/022_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"On which date did the Government of India declare the Pradhan Mantri Jan Aushadhi Yojana?\",\"options\":[\"1st June 2015\",\"1st July 2015\",\"14th June 2015\",\"7th April 2015\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the Government of India declared the Pradhan Mantri Jan Aushadhi Yojana on 1st July 2015.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which day is celebrated as World Health Day?\",\"options\":[\"14th June\",\"29th September\",\"7th April\",\"14th November\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists 7th April as World Health Day.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, what does \'lifestyle\' include?\",\"options\":[\"Only physical exercise and sleep\",\"Only the type of food consumed\",\"Daily routine and the type of food\",\"Only the medicines and vaccinations taken\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that lifestyle includes the daily routine and the type of food.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Under whose supervision should pranayam and yogasanas be performed?\",\"options\":[\"Medical doctors\",\"Expert supervision\",\"School teachers\",\"Self-study from videos\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that pranayam and yogasanas should be performed under expert supervision.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of vaccination as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To cure junk food habits\",\"To improve meal timings\",\"To prevent infectious diseases\",\"To reduce the price of medicines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that vaccination is important to prevent infectious diseases.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of medicines used in America are generic medicines?\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"70%\",\"80%\",\"90%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in America, 80% of medicines used are generic.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the importance of blood donation is highlighted. According to the text, how many lives can a person save by donating blood four times a year?\",\"options\":[\"3 lives\",\"4 lives\",\"8 lives\",\"12 lives\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that one can save the life of twelve persons by donating blood four times a year.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/023_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, a drop of blood is shown. According to the text, one unit of this donated blood can fulfill the need of three persons by providing which specific components?\",\"options\":[\"Plasma, Glucose and Hemoglobin\",\"RBCs, WBCs and platelets\",\"Oxygen, Nutrients and Water\",\"Antibodies, Hormones and RBCs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that one unit can fulfill the need for RBCs, WBCs, and platelets.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/023_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why are generic medicines sold at high prices in India despite being exported on a large scale by Indian companies?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are of better quality than exported ones\",\"Because they are sold under brand names\",\"Because of the lack of Jan Aushadhi Stores\",\"Because they are not used in America\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that generic medicines are sold under brand names with high prices in India.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a habit that contributes to people getting sick frequently nowadays?\",\"options\":[\"Going to bed early at night\",\"Frequent changes in meal timings\",\"Exercising as per one\'s capacity\",\"Waking up early in the morning\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists frequent changes in meal timings as a habit that causes people to get sick frequently.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the recommended limit for physical exercise according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"At least two hours every day\",\"Only under the supervision of a doctor\",\"As per one\'s own capacity\",\"Only through yogasanas and pranayam\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that physical exercise should be as per one\'s own capacity.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"World Diabetes Day is celebrated on which of the following dates?\",\"options\":[\"14th June\",\"29th September\",\"14th November\",\"8th April\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists 14th November as World Diabetes Day.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person wants to follow a \'right lifestyle\' to lower the frequency of becoming sick, which combination should they include?\",\"options\":[\"Junk food and sufficient sleep\",\"Physical exercise and late-night sleeping\",\"Sufficient sleep, right food, and yogasanas\",\"Generic medicines and frequent meal changes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says a right lifestyle must include sufficient sleep, right food, yogasanas, pranayam and physical exercise.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the economic benefit of using generic medicines as seen in the example of America?\",\"options\":[\"It increases the export scale of Indian companies\",\"It saves hundreds of billions of money\",\"It increases the price of brand name medicines\",\"It helps in launching more Jan Aushadhi Stores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the use of generic medicines in America, hundreds of billions of money is saved.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a component of a right lifestyle?\",\"options\":[\"Sufficient sleep\",\"Consumption of junk food\",\"Pranayam\",\"Physical exercise\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions junk food as a reason for getting sick, not as a component of a right lifestyle.\",\"topic\":\"Lifestyle and Health Awareness\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22153,57,8,'Science','en','Force and Pressure','[\"Newton’s first law: an object stays at rest or moves with constant speed unless a net force acts on it.\",\"A force is an interaction that changes the motion of an object; it can be contact (touch) or non‑contact (magnetic, electric, gravitational).\",\"Contact forces act through direct touch: friction, normal force, tension, applied force, muscular force.\",\"Non‑contact forces act at a distance: gravity, magnetism, electrostatic force, air pressure.\",\"A force is a vector: it has magnitude and direction; its SI unit is the Newton (N).\",\"Pressure is the force applied per unit area; it is a scalar quantity.\",\"Formula: Pressure (P) = Force (F) ÷ Area (A). Units: N\\/m², called Pascal (Pa).\",\"If the area over which a force is applied increases, pressure decreases for the same force; if area decreases, pressure increases.\",\"The weight of an object is the gravitational force acting on it: W = mg (m = mass, g ≈ 9.8 m\\/s²).\",\"Buoyant force is the upward force exerted by a fluid on an immersed object; it equals the weight of the fluid displaced (Archimedes’ principle).\",\"An object floats if buoyant force > weight, sinks if buoyant force < weight, and stays at a fixed depth if they are equal.\",\"Atmospheric pressure is the weight of the air column above a unit area; at sea level it is about 101.3 kPa (1 atm).\",\"Friction opposes motion; kinetic friction acts when objects slide, static friction when they are at rest.\",\"Inertia is the tendency of an object to keep its state of motion; it is the property that resists changes in speed or direction.\",\"Examples of contact forces: pushing a box, pulling a rope, friction between shoes and ground.\",\"Examples of non‑contact forces: gravity pulling a falling apple, magnet pulling a nail, electrostatic attraction between a comb and hair.\",\"Pressure in a fluid is the same in all directions at a point; this isotropy is why a submerged object feels equal pressure from all sides.\",\"The pressure exerted by a fluid on a surface depends on the depth of the fluid; deeper points have higher pressure.\",\"When a solid is placed in water, the water exerts a pressure that can be larger or smaller than the solid’s weight, determining whether it floats or sinks.\",\"The pressure of a gas or liquid is independent of the shape of the container; it depends only on the amount of fluid and its density.\",\"The SI unit of pressure is the Pascal, 1 Pa = 1 N\\/m².\",\"A larger contact area reduces pressure for a given force, which is why camel feet spread over a wide area to avoid sinking into sand.\",\"When a vehicle has many wheels, each wheel carries less load, reducing the pressure on the road and preventing damage.\",\"The force of gravity is always a non‑contact force acting between masses.\",\"The normal force is the reaction force exerted by a surface to support an object; it acts perpendicular to the surface.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to Newton’s first law of motion, what happens to an object in motion when no force is acting on it?\",\"options\":[\"It comes to an immediate stop\",\"It continues to move with the same speed and direction\",\"It changes its direction but keeps the same speed\",\"It increases its speed gradually\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Newton’s first law states that an object in motion continues to move with the same speed and direction when no force is acting on it.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what type of force is being applied by the man to move the car and the boy to kick the ball?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Electrostatic force\",\"Contact force\",\"Non contact force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows actions where force acts through direct contact, which is defined as contact force.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/024_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific type of contact force is used by the master to pull the reluctant dog?\",\"options\":[\"Gravitational force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Electrostatic force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Muscular force is a contact force applied with the help of muscles for actions like pulling.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/024_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is defined as a force that is applied between two objects even if they are not in contact?\",\"options\":[\"Frictional force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Non contact force\",\"Mechanical force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A force applied between two objects even if they are not in contact is called a non contact force.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do small pieces of paper stick to a comb that has been rubbed against hair?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the force of gravity between them\",\"Due to the magnetic poles of the paper\",\"Due to electrostatic charge and induced opposite charge\",\"Due to the frictional force between the comb and paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The comb has an electrostatic charge and induces an opposite charge on the paper, causing them to stick.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A ball pushed on a table eventually stops because of which force?\",\"options\":[\"Gravitational force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Frictional force\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Objects in motion stop due to the force of friction between the object and the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does the frictional force always act on an object in motion?\",\"options\":[\"In the same direction as the motion\",\"In the direction opposite to the motion\",\"In a downward direction towards the earth\",\"In a perpendicular direction to the motion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Frictional force acts in the direction opposite to the direction of motion.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen to a moving object in the complete absence of frictional force?\",\"options\":[\"The object would stop immediately\",\"The object would remain in motion\",\"The object would change its shape\",\"The object would move in circles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the absence of frictional force, the object would have remained in motion.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it difficult to walk on a street where there is a banana peel or mud?\",\"options\":[\"Because the gravitational force increases\",\"Because the muscular force is not applied\",\"Because the frictional force is reduced\",\"Because the electrostatic force becomes active\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Slipping on banana peels or mud occurs due to reduced friction.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a non contact force acting on an object falling from a tree?\",\"options\":[\"Frictional force\",\"Force of gravity\",\"Muscular force\",\"Mechanical force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Objects are attracted to the earth due to the force of gravity, which acts without contact.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with two floating plastic bottles and magnets, why do the bottles move towards each other?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the direct contact between bottles\",\"Because unlike poles of magnets attract each other\",\"Because of the water pressure in the tray\",\"Because of the weight of the lids\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If the north pole of one magnet is near the south pole of the other, they head towards each other due to attraction between unlike poles.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following lists contains only non contact forces?\",\"options\":[\"Muscular force, Frictional force, Magnetic force\",\"Magnetic force, Gravitational force, Electrostatic force\",\"Gravitational force, Muscular force, Electrostatic force\",\"Frictional force, Magnetic force, Gravitational force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Magnetic, gravitational, and electrostatic forces act without contact, making them non contact forces.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary requirement for a force to act between two bodies according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They must be made of iron\",\"They must be moving at high speed\",\"There must be an interaction between them\",\"They must be in direct physical contact\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a force acts on two bodies through an interaction between them.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Lifting, pushing, and pulling are examples of which type of force application?\",\"options\":[\"Electrostatic force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Muscular force\",\"Gravitational force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Muscular force is applied in several cases such as lifting, pushing, and pulling.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment with magnets on bottles, what does the change in motion without direct contact prove?\",\"options\":[\"Magnets only work in water\",\"Magnetic force is a non contact force\",\"Plastic bottles are magnetic\",\"Friction does not exist in water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The observation of motion change without contact means there exists a non contact force between the magnets.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of Force\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Force is expressed in which of the following two terms?\",\"options\":[\"Mass and weight\",\"Speed and velocity\",\"Magnitude and direction\",\"Distance and time\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a force is expressed in magnitude and direction.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What kind of quantity is force according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Scalar quantity\",\"Vector quantity\",\"Chemical quantity\",\"Mass quantity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that force is a vector quantity because it has both magnitude and direction.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with the cardboard box and weighing pans, what happens when equal masses are kept in both pans?\",\"options\":[\"The box moves to the left\",\"The box moves to the right\",\"The box does not move\",\"The box moves upwards\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, if equal masses are kept in both pans, the box does not move on the table.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When equal masses are in the pans, what is the effective force acting on the box?\",\"options\":[\"Equal to the mass\",\"Equal to zero\",\"Double the gravitational force\",\"Equal to the weight of one pan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that balanced forces act on the box with effective force equal to zero.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If several forces are applied on an object in the same direction, the total force acting on it is equal to their ______.\",\"options\":[\"Difference\",\"Product\",\"Division\",\"Addition\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that if forces are in the same direction, a force equal to their addition acts on the object.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result when two forces are applied on one object in opposite directions to each other?\",\"options\":[\"A force equal to their addition acts on the object\",\"A force equal to their difference acts on the object\",\"The object always stays stationary\",\"The forces cancel out to become double\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if two forces are applied in opposite directions, a force equal to their difference acts on the object.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a game of tug of war, if the rope moves in one direction, what does it indicate about the forces?\",\"options\":[\"The forces are balanced\",\"The forces have become unbalanced\",\"No force is being applied\",\"The forces are equal in magnitude\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the rope moves in the direction of higher force when the forces become unbalanced.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of force changing the shape of an object mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Pushing a grain container\",\"Kneading a dough made from flour\",\"Pulling a rope in tug of war\",\"Keeping equal masses in weighing pans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that while kneading dough, it changes its shape when force is applied.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it easier for two persons to push a big grain storage container in the same direction than for one person?\",\"options\":[\"Because the forces are in opposite directions\",\"Because the net force is the difference of their forces\",\"Because the forces add up, making the movement easy\",\"Because the container becomes lighter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states movement is easy when force is applied by both in the same direction due to the addition of forces.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When a force is applied to a stationary object, what changes can occur according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only its mass changes\",\"Its speed and direction change\",\"It always remains stationary\",\"Only its color changes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when a force is applied on a stationary object, it moves, and its speed and direction change.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a rubber band when it is stretched?\",\"options\":[\"It contracts\",\"It expands\",\"It disappears\",\"It changes its mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'Rubber band, when stretched, expands.\'\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If more than one force acts on a body, the effect on the body is due to the ______.\",\"options\":[\"Smallest force\",\"Largest force\",\"Net force\",\"Initial force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if more than one force acts on a body, the effect is due to the net force.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A potter applies force in a specific direction while shaping a pot. This is an example of force being used to:\",\"options\":[\"Increase the mass of the pot\",\"Stop the motion of the pot\",\"Change the shape of the pot\",\"Decrease the gravitational force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the potter as an example of an object changing its shape due to force.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the box experiment, if the box starts moving towards the right pan, what can be concluded?\",\"options\":[\"The left pan has more mass\",\"The right pan has more mass\",\"Both pans have equal mass\",\"No gravitational force is acting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the box starts moving in the direction of the pan that has more mass.\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What force acts on the weighing pans in the experiment described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Electrostatic force\",\"Frictional force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Equal gravitational force acts on both the pans when equal masses are kept in them.\'\",\"topic\":\"Balanced and Unbalanced Force\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the tendency of an object to remain in its existing state called?\",\"options\":[\"Force\",\"Pressure\",\"Inertia\",\"Motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines the tendency of an object to remain in its existing state as inertia.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the absence of an external force, what happens to an object in a stationary state?\",\"options\":[\"It starts moving slowly\",\"It remains in the same state\",\"It changes its direction\",\"It loses its mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, an object in a stationary state remains in that state if no external force is applied.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 1, when the postcard is pushed skillfully, why does the coin fall into the glass?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the force of the push\",\"Due to the movement of the glass\",\"Due to the inertia of the coin\",\"Due to the weight of the postcard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The coin falls into the glass because it tends to remain in its state of rest due to inertia.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 2, what happens when string 2 is pulled with a jerk?\",\"options\":[\"String 1 breaks and the mass falls\",\"String 2 breaks and the mass does not fall\",\"Both strings break simultaneously\",\"The mass moves upwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that pulling string 2 with a jerk causes string 2 to break while the heavy mass does not move.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of an object prevents it from changing its state of rest?\",\"options\":[\"Inertia of motion\",\"Directional inertia\",\"Inertia of the state of rest\",\"External force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The inherent property due to which an object cannot change its state of rest is called inertia of the state of rest.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a revolving electric fan continue to move for some time after being switched off?\",\"options\":[\"Due to inertia of rest\",\"Due to inertia of motion\",\"Due to directional inertia\",\"Due to the electric current\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The fan continues to revolve due to its inherent property of inertia of motion.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to passengers in a running bus when it suddenly stops?\",\"options\":[\"They get a jerk in the backward direction\",\"They are thrown sideways\",\"They get a jerk in the forward direction\",\"They remain completely still\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that passengers in a running bus get a jerk in the forward direction if the bus stops suddenly.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the inherent property that prevents an object from changing its direction of motion?\",\"options\":[\"Directional inertia\",\"Inertia of rest\",\"Inertia of motion\",\"Applied force\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Directional inertia is the property due to which an object cannot change its direction of motion.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When a moving vehicle turns suddenly, why are passengers thrown opposite to the direction of turning?\",\"options\":[\"Because of inertia of rest\",\"Because of directional inertia\",\"Because of inertia of motion\",\"Because of the speed of the vehicle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This is a specific example of directional inertia where the body resists a change in the direction of motion.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 2, why does string 1 break when string 2 is pulled slowly?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the jerk applied\",\"Because the mass becomes lighter\",\"Because of tension developed in string 2 due to the mass\",\"Because string 1 is weaker than string 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a slow pull develops tension in the string due to the mass, causing string 1 to break.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"An object changes its state of motion due to ____.\",\"options\":[\"Inertia\",\"Mass\",\"Force\",\"Direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The very first sentence of the text states that an object changes its state of motion due to force.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of inertia mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Inertia of the state of rest\",\"Inertia of motion\",\"Directional inertia\",\"Inertia of weight\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists three types: state of rest, motion, and directional. Inertia of weight is not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an object is in motion and no external force acts on it, what will happen according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"It will come to a stop immediately\",\"It will remain in the state of motion\",\"It will change its direction\",\"It will increase its speed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an object in motion remains in the state of motion in the absence of an external force.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Activity 2, the fact that the \'Heavy mass does not move\' when string 2 is jerked is an example of which concept?\",\"options\":[\"Directional inertia\",\"Inertia of the state of rest\",\"Inertia of motion\",\"Tension force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The mass is at rest and resists moving when jerked, demonstrating inertia of the state of rest.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The inherent property of an object due to which its state of motion cannot change is called ____.\",\"options\":[\"Inertia of motion\",\"Inertia of rest\",\"Directional inertia\",\"Stationary state\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifically defines the inability to change the state of motion as \'inertia of motion\'.\",\"topic\":\"Inertia\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the force exerted perpendicularly on a unit area called?\",\"options\":[\"Friction\",\"Pressure\",\"Gravity\",\"Velocity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines pressure as the force exerted perpendicularly on a unit area.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the SI unit of pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Newton (N)\",\"Meter (m)\",\"Pascal (Pa)\",\"Bar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The SI unit of pressure is N\\/m², which is known as Pascal (Pa).\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In atmospheric science, 1 bar is equal to how many Pascals?\",\"options\":[\"10^2 Pa\",\"10^3 Pa\",\"10^4 Pa\",\"10^5 Pa\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in atmospheric science, 1 bar = 10^5 Pa.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Pressure is a __________ quantity.\",\"options\":[\"Vector\",\"Scalar\",\"Fundamental\",\"Chemical\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook explicitly states that pressure is a scalar quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the area on which a constant force is applied increases, what happens to the pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure increases\",\"Pressure reduces\",\"Pressure remains the same\",\"Pressure becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that if area increases, pressure reduces for the same force.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it easier to cut vegetables with a sharp knife than a blunt one?\",\"options\":[\"A sharp knife has a larger area of contact\",\"A sharp knife reduces the force required\",\"A sharp knife has a smaller area of contact, increasing pressure\",\"A sharp knife is heavier than a blunt knife\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A sharp edge has less area, which increases the pressure exerted for the same force, making cutting easier.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a camel walk easily on sand without its feet penetrating deep into it?\",\"options\":[\"Its feet are very small\",\"Its weight is very low\",\"The bottom surfaces of its feet are broad, reducing pressure\",\"It applies more force while walking\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Broad feet exert weight over a large area, reducing the pressure on the sand.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A force of 50 N is applied perpendicularly on an area of 2 m². What is the pressure?\",\"options\":[\"100 Pa\",\"25 Pa\",\"50 Pa\",\"10 Pa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula Pressure = Force \\/ Area, 50 N \\/ 2 m² = 25 Pa.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a vegetable vendor keep a twisted piece of cloth on her head below the basket?\",\"options\":[\"To balance the basket better\",\"To increase the area of contact and reduce pressure on the head\",\"To increase the force acting on the head\",\"To make the basket feel heavier\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The cloth increases the area of contact between the basket and the head, thereby reducing the pressure exerted by the weight.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when you try to push a nail into a wooden plank from its head instead of its pointed end?\",\"options\":[\"It penetrates easily because the head is flat\",\"It does not penetrate easily because the area is larger, reducing pressure\",\"It penetrates faster because the force is distributed\",\"The wood breaks instantly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The head has a larger area than the pointed end; for the same force, the pressure is lower, making penetration difficult.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Pressure on a solid depends on which two factors?\",\"options\":[\"Mass and Volume\",\"Force and Velocity\",\"Value of weight and contact area\",\"Atmospheric pressure and temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that pressure on a solid depends on the value of the weight and the contact area between the two.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are long flat skis used for skiing on ice?\",\"options\":[\"To increase friction with the ice\",\"To decrease the area of contact\",\"To increase the area of contact to reduce pressure on the ice\",\"To increase the speed of the skier\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Long flat skis increase the area over which the weight is distributed, reducing pressure so the skier doesn\'t sink into the ice\\/snow.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the concept of pressure, why is it more comfortable to sleep than to stand for a long time?\",\"options\":[\"Sleeping requires no force\",\"When sleeping, the body weight is spread over a larger area, reducing pressure\",\"Standing increases the total weight of the body\",\"Pressure is zero when we are lying down\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lying down increases the contact area compared to standing on two feet, which reduces the pressure exerted on the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When using a hand pump to fill air in a bicycle tyre, how is the air pushed into the tyre?\",\"options\":[\"By reducing the force\",\"By increasing the area of the pump\",\"By applying force to increase air pressure\",\"By decreasing the air pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that by applying force, air pressure is increased and then the air is pushed into the tyre.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you use your thumb to push a drawing pin and an ordinary pin into a board, why might the ordinary pin hurt your thumb?\",\"options\":[\"The ordinary pin is made of different material\",\"The ordinary pin has a smaller head area, creating higher pressure on the thumb\",\"The drawing pin requires more force\",\"The board is harder for ordinary pins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ordinary pins have smaller heads; applying force on a smaller area results in higher pressure, which can hurt the thumb.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common name given to all gases and liquids as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Solids\",\"Fluids\",\"Vapours\",\"Solutions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all gases and liquids have a common name ‘fluid’.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why does the rubber balloon inflate when water is filled in the bottle?\",\"options\":[\"Because water is lighter than air\",\"Because the glass tube is 10 cm long\",\"Because the pressure of water acts on the side of the bottle\",\"Because molten wax makes the balloon expand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment in the figure demonstrates that water pressure acts on the sides of the container, causing the balloon to inflate.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/028_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which hole produces a water jet that falls farthest from the bottle?\",\"options\":[\"Hole 1\",\"Hole 2\",\"Hole 3\",\"All jets fall at the same distance\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that the jet from the lowest hole (1) falls farthest because pressure increases with depth.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/028_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what happens to the liquid pressure as the depth of the fluid increases?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure decreases\",\"Pressure remains constant\",\"Pressure increases\",\"Pressure becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the pressure increases as the depth of the fluid increases.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A fluid enclosed in a container exerts its pressure in which way at a point within the fluid?\",\"options\":[\"Only in the downward direction\",\"Equally in all directions\",\"Only towards the sides\",\"Only towards the bottom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a fluid enclosed in a container exerts its pressure equally in all directions at a point within the fluid.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of applying molten wax in the experiment involving the plastic bottle and glass tube?\",\"options\":[\"To color the water\",\"To heat the glass tube\",\"To avoid water leakage\",\"To help the balloon inflate faster\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions applying molten wax on the side of the glass tube to avoid water leakage.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If an inflated balloon is pierced with a pin, what is the observed result according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The balloon inflates more\",\"Air leaks out and the balloon does not inflate fully\",\"The balloon changes its shape but stays full\",\"The pressure inside the balloon increases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if a pin hole is created, air leaks out and the balloon does not inflate fully.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"At what height from the bottom of the bottle should the glass tube be pushed in Activity 1?\",\"options\":[\"2 cm\",\"5 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"15 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity 1 specifies pushing the glass tube into the bottle at about 5 cm from the bottom.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What can be concluded if water jets coming out from two different holes fall at the exact same distance from a bottle?\",\"options\":[\"The holes are at different heights\",\"The bottle is not full of water\",\"The holes are at the same level\",\"One hole is larger than the other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that jets coming out from two holes at the same level fall at the same distance.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where do fluids in a container exert pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Only on the bottom of the container\",\"Only on the walls of the container\",\"From within, on the walls and the bottom of the container\",\"Only on the top surface of the fluid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that fluids in a container exert pressure from within, on the walls and the bottom of the container.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the length of the glass tube used in the plastic bottle experiment described in Activity 1?\",\"options\":[\"5 cm\",\"10 cm\",\"15 cm\",\"20 cm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Activity 1 mentions taking a 10 cm long piece of a glass tube.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you inflate a balloon by mouth, why does it inflate on all sides?\",\"options\":[\"Because gas exerts pressure on the walls of the container\",\"Because the balloon is made of rubber\",\"Because air has no weight\",\"Because the balloon is being pulled from outside\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that gas exerts pressure on the wall of the container in which it is enclosed, causing it to inflate on all sides.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the principles of liquid pressure, which jet will fall closest to the bottle in a multi-hole experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The jet from the lowest hole\",\"The jet from the middle hole\",\"The jet from the hole at the top\",\"All jets fall at the same distance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the water jet emerging from the hole at the top will fall closest to the bottle.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of liquid pressure at a specific level?\",\"options\":[\"It is different at different points of the same level\",\"It is the same at any one level\",\"It only acts downwards at that level\",\"It depends on the color of the liquid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that at any one level, the liquid pressure is the same.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a diver goes deeper into a lake, what happens to the pressure exerted by the water on the diver?\",\"options\":[\"It decreases because the diver is further from the air\",\"It stays the same as it was at the surface\",\"It increases because the depth of the fluid increases\",\"It disappears because the diver is enclosed in a suit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Based on the text, pressure increases as the depth of the fluid increases.\",\"topic\":\"Pressure of Liquids and Gases\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the layer of air that surrounds the earth from all sides called?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrosphere\",\"Lithosphere\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Biosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the layer of air surrounding the earth as the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Up to what height does the atmosphere exist in a very dilute form?\",\"options\":[\"16 km\",\"101 km\",\"400 km\",\"103 km\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the atmosphere extends up to about 400 km in a very dilute form.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the value of 1 Atmosphere pressure at sea level?\",\"options\":[\"101 x 10³ Pa\",\"103 x 10¹ Pa\",\"1 bar\",\"10³ mbar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that 1 Atmosphere = 101 x 10³ Pa.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit is equivalent to 1 bar of pressure?\",\"options\":[\"101 Pa\",\"10³ mbar\",\"16 km\",\"1 mbar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that 1 bar = 10³ mbar.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which units is atmospheric pressure commonly specified?\",\"options\":[\"Kilograms or grams\",\"mbar or hectopascal (hPa)\",\"Kilometers or meters\",\"Newtons or Joules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that atmospheric pressure is specified in the units mbar or hectopascal (hPa).\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is atmospheric pressure defined in terms of the weight of air?\",\"options\":[\"Ratio of weight divided by the volume of the cylinder\",\"Ratio of weight divided by the height of the cylinder\",\"Ratio of weight divided by the area of the surface\",\"Product of weight and the area of the surface\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines atmospheric pressure as the ratio of the weight of the air column divided by the area of the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the air pressure as one goes up in height from the sea level?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It decreases\",\"It remains constant\",\"It first increases then decreases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that air pressure decreases as one goes up in height from the sea level.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is pressure created inside a closed container containing air?\",\"options\":[\"By the weight of the container walls\",\"By molecules hitting the walls of the container\",\"By the gravity of the earth pulling the container\",\"By the blood flowing in the arteries\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pressure is created when air molecules in random motion continuously hit the walls of the container.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do humans not get crushed under the heavy atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Because our skin is very thick and hard\",\"Because we live below the sea level\",\"Because internal air and blood pressure balance the external pressure\",\"Because atmospheric pressure only acts from the top\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that cavities filled with air and vessels filled with blood balance the atmospheric pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 1 mbar is approximately equal to 10³ Pa, how many Pascals are there in 10 mbar?\",\"options\":[\"10¹ Pa\",\"10² Pa\",\"10³ Pa\",\"10⁴ Pa\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"If 1 mbar = 10³ Pa, then 10 mbar = 10 x 10³ = 10⁴ Pa.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A mountaineer climbs a high peak. What change will they experience regarding atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"The pressure will be higher than at sea level\",\"The pressure will be lower than at sea level\",\"The pressure will be exactly 1 bar\",\"The pressure will be 101 x 10³ Pa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since pressure decreases with height, it will be lower on a mountain peak than at sea level.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a table top has an area of 1 m², what is the total atmospheric pressure acting on it at sea level?\",\"options\":[\"1 bar\",\"101 x 10³ Pa\",\"10³ mbar\",\"101 x 10³ hPa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that at sea level, the atmospheric pressure is 101 x 10³ Pa.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the decreasing density of dots at higher levels represent?\",\"options\":[\"Air becomes more dense at higher altitudes\",\"Air becomes more dilute at higher altitudes\",\"The temperature increases at higher altitudes\",\"The area of the cylinder increases at higher altitudes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The dots represent air molecules; fewer dots higher up indicate that air is in a very dilute form at greater heights.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/028_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific cross-sectional area of the air column standing on the surface?\",\"options\":[\"1 m²\",\"1 cm²\",\"16 km\",\"400 km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure explicitly labels the base area of the column as 1 cm².\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/028_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' section, why doesn\'t a table top crumble under heavy atmospheric pressure?\",\"options\":[\"The table is made of a material that ignores air pressure\",\"The pressure acts only on the top surface of the table\",\"The pressure acts equally from all sides, including from below the table\",\"The weight of the table is greater than the air pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that atmospheric pressure at a point is equal from all sides, which implies it is balanced.\",\"topic\":\"Atmospheric Pressure\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22154,57,8,'Science','en','Current Electricity and Magnetism','[\"- An atom contains protons (positive) and electrons (negative).\",\"- In a neutral object, equal numbers of protons and electrons cancel charge.\",\"- Static charge: charge that does not move. Moving charge: electrons moving through a conductor.\",\"- Current is the flow of electrons in a conductor.\",\"- Electric current is measured in amperes (A). 1 A = 1 coulomb per second.\",\"- Potential difference (voltage) is the electric ‘height’ that drives electrons from one point to another.\",\"- The unit of potential difference is the volt (V).\",\"- A dry cell supplies a constant voltage (e.g., 1.5 V).\",\"- In a dry cell, zinc reacts with electrolyte to produce electrons at the negative terminal.\",\"- A lead‑acid cell (battery) gives about 2 V and can be recharged.\",\"- Ni‑Cd cells give about 1.2 V and are rechargeable.\",\"- Connecting cells in series adds their voltages; in parallel the voltage stays the same but capacity increases.\",\"- A simple electric circuit consists of a power source, a load (e.g., bulb) and connecting wires.\",\"- When a current flows through a wire, it creates a magnetic field around the wire.\",\"- An electromagnet is a coil of wire that becomes magnetic when current passes through it.\",\"- In an electric bell, the coil attracts a metal strip, causing a striker to hit a gong and produce sound.\",\"- The current stops when the contact is broken, the magnetism disappears, and the strip returns to the contact, restarting the cycle.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following particles in an atom are positively charged?\",\"options\":[\"Electrons\",\"Protons\",\"Neutrons\",\"Atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an atom has a number of positively charged protons.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which particles in an atom carry a negative charge?\",\"options\":[\"Protons\",\"Nuclei\",\"Electrons\",\"Positrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, an atom contains negatively charged electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does an object generally not show any net electrical charge?\",\"options\":[\"It contains no charged particles\",\"It contains only neutrons\",\"Its atoms contain both positively and negatively charged particles that balance out\",\"The charges are hidden deep inside the nucleus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that objects don\'t show charge because their atoms contain both positively charged protons and negatively charged electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the moving charges that get transferred from one object to another?\",\"options\":[\"Protons\",\"Electrons\",\"Atoms\",\"Silk cloth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that moving negatively charged particles are the electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is required to put a stationary object into motion, according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Heat\",\"A force\",\"Water\",\"A glass rod\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a force will have to be applied to put a stationary object into motion.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Current electricity is produced when which particles in a conductor are made to flow?\",\"options\":[\"Protons\",\"Atoms\",\"Electrons\",\"Molecules\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines current electricity as the flow of electrons in an electrical conductor.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The flow of electricity is compared in the text to which natural phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"Wind blowing from high to low pressure\",\"Water flowing from higher to lower level\",\"Sunlight traveling through space\",\"Growth of a plant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks if electricity can be made to flow like water flowing from higher to lower level.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a glass rod is rubbed on a silk cloth, what is the mechanism by which they get charged?\",\"options\":[\"Protons are created\",\"Electrons are destroyed\",\"Moving charges get transferred from one to the other\",\"The atoms stop moving\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that moving charges (electrons) get transferred from one object to the other during charging.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to create a current in a copper wire, what must you do to the electrons inside it?\",\"options\":[\"Keep them stationary\",\"Make them flow by applying a force\",\"Remove them from the wire\",\"Convert them into protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we get current electricity when electrons in a conductor are made to flow, which requires a force.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an object is described as having \'plenty of electrical charge filled\' in it, why might it still feel neutral to touch?\",\"options\":[\"The charges are moving too fast to feel\",\"The charges are only on the surface\",\"The number of protons and electrons are equal\",\"The object is a glass rod\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that despite containing many charged particles (protons and electrons), objects don\'t show charge because they balance each other.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a characteristic of the particles that move during the transfer of charge between objects?\",\"options\":[\"They are positively charged\",\"They are stationary\",\"They are negatively charged\",\"They are found only in silk cloth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that moving charges are negatively charged electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the analogy provided in the text, what is the necessary condition for electricity to flow?\",\"options\":[\"It must flow from a lower level to a higher level\",\"It must flow from a higher level to a lower level\",\"It must stay in a stationary object\",\"It must only contain protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text compares the flow of electricity to water flowing from a higher to a lower level.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where does a large current flow during a natural phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"From the ground to a cloud\",\"From a cloud to the ground\",\"Through the brain\",\"Inside an electric bulb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a large current flows when lightning takes place from a cloud to the ground.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of current is responsible for the sensations felt by the human brain?\",\"options\":[\"A large current\",\"A microscopically small current\",\"A current of positively charged particles only\",\"A current from a car battery\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that sensation is felt by us due to a microscopically small current flowing to the brain.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which of the following devices is current produced by the flow of both negatively and positively charged particles?\",\"options\":[\"Electric bulbs\",\"Household wires\",\"Electric cells of a radio\",\"Lightning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that in electric cells of a radio, current is produced by the flow of both negatively and positively charged particles.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is mentioned as a medium through which current flows in a house?\",\"options\":[\"Clouds\",\"Wires\",\"Microscopic sensations\",\"Charged particles only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that you are aware of the current flowing through wires, electric bulbs, and equipment in the house.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is common between a car battery and the electric cells of a radio regarding current production?\",\"options\":[\"They both produce a microscopically small current for the brain\",\"They both involve lightning from a cloud to the ground\",\"They both produce current by the flow of both negative and positive charges\",\"They both only use negatively charged particles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text groups electric cells of a radio and car batteries together as having current produced by both negatively and positively charged particles.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is using a battery-operated toy, what can be inferred about the current inside the battery based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"It is caused by lightning\",\"It consists of only positively charged particles\",\"It consists of both negatively and positively charged particles\",\"It is a large current flowing to the ground\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Similar to a radio cell or car battery, current in such cells is produced by the flow of both types of charged particles.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Compare the current in lightning to the current that flows to the brain.\",\"options\":[\"Lightning current is microscopically small, brain current is large\",\"Lightning current is large, brain current is microscopically small\",\"Both currents are produced by car batteries\",\"Both currents are exactly the same size\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text contrasts the large current of lightning with the microscopically small current flowing to the brain.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as a place where current flows in a house?\",\"options\":[\"Wires\",\"Electric bulbs\",\"Equipments\",\"Clouds\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists wires, bulbs, and equipment in the house; clouds are associated with lightning, not household current.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A technician is checking a car battery. Based on the textbook, what should they understand about the particles creating the current?\",\"options\":[\"Only electrons are moving\",\"Only protons are moving\",\"Both negatively and positively charged particles are flowing\",\"No charged particles are flowing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that in a car battery, current is produced by the flow of both negatively and positively charged particles.\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement best describes the nature of electric current?\",\"options\":[\"It only occurs in man-made equipment like bulbs\",\"It can range from microscopically small to very large flows\",\"It only involves negatively charged particles\",\"It only flows from the ground to the clouds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows the diversity of current, from large (lightning) to microscopically small (brain sensation).\",\"topic\":\"Current Electricity\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the electric level that decides the direction of flow of electric charges called?\",\"options\":[\"Electric current\",\"Electrostatic potential\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Heat energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the electric level deciding the direction of flow of electric charges as electrostatic potential.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Positive charges have a tendency to flow from a point of ______.\",\"options\":[\"lower electric level to higher electric level\",\"higher electric level to lower electric level\",\"lower temperature to higher temperature\",\"lower level to higher level\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, positive charges tend to flow from a point of higher electric level to a point of lower electric level.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, the flow of electric charge is similar to the flow of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Light\",\"Sound\",\"Water\",\"Magnetism\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text compares the flow of electric charges to the flow of water or a liquid from a higher level to a lower level.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the direction of flow of electric charges between two points?\",\"options\":[\"The amount of charge\",\"The electrostatic potential\",\"The temperature of the points\",\"The weight of the bodies\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that electrostatic potential is the electric level that decides the direction of flow of electric charges.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Heat always flows from a body at higher temperature to a body at ______.\",\"options\":[\"lower temperature\",\"higher temperature\",\"higher level\",\"lower level\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that heat always flows from a body at higher temperature to a body at lower temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If Point A is at a higher electric level than Point B, in which direction will a positive charge flow?\",\"options\":[\"From B to A\",\"From A to B\",\"It will not flow\",\"In both directions simultaneously\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since positive charges flow from higher to lower electric levels, it will flow from A to B.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which physical quantity is analogous (similar) to \'temperature\' when discussing the flow of electric charges?\",\"options\":[\"Electric current\",\"Electrostatic potential\",\"Liquid level\",\"Magnetic field\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Just as temperature difference causes heat flow, electrostatic potential (electric level) causes charge flow.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is required for a positive charge to flow between two points?\",\"options\":[\"A difference in electric level\",\"A difference in magnetic level\",\"A difference in weight\",\"A difference in volume\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Flow occurs from a higher electric level to a lower one, implying a difference in level is necessary.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If Point P has a higher electrostatic potential than Point Q, the positive charge will flow ______.\",\"options\":[\"from Q to P\",\"from P to Q\",\"in both directions\",\"in no direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Electrostatic potential is the electric level; charges flow from the higher level (P) to the lower level (Q).\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the comparison provided in the text, what does the \'level\' of water represent in the context of electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Electric current\",\"Electrostatic potential\",\"Heat energy\",\"Magnetic level\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text compares the water level to the \'electric level\', which is defined as electrostatic potential.\",\"topic\":\"Electrostatic Potential\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the SI unit of potential difference?\",\"options\":[\"Ampere (A)\",\"Volt (V)\",\"Ohm (Ω)\",\"Watt (W)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the unit of potential difference in the SI system is Volt (V).\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction do electrons flow in a wire connected to a dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"From the positive terminal to the negative terminal\",\"From the negative terminal to the positive terminal\",\"In both directions simultaneously\",\"They do not flow in any specific direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that electrons flow from the negative terminal of the cell to the positive terminal.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, potential difference is similar to which of the following physical concepts?\",\"options\":[\"The brightness of a bulb\",\"The thickness of a copper wire\",\"The height of a waterfall\",\"The speed of an electron\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text compares potential difference to the height of a waterfall and the temperature difference of bodies.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why is no current seen to flow in the bulb?\",\"options\":[\"The copper wires are broken\",\"The bulb is fused\",\"There is no potential difference in the absence of a cell\",\"The wires are not made of conducting material\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in fig 4.1 (a), there is no current because there is no potential difference without a cell.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/033_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the connecting wires made of as per the experiment description?\",\"options\":[\"Aluminium\",\"Iron\",\"Copper\",\"Silver\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Take connecting copper wires\' to connect the circuit shown in the figure.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/033_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the bulb when a 1.5 V dry cell is connected to the circuit described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The bulb stops working\",\"The bulb glows, indicating current flow\",\"The bulb heats up but does not glow\",\"The electrons stop moving in the wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that connecting a 1.5 V dry cell causes the bulb to glow, showing current is flowing.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How is the direction of conventional current represented in a circuit diagram?\",\"options\":[\"By a circular loop\",\"By the sign of an arrow\",\"By a dotted line\",\"By the letter \'E\'\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that conventional current is shown in the figure by the sign of an arrow.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the potential difference between two ends of a wire is zero, what can be concluded about the current?\",\"options\":[\"The current is at its maximum\",\"The current flows from positive to negative\",\"No current flows in the circuit\",\"The current flows only through the bulb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that current starts flowing as soon as the potential difference is applied; without it, there is no current.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the relationship between electron flow and conventional current?\",\"options\":[\"They flow in the same direction\",\"Conventional current flows in the opposite direction to electrons\",\"They both flow from positive to negative\",\"There is no relationship between them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that conventional current flows in the opposite direction to the flow of electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student connects a bulb to a wire but it does not glow. Based on the text, what is the most likely missing component required to create a potential difference?\",\"options\":[\"A switch\",\"A dry cell\",\"More copper wire\",\"A second bulb\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that adding a dry cell provides the potential difference necessary for current to flow.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why does current start flowing in a circuit only after a cell is connected?\",\"options\":[\"Because the cell provides the copper for the wires\",\"Because the cell creates a potential difference between the two ends\",\"Because the cell changes the direction of the arrow\",\"Because the cell removes the electrons from the wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that electrons flow due to the potential difference between the two ends of the dry cell.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific voltage of the dry cell mentioned in the textbook\'s experimental setup?\",\"options\":[\"1.0 V\",\"1.5 V\",\"2.0 V\",\"5.0 V\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifically mentions using a \'1.5 V dry cell available in the market\'.\",\"topic\":\"Potential difference\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the SI unit of electric current mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Volt\",\"Ampere\",\"Ohm\",\"Watt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the SI unit of electric current is Ampere.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the passage, how is electric current produced?\",\"options\":[\"By the flow of water through a pipe\",\"By the flow of charged particles\",\"By the movement of scalar quantities\",\"By the increase in time periods\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that electric current is produced due to the flow of charged particles.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If 5 Coulombs of electrical charge flow through a wire in 1 second, what is the electric current?\",\"options\":[\"1 Ampere\",\"0.2 Ampere\",\"5 Ampere\",\"10 Ampere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the formula 1A = 1C \\/ 1s, 5C \\/ 1s equals 5A.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Electric current is classified as which type of physical quantity?\",\"options\":[\"Vector quantity\",\"Scalar quantity\",\"Unit quantity\",\"Variable quantity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes by stating that electric current is a scalar quantity.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the flow of water emerging from a pipe measured?\",\"options\":[\"By the length of the pipe\",\"By the weight of the pipe\",\"By the amount of water in litres coming out in a specific time\",\"By the speed of the charged particles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains water flow measurement as the amount (litres) coming out in a specific time period.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is mathematically equivalent to 1 Ampere (1A)?\",\"options\":[\"1 Coulomb \\/ 1 second\",\"1 Litre \\/ 1 second\",\"1 Coulomb × 1 second\",\"1 Second \\/ 1 Coulomb\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula 1 Ampere = 1 Coulomb \\/ 1 second.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the same amount of charge takes a longer time to flow through a wire, what happens to the electric current based on the formula provided?\",\"options\":[\"The current increases\",\"The current decreases\",\"The current remains the same\",\"The current becomes a vector\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since current is charge divided by time, increasing the time for the same charge results in a lower current value.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is another way to express the unit \'Coulomb per second\'?\",\"options\":[\"Joule\",\"Newton\",\"Ampere\",\"Litre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the SI unit is Coulomb per second or Ampere.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'unit current\' refer to in the context of the text?\",\"options\":[\"Water flowing for 1 second\",\"1 Litre of water in a pipe\",\"Electrical charge flowing through a wire in 1 second\",\"The total number of charged particles in a wire\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines unit current as electrical charge flowing through a wire in 1 second.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the analogy provided, if \'litres\' represents the amount of water, what represents the \'amount\' in electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Seconds\",\"Electrical charge\",\"Pipes\",\"Scalar quantity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text compares water flow (litres\\/time) to electric current (charge\\/time), making charge the electrical equivalent of water volume.\",\"topic\":\"Think about it\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main function of various electric cells according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"To maintain a constant potential difference between its two terminals\",\"To increase the resistance in a circuit\",\"To stop the flow of electric charges\",\"To change the direction of the current\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the main function of various electric cells is to maintain a constant potential difference between its two terminals.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal layer forms the negative terminal of a dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"Copper layer\",\"Zinc (Zn) metal layer\",\"Aluminum layer\",\"Lead layer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the whitish, metal Zinc (Zn) layer is the negative terminal of the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What material is used for the rod at the centre of a dry cell, which acts as the positive terminal?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Iron\",\"Graphite\",\"Zinc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, there is a graphite rod at the centre of the cell which is the positive terminal.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the carriers of electricity in the electrolyte of a dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"Neutral atoms\",\"Only electrons\",\"Only protons\",\"Negatively charged and positively charged ions\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that the electrolyte contains negatively charged and positively charged ions, which are the carriers of electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a large electric current NOT be obtained from a dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"Because the zinc layer is too thin\",\"Because the chemical reaction proceeds very slowly due to the wet pulp\",\"Because the graphite rod is a poor conductor\",\"Because the cell has no electrolyte\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that due to the wet pulp used, the chemical reaction proceeds very slowly, so a large current cannot be obtained.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Compared to electric cells using liquids, what is an advantage of dry cells?\",\"options\":[\"They produce a much larger current\",\"They have a longer shelf life\",\"They are much heavier\",\"They do not use any chemicals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that compared to cells using liquids, the shelf life of dry cells is longer.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following chemicals is part of the electrolyte pulp in a dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"Ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl)\",\"Sodium chloride (NaCl)\",\"Copper sulphate (CuSO₄)\",\"Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the electrolyte as a wet pulp of Zinc chloride (ZnCl₂) and ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl).\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which property makes dry cells convenient for use in mobile instruments like torches?\",\"options\":[\"They must be kept vertical at all times\",\"They can be held in any direction with respect to ground\",\"They only work when stationary\",\"They require a liquid to be added regularly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions dry cells are convenient because they can be held in any direction with respect to ground.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the direct result of the chemical reactions between the chemicals in the dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"The zinc layer turns into graphite\",\"Electrical charge is produced in the two terminals\",\"The wet pulp turns into a solid metal\",\"The potential difference becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that because of the chemical reactions, electrical charge is produced in the two terminals.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the dry cell, why is it used in wall clocks and radio sets rather than heavy machinery requiring high current?\",\"options\":[\"It is too large in size\",\"The chemical reaction is too slow to provide large currents\",\"It has a very short shelf life\",\"It cannot be used in mobile instruments\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that a large electric current cannot be obtained because the reaction proceeds slowly due to the wet pulp.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which paste is filled around the graphite rod in a dry cell?\",\"options\":[\"Zinc chloride paste\",\"Ammonium chloride paste\",\"Manganese dioxide (MnO₂) paste\",\"Lead oxide paste\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a paste of Manganese dioxide (MnO₂) is filled in the rod (around the graphite rod).\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is defined as a general device required to produce a uniform flow of charges in a circuit?\",\"options\":[\"Electric cell\",\"Resistor\",\"Switch\",\"Insulator\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines an electric cell as a general device required to produce a uniform flow of charges in a circuit.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the shelf life and current capacity of a dry cell compared to liquid cells?\",\"options\":[\"The size of the graphite rod\",\"The use of a wet pulp instead of a liquid\",\"The color of the outer coating\",\"The number of terminals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the slow reaction and longer shelf life to the use of wet pulp instead of liquids.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the \'Positive terminal\' is located at the top of which component?\",\"options\":[\"Zinc cover\",\"Divisor\",\"Carbon electrode\",\"Protective cover\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the \'Positive terminal\' at the top of the central \'Carbon electrode\'.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/034_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part is shown as the outermost metal layer that also serves as the negative terminal?\",\"options\":[\"Manganese dioxide\",\"Carbon electrode\",\"Zinc cover\",\"Electrolyte\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the \'Zinc cover\' as the layer connected to the \'negative terminal\' at the base.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Cell and Dry Cell\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/034_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the potential difference of a single Ni-Cd cell?\",\"options\":[\"1.5 V\",\"2.0 V\",\"1.2 V\",\"12 V\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Ni-Cd cells deliver a 1.2 V potential difference.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is used as the electrolyte in a Lead-Acid cell?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Dilute sulfuric acid\",\"Nitric acid\",\"Acetic acid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that both electrodes are dipped in dilute sulfuric acid.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which chemical substance is used as the electrolyte?\",\"options\":[\"Pb\",\"PbO₂\",\"H₂SO₄\",\"H₂O\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the liquid electrolyte as H₂SO₄.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/035_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is a major advantage of Lead-Acid cells that makes them suitable for use in cars and trucks?\",\"options\":[\"They are very small in size\",\"They deliver a low potential difference\",\"They have the capacity to deliver large current\",\"They do not contain any chemicals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that lead-acid cells have a capacity to deliver large current, hence they are used in cars and trucks.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which electrode carries the positive charge?\",\"options\":[\"Lead (Pb)\",\"Lead dioxide (PbO₂)\",\"Sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄)\",\"The glass container\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text indicate that PbO₂ carries a positive charge and is the positive terminal.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/035_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a Lead-Acid cell after it gets electrically discharged?\",\"options\":[\"It must be thrown away\",\"It can be recharged\",\"It turns into a Ni-Cd cell\",\"It stops having a potential difference forever\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that this type of cell can be recharged after getting electrically discharged.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a portable gadget requires 2.4 V to operate, how many Ni-Cd cells would be needed if they are connected to combine their potential?\",\"options\":[\"1 cell\",\"2 cells\",\"3 cells\",\"4 cells\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since one Ni-Cd cell provides 1.2 V, two cells would provide 1.2 + 1.2 = 2.4 V.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these devices is most likely to use a Lead-Acid cell based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"A handheld calculator\",\"A mobile phone\",\"An Uninterrupted Power Supply (UPS)\",\"A digital wristwatch\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically lists UPS as an application for lead-acid cells.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the potential difference values mentioned, which statement is correct?\",\"options\":[\"A Lead-Acid cell has a higher potential difference than a Ni-Cd cell.\",\"A Ni-Cd cell has a higher potential difference than a Lead-Acid cell.\",\"Both cells have the same potential difference of 2V.\",\"Ni-Cd cells are only used when large current is needed.\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lead-acid cells have nearly 2V, while Ni-Cd cells have 1.2V.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the electrical charge to be produced on the electrodes of a Lead-Acid cell?\",\"options\":[\"Physical friction between electrodes\",\"Heat from the surroundings\",\"Chemical reaction between substances in the cell\",\"Magnetic induction from the motor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that charge is produced because of the chemical reaction between the substances in the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are Ni-Cd cells preferred for modern portable gadgets over Lead-Acid cells?\",\"options\":[\"Because gadgets need to be carried to different places\",\"Because gadgets require a very large current like a truck\",\"Because Lead-Acid cells cannot be recharged\",\"Because Ni-Cd cells contain sulfuric acid\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions gadgets that are required to be carried to different places use Ni-Cd cells.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which chemical formula represents the negative electrode in a Lead-Acid cell?\",\"options\":[\"PbO₂\",\"H₂SO₄\",\"Pb\",\"Ni-Cd\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Pb electrode carries a negative charge.\",\"topic\":\"Lead-Acid and Ni-Cd cell\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the connection of electrical components like a cell holder, bulb, and plug key called?\",\"options\":[\"Electric current\",\"Electrical circuit\",\"Magnetic circuit\",\"Cell holder\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the connection of electrical components as an electrical circuit.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a home supply, what is used as the source of electricity instead of electric cells?\",\"options\":[\"Dry cells\",\"Plug keys\",\"Electric supply from outside\",\"Cell holders\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in home supply, the electric supply is made from outside instead of electric cells.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which component is used to connect the cell holder, bulb, and plug key together?\",\"options\":[\"Connecting wires\",\"Magnetic wires\",\"Plastic strings\",\"Rubber bands\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that components are connected by connecting wires.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the electrical component shown?\",\"options\":[\"Plug key\",\"Cell holder\",\"Electric bulb\",\"Dry cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The caption for figure 4.4 (a) identifies it as a \'Cell holder\'.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/035_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the bulb when the plug key in a complete circuit is closed?\",\"options\":[\"It ceases to glow\",\"It lights up\",\"It breaks\",\"It changes color\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the bulb lights up by closing the plug key.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the bulb ceasing to glow indicate in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The current is flowing faster\",\"The cell is fully charged\",\"The electric current has stopped flowing\",\"The plug key is closed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the bulb ceasing to glow indicates that the electric current flowing through the circuit stops.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is a cell represented in an electrical circuit diagram?\",\"options\":[\"By a photograph\",\"By a symbol\",\"By a written word\",\"By a colored dot\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the cell is shown by the symbol in a circuit diagram.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of removing the cell from the circuit?\",\"options\":[\"The bulb glows brighter\",\"The current continues to flow\",\"The electric current stops flowing\",\"The plug key closes automatically\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that on the removal of the cell, the electric current stops flowing.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to stop the current without removing the cell or wires, what should they do?\",\"options\":[\"Open the plug key\",\"Change the bulb\",\"Close the plug key\",\"Add more wires\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since closing the key starts the current, opening it would stop the current without removing components.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is necessary to provide the energy for the bulb to light up in the described experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Plug key\",\"Connecting wires\",\"Dry cell\",\"Cell holder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text indicates that the bulb lights up when a dry cell is fitted and the current flows.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, how many dry cells can be fitted into this specific holder at one time?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The image of the cell holder shows three distinct slots for dry cells.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/035_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the state of the circuit when the bulb is not glowing but all components are present?\",\"options\":[\"The plug key must be closed\",\"The current is not flowing\",\"The cell is providing maximum current\",\"The wires are not needed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the bulb not glowing (ceasing to glow) with the current stopping.\",\"topic\":\"Electric Circuit\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of connecting more than one electric cell in a circuit as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To reduce the total current\",\"To obtain a higher potential difference\",\"To decrease the number of wires\",\"To make the circuit smaller\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the purpose is to obtain a higher potential difference than that of a single cell.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which device are 2-3 dry cells typically seen to be connected in series?\",\"options\":[\"Electric heater\",\"Transistor radio\",\"Ceiling fan\",\"Electric iron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions the transistor radio as an example where cells are connected in series.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a connection of multiple cells, as seen in fig 4.5 (b), called?\",\"options\":[\"A single cell\",\"A Battery of cells\",\"A terminal\",\"A dry cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the connection of cells as seen in fig 4.5 (b) as a Battery of cells.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a series connection of cells, how is the positive terminal of the first cell connected?\",\"options\":[\"To the positive terminal of the second cell\",\"To the negative terminal of the second cell\",\"To the negative terminal of the first cell\",\"To the positive terminal of the third cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in a series connection, the positive terminal of one cell is connected to the negative terminal of the second cell.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the result of obtaining a higher potential difference by connecting cells?\",\"options\":[\"It is possible to obtain higher current\",\"The current stops flowing\",\"The potential difference becomes zero\",\"The cells become dry\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that by obtaining higher potential difference, it is possible to obtain higher current.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If each cell has a potential difference of 1V, what will be the total potential difference of 3 cells connected in series?\",\"options\":[\"1V\",\"2V\",\"3V\",\"4V\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the calculation that if each cell is 1V, the total for 3 cells in series is 3V.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a series connection involving three cells, the positive terminal of the second cell is connected to which part of the third cell?\",\"options\":[\"Positive terminal\",\"Negative terminal\",\"Switch\",\"Radio antenna\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the positive terminal of the second cell is connected to the negative terminal of the third one.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student connects four cells in series, and each cell has a potential difference of 1.5V (following the logic in the text), what would be the total potential difference?\",\"options\":[\"1.5V\",\"3.0V\",\"4.5V\",\"6.0V\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Following the text\'s logic (3 cells of 1V = 3V), 4 cells of 1.5V would equal 6.0V (1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5).\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the relationship between the number of cells in series and the total potential difference?\",\"options\":[\"Total potential difference decreases as cells are added\",\"Total potential difference remains the same regardless of cell count\",\"Total potential difference is the sum of the potential differences of individual cells\",\"Total potential difference only depends on the first cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that 3 cells of 1V result in 3V, implying the potential differences are additive in series.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why would a designer choose to use a \'Battery of cells\' instead of a single cell for a device?\",\"options\":[\"To make the device heavier\",\"To achieve a higher current by increasing potential difference\",\"To ensure the positive terminals are all connected together\",\"To reduce the voltage of the circuit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the purpose is to obtain higher potential difference and thus higher current.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the correct sequence for connecting three cells in series based on the text.\",\"options\":[\"Pos to Pos, then Neg to Neg\",\"Neg to Neg, then Pos to Pos\",\"Pos to Neg, then Pos to Neg\",\"Pos to Neg, then Neg to Pos\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes connecting the positive of one to the negative of the next, repeating the pattern.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a circuit requires a higher current than a single 1V cell can provide, what configuration does the text suggest?\",\"options\":[\"Connecting cells in a way that the positive terminal of one meets the negative of another\",\"Connecting only the positive terminals together\",\"Using only one cell but increasing its size\",\"Connecting the negative terminal of the first cell to the negative of the second\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text suggests a series connection (Pos to Neg) to obtain higher potential difference and higher current.\",\"topic\":\"Connecting cells\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of cells are commonly used in modern equipment like smartphones and laptops?\",\"options\":[\"Dry cells\",\"Ni-Cd cells\",\"Lithium ion cells\",\"Lead-acid cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Lithium ion cells are used in modern equipment like smartphones and laptops.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a major advantage of Lithium ion cells compared to Ni-Cd cells?\",\"options\":[\"They are cheaper to produce\",\"They can store more electrical energy\",\"They are larger in size\",\"They cannot be recharged\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, more electrical energy can be stored in Lithium ion cells as compared to Ni-Cd cells.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the first scientist to observe that a magnetic field is created when an electric current flows in a wire?\",\"options\":[\"Isaac Newton\",\"Hans Christian Oerstead\",\"Michael Faraday\",\"Thomas Edison\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Hans Christian Oerstead made this observation first.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment described, what happens to the magnetic needle when the plug key is closed and the bulb lights up?\",\"options\":[\"It stays in its original position\",\"It changes its direction\",\"It starts spinning rapidly\",\"It loses its magnetism\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the magnetic needle changes its direction when a current starts flowing in the circuit.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the magnetic needle when the plug key is opened, stopping the current flow?\",\"options\":[\"It continues to point in the new direction\",\"It spins in the opposite direction\",\"It comes back to the original position\",\"It becomes permanently magnetized\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks if the needle comes back to the original position when the key is opened, implying the observation that it does.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A magnetic needle is essentially a ________.\",\"options\":[\"Piece of plastic\",\"Non-magnetic metal\",\"Magnet\",\"Resistor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'You know that a magnetic needle is indeed a magnet.\'\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to build a circuit to demonstrate the magnetic effect of current, which component is used to detect the field in the text\'s experiment?\",\"options\":[\"A bulb\",\"A magnetic needle\",\"A plug key\",\"A connecting wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The magnetic needle is used to observe the change in direction caused by the magnetic field.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a car battery called a \'battery\' rather than a \'cell\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is larger in size\",\"Because it is rechargeable\",\"Because it consists of multiple cells\",\"Because it is used in vehicles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In physics, a battery refers to a combination of two or more cells.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, what can be concluded about electric current passing through a wire?\",\"options\":[\"It produces heat only\",\"It produces a magnetic field around the wire\",\"It destroys the magnetic properties of nearby objects\",\"It turns the wire into a permanent magnet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that when an electric current passes through a wire, a magnetic field is produced around that wire.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If the bulb in the experiment does not light up when the key is closed, what will likely happen to the magnetic needle?\",\"options\":[\"It will change its direction\",\"It will not change its position\",\"It will point towards the bulb\",\"It will point towards the cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"If the bulb doesn\'t light, current isn\'t flowing; without current, no magnetic field is produced to move the needle.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the purpose of the component marked with the symbol \'( . )\'?\",\"options\":[\"To store electrical energy\",\"To provide light\",\"To open or close the electric circuit\",\"To measure the magnetic field\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The symbol represents the plug key, which is used to start or stop the flow of current.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/036_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why is the connecting wire wound around the matchbox tray?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the tray closed\",\"To increase the resistance of the bulb\",\"To concentrate the magnetic effect on the needle\",\"To prevent the needle from falling out\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Winding the wire around the tray ensures the magnetic field produced by the current affects the needle inside.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/036_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of Lithium ion cells mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They cannot be recharged\",\"They are used only in car batteries\",\"They can be recharged\",\"They store less energy than Ni-Cd cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Lithium ion cells can be recharged.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the bar magnet and the magnetic needle as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The bar magnet has no effect on the needle\",\"The needle changes direction when the magnet is near\",\"The needle loses its magnetism near the magnet\",\"The magnet turns into a wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the magnetic needle changes its direction when a bar magnet is taken near it.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you replace a Ni-Cd cell with a Lithium ion cell in a laptop, what is the most likely outcome based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"The laptop will become heavier\",\"The laptop will store more electrical energy\",\"The laptop will no longer be rechargeable\",\"The laptop will produce a stronger magnetic field\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lithium ion cells store more electrical energy than Ni-Cd cells.\",\"topic\":\"Magnetic effects of electric current\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the system consisting of a coil and an iron screw called when used as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Electric cell\",\"Resistive wire\",\"Electromagnet\",\"Plug key\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines the system of the coil and the screw as an electromagnet.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of coating should the flexible copper wire have for this activity?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic coating\",\"Resistive coating\",\"Metallic coating\",\"No coating\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using a flexible copper wire having a resistive coating.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the magnetism of the screw as soon as the electric current is stopped?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It vanishes\",\"It reverses direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that as soon as the current is stopped, the magnetism vanishes.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the iron screw attain magnetism in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is connected to the plug key\",\"Because magnetism is produced in the coil around it\",\"Because it is a natural magnet\",\"Because of the resistive coating on the wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that magnetism is produced in the coil, which causes the screw to attain magnetism.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are electromagnets used for in scientific research?\",\"options\":[\"To stop electric current\",\"To coat copper wires\",\"To produce strong magnetic fields\",\"To connect plug keys\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states electromagnets are used to produce strong magnetic fields useful in scientific research.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student opens the plug key in the circuit, what will be the observation regarding the iron pins?\",\"options\":[\"They will stick more tightly\",\"They will no longer stick to the screw\",\"They will start spinning\",\"They will melt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Opening the plug key stops the current, causing the magnetism to vanish and the pins to fall off.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the magnetism produced first when the current starts flowing in the wire?\",\"options\":[\"In the electric cell\",\"In the plug key\",\"In the coil around the screw\",\"In the iron pins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says magnetism is produced in the coil around the screw when current flows.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a necessary condition for the screw to act as a magnet in this setup?\",\"options\":[\"The plug key must be open\",\"The wire must be made of iron\",\"Electric current must flow in the wire\",\"The screw must be a meter long\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The magnetism is only present when the electric current flows in the wire.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the component shown at the bottom with \'+\' and \'-\' signs?\",\"options\":[\"Iron screw\",\"Electric cell\",\"Plug key\",\"Copper wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The component with \'+\' and \'-\' terminals in the circuit diagram represents an electric cell.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/036_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, where do the iron pins or small nails stick when the current is flowing?\",\"options\":[\"To the electric cell\",\"To the plug key\",\"To the tip of the screw\",\"To the middle of the wire\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and figure indicate that pins\\/nails stick to the tip of the screw.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/036_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the immediate result of plugging the key in the circuit according to the activity?\",\"options\":[\"The wire breaks\",\"The magnetism vanishes\",\"Current starts and pins stick to the screw\",\"The screw becomes a permanent magnet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plugging the key starts the current, which produces magnetism and makes the pins stick.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement best describes the nature of the magnetism in this electromagnet?\",\"options\":[\"It is permanent and never vanishes\",\"It depends on the length of the screw\",\"It is temporary and depends on the flow of current\",\"It is produced by the iron pins themselves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows magnetism exists only while current flows and vanishes when it stops, making it temporary.\",\"topic\":\"Electro magnet\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22155,57,8,'Science','en','Inside the Atom','[\"Matter is made of atoms, and atoms are the smallest units that keep an element’s identity.\",\"An atom is indivisible and indestructible (Dalton’s view).\",\"Atoms of the same element are identical; different elements have different atoms.\",\"The smallest particle of a compound is a molecule; of an element it is an atom.\",\"Atoms contain a nucleus (protons + neutrons) and electrons orbiting it.\",\"Protons carry +1 charge, neutrons are neutral, electrons carry –1 charge.\",\"Atomic number (Z) = number of protons; mass number (A) = protons + neutrons.\",\"Electrons are arranged in shells (K, L, M, N…) with capacities 2n² (n=1,2,3…).\",\"Valence electrons are those in the outermost shell; they determine chemical bonding.\",\"Valency = number of bonds an atom can form; for most elements it equals the number of valence electrons if ≤4, otherwise 8‑x where x is valence electrons.\",\"Rutherford’s gold‑foil experiment showed most α‑particles pass through, proving atoms have a small, dense nucleus.\",\"Bohr’s model added quantised electron orbits to explain atomic stability and spectral lines.\",\"Isotopes have same Z but different A (different neutron numbers).\",\"Heavy water (D₂O) contains deuterium (²H) instead of normal hydrogen.\",\"In a nuclear reactor, slow neutrons are moderated (e.g., by heavy water) to sustain fission of U‑235.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the smallest unit of matter that retains its chemical identity in all physical and chemical changes?\",\"options\":[\"Molecule\",\"Atom\",\"Compound\",\"Element\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an atom is the smallest particle of an element which retains its chemical identity in all changes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how old is the concept of the atom?\",\"options\":[\"More than 500 years old\",\"More than 1000 years old\",\"More than 2500 years old\",\"More than 5000 years old\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the concept of the atom is more than 2500 years old.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist\'s atomic theory marked the beginning of modern explanations of the nature and internal structure of the atom?\",\"options\":[\"Newton\'s theory\",\"Dalton\'s theory\",\"Aristotle\'s theory\",\"Einstein\'s theory\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the modern explanation of the atom started with Dalton’s atomic theory.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are molecules formed from?\",\"options\":[\"Only compounds\",\"Only elements\",\"Atoms\",\"Physical mixtures\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text clearly states that molecules are formed from atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the smallest particle of an element that takes part in a chemical combination?\",\"options\":[\"A molecule\",\"A compound\",\"An atom\",\"A mixture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the smallest particle of an element taking part in chemical combination is an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the smallest particle for most substances?\",\"options\":[\"Atoms\",\"Molecules\",\"Electrons\",\"Protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the smallest particles of most substances are molecules.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are molecules formed according to the provided information?\",\"options\":[\"By physical mixing of substances\",\"By chemical combination of atoms\",\"By breaking down compounds\",\"By cooling elements to low temperatures\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that molecules are formed by chemical combination of atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which substance is identified as having an \'atom\' as its smallest particle?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Oxygen\",\"Helium\",\"Hydrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In table 5.1, the column \'is an atom\' is ticked only for Helium among the first four entries.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/038_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the common characteristic between Oxygen (O2) and Hydrogen (H2)?\",\"options\":[\"They are both compounds\",\"Their smallest particles are atoms\",\"They contain different types of atoms in the molecule\",\"They contain a single type of atoms in the molecule\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table shows that both O2 and H2 have ticks in the \'single type of atoms in the molecule\' column.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/038_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance has the formula NH3 (Ammonia), how should it be classified based on the types of atoms in its molecule?\",\"options\":[\"Single type of atoms in the molecule\",\"Different types of atoms in the molecule\",\"It is not a molecule\",\"It is an element\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"NH3 contains Nitrogen (N) and Hydrogen (H), which are different types of atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the pattern for Helium (He) in the text, what would be the smallest particle of Neon (Ne)?\",\"options\":[\"A molecule with two atoms\",\"An atom\",\"A compound\",\"A molecule with different types of atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Like Helium, Neon (Ne) is a single-atom substance as shown in the formula column.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following substances from the text is classified as a compound?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen (N2)\",\"Chlorine (Cl2)\",\"Methane (CH4)\",\"Argon (Ar)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Methane (CH4) contains different types of atoms (C and H), making it a compound.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What distinguishes a molecule of an element from a molecule of a compound according to the table?\",\"options\":[\"The number of atoms present\",\"Whether the atoms are of the same type or different types\",\"The physical state of the substance\",\"The age of the concept\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table differentiates them based on \'single type of atoms\' (element) vs \'different types of atoms\' (compound).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Compare Helium (He) and Argon (Ar) based on the information provided. What do they have in common?\",\"options\":[\"They are both molecules with two atoms\",\"They are both compounds\",\"Their smallest particles are single atoms\",\"They contain different types of atoms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both He and Ar are listed with single-atom formulas, meaning their smallest particle is an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is Water (H2O) categorized as a compound in the provided table?\",\"options\":[\"Because its smallest particle is an atom\",\"Because it contains only one type of atom\",\"Because it is a liquid\",\"Because its molecule contains different types of atoms\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table ticks \'different types of atoms in the molecule\' for Water, which corresponds to the \'Compound\' column.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Types of substances\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which Indian philosopher stated that there is a limit to divide matter into small particles?\",\"options\":[\"Democritus\",\"Kanad\",\"Dalton\",\"Aristotle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Indian philosopher Kanad (6th century B.C.) stated there is a limit to divide matter.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Kanad Muni, what is the meaning of the term \'Paramānu\'?\",\"options\":[\"Indestructible energy\",\"Invisible light\",\"The smallest particles\",\"Large molecules\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Kanad Muni named the indivisible particles \'Paramānu\', which means the smallest particles.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which century B.C. did the Greek philosopher Democritus state his theory about matter?\",\"options\":[\"6th century B.C.\",\"4th century B.C.\",\"5th century B.C.\",\"3rd century B.C.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Greek philosopher Democritus lived in the 5th century B.C.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Greek word \'Atomos\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"The one which can be seen\",\"The one which cannot be cut\",\"The one which is heavy\",\"The one which is round\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the Greek language, \'Atomos\' means the one which cannot be cut.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Besides being indivisible, what other property did Kanad attribute to \'Paramānu\'?\",\"options\":[\"It is indestructible\",\"It is always liquid\",\"It can be divided further\",\"It is made of Greek atoms\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Kanad stated that \'Paramānu\' is indestructible.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you follow Kanad\'s philosophy and keep dividing a piece of iron until you reach a particle that can no longer be divided, what would you call that particle?\",\"options\":[\"Atomos\",\"Element\",\"Paramānu\",\"Compound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Kanad named the indivisible particles that constitute matter as \'Paramānu\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a particle that is so small it cannot be divided. Based on Democritus\'s naming, what is this particle called?\",\"options\":[\"Paramānu\",\"Atom\",\"Muni\",\"Matter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Democritus named the smallest particle of matter that cannot be divided as \'Atom\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the time periods mentioned in the text, who proposed the idea of indivisible particles first?\",\"options\":[\"Democritus\",\"Kanad Muni\",\"Both at the same time\",\"The text does not provide dates\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Kanad lived in the 6th century B.C., while Democritus lived in the 5th century B.C., making Kanad\'s statement earlier.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common conclusion reached by both Kanad and Democritus regarding matter?\",\"options\":[\"Matter is made of large, breakable parts\",\"Matter is continuous and has no end to division\",\"Matter is made of small particles that cannot be divided further\",\"Matter is only made of Greek \'Atomos\'\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Both philosophers concluded that matter is made of small particles (Paramānu\\/Atom) that are indivisible.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the relationship between \'Atom\' and \'Atomos\' as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"Atom is the Greek word for Paramānu\",\"Atomos is the name of the philosopher who discovered the Atom\",\"Atom is the name given to the particle, derived from the Greek word Atomos\",\"Atom and Atomos have completely different meanings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The name \'Atom\' was given by Democritus, based on the Greek word \'Atomos\' meaning \'cannot be cut\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Who put forth the celebrated \'Atomic Theory\' in 1803 A.D.?\",\"options\":[\"Aristotle\",\"John Dalton\",\"Maharshi Kanad\",\"Democritus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that British scientist John Dalton put forth his \'Atomic Theory\' in 1803 A.D.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Dalton\'s atomic theory, what is the nature of an atom?\",\"options\":[\"Divisible and soft\",\"Indivisible and indestructible\",\"Complex and hollow\",\"Liquid and changing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dalton\'s theory states that matter is made of atoms and atoms are indivisible and indestructible.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did John Dalton state about atoms of the same element?\",\"options\":[\"They have different masses\",\"They are all alike\",\"They have different shapes\",\"They are made of smaller particles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The theory mentions that all atoms of an element are alike.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the \'Try this\' activity, what is the main difference found between a \'Bundi Laddu\' and a solid ball?\",\"options\":[\"The laddu is heavier than the ball\",\"The laddu has an internal structure while the ball does not\",\"The ball is made of smaller particles called Bundis\",\"The ball is easier to press with palms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a Bundi Laddu has an internal structure formed by smaller particles, whereas a solid ball broadly lacks one.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to Dalton\'s atomic theory, how is mass distributed in an atom?\",\"options\":[\"Concentrated at the center\",\"Distributed on the surface only\",\"Distributed uniformly\",\"Distributed randomly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that according to Dalton\'s atomic theory, the mass is distributed uniformly in an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you have two different elements, what does Dalton\'s theory predict about their atoms?\",\"options\":[\"They will have the same mass\",\"They will be identical in every way\",\"They will have different atoms with different mass\",\"They will be divisible into the same particles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The theory states that different elements have different atoms with different mass.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description in the text, which object best represents Dalton\'s model of an atom?\",\"options\":[\"A Bundi Laddu\",\"A hollow rubber ball\",\"A hard, solid sphere\",\"A sponge ball\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes Dalton\'s atom as a hard, solid sphere with no internal structure.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text compare an atom to a solid ball rather than a Bundi Laddu?\",\"options\":[\"Because atoms are sweet like laddus\",\"Because atoms, like the ball, have no internal structure according to Dalton\",\"Because atoms are made of smaller particles like Bundis\",\"Because the mass of an atom is concentrated in one spot\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dalton\'s atom is described as having no internal structure, similar to the solid ball in the experiment.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the physical shape used to represent the atom in Dalton\'s model?\",\"options\":[\"A ring\",\"A solid sphere\",\"A cube\",\"A triangle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Dalton\'s atomic model as a simple, solid circular\\/spherical shape.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/039_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure of Dalton\'s atomic model, what can be inferred about the internal parts of the atom?\",\"options\":[\"It contains many small dots\",\"It has a nucleus at the center\",\"It has no internal structure shown\",\"It is made of several layers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a plain sphere without any internal components, matching Dalton\'s description of \'no internal structure\'.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/039_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What property of an atom is implied when Dalton says it is \'indestructible\'?\",\"options\":[\"It can be broken into smaller pieces\",\"It cannot be destroyed\",\"It changes its mass constantly\",\"It has a very soft structure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Indestructible means something that cannot be destroyed.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an element is made of atoms that are all alike, what can we conclude about their mass according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The mass will vary from atom to atom\",\"The mass will be the same for all atoms of that element\",\"The mass is not distributed uniformly\",\"The mass depends on the internal structure\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The theory states all atoms of an element are alike, and different elements have different atoms with different mass, implying same elements have same mass.\",\"topic\":\"Dalton’s atomic theory and Try this\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist demonstrated the existence of negatively charged particles inside an atom through experiments?\",\"options\":[\"Dalton\",\"J.J. Thomson\",\"Rutherford\",\"Newton\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that J.J. Thomson demonstrated experimentally that negatively charged particles exist inside an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the mass of an electron compared to a hydrogen atom according to Thomson\'s findings?\",\"options\":[\"1800 times more\",\"Equal to a hydrogen atom\",\"1800 times less\",\"Half of a hydrogen atom\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Thomson demonstrated that negatively charged particles (electrons) have a mass 1800 times less than a hydrogen atom.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the plum pudding model of atomic structure first put forth?\",\"options\":[\"1800\",\"1904\",\"1890\",\"1911\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the plum pudding model was put forth by Thomson in the year 1904.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Thomson’s model, how is the positive charge arranged in an atom?\",\"options\":[\"Concentrated at the center\",\"Distributed throughout the atom\",\"Located only on the surface\",\"Orbiting around the electrons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the model, the positive charge is distributed throughout the atom.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is an atom considered electrically neutral in the plum pudding model?\",\"options\":[\"It contains no charged particles\",\"The negative charges are hidden inside\",\"The distributed positive charge is balanced by the negative charge on electrons\",\"The electrons have no mass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The atom is neutral because the distributed positive charge is balanced by the negative charge of the embedded electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the plum pudding model, what do the \'plums\' or raisins represent?\",\"options\":[\"Positive charges\",\"The entire atom\",\"Negatively charged electrons\",\"Hydrogen atoms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the plum pudding analogy, the dried fruits (plums\\/raisins) represent the electrons embedded in the positive charge.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a hydrogen atom has a mass of 1800 units, what would be the approximate mass of one electron according to Thomson\'s discovery?\",\"options\":[\"1 unit\",\"1800 units\",\"900 units\",\"3600 units\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since an electron is 1800 times lighter than a hydrogen atom, its mass would be 1800\\/1800 = 1 unit.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an atom has 10 units of distributed positive charge, how many units of negative charge must the embedded electrons have to keep the atom neutral?\",\"options\":[\"5 units\",\"10 units\",\"20 units\",\"0 units\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"For an atom to be neutral, the positive charge must be exactly balanced by the negative charge.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Use your brain power\' section, how is the atomic mass distributed in Thomson\'s model?\",\"options\":[\"Non-uniformly\",\"Only at the edges\",\"Uniformly\",\"Only where electrons are present\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because the positive charge is distributed throughout the atom, the mass distribution is considered uniform.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the small circles containing the minus (-) sign represent?\",\"options\":[\"Protons\",\"Positively charged pudding\",\"Negatively charged electrons\",\"Hydrogen atoms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the diagram of Thomson\'s model, the circles with the minus sign represent the embedded electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/039_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the large shaded sphere containing the plus (+) signs represent?\",\"options\":[\"A single electron\",\"The distributed positive charge\",\"An empty space\",\"A molecule\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the positive charge (+) distributed throughout the volume of the atom.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/039_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What modern ingredients are mentioned as substitutes for \'plums\' in the Christmas dish today?\",\"options\":[\"Apples or oranges\",\"Raisins or dates\",\"Chocolate or nuts\",\"Cherries or berries\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that these days, raisins or dates are used in the dish instead of dried plums.\",\"topic\":\"Thomson’s plum pudding model of atom\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did Rutherford put forth his nuclear model of the atom?\",\"options\":[\"1901\",\"1911\",\"1921\",\"1891\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Rutherford put forth the nuclear model of atom in the year 1911.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the thickness of the gold foil used by Rutherford in his scattering experiment?\",\"options\":[\"10-5 mm\",\"10-6 mm\",\"10-7 mm\",\"10-8 mm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Rutherford took a very thin gold foil with a thickness of 10-7mm.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of particles were bombarded on the gold foil in Rutherford\'s experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Negatively charged β-particles\",\"Positively charged α-particles\",\"Neutral neutrons\",\"Negatively charged electrons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rutherford bombarded the foil with positively charged α-particles emitted by a radioactive element.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the experiment, what did the large number of α-particles going straight through the foil indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The atom is a solid sphere\",\"There is mainly empty space inside the atom\",\"The nucleus is very large\",\"Electrons are very heavy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the large number of particles going straight meant there must be mainly an empty space inside the atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the function of the fluorescent screen placed around the gold foil?\",\"options\":[\"To emit α-particles\",\"To block the α-particles\",\"To observe the path of α-particles\",\"To create a vacuum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show that the fluorescent screen is used to observe the path of α-particles after they hit the foil.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/040_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on Rutherford\'s observation that one α-particle out of 20,000 bounced back, how many particles would likely bounce back if 60,000 particles were bombarded?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If 1 out of 20,000 bounces back, then for 60,000 particles (3 times 20,000), 3 particles would bounce back.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What was expected to happen if the positively charged mass were evenly distributed inside the atoms?\",\"options\":[\"Particles would go straight through\",\"Particles would get reflected from the foil\",\"Particles would be absorbed by the foil\",\"Particles would turn into electrons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says it was expected that the α-particles would get reflected if the mass were evenly distributed.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure showing the atomic model, what does the central red part represent?\",\"options\":[\"Electron\",\"Empty space\",\"Nucleus\",\"α-particle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure and text describe the positively charged nucleus at the centre of an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/040_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is an atom considered to be electrically neutral according to Rutherford\'s model?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has no charged particles\",\"Because the nucleus has no charge\",\"Because opposite charges on electrons and nucleus are balanced\",\"Because electrons have no charge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that as the opposite charges are balanced (total negative on electrons = positive on nucleus), the atom is electrically neutral.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where is almost the entire mass of the atom concentrated according to Rutherford?\",\"options\":[\"In the revolving electrons\",\"In the empty space\",\"In the nucleus\",\"On the fluorescent screen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 2 of Rutherford\'s model states that almost the entire mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an α-particle (positive charge) is deflected by the center of the atom, what can be inferred about the charge of the nucleus?\",\"options\":[\"The nucleus is negatively charged\",\"The nucleus is positively charged\",\"The nucleus is neutral\",\"The nucleus has no mass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since α-particles are positively charged, their deflection by the center indicates the center (nucleus) is also positively charged due to repulsion.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the movement of electrons in Rutherford\'s model?\",\"options\":[\"They are stationary inside the nucleus\",\"They revolve around the nucleus\",\"They move in straight lines through the nucleus\",\"They stay fixed in the empty space\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Point 3 of the model states that negatively charged particles called electrons revolve around the nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What did the small number of α-particles that deflected through small or big angles indicate to Rutherford?\",\"options\":[\"They hit the empty space\",\"They faced a heavy, positively charged obstacle at the centre\",\"They were absorbed by the gold atoms\",\"The gold foil was too thick\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that deflection meant they faced a positively charged and heavy part causing obstruction at the centre.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the structure of the atom based on the scattering experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The atom is mostly solid with small holes\",\"The atom is a uniform sphere of positive charge\",\"The atom consists of a tiny dense nucleus surrounded by mostly empty space\",\"The atom\'s mass is spread out near the electrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rutherford concluded there is a nucleus at the center and empty space between it and the revolving electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is located between the revolving electrons and the atomic nucleus?\",\"options\":[\"Positively charged mass\",\"Radioactive elements\",\"Empty space\",\"α-particles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Point 5 of Rutherford\'s model explicitly states there is an empty space between the revolving electron and the atomic nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Rutherford’s nuclear model of atom\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Who put forth the stable orbit atomic model in the year 1913?\",\"options\":[\"Ernest Rutherford\",\"Niels Bohr\",\"J.J. Thomson\",\"John Dalton\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in 1913, Danish scientist Niels Bohr explained the stability of the atom by putting forth the stable orbit atomic model.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to Bohr\'s model, in what path do electrons revolve around the nucleus?\",\"options\":[\"Random zig-zag paths\",\"Elliptical orbits at varying distances\",\"Concentric circular orbits at certain distances\",\"Straight lines moving away from the nucleus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first postulate states that electrons revolve in concentric circular orbits at certain distances from the nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the energy of an electron while it remains in a particular orbit?\",\"options\":[\"It increases continuously\",\"It decreases continuously\",\"It changes randomly\",\"It remains constant\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The second postulate states that the energy of an electron is constant while it is in a particular orbit.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When an electron jumps from an inner orbit to an outer orbit, what occurs?\",\"options\":[\"It emits energy\",\"It absorbs energy\",\"Its energy remains the same\",\"It loses all its energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The third postulate states that when an electron jumps from an inner orbit to an outer orbit, it absorbs energy.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An electron moves from an outer shell to an inner shell. What is the result of this transition?\",\"options\":[\"Energy is emitted\",\"Energy is absorbed\",\"The atom becomes unstable\",\"The electron disappears\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when an electron jumps from an outer orbit to an inner orbit, it emits energy.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a sodium vapour lamp give off a specific yellow light?\",\"options\":[\"Because the lamp is painted yellow\",\"Because the energy difference between the two shells of sodium is fixed and corresponds to yellow light\",\"Because sodium electrons only stay in the outermost shell\",\"Because sodium metal reacts with the glass of the lamp\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the difference of energy level of the two shells of sodium is fixed and similar to the energy of yellow light.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What amount of energy is absorbed or emitted when an electron jumps between orbits?\",\"options\":[\"A random amount of energy\",\"Energy equal to the sum of the two energy levels\",\"Energy equal to the difference of the energy levels\",\"Energy equal to the distance from the nucleus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The third postulate states that the energy absorbed or emitted is equal to the difference of the electron\'s energy levels.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following observations is mentioned in the text as a result of sodium electrons emitting energy?\",\"options\":[\"A blue flame on a gas stove\",\"A green spark when salt is heated\",\"A yellow spark when table salt is thrown on an LPG flame\",\"A white light from a sodium lamp\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when table salt is thrown on an LPG gas stove flame, a yellow spark forms immediately.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the postulates, if an electron absorbs energy, what can you conclude about its movement?\",\"options\":[\"It is moving from an outer orbit to an inner orbit\",\"It is moving from an inner orbit to an outer orbit\",\"It is staying in the same orbit\",\"It is leaving the atom entirely\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an electron absorbs energy when it jumps from an inner orbit to an outer orbit.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does Bohr\'s model explain the stability of the atom compared to previous models?\",\"options\":[\"By stating electrons are fixed in the nucleus\",\"By proposing that electrons have constant energy while in a particular orbit\",\"By suggesting electrons move in straight lines\",\"By stating that electrons never change their orbits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bohr explained stability by proposing stable orbits where the energy of an electron remains constant.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is used as an example to show that energy levels of shells are fixed?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen\",\"Oxygen\",\"Sodium\",\"Carbon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses sodium (in table salt, metal form, and vapour lamps) to illustrate fixed energy differences between shells.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the geometric relationship between the orbits in Bohr\'s model?\",\"options\":[\"They are parallel lines\",\"They are concentric circles\",\"They are intersecting circles\",\"They are random curves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first postulate describes the orbits as concentric circular orbits.\",\"topic\":\"Bohr’s stable orbit atomic model\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two types of subatomic particles present in the nucleus together called?\",\"options\":[\"Electrons\",\"Nucleons\",\"Orbits\",\"Shells\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that protons and neutrons are together called nucleons.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used to denote the atomic number of an element?\",\"options\":[\"A\",\"N\",\"Z\",\"e-\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The number of protons in the nucleus is the atomic number and is denoted by \'Z\'.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate mass of one proton?\",\"options\":[\"1837 u\",\"1 u\",\"0 u\",\"1.6 x 10^-19 u\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the mass of one proton is approximately 1u (1 Dalton).\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the atomic nucleus positively charged?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the presence of neutrons\",\"Due to the presence of electrons\",\"Due to the presence of protons\",\"Due to the empty space in the nucleus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the positive charge on the nucleus is due to the proton in it.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where is almost the entire mass of an atom concentrated?\",\"options\":[\"In the electrons\",\"In the extranuclear part\",\"In the nucleus\",\"In the empty space\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, almost the entire mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the energy of an electron in an atom?\",\"options\":[\"The number of neutrons\",\"The shell in which it is present\",\"The size of the nucleus\",\"The total mass number\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the energy of an electron is determined by the shell in which it is present.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An atom is electrically neutral because it contains equal numbers of:\",\"options\":[\"Protons and neutrons\",\"Neutrons and electrons\",\"Protons and electrons\",\"Nucleons and electrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The number of electrons is equal to the number of protons (Z), balancing the electrical charges.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an atom has 11 protons and 12 neutrons, what is its atomic mass number (A)?\",\"options\":[\"11\",\"12\",\"23\",\"1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mass number (A) is the total number of protons and neutrons (11 + 12 = 23).\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the conventional symbol 12 6 C, how many neutrons are present in the nucleus?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"6\",\"18\",\"0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Neutrons = Mass number (A) - Atomic number (Z). So, 12 - 6 = 6.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the atomic number (Z) of an element is 8, how many electrons revolve in its extranuclear part?\",\"options\":[\"16\",\"4\",\"8\",\"0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The number of electrons in the extranuclear part is equal to the number of protons (Z).\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which element is an exception as its nucleus does not contain any neutrons (for atomic mass 1u)?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Helium\",\"Oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the atomic nucleus of all elements except hydrogen contain neutrons.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the mass of an electron treated as negligible when calculating the mass of an atom?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has no charge\",\"Because it is located outside the nucleus\",\"Because its mass is 1837 times less than a hydrogen atom\",\"Because it moves at very high speeds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because the mass is 1837 times less than hydrogen, it can be treated as negligible.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If an atom has Z = 11 and A = 23, which of the following is correct?\",\"options\":[\"It has 23 protons\",\"It has 11 neutrons\",\"It has 12 neutrons\",\"It has 34 nucleons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Neutrons (N) = A - Z = 23 - 11 = 12.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The extranuclear part of the atom consists of:\",\"options\":[\"Protons and neutrons\",\"Only revolving electrons\",\"Electrons and empty space\",\"Nucleons and shells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the extranuclear part as electrons revolving around the nucleus and the empty space in between.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which subatomic particle has a mass almost equal to that of a proton?\",\"options\":[\"Electron\",\"Neutron\",\"Nucleus\",\"Positron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the mass of a neutron is approximately 1 u, which is almost equal to that of a proton.\",\"topic\":\"Atomic structure and Mass number\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What symbol is used to represent the ordinal number of an electron shell?\",\"options\":[\"x\",\"n\",\"e\",\"s\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a symbol ‘n’ is used for the ordinal number of a shell.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which shell is located nearest to the nucleus of an atom?\",\"options\":[\"L shell\",\"M shell\",\"K shell\",\"N shell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the shell nearest to the nucleus is called the first shell, which is the K shell.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the formula used to calculate the maximum number of electrons a shell can contain?\",\"options\":[\"n^2\",\"2n\",\"2n^2\",\"n + 2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The maximum number of electrons a shell can contain is obtained by the formula ‘2n^2’.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the energy of an electron change as the ordinal number \'n\' of the shell increases?\",\"options\":[\"The energy decreases\",\"The energy remains constant\",\"The energy increases\",\"The energy first increases then decreases\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As the magnitude of ‘n’ increases, the energy of an electron in that shell increases.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the shellwise distribution of electrons in an atom of an element?\",\"options\":[\"Atomic number\",\"Electronic configuration\",\"Shell capacity\",\"Ordinal distribution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The shellwise distribution of the electron in an atom of an element is called the electronic configuration of that element.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the ordinal number of a shell is n = 3, what is its maximum electron capacity?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"18\",\"32\",\"2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula 2n^2, for n=3, capacity = 2 x (3)^2 = 2 x 9 = 18.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The electronic configuration of sodium is written as 2, 8, 1. What does the number 1 represent?\",\"options\":[\"Electrons in the K shell\",\"Electrons in the L shell\",\"Electrons in the M shell\",\"Total number of protons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the configuration 2, 8, 1, the numbers indicate electrons in shells K, L, and M respectively.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the maximum capacity of shells, what is the electronic configuration of an atom with 7 electrons?\",\"options\":[\"2, 5\",\"5, 2\",\"7\",\"2, 4, 1\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The K shell takes 2 electrons (max capacity), and the remaining 5 go to the L shell.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following shells has the lowest energy level?\",\"options\":[\"N shell\",\"M shell\",\"L shell\",\"K shell\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Energy of an electron in the first shell (K shell) is the lowest.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the total electron number for the element Bromine (Br)?\",\"options\":[\"17\",\"18\",\"35\",\"9\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Table 5.7 shows the electron number for Bromine (Br) is 35.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/043_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what would be the numerical electronic configuration for Carbon (C), which has 6 electrons?\",\"options\":[\"2, 4\",\"4, 2\",\"6\",\"2, 2, 2\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Carbon has 6 electrons; 2 fill the K shell and the remaining 4 go to the L shell.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/043_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the maximum number of electrons that the N shell (n=4) can accommodate?\",\"options\":[\"18\",\"8\",\"32\",\"16\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text and table, the N shell (n=4) has a capacity of 32 electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the electronic configuration of Lithium (3 electrons) written as 2, 1 and not 3?\",\"options\":[\"Because the L shell must be filled first\",\"Because the K shell has a maximum capacity of 2 electrons\",\"Because the M shell is closer to the nucleus\",\"Because Lithium only has one shell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Electrons are distributed according to the maximum capacity of shells; K shell can only hold 2.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"An atom has an electronic configuration of 2, 8, 7. How many electrons are present in its L shell?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"7\",\"8\",\"17\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the numerical form 2, 8, 7, the second number (8) represents the L shell.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly describes the distribution of electrons in shells?\",\"options\":[\"Electrons fill shells with higher energy first\",\"Electrons are distributed randomly regardless of shell capacity\",\"Electrons fill shells in accordance with maximum capacity and increasing order of energy\",\"All shells have the same maximum capacity of 8 electrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states electrons get a place in shells in accordance with maximum capacity and increasing order of energy.\",\"topic\":\"Electronic configuration of elements\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for the outermost shell of an atom which determines its valency?\",\"options\":[\"Inner shell\",\"Valence shell\",\"Nucleus\",\"Core shell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the outermost shell is called the valence shell and it determines the valency of an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes \'valence electrons\'?\",\"options\":[\"Electrons in the first shell\",\"Total number of electrons in an atom\",\"Electrons in the outermost shell\",\"Electrons present inside the nucleus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the electrons in the outermost shell are called valence electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the gaseous elements Helium and Neon considered chemically inert?\",\"options\":[\"They have a very high valency\",\"They do not combine with any other atom\",\"They have only one shell\",\"They have an incomplete octet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Helium and Neon do not combine with any other atom, meaning they are chemically inert.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many valence electrons are shown in the outermost shell of the Carbon atom?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"6\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Looking at the Carbon atom in the figure, there are 4 electrons (dots) in its outermost shell.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/044_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the valency of an element that has a complete electron octet or duplet in its valence shell?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Eight\",\"Zero\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that valency is \'Zero\' when the electron octet (or duplet) is complete.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which atom has three electron shells (K, L, and M) in its electronic configuration?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen\",\"Carbon\",\"Neon\",\"Sodium\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Sodium (Na) with three concentric circles representing the K, L, and M shells.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/044_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The electronic configuration of Sodium is (2, 8, 1). What is its valency?\",\"options\":[\"8\",\"2\",\"1\",\"0\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if the number of valence electrons is four or less, the valency is the same as the number of valence electrons. Sodium has 1 valence electron.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an element has 7 valence electrons, what will be its valency according to the rule provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"7\",\"1\",\"8\",\"0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when an element has four or more valence electrons, the valency is the number by which the octet is short of completion (8 - 7 = 1).\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the electron capacity of the \'M\' shell according to the 2n² formula mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"2\",\"8\",\"18\",\"32\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text calculates the capacity of the M shell as 2 x 3² = 18.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Argon is an inert gas with 8 electrons in its \'M\' shell. Why is its valency zero even though the \'M\' shell capacity is 18?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has an electron octet\",\"Because it has an electron duplet\",\"Because it has no electrons in the K shell\",\"Because it is a gas\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that valency is zero when an electron octet is complete, and Argon has eight electrons in its valence shell.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Hydrogen has one electron in its \'K\' shell. Why is its valency \'one\'?\",\"options\":[\"It has a complete octet\",\"It has a complete duplet\",\"Its electron duplet is short by one electron\",\"It has zero valence electrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Hydrogen\'s electronic configuration shows one electron less than the complete duplet state, matching its valency of one.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct definition of valency as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"The total number of protons in the nucleus\",\"The number of chemical bonds formed by an atom\",\"The number of shells in an atom\",\"The mass of the atom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text begins by stating that valency means the number of chemical bonds formed by an atom.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An atom of an element has 3 valence electrons. What is its valency?\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"8\",\"0\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that valency is the same as the number of valence electrons if that number is four or less.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'electron duplet\' refer to in the context of Helium?\",\"options\":[\"Having two shells\",\"Having two electrons in the outermost K shell\",\"Having two protons in the nucleus\",\"Having two chemical bonds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says Helium has only one \'K\' shell containing two electrons, which is a completely filled outermost shell called a duplet.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which shell is the valence shell for the element Neon (2, 8)?\",\"options\":[\"K shell\",\"L shell\",\"M shell\",\"N shell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'L\' is the valence shell of neon.\",\"topic\":\"Valency and electronic configuration\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is considered the fundamental property of an element and its chemical identity?\",\"options\":[\"Mass number\",\"Atomic number\",\"Neutron number\",\"Number of shells\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the atomic number is a fundamental property of an element and its chemical identity.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Atoms of the same element having the same atomic number but different mass numbers are called ______.\",\"options\":[\"Isotones\",\"Isobars\",\"Isotopes\",\"Isomers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the definition in the text, atoms of the same element with different mass numbers are called isotopes.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a correct representation of the three isotopes of carbon?\",\"options\":[\"C-10, C-11, C-12\",\"C-12, C-13, C-14\",\"C-6, C-7, C-8\",\"C-14, C-15, C-16\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions carbon has three isotopes: C-12, C-13, and C-14.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Isotopes of an element have the same proton number but different ______ number.\",\"options\":[\"Electron\",\"Atomic\",\"Neutron\",\"Proton\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that isotopes have the same proton number but different neutron number.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the formula n = A - Z, how many neutrons are present in the isotope ¹⁴C?\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"14\",\"8\",\"20\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"From the table, for ¹⁴C: A=14, Z=6, so n = 14 - 6 = 8.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which isotope is used for nuclear fission and the production of electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Cobalt - 60\",\"Iodine - 131\",\"Sodium - 24\",\"Uranium - 235\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states Uranium-235 is used for nuclear fission and production of electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the medical treatment of fatal diseases like cancer, which radioactive isotope is used?\",\"options\":[\"Cobalt - 60\",\"Carbon - 14\",\"Sodium - 24\",\"Uranium - 235\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Cobalt-60 is used in the medical treatment of cancer.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does ¹³C have a mass number of 13 while its atomic number remains 6?\",\"options\":[\"It has 13 protons\",\"It has 7 neutrons\",\"It has 13 neutrons\",\"It has 7 protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the table, ¹³C has a proton number (Z) of 6 and a neutron number (n) of 7, making the mass number A = 6 + 7 = 13.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which isotope is specifically used in the treatment of goiter, a disease of the thyroid gland?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium - 24\",\"Iodine - 131\",\"Cobalt - 60\",\"Carbon - 14\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states Iodine-131 is used in the treatment of goiter.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An engineer needs to detect a crack in an underground pipe. Which isotope should be used?\",\"options\":[\"Uranium - 235\",\"Carbon - 14\",\"Sodium - 24\",\"Iodine - 131\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions Sodium-24 is used for detection of cracks (leakage) in underground pipes.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How are radioactive isotopes used in the food industry according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"To add flavor to food\",\"To increase the mass of food\",\"For food preservation from microbes\",\"To cook food faster\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states radioactive isotopes are used for food preservation from microbes.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An archaeologist finds an ancient wooden object. Which isotope will help determine its age?\",\"options\":[\"C - 12\",\"C - 13\",\"C - 14\",\"Cobalt - 60\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states C-14 is used for determining the age of archaeological objects.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table provided, what remains constant across all isotopes of Carbon?\",\"options\":[\"Mass number (A)\",\"Neutron number (n)\",\"Proton number (Z)\",\"The sum of A and Z\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows that for ¹²C, ¹³C, and ¹⁴C, the proton number (Z) is always 6.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If an isotope of an element has a higher mass number than another isotope of the same element, what must be true?\",\"options\":[\"It has more protons\",\"It has more neutrons\",\"It has a different atomic number\",\"It has fewer electrons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since isotopes have the same atomic (proton) number, a higher mass number must result from a higher number of neutrons (A = Z + n).\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following fields is NOT mentioned in the text as a field where isotopes are used?\",\"options\":[\"Agriculture\",\"Medicine\",\"Research\",\"Telecommunications\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions industry, agriculture, medicine, and research, but does not mention telecommunications.\",\"topic\":\"Isotopes\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the first nuclear reactor in India that went critical on 4th August 1956?\",\"options\":[\"Dhruva\",\"Apsara\",\"Cirus\",\"Purnima\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, \'Apsara\' at Bhabha Atomic Research Centre in Mumbai is the first nuclear reactor in India.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of a moderator in a nuclear reactor?\",\"options\":[\"To absorb neutrons\",\"To increase the speed of neutrons\",\"To reduce the speed of neutrons\",\"To generate electricity directly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a moderator like graphite or heavy water is used for reducing the speed of neutrons.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which substances are used as controllers to reduce the number of neutrons in a nuclear reactor?\",\"options\":[\"Graphite and heavy water\",\"Uranium and Thorium\",\"Boron and Cadmium\",\"Krypton and Barium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rods of boron, cadmium, beryllium etc. are used as controllers to reduce the number of neutrons by absorbing them.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the process of nuclear fission of Uranium-235, which two different elements are formed as nuclei?\",\"options\":[\"Thorium and Barium\",\"Krypton and Barium\",\"Krypton and Boron\",\"Uranium and Thorium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The nucleus of Uranium-235 undergoes nuclear fission to form nuclei of two different elements: Krypton-92 and Barium-141.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the chain reaction in a nuclear reactor kept under control?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the production of Barium\",\"To prevent a probable explosion\",\"To stop the generation of steam\",\"To reduce the amount of electricity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the chain reaction is kept under control to prevent the probable explosion.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many neutrons are released when the Uranium-236 nucleus disintegrates?\",\"options\":[\"1 neutron\",\"2 neutrons\",\"3 neutrons\",\"4 neutrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows 3 neutrons being released during the disintegration process.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/047_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The facility shown in the given figure is the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre. In which city is it located?\",\"options\":[\"New Delhi\",\"Mumbai\",\"Chennai\",\"Kolkata\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The caption for the photo identifies the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre as being in Mumbai.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/047_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a technician needs to remove heat from the fission process to generate steam, which substance should they use as a coolant?\",\"options\":[\"Boron\",\"Cadmium\",\"Water\",\"Graphite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the heat produced in the fission process is taken out by using water as coolant.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How is electricity finally generated in a nuclear reactor after the fission process?\",\"options\":[\"By direct conversion of neutrons into current\",\"By using steam to drive turbines\",\"By the chemical reaction of Krypton and Barium\",\"By the cooling of Boron rods\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water is transformed into steam, and by means of this steam, turbines are driven and electricity is generated.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why have Indian scientists developed a future plan for reactors based on the production of U-233 from Th-232?\",\"options\":[\"Because Uranium-235 is too dangerous\",\"Because India has large reserves of Thorium-232\",\"Because Thorium-232 does not produce heat\",\"Because Krypton-92 is unavailable in India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"India has large reserves of the element Thorium-232, leading to the development of this future plan.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What must happen to the neutrons released during fission for them to bring about the fission of more U-235 nuclei?\",\"options\":[\"Their speed must be increased\",\"Their speed must be decreased\",\"They must be absorbed by Boron\",\"They must be converted into protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that on decreasing the speed, these neutrons bring about fission of more U-235 nuclei.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which isotope is formed as an unstable intermediate when Uranium-235 is bombarded with a neutron, as shown in the given figure?\",\"options\":[\"Uranium-233\",\"Uranium-236\",\"Thorium-232\",\"Krypton-92\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows that U-235 plus a neutron results in the formation of U-236 before splitting.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/047_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how many nuclear reactors are currently functioning in India?\",\"options\":[\"Eight\",\"Fifty six\",\"Twenty two\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in India, a total of twenty two nuclear reactors are functioning.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a necessary condition to initiate the nuclear fission of the Uranium-235 isotope?\",\"options\":[\"Bombardment with high-speed protons\",\"Bombardment with slow-speed neutrons\",\"Cooling the nucleus with heavy water\",\"Absorbing neutrons with cadmium rods\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fission occurs on bombardment with slow speed of neutrons on the nucleus of Uranium-235.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of heavy water in the context of a nuclear reactor\'s operation?\",\"options\":[\"It acts as a fuel for the reactor\",\"It is used as a controller to absorb neutrons\",\"It is used as a moderator to reduce neutron speed\",\"It is the final product of the chain reaction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Graphite or heavy water is used as a moderator for reducing the speed of neutrons.\",\"topic\":\"Nuclear Reactor\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22156,57,8,'Science','en','Composition of Matter','[\"States of matter are solid, liquid and gas.\",\"Solid has definite shape and volume, particles are close and vibrate in fixed positions.\",\"Liquid has definite volume but takes shape of container, particles are close but can move past each other.\",\"Gas has no definite shape or volume, particles are far apart and move freely.\",\"Intermolecular forces are attractive forces between particles; stronger forces give more rigidity.\",\"In solids the forces are strong, in liquids moderate, in gases weak.\",\"Definite shape and volume → solid; definite volume only → liquid; neither → gas.\",\"Density is mass per unit volume; solids usually have higher density than liquids and gases.\",\"Compressibility is the ability to reduce volume under pressure; solids are hardly compressible, gases are highly compressible.\",\"Molecular formula shows the number of atoms of each element in a molecule.\",\"Valency is the number of chemical bonds an atom can form; hydrogen has valency 1, oxygen 2, nitrogen 3, carbon 4.\",\"Element is a pure substance made of one type of atom; compound is made of two or more different atoms bonded together; mixture is a combination of two or more substances that are not chemically bonded.\",\"Mixtures can be homogeneous (uniform composition, e.g. salt solution) or heterogeneous (different phases, e.g. sand in water).\",\"Colloids are heterogeneous mixtures with particle size between 1 µm and 100 µm, e.g. milk, fog.\",\"In a chemical reaction new substances are formed; the products have properties different from the reactants.\",\"When a solid is heated, it may melt (solid→liquid) or evaporate (solid→gas).\",\"When a liquid is heated, it may boil (liquid→gas).\",\"When a gas is cooled, it may condense (gas→liquid) or freeze (gas→solid).\",\"The law of conservation of mass states that mass is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.\",\"In a mixture, the components retain their individual properties; in a compound, the properties are new.\",\"The ratio of elements in a compound is fixed and expressed by its molecular formula.\",\"The strength of intermolecular forces determines the state of matter at a given temperature.\",\"The boiling point of a liquid increases with stronger intermolecular forces.\",\"The melting point of a solid increases with stronger intermolecular forces.\",\"The density of a substance is related to the mass and volume of its particles.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are the smallest particles of matter called?\",\"options\":[\"Molecules\",\"Atoms\",\"Cells\",\"Grains\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In science, the smallest particles that make up matter are referred to as atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary difference between ice, water, and steam?\",\"options\":[\"They are made of different substances\",\"They have different colors\",\"They are in different states of matter\",\"They have different weights\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ice (solid), water (liquid), and steam (gas) represent the three different physical states of the same substance.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are all objects (both seen and unseen) made of?\",\"options\":[\"Air\",\"Matter\",\"Water\",\"Energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that all objects around us, visible or invisible, are made of some form of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Into which state of matter would you classify \'Air\' from the given list?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gas\",\"Soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Air is a mixture of gases and therefore belongs to the gaseous state of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the classification criteria, which of these is a solid?\",\"options\":[\"Sherbat\",\"Cold drink\",\"Water\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Wood has a definite shape and volume, which are characteristics of the solid state.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What criteria are used to classify substances like soil, water, and air into three groups?\",\"options\":[\"Their color\",\"Their states of matter\",\"Their taste\",\"Their weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to classify matter into three groups based on their physical states: solid, liquid, and gas.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following pairs from the list belong to the same state of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Wood and Air\",\"Water and Sherbat\",\"Soil and Cold drink\",\"Cement and Air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Both water and sherbat are in the liquid state.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are asked to identify the \'odd one out\' based on the state of matter, which would it be?\",\"options\":[\"Soil\",\"Wood\",\"Cement\",\"Air\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Soil, wood, and cement are solids, while air is a gas.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which group would you place \'Cold drink\' and \'Sherbat\'?\",\"options\":[\"Solids\",\"Liquids\",\"Gases\",\"Vapors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cold drinks and sherbat are substances that flow and take the shape of their container, identifying them as liquids.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many various states of matter are typically identified for basic classification in this topic?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to classify the examples into three groups, corresponding to the three common states of matter: solid, liquid, and gas.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of force acts between the particles (atoms or molecules) of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Gravitational force\",\"Intermolecular force\",\"Magnetic force\",\"Frictional force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an intermolecular force of attraction acts between the particles of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do solids possess properties like a definite shape and volume?\",\"options\":[\"Because their particles move freely in all directions\",\"Because the intermolecular force is very weak\",\"Because particles are very close and vibrate at fixed positions\",\"Because they have high fluidity and low density\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Solids have definite shape and volume because their particles are very close and vibrate at fixed positions due to strong intermolecular forces.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the mustard seed experiment, what is provided to the seeds by pulling the diaphragm up and down?\",\"options\":[\"Mass\",\"Density\",\"Energy\",\"Volume\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that by pulling the diaphragm, we give energy (less or more) to the mustard seeds.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the action of pulling the thread represent in the context of the physical states of matter?\",\"options\":[\"The increase in the size of the atoms\",\"The movement of particles caused by giving energy\",\"The change in the chemical composition of the seeds\",\"The creation of a vacuum inside the jar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment illustrates how giving energy makes particles (represented by seeds) move differently, similar to states of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/049_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which physical state of matter has the \'minimum\' distance between its constituent particles?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gaseous\",\"Fluid\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to table 6.3, the distance between particles is minimum in the solid state.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the intermolecular force in the liquid state?\",\"options\":[\"It is strong enough to fix particles in definite positions\",\"It is very weak, allowing particles to move freely\",\"It is moderate—not strong enough to fix positions but holds particles together\",\"It is completely absent, allowing for high compressibility\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in liquids, the force is moderate; it doesn\'t fix positions but is strong enough to hold particles together.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A substance has a definite volume but its shape changes according to the container. What is its physical state?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gaseous\",\"Rigid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Liquids have a definite volume but an indefinite shape that changes with the container.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table in the given figure, which characteristic is \'Negligible\' for the solid state of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Volume\",\"Intermolecular force\",\"Compressibility\",\"Rigidity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Table 6.3 explicitly lists the compressibility of solids as \'Negligible\'.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/050_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the compressibility of gases as mentioned in the characteristics table?\",\"options\":[\"Negligible\",\"Very small\",\"Moderate\",\"Very high\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Table 6.3 shows that the compressibility of the gaseous state is \'Very high\'.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do gases not have a definite shape or a definite volume?\",\"options\":[\"Because their particles vibrate at fixed positions\",\"Because the intermolecular force is very weak and particles move freely\",\"Because the distance between particles is minimum\",\"Because they have high density and rigidity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gases lack definite shape and volume because very weak intermolecular forces allow particles to move freely and occupy all available space.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance is transitioned from a liquid state to a gaseous state, what happens to the distance between its particles?\",\"options\":[\"It changes from moderate to very large\",\"It changes from minimum to moderate\",\"It remains the same\",\"It decreases significantly\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Based on the table, the distance is \'Moderate\' in liquids and \'Very large\' in gases.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the relationship between the strength of the intermolecular force and the movement of particles?\",\"options\":[\"Stronger force leads to free movement in all directions\",\"Weaker force leads to vibration at fixed positions\",\"The strength of the force determines the extent of the movement\",\"Force and movement are not related to each other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The extent of the particle movement is determined by the strength of this force.\'\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which property is shared by both the liquid and gaseous states of matter according to the characteristics table?\",\"options\":[\"Definite Volume\",\"Fluidity\",\"Rigidity\",\"Negligible Compressibility\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Table 6.3 lists \'Fluid\' under the Fluidity\\/Rigidity column for both Liquid and Gaseous states.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the mustard seed experiment, what was the observed movement of seeds when the diaphragm was pulled \'slowly\'?\",\"options\":[\"Vibration in the same place\",\"Free movement in the whole jar\",\"Movement from one side to another\",\"No movement at all\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The observation table in the text records \'Vibration in the same place\' for the \'Slow\' mode of pulling.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the physical state (solid, liquid, or gas) of a substance according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only the color of the atoms\",\"The size of the container used\",\"The strength of the intermolecular force and particle movement\",\"The length of the thread used in the experiment\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the strength of the intermolecular force determines the extent of particle movement, which defines the state.\",\"topic\":\"Physical state of matter\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the criterion used for the second method of classification of matter described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Physical state of matter\",\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"Color and texture of matter\",\"Mass and volume of matter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the criterion used for this second method of classification is the \'chemical composition of matter\'.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Into which three types is matter classified based on its chemical composition?\",\"options\":[\"Solid, liquid and gas\",\"Metal, non-metal and metalloid\",\"Element, compound and mixture\",\"Atom, molecule and ion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, matter is classified into three types: \'element\', \'compound\', and \'mixture\'.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the smallest particles of an element?\",\"options\":[\"They contain two or more types of atoms.\",\"They contain identical atoms.\",\"They are always made of different elements.\",\"They are always mixtures of atoms.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the smallest particles of an element contain identical atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which \'Type of matter\' should be assigned to the material \'Brass\'?\",\"options\":[\"Element\",\"Compound\",\"Mixture\",\"Pure substance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies brass as a mixture (an alloy) containing atoms of copper and zinc.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/050_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which material would have its \'Chemical formula\\/composition\' column filled with the symbol \'C\'?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Carbon\",\"Oxygen\",\"Aluminium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carbon is an element, and its chemical symbol is \'C\'.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/050_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"How are the molecules of a compound formed?\",\"options\":[\"By mixing two different liquids together.\",\"By joining two or more types of atoms to each other.\",\"By separating identical atoms from a mixture.\",\"By keeping identical atoms in a bonded state.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that molecules of a compound are formed by joining two or more types of atoms to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a constituent molecule of the mixture \'Air\' as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Cu\",\"Zn\",\"Ar\",\"Sn\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Ar (Argon) as one of the main constituent molecules of air.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A molecule of water contains two hydrogen atoms joined to one atom of oxygen. Based on this, water is a\\/an ________.\",\"options\":[\"Element\",\"Compound\",\"Mixture\",\"Alloy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since it is formed by joining two different types of atoms, it is a compound.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which elements are present in the alloy called Bronze?\",\"options\":[\"Copper and Zinc\",\"Copper and Tin\",\"Zinc and Tin\",\"Nitrogen and Oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that bronze contains atoms of the elements copper (Cu) and tin (Sn).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a molecule of oxygen (O₂) considered an element rather than a compound?\",\"options\":[\"Because it contains two atoms.\",\"Because the two atoms are in a bonded state.\",\"Because it contains identical atoms.\",\"Because it is a gas at room temperature.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that smallest particles of an element contain identical atoms, like the two oxygen atoms in an oxygen molecule.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for Carbon dioxide as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"CO\",\"C2O\",\"CO2\",\"C2O2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists CO₂ as a constituent of air and as a material in the table.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is common to both Brass and Bronze?\",\"options\":[\"Both contain Zinc (Zn).\",\"Both contain Tin (Sn).\",\"Both contain Copper (Cu).\",\"Both are compounds.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Brass contains Cu and Zn, while Bronze contains Cu and Sn; therefore, Copper (Cu) is common to both.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance\'s smallest particles are of two or more types, it is classified as a ________.\",\"options\":[\"Element\",\"Compound\",\"Mixture\",\"Pure substance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the smallest particles in a mixture are of two or more types.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which material from the text is an element that exists as a molecule of two identical atoms?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Carbon\",\"Oxygen\",\"Brass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses oxygen as an example where each molecule contains two oxygen atoms in a bonded state.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of the following is NOT a constituent of air?\",\"options\":[\"N₂\",\"H₂O\",\"Sn\",\"CO₂\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists N₂, O₂, Ar, H₂O, and CO₂ as constituents of air; Sn (Tin) is mentioned as a part of bronze, not air.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical composition of matter\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical symbol for the element Iron as mentioned in the activity text?\",\"options\":[\"Fe\",\"Cu\",\"Al\",\"C\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the iron nail is made of the element iron (Fe).\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In early times, into which two categories were elements classified?\",\"options\":[\"Metals and Metalloids\",\"Non-metals and Metalloids\",\"Metals and Non-metals\",\"Lustrous and Pale\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in early times, elements were classified into \'Metals\' and \'Non metals\'.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which new type of element was discovered after the invention of new elements, following the initial classification?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Metalloid\",\"Carbon\",\"Aluminium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, \'After invention of new elements a new type \'Metalloid\' is discovered.\'\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which physical property is associated with an element having a \'shiny surface\'?\",\"options\":[\"Malleability\",\"Brittleness\",\"Lustre\",\"Paleness\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links different physical properties like lustre\\/paleness to the surface appearance.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an element \'flattens on hammering\', which property does it exhibit?\",\"options\":[\"Brittleness\",\"Malleability\",\"Paleness\",\"Lustre\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text pairs physical properties as lustre\\/paleness and malleability\\/brittleness, where flattening corresponds to malleability.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, what will happen to a coal piece (Carbon) when it is hammered?\",\"options\":[\"It will flatten into a sheet\",\"It will break into small pieces\",\"It will become shiny\",\"It will turn into a wire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Coal (Carbon) is a non-metal and the activity implies it belongs to the category of elements that break on hammering.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What observation would you record for the surface of an aluminium (Al) wire?\",\"options\":[\"It is pale\",\"It is dull\",\"It is shiny\",\"It breaks into pieces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Aluminium is a metal, and the table requires categorizing elements like Al as having a shiny surface.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which pair of elements from the text would show similar results in the hammering test?\",\"options\":[\"Iron and Carbon\",\"Copper and Aluminium\",\"Aluminium and Carbon\",\"Copper and Carbon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Iron, Copper, and Aluminium are metals that flatten, while Carbon breaks; thus Copper and Aluminium show similar results.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the basis for the classification of elements into Metals, Non-metals, and Metalloids?\",\"options\":[\"Their chemical symbols\",\"Their discovery date\",\"Their physical properties\",\"Their atomic weight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'According to that [physical properties] elements are classified.\'\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which element mentioned in the text is represented by the symbol \'Cu\'?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon\",\"Copper\",\"Coal\",\"Calcium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly identifies copper as (Cu).\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If an element is described as having \'paleness\' and \'brittleness\', which object from the activity most likely represents it?\",\"options\":[\"Iron nail\",\"Copper wire\",\"Aluminium wire\",\"Coal piece\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Coal (Carbon) is the only non-metal in the list, which typically lacks lustre (paleness) and breaks on hammering (brittleness).\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the relationship between \'lustre\' and \'paleness\' as used in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are the same property\",\"They are contrasting physical properties of surfaces\",\"They both describe how an element hammers\",\"They are chemical properties\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'lustre\\/paleness\' to categorize the surface appearance of different types of elements.\",\"topic\":\"Types of element\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main constituent of the black residue left behind when organic compounds are heated strongly?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Magnesium\",\"Carbon\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the black residue formed after heating organic compounds is mainly made of carbon.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a complex compound containing Magnesium?\",\"options\":[\"Hemoglobin\",\"Chlorophyll\",\"Cyanocobalamine\",\"Blue vitriol\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, chlorophyll is a complex compound that contains magnesium.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What type of compounds are petrol and cooking gas categorized as?\",\"options\":[\"Inorganic compounds\",\"Complex compounds\",\"Hydrocarbons\",\"Metallic compounds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions hydrocarbons like petrol and cooking gas as examples of organic compounds.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal atom is present at the center of the structure of Cyanocobalamine (Vitamin B-12)?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Magnesium\",\"Cobalt\",\"Copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that cyanocobalamine (vitamin B-12) contains cobalt.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When organic compounds are heated strongly in air, they combine with oxygen to form which state of matter?\",\"options\":[\"Solid residue\",\"Gaseous substances\",\"Liquid metal\",\"Crystalline salts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when such compounds are heated in air, they combine with oxygen to form gaseous substances.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are various atoms in the molecules of compounds held together?\",\"options\":[\"By physical mixing\",\"By magnetic force\",\"By chemical bonds\",\"By heat energy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes by stating that various atoms in the molecules of compounds are joined by chemical bonds.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What defines the structure of \'complex compounds\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They only contain carbon and hydrogen atoms\",\"They leave no residue upon heating\",\"They have a complex structure with a central metal atom\",\"They are formed by simple evaporation of water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes complex compounds as having a complex structure formed by many atoms with central metal atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a characteristic of inorganic compounds during the heating experiment?\",\"options\":[\"They always turn into gaseous hydrocarbons\",\"They decompose to leave a residue behind\",\"They always contain magnesium in the center\",\"They never undergo any change when heated\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines inorganic compounds as those that decompose on strong heating to leave a residue behind.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student heats a sample of sugar strongly in an evaporating dish, what is the most likely observation?\",\"options\":[\"It will turn into blue vitriol\",\"It will leave a blackish residue\",\"It will turn into limestone\",\"It will show no change in color\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sugar is an organic compound, and the text states organic compounds leave a blackish residue (carbon) if combustion is incomplete.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist finds a compound that contains Iron (Fe) in a complex molecular structure. Which compound is this likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"Chlorophyll\",\"Hemoglobin\",\"Common salt\",\"Washing soda\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies hemoglobin as a complex compound that contains iron.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment described, which of the following substances would be classified as an inorganic compound?\",\"options\":[\"Glucose\",\"Urea\",\"Limestone\",\"Camphor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists limestone as an example of an inorganic compound.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a black residue remain when some compounds undergo incomplete combustion?\",\"options\":[\"Because the metal atoms turn black\",\"Because oxygen is removed from the air\",\"Because carbon remains behind as residue\",\"Because the chemical bonds become visible\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that in case combustion is not complete, black coloured carbon remains behind as residue.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following groups contains only organic compounds according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Common salt, Soda, Rust\",\"Camphor, Sugar, Urea\",\"Blue vitriol, Limestone, Glucose\",\"Hemoglobin, Chlorophyll, Salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies camphor, sugar, glucose, and urea as organic compounds.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the fundamental difference between organic compounds and complex compounds like hemoglobin as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Organic compounds never contain carbon\",\"Complex compounds must have a central metal atom in their structure\",\"Only organic compounds are joined by chemical bonds\",\"Complex compounds do not contain many atoms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that complex compounds include metal atoms in the center of a structure formed by many atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance is heated and leaves a residue that is NOT black, and the substance is known to be \'Soda\', how is it classified?\",\"options\":[\"Organic compound\",\"Hydrocarbon\",\"Inorganic compound\",\"Complex compound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text classifies soda as an inorganic compound because it leaves a residue after decomposition.\",\"topic\":\"Types of compound\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the part of matter having uniform composition called?\",\"options\":[\"Mixture\",\"Phase\",\"Component\",\"Solution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the part of matter having uniform composition as a phase.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When all the components of a mixture form one phase, it is called a _____.\",\"options\":[\"Heterogeneous mixture\",\"Saturated mixture\",\"Homogeneous mixture\",\"Compound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a mixture forming one phase is a homogeneous mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a single phase according to the \'Always remember\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Oil and water in a container\",\"Sand and water in a beaker\",\"A heap of stones\",\"Blue vitriol and sand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that all particles of a solid that stay together constitute a single phase, like a heap of stones.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is air considered to be in a single phase?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a liquid\",\"Because all gases present together constitute a single phase\",\"Because it has no components\",\"Because it is a heterogeneous mixture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that all gases present together constitute a single phase, giving air as an example.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A liquid substance along with all the soluble substances dissolved in it together constitute _____.\",\"options\":[\"Two phases\",\"A single phase\",\"Multiple phases\",\"A heterogeneous mixture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a liquid and its soluble dissolved substances constitute a single phase.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you mix oil and water in the same container, how many phases will be present?\",\"options\":[\"One phase\",\"Separate phases\",\"No phases\",\"A uniform phase\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that liquids present together but not mixed, like oil and water, constitute separate phases.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Sea water is an example of a single phase because it consists of _____.\",\"options\":[\"Only pure water\",\"Water and insoluble sand\",\"Water and soluble substances dissolved in it\",\"Gases mixed with solids\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sea water is given as an example of a liquid with soluble substances forming a single phase.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment described, which beaker contains a homogeneous mixture after stirring?\",\"options\":[\"Beaker 1 (Sand and water)\",\"Beaker 2 (Blue vitriol and water)\",\"Beaker 3 (Blue vitriol and sand)\",\"None of the beakers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Blue vitriol dissolves in water to form a single phase, making it a homogeneous mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When components of a mixture are distributed into two or more phases, it is called a _____.\",\"options\":[\"Homogeneous mixture\",\"Heterogeneous mixture\",\"Pure substance\",\"Single phase\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines a mixture with two or more phases as a heterogeneous mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Rain drops present together in a container constitute how many phases?\",\"options\":[\"One phase\",\"Two phases\",\"Multiple phases\",\"Separate phases for each drop\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a liquid or all its drops present together constitute a single phase.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if Beaker 1 contains sand and water, what should be recorded in the column \'Number of phases in the mixture\'?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"0\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sand does not dissolve in water, so they remain as two separate phases (solid and liquid).\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/053_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if Beaker 2 contains blue vitriol and water, what is the \'Type of mixture\' that should be recorded after stirring?\",\"options\":[\"Heterogeneous mixture\",\"Homogeneous mixture\",\"Separated mixture\",\"Two-phase mixture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Blue vitriol dissolves in water to form one phase, which is a homogeneous mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Types of mixtures\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/053_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances called according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Suspension\",\"Colloid\",\"Solution\",\"Solvent\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances as a solution.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which component of a solution is present in the largest proportion?\",\"options\":[\"Solute\",\"Solvent\",\"Residue\",\"Filtrate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the component present in the largest proportion is called the solvent.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a \'solid in solid\' solution?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar syrup\",\"Brass\",\"Vinegar\",\"Chlorinated water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists alloys like brass, steel, and stainless steel as examples of solid in solid solutions.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the diameter of the solid particles in a suspension?\",\"options\":[\"Less than 10⁻⁴ m\",\"Larger than 10⁻⁴ m\",\"Exactly 10⁻⁵ m\",\"Smaller than 10⁻⁵ m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the diameter of solid particles in a suspension is larger than 10⁻⁴ m.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with three beakers, which mixture is described as translucent?\",\"options\":[\"Salt and water\",\"Saw dust and water\",\"Milk and water\",\"Sugar and water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the mixture of water and milk in the third beaker as translucent.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a suspension be separated by filtration using ordinary filter paper?\",\"options\":[\"The particles are smaller than the pores\",\"The particles are larger than the pores\",\"The particles are dispersed evenly\",\"The mixture is homogeneous\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Suspension particles are larger than 10⁻⁴ m, causing them to remain on the filter paper as residue.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a \'liquid in gas\' type of colloid?\",\"options\":[\"Smoke\",\"Milk\",\"Fog\",\"Alloy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides fog and clouds as examples of \'liquid in gas\' colloids.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student mixes 10 ml of vinegar in 100 ml of water, what type of solution is formed?\",\"options\":[\"Solid in liquid\",\"Liquid in liquid\",\"Gas in liquid\",\"Solid in solid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Vinegar is listed in the text as an example of a liquid in liquid solution.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to a laser beam when it is passed through a colloid like milk and water?\",\"options\":[\"It is completely absorbed\",\"It is partly transmitted and partly scattered\",\"It passes through without any change\",\"It is completely reflected back\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in a colloid, light is partly transmitted and partly scattered.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following mixtures will NOT leave a residue on a filter paper?\",\"options\":[\"Saw dust and water\",\"Salt and water\",\"A suspension\",\"A mixture with particles larger than 10⁻⁴ m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that solutions (like salt and water) pass through filter paper, unlike suspensions.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The diameter of particles in milk is around ______.\",\"options\":[\"10⁻⁴ m\",\"10⁻⁵ m\",\"10⁻⁶ m\",\"10⁻⁷ m\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the diameter of particles in a colloid like milk is around 10⁻⁵ m.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the properties described, why is a salt solution considered uniform?\",\"options\":[\"It is a heterogeneous mixture\",\"It scatters a laser beam\",\"Its composition is uniform throughout the bulk\",\"It can be separated by ordinary filtration\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the composition of a homogeneous mixture (solution) is uniform throughout the bulk.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A mixture of smoke is categorized as which type of colloid?\",\"options\":[\"Liquid in gas\",\"Solid in liquid\",\"Solid in gas\",\"Gas in liquid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly identifies smoke as a \'solid in gas\' type of colloid.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can\'t a colloid be separated by an ordinary filter paper even though it is a heterogeneous mixture?\",\"options\":[\"The particles are too heavy\",\"The pores of the filter paper are larger than the colloid particles\",\"The particles are dissolved in the solvent\",\"The mixture is transparent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because the pores of ordinary filter paper are larger than the colloid particles (10⁻⁵ m), they cannot be separated.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which observation correctly identifies the mixture in the second beaker (saw dust and water)?\",\"options\":[\"It is transparent and passes through filter paper\",\"It is translucent and scatters light\",\"It is heterogeneous and shows a separate aqueous phase\",\"It is a homogeneous solid in liquid solution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the saw dust mixture as a heterogeneous mixture where a separate water phase is seen.\",\"topic\":\"Solution, Suspension and Colloid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of matter is described as having the simplest composition?\",\"options\":[\"Mixture\",\"Compound\",\"Element\",\"Solution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that an element is a type of matter having the simplest composition.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What determines whether a substance formed from two or more units is a compound or a mixture?\",\"options\":[\"The total mass of the units\",\"Whether the units are in a joined or separate state\",\"The color of the units used\",\"The shape of the container\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that whether units are in a joined state or separate decides if the matter is a compound or a mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment described, what was the mass of iron filings taken in the first dish?\",\"options\":[\"4 g\",\"7 g\",\"11 g\",\"2 g\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies taking 7 g iron filings in the first dish.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic color of sulphur particles mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Black\",\"Silver\",\"Yellow\",\"Red\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies sulphur particles as being yellow.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when a horseshoe magnet is brought near a simple mixture of iron filings and sulphur?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur is attracted to the magnet\",\"Both iron and sulphur are attracted\",\"Iron filings are attracted to the magnet\",\"Neither is attracted to the magnet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a mixture, iron particles retain their property of being attracted towards a magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why were the iron and sulphur components considered to be in a \'free state\' before heating?\",\"options\":[\"Because they were both black in color\",\"Because they were not mixed at all\",\"Because they retained their individual properties like magnetism and color\",\"Because they were in a liquid state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says components were in a free state because the properties of iron (magnetism) and sulphur (yellow color) were unchanged.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What change is observed in the matter after heating the iron filings and sulphur together?\",\"options\":[\"The yellow color becomes brighter\",\"The magnet attracts the iron more strongly\",\"The characteristic yellow color of sulphur disappears\",\"The iron filings turn yellow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that after heating and cooling, the characteristic yellow color of sulphur disappeared.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the effect of a horseshoe magnet on the matter formed after heating iron and sulphur?\",\"options\":[\"It attracts the whole mass\",\"It has no effect on the matter\",\"It only attracts the sulphur\",\"It separates the iron from the sulphur\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when heated together and cooled, there was no effect of the magnet on the resulting matter.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What process takes place between iron and sulphur due to heating?\",\"options\":[\"Physical mixing\",\"Chemical combination\",\"Evaporation\",\"Magnetization\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a chemical combination took place between iron and sulphur due to heating.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are the atoms of iron and sulphur held together in the new compound?\",\"options\":[\"By physical stirring\",\"By magnetic force\",\"By chemical bonds\",\"By gravitational pull\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the atoms became joined by chemical bonds to form molecules of a new compound.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student mixes 7g of iron and 4g of sulphur without heating, what is the resulting matter classified as?\",\"options\":[\"Compound\",\"Element\",\"Mixture\",\"Pure substance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the resulting matter from mixing (without heating) as a mixture where properties remain unchanged.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which observation proves that a new substance was formed after heating the iron and sulphur?\",\"options\":[\"The mass remained 11g\",\"The iron filings remained black\",\"The magnet no longer attracted the iron particles\",\"The glass rod was used for stirring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The loss of magnetic attraction indicates the formation of a new compound different from the original components.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the smallest unit of the new compound formed in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Atom\",\"Molecule\",\"Element\",\"Mixture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that atoms join to form \'molecules of a new compound\'.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, which of the following is a characteristic of a compound?\",\"options\":[\"Components can be separated by a magnet easily\",\"Components retain their original colors\",\"Properties are different from the original components\",\"Units are in a separate state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows the compound formed is different from the original components (no magnetic effect, color change).\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the experiment, what does the disappearance of the yellow color signify?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur has evaporated\",\"Sulphur has undergone a chemical change to form a compound\",\"The iron filings have covered the sulphur\",\"The magnet has removed the sulphur\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The disappearance of the characteristic color indicates a chemical combination into a new compound.\",\"topic\":\"Let us understand compounds\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What indicates the number of atoms of each constituent element present in one molecule of a compound?\",\"options\":[\"Atomic number\",\"Molecular formula\",\"Chemical bond\",\"Valency\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the molecular formula indicates the number of atoms of each constituent element present in one molecule of a compound.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a molecular formula, how is the respective number of atoms of constituent elements shown?\",\"options\":[\"As superscripts\",\"As prefixes\",\"As subscripts\",\"As suffixes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A molecular formula includes the symbols of constituent elements and their respective number as subscripts.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The ability of an atom to join another atom with a chemical bond is called its ______.\",\"options\":[\"Valency\",\"Molecularity\",\"Proportion\",\"Composition\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines valency as the ability of an atom to join another atom with a chemical bond.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many chemical bonds does an atom form with other atoms?\",\"options\":[\"Always one bond\",\"As many as its atomic number\",\"As many as its valency\",\"Twice its valency\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that an atom forms as many chemical bonds with other atoms as its valency.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"During which period did scientists deduce the valencies of elements through experiments?\",\"options\":[\"15th and 16th century\",\"17th and 18th century\",\"18th and 19th century\",\"19th and 20th century\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the \'Do you know?\' section, scientists performed experiments and deduced valencies during the 18th and 19th century.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What generally remains constant for an element in its various compounds?\",\"options\":[\"Number of molecules\",\"Valency\",\"State of matter\",\"Physical property\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that generally the valency of an element remains constant in its various compounds.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the constituent elements for the compound Hydrogen chloride?\",\"options\":[\"H and O\",\"H and Cl\",\"C and H\",\"Mg and Cl\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the table, Hydrogen chloride has the molecular formula HCl, which consists of Hydrogen (H) and Chlorine (Cl).\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/055_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the molecular formula of Methane shown in the given figure, how many atoms of Hydrogen (H) are present in one molecule?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table shows the molecular formula of Methane is CH4, where the subscript 4 indicates four atoms of Hydrogen.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/055_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If an atom has a valency of 3, how many chemical bonds will it form with other atoms?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since an atom forms as many bonds as its valency, a valency of 3 means it will form 3 bonds.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A compound is formed by a ______ proportion of constituent elements.\",\"options\":[\"Random\",\"Definite\",\"Changing\",\"Variable\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text starts by stating there is a definite proportion of the constituent elements in a compound.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"From the molecular formula MgCl2, what can be analyzed about the number of atoms of Magnesium (Mg) and Chlorine (Cl)?\",\"options\":[\"2 atoms of Mg and 1 atom of Cl\",\"1 atom of Mg and 1 atom of Cl\",\"1 atom of Mg and 2 atoms of Cl\",\"2 atoms of Mg and 2 atoms of Cl\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In MgCl2, there is no subscript for Mg (meaning 1) and a subscript 2 for Cl.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is considered a chemical property of an atom according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The symbol of the element\",\"The ability to join another atom with a chemical bond\",\"The number of molecules in a compound\",\"The subscript used in a formula\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the ability of joining to another atom with a chemical bond is a chemical property of each atom.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a molecule of Water (H2O) has 2 atoms of Hydrogen and 1 atom of Oxygen, what is the total number of atoms in one molecule?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"2 atoms of H + 1 atom of O = 3 total atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What information is NOT provided by a molecular formula according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Symbols of constituent elements\",\"Number of atoms as subscripts\",\"The color of the compound\",\"Number of atoms of each constituent element\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions symbols and numbers of atoms, but does not mention the color of the compound.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is valency represented by a number?\",\"options\":[\"To show the weight of the atom\",\"To indicate the number of bonds an atom forms\",\"To count the number of elements in a compound\",\"To show the year the element was discovered\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Valency is a number that indicates the ability of an atom to join another atom with chemical bonds.\",\"topic\":\"Molecular formula and valency\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the valency of Hydrogen, which serves as the basis for deducing the valencies of other elements?\",\"options\":[\"0\",\"1\",\"2\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the basis for deducing valencies is the univalency of hydrogen, meaning its valency is 1.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the cross-multiplication method, what is the first step in writing the molecular formula of a compound?\",\"options\":[\"To cross multiply the valencies\",\"To write the valency of each element\",\"To write the symbols of constituent elements\",\"To divide by a suitable number\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 1 of the cross-multiplication method is to write symbols of constituent elements.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the valency of Carbon as provided in the textbook examples?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text and tables explicitly list the valency of Carbon (C) as 4.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the formula C2O4 simplified to CO2 in the final step of writing the molecular formula?\",\"options\":[\"Because Oxygen is more reactive than Carbon\",\"To ensure the number of atoms are the smallest possible whole numbers\",\"Because the valency of Carbon changes during the reaction\",\"To match the number of bonds formed by Hydrogen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Step 5 states that the number of constituent atoms in the final molecular formula should be the smallest possible whole numbers.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the molecular formula of a compound is H2O, what is the valency of Oxygen based on the number of bonds it forms with Hydrogen?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In H2O, Oxygen forms 2 bonds with Hydrogen, so its valency is 2.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which method is used to write the molecular formula of a compound when the valencies of the constituent elements are known?\",\"options\":[\"Addition method\",\"Division method\",\"Cross multiplication method\",\"Subtraction method\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the molecular formula can be written from known valencies by the method of cross multiplication.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Using the cross-multiplication method, what is the molecular formula for a compound formed by Nitrogen (valency 3) and Hydrogen (valency 1)?\",\"options\":[\"NH\",\"N3H\",\"NH3\",\"N2H3\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cross multiplying N (3) and H (1) gives N1H3, which is written as NH3.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the final molecular formula for a compound formed by Iron (Fe) with valency 2 and Sulfur (S) with valency 2?\",\"options\":[\"Fe2S2\",\"FeS\",\"FeS2\",\"Fe2S\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cross multiplication gives Fe2S2, which is divided by 2 to get the smallest whole number ratio, FeS.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the molecular formula of the compound formed by Carbon (valency 4) and Hydrogen (valency 1)?\",\"options\":[\"CH\",\"C4H\",\"CH2\",\"CH4\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Cross multiplying C (4) and H (1) results in the formula CH4.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the provided text, what is the valency of the element Bromine in the compound HBr?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In HBr, Bromine forms 1 bond with Hydrogen (which is univalent), so its valency is 1.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The valency of Nitrogen is 3. How many chemical bonds are present in a molecule of gaseous Nitrogen (N2)?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks how many bonds are in N2 given the valency of N is 3; the number of bonds corresponds to the valency.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the compound Sodium Hydride (NaH), what is the valency of Sodium (Na)?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In NaH, Sodium is bonded to one Hydrogen atom, so its valency is 1.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, look at the row for the compound CH4. What is the value that should be in the \'Valency X\' column for this row?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In CH4, Carbon (X) forms 4 bonds with Hydrogen, so its valency is 4.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/056_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the correct molecular formula for the compound formed by the pair Carbon (C) and Oxygen (O)?\",\"options\":[\"CO\",\"C2O\",\"CO2\",\"C2O4\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows C (valency 4) and O (valency 2). Cross multiplication and simplification results in CO2.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/056_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the molecular formula of a compound is H2S, how many bonds does the Sulfur atom form with Hydrogen atoms?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In H2S, the subscript 2 for Hydrogen indicates that the Sulfur atom is bonded to 2 Hydrogen atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Writing the molecular formula\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22157,57,8,'Science','en','Metals and Nonmetals','[\"Metals, non‑metals and metalloids are the three broad groups of elements.\",\"Metals are usually solid at room temperature, have a shiny lustre, are hard, ductile and malleable, conduct heat and electricity, and have high melting and boiling points.\",\"Non‑metals are solid, liquid or gas at room temperature, are dull, brittle, poor conductors of heat and electricity, and have low melting and boiling points.\",\"Metalloids have properties intermediate between metals and non‑metals.\",\"Ductility – the ability of a metal to be drawn into a wire; malleability – the ability to be hammered into a sheet.\",\"Lustre – light reflects from a polished metal surface, giving it a shine.\",\"Density – most metals are denser than water; many non‑metals are lighter than water.\",\"Sonority – metals produce a ringing sound when struck.\",\"Noble metals (gold, silver, platinum, palladium, rhodium) are resistant to corrosion and oxidation.\",\"Corrosion – reaction of a metal with oxygen, water or other chemicals forming oxides or other compounds.\",\"Alloys – homogeneous mixtures of two or more metals or a metal with a non‑metal; e.g., stainless steel, bronze, brass.\",\"Oxidation of iron produces rust (Fe₂O₃·nH₂O).\",\"Graphite (an allotrope of carbon) is a good conductor of electricity among non‑metals.\",\"Some non‑metals (e.g., chlorine, iodine) are liquids at room temperature; hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen are gases.\",\"Metals form positive ions (cations) by losing electrons; non‑metals form negative ions (anions) by gaining electrons.\",\"When a metal reacts with a dilute acid it usually forms a salt and releases hydrogen gas.\",\"When a metal reacts with water, only alkali metals (Na, K) react vigorously; others may need steam.\",\"When a metal reacts with oxygen it forms an oxide; metal oxides are usually basic, non‑metal oxides are usually acidic.\",\"The density of a metal can be calculated as mass per unit volume (g\\/cc).\",\"The melting point of a metal is the temperature at which it changes from solid to liquid; the boiling point is when it changes from liquid to gas.\",\"The colour of a metal can change due to oxidation (e.g., copper turns green due to copper carbonate).\",\"The term ‘noble’ refers to metals that do not corrode easily under normal conditions.\",\"The best conductors of heat are silver, copper and aluminium; the best conductors of electricity are copper, aluminium, silver and gold.\",\"The best conductors of electricity are metals except lead, which is a poor conductor of both heat and electricity.\",\"The best conductors of heat are metals except mercury and gallium, which are liquid at room temperature.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Into how many general types have scientists classified all the elements?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that scientists classified all the elements into three general types: metals, nonmetals, and metalloids.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, all objects or materials in the world are made from elements, compounds, or their ________.\",\"options\":[\"Solutions\",\"Mixtures\",\"Atoms\",\"Molecules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that materials are made from elements, compounds, or their mixtures.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed as a metal in the introductory text?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon\",\"Oxygen\",\"Platinum\",\"Sulfur\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Platinum is explicitly mentioned in the list of metals provided in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic shine of metals referred to as in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Hardness\",\"Conductivity\",\"Lustre\",\"Malleability\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Metals have lustre\', which refers to their shine.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance is a good conductor of heat and electricity, it is most likely a ________.\",\"options\":[\"Nonmetal\",\"Metalloid\",\"Metal\",\"Mixture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines metals as being good conductors of heat and electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a material that can be made into a thin wire. Based on the text, this material is likely to be a ________.\",\"options\":[\"Compound\",\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that wires or sheets can be made from metals.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What type of ions do metals produce when they lose their valence electrons?\",\"options\":[\"Negatively charged ions\",\"Neutral ions\",\"Positively charged ions\",\"No ions are formed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that metals lose valence electrons to produce positively charged ions.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific term used for the positively charged ions formed by metals?\",\"options\":[\"Anions\",\"Cations\",\"Valence\",\"Metalloids\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly identifies positively charged ions as \'cations\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical property described, how do metals achieve a positive charge?\",\"options\":[\"By gaining protons\",\"By losing valence electrons\",\"By sharing electrons with nonmetals\",\"By gaining valence electrons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that metals lose their valence electrons to produce cations.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a physical property of metals mentioned in the text that allows them to be used for making sheets?\",\"options\":[\"They are soft\",\"They are good conductors\",\"They are hard and can be made into sheets\",\"They lose electrons easily\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the hardness of metals to the fact that sheets can be made from them.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following metals exists in a liquid state at room temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Mercury\",\"Sodium\",\"Iron\",\"Silver\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that mercury and gallium are exceptions to the solid state and are liquid at room temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to light when it hits a cleaned or freshly cut surface of a metal?\",\"options\":[\"It is absorbed\",\"It gets reflected\",\"It passes through\",\"It changes color\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, light gets reflected from the cleaned or freshly cut surface, making the metal look lustrous.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which metals are exceptions to the general rule that metals are hard and cannot be cut easily?\",\"options\":[\"Copper and Aluminium\",\"Gold and Silver\",\"Sodium and Potassium\",\"Iron and Lead\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that sodium and potassium are soft and can be easily cut by a knife.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The property by which a metal can be pulled through a hole to form a wire is called:\",\"options\":[\"Malleability\",\"Sonority\",\"Lustre\",\"Ductility\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Ductility is defined in the text as the property where a metal is pulled through a hole to form a wire.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When an iron nail is hammered into a thin sheet, which property of metals is being demonstrated?\",\"options\":[\"Ductility\",\"Malleability\",\"Hardness\",\"Sonority\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines malleability as the property where hammering a metal forms a thin sheet.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following metals are considered the \'best\' conductors of heat?\",\"options\":[\"Lead and Mercury\",\"Sodium and Potassium\",\"Silver, Copper and Aluminium\",\"Iron and Lithium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that Silver, copper and aluminium are the best conductors of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal is an exception to the property of conduction, being a poor conductor of both heat and electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Lead\",\"Gallium\",\"Copper\",\"Aluminium\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text identifies lead as an exception that is neither a good conductor of heat nor electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific density of Lithium mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1.0 g\\/cc\",\"0.53 g\\/cc\",\"13.6 g\\/cc\",\"0.92 g\\/cc\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the density of lithium is only 0.53 g\\/cc.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why are sodium, potassium, and lithium considered exceptions regarding density?\",\"options\":[\"They have higher density than water\",\"They have lower density than water\",\"They have the same density as water\",\"They have no density\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions these three metals as exceptions because they have lower density than water.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Metals produce a sound when struck. This property is known as:\",\"options\":[\"Ductility\",\"Malleability\",\"Sonority\",\"Lustre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines sonority as the property where metals produce sound on striking.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A doctor\'s pressure gauge contains a grey coloured liquid. Based on the \'Physical State\' section, what is this metal?\",\"options\":[\"Silver\",\"Lead\",\"Mercury\",\"Iron\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to recall the liquid in a pressure gauge and identifies mercury as a metal in liquid state.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which group of metals are exceptions to the general rule of having high melting and boiling points?\",\"options\":[\"Fe, Cu, Al\",\"Hg, Ga, Na, K\",\"Au, Ag, Pt\",\"Pb, Li, Fe\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists Hg, Ga, Na, and K as exceptions to high melting and boiling points.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given a piece of Sodium and a piece of Iron, how would their hardness differ according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium is hard, Iron is soft\",\"Both are equally hard\",\"Sodium is soft and can be cut by a knife, Iron is hard\",\"Both are soft and can be cut by a knife\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states metals are generally hard, but sodium is an exception that is soft and can be cut with a knife.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of metals is directly responsible for the functioning of a school bell?\",\"options\":[\"Malleability\",\"Ductility\",\"Sonority\",\"Conduction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the school bell\'s function to sonority, the property of producing sound on striking.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the following: A substance is a metal, but it does not conduct electricity well and is not a good conductor of heat. Which metal is it?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Silver\",\"Aluminium\",\"Lead\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies lead as the specific exception that is neither a good conductor of heat nor electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Physical Properties of Metals\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following nonmetals exists in a liquid state at ordinary temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon (C)\",\"Sulphur (S)\",\"Bromine (Br2)\",\"Phosphorus (P)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that Bromine (Br2) occurs as a liquid at ordinary temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic color of the nonmetal Bromine?\",\"options\":[\"Yellow\",\"Brown\",\"Black\",\"Colorless\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, bromine is brown in color.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Although most nonmetals are nonlustrous, which of the following is an exception and possesses lustre?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur\",\"Phosphorus\",\"Iodine crystals\",\"Coal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that nonmetals do not have lustre, except diamond and iodine crystals.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student hammers a piece of coal, what will most likely happen based on the physical properties of nonmetals?\",\"options\":[\"It will be drawn into a thin wire.\",\"It will be flattened into a thin sheet.\",\"It will break into small pieces due to brittleness.\",\"It will change its color to yellow.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Solid nonmetals like coal are brittle, meaning they break when hammered.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which allotrope of carbon is an exception to the rule that nonmetals are bad conductors of electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Diamond\",\"Graphite\",\"Coal\",\"Boron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Graphite is mentioned as a very good conductor of electricity, unlike other nonmetals.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which nonmetal is recognized as the hardest natural substance?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur\",\"Boron\",\"Diamond\",\"Iodine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies diamond (an allotrope of carbon) as the hardest natural substance.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what can be concluded about the ductility and malleability of nonmetals?\",\"options\":[\"They are ductile but not malleable.\",\"They are malleable but not ductile.\",\"They are both ductile and malleable.\",\"They are neither ductile nor malleable.\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that nonmetals are neither ductile nor malleable.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the group of nonmetals that occur in a gaseous state at ordinary temperature.\",\"options\":[\"C, S, P\",\"Br2, I, B\",\"H2, N2, O2\",\"Diamond, Graphite, Coal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists H2, N2, and O2 as examples of nonmetals that occur as gases.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"While most nonmetals have low melting points, which two solid nonmetals melt at high temperatures?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon and Boron\",\"Sulphur and Phosphorus\",\"Bromine and Iodine\",\"Hydrogen and Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions carbon and boron as exceptions that melt at high temperatures.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a general physical property of nonmetals regarding their density?\",\"options\":[\"They have very high densities.\",\"They have low densities.\",\"Their density is equal to metals.\",\"They have no density.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that nonmetals have low densities.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is graphite used in electrical applications despite being a nonmetal?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is the hardest substance.\",\"Because it is a very good conductor of electricity.\",\"Because it has a very low melting point.\",\"Because it is highly malleable.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Graphite is an exception among nonmetals as it is a very good conductor of electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Evaluate the following statement: \'All nonmetals are soft and have low melting points.\' Is this statement entirely correct according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, all nonmetals follow these rules.\",\"No, because Diamond is hard and Carbon\\/Boron have high melting points.\",\"No, because all nonmetals are actually hard.\",\"No, because all nonmetals have high melting points.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides exceptions: Diamond is the hardest substance, and Carbon\\/Boron have high melting points.\",\"topic\":\"Nonmetals and Physical Properties\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following metals is mentioned as being highly malleable in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Arsenic\",\"Silicon\",\"Aluminium\",\"Germanium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists Gold, Silver, and Aluminium as highly malleable metals.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical symbol for Antimony as provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"As\",\"Si\",\"Ge\",\"Sb\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies Antimony with the symbol (Sb).\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Elements that have properties intermediate between metals and nonmetals are known as:\",\"options\":[\"Malleable metals\",\"Metalloids\",\"Noble gases\",\"Nonmetals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines elements with intermediate properties between metals and nonmetals as metalloids.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the definition of metalloids, what can be said about the properties of Silicon (Si)?\",\"options\":[\"It has properties exactly like Gold.\",\"It has properties only of nonmetals.\",\"It has properties intermediate between metals and nonmetals.\",\"It is a highly malleable metal like Silver.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Silicon is listed as a metalloid, which means its properties are intermediate between metals and nonmetals.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these elements is categorized as a metalloid in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Silver\",\"Aluminium\",\"Arsenic\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Arsenic (As) is explicitly listed as an example of a metalloid.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the specific property shared by Arsenic, Silicon, and Germanium?\",\"options\":[\"They are all highly malleable.\",\"They can be made into wires of 1\\/5000 mm diameter.\",\"They have properties intermediate between metals and nonmetals.\",\"They are all types of gold.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"These elements are grouped together as metalloids because they share intermediate properties.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist finds an element that can be beaten into a sheet with a thickness of 1\\/10,000 mm. Which element is this most likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"Silicon\",\"Gold\",\"Antimony\",\"Germanium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a sheet with thickness 1\\/10,000 mm can be made from gold.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you need to create a wire with a diameter of 1\\/5000 mm, which metal should you use according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Arsenic\",\"Silicon\",\"Antimony\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a wire with diameter 1\\/5000 mm can be made from gold.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the classification of Germanium (Ge) differ from that of Aluminium?\",\"options\":[\"Germanium is a metal, while Aluminium is a metalloid.\",\"Germanium is a metalloid, while Aluminium is a highly malleable metal.\",\"Both are highly malleable metals.\",\"Both are metalloids with intermediate properties.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists Aluminium as a highly malleable metal and Germanium as a metalloid.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly pairs an element with its classification based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Silver : Metalloid\",\"Antimony (Sb) : Metalloid\",\"Arsenic (As) : Highly malleable metal\",\"Silicon (Si) : Metal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Antimony is listed under metalloids, whereas Silver is a metal and Arsenic\\/Silicon are metalloids, not metals.\",\"topic\":\"Metalloids\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is considered the basis of the chemical behaviour of elements?\",\"options\":[\"Atomic mass\",\"Electronic configuration\",\"Physical state\",\"Color of the metal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, electronic configuration is the basis of chemical behaviour of elements.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Majority of metals have up to how many electrons in their outermost shell?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Seven\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that majority of metals have upto three electrons in their outermost shell.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the electronic configuration of the metal Aluminium (Al)?\",\"options\":[\"2, 8, 1\",\"2, 8, 2\",\"2, 8, 3\",\"2, 8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows the electronic configuration of Aluminium (atomic number 13) is 2, 8, 3.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/060_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What are the positively charged ions formed by metals called?\",\"options\":[\"Anions\",\"Cations\",\"Valence electrons\",\"Oxides\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Metals lose valence electrons to form positively charged ions, which are called cations.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many electrons (e-) does a Magnesium (Mg) atom lose to form a Magnesium ion (Mg++)?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the equation Mg ⟶ Mg++ + 2e-, indicating the loss of 2 electrons.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/060_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the general nature of metal oxides?\",\"options\":[\"Acidic\",\"Neutral\",\"Basic\",\"Gaseous\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that metal oxides are basic in nature.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What products are formed when a metal oxide reacts with an acid?\",\"options\":[\"Metal and Oxygen\",\"Salt and Water\",\"Salt and Hydrogen gas\",\"Metal and Hydrogen gas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides the reaction: Metal oxide + Acid ⟶ Salt + Water.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When zinc dust reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid, which gas is released?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that most metals react with dilute acids to release hydrogen gas.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student brings a glowing splinter near a test tube where a metal is reacting with dilute acid and hears a sound. What does this observation indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The formation of water\",\"The release of hydrogen gas\",\"The formation of a basic oxide\",\"The loss of protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The sound from the glowing splinter indicates the presence of hydrogen gas released during the metal-acid reaction.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these metals reacts with cold water to produce its hydroxide and hydrogen gas?\",\"options\":[\"Zinc\",\"Aluminium\",\"Potassium\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that sodium and potassium react with cold water to produce hydroxides and hydrogen gas.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What specific condition does magnesium metal require to react with water and produce hydrogen gas?\",\"options\":[\"Cold water\",\"Steam\",\"Dilute acid\",\"Oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that magnesium metal requires steam to give reactions similar to sodium\'s reaction with cold water.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical properties described, which statement is true about the formation of ions in metals?\",\"options\":[\"Metals gain electrons to become anions.\",\"Metals lose valence electrons to become cations.\",\"Metals lose protons to become cations.\",\"Metals share electrons to form metal oxides.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines ion formation in metals as a tendency to lose valence electrons to form positively charged cations.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of Metals\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Most nonmetals have how many electrons in their valence shells?\",\"options\":[\"1 to 3\",\"4 to 7\",\"8 to 10\",\"Exactly 2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, most nonmetals have 4 to 7 electrons in their valence shells.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the negatively charged ions formed by nonmetals called?\",\"options\":[\"Cations\",\"Positrons\",\"Anions\",\"Neutrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nonmetals have a tendency to accept electrons to form negatively charged ions called anions.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the general nature of nonmetal oxides?\",\"options\":[\"Basic\",\"Neutral\",\"Acidic\",\"Amphoteric\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the oxides of nonmetals are acidic in nature.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do nonmetals react with dilute acids?\",\"options\":[\"They react vigorously\",\"They do not react\",\"They form hydrogen gas\",\"They form salt and water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook explicitly states that nonmetals do not react with dilute acids.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical properties described, what is the product of the reaction: SO3 + H2O?\",\"options\":[\"H2SO3\",\"H2SO4\",\"H2CO3\",\"Na2CO3\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows that SO3 + H2O forms H2SO4 (Sulphuric acid).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a Nitrogen atom with electronic configuration (2, 5) accepts 3 electrons, what will be the electronic configuration of the resulting Nitride ion?\",\"options\":[\"(2, 2)\",\"(2, 5)\",\"(2, 8)\",\"(2, 8, 3)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nitrogen (2, 5) accepts 3 electrons to complete its shell, resulting in (2, 8).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific charge shown on the Oxide ion after Oxygen accepts electrons?\",\"options\":[\"O-\",\"O2-\",\"O3-\",\"O+\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows O + 2e- —► O2-, indicating a 2- charge.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/061_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the electronic configuration of the Chloride ion after the Chlorine atom accepts one electron?\",\"options\":[\"(2, 8, 7)\",\"(2, 8, 6)\",\"(2, 8, 8)\",\"(2, 7)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the Chlorine atom (2, 8, 7) becoming a Chloride ion (2, 8, 8).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/061_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is formed when Carbon dioxide (CO2) reacts with water?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphuric acid\",\"Sulphurous acid\",\"Carbonic acid\",\"Hydrochloric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states CO2 + H2O —► H2CO3, which is Carbonic acid.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the products when a nonmetal oxide reacts with a base like NaOH?\",\"options\":[\"Soluble salt and water\",\"Acid and water\",\"Metal and oxygen\",\"Nonmetal and hydrogen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Nonmetal oxides react with bases to form soluble salt and water.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"An element has an atomic number of 17 and a valence shell containing 7 electrons. Based on the text, this element is a ____.\",\"options\":[\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Noble gas\",\"Base\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists Chlorine (Atomic number 17, configuration 2, 8, 7) as a nonmetal.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a Chlorine atom (2, 8, 7) become a negatively charged ion (2, 8, 8)?\",\"options\":[\"It loses one electron\",\"It accepts one electron\",\"It reacts with a dilute acid\",\"It reacts with oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Nonmetals form anions by accepting electrons in their valence shell to achieve a stable configuration.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical properties of nonmetals\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is classified as a noble metal in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Palladium\",\"Sodium\",\"Magnesium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists gold, silver, platinum, palladium, and rhodium as noble metals.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which state do noble metals occur in nature?\",\"options\":[\"Combined state\",\"Liquid state\",\"Elemental state\",\"Gaseous state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Noble metals occur in nature in the elemental state.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which noble metal is specifically mentioned for its antibacterial property?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Platinum\",\"Silver\",\"Rhodium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Silver is used in medicines because it has antibacterial properties.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which metals are used as catalysts according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Gold and Silver\",\"Platinum and Palladium\",\"Silver and Rhodium\",\"Gold and Platinum\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Platinum and Palladium metals are used as catalysts.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the percentage of gold in 24 carat gold?\",\"options\":[\"91.66%\",\"75.00%\",\"100%\",\"58.33%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Gold which is 100 percent pure is called 24 carat gold.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is pure gold (24 carat) not used for making ornaments?\",\"options\":[\"It is too expensive\",\"It is very soft and bends easily\",\"It reacts with air and water\",\"It has no shine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pure gold is soft, so ornaments made from it bend or break due to pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What do goldsmiths add to pure gold to make it suitable for ornaments?\",\"options\":[\"Iron or Zinc\",\"Copper or Silver\",\"Platinum or Palladium\",\"Lead or Tin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Goldsmiths mix pure gold with a certain proportion of copper or silver to make it harder.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these are common uses for both gold and silver?\",\"options\":[\"Catalysts and medicines\",\"Ornaments, medals, and electronic devices\",\"Antibacterial medicines and catalysts\",\"Only for making coins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists ornaments, medals, and electronic devices as uses for both gold and silver.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the rate of gold differ in shops according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because of the shop\'s location\",\"Because of the different carat values (purity)\",\"Because of the age of the gold\",\"Because of the color of the gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The rate differs because gold is sold at different carat values (purity levels).\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the purity table, what is the percentage of gold in 18 carat gold?\",\"options\":[\"91.66%\",\"50.00%\",\"75.00%\",\"58.33%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table provided in the text shows 18 carat gold is 75.00% pure.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a gold ornament is 50% pure, what is its carat value?\",\"options\":[\"10 carat\",\"12 carat\",\"14 carat\",\"22 carat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table indicates that 50.00% purity corresponds to 12 carat gold.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A customer buys a gold coin with 41.66% purity. What carat value is this coin?\",\"options\":[\"10 carat\",\"12 carat\",\"14 carat\",\"18 carat\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table shows that 10 carat gold has a percentage of 41.66%.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which noble metal has a unique medical application not mentioned for the others?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Silver\",\"Platinum\",\"Palladium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Silver is the only metal mentioned as being used in medicines due to its antibacterial property.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing 24 carat and 22 carat gold, which statement is true based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"24 carat is harder than 22 carat\",\"22 carat contains 100% gold\",\"22 carat is more suitable for ornaments than 24 carat\",\"24 carat contains copper and silver\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"22 carat is used for ornaments because 24 carat is too soft and may break.\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the carat value and the percentage of gold?\",\"options\":[\"As carat value increases, purity decreases\",\"As carat value decreases, purity decreases\",\"Carat value and purity are not related\",\"Carat value is always double the percentage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table shows that higher carat values (like 24) have higher percentages (100%), while lower carats (like 10) have lower percentages (41.66%).\",\"topic\":\"Noble Metals and Purity of Gold\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process called when gases in the air react with metals in the presence of moisture to form metal compounds?\",\"options\":[\"Galvanization\",\"Corrosion\",\"Varnishing\",\"Alloying\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines corrosion as the process where metals are affected by reacting with gases in the air and moisture.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A reddish coloured deposit is formed on iron when it reacts with which of the following gases?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Hydrogen sulphide\",\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a reddish coloured deposit is formed on iron by reaction with oxygen gas.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas is responsible for the formation of a blackish coloured deposit on silver?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen sulphide\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon monoxide\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a blackish coloured deposit is formed on silver by reaction with hydrogen sulphide gas.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what specific compound is formed when copper reacts with carbon dioxide and moisture?\",\"options\":[\"Copper oxide\",\"Copper sulphide\",\"Copper chloride\",\"Copper carbonate\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that green coloured copper carbonate is formed by the reaction of copper with carbon dioxide and moisture.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are layers of oil, grease, or paint applied to metal surfaces?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the weight of the metal\",\"To make the metal surface rough\",\"To lose contact with air and prevent chemical reactions\",\"To help the metal react faster with moisture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Applying these layers ensures the contact of the metal surface with air is lost, preventing the chemical reaction of corrosion.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a technician wants to protect iron from corrosion using metal plating, which metal is specifically mentioned in the text for this purpose?\",\"options\":[\"Silver\",\"Zinc\",\"Copper\",\"Gold\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that iron is protected by zinc plating.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds an old copper vessel that has turned green. Based on the textbook, what caused this change?\",\"options\":[\"Reaction with oxygen gas only\",\"Reaction with hydrogen sulphide gas\",\"Reaction with carbon dioxide gas and moisture\",\"Application of a layer of varnish\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that a greenish deposit forms on copper due to reaction with carbon dioxide gas.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed in the text as a substance used to prevent corrosion by coating the metal?\",\"options\":[\"Varnish\",\"Grease\",\"Water\",\"Paint\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists oil, grease, varnish, and paint as protective layers; water actually promotes corrosion.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason the chemical reaction of corrosion cannot occur after a metal is plated with another non-corroding metal?\",\"options\":[\"The metal becomes harder\",\"The contact of the metal surface with air is lost\",\"The metal changes into a gas\",\"The moisture in the air is destroyed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plating prevents corrosion because the contact of the metal surface with air is lost, stopping the chemical reaction.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the information about silver and copper, what can be inferred about the role of different gases in corrosion?\",\"options\":[\"All metals react only with oxygen to corrode\",\"Different metals react with different specific gases to form deposits\",\"Corrosion only happens in the absence of moisture\",\"Only iron undergoes a change in color during corrosion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text shows iron reacts with oxygen, copper with carbon dioxide, and silver with hydrogen sulphide, indicating gas specificity.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what was the original metal used to make the surface of this famous statue?\",\"options\":[\"Iron\",\"Silver\",\"Copper\",\"Zinc\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the figure states that the original surface of the statue was made of copper.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/062_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the statue appears green today. What does this green color represent according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"A layer of green paint applied by artists\",\"An example of corrosion due to copper carbonate formation\",\"A protective layer of zinc plating\",\"The natural color of pure oxygen on the surface\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains the green look is due to copper carbonate formation, which is an example of corrosion.\",\"topic\":\"Corrosion\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/062_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal with nonmetals, called?\",\"options\":[\"Metalloid\",\"Alloy\",\"Compound\",\"Ore\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines an alloy as a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals or a metal with nonmetals.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following elements are combined to form the alloy known as Bronze?\",\"options\":[\"Copper and Zinc\",\"Iron and Carbon\",\"Copper and Tin\",\"Iron and Chromium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the alloy bronze is formed from copper and tin.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Stainless steel utensils used at home are made of an alloy of iron with which other elements?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon, chromium and nickel\",\"Copper, tin and zinc\",\"Silicon, phosphorus and carbon\",\"Nickel, copper and tin\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that stainless steel utensils are made of an alloy of iron with carbon, chromium, and nickel.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How are the constituent elements of an alloy mixed during their preparation?\",\"options\":[\"In equal proportions always\",\"As per the requirement\",\"Only in a 2:1 ratio\",\"Randomly without measurement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Alloys are made by mixing the constituent elements as per the requirement.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nature of the mixture in an alloy?\",\"options\":[\"Heterogeneous\",\"Colloidal\",\"Homogeneous\",\"Suspension\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"An alloy is defined specifically as a homogeneous mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a stainless steel vessel develops vertical cracks, what is the most likely reason?\",\"options\":[\"It contains too much chromium\",\"It was made using copper instead of nickel\",\"It was made using too much carbon\",\"It is over 1500 years old\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that vertical cracks in stainless steel vessels occur when copper is used instead of costly nickel.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the iron pillar at Kutubminar still lustrous even after 1500 years?\",\"options\":[\"It is made of pure, unmixed iron\",\"It is painted with a special rust-proof paint\",\"It is made from an alloy of iron, carbon, silicon, and phosphorus\",\"It is kept in a vacuum-sealed chamber\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The pillar remains lustrous because it is an alloy containing small proportions of carbon, silicon, and phosphorus mixed in iron.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A manufacturer wants to create a \'cheap variety\' of stainless steel. Based on the text, which substitution would they likely make?\",\"options\":[\"Replace iron with carbon\",\"Replace nickel with copper\",\"Replace chromium with tin\",\"Replace phosphorus with silicon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a cheap variety of stainless steel is made sometimes by using copper instead of costly nickel.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a nonmetal used in the alloy of the ancient pillar mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Nickel\",\"Phosphorus\",\"Tin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The pillar contains carbon, silicon, and phosphorus; phosphorus is the nonmetal listed among the options.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of creating different types of alloys as suggested by the text?\",\"options\":[\"To make all metals look like gold\",\"To fulfill specific requirements of properties\",\"To make the metals heavier for construction\",\"To ensure the metals can be easily broken\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Alloys are made by mixing elements \'as per the requirement\', implying specific properties are sought.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, for approximately how many years has the tall pillar remained lustrous?\",\"options\":[\"500 years\",\"1000 years\",\"1500 years\",\"2000 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the figure of the Kutubminar pillar states it was made about 1500 years ago.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/063_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the pillar shown is an alloy. Which of these sets of elements were mixed with iron to prevent it from rusting?\",\"options\":[\"Copper and Tin\",\"Carbon, Silicon and Phosphorus\",\"Chromium and Nickel\",\"Zinc and Carbon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the pillar in the figure contains small proportions of carbon, silicon, and phosphorus mixed in iron.\",\"topic\":\"Alloy\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/063_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22158,57,8,'Science','en','Pollution','[\"Pollution is the contamination of the environment by harmful substances that damage living organisms and ecosystems.\",\"Air pollution is caused by burning fuels, industrial emissions, vehicle exhaust, and natural events like volcanic eruptions.\",\"Key air pollutants: sulphur dioxide (SO₂), nitrogen oxides (NOₓ), carbon monoxide (CO), particulate matter (PM), dust, and methane (CH₄).\",\"Ozone layer protects life from UV‑B rays; its depletion increases skin cancer and eye irritation.\",\"Greenhouse gases (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, CFCs) trap heat and cause global warming and climate change.\",\"Acid rain forms when SO₂ and NOₓ mix with rainwater, producing sulphuric and nitric acids, damaging soil, water bodies and structures.\",\"Water pollution comes from biological (bacteria, algae), inorganic (heavy metals, salts) and organic (pesticides, detergents) pollutants.\",\"Soil pollution reduces fertility, alters pH, and introduces toxic metals that enter the food chain.\",\"Preventive measures: use filters in factories, proper waste disposal, ban on CFCs, clean water treatment, and public awareness.\",\"Air Quality Index (AQI) tells how clean or polluted the air is in a city.\",\"Industrial accidents (e.g., Bhopal gas leak) show the severe health impact of toxic gas releases.\",\"Human activities like burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and improper waste disposal are main pollution sources.\",\"Natural causes of pollution include volcanic eruptions, earthquakes, dust storms, and forest fires.\",\"Vehicles with two‑stroke engines emit more pollutants than four‑stroke engines because of incomplete combustion.\",\"Water bodies become polluted when sewage, industrial effluents, and agricultural runoff enter them.\",\"Air, water, and soil pollution are interrelated; waste dumped in one medium can contaminate the others.\",\"Legislation such as the Water Pollution Prevention Act (1974), Air Pollution Prevention Act (1981), and Environmental Protection Act (1986) regulate pollution control.\",\"Public bodies like the Central Pollution Control Board supervise compliance with pollution laws.\",\"Preventive actions: use clean energy, recycle waste, plant trees, and maintain proper sanitation.\",\"Education and community participation are essential for effective pollution control.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the term used for the contamination of the natural environment that is harmful to ecosystems?\",\"options\":[\"Industrialization\",\"Pollution\",\"Mining\",\"Transportation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines pollution as the contamination of the natural environment that will be harmful to ecosystems.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the primary reason many problems have arisen on the earth?\",\"options\":[\"Natural disasters\",\"Animal migration\",\"Human interference in nature\",\"Changes in the sun\'s heat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that many problems have arisen on the earth due to human interference in the nature.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed in the text as a cause of pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Organic farming\",\"Mining\",\"Tree plantation\",\"Water conservation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mining is explicitly mentioned in the text as one of the factors causing pollution on the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the source of the grey smoke shown in the middle image?\",\"options\":[\"A factory chimney\",\"A burning forest\",\"A yellow car\",\"A mining blast\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The middle image in the figure shows smoke being emitted from the exhaust of a yellow car.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the left-most image shows a factory located next to a body of water. What specific problem does this most likely represent?\",\"options\":[\"Noise pollution\",\"Water pollution\",\"Soil erosion\",\"Overpopulation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The image shows industrial structures next to a river with visible changes in the water, representing water pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/064_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity involves the \'indiscriminate use\' of substances leading to pollution according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Use of pesticides and fertilizers\",\"Use of public transportation\",\"Use of mining tools\",\"Use of industrial machinery\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'indiscriminate use of pesticides and fertilizers\' as a cause of pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the text describe the impact of pollution on living things?\",\"options\":[\"It only affects plants\",\"It is beneficial for new species\",\"It is affecting human beings and harmful to ecosystems\",\"It has no effect on the natural environment\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states pollution is harmful to ecosystems and is affecting human beings.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a city experiences a sudden increase in the number of vehicles on the road, which cause of pollution mentioned in the text is being increased?\",\"options\":[\"Mining\",\"Industrialization\",\"Transportation\",\"Pesticide use\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Transportation is listed in the text as a factor causing pollution, which relates directly to vehicle use.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer decides to spray large amounts of chemicals on his crops every day without measuring the requirement. This is an example of which cause of pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Industrialization\",\"Indiscriminate use of pesticides\",\"Mining\",\"Increasing population\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the indiscriminate use of pesticides and fertilizers as a cause of pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is a direct result of \'increasing population\'?\",\"options\":[\"Improved environment\",\"Decrease in mining\",\"Pollution on the earth\",\"Reduction in transportation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'increasing population\' as one of the factors causing pollution on the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Pollution\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Factors that affect the natural functions of an ecosystem and cause harmful effects on abiotic and biotic factors are known as ____.\",\"options\":[\"Nutrients\",\"Pollutants\",\"Catalysts\",\"Decomposers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines pollutants as factors affecting natural functions of the ecosystem and causing harmful effects on abiotic and biotic factors.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are the two main types of pollutants?\",\"options\":[\"Solid and Liquid\",\"Visible and Invisible\",\"Natural and Man-made\",\"Toxic and Non-toxic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that pollutants may be natural or man-made.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic effect of pollutants on the environment as mentioned in the topic?\",\"options\":[\"They make the environment poisonous and unhealthy\",\"They increase the growth of biotic factors\",\"They stabilize the natural functions of the ecosystem\",\"They help in the recycling of abiotic factors\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that pollutants make the environment poisonous and unhealthy.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does nature handle natural pollutants over time?\",\"options\":[\"They remain in the environment forever\",\"They are converted into man-made pollutants\",\"They are destroyed in due course of time by nature\'s rule\",\"They increase the toxicity of the soil indefinitely\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that natural pollutants are destroyed in due course of time by nature\'s rule.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true regarding man-made pollutants according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are easily destroyed by nature\'s rule\",\"They are not destroyed by nature\'s rule\",\"They are always degradable\",\"They do not affect abiotic factors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that while natural pollutants are destroyed by nature\'s rule, man-made pollutants are not.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance is causing harmful effects on the non-living components of an ecosystem, it is affecting the ____ factors.\",\"options\":[\"Biotic\",\"Abiotic\",\"Natural\",\"Man-made\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Abiotic factors refer to the non-living components of the ecosystem mentioned in the definition of pollutants.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the contrast between the left and right sides of the Earth represent?\",\"options\":[\"Day and night cycles\",\"Summer and winter seasons\",\"A healthy environment versus a polluted environment\",\"Natural pollutants versus man-made pollutants\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a healthy green Earth on one side and a brown, smoke-filled Earth on the other, representing the impact of pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/064_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure and its title, who is making the plea \'Save me my children!\'?\",\"options\":[\"The factories\",\"The Earth\",\"The rainbow\",\"The pollutants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the Earth held in hands with the caption \'Save me my children!\', indicating the Earth is asking for protection.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/064_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"When are natural materials referred to as pollutants?\",\"options\":[\"Whenever they are used by humans\",\"Only when they are man-made\",\"When they affect the natural functions of the ecosystem\",\"When they are destroyed by nature\'s rule\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A material is a pollutant if it affects the natural functions of the ecosystem and causes harmful effects.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Use your brain power\' section, what is one way to classify whether a pollutant will persist in the environment?\",\"options\":[\"By its color and smell\",\"By whether it is degradable or non-degradable\",\"By its weight and volume\",\"By its cost of production\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to identify whether pollutants are degradable or non-degradable under the \'Use your brain power\' section.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why might we not perceive the adverse effects of natural materials during their normal use?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are always man-made\",\"Because they are not pollutants until they interfere with ecosystem functions\",\"Because they never cause harm to biotic factors\",\"Because they are not found in nature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies that materials are only pollutants when they reach levels or states that affect natural ecosystem functions.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a direct consequence of pollutants according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Environment becomes healthy\",\"Natural functions of ecosystem are affected\",\"Nature\'s rule is strengthened\",\"Biotic factors become poisonous\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The definition of pollutants states they are factors affecting natural functions of the ecosystem.\",\"topic\":\"Pollutants\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for the contamination of air by harmful substances like poisonous gases, smoke, and microbes?\",\"options\":[\"Air purification\",\"Air pollution\",\"Atmospheric mixture\",\"Gaseous combustion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines air pollution as the contamination of air by harmful substances like poisonous gases, smoke, particulate matter, and microbes.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is categorized as a natural reason for air pollution in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Atomic energy plants\",\"Industrialization\",\"Volcanic eruption\",\"Burning of fuel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Volcanic eruption is listed under the \'Natural reasons\' section of the text.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which gas is released during both volcanic eruptions and forest fires?\",\"options\":[\"Ammonium chloride\",\"Hydrogen sulfide\",\"Nitrogen oxide\",\"Carbon monoxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hydrogen sulfide is mentioned as an example of materials released in both volcanic eruptions and forest fires.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the third image showing vehicles on a road represents which specific manmade reason for air pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Industrialization\",\"Atomic energy blasts\",\"Burning of fuel\",\"Burning of solid waste\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The third image in the figure shows vehicles, which release pollutants through the burning of fuels like petrol and diesel.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/065_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the table in the given figure, which of these is a gaseous material released specifically during an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur powder\",\"Cotton seed powder\",\"Water vapors\",\"Ammonium chloride\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table under \'Natural reasons\' states that earthquakes release poisonous gases and water vapors from inside the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/065_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which manmade factor is responsible for releasing \'cotton seed powder\' into the air?\",\"options\":[\"Atomic energy plants\",\"Industrialization\",\"Burning of agricultural waste\",\"Volcanic eruptions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that industrialization releases smoke, sulphur powder, nitrogen oxide, and cotton seed powder from factories.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following elements is used in atomic energy plants and can release radiations into the air?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen\",\"Sulphur\",\"Uranium\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the use of elements like uranium, thorium, graphite, and plutonium in atomic energy plants.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a large amount of dust, soil, and pollens are mixed with the air during a storm, what type of pollution reason is this?\",\"options\":[\"Manmade reason\",\"Industrial reason\",\"Natural reason\",\"Atomic reason\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cyclones and dust storms, which mix dust, soil, and pollens with air, are classified as natural reasons.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is released into the air due to the burning of solid waste in open spaces?\",\"options\":[\"Microbes\",\"Radiations\",\"Air pollutants\",\"Spores of fungi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that burning solid waste and agricultural waste in open space causes air pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a solid material that emerges specifically during a volcanic eruption?\",\"options\":[\"Graphite\",\"Ammonium chloride\",\"Cotton seed powder\",\"Thorium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ammonium chloride is listed as an example of solid, gaseous, and liquid materials emerging from a volcanic eruption.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Spores of bacteria and fungi are classified under which reason for air pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Industrialization\",\"Microbes in air\",\"Atomic energy plants\",\"Fuel combustion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Microbes in air\' (spores of bacteria and fungi) as the fifth natural reason for air pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas is NOT mentioned as being released by the burning of fuels like coal and kerosene?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon monoxide\",\"Nitrogen oxide\",\"Sulphur dioxide\",\"Hydrogen\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Hydrogen is mentioned under volcanic eruptions, but not under the burning of fuels like coal or kerosene.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is a common pollutant released by both forest fires and the burning of fuel?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur dioxide\",\"Uranium\",\"Cotton seed powder\",\"Ammonium chloride\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sulphur dioxide is listed as a pollutant for both forest fires (natural) and burning of fuel (manmade).\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a manmade reason for air pollution involving the release of radiations?\",\"options\":[\"Industrialization\",\"Atomic energy plants and blasts\",\"Burning of solid waste\",\"Cyclones and dust storms\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that atomic energy plants and blasts release radiations into the air.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student observes smoke coming from a forest fire. Based on the text, which category of reasons does this belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Manmade reasons\",\"Natural reasons\",\"Industrial reasons\",\"Atomic reasons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Forest fires are explicitly listed under the \'Natural reasons\' section of the textbook text.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollution and its Reasons\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During which period did London experience a thick fog due to air pollution that lasted for five consecutive days?\",\"options\":[\"3rd - 6th Dec. 1962\",\"5th - 9th Dec. 1952\",\"2nd Dec. 1984\",\"August 1948\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that thick fog occurred in London during 5th - 9th Dec. 1952 due to coal smoke.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which city was named as the \'BLACK CITY\' in 1948 due to smoke and soot causing darkness during the day?\",\"options\":[\"London\",\"Bhopal\",\"Petersburg\",\"Maharashtra\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In 1948, Petersburg was named \'BLACK CITY\' when smoke and soot caused a night-like situation during the day.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many people died in the Bhopal gas accident that occurred on the night of 2nd Dec. 1984?\",\"options\":[\"Five thousand\",\"Ten thousand\",\"Eight thousand\",\"Twelve thousand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the \'Do you know?\' section, eight thousand people died due to the accidental gas leakage in Bhopal.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What was the primary cause of the thick SMOG that covered London in December 1952?\",\"options\":[\"Industrial leakage of methane\",\"Smoke due to burning of coal\",\"Excessive use of pesticides\",\"Volcanic eruptions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that smoke due to the burning of coal had mixed to form the smog in London.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the specific effect of Carbon monoxide (CO) on human health?\",\"options\":[\"Irritation of the respiratory tract\",\"Lowered oxygen carrying capacity of blood\",\"Mental weakness\",\"Skin cancer and asthma\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carbon monoxide lowers the O₂ carrying capacity of blood.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A worker in a factory that uses coal and mineral oil as fuel is suffering from excess mucus and a persistent cough. Which pollutant is most likely responsible?\",\"options\":[\"Methane (CH₄)\",\"Dust\",\"Sulphur dioxide (SO₂)\",\"Pesticides\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"SO₂ is sourced from factories using coal\\/mineral oil and causes excess mucus and cough.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which disease is specifically associated with the inhalation of \'Dust\' as an air pollutant?\",\"options\":[\"Silicosis\",\"Asthma\",\"Skin cancer\",\"Mental weakness\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table lists Silicosis as the effect of Dust from vehicular and industrial smoke.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Prolonged exposure to which of the following pollutants can lead to mental weakness and even death?\",\"options\":[\"Oxides of nitrogen\",\"Particulate matter\",\"Pesticides\",\"Sulphur dioxide (SO₂)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table indicates that production and use of pesticides can cause mental weakness and death due to prolonged exposure.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is diagnosed with poisoning and skin cancer due to air pollution, which source of pollutant should be investigated?\",\"options\":[\"Vehicular smoke\",\"Industrial leakage of Methane\",\"Burning of coal in factories\",\"Use of pesticides in fields\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Methane (CH₄) from industrial leakage is linked to poisoning and skin cancer.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the information provided, which two pollutants share the exact same sources: \'Vehicular and industrial smoke\'?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur dioxide and Methane\",\"Particulate matter and Dust\",\"Pesticides and Oxides of nitrogen\",\"Carbon monoxide and Sulphur dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Both Particulate matter and Dust are listed as coming from \'Vehicular and industrial smoke\'.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for Methane as mentioned in the pollutants table?\",\"options\":[\"SO₂\",\"CO\",\"CH₄\",\"O₂\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly identifies Methane as CH₄.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which pollutant is uniquely associated with the source \'Vehicular smoke\' only, without mentioning industrial smoke?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon monoxide (CO)\",\"Oxides of nitrogen\",\"Particulate matter\",\"Dust\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the table, Oxides of nitrogen list only \'Vehicular smoke\' as a source, while others include industrial smoke.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/066_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, which pollutant causes \'Irritation of eyes\' as one of its effects?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur dioxide (SO₂)\",\"Carbon monoxide (CO)\",\"Methane (CH₄)\",\"Pesticides\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table lists \'Irritation of eyes\' under the effects of Sulphur dioxide (SO₂).\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/066_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the effects of pollutants, which one specifically targets the respiratory tract and lungs through vehicular smoke?\",\"options\":[\"Dust\",\"Oxides of nitrogen\",\"Pesticides\",\"Methane (CH₄)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oxides of nitrogen from vehicular smoke cause irritation of the respiratory tract and lungs.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which pollutant\'s source is identified as the \'Production and use\' of the substance itself rather than smoke or leakage?\",\"options\":[\"Particulate matter\",\"Methane\",\"Pesticides\",\"Sulphur dioxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The source for Pesticides is listed as \'Production and use of pesticides\'.\",\"topic\":\"Air Pollutants: Sources and Effects\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"At what height from the Earth\'s surface is the ozone layer located?\",\"options\":[\"18 kilometres\",\"48 kilometres\",\"84 kilometres\",\"100 kilometres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the ozone layer is present at the height of 48 kilometres from earth\'s surface.\",\"topic\":\"Effects on Living World and Ozone\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific type of ultraviolet rays does the ozone layer protect the living world from?\",\"options\":[\"UV-A\",\"UV-B\",\"UV-C\",\"UV-D\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the ozone layer protects the earth from ultraviolet rays (UV-B).\",\"topic\":\"Effects on Living World and Ozone\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the ozone layer situated in relation to the stratosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Above the stratosphere\",\"Inside the troposphere\",\"Below the stratosphere\",\"In the ionosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the ozone layer is present below the stratosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Effects on Living World and Ozone\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the stomata of plants as an effect of air pollution?\",\"options\":[\"They open wider\",\"They multiply in number\",\"They get closed\",\"They change color\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists \'Stomata get closed\' as the first effect of air pollution on plants.\",\"topic\":\"Effects on Living World and Ozone\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does air pollution affect the process of photosynthesis in plants?\",\"options\":[\"It speeds up the rate\",\"It slows down the rate\",\"It changes the color of oxygen\",\"It has no effect on photosynthesis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that air pollution leads to the \'Slowing down of rate of photosynthesis\'.\",\"topic\":\"Effects on Living World and Ozone\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a direct effect of air pollution on animals mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Loss of hearing\",\"Irritation of eyes\",\"Skin yellowing\",\"Stomata closing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies \'Irritation of eyes\' as an effect of air pollution on animals.\",\"topic\":\"Effects on Living World and Ozone\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a city experiences high levels of air pollution, what change might be observed in the local vegetation?\",\"options\":[\"Leaves will turn dark green\",\"Growth will be accelerated\",\"Leaves may fall off or become yellow\",\"Stomata will remain permanently open\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that due to air 