water\",\"It adds biotic and abiotic factors to water\",\"It increases the dissolved oxygen level\",\"It prevents the growth of algae\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that many biotic and abiotic factors are added to water due to soil erosion.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a specific pesticide mentioned that flows and mixes with water?\",\"options\":[\"Endrin\",\"Mercury\",\"Lead\",\"Phosphate\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Endrin, chlorin, and carbonate are listed as specific pesticides in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which \'Other effect\' of water pollution is evidenced by the appearance of the water surface?\",\"options\":[\"Addition of toxic materials to crops\",\"Changes in physical and chemical properties of water\",\"Increase in soil fertility\",\"Growth of useful aquatic fauna\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows changes in the physical appearance and state of the water, which corresponds to changes in physical and chemical properties.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/069_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a person is suffering from bone deformities and hypertension due to contaminated water, which category of effects does this belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Effects on ecosystem\",\"Other effects\",\"Effects on human being\",\"Effects on soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Deformities in bones and hypertension are listed under effects on human beings.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the dissolved oxygen level when an ecosystem is affected by water pollution?\",\"options\":[\"It increases significantly\",\"It remains unchanged\",\"It decreases\",\"It helps in plant growth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'Decreased dissolved oxygen level\' as an effect on the ecosystem.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is classified as a \'manmade reason\' involving the cleaning of tankers?\",\"options\":[\"Industrial effluent\",\"Oil spillage\",\"Domestic sewage\",\"Thermal power plant water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oil spillage occurs during transportation and cleaning of tankers.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Decomposing hemp and flax in water is categorized under which section of the text?\",\"options\":[\"Natural reasons: Decomposing matter\",\"Manmade reasons: Other reasons\",\"Effects on ecosystem\",\"Effects on human being\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Decomposing hemp and flax is listed under \'Other reasons\' within the \'Manmade reasons\' category.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an effect of water pollution on the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Soil fertility is affected\",\"Soil erosion is stopped\",\"Salt content in soil decreases\",\"Biotic factors are removed\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Under \'Other effects\', the text states that soil fertility is affected.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following human organs can be affected by water pollution according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Heart and Lungs\",\"Liver and Kidneys\",\"Stomach and Muscles\",\"Eyes and Ears\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions ailments of liver, kidneys, and brain as effects on human beings.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the use of chemical fertilizers like N, P, and K considered a pollution risk?\",\"options\":[\"They cause soil erosion\",\"They flow and mix with water bodies\",\"They increase the oxygen level in water\",\"They are natural aquatic weeds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that N, P, K containing fertilizers flow and mix with water, causing pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Reasons and Effects of Water Pollution\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the Water Pollution and Prevention Act enacted in India?\",\"options\":[\"1974\",\"1981\",\"1986\",\"1990\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Water pollution and prevention act was enacted in 1974.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Soil is considered polluted when there is a decrease in its ________ due to natural or manmade reasons.\",\"options\":[\"Color\",\"Fertility\",\"Weight\",\"Temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, soil is polluted when its fertility decreases due to changes in its properties.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which act was enacted by the Government of India in 1986?\",\"options\":[\"Air Pollution Act\",\"Water Pollution Act\",\"Environmental Protection Act\",\"Biomedical Waste Act\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the Environmental Protection Act, 1986 as one of the laws for pollution control.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary effect of mixing salty and acidic water from industries into the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Soil becomes more porous\",\"Soil fertility decreases\",\"Soil becomes dry waste\",\"Soil turns into ice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that soil fertility decreases due to mixing of salty, acidic water from industry.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do radioactive pollutants typically reach the human body from the soil?\",\"options\":[\"Through direct skin contact only\",\"Through the food chain\",\"Through sound pollution\",\"Through dry waste only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that radioactive pollutants enter and pass through the food chain like soil, crop, water, and human body.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if wet waste is dumped at wrong places instead of being composted?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into chemical fertilizer\",\"It prevents soil erosion\",\"Pathogens grow upon it\",\"It reduces air pollution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if wet waste is dumped wrongly, pathogens grow upon it, leading to water pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of pollution is caused by the excessive spraying of insecticides and weedicides in agriculture?\",\"options\":[\"Sound pollution\",\"Air pollution\",\"Radioactive pollution\",\"Biomedical pollution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that excessively sprayed insecticides and weedicides are mixed with air causing air pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer uses an excessive amount of chemical fertilizers in his field. According to the text, which type of pollution is this likely to cause besides soil pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Sound pollution\",\"Water pollution\",\"Radioactive pollution\",\"Biomedical waste\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that excessive use of chemical fertilizers causes water pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If toxic substances from polluted soil leach into nearby water bodies, what is the direct consequence?\",\"options\":[\"Air pollution increases\",\"Soil fertility increases\",\"Water pollution increases\",\"Composting increases\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that problems of water pollution increase because toxic substances leach into water from the soil.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the mixing of human, bird, and animal waste in soil lead to air pollution?\",\"options\":[\"It causes soil erosion\",\"It releases stinking gases\",\"It creates radioactive waves\",\"It absorbs all oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says that mixing of these wastes releases various stinking gases causing air pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a change that defines soil pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Physical properties\",\"Biological properties\",\"Chemical properties\",\"Magnetic properties\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text defines soil pollution as changes in physical, biological, and chemical properties; magnetic is not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which body is responsible for supervising whether local governing bodies like Gram Panchayats follow pollution laws?\",\"options\":[\"International Courts\",\"Government statutory bodies\",\"Private industries\",\"Agricultural departments\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Government statutory bodies supervise whether local governing bodies follow the laws.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the relationship between soil pollution and pathogens?\",\"options\":[\"Soil pollution kills all pathogens\",\"Pathogens cause soil to become salty\",\"Diseases spread through pathogens in polluted soil\",\"Pathogens prevent leaching of toxic substances\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that diseases spread through various pathogens as a result of soil pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which act was enacted in 1981?\",\"options\":[\"Environmental Protection Act\",\"Water pollution and prevention act\",\"Air pollution and prevention act\",\"Solid waste management act\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists the Air pollution and prevention act, 1981.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why is very little land area available for human use?\",\"options\":[\"Because of air pollution\",\"Because land is covered by ice, deserts, mountains, and hills\",\"Because of excessive use of chemical fertilizers\",\"Because of the Environmental Protection Act\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that land is limited because parts are covered by ice, desert, mountains, and hills.\",\"topic\":\"Soil Pollution and Prevention\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22159,57,8,'Science','en','Disaster Management','[\"Disaster is a sudden event causing loss of life, property and environment.\",\"Natural disasters: earthquakes, floods, landslides, tsunamis, volcanic eruptions, cyclones, droughts, forest fires.\",\"Man‑made disasters: industrial accidents, chemical spills, nuclear incidents, fires, dam failures.\",\"Earthquake: sudden vibration of earth’s surface caused by release of stress in rocks.\",\"Epicentre: point on surface directly above focus of earthquake.\",\"Seismograph\\/Seismometer records seismic waves; intensity measured on Richter scale.\",\"Richter scale: logarithmic scale; each unit increase = 10× amplitude, 31.6× energy.\",\"Effects of earthquakes: loss of life, economic damage, ecosystem destruction, river course changes, tsunami if ocean floor disturbed.\",\"Precautions during earthquake: stay indoors, drop, cover, hold; avoid windows, tall objects, elevators.\",\"Earthquake‑resistant buildings: designed per IS 1893, IS 456, IS 13920; use ductile detailing, base isolation, reinforced concrete.\",\"Fire: rapid oxidation of material; can be natural or man‑made.\",\"Fire classes: A (solid), B (liquid), C (gas), D (combustible metal), E (electrical).\",\"Extinguishing methods: water (Class A), foam\\/spray (Class B), CO₂ (Class C), dry powder (Class D), CO₂ or dry chemical (Class E).\",\"Landslide: rapid movement of rock\\/soil down slope due to water, earthquakes, deforestation, construction.\",\"Causes of landslides: heavy rains, earthquakes, deforestation, slope cutting, dam failure.\",\"Disaster relief planning: create school disaster plan, conduct mock drills, maintain emergency supplies, map safe zones.\",\"Key terms: epicentre, focus, Richter scale, seismic wave, tsunami, base isolation, ductile detailing, CO₂ extinguisher, foam extinguisher, landslide.\",\"Safety measures: switch off gas and electricity during fire, call fire brigade, use non‑conductive extinguishers for electrical fires.\",\"Emergency map: shows buildings, exits, safe zones, nearest roads.\",\"Mock drill: schedule monthly, record participants, note shortcomings.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are the sudden vibrations on the earth and the shaking of the earth surface or ground called?\",\"options\":[\"Cyclone\",\"Earthquake\",\"Tsunami\",\"Landslide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook defines an earthquake as sudden vibrations on the earth and the shaking of the earth surface\\/ground.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the shocks and waves of an earthquake form according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"In the atmosphere\",\"On the ocean surface\",\"In the interior of the earth\",\"In the clouds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that shocks and waves are formed in the interior of the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, seismic waves lead to which type of movements on the earth\'s surface?\",\"options\":[\"Steady rotation\",\"Tremors and shaking\",\"Constant heating\",\"Magnetic pulling\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Seismic waves cause movements of the earth\'s surface like tremors, shaking, or moving up and down.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which directions do the earthquake shocks and waves spread once they reach the surface?\",\"options\":[\"Only towards the North Pole\",\"Only in a straight line\",\"In all directions\",\"Only towards the ocean\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that shocks and waves spread on the surface in all directions.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a city is located very close to the epicenter, what is the most likely result during an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"No damage will occur\",\"Major loss will occur\",\"The ground will become very hot\",\"The city will move to a higher altitude\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that because strong waves reach the epicenter first, major loss occurs near that area.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of seismic waves, what causes the earth\'s surface to move up or down?\",\"options\":[\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"Seismic waves\",\"Ocean currents\",\"Wind speed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that seismic waves lead to movements of the earth\'s surface like tremors or going up\\/down.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The central point of an earthquake is described as the point above the _____ on the earth surface.\",\"options\":[\"Interior\",\"Epicenter\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Vibration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the central point of an earthquake as the point above the epicenter on the earth surface.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what specific damage is clearly visible on the walls of the yellow buildings?\",\"options\":[\"They are being painted\",\"They have developed large cracks\",\"They are melting\",\"They are turning into trees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and its caption \'Cracks to the buildings\' show structural damage caused by the earthquake.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Looking at the given figure, what has happened to the ground surface or road between the two buildings?\",\"options\":[\"It has been flooded with water\",\"It has developed a deep fissure or crack\",\"It has turned into a mountain\",\"It has been covered with snow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows a large crack running through the ground\\/road surface between the buildings.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/072_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best explains the relationship between the interior of the earth and the epicenter?\",\"options\":[\"Waves start at the epicenter and go to the interior\",\"Shocks form in the interior and reach the epicenter first\",\"The epicenter is located deep in the interior\",\"There is no connection between the interior and the epicenter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states shocks form in the interior and strong waves reach the epicenter first.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which area is most vulnerable to the strongest convulsions during an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"Areas far away from the epicenter\",\"The area near the epicenter\",\"Only the interior of the earth\",\"Only the clouds above the earth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says strong convulse or waves reach the epicenter first, causing major loss near that area.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist observes the ground shaking and moving up and down, which phenomenon from the text are they witnessing?\",\"options\":[\"A volcanic eruption\",\"Seismic wave movements\",\"A thunderstorm\",\"Global warming\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes movements like shaking or going up\\/down as being caused by seismic waves.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Earthquake\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the instrument used to record earthquakes?\",\"options\":[\"Thermometer\",\"Seismometer\",\"Barometer\",\"Anemometer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the machine or instrument which records earthquakes is called a Seismograph or Seismometer.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which unit is the accentuation or intensity of an earthquake measured?\",\"options\":[\"Richter Scale\",\"Celsius Scale\",\"Newton Scale\",\"Pascal Scale\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the intensity (accentuation) is measured in Richter Scale.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the observations of the \'National Earthquakes information center\', approximately how many earthquakes occur on earth every year?\",\"options\":[\"1,000 to 5,000\",\"5,000 to 10,000\",\"12,400 to 14,000\",\"20,000 to 25,000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that every year nearly 12,400 to 14,000 earthquakes occur on the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the frequency of earthquakes mentioned in the text, which statement is true?\",\"options\":[\"Intensified earthquakes occur more frequently than mild ones.\",\"Mild earthquakes occur in a larger number than intensified ones.\",\"Earthquakes only occur once a month on earth.\",\"Earthquakes never occur in the same place twice.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that on the earth, mild earthquakes occur in a larger number than the intensified ones.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'acute\' refer to in the context of earthquake shocks as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"Mild shocks\",\"Mathematical units\",\"Intensified shocks\",\"Vibration records\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'intensified\' and \'acute\' as synonyms for strong earthquake shocks.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which point represents the location on the earth\'s surface directly above the focal point?\",\"options\":[\"Seismic waves\",\"Plate movement\",\"Epicentre\",\"Focal point\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the Epicentre located on the surface, directly above the Focal point.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/072_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the two specific types of instruments shown for recording earthquake vibrations?\",\"options\":[\"Digital and Analog seismometers\",\"Vertical and Horizontal seismometers\",\"Mild and Acute seismometers\",\"Surface and Underground seismometers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The labels in the figure identify a \'Vertical seismometer\' and a \'Horizontal seismometer\'.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/072_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If the National Earthquakes information center records the minimum expected number of earthquakes over a period of two years, what would be the total count?\",\"options\":[\"12,400\",\"24,800\",\"28,000\",\"14,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The minimum annual count is 12,400; for two years, it would be 12,400 x 2 = 24,800.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The Richter Scale is described in the text as which type of measuring unit?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical measuring unit\",\"Biological measuring unit\",\"Mathematical measuring unit\",\"Geological measuring unit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the Richter Scale is one of the mathematical measuring units.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist observes that the earth is \'continuously vibrating\'. Based on the text, what evidence supports this observation?\",\"options\":[\"The movement of buildings\",\"The large number of earthquakes occurring daily and annually\",\"The invention of the seismograph\",\"The presence of the National Earthquakes information center\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes the earth is continuously vibrating because earthquakes are noticed every day and occur 12,400 to 14,000 times a year.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the yellow circular lines spreading outwards from the epicentre represent?\",\"options\":[\"Plate movement\",\"Seismic waves\",\"Focal point\",\"Richter scale\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram labels the concentric waves spreading from the source as \'Seismic waves\'.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/072_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a city experiences a very strong earthquake, which term from the text best describes the \'accentuation\' of that shock?\",\"options\":[\"Mild\",\"Mathematical\",\"Intensified\",\"Vibrating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses \'intensified\' to describe acute or strong earthquake shocks.\",\"topic\":\"Measuring Earthquake\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what happens to water when it percolates through the cracks of the earth and encounters tremendous heat?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into ice\",\"It converts into steam\",\"It disappears completely\",\"It becomes cold water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to tremendous heat, the percolated water converts into steam.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is categorized as an \'Economic loss\' resulting from an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"Loss of wild animals\",\"Change in river flow\",\"Destruction of railway tracks\",\"Tsunami waves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists railway tracks under economic loss at a large scale.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which human-made structure is specifically mentioned as causing stress on the earth?\",\"options\":[\"Mining tunnels\",\"Big dams\",\"Atomic test sites\",\"Electric poles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure lists \'Stress of big dams on the earth\' as the second cause of an earthquake.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/073_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for ecosystem destruction during an earthquake as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Loss of Biodiversity\",\"Change in water-table\",\"Underground atomic tests\",\"Volcanic eruption\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions ecosystem destruction is due to the loss of Biodiversity.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, which event at the bottom of the ocean leads to the destruction of coastal regions?\",\"options\":[\"Volcanic eruption\",\"Mining\",\"Tsunami waves\",\"Change in water-table\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure states that an earthquake at the bottom of the ocean may create tsunami waves causing coastal destruction.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/073_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a direct cause of an earthquake involving human technology?\",\"options\":[\"Loss of human beings\",\"Underground atomic tests\",\"Change in river flow\",\"Fire in cities\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Underground atomic tests are listed as the fourth cause of an earthquake.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the level of the water-table as an effect of an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"It always rises\",\"It always falls\",\"It changes\",\"It remains constant\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the \'Level of water-table changes\' as an effect.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does steam formed inside the earth cause an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"It cools down the earth\'s surface\",\"It tries to come out from the loosened earth surface\",\"It turns back into water immediately\",\"It creates new cracks for mining\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that steam tries to come out from the loosened surface, causing the earthquake.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a city experiences an earthquake, which specific hazard\'s possibility increases according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Volcanic eruption\",\"Atomic tests\",\"Fire\",\"Mining\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the possibility of fire in cities increases.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as a cause of an earthquake in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Mining\",\"Volcanic eruption\",\"Loss of Biodiversity\",\"Stress of big dams\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Loss of Biodiversity is listed as an effect, not a cause.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which natural water bodies may have their direction of flow changed due to an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"Only oceans\",\"Rivers and streams\",\"Only underground water\",\"Only pipelines\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the direction of flow of rivers and streams changes.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"An earthquake resulting in the destruction of pipelines and electric poles is an example of:\",\"options\":[\"Ecosystem destruction\",\"Large scale economic loss\",\"Water-table change\",\"Biodiversity loss\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text groups the destruction of electric poles and pipelines under economic loss at a large scale.\",\"topic\":\"Causes and Effects of Earthquake\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are at home during an earthquake, what is the first thing you should do instead of running here and there?\",\"options\":[\"Open all the windows\",\"Stand still at one place\",\"Run to the balcony\",\"Call your neighbors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises to stand still at one place instead of running during an earthquake.\",\"topic\":\"Precautions and Safety Measures\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following should be used for light after an earthquake to prevent the risk of fire?\",\"options\":[\"Candles\",\"Lantern\",\"Matchbox\",\"Torch\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"To avoid fire, the text recommends using a torch or battery instead of candles, lanterns, or matchboxes.\",\"topic\":\"Precautions and Safety Measures\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it advised to avoid using a lift in a multistoried building during an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"Lifts are slower than stairs\",\"Lifts are for emergency workers only\",\"To ensure safety as lifts may fail or get stuck\",\"Staircases are wider than lifts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically instructs to avoid using lifts and use the staircase instead for safety.\",\"topic\":\"Precautions and Safety Measures\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason for switching off the main electric supply after an earthquake?\",\"options\":[\"To s