ubstances are heavier than water\",\"Liquid substances are lighter than water\",\"Liquid substances react with water to form gas\",\"Water makes the liquid substances more flammable\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that flammable liquid substances are lighter than water, therefore fire extinguishers should be used.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which group of metals reacts with water at normal room temperature in a Class D fire?\",\"options\":[\"Magnesium, aluminum, and zinc\",\"Potassium, sodium, and calcium\",\"Iron, copper, and silver\",\"Gold, platinum, and mercury\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that potassium, sodium, and calcium react with water at normal room temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step recommended to extinguish a Class E (Electrical) fire?\",\"options\":[\"Spray water immediately\",\"Use a carbon dioxide extinguisher\",\"Cut off the power supply\",\"Cover the components with wet clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"For Class E fires, the text states that to extinguish such fire, power is cut off first.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a stack of old clothes and newspapers catches fire, how should it be put out according to the classification?\",\"options\":[\"Using carbon dioxide\",\"Using water\",\"By cutting off the power\",\"Using specialized chemical extinguishers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Clothes and papers are Class A fuels, and the text states this fire can be put out with water.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A fire breaks out in a container of cooking oil. Which class of fire does this represent?\",\"options\":[\"Class A\",\"Class B\",\"Class C\",\"Class E\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cooking oil is listed under Class B fires, which involve flammable liquid substances.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A technician is working with acetylene gas when a leak causes a fire. Which fire classification applies here?\",\"options\":[\"Class A\",\"Class B\",\"Class C\",\"Class D\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Acetylene is specifically mentioned as a fuel for Class C (Gaseous substances) fires.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when water is used to extinguish a Class D fire involving combustible metals?\",\"options\":[\"The fire is immediately put out\",\"The water turns into household gas\",\"It causes an explosion\",\"The metals become non-combustible\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that when combustible metals in Class D combine with water, it explodes.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of fire extinguisher is specifically mentioned as \'non-conductive\' for electrical fires?\",\"options\":[\"Water-based extinguishers\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Liquid solvents\",\"Varnish extinguishers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies carbon dioxide as a non-conductive fire extinguisher used for Class E fires.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how do magnesium, aluminum, and zinc differ from potassium and sodium in a fire situation?\",\"options\":[\"They do not react with water at all\",\"They react with water at high temperatures\",\"They are considered Class B substances\",\"They are non-combustible metals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text distinguishes that while potassium\\/sodium react at room temperature, magnesium, aluminum, and zinc react at high temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following substances belong to the Class B fire category?\",\"options\":[\"Coal and Paper\",\"L.P.G. and Acetylene\",\"Petrol and Varnish\",\"Potassium and Calcium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Petrol and varnish are listed as flammable liquid substances under Class B.\",\"topic\":\"Fire and Types of Fire\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the most common and effective solution for extinguishing fire that is easily available everywhere?\",\"options\":[\"Sand\",\"Soil\",\"Water\",\"Froth\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that water is the most common and effective solution which is easily available everywhere.\",\"topic\":\"Fire Extinction and Safety\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does spraying water around a fire help in controlling it?\",\"options\":[\"It cuts off the air supply\",\"It creates a cooling effect\",\"It removes flammable substances\",\"It increases the fire\'s intensity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Spraying water on or around the fire creates a cooling effect that helps control the fire.\",\"topic\":\"Fire Extinction and Safety\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which materials are specifically mentioned as useful for controlling fires caused by electricity or oil?\",\"options\":[\"Water and froth\",\"Sand or soil\",\"Wooden articles\",\"Gas regulators\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that sand or soil can be used to control fire caused by electricity or oil.\",\"topic\":\"Fire Extinction and Safety\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of spreading a froth-like substance on an oil fire?\",\"options\":[\"To cool the oil down\",\"To remove the oil from the area\",\"To cut off the contact between air and fire\",\"To increase the temperature of the fire\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Froth-like substances cut off the contact between air and fire, which is useful for oil fires.\",\"topic\":\"Fire Extinction and Safety\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the method of \'Keeping away Flammable Substances\', what is the main objective?\",\"o