ptions\":[\"Satara\",\"Pune\",\"Raigad\",\"Thane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the \'Malin\' mishap in Pune district.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the weight of rocks when water enters their cracks during heavy rains?\",\"options\":[\"The weight decreases\",\"The weight remains constant\",\"The weight increases\",\"The rocks lose all weight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that water entering cracks causes weathering and the weight of the rocks increases.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific term used in the text for rocks sliding on a sloppy region and settling at the lower side?\",\"options\":[\"Soil erosion\",\"Collapse of rift\",\"Tsunami effect\",\"Mountain building\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the sliding and settling of rocks on a sloppy region as the \'collapse of rift\'.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the building of roads in mountains contribute to landslides?\",\"options\":[\"By increasing the number of trees\",\"By making the mountain weak due to digging\",\"By preventing soil erosion\",\"By creating artificial reservoirs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that digging while building roads in mountains makes the mountain weak.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an effect of a landslide on water bodies?\",\"options\":[\"Rivers dry up completely\",\"Waterfalls disappear forever\",\"Rivers get flooded and change their paths\",\"Tsunamis are generated in rivers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that as an effect of landslides, rivers get flooded and change their paths.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a mountain slope undergoes unlimited cutting of trees, what is the most likely consequence according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Increased rainfall\",\"Stronger mountain foundation\",\"Soil erosion leading to landslides\",\"Growth of new plant life\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that unlimited cutting of trees causes soil erosion, which is a cause of landslides.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A landslide occurs in a hilly region with a waterfall. What is a possible geological outcome mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The waterfall will turn into a river\",\"Formation of artificial water reservoirs\",\"The rocks will turn into soil immediately\",\"The slope will become flat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that displacement of waterfalls and formation of artificial water reservoirs are effects of landslides.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of \'collapse of rift\', which two factors are necessary for the rocks to slide down?\",\"options\":[\"High temperature and flat land\",\"Heavy rains and a sloppy region\",\"Strong winds and low-lying land\",\"Tree planting and digging\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes that water from heavy rains enters cracks and rocks slide on the sloppy region.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a landslide cause large-scale financial loss?\",\"options\":[\"Because it destroys plant life\",\"Because it changes river paths\",\"Because buildings and structures on sloppy areas collapse\",\"Because it affects traffic jams\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there is financial loss as buildings and rocks on sloppy areas collapse on low-lying land.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific consequence of landslides in Maharashtra is highlighted for discussion?\",\"options\":[\"Loss of plant life\",\"Traffic jams\",\"Soil erosion\",\"Building collapse\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure explicitly asks \'In Maharashtra where do the traffic jams occur due to landslides?\'.\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/075_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity in the given figure, what is the final step students are asked to do after discussing landslide locations?\",\"options\":[\"Build new roads\",\"Suggest preventive measures\",\"Cut more trees\",\"Create artificial reservoirs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure asks students to \'Discuss in the classroom and suggest preventive measures.\'\",\"topic\":\"Landslides - Rift collapse\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/075_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22160,57,8,'Science','en','Cell and Cell Organelles','[\"Cell is the basic unit of life.\",\"There are two main types of cells: plant cells and animal cells.\",\"Plant cells have a cell wall made of cellulose; animal cells lack a cell wall.\",\"The plasma membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins and is selectively permeable.\",\"Osmosis is the movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane.\",\"Isotonic solution: no net water movement; hypotonic: water enters the cell; hypertonic: water leaves the cell.\",\"Cytoplasm is the fluid inside the cell that contains organelles; cytosol is the liquid part without organelles.\",\"Nucleus contains DNA, nucleolus, and chromatin; it controls cell activities and heredity.\",\"Endoplasmic reticulum (ER) has rough (with ribosomes) and smooth (without ribosomes) regions; it synthesises proteins and lipids.\",\"Golgi complex modifies, sorts, and packages proteins into vesicles for transport.\",\"Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes and break down waste and old organelles.\",\"Mitochondria are the powerhouses of the cell, producing ATP through respiration.\",\"Vacuoles store water, ions, and waste; plant vacuoles maintain turgor pressure.\",\"Plastids (chloroplasts, chromoplasts, leucoplasts) are double‑membrane organelles that store pigments or food.\",\"Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll for photosynthesis; chromoplasts contain carotenoids for colour; leucoplasts store starch.\",\"Eukaryotic cells have membrane‑bound organelles and a nucleus; prokaryotic cells lack these and are smaller.\",\"Cell membrane’s selective permeability maintains homeostasis.\",\"Osmotic pressure is the force exerted by water movement across the membrane.\",\"Mitochondria have inner folds called cristae that increase surface area for ATP production.\",\"Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis; they can be free in cytoplasm or attached to rough ER.\",\"The nucleus is surrounded by a double membrane with nuclear pores for transport.\",\"Cell wall provides support and prevents excess water entry.\",\"The cytoplasm is sticky and facilitates chemical reactions.\",\"Plant cells usually have a large central vacuole; animal cells have smaller vacuoles or none.\",\"The Golgi complex is sometimes called the cell’s packing department.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is considered the structural and functional unit of living organisms?\",\"options\":[\"Organ\",\"Tissue\",\"Cell\",\"Mitochondria\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the cell is the structural and functional unit of living organisms.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what determines the different sizes and structures of cells in various organs?\",\"options\":[\"The age of the organism\",\"The functions they perform\",\"The type of instrument used\",\"The color of the cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that according to the functions, cells of different sizes and structures are found in different organs.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which component is listed as \'Present\' in both the Animal cell and the Plant cell in the provided chart?\",\"options\":[\"Cell wall\",\"Plastids\",\"Cell membrane\",\"Centrioles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chart in the text explicitly shows that the Cell membrane is \'Present\' in both Animal and Plant cells.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the comparison chart, which of the following is a characteristic difference between a plant cell and an animal cell?\",\"options\":[\"Animal cells have a cell wall, while plant cells do not.\",\"Plant cells have a cell wall, while animal cells do not.\",\"Only animal cells have mitochondria.\",\"Only plant cells have a Golgi complex.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the chart, the Cell wall is present in plant cells but absent in animal cells.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is observing a cell under a microscope and notices the presence of plastids, which type of cell are they most likely looking at?\",\"options\":[\"Animal cell\",\"Bacterial cell\",\"Plant cell\",\"Human cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the chart, plastids are present in plant cells (implied by the comparison of components).\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary to study each and every part of the cell?\",\"options\":[\"To count the number of cells in an organ\",\"To understand the processes occurring within the cell\",\"To determine the age of the organism\",\"To see the cell without a microscope\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we must study every part to understand the processes occurring within the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which cell is identified by the presence of a large, prominent central vacuole and a thick green outer boundary?\",\"options\":[\"Animal cell\",\"Plant cell\",\"Blood cell\",\"Nerve cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the plant cell with a large central vacuole and a thick cell wall boundary.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/077_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which component is marked as \'Present\' in the Animal cell but \'Absent\' in the Plant cell?\",\"options\":[\"Cell wall\",\"Mitochondria\",\"Centrioles\",\"Golgi complex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The third row of the table shows a component that is Present in Animal cells and Absent in Plant cells, which refers to centrioles.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/077_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following organelles is found in both plant and animal cells according to the provided list?\",\"options\":[\"Only Cell wall\",\"Only Plastids\",\"Golgi complex\",\"None of the organelles are common\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The list includes Golgi complex and Mitochondria as components present in both cell types.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where are cells of different sizes and structures found within a living organism?\",\"options\":[\"Only in the skin\",\"In different organs\",\"Only in the heart\",\"Only in the brain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that cells of different sizes and structures are found in different organs.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelle would you expect to find in a plant cell to help it perform photosynthesis, which is absent in animal cells?\",\"options\":[\"Mitochondria\",\"Plastids\",\"Golgi complex\",\"Vacuole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plastids are unique to plant cells in the provided comparison list.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the introductory text, what can be inferred about the relationship between a cell\'s structure and its location in the body?\",\"options\":[\"Structure is independent of the organ.\",\"All organs have cells of the same structure.\",\"Structure is adapted to the specific functions of the organ.\",\"Size is the only thing that changes between organs.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'According to the functions, cells of different sizes and structures are found in different organs\', implying adaptation to function.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following organisms does NOT possess a cell wall?\",\"options\":[\"Algae\",\"Fungi\",\"Plants\",\"Animals\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that animal cells lack a cell wall.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The cell wall is mainly composed of which two carbohydrates?\",\"options\":[\"Lignin and Suberin\",\"Cellulose and Pectin\",\"Cutin and Lignin\",\"Phospholipids and Proteins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the cell wall is mainly composed of carbohydrates like cellulose and pectin.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of the cell wall regarding water regulation?\",\"options\":[\"To allow all water to enter the cell\",\"To prevent the exit of oxygen\",\"To prevent the entry of excess water\",\"To absorb salt from the environment\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that one function of the cell wall is to protect the cell by preventing entry of excess water.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of the plasma membrane allows it to control the entry and exit of specific substances?\",\"options\":[\"It is a thick and rigid layer\",\"It is a selectively permeable membrane\",\"It is composed of lignin and suberin\",\"It is a strong and inelastic coat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The plasma membrane is called selectively permeable because it allows some substances to enter while preventing others.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the structural arrangement of the plasma membrane?\",\"options\":[\"Cellulose microfibres embedded in pectin\",\"Lignin layers surrounding suberin\",\"Protein molecules embedded in two layers of phospholipids\",\"Middle lamella covering the primary cell wall\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes the structure of the plasma membrane as protein molecules embedded in two layers of phospholipids.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the external environment of a cell changes, but the internal cellular environment remains stable, what is this condition called?\",\"options\":[\"Permeability\",\"Homeostasis\",\"Elasticity\",\"Fragility\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines homeostasis as the condition where the cellular environment does not change despite changes outside the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist finds a polymer called \'suberin\' in the outer layer of a plant cell. Based on the text, what can be inferred about this layer?\",\"options\":[\"It is part of the plasma membrane\",\"It is a protein molecule\",\"It was added to the cell wall as per need\",\"It is a type of phospholipid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that polymers like suberin are added to the cell wall \'afterwards\' as per need.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is mentioned as a waste product that exits the cell through the plasma membrane?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Salt\",\"Water\",\"CO₂\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the property of the plasma membrane, CO₂ exits the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the descriptions provided, how does the plasma membrane differ physically from the cell wall?\",\"options\":[\"The plasma membrane is strong and thick, while the cell wall is fragile\",\"The plasma membrane is thin and fragile, while the cell wall is strong\",\"The cell wall is selectively permeable, while the plasma membrane is not\",\"The cell wall is made of proteins, while the plasma membrane is made of cellulose\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the cell wall as a \'strong coat\' and the plasma membrane as a \'thin, fragile\' covering.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a polymer added to the cell wall \'afterwards\' or \'as per need\'?\",\"options\":[\"Lignin\",\"Suberin\",\"Cutin\",\"Cellulose\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Cellulose is a main component mentioned initially, whereas lignin, suberin, and cutin are added afterwards.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which component is represented as long, rod-like structures within the primary cell wall?\",\"options\":[\"Middle lamella\",\"Cellulose microfibre\",\"Soluble proteins\",\"Plasma membrane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the long, rod-like structures as \'Cellulose microfibre\'.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/078_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, which layer is situated immediately below the primary cell wall?\",\"options\":[\"Middle lamella\",\"Hemicellulose\",\"Pectin\",\"Plasma membrane\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the plasma membrane as the bottom-most layer, located beneath the primary cell wall.\",\"topic\":\"Components of a cell\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/078_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of gulping or engulfing food and other substances from the outer environment called?\",\"options\":[\"Exocytosis\",\"Endocytosis\",\"Diffusion\",\"Plasmolysis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines endocytosis as the process to gulp\\/engulf food and other substances from the outer environment.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following molecules enter or exit the cell through the process of diffusion?\",\"options\":[\"Large proteins\",\"Sugar molecules\",\"Small molecules like O2 and CO2\",\"Complex carbohydrates\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, diffusion involves the entry or exit of small molecules like O2 and CO2.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the water movement when a cell is placed in an isotonic solution?\",\"options\":[\"Water only enters the cell\",\"Water only leaves the cell\",\"Water doesn\'t go in or out\",\"The cell bursts immediately\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In an isotonic solution, the medium inside and outside has the same proportion of water, so water doesn\'t go in or out.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Osmosis is the traveling of water from a part with more water to a part with less water through which type of membrane?\",\"options\":[\"Impermeable membrane\",\"Selectively permeable membrane\",\"Fully permeable membrane\",\"Opaque membrane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines osmosis as water traveling through a selectively permeable membrane.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If raisins are kept in water for an hour, they swell and become turgid. Which process is responsible for this?\",\"options\":[\"Exosmosis\",\"Endosmosis\",\"Plasmolysis\",\"Exocytosis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When a cell has less water than the outside medium, water enters the cell; this is called endosmosis, causing raisins to swell.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What occurs when fruit pieces are kept in a thick saturated sugar solution?\",\"options\":[\"The fruit pieces swell\",\"The fruit pieces shrink\",\"The fruit pieces remain the same size\",\"The sugar solution enters the fruit\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a hypertonic solution (like thick sugar solution), water comes out of the fruit pieces, causing them to shrink.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these cellular activities consumes energy?\",\"options\":[\"Diffusion\",\"Osmosis\",\"Exocytosis\",\"Isotonic balance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists exocytosis under activities that consume cellular energy.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The contraction of cytoplasm caused by water leaving a plant cell in a hypertonic solution is called:\",\"options\":[\"Endocytosis\",\"Turgidity\",\"Plasmolysis\",\"Diffusion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the contraction of cytoplasm due to exosmosis in a hypertonic solution is called plasmolysis.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why do wooden doors fit very tightly in the rainy season?\",\"options\":[\"Due to exocytosis of water\",\"Due to diffusion of CO2\",\"Due to the process of osmosis (absorption of water)\",\"Due to plasmolysis of wood cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the tightening of wooden doors in the rainy season to the research topic of osmosis.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In a hypotonic solution, how does the water proportion in the cell compare to the outside medium?\",\"options\":[\"The cell has more water than the outside\",\"The cell has less water than the outside\",\"The cell and outside have equal water\",\"The cell has no water at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A hypotonic solution means the cell has less water than the outside medium.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which process is described as a \'physical process with 3 possibilities\'?\",\"options\":[\"Endocytosis\",\"Exocytosis\",\"Diffusion\",\"Osmosis\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that osmosis is a physical process with 3 possibilities (isotonic, hypotonic, hypertonic).\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following processes does NOT consume cellular energy?\",\"options\":[\"Endocytosis\",\"Exocytosis\",\"Diffusion\",\"Gulping food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text categorizes diffusion under processes that don\'t consume cellular energy.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary difference between endosmosis and exosmosis as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Endosmosis uses energy, exosmosis does not\",\"Endosmosis involves water entering the cell, exosmosis involves water leaving the cell\",\"Endosmosis happens in sugar, exosmosis happens in water\",\"Endosmosis shrinks the cell, exosmosis swells the cell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Endosmosis is water entering the cell (hypotonic), while exosmosis is water coming out of the cell (hypertonic).\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant cell is placed in a hypertonic solution, what is the specific sequence of events?\",\"options\":[\"Water enters -> Cell swells -> Turgidity\",\"Water leaves -> Cytoplasm contracts -> Plasmolysis\",\"Water leaves -> Cell swells -> Endosmosis\",\"Water enters -> Cytoplasm contracts -> Exocytosis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a hypertonic solution, water comes out by exosmosis, leading to contraction of cytoplasm (plasmolysis).\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Exocytosis is to \'excreting waste\' as Endocytosis is to:\",\"options\":[\"Releasing energy\",\"Gulping food\",\"Shrinking cytoplasm\",\"Diffusing oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text pairs exocytosis with excreting waste and endocytosis with gulping\\/engulfing food.\",\"topic\":\"Cellular Transport and Osmosis\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the fluid present between the plasma membrane and the nucleus called?\",\"options\":[\"Cytosol\",\"Cytoplasm\",\"Nucleoplasm\",\"Cell wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines cytoplasm as the fluid between the plasma membrane and the nucleus.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the cytoplasm stores vital substances like amino acids, glucose, and vitamins?\",\"options\":[\"Cell organelles\",\"Plasma membrane\",\"Cytosol\",\"Nucleolus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The part of the cytoplasm other than organelles is the cytosol, which stores vital substances.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the cytoplasm in plant cells usually pushed to the periphery?\",\"options\":[\"Due to a larger central vacuole\",\"Because it is more granular\",\"To protect the cell wall\",\"Due to the presence of many chloroplasts\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In plant cells, cytoplasm is thin and pushed to the periphery due to a larger central vacuole.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the cytoplasm of an animal cell differ from that of a plant cell?\",\"options\":[\"It is thinner and less dense\",\"It is more granular and dense\",\"It is pushed to the periphery\",\"It lacks a moving substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in animal cells, cytoplasm is more granular and dense compared to plant cells.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these are referred to as the \'organs of the cell\'?\",\"options\":[\"Chromatin fibres\",\"Cell organelles\",\"Genes\",\"Nuclear pores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cell organelles are specialized subunits with specific functions, described as \'organs of the cell\'.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelle can be seen without the use of an electron microscope?\",\"options\":[\"Mitochondria\",\"Ribosomes\",\"Chloroplast\",\"Endoplasmic reticulum\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Except for the nucleus and chloroplast, all other organelles require an electron microscope to be seen.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What stain is used to observe the nucleus while preparing a slide of human cheek cells?\",\"options\":[\"Iodine\",\"Safranin\",\"Methylene blue\",\"Eosin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions specify putting a drop of methylene blue stain on the cheek cell smear.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the thin thread-like structures in the nucleus that condense during cell division?\",\"options\":[\"Genes\",\"Chromatin fibres\",\"Nucleolus\",\"Cytosol\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Chromatin fibres are thin thread-like structures that condense to form chromosomes during cell division.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the functional segments of chromosomes called?\",\"options\":[\"Nucleoli\",\"Chromatin\",\"Genes\",\"Pores\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that functional segments of chromosomes are called genes.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary advantage for Red Blood Cells (RBCs) in losing their nucleus?\",\"options\":[\"It allows them to divide faster\",\"It helps them store more glucose\",\"It allows more hemoglobin to be accommodated for oxygen transport\",\"It makes the cell more granular and dense\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Loss of nucleus allows large quantities of hemoglobin to be accommodated for oxygen transport.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do sieve tubes of the plant phloem become hollow?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the loss of their nucleus\",\"To store vitamins and amino acids\",\"To accommodate more chloroplasts\",\"Because they are pushed to the periphery\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sieve tubes become hollow due to the loss of their nucleus, making food transportation easy.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which function of the nucleus is responsible for passing traits from parents to offspring?\",\"options\":[\"Control of metabolic activities\",\"Control of cell division\",\"Transmission of hereditary characters\",\"Storage of amino acids\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The nucleus is involved in the transmission of hereditary characters from parents to offspring.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the onion peeling, what is the observed shape of the cells?\",\"options\":[\"Circular\",\"Irregular\",\"Rectangular\",\"Spherical\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and the figure of onion peeling show rectangular cells.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/079_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific part of the nucleus is responsible for the passage of material in and out?\",\"options\":[\"Chromatin\",\"Nucleolus\",\"Nuclear pore\",\"Nuclear membrane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram labels and text confirm that nuclear pores allow the passage of material in and out.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/080_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The instrument shown in the given figure is necessary to see which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Onion cell wall\",\"Nucleus of cheek cells\",\"Most cell organelles\",\"Large central vacuole\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The electron microscope is required to see all organelles except the nucleus and chloroplast.\",\"topic\":\"Cytoplasm and Nucleus\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/079_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which cell organelle is described as a net-like structure consisting of interconnected miniature tubes and sheets?\",\"options\":[\"Golgi Complex\",\"Endoplasmic Reticulum\",\"Nucleus\",\"Lysosome\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines Endoplasmic Reticulum (E.R.) as a net-like structure consisting of interconnected miniature tubes and sheets filled with fluid.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"To which part of the cell is the Endoplasmic Reticulum connected on its outer side?\",\"options\":[\"Nucleus\",\"Ribosomes\",\"Plasma membrane\",\"Golgi complex\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, E.R. is connected to the nucleus from the inner side and to the plasma membrane from the outer side.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What makes the surface of Rough ER appear rough?\",\"options\":[\"Enzymes\",\"Cisternae\",\"Ribosome granules\",\"Vesicles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Rough ER has ribosome granules on its outer surface.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If toxins enter a person\'s body through contaminated air, which organelle helps in making them water-soluble to be flushed out?\",\"options\":[\"Golgi Complex\",\"Nucleus\",\"Endoplasmic Reticulum\",\"Ribosomes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"One function of ER is to make toxins that enter the body through food, air, and water water-soluble so they can be flushed out.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The Golgi complex is primarily made up of how many hollow and flat sacs?\",\"options\":[\"2-4\",\"5-8\",\"10-12\",\"15-20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook mentions that the Golgi Complex is made up of 5-8 hollow and flat sacs.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the parallelly placed sacs of the Golgi complex called?\",\"options\":[\"Vesicles\",\"Tubes\",\"Cisternae\",\"Ribosomes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The flat sacs placed parallel to each other in the Golgi complex are called \'cisternae\'.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the cell\'s \'factory\' analogy, which organelle functions as the \'Packing department\'?\",\"options\":[\"Endoplasmic Reticulum\",\"Nucleus\",\"Golgi Complex\",\"Plasma membrane\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that cisternae work like a packing department that packs and distributes substances.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Proteins synthesized in the cell are chemically modified with the help of enzymes as they pass through which part?\",\"options\":[\"Ribosomes\",\"Cisternae\",\"Plasma membrane\",\"Nucleus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that as proteins pass through the cisternae, they are chemically modified with the help of enzymes.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a target to which the Golgi complex dispatches materials?\",\"options\":[\"Plasma membrane\",\"Lysosome\",\"Nucleus\",\"Various targets inside the cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions dispatching to plasma membrane and lysosomes, but does not list the nucleus as a dispatch target for the Golgi.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The Golgi complex plays a role in the formation of which cell component?\",\"options\":[\"Ribosomes\",\"Cell wall\",\"Mitochondria\",\"Cytoplasm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Function 4 of the Golgi complex states it helps in the formation of the cell wall, plasma membrane, and lysosomes.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which staining technique was developed by Camillo Golgi to study the nervous system?\",\"options\":[\"White reaction\",\"Red reaction\",\"Black reaction\",\"Blue reaction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Camillo Golgi developed the staining technique called \'Black reaction\'.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the correct sequence of protein transport between these two organelles?\",\"options\":[\"Golgi complex -> Vesicle -> ER\",\"ER -> Vesicle -> Golgi complex\",\"Nucleus -> ER -> Plasma membrane\",\"Cytoplasm -> Cisternae -> ER\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states proteins coming from ER are enclosed in vesicles, which come towards the Golgi complex.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which structure is shown directly connected to the inner side of the network of tubes?\",\"options\":[\"Plasma membrane\",\"Nucleus\",\"Ribosome\",\"Vacuole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the Endoplasmic Reticulum connected to the large purple circular structure labeled \'Nucleus\'.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/080_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the small spherical structures labeled as \'Secretory vesicle\' doing?\",\"options\":[\"Entering the Cis face\",\"Fusing with the cisternae\",\"Exiting from the maturation face\",\"Turning into ribosomes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows secretory vesicles budding off and exiting from the lower side (maturation face) of the Golgi complex.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/081_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The scientist shown in the given figure won the Nobel Prize in 1906 along with which other scientist?\",\"options\":[\"Robert Hooke\",\"Santiago Cajal\",\"Louis Pasteur\",\"Albert Einstein\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text accompanying the scientist\'s photo states he won the Nobel prize of 1906 along with Santiago Cajal.\",\"topic\":\"ER and Golgi Complex\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/081_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Lysosomes are described as simple, single membrane bound sacs filled with which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Phosphate granules\",\"Digestive enzymes\",\"Ribosomes\",\"DNA\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, lysosomes are simple, single membrane bound sacs filled with digestive enzymes.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the full form of the energy-rich compound ATP produced by mitochondria?\",\"options\":[\"Adenosine Tri Phosphate\",\"Ammonium Tri Phosphate\",\"Adenosine Tetra Phosphate\",\"Active Tri Phosphate\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that ATP stands for Adenosine Tri Phosphate.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelle is referred to as the \'Suicide Bag\' of the cell?\",\"options\":[\"Mitochondria\",\"Ribosome\",\"Lysosome\",\"Nucleus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Lysosomes are called suicide bags because when a cell is damaged, they burst and enzymes digest their own cell.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these components is NOT mentioned as being present in the mitochondrial matrix?\",\"options\":[\"Ribosomes\",\"Phosphate granules\",\"DNA\",\"Digestive enzymes\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions ribosomes, phosphate granules, and DNA in the matrix, but digestive enzymes are found in lysosomes.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of lysosomes acting as \'Demolition squads\'?\",\"options\":[\"To produce energy for the cell\",\"To synthesize proteins and lipids\",\"To destroy worn out cellular organelles and organic debris\",\"To carry oxygen to different parts of the body\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the \'Demolition squads\' function as destroying worn out cellular organelles and organic debris.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are mitochondria able to produce their own proteins?\",\"options\":[\"Because they have a porous outer membrane\",\"Because they contain ribosomes and DNA\",\"Because they store energy in the form of ATP\",\"Because they oxidise carbohydrates\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that because the matrix contains ribosomes and DNA, the mitochondrion can produce its own proteins.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement correctly describes the membranes of a mitochondrion?\",\"options\":[\"Single membrane with many pores\",\"Outer membrane is folded and inner is porous\",\"Outer membrane is porous and inner is deeply folded\",\"Double membrane where both layers are porous\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Under the electron microscope, the outer membrane is seen as porous and the inner membrane is deeply folded.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific name given to the deep folds of the inner membrane?\",\"options\":[\"Matrix\",\"Cristae\",\"Pores\",\"Granules\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The labels in the figure and the text identify the folds of the inner membrane as \'cristae\'.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/082_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a cell is attacked by a virus, which organelle will work to destroy it as part of the immune system?\",\"options\":[\"Mitochondria\",\"Lysosome\",\"ATP\",\"Cristae\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that one function of lysosomes is the immune system, where it destroys viruses and bacteria.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During a period of starvation, how does a lysosome help the cell survive?\",\"options\":[\"By producing more ATP\",\"By folding its inner membrane\",\"By digesting stored proteins and fats\",\"By carrying oxygen to the cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that during starvation, lysosomes digest stored proteins and fats.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist observes a cell and notices it has very few mitochondria compared to another cell. Based on the text, which cell is likely to have fewer mitochondria?\",\"options\":[\"An animal cell\",\"A plant cell\",\"A bacterial cell\",\"A virus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Plant cells have less mitochondria than animal cells.\'\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the physiological consequence of Red Blood Cells (RBCs) lacking mitochondria?\",\"options\":[\"They cannot carry oxygen at all\",\"They use up all the oxygen they carry\",\"The oxygen they carry is not used for themselves\",\"They produce excessive amounts of ATP\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because mitochondria are absent in RBCs, the oxygen they carry is not used for themselves.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"When a lysosome bursts to digest its own damaged cell, the process is called \'Autolysis\'. Which function category does this fall under?\",\"options\":[\"Immune system\",\"Demolition squads\",\"Suicide Bags\",\"Energy production\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the bursting and digestion of its own cell to the \'Suicide Bags\' function.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the substance labeled as being contained within the single membrane?\",\"options\":[\"Phosphate granules\",\"Enzyme complex\",\"DNA matrix\",\"ATP compound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram of the lysosome labels the internal content as the \'Enzyme complex\'.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/081_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Mitochondria use enzymes to oxidise which substances to release energy?\",\"options\":[\"Proteins and DNA\",\"Carbohydrates and fats\",\"Viruses and bacteria\",\"Phosphate and ribosomes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that with the help of enzymes, mitochondria oxidise carbohydrates and fats in the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Lysosomes and Mitochondria\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nature of the membrane that bounds a vacuole?\",\"options\":[\"Double membrane\",\"Single membrane\",\"Triple membrane\",\"No membrane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a vacuole is bound by a single membrane.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the structure of a vacuole change within a cell?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the age of the cell\",\"Due to the type of pigment present\",\"Depending on the need of the cell\",\"Because of the presence of DNA\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the structure of the vacuole changes depending on the need of the cell.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which organism does the vacuole store food specifically before digestion?\",\"options\":[\"Plant cell\",\"Animal cell\",\"Amoeba\",\"Croton\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in amoeba, the vacuole stores food before digestion.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which pigment is responsible for the orange color seen in carrots?\",\"options\":[\"Chlorophyll\",\"Xanthophyll\",\"Anthocyanin\",\"Carotene\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"As per the pigment chart, Carotene gives the orange color to carrots.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A plant cell is observed to be very rigid and turgid. Which organelle is primarily responsible for this state?\",\"options\":[\"Chloroplast\",\"Vacuole\",\"Mitochondria\",\"Leucoplast\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In plant cells, vacuoles are full of cell sap and provide turgidity and rigidity.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens at the molecular level when a raw green tomato turns red?\",\"options\":[\"Chlorophyll breaks down and lycopene is synthesized\",\"Lycopene breaks down and chlorophyll is synthesized\",\"Leucoplasts convert into chloroplasts\",\"Anthocyanin replaces xanthophyll\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that raw green tomatoes turn red due to the breakdown of chlorophyll and synthesis of lycopene.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific part of the chloroplast contains the enzymes, DNA, and ribosomes necessary for photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Single membrane\",\"Cell sap\",\"Stroma\",\"Lycopene\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the stroma in the chloroplast contains enzymes, DNA, ribosomes, and carbohydrates.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are plastids and mitochondria unique compared to other organelles mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are only found in animal cells\",\"They have a single membrane structure\",\"They store only waste products\",\"They can replicate themselves using their own DNA\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Mitochondria and plastids can replicate themselves as they have their own DNA and ribosomes.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist finds a plastid storing starch and oils in a white potato, which type of plastid is it?\",\"options\":[\"Chloroplast\",\"Chromoplast\",\"Leucoplast\",\"Anthocyanin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Leucoplasts are white or colourless plastids involved in the synthesis and storage of starch, oils, and proteins.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which pigment would you expect to find in a flower that is purple or blue?\",\"options\":[\"Betalains\",\"Anthocyanin\",\"Carotene\",\"Chlorophyll\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the pigment chart, Anthocyanin is responsible for purple and blue colors.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How do chloroplasts contribute to the energy needs of a plant?\",\"options\":[\"By storing metabolic byproducts like glycogen\",\"By converting solar energy to chemical energy\",\"By maintaining osmotic pressure\",\"By breaking down lycopene into starch\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Chloroplasts trap solar energy and convert it to chemical energy (food) during photosynthesis.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is a function of vacuoles in animal cells but NOT their primary role in plant cells?\",\"options\":[\"Providing turgidity\",\"Storing cell sap\",\"Storing waste products\",\"Synthesizing starch\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that in animal cells, vacuoles store waste products and food.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which characteristic describes the boundary of the organelle shown?\",\"options\":[\"It is a double-layered membrane\",\"It is a single membrane\",\"It has no membrane\",\"It is a porous cell wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a vacuole, which the text defines as being bound by a single membrane.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/082_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, which pigment is associated with the yellow color of plant parts?\",\"options\":[\"Chlorophyll\",\"Carotene\",\"Xanthophyll\",\"Betalains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure lists Xanthophyll as the pigment for the color yellow.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/083_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If a plant is unable to produce proteins in its plastids, which component is likely missing from the stroma?\",\"options\":[\"Lycopene\",\"Ribosomes\",\"Anthocyanin\",\"Cell sap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that plastids produce proteins because they have their own ribosomes.\",\"topic\":\"Vacuoles and Plastids\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the National Centre for Cell Science (NCCS) located?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Pune\",\"Delhi\",\"Nagpur\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that NCCS is located in the campus of Savitribai Phule National University, Pune.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the typical size range of a prokaryotic cell?\",\"options\":[\"1-10 micrometer\",\"5-100 micrometer\",\"10-50 micrometer\",\"100-200 micrometer\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the comparison table, prokaryotic cells are 1-10 micrometers in size.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a feature of the nucleus in a eukaryotic cell?\",\"options\":[\"It lacks a nuclear membrane\",\"It is called a nucleoid\",\"It contains a nuclear membrane, nucleolus and nucleoplasm\",\"It has no nucleolus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Eukaryotic cells have a nucleus with a nuclear membrane, nucleolus, and nucleoplasm.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How many chromosomes are found in a prokaryotic cell?\",\"options\":[\"More than one\",\"Only one\",\"Two\",\"Zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that prokaryotic cells have only one chromosome.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"NCCS is an independent institute working under which department of the Government of India?\",\"options\":[\"Department of Health\",\"Department of Science and Technology\",\"Biotechnology department\",\"Department of Education\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions NCCS works under the biotechnology department of the Government of India.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist observes a cell that is 50 micrometers in size. Based on the textbook, what type of cell is it most likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"Prokaryotic cell\",\"Bacterial cell\",\"Eukaryotic cell\",\"Nucleoid cell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Eukaryotic cells range from 5-100 micrometers, while prokaryotic cells are only 1-10 micrometers.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which organelles are specifically mentioned as being present in eukaryotic cells but absent in prokaryotic cells?\",\"options\":[\"Ribosomes\",\"Mitochondria and plastids\",\"Chromosomes\",\"Nucleoplasm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states mitochondria and plastids are present in eukaryotic cells, while prokaryotes don\'t have membrane-bound organelles.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Bacteria are examples of which type of cell?\",\"options\":[\"Multicellular cells\",\"Eukaryotic cells\",\"Prokaryotic cells\",\"Developed cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists bacteria as examples of prokaryotic cells.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main area of research for cancer treatment mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"National Animal cell repository\",\"National Centre for Cell Science (NCCS)\",\"Savitribai Phule Pune University\",\"Biotechnology department\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"NCCS is mainly working on research about cancer treatment.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a cell contains more than one chromosome, it can be classified as:\",\"options\":[\"A prokaryotic cell\",\"A bacterial cell\",\"A eukaryotic cell\",\"A nucleoid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states eukaryotic cells have more than one chromosome, whereas prokaryotes have only one.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between the \'nucleus\' of a eukaryote and the \'nucleoid\' of a prokaryote?\",\"options\":[\"The nucleoid has a membrane while the nucleus does not\",\"The nucleus has a membrane and nucleolus, while the nucleoid only resembles a nucleus\",\"The nucleoid is larger than the nucleus\",\"The nucleus is only found in bacteria\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Eukaryotic nuclei have membranes and nucleoli; prokaryotes have a \'nucleoid resembling nucleus\' without membrane-bound structures.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are eukaryotic cells described as \'developed cells\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are only found in bacteria\",\"Because they are smaller in size\",\"Because all functions are taking place smoothly due to cell organelles\",\"Because they have only one chromosome\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that developed cells (eukaryotic) have organelles that allow all functions to take place smoothly.\",\"topic\":\"Eukaryotic and Prokaryotic Cells\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22161,57,8,'Science','en','Human Body and Organ System','[\"- The human body is made of cells, tissues, organs, and organ systems.\",\"- Life processes (growth, reproduction, respiration, circulation, excretion, sensation, movement) are carried out by organ systems.\",\"- An organ system is a group of organs that work together to perform a specific function.\",\"- Major organ systems: digestive, respiratory, circulatory, nervous, excretory, reproductive, skeletal, muscular, endocrine, immune.\",\"- Respiration has three stages: external (inhalation\\/exhalation), internal (gas exchange in tissues), cellular (oxidation of glucose to produce ATP).\",\"- The respiratory system includes nose, pharynx, trachea, bronchi, lungs, alveoli, diaphragm.\",\"- The circulatory system includes heart, arteries, veins, capillaries; it transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste, and immune cells.\",\"- The heart has four chambers: two atria and two ventricles; it pumps blood through systemic and pulmonary circuits.\",\"- Arteries carry oxygenated blood away from the heart (except pulmonary artery); veins carry deoxygenated blood to the heart (except pulmonary vein).\",\"- Capillaries are single‑cell‑thick vessels that allow exchange of gases, nutrients, and waste between blood and tissues.\",\"- Blood is a fluid connective tissue composed of plasma (55%) and cells (45%): red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.\",\"- Red blood cells carry oxygen via hemoglobin; white blood cells defend the body; platelets help blood clotting.\",\"- Blood groups (A, B, AB, O) and Rh factor (+\\/-) determine compatibility for transfusion.\",\"- Blood pressure is measured as systolic\\/diastolic (e.g., 120\\/80 mmHg).\",\"- Healthy blood pressure ranges: 90–119\\/60–79 mmHg; hypertension is >140\\/90 mmHg.\",\"- Blood donation is safe for healthy adults 18–60 years, with adequate weight and health checks.\",\"- The diaphragm and chest wall movements create negative pressure for inhalation and positive pressure for exhalation.\",\"- The heart rate (beats per minute) is about 72–75 in a healthy adult at rest.\",\"- Blood is continuously produced in the bone marrow and replaced every 4–5 months.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are the vital processes that are essential properties of living beings collectively called?\",\"options\":[\"Life processes\",\"Organ systems\",\"Physical activities\",\"Body functions\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that all vital processes essential for living beings are collectively called life processes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is defined as a group of organs working together to perform a specific function?\",\"options\":[\"Tissue group\",\"Organ system\",\"Cellular colony\",\"Vital process\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a group of organs working together to perform a specific function is called an organ system.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How do different organs in the human body ensure that life processes are carried out smoothly?\",\"options\":[\"They work independently of each other\",\"They work in groups\",\"They work only during the day\",\"They work without specific steps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that different organs work in groups so as to smoothly carry out various life processes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In what manner do life processes take place within the body?\",\"options\":[\"In a single, sudden step\",\"Without the help of organs\",\"In various steps\",\"Only when the body is active\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that these life processes take place in various steps.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what can be said about life processes during sound sleep?\",\"options\":[\"They stop completely\",\"They occur continuously\",\"Only the digestive system works\",\"They only start when we wake up\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies through its questions that life processes occur continuously in our body, even during sleep.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which organ system listed in the text is responsible for the transport of materials through the body?\",\"options\":[\"Respiratory system\",\"Excretory system\",\"Circulatory system\",\"Skeletal system\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The circulatory system is listed as one of the functioning organ systems in the human body.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of specific organs within an organ system?\",\"options\":[\"To perform every step of every life process\",\"To carry out specific steps of a function\",\"To replace the work of the nervous system\",\"To work only when other organs fail\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that specific organs carry out the specific steps of life processes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as an organ system in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Nervous system\",\"Muscular system\",\"Reproductive system\",\"Solar system\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The solar system is not a biological organ system mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the relationship between organs and organ systems: Why is the digestive system considered a \'system\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is made of a single organ\",\"Because it is a group of organs working together for a specific function\",\"Because it only works during sound sleep\",\"Because it is the only vital process in the body\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"An organ system is defined as a group of organs working together for a specific function.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which system mentioned in the text is primarily responsible for the framework and support of the human body?\",\"options\":[\"Excretory\",\"Skeletal\",\"Digestive\",\"Respiratory\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The skeletal system is listed as one of the functioning organ systems in the human body.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why are processes like respiration and circulation called \'life processes\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are optional for living beings\",\"Because they are essential properties of living beings\",\"Because they only occur in the skeletal system\",\"Because they happen only in groups of two organs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines life processes as vital processes which are essential properties of living beings.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which system mentioned in the text is responsible for the removal of waste from the body?\",\"options\":[\"Excretory system\",\"Respiratory system\",\"Muscular system\",\"Reproductive system\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The excretory system is listed as one of the functioning organ systems in the body.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction to Organ Systems\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which organ is also referred to as the windpipe?\",\"options\":[\"Larynx\",\"Pharynx\",\"Trachea\",\"Bronchus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that air is sent towards the lungs through the trachea (wind pipe).\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In which form is energy released during the process of cellular respiration?\",\"options\":[\"Glucose\",\"ATP\",\"Oxygen\",\"Tissue fluid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that energy is released in the form of ATP during cellular respiration.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which two systems work together to supply soluble nutrients and oxygen to the cells?\",\"options\":[\"Digestive and Nervous systems\",\"Respiratory and Circulatory systems\",\"Excretory and Respiratory systems\",\"Circulatory and Muscular systems\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the supply of nutrients and oxygen takes place with the help of respiratory and circulatory systems.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the exchange of gases between cells and tissue fluid called?\",\"options\":[\"External respiration\",\"Inhalation\",\"Internal respiration\",\"Exhalation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines internal respiration as the exchange of gases between cells and tissue fluid.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical equation provided, how many molecules of water (H2O) are produced from one molecule of glucose?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"38\",\"12\",\"6\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The equation C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + Energy shows 6 molecules of H2O are produced.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which organism is listed first in the \'Use your brain power\' box for studying respiration?\",\"options\":[\"Earthworm\",\"Cockroach\",\"Amoeba\",\"Birds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text in the figure starts the list of organisms with \'amoeba\'.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/085_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which category of organisms would require a student to research how breathing occurs under water?\",\"options\":[\"Plants\",\"Aquatic animals\",\"Cockroach\",\"Earthworm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure mentions \'various aquatic animals\', which live and respire in water.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/085_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"During internal respiration, what is the correct direction of gas movement?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen moves from blood into tissue fluid\",\"Oxygen moves from tissue fluid into blood\",\"Carbon dioxide moves from blood into tissue fluid\",\"Oxygen moves from lungs into the nose\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that during internal respiration, oxygen moves from blood into tissue fluid.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the waste materials produced during cellular respiration?\",\"options\":[\"Glucose and Oxygen\",\"ATP and Glucose\",\"CO2 and water vapours\",\"Oxygen and water vapours\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies CO2 and water vapours as waste materials produced during cellular respiration.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a person\'s circulatory system fails to transport blood to the tissues, which step of respiration will be directly hindered first?\",\"options\":[\"Inhalation\",\"External respiration\",\"Internal respiration\",\"Air entering the nose\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Internal respiration requires the exchange of gases between blood and tissue fluid, which depends on the circulatory system.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the process of oxidation in cellular respiration?\",\"options\":[\"Glucose is burnt with the help of oxygen to release energy\",\"Carbon dioxide is converted into glucose\",\"Water vapours are absorbed by the cells\",\"ATP is converted into oxygen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that dissolved nutrients like glucose are slowly burnt (oxidized) with the help of oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Analyze the chemical equation for cellular respiration. What is the ratio of Oxygen molecules consumed to Carbon Dioxide molecules produced?\",\"options\":[\"1:6\",\"6:1\",\"1:1\",\"38:6\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The equation shows 6O2 are consumed and 6CO2 are produced, making the ratio 6:6, which simplifies to 1:1.\",\"topic\":\"Respiration and its Steps\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What substances present in the nose help in filtering the air during respiration?\",\"options\":[\"Blood and capillaries\",\"Hair and mucus\",\"Oxygen and hemoglobin\",\"Saliva and water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that air is filtered with the help of hair and mucus present in the nose.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where do the food pipe and wind pipe originate?\",\"options\":[\"Nasal cavity\",\"Larynx\",\"Pharynx\",\"Thorax\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, both the food pipe and wind pipe originate in the pharynx.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the lid located at the beginning of the wind pipe?\",\"options\":[\"To produce sound during speech\",\"To filter dust particles from the air\",\"To prevent food particles from entering the wind pipe\",\"To increase the pressure on the lungs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The lid closes the wind pipe during the passing of food into the food pipe to prevent entry of food particles.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the wind pipe swollen at its beginning?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the presence of mucus\",\"Due to the sound box\",\"To store extra oxygen\",\"To allow bifurcation into the lungs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the wind pipe is swollen at the beginning due to the sound box.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the double-layered covering of the lungs called?\",\"options\":[\"Alveoli\",\"Diaphragm\",\"Pleura\",\"Thorax\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Each lung has a double-layered covering called the pleura.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of the lungs is compared to a sponge in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Their color\",\"Their hardness\",\"Their elasticity\",\"Their temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that lungs are elastic like a sponge.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How does the large number of alveoli affect the process of gaseous exchange?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the lungs heavier\",\"It provides a larger surface for gaseous exchange\",\"It prevents the entry of water vapours\",\"It slows down the circulation of blood\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As a large number of alveoli is present, a larger surface is available for gaseous exchange.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which iron-containing protein in the blood is responsible for absorbing oxygen from the alveoli?\",\"options\":[\"Mucus\",\"Pleura\",\"Hemoglobin\",\"Plasma\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Hemoglobin is an iron-containing protein in RBCs that absorbs oxygen from the air within alveoli.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"During gaseous exchange in the lungs, which substances move from the blood into the alveoli?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen and Hemoglobin\",\"CO2 and water vapours\",\"Oxygen and CO2\",\"Water vapours and Hemoglobin\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Simultaneously with oxygen absorption, CO2 and water vapours move from blood into the alveoli.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the muscular partition present between the thoracic cavity and abdominal cavity called?\",\"options\":[\"Trachea\",\"Pharynx\",\"Diaphragm\",\"Bronchioles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diaphragm is the muscular partition at the base of the thoracic cage, between the thoracic and abdominal cavities.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following actions causes air to move into the lungs through the nose?\",\"options\":[\"Lowering of ribs and rising of diaphragm\",\"Rising up of ribs and lowering of diaphragm\",\"Rising up of ribs and rising of diaphragm\",\"Lowering of ribs and lowering of diaphragm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Simultaneous rising up of ribs and lowering of diaphragm decreases pressure on lungs, causing air to move in.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the pressure on the lungs when the diaphragm rises up and ribs return to their original position?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure decreases and air moves in\",\"Pressure increases and air moves out\",\"Pressure remains constant\",\"Pressure decreases and air moves out\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When ribs return and the diaphragm rises, pressure on the lungs increases, moving air out.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the respiratory system, which structure is shown as the main tube branching into the two lungs?\",\"options\":[\"Pharynx\",\"Larynx\",\"Trachea\",\"Diaphragm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Trachea (wind pipe) is the central tube that bifurcates into the right and left lungs.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/086_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what are the small grape-like structures that provide the surface for gas exchange?\",\"options\":[\"Bronchioles\",\"Alveoli\",\"Larynx\",\"Pleura\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure shows Alveoli as the small compartments where gas exchange occurs.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/086_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure showing the interaction between alveoli and capillaries, what is the role of the RBCs?\",\"options\":[\"To produce water vapours\",\"To filter air entering the alveoli\",\"To transport gases like oxygen via hemoglobin\",\"To act as a lid for the wind pipe\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows RBCs in the capillary next to the alveolus, where they facilitate the transport and exchange of gases.\",\"topic\":\"Respiratory System: Structure\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/086_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate weight of a human heart as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"250 gram\",\"360 gram\",\"500 gram\",\"450 gram\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the weight of the human heart is about 360 gram.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following components make up the human blood circulatory system?\",\"options\":[\"Heart, lungs and ribs\",\"Heart, blood vessels and capillaries\",\"Heart, hormones and water\",\"Heart, muscles and thoracic cage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the circulatory system consists of the heart, blood vessels, and capillaries.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary function of the fluid present between the two layers of the peritoneal membrane covering the heart?\",\"options\":[\"To transport oxygen to the cardiac muscles\",\"To help the heart contract and relax\",\"To protect the heart from friction and mechanical shocks\",\"To divide the heart into four chambers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a fluid is present between two membranes due to which the heart is protected from friction and mechanical shocks.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"The rhythmic contraction and relaxation of the heart is known as ________.\",\"options\":[\"Blood circulation\",\"Mechanical shock\",\"Beating of heart\",\"Thoracic rhythm\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the definite rhythm of contraction and relaxation of cardiac muscles as the beating of the heart.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to estimate the size of their own heart, which method should they use according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Measure the width of their thoracic cage\",\"Check the size of their own fist\",\"Compare it with the size of their lungs\",\"Calculate it based on their body weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the size of our heart is equal to one\'s own fist.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Since the heart must pump blood continuously without our conscious control, it is made up of which specific type of muscles?\",\"options\":[\"Voluntary skeletal muscles\",\"Involuntary cardiac muscles\",\"Voluntary smooth muscles\",\"Involuntary lung muscles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the heart is a muscular organ made up of involuntary cardiac muscles.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the internal structure of the heart achieve the formation of four distinct chambers?\",\"options\":[\"By a single horizontal partition only\",\"By two vertical partitions and one horizontal partition\",\"By a vertical partition and further division of each compartment\",\"By the presence of two separate peritoneal membranes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the heart is divided by a vertical partition into two compartments, and each is again divided into two chambers, totaling four.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the description of the heart\'s location, which of these statements is correct?\",\"options\":[\"The heart is located entirely on the right side of the chest.\",\"The heart is located in the thoracic cage, between the two lungs.\",\"The heart is located above the ribs in the thoracic cage.\",\"The heart is located behind the stomach in the abdominal cavity.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes the heart\'s location as being in the thoracic cage, behind the ribs, and between two lungs.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are the lower chambers of the human heart called?\",\"options\":[\"Atria\",\"Atrium\",\"Ventricles\",\"Peritoneal chambers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly names the upper chambers as atria and the lower chambers as ventricles.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is NOT mentioned in the text as being transported by the circulatory system?\",\"options\":[\"Soluble nutrients\",\"Waste materials\",\"Hormones\",\"Digestive enzymes\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists water, hormones, oxygen, soluble nutrients, and waste materials; digestive enzymes are not mentioned.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which organ of the human body is depicted?\",\"options\":[\"Lungs\",\"Heart\",\"Kidney\",\"Liver\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates the external structure of the human heart as described in the topic.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/087_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the external surface shows various vessels. According to the text, these vessels and the organ itself are covered by which protective layer?\",\"options\":[\"A single layer of ribs\",\"A double layered peritoneal membrane\",\"A thick layer of cardiac muscle only\",\"A vertical partition membrane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the heart is covered by a double layered peritoneal membrane for protection.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Circulatory System: Heart\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/087_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of blood vessels carry blood away from the heart to various parts of the body?\",\"options\":[\"Veins\",\"Capillaries\",\"Arteries\",\"Valves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, arteries are the blood vessels which carry the blood away from the heart.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where are most of the veins located in the human body?\",\"options\":[\"Deeply located\",\"Superficially located\",\"Inside the bones\",\"Only near the lungs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that most of the veins are superficially located in the body.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many layers of cells make up the walls of capillaries?\",\"options\":[\"Double layer\",\"Triple layer\",\"Multiple layers\",\"Single layer\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that walls of capillaries are extremely thin and made up of a single layer of cells.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which artery is the only exception that does not carry oxygenated blood?\",\"options\":[\"The one carrying blood towards the lungs\",\"The one carrying blood towards the brain\",\"The one carrying blood towards the heart\",\"The one carrying blood towards the kidneys\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that except the one carrying blood towards lungs, all arteries carry oxygenated blood.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the diameter of arteries as they branch out and spread in the body to form capillaries?\",\"options\":[\"The diameter increases\",\"The diameter decreases\",\"The diameter remains the same\",\"The walls become thicker\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that arteries gradually branch out with a decrease in their diameter to form capillaries.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which vessels are formed when capillaries unite together?\",\"options\":[\"Arteries\",\"Valves\",\"Veins\",\"Heart chambers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, capillaries unite together to form the vessels of more diameter, called as veins.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"During the exchange of materials in capillaries, which of these moves from the cells into the blood?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Nutrients\",\"Vitamins\",\"Waste materials\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text explains that during exchange, waste materials of the cells move into the blood.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a blood vessel has thin walls and is provided with valves, it is most likely a\\/an:\",\"options\":[\"Artery\",\"Vein\",\"Capillary\",\"Aorta\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes veins as having thin walls and being provided with valves.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific structure is present in the cross-section of the vein but absent in the artery?\",\"options\":[\"Muscle\",\"Intema\",\"Valve\",\"Connective tissue\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure labels a \'Valve\' inside the vein, which is not shown in the artery structure.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/088_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure, how does the thickness of the muscle layer in the artery compare to that in the vein?\",\"options\":[\"The artery has a thinner muscle layer\",\"The artery has a thicker muscle layer\",\"Both have muscle layers of equal thickness\",\"The vein does not have a muscle layer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the red-colored muscle layer is significantly thicker in the artery than in the blue-colored vein.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/088_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the extremely thin wall of capillaries significant for the body\'s function?\",\"options\":[\"It allows the blood to flow faster\",\"It prevents the blood from leaking\",\"It makes the exchange of materials between capillaries and cells easy\",\"It helps the capillaries to withstand high pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the thin walls, exchange of materials between capillaries and cells becomes easy.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary reason that blood continuously circulates through the blood vessels?\",\"options\":[\"Because the walls of arteries are thick\",\"Because the heart is beating continuously\",\"Because of the presence of valves in veins\",\"Because capillaries are hair-like\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text begins by stating that due to the heart beating continuously, blood continuously circulates through blood vessels.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Vessels and Capillaries\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the average number of heartbeats per minute for a healthy person?\",\"options\":[\"60 beats\",\"72 beats\",\"80 beats\",\"90 beats\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a healthy person has 72 beats of heart per minute.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the two types of sounds heard during a heartbeat?\",\"options\":[\"Tick and Tock\",\"Lubb and Dub\",\"Hiss and Hush\",\"Tap and Clap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the two sounds heard during a heart beat are described as \'lubb\' and \'dub\'.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Approximately how much blood does the heart pump during each single beat?\",\"options\":[\"50 ml\",\"75 ml\",\"100 ml\",\"125 ml\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the heart pumps about 75 ml of blood during each beat.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What constitutes a single heart beat?\",\"options\":[\"Two consecutive contractions\",\"Two consecutive relaxations\",\"A single contraction followed by a single relaxation\",\"The pumping of blood to the lungs only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Consecutive single contraction and relaxation of the heart constitutes a single heart beat.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of pumping blood to various body parts and bringing it back to the heart called?\",\"options\":[\"Blood Respiration\",\"Blood Circulation\",\"Blood Filtration\",\"Blood Digestion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The process of pumping blood towards body parts and bringing it back to the heart is called blood circulation.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does the heart maintain continuity in blood circulation?\",\"options\":[\"By staying in a relaxed state\",\"By contracting continuously without stopping\",\"By alternately contracting and relaxing\",\"By pumping blood only when the body moves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"To maintain continuity in circulation, the heart alternately contracts and relaxes.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a healthy person\'s heart beats 72 times in one minute, how much total blood is pumped in that minute?\",\"options\":[\"4500 ml\",\"5000 ml\",\"5400 ml\",\"6000 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If one beat pumps 75 ml, then 72 beats pump 72 x 75 = 5400 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In which of the following situations would a person\'s heart rate most likely increase?\",\"options\":[\"While sleeping deeply\",\"While resting on a sofa\",\"During physical exercise\",\"While sitting quietly in a library\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the rate of heart beat increases due to physical exercise and emotions.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the observations mentioned in the text, who is likely to have the highest number of heartbeats per minute?\",\"options\":[\"A resting adult\",\"An infant\",\"A sleeping teenager\",\"An elderly person at rest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the number of beats is more in case of infants.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the heart rate during rest and sleep?\",\"options\":[\"It increases significantly\",\"It stays exactly at 72 beats\",\"It has been observed to decrease\",\"It stops for short intervals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that it has been observed that the heart rate decreases during rest and sleep.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the \'Try this\' experiment, where should the wide mouth of the funnel be placed to hear the heart sounds?\",\"options\":[\"On the right side of the thorax\",\"On the left side of the thorax\",\"On the center of the abdomen\",\"On the wrist\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment instructions say to keep the wide mouth of the funnel on the left side of the thorax.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between heartbeats and the pulses felt at the wrist?\",\"options\":[\"They are completely unrelated\",\"The pulse is always twice as fast as the heartbeat\",\"There is a correlation between heartbeats and pulses\",\"The pulse only happens during sleep\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Think about it\' section asks to find the correlation between heart beats and pulses felt at the wrist, implying they are related.\",\"topic\":\"Heart Functioning and Pulse\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the typical pH value of human blood?\",\"options\":[\"7.0\",\"7.4\",\"8.2\",\"6.8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the pH of blood is 7.4.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which protein in the plasma is responsible for the distribution of water all over the body?\",\"options\":[\"Globulin\",\"Fibrinogen\",\"Prothrombin\",\"Albumin\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Albumin distributes the water all over the body as mentioned in the plasma components.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate lifespan of Red Blood Corpuscles (RBCs)?\",\"options\":[\"50 - 60 days\",\"100 - 127 days\",\"10 - 20 days\",\"200 - 250 days\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, RBCs live for about 100 - 127 days.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which blood cells are described as \'soldiers\' because they attack pathogens entering the body?\",\"options\":[\"Red Blood Corpuscles\",\"Platelets\",\"White Blood Cells\",\"Plasma cells\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"WBCs act as soldiers by attacking pathogens and protecting us from microbial diseases.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which inorganic ions are responsible for controlling the functions of muscles and nerves?\",\"options\":[\"Iron and Copper\",\"Calcium, Sodium, and Potassium\",\"Magnesium and Zinc\",\"Iodine and Phosphorus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Inorganic ions like Ca, Na, and K control the function of muscles and nerves.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a patient\'s blood report shows 8,000 cells per mm3, which type of blood cell is being measured?\",\"options\":[\"Red Blood Corpuscles\",\"Platelets\",\"White Blood Cells\",\"Plasma proteins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that 5000-10000 WBCs are present per mm3 of blood.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which component of blood is pale yellow, clear, and slightly alkaline?\",\"options\":[\"Hemoglobin\",\"Platelets\",\"RBCs\",\"Plasma\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Plasma is described as a pale yellow, clear, and slightly alkaline fluid.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A person sustains a cut and the bleeding stops after a while. Which protein and cell type worked together to form the clot?\",\"options\":[\"Albumin and RBCs\",\"Globulin and WBCs\",\"Fibrinogen and Platelets\",\"Prothrombin and RBCs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that platelets and a protein called fibrinogen form a clot to seal injuries.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do Red Blood Corpuscles (RBCs) appear red in color?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the presence of oxygen\",\"Due to the presence of hemoglobin\",\"Due to the presence of iron salts\",\"Due to the presence of plasma\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"RBCs appear red due to the presence of hemoglobin.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of White Blood Cell (WBC)?\",\"options\":[\"Basophils\",\"Monocytes\",\"Lymphocytes\",\"Albumin\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Albumin is a plasma protein, not a type of WBC.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where are Red Blood Corpuscles and White Blood Cells produced in the human body?\",\"options\":[\"Yellow bone marrow\",\"Red bone marrow\",\"Liver\",\"Heart\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that both RBCs and WBCs are produced in the red bone marrow.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the composition of plasma, what is the most abundant substance found in it?\",\"options\":[\"Proteins\",\"Inorganic salts\",\"Water\",\"Glucose\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plasma contains 90-92% water, making it the most abundant component.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is a major structural difference between RBCs and WBCs mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"RBCs are nucleated while WBCs are enucleated\",\"RBCs are enucleated while WBCs are nucleated\",\"RBCs are larger than WBCs\",\"WBCs contain hemoglobin while RBCs do not\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes RBCs as enucleated and WBCs as nucleated cells.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which function of blood involves the regulation of body temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Thermoregulation\",\"Protection\",\"Transport of nutrients\",\"Blood clotting\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Thermoregulation is listed as the 6th function of blood in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a sample of 2 cubic millimeters of blood is taken, approximately how many platelets would you expect to find?\",\"options\":[\"50 - 60 lakh\",\"10,000 - 20,000\",\"5 - 8 lakh\",\"100 - 127\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since there are 2.5 - 4 lakh platelets per mm3, 2 mm3 would contain 5 - 8 lakh platelets.\",\"topic\":\"Composition and Functions of Blood\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many main groups of human blood are identified based on proteins like antigens and antibodies?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Four\",\"Six\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states there are four main groups of human blood: A, B, AB, and O.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two types of each blood group based on the Rh factor?\",\"options\":[\"Rh positive and Rh negative\",\"Rh active and Rh passive\",\"Rh major and Rh minor\",\"Rh alpha and Rh beta\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there are two types as \'Rh\' negative and \'Rh\' positive for each of the four groups.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which blood group is referred to as the \'universal donor\'?\",\"options\":[\"Blood group A\",\"Blood group B\",\"Blood group AB\",\"Blood group O\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"A person with blood group \'O\' can donate to any other group, making them the universal donor.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is blood group AB called the \'universal recipient\'?\",\"options\":[\"It can donate blood to any other group\",\"It can receive blood from any other group\",\"It contains both Rh positive and negative factors\",\"It is the most common blood group found in humans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the person with \'AB\' blood group can receive blood from a person with any other blood group.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the blood group of a human being according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The type of food consumed\",\"The amount of blood in the body\",\"Genes inherited from parents\",\"The age of the person\"],\"