n a refrigerator\",\"In a vacuum sealed bag at room temperature\",\"In a sterilized oven\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that collected blood can be stored for some days in a refrigerator.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A healthy person decides to give blood to help an accident victim. What is this person called in medical terms?\",\"options\":[\"Blood recipient\",\"Blood donor\",\"Blood matches\",\"Blood carrier\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A person who donates blood is referred to as a blood donor.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Considering the 4 main groups and the 2 Rh types for each, how many total blood groups are formed in humans?\",\"options\":[\"4\",\"6\",\"8\",\"10\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"With four main groups and two Rh types for each, the text states that in all eight blood groups are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During which of these situations is blood specifically required according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Regular exercise\",\"Parturition\",\"Digestion\",\"Sleep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions blood is required in situations like accidents, bleeding, parturition, and surgical operations.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is blood donation considered the \'best donation\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is done without any expectation to save a life\",\"Because it is a very expensive process\",\"Because it changes the donor\'s blood group\",\"Because it is required only during surgical operations\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states blood donation without expectation is life saving and thus considered the best donation.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which proteins are responsible for the formation of different blood groups?\",\"options\":[\"Vitamins and Minerals\",\"Antigens and Antibodies\",\"Hemoglobin and Iron\",\"Genes and DNA\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that depending upon proteins like antigens and antibodies, different blood groups are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Groups and Blood Donation\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is used to measure blood pressure according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Stethoscope\",\"Thermometer\",\"Sphygmomanometer\",\"Barometer\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that blood pressure is measured with the help of a \'sphygmomanometer\'.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is National Voluntary Blood Donation Day observed in India?\",\"options\":[\"1st January\",\"1st October\",\"14th November\",\"5th September\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that 1st October is observed as National Voluntary Blood Donation Day.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Who discovered the A, B, and O blood groups in the year 1900?\",\"options\":[\"Decastello\",\"Sturli\",\"Carl Landsteiner\",\"Robert Hooke\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that A. B. O blood groups were discovered by Carl Landsteiner in 1900.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the branch of medical science that deals with the study of blood and blood diseases called?\",\"options\":[\"Cardiology\",\"Hematology\",\"Oncology\",\"Physiology\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines hematology as the branch of medical science dealing with the study of blood, hematopoietic organs, and blood diseases.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the pressure recorded during the contraction of the heart called?\",\"options\":[\"Diastolic pressure\",\"Atmospheric pressure\",\"Systolic pressure\",\"Primary pressure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, pressure recorded during the contraction of the heart is called \'systolic pressure\'.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of blood pressure, what does \'diastolic pressure\' refer to?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure during heart contraction\",\"Pressure during heart relaxation\",\"Pressure in the veins\",\"Pressure during blood donation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines diastolic pressure as the pressure recorded during the relaxation of the heart.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the heart\'s workload in a person suffering from hypertension?\",\"options\":[\"Heart performs less function than normal\",\"Heart performs more function than normal\",\"Heart stops performing any function\",\"Heart function remains unchanged\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the heart needs to perform more function than the normal condition in case of hypertension.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A healthy donor is worried about the fluid loss after donating blood. Based on the text, what can be concluded about the recovery of this fluid?\",\"options\":[\"It takes one week to restore the fluid\",\"The fluid is never restored\",\"The body restores the fluid part within 24 hours\",\"The fluid is restored only after drinking 5 liters of water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that our body restores the fluid part of the donated blood within 24 hours.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"A person\'s blood pressure is measured as 150\\/95 mm Hg. According to the categories provided in the text, which condition does this person have?\",\"options\":[\"Normal Blood Pressure\",\"Primary Hypertension\",\"Hypertension stage-I\",\"Hypertension stage-II\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines Hypertension stage-I as 140-159 mm (systolic) \\/ 90-99 mm (diastolic).\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A healthy 25-year-old man wants to donate blood regularly. According to the text, how many times can he donate in one year?\",\"options\":[\"Only once a year\",\"3 - 4 times a year\",\"Every month\",\"10 times a year\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a healthy person of age more than 18 years can donate blood for 3 - 4 times a year.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a healthy person donates the standard amount of blood three times in a single year, what is the total volume of blood they have donated?\",\"options\":[\"350 ml\",\"700 ml\",\"1050 ml\",\"1400 ml\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says about 350 ml is collected per donation; 350 ml x 3 = 1050 ml.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how do the systolic and diastolic pressure levels change during hypertension compared to normal conditions?\",\"options\":[\"Only systolic pressure becomes high\",\"Only diastolic pressure becomes high\",\"Both systolic and diastolic pressures are high\",\"Both systolic and diastolic pressures are low\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Both, systolic and diastolic pressures are high in hypertension\'.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT regarding blood donation eligibility based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"A person must be more than 18 years old\",\"Pregnant women cannot donate blood\",\"Breastfeeding women can donate blood\",\"A healthy person can donate 3-4 times a year\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text clearly states that \'Pregnant and breast feeding women cannot donate the blood\'.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which medical instrument is depicted?\",\"options\":[\"Microscope\",\"Sphygmomanometer\",\"ECG machine\",\"Stethoscope\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure caption identifies the instrument as a \'Sphygmomanometer\'.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/091_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the primary function of the shown device?\",\"options\":[\"To measure body temperature\",\"To measure blood pressure\",\"To count blood cells\",\"To check heart rate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A sphygmomanometer is used to measure blood pressure as per the text.\",\"topic\":\"Blood Pressure and Hypertension\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/091_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22162,57,8,'Science','en','Introduction to Acid and Base','[\"Acid is a substance that tastes sour and gives hydrogen ions (H⁺) in water.\",\"Base tastes bitter, feels slippery and gives hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in water.\",\"Litmus paper: blue turns red in acid, red turns blue in base.\",\"Phenolphthalein: colourless in acid, pink in base.\",\"Methyl orange: orange in acid, yellow in base.\",\"Universal indicator changes through many colours from red (acid) to green (neutral) to blue (base).\",\"Acids are used in food (citric, acetic), industry (sulphuric, hydrochloric) and medicine (antacids).\",\"Bases are used in soap, cleaning, neutralising acids and in industry (sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide).\",\"Neutralisation: Acid + Base → Salt + Water. Example: HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O.\",\"Strong acids (HCl, H₂SO₄) are corrosive; weak acids (acetic, citric) are milder.\",\"Indicators are substances that change colour when they encounter acid or base.\",\"Natural indicators can be made from hibiscus, rose, turmeric, red cabbage, etc.\",\"Baking soda (NaHCO₃) is a base; when added to fruit juice it produces bubbles (CO₂).\",\"Acidic solutions turn blue litmus red; basic solutions turn red litmus blue.\",\"The pH scale: 0–7 acidic, 7 neutral, 7–14 basic.\",\"Acids react with metals to produce hydrogen gas.\",\"Acids react with carbonates to release CO₂ and heat.\",\"Bases react with acids to form salts and water.\",\"Acids and bases are essential for life: stomach acid digests food, enzymes use pH.\",\"Acidic soil can be neutralised by adding lime (Ca(OH)₂).\",\"Safety: never taste or touch strong acids or bases; use gloves and goggles.\"]','[{\"q\":\"According to the text, what typical compound imparts a sour taste to substances like lemon and tamarind?\",\"options\":[\"Bases\",\"Acids\",\"Salts\",\"Sugars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that compounds imparting a sour taste are called acids.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the acids present in food stuffs specifically called?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral acids\",\"Strong acids\",\"Natural or organic acids\",\"Caustic acids\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines acids present in food stuffs as natural acids or organic acids.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a physical property of acids mentioned in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"They are insoluble in water\",\"They have a sweet taste\",\"They are corrosive in nature\",\"They are always tasteless\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that acids are soluble in water and they are corrosive in nature.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are organic acids also referred to as \'weak acids\' in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are found in animals\",\"Because they are weak in nature\",\"Because they cannot be dissolved in water\",\"Because they have no taste\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that organic acids, being weak in nature, are called weak acids.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student is given a list of food items: lemon, sugar, curd, and amla. Which of these will taste sour due to the presence of acids?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar only\",\"Lemon, curd, and amla\",\"Lemon and sugar only\",\"Curd and sugar only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lemon, curd, and amla are listed or implied as sour items containing acids, whereas sugar is sweet.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct and safe method to convert a strong concentrated acid into a dilute acid?\",\"options\":[\"Adding water quickly to the concentrated acid\",\"Adding the concentrated acid slowly into water\",\"Heating the concentrated acid until it thins out\",\"Mixing the acid with baking soda\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that strong concentrated acids are converted into dilute acids by slowly adding them into water.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following groups contains only \'mineral acids\' as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Vinegar and Lemon juice\",\"H₂SO₄, HCl, and HNO₃\",\"Tamarind and Amla\",\"Sugar and Salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄), hydrochloric acid (HCl), and nitric acid (HNO₃) as mineral acids.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for Nitric acid as provided in the topic text?\",\"options\":[\"H₂SO₄\",\"HCl\",\"HNO₃\",\"NaCl\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the formula (HNO₃) for nitric acid.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a potential danger of inhaling the vapours of strong concentrated acids?\",\"options\":[\"They make the air sweet\",\"They can be harmful to health\",\"They turn into organic acids\",\"They have no effect on health\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the vapours of strong acids, if inhaled, can be harmful to health.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why are sulphuric acid and hydrochloric acid described as \'caustic\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are found in plants\",\"Because they are weak in nature\",\"Because they are strong in nature and can burn skin\",\"Because they are used as food additives\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links the caustic\\/inflammatory nature of these acids to them being strong and able to burn skin.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Compare dilute acids and concentrated acids based on the information provided. Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[\"Dilute acids are more harmful than concentrated acids\",\"Concentrated acids are safer to handle than dilute acids\",\"Dilute acids are less harmful than their corresponding strong acids\",\"There is no difference in their harmful nature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that dilute acids are less harmful than their corresponding strong acids.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is described as having a taste other than sour, sweet, or bitter in the \'Can you tell?\' section?\",\"options\":[\"Lemon\",\"Vinegar\",\"Amla (Astringent)\",\"Sugar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text asks to identify tastes including \'astringent\', and later mentions amla as having a sour\\/astringent profile.\",\"topic\":\"Acid\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic taste of a dilute solution of baking soda?\",\"options\":[\"Sour\",\"Sweet\",\"Astringent\\/bitter\",\"Salty\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that if you taste a dilute solution of baking soda, you will find it astringent\\/bitter.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct chemical formula for lime water as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"NaOH\",\"NaHCO₃\",\"Ca(OH)₂\",\"H₂O\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the formula [Ca(OH)₂] for lime water.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How do substances like soap and baking soda feel to the touch?\",\"options\":[\"Rough\",\"Slippery\",\"Sticky\",\"Dry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines bases as substances that are astringent\\/bitter to taste and slippery to touch.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Chemically, how do the properties of bases compare to those of acids?\",\"options\":[\"They have identical properties.\",\"They have properties opposite to that of acids.\",\"They are a milder form of acids.\",\"They are exactly the same as acids.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that chemically, bases have properties opposite to that of acids.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a substance that is inflammatory to the skin in its concentrated form and is slippery to touch. What is this substance likely to be?\",\"options\":[\"Distilled water\",\"An acid\",\"A base\",\"Pure water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes bases as slippery to touch and inflammatory to skin in concentrated form.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following substances is identified as a base in the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Distilled water\",\"Caustic soda (NaOH)\",\"Lemon juice\",\"Sugar solution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists caustic soda (NaOH) as an example of a base.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for baking soda according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Ca(OH)₂\",\"NaOH\",\"NaHCO₃\",\"H₂O\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides (NaHCO₃) as the formula for baking soda.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is distilled water not classified as either acidic or basic in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is slippery to touch.\",\"Because it is inflammatory to skin.\",\"Because it is tasteless.\",\"Because it is bitter.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions distilled water is tasteless and therefore neither acidic nor basic.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What safety warning does the text provide regarding concentrated bases?\",\"options\":[\"They are tasteless.\",\"They are inflammatory to skin.\",\"They are slippery.\",\"They are the same as distilled water.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text warns that bases are inflammatory to skin in their concentrated form.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the examples given (Ca(OH)₂, NaOH, NaHCO₃), what can be inferred about the chemical nature of bases?\",\"options\":[\"They all contain only Sodium (Na).\",\"They are completely different from acids.\",\"They are all found in distilled water.\",\"They are all safe to touch in any concentration.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that bases are completely different from acids.\",\"topic\":\"Base\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Litmus paper is made from the extract of which plant?\",\"options\":[\"Rose plant\",\"Lichen plant\",\"Hibiscus plant\",\"Lotus plant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that litmus paper is made by an extract of the lichen plant.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the original color of the methyl orange indicator?\",\"options\":[\"Pink\",\"Red\",\"Orange\",\"Yellow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"As per the table, the original colour of the methyl orange indicator is orange.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are typical indicators used to recognize acids and bases instead of touching or tasting them?\",\"options\":[\"Indicators are cheaper to use\",\"Touching or tasting acids and bases is very harmful\",\"Indicators provide a faster reaction\",\"Acids and bases have no taste or smell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that touching or tasting of acid or base is very harmful, so typical indicators are used.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical nature of substances used as indicators?\",\"options\":[\"They are highly acidic\",\"They are highly basic\",\"They are chemically inert\",\"They are highly reactive\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines indicators as substances which are chemically inert in nature.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student dips blue litmus paper into a solution and it turns red, what is the nature of the solution?\",\"options\":[\"Acidic\",\"Basic\",\"Alkali\",\"Neutral\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, blue litmus paper turns red on dipping in acid.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student adds methyl orange to a solution and the color changes to pink. What type of substance is the solution?\",\"options\":[\"An alkali\",\"A base\",\"An acid\",\"Pure water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that methyl orange indicator becomes pink in acid.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which indicator remains colourless when tested with an acid?\",\"options\":[\"Litmus paper\",\"Methyl orange\",\"Phenolphthalein\",\"Methyl red\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table shows that Phenolphthalein is colourless in its original state and remains colourless in acid.\",\"topic\":\"Indicator\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/094_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the colour of Methyl red when it is added to an alkali?\",\"options\":[\"Pink\",\"Red\",\"Yellow\",\"Orange\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table indicates t