"Carrot\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists red cabbage, radish, tomato, hibiscus, and rose as sources for natural indicators.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the color of a turmeric stain change when it comes in contact with soap?\",\"options\":[\"Due to the water in the soap\",\"Due to a chemical reaction between turmeric and alkaline material\",\"Because the soap removes the yellow color\",\"Because turmeric is a type of soap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains the change is a result of a chemical reaction between turmeric and the alkaline material in soap.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the reaction with soap, what role does turmeric play?\",\"options\":[\"It acts as a detergent\",\"It acts as a solvent\",\"It acts as an indicator\",\"It acts as an acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that in this reaction, turmeric acts as an indicator.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is hibiscus indicator paper prepared according to the activity?\",\"options\":[\"By boiling the petals in water\",\"By dipping filter paper in hibiscus juice\",\"By rubbing red petals on white filter paper\",\"By drying the petals and grinding them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions say to rub red petals of hibiscus flower on the white filter paper.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in preparing turmeric indicator paper?\",\"options\":[\"Add water to turmeric powder\",\"Heat the turmeric powder\",\"Rub turmeric on filter paper\",\"Cut the paper into strips\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says to take turmeric powder and add a little water to it first.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are red cabbage leaves heated in water during the preparation of the indicator?\",\"options\":[\"To cook the leaves for eating\",\"To extract the indicator solution from the leaves\",\"To make the leaves turn yellow\",\"To clean the leaves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Heating the leaves in water is the method described to create the solution used for the indicator paper.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what type of material is found in soap that reacts with turmeric?\",\"options\":[\"Acidic material\",\"Alkaline material\",\"Neutral material\",\"Salty material\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the reaction occurs between turmeric and the alkaline material in soap.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical name for \'Lime water\' as mentioned in the activity table?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium chloride\",\"Calcium hydroxide\",\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Citric acid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the text identifies lime water as calcium hydroxide.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you wanted to make a \'rose indicator paper\', which material would you rub on white filter paper?\",\"options\":[\"Rose leaves\",\"Rose thorns\",\"Rose petals\",\"Rose stem\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity states to rub rose petals on white filter paper to make rose indicator paper.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the behavior of turmeric with soap, what effect would an alkaline substance have on turmeric paper?\",\"options\":[\"It will turn the paper yellow\",\"It will turn the paper red\",\"It will turn the paper colorless\",\"It will turn the paper blue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since soap (alkaline) turns turmeric red, other alkaline substances will have the same effect.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct sequence for preparing red cabbage indicator paper?\",\"options\":[\"Heat leaves in water -> Cool solution -> Dip paper -> Dry paper\",\"Dip paper -> Heat leaves -> Cool solution -> Dry paper\",\"Dry paper -> Dip in water -> Heat leaves -> Cool solution\",\"Cool solution -> Heat leaves -> Dip paper -> Dry paper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text describes heating the leaves, cooling the solution, dipping the paper, and then drying it.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT listed as a source for natural indicators in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Tomato\",\"Hibiscus\",\"Radish\",\"Mango\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Tomato, hibiscus, and radish are mentioned; mango is not mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of using \'domestic indicators\' as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To make the laboratory more colorful\",\"To use when laboratory indicators are not available\",\"To clean stains from clothes\",\"To prepare food in the kitchen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states natural indicators can be made on the non-availability of laboratory indicators.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If lime water (calcium hydroxide) is basic, what will be its effect on turmeric paper?\",\"options\":[\"No change\",\"It will turn red\",\"It will turn white\",\"It will turn green\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since lime water is basic\\/alkaline, it will react with turmeric to turn it red, similar to soap.\",\"topic\":\"Making of natural indicator\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is observed when baking soda solution is added to an acidic fruit juice?\",\"options\":[\"The solution turns blue\",\"The solution freezes\",\"Effervescence or bubbles are formed\",\"The solution becomes solid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that on addition of baking soda solution in the acidic solution, bubbles come out or effervescence is produced.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, what happens to turmeric indicator paper in certain solutions?\",\"options\":[\"It turns green\",\"It turns red\",\"It turns blue\",\"It turns black\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that turmeric indicator paper turns red in certain solutions.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a purpose of the activities described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To measure the volume of fruit juices\",\"To identify acidic or alkaline substances\",\"To prepare a new type of fruit juice\",\"To study the evaporation of water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that by these simple activities, we can identify acidic or alkaline substances.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which indicator is listed in the fourth column of the table?\",\"options\":[\"Red litmus\",\"Blue litmus\",\"Phenolphthalein\",\"Methyl orange\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows \'Methyl orange\' in the fourth column under indicators.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/095_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which chemical formula is used in the text to represent Ammonium hydroxide?\",\"options\":[\"HCl\",\"NaOH\",\"NH₄OH\",\"KOH\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the formula (NH₄OH) for ammonium hydroxide.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student adds baking soda to apple juice and observes bubbles, what can be inferred about the apple juice?\",\"options\":[\"It is a neutral substance\",\"It is an acidic solution\",\"It is a solid substance\",\"It contains no water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that bubbles or effervescence are produced when baking soda is added to an acidic solution.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is NOT used as a sample solution in the second \'Try this\' activity?\",\"options\":[\"Vinegar\",\"Lime juice\",\"Ammonium hydroxide\",\"Sodium chloride\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists vinegar, lime juice, ammonium hydroxide, and HCl, but not sodium chloride.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the purpose of the last column in the table?\",\"options\":[\"To record the color of the solution\",\"To record the volume of the solution\",\"To record the nature of the solution (Acidic\\/Basic)\",\"To record the temperature of the solution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The last column in the table is titled \'nature of solution (Acidic\\/Basic)\'.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/095_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"A student is given an unknown liquid and told to use the baking soda test. If no bubbles form, what does this suggest compared to the fruit juice activity?\",\"options\":[\"The liquid is definitely vinegar\",\"The liquid is not acidic\",\"The liquid is very hot\",\"The liquid is orange juice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the text says bubbles form in acidic solutions with baking soda, the absence of bubbles suggests the liquid is not acidic.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is HCl included in the observation table alongside lime juice and vinegar?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a type of fruit juice\",\"Because it is an indicator\",\"Because it is a sample solution to be tested for its nature\",\"Because it is a solid powder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"HCl (hydrochloric acid) is listed as a sample solution to be tested with indicators to determine its nature.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common observation when baking soda is added to lime juice, vinegar, and orange juice?\",\"options\":[\"They all turn into turmeric paper\",\"They all produce effervescence\",\"They all turn blue\",\"They all become ammonium hydroxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that addition of baking soda to these juices results in bubbles or effervescence.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the instructions, what must be added to baking powder before it is used to test the juices?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Methyl orange\",\"A little water\",\"Phenolphthalein\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says \'Take baking powder. Add little water to it.\' before adding it to the juices.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the definition provided in the text, an acid is a substance that gives which ions in a solution state?\",\"options\":[\"OH⁻ ions\",\"Cl⁻ ions\",\"H⁺ ions\",\"Na⁺ ions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines acid as a substance which gives H⁺ ions in solution state.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a natural (organic) acid found in our daily diet?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphuric acid\",\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Nitric acid\",\"Citric acid\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Citric acid is mentioned as a natural acid found in lemon and other fruits.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When Hydrochloric acid (HCl) dissociates in a water solution, what are the resulting products?\",\"options\":[\"H⁺ + Cl⁻\",\"H₂ + Cl₂\",\"H⁺ + OH⁻\",\"HCl + H₂O\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text shows the dissociation reaction: HCl (aq) → H⁺ + Cl⁻.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the characteristic taste of acids as mentioned in their properties?\",\"options\":[\"Bitter\",\"Salty\",\"Sour\",\"Sweet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"One of the properties listed is that acids are sour in taste.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific acid is associated with the source \'Tamarind\'?\",\"options\":[\"Acetic acid\",\"Tartaric acid\",\"Oxalic acid\",\"Lactic acid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Table 12.4 in the figure shows Tamarind contains Tartaric acid.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/095_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the left beaker shows a litmus paper changing color. Based on the text, what color does blue litmus turn when dipped in an acid?\",\"options\":[\"Green\",\"Yellow\",\"Pink\",\"Red\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that blue litmus turns red in acids.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/095_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is commonly found in cold aerated drinks?\",\"options\":[\"Carbonic acid (H₂CO₃)\",\"Ascorbic acid\",\"Nitric acid (HNO₃)\",\"Acetic acid\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists Carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) as the acid found in cold aerated drinks.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when an acid reacts with a metal?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide gas is formed\",\"Hydrogen gas is formed\",\"Oxygen gas is formed\",\"Nitrogen gas is formed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Property 3 states that acid reacts with metal to form hydrogen gas.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is specifically used in electric cells (batteries)?\",\"options\":[\"Dil. HCl\",\"Dil. HNO₃\",\"Dil. H₂SO₄\",\"Dil. H₂CO₃\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Use number 4 states that Dil. H₂SO₄ acid is used in the batteries.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why are natural acids considered less dangerous than mineral acids like HCl or HNO₃?\",\"options\":[\"They do not contain hydrogen\",\"They are mild in nature\",\"They are not soluble in water\",\"They are found in wood pulp\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states natural acids are mild in nature and hence are not harmful like mineral acids.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a substance causes methyl orange to turn pink, what can be inferred about the substance?\",\"options\":[\"It is an alkali\",\"It is neutral\",\"It is an acid\",\"It is water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the orange colour of methyl orange turns pink in acid.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is used for the sterilization of water?\",\"options\":[\"Dil. HCl\",\"Dil. H₂SO₄\",\"Acetic acid\",\"Oxalic acid\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Use number 5 states that Dil. HCl is used for sterilization of water.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What gas is liberated when an acid reacts with carbonates?\",\"options\":[\"H₂\",\"O₂\",\"CO₂\",\"Cl₂\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Property 4 states that acid reacts with carbonates and liberates CO₂ gas.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the uses of acids, which industry utilizes acid to produce white paper from wood pulp?\",\"options\":[\"Fertilizer industry\",\"Paper industry\",\"Medicine industry\",\"Explosive industry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Use number 6 states acid is used for making of white paper from wood pulp.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly matches the source to its natural acid?\",\"options\":[\"Tomato - Lactic acid\",\"Orange - Acetic acid\",\"Curd - Lactic acid\",\"Vinegar - Citric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to table 12.4, Curd contains Lactic acid.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Uses of Acid\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the full form of DNA, the acid present in our body that decides heredity property?\",\"options\":[\"Deoxyribo Neutral Acid\",\"Deoxyribo Nucleic Acid\",\"Dextro Nucleic Acid\",\"Deoxyribo Nitrogen Acid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that DNA stands for Deoxyribo Nucleic Acid.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following bases is mentioned as being caustic in nature?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium hydroxide\",\"Ammonium hydroxide\",\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Magnesium hydroxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that bases like sodium hydroxide and potassium hydroxide are caustic in nature.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Proteins, which are part of our body cells, are made up of which acids?\",\"options\":[\"Fatty acids\",\"Mineral acids\",\"Amino acids\",\"Nucleic acids\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that proteins are made up of amino acids.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why should water never be added to concentrated sulphuric acid for dilution?\",\"options\":[\"It turns the acid into a base\",\"It produces enormous heat and causes an explosion\",\"It makes the acid lose its chemical properties\",\"It causes the acid to freeze instantly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, adding water to concentrated sulphuric acid produces enormous heat and causes an explosion.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do concentrated solutions of strong bases like KOH affect the skin?\",\"options\":[\"They hydrate the skin cells\",\"They turn the skin into fat\",\"They burn the skin by decomposing proteins\",\"They strengthen the skin\'s heredity properties\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that concentrated caustic bases burn skin as they decompose the proteins in the skin.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nature of the acids present in plants and our body that are useful to us?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral acids\",\"Organic acids\",\"Caustic acids\",\"Inorganic acids\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that several organic acids are present in our body and in plants which are useful to us.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student needs to dilute concentrated H₂SO₄, what is the correct and safe method to follow?\",\"options\":[\"Add water to the acid very quickly\",\"Add the acid to water slowly\",\"Mix equal parts of water and acid simultaneously\",\"Add water to the acid drop by drop\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that for dilution, concentrated acid is slowly added to water.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"An iron knife shines better after cutting sour fruits like lemon. Based on the text, what causes this?\",\"options\":[\"The proteins in the fruit polish the iron\",\"The fatty acids in the fruit coat the iron\",\"The organic acids in the sour fruits react with the surface\",\"The DNA in the fruit changes the iron\'s property\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sour fruits like lemon contain organic acids that react with the surface of the iron knife, making it shine.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which component of the human body is specifically formed by fatty acids?\",\"options\":[\"Heredity material\",\"Body cells\",\"Fat\",\"Skin proteins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'Fat of our body is formed by fatty acids.\'\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary danger associated with the dissolution of concentrated H₂SO₄ in water?\",\"options\":[\"Generation of a large amount of heat\",\"Decomposition of amino acids\",\"Formation of heredity properties\",\"Absorption of oxygen from the air\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that the dissolution of conc. H₂SO₄ in water generates a large amount of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why are mineral acids generally considered differently than organic acids in a biological context?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral acids are useful, while organic acids are harmful\",\"Mineral acids are harmful to the body, while organic acids are useful\",\"Mineral acids form proteins, while organic acids form DNA\",\"Mineral acids are found in plants, while organic acids are not\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that mineral acids are harmful to the body, whereas organic acids in the body and plants are useful.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following would be the most likely result of accidentally spilling a concentrated solution of Sodium hydroxide on the hand?\",\"options\":[\"The skin will become oily due to fatty acids\",\"The skin will burn due to the decomposition of proteins\",\"The skin will develop new heredity properties via DNA\",\"The skin will be cooled by the dissolution process\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that concentrated solutions of strong bases like sodium hydroxide burn skin by decomposing proteins.\",\"topic\":\"Causticity and Biological Acids\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which ion is released by a base when it is dissolved in water?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen (H⁺) ion\",\"Hydroxide (OH⁻) ion\",\"Sodium (Na⁺) ion\",\"Chloride (Cl⁻) ion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the definition, a base is a substance whose water solution gives hydroxide (OH⁻) ions.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common name for Sodium hydroxide as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Potash\",\"Lime water\",\"Caustic soda\",\"Milk of magnesia\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Table 12.6 lists Sodium hydroxide\'s common name as Caustic soda.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a physical property of alkalies?\",\"options\":[\"They have a sour taste\",\"They are slippery to touch\",\"They are generally acidic in nature\",\"They contain Hydrogen as the main constituent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One of the properties of alkalies mentioned is that they are slippery.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it warned in the text not to taste or touch unknown substances for identification?\",\"options\":[\"Because they all taste bitter\",\"Because it is dangerous to do so\",\"Because they are all slippery\",\"Because they only react in water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that for identification of any substance, it is dangerous to taste, inhale, or touch them.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to prepare a medicine that acts as an antacid. Which base should be used?\",\"options\":[\"NaOH\",\"KOH\",\"Mg(OH)₂\",\"NH₄OH\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Magnesium hydroxide \\/ milk of magnesia [Mg(OH)₂] is used for acidity resistant medicines (Antacids).\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a factory is producing fertilizers, which chemical from the textbook table is most likely to be used?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium hydroxide\",\"Ammonium hydroxide\",\"Potassium hydroxide\",\"Sodium hydroxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ammonium hydroxide (NH₄OH) is used for the production of fertilizers.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the properties of alkalies, what can be concluded about the nature of metal oxides?\",\"options\":[\"They are generally acidic\",\"They are generally basic\",\"They are always neutral\",\"They do not contain oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that metal oxides are generally basic in nature.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the uses of Sodium hydroxide and Potassium hydroxide, which one is specifically used in the manufacturing of shampoo?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Potassium hydroxide\",\"Calcium hydroxide\",\"Ammonium hydroxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Table 12.6 shows that Potassium hydroxide (Potash) is used for soap\\/shampoo.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which chemical formula corresponds to the base used for the production of fertilizers?\",\"options\":[\"NaOH\",\"KOH\",\"Mg(OH)₂\",\"NH₄OH\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In the table, Ammonium hydroxide (NH₄OH) is listed for the production of fertilizers.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/097_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the substance shown is Sodium Hydroxide. According to the textbook table, what is its primary use?\",\"options\":[\"White washing\",\"Washing soap\",\"Antacids\",\"Fertilizers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the text associates Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) with the use in washing soap.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/096_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the chemical formula of the substance named Potassium Hydroxide?\",\"options\":[\"KOH\",\"NaOH\",\"Ca(OH)₂\",\"Mg(OH)₂\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text and table identify Potassium hydroxide by the formula KOH.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/096_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which constituent is common to all the chemical formulae listed in the table provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium (Na)\",\"Hydroxide (OH)\",\"Magnesium (Mg)\",\"Ammonium (NH₄)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that bases contain hydroxide (OH) as a main constituent, which is visible in all formulae in the table.\",\"topic\":\"Base Properties and Uses\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific ion is contained in an acid according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Hydroxide ion (OH⁻)\",\"Hydrogen ion (H⁺)\",\"Sodium ion (Na⁺)\",\"Chloride ion (Cl⁻)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that acid contains (H⁺) hydrogen ions.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two products formed by the combination of an acid and a base?\",\"options\":[\"Salt and Hydrogen\",\"Salt and Oxygen\",\"Salt and Water\",\"Water and Hydroxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, salt and water are formed by the combination of acid and base.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is naturally present in the human stomach to help digestion?\",\"options\":[\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Magnesium hydroxide\",\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Sodium chloride\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Hydrochloric acid is present in our stomach to help the digestion process.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for milk of magnesia mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Mg(OH)₂\",\"NaOH\",\"NaCl\",\"HCl\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly provides the formula [Mg(OH)₂] for milk of magnesia.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the chemical reaction \'Acid + Base → Salt + Water\' defined in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Digestion\",\"Neutralization\",\"Fertilization\",\"Acidification\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the chemical reaction between acid and base to form salt and water as neutralization.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is milk of magnesia used as a medicine for hyperacidity?\",\"options\":[\"It is an acid that increases digestion speed\",\"It is a salt that provides energy\",\"It is an alkaline medicine that neutralizes excess acid\",\"It is a fertilizer that balances soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that such alkaline medicine (milk of magnesia) neutralizes excess acid present in the stomach.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary cause of increased soil acidity mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Excessive use of chemical fertilizers\",\"Mixing of lime stone\",\"Presence of Hydrochloric acid\",\"Excessive use of milk of magnesia\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that the acidity of soil increases due to excessive use of chemical fertilizers.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the reaction HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O, what does NaCl represent?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Sodium hydroxide\",\"Sodium chloride (Salt)\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies NaCl as Sodium chloride, which is the salt formed in the reaction.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a farmer\'s soil has become too acidic due to fertilizers, what should be mixed into the soil based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Chemical fertilizers\",\"Milk of magnesia\",\"Lime stone or lime water\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that lime stone or lime water is mixed in the acidic soil to neutralize excess acid.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A person is suffering from indigestion due to hyperacidity. Which substance would be most effective to control this condition?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Antacids containing Mg(OH)₂\",\"Chemical fertilizers\",\"Sodium chloride\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says antacids containing milk of magnesia [Mg(OH)₂] are used to control hyperacidity.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the neutralization reaction provided, what happens to the (H⁺) ions from the acid and (OH⁻) ions from the base?\",\"options\":[\"They combine to form Water (H₂O)\",\"They combine to form Sodium chloride (NaCl)\",\"They are converted into chemical fertilizers\",\"They remain separate in the stomach\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The reaction shows HCl (H⁺ source) and NaOH (OH⁻ source) forming H₂O (water).\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly describes the chemical nature of lime water used in agriculture?\",\"options\":[\"It is acidic because it treats soil\",\"It is alkaline because it neutralizes excess acid\",\"It is a neutral salt like Sodium chloride\",\"It is a digestive enzyme like Hydrochloric acid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to lime water as an \'alkaline substance\' that neutralizes excess acid in the soil.\",\"topic\":\"Neutralization\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22163,57,8,'Science','en','Chemical Change and Chemical Bond','[\"- A chemical change is a process where the original substance changes its composition and forms new substances with different properties.\",\"- Physical changes alter appearance or state but the substance’s composition remains the same.\",\"- Key signs of a chemical change: change in colour, taste, temperature, gas evolution, or formation of a precipitate.\",\"- In a chemical reaction, reactants are on the left side of the arrow and products on the right.\",\"- The arrow in a chemical equation points from reactants to products.\",\"- A word equation uses the names of substances; a chemical equation uses their formulas.\",\"- Neutralisation is a type of chemical reaction where an acid reacts with a base to form a salt and water.\",\"- Combustion is a fast, irreversible chemical change where a substance reacts with oxygen to produce CO₂ and H₂O.\",\"- Ionic bonds form between a metal cation and a non‑metal anion; the metal donates electrons and the non‑metal accepts them.\",\"- Covalent bonds form when two non‑metal atoms share electrons to complete their valence shells.\",\"- The valency of an atom is the number of bonds it can form; it equals the number of electrons it can lose, gain, or share.\",\"- In ionic compounds, the magnitude of the ionic charge equals the valency.\",\"- In covalent compounds, each shared pair of electrons counts as one bond for both atoms.\",\"- The octet rule states that atoms tend to have eight electrons in their outer shell (or two for hydrogen).\",\"- A precipitate is a solid that forms when two soluble salts react to produce an insoluble salt.\",\"- Carbon dioxide is a common gas produced in many chemical changes, e.g., baking soda + lemon juice.\",\"- Respiration is a natural chemical change where glucose reacts with oxygen to produce CO₂, H₂O, and energy.\",\"- Photosynthesis is the reverse of respiration; plants convert CO₂ and H₂O into glucose and O₂ using sunlight.\",\"- Hard water contains Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions; adding sodium carbonate precipitates CaCO₃ and MgCO₃, softening the water.\",\"- Chemical changes can be written in a balanced equation by ensuring atoms and charge are conserved.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What happens to the composition of the original substance during a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"It remains exactly the same\",\"It changes to form a new substance\",\"Only the shape of the substance changes\",\"The substance disappears completely\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, during a chemical change, the composition of the original substance changes to form a new substance.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of the new substance formed in a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"It has the same composition as the original\",\"It has the same properties as the original\",\"It has a different composition and properties\",\"It is always in a different state of matter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the new substance formed has a different composition and properties from the original.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which of these is an example of a physical change?\",\"options\":[\"Ripening of mango\",\"Melting of ice\",\"Darkening of a cut potato\",\"Foul smell from spoiled food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Melting of ice is a physical change as it involves a change in state without changing the chemical composition.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following changes involves the formation of a new substance?\",\"options\":[\"Boiling of water\",\"Bursting of an inflated balloon\",\"Ripening of banana\",\"Dissolution of salt in water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ripening is a chemical change where the composition changes to form a new substance.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the \'foul smell from a spoiled food\' classified as a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Because the food has changed its physical state\",\"Because the original composition of the food has changed\",\"Because the food has been moved to a different place\",\"Because the food has simply changed its shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A foul smell indicates that the composition of the original substance has changed, forming new substances.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a method used to categorize the different types of changes mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Classification into physical and chemical changes\",\"Classification by weight and height\",\"Classification by cost and availability\",\"Classification by color and texture only\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text focuses on the classification of changes into physical and chemical changes.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the \'fragrance on ripening fruit\' indicate about the change occurring in the fruit?\",\"options\":[\"It is a physical change of state\",\"It is a chemical change resulting in new properties\",\"It is a change in the fruit\'s volume only\",\"It is not a change at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fragrance is a new property indicating a change in the composition of the fruit (chemical change).\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you observe the \'darkening of a cut potato\', what can you conclude about its composition?\",\"options\":[\"The composition is the same as the fresh potato\",\"The composition has changed to form a new substance\",\"The potato has simply lost its water content\",\"The potato has undergone a physical change of shape\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Darkening of a cut potato is a chemical change, meaning its composition has changed.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which group contains only changes that are chemical in nature according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Melting of ice, Boiling of water\",\"Bursting of balloon, Dissolution of salt\",\"Ripening of mango, Sound of bursting fire cracker\",\"Boiling of water, Darkening of a cut potato\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ripening and the sound of fire crackers involve changes in composition and properties, making them chemical.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a key question to ask to identify a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"How much does the substance weigh?\",\"How to identify a chemical change?\",\"What is the temperature of the room?\",\"Is the substance expensive?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text ends by posing the question \'How to identify a chemical change?\' as a method of study.\",\"topic\":\"Can you recall?\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the white solid particles of baking soda when they are added to lemon juice?\",\"options\":[\"They turn into large crystals\",\"They disappear\",\"They change color to blue\",\"They remain unchanged at the bottom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the white solid particles of the baking soda disappear during the activity.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, what sound is heard when you take your ear near the glass after adding baking soda?\",\"options\":[\"A loud explosion\",\"A high-pitched whistle\",\"A low sound\",\"A crackling sound\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a low sound is heard during the chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the change in taste of the lemon juice mixture indicate about its composition?\",\"options\":[\"The composition remains the same\",\"The composition was different at the end\",\"Only the temperature changed\",\"The lemon juice became more concentrated\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if the taste was different, it means the composition was different.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the lemon juice activity, what observation proves that a gas is being liberated?\",\"options\":[\"The liquid turns cloudy\",\"The glass becomes hot\",\"Bubbling is noticed around the particles\",\"The liquid changes to a solid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a gas is seen liberating in the form of bubbles.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student observes that a new substance with different properties has formed, what type of change has occurred?\",\"options\":[\"Physical change\",\"Temporary change\",\"Chemical change\",\"Mechanical change\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that a change where the composition changes to form a new substance with different properties is a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Under what condition does the text state that it is possible to test the \'taste\' of materials in an experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Only if the teacher is present\",\"Only if the apparatus is clean and materials are edible\",\"Only if the mixture is boiling\",\"Only if the mixture is colorful\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that testing taste is only possible if clean apparatus and edible materials are used.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How does the original sour taste of lemon juice change after adding baking soda?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes more sour\",\"It becomes bitter\",\"It becomes mild or diminishes\",\"It disappears completely and tastes like water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that the original sour taste becomes mild or diminishes.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed in the text as a characteristic observation that enables us to know a chemical change has occurred?\",\"options\":[\"Change in color\",\"Change in taste\",\"Release of gases\",\"Change in weight\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Table 13.1 and the text mention color, taste, and gas release, but not weight change.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the activity, why is the bubbling considered a sign of a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Because it shows a new gas is being formed\",\"Because it shows the liquid is boiling\",\"Because it shows the baking soda is melting\",\"Because it shows the glass is breaking\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bubbling indicates the liberation of a gas, which is a characteristic observation of a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what common type of change is represented by the rusted nails, the browned apple, and the toasted bread?\",\"options\":[\"Physical change\",\"Chemical change\",\"Biological change\",\"Reversible change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure illustrates \'Some observations of chemical change\' as per the caption.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/099_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific observation of a chemical change is most clearly demonstrated by the bottom-right flask?\",\"options\":[\"Change in taste\",\"Release of gases\",\"Change in shape\",\"Freezing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The bottom-right flask shows foam and bubbles, representing the release of gases.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/099_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which conclusion can be drawn from the fact that baking soda particles disappear in lemon juice while producing bubbles and a new taste?\",\"options\":[\"The baking soda just dissolved like sugar\",\"A chemical change occurred resulting in a new composition\",\"The lemon juice was spoiled\",\"The particles were hidden by the bubbles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that these observations mean the composition changed to form a new substance, which is a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"During a chemical change, what happens to the chemical composition of the original matter?\",\"options\":[\"It remains the same\",\"It changes\",\"It becomes simpler\",\"It disappears completely\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that during a chemical change, the chemical composition of the original matter changes.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a necessary condition to write a chemical equation for a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"The color change must be visible\",\"The reaction must be fast\",\"The exact change in chemical composition must be known\",\"The temperature must be recorded\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, a chemical equation can be written if the exact change in chemical composition is known.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which acid is naturally present in lemon juice as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Acetic acid\",\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Citric acid\",\"Nitric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that citric acid is present in lemon juice.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What type of reaction is represented by the equation: Acid + Alkali ⟶ CO₂ + Salt?\",\"options\":[\"Combustion reaction\",\"Neutralization reaction\",\"Decomposition reaction\",\"Displacement reaction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the reaction \'Acid + Alkali ⟶ CO₂ + Salt\' is a neutralization reaction.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student adds baking soda to lemon juice, which gas will be formed as a result of the chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that when baking soda is added to lemon juice, the gas formed is carbon dioxide.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What are used to represent substances when writing a chemical equation?\",\"options\":[\"Only common names\",\"Only chemical formulae\",\"Names and chemical formulae\",\"Physical descriptions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that names and chemical formulae of the original and newly formed substances are used.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment described, which substance acts as the alkali?\",\"options\":[\"Citric acid\",\"Sodium bicarbonate\",\"Sodium citrate\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the general equation \'Acid + Alkali ⟶ CO₂ + Salt\', Sodium bicarbonate occupies the position of the alkali.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of new substances formed during a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"They have the same properties as the original matter\",\"They have different properties and different chemical composition\",\"They have the same chemical composition but different color\",\"They have no chemical composition\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that new substances having different properties and different chemical composition are formed.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the salt produced in the reaction between citric acid and sodium bicarbonate.\",\"options\":[\"Sodium chloride\",\"Sodium bicarbonate\",\"Sodium citrate\",\"Citric bicarbonate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The word equation provided in the text shows that Sodium citrate is the salt formed.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the word equation provided, what are the reactants in the lemon juice and baking soda experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide and Sodium citrate\",\"Citric acid and Sodium bicarbonate\",\"Acid and Salt\",\"Sodium bicarbonate and Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The word equation shows Citric acid + Sodium bicarbonate on the reactant side (before the arrow).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the term used to describe the formation of gas bubbles seen in the dish?\",\"options\":[\"Evaporation\",\"Condensation\",\"Effervescence\",\"Freezing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The caption of the figure identifies the phenomenon as \'Formation of CO₂ with effervescence\'.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/100_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The experiment shown in the given figure involves mixing lemon juice with baking soda. Which gas is responsible for the bubbles observed?\",\"options\":[\"CO₂\",\"O₂\",\"H₂\",\"N₂\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The figure caption and the text both state that the gas formed is carbon dioxide (CO₂).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical change and word equation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/100_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in writing a chemical equation according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Writing the chemical formulas\",\"Drawing an arrow\",\"Writing a word equation using names\",\"Identifying the products\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the first step is to write a word equation by using the names of the concerned substances.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where are the original substances written while writing a chemical equation?\",\"options\":[\"On the right side\",\"On the left side\",\"Above the arrow\",\"Below the arrow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, original substances are written on the left side.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the substances written on the left side of the arrow called?\",\"options\":[\"Products\",\"Formulas\",\"Reactants\",\"Equations\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines substances written on the left side of the arrow as reactants.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the new substances formed as a result of a chemical reaction called?\",\"options\":[\"Reactants\",\"Products\",\"Original substances\",\"Word equations\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that new substances formed as a result of the reaction are called products.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What does the arrow in a chemical equation indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The speed of the reaction\",\"The direction of the reaction\",\"The temperature of the reaction\",\"The weight of the substances\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that the arrow indicates the direction of the reaction.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A word equation becomes a chemical equation when names are replaced by what?\",\"options\":[\"Arrows\",\"Reactants\",\"Chemical formulas\",\"Products\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when the chemical formula is written in place of each of the names, it becomes a chemical equation.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In which direction does the arrowhead point in a chemical equation?\",\"options\":[\"Towards the reactants\",\"Towards the left side\",\"Towards the substances formed\",\"Towards the original substances\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the arrow head points towards the substances formed (products).\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student writes \'Hydrogen + Oxygen → Water\', what are \'Hydrogen\' and \'Oxygen\' in this context?\",\"options\":[\"Products\",\"Reactants\",\"Formulas\",\"Arrows\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since they are on the left side and are the original substances taking part, they are reactants.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a reaction results in the formation of \'Carbon dioxide\', where should its name or formula be placed in the equation?\",\"options\":[\"On the left side of the arrow\",\"On the right side of the arrow\",\"In the middle of the arrow\",\"Before the reactants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the place for the products of a reaction is on the right side of the arrow.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the relationship between reactants and the arrow?\",\"options\":[\"Reactants are written where the arrowhead points\",\"Reactants are written on the right side of the arrow\",\"Reactants are the original substances written on the left side of the arrow\",\"Reactants are newly formed substances\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines reactants as original substances written on the left side of the arrow.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the correct sequence for representing a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Products → Reactants\",\"Reactants → Products\",\"Chemical Formula → Word Equation\",\"Arrow → Reactants → Products\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies original substances (reactants) on the left, an arrow in between, and new substances (products) on the right.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the arrow drawn between the substances in a chemical equation?\",\"options\":[\"To separate the names from the formulas\",\"To show that the substances are equal\",\"To indicate the direction of the reaction from reactants to products\",\"To show the first step of the word equation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says the arrow is drawn between them to indicate the direction of the reaction towards the substances formed.\",\"topic\":\"Always remember\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What type of process is respiration described as in the text?\",\"options\":[\"A physical change only\",\"A continuously occurring biological process\",\"A laboratory-only experiment\",\"A process that occurs only during the day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that respiration is a continuously occurring biological process.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these substances is inhaled during the process of respiration?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Water vapour\",\"Air\",\"Glucose\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, in this process, we inhale air.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for Glucose as given in the respiration equation?\",\"options\":[\"CO₂\",\"H₂O\",\"O₂\",\"C₆H₁₂O₆\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The chemical equation provided in the text represents glucose as C₆H₁₂O₆.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two substances produced when glucose reacts with oxygen in the cells?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide and water\",\"Oxygen and air\",\"Glucose and carbon dioxide\",\"Water and oxygen\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that glucose reacts with oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of respiration, what happens to the oxygen from the inhaled air?\",\"options\":[\"It is converted directly into glucose\",\"It reacts with glucose in the cells\",\"It is exhaled unchanged\",\"It turns into water vapour immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that glucose in the cells reacts with oxygen in the inhaled air.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides carbon dioxide, what else is exhaled during respiration according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Glucose\",\"Pure oxygen\",\"Water vapour\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that we exhale carbon dioxide and water vapour.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to write the word equation for respiration, which of the following represents the correct reactants?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide + Water\",\"Glucose + Oxygen\",\"Glucose + Carbon dioxide\",\"Oxygen + Water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The word equation shows \'Glucose + Oxygen\' on the left side (reactants).\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical equation C₆H₁₂O₆ + O₂ ⎯⎯→ CO₂ + H₂O, which molecule is a reactant?\",\"options\":[\"CO₂\",\"H₂O\",\"O₂\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the chemical equation provided, O₂ is on the left side of the arrow, indicating it is a reactant.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the text note about the provided chemical equation: C₆H₁₂O₆ + O₂ ⎯⎯→ CO₂ + H₂O?\",\"options\":[\"It is a balanced equation\",\"It is not a chemical reaction\",\"The chemical equation is not balanced\",\"It only occurs in the laboratory\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically notes: \'(Here, the chemical equation is not balanced.)\'\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How does the biological process of respiration qualify as a chemical change based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because it involves inhaling and exhaling\",\"Because new substances like carbon dioxide and water are formed from glucose and oxygen\",\"Because it occurs continuously in the surrounding\",\"Because it uses air from the atmosphere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A chemical change involves the formation of new substances; here glucose and oxygen react to form CO₂ and H₂O.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Evaluate the following: Which components of the exhaled breath are the direct products of the reaction between glucose and oxygen?\",\"options\":[\"Only air\",\"Glucose and Oxygen\",\"Carbon dioxide and water\",\"Only water vapour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that glucose reacts with oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water, which are then exhaled.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly organizes the chemical symbols for the products of respiration as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"C₆H₁₂O₆ and O₂\",\"CO₂ and H₂O\",\"O₂ and H₂O\",\"C₆H₁₂O₆ and CO₂\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The chemical equation shows CO₂ (Carbon dioxide) and H₂O (Water) as the products on the right side of the arrow.\",\"topic\":\"Natural chemical changes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical name for the \'lime water\' used in the experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium carbonate\",\"Calcium hydroxide\",\"Calcium oxide\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that lime water is a solution of calcium hydroxide.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What change is observed in the lime water immediately after blowing into it for some time?\",\"options\":[\"It turns blue\",\"It becomes hot\",\"It turns milky\",\"It disappears\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the colourless lime water turns milky when carbon dioxide is blown into it.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the white insoluble solid that settles at the bottom of the test tube called?\",\"options\":[\"A solution\",\"A solvent\",\"A precipitate\",\"A mixture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the white insoluble solid that settles at the bottom as a precipitate.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas is responsible for turning the lime water milky in this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Hydrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that turning milky lime water means the blown gas mixed in it was carbon dioxide.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the word equation, what are the products of the reaction between Carbon dioxide and Calcium hydroxide?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium carbonate and Water\",\"Calcium oxide and Water\",\"Calcium carbonate and Oxygen\",\"Calcium hydroxide and Water\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The word equation provided is: Carbon dioxide + Calcium hydroxide → Calcium carbonate + Water.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student blows air into a test tube of lime water, which component of their breath causes the chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Water vapor\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that blowing into lime water introduces carbon dioxide, which reacts with the solution.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical identity of the white precipitate formed in this \'Try this\' activity?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium hydroxide\",\"Calcium oxide\",\"Calcium carbonate\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the white insoluble solid (precipitate) as calcium carbonate.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what was the initial appearance of the lime water before any gas was blown into it?\",\"options\":[\"Milky\",\"Colourless\",\"White and solid\",\"Blue\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text refers to the starting liquid as \'colourless lime water\'.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student wants to prove a gas is carbon dioxide. Based on the experiment, what should they observe when passing the gas through lime water?\",\"options\":[\"The liquid should turn clear\",\"A gas should be released\",\"The liquid should turn milky\",\"The liquid should boil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text indicates that carbon dioxide causes lime water to turn milky, serving as a test for the gas.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the physical state of the Calcium carbonate formed in the reaction?\",\"options\":[\"A dissolved gas\",\"A soluble liquid\",\"An insoluble solid\",\"A colourless solution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes calcium carbonate as a \'white insoluble solid\' that settles at the bottom.\",\"topic\":\"Try this\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What type of chemical change is photosynthesis according to the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Man-made chemical change\",\"Natural chemical change\",\"Natural physical change\",\"Man-made physical change\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly describes photosynthesis as a natural chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct chemical formula for Glucose as given in the equation?\",\"options\":[\"C₆H₁₂O₆\",\"CO₂\",\"H₂O\",\"O₂\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The chemical equation provided in the text represents Glucose as C₆H₁₂O₆.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which organism is specifically mentioned as performing photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"All animals\",\"Fungi\",\"Green plants\",\"Human beings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'green plants perform photosynthesis\'.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the word equation, which two substances are the reactants in photosynthesis?\",\"options\":[\"Glucose and Oxygen\",\"Carbon dioxide and Water\",\"Glucose and Water\",\"Carbon dioxide and Oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The word equation shows Carbon dioxide + Water on the left side of the arrow.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two essential conditions written over and under the arrow in the photosynthesis equation?\",\"options\":[\"Water and Oxygen\",\"Carbon dioxide and Sunlight\",\"Sunlight and Green plant\",\"Glucose and Sunlight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The equations show \'Sunlight\' and \'green plant\' as the conditions for the change.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student is asked to write the chemical formula for the gas released as a product in photosynthesis, what should they write?\",\"options\":[\"CO₂\",\"H₂O\",\"C₆H₁₂O₆\",\"O₂\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Oxygen (O₂) is listed as a product in the chemical equation.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A plant is placed in a dark room where no sunlight reaches it. Which part of the photosynthesis equation will be missing?\",\"options\":[\"The reactants\",\"The products\",\"The necessary condition of sunlight\",\"The green plant itself\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sunlight is a required condition for the natural chemical change of photosynthesis to occur.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The textbook mentions that the chemical equation provided for photosynthesis is in what state?\",\"options\":[\"Balanced\",\"Unbalanced\",\"Incomplete\",\"Incorrect\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states: \'a chemical equation (unbalanced) can be written\'.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the chemical equation \'CO₂ + H₂O ⎯⎯→ C₆H₁₂O₆ + O₂\', what does the arrow represent?\",\"options\":[\"The addition of substances\",\"The direction of the chemical change\",\"The balance of the atoms\",\"The physical state of the plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The arrow indicates the transformation of reactants into products during the chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following correctly evaluates the products formed in photosynthesis based on the word equation?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon dioxide and Water\",\"Sunlight and Glucose\",\"Glucose and Oxygen\",\"Oxygen and Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The word equation lists Glucose + Oxygen on the right side of the arrow, signifying the products.\",\"topic\":\"Photosynthesis\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which common substance is present in fuels like wood, coal, and petrol that burns during combustion?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Calcium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the common substance that burns in all these fuels is \'Carbon\'.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main chemical composition of Shahabad tile?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium chloride\",\"Calcium carbonate\",\"Hydrochloric acid\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the chemical composition of Shahabad tile is mainly calcium carbonate.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the nature of the combustion of fuel?\",\"options\":[\"Slow and reversible\",\"Fast and reversible\",\"Fast and irreversible\",\"Slow and irreversible\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that combustion of fuel is a fast and irreversible chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which product is formed when carbon combines with oxygen during combustion?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium carbonate\",\"Carbon monoxide\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Hydrochloric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the word equation, Carbon + Oxygen yields Carbon dioxide.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is calcium chloride washed away during the cleaning of Shahabad tiles?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a gas\",\"Because it is insoluble in water\",\"Because it is soluble in water\",\"Because it turns into bubbles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that calcium chloride, being soluble in water, gets washed away with water.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the carbon dioxide produced during the cleaning of Shahabad tiles with acid?\",\"options\":[\"It gets washed away with water\",\"It stays on the tile surface\",\"Its bubbles mix up in the air\",\"It reacts to form calcium carbonate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that carbon dioxide bubbles mix up in the air during the cleaning process.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of burning fuels like wood and coal in everyday life?\",\"options\":[\"To produce oxygen\",\"To get energy\",\"To clean tiles\",\"To make soda-lemon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that wood, coal, petrol or cooking gas are burnt for getting energy.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of man-made chemical changes, which beverage is mentioned as using a useful chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"Tea\",\"Coffee\",\"Soda-lemon\",\"Fruit juice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the chemical change used in \'soda-lemon\' as a useful man-made chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the correct unbalanced chemical equation for cleaning Shahabad tile with hydrochloric acid?\",\"options\":[\"CaCO3 + HCl -> CaCl2 + CO2 + H2O\",\"C + O2 -> CO2\",\"Ca + HCl -> CaCl2 + H2\",\"CaO + HCl -> CaCl2 + H2O\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The reactants are Calcium carbonate (CaCO3) and Hydrochloric acid (HCl), and the products are Calcium chloride (CaCl2), Carbon dioxide (CO2), and water (H2O).\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a student wants to represent the combustion of cooking gas using a chemical equation, which of the following is correct?\",\"options\":[\"CO2 -> C + O2\",\"C + O2 -> CO2\",\"CaCO3 + HCl -> CaCl2\",\"C + H2O -> CO2\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text provides C + O2 -> CO2 as the common chemical equation for all combustion processes involving carbon-based fuels.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During the cleaning of Shahabad tile, which observation indicates the formation of a gaseous product?\",\"options\":[\"The tile becomes shiny\",\"The appearance of bubbles\",\"The water turning milky\",\"The acid disappearing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions carbon dioxide is formed and its bubbles mix up in air, indicating a gas is produced.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the common product formed in both the combustion of fuels and the cleaning of Shahabad tiles with acid?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Calcium chloride\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Oxygen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is listed as a product in both the combustion equation and the tile cleaning equation.\",\"topic\":\"Man made chemical changes\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, which source is likely to contain hard water?\",\"options\":[\"Rain water tanks\",\"Glaciers\",\"Tube wells\",\"Distilled water bottles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that some wells or tube wells have hard water.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a characteristic taste of hard water as mentioned in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"Sweet\",\"Brackish\",\"Sour\",\"Bitter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that hard water is brackish to taste.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Hard water is characterized by its inability to do which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Dissolve sugar\",\"Conduct electricity\",\"Form lather with soap\",\"Flow through pipes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that hard water does not form lather with soap.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific salts are responsible for the hardness of water?\",\"options\":[\"Chloride and sulphate salts of calcium and magnesium\",\"Carbonate and bicarbonate salts of sodium\",\"Nitrate and phosphate salts of potassium\",\"Chloride and sulphate salts of iron\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that hardness is because water contains chloride and sulphate salts of calcium and magnesium.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What substance is added to hard water to make it soft?\",\"options\":[\"Common salt solution\",\"Washing soda solution\",\"Baking soda solution\",\"Vinegar solution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that to soften hard water, a solution of washing soda is added to it.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When washing soda is added to hard water, what type of substance is formed that helps in softening?\",\"options\":[\"A soluble gas\",\"A liquid layer\",\"An insoluble precipitate\",\"A colored dye\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The reaction forms a precipitate of insoluble carbonate salts of calcium and magnesium.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the softening process, the dissolved salts of calcium and magnesium are converted into which form?\",\"options\":[\"Chloride salts\",\"Sulphate salts\",\"Carbonate salts\",\"Sodium salts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that dissolved salts go out in the form of precipitate of the carbonate salts.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the word equation provided, what are the products of the reaction between Calcium chloride and Sodium carbonate?\",\"options\":[\"Calcium sulphate and Sodium chloride\",\"Calcium carbonate and Sodium chloride\",\"Magnesium carbonate and Sodium chloride\",\"Calcium carbonate and Sodium sulphate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The word equation given is: Calcium chloride + Sodium carbonate → Calcium carbonate + Sodium chloride.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chemical equation \'CaCl₂ + Na₂CO₃ → CaCO₃ + NaCl\', what is the chemical formula for washing soda?\",\"options\":[\"CaCl₂\",\"Na₂CO₃\",\"CaCO₃\",\"NaCl\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In the equation for softening water, Na₂CO₃ (Sodium carbonate) represents washing soda.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you were to write the word equation for the softening of water containing magnesium chloride, what would be the products?\",\"options\":[\"Magnesium carbonate + Sodium chloride\",\"Magnesium sulphate + Sodium chloride\",\"Calcium carbonate + Sodium chloride\",\"Magnesium chloride + Sodium carbonate\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Following the pattern for calcium, magnesium chloride reacts with sodium carbonate to form magnesium carbonate and sodium chloride.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the formation of a precipitate result in the \'softening\' of water?\",\"options\":[\"It adds more minerals to the water\",\"It removes the dissolved calcium and magnesium ions from the water\",\"It increases the brackish taste of the water\",\"It prevents the soap from reacting with the water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As the dissolved salts go out in the form of precipitate, the water is softened.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Look at the unbalanced equation: CaCl₂ + Na₂CO₃ → CaCO₃ + NaCl. To balance the number of Sodium (Na) and Chlorine (Cl) atoms, what coefficient must be placed before NaCl?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"There are 2 Na atoms and 2 Cl atoms on the left, so 2 NaCl is needed on the right to balance the equation.\",\"topic\":\"Softening of hard water\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the chemical bonds in reactants during a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"They remain unchanged\",\"They become stronger\",\"They break\",\"They turn into valence electrons\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that during a chemical change, some chemical bonds in the reactants break.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How are new substances (products) formed during a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"By breaking the nucleus\",\"By formation of new chemical bonds\",\"By changing the number of protons\",\"By keeping the same composition\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, products are formed by the formation of new chemical bonds.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what defines the valency of an atom?\",\"options\":[\"The number of protons in the nucleus\",\"The total number of electrons\",\"The number of chemical bonds formed by one atom\",\"The mass of the atom\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines valency as the number of chemical bonds formed by one atom.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do noble gases not form any chemical bonds?\",\"options\":[\"They have too many electrons\",\"Their electron octet\\/duplet is complete\",\"They do not have any valence electrons\",\"They are not found in nature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that noble gases do not form bonds because their electron octet\\/duplet is complete.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the atom is used during the formation of a chemical bond?\",\"options\":[\"Inner shell electrons\",\"Protons\",\"Neutrons\",\"Valence electrons\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that an atom uses its valence electrons during the formation of a chemical bond.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ultimate goal of an atom when it forms chemical bonds equal to its valency?\",\"options\":[\"To increase its atomic number\",\"To attain the electronic configuration of a complete octet\\/duplet\",\"To break its existing bonds\",\"To change its physical state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that on forming bonds equal to its valency, the atom attains a complete octet\\/duplet configuration.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If an atom has an incomplete electron octet, what is it likely to do based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"Remain inert like a noble gas\",\"Form chemical bonds\",\"Lose its valency\",\"Change into a reactant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that atoms with incomplete electron octet\\/duplet form chemical bonds.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"During a chemical change, the new substances formed have ________ properties compared to the reactants.\",\"options\":[\"identical\",\"different\",\"no\",\"temporary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that new substances having different properties are formed during a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"An atom forms 3 chemical bonds to complete its octet. What is the valency of this atom?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since valency is the number of chemical bonds formed by one atom, forming 3 bonds means the valency is 3.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the relationship between electronic configuration and valency?\",\"options\":[\"Valency is independent of electrons\",\"Atoms bond to achieve a complete octet\\/duplet configuration\",\"Noble gases bond most frequently\",\"Only inner electrons determine valency\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links bonding to the completion of the electronic configuration (octet\\/duplet).\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what happens to the composition of matter during a chemical change?\",\"options\":[\"It remains the same\",\"It changes\",\"It only changes for noble gases\",\"It disappears\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first sentence of the text states that the composition of matter changes during a chemical change.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In which previous chapter was the relationship between electronic configuration and valency discussed?\",\"options\":[\"Composition of Matter\",\"Chemical Change\",\"Inside the Atom\",\"Chemical Bond\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions this relationship was seen in the chapter \'Inside the Atom\'.\",\"topic\":\"Chemical bond\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the electronic configuration of a neutral sodium (₁₁Na) atom?\",\"options\":[\"2, 8, 2\",\"2, 8, 1\",\"2, 8, 7\",\"2, 8, 8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the electronic configuration of ₁₁Na is 2, 8, 1.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the electronic configuration of a neutral chlorine (₁₇Cl) atom?\",\"options\":[\"2, 8, 1\",\"2, 8, 8\",\"2, 8, 7\",\"2, 7, 8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the electronic configuration of ₁₇Cl is 2, 8, 7.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for an ionic bond as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Covalent bond\",\"Metallic bond\",\"Electrovalent bond\",\"Molecular bond\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that an ionic bond is also called an electrovalent bond.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the valency of sodium considered to be one?\",\"options\":[\"It has one electron in its valence shell\",\"It has one electron in its \'L\' shell\",\"It needs one electron to complete its octet\",\"It has one proton in its nucleus\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states the valency of sodium is one as it has one electron in its valence shell.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When a sodium atom loses its valence electron, which shell becomes its new outermost shell?\",\"options\":[\"K shell\",\"L shell\",\"M shell\",\"N shell\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that on loss of a valence electron from the \'M\' shell, the penultimate shell \'L\' becomes the outermost shell.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the net charge on a sodium cation (Na⁺)?\",\"options\":[\"-1\",\"0\",\"+1\",\"+2\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a Na⁺ cation carries a net positive charge of +1.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, how is the valency of an ion determined?\",\"options\":[\"By the total number of shells\",\"By the number of protons in the nucleus\",\"By the magnitude of positive or negative charge on it\",\"By the number of electrons in the \'K\' shell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the valency of an ion is equal to the magnitude of positive or negative charge on it.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a neutral sodium atom has 11 electrons, how many electrons does a Na⁺ cation have?\",\"options\":[\"11\",\"12\",\"10\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that after losing one electron, the electron number of sodium becomes 10.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What type of force causes the oppositely charged ions Na⁺ and Cl⁻ to attract each other?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Electrostatic force of attraction\",\"Nuclear force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the chemical bond is formed due to the electrostatic force of attraction between opposite charges.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the charge balance of a neutral chlorine atom when it accepts an electron?\",\"options\":[\"It remains neutral\",\"It becomes a cation with +1 charge\",\"It becomes an anion with -1 charge\",\"It becomes an anion with -7 charge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that adding an electron results in the formation of an anion Cl⁻ carrying a net negative charge -1.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which shell is the valence shell for a neutral sodium atom before it forms a bond?\",\"options\":[\"K shell\",\"L shell\",\"M shell\",\"N shell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the loss of a valence electron from the \'M\' shell of the sodium atom.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many electrons are present in the valence shell of a chlorine atom before it reacts?\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"7\",\"8\",\"17\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The electronic configuration 2, 8, 7 shows there are 7 electrons in the valence shell.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If an ion has a valency of 1, how many ionic bonds will it form?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Seven\",\"Eight\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that an ion forms the same number of ionic bonds as its valency.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what does the arrow pointing from the sodium atom to the chlorine atom represent?\",\"options\":[\"Transfer of a proton\",\"Transfer of a valence electron\",\"Sharing of a pair of electrons\",\"Movement of the nucleus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show a sodium atom giving its valence electron to a chlorine atom.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/102_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why does the sodium atom (Na) have three shells while the sodium ion (Na⁺) has only two shells?\",\"options\":[\"Because it gained a proton\",\"Because the \'L\' shell was destroyed\",\"Because it lost its only electron in the \'M\' shell\",\"Because the \'M\' shell became the penultimate shell\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the loss of the single electron in the outermost shell, making the \'L\' shell the new outermost shell.\",\"topic\":\"Ionic bond\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/102_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22164,57,8,'Science','en','Measurement and Effects of Heat','[\"Heat is a form of energy that flows from a hot object to a cold one.\",\"Heat is measured in Joules (SI) or calories (CGS); 1 cal = 4.18 J.\",\"Temperature tells how hot or cold an object is; it is the average kinetic energy of its atoms.\",\"Heat causes expansion of solids, liquids and gases; cooling causes contraction.\",\"When heat is added to a substance, its temperature rises and it expands; when heat is removed, temperature falls and it contracts.\",\"The specific heat (c) of a substance is the heat needed to raise 1 kg of it by 1 °C (J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹).\",\"Heat required: Q = m c ΔT.\",\"Heat sources: Sun, Earth (geothermal), chemical fuels, electrical energy, nuclear fission, air.\",\"Heat transfer occurs by conduction, convection and radiation.\",\"Conduction: heat flows through a solid by molecular collisions.\",\"Convection: heat carried by moving fluid (liquid or gas).\",\"Radiation: heat carried by electromagnetic waves; the Sun is the main source of radiation heat.\",\"Heat and temperature are different: heat is energy content, temperature is a measure of average kinetic energy.\",\"Thermometers measure temperature; mercury or alcohol thermometers use liquid expansion, clinical thermometers use sensors.\",\"Linear expansion: ΔL = α L₀ ΔT; α is linear expansion coefficient (1\\/°C).\",\"Area expansion: ΔA = β A₀ ΔT; β ≈ 2α for solids.\",\"Volume expansion: ΔV = γ V₀ ΔT; γ ≈ 3α for solids.\",\"For gases, volume expands with temperature at constant pressure: V = V₀(1+β ΔT).\",\"Density of a gas decreases when it is heated because its volume increases.\",\"The expansion of railway rails and bridges is accommodated by gaps to avoid damage.\",\"Heat can be measured using a calorimeter, which is thermally isolated and contains a known mass of water.\",\"The final temperature in a calorimeter can be found by energy balance: Q₁+Q₂+Q₃=0.\",\"The specific heat of a substance can be found if the masses, temperatures and final temperature are known.\",\"Heat can be generated by chemical reactions (combustion) or by electrical heating.\",\"Heat can also be produced by nuclear fission in atomic energy projects.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the unit of heat in the SI system?\",\"options\":[\"calorie\",\"Joule\",\"Celsius\",\"Gram\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the unit of heat in the SI system is Joule.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the CGS system, heat is measured in which unit?\",\"options\":[\"Joule\",\"Gram\",\"calorie\",\"Degree\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the unit of heat in CGS units is calorie.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"One calorie is equivalent to how many Joules?\",\"options\":[\"1.48 Joule\",\"4.18 Joule\",\"10 Joule\",\"41.8 Joule\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, one calorie is equivalent to 4.18 Joule.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Heat is a form of energy that flows between two objects in which direction?\",\"options\":[\"From low temperature to high temperature\",\"From a cold object to a hot object\",\"From high temperature to low temperature\",\"Only between objects of the same temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Heat flows from an object at high temperature to an object at low temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to an object when it is cooled?\",\"options\":[\"It expands\",\"It contracts\",\"Its state remains the same\",\"Its temperature increases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an object contracts on cooling.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the correct definition of one calorie of heat?\",\"options\":[\"Heat required to increase the temperature of 1 kg of water by 1°C\",\"Heat required to increase the temperature of 1 gm of water by 10°C\",\"Heat required to increase the temperature of 1 gm of water by 1°C\",\"Heat required to melt 1 gm of ice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"One calorie heat is the heat required to increase the temperature of 1 gm of water through 1°C.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How much heat in calories is needed to raise the temperature of 100 gm of water by 10 °C?\",\"options\":[\"10 cal\",\"100 cal\",\"1000 cal\",\"418 cal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Heat = mass (gm) x change in temperature = 100 x 10 = 1000 cal.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If 300 calories of heat is given to 30 gm of water, what will be the change in its temperature?\",\"options\":[\"1 °C\",\"10 °C\",\"30 °C\",\"300 °C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the formula 300 = 30 x change in temp, the change is 300\\/30 = 10 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If an object has 10 calories of heat energy, what is its value in Joules?\",\"options\":[\"4.18 J\",\"41.8 J\",\"0.418 J\",\"418 J\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since 1 cal = 4.18 J, 10 cal = 10 x 4.18 = 41.8 J.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which statement correctly compares the temperature of ice cream and tea?\",\"options\":[\"Tea has a lower temperature than ice cream\",\"Ice cream has a higher temperature than tea\",\"The temperature of ice cream is less than the temperature of tea\",\"Both have the same temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the temperature of ice cream is less than the temperature of tea.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the image of the melting ice cube demonstrates which effect of heat?\",\"options\":[\"Expansion of solids\",\"Contraction of liquids\",\"Change in the state of matter\",\"Decrease in temperature\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that the state of matter changes due to heat, which is shown by the melting ice in the figure.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/105_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the hot air balloon illustrates which specific effect of heat mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"An object expands when heat is given to it\",\"An object contracts when heat is given to it\",\"Heat flows from low to high temperature\",\"Heat is measured in calories\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that when we give heat to an object it expands; the balloon uses this principle.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/105_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What does the temperature of an object tell us?\",\"options\":[\"The total amount of heat energy in the object\",\"How hot or cold that object is\",\"The mass of the object\",\"The state of matter of the object\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines temperature as telling us how hot or cold an object is.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the mass of water is 1.5 kg, what value should be used in the formula \'mass (gm) x change in temperature\' to calculate heat in calories?\",\"options\":[\"1.5\",\"15\",\"150\",\"1500\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The solved example shows that 1.5 kg must be converted to 1500 gm for the calculation.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what are the three primary effects of heat on an object?\",\"options\":[\"Expansion, contraction, and change in state\",\"Heating, boiling, and freezing\",\"Joule, calorie, and Celsius\",\"Conduction, convection, and radiation\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text mentions expansion (when heated), contraction (on cooling), and change in the state of matter.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction and Units of Heat\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the biggest source of heat received by the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"The Moon\",\"The Sun\",\"The Earth\'s core\",\"Atomic energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the Sun is the biggest source of heat received by the earth.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the process of nuclear fusion occurring in the Sun, which nuclei fuse together?\",\"options\":[\"Helium nuclei\",\"Uranium nuclei\",\"Hydrogen nuclei\",\"Thorium nuclei\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that in the process of nuclear fusion in the Sun, hydrogen nuclei fuse together to form helium nuclei.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the heat originating from the high temperature at the centre of the Earth called?\",\"options\":[\"Solar energy\",\"Chemical energy\",\"Geothermal energy\",\"Atomic energy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines heat from the centre of the earth as geothermal energy.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When fuels like wood or coal burn, heat is generated through a chemical reaction between the fuel and which gas?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Hydrogen\",\"Oxygen\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that heat is generated when there is a chemical reaction between the fuel and oxygen.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these equipments is an example of electricity being used as a source of heat?\",\"options\":[\"Solar cooker\",\"Electric press\",\"Gas stove\",\"Atomic reactor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists the electric press and electric heater as equipments that produce heat with electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, how does the production of heat in the Sun differ from that in atomic energy projects?\",\"options\":[\"Sun uses fission; atomic projects use fusion\",\"Sun uses fusion; atomic projects use fission\",\"Sun uses chemical reactions; atomic projects use fusion\",\"Sun uses geothermal energy; atomic projects use fission\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the Sun uses nuclear fusion (hydrogen nuclei fusing), while atomic energy projects use fission (nuclei of elements like uranium undergoing fission).\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which elements are mentioned as sources for producing atomic energy through fission?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen and Helium\",\"Carbon and Oxygen\",\"Uranium and Thorium\",\"Iron and Copper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that nuclei of elements like uranium and thorium undergo fission to produce atomic energy.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of the nuclear fusion process taking place in the Sun\'s centre?\",\"options\":[\"Hydrogen nuclei are destroyed\",\"Helium nuclei are formed\",\"Oxygen is produced\",\"Uranium is formed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, hydrogen nuclei fuse together to form helium nuclei in the Sun.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a person uses an electric heater to warm a room, what is the primary source of heat being utilized?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical energy\",\"Electrical energy\",\"Geothermal energy\",\"Atomic energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the electric heater as an equipment that produces heat with the help of electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the burning of petrol considered a source of chemical energy for heat?\",\"options\":[\"Because it involves the fusion of nuclei\",\"Because it involves a reaction between fuel and oxygen\",\"Because it uses electricity to generate heat\",\"Because it comes from the centre of the earth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that burning fuels like petrol involves a chemical reaction between the fuel and oxygen, generating heat.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the Earth as a source of heat based on the provided text?\",\"options\":[\"Earth is the biggest source of heat for the solar system\",\"Earth generates heat only when fuels are burned on its surface\",\"Earth is a source of heat because its centre has a high temperature\",\"Earth only provides heat through the air surrounding it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the earth is a source of heat because the temperature of the centre of the earth is high.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where is a large amount of heat present all around us?\",\"options\":[\"In the vacuum of space\",\"In the air\",\"In the water\",\"In the moonlight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a large amount of heat is present in the air around us.\",\"topic\":\"Sources of heat\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which device is mentioned in the text as being used for measuring temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Calorimeter\",\"Stirrer\",\"Thermometer\",\"Vessel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that a thermometer is a device for measuring temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why can we not determine the temperature of an object accurately by simply touching it?\",\"options\":[\"Because our hands are always at a fixed temperature\",\"Because our sense of hot or cold is relative\",\"Because touching objects is always dangerous\",\"Because all objects have the same temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that our sense of \'hot\' or \'cold\' is relative, making touch inaccurate for measurement.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment described, what is vessel B filled with?\",\"options\":[\"Hot water\",\"Lukewarm water\",\"Cold water\",\"Empty\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment instructions specify filling vessel A with hot water and B with cold water.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a potential risk mentioned in the text when trying to determine temperature by touch?\",\"options\":[\"The object might change its temperature\",\"You may hurt yourself by touching very hot or cold objects\",\"The thermometer might break\",\"The water might spill out of the vessel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text warns that you may hurt yourself by touching very hot or cold objects.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment, if you move your hand from vessel B (cold water) to vessel C (mixture), how will the water in vessel C feel to that hand?\",\"options\":[\"Cold\",\"Hot\",\"Exactly the same as vessel B\",\"Freezing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that the left hand (from B) will find the water in C to be hot because it is relative to the cold water.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist needs to record the precise temperature of a chemical solution, which tool should they use based on the text\'s recommendation?\",\"options\":[\"Their sense of touch\",\"A stirrer\",\"A thermometer\",\"A heat-resistant lid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that we need a device like a thermometer to measure temperature accurately.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student dips their hand in ice-cold water and then moves it to a bowl of tap water. Based on the concept of relative sensation, the tap water will feel warmer than it actually is. This is an example of:\",\"options\":[\"Accurate measurement\",\"Relative sensation\",\"Absolute temperature\",\"Heat resistance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This scenario demonstrates that the sensation of heat depends on the previous state, which is \'relative sensation\'.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why do the two hands feel different sensations when dipped into the same vessel C simultaneously?\",\"options\":[\"Because vessel C has two different temperatures of water\",\"Because one hand is larger than the other\",\"Because each hand\'s sensation is relative to the temperature of the vessel it was in previously\",\"Because the thermometer gives different readings for each hand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The sensation is relative; the right hand was in hot water and the left was in cold water, leading to different sensations in the same mixture.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the temperature of the water in vessel C compared to vessels A and B?\",\"options\":[\"It is hotter than vessel A\",\"It is colder than vessel B\",\"It is at an intermediate temperature between A and B\",\"It is at the same temperature as vessel A\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since C contains water from both A (hot) and B (cold), its temperature is between the two extremes.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what is the primary limitation of using the human body as a measuring instrument for temperature?\",\"options\":[\"It cannot feel any temperature changes\",\"It lacks a standardized scale and is influenced by previous sensations\",\"It can only measure hot objects, not cold ones\",\"It is more accurate than a thermometer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that touch is relative and inaccurate, meaning it lacks a consistent standard.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which vessel is used to show that the same water can feel both hot and cold at the same time to different hands?\",\"options\":[\"Vessel A\",\"Vessel B\",\"Vessel C\",\"None of the vessels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Vessel C contains the mixture where both hands are dipped to experience the relative sensation.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/106_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure of the calorimeter, which part is inserted into the inner vessel to measure the temperature of the liquid?\",\"options\":[\"Stirrer\",\"Thermometer\",\"Heat resistant ring\",\"Outer vessel\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the thermometer passing through the lid into the liquid to measure temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature and Relative Sensation\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/110_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a substance made of?\",\"options\":[\"Molecules only\",\"Atoms\",\"Heat energy\",\"Protons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'We know that a substance is made of atoms.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The total kinetic energy of the atoms in a substance is a measure of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Temperature\",\"Mass\",\"Heat\",\"Velocity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines heat as the total kinetic energy of the atoms in a substance.\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the temperature of a substance related to?\",\"options\":[\"The total kinetic energy of atoms\",\"The average kinetic energy of atoms\",\"The potential energy of atoms\",\"The number of atoms in the substance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'the temperature of a substance is related to the average kinetic energy of atoms.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If two objects have equal average kinetic energy of their atoms, what can be concluded about them?\",\"options\":[\"Their heat content is equal\",\"Their masses are equal\",\"Their temperatures are equal\",\"Their total kinetic energy is equal\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'If the average kinetic energy of atoms in two objects is equal then their temperatures will also be equal.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the length and direction of the arrows attached to the atoms represent?\",\"options\":[\"The size and shape of the atoms\",\"The temperature and heat of the gas\",\"The magnitude and direction of the velocity of the atoms\",\"The force of attraction between the atoms\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that \'The direction and the length of the arrows indicate to the atoms show the direction and magnitude of the velocity of the atoms.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/106_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, why are the arrows in diagram (a) longer than the arrows in diagram (b)?\",\"options\":[\"The atoms in (a) are larger than in (b)\",\"The gas in (a) is at a higher temperature than in (b)\",\"The gas in (a) has more atoms than in (b)\",\"The gas in (a) is at a lower temperature than in (b)\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the velocity of atoms (indicated by arrow length) is larger at higher temperatures.\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/106_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why are atoms in a solid object unable to be displaced from their fixed places?\",\"options\":[\"Because they have no kinetic energy\",\"Because they are too heavy to move\",\"Because of the forces acting between them\",\"Because the temperature is too low\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The atoms in a solid object are tied to one another because of the forces acting between them. So they cannot be displaced from their places.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How do atoms in a solid move when they are heated?\",\"options\":[\"They move randomly in all directions\",\"They oscillate around their fixed position\",\"They stop moving completely\",\"They move from one end of the solid to the other\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Because of heat, they oscillate around their fixed position.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the velocity of oscillation in a solid as its temperature increases?\",\"options\":[\"The velocity of oscillation becomes faster\",\"The velocity of oscillation becomes slower\",\"The atoms stop oscillating\",\"The atoms start moving in straight lines\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'Higher the temperature of the solid, faster is their velocity of oscillation.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Suppose Object A has twice the mass of Object B, and both are at the same temperature. Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[\"Object A has half the heat content of Object B\",\"Object A has the same heat content as Object B\",\"Object A has twice the heat content of Object B\",\"Object A has four times the heat content of Object B\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if mass is doubled at the same temperature, the total kinetic energy (heat) is also doubled.\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If Object A has twice the number of atoms as Object B, but they have the same average kinetic energy per atom, what is the ratio of their temperatures?\",\"options\":[\"2:1\",\"1:2\",\"1:1\",\"4:1\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Temperature is related to average kinetic energy; if average KE is the same, temperatures are equal (1:1).\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the difference between heat and temperature as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"Heat is a measure of velocity, temperature is a measure of force\",\"Heat is total kinetic energy, temperature is average kinetic energy\",\"Heat is average kinetic energy, temperature is total kinetic energy\",\"Heat is potential energy, temperature is kinetic energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines heat as the total kinetic energy and temperature as related to the average kinetic energy of atoms.\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What does the direction of the arrows in the gas diagrams (a) and (b) indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The direction of heat flow\",\"The direction of the velocity of the atoms\",\"The direction of the force of gravity\",\"The direction of the solid\'s oscillation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The direction and the length of the arrows indicate to the atoms show the direction and magnitude of the velocity of the atoms.\'\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If we increase the mass of a substance while keeping its temperature constant, what happens to its total kinetic energy?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It decreases\",\"It remains the same\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Since total KE is the sum of KE of all atoms, increasing the number of atoms (mass) at the same average KE (temperature) increases the total KE.\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, why does a larger object contain more heat than a smaller object of the same substance at the same temperature?\",\"options\":[\"Because its atoms move faster\",\"Because it has a higher average kinetic energy\",\"Because it has a greater number of atoms contributing to total kinetic energy\",\"Because the forces between its atoms are stronger\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text illustrates that more atoms (more mass) results in higher total kinetic energy (heat) even if the average KE (temperature) is the same.\",\"topic\":\"Heat and Temperature\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following units is primarily used for scientific experiments?\",\"options\":[\"Celsius\",\"Fahrenheit\",\"Kelvin\",\"Joule\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that Kelvin is used in scientific experiments, while Celsius and Fahrenheit are used in daily life.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct formula showing the relation between Celsius (C) and Fahrenheit (F)?\",\"options\":[\"(F-32)\\/5 = C\\/9\",\"(F-32)\\/9 = C\\/5\",\"(C-32)\\/9 = F\\/5\",\"F = C + 273.15\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the relation is (F-32)\\/9 = C\\/5.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment with two vessels A and B, why did vessel B require more time to increase its temperature by 10 °C?\",\"options\":[\"Vessel B was made of a different material.\",\"The water in vessel B was initially colder.\",\"Vessel B required a larger amount of heat for the same temperature rise.\",\"The spirit lamp used for vessel B was weaker.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that more time was required for B because more amount of heat had to be given to B for the same increase in temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relation between Kelvin (K) and Celsius (C) as per the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"K = C - 273.15\",\"K = C + 32\",\"K = C + 273.15\",\"K = (C × 9)\\/5\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text provides the formula K = C + 273.15.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the temperature of a substance is 68 °F, what is its value in Celsius?\",\"options\":[\"20 °C\",\"32 °C\",\"37 °C\",\"40 °C\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using (F-32)\\/9 = C\\/5, (68-32)\\/9 = 36\\/9 = 4; C = 4 × 5 = 20 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, what is the boiling point of water in the Fahrenheit scale?\",\"options\":[\"100 °F\",\"212 °F\",\"373.15 °F\",\"32 °F\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure explicitly lists the boiling point of water as 212 °F.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/107_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the given figure, what is the freezing point of water in the Celsius scale?\",\"options\":[\"32 °C\",\"273 °C\",\"0 °C\",\"-38.8 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table in the figure shows the freezing point of water is 0 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/107_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"At what specific temperature do the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales show the exact same numerical value?\",\"options\":[\"0°\",\"32°\",\"-40°\",\"100°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The solved example in the text proves that at -40°, C = F.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If the temperature in Celsius is 20 °C, what is the corresponding temperature in Kelvin?\",\"options\":[\"293.15 K\",\"253.15 K\",\"313.15 K\",\"273.15 K\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using K = C + 273.15, K = 20 + 273.15 = 293.15 K.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment with vessels A and B, which statement is true if both vessels are at the same temperature but B required more heat to reach it?\",\"options\":[\"Vessel A has more heat than Vessel B.\",\"Vessel B has more heat than Vessel A.\",\"Both vessels have the same amount of heat.\",\"Heat and temperature are the same thing.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that even though the water in A and B have the same temperature, the amount of heat in B is more than that in A.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the freezing point of mercury as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"356.7 °C\",\"-38.8 °C\",\"0 °C\",\"-40 °C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table in the text lists the freezing point of mercury as -38.8 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Using the formula (F-32)\\/9 = C\\/5, if the temperature is 32 °F, what is the temperature in Celsius?\",\"options\":[\"32 °C\",\"100 °C\",\"0 °C\",\"273.15 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"(32-32)\\/9 = 0\\/9 = 0. Therefore C\\/5 = 0, so C = 0.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which units are commonly used for temperature measurement in daily life?\",\"options\":[\"Kelvin and Celsius\",\"Celsius and Fahrenheit\",\"Kelvin and Fahrenheit\",\"Only Celsius\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that Kelvin is used in scientific experiments, while the other two (Celsius and Fahrenheit) are used in daily life.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the relationship between heat and temperature from the vessel experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Temperature depends only on the time of heating.\",\"Two objects at the same temperature must contain the same amount of heat.\",\"Two objects at the same temperature can contain different amounts of heat.\",\"Heat cannot be measured if the temperature is the same.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The experiment shows that A and B have the same temperature, but the amount of heat in B is more than in A.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist needs to convert a Celsius reading to Kelvin, what mathematical operation should they perform?\",\"options\":[\"Subtract 273.15 from the Celsius value.\",\"Multiply the Celsius value by 273.15.\",\"Add 273.15 to the Celsius value.\",\"Divide the Celsius value by 273.15.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The formula K = C + 273.15 indicates that 273.15 must be added to the Celsius value.\",\"topic\":\"Temperature Units and Formulae\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which liquid was commonly used in thermometers earlier but has now been replaced because it is harmful?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Alcohol\",\"Mercury\",\"Oil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that mercury was used earlier but has been replaced with alcohol as mercury is harmful.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the temperature range designed for a clinical thermometer?\",\"options\":[\"0 °C to 100 °C\",\"35 °C to 42 °C\",\"-40 °C to 110 °C\",\"37 °C to 100 °C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, clinical thermometers are designed to measure temperatures between 35 °C and 42 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the body temperature of a healthy person as mentioned in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"35 °C\",\"37 °C\",\"40 °C\",\"42 °C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the body temperature of a healthy person is 37 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the typical range of temperatures that a laboratory thermometer can measure?\",\"options\":[\"35 °C to 42 °C\",\"0 °C to 50 °C\",\"-40 °C to 110 °C\",\"-10 °C to 40 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that laboratory thermometers have a large range, spanning -40 °C to 110 °C or even larger.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In a liquid-filled thermometer, what is contained in the volume of the tube that is not occupied by the liquid?\",\"options\":[\"Air\",\"Oxygen\",\"Vacuum\",\"Water vapor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the rest of the volume of the tube has vacuum and its other end is closed.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the level of alcohol rise in the thermometer tube when it comes in contact with a hot object?\",\"options\":[\"The alcohol evaporates into gas\",\"The alcohol expands due to increased temperature\",\"The glass tube shrinks due to heat\",\"The vacuum pulls the alcohol upwards\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because of the increased temperature, the alcohol inside expands and its level in the tube rises.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How does a digital thermometer differ from a clinical liquid thermometer in its working principle?\",\"options\":[\"It uses the expansion of mercury\",\"It uses the expansion of alcohol\",\"It uses a sensor to measure heat directly\",\"It uses a vacuum to pull heat from the body\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that digital thermometers do not use the expansion of liquid; instead, they have a sensor to measure heat directly.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of keeping the thermometer bulb in contact with the object being measured?\",\"options\":[\"To prevent the glass from breaking\",\"To ensure the bulb\'s temperature becomes the same as the object\'s\",\"To create a vacuum inside the bulb\",\"To stop the alcohol from expanding too quickly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says the bulb is kept in contact so that its temperature becomes same as that of the object.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of thermometer is specifically used to measure the highest and lowest temperatures reached in a single day?\",\"options\":[\"Clinical thermometer\",\"Laboratory thermometer\",\"Digital thermometer\",\"Maximum minimum thermometer\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text identifies the maximum minimum thermometer as the special type used to measure the minimum and maximum temperatures in a day.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist needs to measure a chemical reaction occurring at -20 °C in a lab, which thermometer is most suitable?\",\"options\":[\"Clinical thermometer\",\"Laboratory thermometer\",\"Digital clinical thermometer\",\"A thermometer with a 35 °C to 42 °C range\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A laboratory thermometer is suitable because its range spans -40 °C to 110 °C, covering -20 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A nurse needs to check a patient\'s fever. Why would they choose a clinical thermometer over a laboratory thermometer?\",\"options\":[\"Because laboratory thermometers do not use liquid\",\"Because the clinical thermometer is specifically designed for the range of human body temperature\",\"Because laboratory thermometers only measure temperatures below 0 °C\",\"Because clinical thermometers use sensors while laboratory ones do not\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Clinical thermometers are designed for the specific range (35-42 °C) relevant to human body temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a standard clinical thermometer NOT be used to measure the temperature of boiling water (100 °C)?\",\"options\":[\"It does not contain enough alcohol\",\"The temperature of boiling water is outside its 35 °C to 42 °C range\",\"It only works when there is a vacuum in the water\",\"It can only measure heat through a sensor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Clinical thermometers only measure up to 42 °C, so 100 °C is far beyond its design capacity.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the fundamental difference between the construction of a laboratory thermometer and a clinical thermometer?\",\"options\":[\"The laboratory thermometer uses mercury while the clinical one uses alcohol\",\"The clinical thermometer is digital while the laboratory one is always liquid-based\",\"The laboratory thermometer has a much larger range of temperature graduations\",\"The clinical thermometer has a bulb while the laboratory thermometer does not\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states laboratory thermometers are similar to clinical ones except that the range of temperatures they can measure is large.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part of the clinical thermometer contains the liquid that expands when heated?\",\"options\":[\"The closed top end\",\"The narrow glass tube\",\"The bulb at the bottom end\",\"The vacuum space\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that the thermometer has a bulb at one end which is filled with liquid.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/108_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the small lines (graduations) marked along the length of the glass tube represent?\",\"options\":[\"The amount of vacuum inside\",\"The different temperature values\",\"The thickness of the glass\",\"The amount of alcohol added\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using graduations in the expansion of alcohol to obtain the temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Thermometer\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/108_102.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22165,57,8,'Science','en','Sound','[\"- Sound is produced when a vibrating object moves air molecules, creating alternating regions of compression and rarefaction.\",\"- A tuning fork has two prongs; when struck, the prongs vibrate and set the surrounding air into waves.\",\"- Compression = high pressure, high density; rarefaction = low pressure, low density.\",\"- The vibration of the prongs is periodic; each complete forward‑backward motion is one cycle.\",\"- One cycle of compression and rarefaction is called a wave cycle; the number of cycles per second is the frequency.\",\"- Frequency is measured in Hertz (Hz); 1 Hz = one cycle per second.\",\"- A tuning fork of 512 Hz vibrates 512 times per second, producing 512 cycles of sound per second.\",\"- The frequency of a sound depends on the vibrating object\'s size, shape, material and tension.\",\"- In a glass cup experiment, the height of the air column determines the frequency; higher column → lower frequency.\",\"- Musical notes are produced by changing the frequency of vibration (strings, air columns, vocal cords).\",\"- Sound needs a medium (solid, liquid or gas) to propagate; in vacuum sound cannot travel.\",\"- Loudspeakers convert electrical energy into mechanical vibration of a diaphragm, producing sound waves.\",\"- Human voice is produced in the larynx where vocal cords vibrate as air passes through.\",\"- Sound level is measured in decibels (dB); loud sounds above ~100 dB can be harmful.\",\"- Sound intensity decreases with distance from the source; intensity ∝ 1\\/(distance²).\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are the two main parts of a tuning fork mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Prongs and stem\",\"Handle and prongs\",\"Base and prongs\",\"Stem and handle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that a tuning fork is made of two prongs and a stem.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is a region of high pressure and high density in air called?\",\"options\":[\"Rarefaction\",\"Vibration\",\"Compression\",\"Propagation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines compression as the region in which air is at high pressure and high density.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the ear-drum when sound waves reach our ear?\",\"options\":[\"It stops moving\",\"It vibrates\",\"It produces signals\",\"It compresses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, when sound waves reach our ear, the ear-drum vibrates.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the tuning fork, what do vertical lines represent?\",\"options\":[\"The material of the fork\",\"The state of air around the fork\",\"The speed of sound\",\"The direction of the stem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that vertical lines are used to show the state of air around the tuning fork.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When the prongs of a tuning fork move away from each other, what happens to the air outside?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes less dense\",\"It is compressed and pressure increases\",\"It is rarefied and pressure decreases\",\"It stays in a steady state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if prongs go away from each other, the air outside is compressed and pressure increases.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the nature of the motion of the prongs of a tuning fork when struck?\",\"options\":[\"Circular motion\",\"Linear motion in one direction\",\"Periodic motion in forward and backward direction\",\"Random motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that vibrations mean the prongs are set into periodic motion in forward and backward direction.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How is the sense of hearing produced in the brain?\",\"options\":[\"By air molecules entering the brain\",\"Through specific signals sent by the vibrating ear-drum\",\"By the prongs touching the ear\",\"By the stem of the tuning fork\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text says that when the ear-drum vibrates, specific signals reach the brain and we get a sense of hearing.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What characterizes a rarefaction region in air?\",\"options\":[\"High pressure and high density\",\"Low pressure and low density\",\"High pressure and low density\",\"Low pressure and high density\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines rarefaction as a region in which air is at low pressure and low density.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the vertical lines representing air are equally spaced, what can be concluded about the air pressure in regions A, B, and C?\",\"options\":[\"Pressure is highest in A\",\"Pressure is lowest in C\",\"Pressure is the same in all three regions\",\"Pressure is changing periodically\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that equally spaced lines indicate the average air pressure in regions A, B, and C is the same.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When a tuning fork\'s prongs come close to each other, what happens to the energy of air molecules that were previously compressed?\",\"options\":[\"The energy is destroyed\",\"They transfer their energy to the air molecules in the next region\",\"The energy returns to the prongs\",\"The energy stops moving\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that air molecules in a compressed state transfer their energy to the air molecules in the next region (region B).\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A student observes a tuning fork but hears no sound. Based on the text, what must be true about the prongs?\",\"options\":[\"The stem is being held steady\",\"The prongs are not vibrating\",\"The prongs are in a vacuum\",\"The prongs are made of plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text establishes that sound is generated from a vibrating object; if there is no sound, there is no vibration.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How do sound waves propagate away from the tuning fork?\",\"options\":[\"By moving the tuning fork through the air\",\"Through the creation of periodic compression and rarefaction\",\"By the stem pushing the air forward\",\"By the ear-drum pulling the air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that periodic motion creates compression and rarefaction which are propagated away as sound waves.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between the movement of the prongs and the density of the air in Region A?\",\"options\":[\"Prongs move away -> Density decreases\",\"Prongs move close -> Density increases\",\"Prongs move away -> Density increases\",\"Prongs move close -> Density remains same\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that when prongs go away from each other, a state of high pressure and high density (compression) is created in region A.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the \'sound waves\' mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The physical prongs moving through space\",\"The signals sent to the brain\",\"The propagation of compression and rarefaction\",\"The steady state of air molecules\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the propagation of compression and rarefaction are nothing but the sound waves.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the air pressure in Region A decrease when the prongs of the tuning fork come close to each other?\",\"options\":[\"Because the air molecules are destroyed\",\"Because the air molecules get more space to move away from each other\",\"Because the stem absorbs the air\",\"Because the prongs push the air away\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that when prongs come close, air molecules get more space to move away, resulting in decreased pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Production of Sound\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what moves away from the source when sound waves are generated in air?\",\"options\":[\"The air itself\",\"The state of compression and rarefaction\",\"The vacuum pump\",\"The electric power supply\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks if it is the air itself or the state of compression and rarefaction that moves, implying the latter.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Through which of the following material mediums can sound travel?\",\"options\":[\"Only solids\",\"Only liquids\",\"Only gases\",\"Solids, liquids, or gases\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that sound travels through material mediums like solid, liquid, or gas.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the bell jar placed on during the experiment to ensure it is vacuum tight?\",\"options\":[\"A rough wooden plank\",\"A smooth horizontal surface\",\"A vibrating platform\",\"A water-filled tray\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the bell jar is placed on a smooth horizontal surface.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the function of the tube connected to the bottom of the jar?\",\"options\":[\"To provide electricity to the bell\",\"To allow sound to escape the jar\",\"To remove air using a vacuum pump\",\"To pump water into the jar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows a tube labeled \'To vacuum pump\', which the text explains is used to remove air.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/115_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the level of ringing sound as the quantity of air inside the bell jar decreases?\",\"options\":[\"The sound level increases\",\"The sound level decreases\",\"The sound level remains constant\",\"The sound becomes higher in pitch\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that as the quantity of air decreases, the level of ringing sound heard outside also decreases.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the vacuum pump is operated for a sufficiently long period, what will be the state of the sound?\",\"options\":[\"It will be very loud\",\"It will be very low\",\"It will change into a musical note\",\"It will stop the bell from vibrating\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if the pump is operated for a long period, the level of ringing sound will be very low.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the electric bell connected to at the top of the jar?\",\"options\":[\"The vacuum pump\",\"The smooth horizontal surface\",\"The power supply and the lid\",\"The bottom of the bell jar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show the bell is connected to the power supply and the lid of the bell jar.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/115_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary conclusion demonstrated by the bell jar experiment?\",\"options\":[\"Sound travels faster in a vacuum\",\"Sound generation and propagation needs a medium\",\"Electricity is needed for sound propagation\",\"Air is the only medium for sound\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The experiment demonstrates that sound generation and propagation needs a medium.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If all the air inside the bell jar is totally removed, what will happen to the sound of the ringing bell?\",\"options\":[\"It will become extremely loud\",\"It will be heard normally\",\"It will not be heard at all\",\"It will be heard only by the vacuum pump\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text implies that without a medium (air), sound cannot propagate, so it will not be heard.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary for the bell jar to be \'vacuum tight\'?\",\"options\":[\"To keep the electric bell from falling\",\"To prevent outside air from entering while the pump removes internal air\",\"To make the bell ring louder\",\"To protect the power supply from the vacuum pump\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A vacuum tight jar is necessary to ensure air can be removed and not replaced, allowing the experiment to work.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a material medium for sound propagation?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gas\",\"Light\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions solid, liquid, and gas as material mediums.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At the start of the experiment, when the vacuum pump is off, what is inside the bell jar?\",\"options\":[\"Vacuum\",\"Water\",\"Air\",\"Nothing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that at the start of the experiment, the bell jar contains air.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a student observes that the sound of the bell is getting fainter, what can they infer about the air pressure inside the jar?\",\"options\":[\"The air pressure is increasing\",\"The air pressure is decreasing\",\"The air pressure is staying the same\",\"There is no air pressure at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fainter sound indicates less air quantity, which means the vacuum pump is decreasing the air pressure.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the experiment, what would happen if the bell was ringing in a place with no air, like the moon?\",\"options\":[\"The sound would travel much further\",\"The sound would not be heard\",\"The sound would be heard as a whistle\",\"The sound would be heard normally\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the moon has no air (medium), sound cannot propagate, similar to the vacuum in the jar.\",\"topic\":\"Propagation of sound and Medium\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which component is essential to prove that sound needs a medium for propagation in this specific setup?\",\"options\":[\"The smooth surface\",\"The vacuu