cing sound\",\"The windpipe closes completely\",\"The muscles become loose\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When lungs force air through the slit, the vocal cords vibrate and produce sound.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How do muscles affect the quality of the human voice?\",\"options\":[\"By changing the length of the windpipe\",\"By making the vocal cords tight or loose\",\"By increasing the amount of air in the lungs\",\"By closing the esophagus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Muscles attached to the vocal cords can make them tight or loose, which changes the quality of the voice.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why are the voices of men and women different?\",\"options\":[\"Because men have a larger esophagus\",\"Because the length of their vocal cords is different\",\"Because women do not have a larynx\",\"Because children have tighter muscles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The difference in voice is due to the different lengths of vocal cords (20 cm in males and 15 cm in females).\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are performing the experiment with two rubber strips from a bicycle tube, what represents the vocal cords?\",\"options\":[\"The air being blown\",\"The gap between the strips\",\"The two stretched rubber strips\",\"The person blowing the air\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the experiment, the two stretched rubber strips act like vocal cords that vibrate when air is blown through them.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When producing a \'meow.. meow..\' sound like a cat compared to a \'bho...bho...\' sound, what physical change can be noticed in the throat?\",\"options\":[\"The larynx disappears\",\"The tension on the vocal cords changes\",\"The windpipe becomes longer\",\"The esophagus opens wider\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text suggests that tension on the vocal cords changes when producing different sounds like barking or mewing.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table of musical notes, which note has the highest frequency in the madhya saptak?\",\"options\":[\"sa\",\"ma\",\"dha\",\"ni\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"In the provided table, \'ni\' has the highest frequency at 480 Hz.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred about the relationship between the tightness of vocal cords and the voice produced?\",\"options\":[\"Tightness has no effect on sound\",\"Tight and thin cords produce a different quality of voice\",\"Loose cords produce no sound at all\",\"Only thick cords can vibrate\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that when vocal cords are tight and thin, the type or quality of voice is different.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a child\'s vocal cords are described as \'even smaller\' than a female\'s (15 cm), what is a logical conclusion about their voice?\",\"options\":[\"It will be identical to a male\'s voice\",\"It will be different from both male and female voices\",\"It will not produce any vibrations\",\"It is produced in the esophagus instead of the larynx\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since cord length determines voice type, and children have smaller cords, their voices are different from adults.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Touching the throat while humming allows one to feel vibrations. What does this demonstrate about sound production?\",\"options\":[\"Sound is produced by stillness\",\"Sound is produced by the movement of the esophagus\",\"Sound production involves physical vibrations of the larynx\",\"Sound is produced only when swallowing food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Feeling vibrations while producing sound confirms that sound production in the larynx involves vibration.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part is described as the flap that closes the air-track during the process of swallowing?\",\"options\":[\"Vocal Chords\",\"Trachea\",\"Epiglottis\",\"Esophagus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The label in the figure identifies the Epiglottis as the flap that closes the air-track during swallowing.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/117_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which tube is located directly behind the Trachea?\",\"options\":[\"Larynx\",\"Esophagus\",\"Epiglottis\",\"Vocal Chords\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the Esophagus positioned behind the Trachea.\",\"topic\":\"Sound Produced by Human\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/117_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of magnet is a basic component used in the construction of a loudspeaker?\",\"options\":[\"Temporary magnet\",\"Permanent magnet\",\"Electromagnet only\",\"Bar magnet only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that a loudspeaker consists of a permanent magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, sound can be harmful to humans if the sound level is around ______.\",\"options\":[\"50 decibels\",\"75 decibels\",\"100 decibels\",\"150 decibels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if the sound level is around 100 decibels, it can be harmful to us.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What component is wound around the permanent magnet in a loudspeaker?\",\"options\":[\"A plastic screen\",\"A metal rod\",\"A coil\",\"A rubber band\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that a coil is wound around the permanent magnet.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens immediately when an electric current flows through the coil in a loudspeaker?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes a permanent magnet\",\"It generates a magnetic field\",\"It stops the sound production\",\"It melts the conical screen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that if a current flows through this coil, it also generates a magnetic field.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How are sound waves produced in the air by a loudspeaker?\",\"options\":[\"By the flow of electricity in the air\",\"By the permanent magnet heating up\",\"Due to the back and forth motion of the loudspeaker screen\",\"By the chemical reaction inside the coil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that due to the back and forth motion of the loudspeaker screen, sound waves are produced in air.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What factors of the coil\'s movement depend on the variation in the current flowing through it?\",\"options\":[\"The weight and size\",\"The frequency and amplitude\",\"The color and shape\",\"The temperature and resistance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text specifies that the frequency and amplitude of the movement of the coil depends on the variation in the current.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which part is attached to the coil and moves to create sound waves in the air?\",\"options\":[\"The outer wooden cabinet\",\"The internal permanent magnet\",\"The conical screen\",\"The electrical connection wires\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text and figure show the conical screen is attached to the coil and moves to produce sound.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/118_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you gently touch the screen of a loudspeaker while it is producing sound, what will you feel?\",\"options\":[\"An electric shock\",\"The screen will stop moving immediately\",\"Vibrations of the screen\",\"The screen will become very hot\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions you can feel these vibrations of the loudspeaker screen just by gently touching it.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the given figure and text, why are these devices commonly used in public places?\",\"options\":[\"They are very small and hidden\",\"They can produce very loud sound\",\"They do not require any current\",\"They are made of magnetic liquid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that very loud sound can be produced by using a loudspeaker, therefore they are used in public places.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/118_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary to have a limit on the loudness of a loudspeaker despite its ability to produce very loud sound?\",\"options\":[\"To save the battery of the device\",\"To prevent the permanent magnet from losing power\",\"Because sound levels around 100 decibels are harmful\",\"To keep the coil from moving too slowly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that sound levels around 100 decibels can be harmful, so there must be a limit on loudness.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"The movement of the coil in a loudspeaker is explained by comparing it to which phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"Two magnets moving depending on their positions\",\"A wire melting due to high heat\",\"A screen changing its color with light\",\"Air moving in a straight line\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text compares the coil\'s movement to how two magnets move when brought near each other.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which sequence correctly describes the process of sound generation in a loudspeaker?\",\"options\":[\"Current -> Magnetic field -> Coil motion -> Screen motion -> Sound waves\",\"Sound waves -> Screen motion -> Coil motion -> Magnetic field -> Current\",\"Magnet -> Screen motion -> Current -> Magnetic field -> Sound waves\",\"Screen motion -> Coil motion -> Magnet -> Current -> Sound waves\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text describes current creating a magnetic field, which moves the coil, which moves the screen, producing sound waves.\",\"topic\":\"Sound generation by loudspeaker\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which unit is used by cell-phone apps to measure the loudness of sound?\",\"options\":[\"Hertz\",\"Decibel\",\"Meter\",\"Pascal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that an \'app\' may be available on cell-phones to measure the loudness of sound in decibel.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is a major cause affecting the environment and social health?\",\"options\":[\"Light pollution\",\"Air pollution\",\"Sound pollution\",\"Water pollution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that sound pollution is a major cause affecting the environment and social health.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you are using a mobile app to check how loud a loudspeaker is, which property of sound are you measuring?\",\"options\":[\"Pitch\",\"Frequency\",\"Loudness\",\"Speed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions using an app to measure the \'loudness of sound\'.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the sound level as you move further away from a loudspeaker at a public place?\",\"options\":[\"The sound level increases\",\"The sound level decreases\",\"The sound level remains constant\",\"The sound level becomes zero immediately\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Measuring at different distances helps observe the relation where sound level decreases as distance increases.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What precaution should be taken while studying sound and its production?\",\"options\":[\"Ensure the sound is as loud as possible\",\"Take care that others are not disturbed\",\"Only measure sound in a closed room\",\"Use only high-frequency sounds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The \'Always remember\' section states we should take care that others are not disturbed.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which device is mentioned in the text as a tool to measure sound levels with the help of an app?\",\"options\":[\"Loudspeaker\",\"Microphone\",\"Cell-phone\",\"Thermometer\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says an \'app\' may be available on cell-phones to measure loudness.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student measures 80 dB near a speaker and 60 dB at a distance of 10 meters. What does this experiment demonstrate?\",\"options\":[\"Sound pollution is good for health\",\"Relation between distance and sound level\",\"The frequency of the loudspeaker\",\"The speed of sound in air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks to measure at different distances to observe the relation between distance and sound level.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the environment, what else is negatively impacted by sound pollution according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Social health\",\"Economic growth\",\"Cell-phone battery\",\"Atmospheric pressure\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states sound pollution affects the environment and social health.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it necessary to find ways to avoid sound pollution?\",\"options\":[\"To increase the loudness of speakers\",\"Because it affects environment and social health\",\"To improve cell-phone app accuracy\",\"To make sound travel faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text links the need to avoid sound pollution to its effects on environment and social health.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the suggested activity, what is the primary variable being changed to observe its effect on the decibel reading?\",\"options\":[\"The type of app used\",\"The volume of the loudspeaker\",\"The distance from the loudspeaker\",\"The time of the day\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to \'Measure the sound level at different distances from the loudspeaker\'.\",\"topic\":\"Sound level and Pollution\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29');
INSERT INTO `ep_topic_packs` VALUES (22166,57,8,'Science','en','Reflection of Light','[\"Reflection of light is the change in direction of light rays when they strike a surface and bounce back.\",\"The incident ray is the light ray that strikes the surface.\",\"The point where the incident ray meets the surface is called the point of incidence.\",\"The line perpendicular to the surface at the point of incidence is called the normal.\",\"The angle between the incident ray and the normal is the angle of incidence (θi).\",\"The ray after bouncing back is the reflected ray.\",\"The angle between the reflected ray and the normal is the angle of reflection (θr).\",\"Law of reflection: θi = θr for all surfaces.\",\"Regular (smooth) surfaces give parallel reflected rays; rough surfaces give scattered rays.\",\"A plane mirror produces a virtual, upright, laterally reversed image that is the same size as the object.\",\"The image in a mirror is formed on the same side of the mirror as the object, so left and right are swapped.\",\"In water, light is reflected and then refracted; the reflected image is also inverted horizontally.\",\"A kaleidoscope uses multiple reflections between mirrors to produce many images.\",\"A periscope uses two mirrors at 45° to see over obstacles.\",\"Light must be reflected or refracted to be seen; in darkness there is no light to reflect.\",\"The laws of reflection hold even for irregular surfaces at each point of incidence.\",\"The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection even when the ray is perpendicular to the surface (both 0°).\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is considered the most important among our five senses according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sense of hearing\",\"Sense of vision\",\"Sense of smell\",\"Sense of touch\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the sense of vision is the most important among our five senses.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to light rays when they fall on an object and result in reflection?\",\"options\":[\"They are completely absorbed by the object\",\"They pass through the object without changing\",\"Their direction changes and they turn back\",\"They disappear upon contact with the surface\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines reflection of light as when light rays fall on an object, their direction changes and they turn back.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are we able to see objects like hills, trees, and people around us?\",\"options\":[\"Because our eyes emit light towards them\",\"Because light coming from these objects enters our eyes\",\"Because the objects are always naturally glowing\",\"Because our sense of vision works without light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we can see objects when the light coming from these objects enters our eyes.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is listed in the text as a \'beautiful view of nature\' that we can see?\",\"options\":[\"Microscopic bacteria\",\"Rainbow\",\"Sound waves\",\"Electric currents\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions clouds, rainbow, flying birds, Moon, and stars as beautiful views of nature.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If you are reading a book in a room with a lamp, how is the light from the book reaching your eyes?\",\"options\":[\"The book is emitting its own light\",\"The light from the lamp is reflected by the book\",\"The book is absorbing all the light from the lamp\",\"The eyes are sending vision rays to the book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that light entering our eyes may be emitted by the object or reflected by it; a book reflects light from a source.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the \'Try this\' activity, what is the immediate result of switching off the light in a room at night?\",\"options\":[\"The objects become transparent\",\"The objects cannot be seen clearly\",\"The objects start reflecting more light\",\"The sense of vision becomes stronger\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity demonstrates that when the light is switched off, objects in the room cannot be seen.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the definition of reflection, what two specific changes occur to light rays?\",\"options\":[\"They increase in speed and change color\",\"They change direction and turn back\",\"They stop moving and stay on the surface\",\"They become invisible and lose energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines reflection as the process where light rays change direction and turn back.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best describes the connection between light and vision mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Vision is possible only when there is no light\",\"Light is only needed to see the Moon and stars\",\"There is a direct connection where light must enter the eyes to see\",\"Vision and light are not related to each other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text concludes from the activity that there is a connection between the sense of vision and light.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two ways light can come from an object to enter our eyes?\",\"options\":[\"It can be emitted or reflected\",\"It can be absorbed or refracted\",\"It can be heated or cooled\",\"It can be bent or broken\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states: \'The light entering our eyes may be emitted by the object or may be reflected by that object.\'\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the specific scientific term for light \'turning back\' from a surface?\",\"options\":[\"Refraction of light\",\"Reflection of light\",\"Emission of light\",\"Vision of light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly names the process of light rays turning back as \'reflection of light\'.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"When we see the Moon at night, what can we conclude about the light reaching our eyes based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"The light is only emitted by our eyes\",\"The light must be either emitted or reflected by the Moon\",\"The Moon does not require light to be seen\",\"The light is changing direction without turning back\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions the Moon as a visible object and explains that light entering our eyes is either emitted or reflected by the object.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If an object neither emits light nor reflects light, what would be the logical consequence for our sense of vision?\",\"options\":[\"We would see it more clearly\",\"We would see it as a rainbow\",\"We would not be able to see the object\",\"The object would change its own direction\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since we see objects only when light from them enters our eyes (via emission or reflection), an object doing neither would be invisible.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following materials is used to cover most of the comb in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"White paper\",\"Black paper\",\"Transparent plastic\",\"Mirror\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states to cover the rest of the comb with black paper so that light can only pass through the open central portion.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where should the white paper be fitted tightly according to the instructions?\",\"options\":[\"On the mirror\",\"On the torch\",\"On a table or drawing board\",\"On the comb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies to fit a white paper tightly over a table or drawing board.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the comb covered with black paper leaving only the central portion open?\",\"options\":[\"To make the comb look dark\",\"To prevent the comb from breaking\",\"So that light can only pass through the open central portion\",\"To reflect light from the comb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The black paper blocks light, ensuring only a narrow beam passes through the central opening.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"How should the comb be held in relation to the white paper to get light rays?\",\"options\":[\"Parallel\",\"Perpendicular\",\"At a 45-degree angle\",\"Horizontal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to hold the comb perpendicular to the white paper.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to observe the reflection of light in this setup, where should the mirror be placed?\",\"options\":[\"Behind the torch\",\"Under the white paper\",\"In the path of the light ray\",\"On top of the comb\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The instructions state to keep a mirror in the path of the ray of light.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of using a torch in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"To heat the mirror\",\"To provide a source of light rays\",\"To see the black paper\",\"To hold the comb in place\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The torch is the light source used to throw light on the comb to create rays.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what do the lines seen on the white paper between the comb and the mirror represent?\",\"options\":[\"Shadows of the comb\",\"Reflected rays\",\"Incident light rays\",\"Cracks on the paper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The lines represent the path of light rays traveling from the comb towards the mirror.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/120_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the light rays after they hit the surface of the mirror?\",\"options\":[\"They disappear\",\"They pass through the mirror\",\"They turn into shadows\",\"They change direction and bounce back\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure shows the rays hitting the mirror and reflecting (bouncing back) in a different direction.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/120_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which component in this activity is responsible for changing the direction of the light rays?\",\"options\":[\"The comb\",\"The mirror\",\"The black paper\",\"The drawing board\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The mirror acts as the reflecting surface that changes the path of the light rays.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT listed as a material required for this activity?\",\"options\":[\"Mirror\",\"Comb\",\"Magnifying glass\",\"Torch light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The materials list includes torch light, mirror, stand, black paper, comb, white paper, and drawing board, but not a magnifying glass.\",\"topic\":\"Try this: Reflection of light\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the rays falling on any surface called?\",\"options\":[\"Reflected rays\",\"Normal rays\",\"Incident rays\",\"Perpendicular rays\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the rays falling on any surface are called incident rays.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The point at which an incident ray falls on a surface is known as the ____.\",\"options\":[\"Point of reflection\",\"Point of incidence\",\"Normal point\",\"Center point\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the point at which an incident ray falls is called the point of incidence.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which line represents the \'Normal\' to the mirror?\",\"options\":[\"Line PQ\",\"Ray AO\",\"Ray OB\",\"Line ON\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The figure and text identify line ON as the normal, which is perpendicular to the mirror line PQ.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/121_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the first law of reflection, if the angle of incidence is 35°, what is the angle of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"70°\",\"90°\",\"35°\",\"55°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The first law of reflection states that the angle of reflection is equal to the angle of incidence.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the measure of the angle ∠QON?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"90°\",\"180°\",\"0°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that since ON is perpendicular to PQ, ∠PON = ∠QON = 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/121_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the position of the incident ray and the reflected ray relative to the normal?\",\"options\":[\"They are on the same side of the normal\",\"They are on opposite sides of the normal\",\"They both lie along the normal\",\"They are parallel to the normal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The third law of reflection states that the incident ray and the reflected ray are on the opposite sides of the normal.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the angle between the incident ray and the normal called?\",\"options\":[\"Angle of reflection\",\"Angle of incidence\",\"Right angle\",\"Normal angle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines the angle ∠AON between the incident ray and the normal as the angle of incidence (i).\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle between the reflected ray and the normal is \'r\', what does \'r\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Angle of incidence\",\"Point of incidence\",\"Angle of reflection\",\"Normal ray\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines the angle ∠BON between the reflected ray and the normal as the angle of reflection (r).\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student draws a diagram where the incident ray, reflected ray, and normal are in different planes, which law of reflection are they violating?\",\"options\":[\"The first law\",\"The second law\",\"The third law\",\"The law of perpendiculars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The second law states that the incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal lie in the same plane.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"How many laws of reflection are mentioned in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states, \'There are three laws of reflection.\'\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the laws of reflection, which statement is true regarding the rays and the normal?\",\"options\":[\"The angle of incidence is always greater than the angle of reflection.\",\"The incident ray and normal are always the same line.\",\"The incident ray, reflected ray, and normal all lie in the same plane.\",\"The reflected ray is always perpendicular to the incident ray.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This is the second law of reflection as stated in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle of reflection (r) is measured to be 60°, what must be the value of the angle of incidence (i)?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"120°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the angle of reflection is equal to the angle of incidence, if r = 60°, then i must also be 60°.\",\"topic\":\"Laws of reflection\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What does the line PQ represent in the experiment described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"The incident ray\",\"The position of the mirror\",\"The normal line\",\"The reflected ray\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to step 2, line PQ is drawn to indicate the position of the mirror.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is specifically mentioned for measuring the angles in this activity?\",\"options\":[\"Scale\",\"Protractor\",\"Compass\",\"Divider\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The equipment list includes a protractor, which is used to measure angles like ∠TON.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name given to the line ON which is drawn perpendicular to PQ at point O?\",\"options\":[\"Incident ray\",\"Reflected ray\",\"Normal\",\"Mirror line\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In step 3, drawing a perpendicular ON to PQ establishes the normal for measuring angles.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the experiment, pins T and U are fixed along the line joining the ______.\",\"options\":[\"incident ray AO\",\"normal ON\",\"bottom of the reflected images\",\"drawing board edges\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 7 states that pins T and U are fixed along the line joining the bottom of the reflected images of pins S and R.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of the activity described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"To measure the length of a mirror\",\"To verify the laws of reflection\",\"To learn how to draw perpendicular lines\",\"To test the sharpness of pins\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text concludes that finding the angle of incidence equal to the angle of reflection verifies the laws of reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle of incidence is increased from 30° to 45°, what will happen to the angle of reflection if the experiment is done carefully?\",\"options\":[\"It remains 30°\",\"It decreases to 15°\",\"It increases to 45°\",\"It becomes 90°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The law of reflection states that the angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, if the angle marked as 30° is the angle of incidence, what is the measure of the angle of reflection ∠TON?\",\"options\":[\"60°\",\"30°\",\"90°\",\"0°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Since the angle of incidence is 30°, the angle of reflection ∠TON must also be 30° according to the laws of reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/121_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the observation table in the text, if the angle of incidence (∠i) is 60°, what should be recorded for the angle of reflection (∠r)?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"45°\",\"60°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection in all cases, including 60°.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which points are used to mark the path of the reflected ray after observing the mirror?\",\"options\":[\"S and R\",\"P and Q\",\"T and U\",\"A and O\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Points T and U are placed along the line of the reflected images to mark the reflected ray.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/121_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What can be inferred if a student finds the angle of incidence to be 30° but the angle of reflection to be 35° during this experiment?\",\"options\":[\"The laws of reflection have changed\",\"The experiment was not performed carefully\",\"The mirror was placed perfectly along PQ\",\"The protractor is not required for this experiment\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if the experiment is done carefully, the angles will be equal; therefore, inequality suggests a lack of care.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Where should the mirror be placed during the activity to ensure correct results?\",\"options\":[\"Along the ray AO\",\"Along the line ON\",\"Along the line PQ\",\"Along the line TU\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Step 6 specifies that the mirror should be placed along PQ, perpendicular to the drawing board.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The ray AO, which makes an angle of 30° with the normal ON, is known as the ______.\",\"options\":[\"Reflected ray\",\"Normal\",\"Incident ray\",\"Perpendicular ray\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ray AO is the ray directed towards the mirror, making it the incident ray.\",\"topic\":\"Verification of the laws of reflection\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the reflection of light from a plane and smooth surface called?\",\"options\":[\"Irregular reflection\",\"Regular reflection\",\"Perpendicular reflection\",\"Diffused reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines reflection from a plane and smooth surface as regular reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In regular reflection, if the angles of incidence for three parallel rays are i₁, i₂, and i₃, what is the relationship between them?\",\"options\":[\"i₁ ≠ i₂ ≠ i₃\",\"i₁ + i₂ = i₃\",\"i₁ = i₂ = i₃\",\"i₁ > i₂ > i₃\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"For regular reflection, the angles of incidence are the same for all parallel rays falling on the surface.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Reflection of light from a rough surface is known as:\",\"options\":[\"Regular reflection\",\"Smooth reflection\",\"Parallel reflection\",\"Irregular reflection\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text states that reflection from a rough surface is called irregular reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In irregular reflection, why are the reflected rays not parallel to one another?\",\"options\":[\"Because the laws of reflection are not followed\",\"Because the incident rays are not parallel\",\"Because the angles of incidence for parallel rays are not equal\",\"Because light rays change their speed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In irregular reflection, the angles of incidence for parallel rays are not equal, leading to unequal angles of reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true regarding the laws of reflection in irregular reflection?\",\"options\":[\"Laws of reflection are not followed at all\",\"Laws of reflection are followed only at certain points\",\"Laws of reflection are followed in both regular and irregular reflection\",\"Laws of reflection are followed only on smooth surfaces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The \'Always remember\' section states that laws of reflection are followed in both regular and irregular reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If three parallel rays are incident on a smooth surface with an angle of incidence of 30°, what will be the angle of reflection for the third ray (r₃)?\",\"options\":[\"10°\",\"30°\",\"60°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In regular reflection, i₁ = i₂ = i₃ = r₁ = r₂ = r₃, so if i = 30°, then r₃ = 30°.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In irregular reflection, if the angle of incidence at a specific point \'1\' is i₁, what is the corresponding angle of reflection r₁?\",\"options\":[\"i₁ = r₁\",\"i₁ ≠ r₁\",\"i₁ + r₁ = 90°\",\"i₁ = r₂\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that in irregular reflection, at any one point, the angles of incidence and reflection are equal (i₁=r₁).\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the reflected rays in an irregular reflection?\",\"options\":[\"They converge at a single point\",\"They remain parallel to each other\",\"They spread over a large surface\",\"They disappear into the surface\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that in irregular reflection, reflected rays are not parallel and spread over a large surface.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A student observes that parallel incident rays result in non-parallel reflected rays. What can be concluded about the surface?\",\"options\":[\"The surface is plane and smooth\",\"The surface is rough\",\"The laws of reflection are being violated\",\"The surface is a mirror\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Non-parallel reflected rays from parallel incident rays are a characteristic of reflection from a rough surface.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"On a rough surface, two parallel rays hit at different points. If i₁ = 25° and i₂ = 40°, what are the values of r₁ and r₂ respectively?\",\"options\":[\"r₁ = 25°, r₂ = 25°\",\"r₁ = 40°, r₂ = 40°\",\"r₁ = 25°, r₂ = 40°\",\"r₁ = 40°, r₂ = 25°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Even on rough surfaces, at any one point i = r, so r₁ = i₁ and r₂ = i₂.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which mathematical statement correctly describes regular reflection for multiple parallel rays?\",\"options\":[\"i₁ = i₂ = i₃ = r₁ = r₂ = r₃\",\"i₁ ≠ i₂ ≠ i₃\",\"i₁ = r₁, i₂ = r₂, but i₁ ≠ i₂\",\"i₁ + r₁ = i₂ + r₂\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly gives the formula i₁ = i₂ = i₃ = r₁ = r₂ = r₃ for regular reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, irregular reflection is obtained because:\",\"options\":[\"The laws of reflection are not followed\",\"The surface is rough\",\"The light rays are blue\",\"The angles of incidence are always zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text clarifies that irregular reflection is obtained because the surface is rough, not because laws are not followed.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If you are given a surface where i₁ ≠ i₂ for parallel incident rays, what type of reflection will occur?\",\"options\":[\"Regular reflection\",\"Irregular reflection\",\"Perpendicular reflection\",\"No reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The condition i₁ ≠ i₂ for parallel incident rays is specified for irregular reflection from a rough surface.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the \'Always remember\' section, which statement is FALSE?\",\"options\":[\"Laws of reflection are followed in irregular reflection\",\"In irregular reflection, angles of incidence at different points are different\",\"Irregular reflection occurs because laws of reflection are not followed\",\"At any one point in irregular reflection, i = r\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that irregular reflection is NOT obtained because laws are not followed, but because the surface is rough.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing Figure 16.4 (a) and (b) described in the text, what is the main difference between the reflected rays in (a) smooth and (b) rough surfaces?\",\"options\":[\"In (a) they are red, in (b) they are blue\",\"In (a) they are parallel, in (b) they are not parallel\",\"In (a) they follow laws, in (b) they do not\",\"In (a) they are incident, in (b) they are reflected\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text and figure description indicate that rays reflected from a smooth surface are parallel, while those from a rough surface are not.\",\"topic\":\"Regular and Irregular reflection\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How can you see the hair cut at the back of your head in a barber shop?\",\"options\":[\"By using a single plane mirror in front\",\"By using two mirrors facing each other\",\"By using a magnifying glass\",\"By using a transparent glass sheet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that to see the hair at the back, two mirrors are used so that the reflection of the back is reflected again to the front mirror.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the left and right sides of an object when seen in a mirror image?\",\"options\":[\"They remain in the same position\",\"They are interchanged\",\"They become invisible\",\"They move to the top and bottom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text asks what happens to the left and right sides, referring to the standard mirror property where they are interchanged.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why are we able to see the Moon?\",\"options\":[\"Because the Moon has its own light\",\"Because light falling on the Moon\'s surface is reflected\",\"Because the Moon is a star\",\"Because the Moon absorbs all sunlight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that light falling on the surface of the Moon is reflected, allowing us to see it.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"When the barber holds a mirror behind your head, the light from your hair is first reflected by which mirror?\",\"options\":[\"The mirror in front of you\",\"The mirror behind you\",\"The mirror on the ceiling\",\"The mirror on the side wall\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To see the back of the head, the light must first reflect off the mirror placed behind the head.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"The process of seeing the back of your head in a saloon is an example of which phenomenon?\",\"options\":[\"Refraction of light\",\"Absorption of light\",\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"Dispersion of light\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes how light is reflected by one mirror and then reflected again by another.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If you want to see an object that is hidden behind a corner using mirrors, which principle from the text would you apply?\",\"options\":[\"Single reflection\",\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"Lateral inversion only\",\"Light absorption\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Applying the concept of multiple reflections (like in a barber shop) allows seeing objects not in direct line of sight.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum number of mirrors required to see the reflection of a reflection as described in the barber shop example?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text implies the use of a second mirror to reflect the image formed by the first mirror.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what happens to the light that is reflected by the Moon?\",\"options\":[\"It disappears in space\",\"It can be reflected again by other surfaces\",\"It turns into heat immediately\",\"It travels only in one direction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that reflected light from the Moon is again reflected by other objects.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the correct sequence of light travel to see the back of the head in a saloon.\",\"options\":[\"Eye -> Front Mirror -> Back Mirror -> Hair\",\"Hair -> Back Mirror -> Front Mirror -> Eye\",\"Front Mirror -> Hair -> Back Mirror -> Eye\",\"Eye -> Hair -> Back Mirror -> Front Mirror\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Light travels from the object (hair) to the first mirror (back), then to the second mirror (front), and finally to the eye.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a characteristic of the image formed in a plane mirror as mentioned in the text\'s questions?\",\"options\":[\"The image is inverted vertically\",\"The image is blurred\",\"Left and right sides are interchanged\",\"The image is larger than the object\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifically asks \'What happens to the left and right sides?\', pointing to lateral inversion.\",\"topic\":\"Reflection of reflected light\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many rectangular mirrors of the same size are required to perform the Kaleidoscope activity described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\",\"Five\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states to take three rectangular mirrors of the same size for the activity.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the shape formed by the arrangement of the mirrors inside the tube?\",\"options\":[\"Square\",\"Rectangle\",\"Triangle\",\"Circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure and text show that the mirrors are stuck together making a triangle.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, the innumerable images seen in a Kaleidoscope are formed due to ______.\",\"options\":[\"refractions by the glass\",\"reflections by the three mirrors\",\"the hole in the paper\",\"the thickness of the cardboard\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that innumerable images are formed due to reflections by the three mirrors.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the speciality of a Kaleidoscope mentioned in the topic?\",\"options\":[\"The designs are always the same.\",\"The designs repeat every five minutes.\",\"The designs do not easily repeat themselves.\",\"The designs are only visible in the dark.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights that the speciality of a Kaleidoscope is that the designs do not easily repeat themselves.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the name of the outer cylindrical part that holds the mirrors?\",\"options\":[\"Glass tube\",\"Cardboard tube\",\"Metal pipe\",\"Plastic cap\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure labels the outer structure as a \'Cardboard tube\'.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/123_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following professionals would use a Kaleidoscope for making new designs for their work?\",\"options\":[\"Carpenters\",\"Cloth designers\",\"Mechanical engineers\",\"Astronomers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that cloth designers and wall paper makers use the Kaleidoscope for making new designs.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"To construct a working Kaleidoscope, how must the reflecting surfaces of the mirrors be positioned?\",\"options\":[\"Facing outwards\",\"Facing each other sideways\",\"Facing inwards\",\"Facing the cardboard tube\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions specify sticking the mirrors with the reflecting surface facing inwards.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What objects are placed in the hollow of the mirrors to create the patterns?\",\"options\":[\"4-5 coloured glass pieces\",\"3 rectangular mirrors\",\"Sticking tape\",\"White triangular paper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text says to insert 4-5 coloured glass pieces in the hollow of the mirrors.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If a wall paper maker needs a variety of unique patterns, why is the Kaleidoscope a suitable choice?\",\"options\":[\"It uses very cheap materials like cardboard.\",\"It creates a different design every time.\",\"It can only be used by professionals.\",\"It reflects only one image at a time.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that every time the design is different, which is why designers use it for new patterns.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of making a hole in the paper at one end of the Kaleidoscope?\",\"options\":[\"To let the glass pieces fall out\",\"To allow sticking tape to be applied\",\"To look through it towards light\",\"To let air inside the mirrors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to make a hole and look through it towards light to see the images.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A student builds a Kaleidoscope but forgets to put the coloured glass pieces inside. What will they likely see?\",\"options\":[\"Innumerable colourful designs\",\"No reflections at all\",\"Reflections of the empty hollow space\",\"A single repeated wall paper design\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Without the glass pieces, there are no objects to create the \'innumerable images of glass pieces\' mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the text suggest looking \'towards light\' while using the Kaleidoscope?\",\"options\":[\"To heat the glass pieces\",\"To make the mirrors transparent\",\"To illuminate the glass pieces for reflection\",\"To see the cardboard tube clearly\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Light is necessary to see the images formed by reflections of the glass pieces inside.\",\"topic\":\"Kaleidoscope\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of light is utilized in the working of a periscope?\",\"options\":[\"Refraction of light\",\"Reflection of light\",\"Absorption of light\",\"Dispersion of light\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that both Kaleidoscope and Periscope use the properties of reflection of light.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which device is specifically mentioned in the text for use in submarines to see objects above the water surface?\",\"options\":[\"Microscope\",\"Telescope\",\"Periscope\",\"Kaleidoscope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that a periscope is used in submarines to see objects above the surface of water.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the activity, at what angle should the mirrors be placed with the surfaces of the cardboard box?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"45°\",\"60°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions specify placing the two mirrors so that they make an angle of 45° with the surfaces of the box.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"How are the two mirrors in a periscope positioned relative to each other?\",\"options\":[\"Perpendicular to each other\",\"At a 60° angle to each other\",\"Parallel to each other\",\"Facing away from each other\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the two mirrors should be parallel to each other.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, how many times does the light ray reflect off a mirror before reaching the eye?\",\"options\":[\"One time\",\"Two times\",\"Three times\",\"Four times\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the light ray reflecting once at the top mirror and once at the bottom mirror, totaling two reflections.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/123_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the size of the windows to be made near the mirrors as per the activity?\",\"options\":[\"0.5 inch each\",\"1 inch each\",\"2 inches each\",\"3 inches each\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text instructs to make two windows of 1 inch each near the two mirrors.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A soldier needs to observe enemy movements on the ground while staying hidden in an underground bunker. Which device should he use?\",\"options\":[\"Kaleidoscope\",\"Magnifying glass\",\"Periscope\",\"Mirror\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the periscope is used to observe and keep a watch on objects or persons on the ground from an underground bunker.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"When looking through the bottom window of a periscope, what does the observer see?\",\"options\":[\"The inside of the cardboard box\",\"The reflection of their own eye\",\"What is in front of the top window\",\"The bottom surface of the box\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explains that from the bottom window, one can see what is in front of the top window.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Besides the periscope, which other device mentioned in the text uses the property of reflection of light?\",\"options\":[\"Microscope\",\"Stethoscope\",\"Kaleidoscope\",\"Telescope\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that Kaleidoscope and Periscope both use the properties of reflection of light.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, where is the periscope positioned to allow the crew to see?\",\"options\":[\"Completely under the water\",\"Extending above the surface of water\",\"At the bottom of the sea\",\"Inside the engine room\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The illustration shows the periscope tube extending from the submarine to a point above the water level.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/124_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"If you are building a periscope and you place the mirrors at an angle of 90° to the surface instead of 45°, what will likely happen?\",\"options\":[\"The light will reflect better\",\"The device will not function as a periscope\",\"The image will be colorful like a kaleidoscope\",\"The windows will need to be larger\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The specific 45° angle is required for the parallel reflection path; changing it would prevent the light from traveling from the top window to the bottom window.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the total number of mirrors required to construct the periscope described in the activity?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The activity instructions specify to \'place the two mirrors\' in the box.\",\"topic\":\"Periscope\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the law of reflection, what is the relationship between the angle of incidence (∠i) and the angle of reflection (∠r)?\",\"options\":[\"∠i is greater than ∠r\",\"∠i is less than ∠r\",\"∠i is equal to ∠r\",\"∠i is double the value of ∠r\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that according to the law of reflection, ∠i = ∠r.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the solved examples, what does the line \'ON\' represent?\",\"options\":[\"Incident ray\",\"Reflected ray\",\"Mirror surface\",\"Normal ray\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Example 3 explicitly defines line ON as the normal ray.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the measure of the angle between the normal (line ON) and the plane mirror (line PQ)?\",\"options\":[\"0°\",\"45°\",\"90°\",\"180°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 3 states that ∠PON = 90° because it is the normal.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the reflected ray makes an angle of 60° with the normal, what is the angle of reflection (∠r)?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"120°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 1 identifies the angle the reflected ray makes with the normal as the angle of reflection (∠r = 60°).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In Example 3, which ray is identified as the incident ray?\",\"options\":[\"Ray AO\",\"Ray OB\",\"Line PQ\",\"Line ON\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Example 3 states: ray AO = incident ray.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is 90°, what is the value of 2 ∠i?\",\"options\":[\"45°\",\"90°\",\"180°\",\"35°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 2 shows that since ∠i + ∠r = 90° and ∠i = ∠r, then 2 ∠i = 90°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle between the plane mirror and the incident ray is 35°, how is the angle of incidence calculated?\",\"options\":[\"90° + 35°\",\"35° \\/ 2\",\"90° - 35°\",\"180° - 35°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Example 3 shows that ∠AON = 90° - ∠POA, which is 90° - 35°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does ∠i = 45° when the total angle between the incident and reflected rays is 90°?\",\"options\":[\"Because the mirror is at a 45° angle\",\"Because the law of reflection states ∠i = ∠r\",\"Because the normal is at 90°\",\"Because the incident ray is 90°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 2 explains that ∠i + ∠r = 90° and since ∠i = ∠r, each must be 45°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In Example 4, if ∠NOB is the angle of reflection and ∠QON is the normal, what does ∠QOB represent?\",\"options\":[\"The angle of incidence\",\"The angle between the reflected ray and the mirror\",\"The angle between the incident ray and the mirror\",\"The total angle of reflection\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Example 4 concludes that ∠QOB is the angle the reflected ray makes with the mirror.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle of incidence is 55°, what is the angle of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"35°\",\"45°\",\"55°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the law of reflection mentioned in Example 3, ∠i = ∠r, so if ∠i = 55°, then ∠r = 55°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If the reflected ray makes an angle of 30° with the normal, what angle must the incident ray make with the normal?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"60°\",\"90°\",\"15°\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Following the logic of Example 1, if ∠r = 30°, then ∠i = 30° due to the law of reflection.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is 80°, what is the angle of incidence?\",\"options\":[\"80°\",\"160°\",\"40°\",\"20°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the method in Example 2: ∠i + ∠r = 80°, and since ∠i = ∠r, 2∠i = 80°, so ∠i = 40°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle between the plane mirror and the incident ray is 50°, what is the angle of reflection?\",\"options\":[\"50°\",\"40°\",\"90°\",\"100°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using the method in Example 3: ∠i = 90° - 50° = 40°. Since ∠i = ∠r, ∠r = 40°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If the angle of incidence is 25°, what angle will the reflected ray make with the mirror?\",\"options\":[\"25°\",\"50°\",\"65°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using the method in Example 4: ∠r = 25°. Angle with mirror = 90° - 25° = 65°.\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In Example 4, if the angle of incidence is 40°, what is the sum of the angle of reflection and the angle the reflected ray makes with the mirror?\",\"options\":[\"40°\",\"50°\",\"80°\",\"90°\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Example 4 shows ∠NOB (40°) + ∠QOB (50°) = ∠QON (90°).\",\"topic\":\"Solved Example\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22167,57,8,'Science','en','Man made Materials','[\"Manmade materials are those produced by humans from natural or synthetic substances.\",\"Examples: glass, plastic, thermocol (polystyrene), artificial threads, rubber, metal alloys.\",\"Plastic is a polymer made of long chains of molecules; it can be linear or branched.\",\"Thermoplastic can be reshaped when heated and re-solidified when cooled (e.g., PVC, PS, PE, PP).\",\"Thermosetting plastic hardens permanently after the first heat treatment; it cannot be remolded (e.g., Bakelite, melamine).\",\"Properties of plastic: non‑corrosive, non‑decomposing, light, mouldable, poor conductor of heat and electricity.\",\"Thermocol (polystyrene) melts above 100 °C and returns to solid on cooling; used for packaging and disposable plates.\",\"Glass is a non‑crystalline solid made mainly of silica (SiO₂) with additives; it is transparent, brittle, and a good electrical insulator.\",\"Types of glass: silica, borosilicate, alkali‑silicate, lead, optical, coloured, processed.\",\"Soda‑lime glass is the most common type; it is made by melting sand, soda, and lime at ~1500 °C.\",\"Glass is non‑degradable and can release harmful gases if burned; it is recyclable but takes long to degrade naturally.\",\"Environmentally, plastics are often non‑degradable; biodegradable alternatives include jute, cotton, paper bags, and certain plastics like biodegradable polymers.\",\"The 4 R principle: Reduce, Reuse, Recycle, Recover – helps minimise plastic waste.\",\"Thermosetting plastics are used where permanent strength is needed (e.g., electrical switches, radio cabinets).\",\"Thermoplastics are used for items that need moulding and re‑use (e.g., bottles, toys).\",\"Thermocol is a form of polystyrene; it is a good shock absorber but can release carcinogenic styrene if heated.\",\"Glass can be coloured by adding metal oxides (e.g., copper for red glass, iron for green).\",\"In vehicles, safety glass (tempered or laminated) is used to prevent injury in accidents.\",\"Plastic production involves polymerisation of monomers, extrusion, moulding, and sometimes additive blending.\",\"Glass production involves melting raw materials, controlling temperature, and rapid cooling to avoid crystallisation.\",\"Both plastic and glass can be recycled; recycling reduces raw material consumption and pollution.\",\"Proper disposal of plastic and glass reduces environmental impact and protects human health.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is explicitly mentioned as a natural material in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Glass\",\"Plastic\",\"Rock\",\"Thermocol\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that wood, rock, minerals, and water are natural materials.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, where does human research on natural materials take place?\",\"options\":[\"In factories\",\"In laboratories\",\"In schools\",\"In forests\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that humans performed research on various natural materials in laboratories.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a manmade material according to the introduction?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Minerals\",\"Soil\",\"Artificial threads\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists glass, plastic, artificial threads, and thermocol as examples of manmade materials.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What name is given to materials that are manufactured in factories with the help of laboratory research?\",\"options\":[\"Natural materials\",\"Manmade materials\",\"Raw materials\",\"Biological materials\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines materials manufactured in factories following research on natural materials as manmade materials.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Where are manmade materials manufactured after research is completed?\",\"options\":[\"In laboratories\",\"In nature\",\"In factories\",\"In soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that with the help of research, various materials are manufactured in factories.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what is the source used by humans to create manmade materials?\",\"options\":[\"Natural materials\",\"Only artificial threads\",\"Only thermocol\",\"Only glass\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text explains that research is performed on natural materials to manufacture manmade materials.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a student finds a piece of \'Glass\' at home, how should it be classified based on the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"As a natural material\",\"As a manmade material\",\"As a mineral\",\"As a rock\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists glass as an example of a manmade material.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following materials would NOT be included in the list of natural materials provided in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Wood\",\"Water\",\"Plastic\",\"Minerals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plastic is listed as a manmade material, whereas wood, water, and minerals are listed as natural.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the relationship between natural materials and manmade materials according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Manmade materials are found inside natural rocks.\",\"Natural materials are manufactured in factories using manmade materials.\",\"Manmade materials are produced by researching and processing natural materials.\",\"There is no relationship between natural and manmade materials.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text describes manmade materials as being manufactured in factories based on research performed on natural materials.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Why is thermocol classified differently than wood in this chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Because thermocol is found in water and wood is not.\",\"Because thermocol is manufactured in factories while wood is natural.\",\"Because wood is used in schools and thermocol is not.\",\"Because thermocol is a mineral and wood is a metal.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text classifies wood as natural and thermocol as a manmade material manufactured in factories.\",\"topic\":\"Introduction\",\"part\":1,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Plastic is a manmade material made up of which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Inorganic polymers\",\"Organic polymers\",\"Natural fibers\",\"Metallic crystals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines plastic as a manmade material made up of organic polymers.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two possible structures of plastic mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Linear and circular\",\"Square and triangular\",\"Hexagonal and pentagonal\",\"Straight and branched\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that some plastic structures are linear while some are circular.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"On what basis is plastic classified into two main types?\",\"options\":[\"Based on their color\",\"Based on their weight\",\"Based on the effect of heat\",\"Based on their price\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, plastic is classified into two types depending upon the effect of heat.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the defining characteristic of thermoplastic?\",\"options\":[\"It cannot be molded into any shape\",\"It can be molded as per our wish\",\"It is only found in nature\",\"Its shape remains fixed even when heated\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines thermoplastic as plastic that can be molded as per our wish.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of thermoplastic used for making toys and combs?\",\"options\":[\"Electric switches\",\"Cooker handles\",\"Polyethene\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text lists Polyethene and PVC as examples of thermoplastic used for toys, combs, etc.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is thermosetting plastic used for making coverings over the handles of cookers?\",\"options\":[\"Because it melts easily when the cooker gets hot\",\"Because it can be reshaped every time it is heated\",\"Because its shape cannot be changed on heating once it is molded\",\"Because it is a natural material like wood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Thermosetting plastic is used because once molded, its shape cannot be changed on heating.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A manufacturer wants to create a plastic bowl that can be melted down and recycled into a plate. Which type of plastic should they use?\",\"options\":[\"Thermosetting plastic\",\"Thermoplastic\",\"Natural polymer\",\"Circular plastic only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Thermoplastics can be molded as per wish, implying they can be reshaped, unlike thermosetting plastics.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what common category of manmade materials is represented by the collection of buckets, bowls, and toys?\",\"options\":[\"Metallic materials\",\"Natural materials\",\"Plastic material\",\"Wooden material\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The caption for the figure identifies these items as \'Plastic material\'.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/126_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"The items shown in the given figure, including the yellow helmet and orange parts, belong to which category because they can be molded as per our wish?\",\"options\":[\"Thermosetting plastic\",\"Thermoplastic\",\"Natural organic polymer\",\"Non-moldable plastic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The figure is captioned \'Thermoplastic\', which the text describes as plastic that can be molded as per our wish.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/126_103.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, items like electric switches and iron components are shown. Why are these classified as the type of plastic mentioned in the caption?\",\"options\":[\"They are made of Polyethene and PVC\",\"They can be easily melted to change their shape\",\"Their shape cannot be changed once they are molded, even on heating\",\"They have a linear structure that allows for flexibility\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows \'Thermosetting plastic\', which by definition cannot change shape on heating once molded.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/126_104.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a property of plastic as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It shows the property of plasticity\",\"It is made of organic polymers\",\"All plastics have the exact same structure\",\"It can be classified based on the effect of heat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that the \'Structure of all the plastics is not same\'.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a technician is repairing an electric switch, why must they be careful not to expect the plastic casing to soften and bend when heated?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a thermoplastic like PVC\",\"Because it is a thermosetting plastic whose shape is permanent\",\"Because it is a natural material that burns immediately\",\"Because it has a linear structure that makes it brittle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Electric switches are examples of thermosetting plastic, which does not change shape on heating.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic property of plastic mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"It corrodes easily\",\"It decomposes quickly\",\"It is not easily affected by humidity\",\"It is a good conductor of electricity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that plastic does not corrode, does not decompose, and is not easily affected by humidity.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Plastic can be molded into any shape. Which specific property allows this to happen?\",\"options\":[\"Conductivity\",\"Plasticity\",\"Corrosion\",\"Decomposition\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, plastic can be molded into any shape due to the property of plasticity.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why is plastic considered easy to carry for daily use?\",\"options\":[\"It is a bad conductor\",\"It is light in weight\",\"It is affected by rain\",\"It cannot be colored\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that being light in weight, it is easy to carry.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, which plastic is used to make milk bags and flexible garden pipes?\",\"options\":[\"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)\",\"Polystyrene (PS)\",\"Polyethylene (PE)\",\"Polypropylene (PP)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Polyethylene (PE) is listed as the material for milk bags and flexible garden pipes.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of plastic is suitable for making thermo-insulating parts of refrigerators and gears of machines?\",\"options\":[\"Polystyrene (PS)\",\"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)\",\"Polyurethane\",\"Bakelite\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Polystyrene (PS) is used for thermo-insulating parts of refrigerators and gears of machines.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which plastic would be selected to manufacture coverings over handles of cookers to ensure safety from heat?\",\"options\":[\"Melamine\",\"Polyester\",\"Bakelite\",\"Polypropylene\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Bakelite is used for coverings over handles of cookers as it is a thermosetting plastic.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"According to the classification in the text, which of the following is a Thermosetting plastic?\",\"options\":[\"Polyvinyl chloride\",\"Polypropylene\",\"Polystyrene\",\"Polyurethane\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Polyurethane is listed under the category of Thermosetting plastic.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which plastic is used in the textile industry and for making fiber glass?\",\"options\":[\"Polyester\",\"Bakelite\",\"Polyethylene\",\"Melamine\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Polyester is used for fiber glass and in the textile industry.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is plastic used for electric cable insulation?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is a good conductor of heat\",\"Because it is a bad conductor of electricity\",\"Because it decomposes easily\",\"Because it is heavy in weight\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plastic is a bad conductor of electricity, making it ideal for insulation.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific item is listed as a use for Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)?\",\"options\":[\"Milk bags\",\"Laboratory appliances\",\"Electric cable insulation\",\"Gears of machines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table for Thermoplastics shows PVC is used for electric cable insulation.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/127_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which plastic is used to make spare parts of airplane engines?\",\"options\":[\"Bakelite\",\"Melamine\",\"Polyurethane\",\"Polyester\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table for Thermosetting plastic lists Melamine for spare parts of airplane engines.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/127_102.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"A company wants to produce laboratory appliances and mattresses. Which plastic should they choose according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Polypropylene (PP)\",\"Polyethylene (PE)\",\"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)\",\"Polystyrene (PS)\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Polypropylene (PP) is used for mattresses and laboratory appliances.\",\"topic\":\"Properties and Types of Plastic\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which material has a degradation period of 1 to 2 weeks?\",\"options\":[\"Vegetable\",\"Cotton cloth\",\"Wood\",\"Plastic\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table shows that vegetables have a degradation period of 1 to 2 weeks.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/128_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Materials that are naturally degraded by the environment are called as ______.\",\"options\":[\"Degradable materials\",\"Non-degradable materials\",\"Synthetic materials\",\"Recycled materials\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines materials that are naturally degraded as degradable materials.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is plastic considered an environment pollutant according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is used in