han 2000 different types of plastics.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the provided table, what is the degradation period of wood?\",\"options\":[\"10 - 15 years\",\"1 year\",\"1 - 2 weeks\",\"Thousands of years\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The table lists the degradation period of wood as 10 to 15 years.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the chart, which material takes the longest time to degrade, making it the most harmful to the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Vegetable\",\"Cotton cloth\",\"Wood\",\"Plastic\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Plastic takes thousands of years to degrade, which is the longest period in the chart.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"To avoid environmental pollution, which type of material should we opt for when buying household items?\",\"options\":[\"Non-degradable material\",\"Degradable material\",\"Synthetic material\",\"Metallic material\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text advises opting for items made up of degradable material instead of plastic.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the provided chart, which material is the most environment-friendly due to its very short degradation period?\",\"options\":[\"Vegetable\",\"Cotton cloth\",\"Wood\",\"Plastic\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Vegetables degrade in 1-2 weeks, the shortest time listed, making them the most environment-friendly option.\",\"topic\":\"Plastic and environment\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"According to the textbook, how many different types of plastics are there?\",\"options\":[\"Less than 1000\",\"Exactly 2000\",\"More than 2000\",\"Approximately 500\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there are more than 2000 different types of plastics.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of plastic is used for manufacturing artificial teeth and lenses?\",\"options\":[\"Teflon\",\"Polyacrylic\",\"Polyester\",\"PVC\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Polyacrylic is specifically mentioned as the plastic used for lenses and artificial teeth.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the degradation period of cotton cloth as mentioned in the table?\",\"options\":[\"1 - 2 weeks\",\"10 - 15 years\",\"1 year\",\"Thousands of years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The table specifies the degradation period of cotton cloth as 1 year.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Vessels used to cook food in which appliance are made up of plastic?\",\"options\":[\"Gas stove\",\"Microwave oven\",\"Solar cooker\",\"Electric heater\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that vessels used to cook food in microwave ovens are made up of plastic.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are vehicles coated with Teflon?\",\"options\":[\"To make them look shiny\",\"To protect them from scratches\",\"To increase their speed\",\"To make them waterproof\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Teflon coating is used on vehicles to protect them from scratches.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the 4R principle, what does the term \'Recover\' stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Reclaiming\",\"Repairing\",\"Replacing\",\"Removing\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text defines Recover as \'Reclaiming\' within the 4R principle.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A doctor uses a plastic syringe to give an injection. This is an example of plastic use in which sector?\",\"options\":[\"Aviation sector\",\"Construction sector\",\"Healthcare sector\",\"Manufacturing sector\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions syringes as an example of plastic use in the healthcare sector.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a factory processes old plastic items to create new products, which \'R\' of the 4R principle is being followed?\",\"options\":[\"Reduce\",\"Reuse\",\"Recycle\",\"Recover\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Recycle is defined as \'Use again after processing\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing the degradation periods, which of the following materials decomposes faster than wood but slower than vegetables?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic\",\"Cotton cloth\",\"Teflon\",\"Polyacrylic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cotton cloth (1 year) is faster than wood (10-15 years) and slower than vegetables (1-2 weeks).\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the table, why is plastic classified as a \'Non-degradable\' material?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is man-made\",\"Because it takes thousands of years to degrade\",\"Because it is used in aeroplanes\",\"Because it can be recycled\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plastic is non-degradable because its degradation period lasts thousands of years.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the ultimate goal for a responsible citizen to follow the 4R principle?\",\"options\":[\"To increase plastic production\",\"To save the environment from pollution\",\"To reduce the cost of vehicles\",\"To manufacture more artificial teeth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that following the 4R principle is necessary to save the environment from pollution.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"A student decides to use a cloth bag instead of multiple plastic bags to ensure \'Minimal use\'. Which part of the 4R principle does this represent?\",\"options\":[\"Reduce\",\"Reuse\",\"Recycle\",\"Recover\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Reduce is defined in the text as \'Minimal use\'.\",\"topic\":\"Do you know?\",\"part\":6,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical name of the complex material that makes up thermocol?\",\"options\":[\"Polyethylene\",\"Polystyrene\",\"Polyvinyl chloride\",\"Polypropylene\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that thermocol is a form of a complex material called polystyrene.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what temperature does thermocol transform into a liquid state?\",\"options\":[\"Less than 50 °C\",\"Exactly 75 °C\",\"More than 100 °C\",\"Exactly 0 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, it transforms into a liquid state on heating at more than 100 °C temperature.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is thermocol used for packing delicate items like glass?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is very heavy\",\"Because it is a good shock-absorber\",\"Because it is a liquid at room temperature\",\"Because it is very expensive\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that being a good shock-absorber, it is used in packing of delicate items.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to thermocol when it is cooled after being heated to a liquid state?\",\"options\":[\"It evaporates into gas\",\"It remains in a liquid state\",\"It returns to a solid state\",\"It turns into glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that it returns to a solid state on cooling.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a manufacturer wants to create a custom-shaped protective casing for a new camera, why is thermocol a suitable choice?\",\"options\":[\"Because it cannot be shaped easily\",\"Because it can be given any desired shape by heating and cooling\",\"Because it is made of glass\",\"Because it stays liquid at room temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because it changes states with temperature, we can give any desired shape to it.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A catering service needs lightweight, disposable plates for a mass feast. Which material mentioned in the text is commonly used for this?\",\"options\":[\"Glass\",\"Thermocol\",\"Metal\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text notes that nowadays, the plates used in mass feasts are also made up of thermocol.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the properties described, which characteristic of thermocol is most critical for transporting fragile electronics?\",\"options\":[\"Its white color\",\"Its ability to absorb shocks\",\"Its ability to dissolve in water\",\"Its liquid state at 50 °C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shock absorption is the property that prevents items from breaking during handling.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the state change of polystyrene mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Solid to gas on heating\",\"Liquid to solid on heating\",\"Solid to liquid on heating at > 100 °C\",\"Liquid to gas on cooling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text specifies it transforms to a liquid state on heating at more than 100 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is thermocol described as a versatile material for packaging?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is always a liquid\",\"Because it is very hard to break\",\"Because it can be shaped by heating and protects items by absorbing shocks\",\"Because it is naturally found in the shape of plates\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text highlights both its ability to be shaped and its shock-absorbing properties.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a piece of thermocol is heated to 110 °C and then poured into a star-shaped mold and cooled, what will be the result?\",\"options\":[\"It will stay liquid forever\",\"It will evaporate completely\",\"It will become a solid star-shaped object\",\"It will turn into a glass-like material\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says it returns to a solid state on cooling, allowing it to take any desired shape.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best justifies the use of thermocol in the packaging industry for glass items?\",\"options\":[\"It is a complex material that is difficult to break\",\"It acts as a shock-absorber to prevent items from breaking during transport\",\"It is used in mass feasts\",\"It only exists in a solid state at all temperatures\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The primary justification for packaging is its shock-absorbing property to prevent breakage.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of wrapping thermocol around items during transport?\",\"options\":[\"To make the box look full\",\"To prevent the items from breaking\",\"To keep the items warm\",\"To increase the weight of the box\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states it is wrapped around items to prevent them from breaking while handling.\",\"topic\":\"Thermocol\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which carcinogenic ingredient is present in thermocol?\",\"options\":[\"Methane\",\"Styrene\",\"Propane\",\"Ethane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, styrene is the carcinogenic ingredient found in thermocol.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Long-term contact with thermocol may lead to which specific type of blood cancer?\",\"options\":[\"Carcinoma\",\"Sarcoma\",\"Leukemia and lymphoma\",\"Melanoma\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that long duration contact may lead to blood cancer like leukemia and lymphoma.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why do many people choose to burn thermocol despite it being hazardous?\",\"options\":[\"It releases oxygen\",\"It is biodegradable\",\"It takes a long duration for natural degradation\",\"It turns into useful compost\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because thermocol is non-biodegradable and takes a long time to degrade naturally, people opt to burn it.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary environmental hazard of burning thermocol?\",\"options\":[\"It produces water vapor\",\"It releases poisonous gases into the atmosphere\",\"It increases soil fertility\",\"It cools the surrounding air\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Burning thermocol is hazardous because it releases poisonous gases into the atmosphere.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the styrene in a thermocol cup if the food inside it is reheated?\",\"options\":[\"It evaporates completely\",\"It turns into a solid crust\",\"It may dissolve in the food\",\"It changes the food into vitamins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that if food kept in thermocol is reheated, styrene may dissolve in the food.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a specific risk for pregnant women working in a thermocol factory?\",\"options\":[\"High blood pressure\",\"Miscarriage\",\"Diabetes\",\"Vitamin deficiency\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that pregnant women working in thermocol factories may face miscarriage.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the immediate effect of liquid styrene coming into contact with human skin?\",\"options\":[\"Skin-burns\",\"Improved skin texture\",\"Immediate tanning\",\"No effect\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"According to the text, liquid styrene may cause skin-burns.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the use of thermocol plates and cups in mass gatherings considered a health concern?\",\"options\":[\"They are too expensive\",\"They make the food taste better\",\"They affect health through potential chemical leaching\",\"They are too heavy to carry\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text notes that using thermocol plates and cups in mass gatherings affects health, especially if food is reheated.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which organ system is NOT mentioned in the text as being affected by long-term contact in a thermocol factory?\",\"options\":[\"Respiratory system\",\"Digestive system\",\"Skeletal system\",\"Skin\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions eyes, respiratory, skin, and digestive systems, but does not mention the skeletal system.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"If a person works in a thermocol factory for many years, which respiratory problem are they likely to develop?\",\"options\":[\"Problems of the respiratory system\",\"Increased lung capacity\",\"Better oxygen absorption\",\"Resistance to poisonous gases\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states that persons in long-term contact may develop problems of the respiratory system.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A catering company decides to stop reheating food in thermocol containers. Based on the text, what health risk are they trying to avoid?\",\"options\":[\"Food poisoning from bacteria\",\"Styrene dissolving into the food\",\"The food becoming too cold\",\"The container becoming too strong\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Reheating food in thermocol causes styrene to dissolve, which the company would want to avoid for health reasons.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best evaluates the \'non-biodegradable\' nature of thermocol based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"It is good for the environment as it lasts forever\",\"It is easily destroyed by natural processes\",\"It creates a dilemma where both natural degradation and burning are problematic\",\"It should be burned to save the atmosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text shows that natural degradation takes too long and burning releases poisonous gases, making both options problematic.\",\"topic\":\"Adverse effects of thermocol\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Who is credited with the accidental discovery of glass while cooking in the desert?\",\"options\":[\"Egyptian traders\",\"Phoenician traders\",\"Greek scientists\",\"Roman merchants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that glass was discovered by chance by Phoenician traders cooking in the desert.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical formula for silica, which is commonly referred to as sand?\",\"options\":[\"Si\",\"SiO\",\"SiO₂\",\"Si₂O\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly defines silica as SiO₂.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the physical nature of glass as a material?\",\"options\":[\"Crystalline and soft\",\"Non-crystalline and brittle\",\"Crystalline and hard\",\"Non-crystalline and flexible\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines glass as a non-crystalline, hard but brittle solid material.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"According to the Phoenician traders\' observation, which two materials reacted under heat to form glass?\",\"options\":[\"Sand and water\",\"Lime-stone and wood\",\"Sand and lime-stone\",\"Silica and silicate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The traders thought the transparent material formed due to heating together sand and lime-stone.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a factory needs to produce \'boro-silicate glass\' instead of \'soda-lime glass\', what must they change according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"The temperature of the furnace\",\"The speed of the rollers\",\"The proportion of silica and other components\",\"The type of fuel used for heating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that different types of glass depend upon the proportion of silica and other components.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of glass makes it likely to break into pieces when subjected to stress?\",\"options\":[\"Hardness\",\"Transparency\",\"Brittleness\",\"Non-crystallinity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Brittleness is the property that causes a hard material to break easily.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the discovery story, what was the primary source of heat that led to the formation of the first glass-like material?\",\"options\":[\"Solar energy in the desert\",\"Cooking fires\",\"Chemical reactions of lime-stone\",\"Volcanic activity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The traders were cooking in the desert, implying the heat came from their cooking fire.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT mentioned in the text as a specific type of glass?\",\"options\":[\"Soda-lime glass\",\"Boro-silicate glass\",\"Lead-crystal glass\",\"Alkali-silicate glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Lead-crystal glass is not listed among the types (soda-lime, boro-silicate, silica, alkali-silicate) in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist wants to create a new type of glass. Based on the definition provided, which two substances must be mixed?\",\"options\":[\"Sand and lime-stone\",\"Silica and silicate\",\"Soda and lime\",\"Silica and boron\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines glass as a material formed from a mixture of silica and silicate.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of the Phoenician traders\' discovery, what role did the lime-stones play?\",\"options\":[\"They acted as a cooling agent\",\"They were used as fuel for the fire\",\"They acted as a support and a raw material\",\"They were used to clean the cooking vessels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The vessels were supported on lime-stones, which then reacted with sand to form glass.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the first component introduced into the system to begin the glass manufacturing process?\",\"options\":[\"Molten glass\",\"Glass strip\",\"Raw material\",\"Roller\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows \'raw material\' being poured into the start of the process at the furnace.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/129_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is the specific function of the rollers in the production line?\",\"options\":[\"To heat the glass to a molten state\",\"To cut the glass into specific lengths\",\"To flatten the molten glass into a continuous strip\",\"To mix the raw materials together\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows the molten glass passing through rollers to become a flat \'glass strip\'.\",\"topic\":\"Glass\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/129_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What is the chemical name for sand, which is a primary ingredient in glass production?\",\"options\":[\"Magnesium oxide\",\"Silicon dioxide\",\"Sodium carbonate\",\"Calcium oxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that sand is silicon dioxide.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"At what temperature does pure silicon dioxide melt?\",\"options\":[\"850 °C\",\"1500 °C\",\"1700 °C\",\"2000 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, sand i.e. silicon dioxide melts at 1700 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why are pieces of discarded glass added to the mixture of sand, soda, and lime?\",\"options\":[\"To make the glass more transparent\",\"To increase the density of the glass\",\"To melt the mixture at a lower temperature\",\"To prevent the glass from cracking\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Discarded glass is added so as to melt the mixture at a low temperature (850 °C).\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is the result of the sudden cooling of the liquified glass mixture?\",\"options\":[\"The mixture becomes crystalline\",\"The mixture becomes homogenous, amorphous and transparent\",\"The mixture turns into pure silicon dioxide\",\"The mixture becomes a good conductor of heat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sudden cooling ensures the mixture becomes homogenous, amorphous, and transparent instead of crystalline.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"A manufacturer wants to lower the melting point of the glass raw materials from 1700 °C to 850 °C. Which action should they take?\",\"options\":[\"Add more magnesium oxide\",\"Add pieces of discarded glass\",\"Increase the furnace temperature to 1500 °C\",\"Remove the lime from the mixture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that adding discarded glass pieces allows the mixture to melt at 850 °C.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a cold glass object often crack if it is heated very quickly?\",\"options\":[\"Because glass is a bad conductor of electricity\",\"Because glass is a slow conductor of heat\",\"Because glass is amorphous\",\"Because glass contains magnesium oxide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Glass is a slow conductor of heat; quick heating or cooling causes it to crack.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which property of glass makes it suitable for use as an insulator in electrical appliances?\",\"options\":[\"It is transparent to light\",\"It is a slow conductor of heat\",\"It is a bad conductor of electricity\",\"It becomes soft on heating\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states glass is used as an insulator because it is a bad conductor of electricity.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a piece of glass appears to absorb a large amount of light instead of being fully transparent, which substance might be present in it?\",\"options\":[\"Silicon dioxide\",\"Magnesium oxide\",\"Oxide of Chromium\",\"Sodium carbonate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Oxides of chromium, vanadium, or iron cause large amounts of light to be absorbed.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the density of a specific piece of glass?\",\"options\":[\"The temperature of the furnace\",\"The speed of cooling\",\"The ingredients used in the mixture\",\"The shape into which it is moulded\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the density of glass depends upon its ingredients.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the production of Soda-lime glass, what is the final temperature the liquified mixture reaches before sudden cooling?\",\"options\":[\"850 °C\",\"1700 °C\",\"1500 °C\",\"1000 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Once liquified, the mixture is heated up to 1500 °C and then immediately cooled.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a primary ingredient for glass production in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Sand\",\"Soda\",\"Lime\",\"Iron oxide\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Sand, soda, lime, and magnesium oxide are the primary ingredients; iron oxide is mentioned only regarding light absorption.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which characteristic allows glass to be shaped into various objects like bottles or bulbs?\",\"options\":[\"It is transparent\",\"It becomes soft on heating\",\"It is an amorphous solid\",\"It has a high density\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that on heating, glass becomes soft and can be moulded into any shape.\",\"topic\":\"Production and Properties of Glass\",\"part\":10,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of glass is also known as \'water glass\' because it is soluble in water?\",\"options\":[\"Silica glass\",\"Alkali silicate glass\",\"Lead glass\",\"Optical glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Alkali silicate glass is produced by heating sand and soda and is soluble in water, hence called \'water glass\'.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following gases is NOT mentioned in the text as a greenhouse gas released during glass production?\",\"options\":[\"Sulphur dioxide\",\"Nitrogen dioxide\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Carbon monoxide\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text mentions sulphur dioxide, nitrogen dioxide, and carbon dioxide as greenhouse gases released during production.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Why is silica glass specifically used to produce laboratory glass-wares?\",\"options\":[\"It is soluble in water\",\"It is very cheap to manufacture\",\"It shows minimum expansion on heating and is not affected by acid or alkali\",\"It is naturally red in colour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Silica glass is resistant to chemicals (acid\\/alkali) and has minimum thermal expansion, making it ideal for labs.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Bottles made of borosilicate glass are used in the pharmaceutical industry because:\",\"options\":[\"They are the most transparent type of glass\",\"They do not show any effect on medicines stored in them\",\"They are made of pure lead oxide\",\"They are easily degradable in the environment\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Borosilicate glass does not react with medicines, ensuring the safety of pharmaceutical products.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a manufacturer wants to produce red-coloured glass, which substance should be mixed during the manufacturing process?\",\"options\":[\"Ferrous oxide\",\"Barium oxide\",\"Copper oxide\",\"Boric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"According to the text, copper oxide is mixed to get red glass.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"A scientist needs to design a high-quality lens for a new microscope. Which type of glass is most suitable for this purpose?\",\"options\":[\"Alkali silicate glass\",\"Optical glass\",\"Processed glass\",\"Silica glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Optical glass is pure and specifically used for production of spectacles, lenses, and microscopic lenses.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"What is the environmental impact of glass pieces flowing into a water body?\",\"options\":[\"They dissolve and provide nutrients to fish\",\"They help in the degradation of other waste\",\"They may affect the ecosystem as glass is non-degradable\",\"They increase the temperature of the water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Glass is non-degradable, so its presence in water bodies can disrupt the ecosystem.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"During the production of glass, the mixture needs to be heated up to which temperature?\",\"options\":[\"500 °C\",\"1000 °C\",\"1500 °C\",\"2000 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that the mixture needs to be heated up to 1500 °C during glass production.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What property of lead glass makes it suitable for manufacturing light bulbs and tubes?\",\"options\":[\"It is soluble in water\",\"It is very clear and transparent\",\"It is bluish green in colour\",\"It is reinforced with fiber\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lead glass is very clear and transparent, which is necessary for light bulbs and tubes.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which specific oxide is added to the mixture of sand, soda, and limestone to produce lead glass?\",\"options\":[\"Lead oxide\",\"Barium oxide\",\"Aluminium oxide\",\"Ferrous oxide\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lead glass is produced by melting a mixture that includes lead oxide.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Comparing Borosilicate glass and Optical glass, which ingredient is unique to the production of Optical glass?\",\"options\":[\"Sand\",\"Soda\",\"Barium oxide\",\"Boric acid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Optical glass uses barium oxide, which is not listed in the ingredients for Borosilicate glass.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"A student finds a piece of glass that dissolves when placed in a beaker of water. This glass is likely made from a mixture of:\",\"options\":[\"Sand and soda\",\"Sand, soda, and lead oxide\",\"Sand, soda, and boric acid\",\"Silica only\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Alkali silicate glass (water glass) is soluble in water and is made from sand and soda.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the non-degradable nature of glass considered a threat to urban infrastructure?\",\"options\":[\"It causes the release of greenhouse gases in drains\",\"It makes the water in drainage systems acidic\",\"It can lead to the blockage of drainage systems\",\"It reacts with the metal pipes in the drainage\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Because glass does not decompose, pieces of waste glass can physically block drainage systems.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the first image shows various laboratory glasswares like beakers and flasks. Based on the text, these are most likely made of which glass to resist chemical reactions?\",\"options\":[\"Lead glass\",\"Silica glass\",\"Alkali silicate glass\",\"Coloured glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states silica glass is used for laboratory glass-wares because it is not affected by acid and alkali.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/130_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the third image shows a glass with a distinct bluish-green tint. Which substance was likely added during manufacturing to achieve this?\",\"options\":[\"Copper oxide\",\"Lead oxide\",\"Ferrous oxide\",\"Barium oxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that ferrous oxide is mixed to get bluish green glass.\",\"topic\":\"Types of Glass and Environment\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/130_101.jpg\"}]','vertex/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22168,57,8,'Science','en','Ecosystems','[\"Biotic factors are living components of an ecosystem such as plants, animals, fungi and microbes.\",\"Abiotic factors are non‑living components like air, water, soil, sunlight, temperature and minerals.\",\"An ecosystem is the community of biotic and abiotic factors that interact and depend on each other.\",\"Producers (autotrophs) use sunlight to make food through photosynthesis.\",\"Primary consumers (herbivores) eat producers.\",\"Secondary consumers (carnivores or omnivores) eat primary consumers.\",\"Tertiary consumers are top predators that eat secondary consumers.\",\"Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) break down dead organic matter into inorganic substances.\",\"The position of an organism in the food chain is called its trophic level.\",\"Niche is the role an organism plays in its ecosystem, including its habitat and food source.\",\"Biomes are large areas of land or water with similar climate and characteristic ecosystems, e.g., forest, grassland, desert, tundra, marine.\",\"Grasslands develop where rainfall is insufficient for large trees and support grasses and grazing animals.\",\"Evergreen forests have dense tree cover year‑round and support diverse wildlife.\",\"Aquatic ecosystems include freshwater (ponds, rivers) and marine (oceans, seas).\",\"Water currents transfer energy in aquatic ecosystems and support a different food web.\",\"Decomposers recycle nutrients back into the soil or water, maintaining the cycle.\",\"Human activities such as deforestation, mining, dam building, and pollution disturb ecosystems.\",\"Urbanization replaces natural habitats with buildings and roads, reducing biodiversity.\",\"Industrialization consumes forest resources and creates waste that harms ecosystems.\",\"Tourism can damage ecosystems through litter, noise and habitat destruction.\",\"Natural disasters like floods, droughts and earthquakes can alter or destroy ecosystems.\",\"Conservation areas such as national parks protect key species and habitats.\",\"Balancing human needs with ecosystem health is essential for long‑term sustainability.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are the living factors in the world around us called?\",\"options\":[\"Abiotic factors\",\"Biotic factors\",\"Physical factors\",\"Chemical factors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that living factors are called biotic factors.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, what is the structure formed due to the reciprocal relationships between living organisms and their environment?\",\"options\":[\"Habitat\",\"Population\",\"Ecosystem\",\"Abiotic structure\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text defines an ecosystem as the structure formed due to reciprocal relationships between organisms and their habitat.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"From the following list provided in the text, which factor is classified as non-living?\",\"options\":[\"Algae\",\"Fern\",\"Sunlight\",\"Ant\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sunlight is listed as a non-living (abiotic) factor in the introductory classification task.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, the \'Chemical\' factors are further divided into which two specific groups?\",\"options\":[\"Physical and Biological\",\"Autotrophic and Heterotrophic\",\"Inorganic and Organic substances\",\"Producers and Consumers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram F1 shows Chemical factors branching into Inorganic substances and Organic substances.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/132_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which specific group of biotic factors is classified as \'Heterotrophic\' and \'Decomposers\'?\",\"options\":[\"Plants\",\"Animals\",\"Microbes\",\"Minerals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram F1 shows Microbes under the categories of Heterotrophic and Decomposers.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/132_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"What type of interaction occurs between biotic and abiotic factors in an ecosystem?\",\"options\":[\"No interaction\",\"Continuous interaction\",\"Occasional interaction\",\"One-way interaction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions there is a continuous interaction between living and non-living factors.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed as an inorganic substance under chemical factors in the ecosystem chart?\",\"options\":[\"Proteins\",\"Lipids\",\"Iron\",\"Carbohydrates\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Iron is listed under Inorganic substances in the provided chart.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the classification chart, which category do \'Proteins, Carbohydrates, and Lipids\' belong to?\",\"options\":[\"Physical factors\",\"Inorganic substances\",\"Organic substances\",\"Autotrophic factors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chart classifies Proteins, Carbohydrates, and Lipids as Organic substances.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the classification in the chart, what is a primary difference between \'Plants\' and \'Animals\' in an ecosystem?\",\"options\":[\"Plants are producers while animals are consumers\",\"Plants are abiotic while animals are biotic\",\"Plants are inorganic while animals are organic\",\"Plants are physical while animals are chemical\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The chart shows Plants are Producers (Autotrophic) and Animals are Consumers (Heterotrophic).\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"According to the components chart, how are \'Animals\' further categorized?\",\"options\":[\"Physical and Chemical\",\"Inorganic and Organic\",\"Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary Consumers\",\"Autotrophic and Heterotrophic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chart shows Animals are divided into Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary Consumers.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a physical abiotic factor mentioned in the ecosystem components?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen\",\"Sodium\",\"Minerals\",\"Proteins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Minerals are listed under Physical abiotic factors in the chart.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The world around us is composed of which two types of factors?\",\"options\":[\"Only living factors\",\"Only non-living factors\",\"Living and non-living factors\",\"Only chemical factors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states the world around us is made up of living and non-living factors.\",\"topic\":\"Ecosystem\",\"part\":2,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Microorganisms that convert organic substances from dead bodies into inorganic substances are known as ________.\",\"options\":[\"Producers\",\"Consumers\",\"Decomposers\",\"Abiotic factors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that microbes are called \'Decomposers\' because they convert organic substances from dead bodies into inorganic substances.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of an inorganic substance released by decomposers?\",\"options\":[\"Proteins\",\"Lipids\",\"Carbohydrates\",\"Potassium\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists hydrogen, oxygen, calcium, iron, sodium, and potassium as inorganic substances converted by microbes.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"The position of a living organism in context to other living organisms and the specific role it plays is called ________.\",\"options\":[\"Habitat\",\"Niche\",\"Adaptation\",\"Ecosystem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines \'Niche\' as the position and role of any living organism in context to other living organisms.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the proportion of abiotic factors in an ecosystem constantly change?\",\"options\":[\"Because abiotic factors are non-living\",\"Because biotic factors use or excrete them\",\"Because sunlight is always constant\",\"Because microbes do not need oxygen\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"According to the text, the proportion of abiotic factors changes as biotic factors use or excrete them.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, which process is responsible for converting abiotic factors like solar energy and CO2 into food by plants?\",\"options\":[\"Decomposition\",\"Respiration\",\"Photosynthesis\",\"Recycling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows an arrow labeled \'Photosynthesis\' going from Abiotic Factors to Plants (producers).\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/133_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"A sunflower plant evolving oxygen and providing shelter for honey-bees is an example of its ________.\",\"options\":[\"Abiotic factor\",\"Inorganic substance\",\"Niche\",\"Decomposition\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text uses the sunflower plant\'s role in providing oxygen and shelter as an example of a \'Niche\'.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If a specific microbe is unable to adapt to the temperature of a new environment, what is the most likely outcome based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"It will change the temperature of the ecosystem\",\"It will not survive in that ecosystem\",\"It will become a producer\",\"It will stop using abiotic factors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that abiotic factors decide which biotic factors will survive based on their capacity to adapt.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a biotic factor affecting an abiotic factor?\",\"options\":[\"Sunlight helping a plant grow\",\"A plant releasing oxygen into the air\",\"Soil providing minerals to a plant\",\"Humidity affecting the growth of microbes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A plant (biotic) releasing oxygen (abiotic) into the air is an example of a biotic factor affecting an abiotic factor.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what happens to the minerals after the decomposition of dead bodies by microbes?\",\"options\":[\"They are destroyed by heat\",\"They are converted into solar energy\",\"They are recycled back into abiotic factors\",\"They are consumed directly by tigers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The figure shows an arrow from Microbes (Decomposers) back to Abiotic Factors labeled \'Free minerals are recycled\'.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/133_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what determines the total number of biotic factors that can exist in a specific ecosystem?\",\"options\":[\"The number of predators\",\"The abiotic factors\",\"The size of the dead bodies\",\"The amount of proteins in plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that the abiotic factors in an ecosystem decide which biotic factors will survive and what will be their number.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as an abiotic factor in the textbook text?\",\"options\":[\"Humidity\",\"Temperature\",\"Lipids\",\"Sunlight\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Lipids are listed as organic substances from dead bodies, while air, water, soil, sunlight, temperature, and humidity are listed as abiotic factors.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In the given figure, what is released into the environment at every stage of the interaction between biotic and abiotic factors?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Heat\",\"Water\",\"Solar energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The diagram shows pink arrows labeled \'Heat\' pointing away from every stage of the ecosystem cycle.\",\"topic\":\"18.1 Components of Ecosystem\",\"part\":3,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"57\\/133_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is mentioned as an example of an ecosystem in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Desert\",\"Forest\",\"Grassland\",\"Tundra\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly lists Forest, pond, ocean, and river as examples of ecosystems.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"Where are many types of ecosystems found according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Atmosphere\",\"Lithosphere\",\"Biosphere\",\"Hydrosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states that many types of ecosystems are found in the biosphere.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How are ecosystems linked to each other according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Only directly\",\"Only indirectly\",\"Directly or indirectly\",\"They are not linked\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that ecosystems are linked to each other directly or indirectly.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What determines the specific functions of an ecosystem?\",\"options\":[\"Human intervention\",\"Their environment\",\"The size of the biosphere\",\"The number of microbes only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that specific functions of ecosystems continue according to their environment.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In a tropical country like India, what is the distribution of species?\",\"options\":[\"All species are found everywhere in large numbers\",\"All species are found in very small numbers\",\"A few species are found everywhere in large numbers\",\"No species are found in large numbers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text says that in tropical countries like India, a few species are found everywhere in large numbers.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Why can we not completely separate small ecosystems from each other?\",\"options\":[\"Because they are too small to see\",\"Because they are linked to each other\",\"Because they have the same climate\",\"Because they are all in India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explains that because ecosystems are linked directly or indirectly, they cannot be separated.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT mentioned as a basis for the formation of different types of ecosystems?\",\"options\":[\"Climate\",\"Size\",\"Types of plants and animals\",\"Human population density\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The text lists size, place, climate, and types of plants and animals as factors, but not human population density.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How is the variety of species in most ecosystems described?\",\"options\":[\"Only quantitative variety\",\"Only qualitative variety\",\"Both quantitative and qualitative variety\",\"Neither quantitative nor qualitative variety\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text states there is a \'tremendous quantitative and qualitative variety of species\' in most ecosystems.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a pond ecosystem is located next to a forest, how should they be viewed based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"As completely independent units\",\"As units linked directly or indirectly\",\"As the same identical ecosystem\",\"As units that have no interaction\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions that even though ecosystems look independent, they are linked to each other.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Since ecosystems cannot be truly separated, how does the text suggest we categorize them?\",\"options\":[\"According to their age\",\"According to their functions\",\"According to their color\",\"According to their depth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states we can classify ecosystems according to their functions.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What is the general nature of most ecosystems as described in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Simple\",\"Complex\",\"Uniform\",\"Isolated\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Most of the ecosystems are complex\'.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"In the context of India, what is said about the \'remaining species\' that are not found everywhere in large numbers?\",\"options\":[\"They are extinct\",\"They are found in small numbers\",\"They are found in large numbers\",\"They are only found in oceans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that remaining species of plants and animals are found in a small number.\",\"topic\":\"18.2 Interactions among factors\",\"part\":4,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist used the word \'ecosystem\' in 1935?\",\"options\":[\"Ray Claffam\",\"A.G. Tansley\",\"Charles Darwin\",\"Robert Hooke\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that A.G. Tansley, a colleague of Ray Claffam, used the word in 1935.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"In 1930, Ray Claffam was asked to describe the interrelationship between which two factors?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical and physical factors\",\"Biological and social factors\",\"Physical and biological factors\",\"Atmospheric and aquatic factors\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ray Claffam was asked how to describe the interrelationship between physical and biological factors in one word.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"What is another name for \'ecosystem\' according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Abiotic community\",\"Biotic community\",\"Physical community\",\"Climatic community\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly mentions that \'Biotic community\' is another name for \'ecosystem.\'\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"How many main types of biomes are found on the earth?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text lists two types of biomes: Land biomes and Aquatic biomes.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"According to the text, why are new words like \'ecosystem\' created?\",\"options\":[\"Due to changes in climate\",\"With the development in science\",\"To replace old biological names\",\"To describe different types of animals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text starts by stating that new words are created with the development in science.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What is a \'biome\' as defined in the passage?\",\"options\":[\"A single living organism in a small area\",\"A specific ecosystem that develops in a vast area\",\"Only the physical factors of a small pond\",\"The study of biological factors in a lab\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines biomes as specific ecosystems that develop in a vast area.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"What characterizes the living organisms found within a specific biome?\",\"options\":[\"They are completely different from each other\",\"They are similar because of the same climate and abiotic factors\",\"They only belong to aquatic environments\",\"They do not depend on physical factors\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that in regions with the same climate and abiotic factors, the living organisms are also similar.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following describes the relationship between biomes and ecosystems?\",\"options\":[\"Biomes are smaller than ecosystems\",\"Biomes contain many small ecosystems\",\"Ecosystems do not exist within biomes\",\"Biomes and ecosystems are the exact same size\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states that \'These biomes contain many small ecosystems.\'\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"If a scientist is studying the entire Earth as a single unit, what term from the text describes it best?\",\"options\":[\"A small ecosystem\",\"A biotic factor\",\"A vast ecosystem\",\"A physical factor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text explicitly states that \'Earth itself is a vast ecosystem.\'\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"If you are classifying biomes based on the text, which category would a forest ecosystem most likely fall under?\",\"options\":[\"Aquatic biome\",\"Biotic community\",\"Land biome\",\"Physical factor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Since a forest is on land, it falls under the \'Land biomes\' category mentioned in the text.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"Based on the text, what determines the similarity of organisms in a large geographic area?\",\"options\":[\"The names given by scientists\",\"The development of new words\",\"The same climate and abiotic factors\",\"The number of small ecosystems present\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text links similar living organisms to regions having the same climate and abiotic factors.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement best represents the hierarchical structure of ecosystems mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Small ecosystems contain biomes, which contain the Earth\",\"The Earth is a vast ecosystem containing biomes, which contain small ecosystems\",\"Biomes are independent of the Earth\'s ecosystem\",\"Physical factors are larger than biomes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text describes Earth as a vast ecosystem, biomes as large ecosystems within it, and small ecosystems within biomes.\",\"topic\":\"Looking back and Biomes\",\"part\":5,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"What are the biomes that exist only on land called?\",\"options\":[\"Aquatic biomes\",\"Land biomes\",\"Atmospheric biomes\",\"Marine biomes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text defines land biomes as biomes which exist only on land.\",\"topic\":\"Land biomes : Grassland Ecosystem\",\"part\":6,