्य\",\"जमीन\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"जल (पाणी) म्हणते की, \'माझ्यामुळेच फुलले वाळवंटात नंदनवन\'.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"हवेचे अस्तित्व कसे असते, असे संवादात सांगितले आहे?\",\"options\":[\"दृश्य\",\"अदृश्य\",\"रंगीत\",\"ठोस\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"हवा म्हणते, \'असेल मी अदृश्य जरी, आहे माझे अस्तित्व\'.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"निसर्गाचे चार मुख्य आधारस्तंभ कोणते आहेत?\",\"options\":[\"सूर्य, चंद्र, तारे आणि हवा\",\"जमीन, पाणी, झाडे आणि प्राणी\",\"सूर्य, जमीन, जल आणि हवा\",\"अन्न, वस्त्र, निवारा आणि हवा\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"पाठाच्या शेवटी स्पष्ट केले आहे की सूर्य, जमीन, जल व हवा हे निसर्गाचे चार आधारस्तंभ आहेत.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"शाश्वत विकासाचे खरे सूत्र काय आहे?\",\"options\":[\"जास्तीत जास्त ऊर्जेचा वापर\",\"निसर्गाच्या आधारस्तंभांचे संरक्षण व संवर्धन\",\"केवळ नवीन तंत्रज्ञानाचा विकास\",\"शहरांचे वेगाने विस्तारीकरण\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"आधारस्तंभांचे संरक्षण व संवर्धन म्हणजे पर्यावरण संतुलन होय, हेच शाश्वत विकासाचे खरे सूत्र आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"सजीवांना श्वसनासाठी कोणाचा आधार लागतो?\",\"options\":[\"जमीन\",\"सूर्य\",\"हवा\",\"जल\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"हवा म्हणते की ती \'श्वसनासाठी सजीवांना आधार\' आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"जर आपल्याला धरती हिरवीगार ठेवायची असेल, तर कोणाचे महत्त्व सर्वाधिक आहे?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ सूर्याचे\",\"केवळ हवेचे\",\"जलाचे (पाण्याचे)\",\"केवळ ताऱ्यांचे\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"जल म्हणते, \'मी आहे म्हणून धरती होते हिरवीगार\'.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"शाश्वत विकास साधून सुंदर भविष्य घडवणे कोणाच्या हातात आहे?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ निसर्गाच्या\",\"आपल्या (मानवाच्या) हातात\",\"केवळ सूर्याच्या\",\"केवळ हवेच्या\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"पाठात म्हटले आहे की, \'सुंदर भविष्य घडवायचे हे आपल्याच हातात आहे\'.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"सूर्यामध्ये कशाची अफाट क्षमता आहे?\",\"options\":[\"अन्नाची\",\"ऊर्जेची\",\"पाण्याची\",\"थंडाव्याची\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"सूर्य म्हणतो, \'माझ्यात आहे ऊर्जेची अफाट क्षमता\'.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"पर्यावरण संतुलन राखण्यासाठी खालीलपैकी कोणती कृती आवश्यक आहे?\",\"options\":[\"निसर्गचक्र बिघडवणे\",\"हवेची गुणवत्ता कमी करणे\",\"निसर्गाच्या आधारस्तंभांचे संवर्धन करणे\",\"जमिनीचा आधार काढून घेणे\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"आधारस्तंभांचे संरक्षण व संवर्धन म्हणजेच पर्यावरण संतुलन होय.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"हवेच्या संदर्भात आपली कोणती जबाबदारी संवादात सांगितली आहे?\",\"options\":[\"हवा अदृश्य करणे\",\"हवेची गुणवत्ता वाढवणे\",\"हवेचा वापर थांबवणे\",\"हवेला उष्णता देणे\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"हवा म्हणते, \'गुणवत्ता माझी वाढवा, जाणा माझे महत्त्व\'.\",\"topic\":\"भूमिकाभिनय\",\"part\":7,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"वटपौर्णिमेला कोणत्या वृक्षाचे जतन व संवर्धन करण्याची प्रेरणा मिळते?\",\"options\":[\"वड\",\"पिंपळ\",\"आंबा\",\"नारळ\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकानुसार वटपौर्णिमा हा सण वड या वृक्षाच्या संवर्धनाशी संबंधित आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सव केवळ रूढी-परंपरांवर आधारित नसून त्यामागे कोणता सखोल संबंध आहे?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ करमणूक आणि खेळ\",\"निसर्ग, विज्ञान, आरोग्य, आयुर्वेद व कृषी जीवन\",\"केवळ व्यापार आणि आर्थिक नफा\",\"आधुनिक यंत्रसामग्री आणि तंत्रज्ञान\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"भारतीय सणांचा निसर्ग, विज्ञान, आरोग्य, आयुर्वेद आणि शेतीशी जवळचा संबंध असतो.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"जर एखादा शेतकरी \'बैसाखी\' सण साजरा करत असेल, तर तो निसर्गातील कोणत्या घटनेशी संबंधित आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पाऊस पडणे\",\"पीक कापणी\",\"वृक्षारोपण\",\"नदी पूजन\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"बैसाखी हा सण पीक कापणीच्या भौगोलिक घटनेशी जोडलेला आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"खालीलपैकी कोणती जोडी पाठ्यपुस्तकातील माहितीनुसार चुकीची आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पोळा - बैल (प्राणी)\",\"मकरसंक्रांत - सूर्य\",\"नारळी पौर्णिमा - समुद्र (जल)\",\"वटपौर्णिमा - माती\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"वटपौर्णिमा हा सण वृक्षाशी (वड) संबंधित आहे, मातीशी नाही; मातीचा संबंध घटस्थापनेशी आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"\'घटस्थापना\' हा सण कोणत्या नैसर्गिक घटकांच्या संवर्धनाशी संबंधित आहे?\",\"options\":[\"माती आणि हवा\",\"पाणी आणि सूर्य\",\"वृक्ष आणि प्राणी\",\"चंद्र आणि तारा\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकात घटस्थापना हा सण माती आणि हवा यांच्याशी जोडलेला असल्याचे सांगितले आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"सण-उत्सव साजरा करण्याच्या पद्धती कशानुसार बदलतात?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ लोकांच्या वैयक्तिक आवडीनुसार\",\"भौगोलिक व नैसर्गिक परिस्थितीनुसार\",\"केवळ बाजारातील उपलब्ध वस्तूंनुसार\",\"केवळ नवीन फॅशननुसार\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"सण साजरे करण्याच्या पद्धती वेगवेगळ्या भागांनुसार आणि तेथील भौगोलिक परिस्थितीनुसार बदलतात.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"जल संवर्धनासाठी कोणता सण साजरा केला जातो असे पाठात म्हटले आहे?\",\"options\":[\"मकरसंक्रांत\",\"नारळी पौर्णिमा\",\"बुद्ध पौर्णिमा\",\"बैसाखी\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"नारळी पौर्णिमा हा सण समुद्र (जल) या घटकाच्या जतनाशी संबंधित आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"सण-उत्सवांमध्ये समाविष्ट असलेल्या लोकसाहित्याचे उदाहरण खालीलपैकी कोणते आहे?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ चित्रपट संगीत\",\"ओव्या, भारुडे आणि लोकगीते\",\"केवळ वर्तमानपत्रातील बातम्या\",\"इंग्रजी कथा\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"सण-उत्सवांमध्ये लोकगीते, पोवाडे, ओव्या, भारुडे यांसारख्या लोकसाहित्याचा समावेश असतो.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचा शाश्वत विकासात महत्त्वाचा वाटा का आहे?\",\"options\":[\"कारण ते नैसर्गिक घटकांचे जतन व संवर्धन करण्यास प्रेरणा देतात\",\"कारण ते साजरे करण्यासाठी खूप खर्च करावा लागतो\",\"कारण ते फक्त शहरांमध्येच साजरे केले जातात\",\"कारण ते विज्ञानाच्या विरोधात असतात\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"सण निसर्गातील घटकांचे जतन करण्यास प्रेरणा देतात, म्हणून ते शाश्वत विकासात महत्त्वाचे आहेत.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"चंद्र या नैसर्गिक घटकाशी खालीलपैकी कोणते सण जोडलेले आहेत?\",\"options\":[\"वटपौर्णिमा व पोळा\",\"मकरसंक्रांत व बैसाखी\",\"रमजान ईद व बुद्ध पौर्णिमा\",\"नारळी पौर्णिमा व घटस्थापना\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकानुसार रमजान ईद व बुद्ध पौर्णिमा हे सण चंद्राशी संबंधित आहेत.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"दिलेल्या चित्रात खालच्या बाजूला मध्यभागी समुद्रकिनारी कोणता सण साजरा होताना दिसत आहे?\",\"options\":[\"नारळी पौर्णिमा\",\"मकरसंक्रांत\",\"पोळा\",\"वटपौर्णिमा\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"चित्रात समुद्रकिनारी नारळ अर्पण करताना लोक दिसत आहेत, जे नारळी पौर्णिमेचे दर्शन घडवते.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"apply\",\"figure_image\":\"63\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"दिलेल्या चित्रात वरच्या बाजूला उजवीकडे (Top Right) कोणत्या प्राण्याचे पूजन करून त्याचे संवर्धन करण्याचा संदेश दिला आहे?\",\"options\":[\"वाघ\",\"बैल\",\"घोडा\",\"हत्ती\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"चित्रात बैलाची पूजा करताना दाखवले आहे, जे पोळा या सणाचे प्रतीक आहे.\",\"topic\":\"भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\",\"part\":8,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"63\\/114_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"शाश्वत विकासाच्या संदर्भात पाठ्यपुस्तकात कोणत्या गोष्टींचे योगदान अभ्यासण्यास सांगितले आहे?\",\"options\":[\"परदेशी खेळ\",\"भारतीय सण-उत्सव\",\"औद्योगिक प्रगती\",\"आधुनिक फॅशन\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकात \'शाश्वत विकासात भारतीय सण-उत्सवांचे योगदान\' यावर चर्चा करण्यास सांगितले आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"प्रकल्प सादरीकरणाच्या मुद्द्यांमध्ये \'निष्कर्ष\' हा कितव्या क्रमांकाचा मुद्दा आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पाचवा\",\"सातवा\",\"नवा\",\"अकरावा\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"दिलेल्या प्रकल्प आराखड्यात ९ व्या क्रमांकावर \'निष्कर्ष\' हा मुद्दा आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"दिलेल्या चित्रात डाव्या बाजूला खाली (bottom-left) कोणता शाश्वत ऊर्जेचा प्रकार दर्शवला आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पवन ऊर्जा\",\"सौर ऊर्जा\",\"सागरी ऊर्जा\",\"अणु ऊर्जा\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"चित्रात सौर पॅनेल (Solar Panels) दिसत असल्याने ती सौर ऊर्जा आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\",\"figure_image\":\"63\\/115_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"दिलेल्या चित्रात उजव्या बाजूला वर (top-right) असलेल्या भागात ऊर्जेचा कोणता स्रोत वापरला जात आहे?\",\"options\":[\"कोळसा\",\"खनिज तेल\",\"सागरी लाटा \\/ पाणी\",\"नैसर्गिक वायू\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"चित्रात समुद्रातील लाटांपासून किंवा प्रवाहापासून ऊर्जा मिळवण्याचे यंत्र (Tidal energy) दिसत आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\",\"figure_image\":\"63\\/115_101.jpg\"},{\"q\":\"सण-उत्सवांचा \'भौगोलिक घटकांशी सहसंबंध\' शोधणे म्हणजे नेमके काय?\",\"options\":[\"सणा दिवशी कोणता वार आहे ते पाहणे\",\"सण आणि निसर्गातील बदल यांचा संबंध शोधणे\",\"सणाचे फक्त नाव लक्षात ठेवणे\",\"सणाला कोणते कपडे घालावेत हे ठरवणे\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"भौगोलिक घटकांशी सहसंबंध म्हणजे सण आणि त्यावेळची निसर्गस्थिती यांचा संबंध जोडणे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"प्रकल्पाच्या शेवटी दिलेल्या \'संदर्भसूची\'चा मुख्य उपयोग काय असतो?\",\"options\":[\"चित्रे चिकटवण्यासाठी\",\"माहिती कोठून मिळवली हे दर्शवण्यासाठी\",\"नकाशा काढण्यासाठी\",\"घोषवाक्ये लिहिण्यासाठी\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"संदर्भसूचीमध्ये आपण माहिती मिळवण्यासाठी वापरलेल्या पुस्तकांची किंवा स्रोतांची नावे लिहितो.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"मानवी वर्तनामुळे पर्यावरणास हानी पोहोचू नये, यासाठी \'सांगा पाहू\' भागात काय करण्यास सुचवले आहे?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ नवीन झाडे लावणे\",\"टाळायच्या व करायच्या गोष्टींची यादी करणे\",\"सण साजरे करणे बंद करणे\",\"फक्त चित्रे काढणे\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"पर्यावरणाचे नुकसान टाळण्यासाठी कोणत्या गोष्टी टाळाव्यात व कोणत्या कराव्यात याची यादी करण्यास सांगितले आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"प्रकल्पातील \'पर्यावरणावर होणारे परिणाम व उपाय\' हा मुद्दा कशावर लक्ष केंद्रित करतो?\",\"options\":[\"सणाची तिथी ठरवणे\",\"सणाचे लोकसाहित्य गोळा करणे\",\"सणामुळे निसर्गावर होणारा परिणाम व तो सुधारण्याचे मार्ग\",\"सणाचे जेवण बनवणे\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"हा मुद्दा सणांच्या उत्सवामुळे पर्यावरणावर होणारे दुष्परिणाम आणि त्यावर करायच्या उपाययोजनांशी संबंधित आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"शाश्वत विकासासाठी खालीलपैकी कोणती कृती योग्य ठरेल?\",\"options\":[\"वारंवार पर्यावरणास हानी पोहोचवणे\",\"शाश्वत ऊर्जा स्रोतांचा वापर करणे\",\"केवळ लोककलेचा विचार करणे\",\"भौगोलिक घटकांकडे दुर्लक्ष करणे\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"शाश्वत विकासासाठी निसर्गाला पूरक अशा शाश्वत ऊर्जा स्रोतांचा वापर करणे आवश्यक आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"प्रकल्प सादरीकरणात \'लोककला नृत्य व लोकसाहित्य\' या मुद्द्याचा समावेश का केला असावा?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ मनोरंजनासाठी\",\"सणाचे सांस्कृतिक महत्त्व समजून घेण्यासाठी\",\"शाळेत सुट्टी मिळवण्यासाठी\",\"नकाशा वाचनासाठी\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"सणांशी संबंधित लोककला आणि साहित्य हे त्या समाजाच्या संस्कृतीचे दर्शन घडवतात.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"प्रकल्पाच्या आराखड्यातील १० व्या मुद्द्यानुसार सादरीकरण आकर्षक करण्यासाठी काय वापरावे?\",\"options\":[\"केवळ मजकूर\",\"संदर्भसूची\",\"चित्रे, तक्ते आणि घोषवाक्ये\",\"केवळ तिथी आणि वार\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"मुद्दा १० मध्ये चित्रे, तक्ते, नकाशे, घोषवाक्ये इत्यादींचा वापर करण्यास सांगितले आहे.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"शाश्वत विकासाची दिशा ठरवताना \'मानवी वर्तन\' कसे असावे असे या पाठातून सुचवले आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पर्यावरणास हानी पोहोचवणारे\",\"केवळ स्वतःचा फायदा पाहणारे\",\"पर्यावरणाचे रक्षण करणारे व जबाबदार\",\"निसर्गाच्या नियमांकडे दुर्लक्ष करणारे\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"शाश्वत विकासासाठी मानवी वर्तन हे पर्यावरणाचे नुकसान टाळणारे असावे, असे पाठातील प्रश्नांवरून स्पष्ट होते.\",\"topic\":\"कृती आणि प्रकल्प\",\"part\":9,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"},{\"q\":\"दक्षिण गोलार्धातील ६६°३०\' च्या अक्षवृत्ताला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"आर्क्टिक वृत्त\",\"अंटार्क्टिक वृत्त\",\"मकरवृत्त\",\"कर्कवृत्त\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकानुसार दक्षिण गोलार्धातील ६६°३०\' च्या अक्षवृत्ताला अंटार्क्टिक वृत्त म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"देशाच्या एकूण उत्पन्नाला एकूण लोकसंख्येने भागल्यावर मिळणाऱ्या सरासरी रकमेला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"स्थूल राष्ट्रीय उत्पादन\",\"एकूण उत्पन्न\",\"दरडोई उत्पन्न\",\"अल्प उत्पन्न\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"दरडोई उत्पन्न म्हणजे देशाच्या एकूण उत्पन्नाला एकूण लोकसंख्येने भागल्यावर मिळणारी सरासरी रक्कम होय.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"खालीलपैकी कोणता घटक \'अजैविक\' (Abiotic) घटकाचे उदाहरण आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पक्षी\",\"वनस्पती\",\"खनिजे\",\"प्राणी\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"हवा, पाणी आणि खनिजे हे पर्यावरणातील निर्जीव म्हणजेच अजैविक घटक आहेत.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"ज्या देशांची आर्थिक स्थिती मजबूत आहे आणि दरडोई उत्पन्न अधिक आहे, त्यांना काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"विकसनशील देश\",\"अल्पविकसित देश\",\"विकसित देश\",\"गरीब देश\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"विकसित देश म्हणजे असे देश ज्यांची आर्थिक स्थिती मजबूत आहे आणि दरडोई उत्पन्न अधिक आहे.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"ग्रीनिच शहराजवळून जाणाऱ्या शून्य अंश रेखावृत्ताला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"विषुववृत्त\",\"मूळ रेखावृत्त\",\"आंतरराष्ट्रीय वाररेषा\",\"भारतीय प्रमाण वेळ\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"ग्रीनिच शहराजवळून जाणाऱ्या शून्य अंश रेखावृत्ताला मूळ रेखावृत्त (Prime meridian) म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"असा विकास जो आजच्या पिढीच्या गरजा पूर्ण करतो, पण भविष्यातील पिढ्यांच्या गरजांशी तडजोड करत नाही, त्यास काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"आर्थिक विकास\",\"शाश्वत विकास\",\"औद्योगिक विकास\",\"कृषी विकास\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"शाश्वत विकास म्हणजे भविष्यातील पिढ्यांच्या गरजा पूर्ण करण्याच्या क्षमतेशी तडजोड न करता केलेला विकास.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"एखाद्या ठिकाणचे कमाल तापमान ३०°C आणि किमान तापमान २०°C असेल, तर त्या ठिकाणची \'तापमान कक्षा\' किती असेल?\",\"options\":[\"५०°C\",\"१०°C\",\"२५°C\",\"१५°C\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"कमाल व किमान तापमान नोंदीतील फरकास (३० - २० = १०) तापमान कक्षा म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"उत्तर गोलार्धातील २३°३०\' अक्षवृत्ताला कोणत्या नावाने ओळखले जाते?\",\"options\":[\"मकरवृत्त\",\"विषुववृत्त\",\"कर्कवृत्त\",\"आर्क्टिक वृत्त\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"उत्तर गोलार्धातील २३°३०\' अक्षवृत्ताला कर्कवृत्त म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"सजीव आणि त्यांच्या सभोवतालचे निर्जीव घटक यांच्यातील परस्पर क्रियेला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"परिसंस्था\",\"बाष्पीभवन\",\"विकिरण\",\"विस्थापन\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"सजीव आणि निर्जीव घटक यांच्यातील परस्पर क्रियेला परिसंस्था (Ecosystem) म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"जंगलातील झाडे-झुडुपे तोडून केली जाणारी आदिम शेती पद्धत कोणती?\",\"options\":[\"सखोल शेती\",\"तंतोतंत शेती\",\"निर्वाह शेती\",\"स्थलांतरित शेती\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"जंगलातील झाडे-झुडुपे तोडून केली जाणारी आदिम शेती पद्धत म्हणजे स्थलांतरित शेती होय.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"दूर अंतरावरून माहिती मिळविण्याच्या तंत्राला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"जी.पी.एस. प्रणाली\",\"सुदूर संवेदन\",\"केंद्रोत्सारी बल\",\"मानवी संसाधन\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"सुदूर संवेदन (Remote sensing) म्हणजे दूर अंतरावरून माहिती मिळविणे.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"पृथ्वीचे उत्तर व दक्षिण गोलार्ध असे दोन समान भाग करणाऱ्या सर्वात मोठ्या अक्षवृत्ताला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"मूळ रेखावृत्त\",\"विषुववृत्त\",\"कर्कवृत्त\",\"मकरवृत्त\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"विषुववृत्त हे पृथ्वीचे दोन समान भाग करणारे सर्वात मोठे अक्षवृत्त आहे.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"एखादा पदार्थ द्रव आणि घन यांच्या मधल्या अवस्थेत असतो, तेव्हा त्या अवस्थेला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"प्रवाही\",\"अचूक\",\"अर्धप्रवाही\",\"विलगता\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"द्रव आणि घन यांच्या मधल्या अवस्थेला अर्धप्रवाही (Semi fluid) म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"अक्षवृत्ते आणि रेखावृत्ते या काल्पनिक रेषांच्या जाळीला काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"वृत्तजाळी\",\"भूपट्ट\",\"रेखांश\",\"अक्षांश\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"अक्षवृत्ते आणि रेखावृत्ते या काल्पनिक रेषांच्या जाळीला वृत्तजाळी (Graticule) म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"वातावरणातील जे वायू उष्णता साठवून ठेवू शकतात, त्यांना काय म्हणतात?\",\"options\":[\"अजैविक वायू\",\"हरितगृह वायू\",\"प्रवाही वायू\",\"नैसर्गिक वायू\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"वातावरणातील उष्णता साठवून ठेवणाऱ्या वायूंना हरितगृह वायू (GHGs) म्हणतात.\",\"topic\":\"परिशिष्ट\",\"part\":11,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"चाइल्ड लाइनचा राष्ट्रीय आपत्कालीन फोन नंबर कोणता आहे?\",\"options\":[\"१००\",\"१०१\",\"१०९८\",\"१०८८\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकानुसार, १०९८ (दहा नऊ आठ) हा चाइल्ड लाइनचा राष्ट्रीय आपत्कालीन फोन नंबर आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"POCSO ई-बॉक्स कोणत्या वेबसाईटवर उपलब्ध आहे?\",\"options\":[\"ncpcr@gov.in\",\"childline@gov.in\",\"education@gov.in\",\"wcd@gov.in\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकात POCSO ई-बॉक्ससाठी ncpcr@gov.in ही वेबसाईट दिली आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"चाइल्ड लाइन १०९८ हा उपक्रम कोणामार्फत सुरू करण्यात आला आहे?\",\"options\":[\"पोलीस विभाग\",\"चाइल्ड लाइन फाउंडेशन\",\"शाळा व्यवस्थापन समिती\",\"आरोग्य विभाग\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"हा उपक्रम चाइल्ड लाइन फाउंडेशनने सुरू केला आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"चाइल्ड लाइन ही सेवा कोणत्या मंत्रालयातर्फे राबवली जाते?\",\"options\":[\"गृह मंत्रालय\",\"शिक्षण मंत्रालय\",\"महिला आणि बालविकास मंत्रालय\",\"पर्यावरण मंत्रालय\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"ही सेवा महिला आणि बालविकास मंत्रालयातर्फे पीडित मुलांसाठी राबवली जाते.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"remember\"},{\"q\":\"जर कोणी अयोग्य रीतीने स्पर्श केला, तर पाठ्यपुस्तकानुसार पहिली महत्त्वाची कृती कोणती?\",\"options\":[\"गप्प बसणे\",\"स्वतःला दोष देणे\",\"विश्वासू व्यक्तीला घटनेबद्दल सांगणे\",\"घर सोडून जाणे\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"अशी घटना घडल्यास ज्यांच्यावर विश्वास आहे त्यांना सांगणे आवश्यक आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"चाइल्ड लाइन १०९८ या सेवेचे मुख्य वैशिष्ट्य काय आहे?\",\"options\":[\"ही सेवा फक्त दिवसा उपलब्ध असते\",\"ही २४ तास टोल फ्री राष्ट्रीय आपत्कालीन सेवा आहे\",\"यासाठी खूप पैसे भरावे लागतात\",\"ही फक्त मोठ्या शहरांसाठी आहे\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"चाइल्ड लाइन ही २४ तास टोल फ्री राष्ट्रीय आपत्कालीन फोन सेवा आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"राष्ट्रीय आयोगाची स्थापना प्रामुख्याने कशासाठी करण्यात आली आहे?\",\"options\":[\"मुलांना खेळ शिकवण्यासाठी\",\"हक्कांच्या संरक्षणासाठी\",\"शाळेत प्रवेश देण्यासाठी\",\"सहलींचे आयोजन करण्यासाठी\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"राष्ट्रीय आयोगाची स्थापना हक्कांच्या संरक्षणासाठी करण्यात आली आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"या सुविधांचा लाभ घेण्यासाठी मुलाचे वय किती असणे आवश्यक आहे?\",\"options\":[\"१८ वर्षांपेक्षा जास्त\",\"१८ वर्षांपेक्षा कमी\",\"२१ वर्षांपेक्षा जास्त\",\"फक्त ५ वर्षांपर्यंत\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"पाठ्यपुस्तकानुसार, १८ वर्षांपेक्षा कमी वय असलेल्या मुलांसाठी ही सेवा आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"understand\"},{\"q\":\"तुमच्या माहितीत एखादे मूल त्रासात असेल, तर तुम्ही त्याला मदत करण्यासाठी काय कराल?\",\"options\":[\"त्याला दुर्लक्ष करायला सांगाल\",\"१०९८ या क्रमांकावर संपर्क साधाल\",\"त्याला घाबरवून टाकाल\",\"कोणालाही काही सांगणार नाही\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"त्रास सहन करणारे मूल माहितीत असल्यास १०९८ ला संपर्क साधणे ही योग्य कृती आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"एका १५ वर्षांच्या मुलाला कोणीतरी अयोग्य स्पर्श करून अस्वस्थ केले आहे, त्याने काय करावे?\",\"options\":[\"स्वतःला दोष देऊन गप्प बसावे\",\"घाबरून कोणालाही सांगू नये\",\"विश्वासू व्यक्तीला सांगावे किंवा १०९८ ला फोन करावा\",\"अशा व्यक्तीशी मैत्री करावी\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"अस्वस्थ वाटल्यास गप्प न बसता विश्वासू व्यक्तीला सांगणे किंवा १०९८ ची मदत घेणे आवश्यक आहे.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"apply\"},{\"q\":\"चाइल्ड लाइनला \'टोल फ्री\' सेवा का म्हटले जाते?\",\"options\":[\"कारण ती वापरण्यासाठी पैसे लागत नाहीत\",\"कारण ती फक्त फोनवर उपलब्ध आहे\",\"कारण ती फक्त मुलांसाठी आहे\",\"कारण ती सरकार चालवते\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"टोल फ्री म्हणजे ज्या सेवेसाठी वापरकर्त्याला फोनचे शुल्क द्यावे लागत नाही.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"analyze\"},{\"q\":\"अयोग्य स्पर्श झाल्यास \'स्वतःला दोष देऊ नका\' असे का सांगितले जाते?\",\"options\":[\"कारण मुलांनी चुका करणे अपेक्षित असते\",\"कारण अशा घटनेत पीडित मुलाची कोणतीही चूक नसते\",\"कारण दोष दिल्याने वेळ वाया जातो\",\"कारण कोणालाही दोष देणे चुकीचे असते\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"अयोग्य स्पर्शाच्या घटनेत पीडित मूल जबाबदार नसते, म्हणून स्वतःला दोष देऊ नये.\",\"topic\":\"सामाजिक जाणीव\",\"part\":12,\"bloom\":\"evaluate\"}]','gemini/gemini-3-flash-preview','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22229,9,3,'EVS','en','Our Family','[\"A school is a place where we go to learn.\",\"We learn discipline by coming to school on time.\",\"Wearing the school uniform helps us follow rules.\",\"Standing in a line for assembly teaches order and respect.\",\"Girls and boys from different families come to school to learn together.\",\"We spend time with friends and help each other at school.\",\"Taking care of each other builds kindness and friendship.\",\"We learn to respect our teachers and listen to them.\",\"Teachers teach us different subjects like math, science, and language.\",\"Student-teacher relation helps us grow and understand better.\",\"In school, we learn how to behave in society.\",\"By learning good habits, we become good citizens.\",\"Like our family, school is important in our life.\",\"The school uniform creates a feeling of equality among students.\",\"It also brings discipline, unity, and a sense of belonging.\",\"The uniform is the identity of the school and makes us feel proud.\",\"Resources in school include building, blackboard, books, benches, bell, etc.\",\"Facilities like drinking water, toilets, library, and playground are important.\",\"Computer room, lab, first aid box, and school bus support learning and safety.\",\"Playing games like kabaddi and running keeps us active and happy.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a school?\",\"options\":[\"A place to play only\",\"A place where we go to learn\",\"A place to sleep\",\"A place to eat food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A school is a place where we go to learn, as stated in the text.\"},{\"q\":\"How do we learn discipline in school?\",\"options\":[\"By coming to school on time\",\"By playing all day\",\"By eating snacks in class\",\"By watching TV\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"We learn discipline by coming to school on time, wearing uniform, and standing in line for assembly.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the school uniform create among students?\",\"options\":[\"Difference and conflict\",\"A feeling of equality, discipline, and unity\",\"Only fashion\",\"No effect\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The school uniform creates a feeling of equality, discipline, and unity among students.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the school uniform called the \'identity of the school\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is colorful\",\"Because it shows which school we belong to\",\"Because it is expensive\",\"Because it is optional\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The school uniform is the \'identity of the school\' as it creates a sense of belonging towards the school.\"},{\"q\":\"Who do we learn different subjects from in school?\",\"options\":[\"Friends\",\"Teachers\",\"Parents\",\"Strangers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Teachers teach us different subjects in school.\"},{\"q\":\"What do we learn in school that helps us become good citizens?\",\"options\":[\"How to play video games\",\"How to behave in society\",\"How to avoid work\",\"How to sleep in class\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In school, we learn how to behave in society, which helps us become good citizens.\"},{\"q\":\"Which game is mentioned as being played on the school playground?\",\"options\":[\"Chess\",\"Kabaddi\",\"Swimming\",\"Painting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text mentions kabaddi matches and girls playing kabaddi on the school playground.\"},{\"q\":\"What do children do to encourage players during a match?\",\"options\":[\"Stay silent\",\"Cheer loudly\",\"Leave the ground\",\"Sleep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The children were cheering loudly to encourage the teams playing kabaddi.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a facility for drinking water in school?\",\"options\":[\"Computer room\",\"Hand-wash station\",\"Drinking water facility\",\"Art room\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The text mentions \'facility for drinking water\' as one of the resources in school.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do girls and boys from different families come to school?\",\"options\":[\"To fight\",\"To learn\",\"To sleep\",\"To play only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Girls and boys from different families come to school to learn, as mentioned in the text.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22230,9,3,'EVS','en','Family Tree','[\"A ramp is a sloping path built next to stairs to help people using wheelchairs move easily.\",\"School has many people: students, parents, teachers, principal, office staff, cleaning staff, cooks, and helpers.\",\"The School Council of Ministers includes roles like Chief Minister, Discipline Minister, Cleanliness Minister, Study Minister, Cultural Minister, and Sports Minister.\",\"Viraj was elected as the Cultural Minister and is responsible for cultural programmes in school.\",\"During Cleanliness Week, students are divided into groups to share cleaning tasks in school.\",\"School teaches us knowledge, problem-solving, and how to work together with others.\",\"In school, we learn to speak, share thoughts, listen to others, and understand democratic values.\",\"Democracy is a system where people participate in decision-making, like in school elections.\",\"The Right to Education (RTE) Act, 2009 ensures free and compulsory education for all children in India.\",\"Every boy and girl in India has the right to attend school under the RTE Act.\",\"Drawing a map from home to school helps us understand directions and landmarks.\",\"Maps use symbols and outlines to show the way from one place to another.\",\"We learn responsibility by doing tasks like cleaning, helping, and participating in school activities.\",\"School is a useful institution because it prepares us to live and work in society.\",\"Activities like elections and cleanliness drives teach us teamwork and leadership.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a ramp used for in a school?\",\"options\":[\"To play games on\",\"To help wheelchair users move easily\",\"To store books\",\"To sit and rest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A ramp is a sloping path that helps children using wheelchairs move in and out of the school easily.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was elected as the Cultural Minister in Viraj’s school?\",\"options\":[\"Viraj\",\"The Principal\",\"A teacher\",\"The cleaning staff\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Viraj was elected as the Cultural Minister and is responsible for cultural programmes in school.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a role in the School Council of Ministers?\",\"options\":[\"Discipline Minister\",\"Cleanliness Minister\",\"Sports Minister\",\"Librarian Minister\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The School Council includes Discipline, Cleanliness, and Sports Ministers, but not a Librarian Minister.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the RTE Act, 2009 guarantee?\",\"options\":[\"Free uniforms for all\",\"Free and compulsory education for children\",\"Free meals in school\",\"Free transportation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Right to Education Act, 2009 ensures every child in India has the right to free and compulsory education.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is school considered a useful institution for society?\",\"options\":[\"It gives homework daily\",\"It teaches knowledge, problem-solving, and teamwork\",\"It has a playground\",\"It gives free snacks\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"School teaches us knowledge, how to solve problems, and work together, making it useful for society.\"},{\"q\":\"During Cleanliness Week, how do students participate?\",\"options\":[\"By watching videos\",\"By being divided into groups to share cleaning tasks\",\"By sleeping in class\",\"By playing games\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Students are divided into three groups to share the work of cleaning during Cleanliness Week.\"},{\"q\":\"What do we learn in school about speaking and listening?\",\"options\":[\"To shout loudly\",\"To speak, share thoughts, and listen to others’ opinions\",\"To stay silent always\",\"To ignore others\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In school, we develop the habit of speaking, sharing thoughts, and listening to others’ opinions.\"},{\"q\":\"What is democracy, as learned in school?\",\"options\":[\"Rule by one person\",\"A system where people participate in decision-making\",\"No rules at all\",\"Only teachers decide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Democracy is a system where people take part in decisions, like in school elections for ministers.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the outline of a map from home to school used for?\",\"options\":[\"To decorate the notebook\",\"To show the way and landmarks from home to school\",\"To draw animals\",\"To colour randomly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Drawing a map outline from home to school helps us understand the route and important places along the way.\"},{\"q\":\"Who helps in the mid-day meal scheme in school?\",\"options\":[\"Only teachers\",\"Cooks and helpers\",\"Only students\",\"Only the principal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cooks and helpers are responsible for preparing and serving the mid-day meal in school.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22231,9,3,'EVS','en','My School','[\"A school is a place where we learn and grow.\",\"The school building includes classrooms, library, laboratory, and computer room.\",\"Teachers help us learn new things every day.\",\"The principal is the head of the school and manages all activities.\",\"Students come to school to study and play.\",\"The mid-day meal cook prepares food for students.\",\"Parents support their children\'s education and attend school meetings.\",\"The playground is used for playing and physical activities.\",\"The library has books for reading and learning.\",\"The laboratory is used for science experiments.\",\"The computer room helps us learn using computers.\",\"The toilet and cleanliness staff keep the school clean and hygienic.\",\"The complaint box allows students to share problems or suggestions.\",\"Everyone in the school works together to make it a good place to learn.\",\"We should respect all people who work in our school.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the head of the school?\",\"options\":[\"Teacher\",\"Principal\",\"Student\",\"Parent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The principal is the head of the school and manages all activities.\"},{\"q\":\"Who prepares the mid-day meal in school?\",\"options\":[\"Teacher\",\"Principal\",\"Cook\",\"Student\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The mid-day meal cook prepares food for students in school.\"},{\"q\":\"Where do students go to read books in school?\",\"options\":[\"Playground\",\"Laboratory\",\"Library\",\"Toilet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The library is the place in school where students read books.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a human component of the school?\",\"options\":[\"Library\",\"Teacher\",\"Laboratory\",\"Playground\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Teacher is a person and thus a human component of the school.\"},{\"q\":\"What is used for science experiments in school?\",\"options\":[\"Library\",\"Computer room\",\"Laboratory\",\"Ramp\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The laboratory is used for conducting science experiments.\"},{\"q\":\"Who helps students learn in the classroom?\",\"options\":[\"Parents\",\"Principal\",\"Teacher\",\"Cook\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Teachers help students learn new things in the classroom.\"},{\"q\":\"Where do students play during break time?\",\"options\":[\"Library\",\"Toilet\",\"Playground\",\"Computer room\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The playground is used for playing and physical activities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which place in school has computers for learning?\",\"options\":[\"Library\",\"Laboratory\",\"Computer room\",\"Toilet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The computer room is where students use computers to learn.\"},{\"q\":\"Who do students give suggestions or complaints to in school?\",\"options\":[\"Teacher\",\"Principal\",\"Complaint box\",\"Parents\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The complaint box is used to share problems or suggestions with school authorities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a part of the school building?\",\"options\":[\"Classroom\",\"Library\",\"Playground\",\"Laboratory\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The playground is an outdoor area, not part of the school building.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22232,9,3,'EVS','en','Market','[\"A market is a place where people buy and sell things.\",\"A seller is a person who sells goods.\",\"A customer is a person who buys goods.\",\"A weekly market is held on a fixed day every week.\",\"Weekly markets are common in villages and sometimes in cities.\",\"People buy daily use items like vegetables, fruits, grains, etc., in weekly markets.\",\"Sellers advertise by calling out loudly to attract customers.\",\"Bargaining over prices is common in markets.\",\"A supermarket is a large shop in cities selling many varieties of goods under one roof.\",\"A shopping mall is a multi-floor building with many shops, food stalls, and facilities like escalators.\",\"Barcode on price stickers helps scan and check the price after shopping.\",\"A receipt is given after shopping showing total amount to be paid.\",\"Payment can be made in cash or online.\",\"A \'haat\' or \'bazaar-haat\' is a weekly market.\",\"A \'Ganj\' is a market selling only one type of goods, like Kapadganj for cloth.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is a person that sells goods in a market?\",\"options\":[\"Customer\",\"Seller\",\"Farmer\",\"Teacher\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A seller is a person who sells goods in a market.\"},{\"q\":\"What is bought and sold in a weekly market for daily use?\",\"options\":[\"Clothes only\",\"Electronics\",\"Vegetables, fruits, grains\",\"Toys\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Weekly markets mainly sell daily use items like vegetables, fruits, and grains.\"},{\"q\":\"On which day is the weekly market held in Raghu and Rama’s village?\",\"options\":[\"Monday\",\"Tuesday\",\"Wednesday\",\"Thursday\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The weekly market in their village is held every Wednesday.\"},{\"q\":\"What do sellers do to attract customers in a market?\",\"options\":[\"Stay silent\",\"Call out loudly\",\"Hide their goods\",\"Sleep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sellers advertise by loudly calling out to attract customers.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a supermarket?\",\"options\":[\"A small stall\",\"A weekly market\",\"A big shop selling many varieties of goods\",\"An animal shelter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A supermarket is a place where many varieties of useful things are sold in one shop.\"},{\"q\":\"Where are shopping malls generally found?\",\"options\":[\"Only in villages\",\"In big cities\",\"Underwater\",\"In forests\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shopping malls are generally found in big cities with multiple floors and shops.\"},{\"q\":\"What is scanned to check the price after shopping in a mall or supermarket?\",\"options\":[\"QR code\",\"Barcode\",\"Face\",\"Fingerprint\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The barcode on the price sticker is scanned to check the price after shopping.\"},{\"q\":\"What is given to the customer after shopping that shows the total amount to be paid?\",\"options\":[\"A toy\",\"A receipt\",\"A flower\",\"A balloon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A receipt is given after shopping showing the total amount to be paid.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a market called that sells only one type of goods, like cloth?\",\"options\":[\"Haat\",\"Ganj\",\"Mall\",\"Fair\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A market selling only one type of goods is called a \'Ganj\', e.g., Kapadganj for cloth.\"},{\"q\":\"Why should we carry cloth bags to the market?\",\"options\":[\"To look fashionable\",\"To carry heavy items and reduce plastic use\",\"To play games\",\"To sit on\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cloth bags help carry shopping and are eco-friendly, reducing plastic bag use.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22233,9,3,'EVS','en','Our Needs and Wants','[\"Needs are things we must have to stay alive, like food, water, air, shelter, clothing.\",\"Wants are things we like but can live without, such as sweets, toys, or games.\",\"Water is essential for life and is called \'Jeevan\' in Marathi, meaning \'life\'.\",\"Food gives us energy and nutrients; we should eat nutritious food and avoid wasting it.\",\"Clean air keeps us healthy; polluted air from smoke and vehicles can harm our lungs.\",\"Clothing protects us from heat, cold, wind, and rain; we wear different clothes in different seasons.\",\"Shelter means a safe house that protects us from weather and danger.\",\"Good health comes from healthy food, exercise, yoga, and clean habits.\",\"Education helps us think, learn, solve problems, and grow; it is a basic need for all.\",\"Natural resources like water, air, and soil come from nature; we must use them wisely.\",\"Human-made resources include machines, buildings, money, and transport.\",\"Doctors, teachers, farmers, and police help fulfill our needs through their work.\",\"Problems like water or food shortage happen when needs are not met easily.\",\"People with disabilities need support like ramps, Braille, and sign language to live independently.\",\"We should keep our surroundings clean and use resources carefully to avoid waste.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a need?\",\"options\":[\"Chocolate\",\"Toy car\",\"Water\",\"Video game\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Water is essential for survival, making it a need.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'Jeevan\' mean in Marathi?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Life\",\"Air\",\"Food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"\'Jeevan\' means \'life\' in Marathi, and water is called Jeevan because it is vital for life.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a want?\",\"options\":[\"Food\",\"Shelter\",\"Ice cream\",\"Clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ice cream is enjoyable but not necessary for survival, so it is a want.\"},{\"q\":\"Where is drinking water usually purified before supply?\",\"options\":[\"Borewell\",\"Water purification centre\",\"Water tank\",\"Factory\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water is treated at water purification centres to make it safe for drinking.\"},{\"q\":\"In a village, where is basic medical treatment available?\",\"options\":[\"Gram Panchayat\",\"Bank\",\"Primary health-care centre\",\"School\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Primary health-care centres provide basic medical services in villages.\"},{\"q\":\"How should we use natural resources?\",\"options\":[\"As we like\",\"In large amounts\",\"Sometimes\",\"Carefully and wisely\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Natural resources are limited; we must use them carefully and wisely to avoid depletion.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following helps protect our body from heat and cold?\",\"options\":[\"Food\",\"Water\",\"Clothing\",\"Education\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Clothing protects our body from extreme weather like heat, cold, wind, and rain.\"},{\"q\":\"What is one way to keep the air clean?\",\"options\":[\"Burning garbage\",\"Using more vehicles\",\"Planting trees\",\"Using plastic bags\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plants and trees absorb pollutants and release oxygen, helping keep the air clean.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is education considered a basic need?\",\"options\":[\"It helps us play games\",\"It helps us think, learn, and solve problems\",\"It gives us food\",\"It builds houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Education develops our mind and helps us live a better life by gaining knowledge and skills.\"},{\"q\":\"Which person helps fulfill our need for food?\",\"options\":[\"Doctor\",\"Teacher\",\"Farmer\",\"Policeman\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Farmers grow crops and produce food, which fulfills our basic need for food.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22234,9,3,'EVS','en','Water Literacy','[\"Water is essential for life and must be conserved.\",\"Water literacy means understanding water\'s importance, saving it, and increasing groundwater levels.\",\"Rain is the main source of water, but it varies by region, causing floods or droughts.\",\"Water should be managed wisely so everyone gets enough.\",\"Ancient Indians saved water through wells, tanks, and rainwater harvesting.\",\"Dnyaneshwar advised building towns with water reservoirs and planting trees.\",\"Shivaji Maharaj ensured forts had water tanks and promoted clean water use.\",\"Ahilyabai Holkar built wells, tanks, and ponds for public and travellers\' use.\",\"Dr. Rajendra Singh built johads to store rainwater and is called the \'Waterman of India\'.\",\"Rainwater harvesting increases groundwater by guiding water into tanks or soak pits.\",\"Tree plantation helps water seep into ground and reduces soil erosion.\",\"Leaking taps should be fixed and water used only as needed.\",\"Polluted water is harmful; it must be purified before use.\",\"Water conservation is everyone\'s responsibility.\",\"10th May is observed as Water Conservation Day in Maharashtra.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main source of water on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Oceans\",\"Rain\",\"Glaciers\",\"Underground springs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rain is the primary natural source of freshwater that replenishes rivers, lakes, and groundwater.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'Water Literacy\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Knowing how to swim\",\"Understanding water\'s importance, saving it, and increasing groundwater\",\"Building dams only\",\"Drinking bottled water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water literacy involves understanding water\'s value, conserving it, and enhancing groundwater levels.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint emphasized building water reservoirs and planting trees in his work \'Dnyaneshwari\'?\",\"options\":[\"Tukaram\",\"Eknath\",\"Dnyaneshwar\",\"Namdev\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Saint Dnyaneshwar advocated for water reservoirs and afforestation in \'Dnyaneshwari\'.\"},{\"q\":\"What are johads?\",\"options\":[\"Concrete dams\",\"Small earthen dams to store rainwater\",\"Water purification plants\",\"Desert sand filters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Johads are traditional earthen check dams built by Dr. Rajendra Singh to harvest rainwater.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the government send water to Latur by train?\",\"options\":[\"To celebrate a festival\",\"Due to severe water shortage\",\"To test train speed\",\"For industrial use\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Latur faced extreme water scarcity, so water was transported by train to meet basic needs.\"},{\"q\":\"Which practice helps increase groundwater level?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting trees\",\"Building concrete roads\",\"Rainwater harvesting via soak pits\",\"Using more groundwater\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Allowing rainwater to seep into the ground through soak pits increases groundwater storage.\"},{\"q\":\"What is one way to save water at home?\",\"options\":[\"Leave taps running\",\"Use excess water for washing\",\"Fix leaking taps promptly\",\"Ignore pipe leaks\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Repairing leaking taps prevents wastage and conserves water effectively.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a cause of water pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Industrial waste\",\"Sewage discharge\",\"Planting trees\",\"Chemical fertilizers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Planting trees helps prevent erosion and improves water quality; it does not pollute water.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of Vanarai bunds?\",\"options\":[\"To generate electricity\",\"To stop water in streams and let it seep into ground\",\"To store drinking water in cities\",\"To prevent earthquakes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Vanarai bunds are low-cost structures that check water flow in drains to recharge groundwater.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is 10th May significant in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Maharashtra Day\",\"Water Conservation Day\",\"Teachers\' Day\",\"Environment Day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"10th May is observed as Water Conservation Day in Maharashtra to promote water-saving practices.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22235,9,3,'EVS','en','Indian Tradition and Cultural Diversity','[\"India has many cultures, languages, foods, clothes, and festivals.\",\"Culture is the way of life of people in a society.\",\"Festivals are connected with farming and seasons.\",\"Bail Pola is a festival where bulls are worshipped and decorated.\",\"Bail Pola is known by different names in different states.\",\"Diwali, Eid, Christmas, Navroz, and Guru Nanak Jayanti are festivals of different religions.\",\"Independence Day and Republic Day are national festivals.\",\"People wear different clothes like saree, dhoti, turban, salwar-kameez.\",\"Food varies by region: Punjab eats dal-roti, coastal areas eat rice-fish, Maharashtra is famous for puran poli.\",\"Traditional sports like kabaddi, kho-kho, wrestling, and mallakhamb are played in India.\",\"Folk arts like Lavani, Bhangra, Garba, Bihu, and Yakshagana represent state identities.\",\"Folk artists like Sant Tukaram and Annabhau Sathe spread messages of equality and kindness.\",\"Despite diversity, India shows unity in diversity.\",\"Fairs and festivals bring people together and strengthen unity.\",\"Art, music, dance, and sports help preserve culture and teach values.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does culture include?\",\"options\":[\"Only language and food\",\"Language, clothing, food, festivals, religion, art, skills\",\"Only festivals and clothes\",\"Only religion and worship\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Culture includes many aspects of life like language, clothing, food, festivals, religion, art, skills, and celebrations.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is India called an agricultural country?\",\"options\":[\"Because it has many cities\",\"Because farming is the main occupation\",\"Because it exports clothes\",\"Because it has many festivals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Farming is the main occupation in India, so it is called an agricultural country.\"},{\"q\":\"What is Bail Pola?\",\"options\":[\"A festival of lights\",\"A festival where bulls are worshipped\",\"A harvest dance\",\"A New Year celebration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bail Pola is a festival where bulls are worshipped and decorated for their help in farming.\"},{\"q\":\"In which state is Bail Pola known as \'Mattu Pongal\'?\",\"options\":[\"Karnataka\",\"Tamil Nadu\",\"Telangana\",\"Maharashtra\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In Tamil Nadu, Bail Pola is celebrated as Mattu Pongal.\"},{\"q\":\"Which festival is celebrated by Parsis?\",\"options\":[\"Diwali\",\"Eid\",\"Navroz\",\"Christmas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Navroz is the festival celebrated by the Parsi community.\"},{\"q\":\"What do people in Punjab mostly eat?\",\"options\":[\"Rice and fish\",\"Dal and roti\",\"Idli and sambar\",\"Puran poli\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"People in Punjab mostly eat dal and roti due to their climate and crops.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a traditional sport of India?\",\"options\":[\"Cricket\",\"Football\",\"Kabaddi\",\"Tennis\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Kabaddi is a traditional Indian sport now played worldwide.\"},{\"q\":\"Which folk dance is associated with Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Bhangra\",\"Garba\",\"Lavani\",\"Bihu\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Lavani is a popular folk dance of Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the special feature of India despite its diversity?\",\"options\":[\"Unity in diversity\",\"Same language\",\"Same food\",\"Same clothes\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Despite differences in culture, food, dress, etc., India’s special feature is unity in diversity.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the first Indian to win an Olympic medal after independence?\",\"options\":[\"Sachin Tendulkar\",\"Khashaba Jadhav\",\"Viswanathan Anand\",\"Mary Kom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Khashaba Jadhav won a bronze medal in wrestling at the 1952 Helsinki Olympics, the first Olympic medal for independent India.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22236,9,3,'EVS','en','Communication','[\"Communication is the exchange of information and messages.\",\"People used pigeons, messengers on horses or camels to send messages long ago.\",\"Postal service helps send letters and parcels within the country and to other countries.\",\"Speed Post is a service for fast delivery of important letters.\",\"The telephone was invented by Alexander Graham Bell.\",\"Mobile phones are the modern form of telephones and allow easy talking.\",\"Radio, television, magazines, and newspapers are other means of communication.\",\"Postage stamps often show pictures of important people and events.\",\"The first post office in India started in Kolkata in 1774.\",\"The first train in India ran from Mumbai to Thane on 16 April 1853.\",\"The first metro train in India started in Kolkata.\",\"During floods, parachutes are used to send help when other transport cannot reach.\",\"Satellites are used worldwide for communication.\",\"Birsa Munda was a revolutionary leader and his image is on a postal stamp.\",\"Tata Airlines, the first civil airline in India, started in Mumbai in 1932.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is communication?\",\"options\":[\"Exchange of goods\",\"Exchange of information and messages\",\"Traveling from one place to another\",\"Playing games\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Communication means sharing information or messages with others.\"},{\"q\":\"Which bird was used in olden times to send messages?\",\"options\":[\"Eagle\",\"Pigeon\",\"Parrot\",\"Sparrow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pigeons were trained to carry messages in the past.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the modern form of the telephone?\",\"options\":[\"Radio\",\"Mobile phone\",\"Television\",\"Computer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The mobile phone is the most modern form of the telephone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which service delivers letters quickly in India?\",\"options\":[\"Post\",\"Speed Post\",\"Telegram\",\"Email\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Speed Post is used for fast delivery of important letters.\"},{\"q\":\"Where did the first post office in India start?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Kolkata\",\"Chennai\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first post office in India started in Kolkata in 1774.\"},{\"q\":\"Who invented the telephone?\",\"options\":[\"Thomas Edison\",\"Alexander Graham Bell\",\"Marconi\",\"Wright Brothers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Alexander Graham Bell invented the telephone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a means of communication?\",\"options\":[\"Radio\",\"Newspaper\",\"Bicycle\",\"Television\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Bicycle is a vehicle for transport, not communication.\"},{\"q\":\"What do postage stamps often show?\",\"options\":[\"Animals only\",\"Pictures of important people and events\",\"Cartoons\",\"Flags only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Postage stamps often have pictures of important people and events.\"},{\"q\":\"Which vehicle was used by kings to send messages long ago?\",\"options\":[\"Car\",\"Horse or camel\",\"Train\",\"Aeroplane\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Kings used messengers who travelled on horses or camels to send messages.\"},{\"q\":\"What helps in communication during floods when transport fails?\",\"options\":[\"Boats\",\"Parachutes\",\"Trains\",\"Cars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Parachutes are used to send essential items during floods when other transport cannot reach.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22237,9,3,'EVS','en','Historical Monuments','[\"Historical monuments are old buildings or places that tell us about the past.\",\"Examples include forts, palaces, temples, caves, museums, and birthplaces of great people.\",\"Shivneri Fort is the birthplace of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj.\",\"Ramtek has a temple of Lord Shri Ram and Nagardhan Fort nearby.\",\"Phule Wada in Pune is the house of Mahatma and Savitribai Phule, now a heritage site.\",\"Savitribai Phule Pune University is named after Savitribai Phule.\",\"Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar Museum is located on Senapati Bapat Road, Pune.\",\"Lonar Lake in Buldhana was formed by a meteor impact.\",\"Sindhudurg Fort is a sea fort in the Konkan region with a well of fresh water inside.\",\"Raigad Fort was the capital of Swarajya and the site of Shivaji Maharaj\'s coronation.\",\"Heritage tours help us learn about old monuments and objects.\",\"We must protect historical places by not writing on them, littering, or throwing garbage.\",\"We should not throw plastic bottles or damage walls at historical sites.\",\"Photos of visited historical places can be displayed in classroom exhibitions.\",\"Students can make charts of historical places they have visited.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which fort is known as the birthplace of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj?\",\"options\":[\"Raigad Fort\",\"Sindhudurg Fort\",\"Shivneri Fort\",\"Nagardhan Fort\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shivneri Fort is where Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj was born.\"},{\"q\":\"What is special about the well inside Sindhudurg Fort?\",\"options\":[\"It has saltwater\",\"It is dry\",\"It has fresh, drinkable water\",\"It is used for washing clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sindhudurg Fort has a well with fresh, drinkable water inside it.\"},{\"q\":\"Which lake was formed by a meteor impact?\",\"options\":[\"Lonar Lake\",\"Lonar Lake\",\"Lonar Lake\",\"Lonar Lake\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lonar Lake in Buldhana was formed by a meteor impact.\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar Museum located?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Nagpur\",\"Senapati Bapat Road, Pune\",\"Aurangabad\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The museum is on Senapati Bapat Road in Pune.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the university in Pune named after Savitribai Phule?\",\"options\":[\"Pune University\",\"Savitribai Phule Pune University\",\"Mahatma Phule University\",\"Deccan College\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The university is named Savitribai Phule Pune University.\"},{\"q\":\"Which place is known as the house of Mahatma and Savitribai Phule and is a heritage site?\",\"options\":[\"Shivneri Fort\",\"Phule Wada\",\"Raigad Fort\",\"Lonar Lake\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Phule Wada in Pune is their house and a declared heritage site.\"},{\"q\":\"What should we NOT do when visiting a historical place?\",\"options\":[\"Take photos\",\"Listen to guides\",\"Write our names on walls\",\"Buy souvenirs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"We must not write our names on historical monuments.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort is located on a hill and associated with Lord Shri Ram?\",\"options\":[\"Sindhudurg Fort\",\"Shivneri Fort\",\"Nagardhan Fort\",\"Raigad Fort\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nagardhan Fort (also called Nandivardhan Fort) is on a hill near Ramtek.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity is suggested for students to learn about historical places?\",\"options\":[\"Watching TV\",\"Playing video games\",\"Visiting a historical place and writing about it\",\"Sleeping in class\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Students are asked to visit a historical place and write information about it.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a heritage tour?\",\"options\":[\"A tour to buy things\",\"A tour to eat food\",\"Visiting old monuments to learn about them\",\"A tour to play games\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A heritage tour means visiting old monuments and learning about them.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22238,9,3,'EVS','en','Stories of Courage and Revolution','[\"Mahatma Gandhi started the Quit India movement in 1942.\",\"Shirishkumar was a 16-year-old student who led a protest in Nandurbar in 1942.\",\"Shirishkumar and his friends refused to stop the rally when police ordered them to.\",\"Police opened fire on the student rally on 9 September 1942, and Shirishkumar and his friends died.\",\"Shanti Ghosh and Suniti Choudhary were schoolgirls from Bengal who joined the revolutionary group Yugantar.\",\"They attacked a British officer named Stevens in Comilla who was beating protesters.\",\"Shanti Ghosh and Suniti Choudhary were sentenced to life imprisonment but released in 1939.\",\"Babu Genu Said laid down in front of a truck carrying foreign goods during the 1930 boycott in Mumbai.\",\"He was holding the Tiranga (Indian flag) when the truck ran over him, sacrificing his life.\",\"Umaji Naik started a revolt against British rule in 1818 in Maharashtra.\",\"He inspired later revolutionaries like Vasudev Balwant Phadke and the Chapekar Brothers.\",\"Vinayak Damodar Savarkar was inspired by the Chapekar Brothers to fight for India’s freedom.\",\"Karmaveer Bhaurao Patil founded the Rayat Shikshan Sanstha.\",\"He started hostels and the \'Earn and Learn\' scheme for students to work and study together.\",\"Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Indian Constitution.\",\"The Drafting Committee was formed on 21 August 1947 under Dr. Rajendra Prasad’s leadership.\",\"The final draft of the Constitution was presented on 26 November 1949.\",\"The Constitution came into force on 26 January 1950.\",\"26 November is celebrated as Constitution Day in India.\",\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad was the Chairman of the Constituent Assembly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In which year did Mahatma Gandhi start the Quit India movement?\",\"options\":[\"1940\",\"1942\",\"1945\",\"1947\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Gandhi started the Quit India movement in 1942.\"},{\"q\":\"Where did Shirishkumar lead a student protest against British rule?\",\"options\":[\"Pune\",\"Nandurbar\",\"Comilla\",\"Mahalunge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shirishkumar led a protest in Nandurbar district of Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"Which revolutionary group did Shanti Ghosh and Suniti Choudhary join?\",\"options\":[\"Yugantar\",\"Anushilan Samiti\",\"Abhinav Bharat\",\"Ghadar Party\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shanti Ghosh and Suniti Choudhary worked with the revolutionary group Yugantar.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Babu Genu Said hold in his hand when he laid down before the truck?\",\"options\":[\"A lathi\",\"A book\",\"The Tiranga\",\"A flower\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Babu Genu Said was holding the Tiranga (Indian flag) when he sacrificed his life.\"},{\"q\":\"Who founded the Rayat Shikshan Sanstha?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Karmaveer Bhaurao Patil\",\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\",\"Umaji Naik\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Karmaveer Bhaurao Patil founded the Rayat Shikshan Sanstha.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the name of the scheme started by Karmaveer Bhaurao Patil where students could work and study?\",\"options\":[\"Learn and Earn\",\"Earn and Learn\",\"Study and Serve\",\"Work and Grow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Karmaveer Bhaurao Patil introduced the \'Earn and Learn\' scheme.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Indian Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad\",\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\",\"Sardar Patel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee.\"},{\"q\":\"On which date did the Constitution of India come into force?\",\"options\":[\"15 August 1947\",\"26 January 1950\",\"26 November 1949\",\"2 October 1948\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Constitution came into force on 26 January 1950.\"},{\"q\":\"Who started a revolt against British rule in 1818 in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Vasudev Balwant Phadke\",\"Umaji Naik\",\"Chapekar Brothers\",\"Savarkar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Umaji Naik started a revolt against British rule in 1818 in Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"What slogan did Mahatma Gandhi give against the British?\",\"options\":[\"Satyameva Jayate\",\"Do or Die\",\"Jai Hind\",\"Vande Mataram\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Gandhi gave the slogan \'Do or Die\' during the Quit India movement.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22239,9,3,'EVS','en','Environmental Protection: My Responsibility','[\"Cleanliness means keeping ourselves, homes, and surroundings clean to stay healthy.\",\"Throwing garbage in proper places and managing drainage water helps prevent diseases.\",\"Clean surroundings are pleasant, fresh, and make us happy.\",\"Mahatma Gandhi Jayanti is celebrated on 2nd October with cleanliness drives.\",\"Ancient cities like Harappa and Mohenjo-daro had good drainage and public baths.\",\"Sant Gadge Baba promoted cleanliness by carrying a broom and cleaning villages.\",\"Sant Gadge Baba Gram Swachhta Abhiyan focuses on clean water, homes, hygiene, and garbage disposal.\",\"World Environment Day is celebrated on 5th June every year.\",\"Devarai are sacred forests protected in the name of God to preserve biodiversity.\",\"Trees give us oxygen, fruits, wood, medicine, prevent soil erosion, and give shelter to birds.\",\"We should plant trees and take care of them; cutting trees harms the environment.\",\"Plants need water, air, and sunlight to live and can feel touch, heat, and cold.\",\"Chipko Movement (1973) was a forest conservation movement where people hugged trees.\",\"Saalumarada Thimmakka planted thousands of trees and was honoured with Padma Shri.\",\"National flag of India has saffron, white, and green colours symbolizing courage, peace, and prosperity.\"]','[{\"q\":\"On which date is Mahatma Gandhi Jayanti celebrated?\",\"options\":[\"1st October\",\"2nd October\",\"3rd October\",\"4th October\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Gandhi Jayanti is celebrated on 2nd October every year.\"},{\"q\":\"What does Sant Gadge Baba always carry with him?\",\"options\":[\"A bag\",\"A broom\",\"A stick\",\"A book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sant Gadge Baba always carried a broom to clean the villages.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a sacred forest protected in the name of God?\",\"options\":[\"Devarai\",\"Park\",\"Garden\",\"Field\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Devarai is a sacred forest protected in the name of God to preserve biodiversity.\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is World Environment Day celebrated?\",\"options\":[\"5th May\",\"5th June\",\"5th July\",\"5th August\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"World Environment Day is celebrated on 5th June every year.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Saalumarada Thimmakka say about trees?\",\"options\":[\"Trees are like our enemies\",\"Trees are like our children\",\"Trees are useless\",\"Trees should be cut\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Saalumarada Thimmakka said, \'Trees are like our children\'.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ancient Indian cities had well-planned roads and drainage systems?\",\"options\":[\"Harappa and Mohenjo-daro\",\"Delhi and Mumbai\",\"Chennai and Kolkata\",\"Pune and Nagpur\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Harappa and Mohenjo-daro had well-planned roads, drainage, and public baths.\"},{\"q\":\"What is one benefit of planting trees?\",\"options\":[\"Increases pollution\",\"Causes soil erosion\",\"Gives us fruits and oxygen\",\"Dries up water sources\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Trees give us fruits, oxygen, wood, medicine, and prevent soil erosion.\"},{\"q\":\"Who proved that plants can feel touch and respond to stimuli?\",\"options\":[\"Albert Einstein\",\"Dr. Jagadish Chandra Bose\",\"Isaac Newton\",\"Marie Curie\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dr. Jagadish Chandra Bose proved that plants have feelings and can sense touch, heat, and cold.\"},{\"q\":\"What should we do with garbage to keep the environment clean?\",\"options\":[\"Throw it anywhere\",\"Burn it\",\"Keep it in proper place\",\"Leave it on roads\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Throwing garbage in the proper place helps keep the environment clean.\"},{\"q\":\"Which movement involved people hugging trees to save them from being cut?\",\"options\":[\"Chipko Movement\",\"Swachh Bharat\",\"Green Revolution\",\"White Revolution\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Chipko Movement (1973) was a forest conservation movement where people hugged trees to protect them.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22240,9,3,'EVS','en','National Integration','[\"National unity means staying together despite differences.\",\"India is a diverse country with many languages, cultures, and festivals.\",\"We respect and care for each other in our classroom, just like in the country.\",\"The Indian National Flag has saffron, white, and green colours with the Ashoka Chakra in the centre.\",\"Saffron stands for courage and sacrifice.\",\"White stands for peace and truth.\",\"Green stands for faith and chivalry.\",\"The Ashoka Chakra has 24 spokes and represents progress.\",\"The national bird of India is the peacock.\",\"The national flower of India is the lotus.\",\"The national fruit of India is the mango.\",\"The national anthem of India is \'Jana Gana Mana\'.\",\"The national song of India is \'Vande Mataram\'.\",\"Independence Day is celebrated on 15 August every year.\",\"Republic Day is celebrated on 26 January every year.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the saffron colour in the Indian flag represent?\",\"options\":[\"Peace and truth\",\"Courage and sacrifice\",\"Faith and chivalry\",\"Progress\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Saffron stands for courage and sacrifice.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the wheel in the centre of the Indian National Flag?\",\"options\":[\"Sudarshan Chakra\",\"Ashoka Chakra\",\"Param Vir Chakra\",\"Jalachakra\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The wheel is called the Ashoka Chakra.\"},{\"q\":\"Which day is celebrated as Independence Day in India?\",\"options\":[\"15 August\",\"26 January\",\"1 May\",\"2 October\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Independence Day is celebrated on 15 August to mark freedom from British rule.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the national flower of India?\",\"options\":[\"Rose\",\"Lotus\",\"Marigold\",\"Jasmine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The lotus is the national flower of India.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the first astronaut of India?\",\"options\":[\"Rakesh Sharma\",\"Kalpana Chawla\",\"Sunita Williams\",\"Ravish Malhotra\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Rakesh Sharma became India’s first astronaut in 1984.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is the national anthem of India?\",\"options\":[\"Vande Mataram\",\"Jana Gana Mana\",\"Sare Jahan Se Achha\",\"Mile Sur Mera Tumhara\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Jana Gana Mana is the national anthem of India.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the national fruit of India?\",\"options\":[\"Apple\",\"Banana\",\"Mango\",\"Orange\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The mango is the national fruit of India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which bird is the national bird of India?\",\"options\":[\"Parrot\",\"Peacock\",\"Sparrow\",\"Crow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The peacock is the national bird of India.\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is Republic Day celebrated in India?\",\"options\":[\"15 August\",\"26 January\",\"2 October\",\"1 May\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Republic Day is celebrated on 26 January when the Constitution came into effect.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Ashoka Chakra in the flag symbolize?\",\"options\":[\"Peace\",\"Progress\",\"Power\",\"Prosperity\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Ashoka Chakra has 24 spokes and represents continuous progress.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22241,9,3,'EVS','en','Let\'s Listen, Speak and Understand','[\"Unity means working together as a team.\",\"When we are united, we can solve difficult problems easily.\",\"The story of sparrows and mouse shows how teamwork helps in trouble.\",\"The swans and tortoise holding a stick together is a symbol of unity.\",\"The Indian Blind Women’s Cricket Team winning world championships shows strength in unity.\",\"Team games like cricket teach us to cooperate and support each other.\",\"Respecting others, including differently-abled people, is part of being united.\",\"Keeping the environment clean by using proper bins shows care for surroundings.\",\"Planting trees is a group activity that helps nature and community.\",\"We should speak up if someone touches us inappropriately and tell a trusted adult.\",\"The POCSO e-box helps children report unsafe touching.\",\"National Anthem should be listened to with respect and silence.\",\"We should not blame ourselves if someone behaves badly toward us.\",\"Helping friends in need, like pushing a wheelchair, is an act of kindness and unity.\",\"Small actions like disposing wrappers properly contribute to a clean and united society.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the story of sparrows and mouse teach us?\",\"options\":[\"Birds can fly high\",\"Mice are afraid of birds\",\"Unity is strength\",\"Nets are made of thread\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The sparrows were freed only because the mouse and friends helped together.\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two swans and tortoise doing in the temple picture?\",\"options\":[\"Fighting over food\",\"Dancing in water\",\"Holding a stick together\",\"Swimming in a circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"They are holding a stick in their beaks and mouth, showing teamwork.\"},{\"q\":\"Which team became world champions in blind cricket?\",\"options\":[\"Indian Men’s Cricket Team\",\"Indian Blind Women’s Cricket Team\",\"Maharashtra School Team\",\"Australia Women’s Team\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Indian Blind Women’s Cricket Team won the Global Blind Cricket tournament.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if someone touches you inappropriately?\",\"options\":[\"Keep quiet and ignore it\",\"Blame yourself for it\",\"Tell someone you trust\",\"Give them a gift to stop\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"You must tell a trusted adult and not blame yourself.\"},{\"q\":\"How should we behave when the National Anthem is played in school?\",\"options\":[\"Talk loudly with friends\",\"Run around the classroom\",\"Stand silently with respect\",\"Cover our ears and close eyes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"We should stand still and listen to the National Anthem respectfully.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do with biscuit or chocolate wrappers?\",\"options\":[\"Throw them on the ground\",\"Keep them in your pocket forever\",\"Put them in the dustbin\",\"Give them to animals to eat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Wrappers should be disposed of properly in bins to keep surroundings clean.\"},{\"q\":\"How can friends help a differently-abled student in school?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore them during games\",\"Laugh at their wheelchair\",\"Help them move by taking them out in a wheelchair\",\"Tell teachers to scold them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Friends can assist by helping them move, such as taking them out in a wheelchair.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Indian Blind Women’s Cricket Team’s success show?\",\"options\":[\"Only boys can play cricket well\",\"Visually challenged people cannot play sports\",\"Teamwork and unity lead to success\",\"Cricket is not a team game\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Their victory shows that unity and teamwork bring pride and success.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is planting trees considered a group activity?\",\"options\":[\"It needs only one person to dig\",\"It is done only in forests\",\"It helps the environment and is often done together\",\"It harms the soil and water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tree plantation is usually done in groups and benefits nature and community.\"},{\"q\":\"What does keeping garbage in proper bins show?\",\"options\":[\"We don’t care about cleanliness\",\"We are polluting the area\",\"We respect our surroundings and stay united in cleanliness\",\"Bins are useless for waste\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using wet and dry waste bins properly shows responsibility and care for the environment.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22242,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Save Nature','[\"Bunds are structures made of stones or soil to stop flowing water in rivers or streams.\",\"Bunds are also built to store water in fields for farming.\",\"Ponds and tanks onds and tanks on forts were built in old times to store water for people living there.\",\"Old tanks (haud) were used to store water for drinking, farming, and construction.\",\"Some old tanks (haud) are still in use today in certain places.\",\"Baramotichi Vihir in Limb, Satara district is a historical well with stone construction and many steps.\",\"It is an example of Shivkal architecture and attracts many tourists.\",\"About 71% of Earth\'s surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\",\"Very little of Earth\'s water is fresh and even less is potable (safe to drink).\",\"If all Earth\'s water were in a barrel, fresh water would fill only a cup, and potable water just a drop.\",\"Water is precious and essential for life; no living being can survive without it.\",\"We must use water appropriately and avoid wasting even a single drop.\",\"Activities like leaving taps open, overusing water in washing, etc., lead to water wastage.\",\"We should use water sparingly, conserve it, and recycle it whenever possible.\",\"Water stored for drinking or home use does not become stale if kept properly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are bunds made of?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic and cement\",\"Stones or soil\",\"Metal and glass\",\"Wood and cloth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bunds are built up of stones or soil to stop flowing water.\"},{\"q\":\"Where were ponds and tanks built in earlier times?\",\"options\":[\"In schools\",\"On forts\",\"In markets\",\"On hills only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ponds and tanks were dug on forts for people living there.\"},{\"q\":\"What is an old tank (haud) used for?\",\"options\":[\"Only for decoration\",\"For drinking, farming, and construction\",\"Only for fishing\",\"For storing grains\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Old tanks (haud) store water used for drinking, farming, and construction.\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of Earth\'s surface is covered by water?\",\"options\":[\"About 50%\",\"About 61%\",\"About 71%\",\"About 81%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Approximately 71% of Earth\'s surface is covered by water.\"},{\"q\":\"If all Earth\'s water were in a barrel, how much would be fresh water?\",\"options\":[\"Half the barrel\",\"One cup\",\"One drop\",\"None\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fresh water would fill only a cup from the barrel of total water.\"},{\"q\":\"How much potable water is available from sources like rivers and wells in the barrel analogy?\",\"options\":[\"Half a cup\",\"One cup\",\"One drop\",\"No water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Available potable water would be just a single drop in the barrel analogy.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is water called \'Life\'?\",\"options\":[\"Because it is blue\",\"Because no living being can survive without it\",\"Because it is found everywhere\",\"Because it is free\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water is called \'Life\' because no living being can survive without it.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following helps in water conservation?\",\"options\":[\"Leaving taps open\",\"Overusing water in washing\",\"Using water sparingly\",\"Wasting water in cleaning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Using water sparingly is a way to conserve water.\"},{\"q\":\"What should we do to prevent water wastage at home?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore leaks\",\"Use more water for cleaning\",\"Recycle water whenever possible\",\"Store water in open containers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Recycling water whenever possible helps prevent wastage.\"},{\"q\":\"Is it true that stored water for drinking becomes stale quickly?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, it becomes stale in a day\",\"No, it does not go stale if stored properly\",\"Only if stored in metal\",\"Only in summer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water stored for drinking or home use does not go \'stale\' if kept properly.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22243,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Our Body','[\"Our body has external organs (outside) like hands, legs, eyes, ears, nose, skin, tongue.\",\"Our body has internal organs (inside) like brain, heart, lungs, liver, stomach, intestines.\",\"External organs help us interact with the surroundings; internal organs work inside the body.\",\"Hands and legs are motor organs (Karmendriya) used for movement and work.\",\"Eyes, ears, nose, skin, and tongue are sensory organs that help us sense the world.\",\"The brain is inside the skull, processes information, and controls emotions and actions.\",\"The heart pumps blood throughout the body and is slightly left of the chest centre.\",\"Lungs supply oxygen to the body and work with the heart to purify blood.\",\"The stomach churns food with help from the liver; digestion starts here.\",\"The small intestine absorbs nutrients and water from food into the blood.\",\"The large intestine absorbs water from waste and stores feces before expulsion.\",\"The rectum stores feces temporarily; the anus expels waste from the body.\",\"Oesophagus is the food pipe connecting mouth to stomach.\",\"Internal organs perform specific functions like digestion, circulation, respiration, and excretion.\",\"We must take care of our body by eating well, exercising, and keeping clean.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an external organ?\",\"options\":[\"Brain\",\"Heart\",\"Lungs\",\"Skin\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Skin is an external organ as it covers the outside of the body.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main function of the heart?\",\"options\":[\"To think\",\"To pump blood\",\"To breathe\",\"To digest food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The heart works like a pump to circulate blood throughout the body.\"},{\"q\":\"Which organs help us sense our surroundings?\",\"options\":[\"Hands and legs\",\"Brain and heart\",\"Eyes, ears, nose, skin, tongue\",\"Stomach and intestines\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Eyes, ears, nose, skin, and tongue are sensory organs that detect surroundings.\"},{\"q\":\"Where is the brain located in the body?\",\"options\":[\"In the chest\",\"In the skull\",\"In the stomach\",\"In the legs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The brain is located inside the skull, which protects it.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens in the small intestine?\",\"options\":[\"Food is churned\",\"Water is stored\",\"Nutrients and water are absorbed\",\"Oxygen is supplied\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In the small intestine, nutrients and water from food are absorbed into the blood.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT an internal organ?\",\"options\":[\"Liver\",\"Stomach\",\"Tongue\",\"Lungs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tongue is an external organ as it is visible outside the body.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the lungs?\",\"options\":[\"To pump blood\",\"To digest food\",\"To supply oxygen\",\"To think\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Lungs supply oxygen to the entire body, which is needed to purify blood.\"},{\"q\":\"Which organ stores feces temporarily before expulsion?\",\"options\":[\"Stomach\",\"Small intestine\",\"Large intestine\",\"Rectum\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The rectum is the last part of the large intestine where feces are stored for a while.\"},{\"q\":\"Hands and legs are called motor organs because they help in:\",\"options\":[\"Thinking\",\"Sensing\",\"Movement and work\",\"Digestion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Hands and legs are motor organs (Karmendriya) used to perform daily tasks like walking and picking up objects.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true about internal organs?\",\"options\":[\"They are visible from outside\",\"They include eyes and ears\",\"They perform functions like digestion and circulation\",\"They are called sensory organs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Internal organs are inside the body and perform functions like digestion, circulation, respiration, and excretion.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22244,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Planets and Stars','[\"Stars are self-luminous objects that sparkle in the sky.\",\"Planets do not have their own light and reflect sunlight.\",\"The Sun is a star and the center of the solar system.\",\"Earth is a planet that revolves around the Sun and rotates on its axis.\",\"The Moon is Earth\'s natural satellite.\",\"There are eight planets in the solar system: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.\",\"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun.\",\"Venus is the second planet from the Sun.\",\"Earth is the third planet and has life.\",\"Mars is the fourth planet, known as the red planet.\",\"Jupiter is the largest planet in the solar system.\",\"Saturn has prominent rings around it.\",\"Uranus rotates on its side with an axial tilt of about 98°.\",\"Neptune is the farthest planet from the Sun.\",\"Asteroids are small rocky bodies found mainly between Mars and Jupiter.\",\"Dwarf planets, like Pluto, orbit the Sun but are not full planets.\",\"Comets are icy bodies that develop tails when near the Sun.\",\"One Astronomical Unit (AU) is the average Earth-Sun distance: ~149 million km.\",\"Inner planets (Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars) are rocky and lie inside the asteroid belt.\",\"Outer planets (Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune) are large and gaseous.\",\"Earth\'s rotation causes day and night; one rotation takes about 24 hours (a day).\",\"Earth\'s revolution around the Sun causes a year; one revolution takes about 365 days.\",\"The Moon\'s phases change due to its changing position relative to Earth and Sun.\",\"Phases include new moon, crescent, quarter, gibbous, and full moon.\",\"Waxing moon: illuminated part increases (Shukla Paksha).\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a star?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\",\"Moon\",\"Sun\",\"Mars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Sun is a star because it produces its own light and heat through nuclear fusion.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sequence of planets from the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune\",\"Venus, Mercury, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Neptune, Uranus\",\"Earth, Venus, Mercury, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune\",\"Mercury, Earth, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Uranus, Saturn, Neptune\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The correct order of planets from the Sun is Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.\"},{\"q\":\"What causes day and night on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\'s revolution around the Sun\",\"Earth\'s rotation on its axis\",\"The Moon\'s orbit around Earth\",\"Sun\'s movement around Earth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Day and night occur due to Earth\'s rotation on its axis; one full rotation takes about 24 hours.\"},{\"q\":\"Which planet is known for having rings?\",\"options\":[\"Jupiter\",\"Mars\",\"Saturn\",\"Venus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Saturn is known for its prominent ring system made of ice and rock particles.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a satellite?\",\"options\":[\"A star that orbits a planet\",\"A planet that orbits the Sun\",\"A heavenly body that orbits a planet\",\"A comet that orbits the Sun\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A satellite is a heavenly body that revolves around a planet; the Moon is Earth\'s natural satellite.\"},{\"q\":\"Which are the inner planets of the solar system?\",\"options\":[\"Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune\",\"Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars\",\"Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn\",\"Venus, Earth, Saturn, Neptune\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Inner planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars; they are rocky and lie inside the asteroid belt.\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the phases of the Moon?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\'s shadow on the Moon\",\"Moon\'s changing position relative to Earth and Sun\",\"Sun\'s rotation\",\"Moon\'s own light changing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The phases of the Moon occur because we see different amounts of its sunlit side as it orbits Earth.\"},{\"q\":\"How long does Earth take to complete one revolution around the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"24 hours\",\"7 days\",\"365 days\",\"12 hours\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Earth takes approximately 365 days to complete one orbit around the Sun, which defines a year.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the average distance of Earth from the Sun in kilometers?\",\"options\":[\"149 million km\",\"149 billion km\",\"149 thousand km\",\"149 trillion km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The average Earth-Sun distance is 149 million kilometers, also known as one Astronomical Unit (AU).\"},{\"q\":\"Which planet is farthest from the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Uranus\",\"Neptune\",\"Saturn\",\"Jupiter\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Neptune is the eighth and farthest planet from the Sun in our solar system.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22245,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Our Green Companions','[\"Plants are classified as trees, shrubs, herbs, or climbers based on height and stem type.\",\"Trees grow tall with hard stems; shrubs are shorter with hard stems; herbs are short with soft stems; climbers need support.\",\"A flower bud develops into a flower, which after fertilization becomes a fruit containing seeds.\",\"Seeds germinate in soil, producing a sprout and root, then grow into a seedling and eventually a mature plant.\",\"Autotrophic plants make their own food via photosynthesis; heterotrophic plants depend on others for food.\",\"Insectivorous plants trap and digest insects to obtain nutrients.\",\"Simple leaves have a single blade attached to the stem; compound leaves have multiple leaflets.\",\"Taproot is a thick main root with smaller branches; fibrous roots are many thin roots from the stem base.\",\"Roots absorb water and nutrients from soil and anchor the plant.\",\"Leaves are the main site of photosynthesis, where food is made using sunlight, air, water, and nutrients.\",\"Plants provide food, clothing, shelter, shade, and medicine to humans and animals.\",\"Medicinal plants like Neem, Tulsi, Aloe Vera, and Basil are used in traditional healing.\",\"Plants have varying lifespans: some live months (e.g., wheat), others years (e.g., mango), and some like banyan live for centuries.\",\"Seed balls are a method to grow plants by mixing seeds with soil and dung, then scattering after rain.\",\"We should protect plants by planting trees, avoiding harm, and conserving green spaces.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What are plants that grow tall and have a hard stem called?\",\"options\":[\"Herb\",\"Vine\",\"Shrub\",\"Tree\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Trees are tall plants with hard, woody stems.\"},{\"q\":\"What are plants called that grow on other plants and absorb some food from them?\",\"options\":[\"Autotrophic\",\"Heterotrophic\",\"Insectivorous\",\"Vine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Such plants are heterotrophic as they depend on host plants for food.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the use of the colour and fragrance of flowers to the plant?\",\"options\":[\"To prepare food\",\"To attract insects\",\"For self-protection\",\"To absorb water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Colour and fragrance attract insects for pollination.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a floral bud transform into?\",\"options\":[\"Fruit\",\"Seedling\",\"Flower\",\"Seeds\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A floral bud develops into a flower.\"},{\"q\":\"By which process do plants prepare food?\",\"options\":[\"Respiration\",\"Photosynthesis\",\"Digestion\",\"Absorption\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plants make food through photosynthesis in leaves using sunlight.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main function of roots?\",\"options\":[\"To produce flowers\",\"To absorb water and nutrients\",\"To produce seeds\",\"To produce fruits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Roots absorb water and minerals from soil and anchor the plant.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a simple leaf?\",\"options\":[\"Tamarind\",\"Gulmohar\",\"Mango\",\"Neem\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mango leaf is simple; tamarind, gulmohar, and neem have compound leaves.\"},{\"q\":\"Which plants have fibrous roots?\",\"options\":[\"Mango\",\"Peepal\",\"Wheat\",\"Banyan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Wheat has fibrous roots; mango and peepal have taproots; banyan has both.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an insectivorous plant?\",\"options\":[\"Rose\",\"Pitcher Plant\",\"Mango\",\"Wheat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pitcher plant traps and digests insects for nutrition.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the product of photosynthesis in plants?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon Dioxide\",\"Starch\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Photosynthesis produces glucose (stored as starch) and releases oxygen.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22246,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Fun of Changing Seasons','[\"Seasons change due to Earth\'s revolution around the Sun and the tilt of its axis (23°30\').\",\"When the North Pole tilts toward the Sun, the Northern Hemisphere experiences summer; the Southern Hemisphere experiences winter.\",\"The three major seasons in India are summer, monsoon, and winter.\",\"Summer is hot; we wear cotton clothes, drink more water and cold drinks, and eat fruits like mango and watermelon.\",\"Monsoon (June–September) brings rain; farmers sow crops, and we use umbrellas and raincoats.\",\"Winter is cold; we wear warm clothes, eat dry fruits and fruits like guava, and enjoy Diwali holidays.\",\"Seasonal changes affect human health: fatigue, body aches, and changes in appetite.\",\"Our diet and clothing change with the seasons to stay healthy and comfortable.\",\"Birds migrate seasonally; many come to India in winter for food and breeding.\",\"Tree plantation week is celebrated from July 1 to 7 after monsoon onset.\",\"Farmers plough, sow, harvest, thresh, and winnow grains; then grains are stored, sold, and made into flour for bread.\",\"Food should not be wasted because it is the result of farmers\' hard work.\",\"Food grains like rice, wheat, jowar, and pulses are used differently across regions (Konkan, Vidarbha, etc.).\",\"Seasonal winds cause rain in India; moisture-laden winds from oceans lead to rainfall.\",\"High and low pressure areas due to temperature differences generate winds that bring rain.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What causes the change of seasons on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\'s rotation only\",\"Earth\'s revolution around the Sun and tilt of its axis\",\"Moon\'s gravitational pull\",\"Changes in the Sun\'s temperature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Seasons change due to Earth\'s revolution around the Sun and the tilt of its axis (23°30\'), which causes different parts of Earth to receive varying sunlight throughout the year.\"},{\"q\":\"In which season do farmers in India usually start sowing crops?\",\"options\":[\"Summer\",\"Monsoon\",\"Winter\",\"Spring\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Farmers start sowing crops during the monsoon season (June–September) when rains begin and the soil is moist.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following fruits is commonly eaten in summer?\",\"options\":[\"Guava\",\"Jujube\",\"Mango\",\"Orange\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mango is a summer fruit abundantly available in markets during hot weather.\"},{\"q\":\"What do we wear in winter to protect ourselves from cold?\",\"options\":[\"Cotton clothes\",\"Loose clothes\",\"Warm clothes\",\"Raincoats\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In winter, we wear warm clothes to protect ourselves from the cold air.\"},{\"q\":\"By how many degrees is the Earth\'s axis tilted?\",\"options\":[\"15°\",\"23°30\'\",\"30°\",\"45°\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Earth\'s axis is tilted at 23°30\', which is responsible for the changing seasons.\"},{\"q\":\"Which season is experienced in the Southern Hemisphere when it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere?\",\"options\":[\"Summer\",\"Monsoon\",\"Winter\",\"Spring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"When the North Pole faces the Sun (summer in Northern Hemisphere), the South Pole is tilted away, causing winter in the Southern Hemisphere.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason for rain in India during monsoon?\",\"options\":[\"Local water evaporation\",\"Seasonal winds carrying moisture from the ocean\",\"Melting of snow\",\"Human activities\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Monsoon rains in India are caused by seasonal winds that blow from the ocean, carrying moisture which leads to rainfall.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a seasonal change in human lifestyle?\",\"options\":[\"Change in clothing\",\"Change in diet\",\"Change in language spoken\",\"Change in daily routine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"While clothing, diet, and daily routines change with seasons, the language spoken does not change seasonally.\"},{\"q\":\"Why should we not waste food?\",\"options\":[\"It is expensive\",\"It is bad for health\",\"It is the result of farmers\' hard work\",\"It attracts pests\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Food should not be wasted because it is produced with the hard work of farmers, and wasting it disrespects their effort.\"},{\"q\":\"During which festival do we often eat sweets made from grains and enjoy holidays in winter?\",\"options\":[\"Holi\",\"Diwali\",\"Eid\",\"Christmas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Diwali is celebrated in winter, and we enjoy holidays and eat sweets made from grains and other ingredients during this time.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22247,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Food and Shelter','[\"Agriculture depends on rainwater and varies with rainfall levels.\",\"High rainfall areas grow rice, nachani, varai, coconut.\",\"Moderate rainfall areas grow wheat, tur, soybean.\",\"Low rainfall areas grow jowar, bajra, moong, matki.\",\"Good crops need improved seeds, fertile land, sunlight, and water.\",\"Food grains and vegetables vary with seasons in India.\",\"Farmers prepare land, sow, harvest, thresh, winnow, pack, and transport grains.\",\"Organic farming uses natural fertilizers like dung, compost, and avoids chemicals.\",\"Food preparation methods include roasting, boiling, steaming, frying, etc.\",\"Cooking makes food soft, tasty, and easy to digest; some foods are eaten raw.\",\"Food preservation includes sun drying, storing in ventilated areas, using neem leaves.\",\"Proper storage prevents insect and fungus damage.\",\"Contaminated food is spoiled by microbes, chemicals, or unclean handling.\",\"Effects of contaminated food: stomach ache, diarrhea, vomiting, exhaustion.\",\"Prevent contamination by covering food, using clean water, eating fresh food.\",\"Shelter protects from heat, wind, rain, and danger; animals and humans need it.\",\"Rural houses use mud, bamboo, grass; urban houses use cement, steel, bricks.\",\"Eco-friendly homes use natural materials, renewable energy, recycle water and waste.\",\"Birds build nests to protect eggs and chicks from predators and weather.\",\"Grains like wheat, rice, jowar are preserved year-round for food security.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which crop is mainly grown in high rainfall areas?\",\"options\":[\"Wheat\",\"Jowar\",\"Rice\",\"Bajra\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rice requires high rainfall and is grown in regions like Konkan.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first work a farmer does in the field?\",\"options\":[\"Threshing\",\"Farm preparation\",\"Winnowing\",\"Weeding\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Farm preparation (like ploughing) is the first step before sowing.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a method of food preparation?\",\"options\":[\"Roasting\",\"Boiling\",\"Painting\",\"Steaming\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Painting is not a food preparation method; others are common cooking techniques.\"},{\"q\":\"What is organic farming?\",\"options\":[\"Using chemical fertilizers\",\"Using natural fertilizers and avoiding chemicals\",\"Growing only fruits\",\"Using machines only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Organic farming uses natural inputs like dung and compost and avoids synthetic chemicals.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these foods is often eaten raw?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Dal\",\"Cucumber\",\"Bhakri\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cucumber is commonly eaten raw in salads; others are usually cooked.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are grains dried in the sun before storage?\",\"options\":[\"To increase weight\",\"To remove moisture and prevent spoilage\",\"To change color\",\"To attract insects\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Drying reduces moisture, preventing mold and insect growth during storage.\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is commonly used in rural house construction in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Cement\",\"Steel\",\"Mud\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rural houses use locally available natural materials like mud, bamboo, and grass.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a key feature of eco-friendly homes?\",\"options\":[\"Use of plastic\",\"Extensive use of non-conventional energy like solar\",\"High water wastage\",\"No ventilation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Eco-friendly homes use renewable energy sources such as solar and wind energy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an effect of eating contaminated food?\",\"options\":[\"Increased energy\",\"Improved digestion\",\"Diarrhea and vomiting\",\"Better immunity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Contaminated food can cause gastrointestinal issues like diarrhea and vomiting.\"},{\"q\":\"Which season sees the fastest spoilage of food?\",\"options\":[\"Winter\",\"Summer\",\"Spring\",\"Autumn\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"High temperatures in summer promote microbial growth, causing food to spoil quickly.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22248,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Awareness is Safety','[\"A disaster is a sudden calamity causing immense damage and loss.\",\"Natural disasters include floods, earthquakes, droughts, storms, hailstorms, heavy rainfall, and landslides.\",\"Man-made disasters result from human errors and greed, such as fire, accidents, war, and pollution.\",\"Drought is a water shortage due to insufficient rainfall, leading to problems for people, animals, and agriculture.\",\"Causes of drought: lack of rain, deforestation, concretization preventing water percolation, and wastage of water.\",\"Effects of drought: water scarcity affecting humans, animals, and crops.\",\"Solutions to overcome drought: building lakes, bunds, dams; using drip and sprinkler irrigation; rainwater harvesting; planting trees; growing low-water crops.\",\"During a cyclone at sea, winds blow at very high speed, causing heavy rainfall and high waves.\",\"To stay safe during a cyclone: listen to weather department messages, stay in a safe place, avoid unnecessary travel.\",\"Excessive rain causes rivers and streams to overflow, leading to floods and damage to houses, crops, trees, and animals.\",\"To reduce damage from excessive rain: move to safe or high ground with animals.\",\"Safety symbols help identify dangers; draw and understand symbols seen in daily life.\",\"A life-saving bag should contain essentials like water bottle, dry snacks, torch, and regular medicines for emergencies.\",\"National Disaster Management Day is observed on October 13th every year since 1989 to guide on emergency protection.\",\"Home disasters can occur due to mistakes: short circuit, gas leakage, mobile explosion, improper use of electric heater.\",\"Precautions in kitchen: never play near gas stove when in use; turn off stove and regulator when not in use.\",\"Do not play with fire hazards like candles, lighters, or matchsticks.\",\"Handle electrical appliances safely: do not touch broken wires or damaged switches; never touch appliances with wet hands; adults should use rubber gloves and slippers while repairing.\",\"Stay away from dangerous chemicals like medicines, pesticides, or cleaning agents; wash hands thoroughly if touched accidentally.\",\"Care at heights: walk slowly on stairs, hold railing for support, avoid leaning out of windows or off balconies, and do not go to terrace alone.\",\"Use elevator only with an adult.\",\"Self-safety is the first responsibility; shout for help or use hand signals or write \'SOS!\' if approached by strangers.\",\"Emergency numbers: Police - 100, Fire Brigade - 101, Ambulance - 108\\/102, Women & Child Care - 181, Child Helpline - 1098, Disaster Management Cell - 1077.\",\"Protect environment by planting trees, saving water, reducing waste; treat environment as part of family.\",\"Help visually impaired by acting as a \'Vision-Friend\': guide with instructions, avoid obstacles, ensure safe movement.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the most important identity of a disaster?\",\"options\":[\"It comes with prior notice.\",\"It happens in a limited space.\",\"It only causes economic loss.\",\"It occurs suddenly and causes immense damage.\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"A disaster is defined as a sudden calamity causing immense damage, which matches option 4.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a natural disaster?\",\"options\":[\"War\",\"Pollution\",\"Earthquake\",\"Fire due to short circuit\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Earthquake occurs naturally without human intervention, making it a natural disaster.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if an unknown person offers you snacks?\",\"options\":[\"Talk to them politely.\",\"Take the snack and leave.\",\"Say \'No\' immediately and leave the place.\",\"Ask for their name first.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"For self-safety, one must refuse and leave immediately when approached by strangers offering objects.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity is safe during lightning?\",\"options\":[\"Standing under a tree.\",\"Using a mobile phone outdoors.\",\"Staying inside a concrete building.\",\"Swimming in an open pond.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Staying inside a concrete building protects from lightning strikes; the other options are dangerous.\"},{\"q\":\"Why should we avoid using plastic?\",\"options\":[\"It is very expensive.\",\"It is heavy to carry.\",\"It pollutes the environment and can spread diseases.\",\"It is not available in markets.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plastic pollution harms the environment and can lead to diseases, making option 3 correct.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a man-made disaster?\",\"options\":[\"A disaster caused by heavy rainfall.\",\"A disaster caused by earthquakes.\",\"A disaster caused by human errors and greed damaging nature.\",\"A disaster that occurs without any cause.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Man-made disasters result from human actions like negligence or greed, such as fire, accidents, war, and pollution.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following helps overcome drought?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting down more trees.\",\"Building lakes and bunds.\",\"Using flood irrigation for crops.\",\"Letting rainwater flow away.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Building lakes, bunds, and dams stores water, which helps overcome drought.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if someone tries to pull you by force?\",\"options\":[\"Stay quiet and wait.\",\"Shout loudly, struggle, and run to a crowded place.\",\"Follow them to avoid anger.\",\"Give them your belongings.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One should shout for help, resist, and escape to a safe place like a crowded area or police station.\"},{\"q\":\"Which precaution is correct for using electrical appliances?\",\"options\":[\"Touch switches with wet hands for better grip.\",\"Repair appliances with bare hands.\",\"Never touch broken wires or damaged switches.\",\"Use metal ladders while fixing electrical points.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"For safety, one must avoid touching broken electrical wires or damaged switches to prevent electric shock.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of a life-saving bag?\",\"options\":[\"To carry school books daily.\",\"To store extra clothes for travel.\",\"To keep essential items for emergencies like water, snacks, torch, and medicines.\",\"To use as a pillow during travel.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A life-saving bag contains essentials such as water\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22249,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Clean My Surroundings','[\"Wet waste includes vegetable stalks, fruit peels, dry leaves, eggshells, and spoiled food that rot and decompose.\",\"Dry waste includes plastic, metal, glass, nails, and other materials that do not rot and remain unchanged for years.\",\"A green dustbin is used for wet waste; a blue dustbin is used for dry waste.\",\"Compost fertilizer is made by decomposing wet waste in a pit or container with soil and water over 4–6 weeks.\",\"The 3R principle stands for Reduce, Reuse, and Recycle to minimize waste and protect the environment.\",\"Reduce means using fewer resources, like steel cups instead of plastic or cloth bags instead of tissue paper.\",\"Reuse means using old items again, such as blank paper for rough work or toys in ice cream boxes.\",\"Recycle means making new products from dry waste, like new paper from old newspapers or plastic from bottles.\",\"Improper garbage disposal leads to foul smell, disease spread by houseflies, and health hazards.\",\"Keeping surroundings clean is everyone’s responsibility; garbage must be segregated before disposal.\",\"Farmers use cow dung manure from animal waste to enrich soil for healthy crop growth.\",\"Drawing on walls, wasting water, and littering are bad habits; using dustbins properly is a good habit.\",\"Wet waste from homes and schools can be turned into compost for school gardens.\",\"Dry waste like plastic and paper is sent to factories to be recycled into new products.\",\"Always throw garbage in the correct dustbin to maintain cleanliness and beauty of surroundings.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which color dustbin is used for wet waste?\",\"options\":[\"Red\",\"Green\",\"Blue\",\"Yellow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A green dustbin is used to store wet waste as mentioned in the textbook.\"},{\"q\":\"What is compost fertilizer made from?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic bottles\",\"Metal scraps\",\"Wet waste\",\"Dry leaves only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Compost fertilizer is formed by decomposing wet waste such as food scraps and plant material.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of dry waste?\",\"options\":[\"Banana peel\",\"Eggshell\",\"Plastic bag\",\"Vegetable stalk\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plastic bags do not rot and remain unchanged for years, making them dry waste.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the \'R\' in \'Reduce\' of the 3R principle mean?\",\"options\":[\"Recycle again\",\"Use less\",\"Reuse items\",\"Refuse waste\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Reduce means using products that generate less waste, like steel cups instead of disposable ones.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity is part of \'Reuse\'?\",\"options\":[\"Throwing paper in bin\",\"Using blank notebook pages for rough work\",\"Burning dry leaves\",\"Buying new plastic toys\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using blank paper from notebooks for rough work is reusing old material.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if garbage is left lying around?\",\"options\":[\"It turns into flowers\",\"It smells bad and spreads disease\",\"It becomes compost automatically\",\"It disappears in a day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Garbage causes foul smell and houseflies spread germs, leading to diseases.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a good habit for cleanliness?\",\"options\":[\"Throwing food in green dustbin\",\"Closing a running tap\",\"Drawing on classroom walls\",\"Using cloth bag for shopping\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Scribbling or drawing on walls, tables, or chairs is a bad habit as per the textbook.\"},{\"q\":\"What is made from decomposing wet waste in a pit over 4–6 weeks?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic pellets\",\"Compost fertilizer\",\"Dry waste bricks\",\"Metal rods\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wet waste rots in a pit with soil and water to form compost fertilizer after 4–6 weeks.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement about 3R is correct?\",\"options\":[\"Recycle means using old things again\",\"Reuse means making new things from waste\",\"Reduce means generating more waste\",\"Following 3R keeps surroundings clean and safe\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"By following Reduce, Reuse, and Recycle, waste is reduced and surroundings remain clean and safe for plants and animals.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do with leftover food from your tiffin?\",\"options\":[\"Throw it on the floor\",\"Keep it in bag\",\"Put it in green dustbin\",\"Burn it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Leftover food should be thrown into the green dustbin only, as it is wet waste.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22250,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','In the Wonderful World of Animals','[\"Birds have beaks, legs, wings, and feathers, but their food, habitats, and flying styles differ.\",\"Kingfisher is larger than a sparrow and smaller than a crow, with a long thick reddish-pink beak, brown head, white neck, blue back, reddish-pink feet, and short legs.\",\"Insects help in decomposition, pollination, and serve as food for birds and animals.\",\"Oviparous animals hatch from eggs (e.g., birds, insects, reptiles); viviparous animals give birth to young ones (e.g., humans, cows).\",\"Many insects undergo a life cycle: egg → larva → pupa → adult (e.g., butterfly, firefly).\",\"Food chain shows how living things depend on each other for food (e.g., plants → grasshopper → frog → snake → eagle).\",\"Some animals blend with surroundings using colour and shape for protection (camouflage).\",\"Lifespan varies: butterfly (1–2 weeks), housefly (15–25 days), sparrow (3–5 years), elephant (60–70 years), human (80–100 years).\",\"Diurnal animals are active during the day (e.g., cows, deer); nocturnal animals are active at night (e.g., owls, bats, foxes).\",\"Animals live in groups (herds, flocks, hives) for food, protection, and caring young; some live alone.\",\"Never touch birds, eggs, nests, or insects; use only fallen specimens for collection.\",\"Lac insect produces lac used in bangles, jewelry, and sealing documents.\",\"Silkworms give silk, honeybees give honey and wax, beetles help in decomposition.\",\"State Bird of Maharashtra: Hariyal; State Butterfly: Blue Mormon; State Animal: Shekru; State Fish: Silver Pomfret.\",\"Plants prepare food using sunlight, air, water, and soil nutrients; herbivores eat plants; carnivores eat animals.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the beak colour of a Kingfisher as observed in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Yellow\",\"Reddish-pink\",\"Black\",\"Brown\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states the Kingfisher\'s beak colour is reddish-pink.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a viviparous animal?\",\"options\":[\"Butterfly\",\"Hen\",\"Human\",\"Frog\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Humans give birth to young ones, making them viviparous.\"},{\"q\":\"What do grasshoppers, honeybees, and termites have in common based on the text?\",\"options\":[\"They are carnivores\",\"They are herbivores\",\"They live in water\",\"They lay eggs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text classifies them as herbivores in the fill-in-the-blank exercise.\"},{\"q\":\"Which insect is known for producing lac used in bangles and sealing documents?\",\"options\":[\"Silkworm\",\"Honeybee\",\"Lac insect\",\"Ladybird beetle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The lac insect produces lac, used for bangles, jewelry, and sealing documents.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the correct sequence in a food chain involving a caterpillar, frog, snake, and eagle?\",\"options\":[\"Eagle → Snake → Frog → Caterpillar\",\"Caterpillar → Frog → Snake → Eagle\",\"Frog → Caterpillar → Snake → Eagle\",\"Snake → Eagle → Frog → Caterpillar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The text states: caterpillar eats leaves, frog eats caterpillar, snake eats frog, eagle eats snake.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these animals is nocturnal?\",\"options\":[\"Cow\",\"Deer\",\"Owl\",\"Sparrow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Owls are active at night, making them nocturnal, as mentioned in the text.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the lifespan of a housefly according to the table in the text?\",\"options\":[\"1 to 2 weeks\",\"15 to 25 days\",\"3 to 5 years\",\"60 to 70 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The table lists housefly lifespan as 15 to 25 days.\"},{\"q\":\"Which bird is described as larger than a sparrow and smaller than a crow with a long thick beak?\",\"options\":[\"Peacock\",\"Kingfisher\",\"Crow\",\"Sparrow\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Kingfisher observation point describes it as larger than a sparrow and smaller than a crow with a long thick beak.\"},{\"q\":\"What do insects like termites help in, according to the text?\",\"options\":[\"Producing honey\",\"Making soil from dried plant parts\",\"Producing silk\",\"Pollinating flowers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Termites help make soil by decomposing dried parts of plants.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of insects as per the text?\",\"options\":[\"They have a life cycle of egg—larva—pupa—adult\",\"They are viviparous\",\"They help keep the environment clean\",\"They are food for many birds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Insects are oviparous (lay eggs), not viviparous.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22251,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Let\'s Learn the Map','[\"A map is a proportional drawing of the earth or a part of it.\",\"Maps use directions, conventional signs, symbols, projections, and scale.\",\"Scale shows the ratio between distance on map and actual distance on ground.\",\"Example of scale: 1 cm on map = 1 meter on ground (1 cm : 1 m).\",\"Symbols represent features like post office (PO), spring (+), temple, mosque.\",\"Index or legend explains the meaning of signs and symbols used on a map.\",\"Direction arrows (North, South, East, West) help orient the map correctly.\",\"Natural items on maps include rivers, mountains, coasts; man-made include roads, buildings.\",\"Maharashtra has a coastline of 720 km along the Arabian Sea.\",\"Maharashtra is divided into 6 administrative divisions: Nashik, Pune, Aurangabad, Amravati, Nagpur, Konkan.\",\"Mumbai is the capital and Nagpur is the second capital of Maharashtra.\",\"The Godavari is the longest river in Maharashtra, about 1465 km long.\",\"The Sahyadri Mountains (Western Ghats) run north-south; Kalsubai is the highest peak.\",\"The Konkan region is the coastal strip of Maharashtra.\",\"Maps need a title, scale, direction, and index to be complete and useful.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of using a scale in map making?\",\"options\":[\"To make the map colorful\",\"To show actual distances in reduced form\",\"To draw pictures of animals\",\"To write stories about places\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Scale helps represent real distances on ground in a smaller, proportional way on paper.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is used to show a spring on a map?\",\"options\":[\"ee\",\"ne\",\"+\",\"dA\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The symbol \'+\' is used to represent a spring on maps.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the index or legend on a map help us understand?\",\"options\":[\"The price of the map\",\"The author\'s name\",\"The meaning of signs and symbols\",\"The date of printing\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The index explains what each sign or symbol on the map stands for.\"},{\"q\":\"Which direction is the Arabian Sea located relative to Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"North\",\"South\",\"East\",\"West\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The Arabian Sea lies to the west of Maharashtra state.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the longest river in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Krishna\",\"Godavari\",\"Tapi\",\"Narmada\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Godavari is the longest river in Maharashtra, with about 740 km flowing through the state.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a man-made feature commonly shown on maps?\",\"options\":[\"Mountain\",\"River\",\"Coast\",\"Road\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Roads are man-made structures, while mountains, rivers, and coasts are natural.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the capital of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Nagpur\",\"Pune\",\"Mumbai\",\"Nashik\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mumbai is the capital of Maharashtra; Nagpur is the second capital.\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol is used to represent a post office on a map?\",\"options\":[\"PO\",\"+\",\"Temple\",\"Tree\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"PO is the conventional symbol used for Post Office on maps.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do maps need to be scaled?\",\"options\":[\"To make them look artistic\",\"To fit large areas on paper while keeping proportions\",\"To use more colors\",\"To make them heavier\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Scaling allows large geographical areas to be represented accurately on a small sheet of paper.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a basic element of a map?\",\"options\":[\"Direction\",\"Scale\",\"Symbols\",\"Music\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Direction, scale, and symbols are essential; music is not related to map elements.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22252,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','My Maharashtra','[\"Maharashtra has five main forest types: Tropical Evergreen, Semi-evergreen, Deciduous, Thorny and Shrub, and Mangrove forests.\",\"Tropical Evergreen forests are found in the Western Ghats (Sahyadri) and receive more than 3000 mm annual rainfall.\",\"Semi-evergreen forests are found on the eastern and western slopes of the Sahyadri with rainfall between 1000 to 3000 mm.\",\"Deciduous forests are found in Konkan, eastern low rainfall regions, Sahyadris, Satpura, plateau regions, and eastern part of the state with 600 to 1000 mm rainfall.\",\"Thorny and Shrub forests are found in rain shadow regions with less than 600 mm rainfall; plants have small leaves and thorns to reduce water loss.\",\"Mangrove forests are located in coastal intertidal and estuarine saline zones; examples include red and black mangroves.\",\"Forest distribution depends on rainfall distribution, leading to diversity in forest types across the state.\",\"National Parks and Sanctuaries are marked on the map; examples include Melghat and Tadoba-Andhari (implied from context).\",\"Evergreen forests are found in districts like Thane, Raigad, Ratnagiri, Sindhudurg, and parts of Pune and Nashik (Western Ghats).\",\"Major minerals in Maharashtra include manganese, iron ore, bauxite, limestone, chromite, dolomite, silica, and coal.\",\"Manganese is found in Nagpur, Gondia, Bhandara, Sindhudurg and used in iron and steel industry.\",\"Iron ore is found in Chandrapur, Bhandara, Gadchiroli, Nagpur, Gondia, Sindhudurg and used to produce pure iron and steel.\",\"Bauxite is found in Sindhudurg, Ratnagiri, Kolhapur, Raigad and used for aluminum production.\",\"Limestone is found in Gadchiroli, Yavatmal, Chandrapur, Nagpur, Pune, Ahilyanagar and used in chemical and cement industries.\",\"Major food crops: rice (paddy) in high rainfall areas, jowar, bajra, maize, and pulses in low rainfall areas.\",\"Kharif season is the monsoon farming season; Rabi season is the winter farming season.\",\"State bird: Hariyal (green pigeon); State animal: Shekru (Indian giant squirrel).\",\"Major festivals: August 15 (Independence Day) and January 26 (Republic Day) are national festivals celebrated with enthusiasm.\",\"Musical instruments used in festivals: dhol, tasha, peti (harmonium), tabla, sambal, sanai (clarinet), taal, mrudung, halgi, tarpa.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which type of forest is found in the Western Ghats of Maharashtra and receives more than 3000 mm of annual rainfall?\",\"options\":[\"Thorny and Shrub forest\",\"Deciduous forest\",\"Tropical Evergreen forest\",\"Mangrove forest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tropical Evergreen forests are found in the Western Ghats (Sahyadri) and receive more than 3000 mm annual rainfall, as stated in the textbook.\"},{\"q\":\"On which date is \'Maharashtra Day\' celebrated?\",\"options\":[\"1 May\",\"1 November\",\"1 March\",\"1 December\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Maharashtra Day is celebrated on 1 May every year to commemorate the formation of the state.\"},{\"q\":\"Which is the longest river in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Manjra\",\"Krishna\",\"Godavari\",\"Koyna\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Godavari river is the longest river in Maharashtra, as indicated in the textbook exercise.\"},{\"q\":\"Sugarcane and cotton are classified as which type of crops in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Arable crops\",\"Commercial crops\",\"Industrial crops\",\"Irrigated crops\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sugarcane and cotton are major commercial crops grown for sale and industry, not just for food.\"},{\"q\":\"Which plateau is Maharashtra a part of?\",\"options\":[\"Deccan Plateau\",\"Chota Nagpur Plateau\",\"Malwa Plateau\",\"Meghalaya Plateau\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Maharashtra is part of the Deccan Plateau, which covers large parts of southern and central India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which mountain range is located to the west of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Himalayas\",\"Aravalli Range\",\"Sahyadri (Western Ghats)\",\"Satpura Range\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Sahyadri range, also known as the Western Ghats, runs along the western border of Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the major food grain crop grown in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Wheat\",\"Rice\",\"Jowar\",\"Bajra\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rice (paddy) is the major food grain crop in Maharashtra, especially in high rainfall areas like Konkan and eastern Vidarbha.\"},{\"q\":\"How many administrative divisions are there in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"6\",\"7\",\"8\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Maharashtra has 6 administrative divisions: Amravati, Aurangabad, Konkan, Nagpur, Nashik, and Pune.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a reason for diversity in forests of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Variation in rainfall\",\"Soil type\",\"Altitude\",\"Uniform temperature throughout the state\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Forests vary due to rainfall, soil, and altitude; temperature is not uniform and does not cause uniformity in forests.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between evergreen and thorny forests in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Evergreen forests have trees that shed leaves annually; thorny forests remain green all year.\",\"Evergreen forests are found in low rainfall areas; thorny forests in high rainfall areas.\",\"Evergreen forests have dense trees that remain green throughout the year; thorny forests have small leaves and thorns to reduce water loss.\",\"Evergreen forests are found only in coastal areas; thorny forests in mountainous regions.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Evergreen forests retain leaves year-round and are found in high rainfall areas; thorny forests have adaptations like small leaves and thorns for dry, low rainfall regions.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22253,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Public Life, Transport and Communication','[\"Public life includes facilities like roads, electricity, water, transport, communication, education, health, and jobs.\",\"Urban areas have better facilities such as roads, electricity, water supply, transport, and communication compared to rural areas.\",\"Rural areas have limited facilities but offer a natural and pollution-free environment.\",\"Transport is the movement of people and goods from one place to another.\",\"Geographical factors like mountains, plateaus, plains, coasts, deserts, and snowy regions affect the choice of transport.\",\"In mountainous regions, only road transport is used due to steep slopes.\",\"In plateaus and plains, road and rail transport are common due to flat terrain.\",\"In coastal areas, water transport is used via sea and large river channels.\",\"Air transport connects large cities and requires airports and runways.\",\"In deserts and snowy regions, animals are used for transport.\",\"Modern highways like Mumbai-Pune Expressway, Atal Setu Sea Link, and Solapur-Vijayapura Highway have improved travel.\",\"Modern transport reduces travel time due to better infrastructure and faster vehicles.\",\"Fuels used in transport include petrol, diesel, CNG, electricity, and solar energy.\",\"Communication is obtaining and sharing information with others.\",\"Traditional means of communication: post, newspaper, telephone, television, radio.\",\"Modern means of communication: mobile phones, Internet, e-mail, computers, artificial satellites.\",\"Mobile phones are widely used today for communication in agriculture, industry, trade, transport, education, and IT.\",\"A traffic garden is a place with models of signals, roads, and signs to teach children traffic rules through play.\",\"Following traffic rules reduces accidents and increases safety for everyone.\",\"Safety rules: wear helmet on two-wheelers, avoid mobile phone use while driving, use internet for work, promote harmony through messaging.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a facility commonly found in urban areas?\",\"options\":[\"Better roads\",\"Limited electricity supply\",\"Advanced transport\",\"Good water supply\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Urban areas have better electricity supply, not limited.\"},{\"q\":\"In which geographical region is animal transport commonly used?\",\"options\":[\"Plateaus\",\"Coastal areas\",\"Deserts and snowy regions\",\"Plains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Animals are used for transport in deserts and snowy regions due to lack of vehicle access.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of a traffic garden?\",\"options\":[\"To grow plants\",\"To teach traffic rules through play\",\"To park vehicles\",\"To conduct driving tests\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A traffic garden uses models of roads and signs to teach children traffic safety while playing.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a modern means of communication?\",\"options\":[\"Newspaper\",\"Radio\",\"Letter\",\"E-mail\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"E-mail is a modern electronic means of communication, unlike traditional tools.\"},{\"q\":\"Which transport is most common in plains and plateaus?\",\"options\":[\"Only water transport\",\"Only air transport\",\"Road and rail transport\",\"Only animal transport\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Flat terrain in plains and plateaus supports road and rail transport.\"},{\"q\":\"What has reduced travel time in recent years?\",\"options\":[\"More traffic jams\",\"Older vehicles\",\"Better highways and expressways\",\"Fewer roads\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Highways like Mumbai-Pune Expressway and Atal Setu Sea Link have made travel faster.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fuel is NOT typically used in transport?\",\"options\":[\"Petrol\",\"Diesel\",\"CNG\",\"Coal (for homes)\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Coal is used in homes for cooking, not commonly in vehicles.\"},{\"q\":\"What is messaging mainly used for in society?\",\"options\":[\"To spread rumors\",\"To promote unity and cooperation\",\"To play games only\",\"To replace all calls\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Messaging helps people stay connected and promotes harmony in society.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true about rural areas?\",\"options\":[\"They have more hospitals than cities\",\"They have better malls than urban areas\",\"People depend on agriculture and small industries\",\"They have metro trains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rural areas rely on agriculture and small-scale industries for employment.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a traditional means of communication?\",\"options\":[\"Mobile phone\",\"Internet\",\"Television\",\"Artificial satellite\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Television is a traditional electronic medium, though now also modern in form.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22254,15,4,'EVS Part 1','en','Friendship with Energy','[\"Energy is the capacity to do any work.\",\"We get energy from food to do activities like playing, running, and speaking.\",\"Solar energy comes from the sun and is used in solar cookers, solar water heaters, and solar lamps.\",\"Wind energy is obtained from wind and used to generate electricity via windmills.\",\"Light energy travels in a straight line.\",\"Light cannot pass through opaque objects, which results in the formation of a shadow.\",\"Shadows are formed when an opaque object blocks the path of light.\",\"On Zero Shadow Day, the sun is exactly overhead and shadows disappear at noon.\",\"Magnetism is the property of a magnet to attract iron objects.\",\"Substances attracted to a magnet are magnetic; those not attracted are non-magnetic.\",\"Uses of magnetism include loudspeakers, electric motors, door latches, handbag locks, toy compasses, geometry boxes, and fridge stickers.\",\"National Energy Conservation Day is observed on December 14th to promote efficient energy use.\",\"To save energy, switch off lights and fans when not needed.\",\"Energy sources include the sun, wind, water, and fuel.\",\"Appliances that run on solar energy help conserve non-renewable resources.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is energy defined as in the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"The ability to feel happy\",\"The capacity to do any work\",\"The heat from the sun\",\"The force of a magnet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Energy is defined as the capacity to do any work.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a form of energy mentioned in the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Solar energy\",\"Wind energy\",\"Sound energy\",\"Gravitational energy\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Gravitational energy is not discussed in the chapter; solar, wind, and sound energy are.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens when an opaque object blocks light?\",\"options\":[\"Light bends around it\",\"A shadow is formed\",\"Light becomes brighter\",\"No change occurs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When light is blocked by an opaque object, a shadow is formed.\"},{\"q\":\"From where is solar energy obtained?\",\"options\":[\"Moon\",\"Wind\",\"Sun\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Solar energy is obtained from the sun.\"},{\"q\":\"Which appliance uses wind energy to generate electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Solar cooker\",\"Windmill\",\"Electric heater\",\"Battery\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Windmills use wind energy to generate electricity.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a magnetic substance?\",\"options\":[\"Paper\",\"Plastic\",\"Iron\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Iron is attracted to a magnet and is a magnetic substance.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of National Energy Conservation Day?\",\"options\":[\"To celebrate the invention of electricity\",\"To promote the use of fossil fuels\",\"To create awareness about energy conservation\",\"To encourage more use of lights\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The day aims to create public awareness about efficient use of energy.\"},{\"q\":\"In which month does Zero Shadow Day usually occur in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"January\",\"March\",\"May\",\"July\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In Maharashtra, Zero Shadow Day usually occurs in the month of May.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a use of magnetism?\",\"options\":[\"Cooking food\",\"Drawing pictures\",\"Door latches\",\"Reading books\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Magnets are used in door latches to keep them closed.\"},{\"q\":\"What should be done to save energy at home?\",\"options\":[\"Keep lights on all day\",\"Run fans in empty rooms\",\"Switch off lights and fans when not needed\",\"Use more electrical appliances\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Switching off lights and fans when not needed helps save energy.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22255,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Maharashtra before the Times of Shivaji Maharaj','[\"Shivaji Maharaj was a brave king who lived in the medieval period.\",\"He fought against the powerful sultans of Ahmadnagar, Bijapur and the Mughal emperor.\",\"Shivaji created Swaraj, meaning self‑government, for the people of Maharashtra.\",\"Swaraj was based on justice, fairness and equal treatment for all castes and religions.\",\"He did not distinguish between Hindus and Muslims and honoured saints of all faiths.\",\"Before him, Maharashtra was ruled by the Nizamshah, Adilshah and the Mughals who oppressed the people.\",\"The people were hungry, unhappy and had no freedom under these rulers.\",\"Shivaji’s Swaraj gave the people a sense of safety and dignity.\",\"He used clever tactics and won many battles to establish his rule.\",\"His legacy is celebrated every year on Shivaji Maharaj’s birthday (Shivaji Jayanti).\",\"The people sang powadas and worshipped him with garlands and songs.\",\"Shivaji’s Swaraj was a model of good governance and fairness.\",\"The term Swaraj means ‘our own rule’ or self‑rule.\",\"Shivaji’s Swaraj lasted for about 200 years after his death.\",\"His story inspires people to fight for justice and freedom.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In which period did Shivaji Maharaj live?\",\"options\":[\"Ancient\",\"Medieval\",\"Modern\",\"Renaissance\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji Maharaj lived during the medieval period.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji Maharaj establish for Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Swaraj\",\"Sultanate\",\"Mughal Empire\",\"British Colony\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He established Swaraj, meaning self‑government.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these rulers did Shivaji fight against?\",\"options\":[\"Nizamshah\",\"Adilshah\",\"Mughal Emperor\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"He fought against all three.\"},{\"q\":\"What is Swaraj?\",\"options\":[\"A type of war\",\"A form of self‑government\",\"A religious festival\",\"A kind of food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Swaraj means self‑government.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji Maharaj was known for treating all people equally. Which term best describes this?\",\"options\":[\"Caste system\",\"Equality\",\"Hierarchy\",\"Exclusion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He practiced equality among all castes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint’s shrine did Shivaji honor?\",\"options\":[\"Shirdi Sai Baba\",\"Sant Tukaram\",\"All of the above\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He honored saints of all religions.\"},{\"q\":\"The people sang powadas to celebrate Shivaji. Powadas are:\",\"options\":[\"Songs\",\"Poems\",\"Dances\",\"Stories\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Powadas are songs praising Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji Maharaj’s Swaraj lasted for how many years?\",\"options\":[\"50 years\",\"100 years\",\"200 years\",\"300 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It lasted about 200 years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a ruler Shivaji fought?\",\"options\":[\"Mughal Emperor Akbar\",\"Nizamshah\",\"Adilshah\",\"British Governor\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The British were not yet present.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s Swaraj was based on which of these principles?\",\"options\":[\"Justice\",\"Fair play\",\"Equal treatment\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All three principles were central.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22256,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Work of the Saints','[\"Sants are saints who taught equality and love for all people.\",\"They came from different backgrounds – princes, farmers, women, and even people of lower castes.\",\"They preached that no one is superior or inferior; everyone should be treated equally.\",\"They sang devotional songs called keertans and abhangs to spread their messages.\",\"Sant Namdeo was a great devotee of Vitthal and preached compassion, non‑violence and brotherhood.\",\"Sant Dnyaneshwar wrote the Dnyaneshwari in Marathi, making religious knowledge accessible to common people.\",\"Sant Eknath continued the work of Namdeo and Dnyaneshwar, teaching devotion (Bhaktimarg) and equality.\",\"Sant Tukaram sang abhangs, performed keertans, and taught forgiveness, pity and peace of mind.\",\"Samarth Ramdas wrote Dasbodh, a guide to good living and self‑reliance.\",\"The saints’ songs and teachings are still sung and read in Maharashtra today.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which saint was a prince before becoming an ascetic?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Namdeo\",\"Sant Dnyaneshwar\",\"Sant Chakradhar Swami\",\"Sant Tukaram\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sant Chakradhar Swami was originally a prince.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a keertan?\",\"options\":[\"A type of food\",\"A devotional song\",\"A religious festival\",\"A holy book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Keertans are devotional songs and stories.\"},{\"q\":\"Sant Dnyaneshwar wrote his famous work in which language?\",\"options\":[\"Sanskrit\",\"English\",\"Marathi\",\"Hindi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He wrote the Dnyaneshwari in Marathi.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint sang abhangs and performed keertans?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Namdeo\",\"Sant Eknath\",\"Sant Tukaram\",\"Samarth Ramdas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sant Tukaram was known for abhangs and keertans.\"},{\"q\":\"What message did Sant Namdeo spread?\",\"options\":[\"Only to the wealthy\",\"Equality and brotherhood\",\"Only to his family\",\"Only to the king\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He preached equality and brotherhood.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint died while helping a poor child?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Namdeo\",\"Sant Dnyaneshwar\",\"Sant Eknath\",\"Sant Tukaram\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Sant Tukaram helped a poor child and died.\"},{\"q\":\"Samarth Ramdas wrote which guide for good living?\",\"options\":[\"Dasbodh\",\"Abhangs\",\"Keertans\",\"Dnyaneshwari\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Dasbodh is his guide to good living.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Sant Eknath teach people about devotion?\",\"options\":[\"Only to the king\",\"Through wealth\",\"Through Bhakti (devotion)\",\"Through violence\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He taught devotion (Bhakti) to reach God.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint’s songs are still sung in every household in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Tukaram\",\"Sant Namdeo\",\"Sant Eknath\",\"Sant Dnyaneshwar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sant Tukaram’s songs are widely sung.\"},{\"q\":\"What did the saints help Shivaji Maharaj with?\",\"options\":[\"Building forts\",\"Teaching him to fight\",\"Giving him money\",\"Teaching him to sing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Their teachings helped Shivaji in his fight for Swaraj.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22257,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Maratha Sardars – The Famous House of the Bhosales','[\"- Maratha Sardars were brave warriors who fought for their people.\",\"- They had private armies and were often hired by sultans of Bijapur and Ahmadnagar.\",\"- The Bhonsales of Verul were a famous Maratha family known for bravery and loyalty.\",\"- Maloji Raje Bhonsale was a Patil of Verul and later received the Jagir of Pune and Supe from Nizamshah.\",\"- His wife Umabai came from the House of Bijapur and helped him gain respect at the Adilshah court.\",\"- Shahaji Raje was a brave general who served both Nizamshah and later the Adilshah before returning to Nizamshah.\",\"- The temple of Ghrushneshwar near Verul was restored by the Jadhavs of Sindkhed, Nimbalkars of Phaltan, Ghorpades of Mudhol, Moreys of Javali, and Bhonsales of Verul.\",\"- The Jadhavs of Sindkhed were descendants of the Yadavs of Devgiri and were known for repairing the temple.\",\"- Malik Amber was a powerful Vazir (Chief Minister) of Nizamshah and was killed in battle by the Moghul emperor.\",\"- The Maratha Sardars were also known for their devotion to Shiva, often visiting temples and offering flowers and prayers.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which Maratha family was known for great bravery and enterprise?\",\"options\":[\"Bhonsales of Verul\",\"Jadhavs of Sindkhed\",\"Nimbalkars of Phaltan\",\"Ghorpades of Mudhol\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Bhonsales of Verul were highlighted for their bravery and enterprise.\"},{\"q\":\"Who restored the temple of Ghrushneshwar to its original condition?\",\"options\":[\"Jadhavs of Sindkhed\",\"Nimbalkars of Phaltan\",\"Ghorpades of Mudhol\",\"Moreys of Javali\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Jadhavs of Sindkhed repaired the temple.\"},{\"q\":\"What title did the Adilshah honour Maloji Raje with?\",\"options\":[\"Sar Lashkar\",\"Jagirdar\",\"Patil\",\"Vazir\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Adilshah honoured him with the title ‘Sar Lashkar’ (Chief of Army).\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the powerful Vazir of Nizamshah?\",\"options\":[\"Malik Amber\",\"Fattekhan\",\"Sharifji\",\"Shahaji Raje\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Malik Amber was the chief minister of Nizamshah.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Shahaji Raje leave Adilshah and return to Nizamshah?\",\"options\":[\"He wanted more land\",\"He was promised a higher title\",\"He was unhappy with the Adilshah\",\"He was forced to leave\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shahaji Raje left Adilshah because he was unhappy with the court.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a Maratha family mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Bhonsales\",\"Jadhavs\",\"Shahajis\",\"Nimbalkars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shahajis is a person, not a family name.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the daughter of Lakhujirao Jadhav?\",\"options\":[\"Jijabai\",\"Babaji Raje\",\"Maloji Raje\",\"Umabai\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jijabai was the daughter of Lakhujirao Jadhav.\"},{\"q\":\"Which sultanate did the Maratha Sardars serve in the 16th century?\",\"options\":[\"Bijapur\",\"Ahmadnagar\",\"Delhi\",\"Mysore\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"They served the sultans of Bijapur and Ahmadnagar.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main duty of a Sardar\'s cavalry?\",\"options\":[\"Defending temples\",\"Riding in procession\",\"Fighting in battles\",\"Collecting taxes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sardar\'s cavalry fought in battles.\"},{\"q\":\"Which temple was near the village of Verul?\",\"options\":[\"Ghrushneshwar\",\"Shivaji\",\"Jain\",\"Hindu\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The temple of Ghrushneshwar was near Verul.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22258,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Shivaji’s Childhood','[\"Shivaji was born on 19 February 1630 at Shivneri Fort.\",\"Shivneri Fort is a strong hill fort with steep cliffs on all sides.\",\"Shivaji’s mother Jijabai was a brave woman who taught him stories of brave men.\",\"Jijabai told Shivaji stories of saints like Namdeo and Dnyaneshwar and heroes like Bhim and Abhimanyu.\",\"Shivaji liked to listen to the sound of drums and pipes while growing up.\",\"He played hide‑and‑seek and built small forts with earth and clay.\",\"Shivaji’s father Shahaji Raje was a soldier who fought against the Moghuls and Adilshah.\",\"Shahaji Raje left the Nizamshahi territory and joined the Moghuls after a conflict.\",\"Shivaji’s childhood was full of learning, games and stories that made him brave.\",\"Shivneri Fort is located near Pune, in the Pune district.\",\"Shivaji’s early life was under the care of his mother and the people of the village.\",\"The stories of brave men helped Shivaji develop confidence and courage.\",\"Shivaji’s father had a jagir (land grant) near Junnar and Pune.\",\"Shivaji’s mother was pregnant when he was born and celebrated his birth with a ceremony.\",\"Shivaji’s early life was influenced by the politics of the Moghuls, Adilshah and Nizamshah.\",\"Shivaji’s childhood shows how a good teacher and stories can shape a future king.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where was Shivaji born?\",\"options\":[\"Pune\",\"Shivneri Fort\",\"Panhala\",\"Kolhapur\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji was born at Shivneri Fort.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji’s mother Jijabai often tell him?\",\"options\":[\"Stories of brave men\",\"Stories of animals\",\"Stories of kings\",\"Stories of rivers\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jijabai told stories of brave men and saints.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort is described as having steep cliffs on all sides?\",\"options\":[\"Panhala\",\"Shivneri\",\"Pune\",\"Kolhapur\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivneri Fort has steep cliffs on all sides.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji liked to listen to the sound of which instruments?\",\"options\":[\"Guitar and drums\",\"Pipes and drums\",\"Flute and violin\",\"Harp and piano\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He liked the sound of pipes and drums.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was Shivaji’s father?\",\"options\":[\"Shahaji Raje\",\"Shahjahan\",\"Adilshah\",\"Nizamshah\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shivaji’s father was Shahaji Raje.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji played which game with the children?\",\"options\":[\"Hide and seek\",\"Chess\",\"Tag\",\"Marbles\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He played hide and seek.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s early life was influenced by the politics of which empire?\",\"options\":[\"Mughals\",\"British\",\"Portuguese\",\"French\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Mughals were a major political force.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji build with earth and clay?\",\"options\":[\"A house\",\"A small fort\",\"A temple\",\"A garden\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He built small forts with earth and clay.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region did Shahaji Raje leave to join the Mughals?\",\"options\":[\"Nizamshahi\",\"Adilshah\",\"Pune\",\"Kolhapur\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He left Nizamshahi.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main lesson Shivaji learned from stories?\",\"options\":[\"How to cook\",\"How to fight\",\"How to read\",\"How to sing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Stories taught him bravery and courage.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22259,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Shivaji’s Education','[\"Shivaji was born in 1630 in Pune and was educated by his mother, Jijabai, and teachers sent by his father, Shahaji Raje.\",\"Jijabai was a strong, wise woman who taught Shivaji values like courage, honesty and love for his people.\",\"Shivaji began learning at age 7, studying Sanskrit, Marathi, Persian, and local languages.\",\"He learned to read and write, and studied the great epics Ramayana, Mahabharata and Bhagavata.\",\"Jijabai also taught him martial skills – riding, wrestling, sword‑fighting and strategy.\",\"Shivaji’s teachers taught him about war tactics, administration, and the importance of good governance.\",\"He learned to manage revenue, build forts, and protect his people from enemies like the Nizamshahi and Adilshah.\",\"Jijabai’s example of bravery inspired Shivaji to fight injustice and protect the Maval people, who were hardworking and loyal.\",\"Shivaji’s education helped him become a great leader who built the Maratha Empire and promoted Swaraj (self‑rule).\",\"Key terms: Shivaji, Jijabai, Shahaji Raje, Maval, Pune, Sanskrit, war tactics, revenue, Swaraj.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who sent teachers to educate Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Jijabai\",\"Shahaji Raje\",\"The Nizamshahi\",\"The Adilshah\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shahaji Raje, Shivaji’s father, sent teachers to Pune.\"},{\"q\":\"At what age did Shivaji start learning?\",\"options\":[\"5\",\"7\",\"10\",\"12\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji began his education when he was seven years old.\"},{\"q\":\"Which epic did Shivaji read?\",\"options\":[\"Ramayana\",\"Mahabharata\",\"Bhagavata\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"He read all three major Sanskrit epics.\"},{\"q\":\"What martial skill did Shivaji NOT learn from his teachers?\",\"options\":[\"Horse riding\",\"Wrestling\",\"Singing\",\"Sword fighting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Singing was not mentioned as part of his training.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the mother of Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Jijabai\",\"Dadaji Konddev\",\"Veermata\",\"Shahaji\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jijabai, also called Veermata, was Shivaji’s mother.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Jijabai teach Shivaji about his ancestors?\",\"options\":[\"They were kings\",\"They were warriors\",\"They were Rama and Krishna\",\"They were scholars\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"She told him he was descendant of Rama and Krishna.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region’s people were called Mavlas?\",\"options\":[\"Pune\",\"Sahyadri\",\"Maval\",\"Karnataka\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"People living in the Maval region were called Mavlas.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji’s teachers teach him about?\",\"options\":[\"Only Sanskrit\",\"Only war tactics\",\"Both Sanskrit and war tactics\",\"Only agriculture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"They taught him both Sanskrit and war tactics.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Jijabai resolve regarding Shivaji’s future?\",\"options\":[\"He would become a king\",\"He would never serve others\",\"He would become a scholar\",\"He would marry early\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"She resolved that Shivaji would never serve others and would rule his own people.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main goal of Shivaji’s education?\",\"options\":[\"To become a poet\",\"To rule with justice\",\"To become a warrior only\",\"To travel the world\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The goal was to rule with justice and protect his people.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22260,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','The Oath of Swaraj','[\"Shivaji was a young prince who dreamed of a free India called Hindavi Swaraj.\",\"He promised to fight for freedom and to stop fighting each other for land.\",\"Shivaji gathered his friends at Raireshwar temple and made an oath to fight for Swaraj.\",\"He trained his friends in sword fighting, riding, and learning the hills and forts.\",\"Shivaji surveyed all forts and secret paths so he could protect his people.\",\"He made a Royal Seal that said he would rule for the welfare of the people.\",\"The seal was written in Sanskrit, showing respect for local culture.\",\"Shivaji wanted to end quarrels among the Deshmukhs who fought over their lands.\",\"He promised to give each Deshmukh a ‘Watan’ (land) to stop wasting money and soldiers.\",\"Shivaji’s oath was a promise to give his life for freedom and to stop being a slave of the Sultan.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was the main goal of Shivaji’s oath?\",\"options\":[\"To become rich\",\"To fight for Swaraj\",\"To build temples\",\"To rule the world\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji’s oath was to fight for freedom (Swaraj).\"},{\"q\":\"Where did Shivaji gather his friends to take the oath?\",\"options\":[\"Lal Mahal\",\"Raireshwar temple\",\"Shivaji’s palace\",\"Pune market\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The oath was taken at Raireshwar temple.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji do after taking the oath?\",\"options\":[\"He left the country\",\"He started training his friends\",\"He built a new fort\",\"He wrote a poem\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He trained his friends in swordsmanship, riding, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Shivaji survey all the forts?\",\"options\":[\"To sell them\",\"To know their secrets\",\"To destroy them\",\"To paint them\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He surveyed to know secret paths and protect his people.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a ‘Watan’?\",\"options\":[\"A type of food\",\"A land given for service\",\"A kind of weapon\",\"A festival\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A Watan is land given to Deshmukhs for service.\"},{\"q\":\"What language was Shivaji’s Royal Seal written in?\",\"options\":[\"Persian\",\"English\",\"Sanskrit\",\"Hindi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shivaji’s seal was written in Sanskrit.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji want to stop among the Deshmukhs?\",\"options\":[\"Building houses\",\"Fighting each other\",\"Eating too much\",\"Singing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He wanted to stop quarrels over land.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji promise to give to his people?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"A new king\",\"Freedom\",\"A new temple\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He promised freedom (Swaraj).\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji’s oath say about slavery?\",\"options\":[\"It was good\",\"It was okay\",\"It was bad\",\"It was allowed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The oath said they would not live as slaves.\"},{\"q\":\"Who helped Shivaji in his training?\",\"options\":[\"Jijabai\",\"His father Shahaji\",\"The Sultan\",\"The British\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jijabai helped him train and support.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22261,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Swaraj : First Conquests','[\"Shivaji was a brave king who wanted Swaraj (self‑rule).\",\"He lived when four powers ruled Maharashtra: the Mughals, the Bijapur Sultanate, the Portuguese, and the Siddi of Janjira.\",\"Shivaji’s first big win was taking the fort of Torana in 1640. It was a natural hill fort with two strong walls called ‘Machis’.\",\"The fort had a temple of the goddess Bhavani, who gave Shivaji a gift of pots filled with money and ammunition. This helped him start his own army.\",\"After Torana, Shivaji captured many other forts: Rajgad, Pratapgad, Sinhagad, Shivneri, and many in the Maval region.\",\"The first capital of his Swaraj was Rajgad, a fort he finished and used for administration.\",\"Shivaji used clever tactics, good soldiers called Mavlas, and the help of local people from all castes. He treated everyone equally.\",\"He sent a message to the Sultan of Bijapur saying he had taken possession of the fort and would not return it. The Sultan was surprised.\",\"Shivaji’s army was small but brave; they could move fast and capture forts quickly.\",\"The story of Torana shows how a strong fort can protect a king and give him power to rule.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji first capture to start his Swaraj movement?\",\"options\":[\"Rajgad\",\"Srinagar\",\"Torana\",\"Pune\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shivaji captured Torana fort first.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the ruler of Bijapur that controlled many forts in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Adilshah\",\"Mughal Emperor\",\"Portuguese Governor\",\"Siddi of Janjira\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Adilshah of Bijapur ruled the region.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a ‘Machi’ in the context of Shivaji’s forts?\",\"options\":[\"A type of fortification\",\"A kind of cannon\",\"A group of soldiers\",\"A mountain pass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A ‘Machi’ refers to a group of Shivaji’s soldiers.\"},{\"q\":\"Which goddess is associated with blessings that helped Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Goddess Parvati\",\"Goddess Bhavani\",\"Goddess Durga\",\"Goddess Saraswati\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Goddess Bhavani is credited with blessings.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the first capital of Shivaji’s Swaraj?\",\"options\":[\"Rajgad\",\"Raigad\",\"Pratapgad\",\"Pune\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Rajgad became the first capital.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort was left unfinished by Adilshah and later completed by Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Rajgad\",\"Torana\",\"Srinagar\",\"Pune\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Adilshah left Rajgad unfinished.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji use the pots of mohurs for?\",\"options\":[\"Decorate the fort\",\"Buy arms\",\"Build a temple\",\"Make jewelry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The pots were used to buy arms.\"},{\"q\":\"Which group of people were called Mavlas?\",\"options\":[\"Farmers\",\"Soldiers\",\"Artisans\",\"Traders\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mavlas were soldiers in Shivaji’s army.\"},{\"q\":\"Which hill is known as the ‘Zunjar Machi’?\",\"options\":[\"Torana\",\"Rajgad\",\"Srinagar\",\"Pune\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Zunjar Machi refers to Torana fort.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Shivaji chose Torana fort?\",\"options\":[\"It was cheap\",\"It had a temple\",\"It was strategically located\",\"It was easy to capture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Torana’s strategic location made it ideal.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22262,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Defeat of Internal Enemies','[\"Shivaji was a brave king who fought for his land called Swaraj.\",\"The Mavlas were people who lived in forests and helped Shivaji.\",\"Morey was a leader of the Mavlas and fought against Shivaji at first.\",\"Shivaji used force and clever plans to defeat enemies inside his own country.\",\"He captured forts like Raigad and Pratapgad to protect his kingdom.\",\"After winning, he gave titles and tribute to those who helped him.\",\"He punished those who betrayed him, like Morey and Yeshwantrao.\",\"Sambhaji Mohite of Supe tried to attack Shivaji but was arrested and sent away.\",\"Shivaji’s main goal was to keep his people safe and keep the kingdom united.\",\"He was known for being fair and giving rewards to loyal soldiers.\",\"The conquest of Javali was a big victory because it added new forts to Swaraj.\",\"Raigad was a strong fort surrounded by forest and water, making it hard to attack.\",\"Shivaji changed the name of the fort to Raigad after conquering it.\",\"He made sure that people who wanted to be king had to give up their claims.\",\"Shivaji’s leadership showed that duty to the country is more important than family ties.\",\"He taught that a king should listen to his people and protect them from danger.\",\"The story shows how Shivaji used both force and kindness to win battles.\",\"It also shows that enemies inside the kingdom can be defeated by good planning.\",\"Shivaji’s victories helped spread the idea of a free and safe land.\",\"The chapter teaches that courage, loyalty and fairness are important values for a king.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who was the leader of the Mavlas that fought against Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Morey\",\"Yeshwantrao\",\"Bajaji Naik\",\"Sambhaji Mohite\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Morey was the chief of the Mavlas.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji conquer that became a landmark for Swaraj?\",\"options\":[\"Raigad\",\"Pratapgad\",\"Rairi\",\"Javali\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Raigad was captured and renamed by Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji do to those who betrayed him?\",\"options\":[\"Made them kings\",\"Punished them\",\"Ignored them\",\"Gave them land\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He punished those who betrayed him.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was Sambhaji Mohite?\",\"options\":[\"A friend of Shivaji\",\"A relative who plotted against Shivaji\",\"A soldier in Shivaji’s army\",\"A king of Javali\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sambhaji Mohite was a relative who tried to attack Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the conquest of Javali important?\",\"options\":[\"It added new forts\",\"It gave Shivaji a title\",\"It made Shivaji rich\",\"It ended the war\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It added new forts to Swaraj.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji give to those who helped him?\",\"options\":[\"A throne\",\"A title\",\"An annual tribute\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"He gave titles, tribute and a throne.\"},{\"q\":\"Which forest was covered by the Mavlas?\",\"options\":[\"Koyna\",\"Javali\",\"Raigad\",\"Pratapgad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Mavlas lived in the Javali forest.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the original name of the fort that Shivaji renamed Raigad?\",\"options\":[\"Pratapgad\",\"Rairi\",\"Javali\",\"Koyna\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rairi fort was renamed Raigad.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was Yeshwantrao?\",\"options\":[\"Shivaji’s brother\",\"A king of Javali\",\"A leader of the Mavlas\",\"A relative of Shivaji who betrayed him\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Yeshwantrao betrayed Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"What was Shivaji’s main goal in fighting?\",\"options\":[\"To become king\",\"To protect his people\",\"To gain wealth\",\"To travel far\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He fought to protect his people.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22263,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Pratapgad makes History','[\"Pratapgad is a fort in Maharashtra where Shivaji Maharaj fought Afzal Khan.\",\"Afzal Khan was a powerful commander from Bijapur who wanted Shivaji captured alive or dead.\",\"Shivaji moved his base from Rajgad to Pratapgad to stay away from the plains and make it hard for Afzal Khan to attack.\",\"Afzal Khan sent a friendly message to Shivaji, saying he would give him a Sardar title if he returned the forts.\",\"Shivaji refused to leave Pratapgad and used clever tactics to avoid open battle with Afzal Khan.\",\"He divided his army into small units and kept them hidden in the forest and hills.\",\"During the meeting, Shivaji used a dagger to stab Afzal Khan, killing him while the Khan was trying to attack him.\",\"After Afzal Khan’s death, the Bijapur army was defeated and Shivaji’s fame spread across the hills.\",\"Pratapgad is famous for its strong walls, thick forest, and high hills that protect it from enemy attacks.\",\"Shivaji’s strategy was to use surprise and terrain advantage rather than direct confrontation.\",\"The story shows Shivaji’s bravery, intelligence, and his ability to protect his people.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who was the ruler of Bijapur who wanted to capture Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Maharaja Shivaji\",\"Afzal Khan\",\"Shivaji\'s father\",\"Shivaji\'s brother\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Afzal Khan was the commander of Bijapur who challenged Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"What fort did Shivaji move his headquarters to after Rajgad?\",\"options\":[\"Raigad\",\"Pratapgad\",\"Sinhagad\",\"Sinhagad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji shifted his base to Pratapgad.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Shivaji refuse to leave Pratapgad?\",\"options\":[\"He feared the plains\",\"He had no army\",\"He wanted to attack the Khan\",\"He was ill\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shivaji stayed because the plains were dangerous for his forces.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Afzal Khan send Shivaji as a friendly message?\",\"options\":[\"A letter of surrender\",\"An offer of friendship\",\"A gift of gold\",\"A request for tribute\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Afzal Khan sent a friendly message offering friendship.\"},{\"q\":\"Which weapon did Shivaji use to stab Afzal Khan?\",\"options\":[\"Sword\",\"Dagger\",\"Bow\",\"Gun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji stabbed the Khan with a dagger.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the name of Shivaji\'s ceremonial sword?\",\"options\":[\"Dandpatta\",\"Jiretope\",\"Bichwa\",\"Waghnakh\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The sword was called Dandpatta.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Afzal Khan do to try to capture Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Attacked the plains\",\"Attacked the fort\",\"Attacked the temple\",\"Attacked the sea\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He attacked the plains to lure Shivaji out.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Marathi proverb was associated with Shivaji\'s cleverness?\",\"options\":[\"Jai Shree Shivaji\",\"Kale Gadhi\",\"Kale Gadhi\",\"Kale Gadhi\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The proverb ‘Kale Gadhi’ reflects Shivaji’s cunning.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Shivaji used strategic moves?\",\"options\":[\"To avoid open battle\",\"To win quickly\",\"To save money\",\"To avoid war\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shivaji avoided open battle because his army was smaller.\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to Afzal Khan after the encounter?\",\"options\":[\"He was captured\",\"He died\",\"He fled\",\"He was promoted\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Afzal Khan was killed by Shivaji.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22264,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','The Battle of Pavankhind','[\"Shivaji was a brave king who fought many battles.\",\"Panhala fort was captured by Shivaji from the Bijapur army.\",\"Siddi Jauhar was a powerful enemy who tried to stop Shivaji.\",\"Shivaji used a clever trick to escape from the siege by sending a fake palanquin.\",\"Baji Prabhu Deshpande was a loyal soldier who defended the Ghodkhind pass.\",\"The Ghodkhind pass is a steep and narrow path that was hard to cross.\",\"Shivaji and Baji Prabu fought together to protect the fort of Vishalgad.\",\"Baji Prabhu died while protecting the pass but his bravery is remembered.\",\"The battle at Ghodkhind made the pass famous as a symbol of courage.\",\"Shivaji’s escape shows his cleverness and determination to protect his people.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji capture from the Bijapur army?\",\"options\":[\"Panhala\",\"Ghodkhind\",\"Vishalgad\",\"Pavankhind\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shivaji captured Panhala fort from the Bijapur garrison.\"},{\"q\":\"Who sent a fake palanquin to help Shivaji escape?\",\"options\":[\"Siddi Jauhar\",\"Siddi Masaud\",\"Baji Prabhu\",\"Shiva Kashid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Baji Prabhu sent a fake palanquin to deceive the enemy.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Siddi Jauhar was angry?\",\"options\":[\"He lost a battle\",\"He wanted to kill Shivaji\",\"He was sad about his father\",\"He was tired of fighting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Siddi Jauhar was angry because he wanted to avenge his father’s death.\"},{\"q\":\"Which pass did Baji Prabhu defend?\",\"options\":[\"Panhala\",\"Ghodkhind\",\"Vishalgad\",\"Pavankhind\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Baji Prabhu defended the Ghodkhind pass.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji do after escaping the siege?\",\"options\":[\"Returned to Panhala\",\"Went to Vishalgad\",\"Surrendered to Siddi\",\"Left the kingdom\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"After escaping, Shivaji went to Vishalgad.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the brave soldier that died at Ghodkhind?\",\"options\":[\"Siddi Jauhar\",\"Siddi Masaud\",\"Baji Prabhu\",\"Shiva Kashid\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Baji Prabhu died while defending the pass.\"},{\"q\":\"What made the Ghodkhind pass famous?\",\"options\":[\"Its beautiful scenery\",\"Its difficult terrain\",\"It was a battle site\",\"It had many forts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Ghodkhind pass became famous because of the battle fought there.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was a clever trick used by Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Using a secret tunnel\",\"Sending a fake palanquin\",\"Building a new fort\",\"Hiring spies\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji used a fake palanquin to escape.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Siddi Masaud pursue Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"He wanted to help Shivaji\",\"He was following orders\",\"He wanted to capture Shivaji\",\"He was tired of fighting\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Siddi Masaud pursued Shivaji to capture him.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main lesson from Shivaji’s escape?\",\"options\":[\"Bravery is enough\",\"Cleverness and courage are both important\",\"Only soldiers can win\",\"Patience is the key\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji’s escape shows that cleverness and courage are both important.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22265,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','The Rout of Shaista Khan','[\"Shaista Khan was a Mughal general sent by Emperor Aurangzeb to attack the Maratha king Shivaji.\",\"He marched from Bijapur and captured the fort of Chakan, but was defeated there.\",\"Shaista Khan then laid siege to the fort of Purandar in 1660, but could not force Shivaji to surrender.\",\"Shivaji fought back and eventually broke the siege, forcing Shaista Khan to retreat.\",\"Later, Aurangzeb sent a larger army of 75,000 men, including elephants and cannon, to Pune.\",\"Shaista Khan tried to escape through the windows of Lal Mahal, the Maratha palace, but was caught by Shivaji’s spies.\",\"Shivaji entered Lal Mahal at night, killed Shaista Khan’s son, and forced the general to flee.\",\"This event was a major blow to Mughal power in Maharashtra.\",\"Shivaji’s use of surprise, spies, and knowledge of the palace’s secret passages was key to his victory.\",\"The incident shows how intelligence and daring tactics can defeat a larger force.\",\"The story is an example of Maratha resistance and the importance of protecting one’s capital.\",\"It also illustrates the consequences of underestimating an opponent’s courage and skill.\",\"The battle at Lal Mahal is remembered as a proud moment in Maratha history.\",\"Key dates: 1660 – siege of Purandar; 1663 – attack on Lal Mahal.\",\"Shivaji’s tactics included disguising his troops as a wedding procession and using the palace’s hidden passages.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shaista Khan capture before being defeated by Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Panhala\",\"Chakan\",\"Purandar\",\"Sinhagad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shaista Khan captured Chakan but was defeated there.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Aurangzeb sent a larger army to Pune?\",\"options\":[\"To celebrate a victory\",\"To punish Shivaji\",\"To protect Shaista Khan\",\"To build a new fort\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Aurangzeb wanted to crush Shivaji after the failed siege.\"},{\"q\":\"How many men were in the Mughal army that attacked Pune?\",\"options\":[\"50,000\",\"60,000\",\"75,000\",\"80,000\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The army numbered 75,000 men.\"},{\"q\":\"What tactic did Shivaji use to enter Lal Mahal?\",\"options\":[\"A direct frontal assault\",\"A surprise night raid\",\"A naval attack\",\"A diplomatic negotiation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji entered at night disguised as a wedding procession.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji do to Shaista Khan’s son?\",\"options\":[\"Gave him a gift\",\"Killed him\",\"Sent him to prison\",\"Made him a spy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji killed the son to demoralise the Mughal troops.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Shaista Khan try to escape through the windows of Lal Mahal?\",\"options\":[\"He wanted to escape the siege\",\"He was looking for a secret passage\",\"He wanted to spy on Shivaji\",\"He was looking for a new palace\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He tried to escape the siege by using the palace windows.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a tactic used by Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Using spies\",\"Using secret passages\",\"Using a wedding procession\",\"Using a large cannon\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Shivaji did not use a large cannon in this attack.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the outcome of the attack on Lal Mahal?\",\"options\":[\"Shaista Khan won\",\"Shivaji was captured\",\"Shivaji won\",\"The battle was a stalemate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shivaji won the battle.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shaista Khan capture on his way to Pune?\",\"options\":[\"Chakan\",\"Panhala\",\"Shivneri\",\"Purandar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He captured Chakan on the way to Pune.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main consequence of Shivaji’s victory at Lal Mahal?\",\"options\":[\"Mughal power increased\",\"Mughal power decreased\",\"Shivaji was captured\",\"Shivaji lost his army\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It was a major blow to Mughal power.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22266,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','The Siege and Treaty of Purandar','[\"The Siege of Purandar was fought between the Marathas and the Mughal army led by Dilerkhan.\",\"Shivaji Maharaj was the king of the Marathas and the ruler of Pune.\",\"The fort of Purandar was a stronghold of Shivaji’s forces.\",\"Dilerkhan, a Mughal general, wanted to capture Purandar to weaken the Marathas.\",\"Murarbaji Deshpande was the commander of the fort and defended it bravely.\",\"During the siege, Murarbaji fought hard but was eventually killed by a cannon shot.\",\"After the battle, Shivaji decided to negotiate a treaty with the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb.\",\"The Treaty of Purandar was signed in 1665 and gave the Mughals control of 23 forts and a revenue of four lakh rupees.\",\"Shivaji kept his promise to return safely to Pune after the treaty.\",\"The treaty helped Shivaji to plan future attacks on the Mughal empire.\",\"The Marathas used the treaty to regroup and strengthen their forces.\",\"The siege showed the courage and skill of Maratha commanders like Murarbaji.\",\"The Mughal army was large and had powerful cannons and elephants.\",\"Shivaji’s raid on Surat earlier showed his bravery and strategic thinking.\",\"The treaty was a diplomatic solution, not a war victory.\",\"The Marathas still fought after the treaty and continued to challenge the Mughals.\",\"The story teaches that courage, strategy and diplomacy are all important in war.\",\"The Treaty of Purandar was a turning point in the Maratha–Mughal conflict.\",\"The Marathas used forts like Purandar to protect their territory.\",\"The Mughal emperor Aurangzeb was a powerful ruler who wanted to control all of India.\",\"The Marathas were known for their guerrilla tactics and quick attacks.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who was the commander of the fort of Purandar during the siege?\",\"options\":[\"Murarbaji Deshpande\",\"Jaising\",\"Dilerkhan\",\"Shivaji\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Murarbaji Deshpande defended Purandar against the Mughal army.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Dilerkhan laid siege to Purandar?\",\"options\":[\"To capture a wealthy city\",\"To defeat Shivaji’s forces\",\"To gain a strategic fort\",\"To raid Surat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Purandar was a strong fort that could help the Mughals control the region.\"},{\"q\":\"How many forts did the Mughal emperor demand in the Treaty of Purandar?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"23\",\"30\",\"15\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The treaty gave the Mughals control of 23 forts.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji promise to do after signing the treaty?\",\"options\":[\"Attack Surat again\",\"Return to Pune safely\",\"Leave the Marathas\",\"Join the Mughal army\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji promised a safe return to Pune.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Mughal general led the siege of Purandar?\",\"options\":[\"Mirza Raje Jaising\",\"Dilerkhan\",\"Aurangzeb\",\"Shivaji\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dilerkhan was the Mughal general who attacked Purandar.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the revenue of the forts given to the Mughals in the treaty?\",\"options\":[\"Two lakh rupees\",\"Four lakh rupees\",\"Six lakh rupees\",\"One lakh rupees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The revenue was four lakh rupees.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the ruler of the Marathas during the siege?\",\"options\":[\"Shivaji Maharaj\",\"Jaising\",\"Murarbaji\",\"Dilerkhan\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shivaji Maharaj was the king of the Marathas.\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to Murarbaji during the siege?\",\"options\":[\"He captured the Mughal army\",\"He was killed by a cannon shot\",\"He negotiated a treaty\",\"He fled to Pune\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Murarbaji was killed by a cannon shot.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main purpose of the Treaty of Purandar?\",\"options\":[\"To end all wars\",\"To give the Mughals control of forts\",\"To make Shivaji a Mughal officer\",\"To unite the Marathas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The treaty gave the Mughals control of 23 forts.\"},{\"q\":\"Which city was raided by Shivaji before the siege of Purandar?\",\"options\":[\"Pune\",\"Surat\",\"Delhi\",\"Agra\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji raided Surat earlier.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22267,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Shivaji Maharaj Outwits the Badshah','[\"Shivaji Maharaj was a brave king who ruled Maharashtra.\",\"He once visited the Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb in Agra.\",\"Shivaji did not like the way the Emperor treated him and his people.\",\"He decided to leave the court secretly and escape from Agra.\",\"Shivaji used a clever plan: he hid in baskets that were allowed to pass without inspection.\",\"The baskets were meant to carry sweets for the Emperor’s guests.\",\"Shivaji and his son Sambhaji were hidden inside two baskets.\",\"When the baskets reached a pre‑arranged spot, they got out and ran away on horses.\",\"The Emperor tried to catch them, but they escaped and never returned to Agra.\",\"After escaping, Shivaji returned to his kingdom and continued to rule bravely.\",\"The story shows how clever thinking can help a leader protect his people.\",\"It also teaches that courage and planning are important for a king.\",\"Shivaji’s escape is remembered as a great act of bravery.\",\"The story is part of the Maharashtra State Board textbook for Standard 4.\",\"It is written in simple English for young students.\",\"The chapter is titled “Shivaji Maharaj Outwits the Badshah”.\",\"Key facts: He hid in baskets, his son was left behind, the Emperor was angry, he escaped to Rajgad.\",\"The story is used to teach history, courage, and problem‑solving.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Why did Shivaji leave the court of the Emperor’s Darbar?\",\"options\":[\"Because he was invited to stay longer\",\"Because he was angry at the Emperor\",\"Because he wanted to visit his mother\",\"Because he was ill\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji was angry at the Emperor’s treatment.\"},{\"q\":\"What strategy did Shivaji use to escape from Agra?\",\"options\":[\"He bribed the guards\",\"He hid in baskets\",\"He disguised as a merchant\",\"He rode a horse into the forest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He hid in baskets that were allowed to pass.\"},{\"q\":\"Where did Shivaji leave young Sambhaji Raje behind?\",\"options\":[\"Jhansi\",\"Mathura\",\"Delhi\",\"Mumbai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sambhaji was left in Mathura.\"},{\"q\":\"Who did Shivaji hand over the administration of Swaraj before leaving?\",\"options\":[\"His mother Jijabai\",\"His chief Sardars\",\"His son Sambhaji\",\"His friend Hiroji\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He gave the administration to his mother Jijabai.\"},{\"q\":\"Who were the only persons left with Shivaji in Agra?\",\"options\":[\"His son Sambhaji\",\"His wife\",\"His brother\",\"His friend Hiroji\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Only his friend Hiroji was left.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji do for his quick recovery?\",\"options\":[\"He took medicine\",\"He rested in a palace\",\"He prayed to the gods\",\"He ate sweets\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He took medicine for quick recovery.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the Emperor’s reaction when he found out Shivaji escaped?\",\"options\":[\"He laughed\",\"He was angry\",\"He invited Shivaji back\",\"He sent a gift\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Emperor was angry.\"},{\"q\":\"Which place did Shivaji escape to after leaving Agra?\",\"options\":[\"Rajgad\",\"Pune\",\"Nagpur\",\"Aurangabad\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He escaped to Rajgad.\"},{\"q\":\"How many baskets were used in Shivaji’s escape plan?\",\"options\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Four\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Two baskets were used.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Shivaji hid in baskets?\",\"options\":[\"To avoid paying taxes\",\"To avoid being seen by guards\",\"To carry food\",\"To hide his weapons\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"To avoid being seen by guards.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22268,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','The Fort is Won but the Lion is Lost','[\"The fort was captured by Shivaji’s army but the brave warrior Tanaji died in the battle.\",\"Tanaji Malusare was a close comrade of Shivaji and a fearless soldier.\",\"The fort of Kondana was one of the 23 forts that Shivaji had to capture from the Mughals.\",\"Jijamata, Shivaji’s mother, urged him to capture Kondana before attending a wedding.\",\"Shivaji decided to launch a campaign to recapture Kondana and asked Tanaji to lead it.\",\"Tanaji planned to attack the fort by scaling a steep precipice and entering through the Kalyan gate.\",\"The fort had two entrances: a main gate and a side gate guarded by a steep slope.\",\"Udaybhan was a Rajput officer appointed by Jaising to guard Kondana; he had a disciplined garrison of 500 men.\",\"During the attack, Tanaji and his men fought bravely but were outnumbered and eventually killed.\",\"After Tanaji’s death, the Mavlas (soldiers) lost their leader and had to retreat from the fort.\",\"The phrase “The fort is won but the lion is dead” means the fort was captured but the brave warrior died.\",\"Shivaji expressed sorrow over Tanaji’s death and performed a mourning ceremony at Umrathe.\",\"The Kalyan gate was the main entrance that Tanaji’s men tried to open during the battle.\",\"The fort’s walls were heavily guarded and the battle was fought in the dark night with torches.\",\"The Mavlas managed to climb the steep precipice but many were cut off by a rope tied to a tree.\",\"The story teaches that courage and sacrifice are more important than material gains.\",\"It also shows the importance of planning and timing in military campaigns.\",\"The name of Tanaji’s younger brother was Murarbaji.\",\"The fort of Kondana later became known as Sinhagad after its capture.\",\"The battle took place in 1670, the same year Shivaji’s son Rajaram was born.\",\"The story is part of the Maharashtra State Board (Balbharati) textbook for Standard 4, EVS Part 2.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the phrase \\\"The fort is won but the lion is dead\\\" mean?\",\"options\":[\"The fort was captured but the brave warrior died\",\"The fort was lost and the lion escaped\",\"The fort was captured and the lion was captured\",\"The fort was lost and the lion died\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It refers to the fort being captured but the brave warrior Tanaji dying.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was appointed by Jaising to guard Kondana?\",\"options\":[\"Udaybhan\",\"Suryaji\",\"Jijamata\",\"Shivaji\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Udaybhan was the Rajput officer guarding the fort.\"},{\"q\":\"Which gate did Tanaji’s men try to open during the battle?\",\"options\":[\"Kalyan gate\",\"Main gate\",\"North gate\",\"South gate\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"They attempted to open the Kalyan gate.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main obstacle to entering the fort?\",\"options\":[\"A steep precipice\",\"A moat\",\"A high wall\",\"A guard tower\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The steep precipice made entry difficult.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was Tanaji’s younger brother?\",\"options\":[\"Murarbaji\",\"Raiba\",\"Suryaji\",\"Jijamata\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Murarbaji was Tanaji’s younger brother.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Jijamata urge Shivaji to do before the wedding?\",\"options\":[\"Capture Kondana\",\"Go to the wedding\",\"Leave the fort\",\"Invite the Mughals\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"She urged him to capture Kondana first.\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did the battle of Kondana take place?\",\"options\":[\"1670\",\"1680\",\"1660\",\"1690\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The battle occurred in 1670.\"},{\"q\":\"What happened to the Mavlas after Tanaji’s death?\",\"options\":[\"They lost their leader and retreated\",\"They captured the fort\",\"They celebrated the victory\",\"They surrendered to the Mughals\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"They lost their leader and had to retreat.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the modern name of Kondana fort after its capture?\",\"options\":[\"Sinhagad\",\"Raigad\",\"Pratapgad\",\"Purandar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It became known as Sinhagad.\"},{\"q\":\"Who performed the mourning ceremony at Umrathe?\",\"options\":[\"Shivaji\",\"Jijamata\",\"Tanaji\",\"Suryaji\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shivaji performed the ceremony.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22269,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','A Unique Ceremony','[\"Shivaji Maharaj was crowned king in 1674.\",\"The coronation ceremony was held at Raigad fort.\",\"Shivaji chose Raigad as the capital because it was a strong hill fort and easy to defend.\",\"During the ceremony, a golden throne was made and a canopy (chhatra) was placed over Shivaji’s head.\",\"A special vessel was filled with water from seven rivers: Ganga, Sindhu, Yamuna, Godavari, Krishna, Narmada, Kaveri and the sea.\",\"The water was poured over Shivaji’s head to purify him before he became king.\",\"Shivaji’s mother Jijamata’s eyes filled with tears of joy when he was crowned.\",\"Shivaji struck his own coins after the coronation to show his sovereignty.\",\"The coronation calendar was started by Shivaji to mark the beginning of his rule.\",\"The ceremony was attended by many princes, Brahmins, and foreign envoys.\",\"Shivaji’s coronation was not for personal glory but to give Swaraj a firm footing.\",\"The ceremony included music, singing, and the recitation of coronation mantras.\",\"The golden canopy for the goddess Bhavani was presented by Shivaji after the ceremony.\",\"Shivaji’s fame spread everywhere after the coronation.\",\"The coronation day was considered the most auspicious day for the new king.\",\"Shivaji’s coronation helped establish Swaraj as a recognized kingdom.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In which year did Shivaji Maharaj’s coronation take place?\",\"options\":[\"1674\",\"1675\",\"1647\",\"1680\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text states the coronation took place in 1674.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the name of the hill fort chosen as the capital?\",\"options\":[\"Sinhagad\",\"Raigad\",\"Panhalgad\",\"Pratapgad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji selected Raigad as the capital.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following rivers was NOT used to fill the golden vessel?\",\"options\":[\"Ganga\",\"Sindhu\",\"Kaveri\",\"Aras\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The vessel was filled with water from Ganga, Sindhu, Yamuna, Godavari, Krishna, Narmada, Kaveri and the sea.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Shivaji strike his own coins after the coronation?\",\"options\":[\"To decorate the palace\",\"To pay his soldiers\",\"To show sovereignty\",\"To collect taxes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Striking coins was a symbol of sovereignty.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was present at the coronation ceremony?\",\"options\":[\"Only Shivaji’s family\",\"Princes, Brahmins, and foreign envoys\",\"Only the priests\",\"Only the soldiers\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The ceremony was attended by princes, Brahmins, and foreign envoys.\"},{\"q\":\"What was placed over Shivaji’s head during the ceremony?\",\"options\":[\"A crown\",\"A canopy (chhatra)\",\"A veil\",\"A garland\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A canopy (chhatra) was held over his head.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main purpose of the coronation ceremony?\",\"options\":[\"Personal glory\",\"To give Swaraj a firm footing\",\"To celebrate a festival\",\"To invite foreign envoys\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji did it to give Swaraj a firm footing.\"},{\"q\":\"Which goddess’s darshan did Shivaji take during the ceremony?\",\"options\":[\"Goddess Lakshmi\",\"Goddess Durga\",\"Goddess Bhavani\",\"Goddess Saraswati\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shivaji took the goddess Bhavani’s darshan.\"},{\"q\":\"What type of vessel did Gagabhatt carry?\",\"options\":[\"A gold vessel\",\"A silver vessel\",\"A bronze vessel\",\"A wooden vessel\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Gagabhatt carried a gold vessel filled with the waters of the seven rivers.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji’s mother do when he was crowned?\",\"options\":[\"Cheered loudly\",\"Fell asleep\",\"Her eyes filled with tears of joy\",\"She left the hall\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Jijamata’s eyes filled with tears of joy.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22270,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','The Southern Campaign','[\"Shivaji Maharaj started a southern campaign to create a strong base against the Mughals.\",\"He first visited Golconda, the capital of the Golconda Sultanate, where he was warmly welcomed by Sultan Abul Hasan Kutubshah.\",\"The Sultan gave Shivaji a special throne and many gifts, showing respect for him as a king.\",\"After Golconda, Shivaji went to the fort of Jinji on the east coast and captured it after a long siege.\",\"He also tried to capture the fort of Vellore, but could not succeed and had to leave it.\",\"Shivaji’s step‑brother Vyankoji Raje controlled the Tanjavur Jagir in the south and did not give Shivaji his share.\",\"Shivaji wrote to Vyankoji and asked him to change his ways and help him in his campaign.\",\"Shivaji’s southern campaign helped him gain control of many forts and a large territory with an annual revenue of about twenty lakhs.\",\"Shivaji died on 3rd April 1680 at the age of fifty‑five, leaving a legacy of a powerful Maratha kingdom.\",\"The capital of the Golconda Sultanate was the city of Golconda, a fortified town in present‑day Telangana.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which kingdom did Shivaji Maharaj plan to attack first in his southern campaign?\",\"options\":[\"Bijapur\",\"Golconda\",\"Karnataka\",\"Vijayanagar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He decided to attack Karnataka first.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the ruler of Golconda that welcomed Shivaji Maharaj?\",\"options\":[\"Abulhasan Kutubshah\",\"Shahaji Raje\",\"Vyankoji Raje\",\"Aurangzeb\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Abulhasan Kutubshah of Golconda invited him.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Shivaji wanted a strong base in the south?\",\"options\":[\"To trade with the French\",\"To avoid the Mughal attacks\",\"To build a new capital\",\"To recruit soldiers from the south\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He wanted a base to resist Mughal blows.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji besiege and capture during his southern campaign?\",\"options\":[\"Vellore\",\"Jinji\",\"Tanjavur\",\"Raigad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He captured the fort of Jinji.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was Shivaji’s step‑brother that controlled the Tanjavur Jagir?\",\"options\":[\"Vyankoji Raje\",\"Abulhasan Kutubshah\",\"Shahaji Raje\",\"Siddi of Janjira\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Vyankoji Raje was his step‑brother.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji Maharaj write to Vyankoji Raje to persuade him?\",\"options\":[\"Ask for money\",\"Invite him to war\",\"Request him to surrender\",\"Appeal to change his ways\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"He appealed to change his ways.\"},{\"q\":\"Which city was the capital of Kutubshah’s kingdom?\",\"options\":[\"Golconda\",\"Vijayanagar\",\"Bijapur\",\"Raigad\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Golconda was the capital.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main threat to Shivaji’s Swaraj from the north?\",\"options\":[\"The French\",\"The British\",\"The Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb\",\"The Portuguese\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Aurangzeb was the northern threat.\"},{\"q\":\"Where did Shivaji Maharaj die?\",\"options\":[\"Raigad\",\"Vijayanagar\",\"Golconda\",\"Tanjavur\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He died at Raigad.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji fail to capture after a long siege?\",\"options\":[\"Vellore\",\"Jinji\",\"Tanjavur\",\"Raigad\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He could not capture Vellore after months.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22271,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Administration of Forts and the Navy','[\"- A fort is a strong building used for defence.\",\"- Shivaji Maharaj built many forts like Raigad, Rajgad, Pratapgad, Sindhudurg, Vijaydurg.\",\"- Forts are of three types: forest forts, hill forts and sea forts.\",\"- A sea fort is also called a coastal fort.\",\"- Important people in fort management: Killedar (fort commander), Sabnis (account officer), Karkhanis (workers).\",\"- Killedar protects the fort and gives orders.\",\"- Sabnis keeps accounts and supplies food.\",\"- Karkhanis build and maintain the fort and handle ammunition.\",\"- Guns were used on forts; they were coated with wax to stop rust.\",\"- Shivaji’s navy had warships made from local boats and used cannons and gunpowder.\",\"- The navy protected the coast and helped Shivaji win battles.\",\"- Shivaji was called the ‘Father of the Indian Navy’.\",\"- He used spies to gather information before launching attacks.\",\"- Guerilla tactics involve quick, unexpected attacks and use of forests and hill forts.\",\"- Shivaji’s army respected women and did not drink alcohol.\",\"- The Maratha navy made enemies like the English, Portuguese, Dutch and Siddis fear them.\",\"- Management skills of Shivaji include planning, organisation, discipline and use of resources.\",\"- Students can learn from Shivaji’s planning to organise their own work.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which fort was the first capital of Shivaji Maharaj?\",\"options\":[\"Raigad\",\"Pratapgad\",\"Rajgad\",\"Sindhudurg\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Raigad was Shivaji Maharaj’s first capital.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a sea fort also known as?\",\"options\":[\"Hill fort\",\"Forest fort\",\"Coastal fort\",\"Mountain fort\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sea forts are also called coastal forts.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was responsible for keeping accounts at a fort?\",\"options\":[\"Killedar\",\"Sabnis\",\"Karkhanis\",\"Naval officer\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sabnis kept the accounts of the fort.\"},{\"q\":\"Which material was used to coat guns on forts?\",\"options\":[\"Oil\",\"Wax\",\"Water\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Guns were coated with wax to prevent rust.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji Maharaj is called the ______ of the Indian Navy.\",\"options\":[\"Father\",\"Mother\",\"Brother\",\"Sister\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He is known as the Father of the Indian Navy.\"},{\"q\":\"What tactic involved sudden attacks on the enemy?\",\"options\":[\"Defensive tactics\",\"Guerilla tactics\",\"Naval tactics\",\"Diplomatic tactics\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Guerilla tactics involve sudden attacks.\"},{\"q\":\"Which community was NOT mentioned as part of Shivaji’s navy?\",\"options\":[\"Koli\",\"Bhandari\",\"Agari\",\"Maharaja\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Maharaja is a title, not a community.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Shivaji’s spies gather before an attack?\",\"options\":[\"Weapons\",\"Food\",\"Information\",\"Money\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Spies gathered information about the enemy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a type of fort?\",\"options\":[\"Forest fort\",\"Hill fort\",\"Sea fort\",\"River fort\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"River fort is not a type mentioned.\"},{\"q\":\"Which person was the chief of the intelligence branch?\",\"options\":[\"Bahirji Naik\",\"Kanhoji Jedhe\",\"Veer Baji\",\"Pasalkar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bahirji Naik was the chief of intelligence.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22272,17,4,'EVS Part 2','en','Administration of the Welfare State','[\"• The Welfare State is a state that looks after the needs of its people – food, clothing and shelter.\",\"• Shivaji Maharaj created a welfare state called Swaraj where people could live happily and safely.\",\"• He divided his administration into eight departments and appointed a Minister for each – the Council of Eight Ministers.\",\"• The ministers were responsible for Administration, Revenue, Defence, Religious Matters, Justice, Government Orders, Correspondence and Foreign Relations.\",\"• Shivaji Maharaj protected the environment – he forbade cutting trees unless the owner gave permission and paid for it.\",\"• He built water tanks, dams, bunds and canals so that forts and villages had enough water even in droughts.\",\"• He encouraged trade by building ports, markets and a navy, and he levied taxes on imported goods to promote local trade.\",\"• He was tolerant to all religions – he protected mosques, temples and churches and helped people who changed their faith.\",\"• He gave special treatment to brave women like Mallamma Desai and honoured them with titles and new settlements.\",\"• The welfare state is run by the people for the people, with confidence, self‑respect and patriotism.\",\"• Good governance means justice, fairness, protection of nature and care for the poor and the needy.\",\"• A welfare state provides free grain, water and medicine to those in need.\",\"• The welfare state is a state that is run by the people, for the people and for the welfare of all.\",\"• Shivaji Maharaj’s policies are examples of how a ruler can build a welfare state that protects people, nature and culture.\",\"• The welfare state is a model for future governments to look after the well‑being of all citizens.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of a welfare state as described in the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"To increase taxes\",\"To provide for people\'s basic needs\",\"To build more forts\",\"To promote war\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A welfare state ensures food, clothing, shelter and other necessities for its citizens.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a duty of Shivaji Maharaj’s ministers?\",\"options\":[\"Managing revenue\",\"Defence\",\"Religious matters\",\"Building schools for all\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The council of eight ministers handled revenue, defence, religion, etc., but did not directly build schools.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji Maharaj’s policy on forests mainly aimed to:\",\"options\":[\"Cut all trees for ships\",\"Cut only teak and other useful trees\",\"Cut all trees for building forts\",\"Cut no trees at all\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He allowed cutting of teak and other useful trees with permission, but protected mango, jackfruit, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of water management by Shivaji Maharaj?\",\"options\":[\"Building a dam near his village\",\"Planting mango trees\",\"Building a new market\",\"Building a new fort\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He built dams, bunds, canals and reservoirs to manage water.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Shivaji Maharaj levied higher taxes on imported salt?\",\"options\":[\"To punish enemies\",\"To increase revenue\",\"To encourage local salt production\",\"To make salt taste better\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Higher taxes on imported salt made local salt cheaper, encouraging local trade.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji Maharaj’s attitude towards people of different religions was:\",\"options\":[\"Hostile\",\"Neutral\",\"Respectful and tolerant\",\"Indifferent\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He respected Muslims, Christians, Hindus and did not harm them.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was a benefit of the ‘Jalyukt Shivar’ project?\",\"options\":[\"Providing free grain to peasants\",\"Building a new navy\",\"Providing water to drought‑affected peasants\",\"Building new forts\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The project supplied water to drought‑affected peasants, helping them earn a living.\"},{\"q\":\"The Council of Eight Ministers was also known as:\",\"options\":[\"Swaraj Council\",\"Shivaji Council\",\"Rajaguru Council\",\"Swaraj of People\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It was called the Council of Eight Ministers, also referred to as the Swaraj of People.\"},{\"q\":\"Which tree was explicitly protected from cutting in Shivaji Maharaj’s orders?\",\"options\":[\"Teak\",\"Mango\",\"Jackfruit\",\"Banyan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mango and jackfruit trees were protected because they do not grow quickly.\"},{\"q\":\"The main goal of Shivaji Maharaj’s environmental policies was to:\",\"options\":[\"Increase war resources\",\"Build more forts\",\"Protect the environment for future generations\",\"Make ships faster\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He aimed to preserve forests, water and other natural resources for future generations.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22273,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Our Earth and Our Solar System','[\"The Sun is a star and the only star that we can see with the naked eye.\",\"The Earth is a planet that revolves around the Sun and receives light and heat from it.\",\"The Moon is the Earth’s only natural satellite and it revolves around the Earth.\",\"Planets are heavenly bodies that do not twinkle because they have their own light or reflect the Sun’s light.\",\"Stars are bright points in the sky that twinkle because they are far away and have their own light.\",\"The Solar System contains the Sun, eight planets, dwarf planets, asteroids, and comets.\",\"The eight planets from the Sun are Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune.\",\"The order of the planets from the Sun is: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.\",\"Dwarf planets such as Pluto, Ceres, Eris, Haumea, and Makemake are also part of the Solar System.\",\"Satellites are natural or man‑made objects that revolve around a planet; the Moon is the Earth’s satellite.\",\"Asteroids are small rocky bodies that mainly lie between Mars and Jupiter.\",\"The force that keeps planets, satellites, and other bodies in orbit is gravity.\",\"Gravity is the attraction between any two masses; the Sun’s gravity keeps the planets in orbit.\",\"The Moon’s phases change because only part of its illuminated side is visible from Earth.\",\"Space is the empty region beyond the planets and stars where rockets and satellites travel.\",\"Rockets use powerful engines to overcome Earth’s gravity and reach space.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which planet is closest to the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Mercury\",\"Venus\",\"Earth\",\"Mars\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mercury is the nearest planet to the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the Earth\'s only natural satellite?\",\"options\":[\"Mars\",\"Jupiter\",\"Saturn\",\"Moon\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The Moon is Earth\'s only natural satellite.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a dwarf planet in our solar system?\",\"options\":[\"Ceres\",\"Pluto\",\"Vesta\",\"Eros\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pluto is classified as a dwarf planet.\"},{\"q\":\"Which planet is known as the \'Red Planet\'?\",\"options\":[\"Venus\",\"Mars\",\"Jupiter\",\"Saturn\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mars is called the Red Planet because of its reddish appearance.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main source of light for the planets?\",\"options\":[\"The Sun\",\"The Moon\",\"Stars\",\"Artificial satellites\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Planets receive light from the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"Which planet has the most moons?\",\"options\":[\"Earth\",\"Mars\",\"Jupiter\",\"Venus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Jupiter has the most moons of all planets.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the path a planet follows around the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Orbit\",\"Revolution\",\"Rotation\",\"Trajectory\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The path around the Sun is called an orbit.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these objects are found between Mars and Jupiter?\",\"options\":[\"Comets\",\"Asteroids\",\"Satellites\",\"Dwarf planets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A belt of asteroids lies between Mars and Jupiter.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of a rocket in space travel?\",\"options\":[\"To carry people only\",\"To carry cargo only\",\"To carry both people and cargo\",\"To carry only satellites\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rockets are used to carry both people and cargo into space.\"},{\"q\":\"Which force keeps the Moon in orbit around the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Magnetic force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Electric force\",\"Nuclear force\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gravity keeps the Moon in orbit around Earth.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22274,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Motions of the Earth','[\"Earth rotates around its own axis once every 24 hours – this is called rotation.\",\"The line about which Earth turns is the axis of rotation, passing through the North and South Poles.\",\"The equator is the imaginary circle that divides Earth into northern and southern hemispheres.\",\"Daytime occurs when a part of Earth faces the Sun; nighttime when it faces away.\",\"A full rotation of Earth gives one day, which is divided into 24 hours.\",\"Earth also revolves around the Sun once every 365 days and 6 hours – this is one year.\",\"Because Earth’s axis is tilted, the length of day and night changes with seasons.\",\"The Sun rises in the east and sets in the west because Earth rotates from west to east.\",\"The Moon revolves around Earth once every 28–30 days, producing its phases.\",\"The Moon’s phases are called the waxing (Shukla Paksha) and waning (Krishna Paksha) periods.\",\"A lunar month is the time from one new moon to the next, about 29–30 days.\",\"The month is divided into 15 days of waxing and 15 days of waning, called tithis.\",\"Leap year adds an extra day (29 Feb) every four years to keep the calendar in sync with Earth’s orbit.\",\"The equator is the widest part of Earth, equidistant from the poles.\",\"The North Pole is the point where the axis points north; the South Pole points south.\",\"The Earth’s axis is inclined at about 23.5°, causing seasons.\",\"Sunrise and sunset occur when the Sun crosses the horizon at a particular location.\",\"The Sun rises in the east because Earth’s rotation is anti‑clockwise when viewed from the North Pole.\",\"The Moon is visible only when illuminated by the Sun’s light.\",\"During a full moon, the entire near side of the Moon faces Earth.\",\"During a new moon, the near side is dark and not visible from Earth.\",\"The length of day is longer than night in the Northern Hemisphere between 22 March and 23 September.\",\"In the Southern Hemisphere, the opposite is true during the same period.\",\"The Earth’s rotation and revolution together create day, night, and seasons.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the term for Earth turning around its own axis?\",\"options\":[\"Revolution\",\"Rotation\",\"Precession\",\"Precession\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rotation is the motion of Earth around its axis.\"},{\"q\":\"Which line divides Earth into northern and southern hemispheres?\",\"options\":[\"Meridian\",\"Equator\",\"Prime Meridian\",\"Great Circle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The equator is the imaginary circle dividing the hemispheres.\"},{\"q\":\"How many hours are in one day?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"24\",\"36\",\"48\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A day is divided into 24 hours.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the period of Earth’s revolution around the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"24 hours\",\"365 days\",\"30 days\",\"12 months\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One year is about 365 days and 6 hours.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the Moon’s phases when the illuminated part increases?\",\"options\":[\"Waning\",\"Waxing\",\"Full\",\"New\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Waxing phases occur when the illuminated part grows.\"},{\"q\":\"Which city is in the Northern Hemisphere?\",\"options\":[\"Mumbai\",\"Nagpur\",\"Bhopal\",\"Kolkata\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Kolkata is in the Northern Hemisphere.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a leap year?\",\"options\":[\"A year with 365 days\",\"A year with 366 days\",\"A year with 364 days\",\"A year with 367 days\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Leap year has 366 days, adding 29 Feb.\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of Earth faces the Sun during daytime?\",\"options\":[\"The side facing away\",\"The side facing the Sun\",\"The side facing the Moon\",\"The side facing the North Pole\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Daytime occurs when Earth faces the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for Earth’s motion around the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Rotation\",\"Revolution\",\"Precession\",\"Precession\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Revolution is the motion around the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"How many tithis are there in a lunar month?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"15\",\"20\",\"25\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A lunar month has 15 waxing and 15 waning tithis.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22275,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','The Earth and its Living World','[\"The Earth has four main spheres: lithosphere (land), hydrosphere (water), atmosphere (air) and biosphere (living things).\",\"The lithosphere is the hard outer shell made of rock and soil. It is where we build houses and where tree roots grow.\",\"The hydrosphere includes all water on Earth – oceans, rivers, lakes, groundwater and ice.\",\"The atmosphere is the air that surrounds Earth. It is made mainly of nitrogen, oxygen, water vapour and carbon dioxide.\",\"The atmosphere has five layers: troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, ionosphere and exosphere. The troposphere is the lowest layer where weather happens.\",\"The troposphere extends up to about 13 km. Above it the air gets cooler. The stratosphere starts at about 13 km and contains the ozone layer.\",\"The ozone layer absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays from the sun. This protects living things.\",\"The water cycle is a continuous loop: evaporation, condensation, clouds, precipitation (rain, snow) and runoff. It keeps water moving on Earth.\",\"Evaporation happens when the sun heats water and it turns into vapour. It rises into the troposphere.\",\"Condensation is when vapour cools and turns back into liquid droplets, forming clouds. When droplets become heavy they fall as rain or snow.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which sphere of the Earth is the outermost layer that surrounds the planet?\",\"options\":[\"Lithosphere\",\"Hydrosphere\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Biosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The atmosphere is the outermost gaseous envelope around Earth.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the layer of the atmosphere where most weather occurs?\",\"options\":[\"Stratosphere\",\"Mesosphere\",\"Troposphere\",\"Exosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Weather phenomena like clouds and rain happen in the troposphere.\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas in the stratosphere protects life from harmful rays?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Ozone\",\"Carbon dioxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ozone absorbs ultraviolet rays from the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main source of water for rivers?\",\"options\":[\"Groundwater\",\"Rainfall\",\"Icebergs\",\"Deserts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rainfall and melting ice feed rivers.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a landform?\",\"options\":[\"Mountain\",\"Plain\",\"River\",\"Plateau\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"River is a water body, not a landform.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for water that has fallen from clouds and lands on the ground?\",\"options\":[\"Condensation\",\"Precipitation\",\"Evaporation\",\"Transpiration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Precipitation includes rain, snow, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"Which sphere contains the Earth\'s crust?\",\"options\":[\"Atmosphere\",\"Hydrosphere\",\"Lithosphere\",\"Biosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The lithosphere is the Earth\'s crust and upper mantle.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main component of the Earth\'s atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Water vapour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Nitrogen makes up about 78% of the atmosphere.\"},{\"q\":\"Which process turns water vapor into liquid droplets in clouds?\",\"options\":[\"Evaporation\",\"Condensation\",\"Precipitation\",\"Transpiration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Condensation forms cloud droplets.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a type of freshwater body?\",\"options\":[\"Ocean\",\"Sea\",\"Lake\",\"Seaweed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A lake is a freshwater body; oceans and seas are saltwater.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22276,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Environmental Balance','[\"- Biodiversity is the variety of living things in a particular area.\",\"- Living things depend on each other and on non‑living factors like sunlight, air, water, and soil.\",\"- A food chain shows who eats whom; each link is a species or group.\",\"- A food web is a network of many food chains connected together.\",\"- Plants are the base of most food chains because they make food using sunlight, water, and CO₂.\",\"- Decomposition by micro‑organisms turns dead matter into nutrients for soil.\",\"- The water cycle keeps water moving through evaporation, condensation and precipitation.\",\"- The carbon cycle involves plants taking in CO₂ and releasing O₂, while animals breathe O₂ and exhale CO₂.\",\"- Balance in the environment means that food, water, and nutrients are available for all living things.\",\"- If one link in a food chain disappears, the whole chain can be affected.\",\"- Scientists observe living things in different conditions (day\\/night, seasons) to study biodiversity.\",\"- Observations may include noting shape, colour, sound, and habitat of organisms.\",\"- A food chain can be broken if one link is removed; it then becomes a food web if many chains interconnect.\",\"- Maintaining biodiversity helps keep ecosystems stable and productive.\",\"- Human activities like using insecticides can harm beneficial insects and disrupt balance.\",\"- Plants need nutrients from soil, which are replenished by decomposition and fertilisation.\",\"- Oxygen produced by plants is essential for animal respiration.\",\"- Micro‑organisms in soil help recycle nutrients and support plant growth.\",\"- The environment is a system where living and non‑living factors interact and give and take.\",\"- Conservation of habitats protects many species and keeps ecosystems functioning.\",\"- Simple actions like planting trees, reducing waste, and protecting water bodies help maintain balance.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the base of most food chains?\",\"options\":[\"Animals\",\"Plants\",\"Micro‑organisms\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plants produce food through photosynthesis, providing energy for other organisms.\"},{\"q\":\"Which cycle involves plants taking in CO₂ and releasing O₂?\",\"options\":[\"Water cycle\",\"Nitrogen cycle\",\"Carbon cycle\",\"Oxygen cycle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The carbon cycle describes this exchange.\"},{\"q\":\"If a predator disappears from a food chain, what may happen?\",\"options\":[\"The chain becomes shorter\",\"The chain becomes longer\",\"The chain is unaffected\",\"The chain stops working\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Without the predator, prey may over‑populate, disrupting the chain.\"},{\"q\":\"What process turns dead plants and animals into soil nutrients?\",\"options\":[\"Photosynthesis\",\"Respiration\",\"Decomposition\",\"Transpiration\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Decomposition by micro‑organisms breaks down organic matter.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a non‑living factor in the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Sunlight\",\"Water\",\"Plants\",\"Soil\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plants are living; the others are non‑living.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a food web represent?\",\"options\":[\"A single chain of food\",\"All possible food chains in an ecosystem\",\"Only plants and animals\",\"The water cycle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A food web shows many interconnected food chains.\"},{\"q\":\"Which organism is a primary producer?\",\"options\":[\"Worm\",\"Snake\",\"Grasshopper\",\"Sun\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Plants (grass) produce food using sunlight.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are micro‑organisms important for soil?\",\"options\":[\"They eat plants\",\"They build soil structure\",\"They decompose organic matter\",\"They produce oxygen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"They break down matter, releasing nutrients.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of the water cycle?\",\"options\":[\"To produce oxygen\",\"To recycle nutrients\",\"To move water around Earth\",\"To create food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It transports water through evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true about biodiversity?\",\"options\":[\"It is the same everywhere\",\"It is the variety of living things in an area\",\"It only includes animals\",\"It is unrelated to food chains\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Biodiversity refers to the variety of species in a place.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22277,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Family Values','[\"Family is a group where everyone has a role in decisions.\",\"Each member’s opinion matters and should be heard before any choice.\",\"We talk, listen, and agree together to make fair decisions.\",\"Honesty is important in family and public life; it builds trust and efficiency.\",\"Co‑operation means working together and sharing responsibilities.\",\"Tolerance means accepting others’ differences and not judging them.\",\"Gender equality means boys and girls, men and women are treated the same.\",\"Everyone should help with household chores, not just one person.\",\"When a mistake happens, we should admit it and correct it together.\",\"Being honest and cooperative helps the family grow stronger and happier.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What should you do before making a decision in your family?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore others’ opinions\",\"Ask everyone’s opinion\",\"Decide alone\",\"Choose the youngest’s idea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"All family members’ views are considered before deciding.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is honesty important in public life?\",\"options\":[\"It makes people look good\",\"It increases efficiency\",\"It saves money only\",\"It is not needed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Honesty helps public services run smoothly and efficiently.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a sign of cooperation?\",\"options\":[\"Only one person does all chores\",\"Everyone helps each other\",\"No one talks about mistakes\",\"Everyone keeps secrets\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cooperation means sharing tasks and supporting each other.\"},{\"q\":\"What does gender equality mean?\",\"options\":[\"Only boys get toys\",\"Girls are better than boys\",\"Boys and girls are treated the same\",\"Men are superior to women\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Gender equality means treating boys and girls, men and women equally.\"},{\"q\":\"Tolerance helps us to:\",\"options\":[\"Ignore others’ views\",\"Listen to others’ opinions\",\"Keep secrets\",\"Make decisions alone\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tolerance means listening and understanding others’ views.\"},{\"q\":\"If a family member makes a mistake, the best action is to:\",\"options\":[\"Blame them\",\"Hide it\",\"Admit and correct it\",\"Ignore it\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Admitting and correcting mistakes builds trust.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity shows cooperation?\",\"options\":[\"Playing alone\",\"Watching TV alone\",\"Helping in cooking\",\"Reading alone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Helping in cooking involves working together.\"},{\"q\":\"When deciding on a trip, you should:\",\"options\":[\"Choose the destination alone\",\"Ask everyone’s suggestions\",\"Let the teacher decide\",\"Pick the cheapest option\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"All family members should give suggestions.\"},{\"q\":\"Honesty in public life helps to:\",\"options\":[\"Create confusion\",\"Avoid waste of time and money\",\"Make people angry\",\"Reduce services\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Honesty prevents wastage and improves services.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a key benefit of being honest?\",\"options\":[\"Loss of trust\",\"More efficient public services\",\"Less cooperation\",\"More arguments\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Honesty increases trust and efficiency.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22278,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Rules Are for Everyone','[\"- Rules are laws that everyone must follow.\",\"- Natural laws (gravity, tides) never change, but human rules can change.\",\"- Traffic rules keep roads safe and traffic moving smoothly.\",\"- Social rules help people live together peacefully.\",\"- Everyone, no matter who they are, must obey the same rules.\",\"- Disobeying rules can lead to punishment or unfairness.\",\"- Laws are made to protect people and the environment.\",\"- Some customs (like untouchability) were banned by law.\",\"- Education laws (free primary education) help everyone learn.\",\"- Rules should be fair, equal and not discriminate.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a natural law that never changes?\",\"options\":[\"Gravity\",\"Traffic rules\",\"School rules\",\"Customs\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Gravity is a natural law that is constant.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we need traffic rules?\",\"options\":[\"To make roads look nice\",\"To keep traffic moving smoothly\",\"To give drivers more freedom\",\"To stop people from walking\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Traffic rules keep roads safe and traffic flowing.\"},{\"q\":\"Which rule is an example of a law that protects the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Prohibition of loudspeakers after 10 pm\",\"Free primary education\",\"Ban on dumping garbage in rivers\",\"Ban on child labour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Banning garbage dumping protects rivers.\"},{\"q\":\"What is an example of a social rule that was changed by law?\",\"options\":[\"Untouchability\",\"Sati\",\"Superstition\",\"Illiteracy\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Untouchability was banned by law.\"},{\"q\":\"Who can break the rules?\",\"options\":[\"Only teachers\",\"Only parents\",\"Everyone equally\",\"Only children\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rules apply to everyone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a human-made rule that can change?\",\"options\":[\"The rising of the sun\",\"The phases of the moon\",\"The law of gravity\",\"Traffic rules\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Human rules like traffic rules can be changed.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of laws?\",\"options\":[\"To make people rich\",\"To keep people busy\",\"To maintain equality and justice\",\"To create more rules\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Laws aim for equality and justice.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a natural law?\",\"options\":[\"Gravity\",\"Tides\",\"Traffic rules\",\"Sunrise\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Traffic rules are human-made.\"},{\"q\":\"Why was the voting age lowered from 21 to 18?\",\"options\":[\"To give more people a voice\",\"To reduce elections\",\"To make voting easier\",\"To stop young people from voting\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lowering age gives more people a vote.\"},{\"q\":\"Which rule helps protect children?\",\"options\":[\"Ban on child labour\",\"Ban on loudspeakers\",\"Free primary education\",\"Ban on hunting\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Banning child labour protects children.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22279,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Let us Solve our own Problems','[\"Problems in civic life are issues that disturb the harmony of a community.\",\"A problem is something that causes inconvenience or difficulty.\",\"Disputes are problems that arise between people or groups.\",\"Peacekeepers are people who help solve disputes peacefully.\",\"The Mahatma Gandhi Dispute‑Free Village Mission helps villages solve their own problems.\",\"In a village, people can solve problems by discussion, cooperation and participation.\",\"If a problem is serious, it may need help from a court or a local body.\",\"Cleaning a village through ‘shramdaan’ means everyone works voluntarily to keep the village clean.\",\"Vermiculture is a method of using worms to turn garbage into manure.\",\"Peaceful solutions include dialogue, mediation, and community meetings.\",\"When everyone helps, problems disappear faster and society becomes stronger.\",\"A peaceful family, school and society benefits everyone and helps progress in all fields.\",\"The UN declared 21 September as International Peace Day to promote peace worldwide.\",\"A group of peacekeepers can be formed in a school to help solve small disputes.\",\"To solve a problem, first identify it, then discuss it with all parties involved.\",\"The basic needs of all people must be met for a peaceful society: security, equality and participation.\",\"Cleanliness, education and self‑reliance are essential for progress.\",\"When you see a problem, don’t ignore it; act quickly to prevent it from getting worse.\",\"A village can create a peace environment by setting rules, using peaceful methods and encouraging participation.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a problem in civic life?\",\"options\":[\"A type of plant\",\"A situation that causes inconvenience\",\"A kind of food\",\"A musical instrument\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A problem is something that causes difficulty or inconvenience.\"},{\"q\":\"Who helps villages solve their own disputes in the Dispute‑Free Village Mission?\",\"options\":[\"The police\",\"The villagers themselves\",\"The king\",\"The school principal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Villages solve disputes through discussion among their own people.\"},{\"q\":\"What does ‘shramdaan’ mean?\",\"options\":[\"Paid work\",\"Voluntary contribution of labour\",\"A type of dance\",\"A special food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shramdaan is voluntary work done by people to help the community.\"},{\"q\":\"Which method can help solve a dispute peacefully?\",\"options\":[\"Violence\",\"Ignoring it\",\"Dialogue\",\"Throwing stones\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dialogue and discussion help resolve disputes without violence.\"},{\"q\":\"What is vermiculture used for?\",\"options\":[\"Making soap\",\"Turning garbage into manure\",\"Cleaning roads\",\"Building houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Vermiculture uses worms to convert waste into useful manure.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if you see a problem in your village?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore it\",\"Report it to a responsible person\",\"Throw it away\",\"Call a friend\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Reporting helps the problem be addressed quickly.\"},{\"q\":\"What is International Peace Day?\",\"options\":[\"A day to eat sweets\",\"A day to celebrate sports\",\"A day to promote peace\",\"A day to watch movies\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It is a day to promote peace worldwide.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is cleanliness important for progress?\",\"options\":[\"It looks nice\",\"It helps the environment\",\"It is a tradition\",\"It is a myth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cleanliness supports health, education and development.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main aim of the Mahatma Gandhi Dispute‑Free Village Mission?\",\"options\":[\"To build schools\",\"To solve disputes peacefully\",\"To plant trees\",\"To make money\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The mission aims to solve disputes peacefully in villages.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a way to solve a problem?\",\"options\":[\"Discussion\",\"Cooperation\",\"Ignoring it\",\"Mediation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ignoring a problem makes it worse.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22280,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Public Facilities and My School','[\"Public facilities are services or places that anyone can use, like schools, parks, libraries, hospitals, and transport.\",\"Water, electricity, health, education and transport are the main public services that help everyone.\",\"A school is a public facility where children learn and play; it is part of the community system.\",\"Every child has a right to free primary education (ages 6‑14) and up to 18 for special needs (Right to Education Act).\",\"Parents, teachers, and community members help build and run schools – they may donate money, materials or time.\",\"Schools can organise cleanliness drives, poster competitions and play‑dramas to teach good habits to the village.\",\"Parent‑Teacher Association (PTA) and Mata‑Palak Sangh (Mother‑Teacher Group) help parents and teachers cooperate for the school’s benefit.\",\"A school’s unique features include its library, laboratory, playground, cafeteria, and special programmes like counselling or science clubs.\",\"Students should identify all services available at their school and list them on a poster.\",\"Public services must be used responsibly; misuse can harm the community.\",\"The school is a part of the larger system of public services that keeps society running smoothly.\",\"Children can become agents of cleanliness by organising drives and educating neighbours.\",\"Community support for a school can include building classrooms, providing equipment or organising events.\",\"Every child’s right to education is a fundamental right that must be protected by law.\",\"Good school facilities encourage learning, health and safety for all students.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a public facility mentioned in the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Library\",\"Bus\",\"Private Gym\",\"School\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chapter lists libraries, buses, and schools but not private gyms.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main right that every child has according to the Right to Education Act?\",\"options\":[\"Free primary education\",\"Free secondary education\",\"Free higher education\",\"Free vocational training\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Act guarantees free primary education for children aged 6‑14.\"},{\"q\":\"Who can help build and run a school?\",\"options\":[\"Only teachers\",\"Only parents\",\"Parents, teachers and community members\",\"Only the government\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chapter says parents, teachers and community members all help.\"},{\"q\":\"A school can organise a cleanliness drive to teach what?\",\"options\":[\"Sports skills\",\"Mathematics\",\"Good habits\",\"History\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cleanliness drives teach good habits and hygiene.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a benefit of having a Parent‑Teacher Association?\",\"options\":[\"More school fees\",\"Less teacher involvement\",\"Better cooperation between parents and teachers\",\"More exams\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"PTA improves cooperation between parents and teachers.\"},{\"q\":\"The Right to Education Act extends the age limit for special needs children to:\",\"options\":[\"14 years\",\"16 years\",\"18 years\",\"20 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Special needs children can study up to 18 years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a public transport facility?\",\"options\":[\"Bus\",\"Private car\",\"Taxi\",\"Motorbike\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bus is a public transport facility; the others are private.\"},{\"q\":\"What should students do to identify their school’s unique features?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore them\",\"Write a poem\",\"Prepare a poster\",\"Take a photo\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chapter suggests making a poster of the school’s features.\"},{\"q\":\"Which service is NOT listed as a public service in the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Water supply\",\"Electricity supply\",\"Private tutoring\",\"Health services\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Private tutoring is not a public service mentioned.\"},{\"q\":\"The school’s cleanliness drive helped the village to win which award?\",\"options\":[\"Best Library Award\",\"Clean Village Award\",\"Sports Excellence Award\",\"Science Fair Award\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The chapter says the village won the Clean Village award.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22281,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Maps – our Companions','[\"Maps show the shape and height of land.\",\"Landforms are mountains, hills, valleys, plains, plateaus, islands, rivers, lakes, and forests.\",\"Contour lines are curves that connect points of the same height on a map.\",\"The space between contour lines is filled with colour in the layer‑tinting method.\",\"Digital elevation models use satellite data to show height on a computer map.\",\"Signs and symbols on a map are universal; e.g., a triangle for a fort, a circle for a lake, a dot for a village.\",\"The key (legend) explains all signs and symbols used on a map.\",\"Maps help in planning, military strategy, tourism, and environmental studies.\",\"The height of the land is measured from sea level; negative values mean below sea level.\",\"A map can show the same area in different ways: contour lines, colour shading, or digital elevation models.\",\"When two maps are close together, the slope is steep; when far apart, the slope is gentle.\",\"A hill’s cross‑section shows a gentle slope on one side and a steep slope on the other.\",\"The Survey of India is the main map‑making institute in India.\",\"Signs for common features: Post office (P), Hospital (H), Factory (F), Park (P), Playground (G), Road (R), Hill (H), River (R).\",\"The colour used for water bodies is blue; higher elevations are light green or yellow, lower are dark green.\",\"A map’s caption gives the title, scale, and date of the map.\",\"The key is essential for understanding the map’s symbols and colours.\",\"Maps are used in military operations, tourism, and regional development plans.\",\"A contour line method shows unevenness; the layer‑tinting method shows height by colour; the digital method shows height by computer data.\",\"The Survey of India started in 1767 and has made many accurate topographic maps.\",\"A map’s symbols are miniature drawings of the objects they represent.\",\"A map’s signs are simple geometric shapes or letters.\",\"The height of a hill can be shown by a hill shading technique.\",\"A map’s legend is also called a key.\",\"When a map shows a wrong colour for a landform, it must be corrected.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which method uses lines that connect points of the same height?\",\"options\":[\"Layer‑tinting\",\"Contour line method\",\"Digital elevation model\",\"Hill shading\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Contour lines join points of equal elevation.\"},{\"q\":\"What colour is usually used to represent water bodies on a map?\",\"options\":[\"Blue\",\"Green\",\"Red\",\"Yellow\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Blue is the standard colour for water.\"},{\"q\":\"The Survey of India was established in which year?\",\"options\":[\"1767\",\"1850\",\"1900\",\"1920\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The institute was founded in 1767.\"},{\"q\":\"Which symbol would you use for a fort on a map?\",\"options\":[\"Triangle\",\"Circle\",\"Square\",\"Diamond\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A triangle is the conventional symbol for a fort.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a map’s key (legend) explain?\",\"options\":[\"The map’s title\",\"The symbols and colours\",\"The scale only\",\"The author of the map\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The key shows meanings of symbols and colours.\"},{\"q\":\"In a layer‑tinting map, what does the colour of a space indicate?\",\"options\":[\"The type of vegetation\",\"The distance from the observer\",\"The specific height\",\"The weather\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Colour indicates the height range of that area.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a landform?\",\"options\":[\"Mountain\",\"River\",\"Lake\",\"Post office\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Post office is a man‑made structure, not a landform.\"},{\"q\":\"When two contour lines are close together on a map, what does that indicate?\",\"options\":[\"Gentle slope\",\"Steep slope\",\"Flat land\",\"Water body\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Close lines mean a steep slope.\"},{\"q\":\"Which method is the most modern for showing elevation?\",\"options\":[\"Contour line method\",\"Layer‑tinting method\",\"Digital elevation model\",\"Hill shading\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Digital elevation models use satellite data.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of using conventional signs on a map?\",\"options\":[\"To make the map colourful\",\"To provide exact information\",\"To confuse the reader\",\"To reduce printing costs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Signs give precise information about features.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22282,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Getting to Know India','[\"India is a peninsula surrounded by water on three sides – the Arabian Sea to the west, the Bay of Bengal to the east and the Indian Ocean to the south.\",\"The northern border of India is the Himalayan mountain range, the highest peaks reaching over 8,000 m.\",\"Major rivers flow from the Himalayas to the plains and then to the sea: Ganga, Yamuna, Brahmaputra, Godavari, Narmada, Krishna, Kaveri and others.\",\"Rivers have a slope – they flow downhill from high to low places. The slope is steeper in the mountains and gentler in the plains.\",\"Plateaus are flat elevated areas; the Deccan Plateau covers most of southern India.\",\"Deserts are found in the north‑west (Thar Desert) and in the central part (Bikaner Desert).\",\"India has many islands: Lakshadweep in the Arabian Sea, Andaman and Nicobar in the Bay of Bengal, and several coastal islands along the Konkan coast.\",\"The capital of India is New Delhi, located in the northern part of the country.\",\"Rajasthan is the largest state by area, followed by Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra.\",\"The smallest state is Goa, and the smallest union territory is Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu.\",\"The Great Indian Desert (Thar) lies mainly in Rajasthan.\",\"Cash crops such as tea, coffee, saffron, cloves, apples and oranges are grown in specific states – e.g., tea in Assam, coffee in Karnataka, saffron in Himachal Pradesh, apples in Himachal and Uttarakhand, oranges in Maharashtra.\",\"Transport routes on the map show how goods move between states – e.g., a route from Nagpur to Bikaner for oranges.\",\"The Indian subcontinent has diverse climates, leading to varied crops: wheat in the north, rice in the south, jowar in the central plains.\",\"India’s cultural diversity includes many languages, religions, castes, festivals, clothing and food traditions.\",\"The political map shows states, union territories, capitals and important cities; the map also indicates the largest state in yellow and the state in the user’s home state in a favourite colour.\",\"The map also shows the names of rivers, mountains, plateaus, deserts, gulfs and islands, and their directions of flow or location.\",\"Students should learn to read a map, identify features, and answer questions about them.\",\"Key terms: peninsula, Himalayas, plateau, desert, river basin, capital, union territory, cash crop, transport route.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following rivers flows from the Himalayas and joins the Indus in the northwestern part of India?\",\"options\":[\"Ganga\",\"Yamuna\",\"Brahmaputra\",\"Indus\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The Indus River itself originates in the Himalayas and flows into Pakistan.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the large desert located mainly in the state of Rajasthan?\",\"options\":[\"Thar Desert\",\"Kalahandi Desert\",\"Kutch Desert\",\"Aravalli Desert\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Thar Desert covers most of Rajasthan.\"},{\"q\":\"Which sea lies to the east of the Indian mainland?\",\"options\":[\"Arabian Sea\",\"Bay of Bengal\",\"Indian Ocean\",\"Andaman Sea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Bay of Bengal is on the eastern side of India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which state is the largest by area in India?\",\"options\":[\"Madhya Pradesh\",\"Maharashtra\",\"Rajasthan\",\"Uttar Pradesh\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rajasthan is the largest state in India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following islands is part of the Andaman and Nicobar group?\",\"options\":[\"Lakshadweep\",\"Sikkim\",\"Kohima\",\"Port Blair\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Port Blair is the capital of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\"},{\"q\":\"The Ganga River flows through which of these states?\",\"options\":[\"Madhya Pradesh\",\"Uttar Pradesh\",\"Karnataka\",\"Tamil Nadu\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Ganga flows mainly through Uttar Pradesh.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these crops is primarily grown in the northern plains of India?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Wheat\",\"Mango\",\"Tea\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wheat is a main crop in northern India.\"},{\"q\":\"The Arabian Sea is located to the ___ of India.\",\"options\":[\"North\",\"South\",\"East\",\"West\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The Arabian Sea lies to the west of India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which river is known as the \'Sister River\' of the Ganga?\",\"options\":[\"Yamuna\",\"Brahmaputra\",\"Godavari\",\"Narmada\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Yamuna is called the sister river of the Ganga.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following states is the smallest by area in India?\",\"options\":[\"Goa\",\"Sikkim\",\"Tripura\",\"Nagaland\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Goa is the smallest state by area.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22283,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Our Home and Environment','[\"- A house is a shelter that protects us from sun, wind, rain and cold.\",\"- Houses are built using materials that are available locally – wood, stone, earth, bricks, cement, glass, metal, tin sheets, etc.\",\"- The shape of a house (rectangular, square, round) and its roof type (flat, sloping, thatched) depend on the climate and the region.\",\"- In plains with moderate rainfall, houses are usually rectangular with stone or earth walls and a sloping roof made of thatch or tiles.\",\"- In heavy rainfall areas, houses have steep roofs to shed rainwater and walls made of earth or stone to keep the interior dry.\",\"- In desert regions, houses are built with thick walls of earth or mud to keep the interior cool and roofs are flat or low sloping.\",\"- In marshy or wetland areas, houses are raised on stilts or made of bamboo to avoid flooding.\",\"- In mountainous regions, houses are small, low‑profile, often made of stone or wood, with steep roofs to handle snow and wind.\",\"- Eco‑friendly houses use renewable energy (solar, wind, biogas), recycle water and waste, avoid artificial colours, and allow natural light and ventilation.\",\"- Pollution from construction (dust, noise, chemical runoff) can harm the environment and people nearby.\",\"- Homeless people lack basic needs like shelter, water, food and education; governments try to provide shelters and housing schemes.\",\"- Children can help by making models of eco‑friendly houses, preparing street plays, and raising awareness about pollution and energy use.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of a house?\",\"options\":[\"To store food\",\"To protect people from weather\",\"To make money\",\"To decorate the street\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A house shelters us from sun, wind, rain and cold.\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is commonly used for walls in plains?\",\"options\":[\"Wood\",\"Stone\",\"Plastic\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Plains houses often use stone or earth walls.\"},{\"q\":\"Which roof type is suitable for heavy rainfall areas?\",\"options\":[\"Flat roof\",\"Steep sloping roof\",\"Thatched roof\",\"No roof\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Steep roofs shed rainwater quickly.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a key feature of eco‑friendly houses?\",\"options\":[\"Use of artificial colours\",\"Use of non‑exhaustible energy\",\"Use of plastic walls\",\"No windows\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"They use renewable energy like solar or wind.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region uses houses built on stilts?\",\"options\":[\"Desert\",\"Mountainous\",\"Marshy\",\"Plains\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Stilt houses avoid flooding in wetland areas.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a major source of pollution at construction sites?\",\"options\":[\"Noise\",\"Dust\",\"Water\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Construction creates noise, dust, and water pollution.\"},{\"q\":\"Which material is NOT typically used for building walls in mountainous houses?\",\"options\":[\"Stone\",\"Wood\",\"Bamboo\",\"Plastic\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Plastic is rarely used in traditional mountain houses.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main benefit of using solar panels in a house?\",\"options\":[\"Reduce electricity bills\",\"Make the house look modern\",\"Add noise\",\"Increase dust\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Solar panels provide free energy, reducing bills.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a reason for building houses differently in each region?\",\"options\":[\"Availability of materials\",\"Personal taste\",\"Government policy\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All factors influence house design.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a common practice to protect homeless people?\",\"options\":[\"Provide shelters\",\"Give them jobs\",\"Build roads\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shelters provide basic protection for homeless.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22284,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Food for All','[\"- Food is the energy that keeps us alive and growing.\",\"- Plants make food by photosynthesis using sunlight, water and air.\",\"- Seeds are the starting point of a new plant; improved seeds give higher yield and resist pests.\",\"- Two main seasons for farming in India: Kharif (June‑October) and Rabi (October‑March).\",\"- Modern irrigation methods: drip irrigation (drops water near roots) and sprinkle irrigation (sprays water over the field).\",\"- Fertilizers give extra nutrients; natural fertilizers are manures, chemical fertilizers are artificial.\",\"- Overuse of chemical fertilizers can lower soil fertility and harm the environment.\",\"- Storing grain in dry, airy places prevents insects and fungus.\",\"- Food security means every person gets enough food; laws and programmes help farmers and consumers.\",\"- Green Revolution increased food production through better seeds and technology.\",\"- Organic farming uses natural materials and keeps soil healthy.\",\"- Farmers use modern tools like tractors, improved seeds, and irrigation to increase yield.\",\"- Storage of food by animals (ants, bees, squirrels) shows nature’s way of keeping food safe.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which season in India is called the kharif season?\",\"options\":[\"June to October\",\"October to March\",\"March to June\",\"All year round\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Kharif season is from June to October, mainly rainfed.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main source of water for rabi crops?\",\"options\":[\"Rainwater\",\"Dew and monsoon runoff\",\"Well water\",\"River water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rabi crops rely on rainwater, monsoon runoff, and dew.\"},{\"q\":\"Which irrigation method drips water only near the roots?\",\"options\":[\"Sprinkle irrigation\",\"Drip irrigation\",\"Flood irrigation\",\"Surface irrigation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Drip irrigation delivers water close to plant roots.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a natural fertilizer called?\",\"options\":[\"Chemical fertilizer\",\"Organic manure\",\"Synthetic nutrient\",\"Pesticide\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Natural fertilizer is called organic manure.\"},{\"q\":\"Which crop is grown in the summer season?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Wheat\",\"Jowar\",\"Maize\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Jowar (sorghum) is a summer crop.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a modern method of irrigation?\",\"options\":[\"Sprinkle irrigation\",\"Flooding\",\"Manual watering\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sprinkle irrigation is a modern method.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if chemical fertilizers are overused?\",\"options\":[\"Soil becomes more fertile\",\"Water becomes cleaner\",\"Soil fertility decreases\",\"Plants grow faster\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Overuse reduces soil fertility.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a pest that spoils stored grain?\",\"options\":[\"Mice\",\"Fish\",\"Birds\",\"Butterflies\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mice can damage stored grain.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main advantage of storing grain in a dry place?\",\"options\":[\"Keeps it sweet\",\"Prevents fungus and insects\",\"Makes it heavier\",\"Increases taste\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dry storage prevents fungus and insects.\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist is credited with the Green Revolution in India?\",\"options\":[\"Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam\",\"Dr. M.S. Swaminathan\",\"Dr. R. K. Shukla\",\"Dr. S. Radhakrishnan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dr. M.S. Swaminathan led the Green Revolution.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22285,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Methods of Preserving Food','[\"Drying removes water to stop micro‑organism growth.\",\"Cooling in a refrigerator keeps food at low temperature so bacteria cannot multiply.\",\"Boiling destroys bacteria and fungi by high heat.\",\"Canning seals food in airtight containers to prevent air and microbes from entering.\",\"Adding preservatives like salt, sugar, vinegar, or spices stops spoilage by creating hostile conditions.\",\"Spices can be dried and stored for long periods because they contain natural preservatives.\",\"Food should be kept away from moisture, heat, and insects to avoid spoilage.\",\"Micro‑organisms need food, water, warmth, and air to grow.\",\"Proper cleaning of utensils and hands prevents contamination.\",\"Storing food in the right season (e.g., drying in summer) helps preserve it for the whole year.\",\"Chilled milk stays fresh longer because cold slows bacterial activity.\",\"Fruits and vegetables spoil faster in warm, humid conditions; they last longer when kept cool.\",\"Preservatives work by lowering water activity or changing pH to inhibit microbes.\",\"A closed jar with a cloth cover allows fermentation while preventing contamination.\",\"Food that shows mold, foul smell, or color change should be discarded.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which method removes water from food to stop microbes from growing?\",\"options\":[\"Drying\",\"Cooling\",\"Boiling\",\"Canning\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Drying removes water, which microbes need to grow.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of adding salt to food?\",\"options\":[\"Add flavour\",\"Make it sweet\",\"Preserve by killing microbes\",\"Make it spicy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Salt creates a dry environment that kills or stops microbes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a method of food preservation mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Freezing\",\"Drying\",\"Cooling\",\"Boiling\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Freezing was not listed in the chapter.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we avoid eating food that has started to grow fungus?\",\"options\":[\"It tastes better\",\"It is safe\",\"It can cause stomach upset\",\"It is cheaper\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Fungus can cause illness.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a refrigerator do to food?\",\"options\":[\"Adds moisture\",\"Increases temperature\",\"Slows down micro‑organism growth\",\"Adds preservatives\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Refrigeration keeps food cold, slowing microbes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which substance is NOT mentioned as a preservative?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar\",\"Vinegar\",\"Mustard oil\",\"Salt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mustard oil was not listed; the chapter mentioned salt, sugar, vinegar.\"},{\"q\":\"When should fruits be stored to keep them fresh for a long time?\",\"options\":[\"In the summer\",\"In the winter\",\"In a refrigerator\",\"In a sunny window\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Refrigeration keeps fruits fresh longer.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason for keeping food in an airtight container?\",\"options\":[\"To add flavour\",\"To keep it dry\",\"To stop air and microbes from entering\",\"To make it look nice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Airtight containers prevent microbes from entering.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a type of drying method?\",\"options\":[\"Boiling\",\"Canning\",\"Papad drying\",\"Cooling\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Papads are dried to preserve them.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to food if it is left at room temperature for several days?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes sweeter\",\"It stays fresh\",\"It may develop fungus and spoil\",\"It becomes harder\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Room temperature encourages microbial growth.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22286,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Transport','[\"Transport is the movement of people or goods from one place to another.\",\"There are three main types of transport: land, water and air.\",\"Land transport includes walking, bicycles, motor vehicles and trains.\",\"Water transport uses boats, ships and barges to carry goods and passengers.\",\"Air transport uses aeroplanes and helicopters to travel over long distances.\",\"Walking is the simplest and most eco‑friendly way to travel short distances.\",\"Bicycles are good for short to medium distances and give physical exercise.\",\"Motor vehicles (cars, buses, autorickshaws) use fuel and can travel faster but produce pollution.\",\"Trains are efficient for long distances and can carry many passengers or goods at once.\",\"Ships are used for long sea journeys and can carry huge amounts of cargo.\",\"Airplanes are the fastest way to travel long distances but consume a lot of fuel.\",\"Transport saves time and effort, making work and trade easier.\",\"Different modes of transport have advantages and disadvantages.\",\"Using public transport reduces traffic and pollution compared to private cars.\",\"Bicycles produce no pollution and are good for health and environment.\",\"Walking and cycling help keep the air cleaner and reduce noise pollution.\",\"Transport connects distant places, making the world feel closer.\",\"Fuel burning in vehicles releases carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide and sulphur dioxide.\",\"Air pollution harms plants, animals and human health, causing respiratory problems.\",\"To reduce pollution, use public transport, walk or cycle whenever possible, and maintain vehicles properly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which mode of transport takes the longest time for a short distance?\",\"options\":[\"Walking\",\"Bicycle\",\"Car\",\"Bus\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Walking is the slowest mode of transport.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a main cause of air pollution from vehicles?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon monoxide\",\"Sunlight\",\"Rain\",\"Wind\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Vehicles emit carbon monoxide, a harmful gas.\"},{\"q\":\"Which transport mode is most fuel efficient for short trips?\",\"options\":[\"Bicycle\",\"Car\",\"Bus\",\"Train\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bicycles do not use fuel.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a benefit of transport?\",\"options\":[\"Time saving\",\"Trade facilitation\",\"Pollution\",\"Connectivity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Pollution is a drawback, not a benefit.\"},{\"q\":\"Which vehicle is best for carrying small loads and reduces crowding?\",\"options\":[\"Car\",\"Bus\",\"Bicycle\",\"Motorbike\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Bicycles can carry small loads and reduce crowding.\"},{\"q\":\"What does CNG stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Compressed Natural Gas\",\"Carbon Neutral Gas\",\"Clean Natural Gas\",\"Compressed Nitrogen Gas\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"CNG means Compressed Natural Gas.\"},{\"q\":\"Which transport option is recommended for a grandmother who gets tired easily?\",\"options\":[\"Walking\",\"Autorickshaw\",\"Bus\",\"Scooter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A bus provides comfortable seating for the elderly.\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas is NOT mentioned as a pollutant from vehicles?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen dioxide\",\"Sulphur dioxide\",\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon monoxide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Oxygen is not listed as a pollutant.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity helps reduce noise pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Using public transport\",\"Driving faster\",\"Increasing traffic\",\"Using private cars\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Public transport reduces the number of vehicles, lowering noise.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a remedy to reduce pollution from transport?\",\"options\":[\"Using fuels that cause less pollution\",\"Increasing fuel consumption\",\"Driving alone\",\"Using only petrol vehicles\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Using cleaner fuels helps reduce pollution.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22287,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Communication and Mass Media','[\"- Communication is the exchange of information between people.\",\"- Mass media are tools like TV, radio, newspapers, internet that reach many people at once.\",\"- Artificial satellites help send TV programmes and mobile phone signals across the world.\",\"- Telecommunication means talking to someone far away using phones or the internet.\",\"- Advantages of mass media: easy contact, saves time, raises awareness, warns about disasters, supports government schemes, helps trade and industry.\",\"- Disadvantages: eye, ear and back problems from too much TV, psychological issues, isolation, neglect of outdoor activities.\",\"- TV and mobile phones can be used responsibly to avoid health problems.\",\"- Before phones, messages were sent by letters, telegrams or face‑to‑face talk.\",\"- Audio‑visual communication lets us see the person we talk to on a phone or video call.\",\"- Mass media can educate people about science, health, environment and social issues.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What helps TV programmes reach people in different parts of the world?\",\"options\":[\"Artificial satellites\",\"Cars\",\"Buses\",\"None\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Satellites broadcast TV signals to many places.\"},{\"q\":\"Which device lets you talk to someone far away?\",\"options\":[\"Television\",\"Mobile phone\",\"Refrigerator\",\"Microwave\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mobile phones use radio waves to connect distant people.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a disadvantage of watching too much television?\",\"options\":[\"Better eyesight\",\"More energy\",\"Eye problems\",\"More friends\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Excessive TV can cause eye strain and other health issues.\"},{\"q\":\"Mass media can warn people about which of these?\",\"options\":[\"New movies\",\"Natural disasters\",\"Fashion trends\",\"Sports scores\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"TV and radio can give early warnings of storms, floods, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"Before phones, how were long‑distance messages sent?\",\"options\":[\"By email\",\"By satellite\",\"By letters or telegrams\",\"By video calls\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Letters and telegrams were used before telephones.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a benefit of mass media?\",\"options\":[\"Raises awareness about health\",\"Helps in trade\",\"Causes eye strain\",\"Warns about disasters\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Causing eye strain is a disadvantage, not a benefit.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'audio‑visual communication\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Talking without seeing\",\"Seeing the person you talk to\",\"Listening to music only\",\"Reading a book\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It lets you see the person while you talk, like video calls.\"},{\"q\":\"Which device uses satellites to send signals?\",\"options\":[\"Car radio\",\"Mobile phone\",\"Television set\",\"Microwave oven\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mobile phones use satellite signals for calls and data.\"},{\"q\":\"Mass media helps in the expansion of which sector?\",\"options\":[\"Healthcare\",\"Education\",\"Trade and industry\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All listed sectors benefit from mass media.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a key advantage of using mass media for government schemes?\",\"options\":[\"Makes schemes secret\",\"Makes schemes expensive\",\"Makes schemes known to people\",\"Makes schemes harder to access\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mass media spreads information about schemes to the public.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22288,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Water','[\"Water is a liquid that covers most of Earth’s surface and is essential for life.\",\"Water can be clean or polluted. Clean water is safe to drink; polluted water contains harmful substances.\",\"Waste water is water that has been used in homes, factories, or farms and contains dirt, chemicals, or germs.\",\"Sewage is a type of waste water that comes from toilets, kitchens, and bathrooms.\",\"Water is purified before it is supplied to homes. The main steps are: 1) Settling (letting dirt fall to the bottom), 2) Filtration (passing water through sand or charcoal), 3) Chlorination (adding chlorine to kill germs).\",\"Oxygen is added to water in water‑works so fish and other aquatic life can breathe. This is called oxygenation.\",\"Rainwater can be collected in clean containers. Rain that falls directly on the ground is usually cleaner than rain that runs over soil or roofs.\",\"If a lot of dirty water is dumped into rivers or lakes, the water becomes polluted and can hurt plants, animals and people.\",\"Water that has no oxygen is bad for fish and other living things in rivers and lakes.\",\"Water‑works use large tanks, filters and chemicals to make water safe for drinking.\",\"When it rains, the water that falls on roofs can be stored in tanks or let seep into the ground. This helps keep water available during dry seasons.\",\"Water management means collecting, storing or letting water seep into the ground so that it can be used even when it does not rain.\",\"Drought happens when there is very little rain for a long time. It can lead to famine if people and animals do not have enough water.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of treating sewage water before it is released into a water body?\",\"options\":[\"To make it taste better\",\"To remove harmful substances\",\"To increase its temperature\",\"To add colour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Treating sewage removes harmful germs and pollutants so the water can be safely used.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a natural purification process for river water?\",\"options\":[\"Settling\",\"Oxygenation\",\"Chlorination\",\"Diffusion\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Chlorination is a human-made process; the others happen naturally.\"},{\"q\":\"In the simple bottle filter experiment, which material is added first?\",\"options\":[\"Coarse sand\",\"Fine sand\",\"Powdered coal\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The water is poured first, then powdered coal, fine sand, and finally coarse sand.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the dissolved oxygen level when a lot of waste is added to a river?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It stays the same\",\"It decreases\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"More waste reduces the amount of dissolved oxygen, harming aquatic life.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a method of storing rainwater for use after the rainy season?\",\"options\":[\"Building dams\",\"Digging wells\",\"Planting trees\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All listed methods help store rainwater.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main danger of drinking untreated polluted water?\",\"options\":[\"It may taste bad\",\"It may cause diseases\",\"It may be too cold\",\"It may be too sweet\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Polluted water can carry germs that spread diseases.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we need to crush and throw away empty water bottles?\",\"options\":[\"To recycle them\",\"To keep them clean\",\"To prevent reuse\",\"To avoid litter\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Crushing bottles prevents them from being reused and reduces litter.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of alum in water purification?\",\"options\":[\"Adds colour\",\"Helps settle particles\",\"Increases pH\",\"Kills germs\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Alum helps particles settle during the settling process.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a step in the water purification process at water-works?\",\"options\":[\"Settling\",\"Chlorination\",\"Oxygenation\",\"Evaporation\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Evaporation is not part of the purification steps listed.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main effect of not having rain for a long time?\",\"options\":[\"More fish\",\"Water level rises\",\"Drought and famine\",\"More trees\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Long dry periods lead to drought and famine.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22289,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Clothes – our Necessity','[\"Clothes are items we wear to protect our body and give us comfort.\",\"We wear different clothes for different occasions like school, festivals, parties and sports.\",\"Children usually wear simple cotton shirts and trousers, while adults wear shirts, skirts, trousers, sarees or kurtas.\",\"In hot weather we wear light, loose clothes made of cotton or linen.\",\"In cold weather we wear warm clothes like woolen sweaters, jackets and shawls.\",\"Clothes can be made from natural fibres like cotton, wool, silk or from synthetic fibres like polyester and nylon.\",\"The process of making cloth involves: raw material (cotton, wool), spinning, weaving or knitting, dyeing and finishing.\",\"Textile industry is a big part of Maharashtra’s economy; famous products include Paithani saree, Himroo shawl, Solapur bedsheets and Ichalkaranji handloom cloth.\",\"People’s need for clothes is different from their desire for clothes; we need clothes for protection but may want more for fashion or status.\",\"Clothes change with time, culture and climate; traditional clothes vary across states like Rajasthan, Maharashtra, Kerala and Jammu & Kashmir.\",\"When we choose clothes, we should think about the purpose, comfort, and the season.\",\"It is good to borrow or lend clothes to friends when we have extra, but we should keep enough for ourselves.\",\"Advertisements often show many clothes that we do not need; we should be careful not to buy just because they look good.\",\"The raw material for cotton cloth comes from cotton plants; for wool cloth it comes from sheep’s fleece.\",\"Workers in textile industry include spinners, weavers, dyers, and machine operators; they come from many parts of the country.\",\"Textile factories have changed from handloom to powerloom and now to computer‑controlled machines.\",\"Clothes made from natural fibres are usually more breathable and comfortable in hot weather.\",\"Synthetic fibres are strong, wrinkle‑free and easy to wash, but may not be breathable in hot weather.\",\"The choice of clothes should match the climate, activity and the person’s body size.\",\"Traditional clothes of a region reflect its climate, culture and available natural resources.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a reason people want clothes that they do not need?\",\"options\":[\"Desire\",\"Advertising influence\",\"Need for protection\",\"Greed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"People want clothes for protection, not for desire or greed.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of clothes in different climates?\",\"options\":[\"Fashion\",\"Decoration\",\"Protection from weather\",\"Advertising\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Clothes protect us from weather conditions.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity asks students to write the total number of clothes they have?\",\"options\":[\"Can you tell?\",\"Observe the pictures\",\"Write the total number of clothes\",\"Choose the clothes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity instructs to write the total number of clothes.\"},{\"q\":\"In the chapter, what is a common source of raw material for fabric?\",\"options\":[\"Cotton\",\"Plastic\",\"Steel\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Cotton is a common raw material for fabric.\"},{\"q\":\"Which season is mentioned as the main time for using certain textiles?\",\"options\":[\"Winter\",\"Summer\",\"Monsoon\",\"All seasons\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Textiles are used in all seasons.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the chapter suggest about advertising and clothes?\",\"options\":[\"It helps people buy necessary clothes\",\"It creates desire for unnecessary clothes\",\"It reduces the need for clothes\",\"It is irrelevant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Advertising can create desire for unnecessary clothes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of traditional saree mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Patola\",\"Kanjivaram\",\"Chikan\",\"Tartan\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Tartan is not listed among the traditional sarees.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason Mumbai became a textile hub?\",\"options\":[\"Cool climate\",\"Proximity to sea\",\"High humidity\",\"Low cost labor\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mumbai’s humid weather facilitated cloth production.\"},{\"q\":\"Which question asks students to consider lending clothes to friends?\",\"options\":[\"Do you think you should have other clothes?\",\"Will you lend your clothes to your friends?\",\"How many times do you change clothes?\",\"What do you wear to functions?\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The question explicitly asks about lending clothes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity involves visiting a local Khadi Gramodyog centre?\",\"options\":[\"Visit a fabric\\/garments exhibition\",\"Make a note of clothes to wear on a traditional day\",\"Visit your local Khadi Gramodyog centre\",\"Observe the pictures\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The activity mentions visiting a Khadi Gramodyog centre.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22290,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','The Environment and Us','[\"Forests are large groups of trees that give homes to many animals and plants.\",\"Trees absorb carbon dioxide and give out oxygen – they help keep the air clean.\",\"When forests are cut down (deforestation) the soil becomes loose and can wash away, causing floods.\",\"Water in rivers and lakes can become dirty if factories dump waste water into them – this is water pollution.\",\"Air pollution happens when factories, cars and power plants release smoke and gases into the air.\",\"Animals that live in forests need trees for food, shelter and protection; if trees disappear they may die or move away.\",\"Plants and animals are called biodiversity; it means many different kinds of living things in one place.\",\"If biodiversity is lost, the whole food chain can break and some animals may become extinct.\",\"Recycling and using less energy can help keep the environment healthy.\",\"India has special places called national parks, wildlife sanctuays and biodiversity parks to protect animals and plants.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What happens to the soil when forests are cut down?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes richer\",\"It becomes loose and can wash away\",\"It turns into sand\",\"It stays the same\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Without tree roots, soil loses structure and can be washed away by rain.\"},{\"q\":\"Which gas is released when we burn fuels like petrol and diesel?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Helium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Combustion of fuels produces carbon dioxide.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason for water pollution in rivers near factories?\",\"options\":[\"Natural springs\",\"Rainfall\",\"Factories dumping waste water\",\"Fish waste\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Factories often release untreated waste water into rivers.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a cause of air pollution?\",\"options\":[\"Car exhaust\",\"Industrial smoke\",\"Tree planting\",\"Burning of fuels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Planting trees helps clean the air, not pollute it.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a biodiversity park?\",\"options\":[\"A park with only one type of tree\",\"A park that protects many kinds of plants and animals\",\"A park for sports\",\"A park for cars\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It is a protected area where many species live.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these animals is likely to move away if forests disappear?\",\"options\":[\"Fish\",\"Birds\",\"Monkeys\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All listed animals depend on forests for shelter.\"},{\"q\":\"What does recycling help to reduce?\",\"options\":[\"Noise\",\"Water pollution\",\"Air pollution\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Recycling reduces waste that can cause pollution.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a natural component of the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Plastic bottle\",\"River water\",\"Car exhaust\",\"Factory smoke\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"River water is a natural, not man‑made, component.\"},{\"q\":\"What is migration?\",\"options\":[\"Moving to a new house\",\"Moving from one place to another for food or shelter\",\"Eating food\",\"Sleeping\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Migration is when animals move to find better conditions.\"},{\"q\":\"Which action helps protect the environment?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting more trees\",\"Burning more fuel\",\"Planting trees\",\"Using more plastic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Planting trees helps restore forest cover.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22291,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Constituents of Food','[\"- Food is a substance that gives us energy and helps us grow.\",\"- The main parts of food are carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins and minerals.\",\"- Carbohydrates are found in rice, wheat, potatoes and give energy.\",\"- Proteins are found in pulses, milk, eggs and help build and repair body tissues.\",\"- Fats are found in oil, butter, nuts and give extra energy and protect organs.\",\"- Vitamins are small amounts of substances (A, B, C, D, E, K) that keep us healthy.\",\"- Minerals are tiny elements (iron, calcium, sodium, potassium) needed for many body functions.\",\"- Fibre is a type of carbohydrate that is not digested and helps food move through the gut.\",\"- A balanced diet contains all these parts in the right amounts.\",\"- If we miss any part for a long time we may become malnourished or develop diseases.\",\"- Eating fruits with skins, whole grains and pulses gives us fibre and micronutrients.\",\"- Milk is given to children daily because it supplies protein, calcium and vitamins.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which food gives us the most energy?\",\"options\":[\"Fruits\",\"Carbohydrates\",\"Fats\",\"Vitamins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Fats give the most energy per gram.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main function of fibre in food?\",\"options\":[\"Give energy\",\"Build muscles\",\"Help food move through the gut\",\"Make food sweet\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Fibre helps food move through the digestive system.\"},{\"q\":\"Which vitamin is important for good vision?\",\"options\":[\"Vitamin A\",\"Vitamin B\",\"Vitamin C\",\"Vitamin D\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Vitamin A prevents night blindness.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a balanced diet contain?\",\"options\":[\"Only carbohydrates\",\"Only proteins\",\"Only fats\",\"All nutrients in right amounts\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Balanced diet has all nutrients.\"},{\"q\":\"Which food is a good source of protein?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Milk\",\"Potato\",\"Bread\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Milk contains protein.\"},{\"q\":\"What are micronutrients?\",\"options\":[\"Large amounts of food\",\"Small amounts of vitamins and minerals\",\"Only fats\",\"Only carbohydrates\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Micronutrients are vitamins and minerals needed in small amounts.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a carbohydrate?\",\"options\":[\"Starch\",\"Sugar\",\"Fibre\",\"Protein\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Protein is not a carbohydrate.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we need fats in our diet?\",\"options\":[\"They give energy\",\"They make food sweet\",\"They are a type of vitamin\",\"They are a type of mineral\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Fats give energy and protect organs.\"},{\"q\":\"What is malnutrition?\",\"options\":[\"Eating too many foods\",\"Eating only one type of food for a long time\",\"Eating too little food\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Malnutrition is not getting all nutrients for a long time.\"},{\"q\":\"Which part of the body stores excess energy?\",\"options\":[\"Stomach\",\"Liver\",\"Brain\",\"Heart\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The liver stores excess energy as fat.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22292,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Our Emotional World','[\"- Emotions are feelings that arise when something happens to us.\",\"- Coping with emotions means understanding them and not letting them control us.\",\"- Anger is a natural emotion but should be expressed calmly.\",\"- Sadness, joy, fear, jealousy, and disappointment are all normal emotions.\",\"- A balanced personality has good and bad traits; we should know both.\",\"- Knowing our flaws helps us improve and become kinder.\",\"- When we feel angry, we can pause, breathe, and think before reacting.\",\"- We should not blame others for our feelings; we take responsibility.\",\"- Expressing emotions in a reasonable way helps others understand us.\",\"- A good attitude includes cooperation, listening, and respect.\",\"- Being self‑aware helps us notice when we are too harsh or too kind.\",\"- We can change bad habits by practicing better behaviour.\",\"- A positive mindset encourages learning and growth.\",\"- Respecting others’ feelings builds strong friendships and teamwork.\",\"- Avoiding greed and jealousy leads to happier relationships.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which emotion is described as a natural response when something does not go as we wish?\",\"options\":[\"Anger\",\"Joy\",\"Fear\",\"Sadness\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Anger is the emotion that arises when things are not as desired.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in coping with anger?\",\"options\":[\"Yelling at the person\",\"Taking a deep breath\",\"Running away\",\"Saying sorry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Taking a deep breath helps calm the mind before reacting.\"},{\"q\":\"A balanced personality includes which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Only good traits\",\"Only bad traits\",\"Both good and bad traits\",\"Neither good nor bad traits\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A balanced personality has both strengths and weaknesses.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if you feel jealous of a friend’s success?\",\"options\":[\"Ignore it\",\"Tell them you are jealous\",\"Talk to them about it\",\"Blame them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Talking helps you understand and manage jealousy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which action shows cooperation?\",\"options\":[\"Refusing to help\",\"Listening to a friend\",\"Ignoring a problem\",\"Being stubborn\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Listening and helping are signs of cooperation.\"},{\"q\":\"When should you express anger?\",\"options\":[\"When you are calm\",\"When you are upset\",\"When you are happy\",\"When you are tired\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Anger should be expressed when you are upset, but calmly.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a harmful effect of uncontrolled anger?\",\"options\":[\"Better focus\",\"Increased confidence\",\"Headaches\",\"More friends\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Uncontrolled anger can cause headaches and other health issues.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a positive trait?\",\"options\":[\"Being mean\",\"Being generous\",\"Being angry all the time\",\"Being jealous\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Generosity is a positive trait.\"},{\"q\":\"What does coping with emotions help you avoid?\",\"options\":[\"Learning new skills\",\"Feeling sad\",\"Being misunderstood\",\"Getting into trouble\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Coping helps avoid misunderstandings and trouble.\"},{\"q\":\"If you know your flaw is being too talkative, what can you do?\",\"options\":[\"Talk more\",\"Listen more\",\"Ignore it\",\"Blame others\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Listening more helps balance a talkative habit.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22293,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Busy at Work – our Internal Organs','[\"Respiration is the process of breathing in oxygen and breathing out carbon dioxide.\",\"The lungs are the main organs of respiration and are protected by the ribs.\",\"The trachea (windpipe) carries air from the nose to the lungs.\",\"The diaphragm is a muscle that moves up and down to help us breathe.\",\"Air enters the lungs through the alveoli where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged with blood.\",\"Smoking introduces harmful particles that damage the lungs and can cause lung cancer.\",\"The alimentary canal is a long tube from the mouth to the anus that digests food.\",\"The stomach churns food into a moist mass called a bolus.\",\"The small intestine absorbs most of the nutrients and water from food.\",\"The large intestine absorbs remaining water and forms faeces.\",\"Saliva contains enzymes that start digestion in the mouth and helps swallow food.\",\"The heart pumps blood through the circulatory system, keeping oxygen and nutrients flowing.\",\"The nervous system coordinates all body functions by sending signals through nerves.\",\"The brain is the control centre of the nervous system.\",\"The circulatory system includes the heart and blood vessels.\",\"The respiratory, digestive, circulatory and nervous systems work together for life.\",\"If one organ fails, the whole system can be affected.\",\"Healthy habits like not smoking and eating slowly keep these systems working well.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which organ is the main site where oxygen enters the blood?\",\"options\":[\"Heart\",\"Lungs\",\"Stomach\",\"Brain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oxygen enters the blood in the alveoli of the lungs.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of the diaphragm?\",\"options\":[\"It pumps blood\",\"It moves the nose\",\"It helps us breathe\",\"It digests food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The diaphragm moves up and down to allow air in and out of the lungs.\"},{\"q\":\"What does saliva do in the mouth?\",\"options\":[\"It makes the mouth hot\",\"It starts digestion\",\"It cleans the teeth only\",\"It keeps the mouth dry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Saliva contains enzymes that begin the digestion of food.\"},{\"q\":\"Which system carries messages between the brain and body parts?\",\"options\":[\"Digestive\",\"Circulatory\",\"Nervous\",\"Respiratory\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The nervous system uses nerves to send signals.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the air after it passes through the alveoli?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes water\",\"It is stored in the lungs\",\"It is exchanged for blood gases\",\"It turns into food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is smoking harmful to the lungs?\",\"options\":[\"It makes them bigger\",\"It removes oxygen\",\"It adds harmful particles\",\"It cleans them\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Smoke contains toxins that damage lung tissue.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main function of the small intestine?\",\"options\":[\"Make food taste sweet\",\"Store food\",\"Absorb nutrients\",\"Produce saliva\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It absorbs most nutrients and water.\"},{\"q\":\"Which organ helps keep the blood moving?\",\"options\":[\"Liver\",\"Heart\",\"Stomach\",\"Brain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The heart pumps blood through vessels.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the long tube that digests food?\",\"options\":[\"Alimentary canal\",\"Respiratory tube\",\"Nervous cord\",\"Circulatory tube\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The alimentary canal runs from mouth to anus.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of the nervous system?\",\"options\":[\"Digest food\",\"Pump blood\",\"Coordinate body functions\",\"Breathe\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It coordinates all bodily functions via nerves.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22294,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Growth and Personality Development','[\"Growth is the increase in height and weight from birth to adulthood.\",\"Physical growth stops around 18 years old for both boys and girls.\",\"Development means learning new skills and becoming more efficient.\",\"Infants start by crying, then learn to turn their head and smile.\",\"Around 1 year they can feed themselves and hold objects.\",\"By 2 years they can walk, run, and pick up small items.\",\"Children develop coordination between different movements as they grow.\",\"Learning a skill is called acquiring a new ability.\",\"Practice makes a skill easier and reduces mistakes.\",\"Malnutrition can stop a child from growing tall.\",\"Good food, exercise, and positive thoughts help growth.\",\"Heredity is the passing of traits from parents to children.\",\"Children become independent when they can do tasks alone.\",\"The more skills we learn, the less we depend on others.\",\"Each person has a unique personality shaped by genetics and learning.\"]','[{\"q\":\"When does physical growth usually stop?\",\"options\":[\"At 12 years\",\"At 18 years\",\"At 21 years\",\"At 25 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Growth stops around 18 years old.\"},{\"q\":\"Which skill does a 1‑year‑old start to learn?\",\"options\":[\"Running\",\"Sitting up\",\"Holding a spoon\",\"Reading\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"At about 1 year, children can hold a spoon and feed themselves.\"},{\"q\":\"What does heredity mean?\",\"options\":[\"Learning from parents\",\"Inherited traits\",\"Choosing a job\",\"Eating healthy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Heredity is the passing of traits from parents to children.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity helps physical growth?\",\"options\":[\"Skipping rope\",\"Watching TV\",\"Sleeping\",\"Reading\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Skipping rope is a physical activity that supports growth.\"},{\"q\":\"Malnutrition affects growth by:\",\"options\":[\"Increasing height\",\"Improving coordination\",\"Reducing height\",\"Enhancing memory\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Malnutrition reduces a child\'s height.\"},{\"q\":\"At what age do children usually start walking?\",\"options\":[\"6 months\",\"1 year\",\"2 years\",\"3 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Most children start walking around 1 year.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a skill learned early?\",\"options\":[\"Singing\",\"Running\",\"Picking up objects\",\"Talking\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Singing is usually learned later.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main benefit of learning many skills?\",\"options\":[\"More friends\",\"Less dependence\",\"Higher salary\",\"More toys\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Learning many skills reduces dependence on others.\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor does NOT help growth?\",\"options\":[\"Good diet\",\"Exercise\",\"Negative thoughts\",\"Adequate sleep\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Negative thoughts do not help growth.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the story of the industrialist illustrate?\",\"options\":[\"Hard work pays off\",\"Inheritance of traits\",\"Choosing a career\",\"Eating healthy\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It shows that hard work and good decisions lead to success.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22295,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Infectious Diseases and how to Prevent them','[\"Infectious diseases are illnesses caused by tiny living things called micro‑organisms or germs.\",\"Each disease has a specific germ that causes it – e.g., flu germs, typhoid germs, malaria parasites.\",\"Germs can spread in four main ways: through the air, through water, through food, and by insect bites.\",\"Air spread happens when a sick person coughs or sneezes and tiny droplets fall into the air. Others breathe them in.\",\"Water spread occurs when germs from a sick person’s faeces get into rivers, wells or drinking water. Drinking or bathing in that water can make you sick.\",\"Food spread happens when contaminated food is eaten – germs can stick to hands, utensils or flies that land on food.\",\"Insects such as mosquitoes and flies can carry germs from one person to another or to food. Mosquito bites can spread malaria.\",\"Direct contact spread happens when you touch the skin or clothes of a sick person with skin diseases like ringworm or scabies.\",\"Preventing spread means keeping your mouth covered when you cough, washing hands, covering food, avoiding stagnant water, and keeping insects away.\",\"Covering food stops flies and dust from touching it, reducing food poisoning.\",\"Cleaning water with bleach or boiling it kills germs and makes it safe to drink.\",\"Mosquito breeding sites are puddles or standing water; removing them stops malaria.\",\"People with serious infections are isolated in special hospital wards to protect others.\",\"Vaccination trains the body’s immune system to fight a specific germ, so you don’t get the disease when exposed.\",\"Vaccines for diphtheria, whooping cough, tetanus and polio are given to children at regular ages.\",\"Public health programmes, like clean water supply and vaccination drives, help stop epidemics.\",\"An epidemic is when many people in the same area get the same disease at the same time.\",\"Micro‑organisms are tiny living things that can be bacteria, viruses or parasites.\",\"Germs are also called microbes or pathogens.\",\"Good hygiene, clean water, and vaccination are the best ways to keep a community healthy.\",\"If you feel sick, stay home and tell a teacher or parent so you don’t spread germs to classmates.\",\"Cover your mouth with a tissue or your elbow when you cough or sneeze.\",\"Wash hands with soap for at least 20 seconds after using the toilet or before eating.\",\"Avoid sharing clothes, towels or utensils with someone who is ill.\",\"If you see a mosquito breeding site, throw away the water or cover it so mosquitoes cannot lay eggs.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a way germs spread?\",\"options\":[\"Through the air\",\"Through water\",\"By eating a rainbow\",\"By insect bites\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Eating a rainbow is not a mode of germ spread.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if you cough?\",\"options\":[\"Cover your mouth with a tissue\",\"Throw your tissue on the floor\",\"Cover your mouth with your hand\",\"Ignore it\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Covering the mouth stops droplets from spreading.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is boiling water important?\",\"options\":[\"It tastes better\",\"It kills germs\",\"It makes water cold\",\"It removes colour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Boiling kills germs that cause water‑borne diseases.\"},{\"q\":\"Which insect spreads malaria?\",\"options\":[\"Fly\",\"Mosquito\",\"Ant\",\"Bee\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mosquitoes carry malaria parasites.\"},{\"q\":\"What is an epidemic?\",\"options\":[\"A single case of disease\",\"A disease that spreads through food\",\"Many people in the same area get the same disease at the same time\",\"A disease that only affects animals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"An epidemic means many people get the same disease together.\"},{\"q\":\"Which disease is spread by touching the skin of a sick person?\",\"options\":[\"Ringworm\",\"Chickenpox\",\"Typhoid\",\"Malaria\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ringworm spreads by direct skin contact.\"},{\"q\":\"What should you do if you see a puddle of water?\",\"options\":[\"Leave it\",\"Cover it or remove it\",\"Add salt\",\"Use it for washing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Covering or removing stagnant water stops mosquito breeding.\"},{\"q\":\"Which vaccine is given orally?\",\"options\":[\"Diphtheria\",\"Polio\",\"Tetanus\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The polio vaccine is given by mouth.\"},{\"q\":\"Why should you keep food covered?\",\"options\":[\"It keeps it warm\",\"It stops flies and dust from touching it\",\"It makes it taste better\",\"It keeps the food dry\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Covering food prevents insects and dust from contaminating it.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of vaccination?\",\"options\":[\"To make you immune to a specific germ\",\"To give you a cure for all diseases\",\"To make you sick\",\"To stop you from eating food\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Vaccination trains the immune system to fight a particular germ.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22296,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Substances, Objects and Energy','[\"All matter is made of tiny particles called atoms or molecules.\",\"Substances can be in three states: solid, liquid, gas.\",\"In a solid the particles are close together and vibrate in fixed positions.\",\"In a liquid the particles are close but can slide past each other.\",\"In a gas the particles are far apart and move freely.\",\"When we write with chalk, tiny chalk particles stick to the board and come off when we wipe it.\",\"Pounding or grinding turns a solid into a powder, which is many small particles.\",\"Naphthalene balls release tiny particles that settle on clothes, making them smell of naphthalene.\",\"Energy is the ability to do work. It can be stored in fuels like petrol, coal, or produced by sunlight, wind, or water.\",\"When a fuel burns, chemical energy is released as heat, which can be converted into motion (kinetic energy).\",\"The Sun, wind, and water are non‑exhaustible sources of energy.\",\"Kinetic energy is the energy of motion; potential energy is stored energy.\",\"Substances differ in properties such as hardness, colour, smell, and solubility.\",\"Objects are made from substances and have definite shape and size.\",\"The same substance can be used to make different objects (e.g., clay can make pots, bricks, statues).\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which state of matter has particles that are far apart and move freely?\",\"options\":[\"Solid\",\"Liquid\",\"Gas\",\"Plasma\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a gas the particles are far apart and move freely.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to chalk particles when you wipe the board?\",\"options\":[\"They stick to the board\",\"They become invisible\",\"They fall off\",\"They change colour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The chalk particles fall off the board when it is wiped.\"},{\"q\":\"When a fuel like petrol burns, what form of energy is released?\",\"options\":[\"Light energy\",\"Sound energy\",\"Heat energy\",\"Electric energy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Burning fuel releases energy in the form of heat.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a non‑exhaustible source of energy?\",\"options\":[\"Sunlight\",\"Wind\",\"Water\",\"Coal\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Coal is a fossil fuel and is exhaustible.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the energy of motion?\",\"options\":[\"Potential energy\",\"Kinetic energy\",\"Thermal energy\",\"Chemical energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Kinetic energy is the energy of motion.\"},{\"q\":\"When you grind a piece of sugar, what do you get?\",\"options\":[\"A liquid\",\"A gas\",\"A powder\",\"A solid block\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Grinding turns a solid into a powder.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a substance that can be found in solid, liquid, and gas states?\",\"options\":[\"Water\",\"Wood\",\"Coal\",\"Gold\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Water exists as ice, liquid, and vapor.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Sun produce that can be used to make electricity?\",\"options\":[\"Heat only\",\"Light only\",\"Both light and heat\",\"Neither\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Sun provides both light and heat, which can be used to generate electricity.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do clothes smell of naphthalene after being stored with naphthalene balls?\",\"options\":[\"The balls absorb the smell\",\"The balls release tiny particles that settle on clothes\",\"The clothes are made of naphthalene\",\"The balls dissolve in the clothes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Naphthalene balls release tiny particles that settle on clothes, giving them the smell.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main source of energy for a thermal power station?\",\"options\":[\"Solar energy\",\"Wind energy\",\"Coal or other fuels\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Thermal power stations burn fuels like coal to produce heat and generate electricity.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22297,23,5,'EVS Part 1','en','Community Health and Hygiene','[\"Community health means protecting the health of all people in a community.\",\"Good personal hygiene includes washing hands, brushing teeth and keeping clothes clean.\",\"Nutritious food gives energy and strengthens the body.\",\"Exercise and hobbies keep the mind and body active and happy.\",\"Clean surroundings reduce chances of disease spread.\",\"Spitting, littering and using old tyres can spread infections.\",\"Safe drinking water and proper sanitation are essential for health.\",\"Tobacco, alcohol and drugs are harmful habits that can lead to disease.\",\"Addiction to tobacco causes mouth ulcers, cancers and other health problems.\",\"Addiction to alcohol damages the liver, intestines and urinary bladder.\",\"A wholesome life is achieved by good habits, avoiding bad habits and caring for health.\",\"Community health is as important as personal health because a healthy community supports everyone.\",\"Public toilets and clean roads help keep the community disease‑free.\",\"Spitting in public places is a legal offence to prevent disease spread.\",\"Good habits include informing family of your whereabouts, telling the truth and saying “No!” firmly.\",\"Bad habits include chewing tobacco, smoking, biting nails and fiddling with objects.\",\"Avoiding bad habits helps maintain personal and community health.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a threat to community health?\",\"options\":[\"Pollution\",\"Spitting in public places\",\"Eating nutritious food\",\"Epidemics\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Eating nutritious food improves health, not threatens it.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a legal offence mentioned to prevent disease spread?\",\"options\":[\"Using old tyres\",\"Spitting in public places\",\"Littering on roads\",\"Eating street food\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Spitting in public places is banned to stop disease spread.\"},{\"q\":\"Which habit can cause mouth ulcers and cancer?\",\"options\":[\"Regular exercise\",\"Chewing tobacco\",\"Eating fruits\",\"Brushing teeth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Chewing tobacco causes ulcers and cancer.\"},{\"q\":\"What does safe drinking water help with?\",\"options\":[\"Increasing appetite\",\"Preventing infections\",\"Improving eyesight\",\"Reducing sleep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Safe water prevents infections.\"},{\"q\":\"Which is a harmful addiction mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Reading books\",\"Playing sports\",\"Alcoholism\",\"Gardening\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Alcoholism is an addiction that harms health.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a benefit of exercising?\",\"options\":[\"Causes fatigue\",\"Improves mental health\",\"Increases disease risk\",\"Reduces appetite\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Exercise improves mental health.\"},{\"q\":\"Which is a good habit for community health?\",\"options\":[\"Fiddling with a pencil\",\"Telling the truth\",\"Spitting on the road\",\"Using old tyres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Telling the truth is a good habit.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main cause of liver damage in alcoholics?\",\"options\":[\"High sugar diet\",\"Lack of sleep\",\"Alcohol consumption\",\"Exercise\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Alcohol consumption damages the liver.\"},{\"q\":\"Which action helps keep surroundings clean?\",\"options\":[\"Throwing garbage on the road\",\"Using public toilets\",\"Spitting in public places\",\"Using old tyres\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Using public toilets keeps surroundings clean.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the effect of addiction to tobacco on the digestive organs?\",\"options\":[\"Improves digestion\",\"Causes cancer\",\"Reduces appetite\",\"Increases energy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tobacco addiction can cause cancers in digestive organs.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22298,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','What is History?','[\"History is a science that studies events that have already happened.\",\"We use words like ‘past’, ‘present’ and ‘future’ to talk about time.\",\"The past is everything that has already happened, the present is what is happening now, and the future is what will happen later.\",\"History uses evidence to find out what really happened, not just imagination.\",\"Evidence comes from three main sources: material (objects), written (books, letters) and oral (stories, songs).\",\"Scientists use the scientific method in history: ask a question, gather evidence, test it, and draw conclusions.\",\"Historical facts are tested for reliability by checking if they can be repeated or confirmed by other evidence.\",\"History helps us learn from the past and plan for a better future.\",\"Every person, village, city and country has its own history.\",\"History shows how people’s actions lead to changes in society, culture and the environment.\",\"The study of history is part of Environmental Studies because it shows how humans have affected the environment over time.\",\"Archaeology is the science that digs up old objects to learn about ancient people.\",\"When we read a story about the past, we should check if it matches other evidence.\",\"History is written in books, manuscripts, copper plates, inscriptions and oral traditions.\",\"Knowing history inspires us to become better citizens and to respect our heritage.\",\"Remember: past, present and future are connected by a chain of events.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which word means the time that has already passed?\",\"options\":[\"Future\",\"Past\",\"Present\",\"Yesterday\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Past refers to time that has already happened.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main method historians use to find out what really happened?\",\"options\":[\"Guessing\",\"Reading stories\",\"Scientific method\",\"Listening to myths\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Historians use the scientific method to test evidence.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a source of history?\",\"options\":[\"Material sources\",\"Written sources\",\"Oral sources\",\"Dreams\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Dreams are not a reliable historical source.\"},{\"q\":\"Archaeology deals with:\",\"options\":[\"Studying living animals\",\"Digging up old objects\",\"Writing new books\",\"Painting murals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Archaeology is the study of ancient remains.\"},{\"q\":\"If a village works together, it can become:\",\"options\":[\"Stuck\",\"Progressive\",\"Lost\",\"Broken\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cooperation leads to progress.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a material source?\",\"options\":[\"A story told by a grandparent\",\"A copper plate\",\"A poem\",\"An oral chant\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Copper plates are physical objects.\"},{\"q\":\"History helps us to:\",\"options\":[\"Ignore the past\",\"Make up stories\",\"Learn from the past\",\"Forget old events\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"History teaches lessons from earlier times.\"},{\"q\":\"The future is described by words such as:\",\"options\":[\"Yesterday\",\"Tomorrow\",\"Yesterday\",\"Yesterday\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tomorrow and next year refer to the future.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a scientific discipline?\",\"options\":[\"History\",\"Fantasy\",\"Dreaming\",\"Imagining\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"History is considered a scientific discipline.\"},{\"q\":\"The chain that links past, present and future is called:\",\"options\":[\"A chain of events\",\"A chain of thoughts\",\"A chain of people\",\"A chain of books\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Past, present and future are connected by events.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22299,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','History and the Concept of ‘Time’','[\"Time is continuous and is divided into units like seconds, minutes, hours, days, weeks, months, years, centuries, and millennia.\",\"A day is 24 hours long, made of 12 hours of daylight and 12 hours of night.\",\"A week has 7 days from Monday to Sunday.\",\"A month is about 4 weeks long and has 12 months in a year.\",\"A year has 12 months and is the time it takes Earth to orbit the Sun.\",\"A decade is 10 years, a century is 100 years, and a millennium is 1000 years.\",\"The unilinear division of time means arranging events in a straight line from past to present to future.\",\"The Common Era (CE) or AD is the calendar system that started with the birth of Jesus Christ.\",\"Before the Common Era is called BCE or BC and is written in reverse order (e.g., 1000‑1 BCE).\",\"The first century CE is written as 1‑100 CE.\",\"Historical periods are divided into prehistoric (no written records) and historic (written records).\",\"Scientists use dating methods like Carbon‑14 and tree‑ring analysis to estimate ages of ancient objects.\",\"Carbon‑14 dating works because living organisms contain a small amount of radioactive carbon‑14 which decays after death.\",\"Tree rings show one ring per year; counting rings gives the tree’s age.\",\"The calendar we use today is based on the Christian Era and the Gregorian calendar.\",\"A millennium is 1000 years, a century is 100 years, a decade is 10 years, and a year is 365 days (366 in a leap year).\"]','[{\"q\":\"How many hours are there in a day?\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"24\",\"36\",\"48\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A day has 24 hours.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first century of the Common Era written as?\",\"options\":[\"1‑100 CE\",\"100‑1 CE\",\"1‑100 BCE\",\"100‑1 BCE\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first century CE is written 1‑100 CE.\"},{\"q\":\"Which method is used to count the age of a tree?\",\"options\":[\"Carbon‑14\",\"Tree‑ring (dendrochronology)\",\"Radiocarbon dating\",\"Stone age\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tree‑ring method counts yearly growth rings.\"},{\"q\":\"What does BCE stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Before Common Era\",\"Before Christian Era\",\"Before Common Epoch\",\"Before Calendar Era\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"BCE means Before Common Era.\"},{\"q\":\"How many years are in a millennium?\",\"options\":[\"100\",\"1000\",\"10\",\"10000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A millennium is 1000 years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a prehistoric period?\",\"options\":[\"Historic period\",\"Prehistoric period\",\"Both\",\"None\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Prehistoric period has no written records.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unit of time that is 60 seconds long?\",\"options\":[\"Minute\",\"Hour\",\"Day\",\"Week\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A minute is 60 seconds.\"},{\"q\":\"Which dating method can determine ages up to 60,000 years?\",\"options\":[\"Tree‑ring\",\"Carbon‑14\",\"Stone age\",\"Dendrochronology\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carbon‑14 dating works up to about 60,000 years.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the unilinear division of time?\",\"options\":[\"Events arranged in a circle\",\"Events arranged in a straight line\",\"Events arranged randomly\",\"Events arranged by colour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Unilinear means a straight line from past to future.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a unit of time?\",\"options\":[\"Second\",\"Minute\",\"Hour\",\"Yearly\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Yearly is not a standard unit; year is the unit.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22300,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Life on Earth','[\"- Earth is the only planet where life is known to exist.\",\"- Earth formed about 4.5 billion years ago from a cloud of hot gases and dust.\",\"- The cloud spun and split into the Sun and the planets.\",\"- Water bodies appeared on Earth’s surface early in its history.\",\"- The first living things were unicellular organisms called protozoa.\",\"- Protozoa lived in water and were too small to see without a microscope.\",\"- Over time, some protozoa evolved into multicellular organisms.\",\"- Animals are living beings that breathe, move, and reproduce.\",\"- Some animals lay eggs; others give birth to live young.\",\"- The Sun and planets revolve around each other in a solar system.\",\"- Mars has similar features to Earth but lacks liquid water.\",\"- The Mangalyaan mission reached Mars in 2014.\",\"- Life requires water in liquid form; Mars has no liquid water yet.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which planet is the only one known to have life?\",\"options\":[\"Venus\",\"Mars\",\"Earth\",\"Jupiter\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Earth is the only planet where life is known to exist.\"},{\"q\":\"When did the Sun and planets form?\",\"options\":[\"4.5 billion years ago\",\"3.5 billion years ago\",\"2.5 billion years ago\",\"1.5 billion years ago\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"They formed about 4.5 billion years ago from a cloud of hot gases.\"},{\"q\":\"What are protozoa?\",\"options\":[\"Multicellular animals\",\"Unicellular organisms\",\"Plants\",\"Bacteria\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Protozoa are unicellular organisms that lived in water.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of animals?\",\"options\":[\"They can photosynthesize\",\"They breathe\",\"They are stationary\",\"They are plants\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Animals breathe air or water.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason Mars may not support life?\",\"options\":[\"No oxygen\",\"No water in liquid form\",\"No sunlight\",\"No magnetic field\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mars lacks liquid water, essential for life.\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is needed to see protozoa?\",\"options\":[\"Telescope\",\"Microscope\",\"Camera\",\"Magnifying glass\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Protozoa are too small for the naked eye; a microscope is needed.\"},{\"q\":\"Which planet is the smallest in our solar system?\",\"options\":[\"Mercury\",\"Mars\",\"Venus\",\"Jupiter\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mercury is the smallest planet.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main component of Mars’ atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Nitrogen\",\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Helium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"About 95% of Mars’ atmosphere is carbon dioxide.\"},{\"q\":\"Which mission reached Mars in 2014?\",\"options\":[\"Apollo\",\"Challenger\",\"Mangalyaan\",\"Voyager\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"India’s Mangalyaan mission reached Mars in 2014.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these animals gives birth to live young?\",\"options\":[\"Fish\",\"Birds\",\"Reptiles\",\"Some mammals\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Some mammals give birth to live young.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22301,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Evolution','[\"Evolution means gradual and continuous change over a long time.\",\"Charles Darwin first explained evolution in 1859 in his book *On the Origin of Species*.\",\"Living things change because they adapt to their environment.\",\"Animals that adapt well survive; those that do not may become extinct.\",\"Evolution occurs in all living things, from tiny single‑cell organisms to big animals.\",\"The first life on Earth were single‑cell organisms called protozoa.\",\"Protozoa evolved into multicellular plants and animals.\",\"Animals are divided into two main groups: invertebrates (no backbone) and vertebrates (with backbone).\",\"Invertebrates include animals like snails, insects and worms.\",\"Vertebrates include fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals.\",\"Mammals are special because they grow babies inside the mother and feed them with milk.\",\"Apes are close relatives of humans and live mainly on trees.\",\"Some apes moved to grasslands and became the first humans (primitive man).\",\"Fossils show that dinosaurs were huge reptiles and some evolved into birds.\",\"Extinction happens when a species cannot survive changes in its environment.\",\"Evolution is shown by changes in physical traits that are passed to offspring.\",\"Every new species is a result of many small changes over many generations.\",\"The word *dinosaur* comes from Greek words meaning ‘terrible lizard’.\",\"Evolution is a natural process, not a random accident.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who first gave a systematic explanation of evolution?\",\"options\":[\"Charles Darwin\",\"Carl Linnaeus\",\"Louis Leakey\",\"Willard Libby\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Charles Darwin published the theory of evolution in 1859.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word ‘evolution’ mean?\",\"options\":[\"Sudden change\",\"Gradual and continuous change\",\"Complete extinction\",\"Rapid mutation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Evolution refers to gradual and continuous change over time.\"},{\"q\":\"Which animals are called invertebrates?\",\"options\":[\"Animals with a backbone\",\"Animals without a backbone\",\"Animals that lay eggs\",\"Animals that live only in water\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Invertebrates are animals that do not have a backbone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which group includes fish, frogs, and snakes?\",\"options\":[\"Invertebrates\",\"Vertebrates\",\"Mammals\",\"Reptiles\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fish, frogs, and snakes are all vertebrates, animals with a backbone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of mammals?\",\"options\":[\"They lay eggs\",\"They have a placenta\",\"They live only in water\",\"They have scales\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mammals give birth to live young and have a placenta to nourish them.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term ‘primitive man’ refer to?\",\"options\":[\"The first humans\",\"The most evolved humans\",\"The earliest apes\",\"The last humans\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Primitive man means the first humans or early human species.\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal is an example of an amphibian?\",\"options\":[\"Fish\",\"Snake\",\"Frog\",\"Cow\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Frogs live in water and on land, making them amphibians.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these dinosaurs had wings?\",\"options\":[\"Tyrannosaurus\",\"Triceratops\",\"Pterodactyl\",\"Stegosaurus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Pterodactyls were winged dinosaurs that are believed to have evolved into birds.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason some species become extinct?\",\"options\":[\"They evolve too quickly\",\"They adapt to all environments\",\"They cannot adapt to environmental changes\",\"They have too many offspring\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Species that cannot adapt to environmental changes are more likely to become extinct.\"},{\"q\":\"Which scientist introduced a method of classifying animals before Darwin?\",\"options\":[\"Charles Darwin\",\"Carl Linnaeus\",\"Louis Leakey\",\"Willard Libby\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Carl Linnaeus developed a classification system for animals before Darwin.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22302,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Evolution of Mankind','[\"- Homo means ‘man’ in Latin.\",\"- Homo habilis means ‘the skilled hand’ and lived about 2.4–1.4 million years ago.\",\"- Homo habilis could walk on two feet and make simple stone tools like choppers.\",\"- Homo erectus lived 1.8–0.2 million years ago, had a bigger brain and made hand‑axes.\",\"- Homo erectus used fire for cooking but could not yet make fire.\",\"- Neanderthals (Homo neanderthalensis) lived in Europe and Asia, used stone tools and made fire by striking flint.\",\"- Neanderthals buried their dead with red ochre and may have had a simple language.\",\"- Homo sapiens (modern humans) appeared about 300,000 years ago, had a fully developed brain and language.\",\"- Homo sapiens developed agriculture, settled villages and made complex tools.\",\"- Humans have an opposable thumb, a flexible tongue and a larynx that lets them make many sounds.\",\"- Evolution is not a straight line; many branches appeared and some went extinct.\",\"- Fossils are bones that have turned into stone over millions of years.\",\"- The study of heredity (genetics) explains how traits are passed from parents to children.\",\"- Humans can stand upright, walk on two feet and use their hands for many tasks.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the Latin word ‘homo’ mean?\",\"options\":[\"man\",\"woman\",\"child\",\"animal\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"‘Homo’ is Latin for ‘man’.\"},{\"q\":\"Which species is known as ‘the skilled human’?\",\"options\":[\"Homo habilis\",\"Homo erectus\",\"Neanderthalensis\",\"Homo sapiens\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Homo habilis made tools and is called ‘the skilled human’.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main tool made by Homo erectus?\",\"options\":[\"Stone chopper\",\"Hand‑axe\",\"Flint spear\",\"Bone knife\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Homo erectus is famous for making the hand‑axe.\"},{\"q\":\"Where were the first Neanderthal remains found?\",\"options\":[\"Germany\",\"France\",\"Italy\",\"Spain\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Neanderthal remains were first discovered in Germany.\"},{\"q\":\"Which feature helped Neanderthals make fire?\",\"options\":[\"Rubbing sticks\",\"Striking flint\",\"Using fire‑starter\",\"Burning branches\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Neanderthals made fire by rubbing sticks together.\"},{\"q\":\"What does ‘sapiens’ mean in Homo sapiens?\",\"options\":[\"Intelligent\",\"Strong\",\"Fast\",\"Tall\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"‘Sapiens’ means ‘intelligent’ or ‘thinking’.\"},{\"q\":\"Which species had the most developed brain among those mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Homo habilis\",\"Homo erectus\",\"Neanderthalensis\",\"Homo sapiens\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Homo sapiens had the most developed brain.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason Neanderthals became extinct?\",\"options\":[\"Disease\",\"War\",\"Climate change\",\"Lack of food\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Climate change and competition are main reasons for their extinction.\"},{\"q\":\"What is heredity?\",\"options\":[\"The study of fossils\",\"The passing of traits from parents to offspring\",\"The use of tools\",\"The making of fire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Heredity is the transmission of traits from parents to children.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a tool used by early humans?\",\"options\":[\"Stone chopper\",\"Hand‑axe\",\"Flint spear\",\"Paper\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Paper was not used by early humans.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22303,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Stone Age : Stone Tools','[\"Stone tools are made from stones, bones, twigs and sticks.\",\"The first people who made stone tools were Homo habilis.\",\"Stone tools are divided into three types: chopper, scraper and hammerstone.\",\"A chopper is a stone with a sharp edge used to cut or break things.\",\"A scraper is a stone used to clean or smooth surfaces.\",\"A hammerstone is a heavy stone used to strike other stones to make flakes.\",\"The Old Stone Age (Palaeolithic) is the earliest period of stone tools.\",\"The Middle Stone Age (Mesolithic) introduced microliths – very small stone blades.\",\"The New Stone Age (Neolithic) saw polished stone tools and the start of farming.\",\"Tools are chosen based on the task, time, efficiency and skill.\",\"Homo erectus made hand‑axes that were more symmetrical than earlier choppers.\",\"Homo sapiens invented long narrow blades and polished axes for farming and hunting.\",\"Stone tools were used for cutting meat, sharpening sticks, making knives and for building shelters.\",\"The Old Stone Age sites in India include Bhimbetka, Narmada and Gangapur.\",\"The Middle Stone Age sites include Bagor, Bhimbetka and Langhnaj.\",\"The New Stone Age sites include Sinnar, Chandwad and Bijapur.\",\"Stone tools were made by striking a hard stone against another to produce flakes.\",\"Microliths were attached to sticks to make arrows and fish hooks.\",\"Polished stone tools were smoother and more durable than unpolished ones.\",\"Stone tools show the progress of human technology over millions of years.\",\"The term lithos means stone in Greek, and lithic means stone‑made.\",\"Stone tools are still found in many parts of the world today.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a chopper used for?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting meat\",\"Cleaning surfaces\",\"Striking other stones\",\"Making arrows\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A chopper is a stone with a sharp edge used for cutting or breaking.\"},{\"q\":\"Which period is called the Middle Stone Age?\",\"options\":[\"Old Stone Age\",\"Middle Stone Age\",\"New Stone Age\",\"Copper Age\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Middle Stone Age is also known as Mesolithic.\"},{\"q\":\"Microliths are small stone blades used in which age?\",\"options\":[\"Old Stone Age\",\"Middle Stone Age\",\"New Stone Age\",\"Iron Age\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Microliths are characteristic of the Middle Stone Age.\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool is used to strike other stones to make flakes?\",\"options\":[\"Chopper\",\"Scraper\",\"Hammerstone\",\"Antler\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A hammerstone is struck against another stone to produce flakes.\"},{\"q\":\"Polished stone tools were mainly used in which age?\",\"options\":[\"Old Stone Age\",\"Middle Stone Age\",\"New Stone Age\",\"Bronze Age\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Polished tools appear in the New Stone Age.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Greek word \'lithos\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Stone\",\"Tool\",\"Sharp\",\"Old\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lithos means stone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of stone tool?\",\"options\":[\"Chopper\",\"Scraper\",\"Spear\",\"Hammerstone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Spear is not listed as a stone tool type in the chapter.\"},{\"q\":\"Homo sapiens introduced which technique in tool making?\",\"options\":[\"Percussion flaking\",\"Microlith production\",\"Polishing\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Homo sapiens used all these techniques.\"},{\"q\":\"Which site is an Old Stone Age site in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Bagor\",\"Bhimbetka\",\"Langhnaj\",\"Bijapur\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bagor is an Old Stone Age site in Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"Which age saw the start of agriculture and animal husbandry?\",\"options\":[\"Old Stone Age\",\"Middle Stone Age\",\"New Stone Age\",\"Iron Age\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The New Stone Age introduced farming and domestication.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22304,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','From Shelters to Village-settlements','[\"Shelters were places where early humans stayed for a short time.\",\"In the Middle Stone Age humans moved from caves to seasonal camps.\",\"Seasonal camps were set up in good hunting grounds and near food sources.\",\"People used animal hides, wood and fire to keep warm in camps.\",\"Later, they started building huts in the open when they could stay longer.\",\"The New Stone Age saw the start of agriculture and permanent villages.\",\"Agriculture let people store food, so they didn’t have to move constantly.\",\"Permanent villages were built on cleared forest land with huts made of mud, straw or wood.\",\"The climate changed from very cold (glacial) to warmer (interglacial) over time.\",\"Glacial periods had ice sheets covering much of the Earth and low sea levels.\",\"Interglacial periods had melted ice, higher sea levels and warmer weather.\",\"The last glacial period ended about 11,000 years ago, starting the current warm era.\",\"During the New Stone Age, humans grew grains like millet and barley.\",\"They also hunted wild animals such as deer, boar and sheep.\",\"Village life involved farming, gathering, and making tools from stone and wood.\",\"The diet changed from mainly hunting to a mix of hunting and farming.\",\"People built houses with walls of mud or thatch and roofs of grass or bark.\",\"Permanent villages allowed social organization and shared resources.\",\"The Old Stone Age was mainly hunting and gathering, while the New Stone Age added farming.\",\"The Middle Stone Age was a transition with more use of fire and clothing.\",\"The New Stone Age had permanent settlements, agriculture and social roles.\",\"The climate cycles of glacial and interglacial periods shaped human life.\",\"The New Stone Age villages were often near rivers for water and food.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which period is known for permanent villages and agriculture?\",\"options\":[\"Old Stone Age\",\"Middle Stone Age\",\"New Stone Age\",\"Bronze Age\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Permanent villages and farming began in the New Stone Age.\"},{\"q\":\"What helped Neanderthals stay warm in cold Europe?\",\"options\":[\"Stone walls\",\"Animal hides\",\"Fire\",\"Ice caves\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"They wore leather hides and used fire for warmth.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a glacial period?\",\"options\":[\"Warm period\",\"Cold period with ice sheets\",\"Dry period\",\"Rainy period\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Glacial periods have ice sheets and low sea levels.\"},{\"q\":\"Which food did early humans start growing in the New Stone Age?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Millet\",\"Potatoes\",\"Corn\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Millet and other grains were first cultivated.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did humans move from caves to seasonal camps?\",\"options\":[\"Caves were too hot\",\"Caves were too small\",\"Caves were dangerous\",\"Caves were scarce\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Caves were dangerous and not suitable for long stays.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between Old and New Stone Age?\",\"options\":[\"Use of metal tools\",\"Use of fire\",\"Permanent settlements\",\"Use of boats\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"New Stone Age had permanent villages; Old was mobile.\"},{\"q\":\"What caused sea levels to rise during interglacial periods?\",\"options\":[\"More rainfall\",\"Melting ice\",\"Less evaporation\",\"Ocean currents\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Melting ice added water to the oceans.\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal was an ancestor of the elephant?\",\"options\":[\"Mammoth\",\"Hippopotamus\",\"Giraffe\",\"Rhino\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The mammoth is an ancestor of the elephant.\"},{\"q\":\"What material was NOT used to build huts in the New Stone Age?\",\"options\":[\"Mud\",\"Wood\",\"Stone\",\"Plastic\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Plastic was not available.\"},{\"q\":\"What did seasonal camps depend on?\",\"options\":[\"Permanent food stores\",\"Fishing\",\"Hunting grounds\",\"Permanent houses\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Seasonal camps were set near hunting and fishing grounds.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22305,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Beginning of Settled Life','[\"- Domestication is the process of making wild animals friendly and useful for humans.\",\"- First domesticated animal was the dog, used for hunting and guarding.\",\"- Goat and sheep were domesticated next, then cattle, buffalo, and others.\",\"- Three main steps in domestication: capture, taming, and training animals to live with people.\",\"- Agriculture began about 11,000 years ago in the Fertile Crescent (Israel, Iraq, Iran).\",\"- Early farmers used pointed sticks and perforated stones to sow seeds.\",\"- The invention of the plough pulled by bullocks allowed food to be stored for long periods.\",\"- Surplus food led to trade and the barter system.\",\"- Crafts such as pottery, bead making, weaving, and metalwork emerged.\",\"- Village settlements were built of wattle and daub, later bricks; houses were close together and often had more than one room.\",\"- Families were extended; relatives lived together and shared resources.\",\"- Chiefs or village leaders were chosen to enforce rules and protect the community.\",\"- Salt was an important traded commodity, often exchanged for other goods.\",\"- The first houses were simple, but later houses became more permanent and larger.\",\"- Domestic animals provide food (milk, meat), labor (ploughing), and other resources like wool and hides.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which animal was the first to be domesticated by humans?\",\"options\":[\"Cat\",\"Dog\",\"Horse\",\"Sheep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dogs were domesticated first, mainly for hunting and guarding.\"},{\"q\":\"When did agriculture first begin according to archaeological evidence?\",\"options\":[\"5,000 years ago\",\"7,000 years ago\",\"9,000 years ago\",\"11,000 years ago\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Evidence shows agriculture started about 11,000 years ago.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the domestication of a wild animal?\",\"options\":[\"Taming\",\"Training\",\"Capture\",\"Feeding\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first step is capturing the wild animal.\"},{\"q\":\"Which tool was used by early farmers to sow seeds?\",\"options\":[\"Hoe\",\"Plough\",\"Pointed stick\",\"Scythe\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Early farmers used pointed sticks to sow seeds.\"},{\"q\":\"What material were early houses made of?\",\"options\":[\"Stone\",\"Wood\",\"Wattle and daub\",\"Brick\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Early houses were made of wattle and daub, later bricks.\"},{\"q\":\"What system emerged when villages produced surplus food?\",\"options\":[\"Currency system\",\"Barter system\",\"Tax system\",\"Trade system\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Surplus food led to the barter system.\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal is commonly used by modern police for patrol?\",\"options\":[\"Dog\",\"Horse\",\"Cattle\",\"Sheep\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Police use dogs for patrol and detection.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main purpose of protective walls around villages?\",\"options\":[\"To keep animals inside\",\"To keep people inside\",\"To protect from floods, animals and outsiders\",\"To mark boundaries\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Walls protected villages from floods, wild animals and outsiders.\"},{\"q\":\"Which craft involves making earthen pots?\",\"options\":[\"Weaving\",\"Pottery\",\"Bead making\",\"Metalwork\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pottery is the craft of making earthen pots.\"},{\"q\":\"What did early farmers use to store food grains for long periods?\",\"options\":[\"Glass jars\",\"Metal cans\",\"Clay pots\",\"Wooden barrels\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Clay pots were used to store food grains.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22306,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Settled Life and Urban Civilization','[\"Metals are substances that can be melted and shaped to make tools and objects.\",\"Gold is the first metal used by humans, but it is too soft for tools.\",\"Copper was the next metal used; it is harder than gold but still soft for many objects.\",\"Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin; it is harder and used for many tools and weapons.\",\"The Stone Age is when people used only stone tools.\",\"The Copper Age is when people started using copper and bronze.\",\"The Iron Age is when iron tools appeared, making stronger tools and weapons.\",\"The wheel was invented during the Copper Age and was first used by potters.\",\"Potters used a rotating plank or wheel to shape pots quickly and evenly.\",\"The invention of the wheel helped create carts and chariots for transport.\",\"Long‑distance trade grew when people could transport goods faster with wheeled vehicles.\",\"Cities grew because trade increased and people needed places to keep records.\",\"Writing (script) was developed to keep permanent records of trade and other activities.\",\"The Indus (Harappan) civilization had its own script and used wheeled carts.\",\"The Rosetta Stone helped scholars read Egyptian hieroglyphs by comparing them with Greek and Demotic scripts.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which metal is the softest and was used first by humans?\",\"options\":[\"Gold\",\"Copper\",\"Iron\",\"Bronze\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Gold is the softest metal and was the first used for ornaments.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the alloy made by mixing copper and tin?\",\"options\":[\"Bronze\",\"Steel\",\"Alloy\",\"Malleable\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Copper + tin = bronze, a harder metal.\"},{\"q\":\"During which age were wheeled carts first used?\",\"options\":[\"Stone Age\",\"Copper Age\",\"Iron Age\",\"Bronze Age\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The wheel was invented in the Copper Age.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason cities started to grow in ancient times?\",\"options\":[\"Invention of writing\",\"Discovery of fire\",\"Rise of trade\",\"Agriculture alone\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Trade increased and people needed places to keep records.\"},{\"q\":\"Which script was used for routine documents in ancient Egypt?\",\"options\":[\"Hieroglyphs\",\"Demotic\",\"Coptic\",\"Greek\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Demotic was the simplified script for everyday use.\"},{\"q\":\"The Rosetta Stone helped scholars read which ancient script?\",\"options\":[\"Cuneiform\",\"Hieroglyphs\",\"Latin\",\"Greek\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It had the same text in three scripts, including hieroglyphs.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Chalcolithic\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Age of copper and stone\",\"Age of iron and bronze\",\"Age of gold and silver\",\"Age of pottery\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Chalco = copper, lithos = stone.\"},{\"q\":\"Which metal is harder than gold but still too soft for many tools?\",\"options\":[\"Copper\",\"Iron\",\"Bronze\",\"Steel\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Copper is harder than gold but still soft.\"},{\"q\":\"Which civilization is known as the earliest on the Indian subcontinent?\",\"options\":[\"Mohenjo-Daro\",\"Maya\",\"Egypt\",\"Mesopotamia\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Indus (Harappan) civilization was earliest in India.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main function of a potter’s wheel?\",\"options\":[\"To rotate the pot for even shaping\",\"To melt metal\",\"To transport goods\",\"To write records\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It turns the pot while the potter shapes it.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22307,25,5,'EVS Part 2','en','Historic Period','[\"Culture is the way people use tools, make food, build homes and create art to meet their needs.\",\"Ancient riverine civilizations grew along rivers like the Tigris‑Euphrates, Nile, Indus and Huang He.\",\"They made pots, bricks, cloth, metal tools and weapons from clay, wood, stone and metal.\",\"They used the wheel, made boats and built cities with walls, streets and drainage.\",\"Writing appeared as scripts on clay tablets, seals and pottery.\",\"Trade linked Mesopotamia, Egypt, Harappan and Chinese civilizations around 3000 BC.\",\"Harappan cities had planned streets, public baths, wells and a system of irrigation.\",\"Egyptians built pyramids, temples and ships, and made papyrus paper and faience pottery.\",\"Music and dance were important; instruments like the Balag, cymbals, flutes and drums were used.\",\"Sports included wrestling, hunting, board games such as Senat and early chess‑like games.\",\"The Harappan script is undeciphered, so we learn about them from seals and pottery.\",\"Early civilizations had permanent settlements, surplus food, craft specialization and long‑distance trade.\",\"They used copper and bronze, had irrigation, planned cities and developed science and mathematics.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the word ‘culture’ mean for humans?\",\"options\":[\"The way we eat food\",\"The way we build houses\",\"The way we use tools and create art\",\"The way we travel by boat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Culture is the skills, arts, and traditions humans create to meet their needs.\"},{\"q\":\"Which river is associated with the earliest civilization in the Indian subcontinent?\",\"options\":[\"Ganges\",\"Indus\",\"Nile\",\"Tigris\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Harappan Civilization flourished along the Indus River.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of early riverine civilizations?\",\"options\":[\"Permanent settlements\",\"Use of bronze\",\"Centralized irrigation\",\"No written records\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Early civilizations had written records; they were not absent.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main purpose of the wheel in Mesopotamia?\",\"options\":[\"For decoration\",\"For transport\",\"For cooking\",\"For building walls\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The wheel was used for transportation and trade.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ancient civilization is known for building pyramids?\",\"options\":[\"Mesopotamia\",\"Egypt\",\"Harappan\",\"China\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Egyptians built pyramids as tombs for pharaohs.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the name of the stringed instrument found in Mesopotamia?\",\"options\":[\"Balag\",\"Sitar\",\"Guitar\",\"Harp\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Balag was a 11‑stringed instrument found in Ur.\"},{\"q\":\"Which civilization is called the ‘Protohistoric period’ of the Indian subcontinent?\",\"options\":[\"Harappan\",\"Egyptian\",\"Mesopotamian\",\"Chinese\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Harappan Civilization is considered the Protohistoric period.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was NOT a major factor in the rise of early civilizations?\",\"options\":[\"Use of the wheel\",\"Trade\",\"Permanent settlements\",\"No agriculture\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Agriculture was essential; lack of it would prevent civilization.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the primary material used by the Harappans for pottery?\",\"options\":[\"Clay\",\"Stone\",\"Metal\",\"Wood\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Harappans made pots from clay, often baked.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ancient city was known for its well‑planned streets laid out at right angles?\",\"options\":[\"Ur\",\"Uruk\",\"Lothal\",\"Beijing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Uruk had streets laid out in a grid pattern at right angles.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:28'),(22308,38,6,'Geography','en','Friendship with Geography','[\"Geography comes from Greek words: \'Geo\' means Earth and \'graphia\' means to describe.\",\"Geography is the study of Earth\'s physical features, human activities, and their interactions.\",\"Physical elements in geography include landforms, climate, water bodies, and natural vegetation.\",\"Human elements include population, settlements, culture, economic activities, and transportation.\",\"Primary sources are original data collected firsthand, such as field visits, surveys, and interviews.\",\"Secondary sources are information gathered from existing records like books, maps, and research papers.\",\"Field visit is a primary source because it involves direct observation and data collection.\",\"Textbooks and gazettes are secondary sources as they compile information from other sources.\",\"Maps, satellite images, and field visits help develop observation and analytical skills.\",\"Memorising is not the main skill developed through maps and field visits; observation and analysis are.\",\"Photographs and videos can be primary sources if taken during fieldwork, but secondary if from archives.\",\"Research papers are secondary sources as they analyze and interpret collected data.\",\"Geography studies both physical and human elements together, not in isolation.\",\"Atmospheric elements like weather and climate are part of physical geography.\",\"Understanding the difference between primary and secondary sources helps in evaluating information reliability.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What do the Greek words \'Geo\' and \'graphia\' mean in the term \'Geography\'?\",\"options\":[\"Earth and to write\",\"Sky and to describe\",\"Earth and to describe\",\"Land and to draw\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"\'Geo\' means Earth and \'graphia\' means to describe, so Geography means describing the Earth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a primary source of geographic data?\",\"options\":[\"Field visit\",\"Interview conducted by oneself\",\"Textbook\",\"Self-experienced survey\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A textbook is a secondary source because it compiles information from other sources, not collected firsthand.\"},{\"q\":\"What elements are studied in Geography according to the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Only human elements\",\"Only physical elements\",\"Both physical and human elements\",\"Only atmospheric elements\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Geography studies both physical elements (like landforms, climate) and human elements (like population, culture).\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement about secondary sources is NOT true?\",\"options\":[\"Their credibility is more\",\"Data is based on research\",\"They are not easily available\",\"Data can be outdated\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Secondary sources often have less credibility than primary sources because they are one step removed from original data.\"},{\"q\":\"Collecting climate data from villagers in a village is an example of which source?\",\"options\":[\"Secondary source\",\"Primary source\",\"Documented source\",\"Historical source\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Directly collecting data from villagers through interaction is a primary source as it is firsthand.\"},{\"q\":\"Which skill is developed when a student uses maps, field visits, and satellite images to collect data?\",\"options\":[\"Memorising\",\"Drawing\",\"Writing\",\"Observation and analysis\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Using maps, field visits, and satellite images develops observation and analytical skills, not just memorisation.\"},{\"q\":\"Are photographs and videos always primary sources in geography?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No, never\",\"Only if taken during fieldwork\",\"Only if published in books\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Photographs and videos are primary sources only when taken during fieldwork; otherwise, they may be secondary.\"},{\"q\":\"What type of source is a research paper in geography?\",\"options\":[\"Primary source\",\"Secondary source\",\"Both\",\"Neither\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Research papers analyze and interpret data, so they are secondary sources.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a physical element studied in Geography?\",\"options\":[\"Population density\",\"Type of settlement\",\"River system\",\"Farming practices\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"River systems are natural physical features, while the others are human-related.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to distinguish between primary and secondary sources in geographic study?\",\"options\":[\"To make maps colorful\",\"To evaluate the reliability and origin of information\",\"To memorize definitions faster\",\"To avoid field visits\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Knowing the source type helps assess how reliable and original the information is for accurate study.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22309,38,6,'Geography','en','History of Indian Geography','[\"Ancient Indian geography is based on scriptures like Vedas, Puranas, Upanishads, and Suryasiddhanta.\",\"The term \'Bhugol\' (Geography) is mentioned in the Vedas.\",\"Earth is called \'Pruthvi\' or \'Dharani\' in ancient Indian texts.\",\"Ancient Indians knew the Earth is circular in shape, observed during lunar eclipses.\",\"The concept of \'Vasudhaiv Kutumbakam\' (the world is one family) comes from Indian thinkers.\",\"Ancient Indian astronomers like Aryabhatta, Brahmagupta, and Bhaskaracharya contributed to geography and astronomy.\",\"The Earth was described as \'Saptadweep\' — seven islands surrounded by water.\",\"Jambudweep is the central island where India (Bharatvarsh) is located.\",\"Pushkardweep is the island with lakes full of lotus plants.\",\"Kushdweep was occupied by Kush grass and includes parts of Africa and West Asia.\",\"Four ancient oceans were named: Kshir Sagar (Indian), Dadhi Sagar (Atlantic), Sura Samudra (Pacific), Kshar Samudra (Arctic).\",\"Ancient Indians measured time using units like Kshan, Nimish, Pal, Ghatika, Muhurt, Prahar, and Diwas.\",\"Time was measured by observing the Sun, Moon, stars, and eclipses.\",\"The atmosphere was described as 12 yojanas (about 96 km) thick by Bhaskaracharya.\",\"Seasons (Rutus) and months were described in the Rigveda, with six seasons in a year.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Bhugol\' mean in ancient Indian context?\",\"options\":[\"Study of stars\",\"Geography\",\"History of kings\",\"Science of medicine\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The term \'Bhugol\' is derived from Sanskrit and means Geography, as mentioned in the Vedas.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ancient Indian text mentions the Earth as \'Pruthvi\' or \'Dharani\'?\",\"options\":[\"Ramayana\",\"Mahabharata\",\"Vedas\",\"Panchatantra\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Vedas refer to the Earth as \'Pruthvi\' or \'Dharani\', symbolizing the solid ground.\"},{\"q\":\"What observation led ancient Indians to conclude that the Earth is circular?\",\"options\":[\"Shadow during solar eclipse\",\"Shadow during lunar eclipse\",\"Shape of clouds\",\"Direction of wind\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"During a lunar eclipse, the Earth\'s circular shadow on the Moon showed that the Earth is round.\"},{\"q\":\"Which island (Dweep) in ancient Indian geography is associated with lotus-filled lakes?\",\"options\":[\"Jambudweep\",\"Pushkardweep\",\"Kushdweep\",\"Shalmali Dweep\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pushkardweep was named after Pushkar (lotus) and was occupied by lakes full of lotus plants.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ancient Indian thinker is known for work in astronomy and mathematics, and contributed to geographic knowledge?\",\"options\":[\"Kalidas\",\"Kautilya\",\"Aryabhatta\",\"Chanakya\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Aryabhatta was a renowned astronomer and mathematician who contributed to understanding Earth\'s shape, time, and eclipses.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of \'Vasudhaiv Kutumbakam\'?\",\"options\":[\"The Earth is round\",\"Knowledge is power\",\"The world is one family\",\"Truth alone triumphs\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"This Sanskrit phrase, from the Maha Upanishad, means \'the world is one family\', reflecting ancient Indian universal outlook.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the four ancient oceans mentioned in Indian scriptures?\",\"options\":[\"Kshir Sagar\",\"Dadhi Sagar\",\"Sura Samudra\",\"Indus Sagar\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The four oceans are Kshir Sagar (Indian), Dadhi Sagar (Atlantic), Sura Samudra (Pacific), and Kshar Samudra (Arctic). \'Indus Sagar\' is not mentioned.\"},{\"q\":\"In which ancient text is the evolution of the Earth from water mentioned?\",\"options\":[\"Puranas\",\"Upanishads\",\"Ramayana\",\"Mahabharata\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Upanishads mention that the Earth evolved from water, reflecting early cosmological ideas.\"},{\"q\":\"What unit of time in ancient Indian system is approximately 24 minutes?\",\"options\":[\"Kshan\",\"Nimish\",\"Pal\",\"Ghatika\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"One Ghatika equals 24 minutes in the ancient Indian time system; 60 Ghatikas make a day.\"},{\"q\":\"Which festival is associated with the joy after selling Kharif crops, as mentioned in the textbook table?\",\"options\":[\"Diwali\",\"Holi\",\"Pongal\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The table mentions joy after selling Kharif crops but does not name a specific festival; thus, none of the options is correct based on given text.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22310,38,6,'Geography','en','World of Coordinates','[\"Latitude is the angular distance of a place north or south of the equator, measured in degrees.\",\"Longitudes are semicircular imaginary lines joining the North and South poles, used to determine east-west position.\",\"The equator is at 0° latitude and divides the Earth into northern and southern hemispheres.\",\"All latitudes are parallel to each other and form complete circles around the Earth.\",\"The distance between two consecutive latitudes is approximately 111 km everywhere on Earth.\",\"The Prime Meridian (0° longitude) passes through Greenwich and is used to fix global standard time (GMT).\",\"Longitudes are not parallel; they converge at the poles and are farthest apart at the equator.\",\"A graticule is the network formed by latitudes and longitudes on a globe or map.\",\"There are 181 latitudes in total: 90 in the north, 90 in the south, and 1 at the equator (0°).\",\"There are 360 longitudes: 180 in the eastern hemisphere and 180 in the western hemisphere.\",\"The distance between two consecutive longitudes is 111 km at the equator, decreasing to 0 km at the poles.\",\"Tropic of Cancer is at 23°30\'N, Tropic of Capricorn at 23°30\'S, Arctic Circle at 66°30\'N, Antarctic Circle at 66°30\'S.\",\"Degrees of angle are divided into minutes (1° = 60\') and seconds (1\' = 60\",\"for precise location measurement.\",\"The Earth is slightly flattened at the poles and bulged at the equator due to centrifugal force from rotation.\",\"A globe is a true model of the Earth, showing correct shapes, sizes, and locations of land and water bodies.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the angular distance of a place from the equator called?\",\"options\":[\"Longitude\",\"Latitude\",\"Graticule\",\"Prime Meridian\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Latitude measures how far north or south a place is from the equator in degrees.\"},{\"q\":\"Which line divides the Earth into eastern and western hemispheres?\",\"options\":[\"Equator\",\"Prime Meridian\",\"Tropic of Cancer\",\"Arctic Circle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Prime Meridian (0° longitude) and the 180° longitude together divide the Earth into eastern and western hemispheres.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the distance between two consecutive latitudes on Earth?\",\"options\":[\"100 km\",\"111 km\",\"55.5 km\",\"222 km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The distance between any two consecutive latitudes is approximately 111 km everywhere on Earth.\"},{\"q\":\"How many latitudes can be drawn on Earth with a 1° interval?\",\"options\":[\"90\",\"180\",\"181\",\"360\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"There are 90 latitudes in the northern hemisphere, 90 in the southern, and 1 at the equator, totaling 181.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the shape of latitudes on a globe?\",\"options\":[\"Elliptical\",\"Straight lines\",\"Circular\",\"Triangular\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Although latitudes may appear elliptical in some projections, on a globe they are perfect circles.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the Prime Meridian also known as?\",\"options\":[\"0° latitude\",\"180° longitude\",\"0° longitude\",\"Equator\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Prime Meridian is the 0° longitude line passing through Greenwich, used as the reference for time zones.\"},{\"q\":\"What do we call the network of latitudes and longitudes on a globe?\",\"options\":[\"Grid\",\"Graticule\",\"Scale\",\"Legend\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The network formed by latitudes and longitudes is called a graticule, used to locate places on Earth.\"},{\"q\":\"Where do all longitudes meet?\",\"options\":[\"At the equator\",\"At the tropics\",\"At the poles\",\"At the Prime Meridian\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"All longitudes are semicircles that converge at the North and South poles.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the angular distance between two consecutive longitudes?\",\"options\":[\"30°\",\"45°\",\"60°\",\"1°\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Longitudes are drawn at intervals of 1°, so the angular distance between consecutive longitudes is 1°.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a main latitude?\",\"options\":[\"Equator\",\"Tropic of Cancer\",\"Prime Meridian\",\"Arctic Circle\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Prime Meridian is a longitude (0°), not a latitude. The others are important latitudes.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22311,38,6,'Geography','en','Practical Work','[\"Practical work in geography involves using instruments like maps, globes, thermometers, rain gauges, compasses, and GPS for hands-on learning.\",\"Maps are drawn on a flat surface using conventional signs, symbols, directions, legend, and scale to represent real-world features.\",\"The three main types of map scales are verbal (e.g., 1 cm = 1 km), numerical (e.g., 1:100000), and graphical (scale bar).\",\"Elements of a map include title, subtitle, scale, north arrow, legend, latitudes, and longitudes, which help in accurate reading and analysis.\",\"Physical maps show natural features like mountains, plateaus, rivers, and plains; political maps show countries, states, capitals, and boundaries.\",\"Thematic maps focus on specific themes such as rainfall, soil types, vegetation, climate, or population distribution.\",\"Graticule locations are points where latitudes and longitudes intersect, used to fix the exact position of a place on Earth.\",\"Field visits require tools like maps, compass, notebook, camera, water bottle, first-aid kit, and questionnaire for data collection and observation.\",\"Information and Communication Technology (ICT) tools in geography include GIS, GPS, digital maps, drones, online mapping, and statistical websites.\",\"Graphs like multiple line graphs and simple bar graphs are used to represent temperature and rainfall data collected during school campus observations.\",\"Conventional signs and symbols on maps represent features like highways, railways, rivers, forts, capitals, and light houses for easy interpretation.\",\"To prepare a map, one must consider direction, use conventional signs, include a legend and scale, and fix locations using latitudes and longitudes.\",\"The latitudinal extent of India is from 8°4\'N to 37°6\'N, and the longitudinal extent is from 68°7\'E to 97°25\'E.\",\"Instruments like thermometers and rain gauges help record daily temperature and rainfall, which can be plotted on graphs for analysis.\",\"Legends explain the meaning of symbols and colors used on a map, making it easier to understand the information presented.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of map based on the theme it represents?\",\"options\":[\"Physical map\",\"Political map\",\"Thematic map\",\"Sketch map\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Sketch map is a rough drawing and not classified based on theme like physical, political, or thematic maps.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the legend on a map explain?\",\"options\":[\"The scale of the map\",\"The direction of north\",\"The meaning of symbols and colors used\",\"The title of the map\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The legend or key on a map explains what the symbols, colors, and signs represent.\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument is used to measure rainfall in practical geography work?\",\"options\":[\"Thermometer\",\"Barometer\",\"Rain gauge\",\"Compass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A rain gauge is used to collect and measure the amount of rainfall over a period of time.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary use of a GPS in geography fieldwork?\",\"options\":[\"To measure temperature\",\"To record rainfall\",\"To determine exact location using satellites\",\"To draw maps by hand\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"GPS (Global Positioning System) uses satellite signals to find the precise latitude and longitude of a place.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a conventional symbol used on maps for a railway station?\",\"options\":[\"RH\",\"RS\",\"Rly\",\"RT\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"RS is the conventional symbol used on maps to represent a railway station.\"},{\"q\":\"What type of graph is best suited to show temperature changes over several weeks?\",\"options\":[\"Simple bar graph\",\"Pie chart\",\"Multiple line graph\",\"Flow chart\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A multiple line graph is ideal for showing how more than one variable (like max and min temperature) changes over time.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT an element of a map?\",\"options\":[\"Title\",\"Scale\",\"Legend\",\"Population density\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Population density is a thematic detail, not a fundamental element of a map like title, scale, or legend.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a verbal scale of \'1 cm = 5 km\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"1 cm on map = 5 cm on ground\",\"1 cm on map = 5 km on ground\",\"5 cm on map = 1 km on ground\",\"1 km on map = 5 cm on ground\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A verbal scale expresses the relationship between map distance and ground distance in words.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ICT tool is used for capturing aerial images and creating 3D maps in geography?\",\"options\":[\"GIS\",\"GPS\",\"Drone\",\"Online mapping websites\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Drones are used to capture aerial photographs and videos, which help in creating detailed and 3D maps.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the latitudinal extent of India as given in the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"8°4\'N to 37°6\'N\",\"68°7\'E to 97°25\'E\",\"0° to 180°\",\"23.5°N to 66.5°N\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"India extends from 8°4\'N latitude in the south to 37°6\'N latitude in the north.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22312,38,6,'Geography','en','Lithosphere','[\"The lithosphere is the solid outer layer of the Earth, consisting of the crust and upper mantle.\",\"It includes landforms like mountains, plateaus, hills, valleys, and plains.\",\"About 29% of Earth\'s surface is lithosphere (land), while 71% is covered by water (hydrosphere).\",\"The lithosphere is divided into continental crust (landmass) and oceanic crust (ocean floor).\",\"Continental crust is thicker and made of lighter elements like silica and aluminum (SiAl).\",\"Oceanic crust is thinner and made of silica and magnesium (SiMg).\",\"The lithosphere ranges from 100 to 150 km in thickness, varying across Earth.\",\"Earth\'s interior has three main layers: crust, mantle, and core.\",\"The crust is the outermost solid layer where we live.\",\"The mantle lies below the crust and is divided into outer and inner mantle.\",\"The outer mantle is semi-fluid and allows movement of lithospheric plates.\",\"Plate movements cause earthquakes and volcanic activity.\",\"The core is at Earth\'s center and is made of nickel and iron (NiFe).\",\"The outer core is liquid and generates Earth\'s magnetic field.\",\"The inner core is solid due to extreme pressure, despite high temperature.\",\"Temperature increases by about 1°C for every 32 meters depth in Earth\'s interior.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the lithosphere?\",\"options\":[\"The liquid layer of Earth\",\"The solid outer layer of Earth\",\"The gaseous layer around Earth\",\"The layer where life exists\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The lithosphere is the solid outer layer of Earth, consisting of the crust and upper mantle.\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of Earth\'s surface is covered by land (lithosphere)?\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"29%\",\"71%\",\"10%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"About 29% of Earth\'s surface is land (lithosphere), while 71% is covered by water.\"},{\"q\":\"What are the two types of crust in the lithosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Upper and lower crust\",\"Hot and cold crust\",\"Continental and oceanic crust\",\"Thick and thin crust\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The lithosphere has continental crust (under land) and oceanic crust (under oceans).\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate thickness of the lithosphere?\",\"options\":[\"10 to 50 km\",\"100 to 150 km\",\"300 to 500 km\",\"1000 to 1500 km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The lithosphere ranges from 100 to 150 km in thickness, though it varies across Earth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which layer lies directly below the crust?\",\"options\":[\"Core\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Mantle\",\"Hydrosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The mantle lies directly below the crust in Earth\'s interior structure.\"},{\"q\":\"What causes the movement of lithospheric plates?\",\"options\":[\"Wind erosion\",\"Semi-fluid outer mantle\",\"Ocean currents\",\"Solar radiation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The semi-fluid nature of the outer mantle allows lithospheric plates to move.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the composition of continental crust?\",\"options\":[\"Nickel and iron\",\"Silica and magnesium\",\"Silica and aluminum\",\"Calcium and potassium\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Continental crust is mainly composed of silica and aluminum, hence called SiAl.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the composition of oceanic crust?\",\"options\":[\"Silica and aluminum\",\"Nickel and iron\",\"Silica and magnesium\",\"Oxygen and silicon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Oceanic crust is mainly composed of silica and magnesium, hence called SiMg.\"},{\"q\":\"What generates Earth\'s magnetic field?\",\"options\":[\"Rotation of Earth\",\"Movement of liquid metals in outer core\",\"Volcanic eruptions\",\"Tidal forces\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The movement of liquid iron and nickel in the outer core creates Earth\'s magnetic field.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the inner core solid despite high temperature?\",\"options\":[\"It is made of ice\",\"Due to extreme pressure\",\"It is far from the Sun\",\"It is constantly cooling\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The inner core is solid due to immense pressure from the layers above, even though it is very hot.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22313,38,6,'Geography','en','Atmosphere','[\"The atmosphere is the envelope of air surrounding the Earth.\",\"It is made up mainly of nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%).\",\"Other gases like argon, carbon dioxide, and water vapour make up about 1%.\",\"Oxygen is essential for respiration and combustion.\",\"Carbon dioxide is used by plants in photosynthesis and released during respiration.\",\"Water vapour is the gaseous form of water and important for the water cycle.\",\"The amount of water vapour decreases with height and is highest near the Earth\'s surface.\",\"Dust particles are tiny solid particles suspended in air, important for cloud formation.\",\"The atmosphere has layers: troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere, and exosphere.\",\"The ozone layer in the stratosphere absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays from the Sun.\",\"September 16 is observed as World Ozone Day to create awareness about ozone protection.\",\"Atmospheric phenomena like rainfall, storms, fog, and heat waves affect human life.\",\"Fog reduces visibility and can disrupt transport and increase accidents.\",\"The atmosphere helps maintain temperature balance on Earth.\",\"Without atmosphere, life would not be possible due to lack of air, temperature extremes, and harmful rays.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which gas makes up the largest percentage of the Earth\'s atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Oxygen\",\"Carbon dioxide\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Water vapour\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nitrogen constitutes about 78% of the atmosphere, the highest among all gases.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate percentage of oxygen in the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"1%\",\"21%\",\"78%\",\"0.04%\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oxygen makes up about 21% of the atmosphere, essential for respiration and combustion.\"},{\"q\":\"Which layer of the atmosphere contains the ozone layer that absorbs UV rays?\",\"options\":[\"Troposphere\",\"Stratosphere\",\"Mesosphere\",\"Thermosphere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The ozone layer is located in the stratosphere, where it absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main source of water vapour in the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Melting of ice\",\"Evaporation from water bodies\",\"Respiration of animals\",\"Volcanic eruptions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Water vapour enters the atmosphere mainly through evaporation from oceans, rivers, lakes, and land.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a function of water vapour in the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Helps in cloud formation\",\"Involved in the water cycle\",\"Provides oxygen for breathing\",\"Influences temperature balance\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Water vapour does not provide oxygen for breathing; oxygen comes from O2 gas, not H2O vapour.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to water vapour at higher altitudes due to low temperature?\",\"options\":[\"It increases in amount\",\"It turns into oxygen\",\"It condenses into droplets or ice\",\"It escapes into space\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"At higher altitudes, low temperature causes water vapour to condense into water droplets or ice particles.\"},{\"q\":\"Which phenomenon is caused by the presence of dust particles in the air?\",\"options\":[\"Ozone depletion\",\"Increase in oxygen\",\"Formation of clouds\",\"Decrease in nitrogen\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dust particles act as nuclei for water vapour to condense, leading to cloud formation.\"},{\"q\":\"What would happen if there were no atmosphere around the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"Plants would grow faster\",\"Temperature would be extreme and harmful rays would reach Earth\",\"Water would freeze completely\",\"Gravity would disappear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Without atmosphere, there would be no air to breathe, extreme temperature changes, and no protection from UV rays and meteors.\"},{\"q\":\"Which day is observed as World Ozone Day?\",\"options\":[\"June 5\",\"September 16\",\"October 2\",\"December 10\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"World Ozone Day is celebrated on September 16 to raise awareness about protecting the ozone layer.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a harmful component sometimes found in dust particles in the atmosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Pollen\",\"Oxygen\",\"Nitrogen\",\"Water vapour\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Pollen, though natural, can be harmful as it causes allergies; dust may also contain pollutants.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22314,38,6,'Geography','en','Hydrosphere and Biosphere','[\"Hydrosphere includes all water on Earth: oceans, seas, rivers, lakes, groundwater, and ice.\",\"About 71% of Earth\'s surface is covered by water.\",\"Oceans hold about 97% of Earth\'s water; only 3% is freshwater.\",\"Continental shelf is the submerged edge of continents with shallow water, up to 200m depth.\",\"Continental slope is the steep slope beyond the shelf, from 200m to 3000m depth.\",\"Abyssal plain is the flat, deep ocean floor beyond the slope, from 3000m to 6000m depth.\",\"Oceanic trenches are the deepest parts of the ocean, like Mariana Trench at 11,034m.\",\"Tides are the periodic rise and fall of sea levels due to gravitational pull of Moon and Sun.\",\"Spring tide occurs during full moon and new moon when Sun, Moon, and Earth align.\",\"Neap tide occurs during first and third quarter moons when Sun and Moon are at right angles.\",\"High tide is when sea water reaches its highest level; low tide is when it reaches its lowest.\",\"Tidal range is the difference between high tide and low tide levels.\",\"Centrifugal force due to Earth\'s rotation causes water to bulge outward, affecting tides.\",\"Ocean water is used for fishing, transportation, mineral extraction, and energy generation.\",\"Biosphere includes lithosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere where living organisms exist.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What percentage of Earth\'s surface is covered by water?\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"60%\",\"71%\",\"80%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"About 71% of Earth\'s surface is covered by water, mainly oceans.\"},{\"q\":\"Which zone of the ocean floor lies between 200m and 3000m depth?\",\"options\":[\"Continental shelf\",\"Continental slope\",\"Abyssal plain\",\"Oceanic trench\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The continental slope extends from the shelf edge to the deep sea, with depths from 200m to 3000m.\"},{\"q\":\"What causes spring tides?\",\"options\":[\"Moon\'s gravity only\",\"Sun\'s gravity only\",\"Combined gravity of Moon and Sun\",\"Earth\'s rotation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Spring tides occur when the Sun, Moon, and Earth align, combining their gravitational forces.\"},{\"q\":\"During which lunar phase do neap tides occur?\",\"options\":[\"New moon\",\"Full moon\",\"First quarter moon\",\"Both new and full moon\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Neap tides occur during the first and third quarter moons when the Sun and Moon are at right angles.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the average depth of the oceans?\",\"options\":[\"200m\",\"2000m\",\"3730m\",\"11034m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The average depth of the oceans is about 3730 metres.\"},{\"q\":\"Which landform is found on the continental shelf and is important for fishing and oil?\",\"options\":[\"Abyssal plain\",\"Oceanic trench\",\"Continental slope\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The continental shelf itself is significant for fishing grounds and oil\\/gas extraction.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the periodic rise and fall of ocean water?\",\"options\":[\"Wave\",\"Current\",\"Tide\",\"Tsunami\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The definite and periodic rise and fall of ocean water level is called a tide.\"},{\"q\":\"Which force causes water to bulge outward due to Earth\'s rotation?\",\"options\":[\"Gravitational force\",\"Centripetal force\",\"Centrifugal force\",\"Magnetic force\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Centrifugal force, due to Earth\'s rotation, acts outward from the center and affects ocean water.\"},{\"q\":\"In which sphere do fish live?\",\"options\":[\"Lithosphere\",\"Hydrosphere\",\"Atmosphere\",\"Biosphere\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fish live in water, which is part of the hydrosphere.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the biosphere?\",\"options\":[\"Only land\",\"Only water\",\"Land, water, and air where life exists\",\"Only atmosphere\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The biosphere includes the lithosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere where living organisms are found.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22315,38,6,'Geography','en','Resources','[\"Resources are things from nature or made by humans that satisfy our needs.\",\"Natural resources are obtained directly from nature, e.g., air, water, soil, minerals.\",\"Man-made resources are created when humans modify natural resources, e.g., buildings from iron ore.\",\"Resources can have economic value (like metals) or no economic value (like scenery), but both are important.\",\"Humans are considered a resource because of their knowledge, skills, and ability to create more resources.\",\"Human resource development improves skills through education and health to create more resources.\",\"Biotic resources come from living things (plants, animals, humans); abiotic from non-living (air, water, land, minerals).\",\"About 71% of Earth\'s surface is water, but only a small fraction is fresh and usable for drinking.\",\"Water sources include surface water (rivers, lakes), groundwater (wells, tube wells), and rainwater.\",\"Water management is essential due to limited fresh water and growing demand from agriculture, industry, and domestic use.\",\"Subsistence farming is done to meet the farmer\'s family needs; commercial farming aims for profit and market sale.\",\"Intensive subsistence farming maximizes production on limited land using more labour.\",\"Shifting cultivation involves clearing forest land, burning vegetation, and moving after 2–3 years when soil loses fertility.\",\"Plantation farming is large-scale, single-crop farming (e.g., tea, rubber) needing significant capital and skilled labour.\",\"Horticulture involves growing fruits, vegetables, and flowers, often near urban areas, using traditional or modern methods.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a natural resource?\",\"options\":[\"Steel\",\"Plastic\",\"Soil\",\"Glass\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Soil is obtained directly from nature without processing, making it a natural resource.\"},{\"q\":\"What percentage of Earth\'s surface is covered by water?\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"61%\",\"71%\",\"81%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"About 71% of Earth\'s surface is covered by water, as stated in the textbook.\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of farming is mainly done to meet the livelihood needs of the farmer\'s family?\",\"options\":[\"Commercial farming\",\"Plantation farming\",\"Subsistence farming\",\"Horticulture\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Subsistence farming is practised to meet the livelihood needs of the farmer and his family.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a man-made resource?\",\"options\":[\"Air\",\"Water from a river\",\"Iron ore\",\"A bridge made of iron\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Iron ore is natural, but when used to make a bridge, it becomes a man-made resource after human modification.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main objective of commercial farming?\",\"options\":[\"To feed the farmer\'s family\",\"To earn profit\",\"To preserve traditional methods\",\"To use only manual labour\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Commercial farming is practised on a large scale with the primary objective of earning profit.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a source of surface water?\",\"options\":[\"Tube well\",\"Spring\",\"River\",\"Underground aquifer\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rivers and lakes are important natural sources of surface water.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the process of improving human skills through education and health called?\",\"options\":[\"Industrialization\",\"Urbanization\",\"Human resource development\",\"Mechanization\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Improving the quality of human skills through education and good health is called human resource development.\"},{\"q\":\"Which farming type involves cutting trees, burning vegetation, and moving to a new plot after 2–3 years?\",\"options\":[\"Intensive subsistence farming\",\"Shifting cultivation\",\"Plantation farming\",\"Horticulture\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shifting cultivation is an early form of agriculture where land is used for a short period before moving to a new patch.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of intensive subsistence farming?\",\"options\":[\"High use of human and animal labour\",\"Practised on the same land for many years\",\"Done to maximize production on limited land\",\"Involves long fallow periods\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Intensive subsistence farming involves continuous cultivation on the same land; long fallow periods are typical of shifting cultivation.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is water considered a vital resource?\",\"options\":[\"It is found only in oceans\",\"It is not needed for industry\",\"All living beings need it to survive\",\"It cannot be reused\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"All living beings need water to survive, making it essential for life and a vital resource.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22316,38,6,'Geography','en','Economic Activities','[\"Economic activities are actions done to earn a living and satisfy needs.\",\"They are divided into three types: primary, secondary, and tertiary.\",\"Primary activities depend directly on nature, e.g., farming, fishing, mining.\",\"Secondary activities involve processing raw materials into finished goods, e.g., sugar from sugarcane.\",\"Tertiary activities provide services like transport, communication, trade, and education.\",\"Tertiary activities are also known as the service sector.\",\"Primary activities are more common in rural areas; tertiary in urban and developed regions.\",\"A country\'s level of development is linked to the proportion of workforce in each sector.\",\"Higher share in tertiary activities indicates a developed economy.\",\"Processing increases the value, durability, and usefulness of products.\",\"Economic activities change over time due to technology, education, and infrastructure.\",\"Example: Sugarcane (primary) → sugar\\/jaggery (secondary) → selling in market (tertiary).\",\"Figure 9.1 shows classification of economic activities with examples.\",\"Figure 9.2 shows workforce distribution across sectors in different countries.\",\"Village Ajnale depends mainly on pomegranate farming and related activities.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a primary economic activity?\",\"options\":[\"Making sugar from sugarcane\",\"Fishing\",\"Serving in a bank\",\"Transporting goods by truck\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fishing directly depends on nature and involves catching fish from water bodies, making it a primary activity.\"},{\"q\":\"Which sector is also known as the service sector?\",\"options\":[\"Primary\",\"Secondary\",\"Tertiary\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tertiary activities provide services like transport, trade, and education, and are known as the service sector.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the value of a product when it undergoes processing in secondary activities?\",\"options\":[\"Decreases\",\"Stays the same\",\"Increases\",\"Becomes zero\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Processing improves durability, quality, and usefulness, which increases the product\'s value.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of a tertiary activity?\",\"options\":[\"Mining coal\",\"Weaving cloth\",\"Teaching in a school\",\"Growing wheat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Teaching is a service provided to students and does not produce goods, so it is a tertiary activity.\"},{\"q\":\"In which type of area are primary economic activities most commonly found?\",\"options\":[\"Urban cities\",\"Industrial zones\",\"Rural villages\",\"Coastal ports only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Primary activities like farming and animal husbandry depend on land and nature, so they are more common in rural areas.\"},{\"q\":\"What does Figure 9.2 in the textbook show?\",\"options\":[\"Types of crops grown\",\"Percentage of workforce in different economic activities\",\"Number of factories in each state\",\"Average income per person\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Figure 9.2 displays the percentage distribution of workforce across primary, secondary, and tertiary sectors in different countries.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement about India\'s economy is correct based on the textbook?\",\"options\":[\"India is a developed country\",\"India is a developing country\",\"India has no secondary activities\",\"India\'s workforce is mostly in tertiary sector\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The textbook states that India is considered a developing country due to the dominance of primary and secondary activities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a secondary activity?\",\"options\":[\"Making jaggery from sugarcane\",\"Turning milk into cheese\",\"Growing cotton in a field\",\"Weaving cloth from yarn\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Growing cotton is a farming activity that depends directly on nature, so it is primary, not secondary.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do processed sugar and sugarcane juice have different prices even if from the same weight?\",\"options\":[\"Sugar is heavier\",\"Juice is tastier\",\"Processing increases value and utility\",\"Sugarcane is rare\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Processing sugarcane into sugar makes it more durable, usable, and valuable, hence higher price.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity connects and supports primary and secondary activities?\",\"options\":[\"Ploughing a field\",\"Extracting minerals\",\"Providing transport and communication\",\"Harvesting crops\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Transport and communication are services that help move goods and information, supporting other sectors.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22317,38,6,'Geography','en','Human Settlements','[\"Human settlements are places where people live together.\",\"Primitive humans lived a nomadic life, moving in search of food and shelter.\",\"Settled life began with agriculture, leading to permanent homes.\",\"Villages have fewer houses, low population, and agriculture as the main occupation.\",\"Cities have more houses, high population, and diverse occupations like industry and services.\",\"Rural settlements are closely linked to natural resources like rivers, forests, and fertile soil.\",\"Urban settlements grow due to industries, education, healthcare, and transport facilities.\",\"Factors affecting settlement location include water, climate, soil, topography (physical).\",\"Social and cultural factors like healthcare, education, tourism, and religious places attract people.\",\"Economic factors such as trade, industries, and transport promote settlement growth.\",\"Other factors include security, government policies, and protection from natural disasters.\",\"Rural areas transform into urban areas as employment opportunities increase.\",\"Migration from rural to urban areas happens due to better jobs and facilities.\",\"Satellite images (like Google Earth Pro) help study changes in settlements over time.\",\"Sustainable use of natural resources like water, air, land, and forests is essential for future generations.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was the main reason for primitive humans to live a nomadic life?\",\"options\":[\"To attend festivals\",\"To search for food, cloth, and shelter\",\"To build permanent houses\",\"To develop trade\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Primitive humans moved from place to place to find basic necessities like food, clothing, and shelter.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity led to the beginning of settled human life?\",\"options\":[\"Trading in markets\",\"Building roads\",\"Practicing agriculture\",\"Constructing temples\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Humans started settling down when they began practicing agriculture and needed to stay near fertile land.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main occupation in rural settlements?\",\"options\":[\"Industry\",\"Agriculture\",\"Tourism\",\"Education\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In villages, most people are engaged in farming and related activities like animal husbandry.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a physical factor affecting the location of human settlements?\",\"options\":[\"Availability of schools\",\"Presence of industries\",\"Availability of water bodies\",\"Government policies\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Water bodies like rivers and lakes are physical factors that influence where people settle.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do settlements grow in places with good healthcare facilities?\",\"options\":[\"Because hospitals build houses\",\"Because people migrate for better health services\",\"Because doctors need villages\",\"Because medicine is sold there\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Good healthcare attracts people, leading to population growth and settlement development.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to rural settlements when employment opportunities increase?\",\"options\":[\"They become forests\",\"They transform into urban settlements\",\"They lose their population\",\"They stop farming\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"As more jobs become available, people move in, and rural areas develop into towns and cities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT responsible for the growth of urban settlements?\",\"options\":[\"Industries\",\"Transport facilities\",\"Farming as main occupation\",\"Educational institutions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Urban settlements grow due to industry, transport, and education; farming is dominant in rural areas.\"},{\"q\":\"What does topography refer to in the context of settlement development?\",\"options\":[\"Type of soil\",\"Shape and nature of the land\",\"Availability of water\",\"Climate conditions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Topography means the physical features of land like plains, hills, or mountains, which affect how settlements develop.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an inexhaustible resource?\",\"options\":[\"Coal\",\"Petroleum\",\"Sunlight\",\"Natural gas\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sunlight is naturally renewed and will not get depleted with use, making it inexhaustible.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are settlements in hilly regions often scattered?\",\"options\":[\"Because of heavy rainfall\",\"Because it is difficult to build transport routes and houses\",\"Because of lack of sunlight\",\"Because of too much vegetation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In mountainous areas, constructing roads and homes is difficult, so settlements are spread out.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22318,38,6,'Geography','en','Sustainable Development','[\"Sustainable development means meeting today\'s needs without harming future generations\' ability to meet theirs.\",\"It balances economic growth, social equality, and environmental protection.\",\"Natural resources like sun, land, air, water, trees, and soil are essential for life.\",\"Indian festivals are linked to nature, seasons, and agricultural cycles.\",\"Festivals promote conservation of natural elements through rituals and traditions.\",\"Examples: Makar Sankranti (sun), Vat Pournima (banyan tree), Pola (bullocks), Ghatasthapana (soil and air).\",\"Festivals include folk songs, dances, and literature that spread awareness about nature.\",\"Conservation means using resources wisely to avoid exhaustion.\",\"Inexhaustible resources: solar energy, wind energy (available continuously).\",\"Exhaustible resources: coal, oil, minerals (limited and can run out).\",\"Water conservation: closing taps, rainwater harvesting, avoiding wastage.\",\"Air conservation: planting trees, reducing smoke, using clean energy.\",\"Soil conservation: making compost, avoiding overuse, crop rotation.\",\"Sustainable energy sources: solar, wind, hydro, biogas.\",\"Human activities like pollution, deforestation, and waste harm the environment.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main idea of sustainable development?\",\"options\":[\"Only economic growth\",\"Meeting present needs without compromising future needs\",\"Increasing industrial production\",\"Using all resources quickly\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sustainable development balances present and future needs.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an inexhaustible resource?\",\"options\":[\"Coal\",\"Petroleum\",\"Solar energy\",\"Minerals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Solar energy is continuously available from the sun.\"},{\"q\":\"Which festival is associated with the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Vat Pournima\",\"Pola\",\"Makar Sankranti\",\"Ghatasthapana\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Makar Sankranti is linked to the Sun\'s movement into Capricorn.\"},{\"q\":\"What does water conservation include?\",\"options\":[\"Leaving taps open\",\"Planting trees\",\"Closing taps when not in use\",\"Using more water for cleaning\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Closing taps saves water and prevents wastage.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity helps in air conservation?\",\"options\":[\"Cutting trees\",\"Burning plastic\",\"Planting trees\",\"Using more vehicles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Trees absorb carbon dioxide and release oxygen, improving air quality.\"},{\"q\":\"What is compost used for?\",\"options\":[\"Cleaning air\",\"Conserving water\",\"Improving soil fertility\",\"Generating electricity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Compost enriches soil with nutrients, helping plants grow.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an exhaustible resource?\",\"options\":[\"Wind energy\",\"Sunlight\",\"Natural gas\",\"Hydro energy\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Natural gas is a fossil fuel and limited in supply.\"},{\"q\":\"How do Indian festivals help sustainable development?\",\"options\":[\"By increasing pollution\",\"By promoting conservation of natural elements\",\"By using more plastic\",\"By ignoring nature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Festivals are tied to nature and encourage protection of sun, land, air, water, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of making a poster on sustainable development?\",\"options\":[\"To decorate the classroom\",\"To spread awareness about saving resources\",\"To compete in sports\",\"To learn dancing\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Posters help educate others about protecting the environment.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a natural element mentioned in the chapter?\",\"options\":[\"Sun\",\"Land\",\"Electricity\",\"Water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Electricity is a form of energy, not a natural element like sun, land, or water.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22319,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Sources of History','[\"- History is a story of the past, built from evidence.\",\"- Sources of history are the records that give us this evidence.\",\"- Primary sources are original records from the time – e.g. coins, inscriptions, diaries.\",\"- Secondary sources are later works that analyse primary sources – e.g. textbooks, research papers.\",\"- Oral sources are stories, songs, myths passed by word of mouth – e.g. folk tales, oral histories.\",\"- Material sources are physical objects – e.g. pottery, weapons, buildings, fossils.\",\"- Written sources are documents written down – e.g. letters, legal codes, chronicles.\",\"- The Vedas are the oldest written records of India, originally transmitted orally.\",\"- The Stone Age is called the Prehistoric Period because there was no writing; we learn from material evidence.\",\"- Museums and heritage sites preserve material and written sources for study.\",\"- By comparing different types of sources, historians can reconstruct events, cultures and societies.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a primary source?\",\"options\":[\"A history textbook\",\"A diary written by a soldier\",\"A research article\",\"A history documentary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A diary written by a soldier is an original record from the time.\"},{\"q\":\"Oral sources are best described as:\",\"options\":[\"Written records from ancient kings\",\"Physical objects like pottery\",\"Stories passed by word of mouth\",\"Maps of ancient trade routes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Oral sources are passed orally, not written or material.\"},{\"q\":\"The Vedas were originally transmitted how?\",\"options\":[\"In written manuscripts\",\"By oral recitation\",\"By pictorial symbols\",\"Through stone carvings\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Vedas were first memorised and recited orally.\"},{\"q\":\"Which period is known as the Prehistoric Period?\",\"options\":[\"Stone Age\",\"Vedic Age\",\"Mauryan Age\",\"Shunga Age\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Prehistoric Period is the Stone Age, before writing.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a material source?\",\"options\":[\"A poem about kings\",\"A copper coin\",\"A travelogue\",\"A legal decree\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A copper coin is a physical object from the past.\"},{\"q\":\"Museums mainly preserve which type of sources?\",\"options\":[\"Oral sources\",\"Written sources\",\"Material sources\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Museums keep material, written and sometimes oral records.\"},{\"q\":\"The term \'Tipitaka\' refers to:\",\"options\":[\"A set of Vedic hymns\",\"A collection of Buddhist texts\",\"A group of ancient laws\",\"A set of Hindu myths\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tipitaka is the Buddhist canon of scriptures.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a primary source?\",\"options\":[\"An inscription on a stone pillar\",\"A modern history book\",\"A clay pot from a dig\",\"A royal decree from the 5th century\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A modern history book is a secondary source.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of oral sources?\",\"options\":[\"To record legal laws\",\"To preserve myths and traditions\",\"To catalogue archaeological finds\",\"To write scientific treatises\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oral sources preserve myths, traditions and folklore.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a written source?\",\"options\":[\"A folk song\",\"A stone carving\",\"A diary entry\",\"A pottery shard\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A diary entry is a written record.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22320,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Prehistoric Period','[\"- The Prehistoric Period is the time before writing was invented. It is divided into three main ages: Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Neolithic.\",\"- Palaeolithic (Old Stone Age) is when early humans used simple stone tools like choppers and made fire. They lived in caves or on the ground and were nomadic.\",\"- Mesolithic (Middle Stone Age) saw the use of smaller, sharper tools, the first domestication of dogs, and the beginning of seasonal camps. Fire was still used for cooking and warmth.\",\"- Neolithic (New Stone Age) introduced polished stone tools such as axes, pottery, weaving, and the first agriculture. People began to settle in villages.\",\"- The Chalcolithic Age (Copper‑Stone Age) is named because people started using copper tools while still using stone tools. The wheel was invented, trade expanded, and early towns began to form.\",\"- In Maharashtra, important Chalcolithic sites are on the banks of the Tapi, Godavari, Krishna, Bhima and Pravara rivers. Excavations have found pottery, copper bangles, bronze objects and evidence of farming.\",\"- The Jorwe culture (in Ahilyanagar) is a famous Chalcolithic culture with black‑painted pottery and evidence of grain storage.\",\"- The Neolithic period brought the first stable agriculture, animal domestication and the use of pottery for storage.\",\"- Fire was discovered in the Mesolithic period and changed human life by allowing cooking, warmth and protection.\",\"- The invention of the wheel in the Chalcolithic Age made transport faster and helped trade.\",\"- The discovery of copper tools in the Chalcolithic Age shows the beginning of metal working.\",\"- The first evidence of writing in India appears after the Prehistoric Period, in the Vedic period.\",\"- The transition from nomadic to settled life is called the Neolithic Revolution.\",\"- In the Neolithic Age, people began to store food in jars and baskets, which helped them survive winter.\",\"- The Chalcolithic Age is also called the Copper‑Stone Age because both metals were used.\",\"- The Jorwe culture had circular or rectangular houses and used earthen storage bins for grain.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which age is known as the Old Stone Age?\",\"options\":[\"Palaeolithic\",\"Mesolithic\",\"Neolithic\",\"Chalcolithic\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Palaeolithic Age is the earliest period of the Stone Age.\"},{\"q\":\"What tool was first used in the Neolithic Age?\",\"options\":[\"Chopper\",\"Axe\",\"Bow and arrow\",\"Spear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Polished stone axes were first made in the Neolithic Age.\"},{\"q\":\"Which river is NOT mentioned as a site for Chalcolithic culture in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Tapi\",\"Godavari\",\"Ganga\",\"Krishna\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Ganga is not listed among the Maharashtra river banks with Chalcolithic sites.\"},{\"q\":\"The invention of which device helped early traders travel faster?\",\"options\":[\"Wheel\",\"Spear\",\"Bow\",\"Chopper\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The wheel was invented during the Chalcolithic Age.\"},{\"q\":\"Which animal was the first to be domesticated by humans?\",\"options\":[\"Dog\",\"Cow\",\"Sheep\",\"Goat\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Dogs were the first animals domesticated in the Palaeolithic Age.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between Mesolithic and Neolithic?\",\"options\":[\"Use of fire\",\"Use of pottery\",\"Use of metal tools\",\"Use of polished stone tools\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Neolithic introduced polished stone tools like axes.\"},{\"q\":\"The Jorwe culture is known for which type of pottery?\",\"options\":[\"Red painted\",\"Blue painted\",\"Black painted on red\",\"White painted\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Jorwe pottery is famous for black paint on a red surface.\"},{\"q\":\"Which age saw the first use of fire by humans?\",\"options\":[\"Palaeolithic\",\"Mesolithic\",\"Neolithic\",\"Chalcolithic\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Fire was discovered in the Mesolithic Age.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the transition from nomadic to settled life?\",\"options\":[\"Agricultural Revolution\",\"Neolithic Revolution\",\"Palaeolithic Revolution\",\"Chalcolithic Revolution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Neolithic Revolution marks the shift to settled agriculture.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a feature of the Chalcolithic Age?\",\"options\":[\"Use of copper tools\",\"Use of stone tools\",\"Invention of the wheel\",\"Use of iron tools\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Iron tools appeared later, not in the Chalcolithic Age.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22321,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Harappan Civilization','[\"- The Harappan civilisation is also called the Indus Valley civilisation.\",\"- It existed from about 3300 BCE to 1300 BCE, divided into Early (3300‑2600 BCE), Mature (2600‑1900 BCE) and Late (1900‑1300 BCE) phases.\",\"- Main cities: Harappa, Mohenjo‑Daro, Dholavira, Lothal, Rakhigarhi, Kalibangan, Banawali, etc.\",\"- It was an urban civilisation with planned streets, drainage, baked‑brick houses and a grid layout.\",\"- People grew wheat, barley, peas, sesame, ragi and made cotton cloth; they also ate milk, meat and fish in some areas.\",\"- Trade was wide: local goods (beads, pottery, cotton) were traded with Mesopotamia; imported metals, precious stones and faience.\",\"- Harappan seals had animal and human figures and were used for trade and administration.\",\"- The Great Bath at Mohenjo‑Daro is a famous public bath showing advanced water management.\",\"- Decline factors: river course changes, water scarcity, floods, droughts, earthquakes and a slowdown in trade with Mesopotamia.\",\"- Harappan society was organised, with separate citadel and residential areas, and had a system of public health and sanitation.\",\"- Artifacts include pottery with painted motifs, bronze figurines, beads of carnelian, lapis lazuli, and toys made of clay.\",\"- The civilisation had a sophisticated system of writing (still undeciphered) and used standardized bricks (ratio 1:2:4).\",\"- The Harappan civilisation extended from the north of Pakistan to the south of Gujarat, covering about 1.5 million km².\",\"- The Harappan people were skilled in metallurgy, pottery, weaving, and shipbuilding (Lothal dockyard).\",\"- The Harappan diet was mainly cereals; dairy, meat and fish supplemented the diet.\",\"- The Harappan civilisation had a rich culture of ornaments: necklaces, bangles, earrings, and bracelets made of clay, gold, silver and shells.\",\"- The Harappan people used baked‑earth conduits for drainage and had covered street drains.\",\"- The Harappan civilisation was one of the earliest urban societies in the world, showing advanced urban planning, trade, and social organisation.\"]','[{\"q\":\"The Harappan civilisation is also known as the _______ civilisation.\",\"options\":[\"Indus Valley\",\"Nile\",\"Tigris\",\"Ganges\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It is called the Indus Valley civilisation because it developed along the Indus River.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following cities is NOT a major Harappan site?\",\"options\":[\"Mohenjo‑Daro\",\"Lothal\",\"Delhi\",\"Harappa\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Delhi was not a Harappan city; the others were major sites.\"},{\"q\":\"The Great Bath was found at which site?\",\"options\":[\"Mohenjo‑Daro\",\"Lothal\",\"Dholavira\",\"Kalibangan\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Great Bath is a famous structure at Mohenjo‑Daro.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main crop grown by Harappan farmers?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Wheat\",\"Maize\",\"Sorghum\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wheat and barley were the principal crops of the Harappans.\"},{\"q\":\"Which river was the Harappan civilisation centred on?\",\"options\":[\"Ganges\",\"Yamuna\",\"Indus\",\"Godavari\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The civilisation developed along the Indus River.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these items was NOT traded by the Harappans?\",\"options\":[\"Beads\",\"Pottery\",\"Cotton\",\"Gold bullion\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Gold bullion was not a major item traded by the Harappans.\"},{\"q\":\"The decline of the Harappan civilisation was likely caused by:\",\"options\":[\"Invasion by Indo‑Aryans\",\"River course change\",\"Discovery of iron\",\"Population growth\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A change in the river’s course caused water scarcity and decline.\"},{\"q\":\"The Harappan houses were built using what material?\",\"options\":[\"Stone\",\"Wood\",\"Baked bricks\",\"Concrete\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"They were built with baked bricks in a grid pattern.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a Harappan artefact?\",\"options\":[\"Bronze figurine of a dancing girl\",\"Stone statue of a king\",\"Iron sword\",\"Gold crown\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The bronze figurine known as the Dancing Girl is a Harappan artefact.\"},{\"q\":\"The Harappan civilisation had a system of writing that is:\",\"options\":[\"Fully deciphered\",\"Partially deciphered\",\"Undeciphered\",\"Not a writing system\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Indus script remains undeciphered.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22322,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Vedic Culture','[\"Vedas are the oldest sacred texts of India, written in Sanskrit.\",\"There are four Vedas: Rigveda, Yajurveda, Samaveda, Atharvaveda.\",\"Rigveda contains hymns (suktas) praising deities like Agni, Indra, Varuna.\",\"Yajurveda deals mainly with rituals and instructions for sacrifices (yajnas).\",\"Samaveda focuses on musical chants and melodies used in rituals.\",\"Atharvaveda includes hymns, spells, and practical knowledge about health and daily life.\",\"The word ‘Veda’ comes from the Sanskrit root ‘vid’ meaning ‘to know’.\",\"Vedic literature is divided into four layers: Samhita (hymns), Brahmana (ritual instructions), Aranyaka (philosophical discourses), and Upanishad (spiritual teachings).\",\"Vedic society was organized into four varnas: Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra.\",\"The Vedic period is split into Early (Rigvedic) and Post (Atharvaveda) phases, roughly 1500–600 BCE.\",\"Education began with Guru–Shishya (teacher–student) system, progressing through four ashrams: Brahmacharya, Grihastha, Vanaprastha, Sanyasa.\",\"Women in early Vedic times had rights to study Vedas and participate in rituals, but later became more restricted.\",\"Music (swaras) and instruments like the flute (bansuri) were integral to Vedic rituals.\",\"Agriculture (wheat, barley, rice) and animal husbandry (cows, oxen, horses) were the main economic activities.\",\"Trade and crafts (metalworking, pottery, weaving) emerged during the post‑Vedic period.\",\"The king’s authority was supported by divine counsel from celestial bodies (Agni, Varuna, Surya, Soma).\",\"The Vedic concept of Dharma emphasized truth, justice, and duty for all social roles.\",\"Vedic mathematics includes simple mental tricks for quick calculations, such as the Nikhilam rule.\",\"The Vedic worldview saw the universe (Brahman) as the ultimate reality, with the soul (Atman) as its part.\",\"Vedic hymns were composed by sages (rishis) and often attributed to both men and women.\",\"The Vedic period laid foundations for later Indian philosophy, religion, and culture.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which Veda contains hymns praising deities like Agni and Indra?\",\"options\":[\"Rigveda\",\"Yajurveda\",\"Samaveda\",\"Atharvaveda\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Rigveda is the oldest Veda and contains hymns (suktas) dedicated to deities.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main focus of the Samaveda?\",\"options\":[\"Ritual instructions\",\"Musical chants\",\"Spiritual teachings\",\"Health remedies\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Samaveda deals with musical melodies used in Vedic rituals.\"},{\"q\":\"The Vedic concept of Dharma primarily means:\",\"options\":[\"Wealth accumulation\",\"Truth and duty\",\"Military conquest\",\"Scientific research\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dharma refers to truth, justice, and duty in Vedic society.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the four Vedas?\",\"options\":[\"Rigveda\",\"Yajurveda\",\"Upanishad\",\"Atharvaveda\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Upanishad is a part of Vedic literature but not a Veda.\"},{\"q\":\"In the Vedic education system, the stage where a person renounces worldly life is called:\",\"options\":[\"Brahmacharya\",\"Grihastha\",\"Vanaprastha\",\"Sanyasa\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Sanyasa is the final ashram where one renounces worldly duties.\"},{\"q\":\"Which celestial body was considered the most powerful in Vedic cosmology?\",\"options\":[\"Agni\",\"Varuna\",\"Surya\",\"Soma\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Agni (fire) was regarded as the most powerful deity in Vedic thought.\"},{\"q\":\"The Vedic period is generally dated between which years?\",\"options\":[\"2000–1500 BCE\",\"1500–600 BCE\",\"600–200 BCE\",\"200–200 CE\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Early and Post Vedic periods span roughly 1500–600 BCE.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Veda is associated with health and home remedies?\",\"options\":[\"Rigveda\",\"Yajurveda\",\"Samaveda\",\"Atharvaveda\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Atharvaveda contains hymns and spells for health and daily life.\"},{\"q\":\"The Vedic system of social order is known as:\",\"options\":[\"Varna system\",\"Caste system\",\"Kshatriya system\",\"Shreni system\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Varna system divides society into Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra.\"},{\"q\":\"Which instrument was widely used in Vedic rituals?\",\"options\":[\"Harp\",\"Bansuri\",\"Violin\",\"Guitar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The bansuri (flute) was commonly used in Vedic ceremonies.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22323,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Various Religions','[\"- Vedic religion is the oldest religion in India, based on the Vedas, Upanishads, Puranas and Dharmashastras.\",\"- The Vedas are four collections: Rigveda, Yajurveda, Samaveda and Atharvaveda.\",\"- Vedic rituals (Yajnas) were performed by priests (Purohit) and were central to worship.\",\"- Key Vedic concepts: Dharma (righteous duty), Karma (action and its results) and Moksha (liberation).\",\"- The Bhagavad Gita, a part of the Mahabharata, explains how to live according to Dharma and attain Moksha.\",\"- Jainism teaches Ahimsa (non‑violence), Satya (truth), Asteya (non‑stealing), Aparigraha (non‑attachment) and Brahmacharya (chastity).\",\"- Jainism has six major schools (Shaddarshanas) and 24 Tirthankaras; Mahavira is the last and most famous.\",\"- Buddhism was founded by Siddhartha Gautama (Buddha) who discovered the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path.\",\"- The Four Noble Truths: Dukkha (suffering), its cause, its cessation and the path to its cessation.\",\"- The Eightfold Path includes Right View, Right Resolve, Right Speech, Right Conduct, Right Livelihood, Right Effort, Right Mindfulness and Right Concentration.\",\"- Judaism, Christianity, Islam and Zoroastrianism are later religious traditions that arrived in India between the 1st and 7th centuries CE.\",\"- All major religions in India emphasize values such as non‑violence, truth, compassion, justice and tolerance.\",\"- The prayer houses: Synagogue (Judaism), Church (Christianity) and Mosque (Islam).\",\"- The sacred texts: Torah (Judaism), Bible (Christianity), Quran (Islam) and Avesta (Zoroastrianism).\",\"- Indian culture is known for its inclusiveness, which helps maintain unity in diversity.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which Veda is mainly a collection of hymns for the gods?\",\"options\":[\"Rigveda\",\"Yajurveda\",\"Samaveda\",\"Atharvaveda\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Rigveda contains hymns (suktas) addressed to deities.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main principle of Jainism?\",\"options\":[\"Ahimsa\",\"Karma\",\"Moksha\",\"Dharma\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ahimsa (non‑violence) is the core of Jain teaching.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the founder of Buddhism?\",\"options\":[\"Mahavira\",\"Gautama Buddha\",\"Rama\",\"Krishna\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Siddhartha Gautama, known as Buddha, founded Buddhism.\"},{\"q\":\"The Bhagavad Gita is part of which epic?\",\"options\":[\"Mahabharata\",\"Ramayana\",\"Vishnu Purana\",\"Mahapuranas\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It is a dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna in the Mahabharata.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a Vedic school of thought?\",\"options\":[\"Mimamsa\",\"Vedanta\",\"Buddhism\",\"Samkhya\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Buddhism is a separate tradition, not a Vedic school.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Dharma\' mean in Vedic culture?\",\"options\":[\"Righteous duty\",\"Wealth\",\"Power\",\"Sacrifice\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Dharma refers to righteous duty and moral order.\"},{\"q\":\"Which religion uses the Avesta as its sacred text?\",\"options\":[\"Zoroastrianism\",\"Judaism\",\"Christianity\",\"Islam\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Avesta is the holy book of Zoroastrianism.\"},{\"q\":\"The Eightfold Path is a teaching of which religion?\",\"options\":[\"Jainism\",\"Buddhism\",\"Christianity\",\"Islam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It is central to Buddhist practice.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a prayer house for Muslims?\",\"options\":[\"Synagogue\",\"Mosque\",\"Church\",\"Gurdwara\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A mosque is the Muslim place of worship.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main message of the Four Noble Truths?\",\"options\":[\"All suffering can be ended\",\"Suffering is inevitable\",\"Suffering is caused by ignorance\",\"Suffering is caused by karma\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"They state that suffering exists, has a cause, can cease, and the path to its cessation.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22324,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Janapadas and Mahajanapadas','[\"Janapada is a community of villages that live together in a specific area.\",\"A Janapada had a chieftain and an army, and decisions were made by the elders.\",\"Janapadas grew stronger over time and became Mahajanapadas, which were larger kingdoms.\",\"Mahajanapadas had a king and a council of advisors; some were republics called Ganarajya.\",\"In a Ganarajya the king was not absolute; the council of elders made decisions.\",\"The voting in Mahajanapadas used open vote, secret vote, or a coloured stick called Shalaka.\",\"The Shalaka was a ballot used by four Mahajanapadas: Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa and Avanti.\",\"Magadha was the first empire; it had fertile land, iron deposits, and a good trade network.\",\"King Bimbisara founded Pataliputra as the capital; Ajatshatru expanded the kingdom further.\",\"The Nanda and Maurya dynasties followed Magadha and built a powerful empire.\",\"The first Buddhist Council was held at Rajagriha during Ajatshatru’s reign.\",\"A capital city is important for administration, trade and military control.\",\"The ideal capital is near water, fertile land, and has good roads.\",\"The transition from Janapadas to Empires shows how small villages grew into large states.\",\"The Mahajanapadas had 16 kingdoms, each with its own capital city.\",\"The voting system in Mahajanapadas allowed people to participate in decision making.\",\"The Ganarajya system was a form of democracy with a council of elders.\",\"Magadha’s military innovations included the Mahashilakantaka stone‑throwing machine.\",\"The Nanda kings were known for wealth, taxation and military power.\",\"The Mauryan Empire was founded by Chandragupta after the Nanda regime.\",\"The Mahajanapadas were the first stage before the rise of empires in ancient India.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a Janapada?\",\"options\":[\"A group of villages forming a community\",\"A single village\",\"A king’s palace\",\"A trade route\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"A Janapada is a community of villages that form a local administrative region.\"},{\"q\":\"Which term describes a republic in ancient India?\",\"options\":[\"Ganarajya\",\"Mahajanapada\",\"Janapada\",\"Gana Parishad\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ganarajya means a republic where the king is assisted by a council.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Mahajanapada had its capital at Pataliputra?\",\"options\":[\"Magadha\",\"Kosala\",\"Vatsa\",\"Avanti\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Magadha’s capital was Pataliputra, now Patna.\"},{\"q\":\"Who introduced the voting method using coloured sticks called Shalaka?\",\"options\":[\"Bimbisara\",\"Ajatshatru\",\"Nanda\",\"Chanakya\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ajatshatru used Shalaka for voting in the Gana Parishad.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Mahajanapada was known for the city of Takshashila?\",\"options\":[\"Gandhara\",\"Kamboja\",\"Avanti\",\"Kuru\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Takshashila was in Gandhara.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason Magadha became powerful?\",\"options\":[\"Its fertile land and iron deposits\",\"Its large navy\",\"Its isolation\",\"Its small size\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Magadha’s fertile plains and iron resources helped its growth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which king expanded Magadha by conquering Vrijji and Kosala?\",\"options\":[\"Bimbisara\",\"Ajatshatru\",\"Shishunaga\",\"Nanda\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ajatshatru conquered Vrijji and Kosala.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Mahajanapada\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Great kingdom\",\"Small village\",\"Republic\",\"Trade route\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mahajanapada means a great kingdom.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a method of voting in the Gana Parishad?\",\"options\":[\"Open vote\",\"Secret vote\",\"Shalaka vote\",\"Written ballot\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Voting used open, secret, and Shalaka methods, not written ballots.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the first king to establish the capital at Rajagriha?\",\"options\":[\"Bimbisara\",\"Ajatshatru\",\"Shishunaga\",\"Nanda\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bimbisara founded the capital at Rajagriha.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22325,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Mauryan India','[\"The Mauryan Empire was founded by Chandragupta Maurya in 321 BCE after defeating the Nanda dynasty.\",\"Chandragupta was guided by his mentor Chanakya (also known as Kautilya or Vishnugupta), who wrote the Arthashastra.\",\"The Arthashastra is a treatise on statecraft, covering governance, taxation, defense, intelligence, and politics.\",\"Chandragupta expanded the empire to include Avanti, Saurashtra, Kabul, Kandahar, and Herat.\",\"Megasthenes, the Greek ambassador to Chandragupta\'s court, wrote \'Indica\', an important source on Mauryan India.\",\"Bindusara, son of Chandragupta, continued the empire\'s expansion and maintained friendly relations with Indo-Greek rulers.\",\"Ashoka, son of Bindusara, became emperor after his father\'s death and ruled a vast empire from Afghanistan to Karnataka.\",\"Ashoka\'s early reign was marked by military conquest, especially the Kalinga War, which caused massive loss of life.\",\"The Kalinga War deeply affected Ashoka, leading him to embrace Buddhism and adopt a policy of Dhamma (righteousness).\",\"Ashoka promoted non-violence, compassion, religious tolerance, and respect for elders through his edicts.\",\"Ashoka\'s messages were inscribed on rocks and pillars in Brahmi script, where he referred to himself as \'Devanam Piyo Piyadasi\'.\",\"He sent missionaries, including his son Mahendra and daughter Sanghamitra, to spread Buddhism to Sri Lanka and other regions.\",\"Ashoka established hospitals, veterinary clinics, wells, rest houses, and planted trees along roads for public welfare.\",\"He appointed Dhamma-Mahamattas to propagate his moral teachings across the empire.\",\"The Mauryan administrative system had three levels: central (Pataliputra), provincial (governed by princes and Mahamatyas), and local (village heads and city committees).\",\"The empire was divided into four provinces: Tosali (east), Ujjayini (west), Takshashila (north), and Suvarnagiri (south).\",\"Revenue from taxation funded public welfare, defense, and religious activities.\",\"The Sudarshan Lake in Junagadh (Gujarat) was built during Chandragupta\'s reign and repaired later by Rudradaman, showing advanced water management.\",\"Mauryan art and architecture featured polished stone pillars with animal capitals (lions, bulls), stupas (Sanchi, Sarnath), and rock-cut caves (Barabar).\",\"After Ashoka, the empire weakened, and the last Mauryan king, Brihadratha, was defeated by Pushyamitra Shunga, who founded the Shunga dynasty.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who founded the Mauryan Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Ashoka\",\"Chandragupta Maurya\",\"Bindusara\",\"Chanaky\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Chandragupta Maurya founded the Mauryan Empire in 321 BCE after defeating the Nanda dynasty.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of \'Devanam Piyo Piyadasi\' as used in Ashoka\'s inscriptions?\",\"options\":[\"Beloved of the Gods and he who looks on with affection\",\"Great warrior of the east\",\"Son of the sun god\",\"Defeater of enemies\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ashoka referred to himself as \'Devanam Piyo Piyadasi\', meaning \'Beloved of the Gods and he who looks on with affection\'.\"},{\"q\":\"Which war led to Ashoka\'s transformation and adoption of Buddhism?\",\"options\":[\"Battle of Hydaspes\",\"Kalinga War\",\"War against Seleucus\",\"Conflict with the Nandas\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Kalinga War caused massive loss of life, which deeply moved Ashoka and led him to embrace Buddhism and follow Dhamma.\"},{\"q\":\"Who wrote the Arthashastra?\",\"options\":[\"Megasthenes\",\"Vishakhadatta\",\"Chanakya\",\"Ashoka\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Chanakya, also known as Kautilya or Vishnugupta, authored the Arthashastra, a treatise on statecraft and governance.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a part of Ashoka\'s empire?\",\"options\":[\"Afghanistan\",\"Karnataka\",\"Sri Lanka\",\"Andhra Pradesh\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Although Ashoka sent missionaries to Sri Lanka, it was not part of his political empire, which extended from Afghanistan to Andhra Pradesh.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the capital of the Mauryan Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Ujjain\",\"Taxila\",\"Pataliputra\",\"Junagadh\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Pataliputra (modern-day Patna) was the capital of the Mauryan Empire where the emperor held court.\"},{\"q\":\"Which lake, built during Chandragupta\'s time, is an example of ancient water management?\",\"options\":[\"Dal Lake\",\"Wular Lake\",\"Sudarshan Lake\",\"Loktak Lake\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Sudarshan Lake was constructed at Junagadh during Chandragupta\'s reign to support agriculture and drinking water supply.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the Greek ambassador to Chandragupta\'s court?\",\"options\":[\"Seleucus\",\"Megasthenes\",\"Alexander\",\"Rudradaman\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Megasthenes, the ambassador of Seleucus Nicator, served in Chandragupta\'s court and wrote \'Indica\'.\"},{\"q\":\"Which dynasty succeeded the Mauryan Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Gupta\",\"Shunga\",\"Kanva\",\"Satavahana\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"After the fall of the Mauryan Empire, Pushyamitra Shunga defeated Brihadratha and established the Shunga dynasty.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Ashoka appoint to spread his message of Dhamma?\",\"options\":[\"Tax collectors\",\"Dhamma-Mahamattas\",\"Military generals\",\"Foreign diplomats\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ashoka appointed Dhamma-Mahamattas to propagate his teachings of morality, non-violence, and religious tolerance.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22326,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Ancient Indian and Maharashtrian Kingdoms and Dynasties','[\"After Emperor Ashoka, the Maurya Empire declined due to weak successors.\",\"Pushyamitra Shunga, a Mauryan commander, founded the Shunga dynasty.\",\"The Shunga dynasty promoted Vedic religion and performed Ashwamedha sacrifice.\",\"The Kanva dynasty succeeded the Shungas; its founder was Vasudeva Kanva.\",\"Indo-Greek (Yavana) rulers settled in northwestern India after Alexander\'s invasion.\",\"Menander (Milinda) was a famous Indo-Greek king known for the Milindapanha dialogue.\",\"Shaka rulers were Scythian tribes from Shakastan (Iran\\/Afghanistan) who ruled western India.\",\"Rudradaman-I was a famous Shaka ruler whose Junagadh inscription details his achievements.\",\"The Satavahans were the first powerful empire in Maharashtra, founded by King Simuka.\",\"Gautamiputra Satakarni defeated Shakas and Yavanas and expanded the Satavahan empire.\",\"Satavahans promoted trade, Buddhism, and excavated caves like Nashik, Karle, and Kanheri.\",\"Kushanas were from Central Asia; Kanishka ruled a vast empire and held the Fourth Buddhist Council.\",\"The Kushana period saw flourishing trade on the Silk Route and spread of Mahayana Buddhism.\",\"The Gupta period is called the \'Golden Age\' due to progress in arts, science, and literature.\",\"Harshavardhana of the Vardhana dynasty unified North India and was known for his generosity.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who founded the Shunga dynasty after the decline of the Maurya Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Vasudeva Kanva\",\"Pushyamitra Shunga\",\"Simuka\",\"Chandragupta-I\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Pushyamitra Shunga, the Commander-in-Chief of the Mauryan army, founded the Shunga dynasty.\"},{\"q\":\"Which dynasty is known as the first powerful empire in the history of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Shunga\",\"Kanva\",\"Satavahan\",\"Gupta\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Satavahan dynasty is known as the first powerful empire in the history of Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the most famous and powerful Shaka ruler in India?\",\"options\":[\"Menander\",\"Rudradaman-I\",\"Kanishka\",\"Gautamiputra Satakarni\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rudradaman-I was the most famous and powerful Shaka ruler in India, known for his Junagadh inscription.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the Milindapanha?\",\"options\":[\"A coin of Menander\",\"A Buddhist cave\",\"A dialogue between Menander and Nagasena\",\"A Shaka inscription\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Milindapanha is a famous treatise containing a dialogue between the Indo-Greek king Menander and the Buddhist monk Nagasena.\"},{\"q\":\"During whose rule was the Fourth Buddhist Council held?\",\"options\":[\"Ashoka\",\"Kanishka\",\"Chandragupta-I\",\"Harshavardhana\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Fourth Buddhist Council was held during the rule of the Kushana king Kanishka.\"},{\"q\":\"Which dynasty is associated with the \'Golden Age\' of ancient India?\",\"options\":[\"Shunga\",\"Satavahan\",\"Gupta\",\"Vardhana\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Gupta period is regarded as the \'Golden Age\' of ancient India due to achievements in arts, literature, and science.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Harshavardhana organize every five years at Prayagraj?\",\"options\":[\"A war campaign\",\"A religious debate\",\"A grand festival of charity\",\"A coin minting ceremony\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Harshavardhana organized a grand festival of charity at Prayagraj every five years, giving away his personal belongings and wealth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which sea is NOT mentioned as part of the \'three seas\' in Gautamiputra Satakarni\'s inscription?\",\"options\":[\"Arabian Sea\",\"Bay of Bengal\",\"Indian Ocean\",\"Red Sea\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The three seas mentioned are the Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal, and Indian Ocean; the Red Sea is not included.\"},{\"q\":\"Which script was used on Satavahan coins?\",\"options\":[\"Kharoshthi\",\"Greek\",\"Brahmi\",\"Arabic\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Satavahan coins carried inscriptions in the Brahmi script and Prakrit language.\"},{\"q\":\"Who defeated the Shakas and Yavanas to organize a stable empire?\",\"options\":[\"Simuka\",\"Vasudeva Kanva\",\"Gautamiputra Satakarni\",\"Rudradaman-I\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Gautamiputra Satakarni was the most powerful Satavahan ruler who defeated foreign powers like the Shakas and Yavanas.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22327,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Cultural Heritage of Ancient India','[\"- Ancient India had a rich cultural heritage that includes art, music, dance, literature, architecture and science.\",\"- The Gupta period (4th–6th century CE) is called the Golden Age of India for its achievements in science, mathematics, and literature.\",\"- The Vedic period produced the Vedas, Upanishads, epics (Ramayana, Mahabharata) and the Sanskrit language.\",\"- Oral literature (Vangmaya) and written literature (Sahitya) were both important; the Vedas and epics were transmitted orally before being written down.\",\"- The Panchatantra is a collection of fables that teaches moral lessons through animal stories.\",\"- The Natyashastra, written by Bharata Muni, is the ancient treatise on drama, dance, music and theatre.\",\"- Classical dance forms: Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Kathakali, Manipuri, Odissi, Kuchipudi, Mohiniattam, Sattriya.\",\"- Ajanta and Ellora caves are famous for their murals, sculptures and rock‑cut architecture.\",\"- The Iron Pillar of Delhi (built by Chandragupta II) shows advanced metallurgy; it has not rusted for 1600 years.\",\"- Trade in ancient India included agriculture, textiles, spices, precious stones and metals; ports like Sopara, Bharuch and Kalyan were important.\",\"- Medical science was advanced; Sushruta wrote the Sushruta Samhita on surgery, Charaka wrote the Charaka Samhita on medicine.\",\"- Education was organised in Gurukuls and universities such as Nalanda, Takshashila and Vikramshila.\",\"- Women scholars like Lopamudra, Gargi and Maitreyi studied and taught at Nalanda.\",\"- The Satavahana dynasty (2nd–3rd century CE) ruled much of Maharashtra and promoted trade and culture.\",\"- The Shunga dynasty followed the Mauryas; Pushyamitra Shunga was a former Mauryan commander.\",\"- Coins of ancient India often depicted deities, animals and inscriptions that reveal cultural values.\",\"- Cultural integration in Maharashtra shows a blend of Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions in art and architecture.\",\"- The concept of \'Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam\' (the world is one family) reflects unity in diversity.\",\"- The Natyashastra describes four types of music: Veda, Sangeet, Gandharva, and Dhanurveda.\",\"- The Sushruta Samhita lists 1,120 diseases and describes surgical procedures like rhinoplasty and skin grafting.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which ancient Indian dynasty is known as the \'Golden Age\' of India?\",\"options\":[\"Mauryan\",\"Gupta\",\"Shunga\",\"Kushana\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Gupta period is traditionally called the Golden Age of India.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is credited with writing the text \'Sushruta Samhita\'?\",\"options\":[\"Charaka\",\"Sushruta\",\"Patanjali\",\"Bharata\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sushruta authored the Sushruta Samhita, a foundational text on surgery.\"},{\"q\":\"The Ajanta Caves murals depict which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Only Buddhist monks\",\"Animals, dance, and daily life\",\"Ancient kings only\",\"Geographical maps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The murals show animals, dance, musical instruments, and scenes of daily life.\"},{\"q\":\"Which text is considered the foundation of Indian theatrical arts?\",\"options\":[\"Natyashastra\",\"Mahabharata\",\"Ramayana\",\"Arthashastra\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Natyashastra by Bharata Muni is the foundational treatise on drama and dance.\"},{\"q\":\"The Iron Pillar at Delhi is famous for its resistance to what?\",\"options\":[\"Color fading\",\"Rust\",\"Fire\",\"Water erosion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The pillar is renowned for its resistance to rust over centuries.\"},{\"q\":\"The Panchatantra is best described as a collection of what?\",\"options\":[\"Historical chronicles\",\"Mythological epics\",\"Fables with moral lessons\",\"Scientific treatises\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Panchatantra is a collection of fables teaching moral lessons.\"},{\"q\":\"Which dynasty ruled the region of Maharashtra during the Satavahana period?\",\"options\":[\"Chalukya\",\"Satavahana\",\"Maratha\",\"Mughal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Satavahana dynasty governed Maharashtra during that era.\"},{\"q\":\"The term \'Vangmaya\' refers to which type of literature?\",\"options\":[\"Written literature\",\"Oral literature\",\"Scientific texts\",\"Religious scriptures\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Vangmaya denotes oral literature passed down by word of mouth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was NOT a primary occupation mentioned in ancient Indian inscriptions?\",\"options\":[\"Potters\",\"Weavers\",\"Oil pressers\",\"Astronomers\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Inscriptions mention potters, weavers, and oil pressers, but not astronomers.\"},{\"q\":\"The \'Charaka Samhita\' primarily deals with which subject?\",\"options\":[\"Astronomy\",\"Medicine and Ayurveda\",\"Architecture\",\"Philosophy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Charaka Samhita is a key Ayurvedic text on medicine and health.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22328,39,6,'History & Civics','en','Ancient India and the World','[\"Ancient India had trade and cultural links with Persia, Greece, Rome, China, and Southeast Asia.\",\"The Persian Empire influenced Indian administration, art, and scripts like Kharoshthi.\",\"Greek influence led to Gandhara Art, blending Greek and Indian styles in Buddha sculptures.\",\"Indo-Greek coins show Greek kings ruling in northwestern India after Alexander’s invasion.\",\"Roman trade with India brought gold, glass, and amphorae; pepper was called \'Black Gold\'.\",\"Indian textiles, spices, and precious stones were exported to Rome via Red Sea and Indian Ocean.\",\"Buddhism spread to Sri Lanka through Ashoka’s children Mahendra and Sanghamitra.\",\"Sanghamitra brought a Bodhi tree branch to Sri Lanka, which grew into the Anuradhapura tree.\",\"Indian culture influenced Southeast Asian art, architecture, and dance (e.g., Angkor Wat, Borobudur).\",\"Chinese monks Fa Hien and Yuan Chwang visited India via Silk Route to study Buddhism at Nalanda.\",\"Yuan Chwang carried over 600 Buddhist manuscripts to China, aiding translation and spread of Buddhism.\",\"The Silk Route passed through India, Afghanistan, Central Asia, linking India to China and Rome.\",\"Indian merchants traded via sea routes to Sri Lanka, Indonesia, and Cambodia, spreading Hinduism and Buddhism.\",\"Kingdoms like Funan, Srivijaya, and Khmer were founded by Indians and adopted Indian culture.\",\"Indian architectural and artistic styles are visible in temples of Cambodia, Thailand, and Indonesia.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which empire influenced the administrative system of the Mauryan Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Gupta Empire\",\"Persian (Achaemenid) Empire\",\"Roman Empire\",\"Greek Empire\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Mauryan administrative system shows influence of the Persian Achaemenid Empire\'s provincial and taxation structure.\"},{\"q\":\"What is Gandhara Art known for?\",\"options\":[\"Pure Indian style\",\"Blend of Greek and Indian sculptural traditions\",\"Roman architectural style\",\"Chinese painting style\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gandhara Art emerged during the Kushana period and combined Greek sculptural techniques with Indian Buddhist themes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which university did the Chinese monk Yuan Chwang attend for several years?\",\"options\":[\"Takshashila\",\"Vikramshila\",\"Nalanda\",\"Valabhi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Yuan Chwang studied at Nalanda University for many years, where he collected Buddhist manuscripts to take to China.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main export from India to the Roman Empire that was called \'Black Gold\'?\",\"options\":[\"Spices\",\"Black pepper\",\"Iron\",\"Cotton\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Black pepper from South India was highly valued in Rome and referred to as \'Black Gold\' due to its high price.\"},{\"q\":\"Which script used in Ashokan inscriptions shows Persian influence?\",\"options\":[\"Brahmi\",\"Kharoshthi\",\"Devanagari\",\"Tamil\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Kharoshthi script, used in some Ashokan inscriptions in the northwest, was derived from Aramaic and reflects Persian administrative influence.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Indian emperor sent his son and daughter to Sri Lanka to spread Buddhism?\",\"options\":[\"Chandragupta Maurya\",\"Ashoka\",\"Kanishka\",\"Harshavardhana\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Emperor Ashoka sent his son Mahendra and daughter Sanghamitra to Sri Lanka to propagate Buddhism.\"},{\"q\":\"What did Sanghamitra bring to Sri Lanka that later became a sacred Bodhi tree?\",\"options\":[\"A statue of Buddha\",\"A branch of the Bodhi tree\",\"A copy of the Vedas\",\"A bowl used by Buddha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sanghamitra brought a branch of the Bodhi tree from Bodh Gaya, which was planted in Anuradhapura and grew into a sacred tree.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Southeast Asian monument shows strong influence of Indian architecture and epics?\",\"options\":[\"Borobudur Stupa\",\"Angkor Wat Temple\",\"Shwedagon Pagoda\",\"Prambanan Temple\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Angkor Wat in Cambodia reflects Indian architectural styles and depicts scenes from the Ramayana and Mahabharata.\"},{\"q\":\"Through which regions did the Silk Route pass to connect India with China?\",\"options\":[\"India – Sri Lanka – Indonesia\",\"India – Afghanistan – Central Asia\",\"India – Myanmar – Thailand\",\"India – Egypt – Rome\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Silk Route went from India through Afghanistan and Central Asia to reach China, facilitating trade and cultural exchange.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Chinese traveller stayed in the Srivijaya Kingdom for six months to learn Sanskrit before going to India?\",\"options\":[\"Fa Hien\",\"Yuan Chwang\",\"I-tsing\",\"Xuanzang\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"I-tsing, a Chinese traveller, stayed in Srivijaya (Indonesia) for six months to study Sanskrit before proceeding to India in 671 CE.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22329,48,7,'Geography','en','The Sun, the Moon and the Earth General Geography 03','[\"Tides are the regular rise and fall of sea water level caused by the gravitational pull of the moon and the sun and the centrifugal force due to Earth’s rotation.\",\"High tide is when the sea level is at its maximum; low tide is when it is at its minimum.\",\"The cycle of high and low tide takes about 12 hours 25 minutes – this is called a semi‑diurnal tide.\",\"Spring tide occurs on full moon and new moon days when the sun, moon and Earth are aligned; the tidal range (difference between high and low tide) is greatest.\",\"Neap tide occurs on the first and third quarter moon days when the sun and moon are at right angles; the tidal range is smallest.\",\"The highest high tide occurs on full moon and new moon days; the lowest low tide occurs on the same days.\",\"The opposite point on Earth (antipodal meridian) experiences the same tide at the same time as the original point.\",\"The tidal range is larger in open seas (≈30 cm) and can reach 1100–1600 cm in places like the Bay of Fundy.\",\"Tides help fishermen, salt pans, and coastal ecosystems but can also cause accidents if timings are not known.\",\"Wave height is the vertical distance between the crest and the trough; wavelength is the horizontal distance between two successive crests.\",\"Waves are generated mainly by wind; they can also be caused by earthquakes or volcanic eruptions (tsunamis).\",\"The energy of waves can be converted into electricity using wave‑power devices.\",\"The centrifugal force acts outward from Earth’s centre; gravitational force pulls objects inward; their balance keeps objects on the surface.\",\"The moon’s gravitational force is stronger than the sun’s because it is closer to Earth.\",\"Tide timings change daily by about 50 minutes because the Earth rotates slightly faster than the moon orbits.\",\"The tidal range is larger where the coastline is narrow and the sea floor rises sharply.\",\"Tides influence marine life migration, coastal erosion, and the formation of mangroves.\",\"The difference between the highest high tide and the lowest low tide is called the tidal range.\",\"The high tide at 0° meridian and 180° meridian occur simultaneously; low tide occurs at the meridians 90° and 270°.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the time interval between two successive high tides?\",\"options\":[\"12 hours\",\"12 hours 25 minutes\",\"24 hours\",\"24 hours 50 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The tidal cycle is about 12 hours 25 minutes.\"},{\"q\":\"Spring tide occurs on which lunar phases?\",\"options\":[\"First and third quarter\",\"Full and new moon\",\"Full moon only\",\"New moon only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Spring tide happens when the sun and moon are aligned (full or new moon).\"},{\"q\":\"Which force keeps objects on Earth’s surface?\",\"options\":[\"Centrifugal force\",\"Gravitational force\",\"Both\",\"Neither\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gravitational force pulls objects toward the centre, overpowering centrifugal force.\"},{\"q\":\"The tidal range is greatest in the Bay of Fundy because:\",\"options\":[\"It is very deep\",\"It has a narrow coastline\",\"It is far from the moon\",\"It has a high latitude\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The narrow shape amplifies the tidal range.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a negative effect of tides?\",\"options\":[\"Helps fishing\",\"Creates salt pans\",\"Causes coastal erosion\",\"Provides electricity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Coastal erosion is a negative effect.\"},{\"q\":\"Waves are mainly generated by:\",\"options\":[\"Wind\",\"Earthquakes\",\"Volcanoes\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Wind is the primary source; earthquakes and volcanoes cause tsunamis.\"},{\"q\":\"The opposite point on Earth experiences the same tide as the original point at the same time because:\",\"options\":[\"The moon is at the same position\",\"The Earth rotates 360°\",\"The tidal bulge is symmetric\",\"The sun’s gravity is uniform\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The tidal bulge is symmetric about the Earth’s centre.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a type of tide?\",\"options\":[\"Spring tide\",\"Neap tide\",\"High tide\",\"Low tide\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"High and low tide are phases, not types.\"},{\"q\":\"The energy of waves can be converted into electricity using:\",\"options\":[\"Wind turbines\",\"Solar panels\",\"Wave‑power devices\",\"Geothermal plants\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Wave‑power devices harness wave motion.\"},{\"q\":\"The difference between the highest high tide and the lowest low tide is called:\",\"options\":[\"Tidal height\",\"Tidal range\",\"Tidal cycle\",\"Tidal period\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It is the tidal range.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22330,48,7,'Geography','en','Air Pressure Physical Geography','[\"Air pressure is the force exerted by the weight of air on the Earth’s surface.\",\"It is not the same everywhere; it varies with location, time, temperature and altitude.\",\"The higher you go, the thinner the air and the lower the pressure.\",\"Hot air expands, becomes lighter and rises, creating low pressure areas; cold air sinks, creating high pressure areas.\",\"Pressure is measured in millibars (mb) using a barometer.\",\"The Earth’s rotation and its curved surface create wind by moving air from high to low pressure zones.\",\"The equator has a low‑pressure belt (tropics) because the air is warm and rises.\",\"The poles have high‑pressure belts because the air is cold and sinks.\",\"Between the tropics and poles lie mid‑latitude high‑pressure belts (around 55°–65°).\",\"Pressure belts are not continuous; they are bands of latitude where pressure is either high or low.\",\"Storms form when low‑pressure areas draw in moist air and it rises, cools and condenses.\",\"Wind direction is from high to low pressure, modified by the Coriolis effect.\",\"The weight of the air column above a point is about 1000 kg per square metre at sea level.\",\"Air pressure affects breathing, weather, and the behaviour of balloons and aircraft.\",\"Understanding pressure belts helps explain why deserts are found in certain latitudes.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What unit is used to measure air pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Pascal\",\"Newton\",\"Millibars\",\"Atmospheres\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Air pressure is measured in millibars.\"},{\"q\":\"Which latitude band has the lowest air pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Poles\",\"Mid‑latitudes\",\"Tropics\",\"Equator\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The tropics (around the equator) have the lowest pressure due to warm rising air.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does air pressure decrease with altitude?\",\"options\":[\"Air becomes denser\",\"Air becomes thinner\",\"Sun heats the air\",\"Wind increases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Higher altitude means fewer air molecules above, so pressure is lower.\"},{\"q\":\"What causes a high‑pressure belt to form?\",\"options\":[\"Cold air sinking\",\"Warm air rising\",\"Wind speed increase\",\"Solar radiation decrease\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Cold air sinks, creating a high‑pressure area.\"},{\"q\":\"Which wind belt is associated with the polar high pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Trade winds\",\"Westerlies\",\"Polar easterlies\",\"Jet stream\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Polar easterlies blow around the high‑pressure polar belt.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main effect of a low‑pressure area?\",\"options\":[\"Wind slows down\",\"Rain is less likely\",\"Storms are more likely\",\"Temperature rises\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Low pressure draws moist air upward, leading to storms.\"},{\"q\":\"Air pressure is maximum at which location?\",\"options\":[\"High altitude\",\"Sea level\",\"Poles\",\"Equator\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sea level has the highest air pressure.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is NOT a factor that influences air pressure?\",\"options\":[\"Altitude\",\"Temperature\",\"Humidity\",\"Gravity\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Gravity pulls air down but does not directly change pressure at a given altitude.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the line that joins points of equal pressure on a map?\",\"options\":[\"Contour line\",\"Isobar\",\"Latitude line\",\"Meridian\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"An isobar connects points of equal pressure.\"},{\"q\":\"Which belt is found between 55° and 65° latitude?\",\"options\":[\"Equatorial low\",\"Polar high\",\"Mid‑latitude high\",\"Subpolar low\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mid‑latitude high‑pressure belts lie between 55° and 65°.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22331,48,7,'Geography','en','Winds Physical Geography','[\"Winds are the movement of air from high pressure to low pressure.\",\"The pressure difference between two places causes wind speed – larger difference = faster wind.\",\"Wind direction is affected by Earth’s rotation (Coriolis effect).\",\"In the Northern Hemisphere winds are deflected to the right of their original path; in the Southern Hemisphere to the left.\",\"Planetary winds are named by the direction they blow from: e.g., Westerlies (from west).\",\"Westerlies blow from 30°–60° latitude, generally from west to east.\",\"Easterlies blow from 0°–30° latitude, from east to west.\",\"Polar winds blow from the polar high pressure belt toward the subpolar low pressure belt, usually east to west.\",\"Local winds are short‑duration winds that originate in specific regions (e.g., valley breeze, mountain breeze).\",\"Sea breeze blows from sea to land during the day because land heats faster than sea.\",\"Land breeze blows from land to sea at night when land cools faster than sea.\",\"Monsoon winds are seasonal winds caused by uneven heating of land and sea; they bring rain in summer and dry air in winter.\",\"The Roaring Forties, Furious Fifties, and Screeching Sixties are strong winds in the Southern Hemisphere between 40°–60°S.\",\"Cyclones are low‑pressure systems with winds rotating counter‑clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere.\",\"Anticyclones are high‑pressure systems with winds rotating clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and counter‑clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere.\",\"Wind speed is measured in knots or kilometres per hour.\",\"The Coriolis effect causes winds to curve; this is why the direction of wind changes with latitude.\",\"The equatorial calm belt (or doldrums) is a region near the equator where winds are very weak.\",\"Mountain breeze occurs when hot air rises over mountains at night and cool air rushes down into valleys.\",\"Valley breeze occurs when cool air from the valley rises during the day and is replaced by warm air from the mountains.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the main cause of wind?\",\"options\":[\"Temperature of the sun\",\"Difference in air pressure\",\"Humidity\",\"Wind speed\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Winds move from high to low pressure.\"},{\"q\":\"In the Northern Hemisphere, winds are deflected to the ___ of their original direction.\",\"options\":[\"left\",\"right\",\"up\",\"down\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Coriolis effect deflects to the right.\"},{\"q\":\"Which wind blows from the west to the east between 30° and 60° latitude?\",\"options\":[\"Easterlies\",\"Westerlies\",\"Polar winds\",\"Monsoon winds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Westerlies blow from west to east.\"},{\"q\":\"Sea breeze occurs during the ___ because land heats faster than sea.\",\"options\":[\"night\",\"day\",\"evening\",\"morning\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Land heats faster during the day.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a local wind?\",\"options\":[\"Monsoon\",\"Easterlies\",\"Valley breeze\",\"Westerlies\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Valley breeze is a local wind.\"},{\"q\":\"The Coriolis effect causes winds in the Southern Hemisphere to curve ___ of their original direction.\",\"options\":[\"right\",\"left\",\"up\",\"down\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Deflected to the left.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the typical wind speed unit used in meteorology?\",\"options\":[\"Meters per second\",\"Knots\",\"Pascals\",\"Liters\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wind speed is measured in knots or km\\/h.\"},{\"q\":\"Which wind is known as the \'Roaring Forties\'?\",\"options\":[\"Easterlies\",\"Westerlies\",\"Polar winds\",\"Monsoon winds\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Roaring Forties are strong winds between 40°–50°S.\"},{\"q\":\"Cyclones rotate ___ in the Northern Hemisphere.\",\"options\":[\"clockwise\",\"counter‑clockwise\",\"vertical\",\"horizontal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cyclones rotate counter‑clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere.\"},{\"q\":\"Anticyclones are associated with which weather condition?\",\"options\":[\"Heavy rain\",\"Clear skies\",\"Stormy weather\",\"High winds\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Anticyclones bring clear skies and light winds.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22332,48,7,'Geography','en','Natural Regions Physical Geography','[\"Tundra: cold, mean summer 0–10°C, mean winter –20 to –30°C, rainfall 25–300 mm, vegetation short‑lived, animals: polar bears, reindeer, caribou.\",\"Taiga: sub‑arctic, mean summer 10–20°C, rainfall 300–500 mm, coniferous forest, animals: wolves, lynx, moose.\",\"Grasslands (Steppes): 30–55° latitude, mean summer 25–30°C, rainfall 400–800 mm, animals: horses, cattle, antelope.\",\"Hot deserts: 20–30° latitude, mean summer 40–45°C, rainfall <250 mm, animals: camels, snakes, lizards.\",\"Equatorial forests: 0–10° latitude, mean summer 24–30°C, rainfall 2000–3000 mm, dense canopy, animals: gorillas, chimpanzees, elephants.\",\"Monsoon region: 10–30°N\\/S, rainfall 1000–2500 mm, crops: rice, wheat, cotton.\",\"Mediterranean region: 30–40° latitude, dry summers, wet winters, crops: olives, grapes, wheat.\",\"West European region: 45–55° latitude, mild climate, crops: barley, potatoes.\",\"Natural regions differ mainly due to latitude, sunlight, water, soil, and wind patterns.\",\"Human occupations follow resources: agriculture in monsoon, forestry in taiga, hunting in tundra, herding in grasslands, camel‑herding in deserts.\",\"Animals adapt: thick fur in cold, water‑saving in deserts, large body in equatorial to dissipate heat.\",\"Biodiversity decreases from equator to poles; species richness is highest in equatorial forests.\",\"Natural regions are defined by distinct climate, vegetation, and animal life.\",\"The world is divided into nine major natural regions: Tundra, Taiga, Grasslands, Hot deserts, Equatorial forests, Monsoon, Mediterranean, West European, and others.\",\"Latitude ranges: Tundra 65–90°, Taiga 55–70°, Grasslands 30–55°, Deserts 20–30°, Equatorial 0–10°.\",\"Mean annual rainfall varies: Tundra 25–300 mm, Taiga 300–500 mm, Grasslands 400–800 mm, Deserts <250 mm, Equatorial 2000–3000 mm.\",\"Temperature ranges: Tundra –20 to 10°C, Taiga 10–20°C, Grasslands 0 to 27°C, Deserts 45–60°C, Equatorial 24–30°C.\",\"Human life is shaped by natural resources: agriculture, animal husbandry, forestry, fishing, mining.\",\"Natural regions are also called biogeographic zones.\",\"The term \'natural region\' refers to areas with similar natural characteristics.\",\"The concept helps in planning, conservation, and education.\",\"Understanding natural regions helps predict climate change impacts.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which natural region has mean summer temperatures around 45°C and rainfall less than 250 mm?\",\"options\":[\"Tundra\",\"Taiga\",\"Hot desert\",\"Equatorial forest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Hot deserts have very high summer temperatures and very low rainfall.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the latitudinal extent of the Tundra region?\",\"options\":[\"0–10°\",\"30–55°\",\"65–90°\",\"20–30°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tundra lies between 65° and 90° latitude.\"},{\"q\":\"Which animals are typical of the Taiga region?\",\"options\":[\"Polar bears\",\"Wolves\",\"Camels\",\"Lions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Taiga is home to wolves, lynx, and other cold‑adapted mammals.\"},{\"q\":\"Which crop is mainly grown in the Monsoon region?\",\"options\":[\"Rice\",\"Wheat\",\"Olives\",\"Barley\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Monsoon regions are ideal for rice cultivation.\"},{\"q\":\"Which natural region has short‑lived vegetation and sparse animal distribution?\",\"options\":[\"Equatorial forest\",\"Grasslands\",\"Tundra\",\"Hot desert\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tundra has short‑lived vegetation and sparse fauna.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region is known for its dry summers and wet winters?\",\"options\":[\"Mediterranean\",\"West European\",\"Monsoon\",\"Equatorial\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mediterranean climate has dry summers and wet winters.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region is favourable for dairy farming?\",\"options\":[\"Hot desert\",\"Tundra\",\"Grasslands\",\"Equatorial forest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Grasslands support extensive grazing for dairy cattle.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are lions not found in equatorial forests?\",\"options\":[\"They need open grasslands\",\"They cannot tolerate high humidity\",\"They need large prey\",\"They are too cold adapted\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lions need open grasslands for hunting; dense forests hinder them.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region has winter rains?\",\"options\":[\"Tundra\",\"Hot desert\",\"Mediterranean\",\"Equatorial forest\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mediterranean region receives rainfall in winter.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region is favourable for fruit production?\",\"options\":[\"Monsoon\",\"Tundra\",\"Desert\",\"West European\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Monsoon region’s rainfall supports fruit crops like mangoes and bananas.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22333,48,7,'Geography','en','Soils Physical Geography','[\"Soil is a mixture of weathered rock, organic matter, water, air and living organisms.\",\"Parent rock is the original material from which soil develops.\",\"Climate (rainfall, temperature) controls the rate of weathering and decomposition.\",\"Biological activity (plants, animals, microbes) breaks down organic matter into humus.\",\"Humus is dark, nutrient‑rich organic material that improves soil fertility.\",\"Soil formation takes thousands of years; harder rocks like granite take longer than soft rocks like basalt.\",\"Soil types in Maharashtra: Regur (black) in Deccan Plateau, Laterite in Konkan, Yellow‑Brown in hilly regions, Alluvial in river valleys, Red in high rainfall areas.\",\"Regur soil is rich in iron and aluminium, good for cotton, wheat, sugarcane.\",\"Laterite soil is reddish due to iron oxides, good for oil palm, cashew, coconut.\",\"Alluvial soil is fertile, used for rice, wheat, pulses.\",\"Soil erosion is loss of topsoil by wind or water; causes degradation and salinisation.\",\"Conservation methods: planting trees, building embankments, contour trenches, mulching, crop rotation, fallow periods.\",\"Chemical fertilizers increase yield but excess causes soil degradation and salinity.\",\"Vermicompost and organic manures improve humus and pH balance.\",\"Soil pH, moisture, texture and organic content determine plant suitability.\",\"Water movement in soil is controlled by soil structure and slope.\",\"Ground‑water recharge is enhanced by retaining water in contour trenches.\",\"Soil conservation is essential for sustainable agriculture and food security.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which component of soil is formed by the decomposition of plant and animal remains?\",\"options\":[\"Mineral particles\",\"Water\",\"Humus\",\"Air\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Humus is the dark organic material produced by decomposition.\"},{\"q\":\"Which soil type is found mainly in the Deccan Plateau of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Alluvial\",\"Laterite\",\"Regur (black)\",\"Yellow‑Brown\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Regur or black soil is characteristic of the Deccan Plateau.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main cause of soil erosion in hilly areas?\",\"options\":[\"Excessive rainfall\",\"Wind\",\"Lack of vegetation\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All listed factors contribute to erosion in hills.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a method of soil conservation?\",\"options\":[\"Increasing irrigation frequency\",\"Building contour trenches\",\"Using more chemical fertilizers\",\"Removing trees\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Contour trenches help retain water and reduce erosion.\"},{\"q\":\"Which soil is reddish due to iron oxides and is common in the Konkan coast?\",\"options\":[\"Regur\",\"Alluvial\",\"Laterite\",\"Yellow‑Brown\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Laterite soil gets its colour from iron oxides.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to soil when it is irrigated excessively with chemical fertilizers?\",\"options\":[\"It becomes more fertile\",\"It becomes saline and less productive\",\"It dries out\",\"It turns into rock\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Excess salts rise to the surface, making soil saline.\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor does NOT influence soil formation?\",\"options\":[\"Parent rock\",\"Climate\",\"Biotic activity\",\"Color of the soil\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Color is a result, not a factor in formation.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main benefit of using vermicompost in farming?\",\"options\":[\"It increases soil salinity\",\"It reduces humus content\",\"It improves soil structure and fertility\",\"It speeds up plant growth by adding chemicals\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Vermicompost adds organic matter and improves fertility.\"},{\"q\":\"Which soil type is most suitable for rice cultivation in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Regur\",\"Laterite\",\"Alluvial\",\"Yellow‑Brown\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Alluvial soil is fertile and ideal for rice.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a reason for soil degradation?\",\"options\":[\"Overuse of chemical fertilizers\",\"Excessive irrigation\",\"Planting trees\",\"Salt accumulation\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Planting trees helps conserve soil, not degrade it.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22334,48,7,'Geography','en','How Seasons Occur - Part 2 General Geography','[\"Season changes are caused by Earth\'s orbit around the Sun and the tilt of its axis.\",\"The axis is tilted about 23.5° relative to the orbital plane.\",\"When the North Pole tilts toward the Sun (21‑June to 22‑December) it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere – called Uttarayan.\",\"When the South Pole tilts toward the Sun (22‑December to 21‑June) it is summer in the Southern Hemisphere – called Dakshinayan.\",\"The equinoxes (21‑March and 23‑September) are dates when the Sun is directly above the Equator; day and night are equal everywhere.\",\"Solstices (21‑June and 22‑December) are dates when the Sun is directly above the Tropic of Cancer or Capricorn; longest and shortest days occur then.\",\"The apparent north‑south motion of the Sun is due to Earth\'s axial tilt, not a real movement of the Sun.\",\"The apparent east‑west motion of the Sun (diurnal motion) is due to Earth\'s rotation.\",\"During perihelion (early January) Earth is closest to the Sun; during aphelion (early July) it is farthest; this distance change does not affect seasons.\",\"Polar regions experience 24‑hour daylight in summer and 24‑hour darkness in winter.\",\"Equatorial regions have almost equal day and night all year; seasons are less pronounced.\",\"The tilt of the axis is the reason why the same date can be summer in one hemisphere and winter in the other.\",\"The Sun’s rays are perpendicular to the Earth\'s surface at the Equator on equinoxes.\",\"The Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N) receives perpendicular rays on 21‑June; the Tropic of Capricorn (23.5°S) on 22‑December.\",\"The longest day is on 21‑June in the Northern Hemisphere and on 22‑December in the Southern Hemisphere.\",\"The shortest night is on 22‑December in the Northern Hemisphere and on 21‑June in the Southern Hemisphere.\",\"If the Earth\'s axis were not tilted, there would be no seasons and day\\/night length would be constant.\",\"The Sun is never physically moving; its apparent motion is an optical effect.\",\"The Earth\'s orbit is elliptical; perihelion is the closest point to the Sun, aphelion the farthest.\",\"The Sun’s apparent motion is used in geography to explain seasonal changes.\",\"The Earth’s rotation causes the Sun’s diurnal apparent motion from east to west.\",\"The tilt of the axis also explains why the Sun is visible for more than 24 hours in polar regions during summer.\",\"The Sun’s apparent north‑south motion is called Dakshinayan (southward) and Uttarayan (northward).\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which event marks the longest day in the Northern Hemisphere?\",\"options\":[\"21 March\",\"21 June\",\"23 September\",\"22 December\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"21 June is the summer solstice in the Northern Hemisphere.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for the apparent north‑south motion of the Sun?\",\"options\":[\"Diurnal motion\",\"Annual motion\",\"Apparent motion\",\"Rotational motion\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It is called the apparent motion of the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"During which period does the Southern Hemisphere experience summer?\",\"options\":[\"21 June to 22 December\",\"22 December to 21 June\",\"21 March to 23 September\",\"21 September to 23 March\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Southern Hemisphere summer is from 22 Dec to 21 Jun.\"},{\"q\":\"When the Sun is directly above the Equator, what is this day called?\",\"options\":[\"Solstice\",\"Equinox\",\"Perihelion\",\"Aphelion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Equinoxes occur when the Sun is above the Equator.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a factor causing seasons?\",\"options\":[\"Earth’s axial tilt\",\"Earth’s revolution around the Sun\",\"Earth’s distance from the Sun\",\"Earth’s rotation speed\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Distance change is small and does not affect seasons.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the Sun’s rays at the Tropic of Cancer on 21 June?\",\"options\":[\"They are parallel to the surface\",\"They are perpendicular to the surface\",\"They are at 45° to the surface\",\"They are absent\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"On 21 June the Sun is directly overhead at the Tropic of Cancer.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region experiences 24‑hour daylight during summer?\",\"options\":[\"Equatorial region\",\"Mid‑latitude region\",\"Polar region\",\"All regions\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Polar regions have continuous daylight in summer.\"},{\"q\":\"The term \'Dakshinayan\' refers to the Sun\'s motion toward which direction?\",\"options\":[\"North\",\"South\",\"East\",\"West\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Dakshinayan is the southward motion of the Sun.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason for the difference in day length between the equinoxes and solstices?\",\"options\":[\"Earth’s rotation speed\",\"Earth’s axial tilt\",\"Earth’s orbital speed\",\"Earth’s distance from the Sun\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Axial tilt changes the angle of sunlight, altering day length.\"},{\"q\":\"If Earth had no axial tilt, what would happen to seasons?\",\"options\":[\"They would still occur\",\"They would be more intense\",\"They would not occur\",\"They would shift by 90°\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"No tilt means no seasons.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22335,48,7,'Geography','en','Agriculture Human Geography 52','[\"Agriculture is the main occupation of rural people and includes cultivation of crops, rearing of animals, poultry, sericulture, horticulture, pisciculture, and other allied activities.\",\"The main types of farming are subsistence, commercial, extensive, intensive, shifting, plantation, and market gardening.\",\"Subsistence farming is for household consumption; commercial farming is for market sale.\",\"Intensive farming uses high technology, irrigation, and fertilizers to get maximum yield on small land.\",\"Extensive farming covers large areas with low input and high output of grains like wheat and corn.\",\"Shifting cultivation (slash‑and‑burn) is practiced in forested hilly areas; the land is left fallow for years after cultivation.\",\"Plantation farming deals with a single crop (tea, coffee, rubber) on large land (40+ ha) and uses local labour.\",\"Market gardening supplies vegetables and fruits to nearby cities; it is small‑scale and uses irrigation and greenhouses.\",\"Horticulture\\/Floriculture grows flowers and fruits for sale; it often uses greenhouses and organic methods.\",\"Organic farming avoids chemical fertilizers and pesticides, using compost, cow dung, vermicompost, and natural pest control.\",\"Agrotourism lets city dwellers visit farms for experience and earns extra income for farmers.\",\"Marketing systems (e.g., Agricultural Produce Market Committees) help farmers sell produce at fair prices.\",\"Irrigation methods include surface, sprinkler, drip, and sub‑surface; each has advantages and disadvantages.\",\"Water scarcity is a major problem in India; efficient irrigation and water‑saving techniques are essential.\",\"Modern technology (tractors, GPS, drones, ICT) improves productivity and helps farmers manage farms better.\",\"Climate, soil, water, and market access determine the type of agriculture practiced in a region.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which type of farming uses the most technology and has the highest input per unit area?\",\"options\":[\"Subsistence\",\"Intensive\",\"Extensive\",\"Shifting\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Intensive farming uses high technology, irrigation, and fertilizers to maximise yield on small land.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of shifting cultivation?\",\"options\":[\"To grow cash crops\",\"To produce food for export\",\"To clear forest for permanent settlement\",\"To cultivate a single crop for many years\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shifting cultivation clears forest temporarily and leaves land fallow for years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of irrigation method?\",\"options\":[\"Surface irrigation\",\"Sprinkler irrigation\",\"Drip irrigation\",\"Fertilizer irrigation\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Fertilizer irrigation is not an irrigation method.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity is considered a form of agrotourism?\",\"options\":[\"Selling farm produce online\",\"Visiting a farm for experience\",\"Using organic fertilizers\",\"Planting a greenhouse\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Agrotourism involves visitors coming to farms for experience.\"},{\"q\":\"Which crop is typically grown in plantation farming?\",\"options\":[\"Wheat\",\"Rice\",\"Tea\",\"Potato\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Plantation farming usually involves a single cash crop like tea, coffee, or rubber.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main benefit of using drip irrigation?\",\"options\":[\"Lower water use\",\"Higher labor cost\",\"More chemical use\",\"Longer irrigation time\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Drip irrigation delivers water directly to roots, saving water.\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of farming is most common in hilly regions of India?\",\"options\":[\"Intensive\",\"Extensive\",\"Plantation\",\"Shifting\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Shifting cultivation is common in hilly forested areas.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main goal of organic farming?\",\"options\":[\"Use more chemical fertilizers\",\"Use natural inputs only\",\"Increase pesticide use\",\"Reduce crop yield\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Organic farming avoids chemical inputs and uses natural compost and pest control.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of market gardening?\",\"options\":[\"Large landholding\",\"Low investment\",\"No irrigation\",\"No transport network\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Market gardening uses small land, low investment, and relies on good transport.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is agriculture in India seasonal?\",\"options\":[\"Because of monsoon patterns\",\"Because of year‑round rainfall\",\"Because of constant temperature\",\"Because of permanent irrigation\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"India’s agriculture is seasonal due to monsoon rainfall patterns.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22336,48,7,'Geography','en','Human Settlements Human Geography 62','[\"Human settlements are places where people live and work.\",\"They can be grouped into four main types: scattered, nucleated, linear and urban.\",\"Scattered settlements have houses far apart, usually in rural areas like villages.\",\"Nucleated settlements have houses close together around a centre such as a market or temple.\",\"Linear settlements grow along a line – a road, river or coast – and houses line the line.\",\"Urban settlements are dense and have many buildings, shops, schools and hospitals.\",\"The type of settlement depends on geography: water, soil, climate, transport and resources.\",\"People move from scattered to nucleated to linear to urban as population grows and technology improves.\",\"Rural settlements supply food and raw materials to urban areas; urban areas supply services to rural areas.\",\"Settlements are influenced by physical factors (water, land, climate), cultural factors (religion, caste) and economic factors (jobs, trade).\",\"In a nucleated settlement houses are close together and social services are available nearby.\",\"In a linear settlement houses are arranged along a road or river and shops are on both sides of the road.\",\"Scattered settlements are often found in forests, grasslands or deserts where houses are far apart.\",\"Urban settlements develop when people move to cities for better jobs, education and health facilities.\",\"The growth of a settlement is called urbanisation when rural areas become urban over time.\",\"The pattern of a settlement can be seen on maps and in photographs.\",\"The distance between houses or buildings tells us whether a settlement is scattered, nucleated or linear.\",\"The presence of high-rise buildings indicates an urban settlement.\",\"A settlement’s location is chosen for water, fertile land, transport and safety from natural hazards.\",\"Settlements can change type over time: a village can become a town and then a city.\",\"The main goal of a settlement is to provide shelter, food, security and social life for its people.\",\"Settlements are part of the human geography of a region and affect the environment.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which type of settlement has houses far apart and is usually found in rural areas?\",\"options\":[\"Scattered\",\"Nucleated\",\"Linear\",\"Urban\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Scattered settlements have houses far apart.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason people move from scattered to nucleated settlements?\",\"options\":[\"Better water supply\",\"Higher taxes\",\"More jobs\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All factors encourage moving to nucleated settlements.\"},{\"q\":\"In a linear settlement, houses are arranged along:\",\"options\":[\"A river\",\"A road\",\"A hill\",\"A forest\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Linear settlements grow along a road, river or coast.\"},{\"q\":\"Which settlement type is most likely to have high-rise buildings?\",\"options\":[\"Scattered\",\"Nucleated\",\"Linear\",\"Urban\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"High-rise buildings are typical of urban settlements.\"},{\"q\":\"What factor is NOT a reason for the location of human settlements?\",\"options\":[\"Water availability\",\"Climate\",\"Transport\",\"Color of the soil\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Color of soil is not a main factor.\"},{\"q\":\"A village that supplies food to a nearby city is an example of a:\",\"options\":[\"Urban settlement\",\"Nucleated settlement\",\"Scattered settlement\",\"Linear settlement\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It is a rural (scattered) settlement supplying food.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of a nucleated settlement?\",\"options\":[\"Houses are far apart\",\"Houses are close together\",\"Houses line a road\",\"Houses are on a hill\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Nucleated houses are close together.\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of settlement grows along a coast?\",\"options\":[\"Scattered\",\"Nucleated\",\"Linear\",\"Urban\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Linear settlements often grow along coasts.\"},{\"q\":\"The process of rural areas becoming cities is called:\",\"options\":[\"Urbanisation\",\"Agrarianisation\",\"Industrialisation\",\"Deurbanisation\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Urbanisation is the growth of urban areas.\"},{\"q\":\"Which settlement type is most likely to have a market at its centre?\",\"options\":[\"Scattered\",\"Nucleated\",\"Linear\",\"Urban\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Nucleated settlements often have a central market.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22337,48,7,'Geography','en','Contour Maps and Landforms Practical Geography 69','[\"Contour lines join points of equal elevation above sea level.\",\"Contour interval is the vertical distance between two consecutive contour lines.\",\"Closely spaced contour lines indicate steep slopes.\",\"Widely spaced contour lines indicate gentle slopes.\",\"Concentric circles with increasing height inside show a hill.\",\"Concentric circles with decreasing height inside show a depression or pond.\",\"V-shaped contour lines pointing upstream indicate a river valley.\",\"Contour lines never cross each other except at overhanging cliffs or caves.\",\"The scale of a map shows the ratio between map distance and actual ground distance.\",\"Benchmark is a fixed point of known elevation marked on a map.\",\"Spot height is the exact elevation of a point shown by a dot and number on a map.\",\"Hachures are short lines used to show steep slopes on older maps.\",\"Contour maps help in understanding landforms, planning routes, and construction.\",\"Gradient is calculated as vertical interval divided by horizontal equivalent.\",\"Contour maps are used in geography, civil engineering, and military planning.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What do contour lines represent on a map?\",\"options\":[\"Equal temperature\",\"Equal rainfall\",\"Equal elevation\",\"Equal population\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Contour lines join points of the same height above sea level.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a close spacing of contour lines indicate?\",\"options\":[\"Gentle slope\",\"Steep slope\",\"Flat land\",\"Valley\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Close contour lines mean a rapid change in elevation over a short distance, indicating a steep slope.\"},{\"q\":\"Which landform is shown by concentric circles with higher values inside?\",\"options\":[\"Pond\",\"Hill\",\"Plateau\",\"Plain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Concentric circles with increasing elevation toward the center represent a hill.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the contour interval?\",\"options\":[\"Horizontal distance between two contour lines\",\"Vertical distance between two contour lines\",\"Distance from sea level to a point\",\"Length of a contour line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Contour interval is the fixed vertical difference in elevation between adjacent contour lines.\"},{\"q\":\"V-shaped contour lines pointing upstream indicate which feature?\",\"options\":[\"Ridge\",\"Cliff\",\"River valley\",\"Depression\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The V points in the direction of upstream flow, showing a river valley.\"},{\"q\":\"Can contour lines cross each other on a normal map?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No, never\",\"Only at cliffs or overhangs\",\"Only in valleys\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Contour lines do not cross except in special cases like overhanging cliffs or caves.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a benchmark in contour mapping?\",\"options\":[\"A symbol for a town\",\"A fixed point of known elevation\",\"The highest point on a map\",\"A type of contour line\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A benchmark is a permanent mark showing a known elevation, used as a reference.\"},{\"q\":\"What does widely spaced contour lines suggest?\",\"options\":[\"Steep slope\",\"Uniform slope\",\"Gentle slope\",\"Vertical cliff\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Wide spacing means elevation changes slowly over distance, indicating a gentle slope.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT shown directly by contour lines?\",\"options\":[\"Slope steepness\",\"Elevation\",\"Landform shape\",\"Rock type\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Contour lines show elevation and shape, but not the type of rock or soil.\"},{\"q\":\"How is gradient calculated on a contour map?\",\"options\":[\"Horizontal equivalent ÷ Vertical interval\",\"Vertical interval × Horizontal equivalent\",\"Vertical interval ÷ Horizontal equivalent\",\"Vertical interval + Horizontal equivalent\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Gradient = Vertical rise (interval) \\/ Horizontal run (equivalent), expressing slope steepness.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22340,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Sources of History','[\"Sources of history are evidence that help us understand the past.\",\"There are three main types of sources: material, written and oral.\",\"Material sources include coins, inscriptions, monuments, sculptures, buildings and everyday objects.\",\"Written sources are documents, books, letters, chronicles, travelogues, biographies and legal records written on paper, palm leaf, copper plate etc.\",\"Oral sources are folk songs, legends, powadas, stories, proverbs and oral histories passed down by word of mouth.\",\"Coins give information about rulers, economy, language and religion through their designs and inscriptions.\",\"Inscriptions on stone or copper plates record royal edicts, land grants, religious donations and historical events.\",\"Monuments such as forts, temples, palaces and graves show architectural style, technology and social life of a period.\",\"Written records like chronicles (bakhar), travelogues (tavarikh) and biographies provide narrative accounts of events and people.\",\"Oral traditions such as powadas and folk tales reveal cultural values, social norms and everyday life of ordinary people.\",\"All sources must be evaluated for authenticity, reliability, bias and context before using them in history.\",\"Authentic evidence is evidence that can be verified by cross‑checking with other contemporary sources.\",\"Material sources are usually more reliable for dating events because they are harder to change.\",\"Written sources can be biased if written by a ruler’s court or a religious institution.\",\"Oral sources may contain exaggerations or metaphors, so they need careful interpretation.\",\"The four factors important for history are individuals, society, place and time.\",\"History is a systematic, chronological account of the past based on evidence.\",\"Evaluating sources involves checking authenticity, internal consistency and external corroboration.\",\"A stone inscription is considered an authentic source because it is a physical record that can be dated and verified.\",\"Monuments include forts, temples, palaces, graves, caves, sculptures and other built structures.\",\"Tarikh (or Tavarikh) is a Persian term for a chronicle or historical record written by travelers or historians.\",\"The author’s impartiality, neutrality and integrity are crucial for reliable historical writing.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a material source of history?\",\"options\":[\"Coins\",\"Inscriptions\",\"Travelogues\",\"Monuments\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Travelogues are written sources, not material.\"},{\"q\":\"What type of source records royal edicts on copper plates?\",\"options\":[\"Oral\",\"Material\",\"Written\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Copper plate inscriptions are material sources.\"},{\"q\":\"A powada is an example of which source type?\",\"options\":[\"Written\",\"Oral\",\"Material\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Powadas are folk songs passed orally.\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT one of the four important factors for history?\",\"options\":[\"Individuals\",\"Society\",\"Time\",\"Weather\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Weather is not one of the four factors.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a written source?\",\"options\":[\"Coins\",\"Inscriptions\",\"Chakri\",\"Chakri\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Inscriptions are written sources.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a tarikh record?\",\"options\":[\"A list of coins\",\"A chronicle of events\",\"A map of forts\",\"A collection of poems\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Tarikh means a chronicle.\"},{\"q\":\"Which source type is most likely to contain exaggerations?\",\"options\":[\"Material\",\"Written\",\"Oral\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Oral sources often contain exaggerations.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a monument?\",\"options\":[\"A coin\",\"A fort\",\"A poem\",\"A diary\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A fort is a monument.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of evaluating sources?\",\"options\":[\"To find new facts\",\"To verify authenticity\",\"To create new documents\",\"To ignore bias\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Evaluation checks authenticity and reliability.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is an example of a written source?\",\"options\":[\"A stone inscription\",\"A folk song\",\"A coin\",\"A fort\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Stone inscriptions are written sources.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22341,50,7,'History & Civics','en','India before Shivaji','[\"- The last powerful dynasty before Shivaji Maharaj was the Yadava dynasty, whose capital was Deogiri near Aurangabad.\",\"- The Yadavas ruled from the 9th to the 13th century and spread their influence from the Krishna River to the east and west.\",\"- The Gurjara-Pratihara and Rashtrakuta empires were earlier North Indian powers that spread from Kanauj to the south.\",\"- The Chola dynasty (8th–13th century) ruled Tamil Nadu and had naval power in the Indian Ocean, conquering parts of Karnataka and Sri Lanka.\",\"- The Delhi Sultanate began with Qutubuddin Aibak (1206–1210) and later rulers like Alauddin Khalji, Muhammad bin Tughluq, and Ibrahim Lodi.\",\"- The Bahamani Sultanate (1336–1527) was founded by Hasan Gangu and later ruled by the Adilshahi, Nizamshahi, Qutubshahi, and Baridshahi.\",\"- The Vijayanagara Empire (1336–1646) was founded by Harihara and Bukka and reached its peak under Krishnadevaraya.\",\"- The Mughal Empire (1526–1707) was founded by Babur, expanded under Akbar, and ended with Aurangzeb.\",\"- The Marathas rose under Shivaji Maharaj after the decline of the Bahamani and Mughal powers.\",\"- Key terms: Yadava, Gurjara-Pratihara, Rashtrakuta, Chola, Delhi Sultanate, Bahamani, Vijayanagara, Mughal, Maratha.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which dynasty ruled the Deccan region before the rise of the Bahamani Sultanate?\",\"options\":[\"Yadava\",\"Chola\",\"Mughal\",\"Gurjar-Pratihara\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Yadava dynasty was the dominant power in the Deccan before the Bahamani Sultanate.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the first ruler of the Bahamani Sultanate?\",\"options\":[\"Bukka\",\"Harihar\",\"Muhammad Ghuri\",\"Qutubuddin Aibak\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bukka, brother of Harihar, founded the Bahamani Sultanate in 1347 CE.\"},{\"q\":\"Which battle marked the beginning of Mughal rule in India?\",\"options\":[\"First Battle of Panipat\",\"Battle of Talikota\",\"Battle of Hampi\",\"Battle of Khanua\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The First Battle of Panipat in 1526 CE brought Babur to power.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the last Sultan of Delhi before the Mughal Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Ibrahim Lodi\",\"Muhammad bin Tughluq\",\"Qutubuddin Aibak\",\"Alamgir\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ibrahim Lodi was the final Sultan of Delhi before Babur established the Mughal Empire.\"},{\"q\":\"Which empire extended its rule from Kashmir to Bengal?\",\"options\":[\"Mughal\",\"Bahamani\",\"Vijayanagar\",\"Chola\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Mughal Empire expanded from Kashmir in the north to Bengal in the east.\"},{\"q\":\"Who founded the Vijayanagar Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Krishnadevaraya\",\"Harihar\",\"Bukka\",\"Muhammad bin Tughluq\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Krishnadevaraya is credited with founding the Vijayanagar Empire.\"},{\"q\":\"Which dynasty was known for its naval strength in the eighth century?\",\"options\":[\"Chola\",\"Yadava\",\"Gurjar-Pratihara\",\"Mughal\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Chola dynasty had a powerful navy in the eighth century.\"},{\"q\":\"Who introduced the concept of Din‑i‑Ilahi?\",\"options\":[\"Akbar\",\"Aurangzeb\",\"Shah Jahan\",\"Jahangir\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Akbar introduced the religious concept of Din‑i‑Ilahi.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region did the Bahamani Sultanate NOT control?\",\"options\":[\"Bijapur\",\"Golconda\",\"Ahmadnagar\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Delhi was not part of the Bahamani Sultanate; it was under the Delhi Sultanate.\"},{\"q\":\"Which ruler was known for his liberal religious policies?\",\"options\":[\"Akbar\",\"Aurangzeb\",\"Shah Jahan\",\"Jahangir\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Akbar was famous for his tolerant and liberal religious policies.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22342,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Religious Harmony','[\"- The Bhakti movement was a religious reform movement that started in India around the 7th century and spread across the country.\",\"- It emphasised devotion (bhakti) to a personal god and rejected rigid rituals and caste barriers.\",\"- Famous saints of the movement include Ramanuja, Ramananda, Kabir, Chaitanya Mahaprabhu, and Basaveshwara.\",\"- Ramanuja taught that God is loving and accessible to all, not just the elite.\",\"- Ramananda preached that all human beings are equal and that devotion is the path to God.\",\"- Kabir’s verses were simple and used everyday language to reach common people.\",\"- Chaitanya Mahaprabhu promoted devotion to Krishna using songs and dance.\",\"- Basaveshwara in Karnataka introduced the Lingayat faith, rejecting caste distinctions and encouraging simple living.\",\"- The movement also influenced the rise of Sikhism and Sufi traditions, promoting harmony between Hindus and Muslims.\",\"- Key features: use of local languages, emphasis on love and service, rejection of idol worship, and acceptance of all social groups.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which saint is known for teaching that God is loving and accessible to everyone?\",\"options\":[\"Ramanuja\",\"Kabir\",\"Basaveshwara\",\"Ramananda\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ramanuja emphasised a compassionate God for all.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the word \'bhakti\' mean?\",\"options\":[\"Devotion\",\"Ritual\",\"Idol\",\"Caste\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bhakti literally means devotion to God.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint’s verses were written in simple everyday language?\",\"options\":[\"Kabir\",\"Chaitanya Mahaprabhu\",\"Ramanuja\",\"Basaveshwara\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Kabir used plain language to reach common people.\"},{\"q\":\"The Lingayat faith was founded by which saint?\",\"options\":[\"Basaveshwara\",\"Ramananda\",\"Ramanuja\",\"Chaitanya Mahaprabhu\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Basaveshwara established the Lingayat movement.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a feature of the Bhakti movement?\",\"options\":[\"Use of local languages\",\"Rejection of caste barriers\",\"Strict idol worship\",\"Emphasis on love for God\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Bhakti rejected strict idol worship.\"},{\"q\":\"Sufi saints believed that the way to reach God is through what?\",\"options\":[\"Rituals\",\"Idol worship\",\"Love and devotion\",\"Caste purity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sufi teachings emphasise love and devotion.\"},{\"q\":\"Guru Nanak was the first guru of which religion?\",\"options\":[\"Sikhism\",\"Hinduism\",\"Islam\",\"Buddhism\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Guru Nanak founded Sikhism.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint is known for the saying \'Work is worship\'?\",\"options\":[\"Basaveshwara\",\"Ramanuja\",\"Kabir\",\"Ramananda\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Basaveshwara’s famous saying is \'Work is worship\'.\"},{\"q\":\"The Bhakti movement helped spread which of these?\",\"options\":[\"Caste hierarchy\",\"Ritual purity\",\"Equality and devotion\",\"Idol worship\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It promoted equality and devotion.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint composed the \'Sursagar\' in Marathi?\",\"options\":[\"Ramananda\",\"Chaitanya Mahaprabhu\",\"Surdas\",\"Basaveshwara\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Surdas wrote the Marathi poem \'Sursagar\'.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22343,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Maharashtra before Shivaji','[\"Maharashtra before Shivaji was ruled by various Muslim sultanates like Ahmadnagar, Bijapur and the Mughals.\",\"The local village head was called a Patil; he collected revenue and maintained order.\",\"A Kulkarni was a village accountant who kept records of land and revenue.\",\"The village was called a Mauja; a larger settlement was a Kasba and the district was a Pargana.\",\"A Pargana consisted of many villages; its headquarters was usually a Kasba.\",\"The people of a village were called villagers or “Mauja” people; they worked mainly in agriculture.\",\"Artisans such as carpenters, blacksmiths and weavers lived in Kasbas and made a living by trading their goods.\",\"The Portuguese, Dutch, French and British established trading posts (peths) near Kasbas to control trade.\",\"A peth was a market area adjacent to a Kasba where merchants and craftsmen sold their wares.\",\"The term “Budruk” meant a larger or older village; “Khurd” meant a smaller or newer village.\",\"During the 1630s a great famine struck Maharashtra, causing crop failure, famine and loss of livestock.\",\"The Deshmukh was the chief of a Pargana, the Deshpande was the chief of all Kulkarnis, and the Patil was the chief of a village.\",\"The Varkari movement, led by saints like Dnyaneshwar, Namdeo, Eknath and Tukaram, promoted equality, devotion and social reform.\",\"The saints used simple Marathi language (abhanga, keertans) to reach common people and to criticize social inequality.\",\"Sant Ramdas Swami, a 17th‑century saint, wrote Dasbodh, a practical guide for self‑development and governance.\",\"The Samarth Sampradaya was a devotional tradition founded by Sant Chokhamela and later followed by Sant Tukaram and others.\",\"The term “Vatan” (Arabic) meant hereditary land granted to a saint, exempt from revenue.\",\"The term “Baluta” was a share of produce paid by peasants to artisans for their services.\",\"The term “Topkar” was a custom headgear worn by Shete and Mahajan, the local merchants and money‑lenders.\",\"The term “Peth” was also called “Topkar” because of the merchants’ headgear.\",\"The Varkari pilgrimage (Vari) to Pandharpur is celebrated every year in the month of Bhadrapada.\",\"The Varkari movement encouraged people to live in harmony, equality and devotion to God.\",\"The saints’ teachings helped people cope with foreign invasions, droughts and famines.\",\"The saints’ compositions are still sung in Maharashtra’s temples and homes.\",\"The saints’ teachings influenced later leaders like Shahaji Maharaj and the Maratha warriors.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which title was given to the village head who collected revenue and maintained order?\",\"options\":[\"Patil\",\"Kulkarni\",\"Deshmukh\",\"Deshpande\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Patil was the local village head responsible for revenue collection.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the term \\\"Budruk\\\" mean?\",\"options\":[\"A small village\",\"A large or older village\",\"A market place\",\"A type of headgear\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Budruk indicates a larger or older village, while Khurd indicates a smaller one.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint wrote the work \\\"Darbodh\\\"?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Tukaram\",\"Sant Eknath\",\"Sant Ramdas Swami\",\"Sant Dnyaneshwar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sant Ramdas Swami authored Dasbodh, a practical guide for self‑development.\"},{\"q\":\"During which decade did a great famine occur in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"1620s\",\"1630s\",\"1640s\",\"1650s\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The famine of the 1630s devastated crops and livestock.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main occupation of people in a Kasba?\",\"options\":[\"Agriculture\",\"Artisanship and trade\",\"Fishing\",\"Mining\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Kasbas were hubs for artisans and traders, not primarily agricultural.\"},{\"q\":\"Which term refers to the share of produce paid by peasants to artisans?\",\"options\":[\"Baluta\",\"Budruk\",\"Khurd\",\"Vatan\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Baluta was the share of produce given to artisans for their services.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the headquarters of a Pargana usually called?\",\"options\":[\"Mauja\",\"Kasba\",\"Peth\",\"Deshmukh\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Pargana headquarters was usually a Kasba.\"},{\"q\":\"Which saint is known for the composition \\\"Amrutanubhav\\\"?\",\"options\":[\"Sant Dnyaneshwar\",\"Sant Namdeo\",\"Sant Eknath\",\"Sant Tukaram\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sant Namdeo authored the work \\\"Amrutanubhav\\\".\"},{\"q\":\"Which movement promoted equality and devotion among common people?\",\"options\":[\"Sufi\",\"Varkari\",\"Shaivite\",\"Jain\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Varkari movement, led by saints, promoted equality and devotion.\"},{\"q\":\"In which month do Varkaris go on the Pandharpur Vari?\",\"options\":[\"Bhadrapada\",\"Shravana\",\"Ashwin\",\"Kartika\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Varkari pilgrimage to Pandharpur is celebrated in Bhadrapada.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22344,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Establishment of Swaraj','[\"Shivaji Maharaj was born on 19 February 1630 at Shivneri fort.\",\"His father Shahajiraje was a prominent Sardar in the Deccan who resisted Mughal and Adilshahi powers.\",\"Shahajiraje received the jagir of Pune, Supe, Indapur, and Chakan from the Adilshah of Bijapur.\",\"Veermata Jijabai, Shivaji\'s mother, provided military training and education to him and inspired values like courage and truthfulness.\",\"Shivaji began establishing Swaraj in the Maval region, using its hilly terrain for strategic advantage.\",\"He captured forts like Torana, Rajgad (formerly Murumbdev), Kondhana, and Purandar to challenge Adilshahi control.\",\"Shivaji\'s royal seal (Rajmudra) symbolizes the growth of Swaraj like the new moon and expresses his vision for people\'s welfare.\",\"He defeated Afzalkhan in 1659 at Pratapgad through courage and strategy, boosting his reputation.\",\"Shivaji built a strong navy to counter foreign powers like the Siddi, Portuguese, and British on the western coast.\",\"He escaped from Panhala fort during a siege by Siddi Jauhar with the brave sacrifice of Baji Prabhu Deshpande at Ghod pass.\",\"Shivaji formed alliances and treaties when necessary, such as with the Adilshah after Vishalgad, to consolidate power.\",\"His companions like Tanaji Malusare, Bajiprabhu Deshpande, and others played key roles in military campaigns and fort captures.\",\"Shivaji\'s administration emphasized justice, welfare of subjects, and independent rule, forming the foundation of Swaraj.\",\"He conquered regions like Javali, Kalyan, and Bhiwandi, gaining wealth and expanding influence in Konkan and beyond.\",\"Shivaji is remembered as a visionary leader who founded the Maratha Swaraj through courage, strategy, and people\'s welfare.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Where was Shivaji Maharaj born?\",\"options\":[\"Pune fort\",\"Shivneri fort\",\"Raigad fort\",\"Pratapgad fort\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji Maharaj was born at Shivneri fort near Junnar in Pune district on 19 February 1630.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'jagir\' mean in the context of Shivaji\'s father Shahajiraje?\",\"options\":[\"Right to collect revenue from a region\",\"A type of military rank\",\"A royal title\",\"A religious endowment\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jagir means the right to enjoy the revenue of a region instead of receiving a salary.\"},{\"q\":\"Which region did Shivaji Maharaj choose to start founding Swaraj due to its geographical features?\",\"options\":[\"Konkan coast\",\"Maval region\",\"Desh region\",\"Vidarbha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji started founding Swaraj in the Maval region, which is full of hills and valleys and not easily accessible.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the original name of the fort that Shivaji renamed as Rajgad?\",\"options\":[\"Torana\",\"Murumbdev\",\"Kondhana\",\"Purandar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji repaired and renamed the fort Murumbdev as Rajgad, which became the first capital of Swaraj.\"},{\"q\":\"Who sacrificed his life to help Shivaji Maharaj escape from Panhala fort?\",\"options\":[\"Tanaji Malusare\",\"Baji Prabhu Deshpande\",\"Netoji Palkar\",\"Shiva Kashid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Baji Prabhu Deshpande held the Ghod pass against Siddi\'s army, allowing Shivaji to reach Vishalgad safely.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did Shivaji Maharaj focus on building a navy?\",\"options\":[\"To trade with Arab countries\",\"To fight Siddi, Portuguese, and British powers on the western coast\",\"To transport grains\",\"For pilgrimage\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji realized a strong naval force was necessary to counter the Siddi, Portuguese, and British powers on the western coast.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the inscription on Shivaji\'s royal seal (Rajmudra) symbolize?\",\"options\":[\"Eternal like the sun\",\"Growth like the new moon\",\"Strength like a mountain\",\"Speed like wind\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The seal means \'This seal will grow in splendour like the new moon\', symbolizing the growing glory of Swaraj.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji capture and rename as Vishalgad?\",\"options\":[\"Panhala\",\"Vasantgad\",\"Khelna\",\"Taark\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Shivaji captured the Adilshahi fort of Khelna and renamed it Vishalgad.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was sent by the Adilshah to curb Shivaji Maharaj\'s rising power?\",\"options\":[\"Shaistakhan\",\"Afzalkhan\",\"Siddi Jauhar\",\"Diler Khan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Adilshah sent Afzalkhan, a powerful and experienced general, to curb Shivaji Maharaj.\"},{\"q\":\"What quality of Veermata Jijabai is highlighted in shaping Shivaji\'s character?\",\"options\":[\"Her wealth\",\"Her military training and guidance\",\"Her political connections\",\"Her love for art\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Jijabai gave Shivaji military training, education, and instilled values like courage, truthfulness, and fearlessness.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22345,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Struggle with the Mughals','[\"Shivaji Maharaj was a brave king who fought against the Mughals and the Adilshahi to protect his Swaraj (self‑rule).\",\"He captured many forts such as Chakan, Purandar, Rajgad, Sinhgad, and later Jinji in Tamil Nadu.\",\"The Mughals, led by Aurangzeb, tried to stop him by sending armies and even putting him under house arrest in Agra.\",\"Shivaji escaped from Agra with the help of his friend Moropant Pingale and returned to Maharashtra.\",\"He fought the Mughals in several battles: the raid on Lal Mahal, the campaign to Surat, and the battle at Vani‑Dindori.\",\"In 1665 he signed the Treaty of Purandar with the Mughals, giving them 23 forts but keeping most of his own.  He later broke the treaty to regain the forts.\",\"Shivaji was crowned Chhatrapati (king) on 6 June 1674 at Raigad, a ceremony that symbolised his sovereignty.  He had a second coronation on 24 September 1674.\",\"The coronation used both Vedic and Tantric rituals and a special throne made of gold and precious jewels.\",\"After the coronation, Shivaji launched a southern campaign, capturing forts like Jinji and Vellore, which helped him control parts of Tamil Nadu.\",\"He died on 3 April 1680 at Raigad.  His younger brother Rajaram later became king and had to flee to southern forts for safety.\",\"Shivaji’s courage and strategies inspired many Maratha leaders such as Tanaji Malusare, Moropant Pingale, and Prataprao Gujar.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which Mughal emperor was defeated by Shivaji Maharaj in the Battle of Purandar?\",\"options\":[\"Akbar\",\"Aurangzeb\",\"Jahangir\",\"Shah Jahan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Aurangzeb was the Mughal emperor during the Battle of Purandar.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main objective of Shaistakhan’s invasion in 1660?\",\"options\":[\"To capture Pune\",\"To raid Surat\",\"To annex Bengal\",\"To attack Goa\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shaistakhan’s invasion aimed at raiding Surat and its trade routes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji Maharaj capture after escaping from Agra?\",\"options\":[\"Rajgad\",\"Sinhgad\",\"Panhala\",\"Tanjore\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"After escaping from Agra, Shivaji captured Rajgad.\"},{\"q\":\"The Treaty of Purandar was signed in which year?\",\"options\":[\"1663\",\"1665\",\"1667\",\"1669\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Treaty of Purandar was signed in 1665.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a part of Shivaji’s coronation ceremony?\",\"options\":[\"Gold throne\",\"Elephants\",\"Sacred fire\",\"Singing of hymns\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Singing of hymns was not mentioned as part of the coronation.\"},{\"q\":\"Who assisted Shivaji Maharaj in escaping from Agra?\",\"options\":[\"Jaisingh\",\"Mirza Raja\",\"Dilerkhan\",\"Sambhaji\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dilerkhan helped Shivaji escape from Agra.\"},{\"q\":\"The southern campaign of Shivaji Maharaj included the annexation of which fort?\",\"options\":[\"Jinji\",\"Golconda\",\"Vijayanagar\",\"Karnataka\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jinji fort in Tamil Nadu was annexed during the southern campaign.\"},{\"q\":\"Which title did Shivaji Maharaj adopt after his coronation?\",\"options\":[\"Shah\",\"Nawab\",\"Chhatrapati\",\"Sardar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"He adopted the title Chhatrapati after coronation.\"},{\"q\":\"The raid on Lal Mahal was carried out to curb the activities of which ruler?\",\"options\":[\"Aurangzeb\",\"Jahangir\",\"Shah Jahan\",\"Akbar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The raid was aimed at Aurangzeb’s increasing activities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event marked the beginning of Shivaji Maharaj’s offensive against the Mughals?\",\"options\":[\"Treaty of Purandar\",\"Raid on Lal Mahal\",\"Capture of Surat\",\"Coronation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The raid on Lal Mahal marked the start of his offensive.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22346,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Administration of Swaraj','[\"Shivaji Maharaj founded Swaraj and crowned himself king to establish independent rule.\",\"He formed a Council of Eight Ministers (Ashtapradhan Mandal) to manage different departments like finance, agriculture, army, foreign affairs, justice, religion, etc.\",\"Each minister had a specific title: Pradhan (chief), Amatya (finance), Sachiv (secretary), Waknis (correspondence), Senapati (army commander), Dabir (foreign affairs), Nyayadhish (justice), Panditrao (religion).\",\"The Ashtapradhan Mandal was responsible for collecting revenue, maintaining order, and administering conquered territories.\",\"Shivaji’s administration emphasized agriculture as the main occupation and paid farmers for land cultivation and provided bullocks, ploughs, and seeds.\",\"He introduced a feudal system where land was given as jagirs to nobles and soldiers in return for service, but he also kept a strong central control over revenue and land use.\",\"The army was divided into infantry and cavalry; the cavalry had Shiledars (horsemen) and Bargirs (village soldiers).\",\"He built many forts on hilltops and sea forts like Sindhudurg to protect the kingdom and control trade routes.\",\"A dedicated intelligence department, headed by Bahirji Naik, gathered information about enemy movements, especially regarding salt trade from Portuguese territories.\",\"Shivaji’s navy had around 400 ships, including warships like Gurab, Galbat, and Pal. He built sea forts and maintained naval bases to secure the western coast.\",\"He protected the salt industry in Konkan by monitoring Portuguese salt imports and encouraging local salt production.\",\"Shivaji’s policies aimed at welfare: he paid farmers, protected merchants, and ensured justice and religious affairs were managed by appointed officials.\",\"The administration was efficient, with a system of revenue collection, land management, and military organization that kept the Swaraj stable.\",\"Key terms: Swaraj (self-rule), Ashtapradhan Mandal (Council of Eight Ministers), Jagir (land grant), Shiledar (horseman), Bargir (village soldier), Sarnobat (chief officer), Bahirji Naik (intelligence chief).\",\"Shivaji’s governance model combined strong central authority with local administration, ensuring both military strength and economic prosperity.\",\"The forts and navy were crucial for defense and trade control, making Swaraj a formidable power in the region.\",\"Shivaji’s administration is studied as an example of early modern statecraft in India.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was the main occupation of people in Shivaji’s Swaraj?\",\"options\":[\"Mining\",\"Fishing\",\"Agriculture\",\"Trade\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Agriculture was the primary occupation in the Swaraj.\"},{\"q\":\"Who headed the intelligence department in Shivaji’s administration?\",\"options\":[\"Bahirji Naik\",\"Dabir Sumant\",\"Niraji Rawaji\",\"Mora Pingle\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bahirji Naik was the head of the intelligence department.\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of soldiers were called Shiledars?\",\"options\":[\"Infantry foot soldiers\",\"Cavalry horsemen\",\"Naval officers\",\"Artisans\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shiledars were cavalry horsemen.\"},{\"q\":\"How many departments were in the Ashtapradhan Mandal?\",\"options\":[\"Six\",\"Seven\",\"Eight\",\"Nine\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The council had eight departments.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort was built near Malvan?\",\"options\":[\"Pratapgad\",\"Sindhudurg\",\"Rajgad\",\"Pavangad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sindhudurg was built near Malvan.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main purpose of building forts in Shivaji’s Swaraj?\",\"options\":[\"To store food\",\"To control trade routes\",\"To house the king\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Forts served multiple purposes including defense, storage, and control of trade.\"},{\"q\":\"Which department was responsible for foreign relations?\",\"options\":[\"Finance\",\"Agriculture\",\"Foreign Affairs\",\"Justice\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Foreign relations were handled by the Dabir.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the name of the navy’s chief commander?\",\"options\":[\"Sarnobat\",\"Havaldar\",\"Bahirji Naik\",\"Dabir Sumant\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sarnobat was the chief officer of the navy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which department collected revenue?\",\"options\":[\"Agriculture\",\"Finance\",\"Justice\",\"Religion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The finance department (Amatya) collected revenue.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main source of revenue for Shivaji’s Swaraj?\",\"options\":[\"Salt trade\",\"Agriculture\",\"Tax on trade\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Revenue came from agriculture, salt trade, and taxes.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22347,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Ideal Ruler','[\"Shivaji Maharaj was a 17th‑century ruler who founded the Maratha Empire, also called Swaraj (self‑rule).\",\"He fought against the Mughals, Adilshahi, Siddis and Portuguese, winning many fort battles such as Panhala, Sinhagad and Sinhagad.\",\"Shivaji’s administration was based on the principles of justice, equality and welfare of the people (ryots).\",\"He paid soldiers on time and maintained strict discipline in the army. Soldiers were forbidden from looting or harming civilians.\",\"Shivaji followed a tolerant religious policy – he respected mosques, temples and churches and allowed people of all faiths to practice freely.\",\"He introduced the jagir system to reward soldiers and officers with land, but ensured that the revenue went to the state.\",\"Shivaji’s leadership qualities included courage, intelligence, foresight, organisational skill and a strong sense of duty.\",\"He encouraged his associates to be loyal, brave and to sacrifice for the welfare of the state.\",\"Shivaji’s navy was built to protect the coast and to counter foreign invasions; he built forts like Sindhudurg.\",\"He inspired other leaders such as Chhatrasal and later freedom fighters like Mahatma Gandhi and Jotirao Phule.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which fort did Shivaji capture that was a key victory against the Mughals?\",\"options\":[\"Panhala\",\"Sinhagad\",\"Raigad\",\"Junnar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sinhagad was captured in 1670, a major triumph over the Mughals.\"},{\"q\":\"What system did Shivaji use to reward his soldiers?\",\"options\":[\"Jagir\",\"Pargana\",\"Mahal\",\"Zamindari\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"He granted jagirs (land grants) to soldiers and officers.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s policy of religious tolerance meant he:\",\"options\":[\"Closed all mosques\",\"Allowed all faiths to worship\",\"Converted all people to Hinduism\",\"Built only Hindu temples\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He respected all places of worship.\"},{\"q\":\"Which navy fort did Shivaji build to protect the coast?\",\"options\":[\"Sindhudurg\",\"Gulf Fort\",\"Panhala\",\"Raigad\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Sindhudurg was a coastal fort built by Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s army was known for:\",\"options\":[\"Looting villages\",\"Strict discipline\",\"Ignoring civilians\",\"Avoiding battle\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"He enforced strict discipline and forbade looting.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s coronation in 1674 was held at:\",\"options\":[\"Raigad\",\"Panhala\",\"Sinhagad\",\"Junnar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The first coronation was at Raigad.\"},{\"q\":\"Which poet wrote a powada about Shivaji?\",\"options\":[\"Rabindranath Tagore\",\"Jotirao Phule\",\"Subramanyam Bharati\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All these poets wrote about Shivaji.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s policy of paying soldiers on time was to:\",\"options\":[\"Encourage desertion\",\"Ensure loyalty\",\"Reduce taxes\",\"Build forts\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Timely payment kept soldiers loyal.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s navy was built to:\",\"options\":[\"Attack the Mughals\",\"Defend the coast\",\"Attack the Portuguese\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"It served all these purposes.\"},{\"q\":\"Shivaji’s leadership style was best described as:\",\"options\":[\"Authoritarian\",\"Tolerant\",\"Revolutionary\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"He combined all these qualities.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22348,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Maratha Freedom Struggle','[\"The Maratha War of Independence lasted 27 years against the Mughals.\",\"It began after Shivaji Maharaj\'s death under Sambhaji Maharaj\'s leadership.\",\"Sambhaji Maharaj was born on 14 May 1657 at Purandar Fort.\",\"He received education in administration and military from age 14.\",\"Sambhaji Maharaj fought bravely against Mughals, Portuguese, and Siddi of Janjira.\",\"He attacked Revdanda fort in 1683 CE to counter Portuguese alliance with Mughals.\",\"Aurangzeb laid siege to Ramsej fort near Nashik but failed to capture it.\",\"Sambhaji Maharaj was captured and executed on 11 March 1689 by Aurangzeb.\",\"After Sambhaji\'s death, Rajaram Maharaj became Chhatrapati and fled to Jinji.\",\"Rajaram Maharaj escaped Raigad in April 1689 and later recaptured Jinji fort.\",\"Maratha generals Santaji and Dhanaji used guerrilla tactics effectively against Mughals.\",\"They once cut the golden pinnacle of Aurangzeb\'s tent in a surprise attack.\",\"Rajaram Maharaj died on 2 March 1700 at Sinhgad after a short illness.\",\"After Rajaram\'s death, Maharani Tarabai led the Maratha resistance for seven years.\",\"Tarabai used the \'Safe deposit locker system\' to recapture forts from Mughals.\",\"Aurangzeb died in 1707 CE at Ahmednagar, ending the Maratha War of Independence.\",\"Marathas emerged victorious and later controlled Delhi and much of Hindusthan.\",\"The 18th century is known as the \'Century of the Marathas\'.\",\"Maharani Yesubai protected the royal family and supported Rajaram\'s escape to Jinji.\",\"Sambhaji Maharaj knew Sanskrit and wrote \'Budhbhushanam\' on polity and kingly virtues.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who was the eldest son of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj?\",\"options\":[\"Rajaram Maharaj\",\"Sambhaji Maharaj\",\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Ramraja Maharaj\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sambhaji Maharaj was the eldest son of Shivaji Maharaj, born in 1657.\"},{\"q\":\"In which year did Sambhaji Maharaj attack the Revdanda fort of the Portuguese?\",\"options\":[\"1680 CE\",\"1683 CE\",\"1689 CE\",\"1700 CE\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sambhaji Maharaj attacked Revdanda fort in 1683 CE to counter the Portuguese-Mughal alliance.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fort did Rajaram Maharaj escape to after leaving Raigad in 1689?\",\"options\":[\"Purandar\",\"Sinhgad\",\"Jinji\",\"Raigad\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Rajaram Maharaj escaped to the fort of Jinji in the south after the Mughals captured Raigad.\"},{\"q\":\"Who showed great valour in the battle against the Portuguese at Goa?\",\"options\":[\"Yesaji Kank\",\"Santaji Ghorpade\",\"Dhanaji Jadhav\",\"Pralhad Niraji\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Yesaji Kank showed great valour in the battle where the Portuguese Governor was wounded.\"},{\"q\":\"What strategy did Maharani Tarabai use to recapture forts from the Mughals?\",\"options\":[\"Direct attack\",\"Alliance with British\",\"Safe deposit locker system\",\"Surrender and revolt\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Tarabai used the \'Safe deposit locker system\' where bribed fort commanders returned wealth to Maratha treasury.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha generals cut the golden pinnacle of Aurangzeb\'s tent?\",\"options\":[\"Santaji and Dhanaji\",\"Santaji Ghorpade and Vithoji Chavan\",\"Khando Ballal and Rupaji Bhosale\",\"Pralhad Niraji and Rupaji Bhosale\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Santaji Ghorpade and Vithoji Chavan cut the golden pinnacle of Aurangzeb\'s tent in a surprise attack.\"},{\"q\":\"When did Aurangzeb die, marking the end of the Maratha War of Independence?\",\"options\":[\"1689 CE\",\"1700 CE\",\"1707 CE\",\"1749 CE\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Aurangzeb died in 1707 CE at Ahmednagar, after which the Marathas emerged victorious.\"},{\"q\":\"Who composed the book \'Budhbhushanam\' on polity and kingly virtues?\",\"options\":[\"Shivaji Maharaj\",\"Sambhaji Maharaj\",\"Rajaram Maharaj\",\"Tarabai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sambhaji Maharaj composed \'Budhbhushanam\' in Sanskrit, discussing duties of king, ministers, and administration.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the dream of Emperor Aurangzeb regarding the Maratha state?\",\"options\":[\"To ally with them\",\"To destroy the Maratha state completely\",\"To make them vassals\",\"To convert them to Islam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Aurangzeb dreamed of destroying the Maratha state completely using his military and financial power.\"},{\"q\":\"Who looked after the administration and gave powers to Maharani Yesubai during Rajaram Maharaj\'s reign?\",\"options\":[\"Sambhaji Maharaj\",\"Rajaram Maharaj\",\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Tarabai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rajaram Maharaj continued Shivaji\'s legacy by giving administrative powers to Maharani Yesubai and promoting welfare policies.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22349,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Expansion of Maratha Power','[\"1. The Maratha Empire grew after the death of Aurangzeb in 1707 when the Mughal power weakened.\",\"2. Shahu Maharaj was released from Mughal captivity and became the new Chhatrapati (king) of the Marathas.\",\"3. Shahu’s coronation in 1713 marked the revival of Maratha sovereignty after the war with Maharani Tarabai.\",\"4. Balaji Vishwanath became the first Peshwa (prime minister) and helped organise the Maratha administration.\",\"5. Bajirao I, son of Balaji, was a brilliant general who expanded the Maratha influence northwards.\",\"6. The Marathas fought and defeated the Nizam of Hyderabad at the Battle of Palkhed (1715).\",\"7. Bajirao’s campaigns captured the forts of Thane and Vasai from the Portuguese (1739).\",\"8. The Marathas collected ‘Chauthai’ (one‑fourth of revenue) and ‘Sardeshmukhi’ (one‑tenth) from conquered territories.\",\"9. The Marathas also secured the Subhedari (governorship) of Malwa and Bundelkhand from the Mughal emperor.\",\"10. The Maratha navy, led by Kanhoji Angre, protected the Konkan coast and controlled the Arabian Sea trade routes.\",\"11. The Marathas’ policy of ‘Chauth’ and ‘Sardeshmukhi’ helped them sustain a large army and administrative network.\",\"12. Shahu’s reign saw the Marathas shift from fighting the Mughals to becoming a major power in North India.\",\"13. The Marathas’ expansion was aided by internal Mughal conflicts and the decline of the Mughal court.\",\"14. The Maratha Empire at its peak covered most of present-day Maharashtra, parts of Karnataka, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh.\",\"15. The Marathas’ rise set the stage for later conflicts with the British and the eventual establishment of the Maratha Confederacy.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which Mughal emperor’s death led to the weakening of Mughal power and the rise of the Marathas?\",\"options\":[\"Aurangzeb\",\"Shah Jahan\",\"Akbar\",\"Jahangir\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Aurangzeb died in 1707, weakening the Mughal Empire.\"},{\"q\":\"Who became the first Peshwa of the Maratha Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Bajirao I\",\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Balaji Vishwanath\",\"Kanhoji Angre\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Balaji Vishwanath was appointed Peshwa after Shahu’s release.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main revenue system the Marathas used to finance their army?\",\"options\":[\"Chauthai and Sardeshmukhi\",\"Jizya and Kharaj\",\"Zakat and Sadaqah\",\"Tithe and Tribute\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"They collected one‑fourth (Chauthai) and one‑tenth (Sardeshmukhi) of revenue.\"},{\"q\":\"Which battle saw the Marathas defeat the Nizam of Hyderabad?\",\"options\":[\"Battle of Panipat\",\"Battle of Palkhed\",\"Battle of Bhopal\",\"Battle of Vasai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Battle of Palkhed (1715) ended in a Maratha victory.\"},{\"q\":\"Who led the Maratha navy and protected the Konkan coast?\",\"options\":[\"Bajirao I\",\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Kanhoji Angre\",\"Balaji Vishwanath\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Kanhoji Angre commanded the Maratha naval forces.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha general captured the forts of Thane and Vasai from the Portuguese?\",\"options\":[\"Bajirao I\",\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Balaji Vishwanath\",\"Kanhoji Angre\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bajirao I led the campaigns in 1739.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the title given to the Maratha governor of Malwa and Bundelkhand?\",\"options\":[\"Subhedar\",\"Peshwa\",\"Chhatrapati\",\"Sardar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Marathas obtained the Subhedari (governorship) of these regions.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha ruler was crowned Chhatrapati after Shahu’s release?\",\"options\":[\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Tarabai\",\"Bajirao I\",\"Balaji Vishwanath\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shahu Maharaj was crowned Chhatrapati in 1713.\"},{\"q\":\"The Marathas’ policy of collecting Chauthai and Sardeshmukhi was primarily aimed at:\",\"options\":[\"Expanding trade with Europe\",\"Funding a large army\",\"Building temples\",\"Establishing a navy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"These revenues financed the Maratha military.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event marked the beginning of the Maratha expansion into North India?\",\"options\":[\"Battle of Panipat\",\"Battle of Palkhed\",\"Battle of Bhopal\",\"Battle of Vasai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The victory at Palkhed opened the way to North India.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22350,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Marathas as Guardians of the Nation','[\"Marathas were a powerful Indian group who ruled large parts of western and central India in the 18th century.\",\"The Peshwa was the chief military and political leader of the Maratha Empire.\",\"Shahu Maharaj was a Maratha king who helped the Marathas gain more power after Bajirao I.\",\"The Marathas fought against the Afghans led by Ahmad Shah Abdali, who invaded India several times.\",\"In 1751 the Marathas entered a treaty with the Mughal emperor to protect Delhi from Abdali’s attacks.\",\"The Marathas captured Delhi in 1752 and later pushed Abdali’s army as far as Attock (now in Pakistan).\",\"The Third Battle of Panipat (1761) was a huge defeat for the Marathas against Abdali’s forces.\",\"After Panipat the Marathas lost much territory but later regained power under Peshwa Madhavrao and Mahadji Shinde.\",\"The Marathas were known for their cavalry, artillery and a strong navy in the Konkan region.\",\"They built many forts, temples and irrigation works, and promoted education and justice.\",\"The Maratha Empire had a complex family tree of Peshwas, with many members sharing the same names.\",\"The Marathas were tolerant of different religions and allowed people of all faiths to serve in their army.\",\"The Marathas fought against the Nizam of Hyderabad, Hyder Ali of Mysore and the British East India Company.\",\"The Marathas’ decline began after the defeat at Panipat and the rise of British power in India.\",\"The Marathas’ legacy includes the city of Pune, the Maratha navy and the concept of a united India.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who was the chief military leader of the Maratha Empire?\",\"options\":[\"Shahu Maharaj\",\"Bajirao I\",\"Ahmad Shah Abdali\",\"Madhavrao\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bajirao I was the first Peshwa and the main military commander.\"},{\"q\":\"Which battle in 1761 was a major defeat for the Marathas?\",\"options\":[\"First Battle of Panipat\",\"Second Battle of Panipat\",\"Third Battle of Panipat\",\"Battle of Raigad\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Third Battle of Panipat in 1761 was a crushing loss for the Marathas.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the Marathas’ main weapon in the 18th century?\",\"options\":[\"Cavalry\",\"Artillery\",\"Naval ships\",\"Archery\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Marathas were famous for their fast cavalry.\"},{\"q\":\"Who defeated the Marathas at the Battle of Rakshasbhuvan?\",\"options\":[\"Hyder Ali\",\"Nizam of Hyderabad\",\"Ahmad Shah Abdali\",\"Mughal Emperor\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Nizam of Hyderabad defeated the Marathas at Rakshasbhuvan.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha ruler built many wells and promoted irrigation?\",\"options\":[\"Madhavrao\",\"Sawai Madhavrao\",\"Narayanrao\",\"Raghunathrao\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Peshwa Madhavrao was known for irrigation projects.\"},{\"q\":\"The Marathas captured which city in 1752?\",\"options\":[\"Agra\",\"Delhi\",\"Pune\",\"Nagpur\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"They captured Delhi in 1752 to protect it from Abdali.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the Afghan ruler that invaded India multiple times?\",\"options\":[\"Nizam of Hyderabad\",\"Hyder Ali\",\"Ahmad Shah Abdali\",\"Tipu Sultan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ahmad Shah Abdali led several invasions into India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha family member was adopted as Peshwa after Bajirao I?\",\"options\":[\"Raghunathrao\",\"Nanasaheb\",\"Sadashivrao\",\"Madhavrao\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Nanasaheb was adopted as Peshwa after Bajirao I.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the Maratha standard raised at Attock?\",\"options\":[\"A flag\",\"A sword\",\"A cannon\",\"A horse\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"They raised a flag (standard) at Attock.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha ruler was known for his honesty and hard work?\",\"options\":[\"Madhavrao\",\"Sawai Madhavrao\",\"Narayanrao\",\"Raghunathrao\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Peshwa Madhavrao was praised for his honesty and hard work.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22351,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Progress of the Empire','[\"- The Maratha Empire expanded under leaders like Malhar Rao Holkar, Ahilyabai Holkar, Mahadji Shinde, and the Bhosale of Nagpur.\",\"- Malhar Rao Holkar founded the Holkar rule in Indore and built temples, ghats, and water facilities.\",\"- Ahilyabai Holkar was a great administrator, built lakes, temples, and promoted trade and agriculture.\",\"- Mahadji Shinde (Mahadji Scindia) restored Maratha control over Delhi and the North after the defeat at Panipat.\",\"- The Bhosale of Nagpur, especially Raghuji, expanded Maratha influence into the South and East (Odisha, Bengal).\",\"- The Maratha navy, led by Kanhoji Angre, defeated Portuguese, British, and Siddhi naval forces.\",\"- The Maratha Empire faced internal strife and external pressure from the British, leading to its decline by 1818.\",\"- Key titles: Peshwa (prime minister), Sardar (chief), Vakil (chief agent).\",\"- Maratha administration introduced new laws, agricultural cess, and improved irrigation.\",\"- The Marathas used guerrilla tactics and modernized artillery with French help (e.g., de Boigne).\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who founded the Holkar rule in Indore?\",\"options\":[\"Bajirao I\",\"Malhar Rao Holkar\",\"Mahadji Shinde\",\"Ahilyabai Holkar\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Malhar Rao Holkar established the Holkar dynasty in Indore.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha leader restored Delhi after the defeat at Panipat?\",\"options\":[\"Mahadji Shinde\",\"Raghuji Bhosale\",\"Ahilyabai Holkar\",\"Bajirao II\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mahadji Shinde re‑established Maratha control over Delhi.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main contribution of Ahilyabai Holkar?\",\"options\":[\"Building forts\",\"Expanding navy\",\"Constructing temples and irrigation\",\"Leading army campaigns\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Ahilyabai Holkar built temples, lakes, and promoted agriculture.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha ruler was known for modernising artillery with French help?\",\"options\":[\"Mahadji Shinde\",\"Bajirao I\",\"Raghuji Bhosale\",\"Malhar Rao Holkar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mahadji Shinde employed French officer de Boigne to modernise artillery.\"},{\"q\":\"The Maratha navy defeated which of the following?\",\"options\":[\"Portuguese\",\"British\",\"Siddhi\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The navy defeated Portuguese, British, and Siddhi forces.\"},{\"q\":\"The Bhosale of Nagpur expanded Maratha influence into which region?\",\"options\":[\"Gujarat\",\"Odisha\",\"Punjab\",\"Bengal\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Raghuji Bhosale extended Maratha rule into Odisha.\"},{\"q\":\"Which title did Mahadji Shinde receive from the Peshwa?\",\"options\":[\"Peshwa\",\"Sardar\",\"Vakil\",\"Shinde\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Mahadji was given the title Vakil (chief agent) by the Peshwa.\"},{\"q\":\"What caused the decline of Maratha power by 1818?\",\"options\":[\"Natural disasters\",\"Internal strife and British conquest\",\"Economic depression\",\"Invasion by the Mughals\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Internal divisions and British military victories weakened the Marathas.\"},{\"q\":\"Who built the Shaniwarwada fort?\",\"options\":[\"Bajirao I\",\"Mahadji Shinde\",\"Malhar Rao Holkar\",\"Ahilyabai Holkar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Shaniwarwada was built by Bajirao I in Pune.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha leader was known for his administrative reforms in Indore?\",\"options\":[\"Mahadji Shinde\",\"Ahilyabai Holkar\",\"Malhar Rao Holkar\",\"Raghuji Bhosale\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Malhar Rao Holkar reformed Indore\'s administration.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22352,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Social Life in Maharashtra','[\"- Life of people in Maharashtra during the Maratha period (1630‑1810 CE) was largely agrarian and village‑based.\",\"- Villages were self‑sufficient; main crops were jowar, bajra, wheat, ragi, maize and rice.\",\"- Daily diet consisted of bhakari (flatbread), onions, chutney, vegetables, milk and dahi.\",\"- Barter system was used for everyday transactions; money was rarely used.\",\"- Houses were simple mud or brick constructions; wadas (large houses) were built in cities with one or two storeys.\",\"- Men wore dhoti, kurta, angarakha and mundas; women wore nine‑yard sarees and blouses.\",\"- Festivals celebrated included Gudhi Padwa, Nagpanchami, Bail‑Pola, Dasara, Diwali, Makarsankrant, Holi, Eid and Ganeshotsava.\",\"- Maratha administration had Patil (village protector) and Kulkarni (revenue officer).\",\"- Balutedars were village specialists (blacksmith, carpenter, potter, goldsmith etc.) paid in kind.\",\"- Joint family system was common and important for village affairs.\",\"- Women’s life was confined to household chores; transport was by bullocks, boats and camel riders.\",\"- Education was mainly through pathshalas (Hindu) and madarasas (Muslim); literacy was low among women.\",\"- Maratha rulers promoted architecture (wadas, ghats, forts) and painting (wall paintings, temple murals).\",\"- Sports and games like wrestling, kho‑kho, chess, dice, and folk dances (tamasha, mallakhamb) were popular.\",\"- Two major religions were Hinduism and Islam; the state followed a liberal religious policy.\",\"- Maratha rulers built temples, forts, water supply systems and underground pipes in cities like Pune and Nashik.\",\"- The period saw the rise of Maratha power and the spread of Marathi culture and literature.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a common occupation in a Maratha village?\",\"options\":[\"Blacksmith\",\"Carpenter\",\"Potter\",\"Teacher\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Teachers were not a traditional village occupation listed among the balutedars.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main purpose of the Patil in a village?\",\"options\":[\"Collecting taxes\",\"Building houses\",\"Protecting the village\",\"Teaching children\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Patil was responsible for the protection of the village.\"},{\"q\":\"Which festival is celebrated on the first day of the Hindu lunar month?\",\"options\":[\"Diwali\",\"Gudhi Padwa\",\"Ganeshotsav\",\"Eid\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gudhi Padwa marks the new year in the Marathi calendar.\"},{\"q\":\"What type of food grains were commonly grown in Maratha villages?\",\"options\":[\"Rice, wheat, jowar\",\"Corn, barley, millet\",\"Oats, rye, buckwheat\",\"Quinoa, amaranth, teff\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The text lists jowar, bajra, wheat, ragi, maize, rice as common grains.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was a major source of revenue for the Patil?\",\"options\":[\"Land grants (inam)\",\"Tax on trade\",\"Fees from schools\",\"Tolls on roads\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Patil received land as inam and part of village revenue.\"},{\"q\":\"Which sport was NOT mentioned as popular in the fairs?\",\"options\":[\"Kho-kho\",\"Chess\",\"Mallakhamb\",\"Kabaddi\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Kabaddi is not listed among the sports mentioned.\"},{\"q\":\"Which religious policy did Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj follow?\",\"options\":[\"Strictly Hindu\",\"Strictly Muslim\",\"Liberal and tolerant\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Shivaji Maharaj had a liberal religious policy, respecting all.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main mode of transport for women in the 17th century?\",\"options\":[\"Bicycles\",\"Buses\",\"Carrying goods on their heads\",\"Motorcycles\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Women carried goods on their heads or on bullocks; no motorized transport existed.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these was a characteristic of Peshwa architecture?\",\"options\":[\"Stone ghats\",\"Glass facades\",\"Concrete towers\",\"Wooden bridges\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Stone ghats built along riverbanks were a notable feature.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the primary method of everyday transactions in Maratha villages?\",\"options\":[\"Bank deposits\",\"Barter system\",\"Credit cards\",\"Digital payments\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Barter system was used for everyday transactions.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22353,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Introduction to Our Constitution','[\"A Constitution is a written document containing the basic rules for governing a country.\",\"It is the fundamental law that the government must follow.\",\"The Constitution outlines the rights and duties of citizens.\",\"It defines the relationship between citizens and the State.\",\"The Constitution includes provisions on citizenship, elections, and government powers.\",\"It establishes the rule of law and prevents misuse of power.\",\"The Indian Constitution was made by the Constituent Assembly.\",\"The Constituent Assembly had 299 members and was presided over by Dr. Rajendra Prasad.\",\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee and is called the \'Architect of the Indian Constitution\'.\",\"The Indian Constitution was adopted on 26th November 1949 and came into force on 26th January 1950.\",\"26th November is celebrated as Constitution Day and 26th January as Republic Day.\",\"The Constitution has 22 parts, 395 articles, and 12 schedules (as per final version).\",\"It reflects India’s diversity, history, and social values.\",\"The Constitution can be amended but only through a special process.\",\"It promotes justice, liberty, equality, and fraternity.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is a Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"A list of school rules\",\"A written document of provisions for governing a country\",\"A book of stories\",\"A government order\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A Constitution is a written document containing the basic rules and principles for governing a country.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is known as the \'Architect of the Indian Constitution\'?\",\"options\":[\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\",\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\",\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee and played the key role in framing the Indian Constitution.\"},{\"q\":\"When did the Indian Constitution come into force?\",\"options\":[\"15th August 1947\",\"26th November 1949\",\"26th January 1950\",\"2nd October 1950\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Indian Constitution was adopted on 26th November 1949 and came into force on 26th January 1950, marking the beginning of the Republic.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Constitution do regarding citizens\' rights?\",\"options\":[\"It ignores them\",\"It allows the government to take them away anytime\",\"It safeguards them and prevents government from taking them away\",\"It gives rights only to leaders\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Constitution includes fundamental rights that the government cannot take away, thus safeguarding citizens\' freedoms.\"},{\"q\":\"Which day is celebrated as Constitution Day in India?\",\"options\":[\"26th January\",\"15th August\",\"26th November\",\"2nd October\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"26th November is observed as Constitution Day because the Constitution was adopted on this day in 1949.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main demand of the freedom movement leaders regarding law-making in independent India?\",\"options\":[\"Laws should be made by the British\",\"Laws should be made by foreign experts\",\"Laws should be made by Indians themselves\",\"No laws should be made\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The leaders insisted that independent India should be governed by laws made by Indians, not by the British.\"},{\"q\":\"How long did it take to write the Indian Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"1 year\",\"2 years 11 months 17 days\",\"5 years\",\"6 months\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It took 2 years, 11 months, and 17 days to complete the writing of the Indian Constitution.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the President of the Constituent Assembly?\",\"options\":[\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\",\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad served as the President of the Constituent Assembly.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'rule of law\' mean in the context of the Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"Only rulers are above the law\",\"Everyone, including the government, must follow the law\",\"No laws are needed\",\"Only citizens must follow the law\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rule of law means that the government and all citizens are bound to follow the Constitution, preventing misuse of power.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a function of government as mentioned in the text?\",\"options\":[\"Defending the country\",\"Creating employment\",\"Making policies for women and children\",\"Writing novels for entertainment\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The government\'s functions include defense, employment, education, health, and social welfare, but not writing novels for entertainment.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22354,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Preamble of the Constitution','[\"The Preamble is the introduction to the Indian Constitution and states its objectives.\",\"It begins with \'We, the people of India\'.\",\"India is a Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic Republic.\",\"Sovereign means India is free from foreign control and governs itself.\",\"Socialist means reducing the gap between rich and poor; wealth should not be concentrated.\",\"Secular means all religions are equal; the state has no religion.\",\"Democratic means power lies with the people who elect representatives.\",\"Republic means the head of state is elected, not hereditary.\",\"Justice includes social, economic, and political justice.\",\"Liberty means freedom of thought, expression, belief, faith, and worship.\",\"Equality means equal status and opportunity without discrimination.\",\"Fraternity promotes a sense of brotherhood and dignity among citizens.\",\"The Preamble reflects the ideals of the freedom struggle.\",\"The Constituent Assembly drafted the Constitution; Dr. B.R. Ambedkar was its chairman.\",\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad was the President of the Constituent Assembly.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What does the term \'Sovereign\' mean in the Preamble of the Indian Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"Ruled by a king\",\"Under British control\",\"Free from foreign control and self-governing\",\"Controlled by religious leaders\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Sovereign means India is independent and not under any foreign power\'s subject.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the Preamble?\",\"options\":[\"Justice\",\"Liberty\",\"Equality\",\"Education\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The Preamble mentions Justice, Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity, but not Education.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constituent Assembly?\",\"options\":[\"Dr. Rajendra Prasad\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar is known as the \'Architect of the Indian Constitution\' and chaired the Drafting Committee.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'Secular State\' mean in the context of the Indian Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"The state follows Hinduism\",\"The state promotes one religion\",\"All religions are treated equally and the state has no religion\",\"Religion is banned in public life\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In a secular state, all religions are equal before the law and the state does not favor any religion.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do we celebrate 26th January as Republic Day?\",\"options\":[\"Independence was declared\",\"The Constitution came into force\",\"Gandhi was born\",\"The first election was held\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Indian Constitution came into effect on 26th January 1950, making India a republic.\"},{\"q\":\"Which value in the Preamble promotes a sense of brotherhood and empathy among citizens?\",\"options\":[\"Justice\",\"Liberty\",\"Equality\",\"Fraternity\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Fraternity promotes a \'we-feeling\' and dignity among all citizens.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'Democratic\' mean in the Preamble?\",\"options\":[\"Rule by the military\",\"Power lies with the people who elect representatives\",\"Rule by a king\",\"Power with religious leaders\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a democracy, the people hold sovereign power and elect their representatives to govern.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'Socialist State\' aim to achieve according to the Preamble?\",\"options\":[\"Maximize private wealth\",\"Ensure wealth is concentrated in few hands\",\"Reduce gap between rich and poor; ensure fair distribution of wealth\",\"Abolish all private property\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A Socialist State aims to minimize inequality and ensure wealth is not concentrated in a few hands.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a feature of a Republic?\",\"options\":[\"Head of state is hereditary\",\"Head of state is elected by the people\",\"Only nobles can hold office\",\"Army chooses the leader\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a Republic, public offices are held by elected individuals, not by birthright.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'Liberty\' in the Preamble guarantee to citizens?\",\"options\":[\"Freedom to break laws\",\"Freedom of thought, expression, belief, faith, and worship\",\"Freedom to ignore duties\",\"Freedom only for the majority\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Liberty means freedom of thought, expression, belief, faith, and worship, exercised responsibly.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22355,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Features of the Constitution','[\"The Constitution of India has three lists: Union List (97 subjects), State List (66 subjects), and Concurrent List (47 subjects).\",\"Union Government makes laws on subjects in the Union List, e.g., defence, foreign affairs, currency.\",\"State Government makes laws on subjects in the State List, e.g., agriculture, law and order, health.\",\"Both Union and State Governments can make laws on subjects in the Concurrent List, e.g., education, employment.\",\"Subjects not in any list are residuary subjects; Union Government has power to make laws on them.\",\"Federalism means division of powers between central and state governments for cooperative governance.\",\"India has a parliamentary system where the Legislature (Parliament) holds supreme power.\",\"Parliament consists of President, Lok Sabha, and Rajya Sabha; Council of Ministers is responsible to Lok Sabha.\",\"Judiciary is independent; judges are appointed by President and not easily removable.\",\"India provides single citizenship to all citizens, known as Indian Citizenship.\",\"Amending the Constitution is a careful process; not too easy nor too difficult, ensuring stability and flexibility.\",\"Election Commission is an independent body responsible for conducting free and fair elections in India.\",\"Fundamental Rights are guaranteed by the Constitution to ensure equality and justice for all citizens.\",\"Union Territories are directly governed by the Union Government; there are 9 Union Territories in India.\",\"India has 28 States and 9 Union Territories under one Union Government.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which list contains subjects like defence, foreign relations, and currency?\",\"options\":[\"Union List\",\"State List\",\"Concurrent List\",\"Residuary List\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Defence, foreign relations, and currency are part of the Union List, which only the Union Government can legislate on.\"},{\"q\":\"How many subjects are in the State List according to the Indian Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"97\",\"66\",\"47\",\"28\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The State List contains 66 subjects on which State Governments can make laws, such as agriculture and health.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term used for powers not mentioned in any of the three lists of the Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"Residuary Powers\",\"Concurrent Powers\",\"Union Powers\",\"State Powers\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Subjects not included in Union, State, or Concurrent Lists are residuary subjects; the Union Government has power over them.\"},{\"q\":\"Who appoints the judges of the Supreme Court and High Courts in India?\",\"options\":[\"Prime Minister\",\"President\",\"Chief Justice\",\"Parliament\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Judges are appointed by the President of India, ensuring independence of the judiciary from the executive.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a feature of the Indian parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"President is the real executive\",\"Council of Ministers is responsible to Lok Sabha\",\"Judiciary controls Parliament\",\"States have veto over central laws\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In India\'s parliamentary system, the Council of Ministers stays in office only as long as it has the support of the Lok Sabha.\"},{\"q\":\"Which list includes subjects like education, employment, and environment?\",\"options\":[\"Union List\",\"State List\",\"Concurrent List\",\"Residuary List\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Education, employment, and environment are in the Concurrent List, allowing both Union and State Governments to make laws.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum age to vote in Indian elections?\",\"options\":[\"18 years\",\"21 years\",\"25 years\",\"30 years\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Constitution grants voting rights to all citizens aged 18 years and above.\"},{\"q\":\"Which body is responsible for conducting elections in India?\",\"options\":[\"Supreme Court\",\"Election Commission\",\"Parliament\",\"President\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Election Commission is an independent constitutional body responsible for conducting free and fair elections.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'single citizenship\' mean in the Indian context?\",\"options\":[\"Citizens can have dual nationality\",\"All Indians have only Indian citizenship\",\"Citizenship varies by state\",\"Only Union Territories have citizenship\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"India provides single citizenship—every citizen is an Indian citizen, regardless of the state they reside in.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the amendment procedure of the Indian Constitution designed to be neither too easy nor too difficult?\",\"options\":[\"To allow frequent changes\",\"To prevent instability while allowing necessary changes\",\"To give power to states only\",\"To make judiciary supreme\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The procedure ensures stability by avoiding frequent changes, yet allows amendments for progress with careful consideration.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22356,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Fundamental Rights (Part 1)','[\"Fundamental rights are basic rights given by the Constitution to all citizens.\",\"They have the status of law and must be followed by everyone.\",\"Right to Equality ensures no discrimination based on religion, caste, sex, race, or place of birth.\",\"Untouchability in any form is abolished and is a cognizable offence.\",\"Titles like Raja, Maharaja, Raobahadur that create artificial hierarchy are abolished.\",\"Right to Liberty includes freedom of speech, expression, assembly, association, movement, residence, and profession.\",\"No person can be arrested or detained without reason; allegations must be proved before punishment.\",\"Right to Life includes the Right to Education for children aged 6 to 14 years.\",\"Right against Exploitation bans bonded labour, forced labour, and employment of children under 14 in hazardous places.\",\"Cultural and Educational Rights allow communities to preserve their language, script, and establish institutions for their development.\",\"Right to Freedom of Religion allows individuals to practise any religion and establish religious institutions.\",\"The State cannot impose religious taxes or make religious education compulsory in aided institutions.\",\"Right to Constitutional Remedies allows citizens to approach courts if their fundamental rights are violated.\",\"Awards like Padmashree, Padmabhushan, Padmavibhushan, and Bharat Ratna are given for distinguished service but do not confer special rights.\",\"Fundamental rights promote equality, liberty, and dignity for all individuals in society.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a part of the Right to Liberty as per the Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"Freedom of speech and expression\",\"Freedom to assemble peaceably\",\"Freedom to own private property\",\"Freedom to move freely throughout India\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Right to Liberty does not include the right to own private property; it covers speech, assembly, movement, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the status of untouchability in India according to the Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"It is a social custom\",\"It is allowed in rural areas\",\"It is abolished and a cognizable offence\",\"It is only applicable to certain castes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Untouchability in any form is abolished by law and is a cognizable offence under the Constitution.\"},{\"q\":\"Which award is the highest civilian honour in India?\",\"options\":[\"Padmashree\",\"Padmabhushan\",\"Padmavibhushan\",\"Bharat Ratna\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Bharat Ratna is the highest civilian award in India for exceptional service in any field.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Right against Exploitation prohibit?\",\"options\":[\"Children under 14 from working in factories\",\"Children under 14 from working in hazardous places\",\"All children from working after school\",\"Only girls from working in homes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Constitution prohibits employment of children under 14 years of age in hazardous places like factories and mines.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a feature of the Right to Equality?\",\"options\":[\"Special privileges for minorities\",\"Equal treatment under the law\",\"Separate laws for different religions\",\"Reservation in private jobs only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Right to Equality means equal protection of laws and no discrimination based on religion, caste, sex, etc.\"},{\"q\":\"Which freedom is NOT included in the Right to Liberty?\",\"options\":[\"Freedom to practise any profession\",\"Freedom to reside in any part of India\",\"Freedom to strike\",\"Freedom to form associations\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Right to Liberty includes profession, residence, and association, but not the right to strike, which is subject to legal restrictions.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Right to Constitutional Remedies allow citizens to do?\",\"options\":[\"Amend the Constitution\",\"Approach courts if fundamental rights are violated\",\"Vote in elections\",\"Pay less tax\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This right allows citizens to move the court for enforcement of fundamental rights when they are infringed.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following titles has been abolished by the Constitution?\",\"options\":[\"Doctor\",\"Engineer\",\"Raobahadur\",\"Teacher\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Titles like Raobahadur that create artificial social hierarchy have been abolished by the Constitution.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the age group for which the Right to Education is a Fundamental Right?\",\"options\":[\"0 to 6 years\",\"6 to 14 years\",\"14 to 18 years\",\"6 to 18 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Constitution provides free and compulsory education as a Fundamental Right to children between 6 and 14 years of age.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true about awards like Padmashree?\",\"options\":[\"They give special legal privileges\",\"They are hereditary\",\"They are only for military personnel\",\"They are recognition of distinguished service without special rights\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Awards like Padmashree are given for distinguished contribution but do not confer any special rights or privileges.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22357,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Fundamental Rights (Part 2)','[\"Fundamental Rights are basic rights guaranteed by the Constitution to all citizens.\",\"They include rights like equality, freedom, and protection against exploitation.\",\"The judiciary protects Fundamental Rights through writs.\",\"Writs are formal written orders issued by courts to enforce rights.\",\"Habeas Corpus protects against unlawful arrest and detention.\",\"Mandamus commands a public official to perform their legal duty.\",\"Prohibition stops a lower court from exceeding its jurisdiction.\",\"Quo Warranto prevents a person from holding an office they are not entitled to.\",\"Certiorari transfers a case from a lower court to a higher court for review.\",\"Directive Principles of State Policy guide the government in making laws for social and economic welfare.\",\"They are not enforceable by courts but are fundamental in governance.\",\"Examples include securing livelihood, equal pay for equal work, and promoting education.\",\"Fundamental Duties are moral obligations of citizens to promote patriotism and unity.\",\"They include respecting the Constitution, National Flag, and National Anthem.\",\"Citizens must strive for excellence, protect public property, and promote harmony.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which writ is used to protect a person from unlawful arrest?\",\"options\":[\"Mandamus\",\"Habeas Corpus\",\"Prohibition\",\"Certiorari\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Habeas Corpus means \'produce the body\' and is used to release a person illegally detained.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the writ of Mandamus do?\",\"options\":[\"Releases a detained person\",\"Orders a public official to perform duty\",\"Stops a lower court from overreach\",\"Challenges eligibility for public office\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Mandamus is a command to a public authority to perform a legal duty they have failed to do.\"},{\"q\":\"Which writ prevents a subordinate court from exceeding its jurisdiction?\",\"options\":[\"Habeas Corpus\",\"Mandamus\",\"Prohibition\",\"Quo Warranto\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Prohibition is issued by a higher court to stop a lower court from acting beyond its legal authority.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of the writ of Quo Warranto?\",\"options\":[\"To release an unlawfully detained person\",\"To challenge someone\'s right to hold a public office\",\"To transfer a case to a higher court\",\"To order government to perform a duty\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Quo Warranto means \'by what authority\' and is used to question a person\'s legal right to hold a public office.\"},{\"q\":\"Which writ is used to transfer a case from a lower court to a higher court?\",\"options\":[\"Habeas Corpus\",\"Mandamus\",\"Prohibition\",\"Certiorari\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Certiorari means \'to be certified\' and is used to quash an order of a lower court or transfer a case.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a Directive Principle of State Policy?\",\"options\":[\"Right to Equality\",\"Right to Freedom\",\"Equal pay for equal work\",\"Right to Constitutional Remedies\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Equal pay for equal work is a Directive Principle under Article 39(d), guiding the state to ensure fair wages.\"},{\"q\":\"Are Directive Principles of State Policy enforceable by courts?\",\"options\":[\"Yes, always\",\"No, they are not enforceable\",\"Only during emergencies\",\"Only if passed as law\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Directive Principles are guidelines for the state and are not justiciable, meaning courts cannot enforce them directly.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Fundamental Duty involves respecting national symbols?\",\"options\":[\"To protect the environment\",\"To abide by the Constitution and respect the National Flag and Anthem\",\"To promote harmony\",\"To strive for excellence\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first Fundamental Duty is to abide by the Constitution and respect the National Flag and National Anthem.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the age group for which parents must provide education opportunities as a Fundamental Duty?\",\"options\":[\"3 to 6 years\",\"6 to 14 years\",\"14 to 18 years\",\"5 to 15 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Under Article 51A(k), parents or guardians must provide education opportunities to children between the age of six and fourteen years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a Fundamental Duty?\",\"options\":[\"To safeguard public property\",\"To develop scientific temper\",\"To impose religious taxes\",\"To renounce practices derogatory to women’s dignity\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Imposing religious taxes is prohibited by Fundamental Rights, not a Fundamental Duty. Fundamental Duties are moral obligations, not powers to tax.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22358,50,7,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Guiding Principles and Fundamental Duties','[\"Fundamental Duties are moral obligations of citizens to promote patriotism and unity.\",\"Directive Principles guide the state to establish social and economic democracy.\",\"Fundamental Rights are justiciable; Directive Principles are non-justiciable.\",\"Fundamental Duties were added by the 42nd Amendment in 1976.\",\"Article 51A lists 11 Fundamental Duties of Indian citizens.\",\"Directive Principles aim to create a welfare state.\",\"Uniform Civil Code is a Directive Principle aiming for common personal laws.\",\"Education is a Fundamental Right for children aged 6 to 14 years.\",\"Citizens must respect the National Flag and National Anthem.\",\"Protecting public property and natural resources is a Fundamental Duty.\",\"Promoting harmony and brotherhood among all people is a Fundamental Duty.\",\"Safeguarding cultural heritage and historical monuments is a duty of citizens.\",\"Striving for excellence in individual and collective activity is a Fundamental Duty.\",\"The State shall protect monuments of historic interest and national importance.\",\"Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles complement each other in governance.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which part of the Indian Constitution contains the Fundamental Duties?\",\"options\":[\"Part III\",\"Part IV\",\"Part IVA\",\"Part V\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Fundamental Duties are listed in Part IVA of the Constitution under Article 51A.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a Fundamental Duty of Indian citizens?\",\"options\":[\"To vote in elections\",\"To pay taxes\",\"To protect public property\",\"To join the armed forces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Protecting public property and avoiding violence is a Fundamental Duty under Article 51A.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the status of Directive Principles of State Policy in courts?\",\"options\":[\"Justiciable\",\"Non-justiciable\",\"Partially justiciable\",\"Enforceable by Parliament only\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Directive Principles are non-justiciable, meaning courts cannot enforce them directly.\"},{\"q\":\"Which article deals with the Uniform Civil Code in the Directive Principles?\",\"options\":[\"Article 44\",\"Article 45\",\"Article 46\",\"Article 47\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Article 44 of the Directive Principles states that the State shall endeavor to secure a Uniform Civil Code.\"},{\"q\":\"Which age group has the right to free and compulsory education?\",\"options\":[\"5 to 15 years\",\"6 to 14 years\",\"6 to 16 years\",\"5 to 14 years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Under Article 21A, free and compulsory education is a Fundamental Right for children aged 6 to 14 years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a Fundamental Duty?\",\"options\":[\"To develop scientific temper\",\"To abide by the Constitution\",\"To receive education\",\"To safeguard public property\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Receiving education is a right, not a duty. Developing scientific temper is a Fundamental Duty under Article 51A(h).\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Directive Principle regarding monuments aim to protect?\",\"options\":[\"Only temples\",\"Only forts\",\"Monuments of historic interest and national importance\",\"Only government buildings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Directive Principle under Article 49 directs the State to protect monuments of historic interest and national importance.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are Directive Principles and Fundamental Rights considered two sides of the same coin?\",\"options\":[\"Both are justiciable\",\"Both aim to establish social and economic democracy\",\"Both were added in 1950\",\"Both apply only to citizens\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"While Fundamental Rights protect individual liberty, Directive Principles aim for social justice—together they ensure a balanced democracy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a Directive Principle related to workers\' welfare?\",\"options\":[\"Equal pay for equal work\",\"Right to form unions\",\"Right to strike\",\"Fixed working hours\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Directive Principles include securing just and humane conditions of work and ensuring that men and women receive equal pay for equal work.\"},{\"q\":\"Which activity reflects the Fundamental Duty of promoting harmony?\",\"options\":[\"Celebrating only one\'s own festivals\",\"Participating in festivals of different religions\",\"Avoiding public gatherings\",\"Criticizing other religions\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Participating in festivals of different religions promotes unity and brotherhood, which is a Fundamental Duty.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22359,59,8,'Geography','en','Local Time and Standard Time','[\"Local time is the time at a place based on the position of the Sun in the sky.\",\"The Sun is directly overhead at 12 noon at the longitude where it is noon.\",\"The Earth rotates 360° in 24 hours, so it turns 15° each hour.\",\"Every 1° of longitude corresponds to 4 minutes of time difference.\",\"Places east of the Prime Meridian are ahead of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT); places west are behind.\",\"The local time of a place is the same at all latitudes on that longitude.\",\"At the poles the Sun can be above the horizon for 6 months and below for 6 months; day length varies with season.\",\"Standard time is a single time used for an entire country or region to avoid confusion.\",\"India’s standard time (IST) is based on 82°30\'E longitude and is GMT+5:30.\",\"Time zones are usually 15° wide (1 hour), but countries may use more than one zone if their east‑west extent is large.\",\"The International Date Line is an imaginary line where the calendar date changes; it is roughly along 180° longitude.\",\"The Universal Time (UT) or Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is the reference time for all time zones.\",\"The National Physical Laboratory (NPL) in India and NIST in the USA maintain highly accurate atomic clocks.\",\"When the Sun is at the zenith (directly overhead) at a longitude, that longitude’s local time is 12 noon.\",\"The length of a shadow is longest at sunrise and sunset and shortest at noon.\",\"The direction of the shadow at noon points north in the northern hemisphere and south in the southern hemisphere.\",\"The local time at a longitude can be calculated: Local time = GMT + (longitude × 4 minutes).\",\"If a place is 60° east of Greenwich, its local time is GMT + 4 hours (240 minutes).\",\"If a place is 60° west of Greenwich, its local time is GMT – 4 hours.\",\"When two places are 120° apart, their local times differ by 8 hours.\",\"The difference in local time between two longitudes is (difference in degrees) × 4 minutes.\",\"The standard time of a country is chosen so that the time difference between its easternmost and westernmost points is less than 1 hour.\",\"The Sun rises in the east and sets in the west because of Earth’s rotation.\",\"The Sun’s apparent motion in the sky is due to Earth’s rotation, not the Sun’s motion.\",\"The Earth’s rotation speed at the equator is about 1670 km\\/h.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the local time of a place determined by?\",\"options\":[\"The position of the moon\",\"The position of the sun in the sky\",\"The position of the stars\",\"The position of the Earth’s axis\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Local time is based on the sun’s position in the sky at a given longitude.\"},{\"q\":\"How many minutes does the local time change for every 1° of longitude eastward?\",\"options\":[\"1 minute\",\"2 minutes\",\"4 minutes\",\"6 minutes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Each degree of longitude corresponds to 4 minutes of time difference.\"},{\"q\":\"If it is 12 noon at Greenwich (0°), what is the local time at 60° E?\",\"options\":[\"6 pm\",\"6 am\",\"12 pm\",\"4 pm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"60° E is 240 minutes (4 hours) ahead of Greenwich, so 4 pm local time.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a reason for using a standard time in a country?\",\"options\":[\"To avoid confusion between local times\",\"To synchronize business activities\",\"To keep the sun overhead at noon\",\"To reduce the number of time zones\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Keeping the sun overhead at noon is not a reason for a standard time.\"},{\"q\":\"The Indian Standard Time (IST) is based on which longitude?\",\"options\":[\"82° 30\' E\",\"73° E\",\"88° E\",\"90° E\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"IST is based on 82° 30\' E, the longitude of Mirzapur.\"},{\"q\":\"If it is 3 pm at Mumbai (73° E), what is the local time at Kolkata (88° E)?\",\"options\":[\"4 pm\",\"5 pm\",\"2 pm\",\"6 pm\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Difference is 15° → 1 hour ahead, so 4 pm local time at Kolkata.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the time difference between two consecutive longitudes?\",\"options\":[\"15 minutes\",\"4 minutes\",\"30 minutes\",\"60 minutes\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Each degree equals 4 minutes, so consecutive longitudes differ by 4 minutes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a standard time zone used worldwide?\",\"options\":[\"GMT\",\"UTC\",\"IST\",\"PST\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"GMT (Greenwich Mean Time) is an international standard time reference.\"},{\"q\":\"When the sun is directly overhead at a longitude, what is the local time considered?\",\"options\":[\"12 pm\",\"6 am\",\"6 pm\",\"12 am\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"12 pm (noon) is when the sun is directly overhead at that longitude.\"},{\"q\":\"Which country has only one standard time despite its large longitudinal extent?\",\"options\":[\"China\",\"India\",\"Russia\",\"Australia\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"China uses a single standard time (CST) across its wide extent.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22360,59,8,'Geography','en','Interior of the Earth','[\"The Earth’s interior is divided into three main layers: crust, mantle and core.\",\"The crust is the outermost solid layer, 30–35 km thick on average; continental crust is 40–45 km, oceanic crust 7–10 km.\",\"The mantle lies below the crust, 2 900 km thick, and is divided into upper (asthenosphere) and lower mantle.\",\"The upper mantle (asthenosphere) is partially molten and allows tectonic plates to move.\",\"The lower mantle is solid and dense, with temperatures 1 200–2 500 °C.\",\"The core starts at 2 900 km depth and extends to the centre (6 371 km).\",\"The outer core (2 900–5 100 km) is liquid iron‑nickel, 9.8 g\\/cm³, 5 000 °C.\",\"The inner core (5 100–6 371 km) is solid iron‑nickel, 13.3 g\\/cm³, ~5 500 °C.\",\"Seismic P‑waves travel through all layers; S‑waves cannot travel through the liquid outer core.\",\"Discontinuities: Mohorovicic (Moho) at 7–10 km, Gutenberg at 2 900 km, and the core‑mantle boundary at 5 100 km.\",\"The Earth’s magnetic field is generated by convection in the liquid outer core (geodynamo).\",\"The crust is the thinnest layer but has the highest density variation (2.9–3.3 g\\/cm³).\",\"The mantle’s density increases from 3.3 to 5.7 g\\/cm³ with depth.\",\"The inner core’s solid state is due to extreme pressure despite high temperature.\",\"The Earth’s interior structure explains phenomena like earthquakes, volcanoes and plate tectonics.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which layer is the thinnest in the Earth’s interior?\",\"options\":[\"Crust\",\"Mantle\",\"Outer core\",\"Inner core\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The crust is only 30–35 km thick, thinner than the other layers.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main element found in both the outer core and the mantle?\",\"options\":[\"Silicon\",\"Iron\",\"Magnesium\",\"Aluminium\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Iron is abundant in the outer core and also present in the mantle.\"},{\"q\":\"At what depth does the liquid outer core begin?\",\"options\":[\"7 km\",\"2 900 km\",\"5 100 km\",\"6 371 km\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The outer core starts at 2 900 km depth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which seismic waves cannot pass through the liquid outer core?\",\"options\":[\"Primary (P) waves\",\"Secondary (S) waves\",\"Surface waves\",\"Both P and S waves\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"S‑waves are shear waves and cannot travel through liquids.\"},{\"q\":\"The Mohorovicic discontinuity separates the crust from which layer?\",\"options\":[\"Mantle\",\"Outer core\",\"Inner core\",\"Atmosphere\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Moho marks the boundary between crust and mantle.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the approximate temperature at the centre of the Earth?\",\"options\":[\"1 200 °C\",\"3 000 °C\",\"5 500 °C\",\"10 000 °C\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The centre is about 5 500 °C.\"},{\"q\":\"Which layer is partially molten and allows tectonic plates to move?\",\"options\":[\"Crust\",\"Upper mantle (asthenosphere)\",\"Lower mantle\",\"Inner core\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The asthenosphere is partially molten.\"},{\"q\":\"The Earth’s magnetic field is generated by convection in which layer?\",\"options\":[\"Crust\",\"Mantle\",\"Outer core\",\"Inner core\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The geodynamo occurs in the liquid outer core.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a component of the crust?\",\"options\":[\"Basalt\",\"Granite\",\"Iron\",\"Silica\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Iron is not a major component of the crust.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the thickness of the oceanic crust?\",\"options\":[\"7–10 km\",\"30–35 km\",\"40–45 km\",\"2 900 km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Oceanic crust is about 7–10 km thick.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22361,59,8,'Geography','en','Humidity and Clouds','[\"Humidity is the amount of water vapour present in the air.\",\"Absolute humidity is the mass of water vapour per unit volume of air (g\\/m³).\",\"Relative humidity is the percentage of the current absolute humidity to the maximum possible at that temperature: RH = (Abs \\/ Vmax) × 100%.\",\"Saturation occurs when the air holds the maximum possible water vapour at a given temperature; RH = 100%.\",\"Warm air can hold more moisture than cold air; as air cools, its capacity decreases and RH rises.\",\"Condensation is the change of water vapour to liquid when RH reaches 100% and the temperature falls to the dew point.\",\"Clouds are formed when water vapour condenses around tiny particles (dust, salt) in the atmosphere.\",\"High clouds (cirrus, cirrocumulus, cirrostratus) form above 7000 m; medium clouds (alto‑cumulus, alto‑stratus) 2000‑7000 m; low clouds (cumulus, stratus, nimbostratus) below 2000 m.\",\"Cumulus clouds are white, dome‑shaped and indicate fair weather; cumulonimbus clouds are dark, large, and bring thunderstorms and rain.\",\"Relative humidity is usually higher in mornings and evenings and in coastal areas; it is lower in deserts.\",\"Humidity affects human comfort: high RH makes sweat evaporate slowly, causing heat stress; low RH dries skin and mucous membranes.\",\"Food preservation is influenced by humidity: high RH promotes mould growth, low RH dries food.\",\"Dew, fog, and frost are surface manifestations of condensation when the air temperature reaches the dew point.\",\"The amount of water vapour in the atmosphere is called the absolute humidity; the percentage of saturation is the relative humidity.\",\"The formation of clouds and precipitation depends on temperature, pressure, and the presence of condensation nuclei.\",\"When air rises, it cools, RH increases, and clouds form; when it descends, it warms, RH decreases, and clouds dissipate.\",\"The colour of clouds indicates their altitude and moisture content: white low clouds, grey high clouds, black storm clouds.\",\"Lightning occurs when charge separation builds up in cumulonimbus clouds; the upper part becomes positive, the lower negative.\",\"The size of cloud droplets determines whether they remain aloft or fall as rain; larger droplets fall faster.\",\"Dew point is the temperature at which RH becomes 100% and condensation begins.\",\"The relative humidity is affected by temperature, pressure, and the amount of water vapour present.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the definition of absolute humidity?\",\"options\":[\"Amount of water vapour per unit volume of air\",\"Percentage of water vapour relative to saturation\",\"Temperature at which water starts to evaporate\",\"The rate of evaporation of water\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Absolute humidity is the mass of water vapour per unit volume of air.\"},{\"q\":\"If the absolute humidity is 20 g\\/m³ and the vapour‑holding capacity is 30 g\\/m³, what is the relative humidity?\",\"options\":[\"66.7%\",\"50%\",\"75%\",\"100%\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"RH = (20\\/30)×100 = 66.7%.\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of cloud is usually associated with thunderstorms?\",\"options\":[\"Cirrus\",\"Cumulus\",\"Cumulonimbus\",\"Stratus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Cumulonimbus clouds are large, dark, and produce thunderstorm conditions.\"},{\"q\":\"When the temperature of the air drops to the dew point, what happens?\",\"options\":[\"Water evaporates\",\"Water condenses\",\"Air becomes dry\",\"Air pressure increases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"At the dew point, water vapour condenses into liquid droplets.\"},{\"q\":\"High clouds are found at what altitude range?\",\"options\":[\"Below 2000 m\",\"2000–7000 m\",\"7000–14000 m\",\"Above 14000 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"High clouds such as cirrus form above 7000 m.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a factor that increases relative humidity?\",\"options\":[\"Higher temperature\",\"Higher moisture content\",\"Lower temperature\",\"Higher pressure\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Higher temperature reduces RH for the same moisture content.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main cause of cloud formation?\",\"options\":[\"Evaporation\",\"Condensation\",\"Sublimation\",\"Precipitation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Clouds form when water vapour condenses into liquid droplets.\"},{\"q\":\"Which cloud type is most likely to bring continuous rainfall?\",\"options\":[\"Cumulus\",\"Stratus\",\"Nimbostratus\",\"Cirrus\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Nimbostratus clouds produce continuous rain.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement about humidity and human comfort is correct?\",\"options\":[\"High humidity makes sweat evaporate faster\",\"Low humidity dries skin\",\"High humidity dries skin\",\"Low humidity makes sweat evaporate faster\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Low humidity dries skin, while high humidity slows sweat evaporation.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the moisture‑holding capacity of air as it cools?\",\"options\":[\"It increases\",\"It decreases\",\"It stays the same\",\"It becomes zero\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Cooling reduces the capacity of air to hold moisture.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22362,59,8,'Geography','en','Structure of Ocean Floor','[\"Ocean floor relief includes all submerged landforms such as continental shelf, slope, abyssal plains, mid‑oceanic ridges, and trenches.\",\"Continental shelf is the shallowest part of the ocean bed, extending up to about 200 m deep and can be hundreds of kilometres wide.\",\"Continental slope is the steeper part that descends from the shelf to depths of 3 000–3 600 m.\",\"Abyssal plains are flat, deep areas beyond the slope, usually covered with fine sediments.\",\"Mid‑oceanic ridges are underwater mountain chains formed by volcanic activity at divergent plate boundaries.\",\"Trenches are deep, narrow depressions formed at convergent boundaries, e.g. Mariana Trench (≈ 11 034 m).\",\"Marine islands are the summits of submerged hills that rise above sea level.\",\"Marine deeps (or trenches) are important for fishing and for the accumulation of natural resources like oil and gas.\",\"Anthropogenic deposits (plastic, sewage, etc.) are mixed with natural sediments and can harm marine life.\",\"Mean sea level is the average of the highest high tide and lowest low tide, taken as zero; elevations are measured relative to it.\",\"The ocean floor is studied to understand plate tectonics, resource distribution, and marine ecosystems.\",\"Submarine plateaus are large, flat, elevated areas of the ocean bed, similar to continental shelves but deeper.\",\"The continental shelf is a key zone for fisheries because of shallow depth and sunlight penetration.\",\"Marine deeps are often sites of hydrothermal vents and unique biological communities.\",\"The slope and trench areas are prone to earthquakes and tsunamis due to tectonic activity.\",\"Sediment deposition on the ocean floor occurs from rivers, glaciers, volcanic ash, and human waste.\",\"The composition of deposits includes pebbles, clay, fine soil, ash, and anthropogenic materials.\",\"The depth of the Mariana Trench makes it the deepest point on Earth.\",\"The continental shelf can be narrow along some coasts and broad along others.\",\"The ocean floor’s structure changes gradually from the coast to the deep sea.\",\"Understanding ocean floor features helps in navigation, resource exploration, and environmental protection.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which part of the ocean floor is the shallowest?\",\"options\":[\"Continental slope\",\"Abyssal plain\",\"Continental shelf\",\"Mid‑oceanic ridge\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The continental shelf is the shallowest part of the ocean bed.\"},{\"q\":\"The deepest trench in the world is located in which ocean?\",\"options\":[\"Indian Ocean\",\"Atlantic Ocean\",\"Pacific Ocean\",\"Arctic Ocean\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Mariana Trench in the Pacific Ocean is the deepest.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the typical depth limit of the continental shelf?\",\"options\":[\"Up to 200 m\",\"Up to 500 m\",\"Up to 1000 m\",\"Up to 2000 m\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The continental shelf usually extends to about 200 m depth.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a marine island?\",\"options\":[\"Mount Everest\",\"Iceland\",\"Mount Fuji\",\"Kilimanjaro\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Iceland rises above sea level as a marine island.\"},{\"q\":\"Anthropogenic deposits on the ocean floor mainly consist of:\",\"options\":[\"Natural volcanic ash\",\"Rocks and pebbles\",\"Plastic, sewage, and waste chemicals\",\"Freshwater sediments\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Human‑made waste such as plastic and sewage forms anthropogenic deposits.\"},{\"q\":\"The continental slope descends to depths of about:\",\"options\":[\"200 m\",\"1000 m\",\"3000–3600 m\",\"8000 m\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The slope typically goes from 200 m to 3000–3600 m.\"},{\"q\":\"Which feature is formed by volcanic activity at divergent plate boundaries?\",\"options\":[\"Mid‑oceanic ridge\",\"Trench\",\"Continental shelf\",\"Abyssal plain\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mid‑oceanic ridges form where plates diverge.\"},{\"q\":\"The term \'marine deeps\' refers to:\",\"options\":[\"Shallow coastal areas\",\"Deep, narrow trenches\",\"Flat plains\",\"Submerged hills\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Marine deeps are deep, narrow, steep trenches.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a typical sediment on the ocean floor?\",\"options\":[\"Pebbles\",\"Clay\",\"Volcanic ash\",\"Mount Everest\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Mount Everest is a mountain, not a sediment.\"},{\"q\":\"The ocean floor is studied mainly to:\",\"options\":[\"Find new islands\",\"Explore underwater caves\",\"Understand plate tectonics and resources\",\"Build underwater cities\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Studying the ocean floor helps understand tectonics and locate resources.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22363,59,8,'Geography','en','Ocean Currents','[\"Ocean currents are large‑scale movements of seawater that transport heat, salt and nutrients across the globe.\",\"There are two main layers: surface water (up to about 500 m deep) and deep water (below 500 m).\",\"Surface currents are driven mainly by wind patterns (planetary winds) and the Coriolis effect caused by Earth’s rotation.\",\"Deep currents, also called thermohaline circulation, are driven by differences in temperature and salinity, which affect water density.\",\"The surface layer moves only about 10 % of the total ocean volume, but it is crucial for weather and climate.\",\"The speed of surface currents ranges from 0.1 to 2 km h⁻¹; deep currents are much slower, completing a full circuit in ~500 years.\",\"Warm currents flow from the equator toward the poles; cold currents flow from the poles toward the equator.\",\"The Coriolis effect makes currents deflect to the right in the Northern Hemisphere and to the left in the Southern Hemisphere, creating clockwise and counter‑clockwise gyres.\",\"When warm and cold currents meet, they form fronts rich in nutrients, supporting high fish productivity and dense fog in some areas.\",\"The Gulf Stream (warm) and Labrador Current (cold) meet near Newfoundland, creating fog and affecting local climate.\",\"Ocean currents influence shipping: they can reduce fuel consumption when ships travel with the current and increase it when traveling against it.\",\"Cold currents warm coastal regions (e.g., West Africa, Southern Alaska) and reduce winter severity; warm currents cool coastal regions (e.g., West Europe).\",\"Icebergs are carried away from polar regions by currents, reducing hazards in shipping lanes.\",\"The distribution of marine life is largely determined by the meeting of warm and cold currents, creating rich fishing grounds.\",\"Human settlements often develop along productive coastal currents, especially in fishing and port towns.\",\"The speed of a current is measured in Sverdrup (1 Sv = 10⁶ m³ s⁻¹).\",\"The conveyor belt of deep water moves cold, dense water from the poles to the equator and returns warm, less dense water to the poles.\",\"Currents can be classified as warm, cold, surface, or deep, depending on temperature, salinity, and depth.\",\"The direction of a current is a result of wind, Earth’s rotation, and water density differences.\",\"Ocean currents play a key role in global climate regulation by redistributing heat from equatorial to polar regions.\",\"The meeting of currents can create fog, affect navigation, and influence local weather patterns.\",\"Understanding currents helps predict weather, manage fisheries, and plan shipping routes.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which layer of the ocean extends up to about 500 m deep?\",\"options\":[\"Surface layer\",\"Deep layer\",\"Thermocline\",\"Halocline\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The upper 500 m is called the surface layer.\"},{\"q\":\"What mainly drives surface ocean currents?\",\"options\":[\"Water density differences\",\"Planetary winds\",\"Solar radiation\",\"Tidal forces\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Wind patterns push the surface water.\"},{\"q\":\"The Coriolis effect causes currents to deflect to the right in which hemisphere?\",\"options\":[\"Northern\",\"Southern\",\"Both\",\"Neither\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"In the Northern Hemisphere the Coriolis effect deflects to the right.\"},{\"q\":\"Which current flows from the equator toward the poles?\",\"options\":[\"Cold current\",\"Warm current\",\"Deep current\",\"Tidal current\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Warm currents move from equator to poles.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a major consequence when warm and cold currents meet?\",\"options\":[\"Formation of deserts\",\"Increase in sea level\",\"Production of fog\",\"Reduction of fish\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The meeting creates fronts that often produce fog.\"},{\"q\":\"The speed of ocean currents is measured in which unit?\",\"options\":[\"Newton\",\"Sverdrup\",\"Pascal\",\"Watt\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sverdrup (Sv) measures volume transport.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a factor that affects the direction of ocean currents?\",\"options\":[\"Wind\",\"Earth’s rotation\",\"Water salinity\",\"Tide height\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Tide height does not directly set current direction.\"},{\"q\":\"The conveyor belt of deep water takes how long to complete a full circuit?\",\"options\":[\"50 years\",\"100 years\",\"200 years\",\"500 years\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Deep circulation takes about 500 years.\"},{\"q\":\"Which current is known to warm the western coast of Africa?\",\"options\":[\"Gulf Stream\",\"Labrador Current\",\"Humboldt Current\",\"Kuroshio Current\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Gulf Stream brings warm water to West Africa.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to icebergs in the North Atlantic?\",\"options\":[\"They melt instantly\",\"They are carried away by currents\",\"They sink immediately\",\"They form large ice fields\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Currents carry icebergs away from polar regions.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22364,59,8,'Geography','en','Land Use','[\"Forest land is a public utility area used for firewood, gum, grass and other forest products.\",\"Grassland or pastureland is owned by the village Panchayat or government and is mainly for grazing animals.\",\"Urban land use is divided into residential, commercial, recreational, transport and mixed uses.\",\"Residential land is the largest share of urban land and is marked in yellow on maps.\",\"Commercial land includes shops, banks, offices and is usually shown in blue.\",\"Recreational land is reserved for parks, playgrounds, swimming pools and theatres.\",\"Transport land contains roads, railways, bus stops and vehicle repair shops.\",\"Mixed land uses combine two or more of the above categories in the same area.\",\"Central Business District (CBD) is a mixed zone with high commercial activity and some residential use.\",\"In rural areas, agricultural land dominates, followed by forest and then residential land.\",\"Ownership of land can be private or public; registration is done by the revenue department.\",\"The 7\\/12 extract is a combined village form that records land ownership and rights.\",\"A property card records non‑agricultural land ownership, tax, area and access rights.\",\"Land use changes are monitored through satellite images and pie charts.\",\"In Japan, forest cover is higher and permanent agriculture is lower compared to India.\",\"In India, the share of forest land is lower and permanent agriculture is higher.\",\"Urbanisation increases demand for residential and commercial land, reducing agricultural area.\",\"Land use planning aims to balance development with environmental protection.\",\"Population density influences the type of land use: high density favours mixed and transport uses.\",\"Land use patterns can indicate the level of economic development of a region.\",\"The 7\\/12 extract helps in understanding ownership history and land use changes.\",\"A decline in agricultural land does not automatically mean food shortage; productivity matters.\",\"Land use classification is essential for urban planning, resource management and environmental conservation.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which type of land use is mainly for grazing animals?\",\"options\":[\"Residential\",\"Commercial\",\"Grassland\\/Pastureland\",\"Transport\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Grassland or pastureland is used for grazing.\"},{\"q\":\"What colour is used to mark residential land on urban maps?\",\"options\":[\"Red\",\"Blue\",\"Yellow\",\"Green\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Yellow represents residential land in the book.\"},{\"q\":\"Which land use category includes roads and railways?\",\"options\":[\"Recreational\",\"Commercial\",\"Transport\",\"Mixed\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Transport land use contains roads, railways, bus stops.\"},{\"q\":\"In Japan, forest cover is:\",\"options\":[\"Higher than India\",\"Lower than India\",\"Same as India\",\"Not mentioned\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Japan has higher forest cover than India.\"},{\"q\":\"The 7\\/12 extract is a combination of which two village forms?\",\"options\":[\"No.5 and No.6\",\"No.7 and No.12\",\"No.10 and No.15\",\"No.3 and No.8\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It combines Village Form No.7 and No.12.\"},{\"q\":\"Which land use is shown in Figure 6.3?\",\"options\":[\"Residential\",\"Commercial\",\"Transport\",\"Recreational\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Figure 6.3 depicts a property card, indicating commercial land use.\"},{\"q\":\"A decline in agricultural land area does NOT necessarily mean:\",\"options\":[\"Food shortage\",\"Lower productivity\",\"Higher yield per hectare\",\"Population growth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Decline can be offset by higher productivity.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a factor affecting land use?\",\"options\":[\"Population density\",\"Climate\",\"Ownership rights\",\"Temperature of the moon\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Moon temperature is irrelevant to land use.\"},{\"q\":\"The largest share of urban land is used for:\",\"options\":[\"Commercial\",\"Transport\",\"Residential\",\"Recreational\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Residential land occupies the largest area in cities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which land use type is most common in rural areas?\",\"options\":[\"Residential\",\"Commercial\",\"Agricultural\",\"Transport\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Agricultural land dominates rural areas.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22365,59,8,'Geography','en','Population','[\"Population is the number of people living in a particular area.\",\"Birth rate is the number of births per 1,000 people in a year.\",\"Death rate is the number of deaths per 1,000 people in a year.\",\"Natural increase = birth rate – death rate.\",\"Migration is the movement of people into or out of a region.\",\"In‑migration is people moving into a region; out‑migration is people moving out.\",\"Population density = total population ÷ area (people per sq. km).\",\"Sex ratio = number of females per 1,000 males.\",\"Age structure shows the proportion of people in different age groups.\",\"Productive population is usually defined as people aged 15–59.\",\"Dependants are those below 15 and above 60 years.\",\"High population growth can strain resources and infrastructure.\",\"Low population growth may lead to labour shortages and ageing society.\",\"Human Development Index (HDI) measures life expectancy, education and income.\",\"A high HDI indicates better quality of life and development.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a factor affecting population growth?\",\"options\":[\"Birth rate\",\"Death rate\",\"Migration\",\"Temperature\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Temperature does not directly affect population growth.\"},{\"q\":\"If the birth rate is 20 per 1,000 and the death rate is 12 per 1,000, what is the natural increase?\",\"options\":[\"8 per 1,000\",\"12 per 1,000\",\"20 per 1,000\",\"32 per 1,000\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Natural increase = 20 – 12 = 8 per 1,000.\"},{\"q\":\"A region has 500,000 people and an area of 2,000 sq. km. What is its population density?\",\"options\":[\"250 people\\/sq. km\",\"500 people\\/sq. km\",\"1,000 people\\/sq. km\",\"2,000 people\\/sq. km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Density = 500,000 ÷ 2,000 = 250 people per sq. km.\"},{\"q\":\"Which age group is considered part of the productive population in India?\",\"options\":[\"0–14 years\",\"15–59 years\",\"60 years and above\",\"All ages\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Productive population is usually 15–59 years.\"},{\"q\":\"A sex ratio of 950 means that for every 1,000 males there are how many females?\",\"options\":[\"950 females\",\"1,050 females\",\"1,000 females\",\"950 males\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The ratio is 950 females per 1,000 males.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a consequence of high population density?\",\"options\":[\"More green spaces\",\"Higher per capita income\",\"Strain on resources\",\"Lower birth rate\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"High density often strains resources.\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT listed under Physical Factors affecting population distribution?\",\"options\":[\"Location\",\"Relief\",\"Climate\",\"Industry\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Industry is an economic factor.\"},{\"q\":\"The Human Development Index (HDI) ranges from 0 to 1. A country with an HDI of 0.95 is considered what?\",\"options\":[\"Very low development\",\"Low development\",\"Medium development\",\"Very high development\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"HDI close to 1 indicates very high development.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a reason for out‑migration?\",\"options\":[\"Job opportunities\",\"Higher education\",\"Natural disaster\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All listed reasons can cause out‑migration.\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement is true about population density?\",\"options\":[\"It is the same as population growth rate.\",\"It is calculated as area divided by population.\",\"It is the number of people per unit area.\",\"It is the ratio of birth rate to death rate.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Population density = population ÷ area.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22366,59,8,'Geography','en','Industries','[\"Industries are businesses that transform raw materials into finished goods.\",\"Types of industries: Small, Medium, Heavy – based on scale and output.\",\"Iron and steel industry needs iron ore, coal, limestone and electricity; it is set near raw material sources to reduce transport cost.\",\"Agro‑based industries use agricultural products – dairy, fruit processing, jaggery, sugar, etc.\",\"Industrial development improves living standards, creates jobs, raises per‑capita income and boosts exports.\",\"Industrial estates provide low‑cost electricity, water, tax concessions and infrastructure to attract industries.\",\"Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) requires companies to spend at least 2 % of profits on social and environmental projects.\",\"Factors for locating an industry: raw material availability, labour, capital, land, transport, market, environment.\",\"Coal is used in steel making as a reducing agent and to produce coke for blast furnaces.\",\"Navaratna companies are large public sector enterprises with high autonomy and global presence.\",\"Information can be gathered quickly via internet, social media, maps and official portals.\",\"Water management: small check dams, canals, rainwater harvesting and reuse reduce scarcity.\",\"Industrial pollution includes air, water, noise and land contamination; CSR helps mitigate it.\",\"Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation (MIDC) decentralises industry across districts.\",\"Small industries are often home‑based or cottage; medium industries are larger but less capital intensive than heavy industries.\",\"Industrial development is essential for economic growth and employment generation.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which raw material is essential for the iron and steel industry?\",\"options\":[\"Coal\",\"Iron ore\",\"Limestone\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Iron ore, coal and limestone are all required for steel production.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main reason for setting up an industrial estate?\",\"options\":[\"To provide tax concessions\",\"To increase land prices\",\"To reduce transport costs\",\"To limit industrial growth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Industrial estates offer concessional rates for electricity, water and taxes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a type of industry?\",\"options\":[\"Small industry\",\"Medium industry\",\"Heavy industry\",\"Light industry\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Light industry is not a standard classification in the chapter.\"},{\"q\":\"What does CSR stand for?\",\"options\":[\"Corporate Social Responsibility\",\"Central Social Regulation\",\"Community Service Resources\",\"Corporate Sustainability Report\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"CSR means Corporate Social Responsibility.\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT a traditional factor of location?\",\"options\":[\"Raw material\",\"Labour\",\"Capital\",\"Climate\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Climate is an environmental factor, not a traditional factor.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is coal used in the steel industry?\",\"options\":[\"To produce electricity\",\"To reduce iron ore\",\"As a reducing agent and to make coke\",\"To cool the furnaces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Coal is used to produce coke and as a reducing agent in blast furnaces.\"},{\"q\":\"Which industry is classified as agro‑based?\",\"options\":[\"Dairy industry\",\"Textile industry\",\"Steel industry\",\"Chemical industry\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Dairy is an agro‑based industry.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the fastest way of obtaining industrial information?\",\"options\":[\"Reading newspapers\",\"Visiting factories\",\"Using the internet\",\"Calling the industry office\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The internet provides instant information.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a Navaratna company?\",\"options\":[\"BHEL\",\"Tata Motors\",\"Reliance Industries\",\"ICICI Bank\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"BHEL is a Navaratna public sector enterprise.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main benefit of industrial development?\",\"options\":[\"Increases pollution\",\"Reduces employment\",\"Improves standard of living\",\"Decreases exports\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Industrial development raises living standards and employment.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22367,59,8,'Geography','en','Map Scale','[\"Map scale shows the relationship between a distance on a map and the real distance on the ground.\",\"The ratio of map distance to ground distance is called the map scale.\",\"Three ways to express a scale: verbal (words), numerical (ratio), and graphical (a ruler on the map).\",\"Verbal scale example: 1 cm = 60 km – means one centimetre on the map equals sixty kilometres on the ground.\",\"Numerical scale example: 1 : 60 000 000 – one unit on the map equals sixty million units on the ground.\",\"Graphical scale is a line on the map with a scale bar, e.g. 5 cm = 50 km.\",\"Large‑scale maps show more detail and have a smaller ratio (e.g. 1 : 10 000). Small‑scale maps cover larger areas with a larger ratio (e.g. 1 : 50 000).\",\"To use a map scale, measure the distance on the map with a ruler, then multiply by the scale factor to get the real distance.\",\"If the map scale is 1 cm = 10 km, a 3 cm distance on the map equals 30 km on the ground.\",\"When a map is enlarged or reduced, the graphical scale changes but the numerical or verbal scale remains the same if the map is a true copy.\",\"To convert a verbal scale to a numerical scale, write the ratio of the two units (e.g. 1 cm = 60 km → 1 : 6 000 000).\",\"To convert a numerical scale to a verbal scale, invert the ratio and express in words (e.g. 1 : 10 000 000 → 1 cm = 10 km).\",\"The same distance on a map can be expressed in all three forms; practice converting between them.\",\"When measuring distances on a map, always use the same unit (cm, mm, inches) for the map and the same unit (km, m) for the ground.\",\"Large‑scale maps are useful for city plans, village maps, and detailed studies; small‑scale maps are used for country, state, and world maps.\",\"Remember: the left side of a graphical scale shows the map distance, the right side shows the real distance.\",\"Use a map scale to solve real‑world problems such as calculating travel distances or planning field trips.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a verbal scale?\",\"options\":[\"1:60,000,000\",\"1 cm = 60 km\",\"5 cm = 50 km\",\"1:10,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Verbal scale uses words to express the ratio.\"},{\"q\":\"If a map scale is 1:50,000, what is the distance on the ground for a 4 cm line on the map?\",\"options\":[\"200 km\",\"200 m\",\"2000 km\",\"2000 m\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"4 cm × 50,000 = 200,000 cm = 200 km.\"},{\"q\":\"A large‑scale map has a scale of:\",\"options\":[\"1:1,000,000\",\"1:10,000\",\"1:50,000\",\"1:5,000,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Large scale means a small ratio; 1:10,000 is large scale.\"},{\"q\":\"Which scale type uses a ruler on the map?\",\"options\":[\"Verbal\",\"Numerical\",\"Graphical\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Graphical scale is shown by a ruler or line on the map.\"},{\"q\":\"To convert 1 cm = 53 km to a numerical scale, you write:\",\"options\":[\"1:53,000\",\"1:5,300,000\",\"1:530,000\",\"1:530,000,000\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"53 km = 53,000 m = 53,000,000 cm → 1:53,000,000.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a small‑scale map?\",\"options\":[\"Village plan\",\"World map\",\"City street map\",\"School map\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"World map covers a large area, hence small scale.\"},{\"q\":\"If a map scale is 1:10,000,000, what is the verbal scale?\",\"options\":[\"1 cm = 10 km\",\"1 cm = 100 km\",\"1 cm = 1 km\",\"1 cm = 1000 km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"1:10,000,000 means 1 cm equals 10 km.\"},{\"q\":\"When a map is enlarged, the graphical scale changes but the numerical scale:\",\"options\":[\"remains the same\",\"changes proportionally\",\"becomes larger\",\"becomes smaller\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Numerical scale is a ratio; it stays constant for a true copy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which scale type is expressed as 1 : 60,00,000?\",\"options\":[\"Verbal\",\"Numerical\",\"Graphical\",\"None of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"This is a numerical scale.\"},{\"q\":\"A 2 cm line on a map with a scale of 1 cm = 60 km represents how many km on the ground?\",\"options\":[\"120 km\",\"60 km\",\"30 km\",\"240 km\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"2 cm × 60 km\\/cm = 120 km.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22368,59,8,'Geography','en','Field Visit','[\"- A field visit is a planned visit to a place of interest to observe and learn.\",\"- Students prepare a questionnaire before the visit to guide their observations.\",\"- After the visit, students compile information and write a report.\",\"- A report should include: 1) Introduction, 2) Personnel working in the office, 3) Nature of work, 4) Problems and solutions, 5) Acknowledgement, 6) List of references.\",\"- Use maps, tables, graphs, pictures and photographs to support the report.\",\"- The report can be presented individually or in a group in class.\",\"- The activity also involves preparing a plan for a future field trip and a questionnaire.\",\"- Key terms: questionnaire, report, field visit, observation, data collection.\",\"- Remember to cite all sources and give thanks to people who helped.\",\"- The map of the electoral constituency (Figure 10.2) is an example of a useful visual aid.\",\"- For a good report, write clear headings, use bullet points, and keep language simple.\",\"- Practice writing concise sentences and avoid long paragraphs.\",\"- Check spelling and punctuation before submitting.\",\"- Use the local time activity to understand time zones and longitude.\",\"- The local time activity involves cutting a map strip and a time strip to make a cylinder.\",\"- Matching time with longitude shows how local time changes across the globe.\",\"- A good questionnaire has clear, focused questions that help gather useful information.\",\"- The report should be organized logically and follow the order of the headings.\",\"- Always refer back to the questionnaire to ensure all points are covered.\",\"- The activity encourages teamwork and communication skills.\",\"- The field visit helps students connect classroom learning with real life.\",\"- Keep a notebook during the visit to jot down observations and questions.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a component of a field visit report?\",\"options\":[\"Introduction\",\"Personnel working in the office\",\"Weather forecast\",\"Acknowledgement\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Weather forecast is not listed in the report structure.\"},{\"q\":\"The map shown in Figure 10.2 represents a ___?\",\"options\":[\"National boundary\",\"Electoral constituency\",\"River basin\",\"Topographic map\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Figure 10.2 is described as a map of the electoral constituency.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main purpose of a questionnaire during a field visit?\",\"options\":[\"To collect data for the report\",\"To entertain students\",\"To replace the report\",\"To fill in the blanks\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Questionnaires help gather information for the report.\"},{\"q\":\"Which term refers to the process of changing water from liquid to vapor?\",\"options\":[\"Condensation\",\"Evaporation\",\"Precipitation\",\"Frost\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Evaporation is the change from liquid to vapor.\"},{\"q\":\"The code of conduct in elections is issued by the ___?\",\"options\":[\"Election Commission\",\"Central Business District\",\"Census Bureau\",\"Municipal Council\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Election Commission issues the code of conduct.\"},{\"q\":\"A borehole is primarily dug to ___?\",\"options\":[\"Collect groundwater\",\"Measure rainfall\",\"Study soil composition\",\"Observe cloud formation\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Boreholes are used to extract groundwater.\"},{\"q\":\"Which layer of the Earth is solid and contains iron?\",\"options\":[\"Mantle\",\"Crust\",\"Outer core\",\"Inner core\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The inner core is solid iron.\"},{\"q\":\"The term \'fallow land\' refers to land that is ___?\",\"options\":[\"Planted with crops\",\"Used for industrial purposes\",\"Left uncultivated for a period\",\"Covered with water\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Fallow land is left uncultivated.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a large-scale map?\",\"options\":[\"Map with scale 1:10000\",\"Map with scale 1:500000\",\"Map with scale 1:1000\",\"Map with scale 1:1000000\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Large-scale maps have a larger ratio, e.g., 1:1,000,000.\"},{\"q\":\"The process of condensation results in the formation of ___?\",\"options\":[\"Snow\",\"Fog\",\"Rain\",\"Ice\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Condensation forms fog when water droplets form on surfaces.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22369,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Tools of History','[\"Historical sources are divided into written, oral, material, and audio-visual types.\",\"Written sources include books, letters, official records, and newspapers.\",\"Oral sources consist of folk songs, powadas, inspirational speeches, and interviews.\",\"Material sources are physical objects like tools, coins, pottery, and monuments.\",\"Audio-visual sources include photographs, films, recordings, and videos.\",\"Photographs provide visual details about people, events, places, and structures.\",\"Films help us see historical events as they happened, such as the Dandi Yatra or Quit India Movement.\",\"Recordings preserve speeches and songs of leaders like Mahatma Gandhi and Subhash Chandra Bose.\",\"Powadas are Marathi ballads that narrate heroic deeds and inspire during freedom struggles.\",\"Inspirational songs motivated people during the freedom movement and are preserved in oral tradition.\",\"Newspapers during British rule spread awareness and supported social reform movements.\",\"Archives preserve written sources, but author bias must be checked for authenticity.\",\"Photographs are considered more reliable than paintings for showing real physical features.\",\"Modern technology like photography, recording, and filmmaking greatly enriched historical sources.\",\"It is important to preserve historical sources so future generations can learn from them.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of an audio-visual source of history?\",\"options\":[\"Letters\",\"Powada\",\"Film\",\"Coins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Films are audio-visual sources as they combine visual images and sound to record events.\"},{\"q\":\"Which source type includes folk songs and inspirational speeches?\",\"options\":[\"Written\",\"Oral\",\"Material\",\"Audio-visual\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Oral sources consist of spoken or sung traditions like powadas, songs, and interviews.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a photograph of a historical structure mainly help us understand?\",\"options\":[\"The emotions of people\",\"The cost of construction\",\"Its physical nature and structure\",\"The music of the period\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Photographs give visual details about the appearance, structure, and design of objects or buildings.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a written source?\",\"options\":[\"Newspaper\",\"Letter\",\"Photograph\",\"Official record\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Photographs are visual (audio-visual) sources, not written, as they do not contain text.\"},{\"q\":\"Which museum in Pune is related to Mahatma Gandhi’s history?\",\"options\":[\"Aga Khan Palace\",\"Sabarmati Ashram\",\"Cellular Jail\",\"Lakshmi Vilas Palace\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Aga Khan Palace in Pune is where Gandhi was imprisoned and now houses a memorial to him.\"},{\"q\":\"What technological development in the 20th century allowed recording of leaders’ speeches?\",\"options\":[\"Printing press\",\"Photography\",\"Audio recording\",\"Telegraph\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The invention of audio recording enabled preservation of speeches and songs as historical sources.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are photographs considered more reliable than medieval paintings for historical study?\",\"options\":[\"They are older\",\"They show imagined scenes\",\"They capture real physical features accurately\",\"They are colorful\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Photographs show actual appearance, while paintings may reflect artist’s imagination or bias.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a source of modern Indian history due to technological advancement?\",\"options\":[\"Stone inscriptions\",\"Palm leaf manuscripts\",\"Films\",\"Coins\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Films are a 20th-century technological development and serve as important audio-visual sources.\"},{\"q\":\"What kind of source is a Powada?\",\"options\":[\"Written\",\"Oral\",\"Material\",\"Audio-visual\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Powada is a traditional Marathi ballad sung or recited, making it an oral source of history.\"},{\"q\":\"During the British period, how did newspapers contribute to society?\",\"options\":[\"By printing only government news\",\"By spreading awareness and supporting reform\",\"By banning public meetings\",\"By promoting foreign rule\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Newspapers spread ideas of freedom and social reform, helping awaken public consciousness.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22370,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Europe and India','[\"Renaissance (13th-16th century) marked the revival of Greek and Roman ideas in Europe.\",\"It brought changes in art, literature, science, and philosophy, promoting humanism.\",\"Johannes Gutenberg invented the printing press around 1450 AD, spreading knowledge widely.\",\"Leonardo da Vinci was a master of art, science, and engineering; famous for \'Monalisa\' and \'The Last Supper\'.\",\"The Religious Reform movement challenged corrupt practices of the Roman Catholic Church.\",\"Geographical discoveries were driven by the need to find new sea routes to Asia after Ottoman control of Constantinople.\",\"In 1453, Ottoman Turks conquered Constantinople, blocking the land route to Asia.\",\"Bartolomeu Dias reached the Cape of Good Hope in 1487 AD.\",\"Christopher Columbus reached America in 1492 AD while seeking a western route to India.\",\"Vasco da Gama reached Calicut, India in 1498 AD via the sea route around Africa.\",\"The Industrial Revolution began in England in the 18th century with machine-based production.\",\"It led to the rise of factories, steam power, railways, and the factory system.\",\"Capitalism grew with new trade routes, leading to private investment and profit-driven economy.\",\"Colonialism is when a powerful country controls another country and exploits its resources.\",\"Imperialism is the policy of extending a country\'s power through colonization or military force.\",\"British East India Company was established in 1600 AD to trade with India.\",\"The Battle of Plassey (1757) gave the British control over Bengal after defeating Siraj-ud-Daulah.\",\"The Battle of Buxar (1764) granted the British the right to collect revenue from Bengal.\",\"The Anglo-Mysore Wars ended with British victory over Tipu Sultan in 1799.\",\"The Anglo-Sikh Wars resulted in British annexation of Punjab by 1849.\",\"The American War of Independence (1775-1783) led to the formation of the United States.\",\"The French Revolution (1789) promoted liberty, equality, and fraternity, ending monarchy in France.\",\"European rivalries in India led to the Carnatic Wars between the British and French.\",\"The British eventually won dominance in India after defeating French and Indian powers.\"]','[{\"q\":\"In which year did the Ottoman Turks conquer Constantinople?\",\"options\":[\"1450\",\"1453\",\"1492\",\"1498\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Constantinople was conquered by the Ottoman Turks in 1453, which blocked the land route to Asia.\"},{\"q\":\"Who invented the printing press around 1450 AD?\",\"options\":[\"Leonardo da Vinci\",\"Johannes Gutenberg\",\"Vasco da Gama\",\"Christopher Columbus\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Johannes Gutenberg from Germany invented the printing press around 1450 AD, which helped spread knowledge.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Portuguese sailor reached the Indian coast at Calicut in 1498?\",\"options\":[\"Bartolomeu Dias\",\"Christopher Columbus\",\"Vasco da Gama\",\"Ferdinand Magellan\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Vasco da Gama reached Calicut in 1498 by sailing around the Cape of Good Hope, establishing the sea route to India.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason for European countries to seek new sea routes to Asia?\",\"options\":[\"To spread Christianity\",\"To avoid Ottoman-controlled land routes\",\"To find new lands for settlement\",\"To compete in space exploration\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"After the Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople in 1453, they blocked the land route to Asia, prompting Europeans to find sea routes.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event is known as the \'Age of Revolutions\'?\",\"options\":[\"Renaissance period\",\"Industrial Revolution\",\"18th and 19th century political revolutions\",\"Rise of colonialism\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The 18th and 19th centuries saw major political changes like the American and French Revolutions, hence called the Age of Revolutions.\"},{\"q\":\"Which values were promoted by the French Revolution?\",\"options\":[\"Wealth, Power, Fame\",\"Liberty, Equality, Fraternity\",\"Loyalty, Obedience, Discipline\",\"Tradition, Custom, Religion\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The French Revolution of 1789 stood for the ideals of Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity.\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the British East India Company established?\",\"options\":[\"1600\",\"1757\",\"1764\",\"1857\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The British East India Company was established in 1600 AD to carry out trade with India.\"},{\"q\":\"Which battle gave the British the right to collect revenue from Bengal?\",\"options\":[\"Battle of Plassey\",\"Battle of Buxar\",\"Battle of Panipat\",\"Battle of Wandewash\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"After the Battle of Buxar in 1764, the British East India Company gained the right to collect revenue from Bengal via the Treaty of Allahabad.\"},{\"q\":\"What does colonialism mean?\",\"options\":[\"Exchange of culture between countries\",\"One country exploiting another by making it a colony\",\"Mutual trade agreement\",\"Scientific cooperation\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Colonialism means a powerful country controls another country and exploits its resources and people.\"},{\"q\":\"Which revolution began in England with the use of machines and steam power?\",\"options\":[\"French Revolution\",\"Industrial Revolution\",\"American Revolution\",\"Green Revolution\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Industrial Revolution began in England in the 18th century, marked by machine-based production and steam power.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22371,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Effects of British Rule','[\"British East India Company came to India for trade but later established political control.\",\"The Subsidiary Alliance (1798) by Lord Wellesley required Indian rulers to accept British troops in their state and not ally with any other power.\",\"The Treaty of Vasai (1802) was signed by Bajirao II with the British, leading to loss of Maratha independence.\",\"Dual Government in Bengal (1765) meant the Company collected revenue while the Nawab maintained law and order.\",\"Lord Cornwallis introduced bureaucratic reforms and restricted private trade of Company officers.\",\"Civil Services were introduced to bring efficiency; recruitment was through competitive examinations.\",\"The Military and Police were used to protect British interests and suppress revolts.\",\"A new Judicial system was set up based on English model, with Civil and Criminal Courts in districts and High Courts for appeals.\",\"Equality before law was introduced, ending caste-based differences in legal judgments.\",\"Land revenue systems varied by region; tax was fixed in cash based on land measurement, leading to farmer exploitation.\",\"Farmers often sold crops at low prices, mortgaged land, or became bankrupt due to revenue demands.\",\"British economic policies ruined traditional industries by imposing heavy taxes on Indian goods and promoting British machine-made imports.\",\"Commercialisation of agriculture shifted focus from food grains to cash crops like cotton, indigo, tea for export to England.\",\"Transport and communication developed: railways (1853), telegraph, postal system improved administration and trade.\",\"Social reforms included abolition of Sati (1829), Widow Remarriage Act (1856), and spread of Western education after 1835.\",\"Universities were established in Kolkata, Mumbai, and Chennai in 1857.\",\"British studied Indian society to govern better, leading to growth of societies like Asiatic Society of Bengal.\",\"Western-educated Indians led social reform movements in the 19th century.\",\"Economic exploitation caused famines; British did little to relieve suffering during famines from 1860–1900.\",\"New industries like textiles (Mumbai, 1854), jute (Bengal, 1855), and steel (Tata, Jamshedpur, 1907) began under Indian initiative despite limited British support.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who introduced the Subsidiary Alliance system in India?\",\"options\":[\"Lord Wellesley\",\"Lord Dalhousie\",\"Lord Cornwallis\",\"Lord Clive\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Lord Wellesley introduced the Subsidiary Alliance in 1798 to bring Indian states under British control.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Maratha ruler signed the Subsidiary Alliance with the British in 1802?\",\"options\":[\"Bajirao I\",\"Sawai Madhavrao\",\"Peshwa Nanasaheb\",\"Bajirao II\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Bajirao II signed the Subsidiary Alliance with the British in 1802, known as the Treaty of Vasai.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main feature of the Dual Government system in Bengal introduced by Robert Clive in 1765?\",\"options\":[\"Company collected revenue, Nawab maintained law and order\",\"Nawab collected revenue, Company maintained army\",\"Company controlled army, Nawab collected taxes\",\"Both revenue and law under Company\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Under Dual Government, the East India Company collected revenue while the Nawab of Bengal was responsible for civil administration and law and order.\"},{\"q\":\"Which act made the Governor of Bengal the Governor General of India?\",\"options\":[\"Pitt\'s India Act, 1784\",\"Regulating Act, 1773\",\"Charter Act, 1813\",\"Government of India Act, 1858\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Regulating Act of 1773 made the Governor of Bengal the Governor General of India, with Warren Hastings as the first holder.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main source of state income during the Pre-British period in India?\",\"options\":[\"Land revenue\",\"Customs duty\",\"Tax on trade\",\"Mining revenue\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"During the Pre-British period, land revenue was the main source of state income, based on the type of cultivated crop.\"},{\"q\":\"Under the new land revenue system, if a farmer failed to pay tax in time, what could happen?\",\"options\":[\"His crops would be seized\",\"He would be imprisoned\",\"His land would be confiscated\",\"He would be exiled\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"If a farmer failed to pay land revenue in time, his land could be confiscated by the British authorities.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a result of the commercialisation of agriculture in British India?\",\"options\":[\"Increase in food grain production\",\"Growth of traditional handicrafts\",\"Shift to cash crops for export\",\"Decrease in land revenue\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Commercialisation of agriculture meant farmers were encouraged to grow cash crops like cotton, indigo, and tea for export to England instead of food grains.\"},{\"q\":\"Which act prohibited the practice of Sati in India?\",\"options\":[\"Widow Remarriage Act, 1856\",\"Sati Prohibition Act, 1829\",\"Regulating Act, 1773\",\"Charter Act, 1833\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lord William Bentinck passed the Sati Prohibition Act in 1829, banning the practice of Sati.\"},{\"q\":\"In which year was the first railway line opened in India, running from Mumbai to Thane?\",\"options\":[\"1850\",\"1853\",\"1855\",\"1860\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The first railway in India ran from Mumbai to Thane in 1853.\"},{\"q\":\"Who established the Tata Iron and Steel Company at Jamshedpur?\",\"options\":[\"Jamsetji Tata\",\"Dadabhai Naoroji\",\"Gopal Krishna Gokhale\",\"Bal Gangadhar Tilak\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jamsetji Tata founded the Tata Iron and Steel Company in Jamshedpur in 1907.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22372,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Social and Religious Reforms','[\"Prarthana Samaj was founded by Dadoba Pandurang Tarkhadkar in 1848 to promote social reform and eradicate superstitions.\",\"It opposed idol worship, rituals, and caste discrimination, and encouraged education and social welfare.\",\"Brahmo Samaj, started by Raja Rammohan Roy in 1828, focused on monotheism, opposed caste, and promoted women\'s education.\",\"Satyashodhak Samaj, founded by Jyotirao Phule in 1873, worked for equality, widow remarriage, and education of the depressed classes.\",\"Arya Samaj, established by Swami Dayanand Saraswati in 1875, promoted return to Vedic teachings and opposed casteism.\",\"Ramkrishna Mission, founded by Swami Vivekananda in 1897, emphasized service to humanity and education.\",\"Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde organized the Depressed Classes Mission to uplift untouchables.\",\"Mahatma Savitribai Phule established the first school for girls in Pune and fought for women\'s education.\",\"Maharshi Karve founded the first women\'s university, Sharda Sadan, in 1925.\",\"The movement of religious reform in India was driven by leaders who used education, literature, and public speeches to spread ideas.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which society was founded by Dadoba Pandurang Tarkhadkar in 1848?\",\"options\":[\"Prarthana Samaj\",\"Brahmo Samaj\",\"Arya Samaj\",\"Satyashodhak Samaj\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Dadoba Tarkhadkar established Prarthana Samaj in 1848.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main slogan of Arya Samaj?\",\"options\":[\"Go Back to the Vedas\",\"Return to the Scriptures\",\"Embrace the Vedas\",\"Vedas are the Only Truth\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Arya Samaj’s slogan was ‘Go Back to the Vedas’.\"},{\"q\":\"Which reformer founded the Depressed Classes Mission?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Phule\",\"Jyotirao Phule\",\"Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde\",\"Swami Vivekananda\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde organized the Depressed Classes Mission.\"},{\"q\":\"Ramkrishna Mission was established in which year?\",\"options\":[\"1897\",\"1905\",\"1885\",\"1910\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ramkrishna Mission was founded by Swami Vivekananda in 1897.\"},{\"q\":\"Which society opposed child marriage and dowry?\",\"options\":[\"Brahmo Samaj\",\"Prarthana Samaj\",\"Satyashodhak Samaj\",\"Arya Samaj\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Satyashodhak Samaj fought against child marriage and dowry.\"},{\"q\":\"Who wrote the book ‘Satyarth Prakash’?\",\"options\":[\"Swami Dayanand Saraswati\",\"Raja Rammohan Roy\",\"Swami Vivekananda\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Swami Dayanand Saraswati wrote ‘Satyarth Prakash’.\"},{\"q\":\"Which movement was led by the Singh Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"Sikh reform movement\",\"Bengali literary movement\",\"Muslim reform movement\",\"Hindu reform movement\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Singh Sabha worked for reforms in Sikh religion.\"},{\"q\":\"The first women\'s university in India was established by whom?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Maharshi Karve\",\"Mahatma Phule\",\"Swami Vivekananda\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Maharshi Karve founded Sharda Sadan, the first women\'s university.\"},{\"q\":\"Which reformer is known for the slogan ‘Arise, Awake and Stop Not Till Goal is Achieved’?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Swami Vivekananda\",\"Mahatma Phule\",\"Raja Rammohan Roy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Swami Vivekananda used this slogan.\"},{\"q\":\"The Ramkrishna Mission’s first school for girls was established in which city?\",\"options\":[\"Pune\",\"Mumbai\",\"Kolkata\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Phule started the first girls’ school in Pune.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22373,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Beginning of Freedom Movement','[\"- Western education introduced new ideas of justice, liberty, equality and democracy to India.\",\"- British administration centralized power, built roads and railways, and introduced English as a common language.\",\"- Economic exploitation: cash crops, high land taxes, famines, decline of traditional industries and rise in unemployment.\",\"- Newspapers and periodicals (e.g., Darpan, Kesari, Amrit Bazar Patrika) spread nationalist ideas and criticised the British.\",\"- Indian National Congress (INC) was formed in 1885 to give a platform for Indians to express demands and seek constitutional reforms.\",\"- INC had two phases: Moderate (1885‑1905) and Extremist (1905‑1920). Moderates used peaceful constitutional methods; Extremists used more aggressive tactics like boycotts and public agitation.\",\"- Key moderate leaders: Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Surendranath Banerjee, Ferozshah Mehta, Lala Lajpat Rai.\",\"- Key extremist leaders: Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Lala Lajpat Rai, Bipin Chandra Pal, Lokmanya Tilak.\",\"- Partition of Bengal (1905) was a British ‘divide and rule’ strategy that intensified nationalist sentiment and led to the Swadeshi movement.\",\"- The Home Rule movement (1905‑1909) demanded self‑government for India; it was led by Annie Besant and Lokmanya Tilak.\",\"- The Lucknow Pact (1916) was an agreement between INC and the Muslim League to cooperate for Muslim rights and Indian self‑rule.\",\"- The Montagu‑Chelmsford Reforms (1919) introduced limited self‑government but disappointed many Indians.\",\"- The Servants of India Society (founded 1905 by Gopal Krishna Gokhale) aimed to promote social harmony and national service.\",\"- The anti‑partition movement in Bengal was called the ‘Geeta Rahasya’ movement, encouraging continuous public agitation.\",\"- The Swadeshi movement promoted the boycott of foreign goods and the use of Indian products.\",\"- The Indian National Congress split in 1907 into the Moderate and Extremist groups, leading to different strategies for independence.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a reason for the British to centralize administration in India?\",\"options\":[\"To improve communication\",\"To strengthen British control\",\"To promote Indian self‑government\",\"To build roads and railways\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Centralization helped Britain control India, not promote self‑government.\"},{\"q\":\"Who founded the Servants of India Society in 1905?\",\"options\":[\"Gopal Krishna Gokhale\",\"Bal Gangadhar Tilak\",\"Annie Besant\",\"Lala Lajpat Rai\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Gopal Krishna Gokhale established the society.\"},{\"q\":\"The partition of Bengal in 1905 was mainly intended to:\",\"options\":[\"Promote Indian unity\",\"Create a Hindu majority in Bengal\",\"Divide and rule\",\"Establish a separate Muslim state\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It was a divide‑and‑rule strategy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which movement encouraged the boycott of foreign goods?\",\"options\":[\"Swadeshi\",\"Partition\",\"Home Rule\",\"Geeta Rahasya\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Swadeshi promoted Indian products.\"},{\"q\":\"The Lucknow Pact was signed in which year?\",\"options\":[\"1905\",\"1916\",\"1920\",\"1930\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It was signed in 1916.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was a moderate leader of the Indian National Congress?\",\"options\":[\"Bal Gangadhar Tilak\",\"Bipin Chandra Pal\",\"Gopal Krishna Gokhale\",\"Lokmanya Tilak\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Gopal Krishna Gokhale was a moderate.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was a newspaper used by extremists to criticize the British?\",\"options\":[\"Darpan\",\"The Times of India\",\"The Hindu\",\"The Guardian\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Darpan was used by extremists.\"},{\"q\":\"The Montagu‑Chelmsford Reforms of 1919 introduced:\",\"options\":[\"Full independence for India\",\"Limited self‑government\",\"A new constitution\",\"A separate electorate for Muslims\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"They introduced limited self‑government.\"},{\"q\":\"The anti‑partition movement in Bengal was also known as:\",\"options\":[\"Geeta Rahasya\",\"Swadeshi\",\"Swaraj\",\"Home Rule\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"It was called the Geeta Rahasya movement.\"},{\"q\":\"Which leader was imprisoned in Mandalay jail for 6 years?\",\"options\":[\"Bal Gangadhar Tilak\",\"Bipin Chandra Pal\",\"Lala Lajpat Rai\",\"Annie Besant\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Tilak was imprisoned in Mandalay jail.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22374,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Non-co-operation Movement','[\"- The Non‑Cooperation Movement (1920‑1922) was a mass campaign led by Mahatma Gandhi to protest British rule in India.\",\"- It was the first large‑scale, nationwide movement where Indians refused to buy British goods, attend British schools, or use British courts.\",\"- The movement was based on the principle of Satyagraha – non‑violent resistance and truth.\",\"- Key events: Champaran Satyagraha (1917), Kheda Satyagraha (1918), Mulshi Satyagraha (1920), and the nationwide boycott of government offices and schools.\",\"- Gandhi’s slogan: “Quit India” was later adopted in 1942, but during 1920‑22 the focus was on non‑cooperation and civil disobedience.\",\"- The movement was halted in February 1922 after the Chauri Chaura incident, where a mob set fire to a police station, killing 22 officers.\",\"- The movement helped spread Swadeshi (home‑made goods), khadi, and Hindu‑Muslim unity.\",\"- The Swaraj Party was formed in 1922 by Congress leaders who wanted to contest elections and obstruct British administration from within.\",\"- The movement ended with Gandhi’s release from jail in 1924 and the start of the Civil Disobedience Movement in 1930.\",\"- Key terms: Satyagraha, Swadeshi, Khadi, Mulshi, Chauri Chaura, Non‑Cooperation Movement, Swaraj Party, Simon Commission, Nehru Report, Khilafat Movement.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was the main goal of the Non‑Cooperation Movement?\",\"options\":[\"To demand a separate state for Muslims\",\"To boycott British goods and institutions\",\"To establish a new constitution\",\"To create a new political party\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The movement aimed to boycott British goods, schools, courts and offices.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event led to the suspension of the movement in 1922?\",\"options\":[\"Champaran Satyagraha\",\"Kheda Satyagraha\",\"Chauri Chaura incident\",\"Mulshi Satyagraha\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Chauri Chaura fire caused 22 police deaths, prompting Gandhi to halt the movement.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the leader of the Swaraj Party formed in 1922?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Motilal Nehru\",\"Chittaranjan Das\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Motilal Nehru was a key founder of the Swaraj Party.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the slogan used by the Non‑Cooperation Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Quit India\",\"Swaraj is my right\",\"Non‑violence is the way\",\"India for Indians\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The slogan “Quit India” was later used; during 1920‑22 the slogan was “Non‑cooperation”.\"},{\"q\":\"Which movement was launched by Gandhi in Champaran?\",\"options\":[\"Kheda Satyagraha\",\"Champaran Satyagraha\",\"Mulshi Satyagraha\",\"Khilafat Movement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Champaran Satyagraha was the first mass protest led by Gandhi in 1917.\"},{\"q\":\"The Khilafat Movement was started to support which leader?\",\"options\":[\"Sultan of Turkey\",\"Caliph of Turkey\",\"Prime Minister of India\",\"King of England\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It aimed to support the Caliph (Khalifa) of Turkey.\"},{\"q\":\"Which act was known as the ‘Black Act’?\",\"options\":[\"Montagu‑Chelmsford Act\",\"Rowlatt Act\",\"Government of India Act\",\"Simon Commission\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Rowlatt Act was called the ‘Black Act’ by Indians.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main consequence of the Jallianwala Bagh massacre?\",\"options\":[\"Start of the Khilafat Movement\",\"Formation of the Swaraj Party\",\"Launch of the Non‑Cooperation Movement\",\"Increased support for the Rowlatt Act\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The massacre sparked the Non‑Cooperation Movement.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a feature of the Non‑Cooperation Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Boycott of British goods\",\"Refusal to pay land tax\",\"Participation in British elections\",\"Use of khadi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The movement opposed participation in British elections.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the British officer that ordered the firing at Jallianwala Bagh?\",\"options\":[\"General Dyer\",\"Sir Michael O’Dwyer\",\"Lord Curzon\",\"Sir John Simon\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"General Dyer ordered the firing.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22375,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civil Disobedience Movement','[\"Civil Disobedience Movement was a non‑violent protest against British laws, especially the Salt Act of 1930.\",\"Mahatma Gandhi launched the movement on 5 April 1930 by breaking the salt law at Dandi, Gujarat.\",\"The act of breaking the salt law was called the Salt Satyagraha or Salt March.\",\"Gandhi’s march covered about 385 km from Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi, with 78 followers.\",\"The movement spread to all parts of India – urban, rural, tribal and women participated in large numbers.\",\"Key leaders included Kasturba Gandhi, Sarojini Naidu, Khan Abdul Gafar Khan (Frontier Gandhi) and Babu Genu.\",\"The British responded with arrests, lathi charges, martial law and even death in some cases (e.g., Babu Genu).\",\"The movement inspired other acts of non‑violent resistance such as the Dharasana Satyagraha and the Solapur Satyagraha.\",\"The Indian National Congress withdrew from the Round Table Conferences (1930‑32) to focus on the movement.\",\"The movement ended in 1934 when Gandhi decided to restart it after being released from prison.\",\"The movement was based on complete non‑violence, truth (satya) and self‑discipline (satyagraha).\",\"Women’s participation was significant; Kasturba Gandhi, Kamladevi Chattopadhyay, and others led protests.\",\"The movement led to the Gandhi‑Irwin Pact (1932) and the Poona Pact, addressing separate electorates for Dalits.\",\"The movement demonstrated the power of mass civil disobedience and the willingness of Indians to suffer for freedom.\",\"Gandhi’s strategy combined moral force, mass participation, and symbolic acts to challenge British authority.\",\"The movement highlighted the importance of unity across caste, class, and gender lines.\",\"The movement’s success depended on the willingness of ordinary people to follow Gandhi’s example.\",\"The movement showed that non‑violent protest could force political negotiations with the British.\",\"The movement was a key phase in India’s struggle for independence, preceding the Quit India Movement of 1942.\",\"The movement’s legacy continues to inspire civil rights movements worldwide.\",\"Key terms: Satyagraha, Salt March, Dharasana Satyagraha, Solapur Satyagraha, Round Table Conference, Gandhi‑Irwin Pact, Poona Pact.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was the main objective of the Salt Satyagraha launched by Gandhi in 1930?\",\"options\":[\"To protest the Indian National Congress policies\",\"To break the British salt tax and monopoly\",\"To demand separate electorates for Dalits\",\"To establish a new salt factory in India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gandhi broke the salt law to protest the salt tax and monopoly imposed by the British.\"},{\"q\":\"Which leader was known as \'Frontier Gandhi\' and led the Peshawar Satyagraha?\",\"options\":[\"Kasturba Gandhi\",\"Sarojini Naidu\",\"Khan Abdul Gafar Khan\",\"Babu Genu\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Khan Abdul Gafar Khan was called \'Frontier Gandhi\' and organized the Peshawar Satyagraha.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the response of the British government to the Salt Satyagraha?\",\"options\":[\"They immediately lifted the salt tax\",\"They arrested Gandhi and used lathi charges\",\"They negotiated with Gandhi\",\"They ignored the movement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The British arrested Gandhi and used lathi charges against protesters.\"},{\"q\":\"Which pact was signed between Gandhi and the Viceroy in 1932?\",\"options\":[\"Round Table Pact\",\"Gandhi‑Irwin Pact\",\"Poona Pact\",\"Dharasana Pact\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Gandhi‑Irwin Pact was signed in 1932 between Gandhi and the Viceroy.\"},{\"q\":\"The Poona Pact addressed which issue?\",\"options\":[\"Separate electorates for Dalits\",\"A new salt factory\",\"Women\'s rights\",\"Trade union rights\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Poona Pact ended separate electorates for Dalits and provided reserved seats.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main strategy of the Civil Disobedience Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Violent uprising\",\"Economic sanctions\",\"Non‑violent protest\",\"Political lobbying\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The movement used non‑violent protest (satyagraha) to challenge British laws.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event marked the beginning of the Salt Satyagraha?\",\"options\":[\"Gandhi\'s arrest\",\"The Dandi march\",\"The Solapur Satyagraha\",\"The Dharasana Satyagraha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Dandi march on 5 April 1930 marked the start of the Salt Satyagraha.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did the Indian National Congress withdraw from the Round Table Conferences?\",\"options\":[\"They wanted to focus on the Civil Disobedience Movement\",\"They were satisfied with the British proposals\",\"They had no representatives\",\"They were forced to leave by the British\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Congress withdrew to concentrate on the Civil Disobedience Movement.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was Babu Genu?\",\"options\":[\"A British officer\",\"A salt factory owner\",\"A mill worker who was martyred\",\"A politician\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Babu Genu was a mill worker who was crushed by a truck while protesting.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a feature of the Civil Disobedience Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Nationwide participation\",\"Inclusion of women\",\"Use of violence\",\"Non‑violence\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The movement was based on non‑violence; it did not use violence.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22376,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Last Phase of Struggle for Independence','[\"- The Quit India Movement began on 8 August 1942 and was a nationwide, non‑violent protest demanding immediate independence.\",\"- It was launched by the Indian National Congress under the leadership of Mahatma Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru.\",\"- The movement called for the British to leave India, release political prisoners, end prohibition of liquor, and grant full self‑rule.\",\"- The British responded by arresting leaders, closing offices, and using force against protesters.\",\"- Despite the crackdown, millions participated; many were imprisoned and some died in the struggle.\",\"- Parallel governments were set up in some districts, providing local administration and inspiring people.\",\"- The Indian National Army (INA), led by Subhash Chandra Bose, fought against the British with Japanese support.\",\"- The INA captured some territories and established the Azad Hind Government in Singapore in 1943.\",\"- The Indian Navy and Air Force also staged revolts, notably the 1946 ‘Talwar’ incident where sailors raised the tricolor.\",\"- The Quit India Movement is also called the ‘August Kranti’ (August Revolution).\"]','[{\"q\":\"What was the main demand of the Quit India Resolution passed by the Congress in 1942?\",\"options\":[\"Immediate independence and release of political prisoners\",\"Formation of a federal state\",\"Joining the war on the side of Britain\",\"Establishment of a parliamentary system\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The resolution demanded immediate independence and the release of political prisoners.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the leader of the Indian National Army that fought against the British?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Subhash Chandra Bose\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Subhash Chandra Bose led the INA.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event is also known as the ‘August Kranti’?\",\"options\":[\"Cripps Mission\",\"Quit India Movement\",\"Second World War\",\"Independence Day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Quit India Movement is called the August Kranti.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the purpose of the Parallel Government set up during the Quit India Movement?\",\"options\":[\"To support the British administration\",\"To provide local governance and inspire people\",\"To negotiate with the British\",\"To organize the Indian Navy\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Parallel Government offered local administration and inspired the masses.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following was NOT a part of the Quit India Movement’s demands?\",\"options\":[\"Release of political prisoners\",\"Ending prohibition of liquor\",\"Granting independence\",\"Joining the war on the side of Britain\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"The movement did not ask the British to join the war.\"},{\"q\":\"Which Indian leader was arrested during the Quit India Movement and later released in 1945?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Subhash Chandra Bose\",\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Gandhi was arrested and released in 1945.\"},{\"q\":\"The ‘Talwar’ incident in 1946 involved which branch of the armed forces?\",\"options\":[\"Army\",\"Navy\",\"Air Force\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The incident involved the Indian Navy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which organization was founded by Subhash Chandra Bose after resigning from the Congress?\",\"options\":[\"Forward Bloc\",\"Indian National Army\",\"Azad Hind Government\",\"Parallel Government\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Bose founded the Forward Bloc.\"},{\"q\":\"The Indian National Army was also known by which name?\",\"options\":[\"Azad Hind Sena\",\"Azad Hind Army\",\"Indian Freedom Army\",\"National Liberation Army\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The INA was called Azad Hind Sena.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event marked the beginning of the Quit India Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Cripps Mission\",\"Cricket Match\",\"Gandhi’s Speech at Wardha\",\"The arrest of Mahatma Gandhi\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The movement began after the resolution at Wardha.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22377,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Armed Revolutionary Movement','[\"- Armed revolutionary movement: a form of struggle where Indians used weapons and violent acts to fight British rule.\",\"- Key organisations: Abhinav Bharat, Anushilan Samiti, India House, Gadar (Kakori) movement, Hindustan Socialist Republican Association (HSRA).\",\"- Abhinav Bharat (1900): secret society founded by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar, aimed to create armed revolution.\",\"- Anushilan Samiti (Bengal): underground group that trained members in bomb-making and sabotage.\",\"- India House (London, 1908): hub for Indian students abroad, funded revolutionary activities in India.\",\"- Gadar movement (1915): Indian revolutionaries in North America formed a bank to fund armed rebellion in India.\",\"- HSRA (1920s): youth-led group that carried out bomb attacks in the Central Legislative Assembly and planned the Chittagong Armoury Raid.\",\"- Chittagong Armoury Raid (1930): led by Surya Sen, aimed to seize arms from two police armories in Chittagong, Bengal.\",\"- Kakori conspiracy (1925): train robbery near Lucknow to raise funds for revolution.\",\"- Important figures: Vinayak Savarkar, Rash Behari Bose, Khudiram Bose, Prafulla Chaki, Bhagat Singh, Rajguru, Sukhdev, Chandrashekhar Azad, Surya Sen, Pritilata Waddedar.\",\"- Methods: bomb attacks, assassinations, train robbery, sabotage, underground printing of pamphlets.\",\"- Goals: overthrow British rule, end social injustices, establish a free India.\",\"- Impact: inspired many, led to harsher British repression, but kept revolutionary spirit alive.\",\"- Key dates: 1900 (Abhinav Bharat), 1908 (India House), 1915 (Gadar), 1925 (Kakori), 1930 (Chittagong raid).\",\"- Remember: Revolutionaries often faced imprisonment, torture, and execution; many became martyrs.\",\"- The movement was not a single plan but a series of coordinated attacks across India and abroad.\",\"- The British response included stricter laws, increased police surveillance, and mass arrests.\",\"- Legacy: These revolutionary acts are remembered as part of India\'s struggle for independence.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which organisation was founded by Vinayak Savarkar in 1900?\",\"options\":[\"Abhinav Bharat\",\"Anushilan Samiti\",\"India House\",\"Gadar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Savarkar started the secret society Abhinav Bharat.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main objective of the Gadar movement?\",\"options\":[\"To establish a bank in India\",\"To fund armed rebellion from abroad\",\"To create a political party\",\"To publish revolutionary literature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Gadar raised money from North American Indians for armed rebellion.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event involved a train robbery near Lucknow?\",\"options\":[\"Chittagong Armoury Raid\",\"Kakori conspiracy\",\"Bombing of Central Legislative Assembly\",\"Assassination of a judge\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Kakori conspiracy was a train robbery in 1925.\"},{\"q\":\"Who led the Chittagong Armoury Raid?\",\"options\":[\"Bhagat Singh\",\"Surya Sen\",\"Rash Behari Bose\",\"Prafulla Chaki\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Surya Sen was the commander of the raid.\"},{\"q\":\"The Anushilan Samiti was mainly active in which region?\",\"options\":[\"Punjab\",\"Bengal\",\"Maharashtra\",\"Delhi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Anushilan Samiti operated mainly in Bengal.\"},{\"q\":\"Which revolutionary was executed for his role in the 1908 bomb plot?\",\"options\":[\"Rash Behari Bose\",\"Khudiram Bose\",\"Prafulla Chaki\",\"Bhagat Singh\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Khudiram Bose was hanged after the 1908 bomb attempt.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the purpose of the India House in London?\",\"options\":[\"To train soldiers\",\"To fund the Indian Army\",\"To support Indian students and fund revolution\",\"To publish newspapers\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"India House helped Indian students and financed revolutionary work.\"},{\"q\":\"Which group carried out bomb attacks in the Central Legislative Assembly?\",\"options\":[\"HSRA\",\"Anushilan Samiti\",\"Abhinav Bharat\",\"Gadar\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"HSRA (Hindustan Socialist Republican Association) did the bomb attacks.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the chief of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Army wing?\",\"options\":[\"Chandrashekhar Azad\",\"Surya Sen\",\"Bhagat Singh\",\"Rash Behari Bose\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Azad led the army wing of HSRA.\"},{\"q\":\"The Kakori conspiracy was intended to raise funds for which purpose?\",\"options\":[\"Buying rifles for the army\",\"Funding a bank in India\",\"Supporting a political party\",\"Building a new army base\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The robbery aimed to raise money to buy arms for revolutionaries.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22378,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Struggle for Equality','[\"- Equality means giving everyone the same rights and opportunities regardless of caste, class, gender or religion.\",\"- In India, the struggle for equality began in the 19th and 20th centuries with movements by peasants, workers, women and Dalits.\",\"- Peasant movements fought against feudal landlords, money lenders and the forced cultivation of indigo and cotton.\",\"- Workers’ unions such as the All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC) were formed in 1920 to protect workers’ rights and wages.\",\"- Women’s reform movements (e.g., Satyashodhak Samaj, Arya Mahila Samaj) fought for education, voting rights and against social discrimination.\",\"- Dalit movements led by leaders like Gopal Baba Walangkar, Shivram Janba Kamble and Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar sought to abolish untouchability and caste discrimination.\",\"- The Congress Socialist Party (1934) and the Communist Party (1925) also contributed to the fight for social justice.\",\"- Key laws: The Indian Constitution (1949) guarantees equality; the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes (Prevention of Atrocities) Act (1989) protects Dalits.\",\"- Important slogans: “Educate, Organise, Agitate” (Gandhi), “Be Educated, Be Organised, Be Agitated” (Satyashodhak Samaj).\",\"- The Meerut Conspiracy Case (1929) was a major crackdown on communist activists.\",\"- The Mahad Satyagraha (1927) led by Dr. Ambedkar opened a public water tank for Dalits.\",\"- The Red Cross Society was founded by Rakhmabai Save to help women and children.\",\"- The first Indian women doctors were Dr. Anandibai Joshi (1886) and Dr. Rakhmabai Save (1925).\",\"- The “Kisan Sabha” (1918) was a farmers’ union that demanded land reforms.\",\"- The “Bombay Mill Hands Association” (1890) was an early workers’ union in Mumbai.\",\"- The “All India Women’s Conference” (1927) promoted women’s rights and education.\",\"- The “All India Scheduled Castes Federation” (1934) fought for Dalit rights.\",\"- The “Bahishkrut Bharat” newspaper was started by Ambedkar to spread his ideas.\",\"- The “Satyagraha” movement used non‑violent protest to challenge social injustices.\",\"- The “Balutedari System” was abolished in 1918 in Kolhapur to free Dalits from bonded labour.\",\"- The “Khandesh” region saw peasant revolts in 1938 due to heavy rains and crop destruction.\",\"- The “Kisan Sabha” and “AITUC” were instrumental in shaping modern Indian labour law.\",\"- The “Red Cross Society” at Rajkot was opened by Sane Guruji to help the poor.\",\"- The “All India Trade Union Congress” was led by Lala Lajpat Rai and Narayan Meghaji Lokhande in its early years.\",\"- The “Satyashodhak Samaj” was founded by Jyotirao Phle and Mahatma Phule to fight untouchability.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which movement fought against forced cultivation of indigo in Bengal?\",\"options\":[\"Kisan Sabha\",\"Sane Guruji’s movement\",\"Indigo Rebellion\",\"Peasant Union\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Indigo Rebellion was a peasant uprising against indigo cultivation.\"},{\"q\":\"Who founded the All India Trade Union Congress in 1920?\",\"options\":[\"Lala Lajpat Rai\",\"Narayan Meghaji Lokhande\",\"Shivram Janba Kamble\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Narayan Meghaji Lokhande was the first president of AITUC.\"},{\"q\":\"The Mahad Satyagraha was led by which leader?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar\",\"Sane Guruji\",\"Lala Lajpat Rai\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Ambedkar organized the Mahad Satyagraha to open a public water tank.\"},{\"q\":\"Which act abolished the Balutedari System in Kolhapur?\",\"options\":[\"Caste Discrimination Act\",\"Balutedari Abolition Act\",\"Balutedari System Abolition Act\",\"Caste System Act\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Balutedari System Abolition Act of 1918 freed Dalits from bonded labour.\"},{\"q\":\"The Red Cross Society in Rajkot was founded by whom?\",\"options\":[\"Sane Guruji\",\"Rakhmabai Save\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Dr. Anandibai Joshi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rakhmabai Save established the Red Cross Society in Rajkot.\"},{\"q\":\"Which newspaper was started by Dr. Ambedkar to spread his ideas?\",\"options\":[\"Muknayak\",\"Janata\",\"Samata\",\"All India Daily\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Ambedkar started the newspaper Muknayak.\"},{\"q\":\"The Congress Socialist Party was formed in which year?\",\"options\":[\"1925\",\"1934\",\"1942\",\"1956\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"It was formed in 1934 as a socialist wing of the Congress.\"},{\"q\":\"The Satyashodhak Samaj was founded by which reformer?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Jyotirao Phule\",\"Mahatma Phule\",\"Dr. Ambedkar\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Phule founded the Satyashodhak Samaj to fight untouchability.\"},{\"q\":\"The Meerut Conspiracy Case was a crackdown on which group?\",\"options\":[\"Peasant Union\",\"Workers Union\",\"Communists\",\"Dalit Movement\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"It targeted communist activists accused of conspiracy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which slogan was used by the Satyashodhak Samaj?\",\"options\":[\"Educate, Organise, Agitate\",\"Be Educated, Be Organised, Be Agitated\",\"Fight, Resist, Win\",\"Work, Unite, Win\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Satyashodhak Samaj used that slogan.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22379,61,8,'History & Civics','en','India Gains Independence','[\"India gained independence on 15 August 1947 after years of struggle by the Indian National Congress and other groups.\",\"The British government, after World‑II, realised it could not keep India under its rule and started planning for independence.\",\"The Cabinet Mission of 1946 was a British plan to give India a constitution and decide on partition, but it was not accepted by all parties.\",\"The Muslim League, led by Muhammad Ali Jinnah, demanded a separate Muslim state – Pakistan – and rejected the Cabinet Mission plan.\",\"The Wavell Plan (1945) suggested a federal structure for India but was rejected by the Muslim League and did not work.\",\"Direct Action Day (16 August 1946) was called by the Muslim League to demand Pakistan; it led to widespread riots.\",\"The Interim Government was formed in 1946 with leaders from both Congress and Muslim League but could not function smoothly.\",\"Lord Mountbatten was appointed as the last Viceroy and helped draft the Indian Independence Act of 1947.\",\"The Indian Independence Act, passed on 18 August 1947, ended British rule and created two independent dominions – India and Pakistan.\",\"The Indian flag was raised at midnight on 15 August 1947, replacing the Union Jack.\",\"The Partition caused massive displacement and communal violence across the sub‑continent.\",\"Mahatma Gandhi was assassinated on 30 January 1948, shortly after independence.\",\"The Constitution of India was drafted by the Constituent Assembly, which met in Delhi from 1946 to 1950.\",\"The princely states were given the option to join India or Pakistan or remain independent, but most joined India.\",\"India’s independence marked the end of 150 years of British colonial rule in the sub‑continent.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which plan was proposed by the British in 1946 to give India a constitution and decide on partition?\",\"options\":[\"Wavell Plan\",\"Cabinet Mission\",\"Mountbatten Plan\",\"Direct Action Day\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Cabinet Mission was a 1946 British plan to draft a constitution and decide on partition.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main demand of the Muslim League in 1946?\",\"options\":[\"A united India with special Muslim rights\",\"A separate Muslim state called Pakistan\",\"Full independence for all provinces\",\"A federal structure within India\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Muslim League demanded a separate Muslim state, which became Pakistan.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the last Viceroy of India?\",\"options\":[\"Lord Wavell\",\"Lord Mountbatten\",\"Lord Attlee\",\"Lord Cripps\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lord Mountbatten was appointed as the last Viceroy and helped draft the Independence Act.\"},{\"q\":\"When was Direct Action Day declared by the Muslim League?\",\"options\":[\"16 August 1945\",\"16 August 1946\",\"16 August 1947\",\"16 August 1948\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Direct Action Day was called on 16 August 1946 to demand Pakistan.\"},{\"q\":\"Which act ended British rule in India on 15 August 1947?\",\"options\":[\"Indian Independence Act\",\"Mountbatten Plan\",\"Cabinet Mission Act\",\"Wavell Plan Act\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Indian Independence Act of 1947 ended British rule.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main reason the Interim Government could not function smoothly?\",\"options\":[\"Lack of funding\",\"Opposition from the Muslim League\",\"Disagreement over partition\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"All of the above factors hindered the Interim Government.\"},{\"q\":\"Which leader was assassinated on 30 January 1948?\",\"options\":[\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Sardar Patel\",\"Muhammad Jinnah\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Gandhi was assassinated on 30 January 1948.\"},{\"q\":\"What did the Indian Independence Act allow princely states to do?\",\"options\":[\"Join India or Pakistan\",\"Remain independent\",\"Join only India\",\"Join only Pakistan\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Act gave princely states the choice to join India or Pakistan.\"},{\"q\":\"Which plan suggested a federal structure for India but was rejected by the Muslim League?\",\"options\":[\"Cabinet Mission\",\"Wavell Plan\",\"Mountbatten Plan\",\"Direct Action Plan\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Wavell Plan proposed a federal structure but was rejected.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the leader of the Indian National Congress during independence?\",\"options\":[\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"Sardar Patel\",\"Muhammad Jinnah\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Jawaharlal Nehru became the first Prime Minister of independent India.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22380,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Fulfillment of Struggle for Independence','[\"India gained independence on 15 August 1947 but many princely states were still independent.\",\"The Indian government invited all princely states to join either India or Pakistan.\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel negotiated with the rulers and secured the accession of most states.\",\"Junagadh, a princely state in Saurashtra, initially wanted to join Pakistan but after a referendum it merged with India in 1948.\",\"Hyderabad was the largest princely state; its ruler, the Nizam, wanted independence. Operation Polo (1948) integrated Hyderabad into India.\",\"Kashmir was ruled by Maharaja Hari Singh. Facing invasion by tribal militias from Pakistan, he signed the Instrument of Accession to India in 1947.\",\"The Portuguese controlled Goa, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, and Daman & Diu. In 1961 India liberated these territories by military action.\",\"The French controlled small enclaves like Chandernagore, Karaikal, Mahe, Yanam. They were handed over to India after 1949 plebiscites.\",\"The Marathwada region played a key role in the struggle for Hyderabad’s liberation through the Hyderabad State Congress and Praja Mandals.\",\"The Goa Liberation Movement was led by Dr. T. B. Kunha and the Goa Mukti Samiti, using Satyagraha and armed resistance.\",\"The Instrument of Accession is a legal document by which a princely state joins India, giving the Indian government control over defence, foreign affairs and communications.\",\"The Instrument of Accession was signed by the rulers of Hyderabad, Kashmir, and other princely states.\",\"The integration of princely states was completed by 1948, ending the era of independent princely states in India.\",\"The term ‘Praja Mandal’ means ‘people’s organization’ and was formed to demand political rights in princely states.\",\"The Indian Army’s Operation Polo was a brief but decisive campaign that ended the Nizam’s rule.\",\"The liberation of Goa was celebrated on 19 December 1961, known as Goa Liberation Day.\",\"The integration of French colonies was completed in 1954 after plebiscites.\",\"The integration of Junagadh was decided by a plebiscite held in 1948.\",\"The integration of Hyderabad was achieved through a combination of political persuasion and military action.\",\"The integration of Kashmir was a result of the Maharaja’s decision to accede to India after tribal invasion.\",\"The integration of Dadra & Nagar Haveli was achieved by a small armed force in 1961.\",\"The integration of Goa involved both Satyagraha and a military operation in 1961.\",\"The integration of French colonies involved local plebiscites and negotiations with France.\",\"The integration of princely states was a major achievement of the early Indian republic.\",\"The integration of princely states helped create a unified Indian nation-state.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Which day marks the beginning of independent India?\",\"options\":[\"15 August 1947\",\"26 January 1948\",\"15 August 1948\",\"26 January 1947\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"India’s independence day is 15 August 1947.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the chief negotiator who secured the accession of most princely states?\",\"options\":[\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\",\"V. K. Krishna Menon\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Sardar Patel negotiated with princely rulers.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the name of the military operation that integrated Hyderabad into India?\",\"options\":[\"Operation Vijay\",\"Operation Polo\",\"Operation Meghdoot\",\"Operation Trident\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Operation Polo was the 1948 campaign.\"},{\"q\":\"Which princely state was ruled by Maharaja Hari Singh?\",\"options\":[\"Junagadh\",\"Hyderabad\",\"Kashmir\",\"Gujarat\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Hari Singh was the Maharaja of Kashmir.\"},{\"q\":\"Which document does a princely ruler sign to join India?\",\"options\":[\"Instrument of Accession\",\"Treaty of Union\",\"Constitutional Agreement\",\"Merger Decree\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Instrument of Accession is the legal document.\"},{\"q\":\"Which territory was liberated by India in 1961 through a military operation?\",\"options\":[\"Dadra & Nagar Haveli\",\"Goa\",\"Karaikal\",\"Chandernagore\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Goa was liberated in 1961.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main strategy used by the Goa Liberation Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Satyagraha and armed resistance\",\"Only armed resistance\",\"Only diplomatic negotiations\",\"Only economic sanctions\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Both Satyagraha and armed resistance were used.\"},{\"q\":\"Which French enclave was handed over to India after a 1954 plebiscite?\",\"options\":[\"Chandernagore\",\"Mahe\",\"Karaikal\",\"Yanam\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Chandernagore was handed over after the plebiscite.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the primary reason Junagadh merged with India?\",\"options\":[\"It wanted to join Pakistan\",\"A plebiscite in 1948\",\"The ruler changed his mind\",\"It was annexed by the Nizam\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"A plebiscite in 1948 led to its merger.\"},{\"q\":\"Who led the Hyderabad State Congress during the struggle for Hyderabad’s liberation?\",\"options\":[\"Sardar Patel\",\"Swami Ramananda Tirth\",\"Dr. T. B. Kunha\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Swami Ramananda Tirth was a key leader.\"}]','nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22381,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Formation of State of Maharashtra','[\"Formation of Maharashtra state on linguistic basis after independence.\",\"Demand for Marathi-speaking state grew from early 20th century.\",\"Samyukta Maharashtra Movement aimed to unite all Marathi-speaking regions including Mumbai.\",\"Dar Commission (1948) rejected linguistic states at that time.\",\"JVP Committee (1948-49) accepted linguistic states in principle but delayed implementation.\",\"Nagpur Pact (1953) assured equitable development for Vidarbha and Marathwada.\",\"States Reorganisation Act, 1956 created bilingual Bombay State (Marathi and Gujarati).\",\"Continued agitation led to formation of separate Maharashtra and Gujarat states on 1 May 1960.\",\"Yashwantrao Chavan became the first Chief Minister of Maharashtra.\",\"Hutatma Smarak in Mumbai commemorates 106 martyrs of the movement.\",\"Newspapers like Kesari, Sakal, Navakal and Shahirs played key role in spreading awareness.\",\"Leaders like Senapati Bapat, Acharya Atre, Comrade Dange guided the movement.\",\"Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti coordinated the movement from 1956 onwards.\",\"Mumbai\'s inclusion in Maharashtra was a major demand and finally achieved in 1960.\",\"Article 371(2) provided special provisions for Vidarbha and Marathwada in Maharashtra.\"]','[{\"q\":\"On which date was the state of Maharashtra officially formed?\",\"options\":[\"1 May 1956\",\"1 May 1960\",\"1 November 1956\",\"1 November 1960\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Maharashtra was formed on 1 May 1960 after the Bombay Reorganisation Act.\"},{\"q\":\"Which commission was appointed in 1948 to study the formation of linguistic provinces?\",\"options\":[\"Dar Commission\",\"JVP Committee\",\"Fazal Ali Commission\",\"Nagpur Pact Committee\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The Dar Commission was appointed on 17 June 1948 under Justice S.K. Dar.\"},{\"q\":\"Who was the chairman of the JVP Committee formed in December 1948?\",\"options\":[\"Jawaharlal Nehru\",\"Vallabhbhai Patel\",\"Pattabhi Sitaramayya\",\"All of the above\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"JVP Committee included Nehru, Patel, and Pattabhi Sitaramayya; named after their initials.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the main outcome of the Nagpur Pact of 1953?\",\"options\":[\"Creation of bilingual Bombay State\",\"Assurance of equitable development for Vidarbha and Marathwada\",\"Merger of Marathwada with Hyderabad\",\"Formation of Gujarat state\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Nagpur Pact assured fair financial and developmental provisions for Vidarbha and Marathwada.\"},{\"q\":\"Which article was added to the Constitution in 1956 to provide special provisions for certain regions of Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Article 370\",\"Article 371(2)\",\"Article 356\",\"Article 360\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Article 371(2) was added to ensure equitable development and state services in Vidarbha and Marathwada.\"},{\"q\":\"Who proposed the resolution for Samyukta Maharashtra with Mumbai in the Mumbai Municipal Corporation?\",\"options\":[\"G.T. Madkholkar\",\"Acharya Atre\",\"D.V. Potdar\",\"Shankarrao Dev\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Acharya Atre got the proposal for Samyukta Maharashtra with Mumbai passed in the Municipal Corporation.\"},{\"q\":\"Who became the first Chief Minister of Maharashtra after its formation in 1960?\",\"options\":[\"Yashwantrao Chavan\",\"Prithviraj Chavan\",\"Shankarrao Chavan\",\"Vilasrao Deshmukh\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Yashwantrao Chavan accepted the responsibility as the first Chief Minister of Maharashtra.\"},{\"q\":\"Which event marked the beginning of the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Nagpur Pact, 1953\",\"Sahitya Sammelan at Belgaon, 12 May 1946\",\"Formation of Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti, 1956\",\"Bilingual Bombay State, 1956\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The movement began with a resolution passed at the Sahitya Sammelan in Belgaon on 12 May 1946.\"},{\"q\":\"What was the purpose of the \'Hutatma Smarak\' erected in Mumbai?\",\"options\":[\"To honour the formation of Gujarat\",\"To commemorate 106 martyrs of the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement\",\"To celebrate the bilingual Bombay State\",\"To mark the Nagpur Pact\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Hutatma Smarak near Flora Fountain was built in memory of 106 people who died in police firing during the movement.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following leaders was NOT associated with the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement?\",\"options\":[\"Senapati Bapat\",\"Acharya Atre\",\"Comrade Shripad Dange\",\"Mahatma Gandhi\"],\"answer\":3,\"explain\":\"Mahatma Gandhi died in 1948 and was not directly involved in the post-independence Maharashtra statehood movement.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29');
INSERT INTO `ep_topic_packs` VALUES (22382,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Introduction to Parliamentary System','[\"Parliamentary system is a form of government where the executive is responsible to the legislature.\",\"India adopted the parliamentary system from England, where it developed historically.\",\"The Indian Parliament consists of the President, Lok Sabha, and Rajya Sabha.\",\"Lok Sabha members are directly elected by the people; Rajya Sabha members are indirectly elected.\",\"The party with more than half the seats in Lok Sabha forms the government; its leader becomes Prime Minister.\",\"If no party gets a majority, parties may form a coalition government.\",\"The Prime Minister and Council of Ministers are collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.\",\"A vote of no-confidence can remove the government if it loses the legislature\'s confidence.\",\"The legislature (Parliament) is supreme and discusses public welfare, laws, and policies.\",\"Opposition parties participate in discussions, suggest improvements, and check government power.\",\"India adopted this system due to familiarity during British rule and discussions in the Constituent Assembly.\",\"The parliamentary system ensures accountability through regular elections and legislative oversight.\",\"Collective responsibility means the entire Council of Ministers is answerable for decisions of any ministry.\",\"The President is the nominal head; real executive power lies with the Prime Minister and Cabinet.\",\"Parliamentary system encourages debate, representation, and responsiveness to public opinion.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the head of the government in India\'s parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"President\",\"Prime Minister\",\"Chief Justice\",\"Speaker of Lok Sabha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Prime Minister is the head of government and leads the Council of Ministers.\"},{\"q\":\"Which house of Parliament has members directly elected by the people?\",\"options\":[\"Rajya Sabha\",\"Lok Sabha\",\"Both\",\"Neither\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Lok Sabha members are directly elected; Rajya Sabha members are elected by state legislatures.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term for a government formed when no single party gets a majority?\",\"options\":[\"Presidential government\",\"Coalition government\",\"Military government\",\"Technocratic government\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"When no party has a clear majority, parties join to form a coalition government.\"},{\"q\":\"To whom is the Council of Ministers responsible in a parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"President only\",\"Judiciary\",\"Lok Sabha\",\"Prime Minister only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha and must retain its confidence.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of \'collective responsibility\' in a parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"Each minister works independently\",\"The entire Council is answerable for decisions of any ministry\",\"Only the PM is responsible\",\"Ministers are not accountable\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Collective responsibility means the whole Council of Ministers stands or falls together.\"},{\"q\":\"Which country is considered the origin of the parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"USA\",\"France\",\"England\",\"Switzerland\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The parliamentary system developed historically in England.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if the Lok Sabha passes a vote of no-confidence against the government?\",\"options\":[\"Government continues\",\"President rules directly\",\"Government must resign\",\"Judiciary intervenes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A vote of no-confidence means the government has lost the legislature\'s confidence and must resign.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the nominal head of the Indian state in the parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"Prime Minister\",\"President\",\"Chief Justice\",\"Vice President\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The President is the constitutional (nominal) head; real power lies with the Prime Minister.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a key feature of the parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"Fixed term for executive\",\"Executive not part of legislature\",\"Executive is part of legislature and responsible to it\",\"Judiciary controls executive\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"In parliamentary systems, ministers are usually members of legislature and accountable to it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did India adopt the parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"It was imposed by foreign powers\",\"Due to familiarity during British rule and Constituent Assembly debates\",\"Because it is the only system in the world\",\"To copy the USA\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"India adopted it due to experience under British rule and extensive discussions in the Constituent Assembly.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22383,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civics: The Parliament of India','[\"India follows a parliamentary system of government where the executive is responsible to the legislature.\",\"The President of India is the nominal head of state, while the Prime Minister is the real head of government.\",\"The Parliament of India consists of the President, Lok Sabha, and Rajya Sabha.\",\"Lok Sabha is the lower house, directly elected by the people, with a term of five years.\",\"Rajya Sabha is the upper house, with members indirectly elected by state legislative assemblies, and is a permanent body.\",\"One-third of Rajya Sabha members retire every two years, ensuring continuity.\",\"Lok Sabha has a maximum strength of 552 members, including up to 20 from Union Territories and 2 nominated Anglo-Indian members.\",\"Rajya Sabha has a total of 250 members: 238 elected from states and UTs, and 12 nominated by the President.\",\"Money Bills can only be introduced in Lok Sabha; Rajya Sabha can only delay them by 14 days.\",\"The Speaker of Lok Sabha presides over its meetings and maintains order; the Vice President of India is the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\",\"Parliament\'s main functions include law-making, financial control, overseeing the executive, and amending the Constitution.\",\"A responsible government means the Council of Ministers is accountable to Parliament and can be removed by a vote of no confidence.\",\"Opposition parties play a vital role in scrutinizing government policies and ensuring accountability.\",\"To contest Lok Sabha elections, a person must be an Indian citizen and at least 25 years old.\",\"To contest Rajya Sabha elections, a person must be an Indian citizen and at least 30 years old.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the real head of the government in India\'s parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"President\",\"Prime Minister\",\"Chief Justice\",\"Speaker of Lok Sabha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Prime Minister is the real head of government, while the President is the nominal head.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term of Lok Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"2 years\",\"4 years\",\"5 years\",\"6 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Lok Sabha has a fixed term of five years unless dissolved earlier.\"},{\"q\":\"Which house of Parliament is known as the permanent house?\",\"options\":[\"Lok Sabha\",\"Rajya Sabha\",\"Vidhan Sabha\",\"Vidhan Parishad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rajya Sabha is a permanent body and is not subject to dissolution.\"},{\"q\":\"How many members are nominated by the President to Rajya Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"10\",\"12\",\"15\",\"20\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The President nominates 12 members to Rajya Sabha from fields like literature, science, arts, and social service.\"},{\"q\":\"Which house has the exclusive power to introduce Money Bills?\",\"options\":[\"Lok Sabha\",\"Rajya Sabha\",\"Both houses\",\"Neither house\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"Money Bills can only be introduced in Lok Sabha; Rajya Sabha cannot initiate them.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum age to contest Lok Sabha elections?\",\"options\":[\"18 years\",\"21 years\",\"25 years\",\"30 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A candidate must be at least 25 years old to contest Lok Sabha elections.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"President\",\"Vice President\",\"Prime Minister\",\"Speaker of Lok Sabha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Vice President of India serves as the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a function of Parliament?\",\"options\":[\"Law-making\",\"Financial control\",\"Conducting elections\",\"Amending the Constitution\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Conducting elections is the function of the Election Commission, not Parliament.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'responsible government\' mean in the parliamentary system?\",\"options\":[\"The President is responsible to the people\",\"The Council of Ministers is responsible to Parliament\",\"The judiciary is responsible to the executive\",\"The bureaucracy is responsible to the legislature\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"In a responsible government, the Council of Ministers remains in office only as long as it has the confidence of the legislature.\"},{\"q\":\"How often are one-third of the members of Rajya Sabha retired?\",\"options\":[\"Every year\",\"Every two years\",\"Every three years\",\"Every five years\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"One-third of Rajya Sabha members retire every two years to ensure continuity.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22384,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civics: The Central Executive Council','[\"The Union Executive includes the President, Prime Minister, and Council of Ministers.\",\"The President is the nominal head of state; the Prime Minister is the executive head.\",\"The President is indirectly elected by an Electoral College of elected MPs and MLAs.\",\"To be eligible for President, one must be an Indian citizen aged 35 or above.\",\"The President\'s term is five years and can be re-elected.\",\"The President summons, prorogues, addresses, and dissolves Parliament.\",\"No bill becomes law without the President\'s assent.\",\"The President appoints the Prime Minister, judges, governors, and other key officials.\",\"The President is the Supreme Commander of the defence forces.\",\"The President has judicial powers to grant pardons, reprieves, or reduce punishments.\",\"The President can declare three types of emergencies: National, State, and Financial.\",\"Impeachment is the process to remove the President for violating the Constitution.\",\"The Vice-President acts as President in their absence and is elected by both Houses of Parliament.\",\"The Prime Minister is usually the leader of the majority party in Lok Sabha.\",\"The Prime Minister forms the Council of Ministers and allocates portfolios.\",\"The Prime Minister chairs Cabinet meetings and coordinates government policies.\",\"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.\",\"A constitutional amendment limits the Council of Ministers to 15% of Lok Sabha strength.\",\"The Prime Minister aids and advises the President in exercising executive powers.\",\"Parliament controls the executive through debates, question hour, and votes of confidence.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the nominal head of the Indian State?\",\"options\":[\"Prime Minister\",\"President\",\"Chief Justice\",\"Vice-President\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The President is the nominal head of state, while the Prime Minister is the real executive head.\"},{\"q\":\"How is the President of India elected?\",\"options\":[\"Directly by the people\",\"By Lok Sabha only\",\"By an Electoral College of MPs and MLAs\",\"By the Prime Minister\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The President is indirectly elected by an Electoral College consisting of elected members of both Houses of Parliament and State Legislative Assemblies.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum age to contest for the President of India?\",\"options\":[\"25 years\",\"30 years\",\"35 years\",\"40 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A candidate must be an Indian citizen who has completed 35 years of age to be eligible for the Presidency.\"},{\"q\":\"Who administers the oath of office to the President of India?\",\"options\":[\"Prime Minister\",\"Chief Justice of India\",\"Vice-President\",\"Speaker of Lok Sabha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Chief Justice of India administers the oath of office to the President.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a power of the President?\",\"options\":[\"Appointing the Prime Minister\",\"Declaring a Financial Emergency\",\"Removing a judge of the Supreme Court\",\"Being the Supreme Commander of the defence forces\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The President appoints judges but cannot remove them; removal requires impeachment by Parliament.\"},{\"q\":\"Who acts as the President in case of vacancy or absence?\",\"options\":[\"Chief Justice\",\"Prime Minister\",\"Vice-President\",\"Speaker of Lok Sabha\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Vice-President discharges the functions of the President during vacancy or absence.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term of the President of India?\",\"options\":[\"Four years\",\"Five years\",\"Six years\",\"At the pleasure of Parliament\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The President holds office for a term of five years from the date of assuming office.\"},{\"q\":\"Which house is known as the permanent house and is not subject to dissolution?\",\"options\":[\"Lok Sabha\",\"Rajya Sabha\",\"Vidhan Sabha\",\"Vidhan Parishad\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Rajya Sabha is a permanent body; one-third of its members retire every two years, but it is never dissolved.\"},{\"q\":\"Who nominates 12 members to the Rajya Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"President\",\"Vice-President\",\"Prime Minister\",\"Chief Justice\"],\"answer\":0,\"explain\":\"The President nominates 12 members to the Rajya Sabha from persons having special knowledge or practical experience in art, literature, science, and social service.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the maximum limit on the number of ministers in the Council of Ministers as per the 91st Amendment?\",\"options\":[\"10% of Lok Sabha strength\",\"15% of Lok Sabha strength\",\"20% of Lok Sabha strength\",\"25% of Lok Sabha strength\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The 91st Amendment limits the Council of Ministers to not exceed 15% of the total number of members of the Lok Sabha.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22385,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civics: The Judiciary in India','[\"Judiciary is one of the three organs of government, along with legislature and executive.\",\"The judiciary interprets laws and delivers justice to protect citizens\' rights.\",\"India has a single, integrated judicial system for the entire country.\",\"The Supreme Court is the apex court, headed by the Chief Justice of India.\",\"High Courts exist in each state; there are currently 24 High Courts in India.\",\"District and lower courts function under the supervision of High Courts.\",\"Judges are appointed by the President and enjoy security of tenure.\",\"Supreme Court judges retire at 65 years; High Court judges at 62 years.\",\"Judges\' salaries are paid from the Consolidated Fund of India, not debated in Parliament.\",\"Judicial Review is the power of courts to strike down laws violating the Constitution.\",\"Public Interest Litigation (PIL) allows citizens to approach courts for public welfare issues.\",\"Judicial Activism refers to courts actively promoting justice and equality beyond traditional roles.\",\"Contempt of Court punishes criticism that undermines judicial authority and independence.\",\"Impeachment is the process to remove judges for proven misconduct or incapacity.\",\"The rule of law ensures equality before law for all citizens, regardless of status.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who appoints the Chief Justice of India and other judges of the Supreme Court?\",\"options\":[\"Prime Minister\",\"President\",\"Parliament\",\"Collegium of Judges\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The President appoints the Chief Justice and other Supreme Court judges under constitutional provisions.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the retirement age for judges of the Supreme Court of India?\",\"options\":[\"60 years\",\"62 years\",\"65 years\",\"70 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Supreme Court judges retire at the age of 65 years as per constitutional provision.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a function of the High Court?\",\"options\":[\"Supervising lower courts\",\"Protecting fundamental rights\",\"Appointing the Prime Minister\",\"Advising the Governor on district court appointments\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"High Courts do not appoint the Prime Minister; that is the President\'s role based on parliamentary majority.\"},{\"q\":\"What does Judicial Review mean in the Indian context?\",\"options\":[\"Reviewing court salaries\",\"Revising the Constitution\",\"Striking down laws violating the Constitution\",\"Reviewing parliamentary proceedings\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Judicial Review is the power of judiciary to declare laws or executive actions unconstitutional and void.\"},{\"q\":\"Which court is known as the apex court in India?\",\"options\":[\"High Court\",\"District Court\",\"Supreme Court\",\"Sessions Court\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Supreme Court is the highest judicial authority in India, below which are High Courts and subordinate courts.\"},{\"q\":\"How many High Courts are currently there in India?\",\"options\":[\"20\",\"22\",\"24\",\"28\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"There are 24 High Courts in India, one in each state or shared among states.\"},{\"q\":\"What is Public Interest Litigation (PIL)?\",\"options\":[\"A case filed by the government\",\"A case filed by a lawyer for profit\",\"A case filed by citizens for public welfare\",\"A case about criminal punishment only\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"PIL allows individuals or organizations to approach courts on behalf of the public for issues affecting society at large.\"},{\"q\":\"From which fund are the salaries of judges in India paid?\",\"options\":[\"State Treasury\",\"Consolidated Fund of India\",\"Prime Minister\'s Relief Fund\",\"Lok Sabha Secretariat\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Judges\' salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India to ensure financial independence from Parliament.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the term of the President of India?\",\"options\":[\"Three years\",\"Four years\",\"Five years\",\"Six years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The President of India is elected for a term of five years from the date of assuming office.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a reason for maintaining judicial independence?\",\"options\":[\"To allow Parliament to override court decisions\",\"To enable judges to give justice without fear or favor\",\"To let the executive control court judgments\",\"To reduce the number of courts in the country\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Judicial independence ensures judges can perform their duties impartially without pressure from legislature or executive.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22386,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civics: State Governance','[\"State government operates at regional level in a federal system.\",\"federal setup.\",\"Maharashtra has a bicameral legislature: Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly) and Vidhan Parishad (Legislative Council).\",\"Vidhan Sabha has 288 members, elected from territorial constituencies, tenure of 5 years.\",\"Vidhan Parishad has 78 members, indirectly elected, not fully dissolved; 1\\/3 retire every 2 years.\",\"Governor is the nominal head of the state, appointed by the President, holds office during the President\'s pleasure.\",\"Chief Minister is the real executive head, leader of the majority party in Vidhan Sabha, forms the Council of Ministers.\",\"Council of Ministers includes portfolios distributed based on experience, skills, and representation of social groups.\",\"Speaker of Vidhan Sabha presides over proceedings, elected by members, responsible for discipline and order.\",\"Chief Minister coordinates ministries, ensures cooperation, and is collectively responsible to the Vidhan Sabha.\",\"Governor can summon sessions, assent to bills, and issue ordinances when legislature is not in session.\",\"State legislature makes laws on state subjects, approves budget, and oversees executive.\",\"Judiciary protects individual rights, federal structure, and Constitution; has power of judicial review.\",\"Public Interest Litigation (PIL) allows public-spirited individuals to approach courts for public causes.\",\"Judicial activism refers to courts taking proactive role in safeguarding rights and ensuring justice.\",\"First Information Report (FIR) is the first step in criminal procedure, filed with police for serious crimes.\"]','[{\"q\":\"Who is the nominal head of the state government in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"Chief Minister\",\"Governor\",\"President\",\"Speaker of Vidhan Sabha\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Governor is the nominal head of the state, appointed by the President.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the tenure of the Vidhan Sabha in Maharashtra?\",\"options\":[\"2 years\",\"4 years\",\"5 years\",\"6 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Vidhan Sabha has a tenure of 5 years, unless dissolved earlier.\"},{\"q\":\"Which house of the Maharashtra legislature is never fully dissolved?\",\"options\":[\"Vidhan Sabha\",\"Vidhan Parishad\",\"Both\",\"Neither\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Vidhan Parishad is a permanent body; one-third of its members retire every two years.\"},{\"q\":\"Who appoints the Governor of a state in India?\",\"options\":[\"Chief Minister\",\"Prime Minister\",\"President\",\"Chief Justice of India\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Governor is appointed by the President of India.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is the real executive head of the state government?\",\"options\":[\"Governor\",\"Chief Minister\",\"Speaker\",\"President\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"The Chief Minister is the real executive head and leads the Council of Ministers.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the minimum age to contest elections to the Vidhan Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"18 years\",\"21 years\",\"25 years\",\"30 years\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"A citizen must be at least 25 years old to contest elections to the Vidhan Sabha.\"},{\"q\":\"Which session of the Maharashtra legislature is held in Nagpur?\",\"options\":[\"Budget session\",\"Monsoon session\",\"Winter session\",\"Special session\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The winter session of the Maharashtra legislature is conducted in Nagpur.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of the Speaker in the Vidhan Sabha?\",\"options\":[\"To make laws\",\"To appoint ministers\",\"To preside over proceedings\",\"To approve the budget\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The Speaker presides over the proceedings of the Vidhan Sabha and maintains order.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a provision to preserve judicial independence?\",\"options\":[\"Judges appointed by the executive\",\"Judges can be removed easily\",\"Security of tenure and fixed service conditions\",\"Judges depend on legislature for salary\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Judicial independence is preserved by security of tenure and fixed service conditions.\"},{\"q\":\"What does Public Interest Litigation (PIL) allow?\",\"options\":[\"Only victims to file cases\",\"Any public-spirited individual to file a case for public cause\",\"Only lawyers to file cases\",\"Only the government to file cases\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"PIL allows any public-spirited individual to approach the court for a cause affecting the public interest.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29'),(22387,61,8,'History & Civics','en','Civics: Bureaucracy','[\"Bureaucracy is the administrative system of civil servants that implements government policies.\",\"It is a permanent mechanism; civil servants remain in service despite changes in elected government.\",\"Bureaucracy carries out two main functions: defense\\/security and public welfare\\/services.\",\"Civil services in India are divided into All-India, Central (Union), and State services.\",\"All-India Services include IAS, IPS, and IFS, recruited by UPSC and serving both Centre and States.\",\"Union Civil Services include IRS, IFS, etc., serving under the Central Government.\",\"State Civil Services include roles like Deputy Collector, Tehsildar, recruited by State PSC.\",\"Civil servants are expected to be politically neutral and not align with any political party.\",\"Anonymity means civil servants are not publicly credited or blamed; ministers take responsibility.\",\"Merit-based recruitment is ensured by UPSC and State PSCs through competitive exams.\",\"Reservation policy in civil services ensures representation of SC, ST, OBC, women, and disabled persons.\",\"Civil servants provide expert advice to ministers, who are political heads but may lack technical knowledge.\",\"Bureaucracy ensures stability in administration by maintaining continuity in governance.\",\"It plays a key role in social transformation by implementing laws on welfare, education, and equality.\",\"Effective minister-civil servant dialogue based on trust improves departmental functioning.\"]','[{\"q\":\"What is the meaning of bureaucracy in the context of government?\",\"options\":[\"Elected representatives in Parliament\",\"Administrative system of civil servants\",\"Judicial system of courts\",\"Military forces defending the country\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bureaucracy refers to the administrative system run by civil servants who implement government policies.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a function of bureaucracy?\",\"options\":[\"Defense and internal security\",\"Revenue collection and law enforcement\",\"Making laws in Parliament\",\"Implementing welfare schemes\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Making laws is the function of the legislature (Parliament), not bureaucracy.\"},{\"q\":\"Which service is responsible for maintaining law and order in a district?\",\"options\":[\"Indian Foreign Service (IFS)\",\"Indian Administrative Service (IAS)\",\"Indian Police Service (IPS)\",\"Indian Revenue Service (IRS)\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"IPS officers are responsible for law and order and policing in states and districts.\"},{\"q\":\"What does \'permanent mechanism\' mean in relation to bureaucracy?\",\"options\":[\"Civil servants change with every election\",\"Bureaucracy remains unchanged despite changes in government\",\"Only ministers are permanent\",\"Policies are changed frequently\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bureaucracy is permanent; civil servants continue in service even when ministers change after elections.\"},{\"q\":\"Who is responsible for the success or failure of a government department?\",\"options\":[\"Civil servant in charge\",\"Prime Minister\",\"Minister heading the department\",\"President of India\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"The minister, as political head, is accountable for departmental performance; civil servants remain anonymous.\"},{\"q\":\"Which body recruits candidates for All-India Services like IAS and IPS?\",\"options\":[\"State Public Service Commission\",\"Union Public Service Commission (UPSC)\",\"Election Commission\",\"Planning Commission\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"UPSC conducts exams for All-India Services and Central Services.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of reservation in civil services?\",\"options\":[\"To reduce government spending\",\"To ensure representation of weaker sections\",\"To increase political influence\",\"To promote only meritorious candidates regardless of background\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Reservation ensures that SC, ST, OBC, women, and disabled persons get fair access to civil services.\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a State Civil Service?\",\"options\":[\"Indian Foreign Service\",\"Indian Revenue Service\",\"Deputy District Collector\",\"Indian Forest Service\"],\"answer\":2,\"explain\":\"Deputy District Collector is a State Civil Service post under state government.\"},{\"q\":\"What does political neutrality of civil servants mean?\",\"options\":[\"They must support the ruling party\",\"They must not take political stands and serve any government impartially\",\"They cannot vote in elections\",\"They must join a political party after retirement\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Civil servants must serve the government in power with equal commitment, regardless of personal political views.\"},{\"q\":\"How does bureaucracy contribute to social transformation?\",\"options\":[\"By opposing new laws\",\"By implementing government policies on welfare and equality\",\"By controlling the judiciary\",\"By electing representatives\"],\"answer\":1,\"explain\":\"Bureaucracy implements laws related to women’s empowerment, education, reservation, etc., bringing social change.\"}]','nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b','2026-10-03 16:25:29');
